var e=[];e.push({id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination`,title:`Cardiovascular examination: pulse, JVP, apex, heart sounds and murmurs`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`The **cardiovascular examination** is a structured bedside assessment - general survey, **arterial pulse, blood pressure (both arms and postural), JVP, precordium, heart sounds and murmurs tested with dynamic manoeuvres** - that names the lesion, grades its **severity** from signs rather than loudness, and decides which test (ECG, echo) comes next.`,keywords:[`CVS examination`,`JVP`,`jugular venous pressure`,`pulsus paradoxus`,`collapsing pulse`,`Kussmaul sign`,`apex beat`,`parasternal heave`,`S3 gallop`,`S4`,`splitting of S2`,`opening snap`,`Levine grading`,`Valsalva manoeuvre`,`Austin Flint murmur`,`Graham Steell murmur`,`Carvallo sign`,`Duroziez sign`,`radio-femoral delay`,`innocent murmur`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, purpose and the Indian burden`,points:[`The **cardiovascular examination** answers four questions in order: is there heart disease, which **lesion**, how **severe**, and is there **decompensation** (failure, pulmonary hypertension, endocarditis, arrhythmia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular disease** is the leading cause of death in India, accounting for about **28% of all deaths**, and is rising fastest in younger adults and rural areas. [India State-Level Disease Burden Initiative 2018]`,`**Rheumatic heart disease** still supplies most valve long cases in India - India carries the largest share of the global RHD burden, mostly young adults and women of child-bearing age. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Auscultation misses most early valve disease**: in Indian schoolchildren echo found RHD in about **20 per 1000** against under **1 per 1000** by clinical examination. [RHEUMATIC study, Heart 2011]`,`Despite echo, the bedside examination remains the **gatekeeper**: it decides who needs echo and detects what echo does not show - pulse character, JVP, postural BP and peripheral signs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Degenerative valve disease** (calcific AS, MR) is rising in India with longer life expectancy, so every older adult needs an active search for a **slow-rising pulse and ejection murmur**. [ESC/EACTS 2025 VHD]`,`**Congenital heart disease** affects about **8 per 1000 live births**; an ASD or PDA can first present as an incidental murmur in an adult OPD or in pregnancy. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Order for a long case**: general survey, hands, pulse, BP, face and eyes, neck (JVP, carotids), precordium (inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate), lung bases and sacrum, abdomen (liver), legs (oedema, peripheral pulses). [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`General survey: clues to the cause`,points:[`**Posture and breathing**: propped up with orthopnoea, tachypnoea at rest and accessory muscle use suggest pulmonary venous congestion; **Cheyne-Stokes breathing** marks advanced heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Central cyanosis** (tongue, lips) means a right-to-left shunt or lung disease; **peripheral cyanosis** alone means low output or vasoconstriction; **differential cyanosis** (feet blue, hands pink) is a reversed PDA shunt. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Clubbing** in a cardiac patient means **cyanotic congenital heart disease** or **subacute infective endocarditis** - always look for both. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endocarditis stigmata**: **splinter haemorrhages**, **Osler nodes** (tender, finger pulps), **Janeway lesions** (painless, palms and soles), **Roth spots** on fundoscopy, splenomegaly and microscopic haematuria. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Lipid stigmata**: **tendon xanthomas** (Achilles, hand extensors - familial hypercholesterolaemia), xanthelasma, **corneal arcus under 45 years** and eruptive xanthomas (severe hypertriglyceridaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Syndromic clues**: **Marfan** (AR, MVP, dissection), **Down** (AV septal defect), **Turner** (coarctation, bicuspid valve), **Noonan** (pulmonary stenosis), **Holt-Oram** (thumb anomaly with ASD), **Williams** (supravalvar AS). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Face and neck**: **malar flush** (severe MS), stooped spine of **ankylosing spondylitis** (AR), **thyroid eye signs and goitre** (AF, high-output state) and **pallor** (anaemia with a flow murmur). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Rheumatic fever in the young**: subcutaneous nodules over extensor surfaces, erythema marginatum and migratory arthritis - active carditis changes management. [AHA 2015 Jones criteria]`,`**Oedema**: pitting ankle oedema with a **raised JVP** points to the heart; oedema with a **normal JVP** points to drugs (amlodipine), venous disease, hypoalbuminaemia or lymphoedema - see the leg oedema topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`Record **respiratory rate, SpO2 and temperature** at the start - **fever with a murmur is endocarditis until proved otherwise**. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Physiology behind the signs`,points:[`**S1** is closure of the **mitral then tricuspid** valve at the start of ventricular systole; **S2** is closure of the **aortic (A2) then pulmonary (P2)** valve at the end of systole. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inspiration** lowers intrathoracic pressure, increasing venous return to the **right heart** and pooling blood in the lungs: RV ejection lengthens (P2 later) and LV filling falls (A2 earlier) - hence **physiological splitting** and louder right-sided murmurs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Murmurs** are turbulence from high-velocity flow across a narrowed or leaking valve, high flow across a normal valve, or flow into a dilated chamber; loudness reflects **velocity and gradient, not severity**. [Harrison 22e]`,`So a **tiny VSD is loud** (maladie de Roger) while **severe AS with a failing LV can be soft** - the reason severity is judged by the company the murmur keeps. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulse pressure** depends on **stroke volume and arterial compliance**: wide in AR, PDA, high-output states and stiff elderly arteries; narrow in severe AS, tamponade, severe MS and shock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pressure overload** (AS, hypertension) causes concentric hypertrophy with a **sustained, heaving apex**; **volume overload** (MR, AR, VSD) causes eccentric dilatation with a **displaced, hyperdynamic apex**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`The **JVP mirrors right atrial pressure** because no valve separates the right internal jugular vein from the right atrium and they lie in a straight line - so the **right IJV**, not the external jugular, is used. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus** in tamponade: the fixed pericardial volume makes the ventricles compete - inspiratory RV filling bows the septum left, LV filling and stroke volume fall, and systolic BP drops **more than 10 mmHg**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Kussmaul sign** appears when a rigid pericardium or stiff RV cannot accept the inspiratory rise in venous return, so the **JVP rises instead of falling** on inspiration. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**S3** is abrupt deceleration of blood rapidly filling an overfilled or failing ventricle in **early diastole**; **S4** is the atrial kick into a **stiff ventricle** in late diastole, so it needs atrial contraction and **disappears in AF**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Arterial pulse`,points:[`Assess **rate and rhythm at the radial**, **character and volume at the carotid** (or brachial), then **radio-radial and radio-femoral** timing and all peripheral pulses down to dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial. [Hutchison 25e]`,`Count for **at least 30 seconds** and a **full minute when irregular**; compare with the **apical rate** - a **pulse deficit** (apex faster than radial) means beats too weak to reach the wrist, typical of fast AF. [Hutchison 25e]`,`Palpate the carotid **one side at a time**, gently, at the level of the thyroid cartilage - never press both together and never massage a carotid with a bruit. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Rhythm**: regularly irregular suggests ectopics in bigeminy or second-degree AV block; **irregularly irregular** suggests **AF**, multifocal atrial tachycardia or frequent ectopics - the ECG decides. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Slow-rising (anacrotic, parvus et tardus)** pulse - low amplitude with a delayed peak - is the most reliable bedside sign of **severe valvular AS**, though stiff elderly carotids can mask it. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Collapsing (water-hammer) pulse** is felt as a tapping impulse with the patient's arm raised and your palm over the wrist: **AR**, PDA, AV fistula and high-output states (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, beriberi, Paget disease). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Pulsus bisferiens** (two systolic peaks) occurs in severe AR, mixed AS with AR and **HCM**; a **jerky (spike-and-dome)** pulse with a brisk upstroke is typical of **obstructive HCM**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Dicrotic pulse** (a second peak in diastole) needs low stroke volume with low resistance - **dilated cardiomyopathy, septic shock, typhoid fever**; **pulsus alternans** (alternate strong and weak beats in regular rhythm) means **severe LV systolic dysfunction**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Radio-femoral delay** with weak femoral pulses in a young hypertensive is **coarctation of the aorta**; an absent or weaker radial suggests **subclavian stenosis, Takayasu arteritis or aortic dissection**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Relative bradycardia** (pulse slower than expected for the fever) suggests **typhoid, dengue, leptospirosis or legionella** - or a beta-blocker or conduction disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Blood pressure: technique, both arms and postural`,points:[`**Technique**: seated 5 minutes, back supported, feet flat, arm at **heart level**, cuff bladder encircling **80% of arm circumference** (width about 40%), validated upper-arm device, no talking. [ESC 2024]`,`Estimate systolic by **palpation first**, then inflate 20-30 mmHg above it - this avoids missing the **auscultatory gap** (a silent interval in elderly hypertensives) and under-reading systolic BP. [AHA 2019 BP measurement]`,`Deflate at **2-3 mmHg per second**; systolic is **Korotkoff phase I** and diastolic **phase V** (disappearance); use **phase IV** (muffling) only when sounds persist to zero - AR, high-output states, some pregnancies, children. [AHA 2019 BP measurement]`,`Measure **both arms at the first visit** and use the higher arm; a reproducible **inter-arm difference over 10 mmHg** raises cardiovascular risk, and **over 20 mmHg** suggests **subclavian stenosis, Takayasu arteritis or, with chest pain, dissection**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Postural BP**: lie 5 minutes, stand, measure at **1 and 3 minutes**; **orthostatic hypotension** is a sustained fall of **20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic**, or systolic under 90 - see the Syncope topic. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`A **blunted heart-rate rise** on standing (ratio of heart-rate rise to systolic fall under 0.5 beats/min per mmHg) suggests **neurogenic** orthostatic hypotension; a brisk rise suggests **volume depletion or drugs**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Leg BP** (thigh cuff) is normally equal to or slightly higher than arm BP; a leg systolic **lower than the arm** suggests **coarctation** or aorto-iliac disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ankle-brachial index** = higher ankle systolic (dorsalis pedis or posterior tibial) divided by the higher brachial systolic: **0.90 or less is PAD**, 0.91-0.99 borderline, **over 1.40 non-compressible** (diabetes, CKD). [ACC/AHA 2024 PAD]`,`**Pulse pressure of 60 mmHg or more** in an older adult marks large-artery stiffness and counts as hypertension-mediated organ damage. [ESC 2024]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus by cuff**: inflate above systolic, deflate slowly, note when Korotkoff sounds appear **only in expiration** and when they are heard **throughout**; a difference **over 10 mmHg** is pulsus paradoxus. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Jugular venous pressure and waveform`,points:[`**Position**: reclined at **45 degrees**, head on a pillow turned slightly to the left, neck relaxed, tangential light - look for the **right IJV** pulsation between and behind the two heads of sternocleidomastoid. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**JVP** is the **vertical height** of the top of the venous column above the **sternal angle**; normal is **up to 3-4 cm**, and adding 5 cm (sternal angle to mid right atrium) gives right atrial pressure in cm of water. [Harrison 22e]`,`Lay the patient flatter if the JVP is low (hypovolaemia) and **sit upright at 90 degrees** if very high - a JVP at the angle of the jaw is easily missed at 45 degrees. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Venous vs carotid**: the venous pulse is **impalpable**, has a **double waveform**, is **obliterated by light pressure** at the root of the neck, **falls on inspiration**, rises with abdominal pressure and changes with posture. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Waveform**: **a** wave (atrial contraction, just before S1), **c** wave (tricuspid bulging), **x descent** (atrial relaxation - the dominant inward movement in systole), **v** wave (atrial filling, peaks just after S2), **y descent** (tricuspid opening). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Absent a wave** = **AF**; **giant a wave** = tricuspid stenosis, pulmonary stenosis or pulmonary hypertension; **cannon a waves** coming irregularly = **AV dissociation** (complete heart block, VT). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Large cv wave** with earlobe movement and a **pulsatile liver** = **tricuspid regurgitation**; **steep x and y descents** (Friedreich sign) = **constrictive pericarditis**; **absent y descent** = **tamponade**; **slow y descent** = tricuspid stenosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Kussmaul sign** (JVP rises on inspiration) is seen in **constrictive pericarditis**, restrictive cardiomyopathy, **RV infarction** and severe right heart failure - it is unusual in tamponade. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Abdominojugular reflux**: firm periumbilical pressure for **10-15 seconds**; a **sustained rise of 3 cm or more** that falls abruptly on release indicates raised right-sided filling pressure and supports heart failure in a breathless patient. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Raised non-pulsatile JVP** with facial plethora, dilated chest wall veins and no abdominojugular reflux is **superior vena cava obstruction** (bronchogenic carcinoma, lymphoma). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Precordium: inspection, palpation, percussion`,points:[`**Inspect** for deformity (pectus excavatum, kyphoscoliosis) and scars - **left lateral thoracotomy** (closed mitral valvotomy, PDA ligation, coarctation repair), **midline sternotomy** (CABG, valve surgery), **infraclavicular pocket** (pacemaker or ICD). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Apex beat** is the **lowest and outermost point of definite cardiac impulse**; normal in the **left 5th intercostal space at or just inside the midclavicular line**, no larger than about 2.5 cm (one intercostal space). [Harrison 22e]`,`Locate the apex **supine**; the left lateral position helps feel its character but **displaces it**, so do not judge position there. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Displaced apex**: LV dilatation (MR, AR, DCM) or mediastinal shift from chest deformity, effusion, pneumothorax or collapse - check the **trachea** before calling cardiomegaly. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Impalpable apex**: obesity, emphysema, large pericardial effusion, apex behind a rib, or **dextrocardia** (feel on the right). [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Apex character**: **tapping** (palpable S1 of MS), **heaving/sustained** (pressure overload - AS, hypertension, HCM), **hyperdynamic/thrusting** (volume overload - MR, AR, VSD, PDA), **double** (HCM), **diffuse** (DCM), **ectopic** (LV aneurysm). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Left parasternal heave** (heel of the hand at the left sternal edge) means **RV hypertrophy** - pulmonary hypertension, MS, PS - or the enlarged LA pushing the heart forward in severe MR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Palpable P2** in the left 2nd intercostal space signals **pulmonary hypertension**; **epigastric pulsation** striking the fingertips from above in inspiration is RV, and one felt anteriorly is aortic. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Thrills** are palpable murmurs (grade 4 or louder): systolic at the base (AS, PS), systolic at the left lower sternal edge (VSD), systolic at the apex (MR), diastolic at the apex (MS). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Percussion** of cardiac borders adds little; it detects **loss of cardiac dullness** in emphysema and dullness extending beyond the apex in a **large pericardial effusion**. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`Heart sounds, splitting and added sounds`,points:[`**Loud S1**: **MS with a pliable valve**, short PR, tachycardia and high-output states; **soft S1**: MR, calcified MS, long PR, severe LV dysfunction; **variable S1**: **AF and complete heart block**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Physiological splitting** of S2 is heard best in the **left 2nd intercostal space** - split on inspiration, single on expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wide split that still moves** with respiration: **RBBB**, pulmonary stenosis, MR (early A2) and VSD; **fixed wide splitting** is the hallmark of **ostium secundum ASD**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Reversed (paradoxical) splitting** - split on expiration, single on inspiration - means a delayed A2: **LBBB**, RV pacing, **severe AS**, HCM, large PDA, acute ischaemia. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Single S2**: severe calcific AS (A2 lost), tetralogy of Fallot or pulmonary atresia (P2 lost), **Eisenmenger** VSD and normal old age; **loud P2** (P2 audible at the apex) means **pulmonary hypertension**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S3** - low-pitched, early diastolic, heard with the **bell at the apex in left lateral position** - is normal under about **40 years**, in athletes and pregnancy, and pathological in **heart failure and volume overload (MR)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S4** - late diastolic, just before S1 - is pathological: **LVH (hypertension, AS, HCM), acute MI** and ischaemia; it is **never heard in AF**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opening snap**: high-pitched early diastolic sound after S2 in **MS with a mobile valve**, normally 40-120 ms after A2; the **shorter the A2-OS interval**, the higher the LA pressure and the **more severe** the stenosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ejection click** just after S1: **bicuspid aortic valve** or valvular PS - the pulmonary click is the only right-sided sound that **softens on inspiration**; a **mid-systolic click** with a late systolic murmur = **mitral valve prolapse**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pericardial knock** (earlier and higher-pitched than S3) = **constrictive pericarditis**; **tumour plop** = **atrial myxoma**; scratchy **pericardial rub** = pericarditis; a muffled **mechanical valve click** suggests **valve thrombosis**. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Murmurs: description and grading (the classification examiners ask)`,points:[`Describe every murmur in this order: **timing, site of maximal intensity, radiation, grade, pitch, character, configuration and response to respiration and manoeuvres**. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Levine grading of systolic murmurs**: **1** heard only with effort, **2** soft but readily heard, **3** moderately loud without thrill, **4** loud **with a thrill**, **5** heard with the stethoscope edge, **6** heard with the stethoscope **off** the chest. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diastolic murmurs** are graded **1-4**, and any diastolic murmur is pathological and needs echocardiography. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Time every murmur against the carotid pulse** (the upstroke follows S1) - essential at fast rates, when systole and diastole are of similar length. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Systolic**: **ejection (crescendo-decrescendo)** - AS, PS, HCM, flow murmurs; **pansystolic (plateau, S1 to S2)** - MR, TR, VSD; **late systolic** - MVP, papillary muscle dysfunction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diastolic**: **early diastolic decrescendo**, high-pitched, with the diaphragm - AR, PR; **mid-diastolic rumble**, low-pitched, with the bell - MS, TS, Austin Flint, flow murmurs; **presystolic accentuation** - MS in sinus rhythm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Continuous** murmurs run through S2: **PDA (machinery or Gibson murmur, left infraclavicular)**, AV fistula, ruptured sinus of Valsalva, coronary AV fistula and the benign **venous hum**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Positions**: **left lateral with the bell** at the apex for MS; **sitting forward in held expiration with the diaphragm** at the left 3rd-4th space for AR. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Severity is in the signs**: AS - slow-rising pulse, late-peaking murmur, soft A2, S4; MS - short A2-OS gap, long rumble, pulmonary hypertension; AR - wide pulse pressure, Austin Flint; MR - S3, displaced apex, soft S1. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Innocent murmurs - the 7 S**: **soft** (grade 1-2), **systolic**, **short**, **single** (no click), **small** (localised), **sweet** (not harsh) and **sensitive** to posture, with normal S2 and no symptoms. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Dynamic auscultation: manoeuvres`,points:[`**Inspiration**: right-sided murmurs get louder - **TR (Carvallo sign)**, TS, PS, PR; most left-sided murmurs soften slightly. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Valsalva strain and standing from squatting** reduce venous return: almost every murmur softens **except HCM (louder)** and **MVP (click earlier, murmur longer)**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Squatting and passive leg raising** increase venous return (squatting also raises afterload): most murmurs get louder, **HCM softens** and the **MVP click moves later**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sustained isometric handgrip** (1-2 minutes) raises afterload: **MR, VSD and AR louder**, **AS and HCM softer** - a quick way to separate MR from AS or HCM. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Amyl nitrite inhalation** drops afterload: **AS and HCM louder**, MR, VSD and AR softer; it separates **MS (louder) from Austin Flint (softer)** - now mostly of viva value. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Beat after a long pause** (post-ectopic or a long AF cycle): ejection murmurs (AS, HCM) get **louder**, while the MR murmur **hardly changes** - a classic way to tell AS from MR at the apex. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Square-wave BP response** to Valsalva - BP stays up through the strain with no overshoot on release - is an old bedside marker of **raised filling pressure** in heart failure. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Do manoeuvres after the basic description and **state the expected change before doing them** - examiners reward a hypothesis-driven examination. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`Eponymous and peripheral signs`,points:[`**Aortic regurgitation**: **Corrigan** (visible carotid pulsation), **de Musset** (head nodding), **Quincke** (nail-bed capillary pulsation), **Traube** (pistol-shot femorals), **Duroziez** (to-and-fro femoral murmur on compression), **Muller** (uvular pulsation), **Becker** (retinal artery pulsation). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Hill sign** (popliteal systolic exceeding brachial by **more than 60 mmHg**) suggests **severe AR**; the **Austin Flint** murmur is an apical mid-diastolic rumble from the AR jet impinging on the anterior mitral leaflet. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mitral stenosis**: **malar flush**, **Ortner syndrome** (hoarseness from an enlarged LA or pulmonary artery compressing the left recurrent laryngeal nerve), **Graham Steell murmur** (PR from pulmonary hypertension), **Lutembacher syndrome** (MS with ASD). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Carey Coombs murmur** is a soft apical mid-diastolic murmur of **acute rheumatic mitral valvulitis** - a sign of active carditis, not established MS. [AHA 2015 Jones criteria]`,`**Gallavardin phenomenon**: the high-frequency musical part of an **AS** murmur radiates to the apex in the elderly and mimics MR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pericardium**: **Beck triad** (hypotension, raised JVP, muffled sounds) in tamponade; **Ewart sign** (dullness and bronchial breathing below the left scapula) in large effusion; **Broadbent sign** (posterior systolic retraction) in adherent pericardium. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Tricuspid regurgitation**: **Carvallo sign** (murmur louder on inspiration), **Lancisi sign** (giant systolic v waves) and a **pulsatile liver**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Other classics**: **Levine sign** (clenched fist over the chest in angina), **Frank sign** (diagonal earlobe crease, linked with CAD), **Means-Lerman scratch** (pulmonary-area systolic scratch in thyrotoxicosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`Most AR eponyms have **poor sensitivity and specificity** - echo grades AR; quote two or three in the exam and move on to pulse pressure and the murmur. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: telling look-alike murmurs apart`,points:[`**Basal ejection systolic murmur**: **AS** (slow pulse, soft A2, radiates to carotids) vs **aortic sclerosis** (normal pulse and A2, no LVH) vs **HCM** (left sternal edge, louder on Valsalva, jerky pulse) vs **flow murmur** (anaemia, fever, pregnancy, thyrotoxicosis). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary-area ESM**: **PS** (ejection click softening on inspiration, wide split, RV heave) vs **ASD** (fixed split, tricuspid flow rumble) vs innocent pulmonary flow murmur (normal splitting). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pansystolic murmur**: **MR** (apex to axilla, soft S1, S3) vs **TR** (left lower sternal edge, louder on inspiration, large v wave, pulsatile liver) vs **VSD** (left lower sternal edge, harsh, thrill, radiates across the precordium). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Apical mid-diastolic murmur**: **MS** (loud S1, opening snap) vs **Austin Flint** (AR signs, no OS) vs **Carey Coombs** (acute rheumatic fever) vs **flow rumble** (severe MR, VSD, PDA - with S3) vs **LA myxoma** (tumour plop, varies with posture). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Early diastolic murmur**: **AR** (wide pulse pressure, peripheral signs; loudest at the right sternal edge suggests aortic root disease) vs **Graham Steell PR** (pulmonary hypertension, loud P2, no peripheral signs). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Continuous murmur**: **PDA** (left infraclavicular, wide pulse pressure) vs **venous hum** (right supraclavicular, disappears lying flat or on pressing the jugular) vs **mammary souffle** (late pregnancy, lactation; changes with stethoscope pressure). [Braunwald 12e]`,`A **to-and-fro murmur** (systolic plus early diastolic, as in mixed AS with AR) stops at S2, whereas a true continuous murmur runs through it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neck and leg look-alikes**: a palpable single-peaked carotid is not a raised JVP, and **ankle oedema with a normal JVP is not heart failure**. [Hutchison 25e]`]},{heading:`Investigations that confirm the bedside findings`,points:[`**12-lead ECG** for every cardiac case: LVH, RVH, **P mitrale** (LA enlargement), AF, bundle branch block (explains abnormal splitting), Q waves, low voltage (effusion). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: cardiothoracic ratio over **0.5** on a PA film, LA enlargement (double right heart border, splayed carina), pulmonary venous hypertension, valve calcification - see the Cardiac investigations topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is indicated for any **diastolic or continuous** murmur, a **pansystolic or late systolic** murmur, a murmur with a **click** or radiating to neck or back, a **grade 3 or louder** mid-systolic murmur, or cardiac symptoms. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`Echo is **not needed** for a grade 1-2 mid-systolic murmur in an asymptomatic young person with an otherwise normal examination and ECG - an innocent murmur. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Focused cardiac ultrasound** with a handheld device extends the examination (effusion, gross LV function, IVC, B-lines) but **does not replace** a full echo for grading valve lesions. [EACVI FoCUS statement]`,`**NT-proBNP** (outpatient rule-out **under 125 pg/mL**) helps when breathlessness with a raised JVP or S3 suggests heart failure. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**New murmur with fever**: three sets of blood cultures before antibiotics and urgent echo - see the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Prognostic signs**: a **raised JVP and an S3** in heart failure each independently predict hospitalisation and death; **pulsus alternans** marks severe LV dysfunction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Refer to cardiology**: any diastolic murmur, suspected severe AS (exertional angina, syncope, breathlessness), suspected congenital heart disease, new AF with a murmur, signs of pulmonary hypertension. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Programme link**: NP-NCD screens adults **30 years and older** for hypertension at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; **RBSK** screens children 0-18 years for congenital and rheumatic heart disease with referral to the **District Early Intervention Centre**. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: bounding pulse, wide pulse pressure, louder S1, **S3**, a soft **ejection systolic flow murmur**, venous hum and **mammary souffle** are normal; a **diastolic murmur, a grade 3+ systolic murmur, S4 or cyanosis** is not and needs echo. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**MS unmasked by pregnancy** is the classic Indian presentation - breathlessness or pulmonary oedema in the second trimester with a previously missed diastolic rumble. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Elderly**: **aortic sclerosis** is present in about a quarter of people over 65; stiff arteries can mask a slow-rising pulse, and an **S4** and wide pulse pressure are common. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Children**: innocent murmurs are very common - **Still's murmur** (vibratory, musical, left lower sternal edge, pre-school and school age), pulmonary flow murmur and venous hum; always feel the **femoral pulses**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Newborn screening**: pulse oximetry after 24 hours - **pass** if SpO2 is **95% or more** in the right hand or a foot with a difference of **3% or less**; **under 90%** in either is a fail needing echo. [AAP 2020 CCHD]`,`**Athletes**: sinus bradycardia, an S3 and a soft flow murmur can be physiological; a murmur that gets **louder on Valsalva** needs echo to exclude **HCM**. [ESC 2020 sports cardiology]`,`**Obesity, COPD and emphysema**: apex impalpable and sounds distant - listen in the **subxiphoid** area and lean on JVP, oedema and liver size. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Atrial fibrillation**: S4 absent and S1 variable; judge murmurs after long cycles and report the **apical rate** and pulse deficit. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: a careful examination with BP cuff, ECG and chest X-ray triages most patients; echo is reserved for murmurs that meet the referral criteria. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2024 hypertension**: validated upper-arm devices, **both arms at the first visit**, and checking for **orthostatic hypotension at diagnosis** (standing BP at 1 and/or 3 minutes), especially in older and diabetic patients. [ESC 2024]`,`**AHA/ACC 2025 hypertension**: re-emphasises **correct office technique** and out-of-office confirmation (home or ambulatory BP) before labelling or escalating treatment. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2024 PAD**: resting **ABI** in adults at increased risk, with categories - abnormal **0.90 or less**, borderline 0.91-0.99, normal 1.00-1.40, non-compressible **over 1.40**. [ACC/AHA 2024 PAD]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular heart disease**: echo remains the first test for any suspected valve lesion, with a shift toward **earlier intervention in asymptomatic severe AS** - the bedside job is to suspect it early. [ESC/EACTS 2025 VHD]`,`**WHF 2023 echo criteria for RHD**: simplified criteria support **task-shifted screening** with handheld echo by trained non-experts, because auscultation misses most latent RHD. [WHF 2023]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis**: keeps **fever with a new murmur** as the trigger for blood cultures and early echo, and adds the endocarditis team for complex cases. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Long-case diagnosis** in four parts - anatomical, pathological, functional and aetiological: e.g. 'chronic rheumatic heart disease, severe MS with pulmonary hypertension, in AF, NYHA class III, no endocarditis'. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Common mistakes**: measuring JVP from the clavicle, calling carotid pulsation a raised JVP, timing murmurs without the carotid, forgetting **radio-femoral delay**, BP in one arm only, and equating a loud murmur with severe disease. [Hutchison 25e]`,`**Classic questions**: why splitting is fixed in ASD, why S4 disappears in AF, what the A2-OS interval means, signs of severe AS, MS, AR and MR, and how to tell MS from Austin Flint. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Fixed splitting in ASD**: the left-to-right shunt falls as systemic venous return rises in inspiration, so RV volume and P2 timing hardly change with respiration. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus** is not paradoxical - it exaggerates the normal inspiratory fall; causes are tamponade, acute severe asthma, COPD exacerbation, massive PE, hypovolaemic shock and some constriction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Kussmaul vs paradoxus**: **Kussmaul favours constriction**, **paradoxus favours tamponade**; both may coexist in effusive-constrictive pericarditis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Short case**: inspect, palpate, auscultate and give a one-line conclusion within about 5 minutes, then ask to check BP in both arms, fundus, lung bases and liver. [Hutchison 25e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Arterial pulse: character, mechanism and causes`,columns:[`Pulse`,`Mechanism`,`Causes`],rows:[[`Slow-rising (anacrotic, parvus et tardus)`,`Fixed LV outflow obstruction prolongs and damps ejection`,`Severe valvular aortic stenosis`],[`Collapsing (water-hammer)`,`Large stroke volume with rapid diastolic run-off`,`AR, PDA, AV fistula, anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, beriberi, Paget disease`],[`Bisferiens`,`Two systolic peaks (percussion and tidal waves)`,`Severe AR, mixed AS with AR, HCM`],[`Jerky (spike-and-dome)`,`Brisk early ejection, then dynamic mid-systolic obstruction`,`Obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`],[`Dicrotic`,`Exaggerated diastolic wave with low stroke volume and low resistance`,`Dilated cardiomyopathy, septic shock, typhoid fever`],[`Pulsus alternans`,`Alternating stroke volume from a failing ventricle`,`Severe LV systolic dysfunction`],[`Pulsus paradoxus`,`Inspiratory systolic fall over 10 mmHg from ventricular interdependence`,`Tamponade, acute severe asthma, COPD, massive PE, hypovolaemia`],[`Small volume (thready)`,`Low stroke volume`,`Shock, severe MS, tamponade, heart failure`],[`Irregularly irregular`,`Random ventricular response`,`AF, multifocal atrial tachycardia, frequent ectopics`],[`Radio-femoral delay`,`Femoral flow arrives late via collaterals`,`Coarctation of the aorta`]]},{heading:`JVP: abnormalities of waveform and height`,columns:[`Finding`,`Mechanism`,`Causes`],rows:[[`Absent a wave`,`No effective atrial contraction`,`Atrial fibrillation`],[`Giant a wave`,`RA contracts against high resistance`,`Tricuspid stenosis, pulmonary stenosis, pulmonary hypertension`],[`Irregular cannon a waves`,`RA contracts against a closed tricuspid valve (AV dissociation)`,`Complete heart block, VT, VVI pacing`],[`Regular cannon a waves`,`Atria and ventricles contract together every beat`,`Junctional rhythm, AV nodal re-entrant tachycardia`],[`Large cv wave, pulsatile liver`,`Systolic regurgitation into the RA`,`Tricuspid regurgitation`],[`Steep x and y descents (Friedreich)`,`Rapid early filling then abrupt halt`,`Constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy`],[`Absent y descent`,`Pericardial pressure blocks early diastolic filling`,`Cardiac tamponade`],[`Slow y descent`,`Obstructed RA emptying`,`Tricuspid stenosis, RA myxoma`],[`Kussmaul sign`,`Inspiratory venous return cannot be accommodated`,`Constriction, restrictive cardiomyopathy, RV infarction`],[`Raised, non-pulsatile`,`Obstruction above the right atrium`,`Superior vena cava obstruction`]]},{heading:`Heart sounds and added sounds`,columns:[`Sound`,`Timing and features`,`Causes and meaning`],rows:[[`Loud S1`,`Mitral leaflets widely open at onset of systole`,`Pliable MS, short PR, tachycardia, high output`],[`Soft S1`,`Leaflets already near-closed or immobile`,`MR, calcified MS, long PR, poor LV function`],[`Physiological split S2`,`Split on inspiration, single on expiration`,`Normal`],[`Wide split, still moves`,`Delayed P2 or early A2`,`RBBB, pulmonary stenosis, MR, VSD`],[`Fixed wide split`,`No change with respiration`,`Ostium secundum ASD`],[`Reversed split`,`Split on expiration (A2 delayed)`,`LBBB, RV pacing, severe AS, HCM`],[`Single S2`,`One component lost or fused`,`Severe AS, Fallot, Eisenmenger VSD, old age`],[`Loud P2`,`High pulmonary artery pressure`,`Pulmonary hypertension`],[`S3`,`Early diastole, low pitch, bell at apex`,`Normal under 40, pregnancy; heart failure, MR`],[`S4`,`Late diastole, before S1; needs atrial kick`,`LVH, AS, HCM, acute MI; absent in AF`],[`Opening snap`,`Early diastole, high pitch, 40-120 ms after A2`,`Mobile mitral stenosis; short A2-OS = severe`],[`Ejection click`,`Early systole, just after S1`,`Bicuspid aortic valve, valvular PS`],[`Mid-systolic click`,`Mid systole, moves with manoeuvres`,`Mitral valve prolapse`],[`Pericardial knock`,`Early diastole, before an S3 would fall`,`Constrictive pericarditis`],[`Tumour plop`,`Early diastole, varies with posture`,`Left atrial myxoma`],[`Pericardial rub`,`Scratchy, up to three components`,`Acute pericarditis, uraemia`]]},{heading:`Murmurs: bedside differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Murmur`,`Site and radiation`,`Manoeuvre clue`,`Associated signs`],rows:[[`Aortic stenosis`,`Harsh ejection systolic, late peaking if severe`,`Right 2nd space to carotids`,`Softer with handgrip and Valsalva`,`Slow-rising pulse, soft A2, heaving apex, S4`],[`Aortic sclerosis`,`Soft short ejection systolic`,`Right 2nd space, no radiation`,`Little change`,`Normal pulse, normal A2, no LVH`],[`HCM (obstructive)`,`Ejection systolic`,`Left lower sternal edge, not to carotids`,`Louder on Valsalva and standing`,`Jerky pulse, double apex, S4`],[`Chronic mitral regurgitation`,`Blowing pansystolic`,`Apex to axilla`,`Louder with handgrip`,`Soft S1, S3, displaced hyperdynamic apex`],[`Mitral valve prolapse`,`Mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur`,`Apex`,`Valsalva: click earlier, murmur longer`,`Often thin young woman, chest wall deformity`],[`VSD`,`Harsh pansystolic`,`Left lower sternal edge, across precordium`,`Louder with handgrip`,`Thrill; loud P2 if pulmonary hypertension`],[`Tricuspid regurgitation`,`Pansystolic`,`Left lower sternal edge`,`Louder on inspiration (Carvallo)`,`Large cv wave, pulsatile liver`],[`Pulmonary stenosis`,`Ejection systolic with ejection click`,`Left 2nd space to left shoulder`,`Click softens on inspiration`,`Wide split, RV heave, giant a wave`],[`ASD (secundum)`,`Soft pulmonary flow ejection systolic`,`Left 2nd space`,`Little change`,`Fixed split S2, RV heave`],[`Aortic regurgitation`,`High-pitched early diastolic decrescendo`,`Left 3rd space (Erb), sitting forward`,`Louder with handgrip`,`Collapsing pulse, wide pulse pressure, eponymous signs`],[`Pulmonary regurgitation (Graham Steell)`,`Early diastolic decrescendo`,`Left 2nd-3rd space`,`Louder on inspiration`,`Loud P2, RV heave, no peripheral signs`],[`Mitral stenosis`,`Low-pitched mid-diastolic rumble, presystolic accentuation`,`Apex, localised, left lateral, bell`,`Louder after exercise and with amyl nitrite`,`Tapping apex, loud S1, opening snap, malar flush`],[`Austin Flint`,`Mid-diastolic rumble`,`Apex`,`Softer with amyl nitrite`,`Signs of severe AR, no opening snap`],[`PDA`,`Continuous machinery`,`Left infraclavicular`,`Little change`,`Collapsing pulse, wide pulse pressure`],[`Venous hum`,`Continuous, louder in diastole`,`Right supraclavicular`,`Disappears lying flat or on jugular pressure`,`Child or pregnancy; benign`]]},{heading:`Dynamic manoeuvres and their effects`,columns:[`Manoeuvre`,`Haemodynamic effect`,`Murmurs louder`,`Murmurs softer`],rows:[[`Inspiration`,`More venous return to the right heart`,`TR, TS, PS, PR (right-sided)`,`Most left-sided; pulmonary ejection click`],[`Valsalva strain or standing`,`Less preload, smaller LV`,`HCM; MVP murmur longer`,`AS, MR, VSD, AR, MS`],[`Squatting or passive leg raise`,`More preload (squatting also more afterload)`,`AS, MR, VSD, AR`,`HCM; MVP click later`],[`Sustained handgrip`,`More afterload, heart rate and output`,`MR, VSD, AR, MS`,`AS, HCM`],[`Amyl nitrite`,`Less afterload, then reflex tachycardia`,`AS, HCM, PS, MS`,`MR, VSD, AR, Austin Flint`],[`Beat after a long pause`,`Longer filling, stronger contraction`,`AS, HCM`,`MR unchanged`]]},{heading:`Drugs that change bedside findings (and the pharmacological manoeuvre)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amyl nitrite (diagnostic manoeuvre)`,`0.3 mL ampoule crushed and inhaled for 2-3 breaths`,`Effect within 30 seconds, lasts about 2-3 minutes`,`Flushing, headache, hypotension, reflex tachycardia; avoid in hypotension`],[`Metoprolol succinate (beta-blocker)`,`25-200 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate every 2 weeks`,`Bradycardia masks compensatory tachycardia; may soften HCM murmur; never stop abruptly`],[`Digoxin`,`0.0625-0.25 mg oral once daily (lower in elderly and CKD)`,`Long term; check level if toxicity suspected`,`A regular slow pulse in a patient with AF suggests toxicity with junctional rhythm or heart block`],[`Amlodipine`,`2.5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Ankle oedema with a normal JVP - not heart failure; flushing, gum hypertrophy`],[`Glyceryl trinitrate`,`0.4-0.5 mg sublingual, repeat every 5 minutes up to 3 doses`,`Acute use`,`Postural hypotension, headache; softens MR, louder HCM murmur; avoid with PDE5 inhibitors and severe AS`],[`Prazosin or tamsulosin (alpha-blockers)`,`Prazosin 0.5-1 mg oral at night first dose; tamsulosin 0.4 mg once daily`,`Titrate slowly`,`First-dose orthostatic hypotension and syncope - check standing BP`],[`Furosemide`,`20-80 mg oral or IV once or twice daily`,`Adjust to weight and JVP`,`Overdiuresis: low JVP, postural hypotension, hypokalaemia, prerenal AKI`]]}],redFlags:[`New murmur with fever, embolic signs or a prosthetic valve - suspected infective endocarditis: blood cultures and admit.`,`Hypotension with a raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg and muffled heart sounds - cardiac tamponade needing urgent echo and drainage.`,`Chest or back pain with a pulse deficit, an inter-arm systolic difference over 20 mmHg or a new early diastolic murmur - aortic dissection until proved otherwise.`,`Ejection systolic murmur with exertional syncope, angina or breathlessness - severe aortic stenosis needing urgent echo and cardiology.`,`New pansystolic murmur after myocardial infarction with hypotension or pulmonary oedema - papillary muscle or ventricular septal rupture.`,`Irregular cannon a waves with a pulse under 40/min or syncope - complete heart block needing admission for pacing.`,`S3 gallop with crackles, hypotension, cold peripheries or SpO2 under 90% - acute heart failure or cardiogenic shock.`,`Raised non-pulsatile JVP with facial swelling and dilated chest wall veins - superior vena cava obstruction.`],pearls:[`**Right-sided murmurs get louder on inspiration (Carvallo)** - except the pulmonary ejection click, which softens.`,`**Valsalva softens everything except HCM and MVP.**`,`**Loud S1 plus opening snap = pliable mitral valve**; once calcified, both soften.`,`**Severity is never the loudness** - grade by pulse, A2, A2-OS gap, S3 and pulmonary hypertension.`,`**S4 is never heard in AF**; an S3 after 40 is heart failure until proved otherwise.`,`**Fixed split = ASD; reversed split = LBBB or severe AS; single S2 = severe AS or Fallot.**`,`**Absent y = tamponade; steep y with Kussmaul = constriction.**`,`**Irregular cannon a waves = AV dissociation** - complete heart block or VT.`,`**Innocent murmur - 7 S**: soft, systolic, short, single, small, sweet, sensitive.`,`**Handgrip: MR louder, AS softer** - the quickest way to separate the two at the apex.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has a soft ejection systolic murmur in the left 2nd intercostal space and splitting of S2 that does not change with respiration. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Right bundle branch block`,`Ostium secundum atrial septal defect`,`Valvular pulmonary stenosis`,`Left bundle branch block`],answer:1,explanation:`Fixed wide splitting is the hallmark of ostium secundum ASD: the left-to-right shunt falls as systemic venous return rises, so RV volume and P2 timing barely change with respiration, and the murmur is a pulmonary flow murmur. RBBB and pulmonary stenosis give wide splitting that still widens on inspiration, while LBBB gives reversed (paradoxical) splitting.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-q2`,stem:`Which bedside manoeuvre makes the systolic murmur of hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy louder?`,options:[`Squatting from standing`,`Sustained isometric handgrip`,`Passive leg raising`,`Standing up from squatting`],answer:3,explanation:`Standing from squatting, like the Valsalva strain, reduces venous return and LV cavity size, bringing the septum and anterior mitral leaflet closer and raising the outflow gradient, so the HCM murmur gets louder. Squatting and passive leg raising increase preload and soften it, and handgrip raises afterload, which also reduces the gradient and softens the murmur.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-q3`,stem:`A patient with a large pericardial effusion has hypotension and a raised JVP. Which jugular venous finding is characteristic of cardiac tamponade?`,options:[`Absent y descent`,`Giant a waves`,`Steep y descent with Kussmaul sign`,`Large cv wave with a pulsatile liver`],answer:0,explanation:`In tamponade the raised pericardial pressure prevents early diastolic filling, so the y descent is blunted or absent while the x descent is preserved. A steep y descent with Kussmaul sign points to constrictive pericarditis, giant a waves to tricuspid stenosis or pulmonary hypertension, and a large cv wave with a pulsatile liver to tricuspid regurgitation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-q4`,stem:`In a patient with rheumatic mitral stenosis, which bedside finding best indicates severe stenosis?`,options:[`A loud first heart sound`,`A loud grade 4 mid-diastolic murmur`,`A short interval between A2 and the opening snap`,`Presystolic accentuation of the murmur`],answer:2,explanation:`The higher the left atrial pressure, the earlier the mitral valve opens after aortic closure, so a short A2-opening snap interval (with a long rumble and signs of pulmonary hypertension) marks severe MS. A loud S1 shows a pliable valve rather than severity, loudness of the rumble reflects flow rather than orifice size, and presystolic accentuation only shows the patient is in sinus rhythm.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-q5`,stem:`Pulsus paradoxus is best defined as:`,options:[`Alternate strong and weak beats in a regular rhythm`,`An inspiratory fall in systolic BP of more than 10 mmHg`,`A rise in jugular venous pressure on inspiration`,`Disappearance of the radial pulse on deep inspiration`],answer:1,explanation:`Pulsus paradoxus is an exaggeration of the normal inspiratory fall in systolic pressure to more than 10 mmHg, measured with the cuff; it is typical of tamponade and acute severe asthma. Alternate strong and weak beats describe pulsus alternans of severe LV dysfunction, an inspiratory rise in JVP is Kussmaul sign of constriction, and the pulse seldom disappears completely.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the jugular venous pulse. Discuss the method of examination, the normal waveform and the abnormalities of the JVP with their clinical significance.`,openingLines:[`The jugular venous pulse is the oscillating column of blood in the right internal jugular vein, which reflects right atrial pressure and its waveform because no valve separates the vein from the right atrium.`,`The jugular venous pressure is the vertical height of the top of this column above the sternal angle - normally up to 3-4 cm, corresponding to a right atrial pressure of about 8-9 cm of water.`],answer:[{heading:`Why the right internal jugular vein`,points:[`Lies in a **straight line** with the SVC and right atrium, with no valve - pressure and waveform are transmitted faithfully.`,`The external jugular is affected by valves, posture and kinking - use it only for height if the IJV cannot be seen.`,`The **sternal angle** is about 5 cm above the mid right atrium in any position from lying to sitting.`]},{heading:`Method of examination`,points:[`Patient reclined at **45 degrees**, head on a pillow turned slightly left, neck relaxed, tangential light.`,`Identify the pulsation between and behind the heads of sternocleidomastoid; measure the **vertical height above the sternal angle**.`,`Adjust the angle - flatter if low, **upright at 90 degrees** if very high - until the top is seen.`,`Time the waves against the **opposite carotid** or heart sounds.`,`Perform the **abdominojugular test**: firm periumbilical pressure for 10-15 seconds; a sustained rise of 3 cm or more is positive.`]},{heading:`Normal waveform`,points:[`**a wave**: atrial contraction, just before S1 and the carotid upstroke.`,`**c wave**: bulging of the tricuspid valve into the RA at the onset of systole (rarely visible).`,`**x descent**: atrial relaxation and descent of the tricuspid ring - the dominant visible systolic collapse.`,`**v wave**: venous filling of the RA against a closed tricuspid valve, peaking just after S2.`,`**y descent**: tricuspid opening and rapid emptying of the RA in early diastole.`]},{heading:`Venous versus arterial pulsation`,points:[`Venous: impalpable, double waveform, obliterated by light pressure, falls on inspiration, rises with abdominal pressure, varies with posture.`,`Arterial: palpable, single brisk upstroke, not obliterated, unaffected by posture or respiration.`]},{heading:`Abnormalities of height`,points:[`**Raised**: right heart failure, fluid overload, tamponade, constriction, tricuspid disease, pulmonary hypertension, COPD with cor pulmonale.`,`**Raised non-pulsatile**: superior vena cava obstruction - no waveform, no abdominojugular reflux.`,`**Low**: hypovolaemia - seen only with the patient flat.`,`**Kussmaul sign**: rise on inspiration - constriction, restrictive cardiomyopathy, RV infarction.`]},{heading:`Abnormalities of waveform`,points:[`**Absent a** - AF; **giant a** - TS, PS, pulmonary hypertension.`,`**Irregular cannon a** - AV dissociation (complete heart block, VT); **regular cannon a** - junctional rhythm, AVNRT.`,`**Large cv wave** with pulsatile liver - tricuspid regurgitation.`,`**Steep x and y (Friedreich)** - constriction; **absent y** - tamponade; **slow y** - tricuspid stenosis.`]},{heading:`Clinical significance`,points:[`A **raised JVP** in heart failure independently predicts admission and death and guides diuretic dosing.`,`The JVP separates cardiac from non-cardiac oedema and **tamponade from constriction** at the bedside.`,`Pitfalls: measuring from the clavicle, mistaking carotid pulsation, missing a very high JVP at 45 degrees.`]}],mustDraw:[`Normal JVP waveform (a, c, x, v, y) timed against S1, S2 and the carotid pulse`,`Diagram of measuring JVP height above the sternal angle at 45 degrees`,`Table of waveform abnormalities with causes`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and why the right IJV`,marks:1},{part:`Method of examination and abdominojugular test`,marks:2},{part:`Normal waveform with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Venous versus arterial pulsation`,marks:1},{part:`Abnormal height and Kussmaul sign`,marks:2},{part:`Abnormal waveform with causes`,marks:2}],keywords:[`JVP`,`a wave`,`v wave`,`y descent`,`Kussmaul sign`,`abdominojugular reflux`,`cannon waves`,`sternal angle`]},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-examination-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate innocent from pathological heart murmurs.`,openingLines:[`An innocent (functional) murmur is produced by normal flow through a structurally normal heart, while a pathological murmur reflects a structural lesion of a valve, septum or great vessel.`,`The distinction is made from the murmur's timing, grade and company - symptoms, heart sounds and other signs - and echo settles doubt.`],answer:[{heading:`Innocent murmur - the 7 S`,points:[`**Soft** (grade 1-2), **systolic** (ejection), **short**, **single** (no clicks or added sounds).`,`**Small** (localised, little radiation), **sweet** (not harsh), **sensitive** to posture and respiration.`,`Normal S2 splitting, normal pulses, no thrill, no symptoms, normal ECG.`]},{heading:`Pathological murmur - features`,points:[`Any **diastolic** or **continuous** murmur (except venous hum and mammary souffle).`,`Pansystolic or late systolic murmur, **grade 3 or louder**, or with a **thrill**.`,`Abnormal S2 (fixed, reversed or single), clicks, opening snap, S4.`,`Symptoms (breathlessness, syncope, chest pain), cyanosis, failure to thrive, abnormal pulses.`]},{heading:`Common innocent murmurs`,points:[`**Still's murmur** - vibratory, musical, left lower sternal edge in young children.`,`**Pulmonary flow murmur** - left 2nd space in children, thin adults and pregnancy.`,`**Venous hum** and **mammary souffle** - continuous but benign.`,`**Flow murmurs** of anaemia, fever, thyrotoxicosis and pregnancy.`]},{heading:`When to order echo or refer`,points:[`Any pathological feature, a murmur in a symptomatic patient, or uncertainty.`,`Infants, syndromic children, and murmurs found in pregnancy with any abnormal feature.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: innocent versus pathological murmur, feature by feature`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Comparison table`,marks:2},{part:`Types of innocent murmur`,marks:1},{part:`When to echo and refer`,marks:1}],keywords:[`innocent murmur`,`Still's murmur`,`venous hum`,`flow murmur`,`7 S`,`pathological murmur`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Physical examination of the cardiovascular system; Approach to the patient with a heart murmur`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Physical examination of the heart and circulation`,`Hutchison's Clinical Methods, 25th edition, 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Valvular Heart Disease; 2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease`,`2024 ACC/AHA Multisociety Guideline for the Management of Lower Extremity Peripheral Artery Disease`,`2023 World Heart Federation Guidelines for the echocardiographic diagnosis of rheumatic heart disease`,`MoHFW: Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram operational guidelines; NP-NCD operational guidelines 2023`]}),e.push({id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations`,title:`Cardiac investigations: chest X-ray, echo, stress testing, Holter, CT and MRI, catheterisation`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`Cardiac investigations are chosen by the **clinical question and pre-test likelihood**: **chest X-ray, hs-troponin and NT-proBNP** in the first hour, **echocardiography** for structure and function, **CT coronary angiography or stress imaging** for suspected coronary disease, **ambulatory ECG** matched to symptom frequency, **CMR** for tissue characterisation, and **invasive angiography with FFR** only when the result will change management.`,keywords:[`chest X-ray`,`cardiothoracic ratio`,`high-sensitivity troponin`,`NT-proBNP`,`echocardiography`,`ejection fraction`,`PASP`,`treadmill test`,`Duke treadmill score`,`stress echo`,`myocardial perfusion imaging`,`CT coronary angiography`,`calcium score`,`cardiac MRI`,`late gadolinium enhancement`,`Holter`,`implantable loop recorder`,`coronary angiography`,`FFR`,`pre-test probability`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, purpose and the Indian context`,points:[`**Cardiac investigations** answer one of five questions: is this **ACS**, is there **heart failure** and why, is there **valve or structural disease**, is there **obstructive coronary disease**, and is there an **arrhythmia** explaining symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`Every test should **change management**: a test whose result cannot alter treatment is inappropriate, whatever its availability. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`,`**Risk-factor burden in India is high**: ICMR-INDIAB found **diabetes in 11.4%**, prediabetes in 15.3% and hypertension in 35.5% of adults - the main drivers of demand for coronary testing. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Indians develop **coronary disease about a decade earlier** than Western populations, often with diffuse multivessel disease, so a young age does not exclude CAD. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**District hospitals** usually offer chest X-ray, ECG, troponin, echo and treadmill testing; **CT, CMR, nuclear imaging and catheterisation** are tertiary or private facilities, with angiography and PCI covered under **PM-JAY**. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Bayesian reasoning**: the same positive treadmill test means a high chance of CAD in a 60-year-old diabetic man with typical angina and a low chance in a 30-year-old woman with atypical pain - **test only when pre-test likelihood is intermediate**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **ECG** is the first test in every cardiac complaint and is covered in the ECG reading topic; this topic covers the tests that follow it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Choosing the test: pre-test likelihood and appropriateness`,points:[`**ESC 2024 risk-factor-weighted clinical likelihood (RF-CL) model** estimates the chance of obstructive CAD from **age, sex, symptom type** and the number of risk factors (family history, smoking, dyslipidaemia, hypertension, diabetes). [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`Likelihood bands: **very low 5% or less** (defer testing), **low over 5-15%**, **moderate over 15-50%**, **high over 50-85%**, **very high over 85%**. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**CT coronary angiography** is recommended for **low-to-moderate likelihood (over 5-50%)**; **functional imaging** for **moderate-to-high likelihood (over 15-85%)**; **invasive angiography** for **very high likelihood** or high-risk features. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Calcium scoring** can reclassify likelihood (CACS-weighted clinical likelihood) - a zero score pushes many patients into the very-low band where testing can be deferred. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`US practice: for stable chest pain with intermediate-high risk and no known CAD, **CCTA and stress imaging are both Class I**; CCTA favoured under 65 years, functional testing over 65 or with known CAD. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`,`**Do not screen** asymptomatic low-risk adults with treadmill tests, echo or CT angiography - false positives lead to unnecessary angiography. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`,`**Acute chest pain** goes down a different path: ECG within 10 minutes and **hs-troponin** - not a treadmill test, which is contraindicated until ACS is excluded. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Match the **monitor to symptom frequency**: daily symptoms - Holter; weekly-to-monthly - patch or external loop recorder; rare - implantable loop recorder. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Principles behind the tests`,points:[`**Ischaemic cascade**: after a flow-limiting stenosis, the order is **perfusion heterogeneity, diastolic dysfunction, regional wall-motion abnormality, ECG change, then angina** - why perfusion and stress imaging are more sensitive than the treadmill ECG. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Exercise or dobutamine** raises myocardial oxygen demand; **vasodilators** (adenosine, regadenoson, dipyridamole) raise flow in normal arteries but not across a stenosis, creating a **perfusion difference** without needing demand. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Troponin** is released from injured myocytes; **high-sensitivity assays** detect concentrations below the **99th percentile upper reference limit** in most healthy people, so small rises and the **rise-and-fall pattern** become interpretable. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**BNP and NT-proBNP** are released from ventricular myocytes in response to **wall stretch**; the inactive NT-proBNP fragment has a longer half-life and is **not degraded by neprilysin**, so it stays valid on sacubitril-valsartan. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**Doppler echo** converts velocity to pressure by the **simplified Bernoulli equation: gradient = 4V squared** - the basis of valve gradients and pulmonary artery systolic pressure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late gadolinium enhancement** on CMR shows where gadolinium is retained in expanded extracellular space - **scar or fibrosis** - and its pattern separates ischaemic from non-ischaemic disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Fractional flow reserve** is the ratio of distal coronary to aortic pressure at maximal hyperaemia; it tells whether a stenosis is **functionally significant**, which anatomy alone does not. [ESC 2024 CCS]`]},{heading:`Chest X-ray in heart disease`,points:[`**Cardiothoracic ratio over 0.5** on a good-inspiration **PA film** means cardiomegaly; an AP or supine film magnifies the heart and cannot be used. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LA enlargement** (MS, MR): **double right heart border**, straightened left heart border, **widened carinal angle** and elevated left main bronchus. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary venous hypertension** stages roughly with wedge pressure: **upper lobe diversion** (about 13-18 mmHg), **interstitial oedema with Kerley B lines** (18-25 mmHg), **alveolar bat-wing oedema** (over 25 mmHg). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Globular or flask-shaped heart** with clear lungs suggests **pericardial effusion** (usually over 200 mL); **pericardial calcification** on the lateral film suggests constriction, classically tuberculous in India. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary plethora** suggests a left-to-right shunt (ASD, VSD, PDA); **oligaemia** suggests reduced pulmonary flow (Fallot); **prominent central arteries with peripheral pruning** suggest pulmonary hypertension. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Classic shapes**: **boot-shaped** heart (Fallot), **egg on a string** (transposition), **snowman** (supracardiac TAPVC), **figure-of-3 sign with rib notching** of ribs 3-8 (coarctation). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Widened mediastinum** with chest pain suggests **aortic dissection** - but a normal X-ray does not exclude it. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`Also check **valve calcification**, prosthetic valves, pacemaker or ICD leads and their position, sternal wires and pleural effusions. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Biomarkers: high-sensitivity troponin and natriuretic peptides`,points:[`**Myocardial infarction** needs a **rise and/or fall of cardiac troponin with at least one value above the 99th percentile URL** plus clinical evidence of ischaemia (symptoms, ECG, imaging) - troponin alone means **injury**, not infarction. [UDMI 4 2018]`,`**ESC 0/1-hour algorithm** with hs-cTnT (Elecsys): **rule out** if under 5 ng/L at presentation (pain over 3 hours) or under 12 ng/L with a 1-hour rise under 3; **rule in** if 52 ng/L or more, or a 1-hour rise of 5 or more. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Cut-offs are **assay-specific** - use the values for the local assay; the **0/2-hour algorithm** is the alternative, and a **3-hour repeat** is needed if the patient presents within 3 hours or stays in the observe zone. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Non-ACS troponin rise**: myocarditis, PE, heart failure, sepsis, tachyarrhythmia, Takotsubo, aortic dissection, stroke or subarachnoid haemorrhage, strenuous exercise and **chronic elevation in CKD** (a stable value, not a rise and fall). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Point-of-care troponin** kits common in Indian casualties are less sensitive than laboratory hs-assays - a negative early POC test does not exclude MI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**NT-proBNP rule-out**: **under 125 pg/mL** in non-acute settings and **under 300 pg/mL** in acute dyspnoea (BNP under 35 and under 100 pg/mL) - heart failure is then unlikely. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**Age-specific rule-in** for acute heart failure (NT-proBNP): over **450 pg/mL under 50 years**, over **900 at 50-75 years**, over **1800 over 75 years**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Confounders**: higher with age, AF, CKD, PE and sepsis; **lower in obesity** (a normal value in a very obese patient is less reassuring); **sacubitril raises BNP but not NT-proBNP**. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**D-dimer** for PE and dissection is covered in the PE topic; **lipids, HbA1c, creatinine, TSH and haemoglobin** belong to every cardiac work-up as risk and cause markers. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Echocardiography: what the report tells you`,points:[`**LVEF** (Simpson biplane) classifies heart failure: **HFrEF 40% or less**, **HFmrEF 41-49%**, **HFpEF 50% or more**; normal LVEF is about **52% or more in men and 54% or more in women**. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**Diastolic dysfunction and raised filling pressure**: average **E/e' over 14**, septal e' under 7 or lateral e' under 10 cm/s, **LA volume index over 34 mL/m2** and **TR velocity over 2.8 m/s**. [ASE/EACVI 2016]`,`**PASP = 4 x (peak TR velocity) squared + right atrial pressure**; RAP is about **3 mmHg** if the IVC is 2.1 cm or less and collapses over 50% on sniff, and about **15 mmHg** if it is wider and collapses less. [ASE 2015]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension probability** by echo: TR velocity **2.8 m/s or less** low, **2.9-3.4** intermediate, **over 3.4 m/s** high - confirmation needs right heart catheterisation. [ESC 2022 PH]`,`**RV dysfunction**: **TAPSE under 17 mm** or tissue Doppler S' under 9.5 cm/s; RV dilatation and D-shaped septum point to pressure or volume overload. [ASE 2015]`,`**Severe AS**: peak velocity **4.0 m/s or more**, mean gradient **40 mmHg or more**, valve area **under 1.0 cm2**; **severe MS**: valve area **1.5 cm2 or less**, with Wilkins score guiding balloon valvotomy. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Severe MR**: effective regurgitant orifice **0.40 cm2 or more**, regurgitant volume 60 mL or more, vena contracta 0.7 cm or more; **severe AR**: vena contracta **over 0.6 cm**, regurgitant volume 60 mL or more. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Pericardial effusion** size by diastolic echo-free space: **small under 10 mm**, moderate 10-20 mm, **large over 20 mm**; **tamponade** shows RA systolic and RV diastolic collapse with a plethoric IVC. [ESC 2015 pericardial]`,`**Transoesophageal echo** for suspected endocarditis (especially prosthetic valve), LA appendage thrombus before cardioversion, aortic dissection, source of embolism and peri-procedural guidance; **bubble contrast** detects PFO. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Global longitudinal strain** (normal around -20%; a relative fall over 15% from baseline) detects early **cancer-therapy cardiotoxicity** before EF falls. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Exercise treadmill test (TMT)`,points:[`**Bruce protocol**: 3-minute stages of rising speed and gradient; aim for **85% of age-predicted maximum heart rate (220 minus age)** for an adequate test. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Positive test**: **1 mm or more of horizontal or downsloping ST depression** 60-80 ms after the J point; **ST elevation** in leads without Q waves localises severe ischaemia. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Duke treadmill score** = exercise time (min) - 5 x ST deviation (mm) - 4 x angina index (0 none, 1 non-limiting, 2 limiting): **+5 or more low risk**, -10 to +4 intermediate, **-11 or less high risk**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Uninterpretable ECG** for ischaemia: **LBBB**, paced rhythm, WPW, **resting ST depression 1 mm or more**, LVH with strain and **digoxin** - use stress imaging instead. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Absolute contraindications**: acute MI within 2 days, high-risk unstable angina, uncontrolled symptomatic arrhythmia, **symptomatic severe AS**, acute PE, acute myocarditis or pericarditis, **acute aortic dissection**, decompensated heart failure. [AHA exercise testing statement]`,`**Stop the test** for ST elevation, **systolic BP fall over 10 mmHg** with other signs of ischaemia, sustained VT, moderate-to-severe angina, ataxia or near-syncope, or the patient's request. [AHA exercise testing statement]`,`**Accuracy is modest** (sensitivity and specificity each around 60-70%) and worse in women; **ESC 2024** no longer recommends it as a diagnostic test when imaging is available, but it remains useful for **exercise capacity, BP response and prognosis**. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**High-risk TMT features**: ST depression 2 mm or more at low workload, ST change in many leads persisting into recovery, **exercise-induced hypotension**, sustained VT and poor exercise capacity (under 5 METs). [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Stress imaging: stress echo, nuclear perfusion, PET and stress CMR`,points:[`**Stress echocardiography** (exercise or dobutamine) detects **new regional wall-motion abnormality** - no radiation, cheap, widely available in India, but operator- and window-dependent. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Dobutamine protocol**: 5, 10, 20, 30 then **40 mcg/kg/min** in 3-minute stages, with **atropine** in divided doses if target heart rate is not reached; low-dose dobutamine also assesses viability and **low-flow AS**. [ASE stress echo]`,`**SPECT myocardial perfusion imaging** with technetium-99m sestamibi or tetrofosmin compares stress and rest images: a **reversible defect is ischaemia**, a **fixed defect is scar**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vasodilator stress** (adenosine, regadenoson, dipyridamole) suits those who cannot exercise and those with **LBBB or pacing**; avoid **caffeine for 12-24 hours** before and avoid in bronchospasm or high-grade AV block. [ASNC]`,`**PET perfusion** gives the best accuracy and **absolute myocardial blood flow**, useful in balanced three-vessel disease and microvascular dysfunction - limited in India by cost and availability. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**High-risk ischaemia** threshold: **10% or more of LV myocardium** on SPECT or PET, **3 or more of 16 segments** on stress echo, or **2 or more of 16 segments** on stress CMR perfusion. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Technetium pyrophosphate (PYP) scintigraphy**: grade 2-3 cardiac uptake with **no monoclonal protein** in serum and urine diagnoses **transthyretin amyloidosis** without biopsy. [ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy]`,`**FDG-PET/CT** detects active cardiac **sarcoidosis** and **prosthetic valve or device infection** - now a major imaging criterion for endocarditis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`CT: calcium score and CT coronary angiography`,points:[`**Coronary artery calcium (Agatston) score**: **0** very low risk ('power of zero'), 1-99 mild, 100-399 moderate, **400 or more** high risk - low radiation, no contrast. [ACC/AHA 2018 cholesterol]`,`In intermediate-risk adults undecided about statins, **CAC 0** allows deferring statin (unless diabetic, smoker or strong family history), and **CAC 100 or more or 75th percentile or higher** favours a statin. [ACC/AHA 2018 cholesterol]`,`**CT coronary angiography** has a **negative predictive value near 99%** - its strength is ruling out obstructive CAD in low-to-moderate likelihood patients. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Preparation**: heart rate **under 60-65/min** (oral or IV metoprolol, or ivabradine), **sublingual nitrate** just before, a breath-hold, sinus rhythm preferred, and **eGFR** checked for iodinated contrast. [SCCT]`,`**Limitations**: heavy calcification and stents cause blooming artefact, fast AF and obesity degrade images, and contrast allergy or eGFR under 30 are relative contraindications. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**CAD-RADS 2.0** reports stenosis grade: 0 none, 1 under 25%, 2 25-49%, 3 50-69%, **4A 70-99%**, **4B left main 50% or more or three-vessel 70% or more**, 5 occlusion - with a plaque burden (P) modifier. [CAD-RADS 2.0 2022]`,`**CT-derived FFR** and plaque features (low attenuation, positive remodelling, spotty calcification) add functional and prognostic information to anatomy. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Other cardiac CT uses**: CT pulmonary angiography, aortic dissection, **TAVI planning**, pericardial thickening and calcification, and **aortic valve calcium score** in discordant AS grading. [ESC/EACTS 2025 VHD]`]},{heading:`Cardiac MRI`,points:[`**CMR is the reference standard** for ventricular volumes, EF and mass - preferred when echo windows are poor or exact EF decides a device or chemotherapy decision. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**LGE patterns**: **subendocardial or transmural** in a coronary territory = **infarction**; **mid-wall** = DCM or HCM fibrosis; **subepicardial** = **myocarditis**; patchy = sarcoidosis; **diffuse subendocardial** with abnormal nulling = **amyloidosis**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Viability**: LGE involving **under 50% of wall thickness** predicts recovery of function after revascularisation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Myocarditis**: the **2018 Lake Louise criteria** need one T2-based marker (oedema) and one T1-based marker (T1 mapping, ECV or LGE). [Lake Louise 2018]`,`**T2-star imaging** quantifies myocardial iron: **under 20 ms** means iron loading and **under 10 ms** severe loading - guiding chelation in **thalassaemia major**, common in India. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Stress CMR perfusion** is an accurate functional test with no radiation. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Cautions**: non-MR-conditional pacemakers or ICDs, some metal implants, claustrophobia; gadolinium in **eGFR under 30** only with group II agents and only if essential (nephrogenic systemic fibrosis). [ACR 2023 contrast manual]`]},{heading:`Ambulatory ECG monitoring`,points:[`**Holter monitor** (24-48 hours) suits **daily symptoms** or quantifying ectopic burden and AF rate control. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Patch monitors** (7-14 days, single lead) and **external loop or event recorders** (2-4 weeks) suit **weekly symptoms** and are more sensitive than Holter for paroxysmal AF and SVT. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Implantable loop recorder** (battery up to about 3 years) is for **recurrent unexplained syncope** with infrequent events and for AF detection after cryptogenic stroke. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Symptom-rhythm correlation** is the goal: symptoms with sinus rhythm exclude arrhythmia as the cause; an arrhythmia without symptoms may still be relevant (pauses, VT). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Smartwatch and smartphone ECGs**: a photoplethysmography alert must be confirmed by ECG - AF is diagnosed on a **12-lead ECG or a single-lead tracing of 30 seconds or more**. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Significant Holter findings**: pauses **over 3 seconds while awake**, Mobitz II or complete heart block, sustained VT, and symptomatic bradycardia - refer the same day. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Ambulatory BP monitoring** confirms hypertension and nocturnal patterns - see the Hypertension topic. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Invasive: coronary angiography, FFR and right heart catheterisation`,points:[`**Invasive coronary angiography** is indicated for **high-risk ACS**, chronic coronary syndrome with **very high likelihood**, high-risk non-invasive results or symptoms refractory to medicines, and before valve surgery in those at risk of CAD. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Radial access** is the default - fewer bleeding and vascular complications than femoral access. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**FFR 0.80 or less** (or **iFR 0.89 or less**) marks a functionally significant stenosis; deferring PCI when FFR is above 0.80 is safe. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Intravascular imaging (IVUS or OCT)** is recommended to guide PCI of complex lesions such as left main, long lesions and bifurcations. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Coronary function testing** (coronary flow reserve, index of microcirculatory resistance, acetylcholine spasm testing) diagnoses **ANOCA/INOCA** - angina with non-obstructive arteries. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Right heart catheterisation** confirms pulmonary hypertension: **mean PA pressure over 20 mmHg**, with **PAWP 15 mmHg or less and PVR over 2 Wood units** for pre-capillary PH. [ESC 2022 PH]`,`**Complications** (major under 1%): access bleeding and haematoma, pseudoaneurysm, **contrast-associated AKI**, contrast reaction, stroke, MI, arrhythmia and, rarely, death. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Contrast AKI prevention** in significant CKD: isotonic saline **1 mL/kg/h for 12 hours before and 24 hours after** (0.5 mL/kg/h if EF 35% or less or NYHA over II), low- or iso-osmolar contrast, minimum volume. [ESC 2018 revascularisation]`,`**Metformin**: withhold at the time of contrast if eGFR is under 30 or there is AKI, and restart after 48 hours once renal function is stable. [ACR 2023 contrast manual]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: echo is first-line and safe; a shielded chest X-ray is acceptable; prefer **CMR without gadolinium** over CT; troponin is unaffected by normal pregnancy, and NT-proBNP remains useful. [ESC 2018 pregnancy]`,`**Women**: lower treadmill specificity (false-positive ST changes) - favour **stress imaging or CCTA**, and consider microvascular angina when arteries are normal. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**CKD**: troponin is often chronically raised - look for a **dynamic rise or fall**; NT-proBNP runs higher; weigh contrast and gadolinium risks. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Diabetes**: ischaemia is often silent, but **routine screening of asymptomatic diabetics** with stress testing does not improve outcomes. [ADA 2025]`,`**Elderly**: high calcium scores limit CCTA - prefer **functional imaging**, with pharmacological stress if unable to walk on a treadmill. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`,`**LBBB or paced rhythm**: use **vasodilator perfusion imaging** - exercise causes false septal defects on SPECT and uninterpretable ST segments. [ASNC]`,`**Obesity or COPD**: poor echo windows - use **contrast echo**, CMR or PET; SPECT suffers attenuation artefact. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cancer therapy** (anthracyclines, trastuzumab): baseline echo with GLS and troponin, then **echo every 3 months during trastuzumab**. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: ECG, chest X-ray, point-of-care troponin, echo and TMT answer most questions; refer for CT, nuclear or invasive testing when the result will change management. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`]},{heading:`Radiation, risks and follow-up imaging intervals`,points:[`**Radiation doses (approximate)**: chest X-ray about **0.02 mSv**, CCTA about **1-5 mSv** with modern prospective gating, SPECT about 10 mSv; **echo and CMR involve none**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Contrast reactions**: ask about prior reactions and asthma; mild reactions are common, anaphylaxis is rare and needs **IM adrenaline 0.5 mg**. [ACR 2023 contrast manual]`,`**Echo surveillance of AS**: mild (peak velocity 2.0-2.9 m/s) every **3-5 years**, moderate (3.0-3.9 m/s) every **1-2 years**, severe asymptomatic every **6-12 months**. [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Echo surveillance of primary MR**: mild every 3-5 years, moderate every 1-2 years, severe asymptomatic every **6-12 months** (watch LVEF 60% or less and LV end-systolic diameter 40 mm or more). [ACC/AHA 2020 VHD]`,`**Heart failure**: repeat echo **3-6 months after GDMT optimisation** to decide on ICD or CRT; NT-proBNP trend supports, but does not replace, clinical review. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**After ACS**: echo before discharge for LVEF, and repeat at **6-12 weeks** if EF was 40% or less, before device decisions. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Do not repeat** stress tests routinely in stable, asymptomatic patients after revascularisation - test only for new or changed symptoms. [ACC/AHA 2021 chest pain]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2024 chronic coronary syndromes**: RF-CL likelihood model with calcium-score adjustment; **CCTA for over 5-50%**, **functional imaging for over 15-85%**, invasive angiography for over 85%; formal testing for **ANOCA/INOCA**. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**ESC 2023 ACS**: hs-troponin **0/1-hour** (or 0/2-hour) algorithms, **radial access** as default, and **CCTA** as an option in low-to-intermediate likelihood NSTE-ACS when troponin and ECG are normal or inconclusive. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis**: **cardiac CT and FDG-PET/CT** added as major imaging criteria, especially for prosthetic valve and device infection. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2022 pulmonary hypertension**: PH redefined as **mean PA pressure over 20 mmHg** and PVR over 2 Wood units, with echo TR-velocity probability bands. [ESC 2022 PH]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: wearable alerts need ECG confirmation; AF is diagnosed on a 12-lead ECG or a **30-second single-lead** tracing. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**ASE 2025 diastolic function update**: adds **LA strain** and supplementary markers to the E/e', LA volume and TR velocity algorithm for estimating filling pressure and diagnosing HFpEF. [ASE 2025]`,`**ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy**: CMR with LGE recommended in all cardiomyopathy at baseline, and **bone scintigraphy** in the amyloid pathway. [ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: the ischaemic cascade, Duke treadmill score, TMT contraindications and stopping criteria, **PASP calculation**, FFR cut-off, LGE patterns and NT-proBNP cut-offs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistakes**: ordering a TMT in **LBBB** or acute chest pain, treating **any raised troponin as MI**, ordering CCTA in fast AF or heavy calcification, and judging EF in AF from a single beat. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Worked PASP**: TR velocity 3.5 m/s gives a gradient of 4 x 12.25 = 49 mmHg; with a dilated non-collapsing IVC (RAP 15), PASP is **64 mmHg**. [ASE 2015]`,`**Viva on the Bayes theorem**: a test is most useful at **intermediate pre-test probability**; at very low probability positives are mostly false, at very high probability negatives are mostly false. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anatomy vs function**: a 70% stenosis on angiography may not be ischaemic - **FFR or stress imaging** decides who benefits from PCI. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Viva on CMR**: subendocardial LGE in a coronary territory = infarct; subepicardial = myocarditis; T2-star under 20 ms = iron. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Which test for which question`,columns:[`Clinical question`,`First test`,`Next test`,`Key point`],rows:[[`Acute chest pain - is it ACS?`,`ECG in 10 min, hs-troponin 0/1 h`,`Echo; invasive angiography if confirmed`,`No treadmill until ACS excluded`],[`Stable chest pain, likelihood over 5-50%`,`CT coronary angiography`,`Functional imaging or FFR if stenosis uncertain`,`High negative predictive value`],[`Stable chest pain, likelihood over 15-85%`,`Stress echo, SPECT, PET or stress CMR`,`Invasive angiography if high-risk ischaemia`,`Ischaemia 10% or more = high risk`],[`Very high likelihood or high-risk features`,`Invasive angiography with FFR/iFR`,`PCI or CABG by heart team`,`Radial access preferred`],[`Breathlessness - is it heart failure?`,`NT-proBNP, ECG, chest X-ray`,`Echo for EF and cause`,`Under 125 pg/mL makes HF unlikely`],[`Murmur`,`Echo`,`TEE, CT or CMR if needed`,`Severity by echo, not loudness`],[`Palpitations or syncope`,`12-lead ECG`,`Holter, patch, loop or ILR by frequency`,`Seek symptom-rhythm correlation`],[`Cardiomyopathy or myocarditis`,`Echo, troponin`,`CMR with LGE and mapping`,`PYP scan if amyloid suspected`],[`Suspected pulmonary hypertension`,`Echo TR velocity`,`Right heart catheterisation`,`mPAP over 20 mmHg defines PH`]]},{heading:`Key cut-offs and their meaning`,columns:[`Test`,`Normal or rule-out`,`Abnormal threshold`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`hs-cTnT (Elecsys) 0/1 h`,`Under 5 ng/L at 0 h (pain over 3 h)`,`52 ng/L or more, or 1-h rise of 5 or more`,`Rule in NSTEMI with clinical context`],[`NT-proBNP`,`Under 125 (chronic), under 300 pg/mL (acute)`,`Over 450 / 900 / 1800 by age band`,`Heart failure likely`],[`LVEF`,`About 52-54% or more`,`40% or less`,`HFrEF; 41-49% mildly reduced`],[`E/e' average`,`14 or less`,`Over 14`,`Raised LV filling pressure`],[`LA volume index`,`34 mL/m2 or less`,`Over 34 mL/m2`,`Chronic raised LA pressure`],[`TR velocity`,`2.8 m/s or less`,`Over 3.4 m/s`,`High probability of pulmonary hypertension`],[`TAPSE`,`17 mm or more`,`Under 17 mm`,`RV systolic dysfunction`],[`Aortic stenosis`,`Peak velocity under 2.5 m/s`,`4.0 m/s or more, mean 40 mmHg, area under 1.0 cm2`,`Severe AS`],[`Cardiothoracic ratio (PA)`,`0.5 or less`,`Over 0.5`,`Cardiomegaly`],[`Duke treadmill score`,`+5 or more`,`-11 or less`,`High annual mortality - angiography`],[`Calcium score (Agatston)`,`0`,`100 or more, or 400 or more`,`Statin favoured; high risk`],[`FFR / iFR`,`Over 0.80 / over 0.89`,`0.80 or less / 0.89 or less`,`Functionally significant stenosis`],[`Myocardial T2-star`,`20 ms or more`,`Under 20 ms; under 10 ms severe`,`Myocardial iron overload`]]},{heading:`Stress tests compared`,columns:[`Test`,`How stress is given`,`Best for`,`Avoid or limited when`],rows:[[`Exercise ECG (TMT)`,`Treadmill, Bruce protocol`,`Exercise capacity, BP response, prognosis`,`LBBB, paced, WPW, digoxin, resting ST depression, women (low specificity)`],[`Stress echo`,`Exercise or dobutamine`,`Ischaemia, viability, low-flow AS; no radiation`,`Poor windows, operator inexperience`],[`SPECT perfusion`,`Exercise or vasodilator`,`Ischaemia burden, LBBB or pacing (vasodilator)`,`Radiation, attenuation in obesity, balanced 3-vessel disease`],[`PET perfusion`,`Vasodilator`,`Absolute blood flow, microvascular disease, obesity`,`Cost, availability`],[`Stress CMR`,`Vasodilator or dobutamine`,`Ischaemia plus scar in one study`,`Devices, claustrophobia, eGFR under 30 for gadolinium`],[`CT coronary angiography`,`None (anatomical test)`,`Ruling out CAD at low-moderate likelihood`,`Heavy calcium, fast AF, contrast allergy, eGFR under 30`]]},{heading:`Drugs used during cardiac testing`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Adenosine (vasodilator stress)`,`140 mcg/kg/min IV infusion`,`4-6 minutes; half-life seconds`,`Flushing, chest tightness, AV block, bronchospasm; avoid in asthma, high-grade AV block, SBP under 90`],[`Regadenoson`,`0.4 mg IV bolus over 10 s, saline flush`,`Single dose`,`Dyspnoea, headache, rarely seizures; reverse with aminophylline`],[`Dipyridamole`,`0.56 mg/kg IV over 4 minutes`,`Imaging 3-5 minutes after`,`Bronchospasm, headache; reverse with aminophylline`],[`Aminophylline (reversal)`,`50-250 mg slow IV`,`Repeat if symptoms persist`,`Arrhythmia, seizures at high dose`],[`Dobutamine (stress echo)`,`5, 10, 20, 30, 40 mcg/kg/min IV`,`3-minute stages to target heart rate`,`Arrhythmia, hypertension or hypotension; avoid in severe LVOT obstruction, uncontrolled arrhythmia`],[`Atropine (adjunct)`,`0.25-0.5 mg IV boluses, up to 2 mg`,`If target heart rate not reached`,`Avoid in glaucoma, prostatic obstruction`],[`Metoprolol (before CCTA)`,`50-100 mg oral 1 h before or 5 mg IV boluses`,`Target heart rate under 60-65`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm; ivabradine if beta-blocker contraindicated`],[`Glyceryl trinitrate (CCTA)`,`0.4-0.8 mg sublingual`,`Just before the scan`,`Hypotension; avoid after PDE5 inhibitors and in severe AS`],[`Isotonic saline (contrast prophylaxis)`,`1 mL/kg/h IV (0.5 if EF 35% or less)`,`12 h before and 24 h after`,`Fluid overload in heart failure`],[`Gadolinium (CMR)`,`0.1-0.2 mmol/kg IV`,`Single dose`,`Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis risk with eGFR under 30 - use group II agents only if essential`]]},{heading:`Ambulatory rhythm monitoring`,columns:[`Device`,`Duration`,`Best for`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Holter`,`24-48 hours`,`Daily symptoms, ectopic burden, AF rate control`,`Misses infrequent events`],[`Patch monitor`,`7-14 days`,`Weekly symptoms, paroxysmal AF`,`Single lead; skin irritation`],[`External loop or event recorder`,`2-4 weeks`,`Weekly to monthly palpitations or presyncope`,`Patient must wear and activate it`],[`Implantable loop recorder`,`Up to about 3 years`,`Rare recurrent syncope, cryptogenic stroke AF`,`Minor procedure, cost`],[`Smartwatch or phone ECG`,`On demand`,`Patient-captured episodes, AF screening`,`Needs ECG confirmation (30 s single-lead)`]]},{heading:`CMR late gadolinium enhancement patterns`,columns:[`LGE pattern`,`Diagnosis`],rows:[[`Subendocardial to transmural, coronary territory`,`Myocardial infarction (viability if under 50% thickness)`],[`Subepicardial, inferolateral wall`,`Myocarditis`],[`Mid-wall septal stripe`,`Dilated cardiomyopathy`],[`Patchy in hypertrophied segments, RV insertion points`,`Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`],[`Patchy, basal septum, non-coronary`,`Cardiac sarcoidosis`],[`Diffuse subendocardial, difficult myocardial nulling`,`Cardiac amyloidosis`]]}],redFlags:[`Rising or falling hs-troponin with chest pain or new ischaemic ECG change - admit on the ACS pathway.`,`Treadmill test with ST elevation, a fall in systolic BP during exercise, sustained VT or Duke score of -11 or less - urgent cardiology referral.`,`Echo showing a large pericardial effusion with RV diastolic collapse, LV thrombus or a new severe valve lesion in a symptomatic patient.`,`Holter showing awake pauses over 3 seconds, Mobitz II or complete heart block, or sustained VT - same-day referral.`,`Chest X-ray with a widened mediastinum in a patient with chest or back pain - suspect aortic dissection.`,`NT-proBNP markedly raised with hypoxia, crackles or hypotension - acute heart failure needing admission.`,`Wheeze, hypotension or angioedema after iodinated contrast - treat as anaphylaxis with IM adrenaline.`],pearls:[`**Ischaemic cascade**: perfusion, diastole, wall motion, ECG, pain - why imaging beats the treadmill.`,`**Troponin means injury, not infarction** - MI needs a rise or fall plus ischaemic context.`,`**NT-proBNP 125 chronic, 300 acute** - below these, heart failure is unlikely.`,`**PASP = 4V squared + RAP.**`,`**FFR 0.80 or less - treat; above 0.80 - defer.**`,`**Calcium score zero** - the power of zero.`,`**No TMT for diagnosis** in LBBB, paced rhythm, WPW, digoxin or resting ST depression.`,`**Holter for daily, loop for monthly, ILR for rare** symptoms.`,`**LGE subendocardial = ischaemic; mid-wall or subepicardial = non-ischaemic.**`,`**PYP grade 2-3 with no monoclonal protein = ATTR amyloid** without biopsy.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-q1`,stem:`In which of the following patients is an exercise ECG (treadmill test) unsuitable for diagnosing myocardial ischaemia, so that stress imaging should be chosen instead?`,options:[`A man with complete left bundle branch block`,`A woman with resting sinus bradycardia of 56/min`,`A man with first-degree AV block`,`A woman with a normal resting ECG and typical angina`],answer:0,explanation:`Complete LBBB makes exercise ST changes uninterpretable and causes false septal defects on exercise SPECT, so vasodilator perfusion imaging is chosen. Sinus bradycardia and first-degree AV block do not distort the ST segment, and a patient with a normal resting ECG can be assessed by treadmill if imaging is unavailable.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old man in the OPD has exertional breathlessness for 3 months. Below which NT-proBNP value is heart failure unlikely in this non-acute setting?`,options:[`300 pg/mL`,`450 pg/mL`,`125 pg/mL`,`900 pg/mL`],answer:2,explanation:`ESC 2021 uses an NT-proBNP under 125 pg/mL to make heart failure unlikely in non-acute presentations. The 300 pg/mL cut-off applies to acute dyspnoea in the emergency department, and 450 and 900 pg/mL are age-specific rule-in thresholds for acute heart failure, not rule-out values.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-q3`,stem:`Echo shows a peak tricuspid regurgitation velocity of 3.5 m/s and a dilated IVC (2.5 cm) that collapses less than 50% on sniffing. The estimated pulmonary artery systolic pressure is about:`,options:[`49 mmHg`,`52 mmHg`,`57 mmHg`,`64 mmHg`],answer:3,explanation:`The simplified Bernoulli equation gives the RV-RA gradient as 4 x 3.5 squared = 49 mmHg; a dilated IVC with less than 50% collapse means a right atrial pressure of about 15 mmHg, so PASP is about 64 mmHg. The value 49 mmHg forgets to add RAP, and 52 mmHg adds the 3 mmHg used only for a small collapsing IVC.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-q4`,stem:`During coronary angiography a 70% stenosis of the left anterior descending artery is assessed with a pressure wire. Which fractional flow reserve value indicates a functionally significant lesion that benefits from revascularisation?`,options:[`0.92`,`0.76`,`0.85`,`0.81`],answer:1,explanation:`An FFR of 0.80 or less marks a haemodynamically significant stenosis, so 0.76 favours PCI. Values above 0.80 such as 0.81, 0.85 and 0.92 mean the lesion is not flow-limiting despite its angiographic appearance, and deferring PCI is safe - anatomy alone over-calls stenoses.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-q5`,stem:`A 50-year-old non-smoking, non-diabetic man with an intermediate 10-year risk is unsure about starting a statin. His coronary artery calcium score is 0. What is the most appropriate interpretation?`,options:[`Proceed to CT coronary angiography`,`Start high-intensity statin regardless`,`Statin can reasonably be deferred, with reassessment in some years`,`Arrange invasive coronary angiography`],answer:2,explanation:`A calcium score of zero in an intermediate-risk adult without diabetes, smoking or strong family history carries a very low event rate, so a statin can reasonably be deferred and risk reassessed later (the power of zero). CT or invasive angiography is not indicated in an asymptomatic person, and a high-intensity statin regardless ignores the reclassification the score provides.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the non-invasive evaluation of a patient with suspected chronic coronary syndrome. Describe the choice of test by pre-test likelihood, the exercise ECG and its interpretation, stress imaging and CT coronary angiography.`,openingLines:[`Chronic coronary syndrome is suspected when chest discomfort is provoked by exertion and relieved by rest; the choice of test depends on the clinical likelihood of obstructive coronary disease, estimated from age, sex, symptoms and risk factors.`,`ESC 2024 recommends CT coronary angiography at low-to-moderate likelihood, functional imaging at moderate-to-high likelihood and invasive angiography at very high likelihood, with the exercise ECG kept for capacity and prognosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Initial assessment for all`,points:[`History, examination, **resting ECG**, haemoglobin, creatinine, glucose or HbA1c, lipids, TSH if indicated.`,`**Echo** for LV function, regional wall motion, valve disease and HCM.`,`**Chest X-ray** if heart failure or lung disease suspected.`]},{heading:`Clinical likelihood (ESC 2024 RF-CL model)`,points:[`Inputs: age, sex, typical/atypical/non-anginal symptoms or dyspnoea, number of risk factors.`,`Bands: **very low 5% or less**, low over 5-15%, moderate over 15-50%, high over 50-85%, very high over 85%.`,`Calcium score can reclassify likelihood downward or upward.`,`Bayesian logic: testing is most informative at intermediate likelihood.`]},{heading:`Exercise ECG`,points:[`Bruce protocol; target 85% of 220 minus age.`,`Positive: **1 mm or more horizontal or downsloping ST depression** 60-80 ms after J point.`,`**Duke score** = time - 5 x ST deviation - 4 x angina index; -11 or less high risk.`,`Contraindications: acute MI under 2 days, unstable angina, symptomatic severe AS, acute PE, myocarditis, dissection.`,`Limitations: modest accuracy, poor in women; uninterpretable with LBBB, pacing, WPW, digoxin.`]},{heading:`Stress imaging`,points:[`**Stress echo** (exercise or dobutamine) - new wall-motion abnormality; no radiation.`,`**SPECT** - reversible defect is ischaemia, fixed defect is scar; vasodilator for LBBB/paced.`,`**PET** - absolute flow, microvascular disease; **stress CMR** - perfusion plus scar.`,`High risk: ischaemia **10% or more of LV**, 3 or more segments on stress echo, 2 or more on CMR.`]},{heading:`CT coronary angiography and calcium score`,points:[`Negative predictive value near 99%; first choice at over 5-50% likelihood.`,`Needs heart rate under 60-65, sinus rhythm, eGFR check; limited by heavy calcium and fast AF.`,`CAD-RADS reporting; CT-FFR adds function.`,`Agatston calcium score 0 = very low risk.`]},{heading:`When to proceed to invasive angiography`,points:[`Very high likelihood, high-risk non-invasive result, symptoms despite medicines, or LV dysfunction suggesting CAD.`,`Use **FFR or iFR** for intermediate stenoses; consider coronary function testing for ANOCA/INOCA.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: clinical likelihood bands leading to deferral, CCTA, functional imaging or invasive angiography`,`The ischaemic cascade as a sequence diagram`,`Table comparing exercise ECG, stress echo, SPECT, PET, stress CMR and CCTA`],markSplit:[{part:`Initial assessment and likelihood model`,marks:3},{part:`Exercise ECG with Duke score and contraindications`,marks:3},{part:`Stress imaging modalities and high-risk thresholds`,marks:2},{part:`CCTA, calcium score and when to do invasive angiography`,marks:2}],keywords:[`pre-test likelihood`,`RF-CL model`,`treadmill test`,`Duke treadmill score`,`stress echo`,`SPECT`,`CCTA`,`FFR`]},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiac-investigations-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on interpreting an echocardiography report for the family physician.`,openingLines:[`Transthoracic echocardiography is the first-line imaging test for cardiac structure and function, reporting ventricular size and function, valve disease, pressures and the pericardium.`,`The family physician needs to read five lines of it: LVEF, diastolic function, valves, pulmonary artery pressure with RV function, and pericardium.`],answer:[{heading:`LV size and systolic function`,points:[`LVEF by Simpson biplane: **40% or less HFrEF**, 41-49% HFmrEF, **50% or more** preserved.`,`Regional wall-motion abnormality suggests ischaemia or infarction; LVH and wall thickness 15 mm or more suggest HCM.`]},{heading:`Diastolic function`,points:[`Raised filling pressure: average **E/e' over 14**, LA volume index over 34 mL/m2, TR velocity over 2.8 m/s.`]},{heading:`Valves`,points:[`Severe AS: velocity 4 m/s or more, mean gradient 40 mmHg or more, area under 1.0 cm2.`,`Severe MS: area 1.5 cm2 or less; severe MR: EROA 0.40 cm2 or more.`,`Rheumatic features: thickened leaflets, restricted motion, commissural fusion.`]},{heading:`Right heart and pressures`,points:[`**PASP = 4V squared + RAP**; TR velocity over 3.4 m/s = high probability of PH.`,`TAPSE under 17 mm = RV dysfunction.`]},{heading:`Pericardium and action`,points:[`Effusion over 20 mm is large; chamber collapse means tamponade - same-day referral.`,`Act on EF 40% or less (start GDMT), severe valve disease (refer) and high PH probability (refer).`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of key echo parameters with normal and abnormal values`],markSplit:[{part:`LV systolic and diastolic function`,marks:2},{part:`Valve severity`,marks:1},{part:`Right heart and PASP`,marks:1},{part:`Pericardium and action points`,marks:1}],keywords:[`LVEF`,`E/e'`,`PASP`,`TAPSE`,`aortic stenosis severity`,`pericardial effusion`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Noninvasive cardiac imaging; Diagnostic cardiac catheterisation`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Chest radiography, Echocardiography, Exercise testing, Nuclear cardiology, Cardiac CT and CMR`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of chronic coronary syndromes`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes`,`2021 ESC Guidelines for heart failure and 2023 focused update; 2022 ESC/ERS Guidelines for pulmonary hypertension`,`2021 AHA/ACC Guideline for the Evaluation and Diagnosis of Chest Pain; 2020 ACC/AHA Valvular Heart Disease Guideline`,`2018 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of syncope; 2024 ESC Guidelines for atrial fibrillation`,`ICMR-INDIAB national study, Lancet Diabetes and Endocrinology 2023`]}),e.push({id:`cardiovascular-palpitations`,title:`Approach to palpitations`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Palpitations** are an unpleasant **awareness of the heartbeat** - fast, irregular, forceful or missed; the diagnosis rests on **symptom-rhythm correlation** (an ECG during symptoms), most causes are **benign ectopics, sinus tachycardia or anxiety**, and the task in the OPD is to pick out the **SVT, AF and VT** and the red flags of **syncope, exertional symptoms, structural heart disease, an abnormal resting ECG or family history of sudden death**.`,keywords:[`palpitations`,`ectopic beats`,`premature ventricular complex`,`PVC burden`,`SVT`,`AVNRT`,`WPW`,`atrial fibrillation`,`ventricular tachycardia`,`Holter`,`loop recorder`,`symptom-rhythm correlation`,`modified Valsalva`,`adenosine`,`thyrotoxicosis`,`panic disorder`,`POTS`,`inappropriate sinus tachycardia`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Palpitations** are a subjective awareness of the heartbeat, described as racing, pounding, fluttering, skipping or thumping - a symptom, not a diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`They are among the **commonest reasons for primary care and cardiology referral**, second only to chest pain among cardiac symptoms. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`In a classic series of patients presenting with palpitations, causes were **cardiac in about 43%**, **psychiatric in about 31%**, miscellaneous (drugs, thyrotoxicosis, anaemia, caffeine) in about 10% and **unexplained in about 16%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Most palpitations are benign**, but they can be the only warning of **WPW, long QT, Brugada syndrome, HCM, ARVC or VT** - conditions that cause sudden death in the young. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Indian context**: anaemia affects about **57% of women aged 15-49** (NFHS-5), thyroid disease is common, and **rheumatic MS with AF** still causes palpitations in young adults. [NFHS-5 2021]`,`**AF** is the commonest sustained arrhythmia overall and rises steeply with age; **AVNRT** is the commonest regular SVT in adults, more often in women. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**SVT is often misdiagnosed as panic disorder** - in one series over half of patients with documented SVT had first been told they had anxiety. [ESC 2019 SVT]`]},{heading:`Causes and classification`,points:[`**EHRA classification** by description: **extrasystolic** (skips, thumps), **tachycardic** (fast regular or irregular), **anxiety-related** (gradual, with anxiety symptoms) and **pulsating** (forceful regular beats). [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`**Arrhythmic causes**: atrial and ventricular **ectopics**, **AVNRT, AVRT (WPW)**, atrial tachycardia and flutter, **AF**, VT, and bradyarrhythmias with compensatory ectopics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Structural or non-arrhythmic cardiac causes**: valve disease (AR, MR), HCM, cardiomyopathy, prosthetic valves, pacemaker syndrome, pericarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High-output and systemic causes**: **thyrotoxicosis, anaemia, fever, pregnancy, hypoglycaemia, hypovolaemia**, phaeochromocytoma, AV fistula, Paget disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Substances and drugs**: **caffeine, energy drinks, nicotine, alcohol** (holiday heart), salbutamol, theophylline, **excess levothyroxine**, decongestants, cannabis, cocaine, amphetamines, and **beta-blocker withdrawal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**QT-prolonging drugs** - azithromycin, levofloxacin, ondansetron, domperidone, haloperidol, hydroxychloroquine - cause torsades with palpitations and syncope; see the Long QT topic. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Psychiatric**: panic disorder, generalised anxiety, depression and somatic symptom disorder - a diagnosis made **after** arrhythmia is excluded. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`**Postural tachycardia syndrome (POTS)** and **inappropriate sinus tachycardia** cause sinus tachycardia out of proportion to need, typically in young women. [HRS 2015 POTS/IST]`]},{heading:`Mechanism: why patients feel their heart`,points:[`A **premature beat** is often not felt itself; the **compensatory pause** lets the ventricle overfill, and the **next beat is forceful** - felt as a thump or 'heart stopping'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AVNRT** uses a re-entry circuit of slow and fast pathways in the AV node; atria and ventricles contract almost together against closed AV valves, giving **regular neck pounding (frog sign)**. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**AVRT** uses an accessory pathway; when the pathway conducts antegradely in sinus rhythm, the resting ECG shows **pre-excitation (short PR, delta wave)** - the WPW pattern. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Polyuria after an SVT episode** reflects atrial natriuretic peptide released by atrial stretch. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**AF** causes an irregularly irregular, often fast pulse; loss of atrial kick reduces output, so breathlessness and fatigue may dominate over palpitation. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Frequent PVCs** (high burden) cause dyssynchronous contraction and can lead to **PVC-induced cardiomyopathy**, reversible when the burden is reduced. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Anxiety and hyperthyroidism** increase sympathetic tone and beta-receptor sensitivity, causing sinus tachycardia and more ectopics - why **beta-blockers** relieve both. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`History: the patterns that point to the rhythm`,points:[`Ask the patient to **tap out the rhythm** on the table - irregular skips (ectopics), fast regular (SVT, flutter, VT), or fast irregular (AF). [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`**Onset and offset**: **abrupt start and stop** suggests re-entrant SVT; **gradual** onset and offset suggests sinus tachycardia (anxiety, exertion, fever). [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Termination by vagal manoeuvres** (breath-holding, straining, cold water) strongly suggests AV-node-dependent SVT. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Triggers**: exercise (catecholaminergic VT, ARVC, long QT 1, AF in athletes), **sudden noise or emotion** (long QT 2), sleep (long QT 3, Brugada), bending (SVT), alcohol binges (AF), caffeine and lying on the left side (ectopics). [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Associated symptoms**: **syncope or near-syncope**, chest pain, breathlessness and exertional onset are **red flags**; tingling, chest tightness and fear before the palpitation suggest panic. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Age at first episode**: childhood or adolescence suggests AVRT or AVNRT; later life suggests AF, flutter or VT from structural disease. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Drug and substance history**: tea, coffee, energy drinks, alcohol, tobacco, cannabis, inhalers, thyroxine, decongestants, weight-loss and herbal products, and recent QT-prolonging antibiotics or antiemetics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Past and family history**: MI, cardiomyopathy, valve disease, rheumatic fever, heart surgery; **unexplained sudden death, drowning or road accident under 40** in the family, or known channelopathy. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Systemic symptoms**: weight loss, heat intolerance and tremor (thyrotoxicosis); fatigue and breathlessness (anaemia); episodic headache, sweating and hypertension (phaeochromocytoma); sweating and hunger in diabetics on insulin (hypoglycaemia). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`**If symptomatic now**: pulse rate and regularity, BP, perfusion, SpO2 and an **immediate 12-lead ECG** - this single tracing is the most valuable test. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Irregularly irregular pulse with a pulse deficit** suggests AF; **regular 150/min** suggests atrial flutter with 2:1 block. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cannon a waves** in the neck during a regular tachycardia suggest AVNRT (regular) or VT with AV dissociation (irregular); **variable S1** in a broad-complex tachycardia favours **VT**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Look for structural disease**: murmurs (MS, MR, HCM - louder on Valsalva), displaced apex, S3, raised JVP, signs of heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Look for systemic causes**: **pallor**, goitre, tremor, lid lag, warm moist skin, fever, signs of dehydration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Postural BP and heart rate**: a sustained heart-rate rise of **30/min or more within 10 minutes of standing** (40 or more at age 12-19) without orthostatic hypotension suggests **POTS**. [HRS 2015 POTS/IST]`,`**Between episodes** the examination is usually normal - which reassures about structure but **does not exclude an arrhythmia**. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Ectopics vs AF**: ectopics give isolated skips in an otherwise regular rhythm; AF is continuously irregular - only the ECG settles it. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**SVT vs sinus tachycardia**: SVT starts and stops abruptly at a fixed fast rate (often 150-250/min) and stops with vagal manoeuvres; sinus tachycardia speeds up and slows down gradually. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**SVT vs panic attack**: panic builds over minutes with fear, tremor, paraesthesia and air hunger; regular pounding in the neck with abrupt offset favours SVT - **do not diagnose panic without an ECG during symptoms**. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Broad-complex tachycardia**: treat as **VT until proved otherwise**, especially with prior MI or heart failure - haemodynamic stability does not exclude VT. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Atrial flutter with 2:1 block** at a steady 150/min is often mistaken for sinus tachycardia; look for flutter waves in II, III, aVF and V1. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis** presents as persistent sinus tachycardia or new AF, especially in the elderly (apathetic hyperthyroidism) - check TSH in every new AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Hypoglycaemia** in a diabetic on insulin or a sulphonylurea mimics anxiety with palpitations - check capillary glucose during the episode. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line`,points:[`**12-lead ECG during symptoms** is the gold standard; ask patients to attend the nearest ECG facility during an episode or use a validated smartphone ECG. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Resting ECG between episodes** - look for **pre-excitation** (PR under 120 ms, delta wave), **long QTc** (470 ms or more in men, 480 ms or more in women), **Brugada type 1**, LVH, Q waves, epsilon waves or T inversion in V1-V3 (ARVC), and AV block. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Blood tests**: **haemoglobin, TSH, potassium, magnesium, calcium**, glucose or HbA1c, creatinine; consider urine or plasma metanephrines only if paroxysmal hypertension suggests phaeochromocytoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** if there is a murmur, abnormal ECG, heart failure, frequent PVCs, sustained arrhythmia or family history of cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Ambulatory monitoring matched to frequency**: Holter for daily symptoms, **patch or external loop recorder** for weekly, **implantable loop recorder** for rare symptoms with syncope - see the Cardiac investigations topic. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Smartwatch photoplethysmography** alerts need confirmation - AF is diagnosed on a 12-lead ECG or a **30-second single-lead** tracing. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**PVC burden** on Holter: **over 10%** warrants echo and follow-up for PVC-induced cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Investigations: advanced`,points:[`**Exercise testing** when palpitations are exertional - unmasks catecholaminergic VT, exercise-induced AF or SVT, and shows whether PVCs **suppress (reassuring)** or **increase** with exercise. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Cardiac MRI** for frequent PVCs or VT with suspected structural disease - ARVC, myocarditis, sarcoidosis, scar. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Electrophysiology study** for documented or strongly suspected SVT, to map and **ablate in the same sitting**; also for risk stratification of asymptomatic pre-excitation in selected cases. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Sodium channel blocker challenge** (ajmaline or flecainide) unmasks Brugada type 1 in suspected cases - specialist only. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Genetic testing** with family screening when a channelopathy or cardiomyopathy is diagnosed. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD`,points:[`**Reassure with evidence**: a normal ECG, normal echo and documented ectopics or sinus tachycardia during symptoms - explain the compensatory pause mechanism; reassurance itself reduces symptoms. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`**Lifestyle**: reduce caffeine, energy drinks and alcohol, stop tobacco and stimulants, regular sleep, treat anxiety, correct anaemia and dehydration. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Symptomatic ectopics with a normal heart**: **beta-blocker** such as **metoprolol 25-100 mg twice daily** or **bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg once daily**, or a non-dihydropyridine CCB (**verapamil 40-120 mg three times daily** or **diltiazem**). [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Frequent symptomatic PVCs from the RV outflow tract or left fascicles** with a normal heart: **catheter ablation is first-line** if drugs fail or are not wanted; **flecainide** is an alternative only when the heart is structurally normal. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**High PVC burden with falling EF**: refer for **ablation** or amiodarone - EF often recovers when the burden falls. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Recurrent SVT**: teach the **modified Valsalva manoeuvre**; refer for **catheter ablation (success over 95%)** rather than lifelong drugs; a **pill-in-the-pocket** regimen suits infrequent, well-tolerated episodes. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**AF**: rate control, stroke risk by **CHA2DS2-VA**, and anticoagulation - see the Atrial fibrillation topic. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis**: **propranolol 10-40 mg three to four times daily** for symptoms while antithyroid treatment starts. [ATA 2016]`,`**Panic disorder**: CBT and an SSRI such as **sertraline 25 mg daily, rising to 50-200 mg**; avoid long-term benzodiazepines. [NICE CG113]`,`**POTS and inappropriate sinus tachycardia**: fluids 2-3 L/day and salt, compression, graded recumbent exercise; **ivabradine 2.5-7.5 mg twice daily** or low-dose beta-blocker for persistent symptoms. [HRS 2015 POTS/IST]`]},{heading:`Emergency management of a patient in tachycardia`,points:[`**Unstable** (shock, syncope, ischaemic chest pain, acute heart failure): **synchronised DC cardioversion** after sedation, whatever the QRS width. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Stable regular narrow-complex**: **modified Valsalva** - strain against 40 mmHg for 15 seconds semi-recumbent, then lie flat with legs raised to 45 degrees for 15 seconds; it terminates about 4 in 10 episodes. [REVERT trial / ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV bolus** with saline flush via a large antecubital vein, then **12 mg** and **18 mg** if needed; warn about transient chest tightness and flushing; avoid in severe asthma. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`If adenosine fails or is contraindicated: **IV verapamil 5 mg over 2 minutes** (repeat to 10-15 mg) or IV metoprolol - never verapamil in a broad-complex tachycardia or pre-excited AF. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Broad-complex tachycardia, stable**: treat as **VT** - IV amiodarone 300 mg over 20-60 minutes or procainamide, with cardioversion ready; see the VT topic. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Pre-excited AF** (fast, irregular, broad and varying QRS): **avoid AV-node blockers** (adenosine, verapamil, digoxin, beta-blockers); cardiovert or give IV procainamide or ibutilide. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`Record a **12-lead ECG during the tachycardia and after conversion** - it is the diagnosis that guides ablation. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Carotid sinus massage** is avoided in those with carotid bruit, prior stroke or TIA and the elderly. [ESC 2019 SVT]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: ectopics and sinus tachycardia increase; for SVT use vagal manoeuvres and **adenosine (safe)**, then **metoprolol**; avoid atenolol; DC cardioversion is safe in all trimesters. [ESC 2018 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: AF, flutter, thyrotoxicosis, digoxin toxicity and **drug interactions** dominate; palpitations with dizziness may be **sick sinus (tachy-brady) syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children and adolescents**: SVT (AVRT, AVNRT) is the commonest sustained arrhythmia; ask about **WPW**, exertional symptoms and family history of sudden death. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Athletes**: ectopics and bradycardia are common, but **exertional palpitations or syncope** need exclusion of HCM, ARVC, myocarditis and channelopathies before return to sport. [ESC 2020 sports cardiology]`,`**Diabetes**: hypoglycaemia mimics arrhythmia; autonomic neuropathy causes fixed tachycardia and orthostatic hypotension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: hyperkalaemia and drug accumulation (digoxin, sotalol, flecainide) - check potassium and dose by eGFR. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: teach patients to reach any ECG during symptoms or record a phone video of the pulse; **resting ECG, Hb and TSH** cover most first-line work-up. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Isolated ectopics with a normal heart** carry an excellent prognosis; symptoms often wax and wane for years. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**PVC burden over about 10-20%** carries a risk of **PVC-induced cardiomyopathy**, reversible after ablation or suppression. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Incessant SVT or AF with fast rates** for weeks can cause **tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy**, which recovers with rate or rhythm control. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**AF** carries a five-fold stroke risk and risk of heart failure and dementia - anticoagulation decisions follow CHA2DS2-VA. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Pre-excited AF** can degenerate to **ventricular fibrillation** via a fast accessory pathway - the sudden-death risk of WPW. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**VT with structural heart disease** signals high sudden-death risk and need for ICD evaluation. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Same-day referral**: palpitations with **syncope, chest pain, breathlessness or hypotension**, documented VT, broad-complex tachycardia, pre-excited AF, or heart rate under 40 or over 150 with symptoms. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Routine cardiology referral**: documented SVT (for ablation), **pre-excitation** on resting ECG, long QT or Brugada pattern, structural heart disease, PVC burden over 10%, or family history of sudden death. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Follow-up of high-burden PVCs**: echo and Holter every **6-12 months** to watch EF and burden. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Unexplained recurrent palpitations** after Holter and loop recorder: consider an ILR if symptoms are disabling or accompanied by presyncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Review drugs** at each visit: thyroxine dose, inhalers, QT-prolonging combinations and electrolyte status on diuretics. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`Counsel on **caffeine and energy drinks**, alcohol binges and tobacco - common, reversible triggers. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`Treat **anaemia** under **Anaemia Mukt Bharat** (iron-folic acid) and screen thyroid function in women with persistent tachycardia. [MoHFW Anaemia Mukt Bharat]`,`**NP-NCD** screening of adults 30 years and older for hypertension and diabetes reduces future AF and structural heart disease. [MoHFW 2024]`,`Teach **pulse self-check** and the modified Valsalva to patients with known SVT, and how to record an episode on a phone ECG. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Family screening** (ECG, echo) of first-degree relatives when a channelopathy, cardiomyopathy or young sudden death is found. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2022 ventricular arrhythmias**: catheter ablation first-line for symptomatic **RVOT and fascicular PVCs**; PVC burden over 10% prompts echo; CMR for suspected structural disease. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: **CHA2DS2-VA** (sex removed from the score), the AF-CARE pathway, and ECG confirmation of wearable-detected AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS 2023 AF**: AF described in **stages** from 'at risk' to permanent, with emphasis on risk-factor modification - weight loss, alcohol reduction, exercise. [ACC/AHA 2023 AF]`,`**ESC 2019 SVT** remains current: modified Valsalva and adenosine first, and **ablation as first-line** for recurrent AVNRT and AVRT. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Smartphone and smartwatch ECGs** are accepted for symptom-rhythm correlation when a clear 30-second tracing is captured. [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'approach to palpitations' - write definition, EHRA types, history patterns, red flags, investigations by frequency, and management of ectopics and SVT. [EHRA palpitations position paper]`,`**Common mistakes**: labelling anxiety without an ECG during symptoms, missing **pre-excitation** on the resting ECG, giving verapamil in broad-complex or pre-excited tachycardia, and forgetting TSH and Hb. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Viva**: 'Why does an ectopic feel like a missed beat followed by a thump?' - the compensatory pause and post-ectopic potentiation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: frog sign (regular neck pounding) means AVNRT, from atria contracting against closed AV valves. [ESC 2019 SVT]`,`**Viva**: PVCs that **disappear with exercise** are usually benign; those that increase or become complex need evaluation. [ESC 2022 VA]`]}],tables:[{heading:`EHRA classification of palpitations`,columns:[`Type`,`Description`,`Onset and offset`,`Likely cause`],rows:[[`Extrasystolic`,`Skips, missed beats, thumps`,`Sporadic`,`Atrial or ventricular ectopics`],[`Tachycardic`,`Fast regular or irregular heartbeat`,`Abrupt`,`SVT, flutter, AF, VT`],[`Anxiety-related`,`Fast regular, with anxiety, tingling, air hunger`,`Gradual`,`Sinus tachycardia, panic`],[`Pulsating`,`Forceful, regular, persistent beats`,`Gradual, persistent`,`High-output states, AR, sinus tachycardia`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of palpitations`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clues`,`ECG clue`,`Confirm with`],rows:[[`Ectopics`,`Skips and thumps at rest, worse lying left, settle with exercise`,`Isolated premature beats`,`Holter with burden`],[`AVNRT`,`Abrupt regular, neck pounding, stops with Valsalva, women`,`Narrow regular, P hidden or pseudo-r' in V1`,`ECG in episode, EP study`],[`AVRT (WPW)`,`Young, abrupt regular; may have syncope`,`Delta wave and short PR at rest`,`ECG, EP study`],[`Atrial fibrillation`,`Irregular, elderly, alcohol, thyroid, MS`,`Irregular, no P waves`,`ECG, 30-s single-lead strip`],[`Atrial flutter`,`Steady fast rate around 150`,`Sawtooth waves in II, III, aVF`,`ECG, adenosine unmasking`],[`VT`,`Prior MI, heart failure, syncope, exertion`,`Broad regular, AV dissociation`,`ECG, echo, CMR`],[`Sinus tachycardia (anxiety)`,`Gradual onset, fear, tingling`,`Sinus rhythm`,`ECG during symptoms`],[`Thyrotoxicosis`,`Weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance, goitre`,`Sinus tachycardia or AF`,`TSH, free T4`],[`Anaemia`,`Fatigue, pallor, menorrhagia`,`Sinus tachycardia`,`Haemoglobin, ferritin`],[`POTS`,`Young woman, symptoms on standing`,`Sinus tachycardia on standing`,`Heart rate rise 30 or more in 10 min`]]},{heading:`Resting ECG findings that need referral`,columns:[`Finding`,`Criterion`,`Concern`],rows:[[`Pre-excitation`,`PR under 120 ms with delta wave`,`WPW; pre-excited AF and sudden death`],[`Long QT`,`QTc 470 ms or more (men), 480 ms or more (women)`,`Torsades, sudden death`],[`Brugada type 1`,`Coved ST elevation 2 mm or more in V1-V2`,`Polymorphic VT, VF`],[`ARVC pattern`,`T inversion V1-V3, epsilon wave`,`VT, sudden death in young`],[`LVH with deep Q waves or giant T inversion`,`Voltage criteria with abnormal repolarisation`,`Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`],[`Pathological Q waves`,`Q 40 ms or more or over 25% of R`,`Scar - VT substrate`],[`High-grade AV block`,`Mobitz II, complete heart block`,`Syncope, pacing needed`]]},{heading:`Drugs used for palpitations`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metoprolol tartrate`,`25-100 mg oral twice daily; IV 2.5-5 mg in tachycardia`,`Titrate every 1-2 weeks to symptoms`,`Bradycardia, fatigue, bronchospasm; avoid in asthma, pre-excited AF`],[`Bisoprolol`,`2.5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Long term or as needed`,`Bradycardia, cold peripheries; avoid in high-grade AV block`],[`Propranolol`,`10-40 mg oral three to four times daily`,`Thyrotoxicosis until euthyroid; anxiety as needed`,`Bronchospasm, hypoglycaemia masking; avoid in asthma`],[`Verapamil`,`40-120 mg oral three times daily; IV 5 mg over 2 min (to 15 mg)`,`Titrate to symptoms`,`Hypotension, constipation; never in broad-complex tachycardia, pre-excited AF, HFrEF`],[`Diltiazem`,`60-120 mg oral two to three times daily (SR forms once or twice daily)`,`Titrate to heart rate`,`Hypotension, bradycardia, oedema; avoid in HFrEF`],[`Adenosine`,`6 mg rapid IV bolus, then 12 mg, then 18 mg`,`Seconds; for termination and diagnosis`,`Flushing, chest tightness, brief asystole; avoid in severe asthma, pre-excited AF`],[`Flecainide`,`50-150 mg oral twice daily; pill-in-pocket 200-300 mg`,`Specialist initiation`,`Proarrhythmia; contraindicated in structural heart disease, CAD, HFrEF`],[`Ivabradine`,`2.5-7.5 mg oral twice daily`,`For IST or POTS`,`Phosphenes, bradycardia; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Sertraline (panic disorder)`,`25 mg oral daily, rising to 50-200 mg`,`At least 6-12 months after remission`,`Initial jitteriness, GI upset, hyponatraemia in elderly`]]},{heading:`Ambulatory monitoring by symptom frequency`,columns:[`Symptom frequency`,`Test`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Daily`,`Holter`,`24-48 hours`],[`Weekly`,`Patch monitor`,`7-14 days`],[`Weekly to monthly`,`External loop or event recorder`,`2-4 weeks`],[`Rare but disabling or with syncope`,`Implantable loop recorder`,`Up to about 3 years`],[`Any, patient-captured`,`Smartphone or watch ECG`,`30-second tracing`]]}],redFlags:[`Palpitations with syncope or near-syncope, especially during exertion or lying down.`,`Palpitations with chest pain, breathlessness, hypotension or signs of heart failure.`,`Broad-complex tachycardia, or a fast irregular broad-complex rhythm suggesting pre-excited AF.`,`Known structural heart disease - prior MI, cardiomyopathy, severe valve disease - with new palpitations.`,`Resting ECG showing pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada type 1, ARVC pattern or high-grade AV block.`,`Family history of unexplained sudden death, drowning or channelopathy under 40 years.`,`Heart rate over 150/min with symptoms or under 40/min with dizziness.`],pearls:[`**Symptom-rhythm correlation is everything** - an ECG during symptoms beats any amount of history.`,`**Tap it out**: skips = ectopics, fast regular = SVT, fast irregular = AF.`,`**Abrupt on, abrupt off, stops with Valsalva = AV-node re-entry.**`,`**Frog sign** (regular neck pounding) = AVNRT.`,`**Never diagnose panic until the ECG during symptoms is normal.**`,`**PVCs that vanish with exercise are usually benign.**`,`**Broad and fast = VT until proved otherwise.**`,`**Pre-excited AF: no AV-node blockers** - cardiovert or procainamide.`,`**Check TSH and Hb in every patient with palpitations.**`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman describes 'skipped beats followed by a thump', worse at night lying on her left side. Examination, resting ECG, haemoglobin and TSH are normal, and a Holter shows isolated monomorphic ventricular ectopics (burden 2%) correlating with symptoms. The best management is:`,options:[`Start amiodarone`,`Explain the benign nature, reduce caffeine and reassure`,`Refer for implantable cardioverter-defibrillator`,`Start flecainide and repeat Holter monthly`],answer:1,explanation:`Low-burden monomorphic ectopics in a structurally normal heart are benign, and explanation of the compensatory pause, reduction of caffeine and alcohol and reassurance are first-line; a beta-blocker is added only if symptoms persist. Amiodarone has serious toxicity, an ICD is for high sudden-death risk, and flecainide with monthly Holters is unnecessary at a 2% burden.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-q2`,stem:`A 22-year-old man has recurrent episodes of abrupt-onset regular palpitations. His resting ECG shows a PR interval of 100 ms with a slurred upstroke of the QRS. Which drug must be avoided if he presents with fast irregular broad-complex tachycardia?`,options:[`Procainamide`,`Ibutilide`,`Synchronised DC cardioversion`,`Verapamil`],answer:3,explanation:`Short PR with a delta wave is the WPW pattern; fast irregular broad complexes suggest pre-excited AF. AV-node blockers such as verapamil (and adenosine, digoxin, beta-blockers) increase conduction down the accessory pathway and may cause VF. Cardioversion, procainamide and ibutilide slow the pathway or restore sinus rhythm and are appropriate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-q3`,stem:`A haemodynamically stable 40-year-old woman presents with a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 180/min. What is the first step?`,options:[`Modified Valsalva manoeuvre`,`IV amiodarone 300 mg`,`Synchronised DC cardioversion`,`IV digoxin 0.5 mg`],answer:0,explanation:`For stable regular narrow-complex tachycardia, vagal manoeuvres come first; the modified Valsalva (strain then lie flat with legs raised) terminates about 4 in 10 episodes, followed by adenosine 6-12-18 mg if needed. DC cardioversion is reserved for instability, amiodarone is not first-line for SVT, and digoxin is slow and ineffective for termination.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man has frequent palpitations. A 24-hour Holter shows monomorphic PVCs with an LBBB-inferior axis morphology and a burden of 22%; echo shows LVEF 42%, previously 60%. The most appropriate next step is:`,options:[`Reassurance and repeat Holter in 2 years`,`Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator`,`Referral for catheter ablation of the PVC focus`,`Start flecainide long term`],answer:2,explanation:`A high PVC burden with a fall in EF suggests PVC-induced cardiomyopathy; ablation of an outflow-tract focus is first-line and EF often recovers. Reassurance ignores the cardiomyopathy, flecainide is contraindicated once LV function is impaired, and an ICD is not indicated for a potentially reversible cardiomyopathy.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-q5`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman has palpitations occurring about twice a month, lasting 20 minutes and ending abruptly. Resting ECG and echo are normal. Which test is most likely to give a diagnosis?`,options:[`24-hour Holter monitor`,`External loop or event recorder for 2-4 weeks`,`Exercise treadmill test`,`Repeat resting 12-lead ECG`],answer:1,explanation:`The monitor must match symptom frequency; with episodes about twice a month, a 2-4 week external loop or event recorder (or patient-activated phone ECG) is most likely to capture one. A 24-hour Holter will usually miss them, a treadmill test only helps exertional symptoms, and a repeat resting ECG between episodes adds little.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the approach to a patient presenting with palpitations in a primary care setting. Outline the investigations and management of benign ectopic beats.`,openingLines:[`Palpitations are an unpleasant awareness of the heartbeat; most are due to ectopic beats, sinus tachycardia or anxiety, but they may be the only warning of a dangerous arrhythmia.`,`The diagnosis depends on symptom-rhythm correlation - an ECG recorded during symptoms - and the primary care task is to identify red flags and match the monitor to symptom frequency.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Arrhythmic: ectopics, SVT (AVNRT, AVRT), AF, flutter, VT.`,`Structural: valve disease, cardiomyopathy.`,`Systemic: thyrotoxicosis, anaemia, fever, pregnancy, hypoglycaemia, phaeochromocytoma.`,`Drugs: caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, salbutamol, thyroxine, stimulants; QT drugs.`,`Psychiatric: panic and anxiety - diagnosis of exclusion.`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`Tap out the rhythm; onset and offset; vagal termination; triggers; duration and frequency.`,`Associated syncope, chest pain, dyspnoea; drug and substance history; family history of sudden death.`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Pulse and BP, ECG now if symptomatic; murmurs, heart failure signs; pallor, goitre, tremor; postural heart rate.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`12-lead ECG at rest: pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada, LVH, Q waves, AV block.`,`Hb, TSH, K, Mg, Ca, glucose; echo if structural suspicion or frequent PVCs.`,`Holter (daily), patch or loop (weekly-monthly), ILR (rare with syncope); smartphone ECG.`]},{heading:`Red flags for referral`,points:[`Syncope, exertional symptoms, chest pain, structural disease, abnormal ECG, family history of sudden death, broad-complex tachycardia.`]},{heading:`Management of benign ectopics`,points:[`Explain and reassure; reduce caffeine, alcohol and tobacco; treat anxiety, anaemia, thyroid disease.`,`Beta-blocker (metoprolol 25-100 mg twice daily or bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg) or verapamil/diltiazem if symptoms persist.`,`Burden over 10%: echo and follow-up; ablation for symptomatic outflow-tract PVCs or falling EF.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: palpitations - ECG during symptoms or resting ECG - red flags - monitor by frequency`,`Table of EHRA palpitation types with likely causes`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`History and examination`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and monitoring by frequency`,marks:2},{part:`Red flags and referral`,marks:1},{part:`Management of benign ectopics`,marks:3}],keywords:[`palpitations`,`symptom-rhythm correlation`,`ectopics`,`Holter`,`loop recorder`,`beta-blocker`,`red flags`]},{id:`cardiovascular-palpitations-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia from a panic attack.`,openingLines:[`Paroxysmal SVT is a re-entrant arrhythmia, usually AVNRT or AVRT, causing abrupt regular tachycardia; a panic attack is a sudden surge of fear with autonomic symptoms and sinus tachycardia.`,`They are commonly confused - the ECG during symptoms separates them.`],answer:[{heading:`Onset, offset and rate`,points:[`SVT: abrupt start and stop, fixed rate 150-250/min; panic: builds over minutes, rate rarely over 140/min, settles gradually.`]},{heading:`Associated features`,points:[`SVT: regular neck pounding (frog sign), polyuria afterwards, stops with Valsalva.`,`Panic: fear of dying, tingling around mouth and fingers, air hunger, trembling, derealisation.`]},{heading:`ECG`,points:[`SVT: narrow regular tachycardia, P waves hidden or retrograde; resting ECG may show pre-excitation.`,`Panic: sinus tachycardia with normal P waves.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`SVT: vagal manoeuvres, adenosine, catheter ablation (over 95% cure).`,`Panic: CBT, SSRI such as sertraline; avoid long-term benzodiazepines.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table of SVT and panic attack: onset, rate, features, ECG, treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical comparison`,marks:2},{part:`ECG differences`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1}],keywords:[`SVT`,`AVNRT`,`panic attack`,`frog sign`,`adenosine`,`ablation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Palpitations`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`,`2019 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia`,`2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation; 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for atrial fibrillation`,`EHRA position paper: Management of patients with palpitations, Europace 2011`,`2015 HRS Expert Consensus Statement on POTS, inappropriate sinus tachycardia and vasovagal syncope`,`National Family Health Survey (NFHS-5) 2019-21, MoHFW`]}),e.push({id:`cardiovascular-syncope`,title:`Syncope and orthostatic hypotension`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Syncope** is a **transient loss of consciousness due to cerebral hypoperfusion**, with rapid onset, short duration and **spontaneous complete recovery**; ESC 2018 classifies it as **reflex, orthostatic or cardiac**, and the initial evaluation - **history, examination, lying and standing BP and a 12-lead ECG** - plus risk scores such as the **Canadian Syncope Risk Score** decides who can go home and who needs admission for a cardiac cause.`,keywords:[`syncope`,`faint`,`transient loss of consciousness`,`vasovagal syncope`,`reflex syncope`,`situational syncope`,`carotid sinus syndrome`,`orthostatic hypotension`,`cardiac syncope`,`San Francisco Syncope Rule`,`Canadian Syncope Risk Score`,`tilt table test`,`implantable loop recorder`,`midodrine`,`fludrocortisone`,`physical counter-pressure manoeuvres`,`Stokes-Adams attack`,`driving advice`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Syncope** is **transient loss of consciousness (TLOC) due to cerebral hypoperfusion**, characterised by rapid onset, short duration and **spontaneous complete recovery**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**TLOC** includes syncope, **epileptic seizures**, psychogenic pseudosyncope and rare causes - the first task is to decide whether the event was syncope at all. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`Syncope is common: up to about **4 in 10 people** faint at least once in a lifetime, with peaks in the **teens and twenties** (reflex) and **over 65 years** (orthostatic and cardiac). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`It accounts for about **1% of emergency department visits**, and a substantial share are admitted - many unnecessarily. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Reflex (vasovagal) syncope** is the commonest cause at all ages; **orthostatic hypotension** rises with age and medications; **cardiac syncope** is less common but carries the worst prognosis. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Indian context**: heat, dehydration, prolonged standing (assemblies, religious gatherings, queues), fasting, **anaemia** and antihypertensive or diuretic over-treatment are common triggers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orthostatic hypotension** affects about **1 in 5 adults over 65** and up to half of nursing-home residents, and is a major cause of **falls and fractures**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Causes and classification (ESC 2018)`,points:[`**Reflex (neurally mediated) syncope**: **vasovagal** (orthostatic or emotional - pain, fear, blood), **situational** (micturition, cough, swallow, defecation, post-exercise, laughter), **carotid sinus syndrome** and atypical forms without trigger. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Syncope due to orthostatic hypotension**: **drug-induced** (commonest - vasodilators, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, antidepressants, phenothiazines, alcohol), **volume depletion** (bleeding, diarrhoea, vomiting), **primary autonomic failure** and **secondary autonomic failure**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Primary autonomic failure**: Parkinson disease, **multiple system atrophy**, Lewy body dementia, pure autonomic failure; **secondary**: **diabetes**, amyloidosis, spinal cord injury, autoimmune and paraneoplastic neuropathy, renal failure. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Cardiac - arrhythmic**: **bradycardia** (sinus node dysfunction, Mobitz II, complete heart block - Stokes-Adams attacks, pacemaker malfunction) and **tachycardia** (VT, SVT, torsades from long QT). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Cardiac - structural**: **severe aortic stenosis**, HCM with outflow obstruction, acute MI or ischaemia, atrial myxoma, tamponade, prosthetic valve dysfunction; **cardiopulmonary**: **pulmonary embolism**, aortic dissection, pulmonary hypertension. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Forms of orthostatic intolerance**: **initial OH** (BP fall of 40 systolic or 20 diastolic within 15 seconds of standing), **classical OH** (within 3 minutes), **delayed OH** (after 3 minutes), **POTS** and orthostatic vasovagal syncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Drugs that cause syncope by arrhythmia**: QT-prolonging drugs (torsades), beta-blockers and non-dihydropyridine CCBs with digoxin (bradycardia), and drugs causing hypokalaemia. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**BP = cardiac output x peripheral resistance**; syncope follows a fall in either - reflex vasodilatation, reflex bradycardia, failed vasoconstriction on standing, or a cardiac cause of low output. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`Cessation of cerebral blood flow for only **6-8 seconds** causes loss of consciousness; a systolic BP of about **50-60 mmHg at heart level** is the threshold when upright. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**On standing** about 500-800 mL of blood pools in the legs and splanchnic bed; the **baroreflex** normally restores BP within seconds by sympathetic vasoconstriction and tachycardia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vasovagal syncope**: reduced venous return triggers a paradoxical reflex - **withdrawal of sympathetic tone (vasodepressor)** and/or **vagal bradycardia or asystole (cardioinhibitory)** - with nausea, sweating and pallor as prodrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension**: failure of noradrenaline release from sympathetic nerves - BP falls without the normal compensatory tachycardia; often accompanied by **supine hypertension**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac syncope** is often abrupt with no prodrome because output falls suddenly (asystole, VT), and may occur **supine or during exertion** when output cannot rise (AS, HCM). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Brief myoclonic jerks** (under 15 seconds) are common in syncope from cerebral hypoxia and do not by themselves mean epilepsy. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history from patient and witness`,points:[`Take the history in three parts: **before** (position, activity, trigger, prodrome), **during** (witness account: colour, movements, duration, breathing) and **after** (recovery, confusion, injury, incontinence). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Reflex syncope**: triggered by pain, fear, prolonged standing, crowded hot places, after meals or exertion; **prodrome** of nausea, sweating, warmth, pallor and tunnel vision; long history of similar faints. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Situational syncope** happens during or immediately after **micturition, cough, swallowing or defecation**; **carotid sinus syncope** with head rotation, tight collars or shaving in older men. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Orthostatic syncope**: occurs **after standing up**, prolonged standing or after meals (post-prandial hypotension), with a new or increased BP-lowering drug, or with Parkinsonism, diabetic neuropathy or dehydration. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Cardiac syncope features**: **during exertion or while supine**, sudden onset without prodrome or with **palpitations**, chest pain or breathlessness before, known structural heart disease, **family history of sudden death under 40**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Duration**: syncope typically lasts **under a minute** (often seconds) with rapid return to full orientation; prolonged confusion after the event suggests a seizure. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Elderly patients** often have **amnesia for loss of consciousness** and report a fall - unexplained falls in older adults should be evaluated as possible syncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Drug history**: antihypertensives (especially recent change), diuretics, alpha-blockers for prostate, nitrates, antidepressants, antipsychotics, levodopa, **QT-prolonging drugs**, insulin or sulphonylureas, alcohol. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`**Lying and standing BP**: after 5 minutes supine, measure at **1 and 3 minutes of standing**; **OH = sustained fall of 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic, or systolic under 90 mmHg**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`In **supine hypertension**, a systolic fall of **30 mmHg or more** is used to define OH. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Heart-rate response**: a blunted rise (ratio of heart-rate rise to systolic fall **under 0.5 beats/min per mmHg**) suggests **neurogenic OH**; a large rise suggests hypovolaemia or drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiovascular examination**: ejection systolic murmur with slow-rising pulse (**AS**), murmur louder on Valsalva (**HCM**), signs of heart failure, irregular pulse, **bradycardia** and pacemaker. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Neurological examination**: Parkinsonism, cerebellar signs (MSA), peripheral neuropathy (diabetes, amyloid), focal deficit (stroke or TIA, which rarely cause TLOC). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Look for injury** (fracture, head injury), **tongue biting** (lateral - seizure), pallor (anaemia, GI bleed - rectal examination if suspected), and dehydration. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Carotid sinus massage** in patients **over 40** with unexplained syncope: 5-10 seconds on each side, supine and upright, with ECG and BP monitoring. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Carotid sinus syndrome** is diagnosed when massage reproduces symptoms with **asystole over 3 seconds and/or a systolic fall over 50 mmHg**; avoid massage after TIA or stroke in the past 3 months or with a carotid bruit unless Doppler excludes significant stenosis. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Epileptic seizure**: aura, **lateral tongue biting**, head turning, prolonged tonic-clonic movements, cyanosis, **prolonged post-ictal confusion**, onset during sleep - syncope has pallor, sweating and rapid recovery. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Psychogenic pseudosyncope**: **eyes closed** during the event, long apparent unconsciousness (often minutes), very frequent attacks, normal BP and heart rate during the event (confirmed on tilt video). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Hypoglycaemia**: gradual onset with sweating, hunger and confusion, does not recover spontaneously until glucose is given - not syncope. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Falls without TLOC**, drop attacks, cataplexy (narcolepsy, triggered by laughter, consciousness preserved) and intoxication are not syncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**TIA**: vertebrobasilar TIA rarely causes loss of consciousness and always has focal brainstem signs; anterior circulation TIA does not cause syncope - **CT head and carotid Doppler are not routine** in syncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Subarachnoid haemorrhage** can cause sudden collapse - ask about thunderclap headache. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subclavian steal** can cause syncope with arm exercise; check BP in both arms. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`**ESC 2018 high-risk features**: new chest pain, breathlessness, abdominal pain or headache; syncope **during exertion or supine**; sudden palpitations before; **structural or coronary heart disease**; family history of sudden death; systolic BP under 90; undiagnosed GI bleeding; persistent bradycardia under 40. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**High-risk ECG features**: acute ischaemia, **Mobitz II or complete heart block**, sinus bradycardia under 40, bifascicular block, **pre-excitation**, long or short QT, **Brugada type 1**, ARVC pattern, NSVT, pauses over 3 seconds. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Low-risk features**: prodrome typical of reflex syncope, trigger (pain, fear, standing, crowd, heat), after a meal, on standing, long history of similar faints, normal examination and ECG. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**San Francisco Syncope Rule (CHESS)**: history of **C**ongestive heart failure, **H**aematocrit under 30%, **E**CG abnormal, **S**hortness of breath, **S**ystolic BP under 90 - any one means higher risk of serious outcome at 7 days. [San Francisco Syncope Rule]`,`**Canadian Syncope Risk Score** (ED, 30-day serious outcomes) uses vasovagal predisposition (-1), heart disease history (+1), systolic BP under 90 or over 180 (+2), raised troponin (+2), abnormal QRS axis (+1), QRS over 130 ms (+1), QTc over 480 ms (+2), and ED diagnosis of vasovagal (-2) or cardiac syncope (+2). [Canadian Syncope Risk Score]`,`**Canadian score interpretation**: **-3 to -2 very low**, -1 to 0 low, 1-3 medium, 4-5 high, **6 or more very high** risk - very low and low risk can be discharged. [Canadian Syncope Risk Score]`,`**Scores support, not replace, judgement**: ESC 2018 notes that clinical judgement with ECG performs as well as published scores, and none should be used alone. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Initial evaluation for everyone**: history, examination, **lying and standing BP** and a **12-lead ECG** - this alone gives the diagnosis in many patients. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**ECG diagnostic of arrhythmic syncope**: persistent sinus bradycardia under 40 awake, **Mobitz II or complete heart block**, **pauses over 3 seconds**, alternating bundle branch block, VT, rapid SVT, pacemaker malfunction. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Bloods only as indicated**: **haemoglobin** (bleeding, anaemia), glucose, electrolytes, **troponin** if ischaemia suspected, D-dimer if PE suspected, BNP if heart failure suspected. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Echocardiography** when structural heart disease is suspected (murmur, abnormal ECG, heart failure, exertional syncope) - to diagnose AS, HCM, LV dysfunction, myxoma or tamponade. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Monitoring**: in-hospital telemetry for high-risk patients; **Holter** if symptoms are frequent (weekly or more); **external loop recorder** within 4 weeks of symptoms; **implantable loop recorder** for recurrent unexplained syncope - see the Cardiac investigations topic. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Implantable loop recorder** is recommended **early** in recurrent syncope of uncertain origin without high-risk criteria, and in high-risk patients when full evaluation is negative. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Tilt table test** (passive 20-45 minutes at 60-70 degrees, with sublingual nitroglycerin provocation) supports reflex syncope, delayed OH, POTS or psychogenic pseudosyncope; a positive test reproduces symptoms with hypotension and/or bradycardia. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Exercise test** for syncope during or soon after exertion; **electrophysiology study** in syncope with bifascicular block or prior MI and scar; **CT head and EEG are not routine** unless a seizure or head injury is suspected. [ESC 2018 syncope]`]},{heading:`Management of reflex syncope`,points:[`**Education and reassurance** for all: explain the benign nature, recognise the prodrome and **sit or lie down immediately** with legs raised. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Avoid triggers**: prolonged standing, hot crowded places, dehydration, alcohol; take **2-3 L of fluid daily** and liberal salt if not hypertensive. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Physical counter-pressure manoeuvres** during the prodrome - **leg crossing with muscle tensing, handgrip, arm tensing, squatting** - raise BP and abort many episodes in patients under 60. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Review and reduce hypotensive drugs** (nitrates, diuretics, alpha-blockers) in patients with reflex or orthostatic syncope - often the most effective intervention. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Fludrocortisone 0.1-0.2 mg daily** may be considered in young patients with orthostatic vasovagal syncope, low-normal BP and no contraindication. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Midodrine 2.5-10 mg three times daily** may be considered in patients with the hypotensive phenotype and frequent episodes. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Beta-blockers are not recommended** for vasovagal syncope - trials show no benefit. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Cardiac pacing** (dual-chamber, preferably with closed-loop stimulation) is considered in patients **over 40** with recurrent unpredictable syncope and documented **asystolic pause of 3 seconds or more with symptoms (or 6 seconds without)**, or cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome. [ESC 2021 pacing]`,`**Cardioneuroablation** (ablation of vagal ganglionated plexi) is an emerging option for young patients with severe cardioinhibitory reflex syncope in specialist centres, not yet routine. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Management of orthostatic hypotension`,points:[`**Stop or reduce culprit drugs** first: alpha-blockers, diuretics, nitrates, vasodilators, tricyclics, antipsychotics, and lower-intensity antihypertensive treatment in frail elderly. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Volume expansion**: **2-3 L of water daily** and **about 10 g of salt (sodium chloride) daily** unless contraindicated by heart failure or hypertension; a **bolus of 500 mL of water** raises BP within 30 minutes. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Sleep with the head of the bed raised more than 10 degrees** - reduces nocturnal diuresis and supine hypertension. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Abdominal binders and waist-high compression stockings** (30-40 mmHg) reduce venous pooling; teach slow staged rising, counter-pressure manoeuvres and avoiding large carbohydrate meals and alcohol. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Midodrine** (alpha-1 agonist) **2.5 mg three times daily, titrated to 10 mg three times daily**, taken during the day with the **last dose at least 4 hours before bedtime** to avoid supine hypertension. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Fludrocortisone 0.1-0.3 mg once daily** expands plasma volume; watch for **hypokalaemia, oedema, supine hypertension and heart failure**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Pyridostigmine** 30-60 mg two to three times daily, and droxidopa where available, are second-line options for neurogenic OH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supine hypertension** in autonomic failure: avoid lying flat in the day, use head-up sleeping, and if needed a short-acting night-time antihypertensive such as **losartan or a nitrate patch** - never long-acting agents that worsen morning OH. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency management`,points:[`**Admit** patients with high-risk features: exertional or supine syncope, structural heart disease, abnormal ECG, family history of sudden death, systolic BP under 90, anaemia from bleeding, or major injury. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Low-risk patients** (typical reflex or orthostatic syncope, normal ECG) can be **discharged from the ED** with education - admission adds cost without benefit. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Intermediate-risk** patients are best managed in an **ED observation unit or syncope unit** with ECG monitoring for up to 24 hours. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Symptomatic bradycardia**: **atropine 1 mg IV**, repeated every 3-5 minutes to a maximum of 3 mg, then transcutaneous pacing or isoprenaline/adrenaline infusion as a bridge to temporary pacing. [AHA ACLS 2020]`,`**Arrhythmic syncope** is treated by its cause: **permanent pacing** for AV block or sinus node disease, **ablation** for SVT or idiopathic VT, **ICD** for VT with structural disease or channelopathy. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Structural causes**: **aortic valve replacement** for symptomatic severe AS (syncope is a class I indication), alcohol septal ablation or myectomy for obstructive HCM, thrombolysis for massive PE. [ESC/EACTS 2025 VHD]`,`**Stop QT-prolonging drugs** and correct potassium and magnesium; give **IV magnesium sulphate 2 g** for torsades. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: vasovagal syncope and **supine hypotension syndrome** from aortocaval compression after 20 weeks - advise left lateral position; exclude PE, haemorrhage (ectopic pregnancy in early pregnancy) and peripartum cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2018 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: multiple causes coexist - **OH, carotid sinus hypersensitivity, bradyarrhythmia, AS and polypharmacy**; treat as a falls assessment with medication review. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Children and adolescents**: reflex syncope and breath-holding spells dominate, but exclude **long QT, HCM, CPVT and WPW** when syncope is exertional, occurs with loud noise or swimming, or there is family history. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Diabetes**: autonomic neuropathy causes OH and silent ischaemia; also exclude hypoglycaemia from insulin or sulphonylureas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Parkinson disease and MSA**: neurogenic OH is common and worsened by levodopa and dopamine agonists - non-drug measures, then midodrine or fludrocortisone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: intradialytic hypotension, volume shifts, hyperkalaemia-related arrhythmia and autonomic dysfunction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Athletes**: syncope **during exercise** needs full cardiac evaluation (HCM, ARVC, anomalous coronary artery, channelopathy) before return to sport; post-exercise syncope is usually reflex. [ESC 2020 sports cardiology]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and driving advice`,points:[`**Reflex syncope** has an excellent prognosis - mortality is not increased - but recurrence and injury impair quality of life. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Cardiac syncope** carries a high risk of death and sudden death, driven by the underlying disease (LV dysfunction, AS, channelopathy). [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Orthostatic hypotension** independently increases risk of **falls, fractures, stroke, dementia and death**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Injury** from syncope - fractures, head injury, road accidents - is commoner in the elderly and when there is no prodrome. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Driving (ESC 2018)**: single or mild reflex syncope - **no restriction** for private drivers; recurrent severe reflex syncope - drive only once symptoms are controlled; **professional (group 2) drivers** need stricter restriction until effective treatment. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Arrhythmic syncope** - no driving until **effective treatment** (pacemaker, ablation, ICD) is established; unexplained syncope with high-risk features restricts driving until diagnosis. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**India**: licence applicants self-declare fainting or giddiness on **Form 1**, and transport-vehicle drivers need a medical certificate (**Form 1A**) - document advice in the notes. [Central Motor Vehicles Rules]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to cardiology**: suspected cardiac syncope, abnormal ECG, structural heart disease, syncope during exertion, family history of sudden death or recurrent unexplained syncope. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Refer to neurology**: suspected seizure, autonomic failure (Parkinsonism, MSA, pure autonomic failure) or suspected psychogenic pseudosyncope with neuropsychiatric input. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Follow-up of OH**: review lying and standing BP, symptoms, falls, supine BP and potassium (fludrocortisone) at each visit - every **4-6 weeks** while adjusting therapy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Counsel on safety**: avoid driving, swimming alone, working at heights or with machinery until the cause is known and controlled. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Prevention**: in hot weather and mass gatherings, hydration, shade and sitting breaks prevent many faints; under **NP-NCD**, avoid over-treatment of BP in frail elderly and check standing BP. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2018 syncope** remains the current European guideline: initial evaluation, risk stratification, **early ILR**, syncope units, and video recording of events to aid diagnosis. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**ESC 2021 pacing**: pacing (with **closed-loop stimulation** preferred) for reflex syncope in patients over 40 with documented asystolic pauses, and for cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome. [ESC 2021 pacing]`,`**ESC 2022 ventricular arrhythmias**: unexplained syncope with structural disease or suspected channelopathy should be considered arrhythmic; ILR and EP study guide ICD decisions. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**ESC 2024 hypertension**: check for orthostatic hypotension before and during treatment, and relax BP targets in the frail elderly and those with symptomatic OH. [ESC 2024]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular heart disease**: syncope remains a symptom-driven indication for intervention in severe AS, with a shift toward earlier intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025 VHD]`,`**Conflict to know**: ACC/AHA/HRS 2017 and ESC 2018 agree on initial evaluation and against routine neuroimaging, but US guidance places less emphasis on syncope units and specific risk scores. [ACC/AHA/HRS 2017 syncope]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: 'approach to syncope' - define TLOC and syncope, ESC classification, history (before, during, after), lying-standing BP and ECG, risk stratification, investigations and management. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Common mistakes**: ordering CT head and EEG routinely, labelling every jerk a seizure, missing **drug-induced OH**, not doing lying and standing BP, and missing exertional syncope from **AS or HCM**. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Viva**: OH definition - fall of **20/10 mmHg within 3 minutes** of standing, or 30 systolic with supine hypertension. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Viva**: carotid sinus syndrome - asystole over 3 seconds or systolic fall over 50 mmHg with reproduction of symptoms on massage. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Viva**: why beta-blockers fail in vasovagal syncope - the problem is reflex vasodilatation and bradycardia, not excess sympathetic drive; trials showed no benefit. [ESC 2018 syncope]`,`**Mnemonic CHESS** for the San Francisco Syncope Rule; **Stokes-Adams** = syncope from complete heart block. [San Francisco Syncope Rule]`]}],tables:[{heading:`ESC 2018 classification of syncope`,columns:[`Type`,`Subtypes`,`Typical clues`],rows:[[`Reflex - vasovagal`,`Orthostatic (standing), emotional (pain, fear, blood)`,`Prodrome of nausea, sweating, pallor; young; recurrent`],[`Reflex - situational`,`Micturition, cough, swallow, defecation, post-exercise`,`Clear trigger, immediate relation`],[`Reflex - carotid sinus syndrome`,`Cardioinhibitory, vasodepressor, mixed`,`Older men; head turning, tight collar`],[`Orthostatic - drug-induced`,`Vasodilators, diuretics, alpha-blockers, nitrates, antidepressants`,`After standing; new or increased drug`],[`Orthostatic - volume depletion`,`Bleeding, diarrhoea, vomiting, heat`,`Tachycardia on standing`],[`Orthostatic - autonomic failure`,`Primary (PD, MSA, LBD, PAF); secondary (diabetes, amyloid)`,`Blunted heart-rate rise, supine hypertension`],[`Cardiac - arrhythmic`,`Bradycardia (SND, AV block), tachycardia (VT, SVT, torsades)`,`No prodrome, palpitations, supine, abnormal ECG`],[`Cardiac - structural`,`AS, HCM, MI, myxoma, tamponade, PE, dissection, PH`,`Exertional, chest pain, murmur, breathlessness`]]},{heading:`Syncope versus seizure versus pseudosyncope`,columns:[`Feature`,`Syncope`,`Epileptic seizure`,`Psychogenic pseudosyncope`],rows:[[`Trigger`,`Standing, pain, heat, exertion`,`Sleep deprivation, flashing lights, none`,`Emotional stress`],[`Prodrome`,`Nausea, sweating, dimming vision`,`Aura (smell, deja vu)`,`Variable`],[`Colour`,`Pale`,`Cyanosed`,`Normal`],[`Eyes`,`Open`,`Open, deviated`,`Closed`],[`Movements`,`Brief myoclonic jerks under 15 s`,`Tonic-clonic over 1 min, head turning`,`Asynchronous, waxing and waning`],[`Tongue biting`,`Rare, tip`,`Lateral`,`Rare`],[`Duration`,`Under 1 minute`,`1-3 minutes`,`Often several minutes`],[`Recovery`,`Rapid, oriented`,`Prolonged confusion, drowsiness`,`Rapid, may be emotional`]]},{heading:`Risk stratification tools`,columns:[`Tool`,`Components`,`Use`],rows:[[`ESC 2018 high-risk features`,`Exertional or supine syncope, chest pain, palpitations, structural heart disease, family SCD, SBP under 90, GI bleed, HR under 40, abnormal ECG`,`Admit or urgent evaluation if any present`],[`San Francisco Syncope Rule (CHESS)`,`CHF history, haematocrit under 30%, abnormal ECG, shortness of breath, SBP under 90`,`Any one positive = higher 7-day risk`],[`Canadian Syncope Risk Score`,`Vasovagal predisposition, heart disease, SBP, troponin, QRS axis and width, QTc, ED diagnosis`,`-3 to -2 very low, 6 or more very high 30-day risk`],[`OESIL score`,`Age over 65, cardiovascular history, no prodrome, abnormal ECG`,`Higher score, higher 1-year mortality`]]},{heading:`Investigations in syncope and their cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When to use`,`Diagnostic finding`],rows:[[`Lying and standing BP`,`Everyone`,`Fall of 20 systolic or 10 diastolic within 3 min, or SBP under 90`],[`12-lead ECG`,`Everyone`,`HR under 40, Mobitz II or CHB, pauses over 3 s, VT, pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada`],[`Carotid sinus massage`,`Age over 40, unexplained syncope`,`Asystole over 3 s and/or SBP fall over 50 mmHg with symptoms`],[`Echocardiography`,`Suspected structural disease`,`Severe AS, HCM, LV dysfunction, myxoma, tamponade`],[`Holter`,`Frequent symptoms (weekly or more)`,`Symptom-rhythm correlation`],[`Implantable loop recorder`,`Recurrent unexplained syncope`,`Pause or arrhythmia during syncope`],[`Tilt table test`,`Suspected reflex syncope, delayed OH, POTS, pseudosyncope`,`Reproduction of symptoms with hypotension or bradycardia`],[`Exercise test`,`Syncope during or after exertion`,`Exercise-induced AV block, VT, hypotension`]]},{heading:`Drugs in syncope and orthostatic hypotension`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Midodrine`,`2.5 mg oral three times daily, up to 10 mg three times daily`,`Daytime only; last dose 4 h before bed`,`Supine hypertension, urinary retention, piloerection, scalp tingling; avoid in severe heart disease, urinary retention`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`0.1 mg oral daily, up to 0.3 mg (0.1-0.2 mg in vasovagal)`,`Review in 2-4 weeks`,`Hypokalaemia, oedema, supine hypertension, heart failure`],[`Pyridostigmine`,`30-60 mg oral two to three times daily`,`Long term in neurogenic OH`,`Abdominal cramps, diarrhoea, sweating, bradycardia`],[`Salt and water`,`About 10 g sodium chloride and 2-3 L water daily; 500 mL water bolus`,`Daily`,`Avoid in heart failure, CKD, hypertension`],[`Atropine`,`1 mg IV, repeat every 3-5 min to 3 mg`,`Acute symptomatic bradycardia`,`Tachycardia, urinary retention; ineffective in infranodal block or heart transplant`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`2 g IV over 10 minutes`,`Torsades de pointes`,`Flushing, hypotension; caution in renal failure`],[`Beta-blockers (for vasovagal)`,`Not recommended`,`Not applicable`,`No benefit in trials; may worsen bradycardia`]]}],redFlags:[`Syncope during exertion or while lying supine.`,`Syncope preceded by palpitations, or with chest pain, breathlessness or new headache.`,`Known structural heart disease, heart failure or severe aortic stenosis.`,`Abnormal ECG: Mobitz II or complete heart block, bradycardia under 40, pauses over 3 seconds, VT, pre-excitation, long QT, Brugada pattern.`,`Family history of sudden cardiac death under 40 years.`,`Systolic BP under 90 mmHg, or evidence of GI bleeding or anaemia.`,`Syncope causing serious injury or occurring while driving.`],pearls:[`**Syncope = TLOC from cerebral hypoperfusion with spontaneous complete recovery.**`,`**History, lying-standing BP and ECG** diagnose most syncope - CT head and EEG are not routine.`,`**Before, during, after** - take the witness account.`,`**Exertional or supine syncope is cardiac until proved otherwise** (AS, HCM, VT).`,`**OH = 20/10 fall within 3 minutes** of standing (30 systolic if supine hypertension).`,`**CHESS** = San Francisco Syncope Rule.`,`**Beta-blockers do not help vasovagal syncope.**`,`**Brief jerks do not make it a seizure**; lateral tongue biting and prolonged confusion do.`,`**Stop the culprit drug** - the best treatment for orthostatic hypotension.`,`**Midodrine by day, never at bedtime** - supine hypertension.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-q1`,stem:`A 72-year-old man on tamsulosin and amlodipine faints on getting out of bed at night to pass urine. Lying BP is 142/80; at 3 minutes standing it is 112/70. What is the most likely mechanism?`,options:[`Carotid sinus syndrome`,`Complete heart block`,`Drug-induced orthostatic hypotension`,`Vertebrobasilar TIA`],answer:2,explanation:`A systolic fall of 30 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing meets the definition of orthostatic hypotension, and the alpha-blocker plus vasodilator is the commonest cause in an older man; nocturnal micturition adds a situational element. Carotid sinus syndrome needs a trigger such as head turning and a positive massage, heart block would show on the ECG, and TIA rarely causes loss of consciousness.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-q2`,stem:`Which feature of a syncopal episode is most suggestive of a cardiac cause?`,options:[`Syncope while standing in a hot, crowded temple queue`,`Nausea and sweating before the faint`,`Syncope while climbing stairs without prodrome`,`Syncope immediately after venepuncture`],answer:2,explanation:`Syncope during exertion without prodrome suggests a cardiac cause - severe AS, HCM or exertional arrhythmia - because output cannot rise to meet demand. Standing in a hot crowd, a nausea-sweating prodrome and fainting after venepuncture are classic triggers and features of reflex (vasovagal) syncope, which carries a benign prognosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-q3`,stem:`Which of the following is a component of the San Francisco Syncope Rule?`,options:[`Age over 65 years`,`Haematocrit under 30%`,`Absence of prodrome`,`QRS duration over 130 ms`],answer:1,explanation:`The San Francisco Syncope Rule is CHESS - congestive heart failure, haematocrit under 30%, abnormal ECG, shortness of breath and systolic BP under 90. Age over 65 and absence of prodrome belong to the OESIL score, and QRS duration over 130 ms is part of the Canadian Syncope Risk Score.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-q4`,stem:`A 68-year-old man has recurrent syncope while shaving and turning his head. Carotid sinus massage reproduces symptoms with a 5-second asystolic pause. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Metoprolol`,`Fludrocortisone`,`Reassurance and avoidance of tight collars only`,`Dual-chamber permanent pacemaker`],answer:3,explanation:`Cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome with recurrent syncope and a documented asystolic pause over 3 seconds is an indication for permanent pacing in patients over 40, preferably dual-chamber. Metoprolol would worsen bradycardia, fludrocortisone addresses volume rather than asystole, and reassurance alone leaves him at risk of injurious recurrent falls.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-q5`,stem:`A 58-year-old woman with Parkinson disease has disabling orthostatic hypotension despite stopping her antihypertensive and increasing fluids and salt. Midodrine is started. Which instruction is most important?`,options:[`Take the last dose at least 4 hours before bedtime`,`Take the full daily dose at bedtime`,`Combine it with a long-acting antihypertensive at night`,`Lie flat during the day after each dose`],answer:0,explanation:`Midodrine is an alpha-1 agonist given during the day; the last dose should be at least 4 hours before bedtime to avoid supine hypertension during sleep. A bedtime dose or lying flat after doses provokes supine hypertension, and long-acting night-time antihypertensives worsen the morning orthostatic fall - a short-acting agent is used only if supine hypertension is troublesome.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define syncope. Classify its causes and describe the evaluation and risk stratification of a patient presenting with syncope. Outline the management of reflex syncope.`,openingLines:[`Syncope is a transient loss of consciousness due to cerebral hypoperfusion, characterised by rapid onset, short duration and spontaneous complete recovery (ESC 2018).`,`It is classified as reflex, orthostatic or cardiac; initial evaluation with history, examination, lying and standing BP and ECG, followed by risk stratification, decides further investigation and admission.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification (ESC 2018)`,points:[`**Reflex**: vasovagal, situational, carotid sinus syndrome, atypical.`,`**Orthostatic**: drug-induced, volume depletion, primary and secondary autonomic failure.`,`**Cardiac**: arrhythmic (brady, tachy), structural (AS, HCM, MI, myxoma), cardiopulmonary (PE, dissection, PH).`]},{heading:`Initial evaluation`,points:[`Is it TLOC and is it syncope? Exclude seizure, pseudosyncope, hypoglycaemia, falls.`,`History before, during, after with witness account; drug and family history.`,`Examination: lying and standing BP (20/10 within 3 min), murmurs, neurological signs, injury.`,`12-lead ECG; carotid sinus massage if over 40.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`ESC high-risk features: exertional or supine syncope, palpitations, chest pain, structural heart disease, family SCD, SBP under 90, abnormal ECG.`,`Scores: San Francisco (CHESS), Canadian Syncope Risk Score - aids, not substitutes, for judgement.`,`Low risk: discharge; intermediate: observation unit; high risk: admit.`]},{heading:`Further investigations`,points:[`Echo for suspected structural disease; Holter, external or implantable loop recorder by frequency.`,`Tilt test for suspected reflex syncope or delayed OH; exercise test for exertional syncope; EP study in bifascicular block or scar.`,`CT head and EEG not routine.`]},{heading:`Management of reflex syncope`,points:[`Education, trigger avoidance, 2-3 L fluid and salt, physical counter-pressure manoeuvres.`,`Withdraw hypotensive drugs; fludrocortisone or midodrine in selected patients.`,`Beta-blockers not recommended.`,`Pacing for over 40 with documented asystole of 3 s or more with symptoms, or cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: TLOC - syncope or not - initial evaluation - risk stratification - discharge, observe or admit`,`Branch diagram of ESC classification: reflex, orthostatic, cardiac`,`Table of high-risk features`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Initial evaluation`,marks:3},{part:`Risk stratification and investigations`,marks:3},{part:`Management of reflex syncope`,marks:2}],keywords:[`syncope`,`TLOC`,`reflex syncope`,`orthostatic hypotension`,`San Francisco rule`,`Canadian Syncope Risk Score`,`tilt test`,`ILR`]},{id:`cardiovascular-syncope-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on orthostatic hypotension: definition, causes and management.`,openingLines:[`Orthostatic hypotension is a sustained fall in systolic BP of 20 mmHg or more or diastolic of 10 mmHg or more within 3 minutes of standing, or a fall to under 90 mmHg systolic.`,`It is common in the elderly, is most often drug-induced, and increases the risk of falls, fractures and death.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and types`,points:[`Classical (within 3 min), initial (within 15 s, 40/20 fall), delayed (after 3 min); 30 mmHg systolic if supine hypertension.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Drugs: alpha-blockers, diuretics, nitrates, vasodilators, antidepressants, antipsychotics, levodopa, alcohol.`,`Volume depletion: bleeding, diarrhoea, heat.`,`Autonomic failure: Parkinson disease, MSA, diabetes, amyloidosis, pure autonomic failure.`]},{heading:`Non-drug management`,points:[`Stop culprit drugs; 2-3 L water and about 10 g salt daily; head-up tilt of bed over 10 degrees.`,`Compression stockings and abdominal binder; staged rising; counter-pressure manoeuvres; small meals.`]},{heading:`Drugs`,points:[`Midodrine 2.5-10 mg three times daily by day, last dose 4 h before bed.`,`Fludrocortisone 0.1-0.3 mg daily - watch potassium, oedema, supine hypertension.`,`Pyridostigmine as second line.`]}],mustDraw:[`Ladder of OH management from drug withdrawal to midodrine and fludrocortisone`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and types`,marks:1},{part:`Causes`,marks:1},{part:`Non-drug management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Drug treatment`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`orthostatic hypotension`,`neurogenic OH`,`midodrine`,`fludrocortisone`,`supine hypertension`]}],references:[`2018 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of syncope`,`2017 ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Patients With Syncope`,`2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiac pacing and cardiac resynchronization therapy`,`2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Syncope; Disorders of the autonomic nervous system`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Hypotension and syncope`,`Thiruganasambandamoorthy V et al. Development of the Canadian Syncope Risk Score, CMAJ 2016; Quinn JV et al. San Francisco Syncope Rule, Ann Emerg Med 2004`,`Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989 (Forms 1 and 1A), Ministry of Road Transport and Highways, India`]});var t=[];t.push({id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach`,title:`Approach to bilateral leg oedema`,oneLiner:`**Oedema** is palpable swelling from expansion of the **interstitial fluid volume** (clinically visible only after about **2.5-3 L** has accumulated); bilateral leg oedema is sorted at the bedside by **JVP, urine protein and serum albumin** into cardiac, renal, hepatic or hypoalbuminaemic (sodium-retaining) causes versus local causes - **venous insufficiency, drugs (dihydropyridine CCBs), lymphatic obstruction, hypothyroidism and lipoedema** - which need no diuretic.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`pedal oedema`,`leg swelling`,`ankle swelling`,`anasarca`,`pitting oedema`,`Starling forces`,`chronic venous insufficiency`,`CEAP`,`lymphoedema`,`Stemmer sign`,`lipoedema`,`amlodipine oedema`,`myxoedema`,`nephrotic syndrome`,`hypoalbuminaemia`,`filariasis`,`epidemic dropsy`,`wet beriberi`,`NT-proBNP`,`furosemide`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Oedema** is an abnormal accumulation of fluid in the **interstitial space**; generalised oedema becomes clinically apparent only after the interstitial volume rises by about **2.5-3 L**, so weight gain precedes visible swelling. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anasarca** is massive generalised oedema with fluid in the subcutaneous tissue of the trunk and serous cavities (ascites, pleural effusion); it is seen in nephrotic syndrome, advanced heart failure and cirrhosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral leg oedema** usually means a systemic cause or a bilateral local cause; **unilateral** oedema points to a local cause (DVT, cellulitis, Baker cyst, lymphatic obstruction) and is a separate approach. [AFP 2013]`,`The commonest cause of bilateral leg oedema in adults over 50 is **chronic venous insufficiency**; in women aged 20-50 it is often **idiopathic (cyclic) oedema**, and **pulmonary hypertension** is a common but under-recognised cause. [AFP 2013]`,`**Heart failure** in India affects patients about a decade younger than in the West (mean age about 60 in the **ICMR National Heart Failure Registry**), with rheumatic and ischaemic disease prominent and high one-year mortality. [NHFR ICMR]`,`**CKD** affects about 1 in 6 Indian adults (**SEEK-India 17.2%**), and **MASLD** (fatty liver) is present in roughly a third of Indian adults - both are rising sources of oedema in the OPD. [SEEK-India 2013]`,`**Hypothyroidism** affects about **11%** of adults in an eight-city Indian study (with a further 8% subclinical), so TSH belongs in the oedema work-up. [Unnikrishnan 2013]`,`India carries about **40% of the global lymphatic filariasis burden** (mainly **Wuchereria bancrofti**, Culex vector), endemic in over 300 districts, and filarial lymphoedema is a common cause of leg swelling in endemic states. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure (cardiac)**: heart failure of any EF, **right heart failure from pulmonary hypertension or cor pulmonale**, severe tricuspid regurgitation, constrictive pericarditis and high-output states (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, beriberi). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal sodium retention**: acute glomerulonephritis, **nephrotic syndrome**, advanced CKD and AKI; in glomerulonephritis oedema is periorbital first and comes with hypertension and haematuria. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low oncotic pressure (hypoalbuminaemia)**: **cirrhosis**, nephrotic syndrome, protein-energy malnutrition (kwashiorkor), protein-losing enteropathy and malabsorption. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Local venous causes**: **chronic venous insufficiency** (varicose veins, post-thrombotic syndrome), prolonged sitting or standing, immobility (**armchair legs**), obesity and pelvic or IVC compression (mass, pregnancy, IVC thrombosis). [ESVS 2022]`,`**Drugs**: **dihydropyridine CCBs** (amlodipine, nifedipine), **NSAIDs**, corticosteroids, oestrogens, **pioglitazone**, insulin, minoxidil, hydralazine, **pregabalin and gabapentin**, dopamine agonists and some anticancer drugs (docetaxel, imatinib). [AFP 2013]`,`**Lymphatic causes**: **filariasis**, malignancy or lymph node dissection, radiotherapy, recurrent cellulitis and primary lymphoedema (Milroy disease congenital, Meige disease at puberty). [ISL 2023]`,`**Endocrine and others**: **hypothyroidism** (myxoedema), Cushing syndrome, pregnancy, premenstrual and **idiopathic oedema**, obstructive sleep apnoea and **lipoedema** (abnormal fat, not fluid). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Indian specials**: **wet beriberi** (thiamine deficiency in heavy drinkers, polished-rice diets, pregnancy) and **epidemic dropsy** (argemone oil adulterating mustard oil). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: Starling forces and renal sodium retention`,points:[`**Starling equation**: net filtration = Kf x [(capillary hydrostatic pressure minus interstitial hydrostatic pressure) minus sigma x (plasma oncotic minus interstitial oncotic pressure)]; oedema follows when filtration exceeds **lymphatic return**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lymph flow is the safety factor**: it can rise many-fold before oedema appears, so oedema means either a large Starling disturbance or impaired lymphatic drainage. [Guyton 14e]`,`In **heart failure and cirrhosis**, reduced **effective arterial blood volume** (low output or splanchnic vasodilatation) activates **RAAS, sympathetic nerves and ADH**, so the kidney retains sodium and water - the 'underfill' state that explains why diuretics and RAAS blockers work. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **nephrotic syndrome** low albumin contributes, but **primary renal sodium retention** ('overfill') also occurs: filtered plasminogen is converted to plasmin, which activates the **epithelial sodium channel (ENaC)** in the collecting duct - one reason amiloride helps. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoalbuminaemia** alone rarely causes oedema until serum albumin falls below about **2-2.5 g/dL**, because interstitial oncotic pressure falls in parallel and buffers the change. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Venous hypertension**: on standing, ankle venous pressure is about **90 mmHg**; walking normally drops it to about 20-30 mmHg via the **calf muscle pump**, but incompetent valves keep it high (**ambulatory venous hypertension**) and fluid leaks out. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Dihydropyridine CCB oedema**: **precapillary arteriolar dilatation** without matching venular dilatation raises capillary hydrostatic pressure - not sodium retention, so diuretics fail while ACE inhibitors and ARBs (postcapillary venodilators) help. [ESC 2024]`,`**Myxoedema** is non-pitting because **hydrophilic glycosaminoglycans (hyaluronic acid)** accumulate in the dermis and bind water; filarial lymphoedema becomes non-pitting as protein-rich fluid drives **fibrosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Increased capillary permeability** (sepsis, burns, IL-2, epidemic dropsy toxins sanguinarine) lets protein leak out, so oedema forms even with normal pressures and albumin. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Pitting oedema** (fluid with low protein: cardiac, renal, hepatic, venous, drug) versus **non-pitting** (myxoedema, late lymphoedema, lipoedema) is the first bedside classification. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pit grading**: 1+ about 2 mm, disappears rapidly; 2+ about 4 mm, gone in 10-15 s; 3+ about 6 mm, lasts over a minute; 4+ about 8 mm, lasts 2-5 minutes. [Bates 13e]`,`**Pit recovery time**: a pit that refills in **under 40 seconds** ('fast oedema') suggests **hypoalbuminaemia**, while a slow pit suggests raised venous pressure. [McGee 5e]`,`**CEAP 2020** for chronic venous disease: C0 none, C1 telangiectasia, C2 varicose veins, **C3 oedema**, C4a pigmentation/eczema, C4b lipodermatosclerosis/atrophie blanche, C4c corona phlebectatica, C5 healed ulcer, C6 active ulcer. [AVF CEAP 2020]`,`**ISL lymphoedema staging**: stage 0 latent; **I** pitting, reduces with elevation; **II** elevation rarely reduces it, fibrosis begins; **III** lymphostatic elephantiasis with skin thickening and warty change. [ISL 2023]`,`**Distribution matters**: dependent (legs when ambulant, sacrum when bed-bound) suggests cardiac or venous; **periorbital on waking** suggests renal or hypoalbuminaemic; ascites before leg oedema suggests liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mechanism classification** (the one examiners ask): raised hydrostatic pressure, low oncotic pressure, increased permeability, lymphatic obstruction and raised interstitial oncotic pressure (myxoedema). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history in the OPD`,points:[`**Onset and course**: acute (days) suggests DVT, acute GN, new drug or acute HF; chronic and slowly progressive suggests venous, lymphatic, liver or hypothyroid causes. [AFP 2013]`,`**Diurnal variation**: swelling worse in the evening and gone after a night in bed is **dependent/venous or CCB oedema**; facial puffiness in the morning suggests **renal** or low albumin. [AFP 2013]`,`**Cardiac questions**: exertional breathlessness, **orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea**, chest pain, palpitations and past rheumatic fever - their presence demands a natriuretic peptide and echo. [ESC 2021]`,`**Renal questions**: **frothy urine**, cola-coloured urine, reduced urine output, recent sore throat or skin infection (post-streptococcal GN), diabetes and hypertension duration. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Liver questions**: alcohol (units and years), jaundice, abdominal distension before leg swelling, haematemesis, past hepatitis B or C and metabolic risk factors. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Drug history in detail**: CCB (especially **amlodipine 10 mg**), NSAIDs, steroids, pioglitazone, gabapentinoids, hormones and **over-the-counter or ayurvedic preparations** - oedema starting weeks after a new drug is drug oedema until proved otherwise. [AFP 2013]`,`**Other clues**: snoring and daytime sleepiness (OSA with pulmonary hypertension), cold intolerance and weight gain (hypothyroidism), recurrent fever with red streaks (filarial lymphangitis), diet and alcohol (beriberi), and **family use of mustard oil from a loose source** in a cluster (epidemic dropsy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Women**: last menstrual period and pregnancy (pre-eclampsia, peripartum cardiomyopathy), cyclical premenstrual swelling, diuretic or laxative use for weight (idiopathic and diuretic-rebound oedema). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Press firmly for at least **5-10 seconds over the medial malleolus and shin** (and the **sacrum** in bed-bound patients); record the upper level, grade and symmetry. [Bates 13e]`,`**JVP is the pivotal sign**: raised JVP with oedema means heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, tricuspid regurgitation, constriction or renal volume overload; a normal JVP points to liver, low albumin, venous, drug or lymphatic causes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kussmaul sign** (JVP rising on inspiration) with a pericardial knock suggests **constrictive pericarditis**; giant **v waves** and a pulsatile liver suggest severe tricuspid regurgitation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac signs**: displaced apex, S3, basal crackles (left failure); **parasternal heave, loud P2** and pansystolic murmur at the left sternal edge (pulmonary hypertension with TR). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Liver signs**: jaundice, **spider naevi, palmar erythema**, gynaecomastia, ascites, splenomegaly and flapping tremor - in cirrhosis the JVP is normal. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Venous signs**: varicose veins, **pigmentation (haemosiderin) and eczema at the gaiter area**, lipodermatosclerosis with an inverted champagne-bottle leg, atrophie blanche and ulcers above the medial malleolus. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Stemmer sign**: inability to pinch and lift a fold of skin at the base of the second toe means **lymphoedema**; also look for squared toes, peau d'orange, papillomatosis and buffalo-hump dorsum of the foot. [ISL 2023]`,`**Lipoedema** is symmetrical, painful, bruises easily, **spares the feet** (an ankle 'cuff'), is non-pitting and Stemmer-negative - in women from puberty, pregnancy or menopause. [ISL 2023]`,`**Hypothyroid signs**: dry coarse skin, slow relaxing ankle jerks, bradycardia, hoarse voice, and **non-pitting** puffiness of face, hands and legs; do not confuse with the localised **pretibial myxoedema of Graves disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other clues**: BP (high in GN and pre-eclampsia), BMI and neck circumference (OSA), urine dipstick at the bedside, bounding pulse with warm peripheries (beriberi, anaemia), and erythematous skin with oedema in a family cluster (epidemic dropsy). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: the discriminating feature`,points:[`**Heart failure**: raised JVP, orthopnoea, crackles, **NT-proBNP raised**; oedema is pitting and dependent. [ESC 2021]`,`**Nephrotic syndrome**: periorbital morning puffiness, frothy urine, **dipstick 3+ protein**, albumin under 3 g/dL, high cholesterol; JVP normal or mildly raised. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis**: ascites out of proportion to leg oedema, stigmata of chronic liver disease, **low albumin with high INR and low platelets**; JVP normal. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Chronic venous insufficiency**: evening swelling, varicose veins, gaiter pigmentation, normal JVP, albumin and urine; **duplex shows reflux**. [ESVS 2022]`,`**CCB oedema**: started weeks to months after amlodipine (dose-related), ankles, no systemic signs, resolves on stopping or dose reduction. [ESC 2024]`,`**Lymphoedema**: **Stemmer positive**, dorsum of foot involved, non-pitting when chronic, filarial history or past surgery/radiation. [ISL 2023]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: non-pitting, with facial puffiness and hypothyroid symptoms; **TSH high**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bilateral DVT or IVC thrombosis** is uncommon but deadly; suspect with pelvic malignancy, nephrotic syndrome, pregnancy or an IVC filter, and image urgently. [ESC 2019 PE]`]},{heading:`Investigations: minimal work-up, then advanced`,points:[`**Minimal OPD work-up** (answers most cases): urine dipstick for protein and blood, **serum albumin, creatinine/eGFR, electrolytes, LFT, TSH, haemoglobin**, ECG and chest X-ray if breathless. [AFP 2013]`,`**Urine protein**: dipstick 2+ or more needs **urine protein-creatinine ratio**; nephrotic range is **over 3.5 g/day** (uPCR roughly over 3-3.5 g/g) with albumin **under 3 g/dL**. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Natriuretic peptides** in a breathless or suspected HF patient: in the non-acute setting **NT-proBNP under 125 pg/mL or BNP under 35 pg/mL** makes HF unlikely; acute rule-out is NT-proBNP under 300 or BNP under 100 pg/mL. [ESC 2021]`,`Interpret peptides with care: **obesity lowers** them (false negative), while **AF, CKD, age and pulmonary embolism raise** them. [ESC 2021]`,`**Echocardiography**: LVEF, LV filling (E/e'), LA size, valves, pericardium and **tricuspid regurgitation velocity over 2.8 m/s** suggesting pulmonary hypertension (now defined as mean PAP over 20 mmHg). [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Liver**: LFT, INR, platelets, ultrasound abdomen with Doppler, viral markers (HBsAg, anti-HCV) and **FIB-4** for fibrosis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Venous duplex**: reflux over **0.5 s** in superficial and deep calf veins or over **1 s** in femoral and popliteal veins confirms incompetence; also excludes DVT. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Ankle-brachial pressure index** before any compression: **under 0.9 means PAD**; compression is reduced at 0.5-0.8 and avoided below about 0.5-0.6 (sources differ). [ESVS 2022]`,`**Filariasis**: night blood smear (10 pm-2 am) for microfilariae, **filariasis antigen test strip** by day, ultrasound '**filarial dance sign**'; **lymphoscintigraphy** is the reference test for lymphoedema. [NCVBDC 2024]`,`**Others as indicated**: polysomnography (OSA), 24-hour urine or stool alpha-1 antitrypsin (protein-losing enteropathy), whole-blood thiamine or a therapeutic thiamine trial (beriberi), and CT abdomen-pelvis (mass, IVC compression). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Treat the cause, not the swelling**: diuretics treat **sodium excess** (heart failure, renal disease, cirrhosis), and are wrong for venous, lymphatic, CCB, idiopathic oedema and lipoedema. [AFP 2013]`,`**Salt restriction** to under **5 g salt (2 g sodium) a day** in sodium-retaining states; fluid restriction only if hyponatraemic. [ESC 2021]`,`**Heart failure**: **furosemide 20-40 mg oral once or twice daily** (or torsemide 10-20 mg daily), aim for **0.5-1 kg weight loss a day** to dry weight, then lowest maintenance dose plus GDMT - see the Heart failure topic. [ESC 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis with ascites/oedema**: **spironolactone 100 mg plus furosemide 40 mg** each morning, raised together every 3-5 days in a **100:40 ratio** to a maximum of **400:160 mg**; weight loss up to 1 kg/day if oedema is present. [AASLD 2021]`,`**CCB oedema**: reduce the amlodipine dose, **add an ACE inhibitor or ARB**, or switch to a lipophilic DHP (**lercanidipine, cilnidipine**) or non-DHP CCB - do not add furosemide. [ESC 2024]`,`**Venous oedema**: leg elevation above the heart for 30 minutes 3-4 times a day, calf exercise and walking, weight loss, and **graduated compression stockings 20-30 mmHg** below knee after checking ABPI. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Venoactive drugs**: **micronised purified flavonoid fraction (diosmin-hesperidin) 1000 mg daily** may be considered to reduce oedema, heaviness and pain in chronic venous disease. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: **levothyroxine 1.6 mcg/kg/day** on an empty stomach; start **12.5-25 mcg** in the elderly or those with coronary disease, recheck TSH in 6-8 weeks. [ATA 2014]`,`**Lymphoedema**: complete decongestive therapy (manual lymphatic drainage, multilayer bandaging, exercise, skin care) then maintenance garments; filarial limbs need **daily washing with soap and water, drying between toes, antifungal and footwear**. [ISL 2023]`,`**Idiopathic oedema**: stop diuretics and laxatives (rebound sodium retention lasts 2-3 weeks), limit salt, avoid prolonged standing, compression; spironolactone if needed. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** oedema with breathlessness at rest, hypoxia, anasarca, oliguria or AKI, **potassium over 6 mmol/L**, sodium under 125 mmol/L, suspected DVT/IVC thrombosis, cellulitis with sepsis, or pregnancy with high BP. [ESC 2021]`,`**Acute HF with congestion**: IV furosemide **20-40 mg, or 1-2 times the daily oral dose** if already on diuretics, as bolus or infusion. [ESC 2021]`,`**Diuretic response check**: spot urine sodium **under 50-70 mmol/L at 2 hours** or urine output **under 100-150 mL/h in the first 6 hours** means poor response - double the loop dose, then add a thiazide or acetazolamide. [ESC 2021]`,`**Monitoring**: daily weight at the same time, strict input-output, daily creatinine and electrolytes during IV diuresis, and lying-standing BP. [ESC 2021]`,`**Refractory nephrotic oedema**: high-dose loop diuretic, add thiazide or amiloride; IV albumin with furosemide is reserved for severe hypoalbuminaemia with intravascular depletion, and evidence is limited. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Nephrotic thrombosis**: consider prophylactic anticoagulation in membranous nephropathy when albumin is very low (about **under 2-2.5 g/dL**) and bleeding risk is low. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Cirrhosis inpatient**: stop diuretics for **sodium under 125 mmol/L, AKI, encephalopathy or severe cramps**; tap ascites for fever or pain to exclude spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Suspected wet beriberi**: give **thiamine 100 mg IV** before any glucose and continue daily; dramatic improvement within hours to days confirms the diagnosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: dependent leg oedema occurs in up to **80%** of normal pregnancies; oedema is no longer a criterion for pre-eclampsia, but sudden face and hand swelling with BP **140/90 or more after 20 weeks** needs urine protein and labs. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Peripartum cardiomyopathy**: new breathlessness and oedema in the last month of pregnancy or 5 months postpartum with **LVEF under 45%** - do not dismiss as normal pregnancy oedema. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: think **venous, HFpEF, CCB, NSAIDs, malnutrition and immobility (armchair legs)**; diuretics in the frail cause falls, dehydration and AKI. [AFP 2013]`,`**Diabetes**: pioglitazone and insulin oedema, diabetic kidney disease with nephrotic proteinuria, and HF; **avoid pioglitazone in NYHA III-IV HF**. [ADA 2025]`,`**CKD**: loop diuretics are needed when eGFR is under 30; **chlorthalidone** lowered BP and volume even in stage 4 CKD (CLICK), and SGLT2 inhibitors add mild natriuresis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Children**: periorbital oedema with heavy proteinuria is **minimal change nephrotic syndrome**; with haematuria, hypertension and cola urine it is **post-streptococcal GN**; bilateral pitting oedema in a malnourished child is **SAM (kwashiorkor)**. [ISPN 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: JVP, liver examination, urine dipstick and a serum albumin together sort most patients without echo or peptides. [AFP 2013]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Cellulitis** and erysipelas are the main local complication: oedematous skin, interdigital tinea and fissures let streptococci in, and each episode damages lymphatics further. [ISL 2023]`,`In filarial limbs, **acute dermatolymphangioadenitis (ADLA)** attacks drive progression to elephantiasis - treat with rest, elevation, analgesia and antibiotics such as amoxicillin. [WHO MMDP 2021]`,`**Venous ulcer** (C6) above the medial malleolus, lipodermatosclerosis and stasis eczema follow untreated venous hypertension; recurrence is high without long-term compression. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Stewart-Treves syndrome**: lymphangiosarcoma arising in long-standing lymphoedema (classically after mastectomy) - a rare but examinable complication. [ISL 2023]`,`**Over-diuresis** causes hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, prerenal AKI, gout, falls and, in cirrhosis, **hepatic encephalopathy and hepatorenal syndrome**. [AASLD 2021]`,`**Prognosis depends on the cause**: persistent congestion in HF and serum albumin under 3 g/dL independently predict higher mortality; venous and CCB oedema carry no excess mortality. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer to cardiology** for raised natriuretic peptides, reduced EF, significant valve disease, pulmonary hypertension or suspected constriction. [ESC 2021]`,`**Refer to nephrology** for nephrotic-range proteinuria, proteinuria with haematuria (biopsy question), falling eGFR or AKI. [KDIGO 2021]`,`**Refer to vascular surgery** for CEAP C4-C6, ulcers, ABPI under 0.8 or symptomatic varicose veins suitable for ablation. [ESVS 2022]`,`**Follow-up after starting or changing a diuretic**: creatinine and potassium in **1-2 weeks**, weight at every visit, and patient-held daily weight chart in HF (call if gain over **2 kg in 3 days**). [ESC 2021]`,`**After stopping or changing a CCB**, recheck in **2-4 weeks**; oedema should settle within weeks if the drug was the cause. [ESC 2024]`,`**Compression stockings** lose elastic pressure and should be replaced about every **3-6 months**; review skin and ABPI periodically in diabetics and the elderly. [ESVS 2022]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**NP-NCD** (renamed from NPCDCS in 2023) screens adults 30 and above at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for hypertension and diabetes and now includes CKD and NAFLD - the main upstream causes of HF and renal oedema. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Lymphatic filariasis elimination**: India targets elimination by **2027** through mass drug administration with **IDA (ivermectin, DEC, albendazole)** and the **MMDP** package (limb care, hydrocele surgery). [NCVBDC 2024]`,`Counsel on **salt** (pickles, papad, namkeen), alcohol, weight, regular walking, avoiding prolonged standing or sitting with legs down, and **self-care of the feet** (daily wash, dry between toes, footwear). [ESVS 2022]`,`Teach HF patients **daily weighing** and a flexible diuretic plan; teach cirrhotics to avoid NSAIDs and alcohol. [ESC 2021]`,`**Food safety**: epidemic dropsy is prevented by buying branded, sealed mustard oil and FSSAI enforcement against **argemone adulteration**. [FSSAI]`,`Avoid starting **amlodipine 10 mg** as monotherapy when a low-dose CCB plus ARB single pill achieves control with less oedema. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2023 focused HF update**: SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin) are class I in HFmrEF and HFpEF as well as HFrEF, reducing congestion and hospitalisation. [ESC 2023]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022 pulmonary hypertension guideline** lowered the haemodynamic threshold to **mean PAP over 20 mmHg**, so more right-sided oedema is now labelled pulmonary hypertension. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**ESVS 2022 chronic venous disease guideline** adopted **CEAP 2020** (C4c corona phlebectatica, C2r and C6r recurrent) and endorses compression and venoactive drugs for symptoms and oedema. [ESVS 2022]`,`**KDIGO 2021 glomerular disease** and **KDIGO 2024 CKD** guidelines guide nephrotic oedema (loop diuretic, salt restriction, ACEi/ARB) and recommend SGLT2 inhibitors for most CKD with eGFR 20 or more. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**MASLD** (metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease) replaced NAFLD in the **2023 multisociety Delphi** nomenclature. [AASLD/EASL 2023]`,`**ESC 2024 hypertension guideline**: starting with a **low-dose CCB plus RAS blocker single pill** reduces CCB oedema compared with high-dose CCB alone. [ESC 2024]`,`**India**: nationwide biannual **MDA rounds** under the 2027 LF elimination target and expansion of NP-NCD to CKD and NAFLD screening. [NCVBDC 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: '**Approach to a patient with bilateral pedal oedema**' - open with definition and Starling forces, then the JVP-urine-albumin algorithm, then management by cause.`,`Classic short question: '**Differentiate cardiac, renal and hepatic oedema**' - onset site, timing, JVP, urine, albumin and associated signs.`,`Viva: why is **amlodipine oedema** not helped by furosemide? Because it is precapillary arteriolar dilatation, not sodium retention; add an ACE inhibitor or ARB.`,`Viva: **Stemmer sign** - lymphoedema; **lipoedema** spares the feet; **pretibial myxoedema** is Graves, not hypothyroidism.`,`Viva: **epidemic dropsy** - argemone oil (sanguinarine), oedema, erythema, diarrhoea, **glaucoma** and high-output failure.`,`Common mistake: prescribing diuretics for every swollen ankle - causing **AKI, hyponatraemia and falls** in the elderly.`,`Common mistake: not dipping the urine - **nephrotic syndrome** is missed and oedema is blamed on the heart.`,`Common mistake: compression stockings without an **ABPI** in a diabetic or smoker with PAD - risk of pressure necrosis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Causes of bilateral oedema by Starling mechanism`,columns:[`Mechanism`,`Causes`,`Bedside clue`],rows:[[`Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure (cardiac)`,`Heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, TR, constrictive pericarditis, high-output states`,`Raised JVP, orthopnoea, crackles, hepatomegaly`],[`Raised hydrostatic pressure (renal sodium retention)`,`Acute GN, advanced CKD, AKI, pregnancy, NSAIDs, steroids, pioglitazone`,`Hypertension, haematuria, low eGFR`],[`Raised hydrostatic pressure (venous/local)`,`Venous insufficiency, post-thrombotic syndrome, pelvic/IVC compression, immobility, DHP-CCB`,`Normal JVP, evening swelling, varicosities, gaiter pigmentation`],[`Low plasma oncotic pressure`,`Nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition (kwashiorkor), protein-losing enteropathy`,`Periorbital morning puffiness, fast pit recovery, albumin under 2.5 g/dL`],[`Increased capillary permeability`,`Sepsis, burns, angioedema, epidemic dropsy, IL-2 therapy`,`Acute onset, systemic illness, erythema`],[`Lymphatic obstruction`,`Filariasis, node dissection, radiotherapy, malignancy, primary lymphoedema`,`Stemmer sign, foot dorsum involved, non-pitting late`],[`Raised interstitial oncotic pressure`,`Hypothyroidism (myxoedema)`,`Non-pitting, facial puffiness, slow-relaxing reflexes`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of bilateral leg oedema`,columns:[`Cause`,`Typical history`,`Discriminating sign`,`Confirmatory test`],rows:[[`Heart failure`,`Exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, PND`,`Raised JVP, S3, basal crackles`,`NT-proBNP over 125 pg/mL (non-acute), echo`],[`Pulmonary hypertension / cor pulmonale`,`Dyspnoea, COPD, snoring (OSA), exertional syncope`,`Loud P2, parasternal heave, TR murmur`,`Echo TR velocity over 2.8 m/s; RHC mPAP over 20`],[`Nephrotic syndrome`,`Frothy urine, morning facial puffiness`,`Periorbital oedema, normal JVP`,`uPCR over 3-3.5 g/g, albumin under 3 g/dL`],[`Cirrhosis`,`Alcohol, jaundice, ascites first`,`Spider naevi, palmar erythema, ascites`,`Low albumin, high INR, low platelets, ultrasound`],[`Chronic venous insufficiency`,`Evening swelling, heaviness, standing job`,`Varicose veins, haemosiderin pigmentation`,`Venous duplex reflux over 0.5 s (superficial)`],[`DHP-CCB oedema`,`Amlodipine, especially 10 mg`,`Ankle oedema, no systemic signs`,`Settles after dose reduction or adding ARB`],[`Lymphoedema (filarial)`,`Recurrent fever with lymphangitis, endemic area`,`Stemmer positive, foot dorsum swollen`,`Antigen test, night smear, lymphoscintigraphy`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation`,`Non-pitting, delayed ankle jerk relaxation`,`TSH high, free T4 low`],[`Lipoedema`,`Woman, symmetrical heavy painful legs since puberty`,`Feet spared (ankle cuff), Stemmer negative`,`Clinical; normal labs`]]},{heading:`Grading and staging systems`,columns:[`System`,`Grade or stage`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Pitting scale`,`1+ to 4+`,`2, 4, 6, 8 mm pits; 1+ rebounds at once, 4+ persists 2-5 minutes`],[`Pit recovery time`,`Under 40 s vs longer`,`Fast pit suggests hypoalbuminaemia; slow pit suggests venous or cardiac`],[`CEAP 2020 (venous)`,`C0-C2`,`No signs, telangiectasia, varicose veins`],[`CEAP 2020 (venous)`,`C3-C4`,`Oedema; C4a pigmentation/eczema, C4b lipodermatosclerosis, C4c corona phlebectatica`],[`CEAP 2020 (venous)`,`C5-C6`,`Healed ulcer; active ulcer (C6r recurrent)`],[`ISL lymphoedema`,`Stage 0-I`,`Latent; pitting, reduces with elevation`],[`ISL lymphoedema`,`Stage II-III`,`Fibrosis, elevation does not help; elephantiasis with skin changes`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in bilateral oedema`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Cut-off / interpretation`],rows:[[`Urine dipstick, then uPCR`,`Every patient`,`2+ protein needs uPCR; nephrotic over 3.5 g/day`],[`Serum albumin`,`Every patient`,`Under 3 g/dL low; oedema from low albumin alone usually under 2-2.5 g/dL`],[`Creatinine, eGFR, Na, K`,`Every patient, before diuretics`,`eGFR under 30 needs loop diuretic; K over 5.5 limits RAAS blockers`],[`NT-proBNP / BNP`,`Dyspnoea or suspected HF`,`Non-acute rule-out NT-proBNP under 125, BNP under 35 pg/mL`],[`TSH`,`Non-pitting or hypothyroid features`,`High TSH with low free T4 confirms hypothyroidism`],[`LFT, INR, platelets, ultrasound`,`Liver suspected`,`Low albumin, high INR, platelets under 150,000 suggest cirrhosis`],[`Echocardiography`,`Raised JVP, raised peptides, murmur`,`LVEF, E/e', TR velocity over 2.8 m/s suggests PH`],[`Venous duplex`,`Venous signs or DVT suspected`,`Reflux over 0.5 s superficial, over 1 s femoropopliteal`],[`ABPI`,`Before compression`,`Under 0.9 PAD; reduced compression 0.5-0.8; avoid below about 0.5-0.6`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in bilateral oedema`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg oral once or twice daily; IV 20-40 mg or 1-2x oral dose in acute HF`,`Titrate to 0.5-1 kg/day weight loss, then lowest maintenance dose`,`Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, AKI, gout, ototoxicity at high IV doses`],[`Torsemide`,`10-20 mg oral once daily`,`Up to 100-200 mg/day in resistant cases`,`As furosemide; better oral bioavailability`],[`Spironolactone`,`100 mg oral daily in cirrhosis (25-50 mg in HF)`,`Increase every 3-5 days with furosemide in 100:40 ratio, max 400 mg`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia; avoid if K over 5 or eGFR under 30`],[`Metolazone or hydrochlorothiazide (add-on)`,`Metolazone 2.5-5 mg oral; HCTZ 25-50 mg`,`Short courses with daily electrolytes`,`Profound hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia with loop diuretic`],[`Telmisartan (added to CCB)`,`20-40 mg oral once daily`,`Long term; check creatinine and K at 1-2 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia, creatinine rise; teratogenic`],[`Levothyroxine`,`1.6 mcg/kg/day oral; 12.5-25 mcg in elderly/CAD`,`Raise by 12.5-25 mcg every 6-8 weeks to normal TSH`,`Angina, AF, osteoporosis if over-replaced`],[`MPFF (diosmin-hesperidin)`,`1000 mg oral once daily (or 500 mg twice)`,`Several months for venous symptoms`,`GI upset; adjunct to compression only`],[`Thiamine`,`100 mg IV/IM before glucose, then daily`,`Until recovery, then oral`,`Rare anaphylaxis with IV; give before glucose`],[`IDA (ivermectin + DEC + albendazole)`,`Single annual dose: DEC 6 mg/kg (300 mg adult), albendazole 400 mg, ivermectin by height`,`Annual MDA rounds`,`Fever, myalgia from dying microfilariae; not in pregnancy or under 2 years`]]},{heading:`Drugs that cause oedema and the fix`,columns:[`Drug class`,`Examples`,`Mechanism`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Dihydropyridine CCB`,`Amlodipine, nifedipine, felodipine`,`Precapillary dilatation raises capillary pressure`,`Lower dose, add ACEi/ARB, switch to lercanidipine or cilnidipine`],[`NSAIDs and coxibs`,`Diclofenac, ibuprofen, etoricoxib`,`Prostaglandin block causes sodium retention`,`Stop; paracetamol or topical NSAID`],[`Thiazolidinediones`,`Pioglitazone`,`PPAR-gamma increases collecting-duct sodium uptake`,`Stop if HF; avoid in NYHA III-IV`],[`Corticosteroids and hormones`,`Prednisolone, fludrocortisone, oestrogens, liquorice`,`Mineralocorticoid sodium retention`,`Lowest dose; review need`],[`Gabapentinoids`,`Pregabalin, gabapentin`,`Not fully known, vasodilatory`,`Dose reduction or alternative`],[`Vasodilators`,`Minoxidil, hydralazine`,`Reflex sodium retention`,`Combine with diuretic and beta-blocker`],[`Insulin`,`Rapid start of insulin`,`Insulin increases renal sodium reabsorption`,`Usually self-limiting over weeks`]]}],redFlags:[`Oedema with breathlessness at rest, orthopnoea, hypoxia or basal crackles - acute heart failure or pulmonary oedema.`,`Anasarca with oliguria, rising creatinine, potassium over 6 mmol/L or sodium under 125 mmol/L.`,`Sudden painful swelling with calf tenderness, pelvic malignancy or nephrotic syndrome - DVT or IVC thrombosis; with chest pain or haemoptysis think PE.`,`Red, hot, tender leg with fever, rapidly spreading erythema or pain out of proportion - cellulitis with sepsis or necrotising fasciitis.`,`Pregnancy after 20 weeks with BP 140/90 or more, headache, visual blurring or sudden facial oedema; or breathlessness in late pregnancy or postpartum (peripartum cardiomyopathy).`,`Cirrhosis with oedema plus fever, abdominal pain, confusion or GI bleeding - SBP, encephalopathy or variceal bleed.`,`Oedema with hypotension, warm peripheries and lactic acidosis in an alcohol user or malnourished patient - Shoshin beriberi.`],pearls:[`JVP first: raised means heart or volume overload; normal JVP with low albumin means kidney, liver or gut; both normal means venous, drug, lymphatic or hypothyroid.`,`The urine dipstick is the cheapest test that changes management in bilateral oedema.`,`Amlodipine oedema is precapillary vasodilatation - furosemide does not work; add an ARB or ACE inhibitor or switch the CCB.`,`Stemmer sign positive = lymphoedema; lipoedema spares the feet; myxoedema is non-pitting.`,`Pretibial myxoedema belongs to Graves disease, not to hypothyroidism.`,`Pit recovery under 40 seconds points to hypoalbuminaemia.`,`Epidemic dropsy = argemone oil in mustard oil: oedema, erythema, diarrhoea, glaucoma and high-output failure.`,`Mnemonic LEGS SWELL: Liver, Endocrine, Gravity/venous, Starvation, Sodium-retaining drugs, Weak heart, Elephantiasis, Leaky kidney, Lungs (PH/OSA).`,`Check ABPI before compression - under 0.5-0.6 compression can cause necrosis.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-q1`,stem:`A 62-year-old man started on amlodipine 10 mg daily three months ago now has bilateral ankle oedema. JVP is normal, urine dipstick is negative and serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL. What is the most appropriate step?`,options:[`Add furosemide 40 mg daily`,`Order venous duplex of both legs`,`Reduce amlodipine and add telmisartan`,`Start spironolactone 25 mg daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Dihydropyridine CCB oedema comes from precapillary arteriolar dilatation that raises capillary hydrostatic pressure; it is not sodium retention, so furosemide or spironolactone does little and risks volume depletion. Lowering the CCB dose and adding an ARB or ACE inhibitor (which dilates postcapillary venules) relieves it while keeping BP control. A venous duplex is unnecessary when the drug history explains the oedema.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old man from coastal Odisha has a firm swollen right leg for 5 years with thickened skin; the examiner cannot pinch a fold of skin at the base of his second toe. This sign indicates:`,options:[`Lymphoedema`,`Lipoedema`,`Chronic venous insufficiency`,`Myxoedema`],answer:0,explanation:`Inability to pinch the skin at the base of the second toe is the Stemmer sign, which indicates lymphoedema - here likely filarial in an endemic area. Lipoedema characteristically spares the feet and is Stemmer negative; venous oedema spares the dorsum of the toes and shows pigmentation and varicosities; myxoedema is non-pitting but generalised with hypothyroid features.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-q3`,stem:`Several members of one family in a north Indian town develop bilateral pitting leg oedema, skin erythema, diarrhoea and raised intraocular pressure after using loose mustard oil. The most likely toxin is:`,options:[`Aflatoxin from Aspergillus flavus`,`Beta-oxalyl-aminoalanine from Lathyrus sativus`,`Triorthocresyl phosphate from contaminated oil`,`Sanguinarine from Argemone mexicana seed oil`],answer:3,explanation:`Epidemic dropsy follows adulteration of mustard oil with argemone oil, whose alkaloid sanguinarine increases capillary permeability, causing oedema, erythema, diarrhoea, glaucoma and high-output failure. Aflatoxin causes hepatotoxicity, Lathyrus toxin causes spastic paraplegia (neurolathyrism), and triorthocresyl phosphate causes a peripheral neuropathy with paralysis, not oedema.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-q4`,stem:`In an ambulatory patient with bilateral leg oedema and mild exertional breathlessness, which NT-proBNP value makes heart failure unlikely according to ESC 2021?`,options:[`Under 450 pg/mL`,`Under 125 pg/mL`,`Under 300 pg/mL`,`Under 900 pg/mL`],answer:1,explanation:`In the non-acute (chronic, OPD) setting ESC 2021 uses NT-proBNP under 125 pg/mL or BNP under 35 pg/mL to make heart failure unlikely. The value of 300 pg/mL is the acute rule-out threshold in the emergency department, while 450 and 900 pg/mL are age-specific rule-in thresholds for acute HF, not rule-out values for an outpatient.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-q5`,stem:`Which mechanism best explains primary renal sodium retention ('overfill') in nephrotic syndrome?`,options:[`Low effective arterial volume activating renin`,`Hyaluronic acid deposition in the dermis`,`Plasmin from filtered plasminogen activating ENaC`,`Precapillary arteriolar dilatation`],answer:2,explanation:`In nephrotic syndrome filtered plasminogen is converted to plasmin in the tubule, which cleaves and activates the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) in the collecting duct, causing primary sodium retention - one reason amiloride helps. Underfill with renin activation is the main mechanism in heart failure and cirrhosis, hyaluronic acid deposition explains myxoedema, and precapillary dilatation explains CCB oedema.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the approach to a 55-year-old woman presenting to the OPD with bilateral leg oedema. Outline the minimal work-up and management principles.`,openingLines:[`Oedema is palpable swelling from expansion of the interstitial fluid volume, visible after about 2.5-3 L accumulates; bilateral leg oedema usually reflects a systemic or bilateral local cause.`,`The bedside triad of JVP, urine protein and serum albumin separates sodium-retaining (cardiac, renal, hepatic, hypoalbuminaemic) causes from local causes (venous, drug, lymphatic, hypothyroid).`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism (Starling forces)`,points:[`Raised capillary hydrostatic pressure: heart failure, renal sodium retention, venous hypertension, DHP-CCB.`,`Low oncotic pressure: nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition.`,`Increased permeability, lymphatic obstruction and myxoedema complete the list.`]},{heading:`History`,points:[`Onset, diurnal pattern, cardiac (orthopnoea, PND), renal (frothy urine), liver (alcohol, jaundice) symptoms.`,`Drug history: amlodipine, NSAIDs, steroids, pioglitazone, pregabalin.`,`Snoring, hypothyroid symptoms, filarial fevers, pregnancy and diuretic misuse.`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`Pitting grade, level, symmetry, sacral oedema; JVP, cardiac and liver signs.`,`Venous signs, Stemmer sign, foot sparing (lipoedema), thyroid signs, BP, BMI.`]},{heading:`Minimal work-up`,points:[`Urine dipstick and uPCR, albumin, creatinine, electrolytes, LFT, TSH, Hb.`,`NT-proBNP and echo if breathless or JVP raised; venous duplex and ABPI for venous disease.`]},{heading:`Management principles`,points:[`Treat the cause; salt under 5 g/day in sodium-retaining states.`,`Loop diuretic for HF and renal oedema; spironolactone plus furosemide (100:40) for cirrhosis.`,`No diuretic for venous, CCB, lymphatic or idiopathic oedema: elevation, compression after ABPI, drug switch.`,`Levothyroxine for hypothyroidism; limb hygiene and MMDP for filarial lymphoedema.`]},{heading:`Red flags and referral`,points:[`Admit for dyspnoea at rest, anasarca with AKI, DVT, cellulitis with sepsis, pre-eclampsia.`,`Refer cardiology, nephrology or vascular surgery by cause; follow creatinine and K in 1-2 weeks after diuretics.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: bilateral oedema - JVP raised or normal - urine protein and albumin - cause groups`,`Table: cardiac vs renal vs hepatic vs venous oedema`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:2},{part:`History and examination`,marks:3},{part:`Work-up with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Management and referral`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Starling forces`,`JVP`,`urine protein`,`albumin`,`NT-proBNP`,`CCB oedema`,`Stemmer sign`,`compression`,`ABPI`]},{id:`cardiovascular-oedema-approach-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate cardiac, renal and hepatic oedema.`,openingLines:[`All three are generalised pitting oedema from renal sodium and water retention, but they differ in site of onset, JVP and associated findings.`,`The discriminators are JVP, urine protein, serum albumin and the stigmata of the organ involved.`],answer:[{heading:`Cardiac oedema`,points:[`Starts in dependent parts (ankles, sacrum), evening worse; preceded by exertional dyspnoea and orthopnoea.`,`Raised JVP, S3, crackles, tender hepatomegaly; NT-proBNP raised, echo abnormal.`]},{heading:`Renal oedema`,points:[`Starts periorbitally on waking, then generalises; frothy urine (nephrotic) or haematuria and hypertension (nephritic).`,`JVP normal or mildly raised; heavy proteinuria, albumin under 3 g/dL, or raised creatinine.`]},{heading:`Hepatic oedema`,points:[`Ascites precedes leg oedema; jaundice, spider naevi, palmar erythema, splenomegaly.`,`JVP normal; low albumin with high INR and low platelets; urine protein negative.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column table: cardiac vs renal vs hepatic oedema`],markSplit:[{part:`Site and onset`,marks:1},{part:`JVP and associated signs`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2}],keywords:[`periorbital oedema`,`ascites`,`JVP`,`proteinuria`,`albumin`,`NT-proBNP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Edema (chapter on cardinal manifestations)`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`Trayes KP et al. Edema: Diagnosis and Management. American Family Physician, 2013`,`ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure, 2021, and 2023 Focused Update`,`ESVS Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Chronic Venous Disease of the Lower Limbs, 2022`,`KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases, 2021; KDIGO CKD Guideline, 2024`,`AASLD Practice Guidance on ascites, spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and hepatorenal syndrome, 2021`,`National Center for Vector Borne Diseases Control (MoHFW), Lymphatic Filariasis elimination and MMDP guidelines, 2024`]}),t.push({id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention`,title:`Cardiovascular risk assessment and primary prevention`,oneLiner:`**Primary prevention** is preventing a first atherosclerotic event in people without ASCVD by estimating **10-year absolute risk** (in India the **WHO/ISH 2019 South Asia chart**, refined by **risk enhancers** and, when undecided, a **coronary calcium score**) and matching intensity to risk: **lifestyle for all**, BP control, a **statin to the LAI/CSI LDL target**, **no routine aspirin**, and a **polypill** where adherence and access are the barrier.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`primary prevention`,`cardiovascular risk score`,`WHO/ISH risk chart`,`HEARTS`,`QRISK3`,`PREVENT equations`,`pooled cohort equations`,`SCORE2`,`risk enhancers`,`lipoprotein(a)`,`coronary artery calcium`,`CAC score`,`aspirin primary prevention`,`polypill`,`TIPS-3`,`Polycap`,`statin`,`Lipid Association of India`,`NP-NCD`,`CBAC`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Primary prevention** prevents the first clinical ASCVD event (MI, stroke, PAD) in someone without known disease; **primordial prevention** stops risk factors from appearing; **secondary prevention** prevents recurrence after an event. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Cardiovascular disease** is the leading cause of death worldwide, about **20 million deaths a year**, over three-quarters in low- and middle-income countries. [WHF 2023]`,`In India CVD causes about **28% of all deaths**, with ischaemic heart disease and stroke the top two causes. [ISLDBI 2017]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB-17**: diabetes **11.4%**, prediabetes **15.3%**, hypertension **35.5%**, generalised obesity **28.6%**, abdominal obesity **39.5%** and some dyslipidaemia in **81%** of Indian adults. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**GATS-2**: **28.6%** of Indian adults use tobacco, mostly **smokeless (21.4%)** - and chewing tobacco also roughly doubles MI risk. [GATS-2 2017]`,`Indians have their **first MI about 5-10 years earlier** than other populations (median about 53 years in INTERHEART), with more disease in young adults. [INTERHEART]`,`**INTERHEART**: nine modifiable factors (apoB/apoA1, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, abdominal obesity, psychosocial stress, low fruit and vegetables, inactivity, alcohol pattern) explain **over 90%** of MI risk in every region, South Asia included. [INTERHEART]`,`**PURE**: about **70%** of cardiovascular events worldwide are attributable to modifiable risk factors, with **hypertension the largest single contributor**. [PURE 2020]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Causal, modifiable**: **LDL and other apoB lipoproteins**, raised BP, tobacco (smoked and smokeless), diabetes, obesity (especially central), physical inactivity, unhealthy diet, harmful alcohol and psychosocial stress. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Non-modifiable**: age, male sex, **family history of premature ASCVD** (first-degree male under 55, female under 65), South Asian ancestry, familial hypercholesterolaemia and **lipoprotein(a)**. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Kidney**: CKD (eGFR under 60 or albuminuria) raises risk independently and in proportion to severity; CKD 3b-4 is high risk without any score. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Women-specific**: **pre-eclampsia** (about twofold later CVD risk), gestational diabetes, preterm delivery, **premature menopause (under 40)** and PCOS. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Inflammatory and infective**: rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, SLE, inflammatory bowel disease and **HIV** raise risk beyond conventional factors. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Others**: severe mental illness and antipsychotics, obstructive sleep apnoea, chronic air pollution (ambient and household), low socioeconomic status and shift work. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**The Indian phenotype**: more visceral fat and less muscle at the same BMI ('**thin-fat Indian**'), early insulin resistance, **atherogenic dyslipidaemia** (high TG, low HDL, small dense LDL) and high Lp(a). [LAI 2023]`,`**Developmental origins**: low birth weight followed by rapid childhood weight gain programmes insulin resistance and later CVD (DOHaD, Barker hypothesis). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why absolute risk and cumulative exposure`,points:[`Atherosclerosis begins in childhood: **apoB-containing lipoproteins** enter and are retained in the intima, are oxidised and taken up by macrophages (foam cells), so plaque grows with **cumulative LDL exposure ('LDL-years')**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Mendelian randomisation shows **lifelong** lower LDL gives a far larger risk reduction per mmol/L than the same reduction started late - the case for starting early ('**lower for longer**'). [EAS consensus 2017]`,`Risk factors **multiply rather than add**, so a smoker with mildly raised BP and cholesterol may carry more risk than someone with one very high value - hence multivariable risk scores. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`Every drug gives a roughly **constant relative risk reduction**, so the **absolute benefit = relative reduction x baseline absolute risk** - the reason treatment intensity follows absolute risk, not a single number. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Statins** inhibit HMG-CoA reductase, upregulate hepatic **LDL receptors** and cut major vascular events by about **22% per 1 mmol/L (38.7 mg/dL) LDL reduction**, whatever the starting LDL. [CTT 2010]`,`**Lipoprotein(a)** is an LDL-like particle with apo(a): pro-atherogenic, pro-inflammatory (oxidised phospholipids) and antifibrinolytic; it is **genetically fixed** and not lowered by statins or lifestyle. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Aspirin** irreversibly blocks platelet COX-1; it prevents thrombotic events but causes a **fixed bleeding risk**, so when baseline event risk is low (and statins and BP control are used) the net benefit disappears. [USPSTF 2022]`,`**Polypill rationale**: combining low doses of drugs acting on independent causal pathways (BP, LDL, platelets) adds their effects with few side effects and simplifies adherence (Wald and Law 2003). [TIPS-3 2021]`,`**Coronary calcium** is a direct measure of plaque burden; it integrates lifetime exposure and genetic susceptibility, which is why it reclassifies people in whom scores are uncertain. [ACC/AHA 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification: levels of prevention and risk categories`,points:[`**Levels of prevention**: primordial (prevent risk factors), primary (treat risk factors), secondary (after an event), tertiary (rehabilitation, limit disability) and quaternary (avoid over-medicalisation). [Park 27e]`,`**Rose's prevention paradox**: most events occur in the large number at moderate risk, so a **population strategy** (salt, trans fat, tobacco tax) prevents more events than a high-risk strategy alone. [Rose 1985]`,`**WHO/ISH 2019 charts** give 10-year risk of fatal and non-fatal MI and stroke in bands of **under 5%, 5-10%, 10-20%, 20-30% and 30% or more**, recalibrated for 21 regions including **South Asia**. [WHO 2019]`,`**No calculator needed** - already high or very high risk: established ASCVD, **diabetes with target organ damage or 2 or more risk factors**, **CKD 3b-4**, **LDL 190 mg/dL or more or familial hypercholesterolaemia**. [LAI 2023]`,`**LAI 2023** counts major risk factors (age 45+ men / 55+ women, premature family history, tobacco, hypertension, low HDL) into **low, moderate, high, very high and extreme** categories with LDL targets of under 100, 100, 70, 50 and 30 mg/dL. [LAI 2023]`,`**ACC/AHA (pooled cohort equations)**: under 5% low, 5-7.5% borderline, **7.5-20% intermediate**, 20% or more high; the **PREVENT equations** now replace them in US guidelines. [ACC/AHA 2026]`,`**ESC SCORE2** (40-69 years) and **SCORE2-OP** (70+) use **age-specific thresholds**, e.g. at 50-69 years under 5% low-moderate, 5-10% high, 10% or more very high. [ESC 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: the risk-assessment history`,points:[`Most people are **asymptomatic**; the visit is opportunistic (BP camp, NP-NCD screening, executive check-up, another complaint) - ask first about **angina, breathlessness, claudication, TIA or stroke**, which move the patient to secondary prevention. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Tobacco in every form**: cigarettes, bidis, hookah and **smokeless** (gutka, khaini, zarda, mishri); quantify pack-years and readiness to quit. [NTCP]`,`**Known risk factors**: diabetes and its duration, hypertension, CKD, dyslipidaemia, and current drugs and adherence. [LAI 2023]`,`**Family history**: premature MI, stroke or sudden death in a **first-degree male under 55 or female under 65**, and known very high cholesterol in relatives (FH). [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Women**: pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, preterm birth, PCOS, menopause age and hormone use. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Lifestyle**: diet in Indian terms (salt, pickles, fried snacks, ghee or vanaspati, sweets, sugary drinks), physical activity minutes per week, alcohol, sleep duration and snoring. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Other enhancers**: rheumatoid arthritis, psoriasis, HIV, severe mental illness, antipsychotics or long-term steroids, and **erectile dysfunction** (an early marker of vascular disease). [QRISK3]`,`**Psychosocial**: stress, depression, social isolation and financial barriers to buying lifelong drugs. [ESC 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**BP** measured correctly (seated, correct cuff, average of the last two of three readings) - the single most important modifiable number. [ESC 2024]`,`**BMI** with Asian cut-offs: overweight **23-24.9**, obese **25 or more** kg/m2. [Misra 2009]`,`**Waist circumference**: **90 cm or more in men, 80 cm or more in women** marks central obesity in Indians. [ICMR 2023]`,`**Tendon xanthomas** (Achilles, knuckles), xanthelasma and **corneal arcus under 45 years** suggest familial hypercholesterolaemia. [LAI 2023]`,`**Acanthosis nigricans** and skin tags signal insulin resistance; look for thyroid signs (hypothyroidism raises LDL). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulse** for irregularity (AF), peripheral pulses and bruits; an **ankle-brachial index under 0.9** means PAD and converts primary to secondary prevention. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Fundus** and urine dipstick for hypertensive or diabetic target organ damage. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Pitfalls: when a risk score misleads`,points:[`**Western scores underestimate risk in South Asians**; no calculator has been validated in a large Indian cohort, which is why LAI counts risk factors and ACC/AHA lists South Asian ancestry as a risk enhancer. [LAI 2023]`,`**Young adults** score low on 10-year risk even with heavy risk-factor burden; use **lifetime or 30-year risk** (PREVENT, QRISK3 lifetime) for them. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Single extreme risk factor**: LDL 190 mg/dL or more, BP 180/110 or more, or heavy smoking need treatment regardless of the score. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Familial hypercholesterolaemia** is not modelled by any score - its lifelong LDL exposure makes it high risk from early adulthood. [LAI 2023]`,`**Elderly**: age dominates the score, so almost everyone over 70 crosses thresholds; weigh frailty, life expectancy and polypharmacy. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Treated patients**: scores use current (treated) BP and lipids and underestimate the original risk; scores also exclude Lp(a), CKD (except PREVENT and QRISK3) and inflammatory disease. [ACC/AHA 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations: what to order and the cut-offs`,points:[`**Baseline for everyone 30+**: BP, BMI, waist, **fasting or random glucose / HbA1c**, lipid profile (non-fasting is acceptable), creatinine with eGFR and **urine albumin-creatinine ratio** where available. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Lipids**: total cholesterol, HDL, triglycerides, calculated LDL and **non-HDL (target = LDL target plus 30 mg/dL)**; Friedewald LDL is invalid when TG is over 400 mg/dL. [LAI 2023]`,`**Lipoprotein(a) once in a lifetime** in every adult: **50 mg/dL or more (about 105-125 nmol/L)** is a risk enhancer and upgrades the category. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Other enhancers (ACC/AHA)**: persistent **TG 175 mg/dL or more**, **hs-CRP 2 mg/L or more**, **apoB 130 mg/dL or more**, ABI under 0.9, LDL 160 mg/dL or more. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Coronary artery calcium score** (non-contrast CT, about 1 mSv) when the statin decision is uncertain: **CAC 0** allows deferral, **1-99** favours statin from age 55, **100 or more (or 75th percentile)** means statin. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`CAC 0 does **not** reassure in **smokers, diabetics or those with a strong premature family history** - treat them anyway. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Carotid ultrasound for plaque** is an alternative risk modifier where CT is unavailable; carotid intima-media thickness alone is no longer recommended. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**ECG** for LVH or silent Q waves in hypertensives and diabetics; routine stress testing or CT angiography is **not** recommended for screening asymptomatic people. [USPSTF 2018]`,`**Before a statin**: baseline ALT; **CK only if muscle symptoms**; TSH if LDL is unexpectedly high (secondary dyslipidaemia). [LAI 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Tobacco**: brief advice at every visit (5 As), the **quitline 1800-11-2356** and mCessation; NRT gum/patch, **varenicline 12 weeks** or **bupropion SR** where available - quitting halves CHD risk within about a year. [NTCP]`,`**Physical activity**: **150-300 minutes a week of moderate** (brisk walking) or 75-150 minutes vigorous activity plus muscle strengthening on 2 days; less sitting. [WHO 2020]`,`**Diet**: salt **under 5 g a day** (Indians average about 8 g), more fruit, vegetables, pulses and nuts, fewer refined grains and sugar, **no trans fat (vanaspati, reheated oil)**, and oil rotation. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`,`**Potassium-enriched salt substitute** (where kidneys are normal) cut stroke by 14% and major events by 13% in SSaSS. [SSaSS 2021]`,`**Weight**: aim for 5-10% loss and waist under 90/80 cm; there is **no safe level of alcohol** for cardiovascular health. [WHO 2023]`,`**BP**: treat to targets per the Hypertension topic; each **10 mmHg systolic fall cuts major CV events by about 20%**. [Ettehad 2016]`,`**Statin thresholds in India**: lifestyle for about 3 months in low/moderate risk, then a statin if LDL stays **100 mg/dL or more**; start at once in **high risk (LDL target under 70)**, diabetes over 40 and LDL 190 or more. [LAI 2023]`,`**Statin intensity**: moderate (**atorvastatin 10-20 mg, rosuvastatin 5-10 mg**) cuts LDL 30-49%; high (**atorvastatin 40-80 mg, rosuvastatin 20-40 mg**) cuts it by 50% or more - used for high risk or LDL 190+. [ACC/AHA 2026]`,`**Aspirin**: **not** for routine primary prevention; consider **75-100 mg daily** only in a 40-70-year-old at high risk (e.g. diabetes with target organ damage or CAC 100+) with **low bleeding risk**, after shared decision. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Polypill**: a once-daily fixed-dose combination (statin + 2-3 BP drugs +/- aspirin) is a reasonable choice for intermediate-risk people, especially where adherence or cost is the barrier; the Hypertension and Dyslipidaemia topics hold the full ladders. [TIPS-3 2021]`]},{heading:`Statin safety and inpatient touchpoints`,points:[`**Every admission is a prevention opportunity**: check BP, HbA1c and lipids, start tobacco cessation (NRT in the ward) and a statin when indicated before discharge. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**Statin-associated muscle symptoms**: most are **nocebo** (SAMSON: about 90% of symptoms also occurred on placebo); stop, let symptoms settle, then **rechallenge** with another statin, lower dose or alternate-day rosuvastatin. [SAMSON 2021]`,`**Admit for rhabdomyolysis**: muscle pain with weakness, dark urine, **CK over 10 times the upper limit** with raised creatinine - stop statin, IV fluids, monitor potassium and renal function. [ACC/AHA 2026]`,`**Interactions** that raise statin levels: clarithromycin, azole antifungals, HIV protease inhibitors, ciclosporin, gemfibrozil, high-dose amlodipine or diltiazem with simvastatin - use rosuvastatin or pitavastatin, or hold during short courses. [LAI 2023]`,`**ALT over 3 times normal** on two occasions: stop and reassess; an isolated mild rise or fatty liver is **not** a contraindication. [LAI 2023]`,`**Aspirin GI bleed**: stop aspirin in primary prevention permanently - the indication does not justify the risk. [USPSTF 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes 40-75 years**: statin for all (at least moderate intensity), high intensity with organ damage or 2 or more risk factors; aspirin only after a bleeding-risk discussion; SGLT2i or GLP-1 RA per the Cardiometabolic topic. [ADA 2025]`,`**CKD (not on dialysis), age 50+**: statin or statin plus ezetimibe regardless of LDL; do **not start** statins de novo in dialysis patients. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**HIV**: **pitavastatin 4 mg** cut major events by 35% in low-to-moderate-risk people with HIV (REPRIEVE); mind antiretroviral interactions. [REPRIEVE 2023]`,`**Pregnancy and lactation**: stop statins when planning pregnancy or on diagnosis and during breastfeeding; low-dose aspirin is used only for pre-eclampsia prevention, not CVD. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Elderly over 75**: individualise statins for primary prevention; **do not start aspirin** (ASPREE: no benefit, more bleeding, higher mortality). [ASPREE 2018]`,`**Young adults with FH or LDL 190+**: high-intensity statin from early adulthood and **cascade screening** of first-degree relatives; children from age 8-10. [LAI 2023]`,`**Severe mental illness**: twice the CVD mortality; screen yearly and choose metabolically neutral antipsychotics where possible. [QRISK3]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: the **non-laboratory WHO chart** (BMI instead of cholesterol) and a fixed-dose statin for risk 20% or more are acceptable when lipids cannot be measured. [WHO HEARTS 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications of prevention and prognosis`,points:[`**Statin and diabetes**: about **1 extra case of diabetes per 255 people treated for 4 years**, against many more vascular events prevented - not a reason to stop. [Sattar 2010]`,`**Aspirin bleeding**: in ASCEND (diabetes) aspirin cut serious vascular events by 12% but raised **major bleeding by 29%**, a near-neutral trade. [ASCEND 2018]`,`**ASPREE** (70 and over): no cardiovascular benefit, **38% more major haemorrhage** and higher all-cause mortality; **ARRIVE** (moderate risk): no benefit, double GI bleeding. [ASPREE 2018]`,`**Polypill** in TIPS-3 cut the composite outcome by **21%**, and polypill plus aspirin by **31%**, over 4.6 years in intermediate-risk people. [TIPS-3 2021]`,`**Untreated trajectory**: Indians with multiple risk factors have high rates of premature MI, stroke and heart failure in their productive years, with catastrophic out-of-pocket costs. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Adherence** falls to about half within a year for preventive drugs - simpler regimens (single pills, polypill) and follow-up systems matter more than drug choice. [WHO HEARTS 2020]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Reassess risk every 5 years** if low and untreated, and **every year** if intermediate or high or a risk factor changes. [ESC 2021 prevention]`,`**After starting or changing a statin**: lipids at **4-12 weeks** to check response and adherence, then every 3-12 months. [ACC/AHA 2026]`,`**On BP drugs**: review every 2-4 weeks until controlled, then 3-6 monthly; creatinine and potassium yearly. [ESC 2024]`,`**Refer** suspected FH (LDL 190 mg/dL or more, xanthomas) for cascade screening, LDL above target on maximal oral therapy, Lp(a) very high with premature family history, and true statin intolerance. [LAI 2023]`,`**Refer** for CAC or carotid imaging when the statin decision remains uncertain after enhancers and the patient wants more information. [ACC/AHA 2019]`,`**Track** at every visit: tobacco status, BP, weight and waist, adherence and side effects; document the 10-year risk in the record. [WHO HEARTS 2020]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`**NP-NCD** (2023, renamed from NPCDCS): population-based screening of everyone **30 and above** at **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for hypertension, diabetes and common cancers, with follow-up and free drugs. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**CBAC form**: ASHA scores age, tobacco, alcohol, waist, physical activity and family history; a **score over 4** flags higher risk for priority screening. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**India Hypertension Control Initiative (IHCI)** and the **75/25 initiative** (75 million people with hypertension or diabetes on standard care by 2025) use protocol-based treatment and digital follow-up. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Tobacco control**: COTPA 2003, graphic warnings, tobacco-free schools, **NTCP** cessation centres and the national quitline. [NTCP]`,`**Food policy**: FSSAI limits **industrial trans fat to 2% or less** in oils and fats since 2022; Eat Right India promotes less salt, sugar and fat. [FSSAI 2022]`,`**Primordial prevention**: school physical activity, limiting junk-food advertising, healthy canteens and Fit India; air-quality action under the National Clean Air Programme. [MoHFW]`,`**Counselling style**: show the patient their risk (chart colour, 'heart age'), agree one or two goals per visit, and involve the family member who cooks. [ESC 2021 prevention]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AHA PREVENT equations (2023)**: ages 30-79, add eGFR and statin use (optional UACR, HbA1c, deprivation), estimate total CVD including heart failure, drop race, and give lower risks than the pooled cohort equations. [AHA 2023]`,`**AHA/ACC 2025 hypertension guideline** uses PREVENT: stage 1 hypertension is treated with drugs when **10-year CVD risk is 7.5% or more**. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2026 dyslipidaemia guideline** reinstates LDL goals (under 100 primary prevention, under 70 high risk, under 55 very high risk), uses PREVENT and recommends **Lp(a) once** in adults. [ACC/AHA 2026]`,`**ESC/EAS 2025 focused update**: Lp(a) 50 mg/dL or more as a risk enhancer, **bempedoic acid** for statin-intolerant patients (CLEAR Outcomes), and no role for supplements. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**CSI 2024 dyslipidaemia guideline** (India): non-fasting lipids, LDL under 100 (low risk), under 70 (high risk, e.g. diabetes or hypertension) and **under 55 mg/dL (very high risk)** - slightly different from LAI's under 50. [CSI 2024]`,`**NICE NG238 (2023)**: QRISK3 for ages 25-84; offer **atorvastatin 20 mg** at 10-year risk of 10% or more, and do not withhold a statin below 10% if the person prefers it. [NICE 2023]`,`**USPSTF 2022**: aspirin is an individual decision at 40-59 with risk 10% or more and **not recommended to start at 60 or older**; statins for 40-75 with a risk factor and risk 10% or more. [USPSTF 2022]`,`**WHO Essential Medicines List 2023** added cardiovascular **polypill** fixed-dose combinations; **REPRIEVE (2023)** extended statins to people with HIV. [WHO EML 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: '**Cardiovascular risk assessment and primary prevention in primary care**' - define levels, describe the WHO/ISH chart, enhancers and CAC, then lifestyle, statin, aspirin and polypill.`,`Short notes asked: **polypill**, **aspirin in primary prevention**, **prevention paradox**, **CBAC form**, **risk enhancers**.`,`Viva: why not aspirin for every diabetic? ASCEND - 12% fewer events but 29% more major bleeding; net benefit only at high risk and low bleeding risk.`,`Viva: the variables of the **WHO/ISH lab chart** (age, sex, smoking, systolic BP, diabetes, total cholesterol) and the **non-lab chart** (BMI replaces cholesterol and diabetes).`,`Viva: **QRISK3** extras - ethnicity, CKD, AF, migraine, SLE, RA, severe mental illness, antipsychotics, steroids, erectile dysfunction, BP variability.`,`Common mistake: running a calculator in someone who already has ASCVD, diabetes with organ damage, CKD 3b-4 or FH - they are high risk by definition.`,`Common mistake: reassuring a 35-year-old smoker with strong family history because the 10-year score is low - use lifetime risk.`,`Common mistake: stopping a statin for mild myalgia or a small ALT rise instead of rechallenging.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Risk calculators compared`,columns:[`Tool`,`Ages`,`Inputs`,`Output and use`],rows:[[`WHO/ISH 2019 (lab)`,`40-74`,`Age, sex, smoking, SBP, diabetes, total cholesterol`,`10-year fatal + non-fatal MI and stroke; South Asia chart; HEARTS and Indian primary care`],[`WHO/ISH 2019 (non-lab)`,`40-74`,`Age, sex, smoking, SBP, BMI`,`Same output where lipids are unavailable`],[`QRISK3`,`25-84`,`Adds ethnicity, deprivation, CKD, AF, RA, SLE, migraine, SMI, antipsychotics, steroids, ED, SBP variability`,`10-year CVD; NICE threshold 10% for atorvastatin 20 mg`],[`PREVENT (AHA 2023)`,`30-79`,`Age, sex, TC, HDL, SBP, BP drugs, diabetes, smoking, eGFR, statin use; optional UACR, HbA1c`,`10- and 30-year total CVD, ASCVD and HF; US guidelines from 2025`],[`Pooled cohort equations`,`40-79`,`Age, sex, race, TC, HDL, SBP, BP drugs, diabetes, smoking`,`10-year ASCVD; 7.5-20% intermediate; replaced by PREVENT`],[`SCORE2 / SCORE2-OP`,`40-69 / 70+`,`Age, sex, smoking, SBP, non-HDL`,`10-year fatal + non-fatal CVD; age-specific thresholds; Europe`],[`LAI risk-factor count`,`Adults`,`Major risk factors plus diabetes, CKD, FH, Lp(a), CAC`,`Indian category with LDL target`]]},{heading:`Indian risk categories and LDL targets (LAI 2023 and CSI 2024)`,columns:[`Category`,`Who is in it (examples)`,`LAI LDL target (mg/dL)`,`CSI 2024 LDL target (mg/dL)`],rows:[[`Low`,`0-1 major risk factor`,`Under 100`,`Under 100`],[`Moderate`,`2 major risk factors`,`Under 100`,`Under 100`],[`High`,`3+ risk factors; diabetes with 0-1 risk factor; CKD 3b-4; LDL 190+ or FH; Lp(a) 50+; CAC 100+`,`Under 70`,`Under 70`],[`Very high`,`ASCVD; diabetes with 2+ risk factors or organ damage`,`Under 50`,`Under 55`],[`Extreme (A and B)`,`CAD plus diabetes, FH, polyvascular disease or recurrent ACS`,`Under 50 (A), 30 or less (B)`,`Not separately defined`]]},{heading:`Risk enhancers and what they do`,columns:[`Enhancer`,`Cut-off`,`Effect on decision`],rows:[[`Premature family history`,`First-degree male under 55, female under 65`,`Favours statin; screen for FH`],[`Lipoprotein(a)`,`50 mg/dL or more (about 105-125 nmol/L)`,`Upgrade category; lower LDL target`],[`hs-CRP`,`2 mg/L or more`,`Favours statin in intermediate risk`],[`Apolipoprotein B`,`130 mg/dL or more`,`Favours statin`],[`Persistent triglycerides`,`175 mg/dL or more`,`Favours statin; lifestyle for TG`],[`CKD`,`eGFR under 60 or albuminuria`,`Statin; CKD 3b-4 is high risk outright`],[`Women-specific`,`Pre-eclampsia, premature menopause under 40`,`Favours statin and closer BP follow-up`],[`Inflammatory disease or HIV`,`RA, psoriasis, SLE, HIV`,`Favours statin`],[`Ankle-brachial index`,`Under 0.9`,`Means PAD - treat as secondary prevention`]]},{heading:`Coronary artery calcium score and action`,columns:[`CAC (Agatston)`,`Meaning`,`Statin action`],rows:[[`0`,`No detectable calcified plaque; very low 10-year event rate`,`Defer statin, reassess in 5-10 years - unless smoker, diabetic or premature family history`],[`1-99`,`Mild plaque`,`Statin favoured, especially age 55 or more`],[`100-299 or 75th percentile or more`,`Moderate plaque; high risk`,`Statin indicated; consider aspirin if bleeding risk low`],[`300 or more`,`Extensive plaque; risk close to established CAD`,`High-intensity statin; treat as very high risk`]]},{heading:`Drugs in primary prevention`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Atorvastatin`,`Moderate 10-20 mg; high 40-80 mg oral once daily`,`Long term; lipids at 4-12 weeks`,`Myalgia, ALT rise, small diabetes risk; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Rosuvastatin`,`Moderate 5-10 mg; high 20-40 mg oral once daily (start 5 mg in Asians)`,`Long term; max 10 mg if eGFR under 30`,`Myalgia, proteinuria at high dose; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Ezetimibe`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`Add if LDL above target on maximal statin`,`Well tolerated; rare ALT rise`],[`Bempedoic acid`,`180 mg oral once daily`,`Statin intolerance (CLEAR Outcomes)`,`Raised uric acid, gout, tendon rupture`],[`Aspirin (selected only)`,`75-100 mg oral once daily after food`,`Only 40-70 years, high risk, low bleeding risk`,`GI and intracranial bleeding; not over 70 or with bleeding risk`],[`Polypill (e.g. Polycap / TIPS-3 type)`,`One capsule daily: statin + ramipril + atenolol + thiazide +/- aspirin 75-100 mg`,`Long term; check BP and potassium`,`Hypotension, cough, bradycardia; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Nicotine replacement`,`Gum 2-4 mg 8-12/day or patch 21 to 14 to 7 mg/day`,`8-12 weeks, then taper`,`Mouth irritation, skin reaction; safe in stable CVD`],[`Varenicline (where available)`,`0.5 mg daily x 3 days, 0.5 mg twice daily x 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks (repeat 12 if quit)`,`Nausea, vivid dreams; reduce dose if eGFR under 30`],[`Bupropion SR`,`150 mg daily x 3 days then 150 mg twice daily`,`7-12 weeks; quit in week 2`,`Seizures (contraindicated in epilepsy, eating disorders), insomnia`]]},{heading:`Landmark trials in primary prevention`,columns:[`Trial`,`Population`,`Result`,`Lesson`],rows:[[`ASPREE 2018`,`19,114 aged 70+ without CVD`,`No CV benefit; major bleeding up 38%; higher all-cause death`,`Do not start aspirin in the elderly`],[`ASCEND 2018`,`15,480 with diabetes, no CVD`,`Vascular events down 12%; major bleeding up 29%`,`Aspirin net benefit marginal in diabetes`],[`ARRIVE 2018`,`12,546 at moderate risk`,`No benefit; GI bleeding doubled`,`Aspirin not for moderate risk`],[`TIPS 2009 (India)`,`2053 Indians with one risk factor`,`Polycap lowered BP, LDL and heart rate as expected`,`Polypill feasible and tolerated in India`],[`TIPS-3 2021`,`5713 intermediate-risk, no CVD`,`Polypill events down 21%; with aspirin down 31%`,`Polypill works in primary prevention`],[`SECURE 2022`,`2499 aged 65+ after MI`,`Polypill MACE down 24%, CV death down 33%`,`Polypill improves adherence in secondary prevention`],[`REPRIEVE 2023`,`7769 with HIV, low-moderate risk`,`Pitavastatin MACE down 35%`,`HIV is a statin indication`]]}],redFlags:[`Exertional chest pain, breathlessness or claudication found at a 'screening' visit - this is not primary prevention; assess for CAD or PAD now.`,`BP 180/110 or more with headache, visual change, chest pain or neurological symptoms - hypertensive emergency pathway.`,`LDL 190 mg/dL or more, tendon xanthomas or premature MI in the family - suspected familial hypercholesterolaemia; refer for cascade screening.`,`Triglycerides 1000 mg/dL or more or abdominal pain with high TG - risk of acute pancreatitis.`,`Muscle pain with weakness and dark urine on a statin - check CK and creatinine the same day (rhabdomyolysis).`,`Melaena, haematemesis or a fall with head injury in a patient on primary-prevention aspirin - stop aspirin and assess urgently.`,`Random glucose 300 mg/dL or more with symptoms or ketones at a screening camp - same-day assessment.`],pearls:[`Treat absolute risk, not a single number: absolute benefit = relative risk reduction x baseline risk.`,`No calculator for ASCVD, diabetes with organ damage, CKD 3b-4 or LDL 190+ - they are already high risk.`,`WHO/ISH non-lab chart swaps cholesterol and diabetes for BMI - use it where lipids cannot be done.`,`CAC 0 is the 'power of zero' - defer statin, except in smokers, diabetics and strong family history.`,`Aspirin in primary prevention: ASPREE, ASCEND, ARRIVE - bleeding cancels benefit; never start after 70.`,`TIPS-3: polypill 21% fewer events, polypill plus aspirin 31% - an Indian-led trial.`,`Measure Lp(a) once in a lifetime; statins do not lower it.`,`Rose's paradox: a large number at small risk gives more cases than a small number at high risk.`,`Indian targets: LDL under 100 (low-moderate), under 70 (high), under 50-55 (very high).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-q1`,stem:`In the WHO/ISH 2019 non-laboratory cardiovascular risk chart used in primary care, which variable replaces total cholesterol and diabetes status?`,options:[`Waist circumference`,`Family history of premature CVD`,`Resting heart rate`,`Body mass index`],answer:3,explanation:`The WHO 2019 non-laboratory chart uses age, sex, smoking, systolic BP and body mass index, so it can be used where lipids and glucose cannot be measured; the laboratory chart uses diabetes and total cholesterol instead of BMI. Waist circumference, family history and heart rate are useful clinically but are not inputs to either WHO chart.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-q2`,stem:`A healthy 74-year-old woman with controlled hypertension and no known vascular disease asks whether she should start daily low-dose aspirin. The best advice is:`,options:[`Do not start aspirin for primary prevention`,`Start aspirin 75 mg daily lifelong`,`Start aspirin only if her LDL is above 130 mg/dL`,`Start aspirin with a proton pump inhibitor`],answer:0,explanation:`ASPREE showed that in people aged 70 and over without CVD, aspirin gave no cardiovascular benefit, increased major haemorrhage by about 38% and was associated with higher all-cause mortality; ACC/AHA and USPSTF advise against starting it at this age. LDL level does not change the aspirin decision, and adding a PPI reduces GI bleeding but not intracranial haemorrhage or the absent benefit.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-q3`,stem:`A 52-year-old non-smoking, non-diabetic man has an intermediate 10-year risk and no family history of premature CAD. His coronary artery calcium score is 0. What is the most appropriate statin decision?`,options:[`High-intensity statin at once`,`Statin plus ezetimibe to LDL under 55 mg/dL`,`Defer statin and reassess risk in 5-10 years`,`Statin only if hs-CRP is above 1 mg/L`],answer:2,explanation:`A CAC score of zero in an intermediate-risk person without diabetes, smoking or premature family history predicts a very low event rate, so ACC/AHA allows the statin to be deferred with reassessment in 5-10 years. High-intensity statin or combination therapy to a very-high-risk target over-treats him, and the hs-CRP enhancer threshold is 2 mg/L, not 1.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-q4`,stem:`In the TIPS-3 trial of intermediate-risk people without cardiovascular disease, which strategy gave the largest reduction in major cardiovascular events?`,options:[`Aspirin 75 mg alone`,`Polypill plus aspirin`,`Polypill alone`,`Placebo with lifestyle advice`],answer:1,explanation:`TIPS-3 found the polypill (simvastatin, atenolol, hydrochlorothiazide and ramipril) cut the composite outcome by 21%, and the polypill plus aspirin by 31%, the largest reduction. Aspirin alone gave a smaller, non-significant reduction, and the placebo group was the comparator rather than an active strategy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-q5`,stem:`Which finding is a recognised risk-enhancing factor that favours starting a statin in a borderline- or intermediate-risk adult?`,options:[`HDL cholesterol of 55 mg/dL`,`Lipoprotein(a) of 60 mg/dL`,`Ankle-brachial index of 1.1`,`Fasting triglycerides of 120 mg/dL`],answer:1,explanation:`Lipoprotein(a) of 50 mg/dL or more is a risk enhancer in ACC/AHA, ESC/EAS 2025 and LAI 2023, because it is genetically determined, pro-atherogenic and not lowered by statins. An HDL of 55 mg/dL is normal, an ABI of 1.1 is normal (under 0.9 indicates PAD), and only triglycerides persistently 175 mg/dL or more count as an enhancer.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`How will you assess cardiovascular risk in an asymptomatic 45-year-old man at an Ayushman Arogya Mandir? Discuss the role of statins, aspirin and the polypill in primary prevention.`,openingLines:[`Primary prevention aims to prevent the first atherosclerotic event in people without known cardiovascular disease, with intensity matched to their 10-year absolute risk.`,`In India risk is estimated with the WHO/ISH 2019 South Asia chart or LAI risk-factor counting, refined by risk enhancers and, when uncertain, a coronary calcium score.`],answer:[{heading:`Levels and burden`,points:[`Primordial, primary, secondary, tertiary prevention; CVD causes about 28% of Indian deaths.`,`INDIAB-17: hypertension 35.5%, diabetes 11.4%; tobacco 28.6% (GATS-2); MI a decade earlier.`]},{heading:`Risk assessment`,points:[`History: tobacco, diabetes, BP, CKD, premature family history, women-specific factors, inflammatory disease.`,`Examination: BP, BMI (23/25), waist (90/80 cm), xanthomas, pulses, ABI.`,`Tests: glucose/HbA1c, lipids (non-fasting), creatinine, UACR, Lp(a) once.`,`WHO/ISH chart (lab or non-lab) bands; skip the chart if ASCVD, diabetes with organ damage, CKD 3b-4 or LDL 190+.`,`Enhancers and CAC (0 defer, 100+ treat) for uncertain cases.`]},{heading:`Lifestyle for all`,points:[`Quit tobacco (quitline, NRT, varenicline), 150-300 min/week activity, salt under 5 g, no trans fat, weight and waist goals, no alcohol.`]},{heading:`Statins`,points:[`LAI 2023 targets: under 100 (low-moderate), under 70 (high), under 50 (very high); CSI 2024 under 55 for very high.`,`Moderate intensity atorvastatin 10-20 mg; high intensity 40-80 mg; lipids at 4-12 weeks.`]},{heading:`Aspirin`,points:[`Not routine: ASPREE, ASCEND, ARRIVE show bleeding offsets benefit.`,`Consider 75-100 mg only at 40-70 years, high risk, low bleeding risk; never start after 70.`]},{heading:`Polypill and programme`,points:[`TIPS-3: polypill 21% and polypill plus aspirin 31% fewer events; WHO EML 2023.`,`NP-NCD screening 30+, CBAC score over 4, IHCI protocols, follow-up every 3-12 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: screen at 30+ - high risk without chart? - WHO/ISH chart band - enhancers/CAC - treatment intensity`,`Table: LAI risk category vs LDL target`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, levels, Indian burden`,marks:2},{part:`Risk assessment tools, enhancers, CAC`,marks:3},{part:`Lifestyle and statins with targets`,marks:3},{part:`Aspirin and polypill evidence`,marks:2}],keywords:[`WHO/ISH chart`,`risk enhancers`,`Lp(a)`,`CAC`,`LAI targets`,`ASPREE`,`ASCEND`,`TIPS-3`,`NP-NCD`,`CBAC`]},{id:`cardiovascular-cv-risk-prevention-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the polypill in cardiovascular prevention.`,openingLines:[`A polypill is a fixed-dose combination of a statin, two or three BP-lowering drugs and sometimes aspirin, taken once daily to reduce several causal risk factors at once.`,`Proposed by Wald and Law in 2003, it was first tested in India (TIPS, Polycap) and reduced events in TIPS-3, PolyIran and SECURE.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale`,points:[`Independent causal pathways (BP, LDL, platelets); low doses add benefit with few side effects.`,`One pill improves adherence and lowers cost - the main barrier in India.`]},{heading:`Evidence`,points:[`TIPS 2009: Polycap lowered BP, LDL and heart rate as expected.`,`TIPS-3 2021: primary prevention, events down 21% (31% with aspirin).`,`PolyIran 2019: events down about a third; SECURE 2022 after MI: MACE down 24%.`]},{heading:`Limitations`,points:[`Fixed doses limit titration; contraindications to any component (pregnancy, bradycardia, hyperkalaemia).`,`Aspirin component not suitable for low-risk or high-bleeding-risk people.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: polypill trials (TIPS, TIPS-3, PolyIran, SECURE) with population and result`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and rationale`,marks:2},{part:`Trial evidence`,marks:2},{part:`Limitations and place in practice`,marks:1}],keywords:[`polypill`,`Polycap`,`TIPS-3`,`PolyIran`,`SECURE`,`adherence`,`fixed-dose combination`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`WHO CVD Risk Chart Working Group. World Health Organization cardiovascular disease risk charts. Lancet Global Health, 2019; WHO HEARTS technical package, 2020`,`2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiovascular disease prevention in clinical practice; ESC/EAS 2025 focused update on dyslipidaemias`,`2019 ACC/AHA Guideline on the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease; 2026 ACC/AHA dyslipidaemia guideline`,`Lipid Association of India (LAI) 2023 update - Consensus Statement IV; Cardiological Society of India dyslipidaemia guidelines, 2024`,`USPSTF recommendations on aspirin (2022) and statins (2022) for primary prevention of cardiovascular disease`,`MoHFW National Programme for Prevention and Control of Non-Communicable Diseases (NP-NCD) operational guidelines, 2023`]}),t.push({id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis`,title:`Hypertensive urgency and emergency`,oneLiner:`A **hypertensive emergency** is severe BP (usually **180/110-120 mmHg or more**) with **acute hypertension-mediated organ damage** (encephalopathy, stroke, ACS, pulmonary oedema, dissection, AKI/TMA, eclampsia, malignant retinopathy) needing **admission and titratable IV drugs** with organ-specific targets - generally **MAP down no more than 20-25% in the first hour** - whereas **severe hypertension without acute organ damage** (formerly 'urgency') is treated with **oral drugs over hours to days**, and **never with sublingual nifedipine**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`hypertensive crisis`,`hypertensive emergency`,`hypertensive urgency`,`severe hypertension`,`malignant hypertension`,`accelerated hypertension`,`hypertensive encephalopathy`,`PRES`,`labetalol`,`nicardipine`,`clevidipine`,`sodium nitroprusside`,`nitroglycerin`,`esmolol`,`phentolamine`,`hydralazine`,`magnesium sulphate`,`sublingual nifedipine`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Hypertensive emergency**: severe BP elevation (ESC **180/110 or more**; ACC/AHA **over 180/120**) with **new or worsening acute organ damage** - the organ damage, not the number, defines it. [ESC 2024]`,`**Severe hypertension without acute organ damage** (formerly **hypertensive urgency**): the same BP with no acute damage; AHA/ACC 2025 drops the word urgency and treats it with oral drugs, often in the outpatient setting. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**Malignant (accelerated) hypertension**: severe BP with **bilateral flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots or papilloedema**, often with thrombotic microangiopathy and AKI - a hypertensive emergency even when the patient feels well. [ESC 2024]`,`**Hypertensive crisis** is the umbrella term for both; emergencies make up roughly a quarter of crises seen in emergency departments. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **1-2% of people with hypertension** have a hypertensive emergency in their lifetime, mostly those with long-standing, untreated or poorly controlled BP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Organ involvement** in a classic series: cerebral infarction about 24%, acute pulmonary oedema 22%, hypertensive encephalopathy 16%, ACS 12%, intracerebral haemorrhage 5%, eclampsia 5% and aortic dissection 2%. [Zampaglione 1996]`,`**India**: about **1 in 3 adults** is hypertensive but control is under 1 in 5 (ICMR-INDIAB, NFHS-5), so emergencies - especially **hypertensive stroke** and malignant hypertension in young men - are common in Indian emergency departments. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Untreated malignant hypertension had about **80% one-year mortality** in the pre-drug era; with treatment most survive, but long-term renal and cardiovascular risk stays high. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and precipitating factors`,points:[`**Commonest precipitant: stopping or never starting antihypertensives** in known essential hypertension - ask about adherence first. [ESC 2024]`,`**Secondary hypertension** underlies **20-40% of malignant hypertension**: renal parenchymal disease, renovascular disease, primary aldosteronism and phaeochromocytoma - always screen after stabilisation. [ESC 2024]`,`**Drugs and toxins**: NSAIDs, steroids, **sympathomimetics** (pseudoephedrine, cocaine, amphetamines), erythropoietin, calcineurin inhibitors, VEGF inhibitors and MAO inhibitors with tyramine. [ESC 2024]`,`**Withdrawal syndromes**: abrupt stopping of **clonidine or beta-blockers**, and alcohol withdrawal, cause rebound sympathetic surges. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: pre-eclampsia, eclampsia and HELLP after 20 weeks and up to 6 weeks postpartum. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Catecholamine excess**: phaeochromocytoma or paraganglioma, autonomic dysreflexia after spinal cord injury above T6, and Guillain-Barre dysautonomia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal crises**: acute glomerulonephritis, renal transplant artery stenosis and **scleroderma renal crisis** (treated with an ACE inhibitor). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contributing**: pain, urinary retention, anxiety, hypoxia and raised intracranial pressure raise BP and must be treated first rather than the BP itself. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: autoregulation and the vicious circle`,points:[`A sudden rise in systemic vascular resistance (angiotensin II, noradrenaline, vasopressin) causes **endothelial injury** when pressure exceeds the arteriole's ability to constrict. [Harrison 22e]`,`Injured arterioles show **fibrinoid necrosis** and **myointimal (onion-skin) proliferation**; platelets and fibrin deposit, causing **thrombotic microangiopathy** with schistocytes, low platelets and AKI. [Robbins 11e]`,`Ischaemia of the kidney releases more renin, and **pressure natriuresis causes volume depletion** - so patients are often dry, and diuretics are avoided unless there is pulmonary oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cerebral autoregulation** keeps flow constant over a MAP of about **60-150 mmHg**; chronic hypertension **shifts the curve to the right**, so the brain tolerates high BP but becomes ischaemic at 'normal' BP. [Harrison 22e]`,`When pressure exceeds the upper limit, autoregulation breaks through, causing **hyperperfusion, vasogenic oedema** and **PRES** (posterior reversible encephalopathy, parieto-occipital white matter). [Harrison 22e]`,`Because the lower limit has also shifted upward, an **abrupt fall in BP** (sublingual nifedipine, large IV boluses) causes **watershed stroke, MI, blindness and AKI** - hence a controlled reduction of about 20-25% in the first hour. [ESC 2024]`,`**Aortic dissection** is driven by the rate of rise of aortic pressure (**dP/dt**), so heart rate and contractility are lowered first with a beta-blocker before vasodilators. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**Acute pulmonary oedema** in hypertensive crisis is usually **afterload mismatch and fluid redistribution**, not volume overload - nitrates at high dose relieve it faster than diuretics. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**Drug choice follows mechanism**: titratable short-acting IV agents (labetalol, nicardipine, clevidipine, nitroprusside, esmolol) allow minute-to-minute control that oral drugs cannot. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Classification: emergency by organ involved`,points:[`**Neurological**: hypertensive encephalopathy, acute ischaemic stroke, intracerebral haemorrhage, subarachnoid haemorrhage. [ESC 2024]`,`**Cardiac**: acute coronary syndrome and acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. [ESC 2024]`,`**Vascular**: acute aortic dissection or intramural haematoma. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**Renal and haematological**: AKI with **thrombotic microangiopathy** (malignant nephrosclerosis). [ESC 2024]`,`**Obstetric**: severe pre-eclampsia (BP **160/110 or more**), eclampsia and HELLP syndrome. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Catecholaminergic**: phaeochromocytoma crisis, cocaine or amphetamine toxicity, MAOI-tyramine reaction, clonidine withdrawal. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Retinopathy grading**: Keith-Wagener-Barker grade III (haemorrhages, exudates, cotton-wool spots) and grade IV (**papilloedema**) mark malignant hypertension; Wong-Mitchell calls these moderate and malignant. [Kanski 10e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history`,points:[`**Headache, vomiting, confusion, seizures and visual blurring** suggest hypertensive encephalopathy or PRES; focal weakness or speech loss suggests stroke. [ESC 2024]`,`**Chest pain** - pressing (ACS) or **tearing, maximal at onset, radiating to the back** (aortic dissection). [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**Breathlessness, orthopnoea and frothy sputum** suggest acute pulmonary oedema. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**Reduced urine output, haematuria or frothy urine** suggest AKI or glomerulonephritis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Paroxysms of headache, palpitations and sweating** suggest phaeochromocytoma; ask about cocaine, amphetamines, decongestants and MAOIs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy status** in every woman of reproductive age, including up to 6 weeks postpartum - headache, epigastric pain or visual disturbance mean severe pre-eclampsia. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Drug and adherence history**: which antihypertensives, when last taken, recent abrupt stopping (clonidine, beta-blocker), NSAIDs, steroids and herbal products. [ESC 2024]`,`Many with severe BP have **no symptoms** or only non-specific headache, nosebleed or anxiety - these are not organ damage and do not by themselves make an emergency. [AHA/ACC 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Confirm BP** in both arms with the right cuff after 5-10 minutes of rest in a quiet room - up to a third fall substantially with rest alone. [ESC 2024]`,`**Inter-arm difference over 20 mmHg**, pulse deficit or a new aortic regurgitation murmur suggests **aortic dissection**. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**Fundoscopy** (dilate if needed): flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots and **papilloedema** confirm malignant hypertension. [ESC 2024]`,`**Neurological examination**: GCS, focal deficit, neck stiffness, cortical blindness (PRES) and seizures. [ESC 2024]`,`**Cardiovascular**: crackles, S3, raised JVP (pulmonary oedema); signs of LVH. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Radio-femoral delay** in a young patient suggests coarctation; renal bruit suggests renovascular disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Volume status**: many are volume depleted from pressure natriuresis - dry mucosa and postural fall argue against diuretics. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: fundal height, oedema, hyperreflexia and clonus (impending eclampsia), right upper quadrant tenderness (HELLP). [ACOG 2020]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: is the BP the cause or the response?`,points:[`**Pain, anxiety, panic attack and urinary retention** raise BP without organ damage - treat the cause, recheck after analgesia or catheterisation. [ESC 2024]`,`**Raised intracranial pressure** (Cushing reflex: hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) - lowering BP reduces cerebral perfusion; manage the ICP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute ischaemic stroke**: BP rises as a protective response; lower only above **220/120**, or below 185/110 if thrombolysis is planned. [AHA/ASA 2019]`,`**Alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal** and **sympathomimetic toxicity**: benzodiazepines first; avoid pure beta-blockers in cocaine toxicity. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Hypoglycaemia and hypoxia** can cause confusion with high BP - check glucose and SpO2 before labelling encephalopathy. [ESC 2024]`,`**Severe hypertension without organ damage vs emergency**: decided by examination, fundus, ECG, troponin, creatinine and urine - not by the BP number. [AHA/ACC 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations: first line and advanced`,points:[`**Bedside**: capillary glucose, ECG (ischaemia, LVH), urine dipstick (protein, blood), pregnancy test, SpO2 and fundoscopy. [ESC 2024]`,`**Bloods**: haemoglobin, **platelets**, creatinine/eGFR, sodium and potassium, **troponin**, and NT-proBNP if breathless. [ESC 2024]`,`**TMA screen**: peripheral smear for **schistocytes**, LDH (raised), haptoglobin (low), platelets (low) - suspect when anaemia and AKI accompany malignant hypertension. [ESC 2024]`,`**Urine**: albumin-creatinine ratio and microscopy for **RBC casts** (glomerulonephritis); toxicology screen if sympathomimetic use is suspected. [ESC 2024]`,`**Chest X-ray** for pulmonary oedema and a widened mediastinum (only suggestive; normal X-ray does not exclude dissection). [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**CT brain** for stroke or haemorrhage; **MRI (FLAIR)** shows parieto-occipital vasogenic oedema in **PRES**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CT aortic angiography** (or transoesophageal echo) when dissection is suspected; **echocardiography** for LV function and regional wall motion. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**After stabilisation**: plasma free metanephrines, aldosterone-renin ratio, renal artery imaging and renal ultrasound to look for a secondary cause. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: severe hypertension without organ damage`,points:[`**Rest the patient 30 minutes** in a quiet room and remeasure; treat pain or anxiety; confirm there is **no acute organ damage** (symptoms, fundus, ECG, urine, creatinine if available). [ESC 2024]`,`**Restart or intensify oral therapy**; the aim is BP under about **160/100 over several hours**, then to target over days to weeks - there is no benefit from rapid lowering. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Oral options**: **amlodipine 5-10 mg** once daily, **labetalol 200 mg** (repeat in 2-3 hours if needed), **captopril 12.5-25 mg** (check potassium and creatinine), or **nifedipine extended-release 20-30 mg**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Clonidine 0.1-0.2 mg** oral can be used, but warn about sedation and rebound hypertension if stopped abruptly. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Never give sublingual or immediate-release nifedipine capsules** to an adult with hypertensive crisis - the uncontrolled fall causes stroke, MI and death. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Do not send home on a new single high dose** without a plan; prefer a **single-pill combination** (ARB plus CCB) and review within **1 week** (24-72 hours if high risk). [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**Address the precipitant**: adherence barriers, cost (Jan Aushadhi, IHCI free drugs), NSAIDs, decongestants, alcohol and salt. [IHCI 2022]`,`**Refer the same day** if any organ-damage symptom or sign appears, BP stays 180/110 or more after oral drugs with symptoms, or the patient is pregnant. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency: hypertensive emergency`,points:[`**Admit to ICU/HDU**, IV access, continuous ECG, **intra-arterial line** for nitroprusside or rapid titration, and hourly urine output. [ESC 2024]`,`**General rule** (no compelling condition): lower **MAP by no more than 20-25% in the first hour**, then to **160/100-110 over the next 2-6 hours**, then to normal over **24-48 hours**. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Compelling conditions - faster targets**: aortic dissection **SBP under 120 and HR under 60 within 20 minutes**; ACS and pulmonary oedema **SBP under 140 immediately**; severe pre-eclampsia **under 160/105**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Labetalol IV** is the first-line workhorse: **20 mg over 2 minutes, then 20-80 mg every 10 minutes to a maximum of 300 mg**, or infusion 1-2 mg/min. [ESC 2024]`,`**Nicardipine IV 5 mg/h**, increased by 2.5 mg/h every 5-15 minutes to **15 mg/h**, is the alternative (preferred in asthma or bradycardia). [ESC 2024]`,`**Sodium nitroprusside 0.25-0.5 mcg/kg/min** (maximum 10 mcg/kg/min for no more than 10 minutes) - potent and titratable, but risks **cyanide toxicity** in renal or hepatic failure and raises intracranial pressure. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Nitroglycerin IV 5-10 mcg/min**, increased every 3-5 minutes (usually up to 200 mcg/min), is first choice in **ACS and pulmonary oedema**; avoid after PDE-5 inhibitors. [ESC 2024]`,`**Volume**: many patients are depleted; give IV saline cautiously if BP falls too fast and **avoid diuretics** unless there is pulmonary oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Switch to oral drugs** once BP is stable for 12-24 hours, overlapping before stopping the infusion; screen for secondary causes before discharge. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Acute ischaemic stroke**: for thrombolysis lower to **under 185/110 before** and keep **under 180/105 for 24 hours**; otherwise treat only if **over 220/120**, lowering by about 15% in the first 24 hours. [AHA/ASA 2019]`,`**Intracerebral haemorrhage**: with SBP 150-220 lower to a target of about **140 mmHg (range 130-150) within an hour**, and avoid dropping below 130. [AHA/ASA 2022]`,`**Severe pre-eclampsia** (160/110 or more for 15 minutes): treat within **30-60 minutes** with IV labetalol, oral nifedipine immediate-release **10 mg (swallowed, not sublingual)** or IV hydralazine 5-10 mg, plus **magnesium sulphate**. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Magnesium sulphate (Pritchard)**: **4 g IV over 5 minutes plus 10 g IM** (5 g each buttock), then **5 g IM every 4 hours** for 24 hours; check knee jerks, respiratory rate and urine output; antidote **calcium gluconate 1 g IV**. [MoHFW 2021]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma crisis**: **phentolamine 5 mg IV** repeated every 10 minutes (or nicardipine/nitroprusside); a beta-blocker only **after** alpha-blockade, never alone. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Cocaine or amphetamine**: benzodiazepines first, then nitroglycerin, phentolamine or a CCB; avoid unopposed beta-blockade. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Scleroderma renal crisis** is the exception where an **ACE inhibitor (captopril)** is the drug of choice, even with rising creatinine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: lower BP by no more than **25% of the planned reduction in the first 8 hours** with IV labetalol, nicardipine or hydralazine, and look for renal causes and coarctation. [AAP 2017]`,`**Resource-limited hospital without pumps**: IV labetalol boluses or oral regimens with close monitoring and early referral; never sublingual nifedipine. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Of the emergency**: stroke, intracerebral haemorrhage, MI, pulmonary oedema, dissection rupture, AKI needing dialysis, blindness and eclamptic fits. [ESC 2024]`,`**Of over-treatment**: watershed cerebral infarction, anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy, MI and renal hypoperfusion from too rapid a fall. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Of drugs**: nitroprusside cyanide or thiocyanate toxicity (metabolic acidosis, confusion), labetalol bronchospasm and heart block, hydralazine reflex tachycardia and angina, nicardipine headache and tachycardia. [ACC/AHA 2017]`,`**Magnesium toxicity**: loss of knee jerks at about 7-10 mEq/L, respiratory depression at higher levels - stop infusion and give calcium gluconate. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Prognosis**: patients surviving a hypertensive emergency have high rates of recurrent admission, CKD progression, cardiovascular events and death over the following years - long-term BP control is the main modifier. [ESC 2024]`,`**Renal recovery** in malignant nephrosclerosis may continue for months after BP control, so early dialysis dependence is not always permanent. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**After an emergency**: review within **1-2 weeks** of discharge, then monthly until controlled; creatinine and potassium at each change of RAS blocker or diuretic. [ESC 2024]`,`**Search for secondary hypertension** in every malignant hypertension, young patient, resistant hypertension or recurrent crisis - see the Secondary and resistant hypertension topic. [ESC 2024]`,`**After severe hypertension without organ damage**: review within days to a week, then every 2-4 weeks while titrating; home BP monitoring. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**Annual organ-damage review**: fundus, ECG or echo for LVH, UACR and eGFR. [ESC 2024]`,`**After pre-eclampsia**: BP check at **3-7 days postpartum** and at 6 weeks, then yearly lifelong CVD risk review. [ACOG 2020]`,`**Refer** to nephrology for TMA or persisting AKI, to cardiology after ACS or dissection, and to endocrinology for suspected phaeochromocytoma or aldosteronism. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and national programme link`,points:[`Most crises are preventable: **diagnose early (NP-NCD screening at 30+)** and keep patients on treatment through IHCI follow-up. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Adherence counselling**: never stop BP drugs because BP is 'normal' or headache is gone; carry a treatment card; use single-pill combinations. [IHCI 2022]`,`**Free and low-cost drugs** through Ayushman Arogya Mandir and Jan Aushadhi Kendras reduce cost-related stopping. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Warn about precipitants**: NSAIDs, cold remedies with decongestants, stopping clonidine suddenly, binge alcohol and stimulant drugs. [ESC 2024]`,`**Antenatal care**: BP and urine protein at every visit, **low-dose aspirin 150 mg at night** from 12-16 weeks for high-risk women, and calcium where intake is low. [FOGSI 2023]`,`**Teach warning symptoms**: severe headache, visual loss, chest pain, breathlessness, weakness or confusion with high BP mean go to hospital now. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AHA/ACC 2025**: 'hypertensive urgency' is replaced by **severe hypertension (over 180/120) without acute target organ damage**, managed with oral drug intensification and early follow-up, not ED IV therapy. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**ESC 2024 hypertension guideline**: keeps 180/110 as the threshold, MAP reduction of 20-25% over hours, and organ-specific targets; labetalol and nicardipine are first-line IV drugs. [ESC 2024]`,`**ESC 2024 aortic guideline**: dissection target SBP **100-120 mmHg and HR under 60** with IV beta-blocker first. [ESC 2024 aorta]`,`**AHA/ASA 2022 ICH guideline**: target SBP about 140 (130-150) in mild-moderate ICH; avoid under 130; **INTERACT3 (2023)** showed a care bundle with early BP control improved outcomes. [AHA/ASA 2022]`,`**INTERACT4 (2024)**: prehospital BP lowering in undifferentiated stroke gave no overall benefit - harm in ischaemic stroke, benefit in haemorrhage - so imaging should come first. [INTERACT4 2024]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline and CHAP (2022)**: treat pregnancy BP from 140/90; 160/110 or more is an emergency needing treatment within an hour. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: '**Define hypertensive emergency and urgency; outline management**' - definitions, organ list, general rule, condition-wise targets table, drug table.`,`Short-note favourites: **malignant hypertension**, **sodium nitroprusside**, **labetalol**, **hypertensive encephalopathy/PRES**.`,`Viva: why not sublingual nifedipine? Unpredictable precipitous fall below the shifted autoregulatory limit causes stroke and MI.`,`Viva: in aortic dissection, why beta-blocker before vasodilator? A vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia and raises dP/dt, extending the tear.`,`Viva: phaeochromocytoma - **alpha before beta**; scleroderma renal crisis - **ACE inhibitor**; cocaine - **benzodiazepine first**.`,`Common mistake: treating the number in asymptomatic severe hypertension with IV drugs.`,`Common mistake: lowering BP aggressively in acute ischaemic stroke not eligible for thrombolysis.`,`Common mistake: forgetting fundoscopy and pregnancy test in a hypertensive crisis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Definitions in hypertensive crisis`,columns:[`Term`,`BP`,`Organ damage`,`Setting and treatment`],rows:[[`Hypertensive emergency`,`Usually 180/110 (ESC) or over 180/120 (ACC/AHA)`,`Acute, new or worsening`,`ICU/HDU, titratable IV drugs`],[`Severe hypertension without organ damage (urgency)`,`Same range`,`None acute`,`Oral drugs, review within days`],[`Malignant (accelerated) hypertension`,`Severe, often diastolic over 120-130`,`Grade III-IV retinopathy, often TMA and AKI`,`Emergency; IV labetalol or nicardipine over hours`],[`Hypertensive encephalopathy`,`Severe`,`Headache, confusion, seizures, PRES`,`Emergency; MAP down 20-25% immediately`],[`Severe pre-eclampsia`,`160/110 or more after 20 weeks`,`Proteinuria or organ dysfunction`,`Treat within 30-60 min plus MgSO4; deliver`]]},{heading:`BP target and drug of choice by condition`,columns:[`Condition`,`Timeline and target`,`First-line drug`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`No compelling condition / malignant HT`,`MAP down 20-25% in first hours; 160/100 by 2-6 h`,`Labetalol, nicardipine`,`Rapid falls; sublingual nifedipine`],[`Hypertensive encephalopathy`,`MAP down 20-25% immediately`,`Labetalol, nicardipine`,`Nitroprusside if raised ICP`],[`Aortic dissection`,`SBP under 120 (100-120), HR under 60 within 20 min`,`Esmolol or labetalol, then nitroprusside/nicardipine`,`Vasodilator before beta-blocker`],[`ACS`,`SBP under 140 immediately`,`Nitroglycerin, labetalol`,`Hydralazine (reflex tachycardia)`],[`Acute pulmonary oedema`,`SBP under 140 immediately`,`Nitroglycerin or nitroprusside plus loop diuretic`,`Beta-blockers acutely`],[`Ischaemic stroke`,`Lysis: under 185/110, then 180/105 for 24 h; no lysis: treat only over 220/120, 15% in 24 h`,`Labetalol, nicardipine`,`Aggressive lowering`],[`Intracerebral haemorrhage`,`SBP about 140 (130-150) within 1 h`,`Labetalol, nicardipine`,`SBP under 130`],[`Severe pre-eclampsia / eclampsia`,`Under 160/105-110 within 1 h`,`Labetalol IV, oral nifedipine IR, hydralazine plus MgSO4`,`ACE inhibitor, nitroprusside`],[`Phaeochromocytoma / sympathomimetic`,`SBP under 140 in first hour`,`Phentolamine; benzodiazepine for cocaine`,`Beta-blocker alone`]]},{heading:`IV and oral drugs in hypertensive crisis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Labetalol`,`20 mg IV over 2 min, then 20-80 mg every 10 min (max 300 mg) or 1-2 mg/min infusion`,`Onset 5-10 min, lasts 3-6 h`,`Bronchospasm, bradycardia, heart block; avoid in asthma, acute HF, cocaine`],[`Nicardipine`,`5 mg/h IV, raise 2.5 mg/h every 5-15 min to 15 mg/h`,`Onset 5-15 min; offset 30-40 min`,`Headache, reflex tachycardia; caution in severe AS`],[`Clevidipine`,`1-2 mg/h IV, double every 90 s, usual 4-6 mg/h (max 32 mg/h)`,`Onset 2-4 min; offset 5-15 min`,`Lipid emulsion - avoid in soy or egg allergy; limited availability in India`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`0.25-0.5 mcg/kg/min IV, up to 10 mcg/kg/min for 10 min max`,`Seconds; arterial line; protect from light`,`Cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity, raises ICP, coronary steal; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`5-10 mcg/min IV, raise every 3-5 min (usually to 200 mcg/min)`,`Onset 2-5 min`,`Headache, tolerance; avoid with PDE-5 inhibitor, RV infarct`],[`Esmolol`,`500 mcg/kg IV over 1 min, then 50-200 mcg/kg/min`,`Onset 1-2 min; offset 10-30 min`,`Bradycardia, heart block, bronchospasm, HF`],[`Phentolamine`,`5 mg IV bolus, repeat every 10 min as needed`,`Onset 1-2 min`,`Tachycardia, angina; for catecholamine crises`],[`Hydralazine`,`5-10 mg IV, repeat every 20-30 min (max 20-30 mg) in pregnancy`,`Onset 10-20 min, unpredictable`,`Reflex tachycardia, headache, maternal hypotension`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`4 g IV over 5 min + 10 g IM, then 5 g IM 4-hourly (Pritchard)`,`24 h after last fit or delivery`,`Loss of reflexes, respiratory depression; antidote calcium gluconate`],[`Oral for severe HT without damage`,`Amlodipine 5-10 mg, labetalol 200 mg, captopril 12.5-25 mg, nifedipine ER 20-30 mg`,`Review in days; titrate weekly`,`Never sublingual or IR nifedipine capsule in adults`]]},{heading:`Investigations in hypertensive crisis`,columns:[`Test`,`Looking for`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Fundoscopy`,`Malignant retinopathy`,`Flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, papilloedema = emergency`],[`ECG and troponin`,`ACS, LVH`,`ST change or troponin rise = cardiac organ damage`],[`Creatinine, electrolytes, urine dipstick and microscopy`,`AKI, glomerulonephritis`,`Rising creatinine, RBC casts, proteinuria; low K suggests aldosteronism`],[`Haemoglobin, platelets, smear, LDH, haptoglobin`,`Thrombotic microangiopathy`,`Schistocytes, low platelets, high LDH, low haptoglobin`],[`CT brain / MRI`,`Stroke, ICH, PRES`,`Parieto-occipital FLAIR oedema in PRES`],[`CT aortic angiography`,`Dissection`,`Intimal flap; type A surgical, type B mostly medical`],[`Chest X-ray, NT-proBNP, echo`,`Pulmonary oedema, LV function`,`Bat-wing shadowing, raised NT-proBNP`],[`Pregnancy test and urine protein`,`Pre-eclampsia`,`Positive with BP 160/110 = obstetric emergency`]]},{heading:`Look-alikes: BP surge as a response`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Pain or anxiety`,`BP falls after analgesia or reassurance`,`Treat cause, recheck after 30 minutes`],[`Urinary retention`,`Palpable bladder, elderly man`,`Catheterise, then recheck`],[`Raised intracranial pressure`,`Bradycardia, irregular breathing, falling GCS`,`Treat ICP; do not drop BP`],[`Acute ischaemic stroke`,`Focal deficit, CT normal early`,`Permissive hypertension unless lysis or over 220/120`],[`Alcohol withdrawal`,`Tremor, sweating, 6-48 h after last drink`,`Benzodiazepines and thiamine`],[`Hypoglycaemia or hypoxia`,`Low glucose or SpO2 with confusion`,`Correct first, then reassess`]]}],redFlags:[`BP 180/110 or more with headache, vomiting, confusion, seizures or visual loss - hypertensive encephalopathy or stroke.`,`Tearing chest or back pain with BP difference between arms - aortic dissection.`,`Chest pain with ST change or troponin rise, or breathlessness with crackles - ACS or pulmonary oedema.`,`Flame haemorrhages, cotton-wool spots or papilloedema on fundoscopy - malignant hypertension.`,`Pregnant or up to 6 weeks postpartum with BP 160/110 or more, headache, epigastric pain or fits.`,`Rising creatinine, haematuria or low platelets with schistocytes - AKI with thrombotic microangiopathy.`,`Paroxysmal crisis with palpitations and sweating, or after cocaine/amphetamine use.`],pearls:[`Organ damage, not the number, makes an emergency.`,`General rule: MAP down no more than 25% in the first hour, 160/100 by 6 hours, normal over 24-48 hours.`,`Exceptions with faster targets: dissection (SBP under 120 in 20 min), ACS and pulmonary oedema (SBP under 140), pre-eclampsia (under 160/105).`,`Ischaemic stroke is the exception the other way: leave BP alone unless over 220/120 or before lysis (185/110).`,`Never sublingual nifedipine - the shifted autoregulation curve turns a fast fall into a stroke.`,`Dissection: beta-blocker first, vasodilator second.`,`Phaeochromocytoma: alpha before beta; scleroderma renal crisis: ACE inhibitor; cocaine: benzodiazepine first.`,`Nitroprusside: cyanide toxicity and raised ICP - use the lowest dose for the shortest time.`,`Pritchard regimen: 4 g IV + 10 g IM, then 5 g IM 4-hourly; antidote calcium gluconate.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old hypertensive man has sudden tearing interscapular pain, BP 210/110 mmHg in the right arm and 170/90 in the left, and heart rate 104/min. CT angiography confirms type B aortic dissection. Which drug should be started first?`,options:[`IV sodium nitroprusside alone`,`IV hydralazine`,`IV esmolol`,`Sublingual nifedipine`],answer:2,explanation:`In aortic dissection the aim is SBP under 120 and heart rate under 60 within 20 minutes, and a short-acting beta-blocker such as esmolol (or labetalol) is given first to reduce the rate of rise of aortic pressure. Nitroprusside or hydralazine alone causes reflex tachycardia that raises shear stress and can extend the tear; a vasodilator is added only after beta-blockade. Sublingual nifedipine is never appropriate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-q2`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman who ran out of her tablets 2 weeks ago has BP 196/118 mmHg after 30 minutes of rest. She has no symptoms, normal fundi, a normal ECG and no proteinuria. What is the best management?`,options:[`Admit for IV labetalol infusion`,`Give sublingual nifedipine 10 mg and recheck`,`Lower BP to 120/80 within 1 hour with IV nicardipine`,`Restart and intensify oral therapy with review within a week`],answer:3,explanation:`This is severe hypertension without acute organ damage (formerly urgency): there is no benefit from rapid lowering, and AHA/ACC 2025 advises restarting or intensifying oral drugs with early outpatient follow-up. IV labetalol or nicardipine is for emergencies with organ damage, lowering to 120/80 in an hour risks cerebral ischaemia, and sublingual nifedipine causes unpredictable dangerous falls.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-q3`,stem:`A 66-year-old man with acute ischaemic stroke presents within 2 hours and is a candidate for IV thrombolysis. His BP is 196/112 mmHg. To what level must BP be lowered before thrombolysis?`,options:[`Below 185/110 mmHg`,`Below 140/90 mmHg`,`Below 160/100 mmHg`,`No lowering is needed before thrombolysis`],answer:0,explanation:`AHA/ASA requires BP under 185/110 before IV thrombolysis and under 180/105 for 24 hours afterwards, to limit haemorrhagic transformation. Lowering to 140/90 or 160/100 risks extending the ischaemic penumbra, and thrombolysis cannot be given safely at 196/112 without lowering; permissive hypertension up to 220/120 applies only when lysis is not planned.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-q4`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman with episodic headache, sweating and palpitations presents with BP 240/130 mmHg during an attack. Phaeochromocytoma is suspected. The most appropriate initial IV drug is:`,options:[`Metoprolol`,`Phentolamine`,`Furosemide`,`Enalaprilat`],answer:1,explanation:`A catecholamine crisis needs alpha-blockade, and phentolamine 5 mg IV repeated every 10 minutes (or nicardipine or nitroprusside) is the drug of choice. A beta-blocker such as metoprolol given alone leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can worsen hypertension; it is added only after alpha-blockade. Furosemide worsens the volume depletion typical of phaeochromocytoma, and enalaprilat does not target the mechanism.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-q5`,stem:`A 42-year-old woman with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis develops headache, BP 200/115 mmHg, rising creatinine and schistocytes on the smear. The drug of choice is:`,options:[`IV labetalol`,`Oral amlodipine`,`Plasma exchange alone`,`Oral captopril`],answer:3,explanation:`Scleroderma renal crisis is driven by renin, and an ACE inhibitor such as captopril, titrated up even as creatinine rises, is the drug of choice and has transformed survival. Labetalol or amlodipine lower BP but do not block the renin-mediated process, and plasma exchange is not the primary treatment because this TMA is caused by scleroderma, not TTP.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define hypertensive emergency and hypertensive urgency. Discuss the evaluation and management of a hypertensive emergency, with drug choice and BP targets in specific situations.`,openingLines:[`A hypertensive emergency is severe BP elevation (usually 180/110-120 mmHg or more) with acute hypertension-mediated organ damage, needing admission and titratable IV drugs.`,`Severe hypertension without acute organ damage (formerly urgency) is treated with oral drugs over hours to days; the presence of organ damage, not the BP level, separates the two.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions and burden`,points:[`ESC 180/110, ACC/AHA over 180/120; AHA/ACC 2025 renames urgency as severe hypertension without acute organ damage.`,`Malignant hypertension: grade III-IV retinopathy, often TMA and AKI; commonest precipitant is non-adherence.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Endothelial injury, fibrinoid necrosis, TMA, RAAS activation and pressure natriuresis.`,`Rightward shift of autoregulation - reason for controlled lowering.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`Symptoms by organ; BP both arms; fundus; neuro exam; pregnancy test.`,`ECG, troponin, creatinine, electrolytes, urine microscopy, platelets, smear, LDH; CT brain or CT aorta as needed.`]},{heading:`General management`,points:[`ICU, arterial line; MAP down no more than 20-25% in first hour, 160/100 by 2-6 h, normal over 24-48 h.`,`IV labetalol 20 mg then 20-80 mg every 10 min (max 300 mg) or nicardipine 5-15 mg/h.`]},{heading:`Condition-specific targets`,points:[`Dissection SBP under 120, HR under 60 in 20 min (esmolol first); ACS and pulmonary oedema SBP under 140 (nitroglycerin).`,`Ischaemic stroke: under 185/110 before lysis, otherwise only over 220/120; ICH SBP about 140.`,`Pre-eclampsia under 160/105 with MgSO4; phaeochromocytoma phentolamine.`]},{heading:`Pitfalls and follow-up`,points:[`Never sublingual nifedipine; avoid diuretics unless pulmonary oedema.`,`Switch to oral drugs at 12-24 h; screen for secondary causes; review in 1-2 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: condition - target - drug of choice`,`Diagram: cerebral autoregulation curve shifted to the right in chronic hypertension`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`General principles and drugs`,marks:3},{part:`Condition-specific targets`,marks:3}],keywords:[`hypertensive emergency`,`organ damage`,`autoregulation`,`labetalol`,`nicardipine`,`nitroprusside`,`esmolol`,`MAP 25%`,`sublingual nifedipine`]},{id:`cardiovascular-hypertensive-crisis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for the management of a patient presenting with BP 200/120 mmHg.`,openingLines:[`The first decision is whether there is acute organ damage; this, not the BP number, separates an emergency from severe hypertension without organ damage.`,`Confirm the reading after rest, then examine the fundus, neurology, heart and urine before choosing IV or oral treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1 - confirm and assess`,points:[`Repeat BP after 30 minutes of rest; treat pain, anxiety, retention.`,`Symptoms, fundus, neuro exam, ECG, troponin, creatinine, urine, pregnancy test.`]},{heading:`Step 2a - organ damage present`,points:[`Admit ICU; IV labetalol or nicardipine; MAP down 20-25% in first hour.`,`Apply compelling-condition targets: dissection, ACS, pulmonary oedema, stroke, eclampsia, phaeochromocytoma.`]},{heading:`Step 2b - no organ damage`,points:[`Restart or intensify oral drugs (amlodipine, labetalol, captopril, nifedipine ER).`,`Review within a week; never sublingual nifedipine.`]}],mustDraw:[`Branching flow chart: BP 200/120 - organ damage? - yes: IV drugs and ICU - no: oral drugs and early review`],markSplit:[{part:`Assessment step`,marks:2},{part:`Emergency arm`,marks:2},{part:`Non-emergency arm`,marks:1}],keywords:[`flow chart`,`organ damage`,`IV labetalol`,`oral therapy`,`follow-up`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Hypertensive emergencies`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension`,`2025 AHA/ACC/multisociety Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults; 2017 ACC/AHA hypertension guideline (emergency drug table)`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of peripheral arterial and aortic diseases`,`AHA/ASA Guidelines for early management of acute ischaemic stroke, 2019, and spontaneous intracerebral haemorrhage, 2022`,`ACOG Practice Bulletin 222: Gestational hypertension and pre-eclampsia, 2020; MoHFW guidance on management of pre-eclampsia and eclampsia`,`India Hypertension Control Initiative (ICMR/MoHFW) treatment protocols, 2022`]}),t.push({id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn`,title:`Secondary hypertension and resistant hypertension`,oneLiner:`**Secondary hypertension** has an identifiable, often curable cause - commonest **renal parenchymal disease** and **primary aldosteronism** (now screened in all hypertensives by the **aldosterone-renin ratio**, Endocrine Society 2025), then renovascular disease, OSA, phaeochromocytoma, Cushing, coarctation and drugs; **resistant hypertension** is BP **140/90 or more despite three drugs including a diuretic at maximal tolerated doses**, confirmed out of office with adherence checked, and its fourth drug is **spironolactone (PATHWAY-2)**, with renal denervation and aprocitentan beyond.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`secondary hypertension`,`resistant hypertension`,`refractory hypertension`,`pseudo-resistance`,`primary aldosteronism`,`Conn syndrome`,`aldosterone-renin ratio`,`renovascular hypertension`,`fibromuscular dysplasia`,`Takayasu arteritis`,`phaeochromocytoma`,`metanephrines`,`obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Cushing syndrome`,`coarctation of aorta`,`Liddle syndrome`,`spironolactone`,`PATHWAY-2`,`renal denervation`,`aprocitentan`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Secondary hypertension** is raised BP due to an identifiable cause; it accounts for about **5-15%** of all hypertension, and a higher share in the young and in resistant hypertension. [ESC 2024]`,`**Primary aldosteronism (PA)** is the commonest curable cause: about **5-10% of all hypertensives** and about **15-20% of resistant hypertension**, yet under 1-2% are ever diagnosed. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Renal parenchymal disease** (CKD, glomerulonephritis, ADPKD) is the commonest secondary cause overall, and in India CKD affects about 1 in 6 adults. [SEEK-India 2013]`,`**Resistant hypertension**: office BP **140/90 or more despite optimal doses of three drugs including a diuretic** (usually a RAS blocker, CCB and thiazide-like diuretic), with **adherence confirmed** and **out-of-office BP** also high. [ESC 2024]`,`**Apparent** resistance includes pseudo-resistance; **true** resistance remains after excluding it; **controlled resistant** hypertension needs four or more drugs to reach target; **refractory** hypertension stays uncontrolled on **five or more drugs including chlorthalidone and an MRA**. [AHA 2018]`,`About **10% of treated hypertensives** appear resistant, but half or more of these have **pseudo-resistance** (poor adherence, white-coat effect, poor technique, under-dosing). [ESC 2024]`,`Resistant hypertension carries about **1.5-2 times** the risk of cardiovascular events and CKD progression compared with controlled hypertension. [AHA 2018]`,`**India**: in young Indians **Takayasu arteritis** is a leading cause of renovascular hypertension, and renal parenchymal disease dominates secondary causes in children. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology: causes of secondary and resistant hypertension`,points:[`**Renal**: CKD of any cause, glomerulonephritis, diabetic kidney disease, **ADPKD**, reflux nephropathy, obstructive uropathy. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Renovascular**: **atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis** (about 90%, older smokers, diabetics) and **fibromuscular dysplasia** (young women), plus **Takayasu arteritis** in India. [ESC 2024]`,`**Endocrine**: **primary aldosteronism**, phaeochromocytoma/paraganglioma, Cushing syndrome, thyroid disease (hyper - systolic; hypo - diastolic), primary hyperparathyroidism, acromegaly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstructive sleep apnoea**: found in over half of patients with resistant hypertension; causes nocturnal and non-dipping hypertension. [ESC 2024]`,`**Vascular**: **coarctation of the aorta** (see the Congenital heart disease topic) and aortitis. [ESC 2024]`,`**Drugs and substances**: NSAIDs, oral contraceptives (oestrogen), steroids, **decongestants**, venlafaxine, ciclosporin, tacrolimus, erythropoietin, **VEGF inhibitors**, liquorice, cocaine, amphetamines and heavy alcohol. [ESC 2024]`,`**Monogenic (rare, young, family history)**: **Liddle syndrome** (ENaC gain of function), glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (FH-I), apparent mineralocorticoid excess, Gordon syndrome, congenital adrenal hyperplasia (11-beta and 17-alpha hydroxylase deficiency). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contributors to resistance**: **high salt intake**, obesity, alcohol, physical inactivity, CKD, OSA, older age, black ethnicity and diabetes. [AHA 2018]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: the renin-aldosterone pattern explains the cause`,points:[`**Primary aldosteronism**: autonomous aldosterone (adenoma or bilateral hyperplasia) acts on the mineralocorticoid receptor to open **ENaC**, retaining sodium and losing potassium and hydrogen; volume expansion **suppresses renin**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Aldosterone also causes **fibrosis of heart, kidney and vessels** independent of BP, so PA has more AF, stroke, LVH and CKD than essential hypertension of the same BP. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Aldosterone escape**: after a few days natriuretic peptides and pressure natriuresis limit sodium retention, so **oedema is absent** in PA while hypokalaemia continues. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renovascular hypertension**: reduced renal perfusion triggers **renin release** (Goldblatt kidney) - high renin and high aldosterone; ACE inhibitors drop GFR in bilateral stenosis because efferent tone maintains filtration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma**: catecholamine release causes paroxysmal or sustained hypertension, tachycardia, sweating, hyperglycaemia and **volume contraction** (hence orthostatic hypotension). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low renin, low aldosterone** hypertension means a non-aldosterone mineralocorticoid effect: **Liddle syndrome**, apparent mineralocorticoid excess or **liquorice** (both block 11-beta-HSD2 so cortisol acts on the MR), Cushing, CAH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**OSA**: intermittent hypoxia and arousals drive sympathetic surges, endothelial dysfunction and aldosterone excess; fluid shifts from the legs to the neck at night worsen obstruction. [ESC 2024]`,`**Why spironolactone works in resistant hypertension**: most resistant hypertension is **salt-and-volume driven with relative aldosterone excess**, so blocking the MR (or ENaC with amiloride) gives the largest fall. [PATHWAY-2 2015]`,`**Endothelin-1** is a potent vasoconstrictor raised in salt-sensitive, low-renin hypertension; **aprocitentan** blocks ET-A and ET-B receptors - a new pathway beyond RAAS and volume. [PRECISION 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification: resistant hypertension and renin-aldosterone patterns`,points:[`**Apparent resistant hypertension**: uncontrolled on three drugs before excluding pseudo-resistance. [AHA 2018]`,`**True resistant hypertension**: confirmed by ABPM (24-h mean 130/80 or more) or home BP (135/85 or more) with adherence verified. [ESC 2024]`,`**Refractory hypertension**: uncontrolled on **five or more drugs including chlorthalidone and spironolactone** - often sympathetically driven. [AHA 2018]`,`**High renin, high aldosterone (secondary aldosteronism)**: renovascular disease, diuretics, coarctation, renin-secreting tumour, malignant hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low renin, high aldosterone**: **primary aldosteronism**. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Low renin, low aldosterone**: Liddle syndrome, apparent mineralocorticoid excess, liquorice, Cushing, CAH, deoxycorticosterone excess. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary aldosteronism subtypes**: aldosterone-producing adenoma (unilateral, surgical) about a third, and **bilateral adrenal hyperplasia** (medical) about two-thirds; rarely unilateral hyperplasia, carcinoma or familial forms. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: when to suspect secondary hypertension`,points:[`**Onset under 40 years** (especially under 30 without obesity or family history), or new hypertension after 60 that is severe. [ESC 2024]`,`**Resistant hypertension**, **sudden deterioration** of previously controlled BP, or a **hypertensive emergency/malignant hypertension**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Spontaneous or diuretic-induced hypokalaemia** (muscle weakness, cramps, polyuria) - but **hypokalaemia is absent in most PA**, so its absence does not exclude it. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Paroxysms of headache, sweating and palpitations**, pallor, tremor, or BP surges with anaesthesia, micturition or abdominal palpation suggest phaeochromocytoma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flash pulmonary oedema**, a **creatinine rise over 30% after an ACE inhibitor/ARB**, or unexplained kidney asymmetry suggest renovascular disease. [ESC 2024]`,`**Snoring, witnessed apnoeas, daytime sleepiness**, morning headache and nocturia suggest OSA. [ESC 2024]`,`**Weight gain, proximal weakness, easy bruising, purple striae** suggest Cushing; **atrial fibrillation** with hypertension in a young person suggests PA. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Adrenal incidentaloma**, early-onset stroke in family members (GRA), and **disproportionate organ damage** for the BP level also prompt screening. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Correct BP technique** and an appropriately sized cuff - a small cuff on a large arm overestimates BP (pseudo-resistance). [ESC 2024]`,`**Radio-femoral delay**, weak femoral pulses and **arm-leg systolic gradient over 20 mmHg** suggest coarctation; scapular collaterals and an interscapular murmur support it. [ESC 2024]`,`**Absent or unequal upper-limb pulses**, carotid or subclavian bruits in a young woman suggest **Takayasu arteritis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Epigastric or flank bruit** (systolic-diastolic) suggests renal artery stenosis. [ESC 2024]`,`**Cushingoid habitus**: moon face, dorsocervical pad, violaceous striae over 1 cm, thin skin, proximal myopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurofibromas and cafe-au-lait spots** (NF1), retinal angiomas (von Hippel-Lindau) point to phaeochromocytoma syndromes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpable kidneys** (ADPKD), **thyroid** signs, **acromegalic** features, and neck circumference over 40 cm with a crowded oropharynx (OSA). [ESC 2024]`,`**Postural drop** in a hypertensive with paroxysms suggests phaeochromocytoma (volume contraction). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis: pseudo-resistance versus true resistance`,points:[`**Non-adherence** is the commonest cause: urine or blood drug screens show partial or complete non-adherence in **up to half** of apparently resistant patients. [ESC 2024]`,`**White-coat effect**: office BP high but ABPM or home BP controlled - confirm every case out of office before escalating. [ESC 2024]`,`**Measurement error**: small cuff, no rest, talking, arm unsupported; **pseudohypertension** from calcified arteries in the elderly (Osler sign). [ESC 2024]`,`**Suboptimal regimen**: no diuretic, low doses, short-acting drugs, a loop diuretic in normal renal function, or **hydrochlorothiazide instead of chlorthalidone/indapamide**. [AHA 2018]`,`**Interfering substances**: NSAIDs, decongestants, oral contraceptives, steroids, alcohol, liquorice and stimulants. [ESC 2024]`,`**Clinical inertia**: failure to up-titrate at each visit is a major cause of apparent resistance in Indian primary care. [IHCI 2022]`,`**True resistant hypertension** remains after correcting these - then search for secondary causes, especially PA and OSA. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations: screening and confirmation by cause`,points:[`**Baseline for all**: creatinine/eGFR, **sodium and potassium**, urine dipstick and albumin-creatinine ratio, glucose, lipids, TSH, ECG; **ABPM** to confirm resistance and nocturnal pattern. [ESC 2024]`,`**Aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR)**: morning, seated, after correcting hypokalaemia; a positive screen is **suppressed renin with an aldosterone that is not low** (commonly ARR over about 20-30 ng/dL per ng/mL/h with aldosterone 10-15 ng/dL or more; cut-offs vary by assay). [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**ARR interference**: beta-blockers, central agonists and NSAIDs raise ARR (false positive); **diuretics, ACEi/ARB, DHP-CCB and MRAs** lower it (false negative); verapamil, hydralazine and doxazosin interfere least. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Confirm PA** (if not obvious): **saline infusion test** (2 L over 4 h; aldosterone over about 10 ng/dL confirms), oral salt loading or captopril challenge; skip confirmation with spontaneous hypokalaemia, undetectable renin and aldosterone over 20 ng/dL. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Subtype PA**: adrenal CT, then **adrenal vein sampling** (lateralisation) in surgical candidates - CT alone misclassifies many, especially over 35 years. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Renovascular**: renal Doppler (peak systolic velocity over about 200 cm/s, renal-aortic ratio over 3.5), **CT or MR angiography**; catheter angiography for intervention. [ESC 2024]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma**: **plasma free metanephrines** (seated or supine) or **24-h urine fractionated metanephrines**; over 3-4 times the upper limit is near-diagnostic; then CT/MRI and genetic testing. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Cushing**: two of **1 mg overnight dexamethasone suppression** (cortisol over 1.8 mcg/dL fails), late-night salivary cortisol, 24-h urinary free cortisol. [Endocrine Society 2008]`,`**OSA**: STOP-BANG 3 or more, then home sleep study or polysomnography (AHI 15 or more moderate-severe). **Coarctation**: echo, then CT/MR aortography. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: resistant hypertension`,points:[`**Step 1 - exclude pseudo-resistance**: confirm with ABPM/home BP, check technique, ask and verify adherence (pill count, pharmacy refill), remove NSAIDs and decongestants. [ESC 2024]`,`**Step 2 - lifestyle**: salt **under 5 g/day** (the most effective single measure in resistant hypertension), weight loss, alcohol cut, exercise; treat OSA with CPAP. [ESC 2024]`,`**Step 3 - optimise the triple**: RAS blocker plus CCB plus **thiazide-like diuretic** (chlorthalidone 12.5-25 mg or indapamide SR 1.5 mg) at full doses as a **single-pill combination**; loop diuretic if eGFR under 30. [ESC 2024]`,`**Step 4 - add spironolactone 25 mg** daily (to 50 mg) if potassium is under about 4.5-5.0 mmol/L and eGFR over 45; check potassium and creatinine at **1-2 and 4 weeks**. [ESC 2024]`,`**PATHWAY-2**: spironolactone lowered home SBP by about **8.7 mmHg more than placebo** and more than doxazosin or bisoprolol, and was the best drug in about 60% of patients. [PATHWAY-2 2015]`,`**If spironolactone is not tolerated**: eplerenone 50-100 mg (less gynaecomastia), **amiloride 10-20 mg** (as effective in a PATHWAY-2 substudy), doxazosin, or a beta-blocker (bisoprolol) - then clonidine or hydralazine/minoxidil. [ESC 2024]`,`**CKD stage 4**: chlorthalidone still lowers BP (CLICK); potassium binders (patiromer) can enable spironolactone. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Device and new drugs**: **renal denervation** at an experienced centre, and **aprocitentan 12.5 mg** daily where approved, for true resistant hypertension despite the above. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Management of specific secondary causes`,points:[`**Unilateral PA (adenoma)**: **laparoscopic adrenalectomy** - cures hypokalaemia in nearly all and normalises BP in about a third to a half, with improvement in most. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Bilateral PA or non-surgical**: **spironolactone 12.5-25 mg, titrated** (often 50-100 mg) or eplerenone, aiming for a **rise in renin (un-suppression)** and normal potassium. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis**: **medical therapy** (RAS blocker if tolerated, statin, antiplatelet, smoking cessation); stenting did not beat medical therapy in **ASTRAL and CORAL** and is reserved for flash pulmonary oedema, rapidly failing renal function or truly resistant BP. [ESC 2024]`,`**Fibromuscular dysplasia**: **balloon angioplasty without stenting** often cures. **Takayasu**: steroids plus steroid-sparing drugs, with angioplasty or surgery for critical stenosis in quiescent disease. [ESC 2024]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma**: **alpha-blockade first for 10-14 days** (phenoxybenzamine 10 mg twice daily titrated, or doxazosin 2-16 mg daily), high-salt diet and fluids, then a beta-blocker if tachycardic, then laparoscopic adrenalectomy. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**OSA**: CPAP lowers BP modestly (about 2-3 mmHg, more in resistant hypertension and good users) and improves symptoms; weight loss. [ESC 2024]`,`**Cushing**: surgery to the source; **coarctation**: stent or surgical repair when gradient is significant; **Liddle**: amiloride; **GRA**: low-dose dexamethasone; **liquorice**: stop it. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for hypertensive emergency, severe hypokalaemia (under 2.5-3.0 mmol/L or with weakness or arrhythmia), phaeochromocytoma crisis, flash pulmonary oedema or AKI on RAS blockade. [ESC 2024]`,`**Hypokalaemic paralysis in PA**: IV potassium chloride (not in dextrose) with cardiac monitoring, then spironolactone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phaeochromocytoma crisis**: IV phentolamine or nicardipine/nitroprusside, IV fluids; **never a beta-blocker before alpha-blockade**; avoid metoclopramide and glucagon (precipitate crises). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Perioperative phaeochromocytoma**: arterial line, magnesium and short-acting vasodilators during tumour handling; watch for **hypotension and hypoglycaemia after removal**. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Hyperkalaemia on spironolactone**: potassium over 5.5 - halve or stop; over 6.0 or with ECG changes - emergency treatment (calcium gluconate, insulin-dextrose). [ESC 2024]`,`**Post-adrenalectomy for PA**: stop spironolactone and potassium supplements, watch for **hyperkalaemia from contralateral suppression** for weeks. [Endocrine Society 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Young (under 30-40)**: screen widely - renal disease, PA, renovascular (FMD, Takayasu), coarctation, monogenic forms. [ESC 2024]`,`**Children**: secondary hypertension is common; renal parenchymal disease and coarctation lead, so every hypertensive child needs urine, creatinine, renal ultrasound and femoral pulses. [AAP 2017]`,`**Pregnancy**: spironolactone (anti-androgenic) and ACEi/ARB are avoided; PA may improve (progesterone is an MR antagonist); phaeochromocytoma in pregnancy is dangerous and needs alpha-blockade. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: atherosclerotic renovascular disease, OSA and drug-induced hypertension; pseudohypertension; caution with spironolactone in CKD and diabetes (hyperkalaemia). [ESC 2024]`,`**CKD and diabetes**: finerenone is used for kidney protection in diabetic CKD, not as a BP drug; potassium monitoring is essential with any MRA. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Women on oral contraceptives**: switch to progestogen-only or non-hormonal contraception; BP usually settles within months. [ESC 2024]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: serum potassium, urine dipstick, renal ultrasound and femoral pulses screen the main causes; refer for ARR, metanephrines and imaging. [IHCI 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**PA** carries more **AF, stroke, MI, heart failure and CKD** than essential hypertension at the same BP; targeted treatment reduces this excess. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**Missed phaeochromocytoma** can cause fatal crises during surgery, labour or after beta-blocker use; about 10-15% are malignant (metastatic). [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Renovascular disease** leads to ischaemic nephropathy, flash pulmonary oedema and AKI with RAS blockade. [ESC 2024]`,`**Resistant hypertension** raises the risk of stroke, heart failure, CKD and death; adequate control removes most of the excess. [AHA 2018]`,`**Spironolactone**: hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia and menstrual irregularity; **renal denervation**: rare access-site or renal artery complications. [ESC 2024]`,`**Cure rates**: adrenalectomy for adenoma and angioplasty for FMD can cure; long-standing hypertension often persists after correction because of vascular remodelling. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and programme link`,points:[`**Refer to a hypertension or endocrine centre**: positive ARR, raised metanephrines, suspected Cushing, refractory hypertension, or candidates for adrenal vein sampling or renal denervation. [ESC 2024]`,`**Refer to nephrology/vascular**: renal artery stenosis with flash pulmonary oedema or declining eGFR, FMD, Takayasu, and ADPKD. [ESC 2024]`,`**After adding spironolactone**: potassium and creatinine at **1-2 weeks, 4 weeks**, then every 3-6 months. [ESC 2024]`,`**On treatment for PA**: monitor potassium, renin (target un-suppression) and BP; after adrenalectomy reassess biochemistry at 3 and 6-12 months. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**After phaeochromocytoma surgery**: metanephrines at 2-6 weeks and **annually lifelong** (recurrence), plus genetic counselling of relatives. [Endocrine Society 2014]`,`**Resistant hypertension**: monthly visits while titrating, ABPM after changes, adherence checks at every visit. [ESC 2024]`,`**NP-NCD screening at 30+** detects hypertension early; IHCI protocols escalate through amlodipine, telmisartan and chlorthalidone - patients failing this ladder are candidates for resistant-hypertension work-up. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Adherence**: single-pill combinations, once-daily dosing, family support, free drugs at Ayushman Arogya Mandir and Jan Aushadhi. [IHCI 2022]`,`**Family screening** in monogenic forms, familial PA and hereditary phaeochromocytoma syndromes (MEN2, VHL, NF1, SDH mutations). [Endocrine Society 2014]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**Endocrine Society 2025 PA guideline**: screen **all people with hypertension** for PA with ARR; most antihypertensives can continue during screening; treat with MRA titrated to un-suppress renin, or adrenalectomy for unilateral disease. [Endocrine Society 2025]`,`**AHA/ACC 2025**: screen **every patient with resistant hypertension** for PA regardless of potassium, and also those with hypokalaemia, OSA, AF or adrenal incidentaloma. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**ESC 2024**: resistant hypertension needs confirmation by ABPM/home BP and adherence; spironolactone is the preferred fourth drug; **renal denervation may be considered (IIb)** with eGFR over 40 at experienced centres. [ESC 2024]`,`**Renal denervation**: sham-controlled trials (SPYRAL, RADIANCE) showed about 4-6 mmHg lower ambulatory SBP; radiofrequency and ultrasound systems received FDA approval in 2023. [RADIANCE II 2023]`,`**Aprocitentan** (dual endothelin antagonist, PRECISION trial) was approved in the US and Europe in 2024 at **12.5 mg once daily** for resistant hypertension; it causes fluid retention and anaemia and is teratogenic. [PRECISION 2022]`,`**Aldosterone synthase inhibitors** (baxdrostat, lorundrostat) lowered SBP by about 9-10 mmHg over placebo in 2025 phase 3 trials (BaxHTN, Launch-HTN) - an emerging fourth-drug option. [BaxHTN 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long question: '**Define resistant hypertension; discuss evaluation and management**' - definition, pseudo-resistance list, secondary causes with screening tests, stepwise drugs, devices.`,`Short-note favourites: **primary aldosteronism**, **phaeochromocytoma**, **renovascular hypertension**, **renal denervation**, **PATHWAY-2**.`,`Viva: name drugs that give a false-positive ARR (beta-blockers, clonidine, NSAIDs) and false-negative ARR (diuretics, ACEi/ARB, DHP-CCB, MRA, hypokalaemia).`,`Viva: **low renin, low aldosterone** hypokalaemic hypertension - Liddle (amiloride), AME and liquorice (11-beta-HSD2), CAH.`,`Viva: phaeochromocytoma - **alpha before beta**; rule of 10 is outdated (about 35-40% are hereditary).`,`Common mistake: escalating to a fifth drug without checking adherence or ABPM.`,`Common mistake: ruling out PA because potassium is normal.`,`Common mistake: stenting every atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis - CORAL and ASTRAL showed no benefit over medical therapy.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Secondary causes: clue, screen, confirm, treat`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clinical clue`,`Screening test`,`Confirmation and treatment`],rows:[[`Primary aldosteronism`,`Resistant HT, hypokalaemia, AF, incidentaloma`,`Aldosterone-renin ratio`,`Saline infusion, CT, adrenal vein sampling; adrenalectomy or MRA`],[`Renal parenchymal`,`Proteinuria, haematuria, raised creatinine, family history (ADPKD)`,`Creatinine, urine ACR, renal ultrasound`,`Nephrology; ACEi/ARB, SGLT2i`],[`Renovascular`,`Flash pulmonary oedema, creatinine rise on ACEi, bruit, asymmetric kidneys`,`Renal Doppler`,`CT/MR angiography; medical therapy, angioplasty for FMD`],[`Phaeochromocytoma`,`Paroxysms of headache, sweating, palpitations`,`Plasma free or urine metanephrines`,`CT/MRI, genetics; alpha-blockade then adrenalectomy`],[`Obstructive sleep apnoea`,`Snoring, sleepiness, obesity, non-dipping`,`STOP-BANG`,`Polysomnography; CPAP, weight loss`],[`Cushing syndrome`,`Striae, proximal myopathy, central obesity`,`1 mg dexamethasone test, salivary cortisol, 24-h UFC`,`ACTH, imaging; surgery`],[`Coarctation`,`Radio-femoral delay, arm-leg gradient over 20 mmHg`,`Echocardiography`,`CT/MR aorta; stent or surgery`],[`Thyroid disease`,`Hyper: systolic HT, tachycardia; hypo: diastolic HT`,`TSH`,`Treat thyroid disorder`],[`Drug-induced`,`NSAID, OCP, steroid, decongestant, VEGF inhibitor`,`Drug history`,`Stop or substitute`]]},{heading:`Renin-aldosterone patterns in hypokalaemic hypertension`,columns:[`Renin`,`Aldosterone`,`Causes`,`Treatment clue`],rows:[[`High`,`High`,`Renovascular disease, diuretics, malignant HT, coarctation, renin-secreting tumour`,`Treat cause; ACEi/ARB caution in bilateral RAS`],[`Low`,`High`,`Primary aldosteronism (adenoma, bilateral hyperplasia, GRA)`,`Adrenalectomy or MRA; dexamethasone for GRA`],[`Low`,`Low`,`Liddle, apparent mineralocorticoid excess, liquorice, Cushing, CAH (11-beta, 17-alpha)`,`Amiloride for Liddle; stop liquorice; treat cortisol excess`]]},{heading:`Classification of resistant hypertension`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`,`Action`],rows:[[`Apparent resistant`,`Uncontrolled on 3 drugs, pseudo-resistance not excluded`,`ABPM, adherence check, technique`],[`Pseudo-resistant`,`White-coat, non-adherence, poor technique, under-dosing`,`Correct the cause; no new drug`],[`True resistant`,`140/90 or more on optimal RAS blocker + CCB + thiazide-like, confirmed out of office`,`Spironolactone; screen secondary causes`],[`Controlled resistant`,`At target but needs 4 or more drugs`,`Continue; still screen for PA`],[`Refractory`,`Uncontrolled on 5 or more drugs incl. chlorthalidone and MRA`,`Specialist; renal denervation, new agents`]]},{heading:`Drugs in resistant and secondary hypertension`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Chlorthalidone`,`12.5-25 mg oral once daily`,`Long term; works even in CKD 4`,`Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, gout, hyperglycaemia`],[`Spironolactone`,`25 mg oral daily, up to 50 mg (PA often 50-100 mg)`,`Long term; K and creatinine at 1-2 and 4 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia; avoid if K over 5 or eGFR under 30-45, pregnancy`],[`Eplerenone`,`50 mg oral daily, up to 50 mg twice daily`,`If gynaecomastia on spironolactone`,`Hyperkalaemia; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Amiloride`,`10-20 mg oral daily (5 mg in combinations)`,`Alternative fourth drug; Liddle syndrome`,`Hyperkalaemia`],[`Doxazosin`,`1 mg at night, titrate to 4-8 mg (max 16 mg)`,`Fifth-line or phaeochromocytoma preparation`,`First-dose postural hypotension, oedema`],[`Bisoprolol`,`5-10 mg oral once daily`,`If heart rate high or compelling indication`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; not before alpha-blockade in phaeo`],[`Clonidine`,`0.1 mg twice daily, up to 0.6 mg/day`,`Late add-on`,`Sedation, dry mouth, rebound hypertension on stopping`],[`Phenoxybenzamine`,`10 mg oral twice daily, titrate to about 1 mg/kg/day`,`10-14 days before phaeochromocytoma surgery`,`Postural hypotension, nasal stuffiness, tachycardia`],[`Aprocitentan`,`12.5 mg oral once daily`,`Resistant HT where approved; not yet marketed in India`,`Oedema, fluid retention, anaemia; teratogenic`]]},{heading:`Drugs and conditions affecting the aldosterone-renin ratio`,columns:[`Factor`,`Effect on renin`,`Effect on ARR`],rows:[[`Beta-blockers, clonidine, methyldopa, NSAIDs`,`Lower`,`False positive`],[`Diuretics, MRAs, amiloride`,`Raise`,`False negative`],[`ACE inhibitors, ARBs, DHP-CCB`,`Raise`,`False negative`],[`Hypokalaemia`,`Lowers aldosterone`,`False negative - correct K first`],[`Verapamil, hydralazine, doxazosin`,`Minimal`,`Preferred during testing`],[`CKD, older age, oestrogen pill`,`Lower (or alter renin assay)`,`False positive`]]}],redFlags:[`BP 180/110 or more with organ damage symptoms - hypertensive emergency.`,`Potassium under 3.0 mmol/L with weakness, paralysis or arrhythmia.`,`Paroxysmal crises with headache, sweating and palpitations, especially after a beta-blocker, contrast or anaesthesia.`,`Flash pulmonary oedema or creatinine rise over 30% after starting an ACE inhibitor or ARB.`,`Potassium over 6.0 mmol/L or ECG changes on spironolactone.`,`Hypertension in a child or young woman with absent pulses or radio-femoral delay.`,`Hypertension in pregnancy with paroxysms - possible phaeochromocytoma.`],pearls:[`Rule out pseudo-resistance first: technique, white coat, adherence, drugs, salt.`,`PATHWAY-2: spironolactone is the best fourth drug; amiloride is an equal alternative.`,`Normal potassium does not exclude primary aldosteronism - most cases are normokalaemic.`,`ARR: beta-blockers give false positives; ACEi/ARB, diuretics and hypokalaemia give false negatives.`,`Low renin, low aldosterone: Liddle, AME, liquorice - think 11-beta-HSD2 and ENaC.`,`Phaeochromocytoma: alpha before beta; metanephrines, not catecholamines; rule of 10 is outdated.`,`CORAL and ASTRAL: stenting atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis is not better than medical therapy; FMD gets angioplasty without stent.`,`Renal denervation: about 5 mmHg off ambulatory SBP - an adjunct, not a cure.`,`In young Indians with renovascular hypertension, think Takayasu.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-q1`,stem:`A 56-year-old man has home BP 152/94 mmHg despite adherence to telmisartan 80 mg, amlodipine 10 mg and chlorthalidone 25 mg. Potassium is 4.2 mmol/L and eGFR 72. According to PATHWAY-2, the most effective fourth drug is:`,options:[`Bisoprolol`,`Spironolactone`,`Doxazosin`,`Clonidine`],answer:1,explanation:`PATHWAY-2 showed spironolactone 25-50 mg lowered home SBP more than placebo, doxazosin and bisoprolol, and was the best drug in about 60% of patients, reflecting the salt-and-aldosterone drive of resistant hypertension. Doxazosin and bisoprolol were inferior, and clonidine is a late add-on limited by sedation and rebound hypertension.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-q2`,stem:`Which of the following drugs is most likely to give a false-positive aldosterone-renin ratio when screening for primary aldosteronism?`,options:[`Amlodipine`,`Telmisartan`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Atenolol`],answer:3,explanation:`Beta-blockers such as atenolol suppress renin release from the juxtaglomerular cells, lowering the denominator and raising the ARR - a false positive. Amlodipine, telmisartan and hydrochlorothiazide all raise renin and therefore lower the ratio, causing false negatives rather than false positives.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-q3`,stem:`A 19-year-old man with severe hypertension, hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis has low plasma renin and low aldosterone. His father had similar problems. BP falls with amiloride but not with spironolactone. The diagnosis is:`,options:[`Liddle syndrome`,`Primary aldosteronism`,`Renovascular hypertension`,`Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism`],answer:0,explanation:`Liddle syndrome is an autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation of ENaC, causing sodium retention with suppressed renin and aldosterone; amiloride blocks ENaC directly, while spironolactone fails because aldosterone is not the driver. Primary aldosteronism and GRA have high aldosterone, and renovascular hypertension has high renin.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-q4`,stem:`A 70-year-old diabetic smoker with controlled BP on three drugs has a 75% atherosclerotic stenosis of the right renal artery with stable creatinine. What is the best management?`,options:[`Renal artery stenting`,`Surgical renal bypass`,`Optimal medical therapy with statin, antiplatelet and BP control`,`Balloon angioplasty without stenting`],answer:2,explanation:`ASTRAL and CORAL showed that stenting atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis adds no benefit over optimal medical therapy for BP, renal function or events, so medical therapy is preferred; intervention is reserved for flash pulmonary oedema, rapidly declining function or truly resistant BP. Balloon angioplasty without stenting is the treatment for fibromuscular dysplasia, and surgical bypass is rarely needed.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-q5`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman with paroxysmal headache, sweating and palpitations has plasma free normetanephrine five times the upper limit and a 4 cm adrenal mass. Before adrenalectomy, the first step in preparation is:`,options:[`Start propranolol to control tachycardia`,`Proceed to surgery within 48 hours`,`Start furosemide to control BP`,`Start alpha-blockade with phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin for 10-14 days`],answer:3,explanation:`Phaeochromocytoma surgery must be preceded by 10-14 days of alpha-blockade (phenoxybenzamine or doxazosin) with a high-salt diet and fluids to restore volume and prevent intraoperative crises. A beta-blocker such as propranolol given first leaves alpha vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a crisis, operating without preparation is dangerous, and furosemide worsens the volume contraction.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define resistant hypertension. Discuss its causes, evaluation and stepwise management.`,openingLines:[`Resistant hypertension is BP of 140/90 mmHg or more despite optimal doses of three drugs including a diuretic, confirmed by out-of-office measurement with adherence verified.`,`About half of apparently resistant patients have pseudo-resistance, and among true cases primary aldosteronism and OSA are the commonest secondary causes.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions`,points:[`Apparent, true, controlled resistant (4+ drugs) and refractory (5+ drugs incl. chlorthalidone and MRA).`,`About 10% of treated hypertensives; 1.5-2 times higher CV and renal risk.`]},{heading:`Pseudo-resistance`,points:[`Technique and cuff, white-coat effect, non-adherence, under-dosing, no diuretic, interfering drugs.`]},{heading:`Secondary causes`,points:[`PA, CKD, renovascular, OSA, phaeochromocytoma, Cushing, coarctation, thyroid, drugs.`,`Clues: onset under 40, hypokalaemia, paroxysms, flash pulmonary oedema, radio-femoral delay.`]},{heading:`Evaluation`,points:[`ABPM, electrolytes, eGFR, urine ACR, ECG, TSH; ARR in all; metanephrines, renal Doppler, sleep study as indicated.`]},{heading:`Stepwise management`,points:[`Salt under 5 g, weight loss, CPAP; optimal RAS blocker + CCB + chlorthalidone as single pill.`,`Add spironolactone 25-50 mg (PATHWAY-2); alternatives amiloride, eplerenone, doxazosin, bisoprolol.`,`Renal denervation (ESC 2024 IIb), aprocitentan where available; treat specific causes.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`K and creatinine at 1-2 and 4 weeks after MRA; monthly visits while titrating; ABPM after changes.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: uncontrolled on 3 drugs - ABPM and adherence - lifestyle - optimise triple - spironolactone - alternatives - devices`,`Table: secondary causes with clue and screening test`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pseudo-resistance and secondary causes`,marks:3},{part:`Evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Stepwise management and follow-up`,marks:3}],keywords:[`resistant hypertension`,`pseudo-resistance`,`ABPM`,`primary aldosteronism`,`ARR`,`spironolactone`,`PATHWAY-2`,`renal denervation`,`aprocitentan`]},{id:`cardiovascular-secondary-resistant-htn-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on primary aldosteronism.`,openingLines:[`Primary aldosteronism is autonomous aldosterone production with suppressed renin, causing sodium retention, potassium loss and hypertension; it is the commonest curable cause of hypertension.`,`The Endocrine Society 2025 guideline recommends screening all hypertensives with the aldosterone-renin ratio.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and causes`,points:[`5-10% of hypertensives, 15-20% of resistant hypertension; adenoma (about a third) vs bilateral hyperplasia (about two-thirds).`,`Hypokalaemia in a minority; more AF, stroke and CKD than essential hypertension.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`ARR: suppressed renin with non-low aldosterone; beware beta-blockers (false positive) and diuretics/ACEi (false negative).`,`Confirm with saline infusion; CT adrenal; adrenal vein sampling before surgery.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Unilateral: laparoscopic adrenalectomy. Bilateral: spironolactone or eplerenone titrated to un-suppress renin.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: ARR screen - confirm - CT - adrenal vein sampling - surgery or MRA`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Screening and confirmation`,marks:2},{part:`Subtyping and treatment`,marks:2}],keywords:[`primary aldosteronism`,`aldosterone-renin ratio`,`saline infusion test`,`adrenal vein sampling`,`spironolactone`,`adrenalectomy`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Hypertensive vascular disease; Disorders of the adrenal cortex`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension`,`2025 AHA/ACC/multisociety Guideline for the Prevention, Detection, Evaluation and Management of High Blood Pressure in Adults`,`Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Management of Primary Aldosteronism, 2025`,`Endocrine Society Clinical Practice Guideline: Pheochromocytoma and Paraganglioma, 2014`,`AHA Scientific Statement: Resistant Hypertension - Detection, Evaluation and Management, 2018; Williams B et al. PATHWAY-2, Lancet 2015`,`India Hypertension Control Initiative (ICMR/MoHFW) treatment protocols, 2022`]});var n=[];n.push({id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion`,title:`STEMI: reperfusion, fibrinolysis and the pharmaco-invasive strategy`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI)** is acute infarction from **thrombotic occlusion of an epicardial coronary artery**, recognised by new ST elevation at the J point in **2 or more contiguous leads** (or a STEMI equivalent); every patient within **12 hours** of onset needs reperfusion - **primary PCI if wire crossing is possible within 120 minutes** of diagnosis, otherwise **fibrinolysis within 10 minutes** (tenecteplase preferred) followed by **angiography at 2-24 hours** (pharmaco-invasive strategy) or **immediate rescue PCI** if lysis fails.`,keywords:[`STEMI`,`ST elevation myocardial infarction`,`primary PCI`,`PPCI`,`fibrinolysis`,`thrombolysis`,`tenecteplase`,`TNK`,`streptokinase`,`alteplase`,`reteplase`,`pharmaco-invasive strategy`,`rescue PCI`,`door-to-needle`,`door-to-balloon`,`hub-and-spoke`,`STEMI equivalent`,`Sgarbossa criteria`,`de Winter T waves`,`STREAM trial`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**STEMI** is a working diagnosis made on the ECG: ischaemic symptoms with **persistent ST elevation** (or an equivalent) signalling acute total occlusion - reperfusion starts before troponin is back. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`The **Fourth Universal Definition of MI** requires a rise and/or fall of cardiac troponin with at least one value above the **99th percentile upper reference limit** plus clinical evidence of acute ischaemia. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Ischaemic heart disease is the leading cause of death worldwide** (about 9 million deaths a year, 16% of all deaths); in high-income countries STEMI is now the minority of ACS presentations. [WHO 2020]`,`In India **cardiovascular disease causes about 28% of all deaths** and ischaemic heart disease is the single leading cause of death and DALYs. [India State-Level Disease Burden Initiative 2018]`,`The **CREATE registry** (20,468 ACS patients, 89 Indian centres) found **about 61% were STEMI** - much higher than in Western registries - with a median symptom-to-hospital time of about 5 hours. [CREATE, Lancet 2008]`,`In CREATE most Indian STEMI patients who were reperfused received **fibrinolysis (largely streptokinase)**, under 10% had PCI, and **30-day mortality in STEMI was 8.6%** - higher in the poor, who received less evidence-based care. [CREATE, Lancet 2008]`,`**South Asians have their first MI 5-10 years earlier**: mean age 53 years versus 58.8 years in other INTERHEART regions. [INTERHEART South Asia, JAMA 2007]`,`In India the largest delay is **pre-hospital** - symptoms mistaken for 'gas', visits to several clinics, lack of ambulance and cost - so many patients reach a cath lab outside the reperfusion window. [CSI STEMI position statement 2017]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`About two-thirds of type 1 STEMI follow **plaque rupture** of a thin-cap fibroatheroma; most of the rest follow **plaque erosion**, commoner in young people, women and smokers. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**INTERHEART**: nine modifiable factors - smoking, ApoB/ApoA1 ratio, hypertension, diabetes, abdominal obesity, psychosocial stress, low fruit and vegetables, inactivity and alcohol pattern - explain **over 90% of the population-attributable risk**. [INTERHEART, Lancet 2004]`,`**South Asian phenotype**: atherogenic dyslipidaemia (low HDL, high triglycerides, small dense LDL), high **lipoprotein(a)**, insulin resistance and central obesity at a lower BMI. [CSI Dyslipidaemia 2024]`,`**Tobacco in every form** - cigarettes, bidis and smokeless tobacco - is the dominant risk factor in **young Indian STEMI** (under 45 years), often with family history and premature diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-atherosclerotic causes**: coronary embolism (AF, endocarditis, prosthetic valves), **spontaneous coronary artery dissection** (young women, peripartum), vasospasm, coronary arteritis and aortic dissection extending into a coronary ostium. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Triggers**: the early-morning circadian peak, heavy unaccustomed exertion, emotional stress, acute infection (especially influenza), cold weather and air pollution. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cocaine and amphetamines** cause STEMI by vasospasm, platelet activation and accelerated atherosclerosis - relevant to young patients without risk factors. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - the why behind the drugs`,points:[`A **thin-cap fibroatheroma** (cap under 65 micrometres, large lipid core, macrophage-rich) ruptures and exposes **tissue factor and collagen** to flowing blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`Platelets **adhere** (GPIb-von Willebrand factor), **activate** (thromboxane A2, ADP acting on P2Y12) and **aggregate** (GPIIb/IIIa binding fibrinogen), while thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin - each step is a drug target: aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors, GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors and heparins. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`STEMI is usually a **fibrin-rich occlusive (red) thrombus**, whereas NSTE-ACS is usually a non-occlusive platelet-rich (white) thrombus - this is why **fibrinolytics help STEMI but not NSTE-ACS**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Wavefront of necrosis** (Reimer-Jennings): cell death begins in the subendocardium after about 20-40 minutes and spreads to the epicardium over **3-6 hours**, so myocardial salvage is greatest in the first 2-3 hours. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Thrombus ages**: fresh fibrin is easily lysed, but after 3-6 hours cross-linked and organised thrombus resists lysis - fibrinolytic efficacy falls steeply with time, whereas mechanical PCI is far less time-dependent. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Fibrinolysis exposes clot-bound thrombin and generates plasmin that **activates platelets**, so lytics must always be given with **aspirin, clopidogrel and an anticoagulant** to prevent reocclusion. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Fibrin specificity**: streptokinase activates circulating plasminogen (systemic lytic state, fibrinogen depletion, antigenic, bradykinin hypotension); alteplase, reteplase and **tenecteplase are fibrin-specific**, and tenecteplase resists PAI-1 and has a long half-life allowing a **single bolus**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**ST elevation** is the current of injury of transmural ischaemia: the injured epicardium is relatively depolarised, so the ST vector points towards the infarct and reciprocal depression appears in opposite leads. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reperfusion injury** (calcium overload, oxygen free radicals, mitochondrial permeability transition pore opening) and **no-reflow** from microvascular obstruction and distal embolisation limit the benefit of an open artery. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification - ECG criteria, equivalents and Killip class`,points:[`**ST elevation criteria** (new, at the J point, in 2 contiguous leads): **1 mm or more** in all leads except V2-V3; in V2-V3 **2 mm or more in men aged 40 or more, 2.5 mm in men under 40, 1.5 mm in women** of any age. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Posterior leads V7-V9**: ST elevation **0.5 mm or more** (1 mm in men under 40) confirms posterior MI, suspected from **ST depression in V1-V3** with tall R waves and upright T waves. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Right-sided leads V3R-V4R**: ST elevation **0.5 mm or more** (1 mm in men under 30) diagnoses right ventricular infarction - record them in every inferior STEMI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Bundle branch block or paced rhythm** with ongoing ischaemic symptoms is managed by a **primary PCI strategy**; the **Sgarbossa criteria** (concordant ST elevation 1 mm or more, concordant ST depression in V1-V3, discordant ST elevation 5 mm or more) support occlusion. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Smith-modified Sgarbossa** replaces the 5 mm rule with a discordant **ST/S ratio of -0.25 or less**, which is more sensitive for occlusion in LBBB. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**de Winter T waves** (upsloping ST depression of 1-3 mm at the J point in V1-V6 with tall symmetrical T waves) indicate **proximal LAD occlusion** and are treated as STEMI. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ST depression of 1 mm or more in 6 or more leads with ST elevation in aVR and/or V1** suggests left main or three-vessel ischaemia - immediate angiography if unstable, but **fibrinolysis is not indicated**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Killip class** at presentation stratifies early mortality: I no heart failure, II crackles or S3, III pulmonary oedema, IV cardiogenic shock (original series mortality 6%, 17%, 38% and 81%). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**By time from onset**: within 12 hours (reperfuse), 12-48 hours (primary PCI if symptomatic or unstable, routine PCI considered if stable) and over 48 hours (no routine PCI of an occluded artery in a stable patient). [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Typical pain**: retrosternal heaviness, pressure or burning **lasting over 20 minutes**, radiating to the left arm, jaw, neck or back, unrelieved by rest or nitrate, with sweating, nausea, breathlessness and a sense of doom. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations** - breathlessness alone, epigastric pain, fatigue, syncope or confusion - are common in **women, the elderly, diabetics and CKD**; diabetic autonomic neuropathy causes **silent infarction**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`In Indian practice epigastric pain is often called **'gas' or acidity** and treated with antacids; any adult over 30 with epigastric or chest discomfort and sweating needs an **ECG within 10 minutes**. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`Record the **exact time of onset of continuous pain** - every reperfusion decision (12-hour window, choice of PCI or lysis) counts from it. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Inferior MI** commonly causes nausea, vomiting, bradycardia and hypotension through the vagal **Bezold-Jarisch reflex**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sudden cardiac arrest** may be the first presentation; most ventricular fibrillation deaths occur in the first hour, often before the patient reaches hospital. [Harrison 22e]`,`Before lysis, ask directly about **contraindications**: prior stroke or intracranial bleed, recent surgery, trauma or GI bleeding, anticoagulants, bleeding disorders, pregnancy and tearing back pain. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Anxious, pale, sweating, cold periphery**; the **Levine sign** (clenched fist over the sternum) describes ischaemic pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tachycardia and hypertension** suggest sympathetic drive (often anterior MI); **bradycardia and hypotension** suggest vagal tone or AV block (often inferior MI). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Crackles, S3 and raised JVP** define Killip class II-IV and predict mortality; an **S4** is common because the ischaemic ventricle is stiff. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypotension, raised JVP and clear lungs** with a Kussmaul sign in inferior MI indicate **right ventricular infarction** - avoid nitrates and diuretics. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`A **new pansystolic murmur** suggests papillary muscle dysfunction or rupture or a ventricular septal rupture - echo urgently. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Check **both radial pulses and BP in both arms** (difference over 20 mmHg) and listen for an aortic regurgitation murmur - **aortic dissection** must be excluded before fibrinolysis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Document a quick **neurological examination and GCS** before lysis, as a baseline for detecting intracranial haemorrhage. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Shock**: SBP under 90 mmHg for over 30 minutes with cold clammy skin, oliguria (under 30 mL/h) or altered sensorium. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of chest pain with ST elevation`,points:[`**Aortic dissection**: tearing pain to the back, pulse or BP differential, widened mediastinum; it can occlude the right coronary ostium and mimic inferior STEMI - **fibrinolysis is fatal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute pericarditis**: diffuse concave ST elevation, **PR depression**, no reciprocal change except aVR and V1, pleuritic positional pain and a rub. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Benign early repolarisation**: young, asymptomatic, notched J point, concave ST elevation in V2-V4, no reciprocal change and stable on serial ECGs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Left ventricular aneurysm**: persistent ST elevation with deep Q waves weeks after an old anterior MI; T waves are small relative to the QRS. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Takotsubo syndrome**: postmenopausal woman after emotional or physical stress, ST elevation and apical ballooning with non-obstructed coronaries - angiography is needed to tell it from anterior STEMI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Other STEMI mimics**: LVH with strain, LBBB, paced rhythm, Brugada pattern, hyperkalaemia, myocarditis and acute pulmonary embolism. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Abdominal mimics** - acute pancreatitis, cholecystitis and perforated ulcer - can cause chest pain and nonspecific ST changes; check lipase and examine the abdomen. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG within 10 minutes** of first medical contact; repeat every 15-30 minutes if the first is non-diagnostic and pain continues; add **V7-V9 and V3R-V4R** in inferior MI or suspected posterior MI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Do not wait for troponin** before reperfusion in STEMI; a baseline hs-troponin is drawn but serial values only size the infarct and detect reinfarction. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Troponin** rises at 3-4 hours and stays raised for **7-14 days**; **CK-MB** peaks at about 24 hours and normalises in 48-72 hours, so it helps detect reinfarction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bedside echocardiography** shows regional wall motion abnormality, LVEF, RV involvement, mechanical complications and effusion - but must **not delay transfer** for PCI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Baseline bloods**: CBC and platelets, creatinine, potassium and magnesium, glucose, lipid profile (ideally within 24 hours), HbA1c, coagulation profile if on anticoagulants, and blood group. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Chest X-ray** shows pulmonary oedema or a widened mediastinum, but it should never delay reperfusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Successful fibrinolysis** is judged at **60-90 minutes**: ST elevation resolved by **50% or more** in the lead with maximal elevation, relief of pain, often an **accelerated idioventricular rhythm** and an early enzyme peak. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**TIMI flow grade** at angiography: 0 no antegrade flow, 1 penetration without perfusion, 2 slow but complete filling, **3 normal flow** - the goal of reperfusion. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac MRI** later defines infarct size, microvascular obstruction and viability, and is the key test when angiography shows non-obstructed arteries. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Management at first contact - clinic, PHC and ambulance`,points:[`Recognise and record a **12-lead ECG within 10 minutes**; transmit it (tele-ECG, WhatsApp to the hub) and activate the STEMI network and **108 ambulance** - the patient must not drive. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Aspirin 150-300 mg chewed** (plain, non-enteric-coated) at once unless truly allergic; ACC/AHA uses 162-325 mg. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg** (or isosorbide dinitrate 5 mg) every 5 minutes up to 3 doses for pain if **SBP over 90 mmHg**, no RV infarction and no PDE5 inhibitor in the last 24-48 hours. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Oxygen only if SpO2 is under 90%**; routine oxygen in normoxic patients gives no benefit (DETO2X, AVOID). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Titrated IV morphine** (2-4 mg increments) with an antiemetic relieves pain, but it delays absorption of oral P2Y12 inhibitors; **avoid intramuscular injections** in a patient who may receive lytics. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Attach a **defibrillator**, secure IV access and keep the patient supine; avoid NSAIDs. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`At a **non-PCI spoke** where PCI cannot be achieved within 120 minutes, give **tenecteplase within 10 minutes of diagnosis** and arrange transfer to the hub. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`A **P2Y12 loading dose** may be given before primary PCI (pretreatment is optional, IIb in ESC 2023); with fibrinolysis the P2Y12 inhibitor is **clopidogrel 300 mg** (75 mg if over 75 years). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Start a **high-intensity statin** (atorvastatin 80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) as early as possible. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Management in hospital - choosing and delivering reperfusion`,points:[`**Reperfusion is Class I for all STEMI within 12 hours**: **primary PCI is preferred** if the expected time from diagnosis to wire crossing is **120 minutes or less**; if longer, give **fibrinolysis** at once, ideally pre-hospital. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 2023 time targets**: diagnosis to wire **60 minutes** at a PCI centre and **90 minutes** if transferred; diagnosis to lytic bolus **10 minutes**; assess lysis at **60-90 minutes**; angiography **2-24 hours** after successful lysis. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025 targets**: first medical contact to device **90 minutes or less** (direct) and **120 minutes or less** (transfer), **door-in-door-out 30 minutes or less**, and **door-to-needle 30 minutes or less** when lysis is chosen. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Primary PCI technique**: **radial access**, **drug-eluting stent**, no routine thrombus aspiration, and **complete revascularisation** of significant non-culprit lesions during the index procedure or within 45 days (culprit-only in shock). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Antithrombotics for primary PCI**: aspirin plus **prasugrel 60 mg or ticagrelor 180 mg** load, and **UFH 70-100 IU/kg IV** bolus (enoxaparin 0.5 mg/kg IV or bivalirudin are alternatives); GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors for bail-out only. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fibrinolysis regimen**: weight-based **tenecteplase** single bolus (**half dose at 75 years or more**), aspirin, clopidogrel 300 mg (75 mg if over 75) and **enoxaparin** (preferred over UFH) until revascularisation or for up to 8 days. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Rescue PCI immediately** if ST resolution is under 50% at 60-90 minutes, or with haemodynamic or electrical instability, worsening ischaemia or persistent pain; **repeat fibrinolysis is not recommended**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Pharmaco-invasive strategy**: after successful lysis, routine angiography with PCI of the infarct artery at **2-24 hours** (STREAM: outcomes similar to primary PCI in patients presenting within 3 hours); immediate PCI straight after full-dose lysis (facilitated PCI) is harmful. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Late presenters**: primary PCI at 12-48 hours if symptoms persist or the patient is unstable; routine PCI considered at 12-48 hours if stable; **no routine PCI of an occluded artery after 48 hours** in an asymptomatic patient (OAT). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Post-reperfusion care**: CCU with **ECG monitoring for at least 24 hours**, early mobilisation at 12-24 hours, oral beta-blocker if no heart failure, ACE inhibitor within 24 hours, and discharge at **48-72 hours** if low risk. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Age 75 or more**: half-dose tenecteplase, clopidogrel 75 mg without a loading dose, enoxaparin 0.75 mg/kg SC without an IV bolus; avoid prasugrel (or use 5 mg) - bleeding and ICH risks are higher, so PCI is preferred where available. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**STREAM-2 (2024)**: in patients aged 60 or more, half-dose tenecteplase followed by angiography gave reperfusion comparable to primary PCI - supporting pharmaco-invasive care for older patients where PCI is delayed. [STREAM-2, Circulation 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: atypical and silent presentations, more multivessel disease; treat glucose above **180 mg/dL** while avoiding hypoglycaemia. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**CKD**: tenecteplase needs no dose change; reduce enoxaparin to once daily if eGFR is under 30; prefer UFH in severe CKD and limit contrast, but **do not withhold primary PCI**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Pregnancy**: **primary PCI is preferred** (fibrinolysis is a relative contraindication); SCAD is the commonest cause - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Patients on oral anticoagulants**: primary PCI is preferred because OAC is a relative contraindication to lysis; give parenteral anticoagulation during PCI regardless of the last DOAC dose. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Cocaine or methamphetamine STEMI**: benzodiazepines and nitrates, **avoid acute beta-blockers**, and proceed to primary PCI. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Resource-limited setting** (taluk or district hospital without a cath lab): tele-ECG, **tenecteplase at the spoke**, streptokinase if TNK is unavailable, then 108 transfer to a hub for angiography at 3-24 hours - the hub-and-spoke model. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Resuscitated cardiac arrest** with ST elevation: **primary PCI** (Class I), with temperature control to avoid fever in comatose survivors. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Complications of reperfusion and prognosis`,points:[`**Intracranial haemorrhage** after fibrinolysis occurs in about **1%**, more in the elderly, women, low body weight, prior stroke and SBP over 170 mmHg on arrival. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`In **STREAM** ICH was 1.0% with full-dose tenecteplase versus 0.2% with primary PCI; **halving the dose at 75 years or more** reduced it to about 0.5%. [STREAM, NEJM 2013]`,`**Streptokinase** causes **hypotension** (slow the infusion, raise the legs - do not stop), allergic reactions and fever, and induces antibodies, so it should not be repeated. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Reperfusion arrhythmias**: accelerated idioventricular rhythm (60-120 per minute) is a benign sign of reperfusion and needs no treatment. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**No-reflow** (TIMI flow under 3 despite an open artery) and **stent thrombosis** reduce the benefit of PCI; access-site bleeding is minimised by radial access. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Time is muscle**: each 30-minute delay to primary PCI raises 1-year mortality by about **7.5%** (relative). [De Luca, Circulation 2004]`,`Fibrinolysis prevents about **30 deaths per 1000 treated within 6 hours** and 20 per 1000 at 7-12 hours, with no clear benefit after 12 hours; treatment in the **first hour saves the most**. [FTT Collaborative, Lancet 1994]`,`**Mortality**: in-hospital mortality of unselected STEMI is about **4-12%** and 1-year mortality about 10%; predictors are age, Killip class, delay, anterior site, low LVEF, diabetes and renal function. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`For arrhythmias, shock, rupture and LV thrombus see the **Complications of myocardial infarction** topic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, transfer and monitoring`,points:[`**Every lysed patient is transferred to a PCI-capable hub** - immediately if lysis fails or the patient is unstable, otherwise for angiography at 2-24 hours. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**After lysis monitor**: pain and ST segment at 60-90 minutes, BP, rhythm, puncture sites, neurological status (ICH), haemoglobin and platelets daily. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`With **UFH beyond 4 days** watch platelet counts for **heparin-induced thrombocytopenia**; adjust UFH to an aPTT of 1.5-2.0 times control at 3, 6, 12 and 24 hours. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Before discharge**: echo for LVEF, risk stratification, discharge bundle (DAPT, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker as indicated) and **cardiac rehabilitation referral** - see the After the MI topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**First review 1-2 weeks after discharge**; repeat echo at **6-12 weeks** if LVEF was 40% or less, to decide on ICD and heart failure therapy. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Quality indicators** a district hospital should audit: proportion of STEMI reperfused, door-to-needle time, door-in-door-out time and 30-day mortality. [ESC ACCA quality indicators]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and the Indian STEMI system`,points:[`**Public message**: chest discomfort lasting more than 15 minutes with sweating means **call 108 and go to the nearest hospital with an ECG** - do not wait, do not drive. [CSI STEMI position statement 2017]`,`**Hub-and-spoke model**: spokes (CHC, taluk, district hospitals) do the ECG, tele-transmit it and give **tenecteplase**; hubs with 24x7 cath labs provide rescue and routine PCI - the backbone of Indian STEMI care. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`The **Tamil Nadu STEMI programme** showed that a state hub-and-spoke system with pharmaco-invasive care improves reperfusion and timeliness in India. [Alexander, JAMA Cardiology 2017]`,`State schemes such as **Karnataka's Puneeth Rajkumar Hrudaya Jyothi Yojane (2023)** supply free tenecteplase and AI-read tele-ECG at taluk hospitals linked to cardiac hubs. [Karnataka DoHFW 2023]`,`**Mission DELHI** (ICMR-AIIMS) uses motorbike first responders to record and transmit an ECG and start treatment at home for chest pain calls. [ICMR 2019]`,`**PM-JAY** (Ayushman Bharat) covers thrombolysis and primary or pharmaco-invasive PCI packages, removing cost as a reason for delay. [NHA PM-JAY HBP]`,`**Primary prevention** belongs to NP-NCD screening at 30 years and above at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs, tobacco cessation (quitline 1800-112-356) and control of BP, sugar and lipids - see the risk assessment topic. [MoHFW NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2023** produced a single **ACS guideline** (STEMI and NSTE-ACS together) built on a 'working diagnosis', kept the **120-minute** PCI-versus-lysis threshold and made **complete revascularisation** in STEMI with multivessel disease Class I. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 2023**: routine beta-blockers for all ACS became IIa (Class I only if LVEF 40% or less), P2Y12 pretreatment before primary PCI IIb, influenza vaccination Class I and **low-dose colchicine** IIb. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI 2025** replaced the 2013 STEMI and 2014 NSTE-ACS guidelines with one ACS guideline: complete revascularisation in STEMI (Class 1), angiography 2-24 hours after successful lysis, and **ticagrelor monotherapy after at least 1 month of DAPT**. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025** gives a **microaxial flow pump** a 2a recommendation in selected STEMI cardiogenic shock (DanGer Shock: 180-day mortality 45.8% versus 58.5%), and routine IABP no benefit. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ECLS-SHOCK (2023)**: routine VA-ECMO in infarct-related cardiogenic shock did not reduce mortality and increased bleeding. [ECLS-SHOCK, NEJM 2023]`,`**Complete revascularisation trials**: FIRE (2023) favoured physiology-guided complete PCI in patients aged 75 or more, and MULTISTARS AMI (2023) found immediate multivessel PCI non-inferior to staged PCI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**STREAM-2 (2024)** supports half-dose tenecteplase pharmaco-invasive therapy in patients aged 60 or more when primary PCI is delayed. [STREAM-2, Circulation 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic long questions: **'Thrombolysis in acute MI - indications, contraindications, drugs and assessment of success'** and **'Pharmaco-invasive strategy'**. [DNB FM papers]`,`**Never wait for troponin** in STEMI - the ECG makes the diagnosis and starts the clock. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fibrinolysis is not indicated for ST depression or NSTE-ACS** (except true posterior MI) - FTT showed no benefit and possible harm. [FTT Collaborative, Lancet 1994]`,`**Commonest viva mistakes**: lysis in suspected aortic dissection, nitrates in RV infarction, IM injections before lysis, repeating streptokinase and not transferring a lysed patient. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Remember **tenecteplase by 10 kg bands**: 30 mg under 60 kg, 35, 40, 45 and **50 mg at 90 kg or more** - halved at 75 years or more. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Know the difference between **rescue PCI** (immediately, failed lysis), **pharmaco-invasive PCI** (2-24 hours, successful lysis) and **facilitated PCI** (immediately after lysis, harmful). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Lytic contraindication period for ischaemic stroke differs**: 6 months in ESC 2023 versus 3 months in ACC/AHA - quote the guideline you are using. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in STEMI reperfusion`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`150-300 mg chewed (or 75-250 mg IV) loading, then 75-100 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI bleeding, true allergy; give with PPI if GI risk`],[`Clopidogrel (with lysis, or if no prasugrel/ticagrelor)`,`300 mg loading with lysis (75 mg if over 75 years); 600 mg before PCI; then 75 mg daily`,`12 months as part of DAPT`,`Bleeding; poor metaboliser variability (CYP2C19)`],[`Ticagrelor`,`180 mg loading, then 90 mg twice daily`,`12 months DAPT (monotherapy after 1 month per ACC/AHA 2025)`,`Dyspnoea, bradypauses, bleeding; not with lysis in first 24-48 h; avoid with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Prasugrel`,`60 mg loading, then 10 mg daily (5 mg if 75+ years or under 60 kg)`,`12 months DAPT`,`Contraindicated after prior stroke or TIA; bleeding; not with lysis`],[`Tenecteplase`,`Single IV bolus over 5-10 s: 30 mg under 60 kg, 35 mg 60-69, 40 mg 70-79, 45 mg 80-89, 50 mg 90 kg or more; half dose at 75+ years`,`Once only`,`ICH about 1%, bleeding; absolute contraindications as per table`],[`Streptokinase`,`1.5 million IU IV in 100 mL saline over 30-60 min`,`Once only; do not repeat`,`Hypotension, allergy, fever; antibodies make re-use ineffective`],[`Alteplase`,`15 mg IV bolus, then 0.75 mg/kg over 30 min (max 50 mg), then 0.5 mg/kg over 60 min (max 35 mg)`,`90-minute regimen`,`Bleeding, ICH; needs infusion pump`],[`Reteplase`,`10 units IV bolus, repeated after 30 min`,`Two boluses`,`Bleeding, ICH`],[`Enoxaparin (with lysis)`,`Under 75 y: 30 mg IV then 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly; 75+ y: 0.75 mg/kg SC 12-hourly, no bolus; eGFR under 30: once daily`,`Until revascularisation or discharge, max 8 days`,`Bleeding; accumulates in renal failure`],[`Unfractionated heparin`,`PCI: 70-100 IU/kg IV bolus; with lysis: 60 IU/kg (max 4000) then 12 IU/kg/h (max 1000)`,`24-48 h with lysis; aPTT 1.5-2.0 times control`,`Bleeding, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia`],[`Fondaparinux (only with streptokinase)`,`2.5 mg IV, then 2.5 mg SC daily`,`Up to 8 days or discharge`,`Not for primary PCI (catheter thrombosis); avoid if eGFR under 20`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`0.4 mg SL every 5 min up to 3 doses; IV 5-10 microgram/min titrated`,`For pain, hypertension, pulmonary oedema`,`Contraindicated if SBP under 90, RV infarct, PDE5 inhibitor in 24-48 h`],[`Metoprolol`,`IV 5 mg up to 3 doses (selected); oral 25-50 mg 6-12 hourly then once daily succinate`,`Long term if LVEF 40% or less`,`Avoid in heart failure, shock risk, PR over 0.24 s, AV block, asthma, cocaine`],[`Atorvastatin`,`80 mg oral daily (or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg)`,`Lifelong; LDL under 55 mg/dL`,`Myalgia, transaminase rise`]]},{heading:`ECG criteria and STEMI equivalents`,columns:[`Pattern`,`ECG criterion`,`Usual culprit / meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`Anterior STEMI`,`ST elevation V1-V4 (V2-V3 thresholds 2/2.5/1.5 mm by sex and age)`,`LAD`,`Reperfuse; highest risk of LV failure and thrombus`],[`Inferior STEMI`,`ST elevation II, III, aVF; reciprocal depression I, aVL`,`RCA (III more than II) or LCx`,`Reperfuse; record V3R-V4R and V7-V9`],[`Lateral STEMI`,`ST elevation I, aVL, V5-V6`,`LCx or diagonal`,`Reperfuse; often electrically silent LCx`],[`Posterior MI`,`ST depression V1-V3 with tall R; ST elevation 0.5 mm or more in V7-V9`,`LCx or RCA`,`Treat as STEMI`],[`RV infarction`,`ST elevation 0.5 mm or more in V3R-V4R`,`Proximal RCA`,`Fluids, no nitrates or diuretics; reperfuse`],[`LBBB, RBBB or paced rhythm with ischaemic pain`,`Sgarbossa or Smith-modified Sgarbossa criteria`,`Possible occlusion`,`Primary PCI strategy`],[`de Winter T waves`,`1-3 mm upsloping ST depression V1-V6 with tall symmetrical T`,`Proximal LAD occlusion`,`Treat as STEMI`],[`aVR elevation with diffuse depression`,`ST depression 1 mm or more in 6+ leads, ST elevation aVR/V1`,`Left main or three-vessel ischaemia`,`Immediate angiography if unstable; no lysis`],[`Hyperacute T waves`,`Broad, tall, symmetrical T waves in a territory`,`Earliest phase of occlusion`,`Repeat ECG every 15-30 min`]]},{heading:`Contraindications to fibrinolysis`,columns:[`Type`,`Contraindication`,`Guideline note`],rows:[[`Absolute`,`Previous intracranial haemorrhage or stroke of unknown origin at any time`,`ESC 2023 and ACC/AHA agree`],[`Absolute`,`Ischaemic stroke in the preceding 6 months`,`ACC/AHA uses 3 months (except stroke within 4.5 h)`],[`Absolute`,`CNS damage, intracranial neoplasm or arteriovenous malformation`,`Both`],[`Absolute`,`Recent major trauma, surgery or head injury (within 1 month)`,`ACC/AHA: intracranial or spinal surgery within 2 months`],[`Absolute`,`Gastrointestinal bleeding within the past month; known bleeding disorder (not menses)`,`Both`],[`Absolute`,`Suspected aortic dissection`,`Both - check pulses and both-arm BP`],[`Absolute`,`Non-compressible puncture in the past 24 h (liver biopsy, lumbar puncture)`,`ESC 2023`],[`Relative`,`TIA in the preceding 6 months; oral anticoagulant therapy`,`Prefer primary PCI`],[`Relative`,`Pregnancy or within 1 week postpartum`,`Prefer primary PCI`],[`Relative`,`Refractory hypertension (SBP over 180 and/or DBP over 110 mmHg)`,`Lower BP first; ICH risk`],[`Relative`,`Advanced liver disease, infective endocarditis, active peptic ulcer, prolonged or traumatic CPR`,`Weigh against benefit`],[`Relative (streptokinase)`,`Prior streptokinase exposure`,`Use a fibrin-specific agent instead`]]},{heading:`Time targets in STEMI`,columns:[`Interval`,`ESC 2023 target`,`ACC/AHA 2025 target`],rows:[[`First medical contact to ECG and diagnosis`,`10 minutes or less`,`10 minutes or less`],[`Diagnosis to wire crossing, presenting at PCI centre`,`60 minutes or less`,`FMC-to-device 90 minutes or less`],[`Diagnosis to wire crossing, transferred`,`90 minutes or less`,`FMC-to-device 120 minutes or less`],[`Threshold to choose lysis instead of PCI`,`Expected delay over 120 minutes`,`Expected FMC-to-device over 120 minutes`],[`Diagnosis or arrival to lytic bolus`,`10 minutes or less from diagnosis`,`30 minutes or less from arrival (door-to-needle)`],[`Door-in-door-out at a non-PCI hospital`,`30 minutes or less (quality indicator)`,`30 minutes or less`],[`Assess success of lysis`,`60-90 minutes after start`,`60-90 minutes`],[`Angiography after successful lysis`,`2-24 hours`,`2-24 hours (2a)`]]},{heading:`Primary PCI, fibrinolysis and pharmaco-invasive strategy compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Primary PCI`,`Fibrinolysis alone`,`Pharmaco-invasive`],rows:[[`When used`,`Wire crossing possible within 120 min`,`Historic, or when no PCI access at all`,`PCI delay over 120 min, onset within 12 h`],[`Patency (TIMI 3 flow)`,`Over 90%`,`About 50-60% at 90 minutes`,`Lysis first, then mechanical opening`],[`Time sensitivity`,`Moderate`,`High - best in first 2-3 h`,`High for the lytic step`],[`ICH risk`,`About 0.1-0.2%`,`About 1%`,`About 0.5-1% (half dose at 75+)`],[`Reinfarction`,`Lowest`,`Higher (reocclusion)`,`Similar to primary PCI`],[`Infrastructure`,`24x7 cath lab and team`,`ECG, drug, monitor`,`Spoke lysis plus hub cath lab`],[`Indian relevance`,`Metro hubs`,`Where no transfer is possible`,`Default for district and taluk spokes`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of ST elevation`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating ECG feature`,`Clinical clue`],rows:[[`STEMI`,`Convex ST elevation in a territory with reciprocal depression; evolves`,`Ischaemic pain over 20 min, sweating`],[`Acute pericarditis`,`Diffuse concave ST elevation, PR depression, no reciprocal change`,`Pleuritic positional pain, rub, recent viral illness`],[`Early repolarisation`,`Notched J point, V2-V4, stable over time`,`Young, asymptomatic, athletic`],[`LV aneurysm`,`Persistent ST elevation with Q waves, small T waves`,`Old anterior MI`],[`Takotsubo`,`Anterior ST elevation, later deep T inversion, QT prolongation`,`Postmenopausal woman, stress trigger`],[`Aortic dissection`,`Often normal or inferior ST elevation`,`Tearing back pain, pulse deficit, wide mediastinum`],[`Hyperkalaemia`,`Peaked T waves, wide QRS, ST elevation V1-V2`,`CKD, ACE inhibitor, spironolactone`],[`Brugada pattern`,`Coved ST elevation V1-V2 with RBBB morphology`,`Syncope, family history of sudden death`]]}],redFlags:[`ST elevation or STEMI equivalent with ischaemic pain - reperfusion clock is running, activate PCI or lyse within 10 minutes`,`Failed fibrinolysis: ST resolution under 50% at 60-90 minutes or ongoing pain - immediate transfer for rescue PCI`,`Hypotension, cold periphery, oliguria or pulmonary oedema (Killip III-IV) - cardiogenic shock, emergency PCI`,`Tearing pain to the back, pulse or BP differential or wide mediastinum - suspect aortic dissection, no lysis`,`New pansystolic murmur or sudden haemodynamic collapse after MI - mechanical complication, urgent echo and surgery`,`Headache, vomiting, falling GCS or new deficit after lysis - suspect intracranial haemorrhage, stop anticoagulants, CT head`,`Recurrent ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, or complete heart block with haemodynamic compromise`],pearls:[`**120 minutes** is the magic number: expected delay to wire crossing over 120 minutes means lyse within 10 minutes.`,`**2-24 hours** after successful lysis for routine angiography; **immediately** if lysis fails - never 'facilitated PCI' straight after full-dose lysis.`,`**Tenecteplase 30-50 mg by 10 kg bands, halved at 75 years**, given as a single bolus - the spoke-friendly lytic.`,`**Streptokinase hypotension**: slow the infusion and raise the legs - do not abandon it; never give it twice.`,`**Lysis works on red (fibrin) clot**, which is why it helps STEMI and not NSTE-ACS.`,`**Accelerated idioventricular rhythm** after lysis is a reperfusion marker - do not treat it.`,`**Inferior STEMI: always record V4R** - right ventricular infarction changes the drugs (fluids, no nitrates).`,`**ESC 2023 ischaemic stroke exclusion is 6 months, ACC/AHA is 3 months** - a favourite viva contrast.`,`**Every 30 minutes of delay costs about 7.5% in relative 1-year mortality** - 'time is muscle'.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man reaches a taluk hospital 2 hours after onset of chest pain with 3 mm ST elevation in V1-V4. The nearest cath lab is 4 hours away by road. He weighs 72 kg and has no contraindications. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Load ticagrelor and transfer for primary PCI`,`Tenecteplase 40 mg IV bolus with aspirin, clopidogrel and enoxaparin, then transfer for angiography`,`Streptokinase 1.5 million IU after troponin confirms infarction`,`Admit for IV nitroglycerin and heparin and arrange elective angiography`],answer:1,explanation:`With an expected PCI delay far beyond 120 minutes and onset within 12 hours, ESC 2023 advises fibrinolysis within 10 minutes of diagnosis; tenecteplase for 70-79 kg is 40 mg, given with aspirin, clopidogrel 300 mg and enoxaparin, followed by transfer for angiography at 2-24 hours (pharmaco-invasive). Transferring for primary PCI wastes 4 hours of salvageable muscle, ticagrelor is not used with lysis, waiting for troponin is wrong in STEMI, and heparin with nitrates alone does not reperfuse.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-q2`,stem:`Ninety minutes after tenecteplase, a patient with inferior STEMI still has chest pain and the ST elevation has fallen by only 30%. What is the recommended management?`,options:[`Repeat the full dose of tenecteplase`,`Give streptokinase as a second lytic`,`Immediate transfer for rescue PCI`,`Continue heparin and plan angiography at 24 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`Less than 50% ST resolution at 60-90 minutes with persistent pain is failed fibrinolysis, and ESC 2023 recommends immediate rescue PCI (REACT trial). Repeating a lytic (same or streptokinase) adds bleeding without the reperfusion benefit of rescue PCI, and waiting for routine angiography at 24 hours is the pathway for successful lysis, not failure.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-q3`,stem:`Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to fibrinolysis in STEMI according to ESC 2023?`,options:[`Systolic BP of 190 mmHg on arrival that falls with nitrate`,`Warfarin with an INR of 2.4`,`Transient ischaemic attack 4 months ago`,`Ischaemic stroke 4 months ago`],answer:3,explanation:`ESC 2023 lists ischaemic stroke within the preceding 6 months as an absolute contraindication (ACC/AHA uses 3 months, so the same patient would be relative there). Refractory hypertension over 180/110, oral anticoagulation and TIA within 6 months are relative contraindications that tilt the choice towards primary PCI but do not absolutely forbid lysis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-q4`,stem:`A 66-year-old woman with central chest pain has 2 mm ST depression in V1-V3 with tall R waves and upright T waves. What is the best way to confirm the likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Record posterior leads V7-V9`,`Repeat troponin after 3 hours`,`Record right-sided leads V3R-V4R`,`Request a stress echocardiogram`],answer:0,explanation:`ST depression in V1-V3 with dominant R waves and upright T waves is the mirror image of posterior infarction; ST elevation of 0.5 mm or more in V7-V9 confirms a posterior STEMI, which qualifies for reperfusion. Waiting for troponin delays treatment of an occlusion, right-sided leads detect RV infarction rather than posterior MI, and stress testing is dangerous in ongoing ischaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-q5`,stem:`The pharmaco-invasive strategy in STEMI is best described as:`,options:[`Primary PCI with adjunctive intracoronary fibrinolytic`,`Fibrinolysis followed by routine angiography and PCI 2-24 hours later, with rescue PCI if lysis fails`,`Full-dose fibrinolysis followed by PCI within 1 hour in every patient`,`Fibrinolysis followed by a stress test before discharge to decide on angiography`],answer:1,explanation:`Pharmaco-invasive care (STREAM) combines early lysis with routine angiography at 2-24 hours, or immediately if lysis fails. Immediate PCI straight after full-dose lysis is facilitated PCI, which increased bleeding and death in ASSENT-4 PCI; intracoronary lysis is not part of the strategy; and an ischaemia-guided approach after lysis is the older conservative strategy that guidelines no longer prefer.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 55-year-old man presents to a district hospital 3 hours after onset of chest pain with ST elevation in II, III and aVF. Discuss the reperfusion options, the fibrinolytic regimen including contraindications, and how you would assess and follow up the result.`,openingLines:[`Inferior STEMI is acute thrombotic occlusion, usually of the right coronary artery, diagnosed on ECG; reperfusion within 12 hours of onset is Class I and must not wait for troponin.`,`Primary PCI is preferred if wire crossing is possible within 120 minutes of diagnosis; otherwise fibrinolysis within 10 minutes, followed by a pharmaco-invasive pathway.`],answer:[{heading:`Immediate assessment (first 10 minutes)`,points:[`ECG within 10 minutes; add **V3R-V4R** (RV infarct) and **V7-V9** (posterior extension).`,`Vitals, Killip class, both-arm BP and pulses (exclude dissection), neurological baseline.`,`Aspirin 150-300 mg chewed, IV line, monitor and defibrillator; oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%.`,`Nitrates only if SBP over 90 and no RV infarction.`]},{heading:`Choosing the reperfusion strategy`,points:[`Estimate time from diagnosis to wire crossing: **120 minutes or less - transfer for primary PCI**.`,`**Over 120 minutes - fibrinolysis** at the district hospital within 10 minutes (door-to-needle 30 minutes or less).`,`Over 12 hours: PCI only if ongoing symptoms or instability.`]},{heading:`Fibrinolytic regimen`,points:[`**Tenecteplase** single bolus by weight (30-50 mg), half dose at 75 years or more; or streptokinase 1.5 million IU over 30-60 minutes.`,`Plus **clopidogrel 300 mg** (75 mg if over 75 years) and **enoxaparin** 30 mg IV then 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly (or weight-based UFH).`,`Atorvastatin 80 mg; avoid IM injections.`]},{heading:`Contraindications (state absolute and relative)`,points:[`Absolute: prior ICH, ischaemic stroke in 6 months, CNS tumour or AVM, major trauma or surgery in 1 month, GI bleed in 1 month, bleeding disorder, **aortic dissection**, non-compressible puncture in 24 h.`,`Relative: TIA in 6 months, oral anticoagulants, pregnancy or 1 week postpartum, refractory BP over 180/110, liver disease, endocarditis, active peptic ulcer, traumatic CPR.`]},{heading:`Assessing success and the pharmaco-invasive pathway`,points:[`At **60-90 minutes**: 50% or more ST resolution, pain relief, AIVR, early enzyme peak = success.`,`**Failure - immediate rescue PCI**; success - routine angiography at **2-24 hours**.`,`Do not repeat lysis; do not perform immediate facilitated PCI.`]},{heading:`Monitoring, complications and follow-up`,points:[`CCU monitoring 24 hours or more: arrhythmias, AV block, RV infarction, ICH, bleeding.`,`Discharge bundle: DAPT, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker if indicated; cardiac rehabilitation.`,`Review at 1-2 weeks, echo at 6-12 weeks if LVEF 40% or less.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: STEMI diagnosis, PCI within 120 minutes or lysis within 10 minutes, assessment at 60-90 minutes, rescue PCI or angiography at 2-24 hours`,`Table of tenecteplase weight-band doses`],markSplit:[{part:`Initial assessment and ECG`,marks:1.5},{part:`Choice of strategy with time targets`,marks:2},{part:`Fibrinolytic and adjunct regimen`,marks:2},{part:`Contraindications`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment of success, rescue and pharmaco-invasive`,marks:1.5},{part:`Follow-up`,marks:1}],keywords:[`120 minutes`,`tenecteplase`,`rescue PCI`,`pharmaco-invasive`,`2-24 hours`,`V4R`,`contraindications`]},{id:`cardiovascular-stemi-reperfusion-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on the hub-and-spoke model of STEMI care in India.`,openingLines:[`The hub-and-spoke model links non-PCI hospitals (spokes) that diagnose STEMI and give fibrinolysis with 24x7 PCI centres (hubs) that provide rescue and routine PCI.`,`It delivers the pharmaco-invasive strategy where primary PCI within 120 minutes is impossible - the reality for most of rural India.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale`,points:[`Over half of Indian ACS is STEMI (CREATE), with long pre-hospital delay and few cath labs outside cities.`,`Lysis is time-critical; PCI is less so - so lyse early at the spoke and open the artery later at the hub.`]},{heading:`Components`,points:[`**Spoke**: ECG within 10 minutes, tele-ECG to the hub cardiologist, tenecteplase within 30 minutes of arrival.`,`**Hub**: 24x7 cath lab, rescue PCI for failed lysis, angiography at 2-24 hours after successful lysis.`,`**Transport**: 108 ambulance with defibrillator; door-in-door-out 30 minutes or less.`,`**Finance**: PM-JAY and state schemes cover lysis and PCI.`]},{heading:`Indian examples`,points:[`Tamil Nadu STEMI programme (JAMA Cardiology 2017).`,`Karnataka Puneeth Rajkumar Hrudaya Jyothi Yojane (2023) - free TNK and AI tele-ECG at taluk hospitals.`,`Mission DELHI (ICMR-AIIMS) motorbike first responders.`]},{heading:`Quality indicators and challenges`,points:[`Audit door-to-needle, door-in-door-out, reperfusion rate and 30-day mortality.`,`Challenges: public awareness, ambulance reach, drug supply, cardiologist availability for tele-ECG.`]}],mustDraw:[`Hub-and-spoke diagram: PHC/CHC to district spoke (TNK) to hub (PCI) with time targets`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and rationale`,marks:1},{part:`Components of spoke and hub`,marks:2},{part:`Indian programmes`,marks:1},{part:`Quality indicators and challenges`,marks:1}],keywords:[`hub-and-spoke`,`spoke`,`tenecteplase`,`tele-ECG`,`pharmaco-invasive`,`PM-JAY`,`door-in-door-out`]}],references:[`Byrne RA et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes. European Heart Journal 2023;44:3720-3826`,`Rao SV et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes. Circulation/JACC 2025`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - ST-Segment Elevation Myocardial Infarction`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - STEMI: pathophysiology and management`,`Thygesen K et al. Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction (2018). European Heart Journal 2019;40:237-269`,`Cardiological Society of India: position statement for the management of ST elevation myocardial infarction in India. Indian Heart Journal 2017`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Acute Coronary Syndrome / STEMI (ICMR-DHR)`,`Xavier D et al. Treatment and outcomes of acute coronary syndromes in India (CREATE). Lancet 2008;371:1435-1442`]}),n.push({id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs`,title:`NSTE-ACS: risk stratification and in-hospital management`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome (NSTE-ACS)** is acute myocardial ischaemia without persistent ST elevation, divided by **high-sensitivity troponin** into **NSTEMI** (rise and/or fall above the 99th percentile) and **unstable angina** (no troponin rise); it is diagnosed or excluded with the **ESC 0/1-hour hs-troponin algorithm**, risk-stratified with **GRACE**, treated with **aspirin, a P2Y12 inhibitor and parenteral anticoagulation** (never fibrinolysis), and taken for angiography **immediately (under 2 hours) if very high risk, within 24 hours if high risk**, or selectively otherwise.`,keywords:[`NSTEMI`,`unstable angina`,`NSTE-ACS`,`high-sensitivity troponin`,`0/1 hour algorithm`,`GRACE score`,`TIMI risk score`,`HEART score`,`early invasive strategy`,`fondaparinux`,`enoxaparin`,`ticagrelor`,`prasugrel`,`MINOCA`,`type 2 myocardial infarction`,`myocardial injury`,`Wellens syndrome`,`SCAD`,`CRUSADE`,`ARC-HBR`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**NSTE-ACS** covers patients with acute ischaemic symptoms and **no persistent ST elevation**; the ECG may show ST depression, T inversion or be normal. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**NSTEMI** = NSTE-ACS with a **rise and/or fall of cardiac troponin** with at least one value above the 99th percentile URL; **unstable angina** = ischaemic symptoms at rest or minimal effort **without** troponin elevation. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`With **hs-troponin assays**, more patients are reclassified from unstable angina to NSTEMI, so unstable angina is now uncommon and carries a lower risk. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`In high-income countries **NSTE-ACS now outnumbers STEMI** (about 2:1) and its incidence is rising with an ageing, more diabetic population. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In India **CREATE** found about **39% of ACS was NSTEMI or unstable angina**, with 30-day mortality of about **3.7%** - lower early but similar or higher long-term mortality than STEMI. [CREATE, Lancet 2008]`,`NSTE-ACS patients are **older, more often diabetic, hypertensive and with CKD**, and have more multivessel disease than STEMI patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`In-hospital mortality of NSTEMI is lower than STEMI (about 3-5%), but **6-month to 4-year mortality is similar or higher** because of age and comorbidity. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and causes of troponin rise`,points:[`**Type 1 MI**: atherothrombotic event - plaque rupture or erosion with a non-occlusive (or occluded with collaterals) thrombus. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Type 2 MI**: ischaemia from **supply-demand mismatch** without acute atherothrombosis - tachyarrhythmia, severe anaemia, hypotension or shock, hypoxaemia, severe hypertension, coronary spasm or embolism. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Non-ischaemic myocardial injury** raises troponin without ischaemia: myocarditis, sepsis, pulmonary embolism, heart failure, CKD, stroke, cardiac contusion, cardiotoxic chemotherapy and strenuous exercise. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Risk factors** for type 1 events mirror STEMI: diabetes, hypertension, smoking, dyslipidaemia, family history, CKD and South Asian ethnicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Spontaneous coronary artery dissection (SCAD)** causes ACS mainly in **women under 50**, around pregnancy and in fibromuscular dysplasia. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Precipitants of unstable angina**: anaemia, fever, thyrotoxicosis, uncontrolled hypertension, tachyarrhythmia and stopping antianginal drugs (Braunwald secondary unstable angina). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cocaine and amphetamine use** cause NSTE-ACS by vasospasm and thrombosis. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The usual lesion is a disrupted plaque with a **platelet-rich (white) non-occlusive thrombus**, causing intermittent ischaemia and distal microembolisation that releases troponin. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Because the thrombus is platelet-rich and the artery is partly open, **antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy** is effective while **fibrinolysis is useless or harmful** (it activates platelets and may convert a non-occlusive thrombus into an occlusive one). [FTT Collaborative, Lancet 1994]`,`**Subendocardial ischaemia** produces ST depression; the subendocardium is most vulnerable because it has the highest wall stress and is perfused only in diastole. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **25% of NSTEMI patients have an occluded culprit artery** at angiography (often circumflex), showing that the ECG misses some occlusion MIs. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Troponin release** follows myocyte necrosis; hs-assays detect very small amounts, so the **dynamic change (delta)**, not a single value, separates acute from chronic injury. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Anti-ischaemic drugs** act on demand and supply: beta-blockers lower heart rate and contractility, nitrates reduce preload and dilate coronaries, and calcium-channel blockers relieve spasm. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Inflammation** (raised CRP, IL-6) destabilises plaque - the rationale for statins and colchicine. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification - MI types, unstable angina and risk groups`,points:[`**UDMI-4 MI types**: 1 atherothrombotic, 2 supply-demand, 3 sudden cardiac death before biomarkers, 4a PCI-related, 4b stent thrombosis, 4c restenosis, 5 CABG-related. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Type 4a** requires troponin over **5 times the URL** within 48 hours of PCI plus new ischaemia evidence; **type 5** requires over **10 times the URL** plus new Q waves, graft occlusion or new loss of viable myocardium. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Unstable angina** presents as rest angina over 20 minutes, new-onset severe angina (CCS III), or crescendo angina (more frequent, longer, lower threshold). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Braunwald classification** of unstable angina: severity I-III (new severe, subacute rest, acute rest within 48 h), circumstance A-C (secondary, primary, post-infarction) - class IIIB troponin-positive carries the highest risk. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ESC 2023 risk groups** decide timing: **very high risk** (immediate invasive, under 2 hours), **high risk** (early invasive, under 24 hours) and **not high risk** (selective invasive). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**MINOCA** is MI with **no stenosis of 50% or more** in a major epicardial artery and no overt alternative diagnosis - a working diagnosis that prompts a search for the cause. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Bleeding risk** is classified by **ARC-HBR** criteria (one major or two minor) or PRECISE-DAPT; it shapes DAPT duration, not the decision to treat. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Rest angina** over 20 minutes, **new-onset** angina or **crescendo** angina, often with sweating, nausea or breathlessness. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Atypical presentations** - breathlessness, epigastric pain, fatigue, syncope - are common in the elderly, women, diabetics and CKD, and delay diagnosis. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Features **favouring ischaemia**: exertional or pressure-like pain, radiation to both arms, similar to prior angina, relief by rest; **features against**: pleuritic, positional, reproducible on palpation, seconds-long pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask the **time of the last episode** of pain - the hs-troponin rule-out at 0 hours applies only if pain began over 3 hours earlier. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Look for **precipitants of type 2 MI**: bleeding or anaemia, fever or sepsis, tachyarrhythmia, hypoxia, severe hypertension. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`Record **bleeding history**, prior stroke, anticoagulant use, recent surgery and renal disease - they decide P2Y12 and anticoagulant choice. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Ask about **PDE5 inhibitor use** (sildenafil in 24 hours, tadalafil in 48 hours) before giving nitrates, and about cocaine. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`Examination is **often normal** between episodes; its purpose is to find complications, precipitants and alternative diagnoses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure signs** (crackles, S3, raised JVP) or **hypotension** mean very high risk and need immediate angiography. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`A **new or louder mitral regurgitation murmur** during pain signals ischaemic papillary muscle dysfunction and extensive ischaemia. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pallor and tachycardia** suggest anaemia or bleeding as a type 2 MI driver; **fever** suggests sepsis or myocarditis. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`Check **both-arm BP, pulses and aortic murmur** for dissection, a **pericardial rub** for pericarditis and calf swelling or hypoxia for pulmonary embolism. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **xanthelasma, arcus, bruits and absent foot pulses** - markers of polyvascular atherosclerosis and higher risk. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Cardiac**: myocarditis, pericarditis, Takotsubo syndrome, tachyarrhythmia, aortic stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and acute heart failure. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Vascular**: aortic dissection and **pulmonary embolism** - both raise troponin and are made worse by mistaken full anticoagulation or antiplatelets (dissection). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary**: pneumothorax, pneumonia and pleurisy - pleuritic pain, hypoxia and chest X-ray changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gastrointestinal**: oesophageal spasm and reflux, peptic ulcer, pancreatitis and biliary colic - the commonest cause of 'acidity' presentations that are really ACS and vice versa. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Musculoskeletal and psychological**: costochondritis (reproducible tenderness), herpes zoster before the rash and panic attacks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic myocardial injury** (CKD, heart failure) gives a stable raised troponin with **no significant delta** - not an MI. [UDMI-4 2018]`]},{heading:`Investigations and the hs-troponin algorithms`,points:[`**12-lead ECG within 10 minutes**; repeat during pain and at 3 hours; add V7-V9 if posterior MI is suspected - a normal ECG does not exclude NSTE-ACS. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**High-risk ECG patterns**: ST depression of 0.5 mm or more (especially in 6 or more leads with aVR elevation), transient ST elevation and **Wellens T waves** (biphasic or deep inverted T in V2-V3, critical proximal LAD - no stress test). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 0/1-hour algorithm** (preferred): rule out if 0-h hs-troponin is **very low** with pain over 3 hours, or low with a **small 1-hour delta**; rule in if 0-h is **high** or the 1-hour delta is **large**; otherwise observe and repeat at 3 hours. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`For **Roche hs-cTnT**: rule out 0 h under 5 ng/L, or under 12 ng/L with delta under 3; rule in 0 h 52 ng/L or more, or delta 5 or more - cut-offs are **assay-specific**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`The 0/1-hour rule-out has a **negative predictive value over 99%** for MI and the rule-in a positive predictive value of about 70-75%; about a quarter of patients fall in the observe zone. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`The **0/2-hour algorithm** is the alternative; with only conventional or point-of-care troponin, repeat at **3-6 hours** - a single early negative point-of-care test does not exclude MI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Echocardiography** in all: regional wall motion, LVEF, mechanical complications and alternative diagnoses (dissection, PE signs, pericardial effusion, aortic stenosis). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**CT coronary angiography** helps when troponin and ECG are non-diagnostic and suspicion is low to intermediate, to rule out obstructive disease. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Other tests**: CBC (anaemia), creatinine and eGFR (drug dosing), electrolytes, glucose, HbA1c, lipid profile, TSH if tachycardic, and chest X-ray. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Risk stratification and timing of invasive strategy`,points:[`**GRACE score** (age, heart rate, SBP, creatinine, Killip class, cardiac arrest, ST deviation, raised troponin) predicts in-hospital and 6-month death; **over 140 = high risk**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Very high risk - immediate invasive (under 2 hours)**: haemodynamic instability or cardiogenic shock, recurrent or refractory pain despite treatment, life-threatening arrhythmia or arrest, mechanical complication, acute heart failure from ischaemia, recurrent dynamic or intermittent ST elevation. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**High risk - early invasive (within 24 hours)**: confirmed NSTEMI by hs-troponin algorithm, dynamic ST or T changes, transient ST elevation, or **GRACE over 140** (ESC 2023 IIa). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Not high risk - selective invasive**: angiography only if recurrent symptoms or ischaemia on non-invasive testing (stress imaging or CT coronary angiography) before or soon after discharge. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025** agrees: immediate invasive for very high risk (Class 1), early invasive within 24 hours reasonable for GRACE over 140 (2a), and an invasive strategy during admission for intermediate and high risk (1). [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**TIMI risk score** for UA/NSTEMI (7 points: age 65+, 3+ risk factors, known stenosis 50%+, aspirin in 7 days, 2+ angina episodes in 24 h, ST deviation 0.5 mm+, raised marker) is simple for bedside triage. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**HEART score** (History, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin) guides emergency discharge: **0-3 low risk** (MACE under 2%), 4-6 moderate, 7-10 high. [ACC/AHA Chest Pain 2021]`,`**Bleeding risk** with ARC-HBR, PRECISE-DAPT or CRUSADE is assessed at the same time to choose radial access, P2Y12 agent and DAPT duration. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Complete revascularisation** should be considered in NSTE-ACS with multivessel disease (ESC 2023 IIa); CABG is preferred for left main or complex three-vessel disease, especially with diabetes. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD or emergency room - first hour`,points:[`Any adult with suspected ACS in a clinic needs an **ECG within 10 minutes**, aspirin and **referral by ambulance** - do not send home on a single normal ECG or troponin. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Aspirin 150-300 mg chewed** loading, then 75-100 mg daily. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg** every 5 minutes up to 3 doses for pain; IV nitroglycerin for persistent pain, hypertension or heart failure (avoid if SBP under 90 or PDE5 inhibitor use). [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%**; **IV morphine** sparingly for refractory pain, as it delays oral P2Y12 absorption. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Routine P2Y12 pretreatment is not recommended** when early angiography (under 24 hours) is planned; it may be considered if angiography is delayed and bleeding risk is low (ESC 2023 IIb). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`In a **district hospital without a cath lab**, a confirmed or high-risk NSTE-ACS should be loaded and **transferred for angiography within 24 hours**; very high risk needs immediate transfer. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Fibrinolysis is contraindicated** in NSTE-ACS (no benefit, possible harm). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Start **high-intensity statin** (atorvastatin 80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg) early, regardless of baseline LDL. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`In-hospital antithrombotic and anti-ischaemic therapy`,points:[`**DAPT**: aspirin plus a potent P2Y12 inhibitor - **ticagrelor 180 mg then 90 mg twice daily** or **prasugrel 60 mg then 10 mg daily** (after the coronary anatomy is known); **clopidogrel 300-600 mg then 75 mg** if these are contraindicated or with OAC. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`ESC 2023 says **prasugrel should be considered in preference to ticagrelor** for NSTE-ACS patients proceeding to PCI (ISAR-REACT 5); prasugrel is avoided after stroke or TIA and reduced to 5 mg at 75+ years or under 60 kg. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Parenteral anticoagulation for all** at diagnosis: **UFH** (70-100 IU/kg during PCI), **enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly** (once daily if eGFR under 30), or **fondaparinux 2.5 mg SC daily**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fondaparinux is preferred when angiography is not expected within 24 hours** (OASIS-5: less bleeding than enoxaparin), but an **extra UFH bolus** is mandatory at PCI to prevent catheter thrombosis; avoid if eGFR under 20. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Stop parenteral anticoagulation **after PCI** unless there is another indication; in conservatively managed patients continue until discharge or up to 8 days. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors** (tirofiban, eptifibatide) are for **bail-out** only - no-reflow or thrombotic complications during PCI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Default DAPT is 12 months**; ESC 2023 allows shortening (1-3 months then monotherapy) in high bleeding risk, and **ACC/AHA 2025 recommends ticagrelor monotherapy after 1 month** in patients tolerating DAPT - see the Antiplatelet therapy topic. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Oral beta-blocker** within 24 hours if no heart failure, low output or shock risk; **avoid beta-blockers in suspected vasospasm or cocaine use**, where a calcium-channel blocker is used. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**PPI** (pantoprazole 40 mg daily) for patients on DAPT at high GI bleeding risk; avoid omeprazole and esomeprazole with clopidogrel. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACE inhibitor** for LVEF 40% or less, diabetes, hypertension or CKD, and **SGLT2 inhibitor** if heart failure or diabetes, as for secondary prevention. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`MINOCA, type 2 MI and unstable angina - special entities`,points:[`**MINOCA** occurs in about **5-10% of MIs**, more often in **women and younger patients**; causes include plaque disruption, coronary spasm, coronary embolism, SCAD and microvascular dysfunction. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Cardiac MRI is Class I** in MINOCA to separate true MI from **myocarditis and Takotsubo** (which are then excluded from the MINOCA label); intravascular imaging and provocative spasm testing find the cause. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**MINOCA treatment follows the cause**: statin and ACE inhibitor or ARB (associated with better outcomes in SWEDEHEART), DAPT for plaque disruption, **calcium-channel blockers for spasm** and anticoagulation for embolism. [AHA MINOCA Statement 2019]`,`**SCAD** is managed **conservatively** where possible (most heal), with beta-blockers and avoidance of unnecessary stenting; PCI only for ongoing ischaemia or instability. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Type 2 MI** is treated by **correcting the trigger** (transfusion, rate control, oxygen, BP control); routine DAPT and anticoagulation are not indicated and may cause harm, especially when bleeding is the trigger. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`**Type 2 MI has a poor prognosis** (2-year mortality often over 30%) because of comorbidity; look for underlying coronary disease once the patient is stable. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Unstable angina** with negative hs-troponin is lower risk; manage with DAPT and anticoagulation while assessing, and angiography for recurrent pain or dynamic ECG changes; others get non-invasive testing. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly (75+)**: same invasive benefit in fit patients (ACC/AHA 2025 2a; SENIOR-RITA reduced non-fatal MI), but adjust doses - clopidogrel or ticagrelor over prasugrel, enoxaparin 0.75 mg/kg, and assess frailty. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**CKD**: invasive strategy still helps; adjust enoxaparin, avoid fondaparinux if eGFR under 20, minimise contrast with isotonic saline hydration, and use UFH in severe CKD. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Diabetes**: higher risk and more multivessel disease; prefer **CABG for multivessel disease**, monitor glucose (treat over 180 mg/dL) and add SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 RA. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Atrial fibrillation on anticoagulant**: short triple therapy (OAC + aspirin + clopidogrel up to 1 week), then **DOAC + clopidogrel** to 12 months, then DOAC alone - see the Anticoagulants topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Women**: present atypically, are under-investigated, and have more MINOCA and SCAD - apply the same algorithms and invasive criteria. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Pregnancy**: SCAD is the commonest cause; troponin algorithms remain valid; manage with a cardio-obstetric team - see Heart disease in pregnancy. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if only point-of-care troponin I is available, repeat at 3-6 hours and use GRACE or HEART with ECG to decide transfer; enoxaparin and clopidogrel are widely available on the NLEM. [ICMR STW ACS]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**In-hospital complications**: recurrent ischaemia, heart failure, arrhythmias, bleeding (strongly linked to mortality), contrast nephropathy and stroke. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Bleeding is prognostic**: a major bleed raises 30-day mortality several-fold, so radial access, correct dosing and PPI matter as much as antithrombotic potency. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Long-term risk** remains high: about 1 in 5 patients has a further event within 3 years without good secondary prevention. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Refer** every confirmed NSTEMI, high-risk unstable angina or GRACE over 140 to a PCI-capable centre; very high-risk features mean same-hour transfer. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Discharge bundle**: DAPT, high-intensity statin with LDL target under 55 mg/dL, ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker if indicated, SGLT2 inhibitor where indicated and **cardiac rehabilitation** - see the After the MI topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Follow-up**: review at 1-2 weeks, lipids at 4-6 weeks, echo at 6-12 weeks if LVEF was low, and adherence checks at each visit. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2023 ACS** merged NSTE-ACS and STEMI, **downgraded routine early invasive (under 24 h) to IIa**, removed the 72-hour intermediate-risk category and added a MINOCA working-diagnosis pathway with CMR Class I. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 2023**: no routine P2Y12 pretreatment when early angiography is planned; prasugrel preferred over ticagrelor in NSTE-ACS going to PCI; complete revascularisation in multivessel NSTE-ACS IIa. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025 ACS** (first joint ACS guideline): early invasive within 24 hours reasonable if GRACE over 140, invasive strategy in older adults reasonable, ticagrelor monotherapy after 1 month of DAPT recommended. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025 lipid thresholds**: add a non-statin if LDL stays **70 mg/dL or more** on maximal statin (Class 1) and consider it at **55-69 mg/dL** (2a). [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**SENIOR-RITA (2024)**: invasive strategy in patients aged 75+ with NSTEMI reduced non-fatal MI and repeat revascularisation without reducing cardiovascular death. [SENIOR-RITA, NEJM 2024]`,`**ESC 2023** endorses the **0/1-hour and 0/2-hour hs-troponin algorithms** with assay-specific cut-offs as the standard rule-in and rule-out pathway. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **'Risk stratification in NSTE-ACS'**, **'High-sensitivity troponin'**, **'MINOCA'** and **'Type 2 MI'**. [DNB FM papers]`,`**Never thrombolyse NSTE-ACS** - examiners always test this. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`A **normal ECG or single normal troponin does not exclude ACS** - insist on serial testing at 1-3 hours. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fondaparinux needs a UFH bolus at PCI** - the OASIS-5 catheter thrombosis lesson. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Wellens T waves** in a pain-free patient mean critical proximal LAD stenosis - angiography, not a stress test. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Type 2 MI is not treated with DAPT**; treat the anaemia, tachycardia or hypotension. [UDMI-4 2018]`,`Know the **very high-risk list** for immediate angiography: shock, refractory pain, arrhythmia or arrest, mechanical complication, acute HF, intermittent ST elevation. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in NSTE-ACS`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`150-300 mg chewed loading, then 75-100 mg daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI bleeding, allergy; PPI if high GI risk`],[`Ticagrelor`,`180 mg loading, then 90 mg twice daily`,`12 months (ACC/AHA 2025: monotherapy after 1 month)`,`Dyspnoea, bradyarrhythmia, bleeding; avoid with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors or prior ICH`],[`Prasugrel`,`60 mg loading once anatomy known, then 10 mg daily (5 mg if 75+ or under 60 kg)`,`12 months`,`Contraindicated after stroke or TIA; bleeding`],[`Clopidogrel`,`300-600 mg loading, then 75 mg daily`,`12 months; preferred with OAC`,`Bleeding; CYP2C19 poor metabolisers; avoid omeprazole`],[`Fondaparinux`,`2.5 mg SC once daily`,`Until discharge or 8 days; UFH bolus at PCI`,`Catheter thrombosis without UFH; avoid if eGFR under 20`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly (once daily if eGFR under 30)`,`Until PCI or up to 8 days`,`Bleeding; avoid switching to UFH mid-course`],[`Unfractionated heparin`,`70-100 IU/kg IV bolus at PCI; conservative: 60 IU/kg (max 4000) then 12 IU/kg/h`,`aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control`,`Bleeding, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`0.4 mg SL every 5 min x 3; IV 5-10 microgram/min titrated`,`For ischaemic pain, BP, HF`,`Hypotension, headache; avoid with PDE5 inhibitors, SBP under 90`],[`Metoprolol`,`25-50 mg oral 6-12 hourly, then succinate once daily`,`Long term if LVEF 40% or less`,`Avoid in HF, shock risk, AV block, asthma, cocaine, spasm`],[`Atorvastatin`,`80 mg oral daily (or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg)`,`Lifelong; LDL under 55 mg/dL`,`Myalgia, transaminase rise`],[`Tirofiban`,`25 microgram/kg IV bolus, then 0.15 microgram/kg/min`,`Bail-out during PCI, up to 18 h`,`Bleeding, thrombocytopenia; halve infusion if eGFR under 30`],[`Pantoprazole`,`40 mg oral daily`,`While on DAPT if GI risk`,`Low; preferred PPI with clopidogrel`]]},{heading:`ESC 0/1-hour hs-troponin cut-offs (ng/L) for two common assays`,columns:[`Assay`,`Rule out: very low 0 h`,`Rule out: low 0 h and 1-h delta`,`Rule in: high 0 h or 1-h delta`],rows:[[`Roche Elecsys hs-cTnT`,`Under 5 (if pain over 3 h)`,`Under 12 and delta under 3`,`52 or more, or delta 5 or more`],[`Abbott Architect hs-cTnI`,`Under 4 (if pain over 3 h)`,`Under 5 and delta under 2`,`64 or more, or delta 6 or more`],[`All assays - action`,`Discharge or outpatient test if clinically low risk`,`Outpatient CTCA or stress test if indicated`,`Admit, antithrombotics, angiography by risk`],[`Observe zone`,`Neither rule-in nor rule-out`,`Repeat hs-troponin at 3 h, echo`,`About 25% of patients`]]},{heading:`Timing of invasive strategy (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Risk group`,`Criteria`,`Timing`],rows:[[`Very high risk`,`Shock or haemodynamic instability; refractory or recurrent pain; life-threatening arrhythmia or arrest; mechanical complication; acute HF from ACS; recurrent dynamic or intermittent ST elevation`,`Immediate - under 2 hours`],[`High risk`,`Confirmed NSTEMI; dynamic ST/T changes; transient ST elevation; GRACE over 140`,`Early - within 24 hours (IIa)`],[`Not high risk`,`None of the above, low clinical suspicion`,`Selective - non-invasive testing first`]]},{heading:`Universal definition: types of MI and myocardial injury`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Example`,`Treatment focus`],rows:[[`Type 1`,`Atherothrombosis (plaque rupture or erosion)`,`Classic NSTEMI or STEMI`,`Antithrombotics, revascularisation`],[`Type 2`,`Supply-demand mismatch`,`Anaemia, AF with fast rate, shock, hypoxia, spasm`,`Correct the trigger; no routine DAPT`],[`Type 3`,`Sudden cardiac death before biomarkers`,`Death with ischaemic symptoms and new ECG change`,`Autopsy diagnosis`],[`Type 4a / 4b / 4c`,`PCI-related (over 5x URL), stent thrombosis, restenosis`,`Post-PCI troponin rise`,`Interventional review`],[`Type 5`,`CABG-related (over 10x URL)`,`Graft occlusion`,`Surgical review`],[`Non-ischaemic injury`,`Troponin rise without ischaemia`,`Myocarditis, sepsis, PE, CKD, HF`,`Treat the cause`]]},{heading:`Risk scores in NSTE-ACS`,columns:[`Score`,`Components`,`Use`,`Key cut-off`],rows:[[`GRACE`,`Age, HR, SBP, creatinine, Killip, arrest, ST deviation, troponin`,`Death in hospital and at 6 months; timing of angiography`,`Over 140 = high risk`],[`TIMI (UA/NSTEMI)`,`Age 65+, 3+ risk factors, known CAD, aspirin in 7 days, 2+ episodes, ST deviation, marker`,`Bedside triage`,`5-7 = high risk`],[`HEART`,`History, ECG, Age, Risk factors, Troponin`,`Emergency chest pain discharge`,`0-3 low risk`],[`CRUSADE`,`Haematocrit, CrCl, HR, sex, HF, vascular disease, diabetes, SBP`,`In-hospital major bleeding`,`Over 40 = high bleeding risk`],[`ARC-HBR / PRECISE-DAPT`,`Major and minor bleeding criteria; Hb, WBC, age, CrCl, prior bleed`,`DAPT duration`,`HBR or score 25+`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of chest pain with raised troponin`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`NSTEMI (type 1)`,`Ischaemic pain, rise and fall of troponin, ischaemic ECG`,`Serial hs-troponin, angiography`],[`Type 2 MI`,`Clear trigger: anaemia, tachyarrhythmia, hypotension, hypoxia`,`CBC, ECG rhythm, SpO2`],[`Myocarditis`,`Young, viral prodrome, diffuse changes`,`Cardiac MRI`],[`Takotsubo`,`Postmenopausal woman, stress, apical ballooning`,`Echo, angiography, CMR`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Hypoxia, tachycardia, RV strain, DVT`,`CT pulmonary angiography`],[`Aortic dissection`,`Tearing pain, pulse deficit`,`CT aortogram`],[`Chronic injury (CKD, HF)`,`Stable raised troponin, no delta`,`Serial troponin`]]}],redFlags:[`Refractory or recurrent rest pain despite nitrates and antithrombotics - immediate angiography`,`Hypotension, cardiogenic shock or acute pulmonary oedema with ischaemic ECG changes`,`Ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation or resuscitated cardiac arrest`,`Intermittent or transient ST elevation, or ST depression in 6 or more leads with ST elevation in aVR`,`New mitral regurgitation murmur or other mechanical complication on echo`,`Wellens T waves in a pain-free patient - critical LAD stenosis, do not stress test`,`Major bleeding on DAPT and anticoagulation - stop anticoagulant, resuscitate, endoscopy`],pearls:[`**Troponin is about the delta**: a stable raised value is chronic injury, a rising and falling value is acute.`,`**0/1 hour rule-out works only if pain began over 3 hours ago** for the very-low single-sample arm.`,`**GRACE over 140 = angiography within 24 hours** (ESC IIa, ACC/AHA 2a).`,`**Very high risk mnemonic 'SHARP-E'**: Shock, Heart failure, Arrhythmia or arrest, Refractory pain, Papillary or mechanical complication, Elevation that comes and goes.`,`**Fondaparinux - fewer bleeds, but always add UFH at PCI.**`,`**Prasugrel after anatomy, never after stroke**; halve to 5 mg at 75 years or under 60 kg.`,`**MINOCA is a working diagnosis** - cardiac MRI separates myocarditis and Takotsubo from true infarction.`,`**Type 2 MI: fix the trigger, not the platelets.**`,`**No lysis in NSTE-ACS** - white platelet clot does not dissolve with fibrinolytics.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old man has had chest pain that started 5 hours ago and settled. ECG is normal. Roche hs-cTnT is 4 ng/L at presentation. What does the ESC 0/1-hour algorithm advise?`,options:[`Repeat hs-cTnT at 1 hour before any decision`,`Rule out MI on the single sample and consider discharge with outpatient evaluation`,`Rule in NSTEMI and load ticagrelor`,`Observe and repeat troponin at 6 hours`],answer:1,explanation:`For Roche hs-cTnT a 0-hour value under 5 ng/L with chest pain onset more than 3 hours earlier rules out MI on a single sample (NPV over 99%), so discharge with outpatient assessment is appropriate if clinically low risk. A 1-hour repeat is needed only when the 0-hour value is low but not very low or pain is recent; rule-in needs 52 ng/L or a large delta; the 6-hour repeat belongs to older conventional assays.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-q2`,stem:`A 64-year-old diabetic woman with confirmed NSTEMI develops recurrent chest pain despite IV nitroglycerin and has a BP of 84/50 mmHg. What is the timing of coronary angiography per ESC 2023?`,options:[`Within 72 hours`,`Within 24 hours`,`Only if a stress test is positive`,`Immediately, within 2 hours`],answer:3,explanation:`Refractory pain and haemodynamic instability are very-high-risk features requiring an immediate invasive strategy (under 2 hours). The 24-hour window is for high-risk patients (confirmed NSTEMI, dynamic ST change, GRACE over 140) who are stable; ESC 2023 removed the 72-hour category; and a selective, stress-test-guided approach is only for low-risk patients.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-q3`,stem:`A patient with NSTEMI is started on fondaparinux 2.5 mg daily because angiography is planned in 48 hours. At the time of PCI, what must be added?`,options:[`A standard bolus of unfractionated heparin`,`Double the fondaparinux dose`,`Routine tirofiban infusion`,`Nothing extra - fondaparinux is sufficient`],answer:0,explanation:`OASIS-5 showed fondaparinux reduces bleeding compared with enoxaparin but carries a risk of catheter thrombosis during PCI, so a full UFH bolus is given in the cath lab. Increasing fondaparinux does not solve catheter thrombosis and adds bleeding, GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors are reserved for bail-out, and giving nothing extra is the error OASIS-5 exposed.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-q4`,stem:`A 70-year-old woman admitted with a GI bleed (Hb 6.2 g/dL) has a heart rate of 118, ST depression in V4-V6 and a rising troponin. What is the most appropriate management of the troponin rise?`,options:[`Aspirin, ticagrelor and enoxaparin with angiography within 24 hours`,`Fibrinolysis after blood transfusion`,`Treat the bleeding and transfuse; no routine antithrombotics`,`Immediate coronary angiography within 2 hours`],answer:2,explanation:`This is a type 2 MI from supply-demand mismatch caused by bleeding and anaemia; management is correcting the trigger (transfusion, endoscopic control, rate settles) while avoiding antithrombotics that worsen bleeding. Full DAPT with anticoagulation suits type 1 NSTEMI, fibrinolysis is never used in NSTE-ACS, and immediate angiography would need heparin in an actively bleeding patient without addressing the cause.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-q5`,stem:`A 46-year-old woman with MI has angiography showing no stenosis of 50% or more. What is the most useful next investigation?`,options:[`Exercise treadmill test`,`Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging`,`Repeat coronary angiography after 1 month`,`24-hour Holter monitoring`],answer:1,explanation:`This is a working diagnosis of MINOCA, and ESC 2023 gives cardiac MRI a Class I recommendation to distinguish true infarction from myocarditis and Takotsubo and to define the cause. A treadmill test adds nothing when the anatomy is known, repeat angiography without a new indication is not useful, and Holter only addresses arrhythmia.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the diagnosis, risk stratification and in-hospital management of non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome, with reference to current guidelines.`,openingLines:[`NSTE-ACS is acute myocardial ischaemia without persistent ST elevation, comprising NSTEMI (dynamic troponin rise above the 99th percentile) and unstable angina (no troponin rise).`,`Diagnosis uses the ESC 0/1-hour hs-troponin algorithm; risk stratification with GRACE decides whether angiography is immediate, within 24 hours or selective.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and pathophysiology`,points:[`Plaque disruption with a platelet-rich non-occlusive thrombus and microembolisation.`,`UDMI-4: type 1 versus type 2 MI and non-ischaemic injury.`,`Why fibrinolysis is not used.`]},{heading:`Clinical diagnosis`,points:[`Rest, new-onset or crescendo angina; atypical in elderly, women, diabetics.`,`ECG: ST depression, T inversion, Wellens, may be normal; repeat during pain.`]},{heading:`hs-troponin algorithm`,points:[`0/1-hour: rule out (very low 0 h, or low with small delta), rule in (high 0 h or large delta), observe (repeat at 3 h).`,`Assay-specific cut-offs (e.g. hs-cTnT under 5 / 52 ng/L); NPV over 99%.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification`,points:[`GRACE (over 140 high), TIMI, HEART for emergency triage.`,`Bleeding risk: ARC-HBR, PRECISE-DAPT, CRUSADE.`,`Very high, high and not high risk groups with timing (under 2 h, under 24 h, selective).`]},{heading:`In-hospital management`,points:[`Aspirin, P2Y12 (prasugrel or ticagrelor; clopidogrel with OAC), no routine pretreatment if early angiography.`,`Anticoagulant: fondaparinux (UFH bolus at PCI), enoxaparin or UFH.`,`Nitrates, beta-blocker, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, PPI; oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%.`,`Revascularisation: PCI or CABG (left main, diabetic multivessel).`]},{heading:`Special entities and discharge`,points:[`MINOCA (CMR), type 2 MI (treat trigger), SCAD (conservative).`,`Discharge: DAPT 12 months (shortening options), LDL under 55 mg/dL, cardiac rehabilitation, follow-up at 1-2 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of the ESC 0/1-hour hs-troponin algorithm`,`Table of risk groups and timing of invasive strategy`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical and ECG diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`hs-troponin algorithm`,marks:2},{part:`Risk stratification and timing`,marks:2},{part:`Antithrombotic and anti-ischaemic treatment`,marks:2.5},{part:`Special entities and discharge`,marks:1}],keywords:[`NSTEMI`,`unstable angina`,`0/1 hour`,`GRACE`,`early invasive`,`fondaparinux`,`prasugrel`,`MINOCA`]},{id:`cardiovascular-nste-acs-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate type 1 from type 2 myocardial infarction.`,openingLines:[`Both meet the Fourth Universal Definition: a rise and/or fall of troponin above the 99th percentile with clinical evidence of ischaemia.`,`Type 1 is caused by acute atherothrombosis; type 2 by an imbalance of oxygen supply and demand without acute atherothrombosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and causes`,points:[`Type 1: plaque rupture or erosion with thrombus.`,`Type 2: anaemia, tachyarrhythmia, hypotension, hypoxia, severe hypertension, spasm, embolism.`]},{heading:`Clinical setting`,points:[`Type 1: typical ACS presentation, often previously well.`,`Type 2: hospitalised or acutely ill patient (sepsis, bleeding, surgery), often elderly with comorbidity.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Type 1: DAPT, anticoagulant, angiography and revascularisation by risk.`,`Type 2: correct the trigger; no routine DAPT or anticoagulation; later assess for CAD.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`Type 2 has higher short- and long-term mortality, driven by comorbidity.`,`Distinguish from non-ischaemic myocardial injury, which lacks ischaemic evidence.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: mechanism, causes, ECG, angiography, treatment, prognosis`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Mechanism and causes`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Prognosis and injury`,marks:1}],keywords:[`type 1 MI`,`type 2 MI`,`supply-demand`,`UDMI-4`,`myocardial injury`]}],references:[`Byrne RA et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes. European Heart Journal 2023;44:3720-3826`,`Rao SV et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes. Circulation/JACC 2025`,`Thygesen K et al. Fourth Universal Definition of Myocardial Infarction (2018). European Heart Journal 2019;40:237-269`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Non-ST-Segment Elevation Acute Coronary Syndrome`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - NSTE-ACS`,`Tamis-Holland JE et al. Contemporary diagnosis and management of MINOCA: AHA Scientific Statement. Circulation 2019`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows - Acute Coronary Syndrome (ICMR-DHR)`,`Xavier D et al. Treatment and outcomes of acute coronary syndromes in India (CREATE). Lancet 2008;371:1435-1442`]}),n.push({id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications`,title:`Complications of myocardial infarction`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`Complications of MI are **electrical** (VF, VT, AF, bradycardia and **AV block**), **pump** (heart failure, **cardiogenic shock**, **RV infarction**), **mechanical** (free wall rupture, **ventricular septal rupture**, **papillary muscle rupture**), **thromboembolic** (LV thrombus) and **inflammatory or structural** (pericarditis, Dressler syndrome, LV aneurysm); each has a characteristic **time window** - arrhythmias in the first 48 hours, ruptures at **3-7 days**, Dressler at weeks - and mechanical complications need **echo-led diagnosis and emergency surgery**.`,keywords:[`complications of MI`,`cardiogenic shock`,`ventricular septal rupture`,`VSR`,`papillary muscle rupture`,`free wall rupture`,`pseudoaneurysm`,`LV aneurysm`,`LV thrombus`,`RV infarction`,`complete heart block`,`accelerated idioventricular rhythm`,`Dressler syndrome`,`post-infarction pericarditis`,`Killip`,`Forrester`,`DARTH VADER`,`Impella`,`IABP`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Complications of MI** are the electrical, haemodynamic, mechanical, thromboembolic and inflammatory events that follow infarction and account for most early and late post-MI deaths. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Reperfusion has transformed the pattern**: mechanical complications now occur in **under 1%** of STEMI, but their mortality remains 40-90%. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Cardiogenic shock** complicates about **5-10% of STEMI** and is the leading cause of in-hospital death, with mortality of about **40-50%**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Ventricular arrhythmias** (VT or VF) occur in about **6-8% of STEMI**, mostly in the **first 48 hours** and often before reaching hospital. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Atrial fibrillation** complicates about **5-20% of ACS** and independently worsens prognosis. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**RV involvement** occurs in up to **a third to half of inferior MIs**, but is haemodynamically important in a smaller fraction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In India, **late presentation and lower reperfusion rates** (CREATE) mean that mechanical complications, LV aneurysm and heart failure are still seen more often than in Western series. [CREATE, Lancet 2008]`]},{heading:`Risk factors for complications`,points:[`**Large, anterior, transmural infarcts** carry the highest risk of heart failure, shock, LV thrombus and aneurysm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Late or no reperfusion** is the strongest modifiable risk for mechanical rupture, aneurysm and heart failure. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Free wall rupture risk**: elderly, **women**, first MI, hypertension, single-vessel disease without collaterals, late fibrinolysis and use of **NSAIDs or steroids**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Shock risk factors**: age over 70, anterior MI, diabetes, prior MI, multivessel disease, and on arrival SBP under 120 with heart rate over 110 (also why early IV beta-blockade is avoided). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Inferior MI from proximal RCA** predisposes to RV infarction, sinus bradycardia and AV nodal block. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Electrolyte disturbance** (potassium under 4.0 mmol/L, low magnesium), ongoing ischaemia and heart failure predispose to VT and VF. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and the time windows`,points:[`**First hours**: ischaemic myocardium is electrically unstable (re-entry, triggered activity, raised catecholamines) - the peak period of **primary VF**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Days 3-7**: neutrophils and macrophages digest necrotic myocardium before collagen is laid down, so the wall is **softest** - the peak period of **free wall, septal and papillary muscle rupture**. [Robbins 11e]`,`**Weeks 2-8**: granulation tissue matures into scar; a thin non-contractile scar bulges as a **true aneurysm**. [Robbins 11e]`,`**LV thrombus** forms on akinetic apical endocardium through **Virchow's triad** - stasis, endocardial injury and post-MI hypercoagulability - mostly in the first 2 weeks. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Remodelling**: infarct expansion and compensatory dilatation of the remaining ventricle raise wall stress, causing heart failure and **functional (ischaemic) mitral regurgitation** - blunted by ACE inhibitors, beta-blockers and MRAs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiogenic shock spiral**: loss of 40% or more of LV mass lowers output and BP, which lowers coronary perfusion and causes more ischaemia, with systemic inflammation adding vasodilatation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RV infarction**: the stunned RV cannot pump enough blood across the lungs, so the LV is underfilled - the patient is **preload-dependent**, explaining hypotension with clear lungs and harm from nitrates. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Inferior MI AV block** is at the **AV node** (RCA supplies the node in about 90%), from vagal tone and nodal ischaemia, so it is usually transient; **anterior MI block** is infranodal from septal necrosis, so it is permanent and ominous. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dressler syndrome** is an **autoimmune** reaction to myocardial antigens released by necrosis, appearing weeks later. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`]},{heading:`Classification of complications`,points:[`**Mnemonic DARTH VADER**: Death, Arrhythmia, Rupture, Tamponade, Heart failure, Valve disease, Aneurysm, Dressler, Embolism, Recurrence. [Exam mnemonic]`,`**Electrical**: VF, VT, AIVR, AF, sinus bradycardia, AV block and bundle branch block. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pump**: LV failure graded by **Killip class** (I none, II crackles or S3, III pulmonary oedema, IV shock) and **Forrester haemodynamic subsets** (cardiac index 2.2 L/min/m2 and wedge pressure 18 mmHg). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Forrester subsets**: I normal (mortality about 2%), II congested only (about 10%), III hypoperfused only (about 22%), IV both (about 55%). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**SCAI shock stages**: A at risk, B beginning (hypotension or tachycardia without hypoperfusion), C classic, D deteriorating, E extremis. [SCAI 2022]`,`**Mechanical**: free wall rupture (acute or subacute), pseudoaneurysm, ventricular septal rupture and papillary muscle rupture. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Thromboembolic**: LV thrombus with stroke or limb embolism; venous thromboembolism from immobility. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Inflammatory**: early infarct-associated pericarditis (days 1-3) and late **post-cardiac injury syndrome (Dressler)** at 1-6 weeks. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Late**: LV aneurysm, chronic heart failure, scar-related VT and sudden death, ischaemic MR and post-MI depression. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Recurrent chest pain** suggests reinfarction, stent thrombosis or pericarditis - pericarditic pain is pleuritic and positional. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Sudden breathlessness** or pulmonary oedema on day 2-7 suggests **papillary muscle rupture** or VSR. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Sudden collapse with electromechanical dissociation** on day 3-5, often after nausea, restlessness or retching, suggests **free wall rupture** with tamponade. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Palpitations, syncope or dizziness** point to tachy- or bradyarrhythmias. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sudden weakness, aphasia or limb ischaemia** after an anterior MI suggests **embolism from LV thrombus**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fever, pleuritic pain and malaise 1-6 weeks** after MI suggest Dressler syndrome. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Low mood, poor sleep and loss of interest** after discharge suggest post-MI depression, which doubles mortality. [AHA 2014 Statement]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and their meaning`,points:[`**Hypotension, cold clammy skin, oliguria and confusion** define cardiogenic shock; lactate over 2 mmol/L confirms hypoperfusion. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Raised JVP, Kussmaul sign, hypotension and clear lungs** in inferior MI indicate RV infarction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**New harsh pansystolic murmur at the left sternal edge with a thrill** (in about half) indicates **ventricular septal rupture**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New soft or even absent apical murmur with flash pulmonary oedema** indicates **papillary muscle rupture** - the murmur is soft because LA and LV pressures equalise quickly. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Raised JVP, muffled heart sounds, pulsus paradoxus and hypotension** indicate tamponade from free wall rupture. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardial friction rub** (scratchy, 1-3 components) in days 1-3 indicates infarct-associated pericarditis. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Dyskinetic apical impulse** (a double or sustained apex) suggests LV aneurysm. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cannon a waves and variable S1** indicate complete heart block with AV dissociation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**New systolic murmur after MI**: VSR (left sternal edge, thrill, O2 step-up in RV) versus papillary muscle rupture (apex, soft, pulmonary oedema) versus functional MR - **echo with colour Doppler** decides. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Hypotension after MI**: LV pump failure, RV infarction, mechanical complication, tamponade, bradyarrhythmia, hypovolaemia (vomiting, diuretics), drug effect (nitrates, beta-blockers, morphine) and bleeding. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Recurrent chest pain**: reinfarction (new ST elevation, troponin or CK-MB rise) versus pericarditis (pleuritic, diffuse ST, PR depression) versus rupture. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Persistent ST elevation**: LV aneurysm (weeks, Q waves) versus reinfarction (pain, biomarker rise) versus pericarditis (diffuse, concave). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Fever after MI**: necrosis itself (low grade, first 48 hours), pericarditis, Dressler, hospital infection, thrombophlebitis at a cannula or drug fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**True aneurysm versus pseudoaneurysm**: wide neck and myocardial wall versus narrow neck and pericardial wall - pseudoaneurysm needs urgent surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Continuous ECG monitoring for at least 24 hours** after STEMI (longer if high risk) to detect VT, VF, AF and AV block. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is the key test for every complication: LVEF, RV function, VSR, papillary muscle rupture, effusion, thrombus and aneurysm. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Contrast echo or cardiac MRI** detects LV thrombus far better than standard echo; CMR is the reference standard. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Right heart catheter** in VSR shows an **oxygen saturation step-up** from right atrium to pulmonary artery; in acute MR shows giant **v waves** in the wedge trace. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Serial ECGs**: right-sided V4R in inferior MI, new Q waves, persistent ST elevation at 4-6 weeks (aneurysm), and AV conduction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Biomarkers**: CK-MB (returns to normal in 48-72 hours) helps detect reinfarction; troponin stays raised for 1-2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lactate, ABG, creatinine and urine output** grade shock; **potassium and magnesium** are corrected in arrhythmias. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESR and CRP** are raised in Dressler syndrome; chest X-ray may show pleural effusion and an enlarged cardiac silhouette. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`]},{heading:`Arrhythmias and conduction block - management`,points:[`**VF or pulseless VT**: immediate defibrillation; for recurrent VT or VF give **amiodarone** (300 mg IV in arrest, 150 mg over 10 minutes for stable VT), **beta-blockers** and urgent revascularisation. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Keep potassium at 4.0-5.0 mmol/L and correct magnesium**; prophylactic antiarrhythmics including lidocaine are **not recommended**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**VF within 48 hours** does not by itself need an ICD; reassess LVEF at **6-12 weeks**, and consider ICD if LVEF stays 35% or less at **40 days or more** - see the VT and ICD topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Accelerated idioventricular rhythm** (rate 60-120) is a benign marker of reperfusion - **do not suppress it**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**AF**: rate control with beta-blocker (or amiodarone, digoxin if hypotensive), urgent cardioversion if unstable, and **anticoagulation** by stroke risk - this creates triple therapy, kept as short as possible. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Symptomatic sinus bradycardia or AV nodal block**: **atropine 0.5-1 mg IV**, repeated to a maximum of 3 mg; temporary pacing if unresponsive with haemodynamic compromise. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Inferior MI AV block** is usually transient (narrow escape at 40-60/min) and resolves in days; **anterior MI** Mobitz II or complete block (wide escape under 40/min) needs **temporary pacing** and usually a permanent pacemaker. [ESC 2021 Pacing]`,`**Permanent pacing** after MI is decided only if high-degree AV block persists beyond **at least 5 days**. [ESC 2021 Pacing]`]},{heading:`RV infarction and cardiogenic shock - management`,points:[`**RV infarction**: early reperfusion, **avoid nitrates, diuretics and excess opioids**, give cautious **IV fluid boluses** with reassessment, and use **dobutamine** if hypotension persists. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`In RV infarction **maintain AV synchrony** - AV sequential pacing for heart block and prompt cardioversion of AF, because the RV depends on atrial contribution. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiogenic shock** (SBP under 90 mmHg for over 30 minutes or need for vasopressors, with hypoperfusion) needs **immediate angiography and PCI of the culprit lesion**, regardless of time from onset. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**SHOCK trial**: early revascularisation reduced 6-month mortality (50.3% versus 63.1%); **CULPRIT-SHOCK**: culprit-only PCI beat immediate multivessel PCI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Norepinephrine** is the vasopressor of choice and **dobutamine** the inotrope for low output; avoid dopamine (more arrhythmia). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Routine IABP is not recommended** (IABP-SHOCK II); IABP is still used to bridge mechanical complications. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Microaxial flow pump (Impella CP)** reduced 180-day mortality in selected non-comatose STEMI shock (DanGer Shock 2024) at the cost of more bleeding and limb ischaemia; routine **VA-ECMO** did not help (ECLS-SHOCK 2023). [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Shock team** care in a tertiary centre: invasive monitoring, ventilation if needed, renal replacement, and early transfer from district hospitals - see the Acute decompensated HF and cardiogenic shock topic. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`]},{heading:`Mechanical complications - management`,points:[`**Free wall rupture** (bimodal: first 24 hours and days 3-7) presents as **tamponade and PEA**; **subacute rupture** may be bridged by pericardiocentesis and fluids to **emergency surgical repair**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Pseudoaneurysm** is a contained rupture walled by pericardium and thrombus, with a **narrow neck**, often inferolateral, and needs **urgent surgery** because it can rupture. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ventricular septal rupture** (about 0.2% in the PCI era, days 3-5): **apical** with anterior MI, **basal** (worse prognosis) with inferior MI; causes biventricular failure and shock. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**VSR treatment**: vasodilators and diuretics if BP allows, **IABP or mechanical circulatory support** as a bridge, and **surgical repair** (or percutaneous closure in selected patients); timing is individualised, as delayed surgery after stabilisation has lower mortality. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**VSR mortality** is over **90% without repair** and about **40% with surgery**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Papillary muscle rupture** affects the **posteromedial muscle 6-12 times more often** (single blood supply from the posterior descending artery) than the anterolateral (dual LAD and circumflex supply). [Harrison 22e]`,`Papillary muscle rupture often follows a **small inferior or posterior MI**, causes acute severe MR with **flash pulmonary oedema and shock**, and needs **emergency mitral valve surgery** (usually replacement); transcatheter edge-to-edge repair is a rescue option at prohibitive risk. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Bridge strategy** for all mechanical complications: afterload reduction (nitroprusside or nitroglycerin if BP permits), IABP or Impella, ventilation and **heart team** decision. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`]},{heading:`LV thrombus, aneurysm and pericardial complications`,points:[`**LV thrombus** occurs in about **5-15% of anterior STEMI** (CMR series), mostly within 2 weeks; screen anterior MI with apical akinesis, using contrast echo or CMR if the standard echo is unclear. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Treat LV thrombus with oral anticoagulation for 3-6 months** (warfarin INR 2-3, or a DOAC as an acceptable alternative), guided by repeat imaging; with DAPT this means short triple therapy, then OAC plus clopidogrel. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**True LV aneurysm** follows large anterior transmural infarcts, shows **persistent ST elevation beyond 4-6 weeks** and dyskinesia, and causes heart failure, VT and embolism - but rarely ruptures. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Aneurysm treatment**: guideline heart failure therapy, anticoagulation if thrombus, and **surgical aneurysmectomy** for refractory heart failure or VT. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Early infarct-associated pericarditis** (days 1-3, transmural infarcts) is usually self-limiting; give **aspirin** and avoid other NSAIDs and steroids, which impair scar formation and raise rupture risk. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Dressler syndrome** (post-cardiac injury syndrome, 1-6 weeks, now under 1%): fever, pleuropericarditis, raised ESR; treat with **aspirin 500-1000 mg every 6-8 hours** for 1-2 weeks plus **colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily** (once daily if under 70 kg) for about 3 months. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`A **pericardial effusion over 10 mm** after MI, especially with fluid that looks haemorrhagic, should prompt suspicion of **subacute rupture**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: more shock, rupture and AV block, and more bleeding with antithrombotics; frailty affects decisions on mechanical support and surgery. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Women**: higher rates of free wall rupture and in-hospital mortality after STEMI, partly from later presentation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Diabetes and CKD**: more heart failure, shock and contrast injury; adjust anticoagulant doses and watch potassium with ACE inhibitors and MRAs. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**District hospital without cath lab**: stabilise (defibrillation, atropine, fluids for RV infarct, norepinephrine), give lysis if within window, and **transfer early** any shock, new murmur or high-degree block. [ICMR STW ACS]`,`**Late presenters (common in India)** have higher rates of mechanical complications - examine daily for new murmurs and do echo before discharge. [CSI STEMI position statement 2017]`,`**Patients on triple therapy** (LV thrombus or AF): keep aspirin to the shortest period (up to 1 week), use clopidogrel as the P2Y12 inhibitor, and add a PPI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Killip class** at admission remains one of the strongest predictors of 30-day mortality. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiogenic shock** mortality is about **40-50%** despite revascularisation; **mechanical complications** carry 40-90% mortality. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Refer immediately** to a tertiary centre with cardiac surgery any patient with shock, a new murmur, pericardial effusion with instability, or recurrent VT or VF. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**Echo before discharge** in every MI patient; repeat at **6-12 weeks** if LVEF was 40% or less, to guide ICD and heart failure therapy. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**LV thrombus follow-up**: repeat imaging at about 3 months to decide whether to stop anticoagulation. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**After discharge**, screen for depression, heart failure symptoms and palpitations at each visit, and enrol in cardiac rehabilitation - see the After the MI topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2023 ACS** recommends immediate culprit-only PCI in shock, routine IABP not recommended, and individualised timing of surgery for mechanical complications with mechanical circulatory support as a bridge. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**DanGer Shock (2024)**: microaxial flow pump reduced 180-day mortality (45.8% versus 58.5%) in selected STEMI shock; **ACC/AHA 2025** gives it a 2a recommendation. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ECLS-SHOCK (2023)**: routine VA-ECMO in infarct-related shock did not reduce 30-day mortality and increased bleeding. [ECLS-SHOCK, NEJM 2023]`,`**ESC 2023** endorses oral anticoagulation for 3-6 months for LV thrombus, guided by repeat imaging, with DOACs an acceptable alternative to warfarin. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ESC 2025 Myocarditis and Pericarditis guideline** groups Dressler syndrome under **post-cardiac injury syndromes**, treated with aspirin or NSAID plus colchicine. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**SCAI shock staging** (updated 2022) is now used by ACC/AHA 2025 to describe shock severity and guide escalation. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **'Complications of acute MI'**, **'RV infarction'**, **'Mechanical complications of MI'** and **'Differentiate VSR from papillary muscle rupture'**. [DNB FM papers]`,`**Draw the timing table** - examiners reward 'arrhythmias in hours, ruptures in days, Dressler in weeks, aneurysm in months'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RV infarction mistakes**: giving nitrates or furosemide - the patient needs volume and AV synchrony. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Inferior MI block is nodal and transient; anterior MI block is infranodal and permanent** - the commonest viva contrast. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Posteromedial papillary muscle ruptures more** because it has a single blood supply. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Avoid NSAIDs and steroids early after MI** - they impair healing and increase rupture; use aspirin for pericarditis. [ESC 2025 Pericarditis]`,`**Do not suppress AIVR** - it is a reperfusion marker. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used for complications of MI`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`VF arrest 300 mg IV bolus (then 150 mg); stable VT 150 mg IV over 10 min, then 1 mg/min for 6 h and 0.5 mg/min for 18 h`,`Short course; reassess`,`Hypotension, bradycardia, QT prolongation, phlebitis`],[`Atropine`,`0.5-1 mg IV, repeat every 3-5 min to 3 mg`,`Bridge to pacing if needed`,`Tachycardia, urinary retention; ineffective in infranodal block`],[`Norepinephrine`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV infusion via central line`,`Titrate to MAP 65 mmHg or more`,`Arrhythmia, peripheral ischaemia, extravasation necrosis`],[`Dobutamine`,`2.5-10 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Titrate to perfusion`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia, hypotension`],[`Normal saline (RV infarct)`,`Cautious IV boluses (e.g. 250 mL) with reassessment`,`Stop if crackles or no BP response`,`Pulmonary oedema if LV also failing`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg IV, repeat by response`,`For LV failure only`,`Hypotension (avoid in RV infarct), hypokalaemia`],[`Nitroglycerin IV`,`5-10 microgram/min, titrate`,`LV failure, MR, VSR bridge if SBP over 90`,`Hypotension; avoid in RV infarct`],[`Warfarin (LV thrombus)`,`Dose to INR 2-3`,`3-6 months, repeat imaging`,`Bleeding with antiplatelets; INR monitoring`],[`Apixaban (LV thrombus, off-label alternative)`,`5 mg twice daily`,`3-6 months`,`Bleeding; avoid in severe renal failure`],[`Aspirin (post-MI pericarditis)`,`500-1000 mg oral every 6-8 hours`,`1-2 weeks, then taper`,`GI bleeding; give PPI`],[`Colchicine`,`0.5 mg twice daily (once daily if under 70 kg)`,`About 3 months`,`Diarrhoea; reduce in CKD; interactions with clarithromycin`],[`Metoprolol`,`25-50 mg oral 6-12 hourly once stable`,`Long term`,`Avoid in shock, AV block, RV infarct with hypotension`]]},{heading:`Timing of complications after MI`,columns:[`Time after MI`,`Complication`,`Clue`],rows:[[`Minutes to 48 hours`,`VF, VT, AIVR, sinus bradycardia, AV block`,`Monitor; most VF deaths are in the first hour`],[`First 24-72 hours`,`Cardiogenic shock, RV infarction, acute LV failure`,`Hypotension, crackles or clear lungs with raised JVP`],[`First 24 hours`,`Early free wall rupture (especially after late lysis)`,`Sudden PEA`],[`Days 1-3`,`Infarct-associated pericarditis, AF`,`Pleuritic pain, rub`],[`Days 3-7 (up to 14)`,`Free wall rupture, VSR, papillary muscle rupture`,`Collapse, new murmur, flash pulmonary oedema`],[`Days to 2 weeks`,`LV thrombus and embolism`,`Anterior apical akinesis, stroke`],[`Weeks 1-6`,`Dressler syndrome`,`Fever, pleuropericarditis, raised ESR`],[`Weeks to months`,`LV aneurysm, chronic HF, scar VT, ischaemic MR, depression`,`Persistent ST elevation, breathlessness, palpitations`]]},{heading:`Mechanical complications compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Ventricular septal rupture`,`Papillary muscle rupture`,`Free wall rupture`],rows:[[`Timing`,`Days 3-5`,`Days 2-7`,`First 24 h or days 3-7`],[`Usual infarct`,`Anterior (apical) or inferior (basal)`,`Small inferior or posterior; posteromedial muscle`,`First anterior or lateral transmural MI`],[`Murmur`,`Loud harsh pansystolic at left sternal edge, thrill`,`Soft or absent apical murmur`,`None; muffled heart sounds`],[`Haemodynamics`,`Biventricular failure, shock`,`Flash pulmonary oedema, shock`,`Tamponade, PEA`],[`Key test`,`Echo colour Doppler; O2 step-up RA to PA`,`Echo flail leaflet; giant v waves`,`Echo effusion; pericardiocentesis`],[`Treatment`,`Bridge with IABP or MCS; surgery or device closure`,`Emergency mitral surgery; TEER at prohibitive risk`,`Pericardiocentesis bridge; emergency surgery`],[`Mortality`,`About 40% with surgery, over 90% without`,`High without surgery`,`Very high; survivors of subacute rupture with surgery`]]},{heading:`AV block in inferior versus anterior MI`,columns:[`Feature`,`Inferior MI`,`Anterior MI`],rows:[[`Artery`,`RCA (AV nodal artery)`,`LAD (septal perforators)`],[`Level of block`,`AV node`,`Infranodal (His-Purkinje)`],[`Usual type`,`First degree, Mobitz I, complete with narrow escape`,`Mobitz II, complete with wide escape`],[`Escape rate`,`40-60 per minute, stable`,`Under 40 per minute, unstable`],[`Response to atropine`,`Often responds`,`Poor`],[`Course`,`Transient, resolves in days`,`Often permanent; large infarct`],[`Pacing`,`Temporary only if haemodynamic compromise`,`Temporary pacing; usually permanent`],[`Prognosis`,`Good`,`Poor - reflects extensive necrosis`]]},{heading:`True LV aneurysm versus pseudoaneurysm`,columns:[`Feature`,`True aneurysm`,`Pseudoaneurysm`],rows:[[`Wall`,`Thinned scarred myocardium`,`Pericardium and thrombus (contained rupture)`],[`Neck`,`Wide (neck to body ratio over 0.5)`,`Narrow (ratio under 0.5)`],[`Usual site`,`Anterior or apical`,`Inferior or posterolateral`],[`Timing`,`Weeks to months`,`Days to weeks`],[`Risk of rupture`,`Low`,`High`],[`ECG`,`Persistent ST elevation with Q waves`,`Non-specific`],[`Management`,`HF therapy, anticoagulate thrombus, aneurysmectomy if refractory`,`Urgent surgical repair`]]},{heading:`Haemodynamic classification of post-MI heart failure`,columns:[`Class`,`Clinical or haemodynamic finding`,`Approximate mortality`,`Management`],rows:[[`Killip I`,`No heart failure`,`About 6% (original series)`,`Standard care`],[`Killip II`,`Crackles in lower half, S3, raised JVP`,`About 17%`,`Diuretic, nitrate, ACE inhibitor`],[`Killip III`,`Frank pulmonary oedema`,`About 38%`,`Oxygen or NIV, IV diuretic, nitrate, urgent revascularisation`],[`Killip IV`,`Cardiogenic shock`,`About 81% (lower with revascularisation)`,`Immediate PCI, vasopressor, inotrope, MCS`],[`Forrester I`,`CI over 2.2, PCWP under 18`,`About 2%`,`Observe`],[`Forrester II`,`CI over 2.2, PCWP over 18 (congested)`,`About 10%`,`Diuretics, vasodilators`],[`Forrester III`,`CI under 2.2, PCWP under 18 (hypovolaemic or RV infarct)`,`About 22%`,`Fluids`],[`Forrester IV`,`CI under 2.2, PCWP over 18 (shock)`,`About 55%`,`Inotropes, MCS, revascularisation`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension with cold periphery, oliguria or lactate over 2 mmol/L - cardiogenic shock, immediate PCI and shock team`,`New pansystolic murmur or sudden pulmonary oedema after day 2 - VSR or papillary muscle rupture, urgent echo and surgery`,`Sudden collapse with PEA or new pericardial effusion with hypotension - free wall rupture`,`Recurrent VT or VF, or electrical storm`,`Mobitz II or complete heart block with anterior MI, or any block with haemodynamic compromise - pacing`,`Hypotension with raised JVP and clear lungs in inferior MI - RV infarction, stop nitrates, give fluids`,`Stroke or limb ischaemia after anterior MI - embolism from LV thrombus`],pearls:[`**DARTH VADER**: Death, Arrhythmia, Rupture, Tamponade, Heart failure, Valve, Aneurysm, Dressler, Embolism, Recurrence.`,`**Hours - electrical; days 3-7 - ruptures; weeks - Dressler; months - aneurysm.**`,`**RV infarct triad**: hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs - fluids, no nitrates.`,`**Inferior block is nodal and narrow; anterior block is infranodal and wide.**`,`**Posteromedial papillary muscle has one artery - so it ruptures more.**`,`**VSR loud with thrill, papillary rupture soft or silent** - echo decides.`,`**Pseudoaneurysm has a narrow neck and a pericardial wall** - urgent surgery.`,`**AIVR is good news** - never suppress it.`,`**Pericarditis after MI: aspirin, not ibuprofen or steroids.**`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-q1`,stem:`On day 4 after an inferior STEMI treated late, a 68-year-old man develops sudden breathlessness and hypotension. There is a soft apical systolic murmur and pulmonary oedema on X-ray. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Ventricular septal rupture`,`Free wall rupture`,`Posteromedial papillary muscle rupture`,`Dressler syndrome`],answer:2,explanation:`Flash pulmonary oedema with a soft or even absent apical murmur a few days after an inferior MI is classic for rupture of the posteromedial papillary muscle, which depends on a single artery. Ventricular septal rupture gives a loud harsh murmur at the left sternal edge with a thrill and biventricular failure; free wall rupture causes tamponade and PEA without a murmur; Dressler syndrome appears weeks later with fever and pleuropericarditis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman with inferior STEMI has BP 82/50 mmHg, JVP raised to the angle of the jaw and clear lung fields. ECG shows 1.5 mm ST elevation in V4R. What should be done first?`,options:[`IV furosemide 40 mg`,`IV nitroglycerin infusion`,`IV morphine 4 mg`,`Cautious IV fluid bolus with reassessment, and urgent reperfusion`],answer:3,explanation:`Hypotension, raised JVP and clear lungs with ST elevation in V4R is right ventricular infarction; the underfilled LV is preload-dependent, so cautious fluid boluses and early reperfusion are first steps, with dobutamine if hypotension persists. Furosemide, nitroglycerin and morphine all reduce preload and can cause profound hypotension in RV infarction.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-q3`,stem:`Which feature best distinguishes AV block in inferior MI from AV block in anterior MI?`,options:[`Inferior MI block is usually nodal, transient and has a narrow-complex escape`,`Inferior MI block usually needs a permanent pacemaker`,`Anterior MI block responds well to atropine`,`Anterior MI block carries a better prognosis`],answer:0,explanation:`In inferior MI the RCA supplies the AV node, so block is nodal, vagally mediated, with a stable narrow escape, usually atropine-responsive and transient. Anterior MI block is infranodal from septal necrosis, with a slow wide escape, poor atropine response, frequent need for pacing and a poor prognosis - the opposite of the other choices.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-q4`,stem:`Echo 5 days after a large anterior STEMI shows a 14 mm mobile apical LV thrombus. The patient had a drug-eluting stent and is on aspirin and ticagrelor. What is the preferred antithrombotic plan per ESC 2023?`,options:[`Continue aspirin and ticagrelor alone and repeat echo in a year`,`Add oral anticoagulation, switch ticagrelor to clopidogrel, keep triple therapy short, and reimage at about 3 months`,`Add enoxaparin for 5 days and stop`,`Refer for urgent surgical thrombectomy`],answer:1,explanation:`LV thrombus needs oral anticoagulation (warfarin or a DOAC) for 3-6 months guided by repeat imaging; with a recent stent, clopidogrel is the P2Y12 inhibitor used with an OAC and aspirin is limited to the shortest possible period to reduce bleeding. DAPT alone does not treat the thrombus, a 5-day heparin course is too short, and surgery is not first-line even for mobile thrombus.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-q5`,stem:`Three weeks after an MI, a patient presents with fever, pleuritic chest pain, a pericardial rub and raised ESR. What is the recommended treatment?`,options:[`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily`,`Ibuprofen 600 mg three times daily`,`Aspirin in high dose with colchicine`,`Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics`],answer:2,explanation:`This is Dressler (post-cardiac injury) syndrome; ESC recommends high-dose aspirin, which is also the antiplatelet the patient needs, plus colchicine for about 3 months. Corticosteroids and non-aspirin NSAIDs such as ibuprofen are avoided after recent MI because they impair scar healing and raise the risk of rupture and recurrence, and antibiotics do not treat this autoimmune process.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Enumerate the complications of acute myocardial infarction. Discuss the diagnosis and management of mechanical complications and right ventricular infarction.`,openingLines:[`Complications of MI are electrical, pump, mechanical, thromboembolic and inflammatory, each with a typical time window after the infarct.`,`Mechanical complications are now rare (under 1%) in the reperfusion era but carry 40-90% mortality, so early echo and surgery are life-saving.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification with timing`,points:[`Electrical: VF, VT, AIVR, AF, bradycardia, AV block (hours to 48 h).`,`Pump: LV failure, cardiogenic shock, RV infarction (first days).`,`Mechanical: free wall rupture, VSR, papillary muscle rupture (days 3-7).`,`Thromboembolic: LV thrombus (2 weeks); inflammatory: pericarditis (days 1-3), Dressler (weeks); late: aneurysm, HF, VT.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of rupture`,points:[`Necrotic wall is softest at days 3-7, when macrophages remove dead tissue before collagen is laid.`,`Risk factors: elderly, women, first MI, late or no reperfusion, NSAIDs or steroids.`]},{heading:`Ventricular septal rupture`,points:[`Loud pansystolic murmur at left sternal edge with thrill, biventricular failure.`,`Echo colour Doppler; O2 step-up RA to PA.`,`Bridge with vasodilator, IABP or MCS; surgical or device closure.`]},{heading:`Papillary muscle rupture`,points:[`Posteromedial (single supply) after small inferior MI; flash pulmonary oedema, soft murmur.`,`Echo: flail leaflet, severe MR; emergency mitral surgery.`]},{heading:`Free wall rupture and pseudoaneurysm`,points:[`Sudden tamponade and PEA; subacute form bridged by pericardiocentesis to emergency surgery.`,`Pseudoaneurysm: narrow neck, pericardial wall - urgent surgery.`]},{heading:`RV infarction`,points:[`Inferior MI with hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs; ST elevation 0.5 mm or more in V4R.`,`Fluids, avoid nitrates and diuretics, dobutamine, maintain AV synchrony, early reperfusion.`]}],mustDraw:[`Timeline of complications from hours to months`,`Comparison table of VSR, papillary muscle rupture and free wall rupture`],markSplit:[{part:`Enumeration with timing`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`VSR`,marks:2},{part:`Papillary muscle rupture`,marks:1.5},{part:`Free wall rupture`,marks:1.5},{part:`RV infarction`,marks:2}],keywords:[`DARTH VADER`,`VSR`,`papillary muscle`,`free wall rupture`,`RV infarction`,`V4R`,`IABP`]},{id:`cardiovascular-mi-complications-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate a true left ventricular aneurysm from a pseudoaneurysm after myocardial infarction.`,openingLines:[`A true aneurysm is a dyskinetic bulge of thinned, scarred myocardium; a pseudoaneurysm is a contained rupture walled only by pericardium and thrombus.`,`The distinction matters because a pseudoaneurysm can rupture and needs urgent surgery, while a true aneurysm rarely ruptures.`],answer:[{heading:`Definitions and timing`,points:[`True aneurysm: scarred, thinned full-thickness wall that bulges in systole; forms over weeks to months.`,`Pseudoaneurysm: free wall rupture sealed by pericardium and thrombus; forms in days to weeks.`]},{heading:`Anatomy and site`,points:[`True: wide neck, myocardial wall, anterior or apical.`,`Pseudo: narrow neck (ratio under 0.5), pericardial wall, inferior or posterolateral.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`True: HF, VT, embolism; persistent ST elevation beyond 4-6 weeks; dyskinetic apex.`,`Pseudo: may be silent or cause chest pain; echo, CMR or CT show narrow neck and to-and-fro flow.`]},{heading:`Management and prognosis`,points:[`True: HF therapy, anticoagulate thrombus, aneurysmectomy for refractory HF or VT; low rupture risk.`,`Pseudo: urgent surgical repair; high rupture risk.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of wide-necked true aneurysm versus narrow-necked pseudoaneurysm`],markSplit:[{part:`Definitions`,marks:1},{part:`Anatomy and site`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:1}],keywords:[`true aneurysm`,`pseudoaneurysm`,`narrow neck`,`persistent ST elevation`,`surgery`]}],references:[`Byrne RA et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes. European Heart Journal 2023;44:3720-3826`,`Rao SV et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes. Circulation/JACC 2025`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Complications of STEMI`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Complications of acute myocardial infarction`,`Glikson M et al. 2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiac pacing and cardiac resynchronization therapy. European Heart Journal 2021`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of myocarditis and pericarditis. European Heart Journal 2025`,`Moller JE et al. Microaxial flow pump or standard care in infarct-related cardiogenic shock (DanGer Shock). NEJM 2024`,`Cardiological Society of India: position statement for the management of ST elevation myocardial infarction in India. Indian Heart Journal 2017`]}),n.push({id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab`,title:`After the MI: secondary prevention and cardiac rehabilitation`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Secondary prevention after MI** is the lifelong bundle that halves recurrent events: **DAPT** (default 12 months), a **high-intensity statin** to an **LDL under 55 mg/dL with a 50% reduction**, **ACE inhibitor**, **beta-blocker** (clearly for LVEF 40% or less; REDUCE-AMI and REBOOT show no benefit when LVEF is preserved), **MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor** when LVEF is reduced or HF or diabetes is present, tobacco cessation, and **structured cardiac rehabilitation** (phase I in hospital, phase II supervised exercise, phase III lifelong maintenance) with planned return to work, driving and sex.`,keywords:[`secondary prevention`,`cardiac rehabilitation`,`DAPT`,`high-intensity statin`,`LDL target 55`,`ezetimibe`,`PCSK9 inhibitor`,`beta-blocker after MI`,`REDUCE-AMI`,`REBOOT`,`BETAMI-DANBLOCK`,`eplerenone`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`colchicine`,`Yoga-CaRe`,`return to work`,`driving after MI`,`post-MI depression`,`ABCDE`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Secondary prevention** is drug and lifestyle therapy that reduces recurrent MI, stroke, heart failure and death in patients who have had an MI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Recurrence risk is front-loaded**: about **1 in 5 MI survivors** has another cardiovascular event within a year, with the highest risk in the first months. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac rehabilitation** is a multidisciplinary programme of exercise training, risk factor management, education and psychosocial support that is **Class I** after ACS. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**PURE** found that in lower-income countries, including India, only a small minority of patients with coronary disease took statins, aspirin and ACE inhibitors - **under 5% were on statins** in low-income countries. [PURE, Lancet 2011]`,`**Cardiac rehabilitation uptake in India is very low** (well under 10%), because few programmes exist and travel and wage loss are barriers. [Yoga-CaRe, JACC 2020]`,`**Adherence falls quickly**: up to half of patients stop at least one secondary-prevention drug within a year, especially statins. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Indians have MI younger (INTERHEART South Asia mean age 53), so **decades of secondary prevention** and return to productive work are at stake. [INTERHEART South Asia, JAMA 2007]`]},{heading:`Risk factors for recurrence`,points:[`**Persisting tobacco use** roughly doubles the risk of reinfarction and death; stopping is the most effective single measure. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**LDL cholesterol** is causal: every **39 mg/dL (1 mmol/L) reduction** lowers major vascular events by about **22%** (CTT). [CTT Collaboration, Lancet 2010]`,`**Diabetes, hypertension, CKD, obesity and inactivity** each raise recurrent risk and need targeted treatment. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Low LVEF and residual ischaemia** predict heart failure, arrhythmic death and recurrent events. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Depression, low social support and poverty** independently worsen outcomes and adherence. [AHA 2014 Statement]`,`**Lipoprotein(a)** is high in many South Asians and predicts recurrence; measure it once. [CSI Dyslipidaemia 2024]`,`**Polyvascular disease** (PAD, stroke) and recurrent events within 2 years define **extreme risk**. [ESC/EAS 2019 Dyslipidaemia]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each drug works`,points:[`**Aspirin and P2Y12 inhibitors** prevent thrombosis on other vulnerable plaques and in new stents - DAPT protects both stent and non-stent territories. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Statins** lower LDL by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase and up-regulating LDL receptors, and **stabilise plaque** (thicker cap, less inflammation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ACE inhibitors** block angiotensin II-driven **remodelling**, fibrosis and afterload, preserving LV function and reducing death after large infarcts. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Beta-blockers** reduce heart rate, oxygen demand and catecholamine-driven arrhythmia - the benefit was proved before reperfusion, when infarcts were large and LVEF low. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**MRAs** block aldosterone-mediated fibrosis and sodium retention; eplerenone reduced mortality after MI with LVEF 40% or less and heart failure or diabetes (EPHESUS). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Colchicine** inhibits neutrophil microtubules and the **NLRP3 inflammasome**, targeting residual inflammatory risk. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Exercise training** improves endothelial function, peak oxygen uptake, insulin sensitivity, BP and autonomic balance. [EAPC 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification - risk tiers and rehabilitation phases`,points:[`**Phase I (inpatient)**: early mobilisation, education, risk factor counselling and referral, in the first days after MI. [AACVPR 6e]`,`**Phase II (early outpatient, supervised)**: begins about 1-3 weeks after discharge, typically **36 sessions over about 12 weeks**, with ECG-monitored exercise for higher-risk patients. [AACVPR 6e]`,`**Phase III (maintenance)**: lifelong self-directed exercise and risk-factor control, in the community or at home (some describe this as phases III-IV). [AACVPR 6e]`,`**Exercise risk stratification (AACVPR)**: low risk (LVEF 50% or more, no complex arrhythmia or ischaemia), intermediate (LVEF 40-49%) and high risk (LVEF under 40%, complex arrhythmia, survivor of arrest) - decides monitoring intensity. [AACVPR 6e]`,`**LDL targets by risk**: very high risk (all post-MI) **under 55 mg/dL and 50% reduction**; recurrent event within 2 years **under 40 mg/dL**. [ESC/EAS 2019 Dyslipidaemia]`,`**Lipid Association of India** sets **under 50 mg/dL** for extreme risk (category A) and **30 mg/dL or less** for category B (recurrent events or polyvascular disease). [LAI 2023]`,`**LVEF categories** decide drugs: 40% or less (beta-blocker, ACE inhibitor, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor all Class I), 41-49% and 50% or more (individualised). [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Clinical review - history at follow-up`,points:[`Ask about **angina** (CCS class), breathlessness (NYHA class), palpitations, syncope and ankle swelling at every visit. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`Check **adherence** drug by drug - what, when and why missed (cost, side effects, belief that the stent cured the disease). [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Ask about **bleeding** on DAPT: gum bleeds, bruising, black stools, haematuria. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Take a **tobacco history in all forms** (cigarette, bidi, gutka, khaini) and second-hand smoke at home or work. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Screen for **depression** with PHQ-2 (and PHQ-9 if positive), and ask about sleep, anxiety and sexual concerns. [AHA 2014 Statement]`,`Ask about **work type, driving, physical activity and diet** in the patient's own terms (oil, ghee, salt, fried snacks, sweets). [ICMR STW ACS]`,`Ask about **erectile dysfunction** and use of PDE5 inhibitors, which interact dangerously with nitrates. [AHA Sexual Activity Statement 2012]`]},{heading:`Examination and monitoring at follow-up`,points:[`**BP target** under 130/80 mmHg if tolerated (systolic 120-129 in ESC 2024); check orthostatic BP in the elderly on several drugs. [ESC 2024 Hypertension]`,`**Resting heart rate** around 55-70 per minute on beta-blocker; bradycardia under 50 with symptoms needs dose reduction. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Weight, BMI and waist** with Indian cut-offs (BMI 23, waist 90 cm men and 80 cm women). [ICMR STW]`,`**Signs of heart failure**: raised JVP, crackles, S3, oedema - prompt echo and GDMT review. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Radial or femoral puncture site** at the first visit for haematoma, pseudoaneurysm or radial occlusion. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Statin side effects**: muscle symptoms (check CK only if symptomatic) and liver enzymes. [ESC/EAS 2019 Dyslipidaemia]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of symptoms after MI`,points:[`**Recurrent chest pain**: stent thrombosis or restenosis, new lesion, pericarditis, Dressler, musculoskeletal pain or anxiety. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Breathlessness**: heart failure, ticagrelor dyspnoea (benign, early), anaemia from bleeding, beta-blocker bronchospasm and deconditioning. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Fatigue**: beta-blocker, depression, anaemia, hypothyroidism, poor sleep and deconditioning. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muscle pain on statin**: true statin myopathy (rare), vitamin D deficiency, hypothyroidism or nocebo effect - rechallenge rather than stop permanently. [ESC/EAS 2019 Dyslipidaemia]`,`**Dry cough**: ACE inhibitor (switch to ARB) versus heart failure or lung disease. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Palpitations or syncope**: scar-related VT, AF, bradycardia from drugs or conduction disease - ECG and Holter. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and follow-up tests`,points:[`**Echocardiography** before discharge; repeat at **6-12 weeks** if LVEF was 40% or less to decide ICD and GDMT. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Lipid profile 4-6 weeks** after starting or changing lipid therapy, then every 3-12 months until at target, then yearly. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Creatinine and potassium** 1-2 weeks after starting or up-titrating ACE inhibitor, ARB or MRA. [ESC 2023 HF]`,`**HbA1c** at discharge and every 3-6 months in diabetics; screen non-diabetics because many have undiagnosed diabetes. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Lipoprotein(a)** once in every patient, especially young or with family history. [CSI Dyslipidaemia 2024]`,`**Symptom-limited exercise test** before phase II training or return to strenuous work, to set the exercise prescription and look for ischaemia. [AACVPR 6e]`,`**Haemoglobin** on DAPT if any bleeding symptoms; ECG at each visit for rhythm and conduction. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Discharge bundle - drugs with doses`,points:[`**DAPT**: aspirin 75-100 mg plus ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily, prasugrel 10 mg or clopidogrel 75 mg, default **12 months**; shorten (1-3 months, then single agent) in high bleeding risk - see the Antiplatelet therapy topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**High-intensity statin** for all: **atorvastatin 40-80 mg** or **rosuvastatin 20-40 mg**, regardless of baseline LDL. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`If LDL is not at target after 4-6 weeks add **ezetimibe 10 mg**, then a **PCSK9 inhibitor** (or inclisiran); ESC/EAS 2025 favours **early statin-ezetimibe combination** when the target is unlikely to be reached with a statin alone. [ESC/EAS 2025 Lipid update]`,`**ACE inhibitor** (ramipril 2.5 mg twice daily titrated to 5 mg twice daily) for LVEF 40% or less, heart failure, diabetes, hypertension or CKD (Class I) and reasonable for all; **ARB** (valsartan) if intolerant. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Beta-blocker** (metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg or bisoprolol 1.25-10 mg daily) is **Class I for LVEF 40% or less**; for preserved LVEF the 2025 trials question routine use - see the next section. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**MRA** (eplerenone 25-50 mg or spironolactone 25 mg daily) for LVEF 40% or less with heart failure or diabetes, if creatinine under 2.5 mg/dL and potassium under 5.0 mmol/L. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor** (dapagliflozin or empagliflozin 10 mg daily) for heart failure, LVEF 40% or less, diabetes or CKD; EMPACT-MI (2024) did not show benefit as routine therapy for every MI, though it reduced HF admissions. [EMPACT-MI, NEJM 2024]`,`**Colchicine 0.5 mg once daily** may be considered (ESC 2023 IIb; ESC 2024 CCS IIa), but **CLEAR SYNERGY (2025)** found no reduction in events after MI, and ACC/AHA 2025 rates routine use as uncertain. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**PPI** with DAPT if high GI bleeding risk, **annual influenza vaccination** (Class I), and **sublingual nitrate** to carry for angina. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Beta-blockers after MI - current evidence`,points:[`**LVEF 40% or less**: long-term beta-blocker remains **Class I** in all guidelines - reduces death and sudden death. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**REDUCE-AMI (2024)**: in 5020 patients with LVEF **50% or more** after revascularised MI, beta-blockers did not reduce death or reinfarction. [REDUCE-AMI, NEJM 2024]`,`**REBOOT (2025)**: in about 8500 patients with LVEF **over 40%**, beta-blockers did not reduce death, reinfarction or HF admission; a sub-analysis suggested possible harm in women with normal LVEF. [REBOOT, NEJM 2025]`,`**BETAMI-DANBLOCK (2025)**: in LVEF **40% or more**, beta-blockers modestly reduced a composite driven by **recurrent MI**, so the evidence is not uniform. [BETAMI-DANBLOCK, NEJM 2025]`,`A **2025 individual-patient meta-analysis** of these trials showed benefit in **mildly reduced LVEF (40-49%)**, supporting beta-blockers in this group. [Lancet 2025 meta-analysis]`,`**Practical approach**: give beta-blockers for LVEF under 50%, angina, arrhythmia or hypertension; in **LVEF 50% or more without these**, routine long-term use is optional and may be reviewed or stopped. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**ABYSS (2024)**: interrupting beta-blockers in stable patients long after MI did not prove non-inferior (more cardiovascular admissions), so stopping should be individualised, not automatic. [ABYSS, NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Cardiac rehabilitation and lifestyle prescription`,points:[`**Refer every ACS patient** to cardiac rehabilitation before discharge (Class I); CR reduces recurrent MI and hospital admissions, and cardiovascular death in longer follow-up. [Cochrane 2021]`,`**Exercise prescription (FITT)**: aerobic exercise **3-5 days a week**, moderate intensity (**40-60% heart rate reserve**, Borg RPE 11-14 or the talk test), **20-60 minutes**, plus resistance training 2-3 days a week. [AACVPR 6e]`,`On beta-blockers, use **perceived exertion** rather than a heart rate target; overall aim **150-300 minutes a week** of moderate activity. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Yoga-CaRe** (3959 Indian patients) found yoga-based rehabilitation did not significantly reduce major events but **improved quality of life and return to pre-infarct activities** - a culturally suited, low-cost option. [Yoga-CaRe, JACC 2020]`,`**Home-based and hybrid rehabilitation** (phone or app supervision) is effective and suits patients who cannot attend a centre. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Diet**: Mediterranean-style or Indian heart-healthy plate - more vegetables, fruit, pulses, whole grains, nuts and fish; limit saturated fat (ghee, vanaspati, coconut oil excess), **avoid trans fat**, salt under 5 g a day. [ESC 2021 Prevention]`,`**Tobacco cessation**: counselling plus **varenicline** (0.5 mg daily titrated to 1 mg twice daily for 12 weeks), nicotine replacement or bupropion; the national quitline is **1800-112-356**; e-cigarettes are banned in India. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Alcohol**: no safe amount for cardiovascular protection; advise to stop or keep to a minimum. [ESC 2021 Prevention]`]},{heading:`Return to work, driving, sex and travel`,points:[`**Return to work**: sedentary work after about **2-4 weeks** for uncomplicated MI with complete revascularisation; heavy manual work after a satisfactory exercise test, often 6-12 weeks. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**Driving (UK DVLA guidance, used in the absence of Indian rules)**: private car after **1 week** if successful PCI, LVEF over 40% and no other disqualifying condition, otherwise **4 weeks**; bus or lorry drivers stop for at least **6 weeks** and need functional testing. [DVLA 2025]`,`**Sexual activity** is reasonable **1 week or more** after uncomplicated MI if the patient can manage mild-moderate exertion (about 3-5 METs, such as climbing two flights of stairs) without symptoms. [AHA Sexual Activity Statement 2012]`,`**PDE5 inhibitors** are safe in stable patients but **never within 24 hours of sildenafil or vardenafil, or 48 hours of tadalafil, of a nitrate**. [AHA Sexual Activity Statement 2012]`,`**Air travel**: low-risk patients (successful revascularisation, LVEF over 45%, no complications) may fly after about **3 days**; others after about 10 days or when stable. [BCS Fitness to Fly 2010]`,`Give **written advice** in the patient's language - families in India often enforce excessive rest, which delays recovery and return to earning. [ICMR STW ACS]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: SGLT2 inhibitor or **GLP-1 receptor agonist** with proven cardiovascular benefit regardless of HbA1c; HbA1c target around 7% - see the Cardiometabolic protection topic. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**CKD**: statin (avoid high-dose rosuvastatin in severe CKD), ACE inhibitor or ARB with potassium checks, SGLT2 inhibitor if eGFR 20 or more, and dose-adjusted antithrombotics. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Elderly and frail**: same targets where possible, but start low, watch orthostatic hypotension and bleeding, and use clopidogrel when bleeding risk is high. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Women**: lower referral and attendance at rehabilitation; actively refer and address caregiving barriers. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Patients on oral anticoagulant**: OAC plus clopidogrel for up to 12 months after a short triple phase, then OAC alone - see the Anticoagulants topic. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Low-resource setting**: a generic polypill or NLEM drugs (aspirin, clopidogrel, atorvastatin, ramipril or enalapril, metoprolol) from Jan Aushadhi Kendras keep monthly cost low and improve adherence. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Young MI**: test for familial hypercholesterolaemia (LDL over 190 mg/dL), Lp(a), substance use and thrombophilia where indicated, and screen first-degree relatives. [CSI Dyslipidaemia 2024]`]},{heading:`Psychosocial care, follow-up schedule and prognosis`,points:[`**Depression affects about 1 in 5** MI survivors and roughly doubles mortality; screen at discharge and follow-up with PHQ-2 then PHQ-9. [AHA 2014 Statement]`,`**SSRIs are safe** after MI: **sertraline** (SADHART) or **escitalopram** (EsDEPACS, which also reduced cardiac events); avoid tricyclics, and limit citalopram to 20-40 mg because of QT prolongation. [ESC 2025 Mental Health Statement]`,`**Follow-up schedule**: review at **1-2 weeks**, lipids at **4-6 weeks**, echo at 6-12 weeks if LVEF was low, then every 3 months in the first year and every 6-12 months thereafter. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Annual review**: lipids, HbA1c, creatinine and potassium, ECG, BMI, BP, tobacco status, vaccination and DAPT plan. [ESC 2024 CCS]`,`**After 12 months** stop the P2Y12 inhibitor and continue single antiplatelet therapy lifelong, unless extended DAPT is chosen for high ischaemic and low bleeding risk. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Prognosis** depends on LVEF, completeness of revascularisation, adherence and risk-factor control; comprehensive secondary prevention cuts recurrent events by roughly half or more. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2023 ACS**: routine beta-blocker for all ACS IIa (Class I if LVEF 40% or less), ACE inhibitor for all reasonable, colchicine IIb, influenza vaccination Class I, cardiac rehabilitation Class I. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025 ACS**: add non-statin therapy if LDL stays **70 mg/dL or more** (Class 1) or **55-69 mg/dL** (2a) on maximal statin; routine colchicine uncertain; cardiac rehabilitation Class 1. [ACC/AHA 2025 ACS]`,`**REDUCE-AMI (2024), REBOOT and BETAMI-DANBLOCK (2025)** have narrowed beta-blocker indications in preserved LVEF; expect guideline downgrades for LVEF 50% or more. [REBOOT, NEJM 2025]`,`**CLEAR SYNERGY (2025)**: neither colchicine nor spironolactone reduced events when given routinely after MI. [CLEAR SYNERGY, NEJM 2025]`,`**ESC/EAS 2025 focused update** on dyslipidaemia supports early combination lipid-lowering and adds bempedoic acid for statin-intolerant patients. [ESC/EAS 2025 Lipid update]`,`**CSI 2024 dyslipidaemia guideline** sets LDL under 55 mg/dL and non-HDL under 85 mg/dL for very high risk Indian patients and recommends measuring Lp(a). [CSI Dyslipidaemia 2024]`,`**EMPACT-MI (2024)** and **DAPA-MI (2023)** showed SGLT2 inhibitors should be used for HF, low LVEF, diabetes or CKD after MI rather than for every patient. [EMPACT-MI, NEJM 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **'Secondary prevention after MI'**, **'Cardiac rehabilitation - phases and components'** and **'Counselling a patient at discharge after MI'**. [DNB FM papers]`,`**ABCDE mnemonic**: Antiplatelets and ACE inhibitor, Beta-blocker and BP, Cholesterol and Cigarettes, Diet and Diabetes, Education and Exercise. [ACC/AHA mnemonic]`,`State the **LDL target precisely**: under 55 mg/dL and at least 50% reduction; under 40 mg/dL if a second event within 2 years. [ESC/EAS 2019 Dyslipidaemia]`,`**Beta-blocker viva**: 'essential if LVEF 40% or less; REDUCE-AMI and REBOOT show no benefit in preserved LVEF' - shows current reading. [REBOOT, NEJM 2025]`,`**Common mistakes**: stopping the statin because LDL is 'normal', stopping DAPT early for dental work, and forgetting to refer for rehabilitation. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Nitrate plus PDE5 inhibitor** within 24-48 hours causes profound hypotension - always ask. [AHA Sexual Activity Statement 2012]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Discharge drugs after MI`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`75-100 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI bleeding; PPI if high risk`],[`Ticagrelor`,`90 mg oral twice daily`,`12 months (monotherapy after 1 month per ACC/AHA 2025)`,`Dyspnoea, bleeding; avoid with prior ICH`],[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg oral daily`,`12 months; with OAC or high bleeding risk`,`Bleeding; avoid omeprazole`],[`Atorvastatin`,`40-80 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong; lipids at 4-6 weeks`,`Myalgia, raised transaminases`],[`Rosuvastatin`,`20-40 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia; reduce in severe CKD`],[`Ezetimibe`,`10 mg oral daily`,`Add if LDL above target`,`Rarely myalgia, GI upset`],[`PCSK9 inhibitor (evolocumab)`,`140 mg SC every 2 weeks or 420 mg monthly`,`If LDL above target on statin plus ezetimibe`,`Injection-site reactions; cost`],[`Ramipril`,`2.5 mg twice daily, titrate to 5 mg twice daily`,`Lifelong; creatinine and K at 1-2 weeks`,`Cough, angioedema, hyperkalaemia; pregnancy`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`25 mg daily, titrate to 200 mg`,`Lifelong if LVEF 40% or less; review if preserved`,`Bradycardia, fatigue, bronchospasm`],[`Eplerenone`,`25 mg daily, titrate to 50 mg`,`If LVEF 40% or less with HF or diabetes`,`Hyperkalaemia; avoid K over 5.0, creatinine over 2.5`],[`Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral daily`,`If HF, LVEF 40% or less, diabetes or CKD`,`Genital infection, volume depletion, rare euglycaemic ketoacidosis`],[`Colchicine`,`0.5 mg oral daily`,`Optional, long term`,`Diarrhoea; avoid with strong CYP3A4 inhibitors and severe CKD`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg daily for 3 days, twice daily to day 7, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks (extend to 24)`,`Nausea, vivid dreams`],[`Sertraline`,`25-50 mg daily, up to 200 mg`,`At least 6-12 months for depression`,`GI upset, hyponatraemia, bleeding with antiplatelets`]]},{heading:`Phases of cardiac rehabilitation`,columns:[`Phase`,`When`,`Setting`,`Main content`],rows:[[`Phase I`,`Days in hospital`,`Ward`,`Mobilise from 12-24 h, education, smoking advice, referral`],[`Phase II`,`1-3 weeks after discharge, about 12 weeks`,`Hospital or supervised home-based`,`Monitored aerobic and resistance exercise, risk factors, psychology`],[`Phase III`,`After phase II, lifelong`,`Community or home`,`Self-directed exercise, diet, adherence, annual review`],[`Core components`,`All phases`,`Multidisciplinary`,`Assessment, exercise, diet, tobacco, BP, lipids, diabetes, psychosocial, return to work`]]},{heading:`Beta-blocker after MI by LVEF`,columns:[`LVEF`,`Evidence`,`Recommendation`],rows:[[`40% or less`,`Pre-reperfusion trials, HFrEF trials`,`Class I, lifelong`],[`41-49%`,`2025 individual-patient meta-analysis shows benefit`,`Recommended`],[`50% or more`,`REDUCE-AMI and REBOOT neutral; BETAMI-DANBLOCK modest benefit`,`Optional unless angina, arrhythmia or hypertension`],[`Stable years after MI`,`ABYSS: interruption not non-inferior`,`Individualise any decision to stop`]]},{heading:`Targets after MI`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Target`,`Source`],rows:[[`LDL cholesterol`,`Under 55 mg/dL and 50% reduction; under 40 if recurrent within 2 years`,`ESC/EAS 2019, CSI 2024`],[`Non-HDL cholesterol`,`Under 85 mg/dL`,`CSI 2024`],[`Blood pressure`,`Under 130/80 (systolic 120-129 if tolerated)`,`ESC 2024`],[`HbA1c (diabetes)`,`About 7% (individualised)`,`ESC 2023 Diabetes`],[`BMI and waist (Indian)`,`BMI under 23; waist under 90 cm men, 80 cm women`,`ICMR`],[`Physical activity`,`150-300 min/week moderate`,`ESC 2021 Prevention`],[`Tobacco`,`Complete cessation, including smokeless`,`ESC 2023 ACS`]]},{heading:`Return to activity after uncomplicated MI`,columns:[`Activity`,`Typical timing`,`Condition`],rows:[[`Walking`,`From day 1-2 in hospital, increasing daily`,`No symptoms`],[`Sexual activity`,`1 week or more`,`Can climb two flights of stairs without symptoms`],[`Car driving (DVLA)`,`1 week if PCI and LVEF over 40%; else 4 weeks`,`No angina or arrhythmia`],[`Bus or lorry driving (DVLA)`,`6 weeks or more`,`Functional test passed`],[`Sedentary work`,`2-4 weeks`,`Uncomplicated course`],[`Heavy manual work`,`6-12 weeks`,`Satisfactory exercise test`],[`Air travel`,`About 3 days (low risk) to 10 days`,`Stable, LVEF over 45% for early travel`]]}],redFlags:[`New or recurrent chest pain at rest after discharge - possible stent thrombosis or reinfarction, ECG and urgent referral`,`Breathlessness, orthopnoea or oedema - new heart failure, echo and GDMT review`,`Syncope or sustained palpitations - ventricular arrhythmia, especially if LVEF 35% or less`,`Black stools, haematemesis or major bleeding on DAPT - do not stop antiplatelets without cardiology input; resuscitate and refer`,`Potassium over 5.5 mmol/L or creatinine rise over 30% on ACE inhibitor or MRA`,`Suicidal thoughts or severe depression after MI`,`Planned surgery or dental extraction within 6-12 months of stenting - discuss DAPT with cardiology, do not stop on your own`],pearls:[`**ABCDE** of secondary prevention: Antiplatelet/ACE inhibitor, Beta-blocker/BP, Cholesterol/Cigarettes, Diet/Diabetes, Education/Exercise.`,`**LDL under 55 and halve it**; under 40 if a second event within 2 years.`,`**Beta-blocker is a must at LVEF 40% or less, a maybe at 50% or more** (REDUCE-AMI, REBOOT).`,`**Lipids at 4-6 weeks**, echo at 6-12 weeks if LVEF was low.`,`**Rehabilitation is Class I** - refer before the patient leaves the ward.`,`**36 sessions over 12 weeks** is the classic phase II programme.`,`**Two flights of stairs = ready for sex** (about 3-5 METs).`,`**Sertraline or escitalopram** for post-MI depression; avoid tricyclics.`,`**Never stop DAPT for a dental extraction** without discussing with cardiology.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-q1`,stem:`Six weeks after an anterior STEMI treated with PCI, a 54-year-old man on atorvastatin 80 mg has an LDL of 82 mg/dL. What is the next step per ESC guidance?`,options:[`Continue the same therapy; LDL under 100 mg/dL is adequate`,`Switch to rosuvastatin 10 mg`,`Add ezetimibe 10 mg daily`,`Add fenofibrate 145 mg daily`],answer:2,explanation:`After MI the target is LDL under 55 mg/dL with at least a 50% reduction; on maximal statin the next step is ezetimibe, then a PCSK9 inhibitor if still above target. Under 100 mg/dL is an outdated goal, rosuvastatin 10 mg is a lower-intensity statin than atorvastatin 80 mg, and fibrates do not lower LDL meaningfully or reduce events on top of statins.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-q2`,stem:`A 60-year-old woman had an inferior NSTEMI treated with complete PCI. LVEF is 58%, BP is controlled and there is no angina or arrhythmia. What does the current evidence say about long-term beta-blockers?`,options:[`Mandatory lifelong because they halve mortality in all MI patients`,`Contraindicated in women`,`Required only for the first 7 days`,`No proven benefit for death or reinfarction in preserved LVEF; use is optional`],answer:3,explanation:`REDUCE-AMI (LVEF 50% or more) and REBOOT (LVEF over 40%) found no reduction in death or reinfarction with routine beta-blockers after revascularised MI, so their long-term use is optional when LVEF is preserved and no other indication exists. The mortality benefit came from older trials of large infarcts with low LVEF; women are not a contraindication (REBOOT only hinted at possible harm), and there is no 7-day rule.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-q3`,stem:`Which of the following is the phase II component of cardiac rehabilitation?`,options:[`Supervised outpatient exercise training with risk-factor education over about 12 weeks`,`Bedside mobilisation and education during the hospital stay`,`Lifelong unsupervised community exercise`,`Pre-discharge coronary angiography`],answer:0,explanation:`Phase II is early outpatient, supervised (centre- or home-based) exercise training with education and psychosocial support, classically 36 sessions over about 12 weeks. Bedside mobilisation in hospital is phase I, lifelong self-directed maintenance is phase III, and angiography is part of acute management, not rehabilitation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-q4`,stem:`Two months after MI, a man on DAPT and statin asks about sildenafil. He uses sublingual nitroglycerin occasionally. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Sildenafil is absolutely contraindicated for life after MI`,`Sildenafil may be used, but no nitrate within 24 hours of it`,`Sildenafil is safe with nitrates if the dose is halved`,`Stop DAPT before taking sildenafil`],answer:1,explanation:`In a stable patient after MI, PDE5 inhibitors are safe, but nitrates must not be taken within 24 hours of sildenafil or vardenafil (48 hours for tadalafil) because of severe hypotension. MI itself is not a lifelong contraindication, halving the dose does not remove the interaction, and DAPT has nothing to do with sildenafil safety.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-q5`,stem:`Which statement about the Yoga-CaRe trial in India is correct?`,options:[`It showed a 50% reduction in mortality with yoga-based rehabilitation`,`It compared yoga with primary PCI`,`It improved quality of life and return to pre-infarct activities without a significant reduction in major events`,`It was stopped early for harm`],answer:2,explanation:`Yoga-CaRe randomised 3959 Indian patients after MI to yoga-based cardiac rehabilitation or enhanced standard care; major events were not significantly reduced, but quality of life and return to pre-infarct activities improved, making it a low-cost, acceptable option. It did not show a mortality reduction, did not involve PCI comparison, and was not stopped for harm.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 50-year-old man is being discharged after STEMI treated with primary PCI. Describe his secondary prevention, cardiac rehabilitation and follow-up plan.`,openingLines:[`Secondary prevention is lifelong drug and lifestyle therapy that reduces recurrent MI, stroke, heart failure and death after an MI.`,`It rests on the discharge bundle (DAPT, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker as indicated), risk-factor control and Class I referral to cardiac rehabilitation.`],answer:[{heading:`Discharge drugs`,points:[`Aspirin 75-100 mg plus ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily for 12 months (shorter if high bleeding risk).`,`Atorvastatin 80 mg; LDL under 55 mg/dL with 50% reduction; ezetimibe then PCSK9 inhibitor if needed.`,`Ramipril titrated; beta-blocker if LVEF under 50% or angina; MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor if LVEF 40% or less, HF or diabetes.`,`PPI if GI risk, influenza vaccine, SL nitrate.`]},{heading:`Current beta-blocker evidence`,points:[`Class I at LVEF 40% or less; REDUCE-AMI and REBOOT show no benefit in preserved LVEF.`]},{heading:`Lifestyle and risk factors`,points:[`Complete tobacco cessation with varenicline or NRT; quitline 1800-112-356.`,`Indian heart-healthy diet, salt under 5 g, weight and waist targets, BP under 130/80, HbA1c about 7%.`]},{heading:`Cardiac rehabilitation`,points:[`Phase I in hospital, phase II supervised 12 weeks (FITT: 3-5 days, 40-60% HRR, 20-60 min), phase III lifelong.`,`Yoga-based or home-based options; psychosocial support.`]},{heading:`Return to normal life`,points:[`Sex after 1 week if two flights of stairs tolerated; no nitrate with PDE5 inhibitors.`,`Driving after 1-4 weeks; sedentary work 2-4 weeks, heavy work after exercise test.`]},{heading:`Depression and follow-up`,points:[`PHQ-2/PHQ-9; sertraline or escitalopram.`,`Review at 1-2 weeks, lipids at 4-6 weeks, echo at 6-12 weeks if LVEF low, then 3-monthly in year 1.`]}],mustDraw:[`ABCDE table of secondary prevention`,`Flow chart of the three phases of cardiac rehabilitation`],markSplit:[{part:`Discharge drugs with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Beta-blocker evidence`,marks:1},{part:`Lifestyle and targets`,marks:2},{part:`Cardiac rehabilitation`,marks:2},{part:`Return to activity, depression and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`DAPT`,`LDL under 55`,`high-intensity statin`,`REDUCE-AMI`,`REBOOT`,`cardiac rehabilitation`,`FITT`,`ABCDE`]},{id:`cardiovascular-post-mi-rehab-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on cardiac rehabilitation after myocardial infarction.`,openingLines:[`Cardiac rehabilitation is a multidisciplinary programme of exercise, education, risk-factor control and psychosocial support after a cardiac event.`,`It is a Class I recommendation after ACS and reduces recurrent MI and hospital admissions.`],answer:[{heading:`Phases`,points:[`Phase I inpatient; phase II supervised outpatient (about 36 sessions, 12 weeks); phase III lifelong maintenance.`]},{heading:`Components`,points:[`Exercise (FITT), diet, tobacco cessation, BP, lipids, diabetes, weight, psychosocial care, return to work.`,`Exercise risk stratification by LVEF, arrhythmia and ischaemia.`]},{heading:`Evidence`,points:[`Cochrane 2021: fewer MIs and admissions; Yoga-CaRe India 2020: better quality of life.`]},{heading:`Barriers in India and solutions`,points:[`Few centres, distance, wage loss, low awareness.`,`Home-based, app or phone-supported and yoga-based programmes.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-phase rehabilitation diagram with timing and content`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and phases`,marks:1.5},{part:`Components`,marks:1.5},{part:`Evidence`,marks:1},{part:`Indian barriers and solutions`,marks:1}],keywords:[`cardiac rehabilitation`,`phase II`,`FITT`,`Yoga-CaRe`,`home-based`]}],references:[`Byrne RA et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes. European Heart Journal 2023;44:3720-3826`,`Rao SV et al. 2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes. Circulation/JACC 2025`,`Vrints C et al. 2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of chronic coronary syndromes. European Heart Journal 2024`,`Mach F et al. 2025 Focused Update of the 2019 ESC/EAS Guidelines for the management of dyslipidaemias. European Heart Journal 2025`,`Cardiological Society of India Clinical Practice Guidelines for Dyslipidaemia Management, 2024`,`Yndigegn T et al. REDUCE-AMI (NEJM 2024) and Ibanez B et al. REBOOT (NEJM 2025) - beta-blockers after MI with preserved ejection fraction`,`Prabhakaran D et al. Effectiveness of an Indian yoga-based cardiac rehabilitation program (Yoga-CaRe). JACC 2020`,`AACVPR Guidelines for Cardiac Rehabilitation Programs, 6th edition; Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`]});var r=[];r.push({id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy`,title:`Antiplatelet therapy: aspirin, P2Y12 inhibitors and DAPT duration`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Antiplatelet therapy** blocks platelet activation at **COX-1 (aspirin)** and the **P2Y12 ADP receptor (clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor)**; after an ACS the default is **12 months of dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT)** and after elective PCI **6 months**, shortened to **1-3 months in high bleeding risk (ARC-HBR, PRECISE-DAPT 25 or more)** with a PPI, then **lifelong single antiplatelet therapy**.`,keywords:[`aspirin`,`acetylsalicylic acid`,`clopidogrel`,`prasugrel`,`ticagrelor`,`cangrelor`,`P2Y12 inhibitor`,`DAPT`,`dual antiplatelet therapy`,`SAPT`,`PRECISE-DAPT`,`ARC-HBR`,`DAPT score`,`de-escalation`,`CYP2C19`,`pantoprazole`,`GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor`,`tirofiban`,`cilostazol`,`stent thrombosis`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and epidemiology`,points:[`**Antiplatelet drugs** inhibit platelet adhesion, activation or aggregation and are the cornerstone of preventing **arterial (white, platelet-rich) thrombosis** in the coronary, cerebral and peripheral circulations; venous (red, fibrin-rich) thrombus responds better to anticoagulants. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Single antiplatelet therapy (SAPT)** is one drug, usually aspirin 75-100 mg or clopidogrel 75 mg, given lifelong in established ASCVD; **dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT)** is aspirin plus a **P2Y12 inhibitor** for a defined period after ACS or stenting. [ESC 2023]`,`In high-risk patients antiplatelet therapy reduces serious vascular events (MI, stroke, vascular death) by about **one quarter**, at the cost of roughly a **1.5-2-fold rise in major extracranial bleeding**. [ATT Collaboration 2002]`,`In **primary prevention** ASCEND, ARRIVE and ASPREE (2018) showed that bleeding largely cancels the small benefit, so routine aspirin is no longer advised - see the Cardiovascular risk assessment and primary prevention topic. [USPSTF 2022]`,`**Indian burden**: cardiovascular disease causes about **28% of all deaths** in India and ischaemic heart disease is the single leading cause. [India State-Level Disease Burden Initiative 2018]`,`The **CREATE registry** (20,468 Indian ACS patients) found about **61% were STEMI**, with younger age, more diabetes and longer pre-hospital delay than in Western registries - so DAPT decisions in India often concern people of working age. [CREATE 2008]`,`South Asians develop CAD about **10 years earlier** than Western populations, often with diabetes and diffuse multivessel disease - features of **high ischaemic risk** that push towards potent or longer DAPT. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Aspirin and clopidogrel** are on the **National List of Essential Medicines 2022** and sold cheaply through **Jan Aushadhi** kendras; ticagrelor and prasugrel are available as branded generics at higher cost. [NLEM 2022]`,`Premature discontinuation of DAPT in the first month after a stent is the strongest predictor of **stent thrombosis**, which usually presents as STEMI and carries a high mortality. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Indications: who needs single or dual antiplatelet therapy`,points:[`**ACS** (STEMI, NSTEMI, unstable angina), whether treated with PCI, CABG or medically: **DAPT for 12 months** by default, then lifelong SAPT. [ESC 2023]`,`**Elective PCI for chronic coronary syndrome**: aspirin plus **clopidogrel for 6 months**, then lifelong SAPT. [ESC 2024]`,`**Chronic coronary syndrome without PCI**: **aspirin 75-100 mg** daily lifelong; **clopidogrel 75 mg** if aspirin is not tolerated. [ESC 2024]`,`**Non-cardioembolic ischaemic stroke or TIA**: aspirin, clopidogrel or aspirin-dipyridamole long term; **minor stroke or high-risk TIA** gets short DAPT for 21-30 days. [AHA/ASA 2021]`,`**Symptomatic peripheral arterial disease**: SAPT (clopidogrel or aspirin); after endovascular revascularisation, DAPT for 1-6 months; low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin for high-risk patients. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**After CABG**: aspirin lifelong; complete 12 months of DAPT if the CABG was done for ACS. [ESC 2023]`,`**After TAVI** without another indication for anticoagulation: **lifelong SAPT** alone (POPular TAVI showed DAPT only added bleeding). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Pregnancy at high risk of pre-eclampsia**: low-dose aspirin from the end of the first trimester to 36 weeks. [FIGO 2019]`,`**Not an indication**: aspirin for **stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation** - it is ineffective and still causes bleeding; AF needs an anticoagulant (see the Atrial fibrillation topic). [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: platelet activation and arterial thrombosis`,points:[`Platelets are anucleate fragments of megakaryocytes with a lifespan of **7-10 days**; about 10% are replaced daily, so the effect of an irreversible inhibitor wears off over **5-7 days** as new platelets appear. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Adhesion**: plaque rupture or erosion exposes collagen and von Willebrand factor; platelets bind vWF through **GP Ib-IX-V** under high shear and collagen through **GP VI and GP Ia-IIa**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Activation**: platelets change shape and release granules; **ADP** from dense granules and **thromboxane A2** made by COX-1 recruit neighbouring platelets, and **thrombin** acting on PAR-1 and PAR-4 is the most potent agonist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**P2Y12** is a Gi-coupled ADP receptor that lowers platelet cAMP and **sustains GP IIb/IIIa activation** - the key amplification loop, which is why P2Y12 blockade is so effective; P2Y1 (Gq) mediates shape change. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Aggregation**: the final common pathway is activation of **GP IIb/IIIa (integrin alpha-IIb beta-3)**, which binds fibrinogen and vWF to bridge platelets - the target of tirofiban and eptifibatide. [Harrison 22e]`,`Activated platelets expose phosphatidylserine and assemble the **tenase and prothrombinase** complexes, coupling platelets to coagulation - the rationale for adding low-dose rivaroxaban (**dual pathway inhibition**). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Endothelial prostacyclin (PGI2)** raises platelet cAMP and inhibits aggregation; low-dose aspirin acetylates platelet COX-1 in the **portal circulation** before reaching systemic endothelium, largely sparing PGI2. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Arterial thrombi form under high shear and are **platelet-rich white thrombi**, while venous thrombi form under stasis and are **fibrin and red-cell rich** - explaining why antiplatelets prevent MI and anticoagulants prevent DVT and AF stroke. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **drug-eluting stent** is a foreign surface; until its struts are **endothelialised** platelets adhere to them, hence obligatory DAPT - shorter with modern thin-strut DES than with older stents. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Mechanism of action of individual drugs`,points:[`**Aspirin** irreversibly **acetylates serine 529 of COX-1**, blocking thromboxane A2 synthesis for the life of the platelet; doses above 100 mg add no antithrombotic efficacy but more GI toxicity. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Clopidogrel** is a thienopyridine **prodrug**: about **85% is hydrolysed by esterases to an inactive metabolite**, and the remaining 15% needs **two CYP steps (mainly CYP2C19)** to form the active thiol that irreversibly blocks P2Y12. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**CYP2C19 loss-of-function alleles (*2, *3)** reduce active metabolite and increase stent thrombosis; they are common in South and East Asians, making clopidogrel response variable in Indian patients. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Prasugrel** is also an irreversible thienopyridine prodrug but needs only **one CYP step** after esterase hydrolysis, so it acts within about **30 minutes** and more consistently than clopidogrel. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ticagrelor** is a direct-acting, **reversible, allosteric P2Y12 antagonist** with a half-life of about **7-9 hours** (hence twice daily); it also blocks ENT1 adenosine reuptake, which explains **dyspnoea and ventricular pauses**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Cangrelor** is an IV, direct, reversible P2Y12 inhibitor with a half-life of **3-6 minutes**; platelet function recovers within about 1 hour of stopping - used during PCI in P2Y12-naive patients and as a surgical bridge. [ESC 2023]`,`**GP IIb/IIIa inhibitors** (tirofiban, eptifibatide; abciximab withdrawn) block the final common pathway; they are IV only and now reserved for **bail-out** in no-reflow or thrombotic complications of PCI. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cilostazol** (PDE3 inhibitor) raises cAMP and is both antiplatelet and vasodilator - used for claudication; **dipyridamole** blocks adenosine uptake and PDE - used with aspirin after stroke; **vorapaxar** (PAR-1 antagonist) is not marketed in India. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification of antiplatelet drugs and regimens`,points:[`**By target**: COX-1 inhibitor (aspirin), P2Y12 inhibitors, GP IIb/IIIa antagonists, phosphodiesterase inhibitors (cilostazol, dipyridamole) and PAR-1 antagonists (vorapaxar). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**P2Y12 inhibitors** are subdivided into **thienopyridines** (ticlopidine, clopidogrel, prasugrel - irreversible prodrugs) and **non-thienopyridines** (ticagrelor oral, cangrelor IV - direct and reversible). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**By reversibility**: irreversible (aspirin, clopidogrel, prasugrel) - effect lasts the platelet lifespan; reversible (ticagrelor, cangrelor, tirofiban, eptifibatide) - offset follows drug clearance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**By potency**: prasugrel and ticagrelor are the **potent P2Y12 inhibitors**, reducing ischaemic events in ACS more than clopidogrel but causing more bleeding. [ESC 2023]`,`**Regimens** examiners expect: SAPT, DAPT, **triple antithrombotic therapy** (oral anticoagulant plus DAPT), **dual antithrombotic therapy** (anticoagulant plus clopidogrel) and **dual pathway inhibition** (aspirin plus rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily). [ESC 2023]`,`**Ticlopidine** is obsolete because of neutropenia, aplastic anaemia and TTP; clopidogrel replaced it. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Bleeding is graded by BARC**: type 2 overt and actionable, type 3a-c major (3c intracranial), type 4 CABG-related and type 5 fatal - the language of every DAPT trial. [BARC 2011]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment before and during therapy`,points:[`Record the **indication and date**: ACS or elective PCI, date of the stent, number and type of stents (from the stent card) and the planned DAPT stop date - every later decision depends on these. [ESC 2023]`,`Take a **bleeding history**: prior GI bleed or peptic ulcer, haematuria, epistaxis needing packing, intracranial haemorrhage, recent surgery or trauma, and liver disease or alcohol excess. [ARC-HBR 2019]`,`Ask about **NSAIDs, steroids, SSRIs, anticoagulants and herbal products** (for example ginkgo) that add bleeding risk, and about **dyspepsia** that may signal an ulcer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **prior stroke or TIA** (contraindicates prasugrel), **bradycardia or heart block** and **COPD/asthma** (ticagrelor side effects), and **aspirin allergy or aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease**. [ESC 2023]`,`Examine for **pallor, bruising, petechiae, melaena on rectal examination**, uncontrolled BP (raises intracranial bleeding risk) and stigmata of chronic liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dyspnoea on ticagrelor** affects about **14%** (PLATO), is usually mild, early and not due to bronchospasm or heart failure - exclude HF, anaemia and ischaemia first, then switch to prasugrel or clopidogrel if it persists. [PLATO 2009]`,`**Recurrent chest pain after PCI**: abrupt pain with ST elevation days to weeks after a stent suggests **stent thrombosis** (often DAPT stopped); gradual return of angina at 3-12 months suggests **in-stent restenosis**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Adherence**: ask whether DAPT was stopped for a dental extraction, a minor procedure or cost - the commonest avoidable cause of stent thrombosis. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations and risk scores`,points:[`**Baseline**: haemoglobin, platelet count, creatinine with eGFR and liver function before starting DAPT - these values feed PRECISE-DAPT and ARC-HBR. [ESC 2023]`,`**PRECISE-DAPT** uses five items - **haemoglobin, white cell count, age, creatinine clearance and prior spontaneous bleeding**; a score of **25 or more** indicates high bleeding risk and favours short DAPT (3-6 months). [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**ARC-HBR** defines high bleeding risk as **one major or two minor criteria**, corresponding to a one-year risk of **BARC 3 or 5 bleeding of 4% or more** or intracranial bleeding of 1% or more. [ARC-HBR 2019]`,`ARC-HBR **major criteria** include long-term oral anticoagulation, **eGFR under 30**, **Hb under 11 g/dL**, platelets under 100,000/mm3, cirrhosis with portal hypertension, active cancer, prior spontaneous ICH and recent major surgery or trauma. [ARC-HBR 2019]`,`ARC-HBR **minor criteria**: **age 75 or more**, eGFR 30-59, Hb 11-12.9 g/dL (men) or 11-11.9 g/dL (women), a bleed in the past 12 months, long-term NSAIDs or steroids, and any ischaemic stroke not meeting the major criterion. [ARC-HBR 2019]`,`The **DAPT score** is applied at 12 months: diabetes, smoking, MI at presentation, prior PCI or MI, stent under 3 mm, heart failure or LVEF under 30% and vein-graft PCI add points, age subtracts; **2 or more favours continuing** DAPT. [ACC/AHA 2016 DAPT]`,`**High ischaemic risk** features: diffuse multivessel disease in diabetics, CKD, prior stent thrombosis on therapy, 3 or more stents or lesions treated, total stent length over 60 mm, bifurcation with two stents and chronic total occlusion. [ESC 2023]`,`**Platelet function testing and CYP2C19 genotyping** are not routine; they may guide de-escalation where available - POPular Genetics showed genotype-guided clopidogrel reduced bleeding without more ischaemic events. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`OPD management: doses, loading and choice of P2Y12 inhibitor`,points:[`**Aspirin**: loading **150-300 mg chewed** (non-enteric-coated) in ACS, then **75-100 mg once daily** lifelong; IV 75-250 mg if oral intake is impossible. [ESC 2023]`,`**Clopidogrel**: loading **300-600 mg** (600 mg before PCI; 300 mg with fibrinolysis, and 75 mg without loading if over 75 years with fibrinolysis), then **75 mg once daily**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Prasugrel**: **60 mg loading, then 10 mg once daily**; **5 mg daily if 75 years or older or weight under 60 kg**; contraindicated after any **prior stroke or TIA**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Ticagrelor**: **180 mg loading, then 90 mg twice daily** for 12 months; **60 mg twice daily** for extended therapy after 12 months in high-ischaemic-risk post-MI patients (PEGASUS); keep aspirin at **75-100 mg** with it. [ESC 2023]`,`**Choice in ACS**: prasugrel or ticagrelor is preferred over clopidogrel when bleeding risk allows (PLATO: CV death, MI or stroke **9.8% vs 11.7%**; TRITON-TIMI 38: **9.9% vs 12.1%**). [ESC 2023]`,`ESC 2023 prefers **prasugrel over ticagrelor** for ACS patients proceeding to PCI (ISAR-REACT 5, class IIa); ACC/AHA 2025 treats the two as equal choices. [ESC 2023]`,`**Clopidogrel** remains the drug with **fibrinolysis**, with an **oral anticoagulant**, after **elective PCI** for chronic coronary syndrome, and when bleeding risk is high or potent drugs are unaffordable. [ESC 2024]`,`**Pretreatment**: routine P2Y12 pretreatment is **not recommended in NSTE-ACS** when angiography is planned within 24 hours (anatomy unknown, CABG possible); it may be considered in STEMI before primary PCI. [ESC 2023]`,`**Morphine** delays absorption of oral P2Y12 inhibitors; crushed or chewed tablets act faster in STEMI. [ESC 2023]`,`**Long-term SAPT**: aspirin 75-100 mg daily, or **clopidogrel 75 mg** as an alternative - HOST-EXAM showed fewer thrombotic and bleeding events with clopidogrel than aspirin after PCI. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`DAPT duration, de-escalation and switching`,points:[`**ACS (PCI or medical)**: default **12 months of DAPT** with a potent P2Y12 inhibitor, then lifelong SAPT. [ESC 2023]`,`**Elective PCI for CCS**: default **6 months** of aspirin plus clopidogrel; **1-3 months** if high bleeding risk. [ESC 2024]`,`**ACS with high bleeding risk**: drop aspirin or the P2Y12 inhibitor after **1 month** (IIb) or 3 months - MASTER DAPT showed 1 month non-inferior for ischaemia with less bleeding. [ESC 2023]`,`**Abbreviated DAPT**: after 3-6 months event-free in patients not at high ischaemic risk, continue a single antiplatelet, **preferably the P2Y12 inhibitor** (TWILIGHT, TICO, STOPDAPT-2), class IIa. [ESC 2023]`,`**ACC/AHA 2025**: in ACS patients who tolerate DAPT, transition to **ticagrelor monotherapy at 1 month or later** is recommended (class 1) to reduce bleeding. [ACC/AHA 2025]`,`**De-escalation** (prasugrel or ticagrelor to clopidogrel, unguided or guided by genotype or platelet function) may be considered (IIb) for bleeding, side effects or cost - preferably after the first 30 days, when thrombotic risk is highest. [ESC 2023]`,`**Switching**: ticagrelor to clopidogrel - **600 mg loading 24 hours after the last ticagrelor dose**; clopidogrel to ticagrelor - **180 mg loading irrespective of timing**; clopidogrel to prasugrel - **60 mg loading irrespective of timing**. [International consensus 2017]`,`**Extended therapy beyond 12 months** in high ischaemic and low bleeding risk: aspirin plus **ticagrelor 60 mg twice daily** (PEGASUS) or aspirin plus **rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily** (COMPASS), class IIa. [ESC 2023]`,`**AF with ACS or PCI**: triple therapy (DOAC + aspirin + clopidogrel) for **up to 1 week** (up to 30 days if high ischaemic risk), then DOAC + clopidogrel to 12 months (6 months after elective PCI), then DOAC alone; no prasugrel or ticagrelor in triple therapy. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`PPI co-prescription and drug interactions`,points:[`A **proton pump inhibitor is recommended with DAPT** (class I) in patients at increased GI bleeding risk - prior ulcer or bleed, age 65 or more, anticoagulant, steroid or NSAID use - and is widely given to all on DAPT. [ESC 2023]`,`**COGENT**: omeprazole added to clopidogrel cut upper GI events (**1.1% vs 2.9%** at 180 days) with no significant increase in cardiovascular events. [COGENT 2010]`,`**Omeprazole and esomeprazole** inhibit CYP2C19 and reduce clopidogrel's active metabolite; prefer **pantoprazole 40 mg daily** (or rabeprazole) with clopidogrel; there is no such interaction with prasugrel or ticagrelor. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**H2-receptor blockers** protect less than PPIs; **test and treat H. pylori** in those with a past ulcer before long-term aspirin. [Maastricht VI 2022]`,`**NSAIDs** add GI bleeding risk and should be avoided with DAPT; ibuprofen taken before aspirin blocks aspirin's access to COX-1 - give aspirin at least **30 minutes before or 8 hours after** ibuprofen. [FDA 2006]`,`**Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors** (ketoconazole, clarithromycin, ritonavir) raise ticagrelor levels and **inducers** (rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin) lower them - avoid the combination; rifampicin matters in Indian patients on TB treatment. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Ticagrelor raises **simvastatin** levels (keep simvastatin at 40 mg or less) and **digoxin** levels; atorvastatin and rosuvastatin are preferred. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Adding an **oral anticoagulant** to DAPT roughly doubles to triples bleeding - the reason triple therapy is kept to the shortest possible period. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Peri-operative and peri-procedural management`,points:[`Continue **aspirin** through most non-cardiac surgery in patients with a stent if bleeding risk allows; stop it only for **closed-space surgery** (intracranial, spinal canal, posterior eye chamber) or very high bleeding risk. [ESC 2022]`,`When a P2Y12 inhibitor must be interrupted, stop **ticagrelor 3-5 days**, **clopidogrel 5 days** and **prasugrel 7 days** before surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Defer elective surgery** until DAPT is complete: **6 months after elective PCI** and **12 months after ACS**; time-sensitive surgery may proceed after at least **1 month** (elective PCI) or **3 months** (ACS) by heart-team decision. [ESC 2022]`,`Very high thrombotic risk (stent within 1 month) with urgent surgery: bridge with IV **cangrelor or tirofiban/eptifibatide** in a cardiac centre; **LMWH does not protect against stent thrombosis**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Restart** the P2Y12 inhibitor as soon as haemostasis allows, usually within **24-48 hours** after surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Dental extraction, cataract surgery, skin surgery and diagnostic endoscopy** can be done on aspirin and usually on DAPT with local haemostasis (pressure, sutures, tranexamic acid mouthwash) - never stop DAPT for a tooth extraction in the first year after a stent. [ESC 2022]`,`**Neuraxial anaesthesia**: aspirin alone is acceptable; stop clopidogrel **5-7 days**, ticagrelor **5-7 days** and prasugrel **7-10 days** before. [ASRA 2018]`,`**CABG**: continue aspirin; stop ticagrelor at least **3 days**, clopidogrel **5 days** and prasugrel **7 days** before non-emergency surgery. [ESC 2023]`,`**High-risk endoscopic procedures** (polypectomy, sphincterotomy): continue aspirin, stop the P2Y12 inhibitor 5 days before only if the stent is beyond the minimum DAPT period; otherwise discuss with cardiology. [BSG/ESGE 2021]`]},{heading:`Bleeding on antiplatelet therapy: IPD and emergency management`,points:[`**Trivial bleeding** (bruising, self-limiting epistaxis): continue DAPT, reassure and reinforce adherence; consider shortening DAPT or switching a potent drug to clopidogrel if it recurs. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**Mild bleeding** (needs medical attention but not admission): continue DAPT, consider shortening or de-escalating, and treat the cause. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**Moderate bleeding** (Hb fall over 3 g/dL or admission, haemodynamically stable): consider SAPT - keep the **P2Y12 inhibitor in upper GI bleeding** - and restart DAPT as soon as safe. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**Severe bleeding** (Hb fall over 5 g/dL, stable): stop DAPT, continue SAPT if possible; **life-threatening bleeding**: stop all antiplatelets, resuscitate and consider platelet transfusion. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`**Platelet transfusion** is ineffective for **ticagrelor** in the first day because circulating drug blocks the transfused platelets; the specific reversal antibody **bentracimab** is still under evaluation and not available in India. [ESC 2023]`,`**Antiplatelet-associated spontaneous ICH**: do **not** routinely transfuse platelets unless surgery is planned (PATCH trial showed harm); **desmopressin 0.4 mcg/kg IV** may be considered. [AHA/ASA 2022 ICH]`,`**Upper GI bleed**: resuscitate, early endoscopy within 24 hours, IV PPI after haemostasis, and **restart aspirin as soon as haemostasis is secured** - early resumption lowered mortality in a randomised trial. [ESGE 2021]`,`**Tranexamic acid** does not reduce death in GI bleeding and increases venous thrombosis (HALT-IT), so it is not routine; it is useful topically for mouth and nose bleeding. [HALT-IT 2020]`,`Never stop all antiplatelets for longer than necessary in a patient with a stent under 1 month old - involve cardiology the same day and plan the restart. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly (75 years or more)**: more bleeding; use prasugrel 5 mg or prefer clopidogrel - POPular AGE (70 years or more with NSTE-ACS) showed less bleeding with clopidogrel than ticagrelor without more ischaemic events. [ESC 2023]`,`**CKD**: no dose change for aspirin, clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor, but data in dialysis are limited and **clopidogrel is generally preferred when eGFR is under 15**; eGFR under 30 is an ARC-HBR major criterion. [ESC 2023]`,`**Diabetes**: higher platelet reactivity and greater benefit from potent P2Y12 inhibitors; aspirin for primary prevention only if cardiovascular risk is high and bleeding risk low, after shared decision. [ADA 2026]`,`**Pregnancy**: low-dose aspirin is safe; **aspirin 150 mg at night from 11-14 weeks until 36 weeks** prevents preterm pre-eclampsia in high-risk women (ASPRE); clopidogrel only if essential. [FIGO 2019]`,`**Children**: avoid aspirin under 16 years (**Reye syndrome**) except in **Kawasaki disease** (high-dose in the acute phase, then **3-5 mg/kg/day** for 6-8 weeks) and after some cardiac surgery. [AHA 2017 Kawasaki]`,`**Minor stroke (NIHSS 3 or less) or high-risk TIA (ABCD2 4 or more)**: **clopidogrel 300-600 mg load plus aspirin for 21 days** (CHANCE, POINT), then SAPT; ticagrelor plus aspirin for 30 days is an alternative (THALES). [AHA/ASA 2021]`,`**PAD**: clopidogrel 75 mg or aspirin long term; after endovascular revascularisation, aspirin plus **rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily** reduces limb and cardiovascular events (VOYAGER-PAD). [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Thrombocytopenia (cancer, dengue)**: expert consensus allows aspirin above **10,000/mm3** and DAPT with clopidogrel above **30,000-50,000/mm3**; avoid prasugrel and ticagrelor below 50,000 - decide with cardiology if the stent is recent. [SCAI 2016]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: aspirin plus clopidogrel is the practical default; for suspected ACS at a PHC give **aspirin 300-325 mg chewed plus clopidogrel 300 mg (600 mg if primary PCI is planned)** with atorvastatin 80 mg before transfer. [ICMR STW]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Aspirin**: dyspepsia, peptic ulcer and GI bleeding (dose-related), bronchospasm in **aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease**, rarely angioedema; desensitisation is possible when aspirin is essential. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clopidogrel**: bleeding, rash and diarrhoea; rarely **thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura** and neutropenia (far rarer than with ticlopidine). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ticagrelor**: dyspnoea, **bradyarrhythmia and ventricular pauses**, raised uric acid and creatinine, and more minor bleeding; caution in sick sinus syndrome or high-grade AV block without a pacemaker. [PLATO 2009]`,`**Prasugrel**: more major bleeding than clopidogrel; net harm with prior stroke or TIA, and no net benefit at 10 mg in those **75 years or older or under 60 kg**. [TRITON-TIMI 38 2007]`,`**Follow-up**: review at about 1 month and then every 3-6 months while on DAPT - adherence, bleeding, side effects and the **planned DAPT stop date written on the discharge card**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Check **Hb and creatinine** at least yearly on long-term therapy, and sooner with melaena, fatigue or breathlessness. [ESC 2024]`,`**Counselling**: never stop DAPT without asking the cardiologist; carry the stent card; tell dentists and surgeons; avoid over-the-counter painkillers; report black stools, blood in urine or vomit. [ESC 2023]`,`**Programme link**: NP-NCD clinics provide free long-term secondary prevention drugs, PCI is covered under **PM-JAY**, and coronary stent prices have been capped by the **NPPA since 2017**. [MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2023 ACS** (one guideline for the whole ACS spectrum): 12-month DAPT default, **prasugrel preferred over ticagrelor** for PCI (IIa), no routine pretreatment in NSTE-ACS, abbreviated DAPT by bleeding risk and de-escalation (IIb). [ESC 2023]`,`**ESC 2024 CCS**: 6-month DAPT after elective PCI, **1-3 months in high bleeding risk**, clopidogrel accepted as an alternative to aspirin for long-term SAPT, and dual pathway inhibition for high ischaemic risk. [ESC 2024]`,`**ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI 2025 ACS**: ticagrelor or prasugrel preferred over clopidogrel for PCI, **ticagrelor monotherapy after at least 1 month of DAPT** (class 1), aspirin 75-100 mg for maintenance. [ACC/AHA 2025]`,`**ESC 2024 PAD**: clopidogrel may be preferred over aspirin for SAPT; low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin after revascularisation. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**ACC/AHA 2024 perioperative**: continue aspirin in most patients with a stent and stop P2Y12 inhibitors by drug-specific intervals, broadly matching **ESC 2022**. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: in AF with ACS or PCI, up to 1 week of triple therapy then **DOAC plus clopidogrel**, with antiplatelet stopped at 12 months (6 months after elective PCI). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Trials shaping 2023-2025 practice**: ULTIMATE-DAPT (ticagrelor alone after 1 month in ACS reduced bleeding without more ischaemia) and STOPDAPT-3 (aspirin-free prasugrel from day 1 did not reduce bleeding and had more coronary events) - so at least 1 month of DAPT remains standard. [ACC/AHA 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic case: a patient 4 months after a DES for NSTEMI needs an **elective hernia repair** - defer to 12 months; if it cannot wait, continue aspirin, stop ticagrelor 3-5 days before, operate in a PCI-capable centre and restart early. [ESC 2022]`,`Viva: why is **low-dose aspirin** enough? Platelet COX-1 is irreversibly saturated by 75-100 mg; higher doses only add GI toxicity and, with ticagrelor, reduce benefit. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Common mistake: stopping DAPT for a **dental extraction** - local haemostasis is enough. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: giving **prasugrel** after a prior stroke or TIA, or **10 mg** to a 78-year-old. [TRITON-TIMI 38 2007]`,`Common mistake: prescribing **omeprazole or esomeprazole** with clopidogrel - use pantoprazole. [ESC 2017 DAPT]`,`Common mistake: aspirin instead of an anticoagulant for **AF stroke prevention** - ineffective and still causes bleeding. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`Know the five **PRECISE-DAPT** items, the **one major or two minor** ARC-HBR rule and the **5-7-3** stop rule (clopidogrel 5, prasugrel 7, ticagrelor 3-5 days). [ARC-HBR 2019]`,`Know which antiplatelets are **reversible** (ticagrelor, cangrelor, tirofiban, eptifibatide) and why platelet transfusion fails for ticagrelor. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antiplatelet drugs: doses and cautions`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`Load 150-300 mg chewed; then 75-100 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong in established ASCVD`,`Dyspepsia, ulcer, GI bleed; bronchospasm in AERD; avoid under 16 years (Reye) except Kawasaki`],[`Clopidogrel`,`Load 300-600 mg; then 75 mg oral once daily`,`6-12 months as part of DAPT, or lifelong SAPT`,`Bleeding, rash, rare TTP; poor response with CYP2C19 loss-of-function; avoid omeprazole/esomeprazole`],[`Prasugrel`,`Load 60 mg; then 10 mg oral once daily (5 mg if 75+ years or under 60 kg)`,`12 months after ACS with PCI`,`Major bleeding; contraindicated after stroke/TIA; stop 7 days before surgery`],[`Ticagrelor`,`Load 180 mg; then 90 mg oral twice daily; 60 mg twice daily after 12 months`,`12 months; extended selectively up to 3 years`,`Dyspnoea, ventricular pauses, raised uric acid; avoid strong CYP3A4 inhibitors/inducers; aspirin 100 mg or less`],[`Cangrelor`,`IV 30 mcg/kg bolus then 4 mcg/kg/min; bridge 0.75 mcg/kg/min`,`During PCI (at least 2 h) or as a pre-surgical bridge`,`Bleeding; effect gone within about 1 hour`],[`Tirofiban`,`IV 25 mcg/kg bolus then 0.15 mcg/kg/min (halve infusion if CrCl 60 or less)`,`Bail-out during PCI, up to 12-18 h`,`Bleeding, thrombocytopenia - check platelets`],[`Eptifibatide`,`IV 180 mcg/kg bolus (repeat once in PCI) then 2 mcg/kg/min (1 mcg/kg/min if CrCl under 50)`,`Bail-out during PCI, 12-18 h`,`Bleeding, thrombocytopenia; avoid on dialysis`],[`Cilostazol`,`100 mg oral twice daily, 30 min before or 2 h after food`,`Claudication; stop if no benefit at 3-6 months`,`Headache, palpitations, diarrhoea; contraindicated in any heart failure`],[`Aspirin-dipyridamole MR`,`25/200 mg oral twice daily`,`Secondary stroke prevention`,`Headache (start once daily), may worsen angina`]]},{heading:`Classification of antiplatelet drugs`,columns:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`,`Reversibility and offset`],rows:[[`COX-1 inhibitor`,`Aspirin`,`Irreversible acetylation of COX-1; no thromboxane A2`,`Irreversible; recovery with new platelets over 5-7 days`],[`Thienopyridine P2Y12 inhibitors`,`Clopidogrel, prasugrel (ticlopidine obsolete)`,`Prodrug; active metabolite binds P2Y12 covalently`,`Irreversible; stop clopidogrel 5 days, prasugrel 7 days`],[`Non-thienopyridine P2Y12 inhibitors`,`Ticagrelor (oral), cangrelor (IV)`,`Direct-acting, no metabolic activation needed`,`Reversible; ticagrelor 3-5 days, cangrelor about 1 hour`],[`GP IIb/IIIa antagonists`,`Tirofiban, eptifibatide (abciximab withdrawn)`,`Block fibrinogen binding - final common pathway`,`Reversible; platelet function returns within hours`],[`Phosphodiesterase inhibitors`,`Cilostazol, dipyridamole`,`Raise platelet cAMP; also vasodilate`,`Reversible`],[`PAR-1 antagonist`,`Vorapaxar (not marketed in India)`,`Blocks the thrombin receptor on platelets`,`Very long half-life; effectively irreversible`]]},{heading:`Clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Clopidogrel`,`Prasugrel`,`Ticagrelor`],rows:[[`Chemical type`,`Thienopyridine prodrug`,`Thienopyridine prodrug`,`Cyclopentyl-triazolopyrimidine; active drug`],[`Activation`,`Two CYP steps (CYP2C19 key)`,`One CYP step`,`None needed`],[`Receptor binding`,`Irreversible`,`Irreversible`,`Reversible`],[`Onset after loading`,`2-6 hours`,`About 30 minutes`,`About 30 minutes`],[`Pivotal ACS trial`,`CURE (plus aspirin vs aspirin alone)`,`TRITON-TIMI 38`,`PLATO`],[`Stop before surgery`,`5 days`,`7 days`,`3-5 days`],[`Main cautions`,`CYP2C19 poor metabolisers; omeprazole`,`Prior stroke/TIA; 75+ years or under 60 kg use 5 mg`,`Dyspnoea, bradyarrhythmia, CYP3A4 drugs, aspirin over 100 mg`],[`With oral anticoagulant`,`Yes - P2Y12 inhibitor of choice`,`No`,`No`]]},{heading:`ARC-HBR criteria (high bleeding risk = 1 major or 2 minor)`,columns:[`Domain`,`Major criterion`,`Minor criterion`],rows:[[`Age`,`Not a criterion`,`75 years or more`],[`Anticoagulation`,`Anticipated long-term oral anticoagulation`,`Not a criterion`],[`Kidney function`,`eGFR under 30 mL/min/1.73 m2`,`eGFR 30-59 mL/min/1.73 m2`],[`Haemoglobin`,`Under 11 g/dL`,`11-12.9 g/dL (men); 11-11.9 g/dL (women)`],[`Prior bleeding`,`Spontaneous bleed needing admission or transfusion in past 6 months, or recurrent`,`Same in past 12 months, not meeting the major criterion`],[`Platelets and coagulation`,`Platelets under 100,000/mm3; chronic bleeding diathesis`,`Not a criterion`],[`Liver`,`Cirrhosis with portal hypertension`,`Not a criterion`],[`Cancer`,`Active malignancy (except non-melanoma skin cancer) in past 12 months`,`Not a criterion`],[`Brain`,`Prior spontaneous ICH; traumatic ICH in 12 months; brain AVM; moderate-severe ischaemic stroke in 6 months`,`Any other prior ischaemic stroke`],[`Drugs`,`Not a criterion`,`Long-term oral NSAIDs or steroids`],[`Surgery or trauma`,`Major surgery or trauma within 30 days before PCI; non-deferrable major surgery on DAPT`,`Not a criterion`]]},{heading:`DAPT duration by clinical setting`,columns:[`Setting`,`Default`,`High bleeding risk`,`High ischaemic, low bleeding risk`],rows:[[`ACS treated with PCI`,`12 months aspirin + prasugrel or ticagrelor`,`1-3 months, then P2Y12 inhibitor or aspirin alone`,`Beyond 12 months: ticagrelor 60 mg BD or rivaroxaban 2.5 mg BD with aspirin`],[`ACS managed medically`,`12 months aspirin + ticagrelor or clopidogrel`,`1-3 months with clopidogrel`,`As above`],[`Elective PCI (CCS)`,`6 months aspirin + clopidogrel`,`1-3 months`,`Longer DAPT after complex PCI`],[`AF on anticoagulant with ACS/PCI`,`Triple therapy up to 1 week, then DOAC + clopidogrel to 12 months (6 months after elective PCI)`,`Shorter dual therapy, then DOAC alone`,`Triple therapy up to 30 days`],[`Minor stroke or high-risk TIA`,`21 days aspirin + clopidogrel (30 days with ticagrelor)`,`SAPT`,`90 days in symptomatic intracranial stenosis`],[`Endovascular PAD revascularisation`,`1-6 months aspirin + clopidogrel`,`1 month`,`Aspirin + rivaroxaban 2.5 mg BD long term`]]},{heading:`Bleeding on antiplatelet therapy: graded response`,columns:[`Severity (example)`,`Antiplatelet action`,`Other measures`],rows:[[`Trivial: bruising, self-limiting epistaxis`,`Continue DAPT`,`Reassure; consider shorter DAPT or switch potent drug to clopidogrel`],[`Mild: needs medical attention, no admission`,`Continue DAPT; consider shortening or de-escalation`,`Local measures, check Hb, add PPI`],[`Moderate: Hb fall over 3 g/dL or admission, stable`,`Consider SAPT (keep P2Y12 inhibitor in upper GI bleed); restart DAPT when safe`,`Endoscopy, IV PPI, iron`],[`Severe: Hb fall over 5 g/dL, stable`,`Stop DAPT, continue SAPT if possible`,`Transfuse, endoscopic or surgical control, cardiology input`],[`Life-threatening or intracranial`,`Stop all antiplatelets`,`Resuscitate; platelets only for surgery or ongoing bleeding (not routine in ICH); desmopressin; plan restart`]]}],redFlags:[`Melaena, haematemesis or a fall in Hb of 2 g/dL or more on DAPT - admit for endoscopy`,`New neurological deficit, sudden severe headache or vomiting on antiplatelet therapy - suspect intracranial haemorrhage`,`Rest chest pain or ST elevation within weeks of a stent, especially after DAPT was stopped - stent thrombosis, activate STEMI pathway`,`Advice to stop DAPT for elective surgery within 6 months of elective PCI or 12 months of ACS - refer to cardiology before any interruption`,`Syncope or pulse under 40/min on ticagrelor - ECG and Holter for ventricular pauses`,`Platelet count under 50,000/mm3 (dengue, chemotherapy) in a patient with a recent stent - same-day cardiology advice`,`Fever, anaemia, thrombocytopenia and confusion on clopidogrel - suspect TTP, urgent smear for schistocytes`],pearls:[`Aspirin acetylates, clopidogrel needs activating (CYP2C19), ticagrelor arrives ready-made and reversible.`,`Stop rule 5-7-3: clopidogrel 5 days, prasugrel 7 days, ticagrelor 3-5 days before surgery.`,`Prasugrel: no to stroke or TIA, half dose (5 mg) for the old (75+) and the light (under 60 kg).`,`With ticagrelor, aspirin must stay at 75-100 mg - higher doses lost the benefit in PLATO.`,`PRECISE-DAPT 25 or more, or ARC-HBR (1 major or 2 minor), means short DAPT of 1-3 months.`,`Pantoprazole, not omeprazole, with clopidogrel.`,`Clopidogrel is the only P2Y12 inhibitor to combine with an oral anticoagulant.`,`Stent thrombosis is a disease of stopping DAPT; restenosis is a slow return of angina months later.`,`Restart aspirin as soon as haemostasis is secured after a GI bleed - delay costs lives.`,`Platelet transfusion does not reverse ticagrelor in the first day and harms in antiplatelet-associated ICH (PATCH).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old man weighing 55 kg with NSTEMI is taken for PCI. He had a TIA two years ago. Bleeding risk is not high. Which P2Y12 inhibitor regimen is most appropriate?`,options:[`Prasugrel 60 mg loading, then 10 mg daily`,`Prasugrel 60 mg loading, then 5 mg daily`,`Ticagrelor 180 mg loading, then 90 mg twice daily`,`Cilostazol 100 mg twice daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Ticagrelor is the right potent P2Y12 inhibitor here because prasugrel is contraindicated after any prior stroke or TIA (net harm in TRITON-TIMI 38), whatever the dose - so neither the 10 mg nor the reduced 5 mg prasugrel regimen is acceptable. Cilostazol is a PDE3 inhibitor for claudication, not a P2Y12 inhibitor for ACS.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-q2`,stem:`A 66-year-old on aspirin and clopidogrel after a stent has a past history of peptic ulcer. Which gastroprotective drug is preferred?`,options:[`Pantoprazole 40 mg once daily`,`Omeprazole 20 mg once daily`,`Esomeprazole 40 mg once daily`,`Famotidine 20 mg twice daily`],answer:0,explanation:`Pantoprazole gives full PPI protection without significant CYP2C19 inhibition. Omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 and reduce formation of clopidogrel's active metabolite, so they are avoided with clopidogrel. Famotidine, an H2 blocker, protects less well than a PPI in a patient with a prior ulcer on DAPT.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-q3`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the five variables of the PRECISE-DAPT score?`,options:[`Haemoglobin`,`White cell count`,`Creatinine clearance`,`Diabetes mellitus`],answer:3,explanation:`PRECISE-DAPT uses haemoglobin, white cell count, age, creatinine clearance and prior spontaneous bleeding; a score of 25 or more means high bleeding risk and short DAPT. Diabetes is an ischaemic-risk variable found in the DAPT score used at 12 months, not a bleeding variable in PRECISE-DAPT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-q4`,stem:`A 52-year-old had a drug-eluting stent for STEMI 3 months ago and is on aspirin plus ticagrelor. He now has asymptomatic gallstones and the surgeon suggests elective cholecystectomy. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Stop both drugs 7 days before and operate now`,`Defer the elective surgery until 12 months of DAPT are complete`,`Replace ticagrelor with LMWH bridging and operate now`,`Switch ticagrelor to prasugrel and operate next week`],answer:1,explanation:`Elective non-cardiac surgery should be deferred until DAPT is complete - 12 months after ACS (ESC 2022). Stopping both drugs 3 months after a stent invites stent thrombosis, LMWH does not protect against stent thrombosis because the clot is platelet-rich, and prasugrel is more potent and would itself need stopping 7 days before surgery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-q5`,stem:`A 70-year-old taking aspirin 75 mg presents with a spontaneous 15 mL basal ganglia haemorrhage that does not need surgery. Regarding platelet transfusion, which statement is correct?`,options:[`It is recommended to reverse the aspirin effect`,`It is indicated whenever the platelet count is under 150,000/mm3`,`It is potentially harmful and not recommended routinely`,`It is required before aspirin can be restarted`],answer:2,explanation:`The PATCH trial showed that platelet transfusion in antiplatelet-associated spontaneous ICH not needing surgery increased death or dependence, so AHA/ASA 2022 labels it potentially harmful. Reversal for aspirin is not recommended routinely, a near-normal platelet count is no indication, and transfusion has no role in deciding when to restart aspirin.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss dual antiplatelet therapy after percutaneous coronary intervention: the drugs and their mechanisms, choice of P2Y12 inhibitor, duration based on bleeding risk, and management of a patient on DAPT who needs non-cardiac surgery.`,openingLines:[`Dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) is the combination of **aspirin 75-100 mg** with a **P2Y12 inhibitor** (clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor), given after an acute coronary syndrome or coronary stenting to prevent stent thrombosis and recurrent ischaemic events.`,`Its duration balances ischaemic against bleeding risk: **12 months after ACS** and **6 months after elective PCI** by default, shortened to **1-3 months in high bleeding risk** (ARC-HBR, PRECISE-DAPT 25 or more) - ESC 2023/2024 and ACC/AHA 2025.`],answer:[{heading:`Rationale and mechanism`,points:[`Arterial thrombus is **platelet-rich**; platelet activation is amplified by **thromboxane A2 (COX-1)** and **ADP acting on P2Y12**, ending in GP IIb/IIIa-mediated aggregation.`,`**Aspirin** irreversibly acetylates COX-1; **clopidogrel and prasugrel** are irreversible prodrug P2Y12 blockers; **ticagrelor** is a direct, reversible P2Y12 blocker.`,`A stent is a thrombogenic surface until endothelialised - DAPT prevents **stent thrombosis**, mostly in the first month.`]},{heading:`Drugs and doses`,points:[`Aspirin 150-300 mg chewed loading, then **75-100 mg daily** lifelong.`,`Clopidogrel 300-600 mg loading, then **75 mg daily**.`,`Prasugrel 60 mg loading, then **10 mg daily** (5 mg if 75+ years or under 60 kg); contraindicated after stroke or TIA.`,`Ticagrelor 180 mg loading, then **90 mg twice daily**; 60 mg twice daily for extended therapy.`]},{heading:`Choice of P2Y12 inhibitor`,points:[`**ACS**: prasugrel or ticagrelor over clopidogrel (TRITON-TIMI 38, PLATO); ESC 2023 prefers prasugrel for PCI (ISAR-REACT 5).`,`**Elective PCI, fibrinolysis, oral anticoagulant, high bleeding risk**: clopidogrel.`,`Elderly 70+ with high bleeding risk: clopidogrel (POPular AGE).`]},{heading:`Assessing bleeding and ischaemic risk`,points:[`**ARC-HBR**: 1 major (OAC, eGFR under 30, Hb under 11, recent bleed, platelets under 100,000, cirrhosis, active cancer, prior ICH) or 2 minor (age 75+, eGFR 30-59, mild anaemia, NSAIDs/steroids).`,`**PRECISE-DAPT**: Hb, WBC, age, creatinine clearance, prior bleed; **25 or more** = short DAPT.`,`**DAPT score** at 12 months: 2 or more favours extension.`]},{heading:`Duration and modification`,points:[`ACS 12 months; elective PCI 6 months; high bleeding risk 1-3 months, then single antiplatelet (preferably P2Y12 inhibitor).`,`ACC/AHA 2025: ticagrelor monotherapy after at least 1 month (class 1).`,`De-escalation to clopidogrel (IIb) for bleeding or cost; extended therapy with ticagrelor 60 mg BD or rivaroxaban 2.5 mg BD if high ischaemic risk.`,`**PPI** (pantoprazole) with DAPT if GI risk; with AF: triple therapy up to 1 week, then DOAC + clopidogrel.`]},{heading:`Patient on DAPT needing non-cardiac surgery`,points:[`Defer elective surgery: **6 months after elective PCI, 12 months after ACS**; time-sensitive surgery after at least 1 or 3 months respectively.`,`Continue **aspirin** unless closed-space surgery; stop clopidogrel 5, prasugrel 7, ticagrelor 3-5 days before.`,`Very recent stent with urgent surgery: bridge with cangrelor or GP IIb/IIIa in a PCI-capable centre; LMWH is not a substitute.`,`Restart P2Y12 inhibitor within 24-48 hours once haemostasis is secure.`]}],mustDraw:[`Platelet activation diagram marking the COX-1, P2Y12 and GP IIb/IIIa drug targets`,`Flow chart of DAPT duration by setting and bleeding risk (ESC 2023/2024)`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and drugs with doses`,marks:2},{part:`Choice of P2Y12 inhibitor`,marks:2},{part:`Bleeding risk tools`,marks:2},{part:`Duration and modification`,marks:2},{part:`Peri-operative management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`DAPT`,`P2Y12`,`PRECISE-DAPT`,`ARC-HBR`,`stent thrombosis`,`ESC 2023`,`perioperative`]},{id:`cardiovascular-antiplatelet-therapy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on high bleeding risk in patients on antiplatelet therapy: the ARC-HBR criteria and the PRECISE-DAPT score.`,openingLines:[`**High bleeding risk (HBR)** after PCI is defined by the Academic Research Consortium (ARC-HBR, 2019) as a one-year risk of **BARC 3 or 5 bleeding of 4% or more** or intracranial haemorrhage of 1% or more.`,`A patient is HBR if he meets **one major or two minor** ARC-HBR criteria, or has a **PRECISE-DAPT score of 25 or more** - both point to a short DAPT of 1-3 months.`],answer:[{heading:`ARC-HBR major criteria`,points:[`Anticipated long-term oral anticoagulation; eGFR under 30; Hb under 11 g/dL.`,`Spontaneous bleed needing admission or transfusion in 6 months; platelets under 100,000; chronic bleeding diathesis.`,`Cirrhosis with portal hypertension; active cancer in 12 months; prior spontaneous ICH, brain AVM; recent major surgery or trauma.`]},{heading:`ARC-HBR minor criteria`,points:[`Age 75 or more; eGFR 30-59; Hb 11-12.9 (men) or 11-11.9 g/dL (women).`,`Bleed in the past 12 months; long-term NSAIDs or steroids; other prior ischaemic stroke.`]},{heading:`PRECISE-DAPT score`,points:[`Five items: **haemoglobin, white cell count, age, creatinine clearance, prior spontaneous bleeding**.`,`Score **25 or more**: short DAPT (3-6 months, 1-3 months in current guidelines); under 25: standard 12 months or longer.`]},{heading:`How it changes management`,points:[`Prefer **clopidogrel**, shorten DAPT to 1-3 months, then single antiplatelet (preferably P2Y12 inhibitor).`,`Add a **PPI** (pantoprazole), avoid NSAIDs, minimise triple therapy with anticoagulants.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table of ARC-HBR major and minor criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition of HBR`,marks:1},{part:`ARC-HBR major and minor criteria`,marks:2},{part:`PRECISE-DAPT`,marks:1},{part:`Management implications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ARC-HBR`,`PRECISE-DAPT`,`BARC`,`high bleeding risk`,`short DAPT`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Antiplatelet, anticoagulant and fibrinolytic drugs`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of acute coronary syndromes, European Heart Journal 2023`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of chronic coronary syndromes, European Heart Journal 2024`,`2025 ACC/AHA/ACEP/NAEMSP/SCAI Guideline for the Management of Patients With Acute Coronary Syndromes, Circulation 2025`,`Urban P et al. Defining high bleeding risk in patients undergoing PCI: Academic Research Consortium for High Bleeding Risk (ARC-HBR) consensus, Circulation 2019`,`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardiovascular assessment and management of patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery`,`ICMR Standard Treatment Workflows of India - Cardiology: Acute coronary syndrome, Indian Council of Medical Research`]}),r.push({id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice`,title:`Anticoagulants in practice: warfarin, DOACs, reversal and bridging`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Oral anticoagulants** are the **vitamin K antagonists** (warfarin, acenocoumarol; INR target usually **2.0-3.0**) and the **direct oral anticoagulants** (dabigatran, rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban) dosed by **creatinine clearance**; DOACs are preferred except with **mechanical valves, moderate-severe mitral stenosis, APS and pregnancy**, bleeding is reversed with **vitamin K plus 4-factor PCC, idarucizumab or andexanet**, and most patients need **no heparin bridging** (BRIDGE).`,keywords:[`warfarin`,`acenocoumarol`,`nicoumalone`,`vitamin K antagonist`,`VKA`,`DOAC`,`NOAC`,`dabigatran`,`rivaroxaban`,`apixaban`,`edoxaban`,`INR`,`time in therapeutic range`,`prothrombin complex concentrate`,`idarucizumab`,`andexanet alfa`,`bridging`,`BRIDGE trial`,`PAUSE`,`INVICTUS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and epidemiology`,points:[`**Anticoagulants** inhibit thrombin generation or activity and prevent **fibrin-rich (red) thrombus** - used for stroke prevention in AF, treatment and prevention of VTE, mechanical heart valves and LV thrombus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Two oral families: **vitamin K antagonists (VKA: warfarin, acenocoumarol)** and **direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs: dabigatran, rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban)**; parenteral heparins start therapy and bridge. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AF** raises stroke risk about **5-fold**; warfarin reduces stroke by about **two-thirds**, while aspirin achieves little - and **VTE** is the third commonest cardiovascular disease after MI and stroke. [Harrison 22e]`,`DOACs versus warfarin in AF (RE-LY, ROCKET-AF, ARISTOTLE, ENGAGE AF meta-analysis): **19% fewer strokes or systemic emboli, about half the intracranial haemorrhage and 10% lower all-cause mortality**, but **25% more GI bleeding**. [Ruff 2014]`,`Anticoagulants are among the drugs most often behind **emergency admissions for adverse drug events** - warfarin alone accounted for about **one-third** of such admissions in older US adults. [Budnitz 2011]`,`**Indian context**: rheumatic heart disease remains a leading cause of AF in young Indians, so a large share need **VKA rather than DOAC**; Indian AF patients in global registries are about a decade younger than Western patients. [RE-LY AF registry 2014]`,`**Acenocoumarol** (nicoumalone) is widely prescribed in India alongside warfarin; its shorter half-life (**about 8-11 hours**) makes the INR swing more with missed or late doses. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Poor INR control (**time in therapeutic range under 70%**) is common where testing is hard to reach, as in much of rural India - ESC 2024 recommends switching such patients to a DOAC if eligible. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Warfarin and unfractionated heparin** are on the National List of Essential Medicines; generic rivaroxaban and apixaban have made DOACs cheaper, but cost still decides the drug for many Indian patients. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Indications and the scores that decide anticoagulation`,points:[`**AF**: anticoagulate when **CHA2DS2-VA is 2 or more** (recommended) and consider at 1; ESC 2024 removed female sex from the score (see the Atrial fibrillation topic). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**HAS-BLED** (hypertension, abnormal renal or liver function, stroke, bleeding, labile INR, elderly over 65, drugs or alcohol): **3 or more** flags high bleeding risk - correct modifiable factors and review more often, but it is **not a reason to withhold** anticoagulation. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**VTE**: at least **3 months** for provoked DVT or PE, and extended therapy for unprovoked VTE or persistent risk factors (see the PAD and VTE topic). [ASH 2020]`,`**Mechanical heart valves**: lifelong VKA; **bioprosthetic valves**: VKA or antiplatelet for the first 3 months depending on position, then only if another indication exists. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Rheumatic mitral stenosis** with AF, prior embolism or left atrial thrombus: VKA regardless of CHA2DS2-VA. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**LV thrombus after MI**: VKA or DOAC for 3-6 months with repeat imaging before stopping. [ESC 2023]`,`**Antiphospholipid syndrome** with thrombosis: warfarin to INR 2.0-3.0 long term. [EULAR 2019]`,`**Falls** are rarely a reason to deny anticoagulation - a decision model estimated a patient would need to fall about **295 times a year** before subdural risk outweighed the stroke benefit. [Man-Son-Hing 1999]`]},{heading:`Pharmacology: the coagulation cascade and how each drug works`,points:[`**Cell-based model**: tissue factor-VIIa initiates a small thrombin burst; on activated platelets the **tenase (IXa-VIIIa) and prothrombinase (Xa-Va)** complexes amplify thrombin, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin - **factor Xa and thrombin** are the drug targets. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin K** is the cofactor for **gamma-glutamyl carboxylation** of factors **II, VII, IX and X and proteins C and S**; the carboxylated (Gla) residues bind calcium and phospholipid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Warfarin** inhibits **VKORC1** (vitamin K epoxide reductase), so reduced vitamin K is not regenerated and under-carboxylated, inactive factors circulate. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Factor half-lives explain the slow onset: **VII about 6 h, IX 24 h, X 40 h, prothrombin 60-72 h** - the INR rises early from factor VII loss before true anticoagulation, hence **at least 5 days of heparin overlap**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Protein C** (half-life about 8 h) falls early, creating a transient **procoagulant state** - the cause of **warfarin skin necrosis** in protein C deficiency and of venous limb gangrene when warfarin is started alone in HIT. [Harrison 22e]`,`Warfarin is a racemate; the potent **S-enantiomer is cleared by CYP2C9**, and **CYP2C9 and VKORC1 variants** lower dose needs - Asian patients often need less than Europeans. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Heparins** act through **antithrombin**: UFH inhibits thrombin and Xa equally, LMWH is mainly anti-Xa (anti-Xa to anti-IIa about 3-4:1), and **fondaparinux** is purely anti-Xa. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Dabigatran** directly and reversibly inhibits **thrombin**; it is a prodrug with low bioavailability (about 6.5%) and about **80% renal excretion**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Rivaroxaban, apixaban and edoxaban** directly inhibit free and clot-bound **factor Xa**; renal clearance is about **one-third (rivaroxaban), 27% (apixaban) and 50% (edoxaban)**, and all are **P-glycoprotein** substrates (rivaroxaban and apixaban also CYP3A4). [EHRA 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification of anticoagulants`,points:[`**Indirect, antithrombin-dependent, parenteral**: unfractionated heparin, LMWH (enoxaparin, dalteparin) and fondaparinux. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Direct parenteral thrombin inhibitors**: bivalirudin and argatroban - used in PCI and heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vitamin K antagonists**: coumarins (warfarin, acenocoumarol, phenprocoumon) and indandiones (phenindione). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Direct oral anticoagulants**: oral direct thrombin inhibitor (**dabigatran**) and oral direct factor Xa inhibitors (**rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban**). [EHRA 2021]`,`**Factor XI/XIa inhibitors** (abelacimab, asundexian, milvexian) are investigational - asundexian was inferior to apixaban in OCEANIC-AF (2023). [OCEANIC-AF 2023]`,`**Valvular vs non-valvular AF**: the useful division is simply that DOACs are excluded in **mechanical valves and moderate-severe mitral stenosis**; other valve disease is managed like ordinary AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**By monitoring**: VKA needs the **INR**; UFH needs **aPTT or anti-Xa**; LMWH needs anti-Xa only in pregnancy, obesity, renal failure or children; DOACs need no routine level monitoring. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Assessment before starting: history and examination`,points:[`Confirm the **indication and intended duration** - lifelong for AF or a mechanical valve, 3 months for provoked VTE - and record it in the prescription. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`Take a **bleeding history**: prior GI or intracranial bleed, peptic ulcer, haematuria, menorrhagia, recent surgery and known bleeding disorder. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`Take a **drug history**: antiplatelets, NSAIDs, SSRIs, antiepileptics, **rifampicin**, azole antifungals, amiodarone, HIV protease inhibitors and herbal or ayurvedic products. [EHRA 2021]`,`Establish **valve status**: a metallic valve click (mechanical valve) or the loud S1, opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble of **rheumatic mitral stenosis** both mean VKA, not DOAC. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Assess **practical factors**: access to INR testing, ability to pay, diet, alcohol, cognition, who supervises tablets, and **pregnancy plans** in women of childbearing age. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Examine**: weight (for DOAC dose and obesity rules), BP (uncontrolled BP raises intracranial bleeding), pallor, bruising, signs of liver disease, murmurs and signs of DVT. [Harrison 22e]`,`Calculate and record **CHA2DS2-VA and HAS-BLED** for AF - examiners expect both scores in the case sheet. [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Differential: interpreting abnormal tests and bleeding`,points:[`**High INR on warfarin**: excess dose, interacting drug (antibiotics, amiodarone, azoles), acute illness with poor intake or diarrhoea, hepatic congestion in heart failure, hyperthyroidism or new liver disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low INR on warfarin**: missed doses, high vitamin K intake (greens, supplements, enteral feeds), enzyme inducers (rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin), hypothyroidism or, rarely, hereditary warfarin resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prolonged PT without warfarin**: liver disease, vitamin K deficiency (malabsorption, antibiotics, malnutrition), DIC or factor VII deficiency; a **mixing study that corrects** suggests factor deficiency rather than an inhibitor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bleeding at a therapeutic INR** suggests an underlying lesion - **haematuria** needs urological and **GI bleeding** needs endoscopic evaluation, including for cancer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DOACs and routine tests**: dabigatran prolongs aPTT and especially the **thrombin time** (a normal TT excludes significant dabigatran); rivaroxaban prolongs the PT; **apixaban may leave PT and aPTT normal** at therapeutic levels. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Painful skin necrosis** in the first week of warfarin (breasts, thighs, buttocks) suggests **protein C deficiency**; **purple toe syndrome** from cholesterol emboli appears after 3-8 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Platelet fall of over 50%** 5-10 days after starting heparin suggests **heparin-induced thrombocytopenia** - score with the 4T score, stop all heparin and use a non-heparin anticoagulant. [ASH 2018 HIT]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring`,points:[`**Baseline**: CBC with platelets, **PT/INR and aPTT**, creatinine with **Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance (actual body weight)** and liver function tests. [EHRA 2021]`,`**INR** = (patient PT / mean normal PT) raised to the power of the thromboplastin's **ISI**; it standardises warfarin monitoring across laboratories. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Target INR 2.0-3.0** for AF, VTE and low-risk mechanical aortic valves; **higher targets (up to 3.5-4.0)** for mechanical mitral valves, more thrombogenic valves or patient risk factors. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Time in therapeutic range (Rosendaal method)** should be **70% or more**; a **SAMe-TT2R2 score over 2** predicts poor VKA control. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**INR frequency**: every 2-3 days at the start until stable, then weekly, then every **4 weeks**; very stable patients may go up to 12 weeks. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Point-of-care INR** testing and patient self-testing improve control where available; confirm very high readings in the laboratory. [ACCP 2012]`,`**DOAC follow-up labs**: creatinine clearance, Hb and LFT at least yearly, **6-monthly if 75 or older or frail**, and every **CrCl/10 months** if CrCl is under 60 (for example 3-monthly at CrCl 30). [EHRA 2021]`,`**DOAC levels** (drug-calibrated anti-Xa; dilute thrombin time or ecarin time for dabigatran) are not routine but help in bleeding, urgent surgery, extremes of body weight or suspected overdose. [EHRA 2021]`]},{heading:`Warfarin in the OPD: initiation, targets and interactions`,points:[`Start **warfarin 5 mg daily**, or **2.5-3 mg** in the elderly, low weight, liver disease, heart failure, interacting drugs or Asian patients; 10 mg starts suit only young healthy outpatients with VTE. [ACCP 2012]`,`In acute VTE, overlap with **LMWH or UFH for at least 5 days and until INR is 2.0 or more for 24 hours**. [ACCP 2012]`,`Check INR on **day 3-5**, adjust in steps of **10-20% of the weekly dose**, and remember an INR reflects a dose change only after 3-5 days. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Acenocoumarol** is used the same way at roughly half the warfarin dose (maintenance usually 1-8 mg daily); its short half-life demands strict daily timing. [BNF 2025]`,`**Raise INR**: **amiodarone** (reduce warfarin by about 30-50%), azole antifungals, metronidazole, **co-trimoxazole**, ciprofloxacin, macrolides, regular paracetamol over 2 g/day, alcohol binges and thyroxine. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Lower INR**: **rifampicin** (may need 2-3 times the dose, with effects lasting weeks after stopping), carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, phenytoin and high vitamin K intake or enteral feeds. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Add bleeding without changing INR**: aspirin, clopidogrel, NSAIDs, SSRIs, high-dose fish oil and ginkgo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Antibiotics** for an intercurrent infection are the commonest cause of a sudden INR rise in the OPD - recheck INR within 3-5 days of starting one. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Diet**: keep vitamin K intake **consistent** rather than avoiding green leafy vegetables (palak, methi, cabbage); report every new medicine, including ayurvedic products. [ACCP 2012]`,`Issue an **anticoagulation booklet** recording target, INR and dose; give the dose in the **evening** so a morning INR can guide that day's dose. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`DOACs in the OPD: choice, doses and interactions`,points:[`**Prefer a DOAC over a VKA** for AF and VTE in eligible patients - except mechanical valves, moderate-severe mitral stenosis, antiphospholipid syndrome and pregnancy. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**AF doses**: dabigatran **150 mg BD** (110 mg BD if 80 or older, on verapamil or high bleeding risk); rivaroxaban **20 mg OD with the evening meal** (15 mg if CrCl 15-49); apixaban **5 mg BD**; edoxaban **60 mg OD**. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Apixaban 2.5 mg BD** only with **2 of 3** - age 80 or more, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5 mg/dL (133 micromol/L) or more - or CrCl 15-29; **edoxaban 30 mg** if CrCl 15-50, weight 60 kg or less or a strong P-gp inhibitor. [EHRA 2021]`,`**VTE doses**: rivaroxaban **15 mg BD for 21 days then 20 mg OD**; apixaban **10 mg BD for 7 days then 5 mg BD**; dabigatran 150 mg BD or edoxaban 60 mg OD after at least 5 days of heparin; extended phase apixaban 2.5 mg BD or rivaroxaban 10 mg OD. [ESC 2019 PE]`,`**Off-label under-dosing** is common in Asia and raises stroke risk without reducing bleeding - reduce the dose only when label criteria are met. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Interactions**: avoid strong **P-gp/CYP3A4 inducers** (rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbitone) with all DOACs, strong inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir) with rivaroxaban and apixaban, and **dronedarone** with dabigatran. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Missed dose**: a twice-daily DOAC may be taken up to **6 hours** late and a once-daily DOAC up to **12 hours** late; otherwise skip it and never double the next dose. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Switching VKA to DOAC**: start apixaban or dabigatran when INR is under 2.0, edoxaban at 2.5 or less and rivaroxaban at 3.0 or less; **DOAC to VKA**: overlap until INR is 2.0 or more, measuring INR just before the next DOAC dose. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Dabigatran capsules** must not be opened (bioavailability rises sharply) and cause dyspepsia in about 1 in 10; rivaroxaban 15 and 20 mg must be taken with food for full absorption. [EHRA 2021]`]},{heading:`Over-anticoagulation and bleeding: IPD and emergency reversal`,points:[`**INR above range but under 4.5, no bleeding**: reduce or omit one dose, recheck, and resume at a lower weekly dose. [ACCP 2012]`,`**INR 4.5-10, no bleeding**: withhold 1-2 doses and recheck in 1-2 days; routine vitamin K is not recommended by ACCP, though British guidance gives **oral vitamin K 1-5 mg when INR is over 8**. [ACCP 2012]`,`**INR over 10, no bleeding**: withhold warfarin and give **oral vitamin K 2.5-5 mg**; recheck INR the next day (the IV ampoule can be given orally where tablets are unavailable). [ACCP 2012]`,`**Major or life-threatening bleeding on a VKA**: stop warfarin, give **vitamin K 5-10 mg by slow IV infusion** plus **4-factor PCC 25-50 IU/kg** by INR (or a fixed 1000-1500 units), and recheck INR 15-30 minutes after PCC. [ACC 2020 ECDP]`,`**FFP 10-15 mL/kg** is the fallback where PCC is unavailable, as in many Indian district hospitals, but it is slow, needs large volumes and corrects the INR incompletely. [ACC 2020 ECDP]`,`**Dabigatran bleeding**: **idarucizumab 5 g IV** (two 2.5 g vials) reverses it within minutes; dabigatran is also removed by **haemodialysis**. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Factor Xa inhibitor bleeding**: **andexanet alfa** where available (not in India) - ANNEXA-I (2024) showed better haemostasis in ICH but more thrombotic events; otherwise **4-factor PCC 50 IU/kg** (or 2000 units). [EHRA 2021]`,`**General measures**: note the time of last dose and renal function, apply pressure, transfuse to Hb 7-8 g/dL (higher with coronary disease), give **activated charcoal** if a DOAC was taken within 2-4 hours, and consider tranexamic acid 1 g IV for non-GI bleeding. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Heparin reversal**: protamine **1 mg per 100 units of UFH** given in the previous 2-3 hours (maximum 50 mg); it only partly reverses LMWH and does not reverse fondaparinux. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Restarting**: after a controlled GI bleed anticoagulation is usually resumed within about **7 days**; after ICH the timing is set with neurology (commonly 4-8 weeks), or left atrial appendage occlusion is considered. [ACC 2020 ECDP]`]},{heading:`Peri-procedural interruption and bridging`,points:[`Classify the **procedure's bleeding risk** (minimal, low-moderate, high) and the **patient's thrombotic risk** - together they decide interruption and bridging. [CHEST 2022]`,`**Minimal-risk procedures** (most dental extractions, cataract, minor skin surgery, diagnostic endoscopy): **continue warfarin** with INR in range, or operate at DOAC trough 12-24 hours after the last dose; tranexamic acid mouthwash for dental work. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Warfarin**: stop **5 days before**; check INR the day before - give **oral vitamin K 1-2 mg if over 1.5**; restart the evening of surgery or next day at the usual dose once haemostasis is secure. [CHEST 2022]`,`**DOACs (PAUSE protocol)**: apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban - omit **1 day before low-moderate** and **2 days before high bleeding-risk** procedures; dabigatran 1-2 days, extended to 2-4 days if CrCl is under 50; no bridging and no pre-operative levels. [PAUSE 2019]`,`**Resume DOACs** 24 hours after low-risk and **48-72 hours** after high-risk surgery, with prophylactic LMWH in between if VTE risk is high. [CHEST 2022]`,`**Neuraxial anaesthesia**: stop Xa inhibitors **72 hours** and dabigatran **72-120 hours** (by creatinine clearance) before; warfarin until the INR is normal. [ASRA 2018]`,`**BRIDGE (2015)**: in AF patients on warfarin, bridging dalteparin did not reduce arterial thromboembolism (**0.4% vs 0.3%**) but more than doubled major bleeding (**3.2% vs 1.3%**) - so most AF patients are **not bridged**. [BRIDGE 2015]`,`**Bridge only very high thrombotic risk**: mechanical mitral or older caged-ball and tilting-disc valves, any mechanical valve with stroke in 6 months, stroke, TIA or VTE within 3 months, and severe thrombophilia. [CHEST 2022]`,`**Bridging protocol**: stop warfarin on day -5; start **enoxaparin 1 mg/kg twice daily** when the INR falls below range (about day -3); give the last dose **24 hours before** surgery at half the daily dose; restart 24 hours (low risk) or 48-72 hours (high risk) after, until the INR is therapeutic. [CHEST 2022]`,`**DOACs never need heparin bridging** because their onset and offset are rapid. [EHRA 2021]`]},{heading:`Choice in special situations: valves, cancer, obesity, kidney, pregnancy`,points:[`**Mechanical valves**: VKA only - dabigatran caused more thrombosis and bleeding (RE-ALIGN), and apixaban was stopped early for excess events in On-X aortic valves (PROACT Xa). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Rheumatic AF with moderate-severe mitral stenosis**: **VKA** - INVICTUS (2022) showed more deaths and vascular events with rivaroxaban than with VKA in rheumatic AF. [INVICTUS 2022]`,`**Bioprosthetic valves and valve repair**: DOACs are acceptable for AF after the first 3 months (RIVER trial); VKA is used early after surgical mitral bioprosthesis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cancer-associated VTE**: apixaban, edoxaban, rivaroxaban or LMWH for at least 6 months and while cancer is active; prefer **apixaban or LMWH in luminal GI or genitourinary cancers** and check chemotherapy interactions. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Obesity (BMI over 40 or weight over 120 kg)**: standard-dose **rivaroxaban or apixaban** may be used for VTE; avoid dabigatran and edoxaban, and avoid DOACs early after bariatric surgery (use parenteral then VKA, or check levels). [ISTH 2021]`,`**CKD**: CrCl under 30 - no dabigatran; CrCl 15-29 - reduced-dose factor Xa inhibitors with caution; **dialysis** - warfarin or apixaban (US label), on weak evidence. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Elderly and frail**: DOACs reduce intracranial bleeding, but FRAIL-AF showed that **switching stable VKA patients to a DOAC increased bleeding** - keep a well-controlled VKA in frail elderly. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Pregnancy**: **LMWH** is the anticoagulant of choice; warfarin causes **embryopathy at 6-12 weeks** (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses) and fetal bleeding; DOACs are contraindicated - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic for mechanical valves. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Breastfeeding**: warfarin, acenocoumarol, UFH and LMWH are compatible; DOACs are avoided. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Antiphospholipid syndrome**: warfarin INR 2.0-3.0; avoid DOACs, particularly in **triple-positive** or arterial APS (TRAPS: more arterial events with rivaroxaban). [EULAR 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`**Major bleeding** on oral anticoagulants runs at about **2-3% a year**; GI bleeding is the commonest site and intracranial bleeding is roughly halved with DOACs compared with warfarin. [Ruff 2014]`,`**Warfarin-specific harms**: skin necrosis, purple toe syndrome, teratogenicity, alopecia and **anticoagulant-related nephropathy** (glomerular haemorrhage causing AKI at high INR). [Harrison 22e]`,`**At each DOAC visit** use the EHRA checklist: adherence, thromboembolic events, bleeding, side effects, co-medications, and blood tests at the interval set by age and renal function. [EHRA 2021]`,`**Refer to a specialist anticoagulation clinic** for labile INR despite adherence, thrombosis on therapy, a mechanical valve in pregnancy, recurrent bleeding where anticoagulation must continue, and APS. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Counselling**: take the dose at the same time daily, carry an anticoagulant card, tell every doctor and dentist, avoid NSAIDs, and report black stools, red urine, severe headache or a fall with head injury at once. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Head injury on an anticoagulant**: CT head within 8 hours even for a minor injury, and reverse if there is bleeding. [NICE 2023]`,`**Programme link**: AF and RHD patients fit the **NP-NCD** continuum of care at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs and district NCD clinics; nurse- or pharmacist-led INR clinics with point-of-care testing improve control. [MoHFW NP-NCD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2024 AF**: **CHA2DS2-VA** replaces CHA2DS2-VASc; DOAC preferred over VKA except mechanical valves and moderate-severe MS; switch to a DOAC if TTR is under 70%; consider keeping stable VKA in patients over 75 with polypharmacy (FRAIL-AF). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS 2023 AF**: DOAC over warfarin; anticoagulate at an annual stroke risk of 2% or more (CHA2DS2-VASc 2 in men, 3 in women); left atrial appendage occlusion if long-term anticoagulation is contraindicated. [ACC/AHA 2023 AF]`,`**CHEST 2022 perioperative**: no heparin bridging for AF or VTE; standardised DOAC interruption without bridging or levels; bridging suggested only for high-risk mechanical valves. [CHEST 2022]`,`**ACC/AHA 2024 perioperative**: DOAC interruption set by drug, renal function and procedural bleeding risk, with no bridging for DOACs. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular**: DOACs remain contraindicated with mechanical valves; VKA for AF with moderate-severe rheumatic MS (INVICTUS). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ANNEXA-I (2024)**: andexanet improved haemostatic efficacy in factor Xa inhibitor-associated ICH (about 67% vs 53%) but increased thrombotic events, including ischaemic stroke. [ANNEXA-I 2024]`,`**Factor XI inhibitors**: asundexian was inferior to apixaban (OCEANIC-AF 2023) while abelacimab caused far less bleeding than rivaroxaban (AZALEA 2023); phase 3 trials continue and none is for routine use yet. [OCEANIC-AF 2023]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline**: LMWH remains the anticoagulant of choice in pregnancy and DOACs are contraindicated. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: why overlap heparin with warfarin for 5 days? Prothrombin's 60-72-hour half-life and the early fall in protein C mean an INR rise from factor VII loss is not yet true anticoagulation. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Classic: rheumatic MS with AF - warfarin, not a DOAC (INVICTUS); mechanical valve - never a DOAC. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Be ready to write the **over-anticoagulation table** (under 4.5, 4.5-10, over 10, bleeding) with vitamin K doses and PCC. [ACCP 2012]`,`Common mistake: bridging an AF patient with LMWH for a hernia repair - BRIDGE showed only more bleeding. [BRIDGE 2015]`,`Common mistake: halving apixaban for age alone - it needs **2 of the 3** criteria. [EHRA 2021]`,`Common mistake: FFP or vitamin K for DOAC bleeding - use idarucizumab for dabigatran and andexanet or PCC for Xa inhibitors. [EHRA 2021]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **rifampicin** - it defeats warfarin and is contraindicated with DOACs, a frequent Indian TB scenario. [EHRA 2021]`,`Know the factor half-lives (VII 6 h, prothrombin 60-72 h) and the reversal agent for each drug. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Anticoagulant classes: target, monitoring and reversal`,columns:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Target`,`Monitoring`,`Reversal`],rows:[[`Vitamin K antagonists`,`Warfarin, acenocoumarol`,`VKORC1 - less carboxylated II, VII, IX, X, protein C and S`,`INR`,`Vitamin K, 4-factor PCC (FFP if no PCC)`],[`Unfractionated heparin`,`Heparin IV or SC`,`Antithrombin - thrombin and Xa equally`,`aPTT or anti-Xa`,`Protamine (full)`],[`Low molecular weight heparin`,`Enoxaparin, dalteparin`,`Antithrombin - mainly Xa`,`Anti-Xa in pregnancy, obesity, CKD`,`Protamine (partial)`],[`Pentasaccharide`,`Fondaparinux`,`Antithrombin - Xa only`,`Anti-Xa (fondaparinux-calibrated)`,`None specific`],[`Direct thrombin inhibitors`,`Dabigatran (oral), bivalirudin, argatroban (IV)`,`Thrombin`,`Thrombin time, dilute TT, aPTT`,`Idarucizumab (dabigatran); dialysis`],[`Direct factor Xa inhibitors`,`Rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban`,`Free and clot-bound Xa`,`Drug-calibrated anti-Xa`,`Andexanet alfa; 4-factor PCC`]]},{heading:`Anticoagulant and reversal drugs: doses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Warfarin`,`5 mg oral daily start (2.5-3 mg if elderly, frail, liver disease); adjust to INR`,`Weekly dose changed by 10-20%; INR 2.0-3.0 for AF/VTE`,`Bleeding, skin necrosis, teratogenic (6-12 weeks); many interactions`],[`Acenocoumarol`,`About half the warfarin dose; maintenance usually 1-8 mg oral daily`,`Same INR targets; strict timing`,`As warfarin; INR less stable (short half-life)`],[`Dabigatran`,`150 mg oral BD; 110 mg BD if 80+, verapamil or high bleeding risk`,`Long term; avoid if CrCl under 30`,`Dyspepsia, GI bleed; do not open capsule; dronedarone contraindicated`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`AF 20 mg OD with food (15 mg if CrCl 15-49); VTE 15 mg BD x 21 days then 20 mg OD`,`Long term or 3-6 months for provoked VTE`,`Bleeding; avoid with strong CYP3A4/P-gp drugs, rifampicin`],[`Apixaban`,`AF 5 mg BD (2.5 mg BD if 2 of: 80+, 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+); VTE 10 mg BD x 7 days then 5 mg BD`,`Long term; extended VTE 2.5 mg BD`,`Bleeding; avoid with strong inducers or inhibitors`],[`Edoxaban`,`60 mg oral OD (30 mg if CrCl 15-50, 60 kg or less, P-gp inhibitor)`,`After 5 days heparin for VTE`,`Bleeding; less effective if CrCl over 95`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC BD (treatment); 40 mg SC OD (prophylaxis)`,`Bridge or 5+ days overlap with warfarin; reduce to 1 mg/kg OD if CrCl under 30`,`Bleeding, HIT (rare), hyperkalaemia; anti-Xa in obesity, pregnancy`],[`Unfractionated heparin`,`80 units/kg IV bolus then 18 units/kg/h; adjust to aPTT 1.5-2.5x`,`Short term; preferred if CrCl under 30 or surgery imminent`,`HIT, bleeding, osteoporosis (long term)`],[`Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione)`,`Oral 1-5 mg (INR high, no bleed); IV 5-10 mg slow infusion (major bleed)`,`Effect starts in 4-6 h, complete by 12-24 h`,`Rare anaphylaxis with IV; high doses cause warfarin resistance for days`],[`4-factor PCC`,`25-50 IU/kg IV by INR (VKA); 50 IU/kg or 2000 units (Xa inhibitors)`,`Single dose; recheck INR at 15-30 min`,`Thrombosis; always give with vitamin K for VKA`],[`Idarucizumab`,`5 g IV (two 2.5 g vials)`,`Single dose; repeat rarely`,`Reverses dabigatran only`],[`Andexanet alfa`,`400 or 800 mg IV bolus then 4 or 8 mg/min for 2 h (by drug, dose, timing)`,`Single course`,`Thrombotic events; not available in India`],[`Protamine sulfate`,`1 mg per 100 units UFH in last 2-3 h (max 50 mg); 1 mg per 1 mg enoxaparin`,`Slow IV over 10 min`,`Hypotension, anaphylaxis (fish allergy, prior protamine insulin)`]]},{heading:`DOAC dose for AF by creatinine clearance (Cockcroft-Gault)`,columns:[`Drug`,`CrCl 50 or more`,`CrCl 30-49`,`CrCl 15-29`,`CrCl under 15 or dialysis`],rows:[[`Dabigatran`,`150 mg BD (110 mg BD if 80+, verapamil, high bleeding risk)`,`150 mg BD; consider 110 mg BD`,`Contraindicated`,`Contraindicated`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`20 mg OD with evening meal`,`15 mg OD`,`15 mg OD with caution`,`Not recommended`],[`Apixaban`,`5 mg BD (2.5 mg BD if 2 of: 80+, 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5 mg/dL+)`,`5 mg BD (same 2-of-3 rule)`,`2.5 mg BD`,`Not recommended in EU; US label permits in dialysis`],[`Edoxaban`,`60 mg OD (30 mg if 60 kg or less or strong P-gp inhibitor); avoid if CrCl over 95 (US)`,`30 mg OD`,`30 mg OD`,`Not recommended`]]},{heading:`Warfarin over-anticoagulation: INR and action`,columns:[`Situation`,`Warfarin`,`Vitamin K`,`Other`],rows:[[`INR above range but under 4.5, no bleeding`,`Reduce or omit one dose; resume lower`,`Not needed`,`Find the cause (drug, illness, diet)`],[`INR 4.5-10, no bleeding`,`Withhold 1-2 doses`,`Not routine (ACCP); oral 1-5 mg if over 8 (BSH)`,`Recheck INR in 1-2 days`],[`INR over 10, no bleeding`,`Withhold`,`Oral 2.5-5 mg`,`Recheck INR next day`],[`Minor bleeding, INR high`,`Withhold`,`IV 1-3 mg`,`Local measures; look for a lesion`],[`Major or life-threatening bleeding`,`Stop`,`IV 5-10 mg slow infusion`,`4-factor PCC 25-50 IU/kg (FFP 10-15 mL/kg if none); admit`],[`Urgent surgery on warfarin`,`Stop`,`IV 1-5 mg if surgery can wait 6-12 h`,`PCC if surgery cannot wait`]]},{heading:`Peri-procedural interruption of oral anticoagulants`,columns:[`Drug`,`Low-moderate bleeding-risk procedure`,`High bleeding-risk procedure`,`Restart`],rows:[[`Warfarin`,`Minimal-risk: continue with INR in range; otherwise stop 5 days before`,`Stop 5 days before; INR 1.5 or less the day before (oral vitamin K 1-2 mg if higher)`,`Evening of surgery or next day at usual dose`],[`Apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban (CrCl 30+)`,`Last dose 24 h or more before (omit 1 day)`,`Last dose 48 h or more before (omit 2 days)`,`24 h after (low risk); 48-72 h after (high risk)`],[`Factor Xa inhibitors (CrCl 15-29)`,`36 h or more`,`48 h or more`,`As above`],[`Dabigatran (CrCl 80+)`,`24 h or more`,`48 h or more`,`As above`],[`Dabigatran (CrCl 50-79)`,`36 h or more`,`72 h or more`,`As above`],[`Dabigatran (CrCl 30-49)`,`48 h or more`,`96 h or more`,`As above`],[`Therapeutic enoxaparin (bridge)`,`Last dose 24 h before at half the daily dose`,`Same`,`24 h after (low risk); 48-72 h after (high risk)`]]},{heading:`Choosing the anticoagulant: special situations`,columns:[`Situation`,`Preferred`,`Avoid`,`Key evidence`],rows:[[`AF without mechanical valve or moderate-severe MS`,`DOAC`,`Aspirin as a substitute`,`RE-LY, ROCKET-AF, ARISTOTLE, ENGAGE AF`],[`Rheumatic AF with moderate-severe MS`,`VKA, INR 2.0-3.0`,`DOACs`,`INVICTUS 2022`],[`Mechanical heart valve`,`VKA, INR by valve and risk factors`,`All DOACs`,`RE-ALIGN, PROACT Xa`],[`Bioprosthetic valve with AF after 3 months`,`DOAC or VKA`,`Nothing specific`,`RIVER`],[`Cancer-associated VTE`,`Apixaban, edoxaban, rivaroxaban or LMWH`,`Edoxaban/rivaroxaban in luminal GI cancer; interacting chemotherapy`,`Hokusai-VTE Cancer, SELECT-D, Caravaggio`],[`Antiphospholipid syndrome (triple positive or arterial)`,`Warfarin, INR 2.0-3.0`,`DOACs`,`TRAPS`],[`Pregnancy`,`LMWH (UFH near delivery)`,`DOACs; warfarin at 6-12 weeks`,`ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline`],[`Severe obesity (BMI over 40 or over 120 kg)`,`Rivaroxaban or apixaban (VTE), VKA, LMWH with anti-Xa`,`Dabigatran, edoxaban; DOAC soon after bariatric surgery`,`ISTH 2021 guidance`],[`Dialysis`,`Warfarin or apixaban`,`Dabigatran, rivaroxaban, edoxaban`,`Observational data only`]]}],redFlags:[`Bleeding with hypotension or tachycardia, melaena or haematemesis on an anticoagulant - resuscitate, reverse and admit`,`Head injury, sudden severe headache or new neurological deficit on an anticoagulant - urgent CT and reversal`,`INR over 10, or INR over 4.5 with any bleeding`,`Breathlessness, embolism or a muffled valve click in a mechanical valve patient with a subtherapeutic INR - suspect valve thrombosis`,`Painful dusky skin lesions in the first week of warfarin - warfarin skin necrosis`,`Pregnancy in a woman taking warfarin or a DOAC - specialist review within days`,`Platelet fall of over 50% 5-10 days after starting heparin - suspect HIT, stop all heparin`,`AKI in a patient on a DOAC (especially dabigatran) - drug accumulation and bleeding risk`],pearls:[`VKA-only list: mechanical valve, moderate-severe mitral stenosis (INVICTUS), triple-positive APS; pregnancy needs LMWH.`,`Vitamin K-dependent factors: 2, 7, 9, 10 (remember '1972') plus proteins C and S.`,`Factor VII (6 h) falls first so INR rises early; prothrombin (60-72 h) falls last - hence 5 days of overlap.`,`Apixaban 2.5 mg needs 2 of ABC: Age 80+, Body weight 60 kg or less, Creatinine 1.5 mg/dL or more.`,`Dabigatran: 80% renal, dialysable, reversed by idarucizumab, capsule never opened.`,`Missed DOAC dose: up to 6 hours late for twice daily, 12 hours for once daily; never double.`,`BRIDGE: bridging AF patients doubles bleeding and prevents nothing; DOACs never need bridging.`,`Normal thrombin time excludes dabigatran; a normal PT does not exclude apixaban.`,`Fall risk is rarely a reason to withhold anticoagulation.`,`Antibiotics are the commonest cause of a sudden INR rise in the OPD.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis (valve area 1.2 cm2) develops atrial fibrillation. She is not pregnant. Which anticoagulant is most appropriate?`,options:[`Apixaban 5 mg twice daily`,`Warfarin to an INR of 2.0-3.0`,`Rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily`,`Aspirin 75 mg plus clopidogrel 75 mg daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Moderate-severe rheumatic mitral stenosis with AF needs a vitamin K antagonist: INVICTUS (2022) showed more deaths and vascular events with rivaroxaban than with VKA in rheumatic AF, and DOACs such as apixaban were never tested in moderate-severe MS. Dual antiplatelet therapy is far less effective than anticoagulation for AF stroke prevention.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-q2`,stem:`A 65-year-old on warfarin for AF has an INR of 7.2 after a course of co-trimoxazole. He has no bleeding. What is the best management?`,options:[`IV vitamin K 10 mg and FFP 15 mL/kg`,`4-factor PCC 25 IU/kg`,`Continue the same dose and repeat INR in a week`,`Withhold 1-2 doses of warfarin and recheck INR; no routine vitamin K`],answer:3,explanation:`For INR 4.5-10 without bleeding, ACCP advises withholding 1-2 doses and rechecking, without routine vitamin K. IV vitamin K with FFP and PCC are for major bleeding and would overshoot and risk thrombosis, while continuing the same dose ignores a co-trimoxazole interaction that will keep pushing the INR up.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-q3`,stem:`A 76-year-old on dabigatran 110 mg twice daily presents with a large GI bleed and hypotension. Which is the specific reversal agent?`,options:[`Idarucizumab 5 g IV`,`Andexanet alfa`,`Protamine sulfate`,`Vitamin K 10 mg IV`],answer:0,explanation:`Idarucizumab is a humanised antibody fragment that binds dabigatran and reverses it within minutes (RE-VERSE AD). Andexanet is a decoy for factor Xa inhibitors such as apixaban and rivaroxaban, protamine neutralises heparin, and vitamin K restores only VKA-dependent factor synthesis, so none of these reverses a direct thrombin inhibitor.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-q4`,stem:`A 70-year-old on warfarin for AF (CHA2DS2-VA 3, no prior stroke, no mechanical valve) is listed for elective inguinal hernia repair. What is the recommended peri-operative plan?`,options:[`Stop warfarin 5 days before and bridge with therapeutic enoxaparin`,`Continue warfarin and operate with INR around 2.5`,`Stop warfarin 5 days before without bridging and restart 12-24 hours after surgery`,`Replace warfarin with aspirin 150 mg for two weeks around surgery`],answer:2,explanation:`BRIDGE showed that in AF patients without a mechanical valve or recent stroke, heparin bridging did not reduce thromboembolism but more than doubled major bleeding, so warfarin is simply stopped 5 days before and restarted 12-24 hours after. Hernia repair is not a minimal-risk procedure to do at full INR, and aspirin offers no meaningful AF stroke protection.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-q5`,stem:`An 82-year-old woman weighing 56 kg with serum creatinine 1.1 mg/dL is started on apixaban for non-valvular AF. What is the correct dose?`,options:[`5 mg twice daily`,`2.5 mg twice daily`,`10 mg twice daily for 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily`,`2.5 mg once daily`],answer:1,explanation:`Apixaban is reduced to 2.5 mg twice daily when two of three criteria are met - age 80 or more and weight 60 kg or less are both present here, even though creatinine is under 1.5 mg/dL. The full 5 mg dose would be correct with only one criterion, the 10 mg loading schedule is for acute VTE, and once-daily dosing is not a licensed AF regimen.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the practical use of oral anticoagulants in family practice: initiation and monitoring of warfarin, choice and dosing of DOACs, management of over-anticoagulation, and reversal of anticoagulant-associated bleeding.`,openingLines:[`Oral anticoagulants are the **vitamin K antagonists** (warfarin, acenocoumarol), which block VKORC1 and need INR monitoring, and the **direct oral anticoagulants** (dabigatran, rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban), which directly inhibit thrombin or factor Xa at fixed doses.`,`DOACs are preferred for AF and VTE (ESC 2024) except in **mechanical valves, moderate-severe mitral stenosis, APS and pregnancy**, where VKA or LMWH is used; every prescriber must know the over-anticoagulation table and the **reversal agent for each drug**.`],answer:[{heading:`Indications and pre-treatment assessment`,points:[`AF (CHA2DS2-VA 2 or more), VTE, mechanical valves, rheumatic MS with AF, LV thrombus, APS.`,`Assess bleeding risk (HAS-BLED 3 or more - fix modifiable factors), renal function (Cockcroft-Gault), liver function, drugs, pregnancy plans, access to INR testing and cost.`]},{heading:`Warfarin: mechanism, initiation and monitoring`,points:[`Blocks VKORC1; reduces II, VII, IX, X and proteins C and S; overlap heparin for at least 5 days and until INR 2.0 or more for 24 h.`,`Start 5 mg (2.5-3 mg in elderly or Asian patients); INR on day 3-5; adjust weekly dose by 10-20%.`,`Target INR 2.0-3.0 (higher for mechanical mitral valves); TTR 70% or more; INR every 4 weeks when stable.`,`Interactions: amiodarone, azoles, metronidazole, co-trimoxazole raise INR; rifampicin, carbamazepine lower it; diet consistent in vitamin K.`]},{heading:`DOACs: choice and dosing`,points:[`Dabigatran 150 mg BD (110 mg if 80+); rivaroxaban 20 mg OD with food (15 mg if CrCl 15-49); apixaban 5 mg BD (2.5 mg if 2 of 3: 80+, 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+); edoxaban 60 mg OD (30 mg if CrCl 15-50).`,`Avoid with rifampicin and strong P-gp/CYP3A4 drugs; no under-dosing; missed dose 6 h (BD) or 12 h (OD).`,`Monitor creatinine clearance yearly, 6-monthly if 75+, every CrCl/10 months if CrCl under 60.`]},{heading:`Over-anticoagulation on warfarin`,points:[`INR under 4.5: reduce or omit a dose; 4.5-10: withhold 1-2 doses; over 10: withhold plus oral vitamin K 2.5-5 mg.`,`Major bleeding: stop, IV vitamin K 5-10 mg plus 4-factor PCC 25-50 IU/kg (FFP 10-15 mL/kg if no PCC).`]},{heading:`Reversal of DOAC bleeding`,points:[`Dabigatran: idarucizumab 5 g IV; haemodialysis.`,`Factor Xa inhibitors: andexanet alfa (not in India) or 4-factor PCC 50 IU/kg; activated charcoal within 2-4 h of ingestion.`,`Supportive: compression, transfusion, tranexamic acid for non-GI bleeds; plan resumption (GI bleed about 7 days; ICH with neurology).`]},{heading:`Peri-procedural points and counselling`,points:[`Warfarin stopped 5 days before; bridging only for very high thrombotic risk (BRIDGE); DOACs stopped 1-2 days before (PAUSE), never bridged.`,`Anticoagulant card, avoid NSAIDs, report bleeding, CT for head injury, INR clinic follow-up.`]}],mustDraw:[`Vitamin K cycle showing the VKORC1 step blocked by warfarin and the factors affected`,`Over-anticoagulation table: INR band, warfarin action, vitamin K dose, PCC`],markSplit:[{part:`Indications and assessment`,marks:1},{part:`Warfarin initiation, targets, interactions`,marks:3},{part:`DOAC choice and dosing`,marks:2},{part:`Over-anticoagulation table`,marks:2},{part:`DOAC reversal and counselling`,marks:2}],keywords:[`warfarin`,`INR`,`DOAC`,`PCC`,`idarucizumab`,`andexanet`,`vitamin K`,`TTR`]},{id:`cardiovascular-anticoagulation-practice-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate between warfarin and the direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs).`,openingLines:[`**Warfarin** is an indirect vitamin K antagonist with a narrow therapeutic window needing INR monitoring; **DOACs** directly inhibit thrombin (dabigatran) or factor Xa (rivaroxaban, apixaban, edoxaban) at fixed doses.`,`DOACs cause about half the intracranial bleeding of warfarin and are preferred in AF and VTE, but warfarin remains essential for mechanical valves, moderate-severe mitral stenosis, APS and dialysis.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and pharmacology`,points:[`Warfarin: VKORC1 inhibition, onset 5-7 days, half-life 36-42 h, CYP2C9 metabolism.`,`DOACs: direct thrombin or Xa inhibition, onset 2-4 h, half-life 5-17 h, renal clearance 27-80%.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and interactions`,points:[`Warfarin: INR target 2.0-3.0, TTR 70% or more; many drug and food interactions.`,`DOACs: no routine levels; renal function monitoring; P-gp/CYP3A4 interactions (rifampicin, azoles).`]},{heading:`Indications and contraindications`,points:[`Warfarin only: mechanical valves, moderate-severe MS, triple-positive APS, dialysis (or apixaban).`,`DOACs preferred: AF and VTE including cancer VTE; contraindicated in pregnancy and breastfeeding.`]},{heading:`Reversal and peri-procedural handling`,points:[`Warfarin: vitamin K plus 4-factor PCC; stop 5 days before surgery; bridging for very high risk only.`,`DOACs: idarucizumab (dabigatran), andexanet or PCC (Xa inhibitors); stop 1-2 days before; no bridging.`]}],mustDraw:[`Side-by-side comparison table of warfarin and DOACs`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and pharmacokinetics`,marks:1},{part:`Monitoring and interactions`,marks:1},{part:`Indications and contraindications`,marks:2},{part:`Reversal and peri-procedural`,marks:1}],keywords:[`warfarin`,`DOAC`,`INR`,`INVICTUS`,`mechanical valve`,`reversal`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Antiplatelet, anticoagulant and fibrinolytic drugs`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation, European Heart Journal 2024`,`Steffel J et al. 2021 European Heart Rhythm Association Practical Guide on the use of non-vitamin K antagonist oral anticoagulants in AF, Europace 2021`,`Douketis JD et al. Perioperative management of antithrombotic therapy: an American College of Chest Physicians clinical practice guideline, CHEST 2022`,`2020 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway on management of bleeding in patients on oral anticoagulants, JACC 2020`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease`,`Connolly SJ et al. Rivaroxaban in rheumatic heart disease-associated atrial fibrillation (INVICTUS), N Engl J Med 2022`]}),r.push({id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac`,title:`Pre-operative cardiac evaluation for non-cardiac surgery`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Pre-operative cardiac evaluation** combines the **surgical risk** (low under 1%, intermediate 1-5%, high over 5% 30-day cardiac death or MI), the **patient's risk** (RCRI, age 65+, known CVD) and **functional capacity** (4 METs or more, DASI over 34) to decide whether to proceed, measure **ECG, hs-troponin and NT-proBNP**, or test further - testing only when it would change management, while **continuing beta-blockers and statins**, timing surgery around **stents**, and stopping **SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days** before (ESC 2022, ACC/AHA 2024).`,keywords:[`pre-operative assessment`,`perioperative cardiovascular evaluation`,`non-cardiac surgery`,`RCRI`,`Lee index`,`functional capacity`,`METs`,`DASI`,`ASA grade`,`NSQIP`,`AUB-HAS2`,`MINS`,`perioperative myocardial injury`,`NT-proBNP`,`high-sensitivity troponin`,`POISE`,`BRIDGE`,`SGLT2 inhibitor withholding`,`pre-anaesthetic check-up`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pre-operative cardiac evaluation** estimates the risk of **major adverse cardiac events** (cardiac death, MI, cardiac arrest, stroke, heart failure) within 30 days of surgery and uses it to plan testing, drugs, timing and monitoring - not to 'clear' a patient. [ESC 2022]`,`More than **300 million operations** are performed worldwide each year, and the ageing, diabetic surgical population makes cardiovascular complications the leading cause of peri-operative death. [ESC 2022]`,`In the **VISION** cohort about **1 in 50** inpatients aged 45 or more died within 30 days of non-cardiac surgery, and **myocardial injury after non-cardiac surgery (MINS)** was the commonest major vascular complication. [VISION 2012]`,`**Perioperative myocardial injury** is usually **silent**: in BASEL-PMI it occurred in **16%** of at-risk patients, only about 6% had chest pain, and 30-day mortality was **8.9% vs 1.5%** without it. [BASEL-PMI 2018]`,`**Indian burden**: ICMR-INDIAB (2023) found **diabetes in 11.4% (about 101 million)** and **hypertension in 35.5%** of adults, and NFHS-5 found **anaemia in 57% of women** - each a peri-operative risk multiplier. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`In India, **rheumatic valve disease** is still a common unexpected finding at the pre-anaesthetic check-up, so a murmur in a young adult deserves an echo before major surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Routine ECG, chest X-ray and echo in every patient over 40, common in Indian PAC clinics, are **not evidence-based** - testing should be selective and change management. [ACC/AHA 2024]`]},{heading:`Risk factors and predictors`,points:[`**Patient factors**: ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, cerebrovascular disease, insulin-treated diabetes, CKD (creatinine over 2 mg/dL), age 65 or more, frailty, anaemia and poor functional capacity. [ESC 2022]`,`**Active cardiac conditions** carry the highest risk: unstable angina or recent MI, decompensated HF, significant arrhythmia (high-grade AV block, uncontrolled AF or VT) and severe symptomatic valve disease. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Surgical factors**: type of surgery (vascular, intraperitoneal, intrathoracic), duration, blood loss, fluid shifts and **urgency** - emergency surgery raises cardiac risk 2-5 fold. [ESC 2022]`,`**Recent coronary stent** (under 6-12 months) or **recent MI** (under 60 days) markedly raises peri-operative MI and stent thrombosis. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Recent stroke or TIA**: risk of major adverse events is highest within 3 months and plateaus at about **9 months**. [Jorgensen 2014]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension**, severe **aortic stenosis**, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with obstruction and cyanotic congenital disease are high-risk lesions under anaesthesia. [ESC 2022]`,`**Sleep apnoea, COPD, obesity and frailty** add to post-operative complications and should be screened (STOP-BANG, Clinical Frailty Scale). [ACC/AHA 2024]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of peri-operative cardiac events`,points:[`Surgery triggers a **stress response**: catecholamine surge, tachycardia, hypertension, hypercoagulability (raised fibrinogen, platelet activation, reduced fibrinolysis) and inflammation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Most peri-operative MIs are **type 2 (supply-demand mismatch)** from tachycardia, hypotension, anaemia or hypoxaemia on fixed coronary stenoses - hence the focus on heart rate, BP and Hb. [ESC 2022]`,`**Type 1 MI** (plaque rupture with thrombosis) also occurs, promoted by the prothrombotic state and by stopping antiplatelet drugs. [ESC 2022]`,`Peak events occur in the **first 48-72 hours**, when analgesia masks chest pain - the reason for scheduled troponin measurement rather than waiting for symptoms. [ESC 2022]`,`**Anaesthetic agents** cause vasodilatation and myocardial depression; **neuraxial block** causes sympathectomy and hypotension, poorly tolerated in fixed-output lesions such as severe AS and MS. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Fluid shifts**: large-volume infusion and third-spacing precipitate pulmonary oedema in HF, and mobilisation of fluid on days 2-3 can cause late decompensation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Post-operative AF** follows sympathetic surge, electrolyte shifts and inflammation, and is a marker of increased stroke and mortality risk. [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Classification: surgical risk, urgency and ASA grade`,points:[`**Low-risk surgery (under 1%)**: breast, dental, thyroid, eye, minor gynaecological, minor orthopaedic (meniscectomy), reconstructive, superficial and minor urological (TURP) surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Intermediate-risk (1-5%)**: intraperitoneal (cholecystectomy, splenectomy, hiatal hernia), carotid endarterectomy for asymptomatic disease, EVAR, head and neck, major orthopaedic (hip, spine), renal transplant, major urological or gynaecological. [ESC 2022]`,`**High-risk (over 5%)**: aortic and major vascular surgery, open lower-limb revascularisation or amputation for limb ischaemia, oesophagectomy, pancreatoduodenal, liver resection, pneumonectomy, cystectomy, perforated bowel repair, lung or liver transplant. [ESC 2022]`,`**Urgency**: **emergency** (life or limb within hours - proceed, optimise in theatre), **urgent** (within 24 hours), **time-sensitive** (for example cancer, within weeks) and **elective** (can wait up to a year). [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**ASA physical status**: I healthy, II mild systemic disease, III severe systemic disease, IV severe disease that is a constant threat to life, V moribund, VI brain-dead donor, with 'E' added for emergency. [ASA 2020]`,`**Endoscopic and endovascular** procedures carry lower cardiac risk than the equivalent open operation - consider them for high-risk patients. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`History: what to ask`,points:[`Ask about **chest pain, exertional breathlessness, orthopnoea, PND, palpitations, syncope and ankle swelling** - new or worsening symptoms need evaluation before elective surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Functional capacity**: can the patient **climb two flights of stairs** or walk uphill briskly without stopping? That equals about **4 METs** and predicts low risk. [ESC 2022]`,`**DASI (Duke Activity Status Index)** - 12 questions on daily activities; a score **over 34** (about 4 METs) predicted fewer complications than subjective assessment in the METS study. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Cardiac history**: prior MI and date, stents (date, type, indication, the stent card), CABG, heart failure admissions, valve disease, pacemaker or ICD, and rheumatic fever. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Drug history**: antiplatelets, anticoagulants, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors or ARBs, diuretics, **SGLT2 inhibitors**, **GLP-1 receptor agonists**, insulin and herbal products. [ESC 2022]`,`Ask about **diabetes control, kidney disease, stroke or TIA, snoring and daytime sleepiness**, smoking and alcohol, and previous anaesthetic problems. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`Note **patient preferences and goals** - shared decision-making is explicitly recommended when risk is high. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination`,points:[`**BP in both arms and pulse** - rate, rhythm (AF) and pulse deficit; BP of **180/110 or more** in clinic warrants deferring elective surgery. [AAGBI/BHS 2016]`,`**Signs of heart failure**: raised JVP, third heart sound, basal crackles, pedal oedema - decompensated HF means postponement. [ESC 2022]`,`**Murmurs**: an ejection systolic murmur radiating to the carotids with a soft A2 suggests **aortic stenosis**; a mid-diastolic rumble with loud S1 and opening snap suggests **mitral stenosis** - both need an echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pallor** (anaemia), cyanosis and clubbing (congenital disease), and **carotid bruits** or absent foot pulses (generalised atherosclerosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**BMI, neck circumference and airway** (sleep apnoea), and frailty measures such as grip strength or gait speed in older patients. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`Check for a **pacemaker or ICD** pocket and ask for the device card - the device team must plan electrocautery. [HRS/ASA 2020]`]},{heading:`Conditions that change the plan (differential of the high-risk patient)`,points:[`**Unstable angina or MI within 60 days** (without revascularisation): defer elective surgery and manage as ACS - see the NSTE-ACS topic. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Decompensated or new heart failure**: defer, establish LVEF by echo and optimise GDMT; peri-operative risk is higher in HFrEF than HFpEF. [ESC 2022]`,`**Severe symptomatic aortic stenosis**: aortic valve replacement (**SAVR or TAVI**) before elective intermediate or high-risk surgery; asymptomatic severe AS may proceed for low-intermediate risk surgery with careful haemodynamics. [ESC 2022]`,`**Significant mitral stenosis**: balloon mitral valvotomy before high-risk surgery if symptomatic or pulmonary artery systolic pressure is over 50 mmHg. [ESC 2022]`,`**Arrhythmias**: control the rate of AF, treat sustained VT, and pace symptomatic high-grade AV block before surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Severe pulmonary hypertension**: high risk under anaesthesia - surgery only after expert assessment in a centre with PH experience. [ESC 2022]`,`**Stent within 6 months (elective PCI) or 12 months (ACS)**: defer elective surgery to complete DAPT - see the Antiplatelet therapy topic. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Risk indices and functional capacity`,points:[`**Revised Cardiac Risk Index (Lee 1999)** has six predictors: **high-risk surgery** (intraperitoneal, intrathoracic, suprainguinal vascular), **ischaemic heart disease**, **heart failure**, **cerebrovascular disease**, **insulin-treated diabetes** and **creatinine over 2 mg/dL**. [Lee 1999]`,`RCRI risk of major cardiac complications in the original cohort: **0 points 0.4%, 1 point 0.9%, 2 points 6.6%, 3 or more 11%**; later cohorts with troponin surveillance found higher absolute rates. [Lee 1999]`,`The **ACS-NSQIP surgical risk calculator** and **AUB-HAS2 index** (history of heart disease, symptoms, age 75+, Hb under 12, vascular surgery, emergency) are accepted alternatives. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**4 METs** = climbing two flights of stairs, walking up a hill, heavy housework; **under 4 METs** = only light housework or walking slowly on the flat. [ESC 2022]`,`**Good functional capacity (4 METs or more, DASI over 34)** with no active cardiac condition: proceed without further cardiac testing, even before high-risk surgery. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Poor or unknown functional capacity** with elevated clinical risk is where biomarkers, echo and stress imaging earn their place. [ESC 2022]`,`**Clinical Frailty Scale** of 5 or more predicts complications and loss of independence and should inform shared decisions in the elderly. [ACC/AHA 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations: when to test`,points:[`**Resting 12-lead ECG**: in patients with known CVD, cardiovascular risk factors or age 65+ before intermediate or high-risk surgery; **not** for asymptomatic patients having low-risk surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**High-sensitivity troponin** at baseline and at **24 and 48 hours** after surgery is recommended in patients with known CVD, risk factors or age 65+ having intermediate or high-risk surgery, to detect silent PMI. [ESC 2022]`,`**BNP or NT-proBNP**: **NT-proBNP 300 pg/mL or more** (BNP 92 or more) identifies higher risk and justifies post-operative troponin surveillance. [CCS 2017]`,`**Echocardiography**: for unexplained dyspnoea, a new murmur, suspected or worsening HF, or poor functional capacity or raised NT-proBNP before high-risk surgery - not routinely. [ESC 2022]`,`**Stress imaging** (stress echo, nuclear perfusion): only for poor functional capacity with high likelihood of CAD before high-risk elective surgery, and only if the result will change management. [ESC 2022]`,`**CT coronary angiography** may rule out CAD in suspected chronic coronary syndrome; **invasive angiography** follows the same indications as in non-surgical patients. [ESC 2022]`,`**Bloods**: Hb (treat iron-deficiency anaemia, IV iron if time short), creatinine with eGFR, potassium, glucose and **HbA1c (aim 8.5% or less for elective surgery)**. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Prophylactic coronary revascularisation** before surgery does not improve outcome in stable CAD (CARP trial); revascularise only for the usual indications. [CARP 2004]`]},{heading:`OPD management: continuing and stopping cardiac drugs`,points:[`**Beta-blockers**: **continue** in patients already taking them; **never start on the day of surgery** - POISE (metoprolol succinate 100 mg 2-4 hours before) reduced MI but increased **death and stroke**. [ESC 2022]`,`Starting a beta-blocker **well in advance** (titrated over days to weeks to heart rate and BP) may be considered before high-risk surgery in patients with 2 or more RCRI factors or known IHD. [ESC 2022]`,`**ACE inhibitors and ARBs**: withholding on the morning of surgery should be considered in patients without HF to avoid hypotension; continue in stable HFrEF; STOP-or-NOT (2024) found no outcome difference, only more intra-operative hypotension with continuation. [ESC 2022]`,`**Statins**: continue peri-operatively; consider starting one before vascular surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Aspirin**: continue in patients with a stent or established ASCVD unless bleeding risk is prohibitive; for primary prevention it can be stopped (POISE-2 showed more bleeding, no benefit). [ESC 2022]`,`**P2Y12 inhibitors**: stop clopidogrel **5 days**, ticagrelor **3-5 days**, prasugrel **7 days** before, only after cardiology agrees if a stent is recent. [ESC 2022]`,`**Oral anticoagulants**: warfarin stopped 5 days before (no bridging for most AF - BRIDGE); DOACs stopped 1-2 days before by renal function and bleeding risk - see the Anticoagulants in practice topic. [CHEST 2022]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors**: stop **3 days before** (ertugliflozin **4 days**) intermediate or high-risk surgery to prevent **euglycaemic DKA**; restart when eating and drinking normally. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Diuretics**: omit on the morning of surgery; **calcium-channel blockers, nitrates, amiodarone, digoxin and clonidine** are continued (sudden clonidine withdrawal causes rebound hypertension). [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**GLP-1 receptor agonists**: most patients may continue; those at high aspiration risk (escalation phase, GI symptoms) need a liquid diet for 24 hours or withholding (weekly drug held 1 week) after discussion with the anaesthetist. [Multisociety 2024]`]},{heading:`Stents, recent MI and timing of surgery`,points:[`**Elective surgery after PCI**: defer until **6 months after elective PCI** and **12 months after ACS** so DAPT is complete. [ESC 2022]`,`**Time-sensitive surgery** (for example cancer) may proceed after a minimum of **1 month of DAPT after elective PCI** and **3 months after ACS**, with heart-team agreement. [ESC 2022]`,`**Continue aspirin** throughout whenever possible; surgery in a hospital with **24-hour PCI** facility is preferred for patients with recent stents. [ESC 2022]`,`**Bridging antiplatelet therapy** with IV cangrelor or a GP IIb/IIIa inhibitor is reserved for very high stent-thrombosis risk; **LMWH does not protect** against stent thrombosis. [ESC 2022]`,`**After MI without revascularisation**: wait **at least 60 days** before elective non-cardiac surgery. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**After stroke or TIA**: defer elective surgery for **at least 3 months**; risk continues to fall up to about 9 months. [ESC 2022]`,`**After balloon angioplasty without stent**: defer elective surgery for about **14 days**; after CABG, risk returns towards baseline once recovered. [ACC/AHA 2024]`]},{heading:`IPD: intra- and post-operative care and monitoring`,points:[`Avoid **hypotension** (keep mean arterial pressure 60-65 mmHg or more and avoid a fall of over 20% from baseline), **tachycardia**, anaemia and hypoxaemia - the triggers of type 2 MI. [ESC 2022]`,`**Post-operative troponin surveillance** (baseline, 24 h, 48 h) in at-risk patients; a rise above the URL from baseline defines **perioperative myocardial injury (PMI)**. [ESC 2022]`,`**PMI/MINS work-up**: ECG, clinical review, echo, look for and treat the cause (bleeding, sepsis, hypotension, PE); start **aspirin and statin** if not contraindicated and arrange cardiology follow-up. [ESC 2022]`,`**MANAGE trial**: dabigatran 110 mg twice daily in MINS reduced major vascular complications (**11% vs 15%**) - may be considered when bleeding risk is low. [MANAGE 2018]`,`**Post-operative AF**: correct potassium, magnesium, pain and fluid status; rate control with a beta-blocker; consider **long-term anticoagulation** if thromboembolic risk is elevated (ESC 2024, IIa). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Post-operative heart failure**: watch fluid balance, daily weight and creatinine; restart GDMT (ACEi/ARB, MRA, SGLT2i) as soon as haemodynamics and intake allow. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Pacemakers and ICDs**: switch ICD tachy-therapies off with continuous monitoring and defibrillator pads on; set pacemaker-dependent patients to asynchronous mode if monopolar cautery is above the umbilicus; re-check after surgery. [HRS/ASA 2020]`,`**Tranexamic acid** 1 g at the start and end of surgery reduced bleeding in POISE-3, though non-inferiority for cardiovascular events was not formally shown. [POISE-3 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Emergency surgery**: do not delay for cardiac tests; optimise in theatre with invasive monitoring and treat cardiac findings post-operatively. [ESC 2022]`,`**Elderly and frail**: screen frailty and cognition, avoid polypharmacy and benzodiazepines, plan early mobilisation and delirium prevention. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: HbA1c 8.5% or less for elective surgery, stop SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days before, adjust insulin (about 80% of long-acting basal insulin the night before), first-on-the-list scheduling. [JBDS 2023]`,`**CKD**: higher MACE and contrast nephropathy risk; check potassium, adjust renally cleared drugs and anticoagulant interruption times. [ESC 2022]`,`**Hypertension**: do not defer surgery for grade 1-2 hypertension; defer elective surgery if clinic BP is **180/110 or more** (primary care referral threshold 160/100). [AAGBI/BHS 2016]`,`**Pregnancy and congenital or valve disease**: non-obstetric surgery is planned with the cardio-obstetric team - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: history, functional capacity, ECG, Hb and creatinine identify most at-risk patients; refer only those with active cardiac conditions, severe valve disease or poor capacity before high-risk surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`**Prosthetic valves and endocarditis prophylaxis**: dental and other procedures need antibiotic prophylaxis only in high-risk patients (prosthetic valve, prior endocarditis, some congenital disease). [ESC 2023 Endocarditis]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and counselling`,points:[`Refer to cardiology **before** elective surgery for active cardiac conditions, new murmur with symptoms, LVEF under 40% not optimised, recent stent, or poor capacity with high clinical risk before high-risk surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`Write a **clear pre-operative letter**: diagnosis, functional capacity, RCRI, drugs to continue and stop with dates, and post-operative monitoring advice - not 'fit for surgery'. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`Counsel on **smoking cessation** (ideally 4-8 weeks before), alcohol reduction, prehabilitation exercise and correction of anaemia. [ESC 2022]`,`After discharge, review patients with **PMI or post-operative AF** within weeks - they have long-term excess cardiovascular risk and need secondary prevention. [ESC 2022]`,`Use the peri-operative visit as a **prevention opportunity**: newly found hypertension, diabetes or dyslipidaemia should be enrolled in **NP-NCD** follow-up. [MoHFW NP-NCD]`,`Patients covered by **PM-JAY** can access cardiac work-up and procedures in empanelled hospitals - relevant when valve or coronary intervention is needed first. [MoHFW]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2022 non-cardiac surgery**: hs-troponin at baseline, 24 and 48 hours in at-risk patients (I), NT-proBNP/BNP for risk stratification (IIa), patient-centred approach, SGLT2 inhibitor interruption, and stent-surgery timing (1 and 3 month minimums). [ESC 2022]`,`**ACC/AHA/multisociety 2024 perioperative guideline**: replaces 2014; endorses RCRI, NSQIP and AUB-HAS2, DASI over 34 as good capacity, natriuretic peptides for risk, no routine stress testing, troponin surveillance in high risk, and guidance on SGLT2i and GLP-1 RA. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`**STOP-or-NOT (2024)**: continuing versus stopping RAS inhibitors 48 hours before major surgery gave **similar complications** (about 22% each) but more intra-operative hypotension with continuation. [STOP-or-NOT 2024]`,`**POISE-3 (2022)**: tranexamic acid cut bleeding; a hypotension-avoidance strategy (withholding antihypertensives) did not change vascular events. [POISE-3 2022]`,`**Multisociety 2024 GLP-1 RA guidance** (ASA, AGA and others): most patients may continue, with a 24-hour liquid diet for those at high aspiration risk - replacing the 2023 'hold for a week' advice. [Multisociety 2024]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: long-term anticoagulation should be considered for **post-operative AF after non-cardiac surgery** in patients at elevated thromboembolic risk. [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Be able to list the **six RCRI predictors** and the **three surgical risk bands** with examples. [Lee 1999]`,`Viva: 'What is 4 METs?' - climbing two flights of stairs; good capacity means no further testing before most surgery. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: starting a **beta-blocker on the morning of surgery** - POISE showed more deaths and strokes. [POISE 2008]`,`Common mistake: ordering **stress tests or angiography** to 'clear' a patient - prophylactic revascularisation does not help (CARP). [CARP 2004]`,`Common mistake: **bridging AF patients** with LMWH or stopping aspirin in a patient with a recent stent. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **SGLT2 inhibitors** - euglycaemic DKA presents with normal glucose and is missed. [ACC/AHA 2024]`,`Avoid the phrase **'fit for surgery'** - write the estimated risk and specific recommendations. [ACC/AHA 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Surgical risk categories (ESC 2022; 30-day cardiovascular death, MI or stroke)`,columns:[`Risk band`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Low (under 1%)`,`Breast, dental, thyroid, eye, superficial and reconstructive surgery; minor gynaecological, orthopaedic (meniscectomy) and urological (TURP); minor VATS`],[`Intermediate (1-5%)`,`Cholecystectomy, splenectomy, hiatal hernia repair; asymptomatic carotid endarterectomy or stenting; EVAR; head and neck; hip and spine surgery; renal transplant; major gynaecological or urological; peripheral angioplasty`],[`High (over 5%)`,`Aortic and major vascular surgery; open limb revascularisation or amputation for limb ischaemia; oesophagectomy; Whipple; liver resection; pneumonectomy; total cystectomy; perforated bowel repair; lung or liver transplant`]]},{heading:`Revised Cardiac Risk Index (Lee) and risk`,columns:[`Item`,`Definition`,`Contribution`],rows:[[`High-risk surgery`,`Intraperitoneal, intrathoracic or suprainguinal vascular`,`1 point`],[`Ischaemic heart disease`,`Prior MI, positive stress test, angina, nitrate use or pathological Q waves`,`1 point`],[`Heart failure`,`History, pulmonary oedema, PND, S3, bilateral crackles or chest X-ray congestion`,`1 point`],[`Cerebrovascular disease`,`Prior stroke or TIA`,`1 point`],[`Diabetes`,`On insulin`,`1 point`],[`Renal dysfunction`,`Creatinine over 2 mg/dL (177 micromol/L)`,`1 point`],[`Total 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 or more`,`Major cardiac complication rate (original cohort)`,`0.4% / 0.9% / 6.6% / 11%`]]},{heading:`Cardiac drugs around non-cardiac surgery`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration (peri-operative plan)`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Beta-blocker (metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol)`,`Usual oral dose; if newly started, bisoprolol 2.5 mg or metoprolol 25 mg daily`,`Continue if already taking; start only days to weeks ahead in high-risk patients, never on the day of surgery`,`Bradycardia, hypotension, stroke and death with high-dose acute start (POISE)`],[`ACE inhibitor / ARB`,`Usual oral dose`,`Omit the morning dose in patients without HF; continue in stable HFrEF; restart when haemodynamically stable`,`Intra-operative hypotension, AKI, hyperkalaemia`],[`Statin (atorvastatin, rosuvastatin)`,`Atorvastatin 40-80 mg oral at night`,`Continue; consider starting before vascular surgery`,`Myalgia; rare rhabdomyolysis`],[`Aspirin`,`75-100 mg oral daily`,`Continue with stents or ASCVD; stop if primary prevention or closed-space surgery`,`Surgical bleeding`],[`Clopidogrel / ticagrelor / prasugrel`,`75 mg daily / 90 mg BD / 10 mg daily`,`Stop 5 / 3-5 / 7 days before; restart within 24-48 h`,`Stent thrombosis if stopped early after PCI`],[`Warfarin`,`Dose to INR`,`Stop 5 days before; INR 1.5 or less; bridge only very high thrombotic risk`,`Bleeding; thrombosis if interrupted carelessly`],[`DOACs`,`Per indication and renal function`,`Stop 1-2 days before (longer for dabigatran with low CrCl); no bridging`,`Bleeding; spinal haematoma with neuraxial block`],[`SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin, empagliflozin)`,`10 mg oral daily`,`Stop 3 days before (ertugliflozin 4); restart when eating normally`,`Euglycaemic DKA, volume depletion`],[`Diuretics`,`Usual oral dose`,`Omit on the morning of surgery`,`Hypovolaemia, hypokalaemia`],[`Clonidine`,`Usual oral dose`,`Continue; do not start de novo (POISE-2)`,`Rebound hypertension if stopped; hypotension if started`]]},{heading:`Pre-operative tests: when and how to read them`,columns:[`Test`,`Indication`,`Interpretation / cut-off`],rows:[[`12-lead ECG`,`Known CVD, risk factors or age 65+ before intermediate/high-risk surgery`,`Baseline for comparison; Q waves, LVH, AF, block change risk`],[`hs-troponin`,`Same group; baseline, 24 h and 48 h after surgery`,`Rise above URL from baseline = perioperative myocardial injury`],[`NT-proBNP / BNP`,`Elevated clinical risk, unclear functional capacity`,`NT-proBNP 300 pg/mL or more or BNP 92 or more = higher risk`],[`Echocardiography`,`Unexplained dyspnoea, murmur, suspected HF, poor capacity before high-risk surgery`,`LVEF, severe AS or MS, pulmonary pressure`],[`Stress imaging`,`Poor capacity, high CAD likelihood, high-risk elective surgery`,`Large ischaemic burden prompts heart-team review`],[`Hb, creatinine, K, HbA1c`,`Intermediate/high-risk surgery`,`Treat anaemia; HbA1c 8.5% or less for elective surgery`],[`DASI questionnaire`,`All patients where capacity is unclear`,`Over 34 = at least 4 METs`]]},{heading:`Cardiac conditions and timing of elective surgery`,columns:[`Condition`,`Action before elective surgery`,`Timing`],rows:[[`Elective PCI with DES`,`Complete DAPT; continue aspirin`,`6 months (minimum 1 month if time-sensitive)`],[`ACS treated with PCI`,`Complete DAPT`,`12 months (minimum 3 months if time-sensitive)`],[`MI without revascularisation`,`Medical optimisation`,`At least 60 days`],[`Stroke or TIA`,`Secondary prevention`,`At least 3 months; risk plateaus at 9 months`],[`Decompensated heart failure`,`Diuresis, GDMT, echo`,`Defer until compensated`],[`Severe symptomatic aortic stenosis`,`SAVR or TAVI first`,`Before intermediate or high-risk surgery`],[`Symptomatic severe mitral stenosis`,`Balloon mitral valvotomy`,`Before high-risk surgery`],[`Clinic BP 180/110 or more`,`Control BP`,`Defer elective surgery until controlled`]]},{heading:`ASA physical status classification`,columns:[`ASA class`,`Definition`,`Example`],rows:[[`I`,`Normal healthy patient`,`Fit non-smoker`],[`II`,`Mild systemic disease`,`Controlled hypertension or diabetes, BMI 30-40, current smoker`],[`III`,`Severe systemic disease`,`Poorly controlled diabetes, COPD, stable angina, LVEF moderately reduced, dialysis`],[`IV`,`Severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life`,`Recent MI or stroke (under 3 months), severe valve dysfunction, sepsis`],[`V`,`Moribund, not expected to survive without the operation`,`Ruptured aortic aneurysm`],[`VI`,`Brain-dead organ donor`,`Organ retrieval`]]}],redFlags:[`Chest pain at rest or crescendo angina before planned surgery - treat as ACS, defer surgery`,`Breathlessness at rest, orthopnoea or a raised JVP - decompensated heart failure, defer and refer`,`Syncope or exertional breathlessness with an ejection systolic murmur - suspect severe aortic stenosis, echo before surgery`,`Pulse under 40 or high-grade AV block, or sustained VT on ECG`,`Plan to stop DAPT within 6 months of elective PCI or 12 months of ACS`,`Post-operative troponin rise, new ECG change, hypotension or AF in the first 72 hours - look for MINS and its cause`,`Vomiting, abdominal pain or breathlessness after surgery on an SGLT2 inhibitor - check ketones for euglycaemic DKA`],pearls:[`Three questions decide most cases: how urgent is the surgery, how risky is the surgery, and can the patient climb two flights of stairs?`,`RCRI six: 'High-risk surgery, Heart disease (ischaemic), Heart failure, Head (stroke), Hyperglycaemia on insulin, Hypercreatininaemia over 2'.`,`Test only if the result will change management - prophylactic revascularisation does not help (CARP).`,`Never start a beta-blocker on the morning of surgery (POISE); never stop one that is already prescribed.`,`Stents and surgery: 6 months after elective PCI, 12 months after ACS; minimum 1 and 3 months if time-sensitive.`,`SGLT2 inhibitors off 3 days before surgery (ertugliflozin 4) - euglycaemic DKA hides behind a normal sugar.`,`Most perioperative MIs are silent and type 2 - measure troponin at 24 and 48 hours in at-risk patients.`,`NT-proBNP 300 or more or BNP 92 or more means higher risk and post-operative troponin surveillance.`,`Write a risk estimate and a drug plan, never 'fit for surgery'.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-q1`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the six predictors in the Revised Cardiac Risk Index (Lee)?`,options:[`Insulin-treated diabetes`,`Serum creatinine over 2 mg/dL`,`Intraperitoneal surgery`,`Age over 70 years`],answer:3,explanation:`RCRI uses high-risk surgery (intraperitoneal, intrathoracic, suprainguinal vascular), ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, cerebrovascular disease, insulin-treated diabetes and creatinine over 2 mg/dL. Age is notably absent from RCRI, although age 75 or more appears in the newer AUB-HAS2 index and age 65 or more triggers biomarker testing in ESC 2022.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-q2`,stem:`A 62-year-old hypertensive diabetic on oral drugs is listed for elective total knee replacement. He climbs three flights of stairs daily without symptoms and his examination is normal. What is the best pre-operative cardiac plan?`,options:[`Proceed to surgery without stress testing; obtain an ECG and continue his usual drugs`,`Stress echocardiography before surgery`,`Invasive coronary angiography before surgery`,`Defer surgery for 3 months of beta-blocker titration`],answer:0,explanation:`Good functional capacity (at least 4 METs) with no active cardiac condition means he can proceed without stress testing, even though he has risk factors; an ECG as a baseline is reasonable before intermediate-risk surgery. Stress echo or angiography would not change management, and prophylactic beta-blocker titration is not indicated for a low-RCRI patient.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old with type 2 diabetes and HFrEF takes dapagliflozin 10 mg daily. She is scheduled for an elective open hemicolectomy. What should be done with dapagliflozin?`,options:[`Continue it including the morning of surgery`,`Stop it 3 days before surgery and restart when eating and drinking normally`,`Stop it 2 weeks before surgery`,`Replace it with empagliflozin on the day of surgery`],answer:1,explanation:`SGLT2 inhibitors are withheld for 3 days before intermediate or high-risk surgery (4 days for ertugliflozin) because fasting and surgical stress precipitate euglycaemic DKA; they are restarted once oral intake is normal. Continuing on the day of surgery carries that DKA risk, two weeks is unnecessary, and switching to another SGLT2 inhibitor changes nothing.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-q4`,stem:`Regarding peri-operative beta-blockers in non-cardiac surgery, which statement is correct?`,options:[`High-dose metoprolol should be started on the day of surgery in all patients with risk factors`,`Beta-blockers should be stopped 48 hours before surgery to prevent hypotension`,`Patients already on a beta-blocker should continue it through surgery`,`Beta-blockers replace the need for statins before vascular surgery`],answer:2,explanation:`Chronic beta-blockers are continued, because abrupt withdrawal causes rebound tachycardia and ischaemia. POISE showed that starting high-dose metoprolol hours before surgery reduced MI but increased stroke and death, so de novo day-of-surgery initiation is harmful; stopping them 48 hours ahead invites rebound, and statins have their own separate role in vascular surgery.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-q5`,stem:`A 74-year-old has exertional syncope and a harsh ejection systolic murmur. Echo shows severe aortic stenosis (mean gradient 52 mmHg) with normal LVEF. He needs an elective hip replacement for osteoarthritis. What is the best course?`,options:[`Proceed with hip surgery under spinal anaesthesia`,`Aortic valve replacement (SAVR or TAVI) first, then hip surgery`,`Start a beta-blocker and proceed after one week`,`Coronary angiography and PCI alone, then hip surgery`],answer:1,explanation:`Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis is an active cardiac condition: ESC 2022 recommends aortic valve replacement (SAVR or TAVI) before elective intermediate or high-risk surgery. Spinal anaesthesia causes sympathectomy and hypotension poorly tolerated with a fixed outflow obstruction, a beta-blocker does not relieve the valve lesion, and PCI alone leaves the stenosis untreated.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`A 68-year-old man with diabetes and hypertension, who had a drug-eluting stent for stable angina 8 months ago, is posted for elective total hip replacement. Discuss his pre-operative cardiac evaluation and peri-operative management.`,openingLines:[`Pre-operative cardiac evaluation estimates the 30-day risk of cardiac death, MI, stroke and heart failure from the **surgical risk**, the **patient's clinical risk** and **functional capacity**, and plans tests, drugs, timing and monitoring - it does not 'clear' a patient (ESC 2022, ACC/AHA 2024).`,`Hip replacement is **intermediate-risk surgery (1-5%)**; this patient has diabetes, hypertension and a stent placed **8 months ago for CCS** - beyond the 6-month DAPT period, so elective surgery can proceed with aspirin continued.`],answer:[{heading:`Step 1: urgency and surgical risk`,points:[`Elective surgery; total hip replacement is intermediate risk (1-5%) in ESC 2022.`,`No active cardiac condition must be present: ACS, decompensated HF, significant arrhythmia, severe symptomatic valve disease.`]},{heading:`Step 2: clinical risk and functional capacity`,points:[`History: angina, dyspnoea, syncope; stent card; drugs (DAPT, SGLT2i, beta-blocker).`,`RCRI: ischaemic heart disease 1 point (+1 if on insulin) - risk about 1-7%.`,`Functional capacity: hip arthritis limits stairs, so use DASI; over 34 = 4 METs or more.`]},{heading:`Step 3: investigations`,points:[`ECG; hs-troponin baseline and at 24 and 48 h after surgery; NT-proBNP (300 pg/mL or more = higher risk).`,`Echo if dyspnoea, murmur or high NT-proBNP; stress imaging only if poor capacity, symptoms and result would change management.`,`Hb, creatinine, potassium, HbA1c (8.5% or less for elective surgery).`]},{heading:`Step 4: drugs`,points:[`Stent 8 months ago for CCS: DAPT (6 months) complete - continue aspirin, stop clopidogrel 5 days before if still on it.`,`Continue beta-blocker and statin; omit ACEi/ARB and diuretic on the morning of surgery; stop SGLT2i 3 days before.`,`Insulin or oral agents per diabetes protocol.`]},{heading:`Step 5: intra- and post-operative care`,points:[`Avoid hypotension, tachycardia, anaemia; VTE prophylaxis after hip surgery.`,`Troponin surveillance; if PMI - ECG, echo, find cause, aspirin and statin, cardiology review.`,`Watch for post-operative AF and heart failure; restart drugs promptly.`]},{heading:`Step 6: communication and follow-up`,points:[`Letter with risk estimate and drug plan, not 'fit for surgery'.`,`Review after discharge; secondary prevention and NP-NCD follow-up.`]}],mustDraw:[`ESC 2022 stepwise flow chart: urgency, surgical risk, patient risk, functional capacity, tests`,`Table of RCRI predictors`],markSplit:[{part:`Surgical risk and urgency`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical risk, RCRI and functional capacity`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations with cut-offs`,marks:2},{part:`Peri-operative drug plan including stent`,marks:3},{part:`Post-operative monitoring and MINS`,marks:2}],keywords:[`RCRI`,`METs`,`DASI`,`hs-troponin`,`NT-proBNP`,`stent`,`DAPT`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`MINS`]},{id:`cardiovascular-preop-cardiac-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`chart-flow`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Draw a flow chart for stepwise pre-operative cardiac assessment of a patient listed for elective non-cardiac surgery (ESC 2022).`,openingLines:[`ESC 2022 assesses **urgency, surgical risk, patient risk and functional capacity** in sequence, adding **ECG and biomarkers** in patients with CVD, risk factors or age 65 or more.`,`Further tests (echo, stress imaging) are reserved for patients in whom the result would **change management** before high-risk surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Steps of the flow chart`,points:[`Emergency or urgent surgery: proceed; optimise and monitor peri-operatively.`,`Active cardiac condition (ACS, decompensated HF, severe symptomatic valve disease, significant arrhythmia): defer and treat.`,`Low-risk surgery or age under 65 without CVD or risk factors: proceed.`,`Age 65+, risk factors or known CVD before intermediate/high-risk surgery: ECG, hs-troponin baseline, NT-proBNP.`,`Good capacity (4 METs, DASI over 34): proceed with troponin at 24 and 48 h.`,`Poor capacity, high risk, high-risk surgery: echo and stress imaging if they would change management; heart team.`]},{heading:`Notes beside the chart`,points:[`Continue beta-blocker and statin; stop SGLT2i 3 days before.`,`Stent timing: 6 months after elective PCI, 12 months after ACS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Vertical flow chart with decision diamonds for urgency, active conditions, surgical risk and functional capacity`],markSplit:[{part:`Correct sequence of decision steps`,marks:3},{part:`Cut-offs and tests at each step`,marks:1},{part:`Drug and stent notes`,marks:1}],keywords:[`ESC 2022`,`flow chart`,`functional capacity`,`biomarkers`,`surgical risk`]}],references:[`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardiovascular assessment and management of patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery, European Heart Journal 2022`,`2024 AHA/ACC/ACS/ASNC/HRS/SCA/SCCT/SCMR/SVM Guideline for Perioperative Cardiovascular Management for Noncardiac Surgery, Circulation 2024`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Medical evaluation of the surgical patient`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Anesthesia and noncardiac surgery in patients with heart disease`,`Lee TH et al. Derivation and prospective validation of a simple index for prediction of cardiac risk of major noncardiac surgery, Circulation 1999`,`Duceppe E et al. Canadian Cardiovascular Society Guidelines on perioperative cardiac risk assessment and management, Can J Cardiol 2017`,`POISE Study Group. Effects of extended-release metoprolol succinate in patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery, Lancet 2008`,`ICMR-INDIAB national survey of diabetes and metabolic NCDs in India, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023`]}),r.push({id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic`,title:`Cardiometabolic protection: SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**SGLT2 inhibitors** (empagliflozin, dapagliflozin 10 mg daily) cut **heart failure hospitalisation and kidney failure** across HFrEF, HFpEF and CKD with or without diabetes, while **GLP-1 receptor agonists** (semaglutide, liraglutide, dulaglutide) and the dual **GIP/GLP-1 agonist tirzepatide** reduce **atherosclerotic events (MACE)** and weight - so ADA 2026 and ESC 2023 recommend them in type 2 diabetes with **ASCVD, HF or CKD independent of HbA1c and metformin**, and semaglutide reduces MACE in **obesity without diabetes (SELECT)**.`,keywords:[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`gliflozin`,`empagliflozin`,`dapagliflozin`,`canagliflozin`,`remogliflozin`,`GLP-1 receptor agonist`,`semaglutide`,`liraglutide`,`dulaglutide`,`tirzepatide`,`GIP/GLP-1`,`incretin`,`cardiovascular outcome trial`,`SELECT`,`EMPA-REG`,`DAPA-HF`,`euglycaemic DKA`,`cardiorenal protection`,`anti-obesity medication`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and epidemiology`,points:[`**Cardiometabolic protection** means using glucose-lowering drugs chosen for proven reduction in **cardiovascular, heart failure and kidney outcomes**, not merely for HbA1c - the change driven by cardiovascular outcome trials (CVOTs) since 2015. [ADA 2026]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** block sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 in the proximal tubule; **GLP-1 receptor agonists** mimic the incretin glucagon-like peptide-1; **tirzepatide** agonises both GIP and GLP-1 receptors. [Harrison 22e]`,`Cardiovascular disease is the **leading cause of death in type 2 diabetes**, and diabetes roughly doubles the risk of MI, stroke and heart failure. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**Indian burden**: ICMR-INDIAB (2023) found **diabetes in 11.4% (about 101 million)**, prediabetes in 15.3%, generalised obesity in 28.6%, abdominal obesity in 39.5% and hypertension in 35.5% of adults. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`Asian Indians develop diabetes and CVD at a **lower BMI and younger age**, with more visceral fat - so Indian cut-offs are **BMI 23 for overweight and 25 for obesity**, waist 90 cm (men) and 80 cm (women). [Indian Consensus 2009]`,`Heart failure and CKD are common and under-recognised in Indian diabetics; **diabetic kidney disease** is the leading cause of end-stage kidney disease in India. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Generic **dapagliflozin and empagliflozin** now cost only a few rupees a day in India, making SGLT2 inhibitors affordable in primary care; GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide remain costlier, though generic liraglutide and semaglutide are lowering prices. [RSSDI 2022]`]},{heading:`Who needs cardiometabolic protection`,points:[`**Established ASCVD** (prior MI, stroke, revascularisation, symptomatic PAD) with T2DM: GLP-1 RA and/or SGLT2 inhibitor with proven benefit, regardless of HbA1c or metformin use. [ADA 2026]`,`**Heart failure** (HFrEF, HFmrEF or HFpEF), with or without diabetes: an **SGLT2 inhibitor** (dapagliflozin or empagliflozin). [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**CKD** with eGFR 20 or more, especially with albuminuria (UACR 200 mg/g or more) or diabetes: an **SGLT2 inhibitor**; in T2DM with CKD add **semaglutide** for kidney and CV protection (FLOW). [KDIGO 2024]`,`**High CV risk without ASCVD** in T2DM (age 55+ with LVH, coronary, carotid or leg stenosis over 50%, or SCORE2-Diabetes 10% or more): SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 RA may be considered. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**Obesity with established CVD, without diabetes** (BMI 27 or more in SELECT): **semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly** to reduce MACE. [SELECT 2023]`,`**HFpEF with obesity**: semaglutide 2.4 mg or tirzepatide improve symptoms and function, and tirzepatide reduced HF events (SUMMIT). [ADA 2026]`,`**Metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD)** with diabetes or obesity: GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide are preferred glucose-lowering agents. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Mechanism: SGLT2 inhibitors`,points:[`**SGLT2** in the S1 segment of the proximal tubule reabsorbs about **90% of filtered glucose** with sodium; inhibition causes glycosuria of about **60-80 g/day** (240-320 kcal) and natriuresis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Restored tubuloglomerular feedback**: more sodium reaches the macula densa, causing afferent arteriolar constriction and lower **intraglomerular pressure** - the basis of renoprotection and the initial **eGFR dip**. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Haemodynamic effects**: osmotic diuresis and natriuresis reduce plasma volume, preload and BP (about **3-5/1-2 mmHg**), with interstitial more than intravascular fluid loss - explaining the fast HF benefit. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Metabolic shift**: lower insulin and higher glucagon increase lipolysis and ketone production - a more efficient myocardial fuel, but also the mechanism of **euglycaemic DKA**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other effects: **haematocrit rise** (more erythropoietin), lower uric acid, weight loss of **2-3 kg**, reduced epicardial fat and less cardiac fibrosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Glucose lowering is **insulin-independent** and falls with eGFR (modest below 45), whereas **cardiorenal benefit persists down to eGFR 20**. [ADA 2026]`,`HbA1c falls only about **0.5-0.8%** and hypoglycaemia is rare unless combined with insulin or sulfonylureas. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Mechanism: GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide`,points:[`**GLP-1** from intestinal L cells augments **glucose-dependent insulin secretion**, suppresses glucagon, slows gastric emptying and acts on the **hypothalamus and hindbrain** to reduce appetite. [Harrison 22e]`,`Native GLP-1 is degraded by **DPP-4 within minutes**; GLP-1 RAs are modified to resist DPP-4 and bind albumin (liraglutide daily, semaglutide and dulaglutide weekly). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Because insulin release is glucose-dependent, **hypoglycaemia is rare** unless combined with insulin or a sulfonylurea. [ADA 2026]`,`**Cardiovascular mechanisms**: weight and BP reduction, better lipids, reduced inflammation (CRP) and slowed atherosclerosis - benefit is mainly on **atherothrombotic events and stroke**, not HF hospitalisation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`GLP-1 RAs raise **resting heart rate by 2-4 beats/min** through a direct sinoatrial effect. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Tirzepatide** adds **GIP receptor agonism**, giving greater HbA1c (about 2%) and weight (up to about 20%) reductions than selective GLP-1 RAs. [SURMOUNT-1 2022]`,`HbA1c reduction: semaglutide about **1-1.8%**; weight loss about 4-6 kg at diabetes doses and about **15% with 2.4 mg** in obesity (STEP-1). [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins)**: empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin, ertugliflozin, **remogliflozin** (developed and marketed in India) and sotagliflozin (dual SGLT1/2, not marketed in India). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Short-acting GLP-1 RAs**: exenatide twice daily and lixisenatide - mainly lower post-prandial glucose, with neutral CVOTs. [ADA 2026]`,`**Long-acting GLP-1 RAs**: liraglutide (daily SC), dulaglutide and semaglutide (weekly SC), and **oral semaglutide** (daily). [ADA 2026]`,`**Dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist**: tirzepatide (weekly SC); triple agonists (retatrutide) and amylin combinations (cagrilintide-semaglutide) are investigational. [ADA 2026]`,`**By cardiovascular evidence**: proven MACE benefit - empagliflozin, canagliflozin, liraglutide, SC semaglutide, dulaglutide, oral semaglutide (SOUL); neutral - ertugliflozin, lixisenatide, exenatide. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**By main organ benefit**: SGLT2 inhibitors - **heart failure and kidney**; GLP-1 RAs - **atherosclerotic events, stroke, weight** and (semaglutide) kidney. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Evidence: SGLT2 inhibitor outcome trials`,points:[`**EMPA-REG OUTCOME (2015)**: empagliflozin in T2DM with ASCVD reduced MACE by **14%**, CV death by **38%**, HF hospitalisation by 35% and all-cause death by 32%. [EMPA-REG 2015]`,`**CANVAS (2017)** and **DECLARE-TIMI 58 (2019)**: canagliflozin reduced MACE (with an amputation signal); dapagliflozin reduced CV death or HF hospitalisation (17%) in a mainly primary-prevention population. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**DAPA-HF (2019)** and **EMPEROR-Reduced (2020)**: in HFrEF, with or without diabetes, worsening HF or CV death fell by **26% and 25%**. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`**EMPEROR-Preserved (2021)** and **DELIVER (2022)**: in LVEF over 40%, the composite fell by **21% and 18%** - the first drugs proven in HFpEF. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**CREDENCE (2019)**, **DAPA-CKD (2020)** and **EMPA-KIDNEY (2022)**: kidney failure or progression fell by **30%, 39% and 28%**, the last two including non-diabetic CKD down to eGFR 20-25. [KDIGO 2024]`,`Benefits appear within **weeks** (HF) and are independent of baseline HbA1c, supporting a haemodynamic and renal mechanism. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ESC 2023 ACS** and **DAPA-MI/EMPACT-MI (2024)**: starting an SGLT2 inhibitor after MI without diabetes or HF did not reduce death or HF hospitalisation significantly - no routine post-MI use without another indication. [EMPACT-MI 2024]`]},{heading:`Evidence: GLP-1 receptor agonist, tirzepatide and obesity trials`,points:[`**LEADER (2016)**: liraglutide reduced MACE by **13%** and CV death by **22%** in T2DM at high CV risk. [LEADER 2016]`,`**SUSTAIN-6 (2016)**: weekly semaglutide reduced MACE by **26%**, driven by non-fatal stroke, with more retinopathy complications in those with rapid HbA1c falls. [SUSTAIN-6 2016]`,`**REWIND (2019)**: dulaglutide reduced MACE by 12% in a population where only about a third had prior CVD. [REWIND 2019]`,`**SOUL (2025)**: oral semaglutide 14 mg reduced MACE by **14%** in T2DM with ASCVD or CKD. [SOUL 2025]`,`**FLOW (2024)**: semaglutide 1 mg in T2DM with CKD reduced major kidney events by **24%** and CV death. [FLOW 2024]`,`**SELECT (2023)**: semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly in **17,604 people with obesity or overweight (BMI 27+) and CVD without diabetes** reduced MACE from **8.0% to 6.5% (HR 0.80)**. [SELECT 2023]`,`**SUMMIT (2024)**: tirzepatide in HFpEF with obesity reduced CV death or worsening HF (HR about 0.62) and improved symptoms; STEP-HFpEF showed symptom benefit with semaglutide. [SUMMIT 2024]`,`**SURPASS-CVOT (2025)**: tirzepatide was non-inferior to dulaglutide for MACE, with lower all-cause mortality. [SURPASS-CVOT 2025]`,`**SURMOUNT-1 (2022)**: tirzepatide 15 mg produced about **21% weight loss** at 72 weeks; **STEP-1** semaglutide 2.4 mg about 15%; about two-thirds of the loss is regained within a year of stopping. [SURMOUNT-1 2022]`]},{heading:`Assessment and choosing the drug`,points:[`Record **ASCVD history, HF symptoms (breathlessness, oedema), CKD (eGFR, UACR)**, weight, waist, BP, HbA1c and current glucose-lowering drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **genital infections, recurrent UTIs**, foot ulcers or amputation, **prior DKA**, low-carbohydrate or ketogenic diets, religious fasting and alcohol (SGLT2 inhibitor cautions). [ADA 2026]`,`Ask about **pancreatitis, gallstones, medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2 in self or family**, proliferative retinopathy and gastroparesis (GLP-1 RA cautions). [ADA 2026]`,`**Predominant HF or CKD**: start an **SGLT2 inhibitor** first; **predominant ASCVD, stroke or obesity**: a **GLP-1 RA** (or tirzepatide) first; with both, use **both** - their benefits appear additive. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**Type 1 diabetes, pregnancy, eGFR under 20 at initiation, active foot ulcer or volume depletion**: avoid SGLT2 inhibitors. [ADA 2026]`,`**Frail, underweight or sarcopenic elderly**: weight-losing GLP-1 RAs need caution; SGLT2 inhibitors may cause dehydration and falls. [ADA 2026]`,`Do not confuse with **DPP-4 inhibitors** (sitagliptin, teneligliptin): CV-neutral, weight-neutral, and saxagliptin increased HF hospitalisation (SAVOR-TIMI 53). [ADA 2026]`,`Do not use **pioglitazone** in HF (fluid retention), and remember sulfonylureas and insulin add hypoglycaemia and weight. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring`,points:[`**Baseline**: HbA1c, creatinine with eGFR, **UACR**, electrolytes, lipids, LFT, BMI and waist; ECG and NT-proBNP or echo if HF is suspected. [ADA 2026]`,`After starting an SGLT2 inhibitor, an **eGFR dip of up to about 30%** at 2-4 weeks is expected and haemodynamic - do not stop the drug; investigate a larger fall. [KDIGO 2022]`,`**Blood beta-hydroxybutyrate** (over 3 mmol/L with acidosis) confirms euglycaemic DKA; urine ketones may underestimate it and glucose may be **under 250 mg/dL**. [ADA 2026]`,`Recheck **HbA1c every 3 months** until at target, then 6-monthly; **eGFR and UACR at least yearly** (more often in CKD stage 3b or worse). [ADA 2026]`,`Before GLP-1 RA in patients with retinopathy, arrange **fundus examination** and repeat if HbA1c falls rapidly. [ADA 2026]`,`Monitor **weight, waist, BP and heart rate** each visit; check lipase only if pancreatitis is suspected clinically (routine monitoring is not useful). [ADA 2026]`,`Track **body composition and strength** where possible during large weight loss to detect lean mass loss. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`OPD management: prescribing SGLT2 inhibitors`,points:[`**Dapagliflozin 10 mg** or **empagliflozin 10 mg** once daily orally for HF and CKD; no titration needed; empagliflozin 25 mg only for extra glucose lowering. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**Canagliflozin 100 mg** daily (300 mg for glycaemia if eGFR is adequate); **ertugliflozin 5-15 mg** daily; **remogliflozin 100 mg twice daily** (Indian product, glycaemic indication). [ADA 2026]`,`Start for cardiorenal benefit at **eGFR 20 or more** and **continue until dialysis or transplant**. [KDIGO 2024]`,`On adding to insulin or a sulfonylurea when HbA1c is near target, **reduce insulin by about 10-20% or the sulfonylurea dose** to prevent hypoglycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`In HF or elderly patients on loop diuretics, consider **reducing the diuretic dose** to avoid volume depletion. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`Teach **genital hygiene** and fluid intake; most genital mycotic infections respond to topical or single-dose oral antifungals without stopping the drug. [ADA 2026]`,`Teach **sick-day rules**: stop during vomiting, diarrhoea, poor intake, fever or acute illness, and **3 days before surgery**; restart once eating and drinking normally. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`OPD management: prescribing GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide`,points:[`**Semaglutide SC (diabetes)**: **0.25 mg weekly for 4 weeks**, then 0.5 mg, then 1 mg (up to 2 mg) at 4-weekly steps. [ADA 2026]`,`**Semaglutide SC (obesity, SELECT)**: titrate 0.25, 0.5, 1, 1.7 to **2.4 mg weekly** every 4 weeks. [SELECT 2023]`,`**Oral semaglutide**: 3 mg daily for 30 days, then 7 mg, then 14 mg - taken **on waking with no more than 120 mL water, 30 minutes before food** or other drugs. [ADA 2026]`,`**Liraglutide SC**: 0.6 mg daily for 1 week, then 1.2 mg, up to **1.8 mg** (diabetes) or **3 mg** (obesity); **dulaglutide** 0.75-1.5 mg weekly (up to 4.5 mg). [ADA 2026]`,`**Tirzepatide SC**: **2.5 mg weekly for 4 weeks**, then increase by 2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 5-15 mg as tolerated. [ADA 2026]`,`**Reduce nausea**: slow titration, small low-fat meals, stop eating when full, avoid lying down after meals; delay the next step if symptoms persist. [ADA 2026]`,`Pair with **lifestyle**: calorie deficit, protein about **1-1.2 g/kg/day**, resistance exercise to preserve muscle, and 150 minutes of weekly activity. [ADA 2026]`,`Explain that therapy is **long term** - stopping usually leads to regain of most of the lost weight and loss of metabolic benefit. [STEP-1 2022]`]},{heading:`Adverse effects, complications and IPD care`,points:[`**Genital mycotic infections** (vulvovaginal candidiasis, balanitis) are 3-5 times more common on SGLT2 inhibitors, mainly in women and uncircumcised men; UTI risk rises only slightly. [ADA 2026]`,`**Euglycaemic DKA** (rare): nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, breathlessness with near-normal glucose; triggers are surgery, fasting, acute illness, insulin dose cuts, alcohol and ketogenic diets. [ADA 2026]`,`**Treating euglycaemic DKA**: admit, stop the SGLT2 inhibitor, IV fluids, **insulin infusion with IV dextrose** (because glucose is not high) and potassium replacement; do not rechallenge without specialist advice. [JBDS 2023]`,`**Fournier gangrene** (necrotising perineal fasciitis) is a rare emergency on SGLT2 inhibitors - perineal pain, swelling and fever need urgent surgical review. [FDA 2018]`,`**Volume depletion and hypotension** (elderly, diuretics); **amputation and fracture signals** were seen with canagliflozin in CANVAS but not consistently with other gliflozins. [ADA 2026]`,`**GI effects of GLP-1 RAs**: nausea in 20-40%, vomiting, diarrhoea or constipation, mostly early and dose-related; dehydration from vomiting can cause **AKI**. [ADA 2026]`,`**Gallbladder disease** (cholelithiasis with rapid weight loss), **acute pancreatitis** (avoid if prior pancreatitis), worsening of established **diabetic retinopathy** with rapid HbA1c fall, and peri-operative **aspiration** risk from delayed gastric emptying. [ADA 2026]`,`**Contraindicated** in personal or family history of **medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2** (C-cell tumours in rodents); **NAION** has been accepted by the EMA as a very rare semaglutide effect. [EMA 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: SGLT2 inhibitors are effective but watch hydration, falls and genital hygiene; avoid excessive weight loss and sarcopenia with GLP-1 RAs. [ADA 2026]`,`**CKD**: SGLT2 inhibitor from eGFR 20 continued to dialysis; GLP-1 RAs need no dose change down to eGFR 15 (avoid exenatide in severe CKD); semaglutide adds kidney protection (FLOW). [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Pregnancy and breastfeeding**: both classes are avoided; stop semaglutide at least **2 months before planned conception**; insulin is used for glycaemia. [ADA 2026]`,`**Type 1 diabetes**: SGLT2 inhibitors are not licensed for type 1 in India because of DKA risk. [ADA 2026]`,`**Religious fasting (Ramadan, Navratri, Ekadashi)**: SGLT2 inhibitors risk dehydration and ketosis - take with the evening meal, maintain fluids or pause during prolonged fasts; GLP-1 RAs are generally safe. [IDF-DAR 2021]`,`**Surgery and procedures**: stop SGLT2 inhibitors 3 days before; most patients may continue GLP-1 RAs with a 24-hour liquid diet if aspiration risk is high (see the Pre-operative cardiac evaluation topic). [Multisociety 2024]`,`**Heart failure without diabetes**: dapagliflozin or empagliflozin 10 mg works equally well - the HbA1c is irrelevant to the HF indication. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**Children and adolescents**: liraglutide and semaglutide are approved for adolescent obesity from 12 years and dapagliflozin for paediatric T2DM - specialist use. [ADA 2026]`]},{heading:`Indian availability, cost, referral and programme link`,points:[`**Dapagliflozin** generics (patent expiry 2020) and **empagliflozin** generics are widely available at low cost; fixed-dose combinations with metformin, sitagliptin or teneligliptin are common. [RSSDI 2022]`,`**Liraglutide** (including generics), **dulaglutide**, **oral semaglutide** (since 2022), **injectable semaglutide 2.4 mg** and **tirzepatide** (launched 2025) are available in India; generic semaglutide is entering the market after patent expiry. [CDSCO]`,`Beware **counterfeit and compounded** semaglutide or tirzepatide bought online - advise prescription supply from licensed pharmacies only. [CDSCO]`,`Refer to an endocrinologist, cardiologist or nephrologist for HF with recurrent admissions, eGFR under 30, rapidly falling eGFR, severe obesity considering bariatric surgery, or recurrent DKA. [ADA 2026]`,`Follow-up: HbA1c 3-monthly until stable, eGFR and UACR at least yearly, weight and BP each visit, foot and eye checks yearly. [ADA 2026]`,`**NP-NCD** screens adults 30+ for diabetes and hypertension at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs; cardiometabolic drugs are started by medical officers and higher centres and continued in the community. [MoHFW NP-NCD]`,`**Lifestyle remains the base**: Indian plate model, salt under 5 g, 150 minutes of exercise, tobacco cessation and Eat Right India messaging. [ICMR-NIN 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ADA Standards of Care 2026**: GLP-1 RA and/or SGLT2 inhibitor for T2DM with ASCVD, HF or CKD regardless of HbA1c or metformin; GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide preferred for obesity, MASLD and HFpEF with obesity. [ADA 2026]`,`**ESC 2023 diabetes-CVD**: SGLT2 inhibitor and/or GLP-1 RA in T2DM with ASCVD (class I), SGLT2 inhibitor for HF and CKD; new **SCORE2-Diabetes** to estimate 10-year risk in those without ASCVD. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`**ESC 2023 HF focused update**: dapagliflozin or empagliflozin is class I for HFmrEF and HFpEF as well as HFrEF. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**KDIGO 2024 CKD**: SGLT2 inhibitor for CKD with eGFR 20 or more and UACR 200 mg/g or more, or HF, with or without diabetes. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**SELECT (2023)** led to approval of semaglutide 2.4 mg for **cardiovascular risk reduction in obesity with CVD** without diabetes. [SELECT 2023]`,`**FLOW (2024)**, **SOUL (2025)**, **SUMMIT (2024)** and **SURPASS-CVOT (2025)** extended GLP-1 RA and tirzepatide evidence to CKD, oral semaglutide, HFpEF and head-to-head CV outcomes. [ADA 2026]`,`**EMPACT-MI and DAPA-MI (2024)**: no significant benefit of routine SGLT2 inhibitor after acute MI without another indication. [EMPACT-MI 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: 'Heart and kidney - SGLT2; arteries and weight - GLP-1' summarises the organ-specific benefits. [ADA 2026]`,`Classic: a diabetic with HFrEF on metformin and glimepiride - add **dapagliflozin 10 mg**; stop or avoid pioglitazone and saxagliptin. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`Common mistake: stopping an SGLT2 inhibitor for the **expected eGFR dip** at 2-4 weeks. [KDIGO 2022]`,`Common mistake: missing **euglycaemic DKA** because blood glucose is under 250 mg/dL - check ketones and pH. [ADA 2026]`,`Common mistake: prescribing GLP-1 RA with a family history of **medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2**. [ADA 2026]`,`Know the landmark trials: EMPA-REG, DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Preserved, DAPA-CKD, LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, SELECT, FLOW. [ESC 2023 Diabetes]`,`State that the indication is **organ protection independent of HbA1c** - a patient at HbA1c 6.8% with HF still needs an SGLT2 inhibitor. [ADA 2026]`]}],tables:[{heading:`SGLT2 inhibitors, GLP-1 RAs and tirzepatide: doses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Dapagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`No titration; long term; start at eGFR 20-25+, continue to dialysis`,`Genital mycosis, volume depletion, euglycaemic DKA; avoid in type 1 DM`],[`Empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily (25 mg for glycaemia)`,`Long term; start at eGFR 20+`,`As dapagliflozin; stop 3 days before surgery`],[`Canagliflozin`,`100 mg oral daily (300 mg if eGFR allows)`,`Long term`,`As above; amputation and fracture signal (CANVAS)`],[`Remogliflozin`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`Glycaemic use (India)`,`Genital mycosis; no CVOT data`],[`Semaglutide SC`,`0.25 mg weekly x 4 weeks, then 0.5, 1 mg (max 2 mg); obesity up to 2.4 mg`,`Step up every 4 weeks; long term`,`Nausea, vomiting, gallstones, retinopathy worsening; MTC/MEN2 contraindicated`],[`Oral semaglutide`,`3 mg daily x 30 days, then 7 mg, then 14 mg; fasting with 120 mL water`,`Monthly steps`,`As SC; absorption fails if taken with food`],[`Liraglutide SC`,`0.6 mg daily x 1 week, then 1.2, max 1.8 mg (3 mg for obesity)`,`Weekly steps`,`Nausea, pancreatitis caution, heart rate rise`],[`Dulaglutide SC`,`0.75-1.5 mg weekly (up to 4.5 mg)`,`Step up after 4 weeks`,`GI effects; MTC/MEN2 contraindicated`],[`Tirzepatide SC`,`2.5 mg weekly x 4 weeks, then +2.5 mg every 4 weeks to 5-15 mg`,`Long term`,`GI effects, gallbladder disease; MTC/MEN2 contraindicated; avoid in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`Classification and cardiovascular evidence`,columns:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Route and frequency`,`Outcome evidence`],rows:[[`SGLT2 inhibitors`,`Empagliflozin, dapagliflozin, canagliflozin`,`Oral once daily`,`HF hospitalisation, CKD progression, CV death (empagliflozin); MACE (empa, cana)`],[`SGLT2 inhibitors - neutral or limited`,`Ertugliflozin, remogliflozin`,`Oral`,`MACE non-inferior (ertugliflozin); no CVOT (remogliflozin)`],[`Long-acting GLP-1 RAs`,`Liraglutide, dulaglutide, semaglutide SC and oral`,`SC daily or weekly; oral daily`,`MACE reduction (LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, REWIND, SOUL); kidney (FLOW)`],[`Short-acting GLP-1 RAs`,`Exenatide, lixisenatide`,`SC once or twice daily`,`Neutral CVOTs (ELIXA, EXSCEL)`],[`Dual GIP/GLP-1 agonist`,`Tirzepatide`,`SC weekly`,`Non-inferior to dulaglutide for MACE; HFpEF events reduced (SUMMIT)`],[`DPP-4 inhibitors (for contrast)`,`Sitagliptin, linagliptin, teneligliptin, saxagliptin`,`Oral daily`,`CV neutral; saxagliptin increased HF hospitalisation`]]},{heading:`Landmark outcome trials`,columns:[`Trial (year)`,`Drug`,`Population`,`Main result`],rows:[[`EMPA-REG OUTCOME (2015)`,`Empagliflozin`,`T2DM with ASCVD`,`MACE -14%, CV death -38%, HF hospitalisation -35%`],[`DAPA-HF (2019)`,`Dapagliflozin`,`HFrEF with or without diabetes`,`Worsening HF or CV death -26%`],[`EMPEROR-Preserved (2021)`,`Empagliflozin`,`HF with LVEF over 40%`,`CV death or HF hospitalisation -21%`],[`DAPA-CKD (2020)`,`Dapagliflozin`,`CKD eGFR 25-75 with albuminuria`,`Kidney composite -39%; death reduced`],[`LEADER (2016)`,`Liraglutide`,`T2DM high CV risk`,`MACE -13%, CV death -22%`],[`SUSTAIN-6 (2016)`,`Semaglutide SC`,`T2DM high CV risk`,`MACE -26% (stroke); retinopathy signal`],[`SELECT (2023)`,`Semaglutide 2.4 mg`,`Obesity/overweight with CVD, no diabetes`,`MACE 6.5% vs 8.0% (HR 0.80)`],[`FLOW (2024)`,`Semaglutide 1 mg`,`T2DM with CKD`,`Major kidney events -24%`],[`SUMMIT (2024)`,`Tirzepatide`,`HFpEF with obesity`,`CV death or worsening HF reduced (HR about 0.62)`]]},{heading:`Choosing the drug by dominant comorbidity`,columns:[`Comorbidity`,`First choice`,`Add or alternative`,`Notes`],rows:[[`Heart failure (any LVEF)`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`GLP-1 RA or tirzepatide if HFpEF with obesity`,`Avoid pioglitazone and saxagliptin`],[`CKD (eGFR 20+, albuminuria)`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Semaglutide (FLOW); ACEi/ARB; finerenone`,`Expect eGFR dip; continue to dialysis`],[`Established ASCVD`,`GLP-1 RA or SGLT2 inhibitor with proven benefit`,`Combine both if HbA1c or risk persists`,`Stroke history favours GLP-1 RA`],[`Obesity`,`Semaglutide 2.4 mg or tirzepatide`,`SGLT2 inhibitor (modest weight loss)`,`SELECT for obesity with CVD`],[`High risk without ASCVD`,`Either class (SCORE2-Diabetes 10%+)`,`Metformin, statin, BP control`,`Cost often decides in India`],[`Frail elderly, low BMI`,`SGLT2 inhibitor with hydration advice`,`Avoid aggressive weight loss`,`Watch falls, hypotension`]]},{heading:`Adverse effects and their management`,columns:[`Adverse effect`,`Drug`,`Prevention and management`],rows:[[`Genital mycotic infection`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Hygiene; topical or single-dose fluconazole; usually continue`],[`Euglycaemic DKA`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Sick-day rules, stop 3 days pre-op; treat with fluids, insulin plus dextrose, potassium`],[`Volume depletion, AKI`,`SGLT2 inhibitor; GLP-1 RA with vomiting`,`Reduce diuretic; hold during acute illness`],[`Fournier gangrene`,`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Urgent surgical debridement and antibiotics`],[`Nausea, vomiting`,`GLP-1 RA, tirzepatide`,`Slow titration, small meals; hold step if persistent`],[`Gallstones, pancreatitis`,`GLP-1 RA, tirzepatide`,`Avoid if prior pancreatitis; stop if pancreatitis confirmed`],[`Retinopathy worsening`,`Semaglutide (rapid HbA1c fall)`,`Baseline fundus; gradual HbA1c reduction`],[`Medullary thyroid carcinoma risk`,`GLP-1 RA, tirzepatide`,`Contraindicated with personal or family MTC or MEN2`]]},{heading:`Monitoring thresholds`,columns:[`Parameter`,`When`,`Action threshold`],rows:[[`eGFR`,`Baseline, 2-4 weeks after SGLT2i start, then at least yearly`,`Dip up to about 30% expected; start only if 20 or more`],[`UACR`,`Baseline and yearly`,`200 mg/g or more strengthens SGLT2i indication (KDIGO)`],[`Blood ketones`,`Any illness on SGLT2i`,`Over 3 mmol/L with acidosis = DKA even with normal glucose`],[`HbA1c`,`3-monthly until target, then 6-monthly`,`Individualised target, usually under 7%`],[`Fundus`,`Before GLP-1 RA if retinopathy; yearly`,`Proliferative retinopathy: slower HbA1c reduction`],[`Weight and waist`,`Each visit`,`5% or more loss at 3 months on full dose suggests response`]]}],redFlags:[`Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain or breathlessness on an SGLT2 inhibitor, even with normal glucose - check ketones and pH for euglycaemic DKA`,`Perineal pain, swelling and fever on an SGLT2 inhibitor - suspect Fournier gangrene, urgent surgical referral`,`Severe persistent epigastric pain radiating to the back on a GLP-1 RA - suspect pancreatitis, stop the drug`,`Persistent vomiting with oliguria on a GLP-1 RA - dehydration and AKI`,`Sudden painless loss of vision on semaglutide - urgent eye review (NAION)`,`Right upper quadrant pain and fever with rapid weight loss - acute cholecystitis`,`eGFR fall of more than 30% after starting an SGLT2 inhibitor - look for volume depletion or renal artery stenosis`],pearls:[`Heart and kidney - SGLT2 inhibitor; arteries, stroke and weight - GLP-1 RA.`,`Organ protection is independent of HbA1c and metformin - indication follows the comorbidity, not the sugar.`,`Dapagliflozin and empagliflozin: one dose (10 mg), no titration, start at eGFR 20, continue to dialysis.`,`The early eGFR dip is a sign the drug is working on the glomerulus - do not stop it.`,`Euglycaemic DKA: think of it whenever a patient on a gliflozin is unwell, whatever the glucose.`,`SELECT: semaglutide 2.4 mg cut MACE by 20% in obesity with CVD without diabetes.`,`GLP-1 RA contraindication to remember: medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2.`,`Saxagliptin and pioglitazone worsen heart failure; SGLT2 inhibitors treat it.`,`Oral semaglutide works only on an empty stomach with a sip of water, 30 minutes before food.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old man with type 2 diabetes (HbA1c 7.4%) on metformin has heart failure with LVEF 32% and eGFR 38 mL/min/1.73 m2. Which addition most reduces his risk of heart failure hospitalisation?`,options:[`Pioglitazone 15 mg daily`,`Saxagliptin 5 mg daily`,`Dapagliflozin 10 mg daily`,`Glimepiride 1 mg daily`],answer:2,explanation:`Dapagliflozin reduced worsening HF or CV death by 26% in DAPA-HF, with benefit independent of diabetes and preserved down to eGFR 20-25. Pioglitazone causes fluid retention and worsens HF, saxagliptin increased HF hospitalisation in SAVOR-TIMI 53, and glimepiride has no HF benefit and adds hypoglycaemia.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-q2`,stem:`A 55-year-old on empagliflozin is admitted two days after a laparoscopic cholecystectomy with vomiting and breathlessness. Glucose is 182 mg/dL, pH 7.14, bicarbonate 10 mmol/L and blood beta-hydroxybutyrate 5.2 mmol/L. What is the best management?`,options:[`Euglycaemic DKA: stop empagliflozin, IV fluids, insulin infusion with IV dextrose and potassium`,`Lactic acidosis: stop metformin and give IV bicarbonate`,`Post-operative ileus: nil by mouth and nasogastric drainage only`,`Hyperosmolar state: IV fluids alone without insulin`],answer:0,explanation:`Acidosis with high ketones and glucose under 250 mg/dL on an SGLT2 inhibitor after surgery is euglycaemic DKA; it needs insulin to switch off ketogenesis, with dextrose because glucose is not high, plus fluids and potassium. The high beta-hydroxybutyrate points away from lactic acidosis, ileus does not cause ketoacidosis, and hyperosmolar state needs very high glucose.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-q3`,stem:`Which statement about the SELECT trial is correct?`,options:[`It tested tirzepatide in people with type 2 diabetes and CKD`,`It showed reduced heart failure hospitalisation with empagliflozin in HFpEF`,`It showed oral semaglutide reduced kidney failure in diabetic kidney disease`,`It showed semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly reduced MACE by 20% in overweight or obese people with CVD but no diabetes`],answer:3,explanation:`SELECT randomised 17,604 people with BMI 27 or more and established CVD but without diabetes to semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly, reducing MACE from 8.0% to 6.5% (HR 0.80). Tirzepatide in CKD is not SELECT, empagliflozin in HFpEF is EMPEROR-Preserved, and the kidney outcome trial of semaglutide was FLOW using the injectable 1 mg dose.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes is being considered for weekly semaglutide. Which finding is an absolute contraindication?`,options:[`eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73 m2`,`Brother treated for medullary thyroid carcinoma as part of MEN2A`,`Past history of genital candidiasis`,`Controlled hypertension on telmisartan`],answer:1,explanation:`GLP-1 receptor agonists and tirzepatide are contraindicated with a personal or family history of medullary thyroid carcinoma or MEN2, because of C-cell tumours in rodents. Moderate CKD needs no dose change for semaglutide, genital candidiasis is a caution for SGLT2 inhibitors rather than GLP-1 RAs, and controlled hypertension is not a contraindication.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-q5`,stem:`A 60-year-old with diabetic kidney disease (eGFR 48, UACR 650 mg/g) starts dapagliflozin. At 3 weeks his eGFR is 41 and he feels well with normal BP and potassium. What should you do?`,options:[`Stop dapagliflozin permanently because of nephrotoxicity`,`Halve the dose to 5 mg`,`Continue dapagliflozin; this initial dip is expected and haemodynamic`,`Add a loop diuretic to improve renal perfusion`],answer:2,explanation:`An early eGFR fall of up to about 30% reflects reduced intraglomerular pressure from restored tubuloglomerular feedback - the same mechanism that slows CKD progression in DAPA-CKD - so the drug is continued. Stopping or halving the dose discards proven renal protection, and a loop diuretic would worsen volume depletion rather than renal perfusion.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the role of SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists in cardiovascular and renal protection: mechanisms, evidence from outcome trials, indications, doses and adverse effects.`,openingLines:[`**SGLT2 inhibitors** (gliflozins) block proximal tubular glucose and sodium reabsorption, and **GLP-1 receptor agonists** mimic the incretin GLP-1; both were developed as glucose-lowering drugs but are now prescribed for **organ protection independent of HbA1c**.`,`SGLT2 inhibitors reduce **heart failure and CKD progression** (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR, DAPA-CKD), while GLP-1 RAs reduce **atherosclerotic events and weight** (LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, SELECT) - ADA 2026 and ESC 2023 recommend them in T2DM with ASCVD, HF or CKD.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanisms`,points:[`SGLT2i: glycosuria 60-80 g/day, natriuresis, restored tubuloglomerular feedback lowering intraglomerular pressure, reduced preload, ketone shift, haematocrit rise.`,`GLP-1 RA: glucose-dependent insulin release, glucagon suppression, slowed gastric emptying, central satiety; anti-atherosclerotic, weight and BP reduction.`]},{heading:`Outcome evidence`,points:[`SGLT2i: EMPA-REG (CV death -38%), DAPA-HF and EMPEROR-Reduced (HF -25%), EMPEROR-Preserved and DELIVER (HFpEF), CREDENCE, DAPA-CKD, EMPA-KIDNEY (kidney -28 to -39%).`,`GLP-1 RA: LEADER, SUSTAIN-6, REWIND, SOUL (MACE -12 to -26%), FLOW (kidney -24%), SELECT (obesity with CVD, MACE -20%); tirzepatide SUMMIT and SURPASS-CVOT.`]},{heading:`Indications (ADA 2026, ESC 2023, KDIGO 2024)`,points:[`T2DM with ASCVD: GLP-1 RA and/or SGLT2i; HF any LVEF: SGLT2i; CKD eGFR 20+: SGLT2i, plus semaglutide in T2DM.`,`Obesity with CVD: semaglutide 2.4 mg; HFpEF with obesity: semaglutide or tirzepatide.`]},{heading:`Doses`,points:[`Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin 10 mg OD; start at eGFR 20, continue to dialysis.`,`Semaglutide 0.25 mg weekly, step every 4 weeks to 1-2 mg (2.4 mg obesity); oral 3-7-14 mg; liraglutide 0.6-1.8 mg daily; dulaglutide 0.75-1.5 mg weekly; tirzepatide 2.5-15 mg weekly.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and precautions`,points:[`SGLT2i: genital mycosis, volume depletion, euglycaemic DKA (sick-day rules, stop 3 days pre-op), Fournier gangrene; expected eGFR dip.`,`GLP-1 RA: nausea, vomiting, gallstones, pancreatitis, retinopathy worsening, aspiration risk; contraindicated with MTC/MEN2; avoid in pregnancy.`]},{heading:`Indian perspective`,points:[`ICMR-INDIAB: 101 million diabetics; low-cost generic gliflozins make SGLT2i feasible in primary care.`,`GLP-1 RA and tirzepatide costlier; generic semaglutide and liraglutide lowering cost; follow-up through NP-NCD.`]}],mustDraw:[`Nephron diagram showing SGLT2 in the proximal tubule and tubuloglomerular feedback at the macula densa`,`Table matching comorbidity (HF, CKD, ASCVD, obesity) to the preferred drug class`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanisms`,marks:2},{part:`Outcome trials`,marks:3},{part:`Indications and doses`,marks:3},{part:`Adverse effects and Indian context`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`GLP-1 RA`,`tirzepatide`,`SELECT`,`DAPA-HF`,`DAPA-CKD`,`euglycaemic DKA`,`ADA 2026`]},{id:`cardiovascular-cardiometabolic-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate SGLT2 inhibitors from GLP-1 receptor agonists.`,openingLines:[`Both classes protect the heart and kidney beyond glucose lowering, but **SGLT2 inhibitors** act on the kidney and chiefly prevent **heart failure and CKD progression**, whereas **GLP-1 RAs** act on the pancreas, gut and brain and chiefly prevent **atherosclerotic events and obesity**.`,`They are complementary and can be combined in patients with several cardiometabolic conditions.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and route`,points:[`SGLT2i: oral, block proximal tubular SGLT2 - glycosuria, natriuresis.`,`GLP-1 RA: SC daily or weekly (oral semaglutide daily), incretin receptor agonism - insulin release, satiety.`]},{heading:`Glycaemic and weight effects`,points:[`SGLT2i: HbA1c -0.5 to -0.8%, weight -2 to -3 kg, efficacy falls with eGFR.`,`GLP-1 RA: HbA1c -1 to -1.8%, weight -4 to -15% depending on dose; tirzepatide up to about 20%.`]},{heading:`Organ benefits`,points:[`SGLT2i: HF hospitalisation, CKD progression, CV death; also in non-diabetics.`,`GLP-1 RA: MACE (especially stroke), kidney (semaglutide FLOW), MACE in obesity without diabetes (SELECT).`]},{heading:`Adverse effects and contraindications`,points:[`SGLT2i: genital infection, euglycaemic DKA, volume depletion, Fournier gangrene; avoid in type 1 DM.`,`GLP-1 RA: GI upset, gallstones, pancreatitis, retinopathy worsening; contraindicated in MTC/MEN2.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: SGLT2 inhibitor versus GLP-1 RA`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and route`,marks:1},{part:`Glycaemic and weight effects`,marks:1},{part:`Organ benefits with trials`,marks:2},{part:`Adverse effects`,marks:1}],keywords:[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`GLP-1 RA`,`MACE`,`heart failure`,`CKD`,`MTC`]}],references:[`American Diabetes Association. Standards of Care in Diabetes - 2026, Diabetes Care 2026 (Section 9: Pharmacologic approaches; Section 10: Cardiovascular disease and risk management)`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease in patients with diabetes, European Heart Journal 2023`,`2023 Focused Update of the 2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure`,`KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease; KDIGO 2022 Diabetes Management in CKD`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Diabetes mellitus: management and therapies`,`Lincoff AM et al. Semaglutide and cardiovascular outcomes in obesity without diabetes (SELECT), N Engl J Med 2023`,`RSSDI Clinical Practice Recommendations for the management of type 2 diabetes mellitus 2022, Int J Diabetes Dev Ctries`,`Anjana RM et al. Metabolic non-communicable disease health report of India: the ICMR-INDIAB national cross-sectional study, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinol 2023`]});var i=[];i.push({id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock`,title:`Acute decompensated heart failure and cardiogenic shock`,oneLiner:`**Decompensated heart failure** (the ESC 2026 term that replaces acute HF) is new or worsening congestion or low output needing **urgent IV therapy**, profiled at the bedside as **warm or cold and wet or dry**; congestion is cleared with an **IV loop diuretic at 1-2.5 times the oral dose, checked by 2-hour urine sodium and 6-hour urine output**, and **cardiogenic shock** - systolic BP under 90 mmHg with hypoperfusion - is staged by **SCAI A-E** and treated with noradrenaline, dobutamine, culprit-only revascularisation and selective mechanical support, with GDMT started before discharge and **rapid up-titration over 6 weeks (STRONG-HF)**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`ADHF`,`acute heart failure`,`decompensated heart failure`,`DHF`,`acute pulmonary oedema`,`flash pulmonary oedema`,`cardiogenic shock`,`SCAI shock stages`,`Stevenson profiles warm wet cold dry`,`Forrester subsets`,`IV furosemide`,`acetazolamide ADVOR`,`hydrochlorothiazide CLOROTIC`,`natriuresis-guided diuresis`,`dobutamine levosimendan milrinone`,`noradrenaline vasopressor`,`IABP Impella VA-ECMO`,`CPAP BiPAP NIV`,`STRONG-HF`,`EMPULSE`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Decompensated heart failure (DHF)** is new or worsening HF symptoms and signs - mostly congestion - severe enough to need **urgent IV therapy or admission**; ESC 2026 replaces the term **acute heart failure** because most patients deteriorate over days, not minutes. [ESC 2026]`,`It is either **de novo** (first presentation, often from ACS, myocarditis or an acute valve lesion) or, far more often, **acutely decompensated chronic HF (ADHF)**, which makes up about **50-70%** of presentations. [ESC 2021]`,`ESC describes four presentations - **ADHF, acute pulmonary oedema, isolated right ventricular failure and cardiogenic shock** - which differ in mechanism and in the first drug (table below). [ESC 2021]`,`**Cardiogenic shock (CS)** is **systolic BP under 90 mmHg for 30 minutes or more, or vasopressors needed to keep it there, with hypoperfusion** (cold clammy skin, urine under 30 mL/h, confusion, **lactate over 2 mmol/L**) from primary cardiac failure; invasively, **cardiac index under 2.2 L/min/m2 with PCWP over 15 mmHg**. [Harrison 22e]`,`CS complicates about **5-10% of acute MI**, but a growing share now arises from end-stage cardiomyopathy, myocarditis and valve disease; in-hospital mortality remains **30-50%**. [Harrison 22e]`,`An HF admission is a turning point: in-hospital mortality is about **4-10%**, and the **vulnerable phase** of the first 3 months after discharge carries the highest risk of death and readmission. [ESC 2021]`,`**India**: the ICMR **National Heart Failure Registry** (53 hospitals, about 10,850 admitted patients) found a mean age of about **60 years** - roughly a decade younger than Western cohorts - and **about 1 in 4 died within a year**, with mortality about twice as high in rheumatic as in ischaemic HF. [NHFR 2025]`,`**Ischaemic heart disease** is the commonest cause of HF admission in India (about 70% in the NHFR), followed by dilated cardiomyopathy and **rheumatic heart disease**, which still dominates in young adults and women. [NHFR 2025]`,`The earlier **Trivandrum Heart Failure Registry** recorded in-hospital mortality of about **8%** and poor prescription of GDMT at discharge - under-treatment, not drug unavailability, is the main Indian gap. [THFR 2015]`]},{heading:`Precipitants and causes`,points:[`**CHAMPIT** is the ESC list of precipitants that need specific treatment within the first hour: acute **C**oronary syndrome, **H**ypertensive emergency, **A**rrhythmia, **M**echanical cause, **P**ulmonary embolism, **I**nfection and **T**amponade. [ESC 2021]`,`The commonest avoidable trigger is **non-adherence** - missed diuretics (travel, cost, urinary frequency), a salt load (pickle, papad, namkeen, festive meals) or excess fluid. [ESC 2021]`,`**Drugs that precipitate HF**: NSAIDs and COX-2 inhibitors, corticosteroids, **pioglitazone**, saxagliptin, **verapamil and diltiazem** in HFrEF, class I antiarrhythmics, dronedarone, anthracyclines and trastuzumab, and excess alcohol. [ESC 2021]`,`**Atrial fibrillation with a fast ventricular rate** removes the atrial kick and shortens diastolic filling - the classic decompensator of mitral stenosis and HFpEF. [Harrison 22e]`,`Systemic triggers: pneumonia and other infections, **anaemia**, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, acute kidney injury, uncontrolled diabetes and surgery. [ESC 2021]`,`Indian causes to remember: progression of **rheumatic MS or MR** or acute rheumatic carditis, severe nutritional anaemia, **wet beriberi** (thiamine deficiency, high output), **peripartum cardiomyopathy** and **scorpion-sting myocarditis**. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**Causes of cardiogenic shock**: LV pump failure after large MI (commonest), mechanical complications (septal rupture, papillary muscle rupture, free-wall rupture), RV infarction, fulminant myocarditis, end-stage DCM, Takotsubo, critical AS or MS, acute severe MR or AR (endocarditis, chordal rupture) and beta-blocker or CCB overdose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Obstructive shock** (tamponade, massive PE, tension pneumothorax) looks like cold cardiogenic shock but needs drainage, reperfusion or decompression - exclude it with bedside echo in the first minutes. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: congestion, diuretic resistance and the shock spiral`,points:[`Raised LV filling pressure is transmitted to the pulmonary capillaries: **PCWP over 18 mmHg** causes interstitial oedema (Kerley B lines) and **over 25 mmHg** alveolar flooding; in chronic HF expanded lymphatic drainage means **crackles may be absent** despite high pressures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fluid accumulation** (days to weeks of renal sodium retention, weight gain, oedema) needs a **diuretic**, whereas **fluid redistribution** (sympathetic venoconstriction shifts splanchnic blood into the lungs with a sudden afterload rise, little weight gain) causes flash pulmonary oedema and needs a **vasodilator**. [ESC 2021]`,`Low output activates the **sympathetic system, RAAS and vasopressin**, which constrict arterioles, retain sodium and water and cause dilutional hyponatraemia; natriuretic peptides oppose them, but the response is blunted in HF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiorenal syndrome** in DHF is driven more by **venous congestion** (high renal venous and intra-abdominal pressure) than by low output, so a modest creatinine rise during effective decongestion is not harmful and is not a reason to stop diuretics. [HFA 2019]`,`**Diuretic resistance**: loop diuretics must be secreted into the tubular lumen (less in CKD and hypoalbuminaemia), have a steep threshold dose-response and a short half-life (post-diuretic sodium retention), and the distal nephron hypertrophies with chronic use - hence higher, repeated doses and **sequential nephron blockade**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gut oedema** makes oral furosemide absorption erratic (bioavailability about 50%, range 10-90%), so admitted patients get the **IV route**; torsemide and bumetanide are absorbed more predictably. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Shock spiral**: a large loss of contracting myocardium lowers stroke volume and BP, which reduces coronary perfusion, worsens ischaemia and further depresses the pump; compensatory vasoconstriction (cold, high SVR) raises afterload, and systemic inflammation causes inappropriate vasodilation in a minority (mixed shock). [Harrison 22e]`,`**RV-predominant shock** shows a high right atrial pressure with a relatively low PCWP - the patient is preload dependent and is harmed by nitrates and diuretics. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac power output** (MAP x cardiac output / 451) under **0.6 W** is the strongest haemodynamic predictor of death in cardiogenic shock. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification: clinical profiles, presentations and SCAI stages`,points:[`**Stevenson clinical profiles**: congestion (wet - orthopnoea, raised JVP, oedema, S3, crackles, hepatomegaly) against perfusion (cold - cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure, oliguria, confusion, symptomatic hypotension) give four boxes that decide the first drug. [ESC 2021]`,`**Warm-wet** is the commonest profile (diuretic with or without vasodilator); **cold-wet** has the worst prognosis (inotrope or vasopressor, then diuretic); **cold-dry** is uncommon (cautious fluid, then inotrope); **warm-dry** is compensated (optimise oral therapy). [ESC 2021]`,`**Forrester haemodynamic subsets** are the invasive counterpart, using **cardiac index 2.2 L/min/m2 and PCWP 18 mmHg** as cut-offs (subset I normal, II congested, III hypoperfused, IV both). [Braunwald 12e]`,`The four ESC presentations differ in mechanism: **ADHF** (gradual accumulation - diuretic), **acute pulmonary oedema** (redistribution and afterload - vasodilator, NIV, diuretic), **isolated RV failure** (RV infarct, PE, pulmonary hypertension - treat cause, avoid overload) and **cardiogenic shock** (inotrope, vasopressor, MCS). [ESC 2021]`,`**Killip class** (I no HF, II crackles or S3, III pulmonary oedema, IV shock) grades HF in acute MI and predicts 30-day mortality - details in the Complications of myocardial infarction topic. [ESC 2023]`,`**SCAI SHOCK stages**: **A** at risk, **B** beginning (relative hypotension or tachycardia without hypoperfusion), **C** classic (hypoperfusion needing a drug or device beyond fluid), **D** deteriorating (failing initial support), **E** extremis (circulatory collapse, CPR or ECMO). [SCAI 2022]`,`The 2022 SCAI update applies staging to **all causes of CS**, re-stages the patient repeatedly (trajectory matters) and adds a modifier **A** for cardiac arrest with risk of brain injury. [SCAI 2022]`,`Name the **phenotype of shock** - LV-dominant, RV-dominant or biventricular - because it decides which mechanical device helps. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Label the LVEF phenotype by ESC 2026: **HFrEF if LVEF under 50%, HFpEF if 50% or more** (HFmrEF abolished) - it decides the discharge regimen. [ESC 2026]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history and examination`,points:[`Breathlessness progresses from exertion to **orthopnoea** (count the pillows) and **paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea** - the most specific symptoms - with nocturnal cough and wheeze (**cardiac asthma**). [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask for **weight gain of 2 kg or more in 3 days**, rising oedema, abdominal fullness, right upper quadrant pain, anorexia and early satiety (gut and hepatic congestion), and reduced urine; fatigue, cold limbs and confusion suggest **low output**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Acute pulmonary oedema** presents with sudden severe dyspnoea, the patient sitting bolt upright, sweating and anxious, with **pink frothy sputum**, often with very high BP. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **elderly** present atypically - delirium, falls, anorexia, insomnia or reduced mobility - and are often misdiagnosed as bronchitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask for the precipitant (**CHAMPIT**), chest pain, palpitations, fever, salt and drug history (NSAIDs, pioglitazone, missed diuretics), alcohol, and pregnancy or recent delivery. [ESC 2021]`,`**Raised JVP with a positive abdominojugular test** (sustained rise during 10-15 seconds of firm abdominal pressure) is the most reliable bedside sign of raised filling pressure; add **S3 gallop**, bibasal crackles, pleural effusion, tender hepatomegaly, ascites and sacral oedema in the bed-bound. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypoperfusion signs**: cool peripheries, capillary refill over 3 seconds, mottling over the knees, **proportional pulse pressure under 25%** ((SBP - DBP)/SBP, predicts cardiac index under 2.2), **pulsus alternans**, Cheyne-Stokes breathing, oliguria and altered sensorium. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vital-sign triggers for HDU or ICU**: respiratory rate over 25/min, SpO2 under 90% on oxygen, SBP under 90 mmHg, heart rate under 40 or over 130/min, accessory muscle use or signs of hypoperfusion. [ESC 2021]`,`Examine for the cause: a **new pansystolic murmur with thrill** (ventricular septal rupture) or without (papillary muscle rupture), ejection systolic murmur of **AS**, mid-diastolic rumble of **MS** in AF, pericardial rub, and **pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg** (tamponade). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raised JVP, clear lungs and hypotension** point to RV infarction, massive PE or tamponade - not to LV failure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**COPD or asthma exacerbation**: smoking history, hyperinflation, prolonged expiration, normal JVP and **NT-proBNP under 300 pg/mL**; the two often coexist, and diffuse B-lines on lung ultrasound favour HF. [ESC 2021]`,`**Pneumonia**: fever, focal consolidation and raised procalcitonin; it is also a common precipitant, so both may need treating. [ESC 2021]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden dyspnoea, clear lungs, hypoxia out of proportion, RV dilatation and DVT signs; confirm with CT pulmonary angiography. [ESC 2019]`,`**Non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (ARDS)**: sepsis, pancreatitis or aspiration, normal heart size, no effusions, peripheral air bronchograms and **PCWP under 18 mmHg**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal volume overload** (AKI, nephrotic syndrome, missed dialysis): heavy proteinuria, oliguria, hypoalbuminaemia - dialysis or ultrafiltration, not inotropes, is the answer. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other shock states**: septic shock has warm peripheries early, a wide pulse pressure and fever; hypovolaemic shock has a **flat JVP**; obstructive shock is diagnosed by bedside echo (effusion, dilated RV, absent lung sliding). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scorpion sting** (Mesobuthus tamulus) in rural India causes an autonomic storm with hypertension, pulmonary oedema and myocarditis - early **prazosin** is the specific treatment, with dobutamine and NIV for shock. [API Textbook 12e]`,`**High-altitude pulmonary oedema** (Ladakh, Sikkim, pilgrim routes) occurs in a structurally normal heart - descent, oxygen and nifedipine, not diuretics. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG within 10 minutes** for STEMI or ischaemia, AF or flutter, VT, heart block, LVH and low voltage (effusion). [ESC 2023]`,`**Natriuretic peptides** rule out acute HF when **BNP is under 100 pg/mL, NT-proBNP under 300 pg/mL or MR-proANP under 120 pmol/L**; age-specific NT-proBNP rule-in values are **450 (under 50 years), 900 (50-75) and 1800 pg/mL (over 75)**. [ESC 2021]`,`Obesity lowers natriuretic peptides; AF, age, CKD and sepsis raise them; **sacubitril raises BNP but not NT-proBNP**, so use NT-proBNP in patients on an ARNI. [ESC 2021]`,`**Chest X-ray**: cardiomegaly, upper-lobe diversion, Kerley B lines, perihilar bat-wing shadowing and effusions - but it is normal in up to **1 in 5** patients with DHF. [ESC 2021]`,`**Lung ultrasound**: three or more B-lines per intercostal space in two or more zones on each side supports pulmonary oedema; an **IVC over 2.1 cm collapsing less than 50%** suggests a right atrial pressure around 15 mmHg - invaluable where BNP is unavailable. [ESC 2021]`,`**Echocardiography immediately** in haemodynamic instability or shock, and **within 48 hours** in de novo HF or unknown cardiac function: LVEF, valves, RV, mechanical complications, tamponade and filling pressures. [ESC 2021]`,`Bloods: troponin, creatinine and urea, sodium, potassium, magnesium, haemoglobin, glucose, liver tests (congestive hepatopathy), TSH and **iron studies**; add procalcitonin if infection and D-dimer if PE is suspected. [ESC 2021]`,`**Arterial or venous blood gas and lactate**: pH and PaCO2 guide ventilation, and **lactate over 2 mmol/L** marks hypoperfusion; repeat lactate to judge the response in shock. [SCAI 2022]`,`**Natriuretic response** after the first loop dose: **spot urine sodium under 50-70 mmol/L at 2 hours or urine output under 100-150 mL/h over the first 6 hours** means an insufficient response. [ESC 2021]`,`A **pulmonary artery catheter** is not routine (ESCAPE) but may be considered in refractory shock or unclear volume status (cardiac index, PCWP, cardiac power output); **coronary angiography is immediate** when ACS causes shock or instability. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`The first hour: OPD, PHC and emergency room`,points:[`Sit the patient upright with legs dependent, attach monitor and pulse oximeter, secure IV access and record an ECG; give **oxygen only if SpO2 is under 90% or PaO2 under 60 mmHg** - hyperoxia causes vasoconstriction. [ESC 2021]`,`Give **IV furosemide 20-40 mg** if diuretic-naive, or **1-2 times the usual total daily oral dose IV** if already on a loop diuretic, without waiting for investigations. [ESC 2021]`,`If SBP is over 110 mmHg, give **sublingual nitroglycerin 0.4 mg every 5 minutes** (or isosorbide dinitrate 5 mg) while arranging an IV nitrate or transfer. [ESC 2021]`,`Start **CPAP (5-10 cmH2O) or BiPAP** early when respiratory rate is over 25/min and SpO2 under 90% - it reduces work of breathing and intubation; watch for hypotension. [ESC 2021]`,`**Morphine is not routine** (Class III): it is linked to more ventilation, ICU stay and death; reserve it for severe pain or anxiety. [ESC 2021]`,`Treat the **CHAMPIT** cause in parallel: reperfusion for STEMI, IV nitrate to lower BP about 25% in the first hours of hypertensive pulmonary oedema, synchronised cardioversion for unstable tachyarrhythmia, echo and surgery for a mechanical cause, reperfusion for high-risk PE, drainage for tamponade. [ESC 2021]`,`**Do not stop GDMT routinely**: continue ARNI or ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor unless there is shock, symptomatic hypotension or bradycardia, potassium over 5.5 mmol/L or severe AKI; halve rather than stop the beta-blocker in low output. [ESC 2021]`,`Stop the culprits - **NSAIDs, pioglitazone, verapamil or diltiazem** and unnecessary IV fluids - and treat fever, anaemia and infection. [ESC 2021]`,`**Mild worsening in the OPD** (weight up 2 kg, one NYHA class worse, stable vitals): double the oral loop diuretic for 3-5 days, consider metolazone 2.5 mg for 1-3 doses, check creatinine and potassium in 3-5 days and review within a week; ESC 2026 accepts ambulatory or day-care IV diuretic care for such patients. [ESC 2026]`]},{heading:`Diuretic strategy and decongestion`,points:[`**IV loop diuretic is Class I** for every patient admitted with congestion, given as boluses two to three times a day or as an infusion - the DOSE trial found bolus and infusion equal, and **high dose (2.5 times oral)** gave more decongestion with only a transient creatinine rise. [ESC 2021]`,`Check the response at **2 hours (urine sodium 50-70 mmol/L or more)** and **6 hours (urine output 100-150 mL/h or more)**; if insufficient, **double the IV dose**, up to **400-600 mg furosemide a day** (up to 1000 mg in severe renal impairment). [ESC 2021]`,`Aim for a **net negative balance of about 3-5 L/day** until euvolaemic: weigh daily at the same time, chart intake and output, check creatinine, sodium and potassium daily, and keep **potassium 4.0-5.0 mmol/L** with magnesium replaced. [ESC 2021]`,`**ADVOR (2022)**: **acetazolamide 500 mg IV once daily for 3 days** added to loop diuretic raised successful decongestion at day 3 (**42% vs 31%**) and shortened stay, without changing death or readmission; patients on SGLT2 inhibitors and with eGFR under 20 were excluded. [ADVOR 2022]`,`**CLOROTIC (2023)**: oral **hydrochlorothiazide 25 mg (eGFR over 50), 50 mg (20-50) or 100 mg (under 20)** added to furosemide gave more weight loss at 72 hours but more hypokalaemia and creatinine rise, with no outcome benefit; metolazone 2.5-5 mg is the usual Indian alternative. [CLOROTIC 2023]`,`**Urinary sodium-guided diuretic therapy** may be considered in the first days of DHF (ESC 2026); PUSH-AHF showed more natriuresis with a natriuresis-guided protocol but no reduction in death or readmission. [ESC 2026]`,`**Worsening renal function**: a creatinine rise of about 0.3-0.5 mg/dL while congestion is improving is acceptable - keep decongesting; reduce diuretics only for hypotension, hypoperfusion or overdiuresis, and suspect low output (inotrope) if creatinine rises while the patient is still wet. [HFA 2019]`,`**Hyponatraemia** is usually dilutional: restrict free water, maximise decongestion, and consider **tolvaptan 15 mg daily** for symptomatic severe hyponatraemia (EVEREST improved sodium and symptoms, not survival); correct no faster than 8-10 mmol/L in 24 hours. [ESC 2021]`,`**Ultrafiltration** is not superior to stepped diuretic therapy (CARRESS-HF) and is reserved for refractory congestion (IIb); dialysis is needed for oliguria unresponsive to fluid strategies, **potassium over 6.5 mmol/L, pH under 7.2** or very high urea. [ESC 2021]`,`Infuse high-dose IV furosemide **no faster than 4 mg/min** to avoid ototoxicity, especially with aminoglycosides or renal failure. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Vasodilators, inotropes, vasopressors and ventilation`,points:[`**IV nitrates** lower preload and afterload and suit hypertensive acute pulmonary oedema with **SBP over 110 mmHg** (IIb): nitroglycerin **10-20 mcg/min, up to 200 mcg/min**, or isosorbide dinitrate 1-10 mg/h; GALACTIC (2019) found that routine early intensive vasodilation did not reduce death or readmission. [ESC 2021]`,`**Sodium nitroprusside 0.3-5 mcg/kg/min** with an arterial line suits severe hypertension and acute MR or AR; limit it to the shortest time because of **cyanide and thiocyanate toxicity** in renal or hepatic failure. [ESC 2021]`,`**Avoid nitrates** with SBP under 90 mmHg, severe AS or obstructive HCM (preload-dependent), RV infarction, and within **24 hours of sildenafil or 48 hours of tadalafil**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Inotropes are for hypotension with hypoperfusion only** (IIb) and are **not recommended routinely** (III) because they raise myocardial oxygen demand, arrhythmia and mortality. [ESC 2021]`,`**Dobutamine 2-20 mcg/kg/min** (beta-1 agonist, no bolus) is the usual first inotrope; tolerance develops after 24-72 hours and its effect is blunted in patients on beta-blockers. [ESC 2021]`,`**Milrinone** (PDE-3 inhibitor, 0.375-0.75 mcg/kg/min, renally cleared) and **levosimendan** (calcium sensitiser, 0.05-0.2 mcg/kg/min for 24 hours, effect lasts about a week) work despite beta-blockade but cause hypotension; DOREMI (2021) found milrinone no better than dobutamine. [ESC 2021]`,`**Noradrenaline 0.2-1.0 mcg/kg/min** is the preferred vasopressor to keep MAP at 65 mmHg or more (IIb); **dopamine** caused more arrhythmias and higher mortality in the cardiogenic subgroup of SOAP II, and **adrenaline** was linked to more refractory shock (OptimaCC) - rescue only. [ESC 2021]`,`**Intubation** is indicated for respiratory failure persisting despite oxygen or NIV - PaO2 under 60 mmHg, PaCO2 over 50 mmHg and pH under 7.35 - or for exhaustion, coma or shock needing deep sedation. [ESC 2021]`,`**Thromboprophylaxis** with **enoxaparin 40 mg SC once daily** (or UFH 5000 units 8-12 hourly) is Class I unless the patient is already anticoagulated or bleeding. [ESC 2021]`,`**AF with a fast rate**: digoxin **0.25-0.5 mg IV** if not already on it (lower dose in renal impairment) or IV amiodarone; avoid IV beta-blockers, verapamil and diltiazem in decompensation; **DC cardioversion** if unstable. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Cardiogenic shock: revascularisation and mechanical support`,points:[`Do an **immediate ECG and echo** (Class I), and transfer the patient early to a **tertiary centre with a 24/7 cath lab, cardiac ICU and short-term MCS** (IIa); place an arterial line, central line and urinary catheter. [ESC 2021]`,`Resuscitation targets: **MAP 65 mmHg or more**, falling lactate, **urine output over 0.5 mL/kg/h**, improving mentation and skin perfusion; re-stage by SCAI at each review. [SCAI 2022]`,`A **small fluid challenge** (about 250 mL crystalloid over 15-30 minutes) is reasonable only when there is no congestion - for example RV infarction or hypovolaemia; never in a wet patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AMI with shock**: emergency angiography and **culprit-lesion-only PCI**, whatever the time from onset (SHOCK trial survival benefit); **CULPRIT-SHOCK** showed lower 30-day death or dialysis with culprit-only PCI (**45.9% vs 55.4%**) than immediate multivessel PCI; fibrinolysis only if PCI is unavailable. [ESC 2023]`,`**IABP**: IABP-SHOCK II found no reduction in 30-day mortality (**39.7% vs 41.3%**), so routine use in AMI shock is **not recommended (III)**; it is still used as a bridge in mechanical complications and where nothing else exists. [ESC 2023]`,`**Microaxial flow pump (Impella CP)**: DanGer Shock (2024) in non-comatose STEMI shock cut 180-day death (**45.8% vs 58.5%**) at the cost of more bleeding, limb ischaemia and dialysis; the AHA/ACC 2025 ACS guideline gives it **Class IIa** in selected STEMI shock. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**VA-ECMO**: ECLS-SHOCK (2023) found no reduction in 30-day mortality (**47.8% vs 49.0%**) with more bleeding and vascular complications, so it is **not routine** in AMI shock; it is reserved for refractory shock or arrest (SCAI E) as a bridge, with LV venting. [ECLS-SHOCK 2023]`,`**Escalation by stage**: SCAI C - one vasopressor plus one inotrope; D - escalate drugs or add MCS; E - ECMO or extracorporeal CPR at capable centres; every device is a **bridge to recovery, decision, durable LVAD or transplant**. [SCAI 2022]`,`**Mechanical complications** (septal rupture, acute MR from papillary muscle rupture) need afterload reduction with nitroprusside or IABP and urgent surgical or transcatheter repair - details in the Complications of myocardial infarction topic. [ESC 2023]`,`In India most cath labs can place an **IABP**, but Impella and ECMO are limited to a few tertiary centres - so early recognition at SCAI B-C and early transfer matter more than devices. [CSI 2023]`]},{heading:`In-hospital GDMT, discharge criteria and STRONG-HF`,points:[`ESC 2026 frames care in three phases: **initial** (decongest, treat the cause), **stabilisation** (start or restart foundational therapy, including the SGLT2 inhibitor) and **pre- and post-discharge** (titrate and follow up). [ESC 2026]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor in hospital**: EMPULSE (2022) started **empagliflozin 10 mg once daily** at a median of 3 days and improved the composite of death, HF events and symptoms (win ratio 1.36); no titration, eGFR 20 or more. [ESC 2026]`,`**Sacubitril-valsartan** can be started in hospital once stable in HFrEF - PIONEER-HF showed a greater NT-proBNP fall than enalapril with similar safety; start **24/26 or 49/51 mg twice daily** if SBP is 100 mmHg or more, **36 hours after the last ACE inhibitor dose**. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`,`**Beta-blocker**: continue if already taken; start at low dose (bisoprolol 1.25 mg once daily or carvedilol 3.125 mg twice daily) once euvolaemic and off inotropes; **MRA**: spironolactone 12.5-25 mg if potassium under 5.0 mmol/L and eGFR over 30. [ESC 2021]`,`**STRONG-HF (2022)**: oral GDMT at half dose before discharge, **full dose within 2 weeks**, with visits at **1, 2, 3 and 6 weeks** checking congestion, BP, heart rate, potassium, creatinine and NT-proBNP, cut 180-day death or HF readmission (**15.2% vs 23.3%**); ESC 2023 made this intensive strategy **Class I**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Discharge criteria**: euvolaemia (no orthopnoea, normal JVP, minimal oedema), stable weight on **oral diuretic for at least 24 hours**, stable creatinine and electrolytes, no symptomatic hypotension, precipitant treated and foundational therapy started. [ESC 2021]`,`**Residual congestion at discharge** is the strongest predictor of readmission - do not discharge a wet patient; a pre-discharge NT-proBNP falling by **30% or more** from admission predicts a better course. [HFA 2019]`,`Before discharge: check **iron studies** and give IV iron if deficient (AFFIRM-AHF: ferric carboxymaltose reduced HF readmissions), reconcile medicines, vaccinate, teach daily weights and a **flexible diuretic plan**, and book review **within 1-2 weeks** (Class I). [ESC 2023]`,`Defer **ICD and CRT decisions** until LVEF is reassessed after **3 months of optimised therapy**, because LVEF often improves - see the Chronic heart failure optimisation topic. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: delirium, orthostatic hypotension, hyponatraemia and falls are common; use smaller diuretic steps, watch for overdiuresis and discuss goals of care early. [ESC 2021]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: higher loop doses and sequential blockade are needed, ultrafiltration or dialysis comes earlier, MRA is limited by potassium, and SGLT2 inhibitors can be started down to eGFR 20. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: start an SGLT2 inhibitor once stable but check ketones in fasting or acute illness (euglycaemic ketoacidosis), and stop **pioglitazone and saxagliptin**. [ADA 2025]`,`**Pregnancy and the peripartum period**: loop diuretics, nitrates, hydralazine and beta-blockers are usable; **ACE inhibitors, ARB, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitors and ivabradine are avoided** in pregnancy; shock needs urgent delivery and MCS at a tertiary centre - see the Dilated cardiomyopathy and Heart disease in pregnancy topics. [ESC 2025]`,`**Severe aortic stenosis**: use vasodilators and diuretics cautiously (preload and afterload dependent) and arrange urgent balloon valvuloplasty or TAVI/SAVR; **severe MS in AF**: rate control with beta-blocker or digoxin, diuretic, anticoagulation and urgent balloon mitral valvotomy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**RV infarction**: avoid nitrates, diuretics and morphine; give cautious fluid, an inotrope if needed, and restore AV synchrony (pacing for block). [ESC 2023]`,`**Hypertensive flash pulmonary oedema** with preserved LVEF: IV nitrate, NIV and a modest diuretic dose; look for **bilateral renal artery stenosis (Pickering syndrome)** and ischaemia. [ESC 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: diagnose with history, JVP, chest X-ray and lung ultrasound; treat with IV furosemide, sublingual or IV nitrate and CPAP; noradrenaline or dobutamine can run briefly through a large peripheral vein while transfer is arranged. [WHO 2023]`,`**Children**: think myocarditis, congenital heart disease, rheumatic carditis and dilated cardiomyopathy; dose by weight and refer to a paediatric cardiac unit. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, follow-up and prevention of readmission`,points:[`**Treatment complications**: hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia (arrhythmia), hyponatraemia, AKI, hypotension, gout, ototoxicity, metabolic alkalosis (corrected by acetazolamide) and inotrope-induced arrhythmias. [ESC 2021]`,`**Disease complications**: VT and VF, AF, venous thromboembolism, delirium, hospital-acquired pneumonia, cardiorenal and cardiohepatic syndromes, multi-organ failure and death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: low SBP, **sodium under 135 mmol/L**, high urea and creatinine, raised troponin, NT-proBNP that does not fall, cold-wet profile, inotrope need, repeated admissions and frailty. [ESC 2021]`,`In cardiogenic shock mortality rises steeply from SCAI C to E; survivors face high readmission and many need advanced-HF evaluation. [SCAI 2022]`,`**First review within 1-2 weeks** of discharge, then the STRONG-HF schedule: weight, BP, heart rate, congestion, creatinine, potassium, sodium and NT-proBNP at each titration visit. [ESC 2023]`,`Teach **daily weights**: call or take an extra diuretic dose if weight rises **2 kg or more in 3 days**; keep a HF diary and a written flexible diuretic plan. [ESC 2021]`,`**Readmission prevention**: a multidisciplinary HF clinic or nurse-led programme (Class I), telephone follow-up, cardiac rehabilitation, vaccination, and treatment of anaemia, iron deficiency, depression and sleep apnoea. [ESC 2021]`,`**Advanced HF triggers** - two or more admissions in 12 months, inotrope dependence, GDMT intolerance from hypotension or renal failure, cardiac cachexia - mean referral for LVAD or transplant assessment or for palliative care. [ESC 2021]`,`Programme links: **NP-NCD** screening and follow-up of hypertension and diabetes at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs prevent the upstream disease, and **PM-JAY** covers inpatient HF care and many cardiac procedures. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2026 HF guideline**: **decompensated HF** replaces acute HF; less severe episodes may be managed without admission; care follows an **initial, stabilisation and pre/post-discharge** framework. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2026**: SGLT2 inhibitor **initiation in hospital after stabilisation**, and **urinary sodium-guided diuretic therapy** may be considered in the first days. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2026**: **HFmrEF is abolished** (LVEF under 50% is HFrEF) and stages A-D are adopted, so patients with LVEF 41-49% leave hospital on the HFrEF regimen. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2023 focused update**: intensive initiation and **rapid up-titration of GDMT with frequent follow-up over 6 weeks** after an HF admission is Class I (STRONG-HF). [ESC 2023]`,`**ADVOR (2022) and CLOROTIC (2023)** support acetazolamide or a thiazide for diuretic resistance, improving decongestion but not hard outcomes; the HFA 2019 stepwise diuretic algorithm remains the backbone. [HFA 2019]`,`**SCAI 2022 update**: staging applies to all cardiogenic shock, is repeated over time and carries a cardiac-arrest modifier. [SCAI 2022]`,`**Mechanical support**: ECLS-SHOCK (2023) showed no benefit of routine VA-ECMO, DanGer Shock (2024) showed benefit of Impella in selected STEMI shock (AHA/ACC 2025 Class IIa), and routine IABP stays Class III. [AHA/ACC 2025]`,`**AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022** and the **2024 ACC decision pathway** endorse in-hospital initiation and rapid sequencing of all foundational drugs in HFrEF. [ACC ECDP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Draw the **Stevenson 2 x 2 box** and write the first treatment in each box - the commonest viva opener on DHF. [ESC 2021]`,`The old **LMNOP** mnemonic is outdated: **morphine is no longer routine** and **oxygen is only for SpO2 under 90%**. [ESC 2021]`,`Common mistake: **stopping the beta-blocker in every admission** - stop only for shock, symptomatic bradycardia or high-grade block; otherwise continue or halve. [ESC 2021]`,`Common mistake: **stopping diuretics for a small creatinine rise** while the patient is still congested - persistent congestion, not creatinine, predicts death. [HFA 2019]`,`Know the response checks: **2-hour urine sodium 50-70 mmol/L, 6-hour urine 100-150 mL/h**, otherwise double the dose and then add acetazolamide or a thiazide. [ESC 2021]`,`Explain why **noradrenaline beats dopamine** (SOAP II), why **IABP is not routine** (IABP-SHOCK II) and why **culprit-only PCI** is preferred (CULPRIT-SHOCK). [ESC 2023]`,`Contrast cardiogenic with septic shock: **cold vs warm peripheries, high vs low SVR, high vs low PCWP, low vs high cardiac output**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Recall **CHAMPIT** for precipitants and the four discharge essentials: **euvolaemic, on oral diuretic 24 hours, GDMT started, review in 1-2 weeks**. [ESC 2021]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in decompensated heart failure and cardiogenic shock`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`Naive: 20-40 mg IV; on oral loop: 1-2.5 times total daily oral dose IV, as 2-3 boluses a day or infusion`,`Double if 2-h urine Na under 50-70 mmol/L; max 400-600 mg/day (1000 mg in severe CKD); switch to oral when euvolaemic`,`Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyponatraemia, AKI, gout, ototoxicity (infuse no faster than 4 mg/min)`],[`Torsemide / bumetanide`,`Torsemide 10-20 mg IV or oral; bumetanide 0.5-1 mg IV (1 mg = furosemide 40 mg)`,`As for furosemide; more predictable oral absorption`,`As for furosemide; bumetanide can cause myalgia`],[`Acetazolamide`,`500 mg IV once daily with loop diuretic (ADVOR)`,`3 days`,`Metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia; not studied with eGFR under 20 or on SGLT2 inhibitor`],[`Hydrochlorothiazide / metolazone`,`HCTZ 25 mg (eGFR over 50), 50 mg (20-50), 100 mg (under 20) oral daily; metolazone 2.5-5 mg oral`,`1-3 days; daily electrolytes`,`Severe hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia, AKI, hypovolaemia`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`0.4 mg SL every 5 min; IV 10-20 mcg/min up to 200 mcg/min`,`Titrate every 5-10 min to symptoms with SBP over 90; tolerance after 24-48 h`,`Hypotension, headache; avoid SBP under 90, severe AS, RV infarct, PDE-5 inhibitor use`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`0.3-5 mcg/kg/min IV with arterial line`,`Shortest possible, usually under 72 h`,`Hypotension, cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity in renal or liver failure, coronary steal`],[`Dobutamine`,`2-20 mcg/kg/min IV, no bolus`,`Wean gradually; tolerance after 24-72 h`,`Tachycardia, arrhythmia, ischaemia; blunted by beta-blockers`],[`Milrinone`,`0.375-0.75 mcg/kg/min IV (bolus usually omitted)`,`Hours to days; reduce dose in renal failure`,`Hypotension, arrhythmia; accumulates in CKD`],[`Levosimendan`,`0.05-0.2 mcg/kg/min IV (optional bolus, omit if SBP under 90)`,`24-hour infusion; effect lasts about 7 days`,`Hypotension, hypokalaemia, AF`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.2-1.0 mcg/kg/min IV (central or large peripheral vein)`,`Titrate to MAP 65 mmHg or more`,`Peripheral ischaemia, arrhythmia, extravasation necrosis`],[`Dopamine`,`3-5 mcg/kg/min inotropic; over 5 mcg/kg/min vasopressor`,`Not preferred`,`Tachyarrhythmia; higher mortality in cardiogenic shock (SOAP II)`],[`Empagliflozin / dapagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`Start once stabilised (EMPULSE); lifelong`,`Genital infection, volume depletion, euglycaemic ketoacidosis; do not start if eGFR under 20`],[`Enoxaparin (prophylaxis)`,`40 mg SC once daily`,`Until fully mobile`,`Bleeding; adjust if creatinine clearance under 30 mL/min`],[`Tolvaptan`,`15 mg oral once daily (max 60 mg)`,`Short course in hospital with 6-hourly sodium`,`Over-rapid sodium correction (osmotic demyelination), liver injury`]]},{heading:`Bedside clinical profiles (Stevenson) and the first treatment`,columns:[`Profile`,`Congestion`,`Perfusion`,`Typical patient`,`First treatment`],rows:[[`Warm-wet (commonest)`,`Present: orthopnoea, raised JVP, oedema, crackles`,`Adequate: warm, normal pulse pressure`,`ADHF after salt load or missed diuretic; hypertensive pulmonary oedema`,`IV loop diuretic; IV nitrate if SBP over 110; NIV if hypoxic`],[`Cold-wet (worst prognosis)`,`Present`,`Poor: cool, narrow pulse pressure, oliguria, confusion`,`Large MI, end-stage DCM, cardiogenic shock`,`Noradrenaline if hypotensive plus dobutamine; diuretic once perfused; consider MCS`],[`Cold-dry (uncommon)`,`Absent: flat JVP, clear lungs`,`Poor`,`Overdiuresis, RV infarct, poor intake`,`Cautious fluid challenge; inotrope if no response; reduce diuretic`],[`Warm-dry (compensated)`,`Absent`,`Adequate`,`Stable chronic HF at OPD review`,`Optimise oral GDMT and lifestyle`]]},{heading:`SCAI SHOCK stages (2022 update)`,columns:[`Stage`,`Description`,`Bedside findings`,`Lactate / haemodynamics`,`Typical action`],rows:[[`A - At risk`,`Large MI or decompensated HF, not in shock`,`Normal BP and perfusion`,`Lactate under 2 mmol/L`,`Treat cause, monitor closely`],[`B - Beginning`,`Relative hypotension or tachycardia without hypoperfusion`,`SBP under 90 or MAP under 60 or fall over 30 mmHg; warm`,`Lactate under 2 mmol/L`,`Fluids if dry, early transfer, echo`],[`C - Classic`,`Hypoperfusion needing intervention beyond fluid`,`Cold, oliguria, confusion`,`Lactate 2 mmol/L or more; CI under 2.2, PCWP over 15`,`Vasopressor plus inotrope, revascularise, consider MCS`],[`D - Deteriorating`,`Failing to stabilise on initial support`,`Worsening despite therapy`,`Rising lactate, worsening organ function`,`Escalate drugs, add or upgrade MCS`],[`E - Extremis`,`Actual or impending circulatory collapse`,`Cardiac arrest, ongoing CPR or ECMO`,`Severe acidosis, lactate often 8 mmol/L or more`,`VA-ECMO or ECPR at capable centre; goals-of-care talk`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acute breathlessness with congestion or shock`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating clues`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Decompensated HF`,`Orthopnoea, PND, raised JVP, S3, bilateral crackles, oedema`,`NT-proBNP over 300 pg/mL, echo, B-lines`],[`COPD or asthma exacerbation`,`Smoker, hyperinflation, prolonged expiration, normal JVP`,`NT-proBNP under 300, spirometry history, ABG`],[`Pneumonia`,`Fever, focal consolidation, purulent sputum`,`Chest X-ray, procalcitonin, cultures`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Sudden onset, clear lungs, hypoxia, DVT, RV strain`,`CT pulmonary angiography, echo RV dilatation`],[`ARDS (non-cardiogenic oedema)`,`Sepsis or pancreatitis, normal heart size, no effusions`,`PCWP under 18 mmHg, normal echo filling pressures`],[`Renal volume overload`,`Oliguria, proteinuria, hypoalbuminaemia, missed dialysis`,`Creatinine, urine protein, albumin`],[`Septic shock`,`Warm peripheries early, wide pulse pressure, fever`,`Low SVR, high cardiac output on echo, lactate, cultures`],[`Cardiac tamponade`,`Hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs, pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg`,`Echo: effusion with RV diastolic collapse`],[`Scorpion sting pulmonary oedema`,`Rural India, sting, priapism, sweating, hypertension then shock`,`History; ECG and troponin for myocarditis`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in decompensated HF and shock`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`NT-proBNP (acute)`,`Under 300 pg/mL`,`Acute HF unlikely`],[`NT-proBNP (acute, rule in)`,`Over 450 (under 50 y), 900 (50-75 y), 1800 pg/mL (over 75 y)`,`Acute HF likely`],[`BNP (acute)`,`Under 100 pg/mL`,`Acute HF unlikely; raised by sacubitril, so use NT-proBNP on ARNI`],[`Spot urine sodium 2 h after loop dose`,`Under 50-70 mmol/L`,`Insufficient natriuresis - double the dose`],[`Urine output first 6 h`,`Under 100-150 mL/h`,`Insufficient diuresis - double the dose`],[`Lactate`,`Over 2 mmol/L`,`Tissue hypoperfusion (SCAI C or worse)`],[`Cardiac index / PCWP`,`Under 2.2 L/min/m2 / over 15-18 mmHg`,`Cardiogenic shock haemodynamics`],[`Cardiac power output`,`Under 0.6 W`,`Strongest haemodynamic predictor of death in shock`],[`Proportional pulse pressure`,`Under 25%`,`Cardiac index likely under 2.2 L/min/m2`],[`IVC on POCUS`,`Over 2.1 cm with under 50% collapse`,`Right atrial pressure about 15 mmHg`]]}],redFlags:[`SBP under 90 mmHg with cold peripheries, confusion, urine under 30 mL/h or lactate over 2 mmol/L - cardiogenic shock: ICU, vasopressor and inotrope, transfer to a shock-capable centre.`,`SpO2 under 90% despite oxygen, respiratory rate over 25/min, rising PaCO2 or exhaustion - start NIV and prepare for intubation.`,`ST elevation or dynamic ischaemia with heart failure - immediate angiography or reperfusion.`,`New pansystolic murmur or sudden pulmonary oedema days after MI - septal or papillary muscle rupture needing urgent surgery.`,`Heart rate over 130 or under 40/min, sustained VT or complete heart block.`,`Poor natriuretic response at 6 hours despite a doubled loop dose, potassium over 6.0 mmol/L or creatinine rising while still congested.`,`Heart failure in late pregnancy or within 5 months of delivery - peripartum cardiomyopathy until proved otherwise.`,`Hypotension with raised JVP and clear lungs - RV infarction, massive PE or tamponade; withhold nitrates and diuretics.`],pearls:[`Two-minute profile: **warm or cold, wet or dry** - it chooses the first drug.`,`**Raised JVP** is the best bedside sign of congestion; crackles can be absent in chronic HF.`,`Check the diuretic: **2-hour urine sodium 50-70 mmol/L and 6-hour urine 100-150 mL/h** - if not, double the dose.`,`**CHAMPIT**: Coronary syndrome, Hypertension, Arrhythmia, Mechanical, PE, Infection, Tamponade.`,`**Proportional pulse pressure under 25%** means cardiac index under 2.2.`,`**Noradrenaline** is the vasopressor and **dobutamine** the first inotrope; use levosimendan or milrinone in patients on beta-blockers.`,`Shock trials in one line: **IABP no** (IABP-SHOCK II), **ECMO not routine** (ECLS-SHOCK), **Impella helps selected STEMI shock** (DanGer), **culprit-only PCI** (CULPRIT-SHOCK).`,`**Morphine is out**, oxygen only if SpO2 is under 90%.`,`**Don't discharge wet**; start the SGLT2 inhibitor before discharge and see the patient within 1-2 weeks (STRONG-HF).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old man with known dilated cardiomyopathy presents with orthopnoea and leg swelling. BP is 84/62 mmHg, pulse 112/min, extremities cold and clammy, JVP raised to the angle of the jaw, bibasal crackles present, urine output 15 mL/h and lactate 3.4 mmol/L. Which is the most appropriate initial management?`,options:[`IV furosemide 80 mg and IV nitroglycerin 20 mcg/min`,`Noradrenaline to MAP 65 mmHg plus dobutamine, adding IV diuretic once perfusion improves`,`Rapid 1 litre normal saline bolus to raise BP`,`Increase oral sacubitril-valsartan and bisoprolol to target doses`],answer:1,explanation:`He is cold-wet with hypoperfusion (SCAI C cardiogenic shock): a vasopressor (noradrenaline) restores MAP and an inotrope (dobutamine) restores output, after which diuretics work. Nitroglycerin with diuretic suits warm-wet patients with SBP over 110 and would worsen hypotension; a litre of saline is for cold-dry patients and will worsen pulmonary oedema; up-titrating GDMT in shock is harmful.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-q2`,stem:`A woman admitted with decompensated HF received IV furosemide 80 mg (twice her daily oral dose). Two hours later her spot urine sodium is 38 mmol/L, and over 6 hours urine output has averaged 60 mL/h. She remains congested with stable BP. According to the ESC algorithm, what is the next step?`,options:[`Stop diuretics and give IV fluids for presumed prerenal AKI`,`Start low-dose dopamine at 2 mcg/kg/min for renal perfusion`,`Proceed directly to ultrafiltration`,`Double the IV loop diuretic dose and reassess natriuresis`],answer:3,explanation:`A 2-hour urine sodium under 50-70 mmol/L or 6-hour output under 100-150 mL/h is an insufficient natriuretic response, and the ESC step is to double the IV loop dose (up to 400-600 mg/day) before adding acetazolamide or a thiazide. Renal-dose dopamine has no benefit, ultrafiltration is reserved for refractory congestion (CARRESS-HF showed no superiority), and fluids would worsen congestion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-q3`,stem:`A 64-year-old with anterior STEMI is in cardiogenic shock on arrival at a PCI-capable centre 14 hours after symptom onset. Angiography shows an occluded proximal LAD and 80% stenoses in the RCA and circumflex. Which strategy is supported by current evidence?`,options:[`Primary PCI of the culprit LAD only, with staged assessment of the other lesions later`,`Immediate PCI of all three vessels in the same sitting`,`Routine intra-aortic balloon pump before any PCI to improve 30-day survival`,`Fibrinolysis because more than 12 hours have passed`],answer:0,explanation:`In AMI shock emergency revascularisation is indicated regardless of time (SHOCK trial), and CULPRIT-SHOCK showed lower 30-day death or dialysis with culprit-only PCI than immediate multivessel PCI. Routine IABP did not reduce mortality in IABP-SHOCK II (Class III), and fibrinolysis is inferior to PCI when the cath lab is available.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-q4`,stem:`In the STRONG-HF trial, which strategy after a hospital admission for acute heart failure reduced 180-day death or HF readmission?`,options:[`Delaying all GDMT until the first outpatient visit at 3 months`,`Routine pulmonary artery catheter-guided therapy during admission`,`Up-titrating GDMT to full doses within 2 weeks of discharge with visits at 1, 2, 3 and 6 weeks`,`Strict fluid restriction to 1 litre a day for 6 months`],answer:2,explanation:`STRONG-HF's high-intensity care started oral GDMT at half dose before discharge and reached full doses within 2 weeks, with close visits checking congestion, BP, potassium, creatinine and NT-proBNP; 180-day death or HF readmission fell from 23.3% to 15.2% (ESC 2023 Class I). Delaying therapy is the usual-care arm that did worse, PA catheters did not help in ESCAPE, and fluid restriction has no outcome benefit.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-q5`,stem:`A patient in cardiogenic shock after MI remains hypotensive (MAP 55 mmHg) despite dobutamine. Which vasopressor is preferred, and why?`,options:[`Dopamine, because it improves renal blood flow at low doses`,`Adrenaline, because it is the strongest inotrope and vasopressor`,`Noradrenaline, because dopamine caused more arrhythmias and higher mortality in cardiogenic shock`,`Vasopressin alone, because it does not raise afterload`],answer:2,explanation:`Noradrenaline is the preferred vasopressor (ESC IIb): in SOAP II, dopamine caused more arrhythmias and higher mortality in the cardiogenic shock subgroup. Renal-dose dopamine has no proven benefit, adrenaline was linked to more refractory shock in OptimaCC and is a rescue drug, and vasopressin alone is not first-line in cardiogenic shock.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define acute decompensated heart failure. Describe its clinical profiles and precipitating factors, and outline the in-hospital management, including diuretic strategy and pre-discharge care.`,openingLines:[`Decompensated heart failure (ESC 2026 term replacing acute HF) is new or worsening symptoms and signs of HF - mainly congestion - needing urgent IV therapy or admission; most cases are acute decompensation of known chronic HF.`,`Management rests on a two-minute bedside profile (warm or cold, wet or dry), rapid treatment of the precipitant (CHAMPIT), monitored IV diuresis, and starting foundational therapy before discharge with rapid up-titration (STRONG-HF).`],answer:[{heading:`Definition, burden and presentations`,points:[`De novo or acutely decompensated chronic HF (50-70%); in-hospital mortality 4-10%; NHFR India: mean age about 60, about 1 in 4 dead at one year.`,`ESC presentations: ADHF, acute pulmonary oedema, isolated RV failure, cardiogenic shock.`]},{heading:`Precipitants (CHAMPIT and others)`,points:[`ACS, hypertensive emergency, arrhythmia (fast AF), mechanical cause, PE, infection, tamponade.`,`Non-adherence and salt load, NSAIDs, pioglitazone, verapamil/diltiazem, anaemia, thyroid, AKI, pregnancy; in India RHD progression, beriberi, peripartum.`]},{heading:`Clinical profiles`,points:[`Wet: orthopnoea, PND, raised JVP, S3, crackles, oedema; cold: cool skin, narrow pulse pressure (under 25%), oliguria, confusion.`,`Warm-wet (commonest) - diuretic plus vasodilator; cold-wet - inotrope or vasopressor then diuretic; cold-dry - cautious fluid; warm-dry - optimise orals.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG in 10 minutes, NT-proBNP (under 300 rules out), chest X-ray, lung ultrasound, echo (immediately if unstable, within 48 h if de novo).`,`Troponin, creatinine, electrolytes, Hb, glucose, LFT, TSH, iron studies, blood gas and lactate.`]},{heading:`Initial management`,points:[`Sit up, oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%, CPAP/BiPAP if RR over 25 and SpO2 under 90%; no routine morphine.`,`IV nitrate if SBP over 110; continue GDMT unless shock or severe hyperkalaemia; enoxaparin prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Diuretic strategy`,points:[`IV furosemide 20-40 mg (naive) or 1-2.5 times oral dose; check 2-h urine Na 50-70 mmol/L and 6-h urine 100-150 mL/h; double if inadequate.`,`Then sequential blockade: acetazolamide 500 mg IV for 3 days (ADVOR) or thiazide/metolazone (CLOROTIC); daily weight, K, creatinine; tolerate modest creatinine rise.`]},{heading:`Cardiogenic shock in brief`,points:[`SBP under 90 with hypoperfusion; SCAI A-E; noradrenaline plus dobutamine, culprit-only PCI in AMI, MCS selectively (no routine IABP).`]},{heading:`Pre-discharge and follow-up`,points:[`Start SGLT2 inhibitor (EMPULSE), ARNI/ACEi, beta-blocker and MRA; discharge euvolaemic on oral diuretic for 24 h.`,`STRONG-HF: full doses within 2 weeks with visits at 1, 2, 3, 6 weeks; iron studies, vaccination, daily weights, HF clinic, review within 1-2 weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Stevenson 2 x 2 profile box with treatment in each quadrant`,`Diuretic algorithm flow chart with the 2-hour and 6-hour checkpoints`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and presentations`,marks:1},{part:`Precipitants`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical profiles with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Initial management and diuretic strategy`,marks:3},{part:`Pre-discharge care and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`CHAMPIT`,`warm-wet`,`natriuresis`,`ADVOR`,`CLOROTIC`,`STRONG-HF`,`EMPULSE`,`NIV`]},{id:`cardiovascular-adhf-cardiogenic-shock-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Cardiogenic shock: definition, SCAI staging and management.`,openingLines:[`Cardiogenic shock is systolic BP under 90 mmHg for 30 minutes or more (or vasopressor dependence) with signs of hypoperfusion - cold skin, oliguria, confusion, lactate over 2 mmol/L - due to primary cardiac failure, with cardiac index under 2.2 L/min/m2 and PCWP over 15 mmHg.`,`It complicates 5-10% of acute MI and carries 30-50% in-hospital mortality; it is staged A-E by SCAI.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Large MI (commonest), mechanical complications, RV infarct, myocarditis, end-stage DCM, critical valve disease, Takotsubo, drug overdose.`]},{heading:`SCAI stages`,points:[`A at risk, B beginning (hypotension without hypoperfusion), C classic (hypoperfusion needing intervention, lactate 2 or more), D deteriorating, E extremis (arrest, ECMO).`]},{heading:`Immediate management`,points:[`ECG and echo at once; arterial line; MAP 65 mmHg or more with noradrenaline; dobutamine for low output; fluid challenge only if not congested.`,`Oxygen or ventilation as needed; correct acidosis, potassium and arrhythmia; transfer to a shock-capable centre.`]},{heading:`Definitive treatment`,points:[`AMI: emergency culprit-only PCI regardless of delay (SHOCK, CULPRIT-SHOCK).`,`MCS: no routine IABP (IABP-SHOCK II) or VA-ECMO (ECLS-SHOCK); Impella in selected STEMI shock (DanGer Shock, AHA/ACC 2025 IIa); bridge to recovery, LVAD or transplant.`]}],mustDraw:[`SCAI stage pyramid A to E with lactate and action for each stage`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:1},{part:`SCAI staging`,marks:1},{part:`Drugs and resuscitation targets`,marks:1.5},{part:`Revascularisation and MCS evidence`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`SCAI`,`noradrenaline`,`dobutamine`,`culprit-only PCI`,`IABP-SHOCK II`,`DanGer Shock`,`ECLS-SHOCK`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on heart failure management and cardiogenic shock.`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022.`,`2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure (Eur Heart J 2026).`,`2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure, and the 2023 Focused Update.`,`Mullens W et al. The use of diuretics in heart failure with congestion - a position statement from the Heart Failure Association of the ESC, 2019.`,`SCAI SHOCK Stage Classification Expert Consensus Update, 2022; 2023 ESC Guidelines for acute coronary syndromes; 2025 ACC/AHA Guideline for acute coronary syndromes.`,`2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure.`,`Harikrishnan S et al. One-year mortality and readmission by aetiology in the National Heart Failure Registry of India (ICMR), Nature Communications, 2025.`]}),i.push({id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation`,title:`Chronic heart failure: GDMT titration, devices and end-of-life care`,oneLiner:`Optimising chronic heart failure means starting all **four foundational drugs - ARNI (or ACE inhibitor/ARB), evidence-based beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor** - at low dose within weeks and **up-titrating every 1-2 weeks** to target or maximum tolerated dose with potassium and creatinine checks, adding **IV iron, ivabradine, digoxin, vericiguat or hydralazine-nitrate** where indicated, offering an **ICD (LVEF 35% or less after 3 months of optimal therapy) and CRT (LBBB with QRS 150 ms or more)**, using **SGLT2 inhibitor plus MRA (spironolactone or finerenone) and GLP-1-based therapy in obese HFpEF**, and integrating vaccination, self-care, an HF clinic and **palliative care** in advanced disease.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`GDMT`,`four pillars`,`foundational medical therapy`,`sacubitril-valsartan ARNI`,`bisoprolol carvedilol metoprolol succinate`,`spironolactone eplerenone`,`finerenone FINEARTS-HF`,`dapagliflozin empagliflozin`,`ivabradine`,`digoxin digitoxin`,`vericiguat`,`hydralazine isosorbide dinitrate`,`ferric carboxymaltose IV iron`,`ICD primary prevention`,`CRT cardiac resynchronisation`,`HFimpEF`,`HFpEF semaglutide tirzepatide`,`advanced heart failure LVAD`,`palliative care heart failure`,`HF clinic`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Chronic HF** is the stable but progressive phase of the HF syndrome - symptoms and signs from a structural or functional cardiac abnormality with raised natriuretic peptides or objective congestion - in which the goal is to **prolong life, prevent admission and improve function**. [ESC 2026]`,`**GDMT optimisation** means every eligible patient receives all **foundational medical therapies** at target or maximum tolerated dose, plus indicated devices, within about **3-6 months** of diagnosis. [ESC 2026]`,`HF affects about **1-2% of adults** worldwide and over 10% of those over 70; lifetime risk at 55 years is about 1 in 3 to 1 in 4. [ESC 2021]`,`Combined four-drug therapy compared with ACE inhibitor plus beta-blocker alone is estimated to add about **1.4 years of life at age 80 and over 6 years at age 55**, and to roughly halve cardiovascular death or HF admission. [Vaduganathan 2020]`,`**India**: NHFR patients are about a decade younger than Western patients and about **1 in 4 die within a year** of admission; registries show large gaps in prescription and dosing of GDMT at discharge and follow-up. [NHFR 2025]`,`Most patients worldwide remain on **sub-target doses** - in CHAMP-HF under 1% of eligible US patients were on target doses of all drugs - so titration, not diagnosis, is the main deficit. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`,`Low-cost generics of **sacubitril-valsartan, dapagliflozin and empagliflozin** are now marketed in India, and spironolactone, enalapril, metoprolol and furosemide are cheap essential medicines - cost is less of an excuse than before. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and drivers of progression`,points:[`Commonest causes in India: **ischaemic heart disease** (about 70%), hypertension, dilated cardiomyopathy and **rheumatic valve disease**; always treat the cause alongside GDMT. [NHFR 2025]`,`Correctable causes to search for: **ischaemia** (revascularise if viable and symptomatic), severe valve disease (TAVI, mitral repair), **tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy**, alcohol, thyroid disease, thiamine deficiency and iron overload. [ESC 2021]`,`Drivers of progression: uncontrolled BP, AF, ongoing ischaemia, alcohol, **non-adherence**, NSAIDs, anaemia and iron deficiency, CKD, diabetes, obesity and sleep apnoea. [ESC 2021]`,`Every HF admission marks a step down: post-discharge mortality and readmission are highest in the first 3 months, and each admission predicts the next. [ESC 2021]`,`Comorbidities determine prognosis as much as LVEF in HFpEF: **obesity, hypertension, diabetes, AF, CKD and anaemia**. [ESC 2026]`,`HFrEF with LVEF that improves (**HFimpEF**) remains HF: stopping therapy causes relapse in about 40% within 6 months (TRED-HF). [TRED-HF 2019]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: why each pillar works`,points:[`After an index injury, chronic activation of the **renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system** and **sympathetic nervous system** drives vasoconstriction, sodium retention, myocyte death, fibrosis and **LV remodelling** (dilatation, sphericity, functional MR). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ACE inhibitors and ARBs** block angiotensin II, unloading the ventricle and slowing remodelling; **sacubitril** inhibits neprilysin, raising natriuretic peptides, bradykinin and cGMP for natriuresis and anti-fibrosis - hence ARNI beats enalapril (PARADIGM-HF). [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Beta-blockers** reduce heart rate and myocardial oxygen demand, prevent catecholamine toxicity and arrhythmia, and produce **reverse remodelling** with LVEF rising over months - why they are started low and titrated slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MRAs** block aldosterone escape (aldosterone rises again despite ACE inhibition), reducing fibrosis, potassium loss and sudden death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors** cause osmotic diuresis and natriuresis without neurohormonal activation, lower intraglomerular pressure, improve myocardial energetics and raise haematocrit - benefit appears within weeks and is independent of diabetes. [ESC 2023]`,`**Ivabradine** blocks the If current in the sinus node - it lowers heart rate only in sinus rhythm; **vericiguat** stimulates soluble guanylate cyclase to restore the NO-cGMP pathway. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Iron deficiency** impairs mitochondrial energy production in skeletal and cardiac muscle, causing fatigue and reduced exercise capacity even without anaemia - corrected only by IV iron because oral iron is poorly absorbed in HF (hepcidin, gut oedema). [ESC 2023]`,`**Dyssynchrony** in LBBB makes the septum and lateral wall contract out of phase; **CRT** re-coordinates them, raising LVEF, reducing MR and reversing remodelling. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**ESC 2026 phenotypes**: **HFrEF - LVEF under 50%** and **HFpEF - LVEF 50% or more**; the former **HFmrEF (41-49%) is abolished** and treated as HFrEF. [ESC 2026]`,`**HF with improved EF (HFimpEF)**: a baseline LVEF of 40% or less that rises by **10 points or more to over 40%** - continue all therapy. [Universal Definition 2021]`,`**Stages A-D** (now adopted by ESC 2026): **A** at risk (hypertension, diabetes, obesity, cardiotoxins), **B** pre-HF (structural disease, no symptoms), **C** symptomatic HF, **D** advanced HF - stages never go backwards. [ESC 2026]`,`**NYHA class I-IV** grades symptoms, changes with treatment and must be recorded at every visit because it drives ICD, CRT and drug decisions. [ESC 2021]`,`**Advanced HF** (HFA-ESC): severe persistent symptoms (NYHA III-IV), severe cardiac dysfunction, episodes of congestion or low output needing IV therapy or **more than one unplanned admission in 12 months**, and severely impaired exercise capacity despite optimal therapy. [ESC 2021]`,`**I NEED HELP** flags advanced HF: **I**notropes, **N**YHA IIIB-IV or high NT-proBNP, **E**nd-organ dysfunction, **E**F 35% or less, **D**efibrillator shocks, **H**ospitalisations (more than one in 12 months), **E**dema despite escalating diuretics, **L**ow BP (SBP 90 or less), **P**rognostic medication intolerance. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical assessment at the HF clinic visit`,points:[`Record **NYHA class, weight trend, orthopnoea, PND, oedema**, exercise tolerance in daily terms (stairs, walking to the market) and admissions since last visit. [ESC 2021]`,`Ask about **adherence and cost**, salt and fluid habits, alcohol, tobacco, NSAIDs and over-the-counter or ayurvedic remedies; check the actual pill strips, not the prescription. [ESC 2021]`,`Ask for **drug side effects** that block titration: dizziness on standing, fatigue, cough (ACE inhibitor), genital itching (SGLT2 inhibitor), breast tenderness (spironolactone), erectile dysfunction. [ESC 2021]`,`Measure **sitting and standing BP**, heart rate and rhythm, JVP, abdominojugular test, lung bases, liver, oedema, and weight on the same scale. [Harrison 22e]`,`Decide the **volume status** first (wet or dry) - diuretic dose is adjusted for congestion, whereas the four pillars are titrated for prognosis regardless of symptoms. [ESC 2021]`,`Screen for **depression, frailty, cognitive impairment and sleep apnoea** (snoring, daytime sleepiness, Cheyne-Stokes) - each worsens self-care and outcome. [ESC 2021]`,`Use the **Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire** or a simple symptom score and the **6-minute walk test** (under 300 m is poor) to track function. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations, monitoring and the patient who is not improving`,points:[`**Baseline**: ECG (rhythm, QRS width and morphology for CRT), echo (LVEF, valves, RV, pulmonary pressure), NT-proBNP, creatinine and eGFR, sodium, potassium, Hb, HbA1c, lipids, TSH, liver tests and **iron studies**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Before and 1-2 weeks after starting or up-titrating** an ACE inhibitor, ARB, ARNI or MRA: check potassium and creatinine; then every 3-4 months once stable. [ESC 2021]`,`**Iron deficiency**: ferritin under 100 ng/mL, or 100-299 ng/mL with **TSAT under 20%** (ESC 2021/2023); ESC 2026 leans on **TSAT under 20%** as the primary marker - check at diagnosis and at least yearly. [ESC 2026]`,`**Repeat echo 3-6 months** after GDMT is optimised to decide on ICD or CRT and to identify HFimpEF; repeat sooner after a clinical change. [ESC 2021]`,`**Serial NT-proBNP** helps judge prognosis and adherence (a falling value is reassuring), but routine NT-proBNP-guided titration did not beat clinical titration (GUIDE-IT). [GUIDE-IT 2017]`,`**Holter** for palpitations, syncope or suspected high PVC burden (over 10-15% may cause cardiomyopathy); **CMR** for aetiology (scar, myocarditis, infiltration); **coronary angiography or CT** if ischaemia is suspected. [ESC 2023]`,`**Not improving - think**: non-adherence or under-dosing, ongoing ischaemia, uncontrolled AF or high PVC burden, severe MR or AS, alcohol, thyroid disease, anaemia or iron deficiency, sleep apnoea, NSAIDs, or a wrong diagnosis (COPD, obesity, deconditioning, constriction, amyloidosis). [ESC 2021]`,`**Amyloidosis red flags** in HFpEF with thick walls: low-voltage ECG, bilateral carpal tunnel, lumbar canal stenosis, intolerance of antihypertensives - see the Restrictive and infiltrative cardiomyopathy topic. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`The four pillars in HFrEF: start early, titrate fast`,points:[`**Start all four within the first 2-4 weeks** at low dose rather than one at a time to target (ESC 2026 foundational medical therapy; ACC 2024 rapid sequencing); the order is flexible and chosen by the patient's profile. [ESC 2026]`,`**ARNI**: sacubitril-valsartan **49/51 mg twice daily** (24/26 mg if ACE-inhibitor-naive, SBP 100-110, eGFR under 30 or elderly), doubling every 2-4 weeks to **97/103 mg twice daily**; stop an ACE inhibitor **36 hours** before the first dose; PARADIGM-HF cut CV death or HF admission by 20% versus enalapril. [ESC 2021]`,`If ARNI is not possible: **enalapril 2.5 mg twice daily to 10-20 mg twice daily** or **ramipril 2.5 mg daily to 10 mg daily** (or 5 mg twice daily); ARB if ACE-inhibitor cough - **candesartan 4-8 mg to 32 mg daily**, losartan 50 to 150 mg, valsartan 40 to 160 mg twice daily. [ESC 2021]`,`**Beta-blocker** (only the evidence-based four): **bisoprolol 1.25 mg to 10 mg daily**, **carvedilol 3.125 mg to 25 mg twice daily** (50 mg twice daily if over 85 kg), **metoprolol succinate 12.5-25 mg to 200 mg daily**, nebivolol 1.25 mg to 10 mg daily (elderly, SENIORS); double every 2-4 weeks, accept resting rate 50-60/min. [ESC 2021]`,`**MRA**: **spironolactone 12.5-25 mg daily to 50 mg** or **eplerenone 25 mg to 50 mg**; start only if potassium is **5.0 mmol/L or less** and eGFR over 30; MRA is now **Class I at any LVEF** in symptomatic HF (ESC 2026). [ESC 2026]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor**: **dapagliflozin 10 mg or empagliflozin 10 mg once daily**, no titration, from eGFR 20-25, with or without diabetes; benefit appears within 30 days (DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced). [ESC 2023]`,`**Profile-guided order**: high heart rate - beta-blocker early; low BP - SGLT2 inhibitor and MRA first (little BP effect); high potassium - SGLT2 inhibitor first (lowers hyperkalaemia risk) then MRA; congestion - SGLT2 inhibitor and diuretic. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**Titration cadence**: increase one drug every **1-2 weeks** (STRONG-HF reached full doses within 2 weeks of discharge), checking BP, heart rate, symptoms, potassium and creatinine; aim for target or maximum tolerated doses by **3-6 months**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Diuretics** relieve congestion but do not prolong life: use the **lowest dose that keeps the patient dry** (furosemide 20-40 mg, torsemide 5-10 mg) and reduce it as GDMT and SGLT2 inhibitor take effect. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Overcoming barriers: low BP, potassium, kidneys and heart rate`,points:[`**Asymptomatic low BP** (SBP 90-100) is not a reason to stop GDMT; for symptomatic hypotension first **stop non-essential BP drugs** (nitrates, CCB, alpha-blockers), reduce diuretic if dry, and space the doses through the day. [ESC 2021]`,`**Creatinine**: a rise **up to 50% above baseline (or to 3 mg/dL / eGFR 25)** is acceptable after ACE inhibitor, ARB or ARNI; halve the dose if it rises more, and stop only if it **doubles or exceeds 3.5 mg/dL (eGFR under 20)**. [ESC 2021]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitor dip**: an initial eGFR fall of 3-5 mL/min is haemodynamic and expected - do not stop the drug. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Potassium**: up to **5.5 mmol/L is acceptable**; **over 5.5 halve the MRA**, **6.0 or more stop it** and recheck; review diet (salt substitutes, coconut water, fruit) and potassium-raising drugs (trimethoprim, NSAIDs). [ESC 2021]`,`**Potassium binders** (patiromer, sodium zirconium cyclosilicate) may be considered to keep RAAS inhibitors and MRA going in recurrent hyperkalaemia (IIb). [ESC 2021]`,`**Bradycardia**: halve the beta-blocker if heart rate is under 50/min with symptoms; stop other rate-slowing drugs (digoxin, amiodarone, ivabradine) first; consider pacing if a pacing indication exists. [ESC 2021]`,`**ACE-inhibitor cough** - switch to ARB or ARNI; **angioedema on ACE inhibitor** contraindicates ARNI. [ESC 2021]`,`**Never combine** an ACE inhibitor with an ARNI, or ACE inhibitor plus ARB plus MRA (triple RAAS blockade). [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Additional therapies and iron`,points:[`**Ivabradine 2.5-5 mg twice daily to 7.5 mg twice daily** for symptomatic HFrEF with **LVEF 35% or less, sinus rhythm and resting heart rate 70/min or more** despite maximum tolerated beta-blocker (SHIFT); useless in AF; causes phosphenes and bradycardia. [ESC 2021]`,`**Digoxin 62.5-125 mcg daily** (serum level **0.5-0.9 ng/mL**) reduces HF admissions (DIG), and **digitoxin** reduced death or HF admission in DIGIT-HF (2025); ESC 2026 upgrades cardiac glycosides to **IIa** in symptomatic HFrEF despite optimal therapy. [ESC 2026]`,`**Vericiguat 2.5 mg daily to 10 mg** for HFrEF after a **worsening HF event** (VICTORIA, modest reduction in CV death or HF admission); not with nitrates or PDE-5 inhibitors. [ESC 2021]`,`**Hydralazine 37.5 mg plus isosorbide dinitrate 20 mg three times daily, titrated to 75/40 mg** - for self-identified Black patients (A-HeFT) and for patients who cannot take any RAAS inhibitor (renal failure, hyperkalaemia, angioedema). [ESC 2021]`,`**IV iron** for symptomatic HFrEF with iron deficiency improves symptoms and exercise capacity (CONFIRM-HF) and reduces HF admissions (AFFIRM-AHF, IRONMAN; meta-analysis), Class IIa; **oral iron does not work** (IRONOUT-HF). [ESC 2023]`,`**Ferric carboxymaltose** is given as **1000 mg IV over at least 15 minutes**, total 1000-2000 mg by weight and Hb, repeated when indices fall; **ferric derisomaltose 20 mg/kg**; **iron sucrose 200 mg per dose** is the cheaper Indian option (multiple visits); watch for **hypophosphataemia** with FCM. [ESC 2023]`,`**Not recommended**: routine omega-3, statins started for HF alone (CORONA, GISSI-HF), aliskiren, DPP-4 inhibitor saxagliptin, thiazolidinediones and non-DHP CCB in HFrEF. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`HFpEF and the former HFmrEF`,points:[`**SGLT2 inhibitor** (empagliflozin or dapagliflozin 10 mg) is **Class I** for symptomatic HF with LVEF over 40% - EMPEROR-Preserved and DELIVER reduced CV death or worsening HF by about 20%. [ESC 2023]`,`**Finerenone** (FINEARTS-HF, 2024; LVEF 40% or more) reduced CV death and total worsening-HF events (**rate ratio 0.84**): start **10 mg (eGFR 25-60) or 20 mg (eGFR over 60)** daily, target 20 mg or 40 mg, with potassium checks at 4 weeks. [FINEARTS-HF 2024]`,`ESC 2026 makes an **MRA foundational (Class I) at every LVEF** - steroidal (spironolactone) or non-steroidal (finerenone) in HFpEF; spironolactone (TOPCAT Americas) is the low-cost Indian choice. [ESC 2026]`,`**Obesity-related HFpEF**: **semaglutide 2.4 mg weekly** (STEP-HFpEF: better symptoms, walk distance, weight) and **tirzepatide up to 15 mg weekly** (SUMMIT: fewer CV deaths or worsening HF events) - ESC 2026 **Class IIa**. [ESC 2026]`,`Control **BP (systolic under 130 mmHg)**, AF (rate or rhythm control, anticoagulation), diabetes, CKD, sleep apnoea and ischaemia; diuretics for congestion. [ESC 2026]`,`ARNI has a weaker signal in HFpEF (PARAGON-HF, benefit at the lower end of normal LVEF and in women) - **IIb** in AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`,`Patients with **LVEF 41-49%** now follow the HFrEF pathway (ESC 2026); patients with HFimpEF continue their full regimen indefinitely. [ESC 2026]`,`**Exercise training** improves exercise capacity and quality of life in HFpEF and is recommended in every phenotype. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Devices: ICD, CRT and beyond`,points:[`**ICD for secondary prevention** after ventricular arrhythmia causing haemodynamic instability (VF or unstable VT) if expected survival is over 1 year in good functional status (Class I). [ESC 2021]`,`**ICD for primary prevention**: symptomatic **ischaemic HFrEF (NYHA II-III), LVEF 35% or less despite at least 3 months of optimal therapy** - recommended; **non-ischaemic** HFrEF - should be considered (DANISH showed no overall mortality benefit); **not within 40 days of MI**. [ESC 2026]`,`**CRT** (CRT-P or CRT-D) is **recommended** in sinus rhythm with **LVEF 35% or less and LBBB with QRS 150 ms or more** despite optimal therapy, **should be considered** for LBBB 130-149 ms or non-LBBB 150 ms or more, and is **contraindicated with QRS under 130 ms** unless pacing is needed. [ESC 2026]`,`In HFrEF with **high-degree AV block needing ventricular pacing**, use **CRT rather than RV pacing** regardless of NYHA class; in AF, CRT needs a strategy to ensure near-100% biventricular capture (often AV-node ablation). [ESC 2021]`,`**Conduction system pacing** (His-bundle or left bundle branch area pacing) is an alternative when a coronary sinus lead fails, and increasingly as primary resynchronisation at experienced centres. [HRS 2023]`,`A **wearable cardioverter-defibrillator** can bridge early high-risk periods (recent MI, peripartum or new cardiomyopathy) while LVEF recovery is awaited. [ESC 2022]`,`**Transcatheter edge-to-edge mitral repair (TEER)** for severe secondary MR persisting on optimal GDMT (COAPT-type patients, LVEF 20-50%) reduces HF admissions and death. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Others at specialist centres: **CardioMEMS** pulmonary artery pressure monitoring (IIb), cardiac contractility modulation, **durable LVAD** and **heart transplantation** for stage D. [ESC 2021]`,`In India **PM-JAY** and several state schemes cover pacemakers and some ICD/CRT procedures, but device uptake remains low - document the indication clearly in the referral. [NHA 2024]`]},{heading:`Non-drug care: self-care, salt, fluid, exercise, vaccination and the HF clinic`,points:[`**Multidisciplinary HF management programme** (HF clinic, nurse-led titration, structured telephone follow-up) is **Class I** - it reduces admissions and death. [ESC 2021]`,`**Self-care education**: daily weight on waking, symptom diary, adherence, recognising worsening; **weight gain of 2 kg in 3 days** triggers a call or the agreed extra diuretic dose. [ESC 2021]`,`**Salt**: avoid more than **5 g salt (2 g sodium) a day** - pickles, papad, namkeen, processed food; stricter restriction (under 1.5 g sodium) gave no benefit in SODIUM-HF. [ESC 2021]`,`**Fluid restriction of 1.5-2 L/day** only for severe congestion or hyponatraemia; FRESH-UP (2025) found liberal fluid intake no worse than restriction in stable outpatients, and thirst was less. [FRESH-UP 2025]`,`**Exercise and cardiac rehabilitation** are **Class I** in all stable patients (HF-ACTION): start with 20-30 minutes of walking at a talk-test intensity on most days. [ESC 2021]`,`**Vaccination**: annual **influenza** and **pneumococcal** vaccine (IIa), COVID-19 per national advice and **RSV vaccine** for older adults with HF where available. [ESC 2021]`,`**Lifestyle**: stop tobacco, avoid alcohol in alcoholic cardiomyopathy (otherwise no more than 2 units a day), lose weight if obese, treat sleep apnoea, avoid NSAIDs; sexual activity is safe in NYHA I-II. [ESC 2021]`,`**Follow-up**: every **1-2 weeks while titrating**, then every **3-6 months** when stable; NP-NCD and Ayushman Arogya Mandirs can share titration with a protocol and potassium checks. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly and frail**: start lower, titrate more slowly, check **standing BP**, prefer nebivolol or bisoprolol, and deprescribe drugs that cause orthostasis or falls. [ESC 2021]`,`**CKD**: SGLT2 inhibitor down to eGFR 20; ARNI or ACE inhibitor with lower start below eGFR 30; MRA if eGFR over 30 and potassium 5.0 or less; finerenone in diabetic CKD; see KDIGO 2024. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Diabetes**: SGLT2 inhibitor first; metformin is safe if eGFR is over 30; GLP-1 RA is safe; **avoid saxagliptin and pioglitazone**; insulin may worsen fluid retention. [ADA 2025]`,`**Women planning pregnancy**: stop **ACE inhibitor, ARB, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor and ivabradine** before conception; continue beta-blocker (metoprolol); use hydralazine-nitrate; refer for pre-pregnancy counselling. [ESC 2025]`,`**COPD or asthma**: **cardioselective beta-blockers** (bisoprolol, metoprolol, nebivolol) are safe in COPD and usually tolerated in mild asthma - not a reason to withhold. [ESC 2021]`,`**AF**: rate control (beta-blocker, then digoxin), anticoagulation by CHA2DS2-VA, and **catheter ablation** improves outcomes in selected HFrEF (CASTLE-AF); ivabradine has no role in AF. [ESC 2024]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: the low-cost four-drug set is **enalapril or losartan, metoprolol succinate or bisoprolol, spironolactone and dapagliflozin** - all available as generics or through Jan Aushadhi. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Advanced heart failure and palliative care`,points:[`Refer early to an **advanced HF centre** when 'I NEED HELP' triggers appear - LVAD and transplant work only if referral precedes end-organ failure. [ESC 2021]`,`**Palliative care is not only for the last days**: integrate it for NYHA IV, frequent admissions, cachexia or poor quality of life alongside GDMT (PAL-HF improved quality of life). [ESC 2021]`,`**Advance care planning**: discuss prognosis, preferred place of care, resuscitation and ICD deactivation while the patient can decide, and record it. [ESC 2021]`,`**ICD deactivation** of shock therapy at end of life prevents painful futile shocks; pacing and CRT can continue. [ESC 2021]`,`**Breathlessness**: continue diuretics, use a fan and positioning; **low-dose oral morphine** (for example 2.5 mg every 4 hours as needed) may relieve refractory dyspnoea; oxygen only if hypoxic. [ESC 2021]`,`**Deprescribe**: stop statins and drugs without short-term benefit, reduce BP drugs causing hypotension; **palliative home inotropes** may relieve symptoms in selected stage D patients. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`,`In India the **National Programme for Palliative Care** (under NP-NCD) and the 2014 NDPS amendment allow trained centres to prescribe oral morphine - link the family to the nearest palliative unit. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2026**: two phenotypes (HFrEF under 50%, HFpEF 50% or more), stages A-D, and new terms - **foundational medical therapy**, additional medical therapy and guideline-directed interventional therapy. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2026**: **MRA Class I at any LVEF**; SGLT2 inhibitor Class I across the spectrum; **semaglutide or tirzepatide IIa in obese HFpEF**; systolic BP target under 130 mmHg in stage B. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2026**: cardiac glycosides (digoxin or digitoxin) upgraded to **IIa** after DIGIT-HF; ICD and CRT indications as above. [ESC 2026]`,`**ESC 2023 focused update**: SGLT2 inhibitor Class I in HFmrEF and HFpEF; IV iron IIa to reduce HF admissions; finerenone for diabetic CKD to prevent HF; rapid up-titration after admission Class I. [ESC 2023]`,`**FINEARTS-HF (2024)** established finerenone in HFpEF and **SUMMIT (2024)** tirzepatide in obese HFpEF. [FINEARTS-HF 2024]`,`**2024 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway for HFrEF** endorses rapid sequencing of all four drugs and patient-profile-based ordering. [ACC ECDP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`List the **four pillars with start and target doses** - the single most-asked table in HF. [ESC 2026]`,`Know why **only four beta-blockers** count (bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, nebivolol) - atenolol and metoprolol tartrate are not evidence-based in HFrEF. [ESC 2021]`,`The **36-hour ACE-inhibitor washout** before ARNI (angioedema) is a favourite MCQ. [ESC 2021]`,`Recite the **potassium rules**: start MRA if 5.0 or less, halve if over 5.5, stop if 6.0 or more. [ESC 2021]`,`**CRT trio**: LVEF 35% or less, LBBB, QRS 150 ms or more on optimal therapy - and never with QRS under 130 ms. [ESC 2026]`,`Common mistake: titrating one drug to target before starting the next - start all four low, then titrate. [ESC 2026]`,`Common mistake: stopping GDMT when LVEF recovers - **HFimpEF relapses** (TRED-HF). [TRED-HF 2019]`,`Know the **iron deficiency definition** and that oral iron fails in HF. [ESC 2023]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Foundational and additional drugs in chronic HF`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Sacubitril-valsartan (ARNI)`,`Start 24/26 or 49/51 mg oral twice daily`,`Double every 2-4 weeks to 97/103 mg twice daily; lifelong`,`Hypotension, hyperkalaemia, AKI; angioedema - 36 h ACEi washout; pregnancy`],[`Enalapril / ramipril (ACEi)`,`Enalapril 2.5 mg twice daily; ramipril 2.5 mg daily`,`To enalapril 10-20 mg twice daily; ramipril 10 mg daily`,`Cough, angioedema, hyperkalaemia, AKI; pregnancy, bilateral RAS`],[`Candesartan / losartan / valsartan (ARB)`,`Candesartan 4-8 mg; losartan 50 mg; valsartan 40 mg twice daily`,`To candesartan 32 mg, losartan 150 mg, valsartan 160 mg twice daily`,`Hyperkalaemia, AKI; pregnancy`],[`Bisoprolol`,`1.25 mg oral once daily`,`Double every 2-4 weeks to 10 mg`,`Bradycardia, fatigue, hypotension; avoid in decompensation or high-grade AV block`],[`Carvedilol`,`3.125 mg oral twice daily`,`To 25 mg twice daily (50 mg if over 85 kg)`,`Hypotension (alpha-blockade), bradycardia; asthma`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`12.5-25 mg oral once daily`,`To 200 mg once daily`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; tartrate form not evidence-based`],[`Spironolactone / eplerenone (MRA)`,`Spironolactone 12.5-25 mg; eplerenone 25 mg daily`,`To 50 mg; K and creatinine at 1-2 weeks, 4 weeks, then 3-4 monthly`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia (spironolactone); avoid K over 5.0, eGFR under 30`],[`Finerenone`,`10 mg (eGFR 25-60) or 20 mg (over 60) once daily`,`To 20 or 40 mg; K at 4 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia; CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`No titration; lifelong`,`Genital mycotic infection, volume depletion, euglycaemic ketoacidosis; sick-day rules`],[`Ivabradine`,`2.5-5 mg oral twice daily`,`To 7.5 mg twice daily; target HR 50-60`,`Phosphenes, bradycardia; sinus rhythm only; pregnancy`],[`Digoxin`,`62.5-125 mcg oral daily`,`Level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL`,`Toxicity with low K, CKD, amiodarone, verapamil; nausea, arrhythmia, xanthopsia`],[`Vericiguat`,`2.5 mg oral daily with food`,`Double every 2 weeks to 10 mg`,`Hypotension, anaemia; no nitrates or PDE-5 inhibitors; pregnancy`],[`Hydralazine + isosorbide dinitrate`,`37.5 mg + 20 mg oral three times daily`,`To 75 mg + 40 mg three times daily`,`Headache, hypotension, drug-induced lupus (hydralazine)`],[`Ferric carboxymaltose`,`1000 mg IV over 15 minutes or more`,`Total 1000-2000 mg by weight and Hb; recheck at 3-6 months`,`Hypophosphataemia, hypersensitivity, skin staining if extravasated`]]},{heading:`Monitoring rules during titration`,columns:[`Problem`,`Acceptable`,`Reduce`,`Stop`],rows:[[`Creatinine on ACEi/ARB/ARNI`,`Rise up to 50% or to 3 mg/dL (eGFR 25)`,`Rise over 50% - halve dose, review diuretic and NSAIDs`,`Rise over 100% or over 3.5 mg/dL (eGFR under 20)`],[`Potassium on MRA/RAASi`,`Up to 5.5 mmol/L`,`Over 5.5 - halve MRA, review diet and drugs`,`6.0 or more - stop MRA and recheck`],[`Systolic BP`,`90-100 mmHg without symptoms`,`Symptomatic - stop nitrates/CCB, reduce diuretic if dry`,`Shock or SBP under 80 with symptoms`],[`Heart rate on beta-blocker`,`50-60/min`,`Under 50 with symptoms - halve dose`,`High-grade AV block, shock`],[`eGFR on SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Initial dip of 3-5 mL/min`,`Not needed`,`Dialysis; pause in acute illness or fasting`]]},{heading:`Device indications in HFrEF`,columns:[`Device`,`Indication`,`Strength`,`Notes`],rows:[[`ICD (secondary)`,`VF or haemodynamically unstable VT, survival over 1 year`,`Recommended`,`Exclude reversible cause`],[`ICD (primary, ischaemic)`,`NYHA II-III, LVEF 35% or less after 3 months optimal therapy`,`Recommended`,`Not within 40 days of MI`],[`ICD (primary, non-ischaemic)`,`NYHA II-III, LVEF 35% or less after 3 months optimal therapy`,`Should be considered`,`DANISH: no overall survival gain; younger patients benefit more`],[`CRT`,`Sinus rhythm, LVEF 35% or less, LBBB, QRS 150 ms or more`,`Recommended`,`CRT-D if ICD also indicated`],[`CRT`,`LBBB 130-149 ms or non-LBBB 150 ms or more`,`Should be considered`,`QRS under 130 ms - CRT contraindicated`],[`CRT instead of RV pacing`,`HFrEF needing ventricular pacing for AV block`,`Recommended`,`Any NYHA class`],[`TEER (mitral)`,`Severe secondary MR on optimal GDMT, suitable anatomy`,`Should be considered`,`COAPT-type patients`],[`LVAD / transplant`,`Stage D despite optimal therapy`,`Specialist decision`,`Refer before end-organ failure`]]},{heading:`HF phenotypes and stages (ESC 2026)`,columns:[`Category`,`Definition`,`Core treatment`],rows:[[`HFrEF`,`Symptoms with LVEF under 50% (includes former HFmrEF 41-49%)`,`ARNI/ACEi/ARB + beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i`],[`HFpEF`,`Symptoms, LVEF 50% or more, structural/diastolic abnormality or raised NP`,`SGLT2i + MRA (spironolactone or finerenone); GLP-1-based therapy if obese; diuretic`],[`HFimpEF`,`LVEF 40% or less rising 10 points or more to over 40%`,`Continue all therapy`],[`Stage A`,`At risk: hypertension, diabetes, obesity, cardiotoxins`,`Treat risk factors; SGLT2i in diabetes/CKD`],[`Stage B`,`Structural disease or raised NP, no symptoms`,`ACEi/ARB, beta-blocker after MI or low LVEF; SBP under 130`],[`Stage C`,`Current or past symptoms`,`Phenotype-specific GDMT and devices`],[`Stage D`,`Advanced HF despite optimal therapy`,`LVAD, transplant or palliative care`]]},{heading:`Patient not improving: causes and checks`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Check / action`],rows:[[`Non-adherence or under-dosing`,`Doses below target, missed refills`,`Pill count, cost, generics, simplify regimen`],[`Ongoing ischaemia`,`Angina, regional wall motion defect`,`Stress imaging or angiography; revascularise if viable`],[`Uncontrolled AF or high PVC burden`,`Irregular pulse, palpitations`,`ECG, Holter; rate/rhythm control, ablation`],[`Severe valve disease`,`Murmur of MR or AS`,`Echo; TEER, TAVI or surgery`],[`Iron deficiency or anaemia`,`Fatigue out of proportion`,`Ferritin, TSAT; IV iron`],[`Alcohol, thyroid, NSAIDs`,`History, tremor, goitre`,`Abstinence, TSH, stop NSAIDs`],[`Wrong or second diagnosis`,`Thick walls, low voltage, COPD, obesity`,`CMR, PYP scan, spirometry, sleep study`]]}],redFlags:[`Weight gain over 2 kg in 3 days with orthopnoea not settling on an extra diuretic dose - decompensation needing IV therapy.`,`Potassium 6.0 mmol/L or more, or creatinine more than doubled, on RAAS inhibitor or MRA.`,`Syncope, sustained palpitations or an ICD shock - arrhythmia evaluation the same day.`,`Symptomatic hypotension (SBP under 85-90 mmHg) with cold peripheries or confusion - low output.`,`Two or more HF admissions in 12 months, inotrope need or cachexia - advanced HF referral.`,`Heart rate under 40/min or high-grade AV block on beta-blocker, digoxin or ivabradine.`,`Suspected digoxin toxicity: vomiting, visual change, new arrhythmia, especially with low potassium or AKI.`],pearls:[`**Four pillars, low and early, then titrate** - ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor within weeks.`,`Only **four beta-blockers** have HF evidence: bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, nebivolol.`,`**36 hours** between the last ACE-inhibitor dose and the first ARNI dose.`,`Potassium **5.0 to start, 5.5 to halve, 6.0 to stop** the MRA.`,`Creatinine up **50% is acceptable** on RAAS blockade; doubling means stop.`,`**Diuretics for symptoms, the pillars for survival.**`,`CRT = **LVEF 35% or less + LBBB + QRS 150 ms or more**; ICD = LVEF 35% or less after **3 months** of optimal therapy, not within 40 days of MI.`,`Iron deficiency: **ferritin under 100, or 100-299 with TSAT under 20%** - treat IV, never orally.`,`**HFimpEF is not cured HF** - never stop therapy (TRED-HF).`,`ESC 2026: **MRA at every LVEF**; semaglutide or tirzepatide in obese HFpEF.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old with HFrEF (LVEF 30%) on sacubitril-valsartan 49/51 mg twice daily, bisoprolol 5 mg, spironolactone 25 mg and dapagliflozin 10 mg has a routine potassium of 5.8 mmol/L and creatinine 1.4 mg/dL (baseline 1.3). ECG is normal. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Stop all four drugs and recheck in a week`,`Stop dapagliflozin because it causes hyperkalaemia`,`Halve spironolactone to 12.5 mg, review diet and drugs, and recheck potassium`,`Continue unchanged because potassium up to 6.0 is acceptable`],answer:2,explanation:`ESC practical rules: potassium up to 5.5 mmol/L is acceptable, over 5.5 means halving the MRA, and 6.0 or more means stopping it. Stopping all four drugs throws away prognostic benefit; dapagliflozin actually lowers the risk of hyperkalaemia; continuing unchanged ignores a potassium already above the 5.5 threshold.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-q2`,stem:`A 58-year-old with non-ischaemic HFrEF has LVEF 30%, NYHA class II, sinus rhythm and LBBB with QRS 160 ms after 4 months of optimised GDMT. Which device therapy is most appropriate?`,options:[`Cardiac resynchronisation therapy (CRT-P or CRT-D)`,`Single-chamber ICD alone`,`No device until NYHA class IV`,`Dual-chamber pacemaker with right ventricular pacing`],answer:0,explanation:`LVEF 35% or less, sinus rhythm and LBBB with QRS 150 ms or more despite optimal therapy is a Class I CRT indication in NYHA II-IV; CRT-D is chosen if an ICD is also indicated. An ICD alone forgoes resynchronisation benefit, waiting for NYHA IV loses the benefit seen in milder patients, and RV pacing worsens dyssynchrony.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-q3`,stem:`Which trial established finerenone for patients with heart failure and LVEF 40% or more?`,options:[`PARADIGM-HF`,`TOPCAT`,`DAPA-HF`,`FINEARTS-HF`],answer:3,explanation:`FINEARTS-HF (2024) showed that finerenone reduced cardiovascular death and total worsening HF events in HF with LVEF 40% or more. PARADIGM-HF compared sacubitril-valsartan with enalapril in HFrEF, TOPCAT tested spironolactone in HFpEF with a neutral overall result, and DAPA-HF tested dapagliflozin in HFrEF.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-q4`,stem:`A woman with HFrEF and NYHA class III fatigue has Hb 12.4 g/dL, ferritin 160 ng/mL and transferrin saturation 14%. What is the best management of her iron status?`,options:[`No treatment because she is not anaemic and ferritin is over 100`,`Intravenous ferric carboxymaltose`,`Oral ferrous sulphate 200 mg three times daily for 3 months`,`Packed red cell transfusion`],answer:1,explanation:`Iron deficiency in HF is ferritin under 100, or 100-299 ng/mL with TSAT under 20% - she qualifies even without anaemia, and IV iron (ferric carboxymaltose or derisomaltose) improves symptoms and reduces HF admissions. Oral iron failed in IRONOUT-HF because hepcidin and gut oedema block absorption; transfusion is not indicated with Hb 12.4.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-q5`,stem:`A patient on enalapril 10 mg twice daily is to be switched to sacubitril-valsartan. What is the required interval between the last enalapril dose and the first sacubitril-valsartan dose?`,options:[`No interval is needed`,`12 hours`,`36 hours`,`7 days`],answer:2,explanation:`Neprilysin and ACE both degrade bradykinin; overlapping them sharply raises the risk of angioedema, so ACE inhibitors must be stopped at least 36 hours before starting an ARNI. No interval or 12 hours is unsafe, and a 7-day gap is unnecessary and leaves the patient untreated; switching from an ARB needs no washout.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe guideline-directed medical therapy for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, including the titration schedule, monitoring, additional therapies and device indications.`,openingLines:[`HFrEF (ESC 2026: symptomatic HF with LVEF under 50%) is treated with four foundational drugs - ARNI or ACE inhibitor/ARB, evidence-based beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor - started at low dose within weeks and titrated every 1-2 weeks.`,`Together they roughly halve cardiovascular death or HF admission; devices (ICD, CRT) are added after 3 months of optimised therapy if LVEF remains 35% or less.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and goals`,points:[`Phenotypes HFrEF under 50%, HFpEF 50% or more; HFimpEF; stages A-D; NYHA class.`,`Goals: survival, fewer admissions, better function and quality of life.`]},{heading:`Four pillars with doses`,points:[`Sacubitril-valsartan 24/26-49/51 to 97/103 mg twice daily (36 h after ACEi) or enalapril 2.5 to 10-20 mg twice daily.`,`Bisoprolol 1.25 to 10 mg, carvedilol 3.125 to 25 mg twice daily, metoprolol succinate 12.5-25 to 200 mg.`,`Spironolactone 12.5-25 to 50 mg (K 5.0 or less, eGFR over 30); dapagliflozin or empagliflozin 10 mg.`]},{heading:`Titration schedule`,points:[`Start all four within 2-4 weeks, order by profile (HR, BP, K, congestion); one step every 1-2 weeks; target by 3-6 months; STRONG-HF after admission.`]},{heading:`Monitoring and barriers`,points:[`K and creatinine 1-2 weeks after each change; creatinine rise up to 50% acceptable; K over 5.5 halve MRA, 6.0 stop.`,`Asymptomatic low BP is acceptable; stop non-essential vasodilators first; halve beta-blocker for symptomatic HR under 50.`]},{heading:`Additional therapies`,points:[`Ivabradine (sinus, HR 70 or more, LVEF 35% or less), digoxin/digitoxin (IIa ESC 2026), vericiguat, hydralazine-ISDN, IV iron (ferritin under 100 or 100-299 with TSAT under 20%).`,`Diuretics for congestion only; avoid NSAIDs, pioglitazone, non-DHP CCB.`]},{heading:`Devices`,points:[`ICD: secondary prevention; primary in ischaemic (recommended) or non-ischaemic (consider) NYHA II-III with LVEF 35% or less after 3 months; not within 40 days of MI.`,`CRT: LVEF 35% or less, sinus, LBBB QRS 150 ms or more (recommended), 130-149 ms (consider); TEER, LVAD, transplant.`]},{heading:`Non-drug care`,points:[`HF clinic, self-care, salt under 5 g, cardiac rehabilitation, influenza and pneumococcal vaccine, palliative care in stage D.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of the four pillars with start dose, target dose and key monitoring`,`Flow chart: start four drugs, titrate, reassess LVEF at 3 months, then ICD/CRT decision`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Four pillars with doses`,marks:3},{part:`Titration and monitoring`,marks:2},{part:`Additional therapies`,marks:1.5},{part:`Devices`,marks:1.5},{part:`Non-drug care`,marks:1}],keywords:[`four pillars`,`ARNI`,`titration`,`potassium rules`,`ICD`,`CRT`,`IV iron`,`HFimpEF`]},{id:`cardiovascular-chronic-hf-optimisation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Palliative and end-of-life care in advanced heart failure.`,openingLines:[`Advanced (stage D) heart failure is persistent NYHA III-IV symptoms with severe dysfunction, repeated admissions or IV therapy and poor exercise capacity despite optimal therapy.`,`Palliative care should run alongside GDMT, not replace it, and begins with recognising the 'I NEED HELP' triggers.`],answer:[{heading:`Recognition`,points:[`I NEED HELP: inotropes, NYHA IV/high NT-proBNP, end-organ dysfunction, EF 35% or less, defibrillator shocks, more than one admission in 12 months, oedema despite diuretics, low BP, prognostic drug intolerance.`]},{heading:`Decisions`,points:[`Refer eligible patients for LVAD or transplant early; otherwise shift goals to comfort with advance care planning and a named contact.`]},{heading:`Symptom control`,points:[`Continue diuretics; low-dose oral morphine for refractory breathlessness; fan, positioning; treat pain, depression, constipation; oxygen only if hypoxic.`,`Deprescribe statins and drugs causing hypotension; palliative home inotropes in selected patients.`]},{heading:`Devices and family`,points:[`Discuss and deactivate ICD shocks at end of life; keep pacing.`,`Support carers, preferred place of death; link to NPPC palliative units and oral morphine access under the NDPS amendment.`]}],mustDraw:[`Box listing the I NEED HELP triggers`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and triggers`,marks:1.5},{part:`Symptom control`,marks:1.5},{part:`Advance care planning and ICD`,marks:1},{part:`Indian programme link`,marks:1}],keywords:[`stage D`,`I NEED HELP`,`advance care planning`,`ICD deactivation`,`morphine`,`NPPC`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Heart failure: management.`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022.`,`2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure (Eur Heart J 2026).`,`2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure, and the 2023 Focused Update.`,`2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure; 2024 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway for Treatment of HFrEF.`,`Solomon SD et al. Finerenone in heart failure with mildly reduced or preserved ejection fraction (FINEARTS-HF), N Engl J Med, 2024.`,`Harikrishnan S et al. National Heart Failure Registry of India (ICMR), Nature Communications, 2025.`,`National List of Essential Medicines, India, 2022; National Programme for Palliative Care, MoHFW.`]}),i.push({id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy`,title:`Dilated cardiomyopathy, peripartum and other secondary cardiomyopathies`,oneLiner:`**Dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM)** is **LV (or biventricular) dilatation with global or regional systolic dysfunction not explained by abnormal loading conditions or coronary disease** (ESC 2023); about **30-40% is genetic** (TTN truncations commonest, LMNA most malignant), the rest secondary to **alcohol, tachycardia, pregnancy (peripartum cardiomyopathy), chemotherapy, myocarditis, nutritional or endocrine causes** - work-up excludes CAD and reversible causes, treatment is HFrEF GDMT plus cause-specific therapy (abstinence, rate control, **bromocriptine with anticoagulation in PPCM**), arrhythmic risk guides ICD, and **first-degree relatives are screened**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`DCM`,`idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy`,`non-dilated left ventricular cardiomyopathy NDLVC`,`familial cardiomyopathy TTN LMNA`,`alcoholic cardiomyopathy`,`tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy`,`PVC-induced cardiomyopathy`,`peripartum cardiomyopathy PPCM`,`bromocriptine BOARD`,`anthracycline cardiotoxicity`,`trastuzumab`,`cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction`,`thalassaemia iron overload T2*`,`beriberi thiamine`,`Duchenne Becker muscular dystrophy`,`family screening cascade genetic testing`,`late gadolinium enhancement`,`wearable cardioverter-defibrillator`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**DCM** is LV or biventricular **dilatation with systolic dysfunction** not explained by abnormal loading (hypertension, valve disease) or coronary artery disease. [ESC 2023]`,`ESC 2023 adds **non-dilated LV cardiomyopathy (NDLVC)** - non-ischaemic LV scar or fatty replacement, or isolated global hypokinesia without dilatation - which shares genes and arrhythmic risk with DCM. [ESC 2023]`,`Prevalence is estimated at **1 in 250-500** adults; DCM is the commonest indication for heart transplantation worldwide. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial DCM** accounts for **30-40%** of apparently idiopathic cases when relatives are screened. [ESC 2023]`,`In the Indian NHFR, **dilated cardiomyopathy** was the second commonest cause of HF admission after ischaemic heart disease. [NHFR 2025]`,`**Peripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM)** incidence varies widely - about 1 in 1000 to 1 in 4000 deliveries globally, much higher in Nigeria and Haiti; Indian hospital series report it regularly, often in young multiparous or pre-eclamptic women. [ESC 2025]`,`Prognosis has improved with modern GDMT: up to **40% show LV reverse remodelling** (HFimpEF) within 1-2 years. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`**Genetic** (autosomal dominant mostly): **TTN truncating variants** (about 20-25% of familial DCM, alcohol and pregnancy unmask them), **LMNA** (conduction disease, AF, malignant arrhythmia), **FLNC, DSP, RBM20, PLN, SCN5A, BAG3**, desmin. [ESC 2023]`,`**Alcohol**: usually more than **80 g/day for 5 years or more**; partial or full recovery with abstinence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tachycardia-induced**: persistent AF or flutter with fast rate, incessant SVT or atrial tachycardia, and **PVC burden over 10-15%** (risk rises above 20-24%). [ESC 2022]`,`**Peripartum**: LV dysfunction towards the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery without another cause. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cancer therapy**: anthracyclines (dose-dependent, irreversible type I), trastuzumab (largely reversible type II), immune checkpoint inhibitors (myocarditis), radiotherapy - details in the Heart in systemic disease topic. [ESC 2022]`,`**Post-myocarditis** (viral, autoimmune, HIV), **nutritional** (thiamine - wet beriberi; selenium), **endocrine** (thyrotoxicosis, hypothyroidism, acromegaly, phaeochromocytoma), **iron overload** (thalassaemia major, haemochromatosis), **drugs** (cocaine, amphetamines). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neuromuscular**: Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy (dystrophin), myotonic dystrophy (conduction disease), Friedreich ataxia - female Duchenne carriers can develop DCM. [ESC 2023]`,`**Chagas disease** is a major cause in Latin America but is **not found in India**; sarcoidosis presents as DCM with heart block or VT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Myocyte loss or sarcomere and cytoskeletal dysfunction lowers contractility; the ventricle dilates (Frank-Starling) to keep stroke volume, raising wall stress (Laplace) and oxygen demand. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurohormonal activation** (RAAS, sympathetic) drives fibrosis and further dilatation - the target of the four pillars. [Harrison 22e]`,`Annular dilatation and leaflet tethering cause **functional mitral regurgitation**, which further volume-loads the LV. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mid-wall fibrosis** (late gadolinium enhancement) forms a re-entry substrate for VT and predicts sudden death independent of LVEF. [ESC 2023]`,`**Alcohol** and its metabolite acetaldehyde impair excitation-contraction coupling and mitochondrial function. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PPCM**: late-pregnancy oxidative stress activates **cathepsin D, which cleaves prolactin into an angiostatic 16-kDa fragment** that damages endothelium and myocytes; placental **sFlt-1** (shared with pre-eclampsia) adds anti-angiogenic injury - the basis for bromocriptine. [ESC 2025]`,`**Anthracyclines** inhibit topoisomerase 2-beta and generate free radicals, causing cumulative irreversible myocyte loss; risk rises steeply above **400 mg/m2 doxorubicin** but exists at any dose. [ESC 2022]`,`Tachycardia depletes myocardial energy and desynchronises contraction; LV function usually **recovers within weeks to months** of rate control. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`ESC 2023 classifies cardiomyopathies by **phenotype** - HCM, DCM, NDLVC, arrhythmogenic RV cardiomyopathy, restrictive - then searches for genetic and acquired causes. [ESC 2023]`,`Aetiological split: **genetic, acquired (toxic, inflammatory, nutritional, endocrine, peripartum, tachycardia) and idiopathic** after full work-up. [ESC 2023]`,`HF phenotype is labelled by ESC 2026 LVEF: **HFrEF under 50%**, which includes almost all DCM. [ESC 2026]`,`**PPCM definition** (HFA-ESC): HF from LV systolic dysfunction with **LVEF under 45%**, towards the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery (mostly the first month), with no other cause; the LV may not be dilated. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction** (ESC 2022): symptomatic, or asymptomatic graded mild (LVEF 50% or more with GLS fall over 15% or new biomarker rise), moderate (LVEF fall of 10 points to 40-49%) or severe (LVEF under 40%). [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Commonest presentation is **HF** - exertional breathlessness, orthopnoea, fatigue, oedema - in a young or middle-aged adult without angina. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other presentations: palpitations or AF, **syncope or sudden death** (especially LMNA), embolic stroke from LV thrombus, or an incidental ECG or echo finding during family screening. [ESC 2023]`,`Ask: alcohol (quantity and years), pregnancy or recent delivery, chemotherapy or radiotherapy, palpitations, recent viral illness, thyroid symptoms, diet (polished rice, alcohol), transfusion history (thalassaemia), muscle weakness. [ESC 2023]`,`Take a **three-generation family history**: HF, unexplained sudden death under 50, pacemakers at a young age, muscular dystrophy. [ESC 2023]`,`**Examination**: displaced diffuse apex, S3, functional MR murmur (pansystolic at apex), raised JVP, crackles, oedema; low pulse volume and pulsus alternans in severe cases. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Clues to cause**: proximal weakness and calf hypertrophy (Duchenne/Becker), myotonia and frontal balding (myotonic dystrophy), bronze skin and diabetes (haemochromatosis), peripheral neuropathy and high-output state (beriberi), goitre or tremor (thyroid). [ESC 2023]`,`**PPCM** symptoms overlap with normal late pregnancy (breathlessness, oedema) - suspect it with **orthopnoea, PND, tachycardia or desaturation** and check NT-proBNP and echo. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Ischaemic cardiomyopathy**: risk factors, angina, prior MI, regional wall motion abnormality in a coronary territory, subendocardial LGE - always exclude with angiography or CT. [ESC 2023]`,`**Hypertensive heart disease** in the dilated phase: long-standing severe hypertension, LVH on ECG, history of hypertensive retinopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Valvular disease**: severe AR or primary MR dilates the LV - the valve is structurally abnormal on echo (rheumatic thickening, prolapse). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Myocarditis**: acute onset after viral illness, chest pain, troponin rise, oedema and non-ischaemic LGE on CMR - see the Myocarditis topic. [ESC 2025]`,`**Athlete's heart**: mild LV dilatation with normal LVEF at exercise or improvement on stress echo, normal GLS, regression on deconditioning. [ESC 2023]`,`**Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy with LV involvement** and **LV non-compaction phenotype** - CMR pattern and genetics distinguish them. [ESC 2023]`,`**Restrictive or infiltrative disease (amyloid, sarcoid)**: thick walls, low-voltage ECG, conduction block - see the Restrictive cardiomyopathy topic. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: LBBB (CRT candidate), low voltage, AF, Q waves (ischaemia or scar), **PR prolongation or AV block with DCM suggests LMNA, sarcoid or myotonic dystrophy**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Echo**: dilated LV (LVEDD over 58 mm in men, 52 mm in women, or indexed), LVEF, functional MR, LA size, RV function, **LV thrombus**, pulmonary pressure. [ESC 2023]`,`**NT-proBNP**, troponin, renal function, electrolytes, **TSH**, ferritin and TSAT (haemochromatosis), **CK** (dystrophinopathies), HIV, HbA1c, Hb, calcium, phosphate; thiamine trial where deficiency is plausible. [ESC 2023]`,`**Exclude CAD**: coronary angiography or **CT coronary angiography** in all with risk factors or age over 35-40. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cardiac MRI** (Class I): volumes, LVEF, **LGE pattern** - mid-wall septal stripe in DCM, subendocardial in infarction, subepicardial in myocarditis; **T2* under 20 ms** indicates myocardial iron (under 10 ms severe). [ESC 2023]`,`**Holter or monitor**: AF burden, NSVT, **PVC burden over 10%** (consider tachycardia-induced cause). [ESC 2022]`,`**Genetic testing** is recommended in DCM and NDLVC (Class I), especially with family history, conduction disease or early onset - yield is about 30-40%. [ESC 2023]`,`**Endomyocardial biopsy** only when the result changes treatment - suspected giant-cell myocarditis, sarcoid, or eosinophilic disease with rapid deterioration. [ESC 2023]`,`In **PPCM**: ECG, echo, NT-proBNP; exclude pre-eclampsia, PE, amniotic fluid embolism and pre-existing DCM; CMR without gadolinium in pregnancy. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Start **HFrEF GDMT**: ARNI or ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor, titrated every 1-2 weeks - see the Chronic HF optimisation topic. [ESC 2026]`,`**Alcoholic cardiomyopathy**: **complete abstinence** (not just reduction), thiamine 100 mg daily, treat withdrawal and dependence (baclofen, naltrexone, counselling); LVEF often improves within 6-12 months. [ESC 2023]`,`**Tachycardia-induced**: rate control (beta-blocker, digoxin) or rhythm control and **catheter ablation** for AF, flutter, SVT or high PVC burden; reassess LVEF after 3 months. [ESC 2022]`,`**Thiamine deficiency**: **thiamine 100 mg IV or IM daily** (higher in Wernicke) gives dramatic response in wet beriberi within days. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iron overload**: intensify chelation - deferoxamine infusion plus deferiprone; myocardial T2* guides therapy. [TIF 2021]`,`**Anticoagulation** for AF (DOAC or warfarin) and for **LV thrombus**; not routinely for sinus rhythm with low LVEF alone. [ESC 2023]`,`Avoid **NSAIDs, verapamil/diltiazem, excess alcohol**; vaccinate; exercise training; HF clinic follow-up. [ESC 2021]`,`Women with DCM need **contraception counselling** - pregnancy is high risk (mWHO III-IV if LVEF under 30%); avoid oestrogen if thrombotic risk; copper IUD or progestogen methods. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Peripartum cardiomyopathy: management`,points:[`**BOARD** (HFA): **B**romocriptine, **O**ral HF therapy, **A**nticoagulation, vaso**R**elaxing agents, **D**iuretics. [ESC 2025]`,`**During pregnancy**: beta-blocker (metoprolol), hydralazine plus nitrate, loop diuretic for congestion, LMWH; **no ACE inhibitor, ARB, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor or ivabradine**. [ESC 2025]`,`**Delivery**: vaginal delivery with epidural is preferred if stable; urgent delivery (usually caesarean) regardless of gestation for advanced HF or shock, with a pregnancy heart team. [ESC 2025]`,`**After delivery**: full GDMT - **enalapril or captopril** (compatible with breastfeeding), beta-blocker, MRA, diuretic; avoid ARNI and SGLT2 inhibitors while breastfeeding (no data). [ESC 2025]`,`**Bromocriptine** blocks prolactin release and **may be considered** to improve LV recovery: **2.5 mg once daily for 1 week** in uncomplicated cases, or **2.5 mg twice daily for 2 weeks then 2.5 mg once daily for 6 weeks** when LVEF is under 25% or in shock; it stops lactation. [ESC 2025]`,`Always give **prophylactic (or therapeutic) LMWH with bromocriptine** - it raises thrombotic risk, and PPCM itself is hypercoagulable; therapeutic anticoagulation for LV thrombus or AF. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cardiogenic shock in PPCM**: avoid dobutamine where possible (catecholamines may worsen PPCM); use levosimendan, early MCS and transfer to a centre with ECMO and transplant. [ESC 2025]`,`A **wearable defibrillator** may bridge the first months when LVEF is 35% or less; delay ICD decisions because about half recover. [ESC 2025]`,`After recovery continue GDMT for at least 12-24 months and taper slowly with echo surveillance; relapse can occur. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD, arrhythmia and device decisions`,points:[`Decompensation is managed as in the Decompensated HF topic: IV diuretic, vasodilator if hypertensive, inotrope or MCS for shock. [ESC 2021]`,`**ICD primary prevention** in non-ischaemic DCM with **LVEF 35% or less after 3 months of optimal therapy** should be considered (DANISH: no overall mortality gain; younger patients benefited). [ESC 2026]`,`**Risk beyond LVEF** (ESC 2022 VA guideline): with LVEF under 50% and two or more of syncope, **LGE on CMR**, inducible sustained VT, or pathogenic **LMNA, PLN, FLNC or RBM20** variants, an ICD should be considered. [ESC 2022]`,`**LMNA**: use the **LMNA risk score** - ICD if estimated 5-year risk of life-threatening arrhythmia is **10% or more** with NSVT, LVEF under 50% or AV conduction delay. [ESC 2023]`,`**CRT** if LVEF 35% or less with LBBB and QRS 150 ms or more; prefer **CRT over RV pacing** for AV block in LMNA or sarcoid. [ESC 2026]`,`**Transplant or LVAD** for stage D DCM; outcomes are good in young patients without comorbidity. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: DCM is the commonest paediatric cardiomyopathy - causes include myocarditis, metabolic and mitochondrial disease, Duchenne; refer to paediatric cardiology. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Duchenne and Becker**: annual echo or CMR from diagnosis; start ACE inhibitor by about age 10 even with normal LVEF; screen female carriers. [AHA 2017]`,`**Thalassaemia major** (common in India): annual T2* MRI from about age 10; heart failure from iron overload is reversible with intensive chelation. [TIF 2021]`,`**Cancer survivors**: baseline risk assessment, echo with GLS before, during and after anthracycline or trastuzumab, dexrazoxane or liposomal doxorubicin in high risk; long-term surveillance. [ESC 2022]`,`**Elderly**: exclude ischaemia and amyloid first; DCM in the elderly is less often genetic. [ESC 2023]`,`**Future pregnancy after PPCM**: if LVEF has not recovered to 50% or more, **pregnancy is discouraged**; even with recovery there is a risk of relapse - counsel and monitor in a tertiary unit. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Progressive HF, **sudden cardiac death** (VT/VF or bradyarrhythmia), AF, functional MR, LV thrombus with stroke, cardiorenal syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognosis**: LVEF under 30%, large LV, RV dysfunction, extensive LGE, NYHA III-IV, hyponatraemia, high NT-proBNP, LMNA or FLNC variants. [ESC 2023]`,`**Better prognosis**: reversible causes (tachycardia, alcohol with abstinence, PPCM), TTN variants (respond well to GDMT), early treatment. [ESC 2023]`,`**PPCM**: about half recover LVEF to 50% or more within 6-12 months (EORP); 6-month mortality about 6%; LVEF under 30% and LVEDD over 60 mm at diagnosis predict non-recovery. [ESC 2025]`,`**Anthracycline cardiomyopathy** is partly reversible if detected early and treated with ACE inhibitor and beta-blocker. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and family screening`,points:[`Refer every new DCM to a cardiologist for **CMR, CAD exclusion and genetic testing**; urgent referral for syncope, VT, AV block or LVEF under 30%. [ESC 2023]`,`**Follow-up**: every 1-2 weeks during titration, then 3-6 monthly; echo at 3-6 months, then yearly or on change; Holter yearly in LMNA or conduction disease. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cascade genetic testing** of first-degree relatives when a pathogenic variant is found; relatives who are **genotype-negative can be discharged**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Clinical screening** (ECG, echo, CMR if needed) of first-degree relatives when no variant is found or genetics is unavailable: every **1-3 years in children (from about 10 years, earlier if early disease in the family)** and every **3-5 years in adults**. [ESC 2023]`,`Counsel genotype-positive relatives to avoid **alcohol excess**, report palpitations or syncope, and plan pregnancies with a cardiologist. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme links`,points:[`**Alcohol**: brief intervention and de-addiction referral (Tele-MANAS 14416, district de-addiction centres) - abstinence is the treatment. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Pregnancy**: ask about breathlessness in late pregnancy and postpartum at ANC and PNC visits; NT-proBNP and echo for suspicious symptoms; family planning after PPCM. [ESC 2025]`,`**Nutrition**: thiamine supplementation in at-risk groups (alcohol, tribal or polished-rice diets, prolonged vomiting); anaemia control under **Anaemia Mukt Bharat**. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Thalassaemia**: carrier screening and prevention, regular chelation through the National Health Mission blood disorder programme. [NHM 2016]`,`**NP-NCD** HF care, PM-JAY for admissions and devices, and Jan Aushadhi generics make long-term GDMT affordable. [MoHFW 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy guideline** (first unified guideline): phenotype-based classification, new **NDLVC** category, **CMR and genetic testing Class I** in DCM, family screening schedules, multidisciplinary cardiomyopathy teams. [ESC 2023]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline**: Pregnancy Heart Team; PPCM managed with BOARD; bromocriptine may be considered with anticoagulation. [ESC 2025]`,`**ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline**: CTRCD grading by LVEF, GLS and biomarkers; baseline HFA-ICOS risk score. [ESC 2022]`,`**ESC 2022 VA guideline**: ICD in DCM considered by LVEF plus risk modifiers (LGE, genotype, syncope). [ESC 2022]`,`**ESC 2026 HF guideline**: LVEF under 50% is HFrEF; four foundational therapies apply to DCM; ICD in non-ischaemic HFrEF 'should be considered'. [ESC 2026]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define DCM with the phrase **'not explained by abnormal loading conditions or coronary artery disease'** - the examiner looks for it. [ESC 2023]`,`List reversible causes with **ATTAINS**: Alcohol, Tachycardia, Thiamine, Anthracyclines, Inflammation (myocarditis), Nutritional/endocrine (thyroid), Pregnancy-related (peripartum) - plus Sarcoid and iron overload. [Harrison 22e]`,`PPCM: **LVEF under 45%, end of pregnancy to months after delivery, no other cause**; treatment BOARD; bromocriptine always with LMWH. [ESC 2025]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing idiopathic DCM **without excluding CAD**. [ESC 2023]`,`Common mistake: implanting an ICD in PPCM or tachycardia-induced DCM before giving the LV time to recover. [ESC 2025]`,`**Chagas is not an Indian cause** - mention it only as a global cause. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in DCM and peripartum cardiomyopathy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Sacubitril-valsartan / enalapril`,`ARNI 24/26-49/51 mg BD; enalapril 2.5 mg BD`,`To 97/103 mg BD or 10-20 mg BD; lifelong`,`Hypotension, high K; contraindicated in pregnancy (enalapril allowed while breastfeeding)`],[`Metoprolol succinate / bisoprolol`,`12.5-25 mg daily / 1.25 mg daily`,`To 200 mg / 10 mg`,`Bradycardia; metoprolol preferred in pregnancy`],[`Spironolactone`,`12.5-25 mg oral daily`,`To 50 mg`,`High K; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`Avoid in pregnancy and breastfeeding`],[`Hydralazine + ISDN`,`25-37.5 mg + 10-20 mg oral TDS`,`Titrate; during pregnancy`,`Headache, hypotension`],[`Bromocriptine (PPCM)`,`2.5 mg oral daily x1 week; or 2.5 mg BD x2 weeks then daily x6 weeks`,`Severe (LVEF under 25% or shock) uses the 8-week regimen`,`Thrombosis (always with LMWH), stops lactation, hypotension`],[`Enoxaparin`,`40 mg SC daily prophylaxis; 1 mg/kg BD therapeutic`,`During bromocriptine; therapeutic for LV thrombus or AF`,`Bleeding`],[`Thiamine`,`100 mg IV/IM daily (beriberi), then 100 mg oral`,`Until recovery; lifelong in alcohol use`,`Rare anaphylaxis with IV`],[`Deferoxamine + deferiprone`,`Deferoxamine 40-50 mg/kg SC/IV over 8-12 h; deferiprone 75-100 mg/kg/day oral`,`Intensified for cardiac iron (T2* under 10 ms)`,`Agranulocytosis (deferiprone), ototoxicity`]]},{heading:`Secondary causes of DCM and specific treatment`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Specific test`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`Alcohol`,`Over 80 g/day for 5 years or more`,`History, GGT, MCV`,`Total abstinence, thiamine`],[`Tachycardia-induced`,`Persistent fast AF/SVT, PVC burden over 10%`,`ECG, Holter`,`Rate/rhythm control, ablation`],[`Peripartum`,`Late pregnancy to months postpartum`,`Echo LVEF under 45%, NT-proBNP`,`BOARD, bromocriptine with LMWH`],[`Anthracycline / trastuzumab`,`Chemotherapy history`,`Echo with GLS, troponin`,`ACEi + beta-blocker; oncology review`],[`Thiamine deficiency`,`Alcohol, polished rice, high output, neuropathy`,`Response to thiamine`,`Thiamine 100 mg IV/IM`],[`Iron overload`,`Thalassaemia major, transfusions`,`CMR T2* under 20 ms`,`Intensive chelation`],[`Thyroid disease`,`Tremor, weight change, AF`,`TSH, FT4`,`Treat thyroid`],[`Muscular dystrophy`,`Proximal weakness, high CK`,`CK, genetic test`,`Early ACEi, pacing/ICD as needed`],[`Genetic (TTN, LMNA)`,`Family history, conduction disease`,`Genetic panel`,`GDMT; LMNA risk score for ICD; cascade testing`]]},{heading:`DCM versus look-alikes`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Test`],rows:[[`Idiopathic/genetic DCM`,`Global hypokinesia, mid-wall septal LGE`,`CMR, genetics`],[`Ischaemic cardiomyopathy`,`Regional defects in coronary territories, subendocardial LGE`,`Coronary angiography/CT`],[`Myocarditis`,`Acute onset, troponin rise, subepicardial LGE and oedema`,`CMR (Lake Louise criteria)`],[`Valvular (AR, primary MR)`,`Abnormal valve structure`,`Echo`],[`Athlete's heart`,`Normal LVEF at exercise, normal GLS, regresses with rest`,`Stress echo, detraining`],[`Amyloid/sarcoid`,`Thick walls or heart block, low voltage, patchy LGE`,`CMR, PYP scan, PET`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in DCM`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off / finding`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Echo LVEDD`,`Over 58 mm (men), over 52 mm (women)`,`LV dilatation`],[`PPCM LVEF`,`Under 45%`,`Diagnostic threshold`],[`Holter PVC burden`,`Over 10-15% (risk high over 20-24%)`,`Possible PVC-induced cardiomyopathy`],[`CMR T2*`,`Under 20 ms; under 10 ms severe`,`Myocardial iron overload`],[`CMR LGE`,`Mid-wall septal stripe`,`DCM scar; higher sudden death risk`],[`LMNA risk score`,`5-year risk 10% or more`,`ICD should be considered`],[`CTRCD`,`LVEF fall 10 points to 40-49% / under 40%`,`Moderate / severe cardiotoxicity`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness, orthopnoea or tachycardia in late pregnancy or within 5 months of delivery - echo and NT-proBNP the same day for PPCM.`,`Syncope, sustained VT or new AV block in DCM - urgent arrhythmia evaluation (LMNA, sarcoid).`,`LVEF under 30% with NYHA III-IV symptoms, hypotension or rising creatinine - specialist and advanced HF referral.`,`LV thrombus on echo or embolic event - anticoagulate and refer.`,`Falling LVEF or new symptoms during anthracycline or trastuzumab therapy - cardio-oncology review before the next dose.`,`Unexplained sudden death in a young relative - family screening and genetics referral.`,`PPCM with shock - transfer to a centre with MCS; avoid catecholamines where possible.`],pearls:[`DCM = dilatation + systolic dysfunction **not due to loading conditions or CAD**.`,`**30-40% of DCM is familial** - always draw a pedigree.`,`**TTN** is the commonest gene; **LMNA** is the most malignant (conduction disease, VT, sudden death).`,`PVC burden **over 10%** can cause cardiomyopathy - ablation can cure it.`,`PPCM = **LVEF under 45%**, late pregnancy to months postpartum; treat with **BOARD**.`,`**Bromocriptine never without LMWH.**`,`Wait **3-6 months** of GDMT (and recovery time in PPCM, alcohol, tachycardia) before an ICD.`,`Mid-wall septal LGE on CMR = DCM scar and higher arrhythmic risk.`,`Genotype-negative relatives of a variant-positive proband **can be discharged**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman presents 3 weeks after a normal delivery with orthopnoea and leg swelling. Echo shows LVEF 30% with a mildly dilated LV and no valve disease. She had no cardiac history. Which statement about management is correct?`,options:[`Bromocriptine may be considered and must be given with prophylactic or therapeutic anticoagulation`,`Sacubitril-valsartan is preferred over enalapril while she is breastfeeding`,`An ICD should be implanted before discharge because LVEF is under 35%`,`Beta-blockers are contraindicated in peripartum cardiomyopathy`],answer:0,explanation:`This is peripartum cardiomyopathy (LVEF under 45% within months of delivery, no other cause). Bromocriptine may be considered to aid recovery but raises thrombotic risk, so LMWH is always added. Enalapril or captopril, not ARNI, is used while breastfeeding; ICD decisions are deferred because about half recover (a wearable defibrillator can bridge); beta-blockers are part of standard therapy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old man with DCM (LVEF 40%) has first-degree AV block, paroxysmal AF and a brother who died suddenly at 38. Which gene is most likely implicated?`,options:[`TTN`,`MYH7`,`LMNA`,`MYBPC3`],answer:2,explanation:`LMNA (lamin A/C) cardiomyopathy typically combines DCM with conduction disease, AF and malignant ventricular arrhythmia causing early sudden death, and ICD thresholds are lower (LMNA risk score). TTN truncations are the commonest DCM gene but less arrhythmic; MYH7 and MYBPC3 are mainly HCM genes.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old man with LVEF 32% and no coronary disease is found on Holter to have a PVC burden of 28% from the RV outflow tract. What is the most appropriate step after starting GDMT?`,options:[`Immediate primary prevention ICD`,`Heart transplant evaluation`,`Start amiodarone lifelong and avoid ablation`,`Catheter ablation of the PVC focus, then reassess LVEF`],answer:3,explanation:`A PVC burden over 10-15% (especially over 20-24%) can cause a reversible cardiomyopathy; ablation of the focus often restores LVEF. An ICD is premature because LVEF may recover, transplant evaluation is not indicated for a potentially reversible cause, and lifelong amiodarone carries toxicity when a curative option exists.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-q4`,stem:`In a patient with newly diagnosed dilated cardiomyopathy, which investigation is essential before labelling it idiopathic?`,options:[`Endomyocardial biopsy in every patient`,`Exclusion of significant coronary artery disease by angiography or CT coronary angiography`,`Serum troponin alone`,`Chest X-ray cardiothoracic ratio`],answer:1,explanation:`DCM by definition is not explained by coronary disease, so CAD must be excluded with invasive or CT coronary angiography in patients with risk factors or age over about 35-40. Endomyocardial biopsy is reserved for suspected giant-cell myocarditis, sarcoid or eosinophilic disease; troponin and chest X-ray cannot exclude ischaemic cardiomyopathy.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-q5`,stem:`A proband with DCM carries a pathogenic TTN variant. His 30-year-old sister tests negative for the variant and has a normal ECG and echo. What is the correct advice for her?`,options:[`Echo every year for life`,`Echo every 3-5 years until age 60`,`She can be discharged from cardiomyopathy surveillance`,`Start a beta-blocker as prophylaxis`],answer:2,explanation:`When a pathogenic variant is known in the family, a genotype-negative relative with a normal assessment is not at increased risk and can be discharged (ESC 2023). Serial echoes are for relatives when genetics is unavailable or uninformative, and prophylactic beta-blockers have no role in a healthy genotype-negative person.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define dilated cardiomyopathy. Enumerate its causes and describe the evaluation and management, including family screening.`,openingLines:[`Dilated cardiomyopathy is LV or biventricular dilatation with global or regional systolic dysfunction not explained by abnormal loading conditions or coronary artery disease (ESC 2023).`,`About 30-40% is genetic; the rest is secondary to alcohol, tachycardia, pregnancy, chemotherapy, myocarditis, nutritional, endocrine or iron-overload causes - many of them reversible.`],answer:[{heading:`Epidemiology and classification`,points:[`Prevalence 1 in 250-500; second commonest cause of HF admission in India (NHFR); ESC 2023 phenotype classification with new NDLVC category.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Genetic: TTN, LMNA, FLNC, DSP, RBM20; dystrophinopathies.`,`Acquired: alcohol, tachycardia/PVC, peripartum, anthracycline/trastuzumab, myocarditis, thiamine, thyroid, iron overload, cocaine; Chagas (not India).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`HF, arrhythmia, syncope, embolism; displaced apex, S3, functional MR; clues to cause; three-generation pedigree.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG, echo, NT-proBNP, TSH, ferritin, CK, HIV; exclude CAD; CMR with LGE and T2*; Holter; genetic testing (Class I); biopsy rarely.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Four-pillar GDMT; treat cause - abstinence, ablation, thiamine, chelation; anticoagulation for AF or LV thrombus.`,`ICD: LVEF 35% or less after 3 months (should be considered) or LMNA score 10% or more; CRT for LBBB QRS 150 ms or more; LVAD/transplant.`]},{heading:`Peripartum cardiomyopathy in brief`,points:[`LVEF under 45% late pregnancy to months postpartum; BOARD; bromocriptine with LMWH; enalapril while breastfeeding; counsel on future pregnancy.`]},{heading:`Family screening and follow-up`,points:[`Cascade genetic testing; clinical screening with ECG and echo every 1-3 years in children and 3-5 years in adults; discharge genotype-negative relatives.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected DCM, exclude CAD and loading, CMR, genetics, treat cause and GDMT, family screening`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management including devices`,marks:2.5},{part:`Family screening`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`DCM`,`NDLVC`,`TTN`,`LMNA`,`LGE`,`genetic testing`,`cascade screening`,`ICD`]},{id:`cardiovascular-dilated-cardiomyopathy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Peripartum cardiomyopathy.`,openingLines:[`Peripartum cardiomyopathy is HF secondary to LV systolic dysfunction with LVEF under 45%, occurring towards the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery, with no other identifiable cause.`,`Risk factors include multiparity, pre-eclampsia, twin pregnancy, older or very young maternal age and African ancestry.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathogenesis`,points:[`Oxidative stress, cathepsin D cleaves prolactin into angiostatic 16-kDa fragment; placental sFlt-1; genetic (TTN) predisposition.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Orthopnoea, PND, tachycardia; NT-proBNP, ECG, echo LVEF under 45%; exclude pre-eclampsia, PE, pre-existing DCM.`]},{heading:`Treatment - BOARD`,points:[`Bromocriptine 2.5 mg daily for 1 week, or BD for 2 weeks then daily for 6 weeks if severe; always with LMWH.`,`Pregnancy: beta-blocker, hydralazine-nitrate, diuretic; postpartum: enalapril, beta-blocker, MRA; shock - levosimendan, MCS; wearable defibrillator.`]},{heading:`Prognosis and counselling`,points:[`About half recover; relapse risk in future pregnancy; discourage pregnancy if LVEF not recovered to 50% or more.`]}],mustDraw:[`BOARD mnemonic box`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5},{part:`Prognosis and counselling`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`PPCM`,`LVEF 45%`,`prolactin 16-kDa`,`bromocriptine`,`BOARD`,`LMWH`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cardiomyopathy and myocarditis.`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022.`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies.`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy.`,`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardio-oncology; 2022 ESC Guidelines for ventricular arrhythmias and prevention of sudden cardiac death.`,`2026 ESC Guidelines for the management of heart failure.`,`Bauersachs J et al. Pathophysiology, diagnosis and management of peripartum cardiomyopathy - HFA-ESC position statement, 2019.`,`Harikrishnan S et al. National Heart Failure Registry of India (ICMR), Nature Communications, 2025.`]}),i.push({id:`cardiovascular-hcm`,title:`Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`,oneLiner:`**Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)** is **LV wall thickness of 15 mm or more (13 mm in a first-degree relative) not explained by abnormal loading conditions**, usually an **autosomal dominant sarcomere disease (MYH7, MYBPC3)** affecting about **1 in 500**; **LVOT obstruction (gradient 30 mmHg or more, 50 or more significant)** from **systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve** gives a murmur that **rises with Valsalva and standing and falls with squatting and handgrip**; treatment is **non-vasodilating beta-blocker, then verapamil or diltiazem, disopyramide or a cardiac myosin inhibitor (mavacamten, aficamten)**, with **septal myectomy or alcohol ablation** for refractory obstruction, **ICD by HCM Risk-SCD (6% or more at 5 years) or major risk markers**, and cascade family screening.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`HCM`,`HOCM`,`obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`,`asymmetric septal hypertrophy`,`systolic anterior motion SAM`,`LVOT obstruction`,`MYH7 MYBPC3 sarcomere`,`HCM Risk-SCD`,`sudden cardiac death young athlete`,`mavacamten`,`aficamten`,`disopyramide`,`septal myectomy Morrow`,`alcohol septal ablation`,`apical HCM Yamaguchi`,`phenocopy Fabry Danon amyloid`,`athlete's heart`,`bifid pulse`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**HCM** is **LV wall thickness 15 mm or more** in any segment (or **13 mm or more** in a first-degree relative of a patient) that is not solely explained by abnormal loading conditions such as hypertension or aortic stenosis; in children a **z-score over 2-2.5**. [ESC 2023]`,`Prevalence is about **1 in 500** by imaging and up to **1 in 200** including genotype-positive carriers - the commonest inherited heart disease; most remain undiagnosed. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`It is the commonest cause of **sudden cardiac death in young people and athletes** in many series, though annual SCD risk in treated adults is now under 1%. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`With contemporary care, annual mortality is about **0.5%** and most patients have near-normal life expectancy. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`About **two-thirds** have LVOT obstruction at rest or on provocation; **one-third** are non-obstructive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**India**: no population survey, but prevalence is assumed similar; an industry estimate in 2025 suggested over 2 million Indians with obstructive HCM, the large majority undiagnosed - opportunistic ECG and echo in young people with murmurs or syncope matter. [CSI 2023]`,`**Apical HCM (Yamaguchi)** is more common in East Asians (and is seen in Indians) - giant negative T waves and spade-shaped LV. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and genetics`,points:[`**Autosomal dominant** inheritance with variable penetrance and expression; a pathogenic sarcomere variant is found in about **30-60%** (higher with family history). [ESC 2023]`,`**MYH7 (beta-myosin heavy chain)** and **MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C)** account for about **70%** of genotype-positive cases; others TNNT2, TNNI3, TPM1, MYL2, MYL3, ACTC1. [ESC 2023]`,`**Phenocopies** (about 5-10%): **Fabry disease** (alpha-galactosidase A, X-linked), **Danon disease** (LAMP2), **PRKAG2** (with WPW), **transthyretin or AL amyloid**, Pompe, Friedreich ataxia, **Noonan syndrome** (RASopathy, infants), mitochondrial disease. [ESC 2023]`,`Phenocopies matter because they have **specific treatments** (enzyme replacement in Fabry, tafamidis in ATTR) and different inheritance. [ESC 2023]`,`Secondary LVH from **hypertension, aortic stenosis, athletic training or obesity** is excluded by definition - but hypertension and HCM often coexist. [ESC 2023]`,`Infants of **diabetic mothers** can have transient septal hypertrophy that regresses. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Sarcomere variants cause **hypercontractility**, impaired relaxation and energy wasting, leading to myocyte hypertrophy, **myocyte disarray** and interstitial fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**LVOT obstruction**: a thick septum narrows the outflow tract; high-velocity flow drags (and Venturi forces pull) the anterior mitral leaflet towards the septum - **systolic anterior motion (SAM)** - creating a dynamic mid-systolic obstruction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`SAM distorts the mitral valve, causing **posteriorly directed mitral regurgitation** - an anteriorly directed jet suggests intrinsic mitral disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Obstruction is **dynamic**: it increases with **less preload** (Valsalva, standing, dehydration, nitrates, diuretics), **less afterload** (vasodilators) and **more contractility** (exercise, inotropes, tachycardia), and decreases with the reverse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diastolic dysfunction** from stiffness and impaired relaxation raises filling pressure - the main cause of breathlessness even without obstruction, and the reason **AF** (loss of atrial kick) is poorly tolerated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Myocardial ischaemia** without coronary disease comes from small-vessel disease, hypertrophy outstripping supply and raised wall stress - angina with normal coronaries. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Disarray and fibrosis** form the substrate for **VT/VF**; extensive LGE (15% or more of LV mass) marks higher SCD risk. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cardiac myosin inhibitors** (mavacamten, aficamten) reduce actin-myosin cross-bridges, directly reversing hypercontractility - they lower the gradient and can lower LVEF. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`A minority progress to **end-stage (burnt-out) HCM** with LVEF under 50%, wall thinning and dilatation. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Obstructive HCM**: peak LVOT gradient **30 mmHg or more at rest or on provocation**; **50 mmHg or more** is haemodynamically significant and the threshold for invasive therapy in symptomatic patients. [ESC 2023]`,`**Labile or latent obstruction**: under 30 mmHg at rest but 30 or more with Valsalva, standing or exercise - provoke it in every patient. [ESC 2023]`,`**Non-obstructive HCM**: gradient under 30 mmHg at rest and on provocation. [ESC 2023]`,`**Morphological types**: asymmetric septal (commonest), concentric, **apical (Yamaguchi)**, mid-ventricular obstruction (with apical aneurysm risk). [Harrison 22e]`,`**End-stage HCM**: LVEF under 50% - treated as HFrEF and a major SCD risk marker. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`Genotype status: **genotype-positive phenotype-negative** relatives need surveillance, not treatment. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Many are **asymptomatic** - found through family screening, a murmur or an abnormal ECG. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`Symptoms: **exertional breathlessness** (commonest), angina, fatigue, palpitations (AF), **exertional presyncope or syncope**, and **sudden death** as the first event. [Harrison 22e]`,`Symptoms typically **worsen after meals, alcohol, dehydration or hot weather**, or after starting vasodilators or diuretics - all lower preload or afterload. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Unexplained syncope** - especially exertional or recent - is a major SCD marker; vasovagal syncope must be separated by history. [ESC 2023]`,`Family history: sudden death under 40-50, HCM, pacemakers or ICDs in relatives. [ESC 2023]`,`Phenocopy clues: **neuropathic pain, angiokeratomas, proteinuria (Fabry)**; **learning difficulty, myopathy (Danon)**; **carpal tunnel and age over 60 (ATTR)**; short stature and webbed neck (Noonan). [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Examination findings and murmur dynamics`,points:[`**Jerky, brisk carotid upstroke** with a **bifid (spike-and-dome) pulse** in obstruction - unlike the slow-rising pulse of AS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Double or triple apical impulse** (palpable atrial impulse plus systolic waves), forceful but not displaced apex, prominent **a wave** in JVP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S4** is common; S2 may be paradoxically split with severe obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`Murmur: **harsh crescendo-decrescendo mid-systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border and apex**, not radiating to the carotids, often with an apical pansystolic MR murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Louder with**: Valsalva strain, **squat to stand**, amyl nitrite, post-extrasystolic beat (with a fall in pulse pressure - Brockenbrough sign). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Softer with**: **squatting, sustained handgrip, passive leg raising**, phenylephrine - opposite to most murmurs except MVP click timing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AS versus HCM**: AS murmur radiates to the carotids, softens with Valsalva, has a slow-rising pulse and often an ejection click or soft A2. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Athlete's heart**: wall 13-15 mm grey zone - favour athlete if LV cavity is large (over 55 mm), symmetrical, with **normal diastolic function**, no LGE, no family history, and regression after **3 months of detraining**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Hypertensive heart disease**: concentric LVH usually under 15 mm, long-standing hypertension, regression with BP control; septum over 15 mm or SAM favours HCM. [ESC 2023]`,`**Aortic stenosis / subaortic membrane**: fixed obstruction on echo, abnormal valve or membrane; murmur does not increase with Valsalva. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cardiac amyloidosis**: thick walls with **low or normal ECG voltage**, biatrial enlargement, apical sparing on strain, PYP scan uptake - see the Restrictive cardiomyopathy topic. [ESC 2023]`,`**Fabry disease**: concentric LVH, short PR, renal and neurological features; low alpha-galactosidase (men) or GLA genetics (women). [ESC 2023]`,`**Apical HCM vs ACS**: giant T inversion with normal troponin trajectory and spade-shaped cavity on echo or CMR. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG** is abnormal in over 90%: LVH, ST-T changes, **deep narrow (dagger) Q waves** in inferolateral leads, **giant negative T waves (over 10 mm) in apical HCM**, left atrial enlargement, AF; short PR with delta wave suggests PRKAG2 or Danon. [ESC 2023]`,`**Echo**: wall thickness (maximum, in which segment), **SAM**, LVOT gradient at rest and with **Valsalva and standing**, MR direction, LA size, LVEF, diastolic function, apical aneurysm. [ESC 2023]`,`If resting and Valsalva gradients are under 50 mmHg in a symptomatic patient, do **exercise (stress) echo** to unmask provocable obstruction. [ESC 2023]`,`**CMR** (Class I at diagnosis): precise wall thickness (detects apical and anterolateral hypertrophy missed on echo), **LGE extent** (15% or more of LV mass raises SCD risk), apical aneurysm, phenocopy patterns. [ESC 2023]`,`**48-hour Holter** at baseline and every 1-2 years for **NSVT** (3 or more beats at 120/min or more) and AF. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cardiopulmonary exercise test**: functional capacity, BP response (failure to rise by 20 mmHg or a fall is abnormal), and baseline for myosin inhibitors. [ESC 2023]`,`**Genetic testing** (Class I) in the proband to enable cascade testing; red-flag tests for phenocopies - alpha-galactosidase, CK, PYP scan, serum free light chains. [ESC 2023]`,`**Natriuretic peptides and troponin** add prognostic information. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: drugs for obstructive HCM`,points:[`**First line**: a **non-vasodilating beta-blocker** titrated to symptoms and resting heart rate about 60/min - **propranolol 40-80 mg two to three times daily, metoprolol succinate 50-200 mg, bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg** or atenolol 50-100 mg (Class I). [ESC 2023]`,`If beta-blockers fail or are not tolerated: **verapamil 40-80 mg three times daily up to 480 mg/day (sustained-release)** or **diltiazem 60-360 mg/day** - avoid in severe obstruction with high gradient and low BP or HF (can cause pulmonary oedema). [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Disopyramide** 100-200 mg three to four times daily (up to 400-600 mg/day) added to a beta-blocker or CCB lowers the gradient (negative inotrope); monitor **QTc** and anticholinergic effects (urinary retention, dry mouth, glaucoma). [ESC 2023]`,`**Cardiac myosin inhibitors**: **mavacamten** (EXPLORER-HCM: better pVO2, gradient and NYHA; VALOR-HCM: fewer patients still needing septal reduction) - Class I in AHA/ACC 2024 for persistent symptoms on beta-blocker or CCB; ESC 2023 IIa. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Mavacamten** starts at **5 mg once daily** with echo at 4, 8 and 12 weeks, titrating 2.5-15 mg by LVOT gradient and LVEF; **hold if LVEF falls under 50%**; CYP2C19/CYP3A4 interactions; **teratogenic** - effective contraception; launched in India in 2025. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Aficamten** (FDA-approved December 2025): SEQUOIA-HCM improved pVO2, gradient and symptoms, and MAPLE-HCM showed it beat metoprolol as monotherapy; starts 5 mg, titrated in 5 mg steps by echo every 2-8 weeks; not started if LVEF is under 55%. [MAPLE-HCM 2025]`,`**Avoid** in obstruction: **vasodilators (nitrates, dihydropyridine CCB, ACEi/ARB at high dose), PDE-5 inhibitors, digoxin, high-dose diuretics**; dehydration and large meals; low-dose diuretic is acceptable for congestion. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Hypertension with obstructive HCM**: use beta-blocker or non-DHP CCB first; add low-dose diuretic or RAAS blocker cautiously with gradient monitoring. [AHA/ACC 2024]`]},{heading:`Non-obstructive HCM, AF and heart failure`,points:[`**Non-obstructive HCM** with symptoms: beta-blocker or verapamil/diltiazem for angina or breathlessness, cautious low-dose diuretic for congestion; ODYSSEY-HCM (2025) found **mavacamten did not help non-obstructive HCM**. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**AF in HCM**: anticoagulate **regardless of CHA2DS2-VA score** (Class I), DOAC first-line; rhythm control is often needed (amiodarone, dofetilide, sotalol or catheter ablation) because AF is poorly tolerated. [ESC 2023]`,`**HCM with LVEF under 50%** (end-stage): stop negative inotropes and myosin inhibitors, give **full HFrEF GDMT**, consider ICD and CRT, and refer for transplant. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Apical aneurysm** with thrombus or embolism needs anticoagulation and is an SCD risk marker. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Hypotension in obstructive HCM** (dehydration, anaesthesia, sepsis): give **IV fluids and phenylephrine**, add beta-blocker; **avoid inotropes, nitrates and diuretics**. [AHA/ACC 2024]`]},{heading:`Septal reduction therapy`,points:[`**Indication**: **LVOT gradient 50 mmHg or more** with **NYHA III-IV symptoms or exertional syncope despite maximum medical therapy** (Class I) - done at experienced comprehensive HCM centres. [ESC 2023]`,`**Surgical septal myectomy (Morrow)**: excision of the basal septum, with mitral or papillary muscle repair as needed; operative mortality under 1% at expert centres; durable gradient relief; causes **LBBB**. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Alcohol septal ablation**: ethanol injected into a septal perforator artery creates a controlled infarct; preferred in older or high-surgical-risk patients with suitable anatomy; causes **RBBB** and **complete heart block needing a pacemaker in about 10-20%**. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`Myectomy is preferred when there is **intrinsic mitral or papillary muscle disease, very thick septum (over 30 mm), or concomitant CABG or valve surgery**. [ESC 2023]`,`Septal reduction is **not done for asymptomatic** patients or for non-obstructive HCM; **dual-chamber pacing** is a limited alternative in the elderly. [ESC 2023]`,`**VALOR-HCM** showed mavacamten can make many patients ineligible for septal reduction by lowering the gradient - offer it before an invasive procedure where available. [AHA/ACC 2024]`]},{heading:`Sudden death risk and the ICD`,points:[`**Secondary prevention ICD** after cardiac arrest, VF or sustained VT (Class I). [ESC 2023]`,`**HCM Risk-SCD** (ESC) uses **age, maximum wall thickness, LA diameter, maximum LVOT gradient, family history of SCD, NSVT and unexplained syncope** to give a 5-year risk; not for under-16s (use HCM Risk-Kids). [ESC 2023]`,`**5-year risk under 4%**: ICD generally not indicated; **4-6%**: ICD may be considered; **6% or more**: ICD should be considered. [ESC 2023]`,`ESC 2023 adds modifiers in low and intermediate risk: **LGE 15% or more of LV mass, LVEF under 50% and apical aneurysm** may tip the decision towards an ICD. [ESC 2023]`,`**AHA/ACC 2024 major risk markers** - any one makes an ICD reasonable: SCD in a first-degree or close relative aged 50 or under, **massive LVH 30 mm or more**, recent unexplained syncope, **apical aneurysm**, **LVEF under 50%**; extensive LGE and NSVT are additional modifiers. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Subcutaneous ICD** is an option when pacing is not needed; amiodarone does not replace an ICD. [ESC 2023]`,`Re-assess SCD risk every **1-2 years** or when clinical status changes. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Special situations: sport, pregnancy, children and anaesthesia`,points:[`**Exercise**: all patients should do regular **low-to-moderate intensity exercise**; ESC 2023 and AHA/ACC 2024 allow **high-intensity or competitive sport** after expert assessment and shared decision-making in low-risk patients without symptoms, obstruction or arrhythmia. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`Avoid **dehydration, heavy isometric exertion and exercise in heat**; an AED should be available at training venues. [ESC 2023]`,`**Pregnancy** is usually well tolerated (mWHO II-III); continue beta-blockers (metoprolol preferred), avoid dehydration and blood loss hypotension, vaginal delivery with careful epidural; **stop mavacamten or aficamten before conception** (teratogenic). [ESC 2025]`,`Offspring risk is **50%** for each child of an affected parent with an autosomal dominant variant - offer genetic counselling and pre-natal or pre-implantation options. [ESC 2023]`,`**Children**: phenocopies (Noonan, Pompe, metabolic) are commoner in infants; use HCM Risk-Kids for SCD; screening of children of affected parents can start at any age. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Anaesthesia and surgery**: maintain preload and afterload, avoid tachycardia and inotropes, use phenylephrine for hypotension - inform the anaesthetist. [ESC 2022]`,`**Endocarditis prophylaxis** is not routinely needed for HCM (only for standard high-risk indications). [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, follow-up and family screening`,points:[`**Complications**: AF (about 20-25%) and stroke, HF (obstructive or end-stage), **sudden cardiac death**, infective endocarditis on the mitral valve, apical aneurysm with thrombus. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Follow-up**: clinical review, ECG and echo every **1-2 years** (more often on myosin inhibitors), **48-hour Holter every 1-2 years**, CMR every 3-5 years or on change, and SCD re-stratification. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cascade genetic testing** of first-degree relatives when a pathogenic variant is found (Class I); genotype-negative relatives can be discharged. [ESC 2023]`,`**Clinical screening** (ECG and echo, CMR if equivocal) of first-degree relatives: children from about **10-12 years** (earlier if early-onset family disease, symptoms or competitive sport) every **1-2 years**, adults every **3-5 years**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Genotype-positive, phenotype-negative** relatives need periodic ECG and echo (every 1-2 years in childhood, 2-5 years in adults) and may play sport with advice. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Refer** every suspected HCM to a cardiologist with CMR and genetics access; same-day referral for syncope, sustained palpitations, chest pain on exertion or a relative's sudden death. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**Counselling**: avoid dehydration, binge alcohol and large meals; check drugs (no nitrates, sildenafil, vasodilators); carry an alert card; cascade screening of family. [AHA/ACC 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**AHA/ACC/multisociety 2024 HCM guideline**: **cardiac myosin inhibitor Class I** for obstructive HCM with persistent symptoms despite beta-blocker or non-DHP CCB; liberalised sport participation; major SCD risk markers including apical aneurysm and LVEF under 50%. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`**ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy guideline**: HCM within a unified cardiomyopathy framework, mavacamten IIa, LGE 15% or more, LVEF under 50% and apical aneurysm as ICD modifiers, CMR and genetic testing Class I. [ESC 2023]`,`**Aficamten** was FDA-approved in **December 2025** (SEQUOIA-HCM; MAPLE-HCM showed superiority over metoprolol monotherapy in 2025), with echo-guided titration and a REMS. [FDA 2025]`,`**ODYSSEY-HCM (2025)**: mavacamten failed to improve non-obstructive HCM - myosin inhibitors remain for obstructive disease. [ODYSSEY-HCM 2025]`,`**Mavacamten** became available in India in **2025** via an import licence; cost and echo monitoring limit its use, so beta-blockers, verapamil and disopyramide remain the backbone. [CDSCO 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Define HCM with **15 mm (13 mm in relatives), not explained by loading** - examiners expect both numbers. [ESC 2023]`,`Murmur dynamics: **Valsalva and standing increase, squatting and handgrip decrease** - the reverse of AS. [Harrison 22e]`,`List the **seven HCM Risk-SCD variables** and the **4% and 6%** thresholds. [ESC 2023]`,`Drugs to avoid in HOCM: **nitrates, vasodilators, digoxin, high-dose diuretics, inotropes, sildenafil**. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`Myectomy causes **LBBB**; alcohol ablation causes **RBBB** and more heart block. [AHA/ACC 2024]`,`AF in HCM: **anticoagulate regardless of CHA2DS2-VA**. [ESC 2023]`,`Common mistake: calling a young athlete with a 14 mm septum normal without CMR, family screening or a detraining trial. [ESC 2023]`,`Common mistake: treating hypotension in HOCM with dobutamine instead of fluids and phenylephrine. [AHA/ACC 2024]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Propranolol / metoprolol succinate / bisoprolol`,`Propranolol 40-80 mg TDS; metoprolol 50-200 mg daily; bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg daily`,`Titrate to symptoms and HR about 60; lifelong`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; avoid vasodilating beta-blockers (carvedilol, nebivolol)`],[`Verapamil`,`40-80 mg TDS; SR up to 480 mg/day`,`If beta-blocker fails or not tolerated`,`Bradycardia, AV block; avoid with severe obstruction, hypotension or HF`],[`Diltiazem`,`60-360 mg/day in divided or SR doses`,`Alternative to verapamil`,`Bradycardia, oedema; same cautions`],[`Disopyramide`,`100-200 mg TDS-QID (max 400-600 mg/day) oral`,`Added to beta-blocker or CCB; monitor QTc`,`Anticholinergic (urinary retention, glaucoma), QT prolongation; give with AV-nodal blocker in AF`],[`Mavacamten`,`5 mg oral once daily; range 2.5-15 mg`,`Echo at weeks 4, 8, 12 then every 12 weeks; hold if LVEF under 50%`,`Systolic dysfunction, CYP2C19/3A4 interactions, teratogenic`],[`Aficamten`,`5 mg oral once daily; 5 mg steps to 20 mg`,`Echo-guided titration every 2-8 weeks`,`Systolic dysfunction; not if LVEF under 55%; REMS (US)`],[`Apixaban / rivaroxaban (AF)`,`Apixaban 5 mg BD; rivaroxaban 20 mg daily`,`Lifelong once AF occurs, regardless of score`,`Bleeding; dose-reduce by renal function`],[`Phenylephrine (acute hypotension)`,`50-100 mcg IV boluses or infusion`,`Acute only`,`Reflex bradycardia; avoid inotropes`]]},{heading:`Murmur dynamics: HCM versus aortic stenosis`,columns:[`Manoeuvre`,`Effect on LV volume`,`HCM murmur`,`AS murmur`],rows:[[`Valsalva strain`,`Decreases preload`,`Louder`,`Softer`],[`Squat to stand`,`Decreases preload`,`Louder`,`Softer`],[`Squatting / passive leg raise`,`Increases preload`,`Softer`,`Louder`],[`Sustained handgrip`,`Increases afterload`,`Softer`,`Softer or unchanged`],[`Post-extrasystolic beat`,`Increases contractility`,`Louder with fall in pulse pressure (Brockenbrough)`,`Louder with rise in pulse pressure`],[`Amyl nitrite`,`Decreases afterload`,`Louder`,`Louder`]]},{heading:`Classification and thresholds in HCM`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Threshold`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Wall thickness (proband)`,`15 mm or more`,`Diagnostic of HCM if not explained by loading`],[`Wall thickness (first-degree relative)`,`13 mm or more`,`Diagnostic in a relative`],[`LVOT gradient`,`30 mmHg or more`,`Obstructive HCM`],[`LVOT gradient`,`50 mmHg or more`,`Significant - septal reduction if NYHA III-IV on drugs`],[`HCM Risk-SCD`,`Under 4% / 4-6% / 6% or more`,`ICD not indicated / may consider / should consider`],[`Massive LVH`,`30 mm or more`,`Major SCD risk marker`],[`LGE extent`,`15% or more of LV mass`,`Higher SCD risk`],[`LVEF`,`Under 50%`,`End-stage HCM; SCD risk marker`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of LV hypertrophy`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating features`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Sarcomeric HCM`,`Asymmetric septum, SAM, family history, dagger Q waves`,`Echo, CMR, genetics`],[`Athlete's heart`,`Wall 13-15 mm, large cavity, normal diastolic function, regresses with detraining`,`CMR, CPET, 3-month detraining`],[`Hypertensive LVH`,`Concentric, usually under 15 mm, regresses with BP control`,`BP history, echo`],[`Aortic stenosis`,`Fixed obstruction, calcified valve, slow-rising pulse`,`Echo`],[`Amyloidosis`,`Low voltage with thick walls, apical sparing, carpal tunnel`,`PYP scan, free light chains`],[`Fabry disease`,`Short PR, renal disease, neuropathic pain, angiokeratoma`,`Alpha-galactosidase, GLA gene`],[`Danon / PRKAG2`,`Pre-excitation, massive LVH, young male, myopathy`,`Genetics, CK`]]},{heading:`Septal myectomy versus alcohol septal ablation`,columns:[`Feature`,`Septal myectomy`,`Alcohol septal ablation`],rows:[[`Approach`,`Open-heart surgery (Morrow)`,`Catheter; ethanol into septal perforator`],[`Best for`,`Young, very thick septum (over 30 mm), mitral or papillary disease, need for other surgery`,`Older or high surgical risk with suitable septal artery`],[`Conduction effect`,`LBBB`,`RBBB; complete heart block in about 10-20%`],[`Gradient relief`,`Immediate and durable`,`Gradual over weeks to months`],[`Mortality at expert centres`,`Under 1%`,`About 1%`]]}],redFlags:[`Unexplained or exertional syncope in a patient with HCM or a young person with a murmur - same-day cardiology review.`,`Resuscitated cardiac arrest or sustained VT - ICD (secondary prevention).`,`Sudden death of a relative under 50 - screen the family urgently.`,`New AF with breathlessness or hypotension - rate/rhythm control and anticoagulation regardless of score.`,`Hypotension after nitrates, diuretics or dehydration - fluids and phenylephrine; stop vasodilators.`,`LVEF falling under 50% on mavacamten or aficamten - hold the drug and refer.`,`Exertional chest pain or breathlessness in an athlete with LVH over 13 mm - stop competitive sport until assessed.`],pearls:[`HCM = **15 mm (13 mm in a relative)**, not explained by loading.`,`**MYH7 and MYBPC3** cause about 70% of genotype-positive HCM; inheritance autosomal dominant.`,`**Valsalva and standing make it louder; squatting and handgrip make it softer.**`,`Obstruction: **30 mmHg defines, 50 mmHg treats invasively**.`,`Beta-blocker, then verapamil/diltiazem, then **disopyramide or a myosin inhibitor**, then septal reduction.`,`**Avoid nitrates, vasodilators, digoxin and inotropes** in obstructive HCM.`,`HCM Risk-SCD: **under 4% no ICD, 4-6% may consider, 6% or more should consider**.`,`AF in HCM: **anticoagulate everyone**.`,`**Myectomy - LBBB; alcohol ablation - RBBB** and heart block.`,`Bifid (spike-and-dome) pulse and **double apical impulse** are the bedside signature.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-q1`,stem:`A 19-year-old footballer has a harsh mid-systolic murmur at the left lower sternal border. Which manoeuvre would make the murmur louder if he has obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?`,options:[`Squatting from standing`,`Sustained handgrip`,`Passive leg raising`,`Standing up from a squat`],answer:3,explanation:`Standing from a squat reduces venous return and LV volume, bringing the septum and mitral leaflet closer and increasing the dynamic LVOT gradient, so the HCM murmur gets louder. Squatting and passive leg raising increase preload, and handgrip increases afterload - all widen the outflow tract and soften the murmur.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old with obstructive HCM (resting gradient 70 mmHg) remains NYHA III on bisoprolol 10 mg. LVEF is 68%. She is not pregnant and uses contraception. What is the most appropriate next drug option per AHA/ACC 2024?`,options:[`Add amlodipine for angina`,`Add a cardiac myosin inhibitor such as mavacamten with echo monitoring`,`Add furosemide 80 mg daily`,`Add isosorbide mononitrate`],answer:1,explanation:`AHA/ACC 2024 gives a cardiac myosin inhibitor a Class I recommendation for obstructive HCM with persistent symptoms despite beta-blocker or non-DHP CCB; it lowers the gradient and improves symptoms, with echo-guided dosing and contraception because it is teratogenic. Amlodipine and nitrates are vasodilators that worsen obstruction, and high-dose diuretics reduce preload and increase the gradient.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-q3`,stem:`Which of the following is NOT one of the variables used in the ESC HCM Risk-SCD calculator?`,options:[`Maximum LV wall thickness`,`Left atrial diameter`,`Late gadolinium enhancement extent`,`Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia`],answer:2,explanation:`HCM Risk-SCD uses age, maximum wall thickness, left atrial diameter, maximum LVOT gradient, family history of SCD, NSVT and unexplained syncope. LGE extent of 15% or more is an additional modifier in ESC 2023 (and AHA/ACC 2024) used alongside the score in low or intermediate risk, but it is not part of the calculator itself.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-q4`,stem:`A 60-year-old with obstructive HCM develops paroxysmal AF. His CHA2DS2-VA score is 0. What is the correct approach to stroke prevention?`,options:[`Oral anticoagulation, preferably a DOAC, regardless of the score`,`No antithrombotic therapy because the score is 0`,`Aspirin 75 mg daily`,`Anticoagulate only if AF lasts more than 48 hours at a time`],answer:0,explanation:`AF in HCM carries a high thromboembolic risk, so ESC and AHA/ACC recommend oral anticoagulation regardless of CHA2DS2-VA score, with DOACs first-line. The score was not validated in HCM; aspirin is not effective stroke prevention in AF; and stroke risk applies to paroxysmal as well as persistent AF.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-q5`,stem:`After alcohol septal ablation for obstructive HCM, which conduction abnormality is most characteristic?`,options:[`Left bundle branch block`,`Wolff-Parkinson-White pattern`,`Right bundle branch block`,`First-degree AV block only`],answer:2,explanation:`Alcohol septal ablation infarcts the basal septum supplied by the first septal perforator, through which the right bundle runs, so RBBB is typical and complete heart block needs a pacemaker in about 10-20%. Surgical myectomy, by contrast, typically causes LBBB; WPW pattern is a clue to PRKAG2 or Danon phenocopy, not a procedural complication.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the genetics, pathophysiology of LVOT obstruction, clinical features, sudden-death risk stratification and management of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.`,openingLines:[`Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is LV wall thickness of 15 mm or more (13 mm in first-degree relatives) not explained by abnormal loading conditions, affecting about 1 in 500 people.`,`It is usually an autosomal dominant sarcomere disease (MYH7, MYBPC3), and two-thirds have dynamic LVOT obstruction from systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve.`],answer:[{heading:`Genetics and phenocopies`,points:[`Autosomal dominant, variable penetrance; MYH7 and MYBPC3 about 70%; yield 30-60%.`,`Phenocopies: Fabry, Danon, PRKAG2, amyloid, Noonan, Friedreich.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Hypercontractility, disarray, fibrosis; SAM causes dynamic LVOT obstruction and posterior MR.`,`Obstruction worse with low preload, low afterload, high contractility; diastolic dysfunction, ischaemia, arrhythmia substrate.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Dyspnoea, angina, syncope, palpitations, sudden death; bifid pulse, double apical impulse, S4.`,`Murmur louder with Valsalva/standing, softer with squatting/handgrip.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG (LVH, dagger Q, giant T), echo with Valsalva and exercise gradient, CMR (LGE), Holter, CPET, genetic testing.`]},{heading:`SCD risk stratification`,points:[`HCM Risk-SCD: age, wall thickness, LA size, gradient, family SCD, NSVT, syncope; under 4%, 4-6%, 6% or more.`,`Modifiers: LGE 15% or more, LVEF under 50%, apical aneurysm, massive LVH 30 mm; secondary prevention ICD after arrest.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Beta-blocker, then verapamil/diltiazem, disopyramide, mavacamten/aficamten; avoid vasodilators, nitrates, digoxin.`,`Septal myectomy or alcohol ablation if gradient 50 mmHg or more with NYHA III-IV; AF - anticoagulate all; end-stage - HFrEF therapy.`]},{heading:`Lifestyle and family`,points:[`Moderate exercise for all, competitive sport after shared decision; pregnancy usually tolerated; cascade genetic testing and clinical screening of relatives.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of SAM with LVOT obstruction and posteriorly directed MR`,`Table of manoeuvres and murmur changes`,`Treatment ladder from beta-blocker to septal reduction`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and genetics`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pathophysiology with diagram`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and murmur dynamics`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`SCD risk and ICD`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and family screening`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`SAM`,`LVOT gradient`,`MYH7`,`MYBPC3`,`HCM Risk-SCD`,`mavacamten`,`myectomy`,`alcohol septal ablation`]},{id:`cardiovascular-hcm-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate hypertrophic cardiomyopathy from athlete's heart.`,openingLines:[`Both cause LV wall thickening; the grey zone is 13-15 mm in men, where misdiagnosis can mean either a needless sporting ban or a sudden death.`,`The distinction rests on cavity size, symmetry, diastolic function, ECG, CMR, family history and regression with detraining.`],answer:[{heading:`Features favouring HCM`,points:[`Wall over 15 mm, asymmetric septum, small LV cavity (under 45 mm), SAM or LVOT gradient, abnormal diastolic function, large LA.`,`Deep T inversion, dagger Q waves, LGE on CMR, family history, abnormal CPET, no regression after detraining, pathogenic variant.`]},{heading:`Features favouring athlete's heart`,points:[`Wall 13-15 mm or less, symmetric, LV cavity over 55 mm, normal or supranormal diastolic function, normal GLS.`,`Normal or training-related ECG, no LGE, high peak VO2, regression after 3 months of detraining.`]},{heading:`Approach`,points:[`Echo, CMR, CPET, Holter, family screening and genetics; detraining trial; expert sports cardiology decision.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: HCM versus athlete's heart`],markSplit:[{part:`Imaging features`,marks:2},{part:`ECG, CMR and functional features`,marks:2},{part:`Approach`,marks:1}],keywords:[`grey zone 13-15 mm`,`detraining`,`LV cavity size`,`diastolic function`,`LGE`,`CPET`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cardiomyopathy and myocarditis.`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022.`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies.`,`2024 AHA/ACC/AMSSM/HRS/PACES/SCMR Guideline for the Management of Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy.`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy.`,`Maron MS et al. Aficamten versus metoprolol monotherapy in obstructive HCM (MAPLE-HCM), N Engl J Med, 2025; US FDA approval of aficamten, December 2025.`,`Olivotto I et al. Mavacamten for obstructive HCM (EXPLORER-HCM), Lancet, 2020; Desai MY et al. VALOR-HCM, J Am Coll Cardiol, 2022.`]});var a=[];a.push({id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative`,title:`Restrictive and infiltrative cardiomyopathy, amyloidosis and Takotsubo`,oneLiner:`**Restrictive cardiomyopathy** is a stiff, non-dilated ventricle with **restrictive filling, near-normal systolic function and biatrial enlargement**, most often caused by **infiltration (AL or transthyretin amyloid, sarcoid)**, **storage (iron, Fabry)** or **endomyocardial disease (EMF)**; **Takotsubo syndrome** is a transient, catecholamine-triggered regional LV dysfunction that extends beyond one coronary territory, mimics STEMI and usually recovers within weeks.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`restrictive cardiomyopathy`,`RCM`,`infiltrative cardiomyopathy`,`cardiac amyloidosis`,`ATTR-CM`,`AL amyloidosis`,`transthyretin`,`tafamidis`,`acoramidis`,`vutrisiran`,`Tc-99m pyrophosphate scintigraphy`,`Perugini grade`,`cardiac sarcoidosis`,`iron overload cardiomyopathy`,`CMR T2-star`,`endomyocardial fibrosis`,`Loeffler endocarditis`,`Takotsubo`,`stress cardiomyopathy`,`apical ballooning`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Restrictive cardiomyopathy (RCM)** is restrictive filling of the left and/or right ventricle with **normal or reduced diastolic and systolic volumes and normal wall thickness**, not explained by pericardial, valvular or ischaemic disease. [ESC 2023]`,`**Infiltrative cardiomyopathy** means the myocardium is expanded by abnormal material between (amyloid, granulomas) or within (iron, glycosphingolipid) the myocytes, so walls are often **thick but not muscular** - ESC 2023 files amyloid under a hypertrophic phenotype with restrictive physiology. [ESC 2023]`,`RCM is the **least common of the classic cardiomyopathy phenotypes**; the commonest identifiable cause in adults is **cardiac amyloidosis**, and in the tropics **endomyocardial fibrosis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wild-type transthyretin amyloid (ATTRwt)** is found in about **13% of patients aged 60 or more admitted with HFpEF and LV wall 12 mm or more**, and in about **1 in 6 older patients with severe aortic stenosis undergoing TAVI** - common and underdiagnosed. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**AL (light-chain) amyloidosis** has an incidence of roughly **10-12 per million per year**; the heart is involved in most patients and **cardiac stage decides survival**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF)** clusters in the tropical belt - **Kerala in India**, Uganda, Nigeria, Mozambique and Brazil - in poor children and young adults; incidence in Kerala has fallen over recent decades. [Braunwald 12e]`,`India's iron-overload cardiomyopathy is mainly **transfusional, in beta-thalassaemia major**: an estimated **10,000-15,000 children with thalassaemia major are born each year**, and cardiac siderosis is their leading cause of death. [NHM Haemoglobinopathy Guidelines 2016]`,`**Takotsubo syndrome (TTS)** accounts for about **1-3% of patients presenting with suspected ACS** (5-6% of women), and about **90% are postmenopausal women** with a mean age near 66 years. [InterTAK 2018]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infiltrative**: amyloidosis (AL, ATTRwt, hereditary ATTRv, rarely AA), **sarcoidosis**, Gaucher disease, Hurler syndrome and primary oxalosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Storage**: **haemochromatosis** (hereditary HFE C282Y - rare in Indians - or transfusional), **Anderson-Fabry disease** (X-linked GLA, alpha-galactosidase A deficiency), glycogen storage disease and Danon disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endomyocardial**: **EMF**, **hypereosinophilic syndrome (Loeffler endocarditis)**, carcinoid heart disease, metastatic tumour, **mediastinal radiation** and drugs (anthracyclines, ergotamine, methysergide). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Non-infiltrative**: idiopathic or familial RCM (sarcomeric variants such as **TNNI3**, desmin, filamin C), systemic sclerosis and diabetic cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2023]`,`**ATTRwt risk**: age over 65-70 and **male sex (about 9 in 10 patients)**; the soft-tissue clues - **bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, lumbar canal stenosis, biceps tendon rupture** - precede heart failure by 5-10 years. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**ATTRv** is autosomal dominant: **V122I (p.Val142Ile)** in about 3-4% of people of West African ancestry causes cardiomyopathy, while **V30M (p.Val50Met)** (Portugal, Sweden, Japan) causes mainly polyneuropathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AL** arises from a small **plasma cell clone** (lambda light chains in about three-quarters); about 10-15% of myeloma patients develop AL, but most AL patients do not have overt myeloma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**EMF** risk factors proposed: poverty and malnutrition, **cassava-based diet**, helminth-driven **eosinophilia**, magnesium deficiency and **cerium or thorium from Kerala monazite sand** - none proven alone. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Takotsubo triggers**: physical (about 36% - surgery, sepsis, asthma, subarachnoid haemorrhage, stroke, seizure, phaeochromocytoma, IV adrenaline or dobutamine), emotional (about 28% - bereavement, conflict) and **none identifiable in about 28%**. [InterTAK 2015]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`A stiff ventricle has a **steep diastolic pressure-volume curve**: filling is rapid in early diastole and then stops abruptly, producing the **dip-and-plateau (square-root) trace** and a prominent y descent. [Harrison 22e]`,`High filling pressures dilate **both atria** (the echo hallmark), cause AF and produce pulmonary and systemic congestion while the ventricles stay small. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Stroke volume is small and fixed, so **cardiac output depends on heart rate** - the reason beta-blockers, verapamil and diltiazem cause hypotension and collapse. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Amyloid**: misfolded protein forms **beta-pleated sheet fibrils** that bind **Congo red (apple-green birefringence under polarised light)** and expand the extracellular space; walls thicken while ECG voltage falls (**voltage-mass discordance**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**AL light chains are directly cardiotoxic** (oxidative stress, apoptosis) as well as depositing, so AL progresses over months and NT-proBNP falls quickly when the clone is suppressed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthyretin (TTR)** is a liver-made tetramer carrying thyroxine and retinol-binding protein; with age or a variant it **dissociates into monomers that misfold** - stabilisers (tafamidis, acoramidis) hold the tetramer together, silencers (vutrisiran, patisiran, eplontersen) cut hepatic TTR production. [ESC 2023]`,`Amyloid in the **conduction system** causes AV block; in the **atria**, atrial standstill and **thrombus even in sinus rhythm**; in **autonomic nerves**, orthostatic hypotension; in **small vessels**, angina with normal coronaries. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sarcoid** non-caseating granulomas favour the **basal septum** (AV block), LV free wall and papillary muscles; healing leaves patchy scar that sustains **re-entrant VT**. [HRS 2014]`,`**Iron overload**: non-transferrin-bound iron enters myocytes through **L-type calcium channels** and generates free radicals - diastolic dysfunction first, then dilatation and arrhythmia, and the damage is **reversible with chelation** if caught early. [TIF 2021]`,`**Eosinophilic endomyocardial disease** passes through **necrotic, thrombotic and fibrotic stages**: granule proteins damage the endocardium, thrombus forms at the apex and inflow tracts, and fibrosis obliterates the apex and tethers the AV valves (regurgitation). [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`ESC 2023 classifies cardiomyopathy by **phenotype - HCM, DCM, non-dilated LV cardiomyopathy (NDLVC), ARVC and RCM** - and then names the cause; amyloid, Fabry and sarcoid are causes, not phenotypes. [ESC 2023]`,`Classic aetiological grouping of RCM: **myocardial** (non-infiltrative, infiltrative, storage) versus **endomyocardial** (EMF, HES, carcinoid, radiation, metastases) - see the table below. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amyloid types that reach the heart**: AL, **ATTRwt** (wild-type, formerly senile), **ATTRv** (hereditary) and rarely AA - AL and ATTR together cause over 95% of cardiac amyloidosis. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Perugini grade** on Tc-99m PYP, DPD or HMDP scintigraphy: **0** no cardiac uptake, **1** less than rib, **2** equal to rib, **3** greater than rib with attenuated bone - grades 2-3 are positive. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**UK NAC staging of ATTR-CM** uses **NT-proBNP over 3000 ng/L and eGFR under 45 mL/min**: stage I neither, stage II one, stage III both - median survival about **69, 47 and 24 months**. [Gillmore 2018]`,`**Mayo 2004 staging of AL** uses **NT-proBNP over 332 ng/L and troponin T over 0.035 µg/L**; the European modification splits stage III at **NT-proBNP over 8500 ng/L (stage IIIb)**, where median survival is only months. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Takotsubo types**: **apical ballooning (about 82%)**, midventricular (about 15%), basal or inverted (about 2%) and focal (about 1.5%); **primary** TTS presents with cardiac symptoms, **secondary** TTS complicates another illness in hospital. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**EMF** is right-sided, left-sided or **biventricular (the most frequent)**; the clinical picture follows the dominant ventricle. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac iron by CMR T2***: **over 20 ms normal, 10-20 ms mild to moderate, under 10 ms severe**, with a high risk of heart failure within a year. [TIF 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Typical OPD picture: **exertional breathlessness, fatigue and right-sided congestion** (oedema, abdominal distension, right upper quadrant ache) with a normal-sized heart on X-ray - often labelled HFpEF. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about **syncope and presyncope** (AV block, VT, autonomic failure, low fixed output) and **palpitations** (AF is very common in amyloid). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Amyloid red flags in the history**: **bilateral carpal tunnel surgery**, lumbar canal stenosis, spontaneous **biceps tendon rupture**, numb feet, postural dizziness, alternating diarrhoea and constipation, erectile dysfunction and weight loss. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`A known hypertensive whose BP has **'normalised' and who now cannot tolerate antihypertensives, beta-blockers or ACE inhibitors** is a classic amyloid clue. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**AL clues**: frothy urine (nephrotic syndrome), easy bruising, enlarged tongue, hoarse voice, hepatomegaly and a known MGUS or myeloma. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Family history** of neuropathy, heart failure or early pacemaker points to **ATTRv**; ask about unexplained deaths in relatives. [ESC 2023]`,`**Sarcoid**: a patient under 60 with unexplained **high-grade AV block or sustained VT**, with or without past lung, eye or skin sarcoid. [HRS 2014]`,`**Thalassaemia**: units transfused per year, chelation drug and adherence, last ferritin and CMR T2*; diabetes, hypothyroidism and hypogonadism indicate heavy iron load. [TIF 2021]`,`**EMF**: a child or young adult from coastal Kerala or a tropical rural area with **massive ascites, breathlessness and palpitations** - ascites out of proportion to leg oedema. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Takotsubo**: a postmenopausal woman with sudden chest pain or breathlessness minutes to hours after bereavement, a quarrel, surgery or an asthma attack; some present with syncope, shock or arrhythmia. [InterTAK 2018]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Raised JVP with prominent y descent** (rapid early filling) and often **Kussmaul sign** (JVP rises on inspiration) - both occur in restriction, not only in constriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low-volume pulse, narrow pulse pressure and orthostatic hypotension** (fall of 20/10 mmHg or more) suggest low fixed output and amyloid autonomic neuropathy. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**S3** from rapid filling is common; **S4 is often absent in amyloid** because the infiltrated atria cannot contract; soft MR and TR murmurs are frequent. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Congestive **hepatomegaly** (pulsatile with severe TR), ascites and pitting oedema; marked ascites with a giant right atrium suggests right-sided EMF. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**AL signs**: **periorbital purpura** (raccoon eyes after coughing, Valsalva or proctoscopy), **macroglossia** with tooth indentations, shoulder-pad sign and nail dystrophy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ATTR signs**: positive **Tinel and Phalen tests**, thenar wasting, distal sensory loss and absent ankle jerks (ATTRv neuropathy), vitreous opacities (ATTRv). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iron**: slate-grey or bronze skin, hepatomegaly and testicular atrophy; in **thalassaemia** add pallor, frontal bossing, maxillary hyperplasia and splenomegaly or a splenectomy scar. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sarcoid**: lupus pernio, erythema nodosum, uveitis, parotid swelling and lymphadenopathy; **Fabry**: angiokeratomas in the bathing-trunk area and cornea verticillata. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Takotsubo**: tachycardia, hypotension and basal crackles; a new **ejection systolic murmur that increases on standing or Valsalva** signals LV outflow tract obstruction. [InterTAK 2018]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Constrictive pericarditis** is the key differential because it is **surgically curable** - thick or calcified pericardium, respiratory septal shift, preserved medial e' and low BNP separate it (table below). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy**: thick walls with **high ECG voltage**, family history and asymmetric septal hypertrophy - amyloid has thick walls with **low or normal voltage**. [ESC 2023]`,`**Hypertensive heart disease**: concentric LVH with high voltage and a long BP history; wall thickness rarely exceeds 15 mm. [ESC 2023]`,`**HFpEF of obesity, diabetes and ageing**: mild LVH, normal voltage, no apical-sparing strain and no red flags. [ESC 2023]`,`**Aortic stenosis**: low-flow low-gradient AS in an elderly man with low voltage and carpal tunnel suggests **coexistent ATTR**; TAVI still benefits these patients. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Fabry disease** mimics HCM: short PR, LVH and **low native T1 on CMR**, whereas amyloid has a high native T1. [ESC 2023]`,`**Cirrhosis with ascites**: the **JVP is not raised** - always examine the neck veins in a patient with ascites. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Takotsubo** is confused with **anterior STEMI** (needs urgent angiography), **acute myocarditis** (LGE on CMR, viral prodrome), **phaeochromocytoma crisis** and other causes of **MINOCA** - see the comparison table. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Investigations - first line to tertiary`,points:[`**ECG**: **low QRS voltage** (all limb leads under 5 mm) in about half of AL and fewer ATTR patients, **pseudo-infarct Q waves** in V1-V3, AV block, bundle branch block and AF; normal voltage does not exclude amyloid. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Echo**: LV wall **12 mm or more**, small cavity, **biatrial enlargement**, thick valves and interatrial septum, small pericardial effusion, granular sparkling myocardium and restrictive filling (**E/A over 2, deceleration time under 150 ms, high E/e'**). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Strain imaging**: reduced global longitudinal strain with **relative apical sparing (cherry-on-top bull's-eye; apical to basal-plus-mid ratio 1 or more)** strongly suggests amyloid. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Biomarkers**: **NT-proBNP disproportionately high** for the clinical state and a **persistently raised troponin** - used for staging and to follow response. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Screen for AL in every suspected case**: **serum free light chain ratio, serum immunofixation and urine immunofixation** together - all three normal makes AL very unlikely (sensitivity about 99%). [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Bone scintigraphy with Tc-99m PYP, DPD or HMDP** with SPECT: **Perugini grade 2-3 with no monoclonal protein diagnoses ATTR-CM without biopsy** (specificity and PPV near 100%); MDP, the commonest bone agent in India, is not validated for this. [ESC 2023]`,`**CMR**: diffuse **subendocardial or transmural LGE**, myocardium nulling before blood pool, **raised native T1 and ECV (over 40% strongly suggests amyloid)**; **T2* under 20 ms** means iron; patchy mid-wall or epicardial basal septal LGE suggests sarcoid; an apical **triple layer** (myocardium, fibrosis, thrombus) suggests EMF. [ESC 2023]`,`**Tissue**: abdominal **fat-pad aspirate** (good yield in AL, poor in ATTRwt), bone marrow for the clone, and **endomyocardial biopsy** (near 100% sensitive) when non-invasive tests are inconclusive; fibrils are typed by **mass spectrometry** or immunoelectron microscopy. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`Once ATTR is confirmed, **TTR gene sequencing is done in every patient regardless of age** to separate ATTRwt from ATTRv and to trigger family screening. [ESC 2023]`,`**Other causes**: ferritin and transferrin saturation (over 45%), absolute eosinophil count (over 1500/µL suggests HES), FDG-PET after dietary preparation for active sarcoid, alpha-galactosidase A activity (Fabry in men), and CT or CMR of the pericardium to exclude constriction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Loop diuretic** is the mainstay: **torsemide 10-40 mg or furosemide 20-80 mg orally daily**, titrated to daily weight; the window is narrow - too little leaves congestion, too much drops a preload-dependent output. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**Avoid verapamil and diltiazem** (bind amyloid fibrils, cause heart block and shock); use **beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, ARBs and ARNI only in low doses if tolerated** - they often cause hypotension in amyloid. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**SGLT2 inhibitors and MRAs** are generally tolerated and reasonable; **digoxin** can be used cautiously at low dose with level monitoring for AF rate control. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**AF or flutter in cardiac amyloidosis: anticoagulate regardless of CHA2DS2-VA score** (DOAC preferred); amiodarone is the usual rhythm drug, and TOE precedes cardioversion even when anticoagulated because atrial thrombus is common. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022]`,`**Tafamidis** (TTR stabiliser) for ATTR-CM: **61 mg free acid or 80 mg meglumine orally once daily** for life - ATTR-ACT cut all-cause death (29.5% vs 42.9%) and CV admissions over 30 months; benefit is greatest in **NYHA I-II**, so start early. [ATTR-ACT 2018]`,`**Acoramidis 712 mg orally twice daily** (stabiliser) and **vutrisiran 25 mg SC every 3 months** (siRNA silencer) are newer disease-modifying options; silencers need **vitamin A supplementation**. [ATTRibute-CM 2024; HELIOS-B 2024]`,`**AL amyloidosis** is a haematological emergency: refer the same week; first line is **daratumumab plus bortezomib, cyclophosphamide and dexamethasone (Dara-VCd)**, with autologous stem-cell transplant if eligible - aim for dFLC under 40 mg/L. [ANDROMEDA 2021]`,`**Cardiac sarcoid**: **prednisolone 30 mg daily (about 0.5 mg/kg) for 4 weeks, tapered by 5 mg every 2-4 weeks to 5-10 mg maintenance**, with methotrexate as steroid-sparing agent - exclude TB first in India. [JCS 2016]`,`**Iron overload**: in thalassaemia adjust chelation (deferasirox, deferiprone, deferoxamine) to ferritin and T2*; in hereditary haemochromatosis **venesect 450-500 mL weekly** until ferritin is about 50 µg/L. [TIF 2021; Harrison 22e]`,`**EMF and HES**: diuretics, AF rate control and **oral anticoagulation** for thrombus; HES gets **prednisolone 1 mg/kg** in the acute phase and **imatinib** for the FIP1L1-PDGFRA variant. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** decompensated congestion, symptomatic hypotension, syncope, high-grade AV block, sustained VT, fast new AF, or suspected AL with heart failure. [ESC 2023]`,`**IV furosemide** bolus or infusion with daily weight, creatinine and potassium; avoid aggressive vasodilators and **avoid hypovolaemia** - small ventricles need adequate filling. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pacing** for symptomatic or high-grade AV block; consider **biventricular or conduction-system pacing** when frequent ventricular pacing is expected. [ESC 2023]`,`**ICD** for secondary prevention after haemodynamically significant VT or VF; in **cardiac sarcoid** consider an ICD when LVEF is 35% or less, or above 35% with extensive LGE or inducible VT. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`Sudden death in amyloid is often **electromechanical dissociation (PEA)** rather than VF, so primary-prevention ICD benefit is uncertain. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Thalassaemia with T2* under 10 ms or heart failure**: admit for **continuous IV deferoxamine 50-60 mg/kg/day over 24 hours** combined with deferiprone, jointly with haematology. [TIF 2021]`,`**Advanced therapies**: heart transplant for selected ATTR, idiopathic RCM and EMF (and AL after deep haematological response); LVADs work poorly in small, stiff ventricles. [ESC 2023]`,`**EMF surgery**: **endocardiectomy with mitral and/or tricuspid repair or replacement** relieves restriction and regurgitation in NYHA III-IV, at significant operative risk - best before end-stage. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Takotsubo syndrome - diagnosis and management`,points:[`**Definition**: acute, usually reversible **regional LV wall motion abnormality extending beyond a single coronary territory**, often after emotional or physical stress, with new ECG changes, modest troponin and disproportionately high BNP. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Mechanism**: a **catecholamine surge** acting on the apex, which has the highest beta-adrenoceptor density; at high adrenaline levels **beta2 receptors switch from Gs to Gi signalling**, producing protective apical stunning, with microvascular spasm and oestrogen loss contributing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**InterTAK criteria**: transient LV dysfunction (apical, midventricular, basal or focal); a trigger is not obligatory; **neurological disorders and phaeochromocytoma may be triggers**; new ECG changes; modest biomarker rise; **coexisting CAD does not exclude TTS**; no infectious myocarditis; mostly postmenopausal women. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Mayo criteria (2008)** excluded obstructive CAD and phaeochromocytoma; InterTAK (2018) dropped both exclusions - a favourite viva contrast. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**ECG**: ST elevation (often anterior and widespread), then **deep symmetrical T inversion with QTc prolongation** over days; **ST depression in aVR without ST elevation in V1** favours TTS over anterior STEMI. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**InterTAK diagnostic score** - female 25, emotional trigger 24, physical trigger 13, no ST depression 12, psychiatric disorder 11, neurological disorder 9, QTc prolongation 6: **over 70 points** gives about 90% probability of TTS. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Diagnosis is made in the cath lab**: every ST-elevation presentation goes for **coronary angiography with left ventriculography** (apical ballooning, the octopus-pot shape); CMR shows **oedema without significant LGE**, separating it from infarction and myocarditis. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Management**: aspirin and heparin until angiography, then supportive care; **ACE inhibitor or ARB** is associated with better 1-year survival, beta-blockers have not been shown to prevent recurrence, and continuous ECG monitoring is kept while QTc is prolonged. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Shock with LVOT obstruction** (basal hyperkinesis with SAM): **IV fluids, a short-acting beta-blocker (esmolol), and stop inotropes, nitrates and diuretics**; shock without LVOTO: **levosimendan or mechanical support (Impella, VA-ECMO)** rather than catecholamines, which are linked to worse outcomes. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**LV thrombus or large apical akinesia**: oral anticoagulation for about 3 months or until wall motion recovers; repeat imaging confirms recovery - failure to recover means another diagnosis. [InterTAK 2018]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Elderly**: ATTRwt in a man over 70 with HFpEF, AS, AF or a pacemaker - do scintigraphy plus the light-chain screen before accepting a label of HFpEF; frailty and cost shape disease-modifying therapy. [ESC 2023]`,`**CKD**: the free light chain ratio rises in CKD (renal reference range about **0.37-3.1**), NT-proBNP is less specific, and AL often presents with nephrotic syndrome - involve nephrology and haematology. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Diabetes**: think of haemochromatosis (bronze diabetes) or transfusional iron when diabetes accompanies restrictive physiology; HbA1c is unreliable in transfused patients - use fructosamine or glucose monitoring. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pregnancy**: thalassaemia needs pre-conception CMR T2* and intensified chelation, because deferasirox and deferiprone are stopped in pregnancy; TTS can occur peripartum and after caesarean section. [TIF 2021]`,`**Children**: idiopathic RCM in children carries a high risk of sudden death and early heart failure - refer early for transplant assessment; EMF and thalassaemic siderosis are the Indian paediatric causes. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: ECG voltage, echo wall thickness and NT-proBNP raise suspicion; send **free light chains and immunofixation** and refer for **Tc-99m PYP SPECT** - never diagnose ATTR on an MDP bone scan. [ESC 2023]`,`**Aortic stenosis with ATTR**: dual pathology is not a reason to deny TAVI - treat both. [ESC position statement 2021]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**AL untreated**: Mayo stage IIIb median survival is a few months; with Dara-VCd, deep haematological response and an NT-proBNP fall of **over 30% and over 300 ng/L (cardiac response)** predict long survival. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ATTR-CM** progresses over years - NAC stage survival about 69, 47 and 24 months; stabilisers and silencers slow decline but do not remove deposits. [Gillmore 2018]`,`**Amyloid complications**: **cardioembolic stroke** (atrial thrombus even in sinus rhythm), AV block, AF, syncope, drug intolerance, renal failure and sudden death (often PEA). [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Iron cardiomyopathy** is the leading cause of death in thalassaemia major, yet it is **reversible** - intensive chelation restores LVEF over months. [TIF 2021]`,`**EMF** has a poor prognosis in NYHA III-IV, with deaths from heart failure, arrhythmia and thromboembolism; surgery improves symptoms and survival in selected patients. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Takotsubo complications**: acute heart failure (12-45%), cardiogenic shock (about 10%), LVOTO, MR, LV thrombus, AF, VT/VF, torsade and rarely free-wall rupture; **in-hospital mortality about 4%**, similar to ACS. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**TTS recurrence is about 1.8% per patient-year**; male sex, physical triggers and shock predict worse long-term outcome - it is not benign. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Follow-up of amyloid**: every 3-6 months - NYHA class, weight, standing BP, NT-proBNP, troponin, eGFR and potassium; yearly echo and **ECG or Holter for conduction disease**. [ACC ECDP 2023]`]},{heading:`Referral, prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Refer to cardiology** every patient with heart failure and thick walls plus a red flag; **refer to haematology the same week** for any monoclonal protein or abnormal light chain ratio - AL kills within months untreated. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**ATTRv**: genetic counselling and **cascade testing of first-degree relatives**; carriers are followed with ECG, echo, NT-proBNP and neuropathy review. [ESC 2023]`,`**Thalassaemia prevention**: carrier screening (**HbA2 over 3.5%** on HPLC) in adolescents, premarital and antenatal clinics with prenatal diagnosis - the core of the NHM haemoglobinopathy programme. [NHM Haemoglobinopathy Guidelines 2016]`,`**Counsel**: daily weight, salt about 5 g/day with a flexible diuretic plan, rise slowly from bed, **avoid NSAIDs, verapamil and diltiazem**, and carry a card listing the diagnosis and drugs. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**Vitamin A at the recommended daily allowance** is advised with TTR silencers, because they lower serum vitamin A. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Takotsubo**: explain that recovery is expected, address the trigger and screen for anxiety and depression (psychiatric comorbidity is common), and offer psychological support. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Programme link**: heart failure and AF are detected through **NP-NCD screening at Ayushman Arogya Mandir**, and pacemakers, ICDs and cardiac surgery are covered by **AB PM-JAY** packages for eligible families. [NP-NCD Operational Guidelines 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy guideline**: phenotype-first classification (adds **NDLVC**), multimodality imaging, **genetic testing** in cardiomyopathy and the non-biopsy ATTR algorithm. [ESC 2023]`,`**ACC 2023 Expert Consensus Decision Pathway** on cardiac amyloidosis: multidisciplinary care, red-flag screening, avoid non-dihydropyridine CCBs, anticoagulate AF regardless of score. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**ATTRibute-CM (2024)**: **acoramidis** improved the hierarchical composite of death, CV hospitalisation, NT-proBNP and functional capacity; approved by the FDA (2024) and EMA (2025). [ATTRibute-CM 2024]`,`**HELIOS-B (2024)**: **vutrisiran** reduced all-cause death and recurrent CV events, including in patients already on tafamidis; FDA approval for ATTR-CM followed in 2025. [HELIOS-B 2024]`,`**Guideline difference**: AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022 gives tafamidis class 1 in **NYHA I-III**, while ESC recommends it in **NYHA I-II** - state both if asked. [AHA/ACC/HFSA 2022; ESC 2023]`,`**ANDROMEDA (2021)** made **Dara-VCd** the first-line standard for newly diagnosed AL amyloidosis. [ANDROMEDA 2021]`,`**ESC 2023 ACS guideline** lists Takotsubo among causes behind a **working diagnosis of MINOCA**, with CMR recommended to reach the final diagnosis. [ESC 2023 ACS]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **how do you differentiate restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis?** - draw the table: pericardium, septal shift, respiratory variation, e', BNP, PA pressure, cath discordance. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Red flags of cardiac amyloidosis**: thick walls with low voltage, bilateral carpal tunnel, biceps rupture, lumbar stenosis, neuropathy, intolerance of antihypertensives, apical sparing, AV block, persistent troponin. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing ATTR on scintigraphy without excluding a monoclonal protein - AL can show uptake and needs urgent chemotherapy. [ESC position statement 2021]`,`**Common mistake**: verapamil or full-dose beta-blocker for AF in amyloid - hypotension and heart block follow. [ACC ECDP 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: dobutamine or noradrenaline for Takotsubo shock without first excluding LVOT obstruction on echo. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Congo red** shows **apple-green birefringence under polarised light** - a one-mark certainty. [Harrison 22e]`,`Know the **Perugini grades**, the **NAC cut-offs (NT-proBNP 3000, eGFR 45)** and the **tafamidis dose (61 mg free acid or 80 mg meglumine)** - favourite MCQ material. [ESC 2023]`,`**Kerala EMF** viva: obliterated apex, giant right atrium, AV valve regurgitation, ascites out of proportion to oedema, treated by endocardiectomy. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Causes of restrictive and infiltrative cardiomyopathy`,columns:[`Group`,`Disease`,`Clue that points to it`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Infiltrative`,`AL amyloidosis`,`Nephrotic syndrome, macroglossia, periorbital purpura, rapid course`,`Free light chains, immunofixation; fat pad, marrow or EMB with typing`],[`Infiltrative`,`ATTRwt amyloidosis`,`Man over 70, HFpEF, bilateral carpal tunnel, biceps rupture, AS`,`Tc-99m PYP/DPD grade 2-3 with no monoclonal protein; TTR gene`],[`Infiltrative`,`ATTRv (hereditary)`,`Family history, neuropathy, autonomic failure, vitreous opacities`,`TTR gene sequencing plus scintigraphy`],[`Infiltrative`,`Cardiac sarcoidosis`,`Age under 60 with AV block or VT; lung, eye or skin disease`,`CMR patchy LGE plus FDG-PET; extracardiac biopsy`],[`Storage`,`Haemochromatosis / transfusional iron`,`Thalassaemia transfusions, diabetes, bronze skin, hepatomegaly`,`Ferritin, transferrin saturation over 45%, CMR T2* under 20 ms`],[`Storage`,`Anderson-Fabry disease`,`Neuropathic pain, angiokeratoma, proteinuria, short PR`,`Alpha-galactosidase A (men), GLA gene, low native T1`],[`Endomyocardial`,`Endomyocardial fibrosis`,`Young, poor, tropical (Kerala); ascites, giant RA, obliterated apex`,`Echo apical obliteration; CMR triple-layer LGE`],[`Endomyocardial`,`Hypereosinophilic syndrome (Loeffler)`,`Eosinophils over 1500/µL, rash, lung and nerve involvement`,`Eosinophil count, FIP1L1-PDGFRA, EMB`],[`Endomyocardial`,`Carcinoid heart disease`,`Flushing, diarrhoea, liver metastases; right-sided valves`,`Urinary 5-HIAA, chromogranin A`],[`Endomyocardial`,`Radiation or anthracycline`,`Mediastinal radiotherapy (Hodgkin, breast) years earlier`,`History; often coexists with constriction`],[`Non-infiltrative`,`Idiopathic or familial RCM`,`Child or young adult, family history, normal wall thickness`,`Genetic testing (TNNI3, desmin, FLNC)`]]},{heading:`AL versus ATTR cardiac amyloidosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`AL (light chain)`,`ATTRwt (wild-type)`,`ATTRv (hereditary)`],rows:[[`Precursor protein`,`Monoclonal kappa or lambda light chain`,`Normal transthyretin from liver`,`Mutant transthyretin`],[`Typical patient`,`50-70 years, either sex`,`Man over 70`,`Variable age; family history; V122I in West African ancestry`],[`Extracardiac`,`Kidney (nephrotic), liver, tongue, bruising, neuropathy`,`Carpal tunnel, lumbar stenosis, biceps rupture`,`Polyneuropathy, autonomic failure, eye, carpal tunnel`],[`Course`,`Rapid - months`,`Slow - years`,`Variable`],[`Bone scintigraphy`,`Usually grade 0-1 (can be positive)`,`Grade 2-3`,`Grade 2-3 (a few variants low)`],[`Key diagnostic test`,`Light chains, immunofixation, tissue typing`,`Scintigraphy with no monoclonal protein`,`TTR gene sequencing`],[`Staging`,`Mayo 2004 with European modification; Mayo 2012`,`UK NAC (NT-proBNP 3000, eGFR 45)`,`UK NAC`],[`Treatment`,`Dara-VCd; autologous stem-cell transplant`,`Tafamidis, acoramidis, vutrisiran`,`Tafamidis, acoramidis, vutrisiran; patisiran or eplontersen for neuropathy`]]},{heading:`Investigation cut-offs in suspected cardiac amyloidosis`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding that supports amyloid`,`Comment`],rows:[[`ECG`,`Limb-lead QRS under 5 mm; pseudo-Q waves V1-V3; AV block`,`Low voltage in about half of AL, less often in ATTR`],[`Echo wall thickness`,`LV wall 12 mm or more with small cavity`,`Voltage-mass discordance is the clue`],[`Diastolic function`,`E/A over 2, DT under 150 ms, septal e' under 6 cm/s`,`Restrictive filling means advanced disease`],[`Longitudinal strain`,`Relative apical sparing, ratio 1 or more`,`Cherry-on-top bull's-eye pattern`],[`NT-proBNP and troponin`,`Disproportionately high; troponin persistently raised`,`Used in Mayo and NAC staging`],[`Free light chain ratio`,`Abnormal kappa/lambda (normal 0.26-1.65; renal 0.37-3.1)`,`Always with serum and urine immunofixation`],[`Tc-99m PYP/DPD/HMDP`,`Perugini grade 2-3; PYP heart-to-contralateral ratio 1.5 or more at 1 h`,`Diagnostic of ATTR only if no monoclonal protein; SPECT needed`],[`CMR`,`Diffuse subendocardial or transmural LGE; ECV over 40%; raised native T1`,`Caution with gadolinium if eGFR under 30 - native T1 still helps`]]},{heading:`Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Restrictive cardiomyopathy`,`Constrictive pericarditis`],rows:[[`Past history`,`Amyloid, sarcoid, iron, radiation, EMF`,`TB, cardiac surgery, radiation, recurrent pericarditis`],[`Extra heart sound`,`S3; S4 often absent in amyloid`,`Pericardial knock (early, high-pitched, 0.09-0.12 s after A2)`],[`MR/TR murmurs`,`Common`,`Uncommon`],[`CXR / CT`,`Normal pericardium, no calcification`,`Calcification (TB); pericardium 4 mm or thicker`],[`Respiratory variation in mitral E`,`Minimal (under 10-15%)`,`Over 25%`],[`Septal motion`,`Normal`,`Septal bounce and respiratory shift`],[`Medial mitral annular e'`,`Reduced (under 6-7 cm/s)`,`Preserved (9 cm/s or more); annulus reversus`],[`Hepatic vein flow`,`Inspiratory diastolic reversal`,`Expiratory diastolic reversal`],[`BNP / NT-proBNP`,`Markedly raised`,`Normal or mildly raised`],[`PA systolic pressure`,`Often over 50 mmHg`,`Usually under 50 mmHg`],[`Catheterisation`,`LVEDP-RVEDP over 5 mmHg; concordant LV/RV systolic pressures`,`Diastolic pressures equal within 5 mmHg; discordant (systolic area index over 1.1)`],[`Treatment`,`Disease-specific therapy, diuretics, transplant`,`Pericardiectomy (ATT first if TB)`]]},{heading:`Drugs in restrictive and infiltrative cardiomyopathy and Takotsubo`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Tafamidis (TTR stabiliser)`,`61 mg free acid or 80 mg meglumine (4 x 20 mg) orally once daily`,`Lifelong; best started in NYHA I-II`,`Well tolerated; diarrhoea, UTI; avoid in pregnancy; very costly`],[`Acoramidis (TTR stabiliser)`,`712 mg orally twice daily`,`Lifelong`,`Diarrhoea, abdominal pain; small early creatinine rise`],[`Vutrisiran (TTR silencer)`,`25 mg SC every 3 months`,`Lifelong`,`Lowers vitamin A - supplement RDA; injection-site reaction`],[`Dara-VCd (AL)`,`Daratumumab 1800 mg SC, bortezomib 1.3 mg/m2 SC, cyclophosphamide 300 mg/m2, dexamethasone 40 mg - weekly`,`28-day cycles; VCd for 6 cycles, daratumumab up to 24 cycles`,`Infections, bortezomib neuropathy, fluid retention (dexamethasone 20 mg if frail or overloaded)`],[`Torsemide / furosemide`,`Torsemide 10-40 mg or furosemide 20-80 mg orally daily; IV in decompensation`,`Titrate to daily weight`,`Hypotension, AKI, hypokalaemia - output is preload-dependent`],[`Apixaban (AF in amyloid)`,`5 mg twice daily; 2.5 mg if two of age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5 mg/dL or more`,`Lifelong, regardless of CHA2DS2-VA`,`Bleeding; avoid in end-stage renal failure`],[`Prednisolone (cardiac sarcoid)`,`30 mg orally daily (about 0.5 mg/kg)`,`4 weeks, then taper 5 mg every 2-4 weeks to 5-10 mg; 1-2 years`,`Exclude TB first; diabetes, osteoporosis; add methotrexate 10-20 mg weekly`],[`Deferasirox (film-coated)`,`14-28 mg/kg orally once daily`,`Long term, guided by ferritin and T2*`,`Creatinine rise, hepatitis, GI bleeding; stop in pregnancy`],[`Deferiprone`,`75-100 mg/kg/day orally in 3 divided doses`,`Long term; best at removing cardiac iron`,`Agranulocytosis - weekly neutrophil count; arthralgia`],[`Deferoxamine`,`40-50 mg/kg SC over 8-12 h, 5-7 nights a week; 50-60 mg/kg/day continuous IV for cardiac siderosis`,`Until T2* over 20 ms and LVEF normal`,`Hearing and visual toxicity, growth delay, Yersinia infection`],[`Esmolol (Takotsubo with LVOTO)`,`500 µg/kg IV over 1 min, then 50-300 µg/kg/min`,`Until gradient and BP improve`,`Bradycardia, hypotension; avoid in low output without LVOTO`],[`Levosimendan (Takotsubo shock)`,`0.05-0.2 µg/kg/min IV for 24 h; omit bolus if hypotensive`,`Single 24-hour infusion`,`Hypotension, AF, hypokalaemia`]]},{heading:`Takotsubo versus anterior STEMI versus acute myocarditis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Takotsubo syndrome`,`Anterior STEMI`,`Acute myocarditis`],rows:[[`Typical patient`,`Postmenopausal woman after stress`,`Adult with risk factors`,`Young adult after viral illness`],[`ECG`,`Widespread ST elevation, none in V1, ST depression in aVR; later deep T inversion and long QT`,`Territorial ST elevation with reciprocal depression`,`Diffuse or regional ST-T change, arrhythmia, AV block`],[`Troponin vs wall motion`,`Modest troponin, large wall motion defect; BNP very high`,`Troponin proportional to infarct size`,`Variable`],[`Wall motion`,`Apical ballooning beyond one coronary territory`,`LAD territory`,`Patchy, often inferolateral`],[`Coronaries`,`No culprit occlusion (CAD may coexist)`,`Occluded LAD`,`Normal`],[`CMR`,`Oedema without significant LGE`,`Subendocardial or transmural LGE in LAD territory`,`Subepicardial or mid-wall LGE with oedema (Lake Louise)`],[`Recovery`,`Days to weeks, near complete`,`Scar persists`,`Variable; some progress to DCM`]]}],redFlags:[`Syncope, high-grade AV block or sustained VT in suspected infiltrative cardiomyopathy - admit for monitoring and pacing or ICD decision.`,`Thick LV walls with any monoclonal protein or abnormal light chain ratio - same-week haematology referral for possible AL amyloidosis.`,`Heart failure with systolic BP under 90 mmHg, rising creatinine or diuretic resistance in restrictive physiology.`,`Takotsubo with shock, LVOT gradient, QTc over 500 ms, LV thrombus or ventricular arrhythmia - CCU care.`,`Chest pain with ST elevation in a stressed postmenopausal woman - treat as STEMI and send to the cath lab; Takotsubo is a diagnosis made after angiography.`,`Thalassaemia with CMR T2* under 10 ms, falling LVEF or new arrhythmia - admit for intensive IV chelation.`,`Unexplained Mobitz II or complete heart block in a patient under 60 - exclude cardiac sarcoidosis before labelling it idiopathic.`],pearls:[`**Thick walls, small voltage** = amyloid until proved otherwise.`,`**Bilateral carpal tunnel + HFpEF in an elderly man** = think ATTRwt; carpal tunnel precedes the heart by 5-10 years.`,`**No monoclonal protein + Perugini 2-3 = ATTR without biopsy**; any monoclonal protein = tissue diagnosis first.`,`The hypertensive whose BP **cures itself** and who cannot tolerate antihypertensives has amyloid until shown otherwise.`,`**Never verapamil or diltiazem in amyloid** - they bind fibrils and cause heart block and shock.`,`**Kussmaul sign occurs in restriction too**; preserved medial e' and respiratory septal shift point to constriction.`,`Takotsubo = the **octopus pot**: apical ballooning with a hyperkinetic base; ST depression in aVR without ST elevation in V1 favours it over anterior STEMI.`,`Takotsubo shock: **check for LVOTO first** - fluids and esmolol for obstruction, levosimendan or Impella without it, avoid catecholamines.`,`**CMR T2* under 10 ms** in thalassaemia = severe cardiac iron; deferiprone is the best cardiac chelator.`,`Kerala EMF: **obliterated apex, giant right atrium, ascites out of proportion to oedema**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-q1`,stem:`A 76-year-old man with heart failure and preserved ejection fraction has LV wall thickness 16 mm, limb-lead QRS voltage under 5 mm and previous bilateral carpal tunnel release. Tc-99m PYP SPECT shows Perugini grade 3 cardiac uptake. Serum free light chain ratio and serum and urine immunofixation are normal. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Endomyocardial biopsy to confirm amyloid before any treatment`,`Diagnose ATTR cardiomyopathy and send TTR gene sequencing`,`Start bortezomib-based chemotherapy for AL amyloidosis`,`Label it hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and start verapamil`],answer:1,explanation:`Grade 2-3 uptake on PYP, DPD or HMDP scintigraphy with no monoclonal protein on light chains and immunofixation diagnoses ATTR cardiomyopathy without biopsy (specificity near 100%), and TTR genotyping is then done at any age to separate wild-type from hereditary disease. Biopsy is needed only when a monoclonal protein is present or tests disagree; chemotherapy treats AL, which normal light-chain tests make very unlikely; and verapamil is dangerous in amyloid, while low voltage argues against sarcomeric HCM.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man treated for tuberculous pericardial effusion two years ago now has a raised JVP, ascites and leg oedema. Which echocardiographic finding most strongly favours constrictive pericarditis over restrictive cardiomyopathy?`,options:[`Septal mitral annular e' velocity of 4 cm/s`,`Pulmonary artery systolic pressure of 65 mmHg`,`Respiratory septal shift with medial e' of 12 cm/s, higher than lateral e'`,`Inspiratory diastolic flow reversal in the hepatic veins`],answer:2,explanation:`In constriction the myocardium is healthy, so medial e' is preserved (9 cm/s or more) and often exceeds lateral e' (annulus reversus, because the lateral annulus is tethered by pericardium), and ventricular interdependence produces respiratory septal shift. A septal e' of 4 cm/s, PA systolic pressure over 50 mmHg and inspiratory hepatic vein reversal all point to restrictive cardiomyopathy, in which the myocardium itself is stiff.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old woman develops chest pain and BP 80/50 mmHg after her son's death. Angiography shows unobstructed coronaries and apical ballooning; echo shows basal hyperkinesis, systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve and an LV outflow gradient of 70 mmHg. Which management is most appropriate?`,options:[`IV fluids and a short-acting beta-blocker such as esmolol, stopping inotropes and nitrates`,`IV dobutamine infusion to support contractility`,`IV nitroglycerin and furosemide to offload the ventricle`,`Noradrenaline infusion with an intra-aortic balloon pump as the first step`],answer:0,explanation:`Hypotension in Takotsubo with dynamic LVOT obstruction is worsened by anything that raises contractility or lowers preload and afterload; volume plus a short-acting beta-blocker reduces the gradient. Dobutamine increases basal hypercontraction and the gradient, nitrates and diuretics shrink the cavity and worsen obstruction, and catecholamines are linked to higher mortality in Takotsubo - levosimendan or Impella/ECMO are reserved for shock without obstruction.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-q4`,stem:`A 19-year-old man with transfusion-dependent beta-thalassaemia, poorly adherent to oral chelation, has breathlessness, ferritin 6500 ng/mL, LVEF 42% and cardiac CMR T2* of 7 ms. What is the best chelation strategy?`,options:[`Continue deferasirox at the current dose and repeat T2* in a year`,`Start weekly venesection of 500 mL`,`Stop transfusions until ferritin falls below 1000 ng/mL`,`Continuous 24-hour IV deferoxamine combined with oral deferiprone`],answer:3,explanation:`Cardiac T2* under 10 ms with falling LVEF is cardiac siderosis with heart failure, a chelation emergency treated with continuous IV deferoxamine (50-60 mg/kg/day) plus deferiprone, the drug best at removing myocardial iron, and the damage is often reversible. Continuing a failing regimen wastes time, venesection is for hereditary haemochromatosis with normal haemoglobin, and stopping transfusion in thalassaemia major worsens anaemia and heart failure.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-q5`,stem:`Tafamidis improves survival in transthyretin amyloid cardiomyopathy by which mechanism?`,options:[`Silencing hepatic TTR messenger RNA with a small interfering RNA`,`Antibody-mediated removal of deposited amyloid fibrils`,`Binding the thyroxine-binding sites so the TTR tetramer does not dissociate`,`Suppressing the plasma cell clone that produces light chains`],answer:2,explanation:`Tafamidis, like acoramidis, is a TTR stabiliser: it occupies the thyroxine-binding sites and holds the tetramer together, so fewer amyloidogenic monomers form. Silencing hepatic TTR production is how vutrisiran and patisiran work, antibody fibril depleters are still investigational, and suppressing the plasma cell clone treats AL rather than ATTR amyloidosis.`,difficulty:`easy`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the aetiology, clinical red flags, diagnostic algorithm and management of cardiac amyloidosis. How will you differentiate restrictive cardiomyopathy from constrictive pericarditis?`,openingLines:[`Cardiac amyloidosis is extracellular deposition of misfolded protein fibrils - immunoglobulin light chain (AL) or transthyretin (ATTR) - in the myocardium, producing a thick-walled, stiff ventricle with restrictive physiology and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction.`,`It is no longer rare: wild-type ATTR is found in about 13% of elderly HFpEF and 1 in 6 older patients with aortic stenosis, ATTR is now diagnosed without biopsy, and stabilisers such as tafamidis improve survival.`],answer:[{heading:`Types and pathophysiology`,points:[`**AL**: plasma cell clone, light chains deposit and are directly toxic - rapid course.`,`**ATTRwt**: elderly men; **ATTRv**: hereditary variants (V122I, V30M); TTR tetramer dissociates into amyloidogenic monomers.`,`Fibrils expand the interstitium: stiff ventricle, **biatrial enlargement**, low fixed stroke volume, conduction disease, atrial thrombus.`]},{heading:`Clinical red flags`,points:[`HFpEF with LV wall 12 mm or more plus: **low ECG voltage**, pseudo-Q waves, AV block.`,`**Bilateral carpal tunnel**, lumbar stenosis, biceps rupture, neuropathy, autonomic failure.`,`Previously hypertensive now hypotensive; intolerance of beta-blockers and ACE inhibitors.`,`AL clues: macroglossia, periorbital purpura, nephrotic syndrome; low-flow aortic stenosis in the elderly.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic algorithm`,points:[`Echo: thick walls, sparkling myocardium, **apical-sparing strain**; NT-proBNP and troponin raised.`,`Step 1: **serum free light chains + serum and urine immunofixation** in every patient.`,`Step 2: **Tc-99m PYP/DPD/HMDP scintigraphy with SPECT** - Perugini 2-3 with no monoclonal protein = ATTR without biopsy.`,`Monoclonal protein present: tissue biopsy (fat pad, marrow, EMB) with typing by mass spectrometry; urgent haematology.`,`CMR: diffuse subendocardial LGE, ECV over 40%; **TTR gene sequencing** in all ATTR; stage (NAC: NT-proBNP 3000, eGFR 45; Mayo for AL).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Loop diuretic to daily weight; avoid **verapamil, diltiazem** and high-dose beta-blockers or ACE inhibitors.`,`AF: **anticoagulate regardless of CHA2DS2-VA**; amiodarone for rhythm; pacing for AV block.`,`ATTR: **tafamidis 61 mg (or 80 mg meglumine) daily**; acoramidis 712 mg twice daily; vutrisiran 25 mg SC 3-monthly.`,`AL: **Dara-VCd**, autologous stem-cell transplant if eligible; monitor dFLC and NT-proBNP response.`,`Transplant in selected; genetic counselling and cascade screening in ATTRv.`]},{heading:`Restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis`,points:[`Constriction: thick or calcified pericardium, **septal bounce**, mitral E variation over 25%, **medial e' 9 cm/s or more**, low BNP, PA systolic under 50 mmHg, discordant ventricular pressures.`,`Restriction: normal pericardium, e' under 6-7 cm/s, high BNP, PA systolic often over 50 mmHg, concordant pressures, LGE in myocardium.`,`Constriction is cured by **pericardiectomy**; restriction needs disease-specific treatment.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagnostic flow chart: suspect, then light chains plus immunofixation plus PYP scintigraphy, branching to ATTR without biopsy or tissue biopsy`,`Table: restrictive cardiomyopathy versus constrictive pericarditis (8-10 rows)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, types and pathophysiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Red flags and clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm with cut-offs`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management`,marks:3},{part:`Restriction versus constriction`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`AL`,`ATTR`,`tafamidis`,`PYP scintigraphy`,`Perugini`,`free light chains`,`apical sparing`,`constriction`]},{id:`cardiovascular-restrictive-infiltrative-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Takotsubo syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Takotsubo syndrome is an acute, usually reversible regional LV dysfunction extending beyond a single coronary territory - typically apical ballooning - triggered by emotional or physical stress, mostly in postmenopausal women.`,`It mimics anterior STEMI, accounts for 1-3% of suspected ACS and carries an in-hospital mortality of about 4%, similar to ACS.`],answer:[{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Catecholamine surge; apex has the highest beta-adrenoceptor density; beta2 switches from Gs to Gi - apical stunning.`,`Microvascular spasm and oestrogen deficiency contribute; types: apical (82%), midventricular, basal, focal.`]},{heading:`InterTAK diagnostic criteria (2018)`,points:[`Transient LV dysfunction beyond one coronary territory; trigger not obligatory.`,`New ECG changes; modest troponin; very high BNP; coexisting CAD and phaeochromocytoma do not exclude it; no myocarditis.`,`InterTAK score over 70 = about 90% probability.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG: ST elevation, then deep T inversion and long QTc; ST depression in aVR without ST elevation in V1.`,`**Coronary angiography with ventriculography** (octopus pot); echo for LVOTO, MR, thrombus; CMR - oedema without LGE.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Treat as ACS until angiography; supportive care; monitor QTc and rhythm.`,`LVOTO: fluids and esmolol, stop inotropes and nitrates; shock without LVOTO: levosimendan or Impella/ECMO.`,`LV thrombus: anticoagulate about 3 months; long term ACE inhibitor or ARB; psychological support.`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Heart failure, shock (10%), arrhythmia, thrombus, rupture; recurrence 1.8% per year; recovery within weeks.`]}],mustDraw:[`Ventriculogram sketch: apical ballooning with hyperkinetic base (octopus pot)`,`Flow: STEMI-like presentation, angiography with LV gram, echo for LVOTO, CMR, supportive care`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnostic criteria`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Complications and prognosis`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`Takotsubo`,`apical ballooning`,`InterTAK`,`LVOTO`,`levosimendan`,`MINOCA`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - cardiomyopathy, amyloidosis and haemochromatosis chapters`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - restrictive, infiltrative and stress cardiomyopathies`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies (Eur Heart J 2023)`,`Diagnosis and treatment of cardiac amyloidosis: position statement of the ESC Working Group on Myocardial and Pericardial Diseases, 2021`,`2023 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway on Comprehensive Multidisciplinary Care for the Patient With Cardiac Amyloidosis`,`International Expert Consensus Document on Takotsubo Syndrome (InterTAK), Parts I and II, Eur Heart J 2018`,`ATTR-ACT (NEJM 2018), ATTRibute-CM (NEJM 2024), HELIOS-B (NEJM 2024) and ANDROMEDA (NEJM 2021) trials`,`MoHFW/NHM Prevention and Control of Haemoglobinopathies in India, 2016; Thalassaemia International Federation Guidelines for Transfusion-Dependent Thalassaemia, 4th edition, 2021`]}),a.push({id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis`,title:`Myocarditis`,oneLiner:`**Myocarditis** is **inflammation of the myocardium** - diagnosed by **histology (Dallas and immunohistochemical criteria) or CMR (Lake Louise criteria)** with a compatible clinical syndrome and raised troponin - caused mostly by **viruses, autoimmunity, drugs such as immune checkpoint inhibitors, and toxins**; ESC 2025 places it with pericarditis on one spectrum, the **inflammatory myopericardial syndrome (IMPS)**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`myocarditis`,`inflammatory myopericardial syndrome`,`IMPS`,`myopericarditis`,`perimyocarditis`,`fulminant myocarditis`,`giant cell myocarditis`,`eosinophilic myocarditis`,`Lake Louise criteria`,`CMR`,`endomyocardial biopsy`,`Dallas criteria`,`immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis`,`mRNA vaccine myocarditis`,`Coxsackie B`,`parvovirus B19`,`diphtheritic myocarditis`,`scorpion sting myocarditis`,`VA-ECMO`,`return to sport`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Myocarditis** is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium diagnosed by established histological, immunological and immunohistochemical criteria, or clinically by **CMR plus troponin rise in a compatible syndrome**. [ESC 2025]`,`ESC 2025 introduces the umbrella term **inflammatory myopericardial syndrome (IMPS)** - isolated myocarditis, isolated pericarditis and the overlap forms (**myopericarditis** when pericarditis predominates, **perimyocarditis** when myocarditis predominates with LV dysfunction). [ESC 2025]`,`**Incidence** is estimated at about **10-22 per 100,000 per year** worldwide, peaking in **young men (20-40 years)**; many mild cases are never diagnosed. [Harrison 22e]`,`Myocarditis is a recognised cause of **sudden cardiac death in young people and athletes** and a precursor of a proportion of **dilated cardiomyopathy**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Acute myocarditis** lasts under about 1 month from onset; **chronic inflammatory cardiomyopathy** is persistent inflammation with LV dysfunction for months. [ESC 2025]`,`In India, systematic incidence data are lacking; infections specific to the region - **dengue, scrub typhus, diphtheria, leptospirosis, chikungunya** - and toxins such as **scorpion sting and aluminium phosphide** add to the viral burden. [API Textbook of Medicine 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Viral (commonest in developed countries)**: enteroviruses (**Coxsackie B**), adenovirus, **parvovirus B19**, HHV-6, influenza, **SARS-CoV-2**, HIV, hepatitis C, EBV, CMV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other infections, important in India**: **diphtheria** (toxin), **dengue**, **scrub typhus**, leptospirosis, typhoid, malaria, **rheumatic carditis**, Chagas (Latin America) and toxoplasmosis in immunocompromised. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immune-mediated**: SLE, systemic sclerosis, sarcoidosis, **giant cell myocarditis** (with thymoma, autoimmune disease), EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndrome, inflammatory bowel disease. [ESC 2025]`,`**Drugs - immune checkpoint inhibitors** (nivolumab, pembrolizumab, ipilimumab; highest risk with **combined PD-1 plus CTLA-4**) cause myocarditis in about **1%**, usually in the first 3 months. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Hypersensitivity (eosinophilic) myocarditis**: clozapine, sulfonamides, beta-lactams, carbamazepine, mesalazine, dobutamine infusions; **clozapine** risk peaks in the first 4-8 weeks. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Vaccines**: **mRNA COVID-19 vaccines** cause myocarditis mainly in **males aged 12-29 after the second dose**, typically 2-4 days later; India used mainly ChAdOx1 (Covishield) and Covaxin, so this is uncommon here; smallpox vaccine is a historical cause. [CDC ACIP 2021; ESC 2025]`,`**Toxins**: **Indian red scorpion (Mesobuthus tamulus)** venom (catecholamine storm), **aluminium phosphide** (celphos), carbon monoxide, alcohol, cocaine, anthracyclines, radiation. [API Textbook of Medicine 2022]`,`**Genetic predisposition**: variants in **desmoplakin** and other cardiomyopathy genes can present as recurrent 'hot phase' myocarditis. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Phase 1 - viral injury (days)**: enteroviruses enter via the **coxsackie-adenovirus receptor (CAR)**; viral protease 2A cleaves **dystrophin**, causing direct myocyte necrosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phase 2 - immune response (weeks)**: innate immunity then T cells clear virus but also attack myocytes; **molecular mimicry with cardiac myosin** generates autoantibodies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Phase 3 - outcome (months to years)**: resolution with recovery, or persistent inflammation and fibrosis leading to **dilated cardiomyopathy**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Parvovirus B19** infects endothelium rather than myocytes, causing microvascular dysfunction; low viral copy numbers are often bystanders. [ESC 2025]`,`**ICI myocarditis**: removing PD-1/CTLA-4 brakes unleashes **clonal T cells recognising shared muscle antigens** - hence overlap with **myositis and myasthenia** (the triple-M syndrome). [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Diphtheria toxin** inhibits protein synthesis (ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2), causing myocyte damage and **conduction block** in the second week. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Scorpion venom** opens sodium channels and releases catecholamines - **adrenergic myocarditis**, pulmonary oedema and shock - the basis for **prazosin** therapy. [Bawaskar, Lancet 2011]`,`Myocardial **oedema** stiffens and thickens the wall in fulminant cases; necrosis and scar provide the substrate for **ventricular arrhythmia and AV block**. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Clinical**: fulminant (cardiogenic shock needing inotropes or mechanical support), **complicated** (LVEF under 50%, heart failure, sustained ventricular arrhythmia, high-grade AV block) and **uncomplicated** (chest pain, preserved function). [ESC 2025]`,`**Histological types**: **lymphocytic** (viral, commonest), **eosinophilic** (hypersensitivity, HES, parasitic), **giant cell** (autoimmune, fulminant) and **granulomatous** (sarcoid) - type decides immunosuppression. [ESC 2025]`,`**Dallas criteria (1986)**: inflammatory infiltrate with myocyte necrosis not typical of ischaemia; insensitive because of sampling error. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immunohistochemical criteria**: **14 or more leucocytes/mm2** including up to 4 monocytes/mm2, with **CD3+ T cells 7 or more/mm2**. [ESC 2013 position statement]`,`**Certainty**: definite (histology, or CMR with clinical syndrome and troponin), probable and possible - the tiered approach of the ACC 2024 pathway and ESC 2025. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**Timing**: acute (under about 1 month), subacute, and chronic or **inflammatory cardiomyopathy** (inflammation with dilatation or dysfunction). [ESC 2025]`,`**ICI myocarditis** is graded fulminant or non-fulminant, and steroid-sensitive or **steroid-refractory**. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Chest pain mimicking ACS** with troponin rise and normal coronaries is the commonest presentation in young adults. [ESC 2025]`,`A **viral prodrome** (fever, myalgia, sore throat, diarrhoea) 1-2 weeks earlier is reported by many, but its absence does not exclude myocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**New heart failure** (breathlessness, orthopnoea, oedema) over days to weeks in a previously healthy person. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpitations, syncope or sudden cardiac arrest** from VT, VF or AV block - think of giant cell myocarditis or sarcoid when AV block and VT come together. [ESC 2025]`,`**Fulminant myocarditis**: rapid onset over days of shock, often after a flu-like illness. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**Drug history**: checkpoint inhibitors, clozapine, recent vaccination, anthracyclines, antibiotics; **toxin history**: scorpion sting, celphos ingestion, alcohol, cocaine. [ESC 2025]`,`**Systemic history**: rash, arthritis (SLE), asthma with eosinophilia (EGPA), ptosis or proximal weakness (ICI myositis-myasthenia), sore throat with membrane (diphtheria). [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tachycardia disproportionate to fever** is a classic bedside clue. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S3 gallop**, soft S1, functional MR murmur and basal crackles in heart failure presentations. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardial rub** indicates myopericarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Shock**: cold peripheries, hypotension, raised JVP, oliguria - fulminant myocarditis. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**Bradycardia or irregular pulse** suggests AV block or arrhythmia; **relative bradycardia** with fever suggests typhoid or scrub typhus. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **eschar** (scrub typhus), **grey pharyngeal membrane and bull neck** (diphtheria), dengue rash and bleeding, sting site with sweating and priapism (scorpion). [API Textbook of Medicine 2022]`,`**Ptosis, diplopia and proximal weakness** in a patient on checkpoint inhibitors point to overlap myositis and myasthenia. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**ACS / MINOCA**: risk factors, territorial ECG and wall-motion pattern; exclude with **coronary angiography or CCTA** in suspected cases. [ESC 2025]`,`**Takotsubo syndrome**: postmenopausal woman, stress trigger, apical ballooning, no LGE on CMR. [InterTAK 2018]`,`**Acute pericarditis**: positional pleuritic pain, rub, PR depression, normal troponin and LV function. [ESC 2025]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: hypoxia, RV strain, D-dimer, CTPA. [ESC 2019 PE]`,`**Dilated or arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy**: chronic course, family history, genetic variants - desmoplakin cardiomyopathy can mimic myocarditis. [ESC 2023]`,`**Sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy** and **stress (catecholamine) cardiomyopathy** in critically ill patients. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Cardiac sarcoidosis**: AV block and VT with mediastinal nodes, FDG-PET uptake. [HRS 2014]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: sinus tachycardia, diffuse or regional ST elevation, T inversion, low voltage, QRS widening (poor prognosis), AV block, ectopy and VT - normal ECG does not exclude it. [ESC 2025]`,`**Troponin** (high-sensitivity) is raised in most; **CRP**, ESR and **NT-proBNP** add support; CK is raised in ICI myositis overlap. [ESC 2025]`,`**Echo**: regional or global hypokinesia; fulminant myocarditis shows a **non-dilated, thick (oedematous) LV** with severe dysfunction, whereas chronic disease shows dilatation; check for effusion and thrombus. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**CMR is recommended in all suspected myocarditis** when stable - **2018 Lake Louise criteria**: one **T2-based** marker (oedema: raised T2 signal or T2 mapping) plus one **T1-based** marker (raised native T1, ECV or **non-ischaemic LGE**). [ESC 2025]`,`Typical LGE is **subepicardial or mid-wall, basal-inferolateral**; **mid-wall septal LGE** carries a worse prognosis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Exclude coronary disease** with invasive angiography or **CCTA** when the presentation resembles ACS or risk factors exist. [ESC 2025]`,`**Endomyocardial biopsy** is recommended for fulminant or complicated myocarditis (shock, heart failure with LV dysfunction, sustained VT, high-grade AV block) or **failure to respond within 1-2 weeks**, and suspected giant cell, eosinophilic or sarcoid myocarditis; complication rate under 1% in experienced hands. [ESC 2025]`,`**Biopsy analysis**: histology, **immunohistochemistry (CD3, CD68)** and **viral PCR** - immunosuppression is safer when viral genome is absent. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cause-directed tests in India**: dengue NS1/IgM, scrub typhus IgM, Leptospira serology, throat swab for diphtheria, ANA and dsDNA, eosinophil count, HIV, thyroid. [API Textbook of Medicine 2022]`,`**Genetic testing** should be considered in recurrent myocarditis or myocarditis with features of genetic cardiomyopathy (family history, LGE ring pattern, arrhythmia). [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Suspected myocarditis with raised troponin needs hospital assessment** - the OPD role is recognition, ECG, troponin and referral. [ESC 2025]`,`**Uncomplicated myocarditis with preserved LVEF**: rest from strenuous activity, symptom control and follow-up with ECG, troponin and echo. [ESC 2025]`,`**LV dysfunction**: standard heart-failure therapy - ACE inhibitor/ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor - titrated as BP allows and continued after recovery for at least several months. [ESC 2021 HF; ESC 2025]`,`**Avoid NSAIDs in isolated myocarditis** (experimental harm); in **pericarditis-predominant myopericarditis**, lower-dose NSAID or aspirin is acceptable. [Harrison 22e; ESC 2025]`,`**Avoid alcohol** and cardiotoxic drugs; stop any suspected culprit drug (clozapine, checkpoint inhibitor, antibiotic causing hypersensitivity). [ESC 2025]`,`**Clozapine monitoring**: troponin and CRP at baseline and weekly for the first 4 weeks; **stop if troponin over twice the upper limit or CRP over 100 mg/L**. [Ronaldson protocol]`,`**No proven antiviral therapy**; IV immunoglobulin is **not recommended in adults** with acute myocarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Exercise restriction**: avoid competitive and intense exercise until remission - ESC 2025 recommends **at least 1 month**, longer when LV dysfunction, extensive LGE or arrhythmia persist (older sports guidance used 3-6 months). [ESC 2025]`,`**Return to sport** after normal symptoms, troponin, LVEF, **24-hour Holter and exercise ECG** without significant arrhythmia, with repeat CMR in selected cases. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit** all with suspected myocarditis and raised troponin plus LV dysfunction, arrhythmia, heart failure or ongoing chest pain - continuous ECG monitoring for at least the first 24-48 hours. [ESC 2025]`,`**Fulminant myocarditis**: early transfer to a centre with **mechanical circulatory support (VA-ECMO, Impella)** and biopsy; inotropes as a bridge; recovery is possible even after severe shock. [ESC 2025]`,`**Pulse IV methylprednisolone** (commonly 500-1000 mg daily for 3 days) is reasonable in fulminant myocarditis or suspected giant cell or eosinophilic myocarditis, ideally after biopsy. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**Giant cell myocarditis**: steroids plus **cyclosporine** (with or without anti-thymocyte globulin) improve transplant-free survival; untreated median survival is only months. [ESC 2025]`,`**AV block**: temporary pacing; defer permanent pacing until recovery is assessed, except in giant cell or sarcoid myocarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Ventricular arrhythmia in the acute phase**: treat acutely; a **wearable cardioverter-defibrillator** may bridge; ICD decisions are usually deferred 3-6 months after the acute phase. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**ICI myocarditis**: stop the checkpoint inhibitor; **IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg daily for 3-5 days**, then oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg tapered as troponin falls; steroid-refractory cases get second-line drugs (mycophenolate, abatacept, tocilizumab, ATG, alemtuzumab). [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Scorpion sting myocarditis**: **prazosin 500 µg orally (30 µg/kg in children) every 3 hours** until extremities warm, plus scorpion antivenom; dobutamine for shock with pulmonary oedema. [Bawaskar, Lancet 2011]`,`**Diphtheritic myocarditis**: **diphtheria antitoxin** early plus penicillin or erythromycin; ECG monitoring for conduction block in weeks 1-3. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart transplant** for refractory heart failure; avoid transplant during active inflammation when possible. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children**: myocarditis is a common cause of new heart failure; IV immunoglobulin is used in some paediatric centres though evidence is weak. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pregnancy and peripartum**: distinguish from peripartum cardiomyopathy; avoid ACE inhibitors, ARNI and MRA in pregnancy; CMR without gadolinium if needed. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Athletes**: myocarditis causes exercise-related sudden death - mandatory restriction and structured return-to-play testing. [ESC 2025]`,`**COVID-19 vaccination**: benefit outweighs risk; myocarditis after SARS-CoV-2 infection is more frequent than after vaccination; a further mRNA dose is generally deferred after vaccine myocarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cancer patients on ICIs**: baseline ECG and troponin, then troponin before doses 2, 3 and 4; rechallenge after significant ICI myocarditis is generally avoided. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Tropical fever with myocarditis** (dengue, scrub typhus, leptospirosis): treat the infection (doxycycline for scrub typhus), careful fluids to avoid pulmonary oedema. [API Textbook of Medicine 2022]`,`**HIV**: myocarditis and cardiomyopathy from HIV itself or opportunistic infection; screen in unexplained LV dysfunction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Uncomplicated** myocarditis with preserved LVEF has an excellent prognosis - most recover fully. [ESC 2025]`,`**Fulminant myocarditis** has high early mortality or transplant (about one-quarter to one-third at 60 days in registries), far higher than non-fulminant disease. [Ammirati, Circulation 2020]`,`**Complications**: cardiogenic shock, heart failure, VT/VF, sudden death, AV block, LV thrombus and progression to **dilated cardiomyopathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Poor prognostic markers**: giant cell histology, LVEF under 35% at presentation, QRS over 120 ms, **mid-wall septal LGE**, persistent LGE at follow-up. [ESC 2025]`,`**Giant cell myocarditis** untreated has a median survival of a few months; it may recur after transplant. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ICI myocarditis** mortality is substantial (historically up to about 40-50% in fulminant cases), higher with myositis-myasthenia overlap. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Vaccine-associated myocarditis** is usually mild with rapid symptom resolution. [CDC ACIP 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer urgently** (same day) any chest pain with raised troponin and normal-looking ECG in a young patient, any new heart failure, syncope or AV block. [ESC 2025]`,`**Follow-up** at about 1-3 months and 6-12 months: symptoms, ECG, troponin, NT-proBNP and echo. [ESC 2025]`,`**Repeat CMR at about 6 months** to assess oedema resolution and residual LGE, which guides risk and return to sport. [ESC 2025]`,`**Holter** at follow-up to detect ventricular arrhythmia before return to exercise. [ESC 2025]`,`Continue **heart-failure therapy** in those with LV dysfunction; reassess before any withdrawal, because relapse can occur. [ESC 2021 HF]`,`Screen first-degree relatives when genetic cardiomyopathy is found. [ESC 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Universal immunisation** - DPT under the **Universal Immunisation Programme** (DTwP-HepB-Hib pentavalent, DPT boosters, Td) prevents diphtheritic myocarditis. [MoHFW UIP 2023]`,`**Vector control and early care** for dengue and scrub typhus reduce myocardial complications - National Centre for Vector Borne Diseases Control. [NCVBDC]`,`**Counsel**: no strenuous exercise or competitive sport until cleared, avoid alcohol and NSAIDs (unless pericarditis-predominant), report palpitations or syncope at once. [ESC 2025]`,`**Scorpion-sting awareness** in rural Maharashtra, Andhra and Karnataka - shake out shoes and bedding, and early prazosin at the PHC. [Bawaskar, Lancet 2011]`,`**Oncologists** should inform patients on checkpoint inhibitors of chest pain, breathlessness, palpitations, weakness and ptosis as warning symptoms. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`Avoid repeat exposure to a drug that caused hypersensitivity myocarditis; record it as an allergy. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2025 myocarditis and pericarditis guideline** (first combined ESC guideline): introduces **IMPS**, recommends **CMR as first-line** diagnostic test in suspected myocarditis, and EMB in complicated or fulminant cases. [ESC 2025]`,`ESC 2025 recommends **shorter, individualised exercise restriction (at least 1 month)** instead of a fixed 3-6 months, with return guided by testing. [ESC 2025]`,`ESC 2025 adds **genetic testing** in recurrent myocarditis or suspected genetic cardiomyopathy, recognising the 'hot phase' of desmoplakin cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2025]`,`**ACC 2024 Expert Consensus Decision Pathway** on myocarditis: tiered certainty (definite, probable, possible), early referral of fulminant cases to MCS centres and empirical pulse steroids in suspected giant cell myocarditis. [ACC ECDP 2024]`,`**ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline**: baseline ECG and troponin before ICI, serial troponin, high-dose methylprednisolone for ICI myocarditis. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**2018 Lake Louise criteria** (T2 plus T1 marker, mapping-based) replaced the 2009 '2 of 3' criteria and remain current. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Young man, chest pain after a viral illness, raised troponin, normal coronaries** - myocarditis; next test: **CMR**. [ESC 2025]`,`**Lake Louise 2018**: at least one T2 criterion (oedema) plus one T1 criterion (native T1, ECV or LGE) - '2 of 2'. [ESC 2025]`,`**Commonest cause**: enteroviruses (Coxsackie B) classically, parvovirus B19 and HHV-6 in biopsy series. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Giant cell myocarditis** = young adult, fulminant heart failure, VT and heart block, poor response to standard therapy - biopsy and cyclosporine. [ESC 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: giving NSAIDs for chest pain in isolated myocarditis, or sending an athlete back to play within days. [ESC 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: missing ICI myocarditis in a cancer patient with mild troponin rise and ptosis - it can become fulminant quickly. [ESC 2022 Cardio-oncology]`,`**Indian viva**: diphtheritic myocarditis in the second week with heart block; scorpion sting pulmonary oedema treated with **prazosin**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Causes of myocarditis`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Clue`,`Specific action`],rows:[[`Viral`,`Coxsackie B, adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, influenza, SARS-CoV-2, HIV`,`Viral prodrome 1-2 weeks earlier`,`Supportive; no proven antiviral`],[`Bacterial / toxin`,`Diphtheria, leptospirosis, typhoid`,`Membrane, bull neck; jaundice and AKI`,`Antitoxin and antibiotic; ECG monitoring`],[`Tropical`,`Dengue, scrub typhus, malaria, chikungunya`,`Fever, thrombocytopenia, eschar`,`Treat infection; cautious fluids`],[`Autoimmune`,`SLE, sarcoid, giant cell, EGPA, IBD`,`Systemic features; AV block with VT`,`Biopsy; immunosuppression`],[`Drug - checkpoint inhibitor`,`Nivolumab, pembrolizumab, ipilimumab`,`First 3 months; myositis, ptosis`,`Stop ICI; pulse methylprednisolone`],[`Drug - hypersensitivity`,`Clozapine, sulfonamides, beta-lactams, carbamazepine`,`Rash, eosinophilia, fever`,`Stop drug; steroids if severe`],[`Vaccine`,`mRNA COVID-19, smallpox`,`Young male, 2-4 days after second dose`,`Supportive; usually mild`],[`Toxin`,`Scorpion (Mesobuthus tamulus), aluminium phosphide, CO, cocaine, alcohol`,`Sting with sweating and priapism; celphos ingestion`,`Prazosin plus antivenom; supportive`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in suspected myocarditis`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`hs-troponin`,`Raised; rise and fall`,`Myocyte injury - essential criterion`],[`ECG`,`ST elevation, T inversion, QRS over 120 ms, AV block, VT`,`QRS widening and AV block are adverse`],[`Echo`,`Thick non-dilated LV with low EF (fulminant); dilated LV (chronic)`,`Classifies severity`],[`CMR T2 criterion`,`Raised T2 signal or T2 mapping`,`Myocardial oedema - acute inflammation`],[`CMR T1 criterion`,`Raised native T1 or ECV, non-ischaemic LGE`,`Necrosis or fibrosis`],[`Lake Louise 2018`,`One T2 plus one T1 criterion`,`CMR diagnosis of myocardial inflammation`],[`EMB immunohistochemistry`,`14 or more leucocytes/mm2 with CD3+ T cells 7 or more/mm2`,`Inflammation; viral PCR guides immunosuppression`],[`Coronary imaging`,`No obstructive disease`,`Excludes ACS`]]},{heading:`Histological types of myocarditis`,columns:[`Type`,`Typical cause`,`Clinical clue`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Lymphocytic`,`Viral, post-viral, ICI`,`Chest pain or heart failure after viral illness`,`Supportive, HF drugs; steroids for ICI or virus-negative chronic disease`],[`Eosinophilic`,`Drug hypersensitivity, HES, EGPA, parasites`,`Peripheral eosinophilia, rash, asthma`,`Stop drug; high-dose steroids`],[`Giant cell`,`Autoimmune, thymoma`,`Fulminant HF, VT and heart block`,`Steroids plus cyclosporine, MCS, transplant`],[`Granulomatous`,`Sarcoidosis (exclude TB)`,`AV block, VT, mediastinal nodes`,`Steroids, methotrexate, ICD`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of chest pain with raised troponin and normal coronaries`,columns:[`Condition`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Myocarditis`,`Young, viral prodrome, subepicardial LGE`,`CMR Lake Louise`],[`Takotsubo syndrome`,`Postmenopausal woman, stress, apical ballooning`,`LV gram, CMR without LGE`],[`MINOCA (plaque disruption, spasm, embolism)`,`Risk factors, territorial changes`,`IVUS/OCT, CMR ischaemic LGE`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Hypoxia, RV strain`,`CTPA`],[`Pericarditis`,`Positional pain, rub, PR depression`,`Echo, CRP; troponin usually normal`],[`Aortic dissection`,`Tearing pain, pulse deficit`,`CT aortogram`]]},{heading:`Drugs in myocarditis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ramipril (LV dysfunction)`,`1.25-2.5 mg orally daily, up to 10 mg`,`Titrate 2-weekly; continue after recovery`,`Hypotension, hyperkalaemia, cough; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`12.5-25 mg orally daily, up to 200 mg`,`Titrate 2-weekly when stable, not in shock`,`Bradycardia, AV block, hypotension`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin`,`10 mg orally daily`,`Long term in LV dysfunction`,`Genital infection, volume depletion, ketoacidosis`],[`Furosemide`,`20-80 mg IV or orally`,`To relieve congestion`,`Hypokalaemia, AKI`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`500-1000 mg IV daily`,`3 days (fulminant, giant cell) or 3-5 days (ICI)`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection; biopsy first when possible`],[`Prednisolone`,`1 mg/kg orally daily`,`Taper over weeks to months by troponin and imaging`,`Diabetes, infection, osteoporosis`],[`Cyclosporine (giant cell)`,`Dose to trough level, with steroids`,`Months to a year or more`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension`],[`Prazosin (scorpion sting)`,`500 µg orally (children 30 µg/kg) every 3 hours`,`Until peripheries warm and BP stable`,`First-dose hypotension`],[`Diphtheria antitoxin`,`20,000-100,000 units IV by severity`,`Single dose after sensitivity test`,`Anaphylaxis, serum sickness`]]}],redFlags:[`Chest pain with raised troponin in a young patient - admit for monitoring even if ECG is unremarkable.`,`Hypotension, cold peripheries or lactate rise - fulminant myocarditis; transfer to an MCS-capable centre.`,`New high-grade AV block or sustained VT - suspect giant cell myocarditis or sarcoid; biopsy.`,`No improvement within 1-2 weeks of standard therapy with LV dysfunction - endomyocardial biopsy.`,`Troponin rise, ptosis or proximal weakness in a patient on checkpoint inhibitors - stop ICI and start pulse steroids.`,`Scorpion sting with sweating, priapism, cold extremities or pulmonary oedema - prazosin and admission.`,`Sore throat with membrane followed by bradycardia or heart block - diphtheritic myocarditis.`],pearls:[`**Tachycardia out of proportion to fever** = think myocarditis.`,`**Lake Louise 2018 = 2 of 2**: one T2 (oedema) plus one T1 (T1, ECV or LGE) criterion.`,`**Subepicardial inferolateral LGE** = myocarditis; **subendocardial territorial LGE** = infarct.`,`**Giant cell myocarditis**: fulminant + VT + heart block in a young adult - steroids and cyclosporine.`,`**Fulminant myocarditis** has a thick, non-dilated LV - and can recover fully if supported.`,`**No NSAIDs in isolated myocarditis**; they are fine in pericarditis-predominant disease.`,`**ICI myocarditis** travels with myositis and myasthenia - check CK and look for ptosis.`,`**Scorpion sting myocarditis**: prazosin, not atropine.`,`**Back to sport only after** normal troponin, LVEF, Holter and exercise test - at least 1 month (ESC 2025).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-q1`,stem:`A 23-year-old man has central chest pain 10 days after a febrile illness. ECG shows ST elevation in II, III, aVF and V5-V6; hs-troponin is markedly raised; coronary angiography is normal. Which test best confirms the diagnosis?`,options:[`Exercise treadmill test`,`Cardiac MRI with T2 and T1 mapping and late gadolinium enhancement`,`Repeat troponin at 24 hours`,`Myocardial perfusion scintigraphy`],answer:1,explanation:`In a stable patient with suspected myocarditis and normal coronaries, CMR is the recommended first-line test: the 2018 Lake Louise criteria need one T2 marker of oedema and one T1 marker (native T1, ECV or non-ischaemic subepicardial LGE). A treadmill test is unsafe in acute myocarditis, serial troponin confirms injury but not its cause, and perfusion scintigraphy detects ischaemia rather than inflammation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-q2`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman presents with rapidly worsening heart failure over 2 weeks, recurrent sustained VT and new complete heart block. She does not improve with standard treatment. Which diagnosis must be excluded urgently by endomyocardial biopsy?`,options:[`Viral lymphocytic myocarditis`,`Peripartum cardiomyopathy`,`Takotsubo syndrome`,`Giant cell myocarditis`],answer:3,explanation:`Fulminant heart failure with ventricular tachycardia and heart block, failing usual care, is the classic presentation of giant cell myocarditis; biopsy is mandatory because combined steroids and cyclosporine transform survival. Lymphocytic viral myocarditis rarely combines VT and complete block with relentless progression, peripartum cardiomyopathy needs a peripartum timeline, and Takotsubo recovers rather than progresses.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-q3`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with metastatic melanoma received his second dose of nivolumab plus ipilimumab three weeks ago. He has fatigue, ptosis, CK 2400 U/L, troponin rising and new right bundle branch block. What is the most appropriate immediate management?`,options:[`Stop the checkpoint inhibitors and give IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg daily`,`Continue immunotherapy and start oral prednisolone 10 mg daily`,`Give pyridostigmine alone for myasthenia`,`Start IV immunoglobulin and continue nivolumab`],answer:0,explanation:`Checkpoint-inhibitor myocarditis with myositis and myasthenic features (the triple-M overlap) carries high mortality; the ICI is stopped and high-dose IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg daily is started at once, with second-line agents if troponin does not fall. Low-dose oral steroid while continuing the drug undertreats a potentially fulminant illness, pyridostigmine treats only the neuromuscular symptom, and continuing nivolumab perpetuates the T-cell attack.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-q4`,stem:`According to the 2018 Lake Louise criteria, CMR diagnosis of myocardial inflammation requires which combination?`,options:[`Any two of T2 oedema, early gadolinium enhancement and LGE`,`Pericardial effusion plus reduced LVEF`,`At least one T2-based criterion plus at least one T1-based criterion`,`Subendocardial LGE in a coronary territory`],answer:2,explanation:`The 2018 update requires one T2-based marker (raised T2 signal or T2 mapping, indicating oedema) together with one T1-based marker (raised native T1 or ECV, or non-ischaemic LGE). The '2 of 3' rule including early gadolinium enhancement was the 2009 version, pericardial effusion and LV dysfunction are only supportive, and subendocardial territorial LGE indicates infarction rather than myocarditis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-q5`,stem:`A 9-year-old boy from rural Maharashtra is brought 2 hours after a scorpion sting with profuse sweating, priapism, cold extremities, BP 150/100 mmHg and basal crackles. Which drug is the cornerstone of treatment?`,options:[`Atropine`,`Nifedipine sublingual`,`Prazosin`,`Calcium gluconate`],answer:2,explanation:`Indian red scorpion venom triggers a massive catecholamine release causing adrenergic myocarditis and pulmonary oedema; the postsynaptic alpha-1 blocker prazosin (30 µg/kg every 3 hours in children), with scorpion antivenom, reduces mortality. Atropine worsens the adrenergic state and tachycardia, sublingual nifedipine causes uncontrolled hypotension and reflex tachycardia, and calcium has no role in this envenomation.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation and management of acute myocarditis, with a note on fulminant myocarditis and return to sport.`,openingLines:[`Myocarditis is inflammation of the myocardium diagnosed by histology or by CMR (Lake Louise criteria) with raised troponin in a compatible clinical syndrome; ESC 2025 places it with pericarditis within the inflammatory myopericardial syndrome.`,`It affects mainly young adults, presents from ACS-like chest pain to fulminant shock and sudden death, and is a precursor of dilated cardiomyopathy.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Viral: Coxsackie B, adenovirus, parvovirus B19, HHV-6, SARS-CoV-2, HIV.`,`India: diphtheria, dengue, scrub typhus, leptospirosis; scorpion sting, aluminium phosphide.`,`Immune: SLE, sarcoid, giant cell; drugs: checkpoint inhibitors, clozapine; mRNA vaccines.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Viral entry via CAR, direct necrosis; immune phase with molecular mimicry; recovery or DCM.`]},{heading:`Clinical presentation`,points:[`ACS-like chest pain with normal coronaries; new heart failure; arrhythmia or sudden death; fulminant shock.`,`Tachycardia out of proportion to fever, S3, rub in myopericarditis.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG, hs-troponin, CRP, NT-proBNP; echo.`,`**CMR Lake Louise 2018**: one T2 plus one T1 criterion; subepicardial LGE.`,`Exclude CAD with angiography or CCTA.`,`**EMB** in fulminant, complicated, non-responding or suspected giant cell/eosinophilic cases; immunohistochemistry and viral PCR.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Admit and monitor; HF therapy for LV dysfunction; avoid NSAIDs and alcohol.`,`Arrhythmia: temporary pacing, wearable defibrillator; ICD decision after 3-6 months.`,`Immunosuppression only for giant cell, eosinophilic, sarcoid, autoimmune, ICI or virus-negative chronic disease.`]},{heading:`Fulminant myocarditis`,points:[`Shock needing inotropes; non-dilated thick LV; transfer for VA-ECMO or Impella; early biopsy; pulse methylprednisolone if giant cell suspected.`]},{heading:`Return to sport and follow-up`,points:[`Restrict intense exercise at least 1 month (ESC 2025), longer if LV dysfunction or LGE persist.`,`Return after normal troponin, LVEF, Holter and exercise test; CMR at about 6 months.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected myocarditis, ECG and troponin, exclude CAD, CMR, biopsy if complicated, treatment arms`,`Table: Lake Louise 2018 T2 and T1 criteria`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical presentation`,marks:1},{part:`Investigations including CMR and EMB`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2.5},{part:`Fulminant myocarditis and return to sport`,marks:2}],keywords:[`myocarditis`,`Lake Louise`,`EMB`,`fulminant`,`giant cell`,`IMPS`,`return to sport`]},{id:`cardiovascular-myocarditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis.`,openingLines:[`Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis is a T-cell mediated myocarditis caused by PD-1, PD-L1 or CTLA-4 blockade, occurring in about 1% of treated patients, usually within the first 3 months.`,`It often overlaps with myositis and myasthenia and carries high mortality, so it is treated as an emergency with high-dose steroids.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk and presentation`,points:[`Highest with combined PD-1 plus CTLA-4 therapy; early after starting.`,`Fatigue, chest pain, dyspnoea, arrhythmia, heart block; ptosis and proximal weakness in overlap.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Troponin rise, ECG changes (conduction block), CK; echo; CMR (less sensitive here); EMB if uncertain.`,`Baseline ECG and troponin before ICI, then troponin before doses 2, 3 and 4 (ESC 2022).`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Stop ICI; IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg daily for 3-5 days, then prednisolone 1 mg/kg tapered by troponin.`,`Steroid-refractory: mycophenolate, abatacept, tocilizumab, ATG, alemtuzumab; temporary pacing, MCS as needed.`]},{heading:`Prognosis`,points:[`High mortality in fulminant cases; rechallenge generally avoided after significant myocarditis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow: troponin rise on ICI, stop drug, pulse steroids, escalate if refractory`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis and surveillance`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and prognosis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`nivolumab`,`ipilimumab`,`triple-M`,`methylprednisolone`,`abatacept`,`cardio-oncology`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - myocarditis chapter`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of myocarditis and pericarditis (Eur Heart J 2025)`,`2024 ACC Expert Consensus Decision Pathway on Strategies and Criteria for the Diagnosis and Management of Myocarditis (JACC 2025)`,`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardio-oncology`,`Ferreira VM et al. Cardiovascular MRI in nonischemic myocardial inflammation: expert recommendations (Lake Louise 2018), JACC 2018`,`Bawaskar HS, Bawaskar PH. Efficacy of prazosin and antivenom in scorpion sting, Lancet 2011; API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]}),a.push({id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis`,title:`Acute and recurrent pericarditis`,oneLiner:`**Acute pericarditis** is inflammation of the pericardium diagnosed by **2 of 4 criteria - pericarditic chest pain, friction rub, widespread ST elevation or PR depression, and new or worsening effusion** - treated with **aspirin or an NSAID plus colchicine for 3 months**; in India **tuberculosis** is the leading cause of effusive pericarditis, and recurrent disease with raised CRP responds to **IL-1 blockers (anakinra, rilonacept)**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`acute pericarditis`,`recurrent pericarditis`,`incessant pericarditis`,`myopericarditis`,`pericardial friction rub`,`PR depression`,`Spodick sign`,`colchicine`,`aspirin`,`ibuprofen`,`anakinra`,`rilonacept`,`tuberculous pericarditis`,`Dressler syndrome`,`post-pericardiotomy syndrome`,`uraemic pericarditis`,`IMPS`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Acute pericarditis** is an inflammatory pericardial syndrome with or without effusion, diagnosed by **at least 2 of 4 criteria**. [ESC 2025]`,`It accounts for about **5% of emergency visits for non-ischaemic chest pain** and about 0.1% of hospital admissions; incidence is about **27 per 100,000 per year** in European data. [ESC 2015]`,`ESC 2025 places pericarditis within the **inflammatory myopericardial syndrome (IMPS)**, alongside myocarditis and their overlaps. [ESC 2025]`,`**Definitions**: **incessant** - lasting over 4-6 weeks without remission; **recurrent** - new episode after a symptom-free interval of 4-6 weeks; **chronic** - over 3 months. [ESC 2025]`,`**Recurrence** occurs in **15-30%** after a first episode, rising to about **50% after a first recurrence** in patients not given colchicine. [ESC 2015]`,`In developed countries **80-90% are idiopathic (presumed viral)**; in India and other TB-endemic countries **tuberculosis** causes the majority of effusive pericarditis and constriction. [ESC 2025; INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Myopericarditis** is pericarditis with raised troponin and preserved LV function; **perimyocarditis** has new LV dysfunction. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Infectious**: viral (Coxsackie, echovirus, adenovirus, CMV, EBV, HIV, SARS-CoV-2), **tuberculosis**, bacterial (Staphylococcus, Pneumococcus - purulent), fungal in immunocompromised. [ESC 2025]`,`**Systemic autoimmune and autoinflammatory**: SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis, Sjogren, vasculitis, familial Mediterranean fever, TRAPS. [ESC 2025]`,`**Post-cardiac injury syndromes**: **post-MI (Dressler)**, **post-pericardiotomy**, post-traumatic and iatrogenic (PCI, pacemaker lead, ablation). [ESC 2025]`,`**Metabolic**: **uraemia** (before or soon after dialysis) and dialysis pericarditis, severe hypothyroidism (effusion more than inflammation). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neoplastic**: metastatic lung and breast cancer, lymphoma, leukaemia; primary mesothelioma is rare. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs and radiation**: hydralazine, procainamide and **isoniazid** (drug-induced lupus), phenytoin, minoxidil, checkpoint inhibitors; mediastinal radiotherapy. [ESC 2025]`,`**Early post-MI pericarditis** (days 1-3, over a transmural infarct) is now uncommon with prompt reperfusion; Dressler syndrome appears weeks later. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`Risk factors for recurrence: **inadequate initial treatment, no colchicine, early use of corticosteroids**, female sex and raised CRP. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The pericardium has a **fibrous outer layer** and a serous inner layer (parietal and visceral); it normally holds **10-50 mL** of plasma ultrafiltrate. [Harrison 22e]`,`Inflammation causes fibrin deposition and exudation; the rough layers rub - the **friction rub** - and fluid may accumulate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pain** arises from the parietal pericardium (lower part supplied by the **phrenic nerve**), hence radiation to the **trapezius ridge** and worsening on inspiration and lying flat. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ECG changes reflect inflammation of the subepicardial myocardium** - hence diffuse ST elevation and PR depression (atrial injury); uraemic pericarditis often lacks them because the myocardium is spared. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Recurrent pericarditis** is largely **autoinflammatory**: the NLRP3 inflammasome releases **interleukin-1**, driving fever, high CRP and recurrences - the rationale for colchicine and IL-1 blockade. [ESC 2025]`,`**Colchicine** binds tubulin and inhibits neutrophil migration and inflammasome activation; **corticosteroids** suppress inflammation but encourage viral persistence and rebound recurrences. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB pericarditis** passes through dry, effusive, absorptive and constrictive stages; delayed hypersensitivity to mycobacterial antigens drives the effusion, and fibrosis leads to constriction. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Diagnosis of acute pericarditis - 2 of 4**: (1) **pericarditic chest pain** (over 85-90%), (2) **friction rub** (up to about one-third), (3) **new widespread ST elevation or PR depression** (up to 60%), (4) **new or worsening pericardial effusion** (up to 60%). [ESC 2025]`,`**Supportive findings**: raised CRP, ESR or white cell count, and evidence of pericardial inflammation on **CT or CMR (pericardial LGE)**. [ESC 2025]`,`**By time course**: acute, incessant (over 4-6 weeks), recurrent (after a 4-6 week free interval) and chronic (over 3 months). [ESC 2025]`,`**By aetiology**: idiopathic/viral, TB, bacterial, autoimmune, post-cardiac injury, neoplastic, metabolic, radiation - see the table below. [ESC 2025]`,`**High-risk major predictors**: fever over 38 C, **subacute onset**, **large effusion (over 20 mm)**, **tamponade**, **no response to aspirin or NSAID after at least 1 week**. [ESC 2025]`,`**Minor predictors**: myopericarditis, immunosuppression, trauma and oral anticoagulant therapy. [ESC 2025]`,`**Recurrent pericarditis phenotypes**: **inflammatory (raised CRP, fever)** - responds to IL-1 blockade - versus non-inflammatory (normal CRP, pain without objective signs). [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Sharp, pleuritic retrosternal or left precordial pain**, worse lying flat, on inspiration and coughing, **relieved by sitting up and leaning forward**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Radiation to the trapezius ridge** is characteristic of pericardial pain (phrenic nerve). [Harrison 22e]`,`A **viral prodrome** (fever, sore throat, myalgia) in the preceding weeks suggests idiopathic/viral disease. [ESC 2025]`,`**Subacute onset over weeks with fever, night sweats, weight loss and cough** suggests **TB** - the default concern in India. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Ask about recent **MI, cardiac surgery, PCI, pacemaker or ablation**, dialysis, cancer, radiotherapy, autoimmune symptoms and drugs (isoniazid, hydralazine). [ESC 2025]`,`**Breathlessness, dizziness or syncope** suggest a large effusion or tamponade. [ESC 2025]`,`Elderly, diabetic or uraemic patients may have **little pain**; myocarditis overlap gives palpitations. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pericardial friction rub**: superficial, scratchy, **triphasic** (atrial systole, ventricular systole, early diastolic filling) in about half; heard best at the **left lower sternal border with the patient leaning forward in expiration**. [Harrison 22e]`,`The rub is **evanescent** - examine repeatedly - and can persist despite an effusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Low-grade fever and tachycardia; high fever suggests purulent or TB pericarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Raised JVP, hypotension and pulsus paradoxus** indicate tamponade - see the Pericardial effusion and tamponade topic. [ESC 2025]`,`Look for **lymphadenopathy and lung signs (TB)**, joint and skin signs (SLE, RA), uraemic features, sternotomy scar and malignancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **pleural rub** disappears when the breath is held; a pericardial rub persists. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis and ECG staging`,points:[`**ECG stage 1** (hours to days): **diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression**, with ST depression and PR elevation in aVR (and often V1). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stage 2**: ST and PR segments normalise; **stage 3**: diffuse T-wave inversion; **stage 4**: normalisation - T inversion appears **only after ST returns to baseline**, unlike STEMI. [Harrison 22e]`,`**STEMI**: convex, **territorial** ST elevation with **reciprocal ST depression**, Q waves, T inversion while ST still elevated, and **ST elevation in III greater than II**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Early repolarisation**: J-point notching in young men, stable over time, no PR depression, **ST/T ratio in V6 under 0.25** (over 0.25 favours pericarditis). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Spodick sign** (downsloping TP segment) and PR depression favour pericarditis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Other differentials: **aortic dissection** (tearing pain, pulse deficit - never give anticoagulant or thrombolytic), **PE**, pneumonia with pleurisy, **myocarditis**, costochondritis, GERD. [ESC 2025]`,`Pericarditis must never be thrombolysed as a STEMI - **haemopericardium and tamponade** can follow. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line (every patient)**: ECG, **chest X-ray**, **CRP**, blood count, renal function, **troponin** and **transthoracic echo**. [ESC 2025]`,`**CRP** is raised in most and **guides the duration of treatment** - continue full dose until CRP normalises. [ESC 2025]`,`**Troponin** is raised in up to about one-third (myopericarditis) - check LV function. [ESC 2025]`,`**Chest X-ray**: usually normal; **water-bottle heart** with clear lungs when effusion exceeds about 250 mL; look for TB lung lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echo**: effusion size and haemodynamics, LV function; a normal echo does not exclude pericarditis. [ESC 2025]`,`**India - TB work-up when subacute, effusive or febrile**: sputum and pericardial fluid **CBNAAT/Xpert MTB/RIF or Truenat**, chest X-ray, HIV test, and **pericardial fluid ADA (40 U/L or more supports TB)**. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Tygerberg score** for TB in endemic areas: fever 1, night sweats 1, weight loss 2, globulin over 40 g/L 3, white cell count under 10 x 10^9/L 3 - **score 6 or more** suggests TB. [ESC 2015]`,`**Selected tests**: ANA and RF (autoimmune features), TSH (effusion), blood cultures (fever), tumour markers and cytology (cancer). [ESC 2025]`,`**CMR** shows pericardial oedema and **LGE** - used in recurrent, incessant or atypical cases to confirm activity and guide therapy; CT shows thickening and calcification. [ESC 2025]`,`**Pericardiocentesis** is not routine: indicated for tamponade, suspected bacterial, TB or neoplastic cause, or large symptomatic effusion. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Low-risk patients** (no major or minor predictor) can be treated as outpatients with review after **1 week** to check response. [ESC 2025]`,`**Aspirin 750-1000 mg every 8 hours for 1-2 weeks**, then taper by 250-500 mg every 1-2 weeks; preferred **after MI** and when antiplatelet therapy is needed. [ESC 2025]`,`**Or ibuprofen 600 mg every 8 hours for 1-2 weeks**, taper by 200-400 mg every 1-2 weeks; indomethacin 25-50 mg every 8 hours is an alternative (start low). [ESC 2025]`,`**Plus colchicine 0.5 mg once daily (under 70 kg) or 0.5 mg twice daily (70 kg or more) for 3 months** - halves recurrence (ICAP: 16.7% vs 37.5%); no loading dose. [ESC 2025]`,`Give **gastroprotection (PPI)** with high-dose aspirin or NSAID. [ESC 2025]`,`Continue the full dose **until symptoms resolve and CRP normalises**, then taper; tapering by CRP reduces relapse. [ESC 2025]`,`**Restrict strenuous activity** until symptoms resolve and CRP normalises; athletes return after an individualised assessment (older advice was at least 3 months; ESC 2025 allows earlier return once remission is documented). [ESC 2025]`,`**Avoid corticosteroids as first line** - they increase recurrence; reserve low dose for contraindication to or failure of NSAIDs, or specific indications (autoimmune disease, pregnancy, renal failure). [ESC 2025]`,`Colchicine causes **diarrhoea** in about 1 in 10; reduce dose in CKD and avoid with strong CYP3A4/P-glycoprotein inhibitors (clarithromycin, ketoconazole, cyclosporine). [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD, TB pericarditis and recurrence`,points:[`**Admit** any patient with a major or minor high-risk predictor - fever over 38 C, subacute onset, effusion over 20 mm, tamponade, NSAID failure, myopericarditis, immunosuppression, trauma or anticoagulation. [ESC 2025]`,`**Recurrent pericarditis**: aspirin or NSAID at full dose **plus colchicine for at least 6 months** (CORP-2: recurrence 21.6% vs 42.5%), tapering guided by CRP. [ESC 2025]`,`**Corticosteroids - second line**: **prednisolone 0.2-0.5 mg/kg/day** with colchicine, **after excluding TB and bacterial infection**, held until CRP normalises and tapered very slowly (2.5 mg steps below 25 mg). [ESC 2025]`,`**IL-1 blockers - class I in recurrent pericarditis with raised CRP that is colchicine-resistant and steroid-dependent**: **anakinra 1-2 mg/kg/day up to 100 mg SC daily** (AIRTRIP) or **rilonacept 320 mg SC loading then 160 mg weekly** (RHAPSODY: recurrence 7% vs 74%). [ESC 2025]`,`**Other options**: azathioprine (1 mg/kg/day rising to 2-3 mg/kg/day), IV immunoglobulin (400-500 mg/kg/day for 5 days), methotrexate; **pericardiectomy** for refractory recurrences at expert centres. [ESC 2025]`,`IL-1 blockers are **costly and of limited availability in India** - azathioprine and steroid-sparing strategies are commonly used; screen for latent TB before biologics. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB pericarditis**: NTEP regimen **2HRZE + 4HRE** (6 months, daily weight-band fixed-dose combination), with drainage of large effusions. [INDEX-TB 2016; NTEP 2021]`,`**Adjunctive prednisolone in TB pericarditis** (HIV-negative): may be used - IMPI regimen **120 mg/day week 1, then 90, 60, 30, 15 and 5 mg** over 6 weeks reduced constriction but not death; avoid in HIV with low CD4 (malignancy risk). [WHO 2022; IMPI 2014]`,`**Purulent pericarditis**: emergency drainage (subxiphoid pericardiostomy), IV antibiotics and intrapericardial fibrinolysis - fatal untreated. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Post-MI pericarditis**: **aspirin is the drug of choice**; avoid other NSAIDs and steroids (impair scar healing, rupture risk); a new effusion should prompt a search for **free-wall rupture**. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Pregnancy**: aspirin or NSAIDs can be used in the first and second trimesters but **all NSAIDs except low-dose aspirin are stopped at 20 weeks** (ductus, oligohydramnios); low-dose prednisolone is acceptable; colchicine was traditionally avoided, though FMF data are reassuring - specialist decision. [ESC 2015; ESC 2025]`,`**Children**: NSAIDs (ibuprofen 30-50 mg/kg/day in 3 doses) preferred; **avoid aspirin (Reye syndrome)**; colchicine 0.5 mg daily under 5 years and 1-1.5 mg/day in divided doses above; steroids especially avoided. [ESC 2015]`,`**CKD**: **uraemic pericarditis** (before or within 8 weeks of dialysis) and **dialysis pericarditis** respond to **intensified dialysis**; ECG changes are often absent; avoid NSAIDs and reduce colchicine. [ESC 2025]`,`**Elderly**: prefer aspirin or low-dose NSAID with PPI, check renal function; indomethacin is avoided. [ESC 2025]`,`**Patients on anticoagulants**: minor risk predictor - admit, consider haemopericardium; avoid NSAIDs where possible. [ESC 2025]`,`**HIV**: TB is the commonest cause of large effusion; start ATT and ART per NTEP/NACO; avoid adjunctive steroids. [WHO 2022]`,`**Post-pericardiotomy syndrome**: colchicine perioperatively reduces its incidence (COPPS). [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Idiopathic/viral pericarditis** has an excellent prognosis; tamponade occurs rarely (more often with TB, neoplastic or purulent causes). [ESC 2025]`,`**Recurrence** in 15-30% - the commonest complication; colchicine halves it. [ESC 2025]`,`**Constriction risk after acute pericarditis**: under 1% for idiopathic/viral, **2-5% for immune-mediated and neoplastic**, **20-30% for bacterial (especially purulent and TB)**. [ESC 2015]`,`**Transient constriction** can follow acute pericarditis and resolves with anti-inflammatory therapy over 2-3 months. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB pericarditis** without treatment has high mortality; even with ATT it carries substantial mortality and risk of constriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Corticosteroid use** is linked to recurrence, dependence and steroid toxicity. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Refer or admit on any **high-risk predictor**, tamponade signs, suspected TB or purulent disease, or failure to respond in 1 week. [ESC 2025]`,`Review at **1 week** (response), then **every 1-2 weeks during taper** with symptoms and CRP. [ESC 2025]`,`Repeat **echo** if an effusion was present or symptoms change. [ESC 2025]`,`On colchicine: watch for diarrhoea, check liver enzymes, CK and blood count if long term or in CKD. [ESC 2025]`,`Recurrent or incessant pericarditis: refer to a **pericardial clinic**; CMR to confirm activity; consider autoinflammatory disease work-up. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB pericarditis**: NTEP follow-up - treatment adherence (Nikshay registration), echo at end of intensive phase and at completion to detect constriction. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`Explain that **most cases recover** and that recurrence is common but rarely dangerous. [ESC 2025]`,`**Take colchicine for the full course** even when well - it prevents recurrence. [ESC 2025]`,`Avoid strenuous exercise until symptoms and CRP settle. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB**: register on **Nikshay**, link to **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** nutritional support, contact tracing and TB preventive therapy for household contacts under NTEP. [NTEP 2021]`,`Report fever, breathlessness, dizziness or swelling, which suggest effusion, tamponade or constriction. [ESC 2025]`,`Perioperative colchicine reduces post-pericardiotomy syndrome in selected cardiac surgery patients. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2025 myocarditis and pericarditis guideline** replaces ESC 2015: introduces **IMPS**, keeps the 2-of-4 diagnostic criteria and adds CMR/CT pericardial inflammation as supportive evidence. [ESC 2025]`,`**IL-1 antagonists (anakinra, rilonacept)** are recommended (class I) for recurrent pericarditis with raised CRP that is colchicine-resistant and corticosteroid-dependent. [ESC 2025]`,`ESC 2025 emphasises **phenotyping recurrences** (CRP-positive inflammatory versus non-inflammatory) and **CRP-guided tapering**. [ESC 2025]`,`**Multimodality imaging** (CMR LGE) is advised in recurrent, incessant or complicated pericarditis to guide treatment duration. [ESC 2025]`,`**Exercise restriction** is individualised and shorter than the 3 months previously advised for athletes, once remission is confirmed. [ESC 2025]`,`**Rilonacept** was FDA-approved for recurrent pericarditis in 2021 after RHAPSODY; long-term extension data support prolonged use. [RHAPSODY 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic question: **ECG differences between pericarditis and STEMI** - draw the table: diffuse concave versus territorial convex, PR depression, reciprocal changes, Q waves, timing of T inversion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Doses examiners expect**: aspirin 750-1000 mg 8-hourly, ibuprofen 600 mg 8-hourly, colchicine 0.5 mg once or twice by weight for 3 months (6 in recurrence). [ESC 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: starting prednisolone first - it **increases recurrence** and masks TB. [ESC 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: thrombolysing pericarditis as STEMI, or giving NSAIDs other than aspirin after MI. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Uraemic pericarditis**: no ECG changes, treated by dialysis - a favourite one-liner. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Indian viva**: TB pericarditis - ADA, Xpert, Tygerberg score, 6-month ATT, IMPI steroid question and constriction risk. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`Mnemonic for high-risk features: **FEVER-S** - Fever, Effusion large, Very slow (subacute) onset, Effusion with tamponade, Refractory to NSAID, plus Specials (myopericarditis, immunosuppression, trauma, anticoagulants). [ESC 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Diagnostic criteria, definitions and high-risk features`,columns:[`Item`,`Content`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Diagnosis (2 of 4)`,`Pericarditic pain; rub; widespread ST elevation or PR depression; new or worse effusion`,`Supportive: raised CRP; CT/CMR pericardial inflammation`],[`Incessant`,`Over 4-6 weeks without remission`,`Look for TB, autoimmune, neoplastic cause`],[`Recurrent`,`New episode after 4-6 week symptom-free interval`,`Colchicine 6 months; IL-1 blocker if CRP-positive and refractory`],[`Chronic`,`Over 3 months`,`Usually effusive`],[`Major high-risk`,`Fever over 38 C, subacute onset, effusion over 20 mm, tamponade, no response to NSAID after 1 week`,`Admit and search for cause`],[`Minor high-risk`,`Myopericarditis, immunosuppression, trauma, oral anticoagulation`,`Admit`]]},{heading:`ECG: pericarditis versus STEMI versus early repolarisation`,columns:[`Feature`,`Acute pericarditis`,`STEMI`,`Early repolarisation`],rows:[[`ST elevation`,`Diffuse, concave, rarely over 5 mm`,`Territorial, convex or straight`,`Mainly V2-V5, J-point notch`],[`Reciprocal ST depression`,`Only aVR and V1`,`Present in opposite leads`,`Absent`],[`PR segment`,`Depressed (elevated in aVR)`,`Usually normal`,`Normal`],[`Q waves`,`Absent`,`Develop`,`Absent`],[`T inversion`,`After ST returns to baseline`,`While ST still elevated`,`Absent`],[`ST/T ratio in V6`,`Over 0.25`,`Not applicable`,`Under 0.25`],[`Evolution`,`Four stages over days to weeks`,`Hours`,`Stable for years`]]},{heading:`Causes of pericarditis and the clue to each`,columns:[`Cause`,`Clue`,`Key test`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`Idiopathic / viral`,`Young, acute, viral prodrome`,`None beyond basics`,`Aspirin or NSAID plus colchicine`],[`Tuberculosis`,`Subacute, fever, weight loss, large effusion, HIV`,`Fluid ADA 40 U/L or more, Xpert, culture`,`6-month ATT; steroids in selected HIV-negative`],[`Purulent bacterial`,`High fever, sepsis, adjacent pneumonia or surgery`,`Fluid Gram stain, culture, glucose low`,`Drainage, IV antibiotics, fibrinolysis`],[`Autoimmune (SLE, RA)`,`Joint and skin disease`,`ANA, dsDNA, RF`,`Treat disease; steroids acceptable`],[`Post-cardiac injury`,`Weeks after MI, surgery, PCI`,`History, CRP`,`Aspirin (post-MI) plus colchicine`],[`Uraemic / dialysis`,`CKD, missed dialysis`,`Urea, creatinine`,`Intensive dialysis`],[`Neoplastic`,`Known cancer, haemorrhagic effusion`,`Cytology, CT`,`Drainage, intrapericardial chemotherapy, window`],[`Hypothyroid`,`Large effusion, no tamponade usually`,`TSH`,`Thyroxine`]]},{heading:`Drugs for pericarditis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`750-1000 mg orally every 8 hours`,`1-2 weeks, then taper by 250-500 mg every 1-2 weeks (by CRP)`,`GI bleeding - add PPI; avoid in children (Reye)`],[`Ibuprofen`,`600 mg orally every 8 hours`,`1-2 weeks, then taper by 200-400 mg every 1-2 weeks`,`GI bleeding, AKI; avoid post-MI and after 20 weeks of pregnancy`],[`Indomethacin`,`25-50 mg orally every 8 hours (start low)`,`1-2 weeks, then taper by 25 mg every 1-2 weeks`,`Headache, dizziness; avoid in elderly`],[`Colchicine`,`0.5 mg once daily (under 70 kg) or twice daily (70 kg or more)`,`3 months (acute); at least 6 months (recurrent)`,`Diarrhoea, myopathy; reduce in CKD; avoid with clarithromycin, cyclosporine`],[`Prednisolone`,`0.2-0.5 mg/kg/day orally`,`Until CRP normal, then slow taper (2.5 mg steps below 25 mg)`,`Exclude TB first; increases recurrence; diabetes, osteoporosis`],[`Prednisolone (TB, IMPI)`,`120, 90, 60, 30, 15, 5 mg/day for one week each`,`6 weeks with ATT`,`Avoid in HIV with low CD4; hyperglycaemia`],[`Anakinra`,`1-2 mg/kg/day SC, up to 100 mg daily`,`Several months, then slow taper`,`Injection-site reaction, infection, neutropenia; screen for TB`],[`Rilonacept`,`320 mg SC loading, then 160 mg SC weekly`,`Long term in recurrent disease`,`Injection-site reaction, infection; screen for TB`],[`Azathioprine`,`1 mg/kg/day orally, up to 2-3 mg/kg/day`,`Months; steroid-sparing`,`Marrow suppression, hepatitis; check TPMT if available`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination`,`Weight band (e.g. 4 tablets for 50-64 kg) daily`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy (add pyridoxine), optic neuritis`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever over 38 C or subacute onset over weeks - admit and search for TB or bacterial cause.`,`Effusion over 20 mm on echo or any sign of tamponade (hypotension, raised JVP, pulsus paradoxus).`,`No response to aspirin or NSAID after 1 week of adequate dosing.`,`Raised troponin with LV dysfunction - perimyocarditis needing monitoring.`,`Pericarditis in a patient on anticoagulants, immunosuppressed or after trauma.`,`Pericardial effusion days after MI - exclude free-wall rupture.`,`High fever with sepsis and effusion - purulent pericarditis needs urgent drainage.`],pearls:[`**2 of 4**: pain, rub, ECG (widespread ST elevation or PR depression), effusion.`,`**PR depression and ST depression in aVR** with diffuse concave ST elevation = pericarditis.`,`**T inversion comes after ST normalises** in pericarditis, but with ST still elevated in STEMI.`,`**Colchicine 0.5 mg by weight**: 3 months for first episode, 6 months for recurrence.`,`**Steroids first = recurrences later** - and in India, exclude TB first.`,`**Aspirin, not other NSAIDs, after MI**.`,`**Uraemic pericarditis has no ECG changes** - dialyse.`,`**Pain radiating to the trapezius ridge** is specific for pericardial pain.`,`**Recurrent, CRP-positive, colchicine-resistant, steroid-dependent** = IL-1 blocker (anakinra or rilonacept).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-q1`,stem:`A 28-year-old man has sharp chest pain relieved by leaning forward. ECG shows concave ST elevation in I, II, aVL, aVF and V2-V6 with PR depression, and PR elevation with ST depression in aVR. Echo shows no effusion; troponin is normal; temperature 37.6 C. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 4 weeks`,`Ibuprofen 600 mg every 8 hours plus colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily for 3 months, if he weighs 70 kg or more`,`Immediate thrombolysis with tenecteplase`,`Ibuprofen alone for 3 days`],answer:1,explanation:`This is low-risk acute idiopathic pericarditis: first-line treatment is high-dose NSAID or aspirin with gastroprotection plus weight-adjusted colchicine for 3 months, which halves recurrence. Corticosteroids as first line increase recurrence, thrombolysis is dangerous because the ECG is pericarditic rather than territorial STEMI and can cause haemopericardium, and a short NSAID course without colchicine leaves a high recurrence risk.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-q2`,stem:`Which ECG feature best distinguishes acute pericarditis from inferior STEMI?`,options:[`ST elevation in lead III greater than in lead II`,`Reciprocal ST depression in I and aVL`,`Pathological Q waves in II, III and aVF`,`PR-segment depression with ST elevation across multiple territories`],answer:3,explanation:`Pericarditis produces diffuse concave ST elevation crossing coronary territories with PR depression from atrial injury, and reciprocal changes only in aVR and V1. ST elevation greater in III than II, reciprocal depression in I and aVL, and evolving Q waves are all features of inferior STEMI from right coronary occlusion.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-q3`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman from Bihar has 5 weeks of fever, night sweats, weight loss and breathlessness. Echo shows a 25 mm circumferential effusion with fibrin strands. Pericardial fluid is an exudate with lymphocytes and ADA 68 U/L; she is HIV-negative. What is the best management?`,options:[`Aspirin and colchicine for 3 months`,`Six months of ATT (2HRZE + 4HRE) with drainage, considering adjunctive prednisolone`,`Empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics for 2 weeks`,`Immediate pericardiectomy`],answer:1,explanation:`A subacute febrile course with a large lymphocytic exudate and pericardial ADA of 40 U/L or more in India means tuberculous pericarditis, treated with 6 months of ATT under NTEP, drainage of the large effusion, and adjunctive steroids that may be used in HIV-negative patients to reduce constriction (IMPI). Aspirin and colchicine treat idiopathic pericarditis and leave TB untreated, antibiotics suit purulent disease, and pericardiectomy is for established constriction that fails to improve after 4-8 weeks of ATT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-q4`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman has her fourth recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis with CRP 60 mg/L despite colchicine; every attempt to taper prednisolone below 10 mg/day causes relapse. TB has been excluded. According to ESC 2025, which is the recommended next step?`,options:[`Increase prednisolone to 1 mg/kg/day`,`Stop colchicine and start indomethacin`,`Start an IL-1 antagonist such as anakinra or rilonacept`,`Immediate pericardiectomy`],answer:2,explanation:`Recurrent pericarditis with raised CRP that is colchicine-resistant and steroid-dependent is the class I indication for IL-1 blockade (anakinra or rilonacept), which dramatically reduces recurrence (RHAPSODY 7% vs 74%) and allows steroid withdrawal. High-dose steroids worsen dependence and recurrence, stopping colchicine removes a proven preventer, and pericardiectomy is reserved for failure of medical therapy at expert centres.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-q5`,stem:`A 58-year-old man on maintenance haemodialysis who has missed several sessions develops pleuritic chest pain and a pericardial rub. His ECG shows no ST-segment changes. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Intensification of dialysis`,`High-dose ibuprofen`,`Prednisolone 0.5 mg/kg/day`,`Full-dose colchicine 0.5 mg twice daily`],answer:0,explanation:`Uraemic or dialysis-associated pericarditis responds to intensified dialysis; the ECG is often normal because the inflammation does not involve the subepicardial myocardium. NSAIDs risk bleeding and further renal harm, steroids have no first-line role, and full-dose colchicine accumulates in renal failure and is contraindicated at this dose.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, diagnosis and management of acute pericarditis. Add a note on recurrent pericarditis and tuberculous pericarditis in the Indian setting.`,openingLines:[`Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial layers, diagnosed when at least 2 of 4 criteria are present - pericarditic chest pain, friction rub, widespread ST elevation or PR depression, and new or worsening effusion (ESC 2025).`,`Most cases in developed countries are idiopathic or viral, but in India tuberculosis is the leading cause of effusive pericarditis and constriction; recurrences occur in 15-30%.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Idiopathic/viral; TB; purulent bacterial; autoimmune; post-cardiac injury (Dressler, post-pericardiotomy); uraemic; neoplastic; drugs and radiation.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Pleuritic pain relieved by leaning forward, radiating to trapezius; triphasic rub at left lower sternal border.`,`TB clues: subacute course, fever, night sweats, weight loss, large effusion.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`2 of 4 criteria; supportive raised CRP and CMR LGE.`,`ECG stages 1-4; differentiate from STEMI and early repolarisation (table).`,`Investigations: ECG, CXR, CRP, troponin, echo; TB work-up - Xpert, ADA, HIV.`,`High-risk predictors decide admission: fever over 38 C, subacute onset, effusion over 20 mm, tamponade, NSAID failure.`]},{heading:`Management of acute pericarditis`,points:[`Aspirin 750-1000 mg 8-hourly or ibuprofen 600 mg 8-hourly for 1-2 weeks then taper, with PPI.`,`Colchicine 0.5 mg once (under 70 kg) or twice daily for 3 months.`,`Restrict exercise; CRP-guided taper; avoid first-line steroids.`]},{heading:`Recurrent pericarditis`,points:[`NSAID plus colchicine for at least 6 months; low-dose steroid if needed after excluding TB.`,`IL-1 blockers (anakinra, rilonacept) for CRP-positive, colchicine-resistant, steroid-dependent disease (ESC 2025 class I); azathioprine, IVIG; pericardiectomy last.`]},{heading:`Tuberculous pericarditis`,points:[`Diagnosis: Xpert, ADA 40 U/L or more, lymphocytic exudate, Tygerberg score 6 or more.`,`Treatment: 2HRZE + 4HRE; drainage; adjunctive prednisolone in HIV-negative (IMPI regimen); pericardiectomy if constriction persists after 4-8 weeks of ATT.`]}],mustDraw:[`ECG table: pericarditis versus STEMI versus early repolarisation`,`Treatment ladder: NSAID plus colchicine, then low-dose steroid, then IL-1 blocker or azathioprine, then pericardiectomy`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnostic criteria`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and ECG`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment of acute pericarditis`,marks:2},{part:`Recurrent pericarditis`,marks:1.5},{part:`TB pericarditis`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`pericarditis`,`colchicine`,`PR depression`,`IL-1`,`anakinra`,`TB pericarditis`,`ADA`,`IMPI`]},{id:`cardiovascular-pericarditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate the ECG of acute pericarditis from that of acute STEMI and early repolarisation.`,openingLines:[`In acute pericarditis, inflammation of the subepicardial myocardium causes diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression that evolves through four stages.`,`STEMI causes territorial convex ST elevation with reciprocal change and Q waves, while early repolarisation is a stable benign variant.`],answer:[{heading:`Pericarditis`,points:[`Diffuse concave ST elevation, PR depression; aVR shows ST depression and PR elevation.`,`Stages: ST elevation, normalisation, T inversion, normal; no Q waves; Spodick sign.`]},{heading:`STEMI`,points:[`Territorial convex ST elevation, reciprocal depression, Q waves, T inversion while ST is elevated; ST III over II in inferior MI.`]},{heading:`Early repolarisation`,points:[`J-point notching, V2-V5, no PR depression, ST/T ratio in V6 under 0.25, stable over time.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column table of ST shape, distribution, reciprocal change, PR segment, Q waves, T-wave timing and ST/T ratio`,`Sketch of the four ECG stages of pericarditis`],markSplit:[{part:`Pericarditis ECG and stages`,marks:2},{part:`STEMI ECG`,marks:1.5},{part:`Early repolarisation`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`PR depression`,`ST/T ratio`,`reciprocal change`,`Spodick sign`,`aVR`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - pericardial disease chapter`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of myocarditis and pericarditis (Eur Heart J 2025)`,`2015 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases`,`INDEX-TB Guidelines: Guidelines on extrapulmonary tuberculosis for India, ICMR and Central TB Division, 2016; NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB, 2021`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 4: Treatment, 2022`,`Trials: ICAP (NEJM 2013), CORP-2 (Lancet 2014), AIRTRIP (JAMA 2016), RHAPSODY (NEJM 2021), IMPI (NEJM 2014)`]}),a.push({id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction`,title:`Pericardial effusion, cardiac tamponade and constrictive pericarditis`,oneLiner:`**Pericardial effusion** is fluid beyond the normal 10-50 mL; **cardiac tamponade** is the life-threatening compression of the heart when intrapericardial pressure exceeds filling pressure - **hypotension, raised JVP, muffled sounds (Beck triad) and pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg** - treated by **urgent pericardiocentesis**; **constrictive pericarditis** is a rigid, often calcified pericardium (in India mostly **tuberculous**) causing right heart failure with **Kussmaul sign and pericardial knock**, cured by **pericardiectomy**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`pericardial effusion`,`cardiac tamponade`,`Beck triad`,`pulsus paradoxus`,`electrical alternans`,`RV diastolic collapse`,`pericardiocentesis`,`pericardial window`,`effusive-constrictive pericarditis`,`constrictive pericarditis`,`Kussmaul sign`,`pericardial knock`,`Friedreich sign`,`square root sign`,`pericardiectomy`,`tuberculous constriction`,`low-pressure tamponade`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pericardial effusion**: more than the normal **10-50 mL** of fluid; size on echo (end-diastolic echo-free space) - **mild under 10 mm, moderate 10-20 mm, large over 20 mm**. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cardiac tamponade**: life-threatening, slow or rapid compression of the heart by fluid, pus, blood, clot or gas, when intrapericardial pressure rises to impair filling. [ESC 2025]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis**: a thickened, fibrotic and often calcified pericardium that impairs diastolic filling, producing right-sided heart failure with preserved systolic function. [ESC 2025]`,`**Effusive-constrictive pericarditis**: tamponade plus constriction by the visceral pericardium - right atrial pressure stays high after the fluid is removed; common with **TB**. [ESC 2025]`,`In high-income countries, effusions are mostly idiopathic, neoplastic, post-surgical or iatrogenic; in India and Africa **TB causes most large effusions**, and HIV raises this further. [ESC 2025; INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Constriction - aetiology** (Western series): idiopathic/viral 42-49%, post-cardiac surgery 11-37%, post-radiation 9-31%, connective tissue disease 3-7%, post-infective (TB, purulent) 3-6%; in India **TB is the commonest cause** in surgical series. [ESC 2015]`,`Large asymptomatic idiopathic chronic effusions carry a small but real risk of progressing to tamponade (historically up to about one-third over time). [ESC 2015]`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`**Infectious**: **TB** (commonest in India), viral, purulent bacterial (Staphylococcus, Pneumococcus), fungal. [ESC 2025]`,`**Neoplastic**: lung and breast cancer, lymphoma, leukaemia, melanoma - often haemorrhagic and recurrent. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemopericardium**: **post-MI free-wall rupture**, **type A aortic dissection**, chest trauma, iatrogenic (PCI, pacing lead, ablation, valve procedures), anticoagulants. [ESC 2025]`,`**Metabolic and others**: uraemia, **hypothyroidism** (large, slow, rarely tamponade), heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome, pregnancy, ovarian hyperstimulation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autoimmune**: SLE, RA, systemic sclerosis; **post-cardiac injury syndromes** (Dressler, post-pericardiotomy). [ESC 2025]`,`**Drugs and radiation**: minoxidil, hydralazine, isoniazid, phenytoin, dasatinib; mediastinal radiotherapy (effusion early, constriction years later). [ESC 2025]`,`**Chylopericardium** after thoracic surgery or lymphatic obstruction (triglycerides over 500 mg/dL). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Risk of constriction after pericarditis**: under 1% idiopathic/viral, 2-5% immune-mediated or neoplastic, **20-30% bacterial, especially purulent and TB**. [ESC 2015]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The pericardial **pressure-volume curve** is flat at first then steep: slow fluid (hypothyroid, TB) can reach **1-2 L** without tamponade, whereas **100-200 mL accumulating rapidly** (trauma, rupture) causes tamponade. [Harrison 22e]`,`In tamponade, rising pericardial pressure equalises with **right atrial, RV diastolic and LV diastolic pressures**, so filling and stroke volume fall; compensation is tachycardia and vasoconstriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus** (inspiratory fall of systolic BP over 10 mmHg): in a fixed pericardial volume, inspiration increases RV filling, the septum bulges left and LV filling and stroke volume fall - **exaggerated ventricular interdependence**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**JVP in tamponade**: x descent preserved, **y descent blunted or absent**, because the heart cannot fill in early diastole. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Constriction**: the rigid shell lets the ventricles fill rapidly in early diastole then **stops filling abruptly** - the **dip-and-plateau (square-root) sign**, prominent **y descent (Friedreich)** and pericardial knock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dissociation of intrathoracic and intracardiac pressures** in constriction: inspiratory fall in thoracic pressure is not transmitted to the heart, so pulmonary venous to LV gradient falls and **mitral inflow drops in inspiration**, while the JVP fails to fall or rises (**Kussmaul sign**). [Braunwald 12e]`,`Chronic venous congestion in constriction produces **cardiac cirrhosis**, ascites, **protein-losing enteropathy** and muscle wasting. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low-pressure tamponade** occurs in hypovolaemia (haemorrhage, dialysis, TB) - pericardial and RA pressures are only 6-12 mmHg, the JVP is not raised, and fluids can unmask it. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Effusion by onset**: acute, subacute, chronic (over 3 months). [ESC 2025]`,`**By size**: mild under 10 mm, moderate 10-20 mm, **large over 20 mm** end-diastolic echo-free space. [ESC 2025]`,`**By distribution**: circumferential or **loculated** (common after cardiac surgery - may compress one chamber and escape echo screening). [ESC 2025]`,`**By composition**: transudate, exudate, **haemorrhagic**, purulent, chylous. [ESC 2025]`,`**Tamponade**: acute surgical (trauma, rupture, dissection), subacute medical (TB, malignancy), low-pressure and regional. [ESC 2025]`,`**Constriction**: **transient** (resolves with anti-inflammatory therapy), **effusive-constrictive**, and **chronic** (fibrocalcific). [ESC 2025]`,`**ESC triage score** for pericardiocentesis weighs aetiology, clinical status and imaging; a score of **6 or more** favours urgent drainage when no surgical cause exists. [ESC 2015]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Effusion** without tamponade is often asymptomatic or causes breathlessness, chest fullness, cough, dysphagia or hoarseness from compression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tamponade**: breathlessness, orthopnoea is less typical than **preferring to sit forward**, dizziness, syncope, confusion, oliguria and abdominal fullness. [ESC 2025]`,`**Acute tamponade** follows chest trauma, procedures, MI (days 1-7) or tearing chest pain of dissection - sudden collapse with electromechanical dissociation. [ESC 2025]`,`**Subacute tamponade**: TB (fever, night sweats, weight loss) or cancer (known primary, weight loss) over weeks. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Constriction**: progressive **abdominal swelling**, leg oedema, breathlessness, fatigue and weight loss; in India often a history of treated or untreated TB. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about previous **pericarditis, cardiac surgery, mediastinal radiation, TB, dialysis and anticoagulants**. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Beck triad** (acute tamponade): **hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds** - described by Claude Beck (1935) for surgical tamponade; often incomplete in slow medical tamponade. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg**: measure by deflating the cuff slowly - note when Korotkoff sounds are heard only in expiration and then throughout; the difference is the paradox. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other tamponade signs: **sinus tachycardia** (absence is unusual except in hypothyroidism or uraemia), tachypnoea, cold peripheries, **Ewart sign** (dullness and bronchial breathing below the left scapula in large effusions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus can be absent** despite tamponade in **ASD, severe AR, LV dysfunction with high LVEDP, positive-pressure ventilation and loculated effusion**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus also occurs** in severe asthma or COPD, massive PE, RV infarction, tension pneumothorax, hypovolaemic shock and in about one-third of constriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constriction**: raised JVP with **prominent y descent (Friedreich sign)**, **Kussmaul sign**, **pericardial knock** (early diastolic, 0.09-0.12 s after A2, higher pitched and earlier than S3), hepatomegaly, **ascites out of proportion to oedema**, AF in some. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Broadbent sign** (systolic retraction of the left posterior lower ribs) and quiet precordium with impalpable apex suggest pericardial adhesion and constriction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Always look at the neck in ascites** - constriction is missed as cirrhosis when the JVP is not examined at 45-90 degrees. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Tamponade versus cardiogenic shock**: small heart with large effusion and collapsing chambers versus dilated poorly contracting LV - echo decides in minutes. [ESC 2025]`,`**Tamponade versus tension pneumothorax and massive PE**: both cause raised JVP and hypotension - absent breath sounds and tracheal shift, or RV dilatation without effusion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constriction versus restrictive cardiomyopathy**: thick pericardium, septal bounce, respiratory variation over 25%, preserved medial e' and low BNP favour constriction (table below). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Constriction versus cirrhosis**: raised JVP, Kussmaul sign and high ascitic protein (SAAG 1.1 g/dL or more with protein over 2.5 g/dL suggests cardiac ascites). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Constriction versus nephrotic syndrome and protein-losing enteropathy**: the JVP is normal in nephrotic syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Large effusion versus dilated cardiomyopathy on CXR**: both give a big globular heart; clear lung fields with a water-bottle shape favour effusion; echo distinguishes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural versus pericardial effusion on echo**: pericardial fluid lies anterior to the descending aorta, pleural fluid posterior to it. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: sinus tachycardia, **low voltage** (limb leads under 5 mm, chest leads under 10 mm) and **electrical alternans** (beat-to-beat QRS variation from the swinging heart - total alternans is highly specific for tamponade). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: **water-bottle (flask-shaped) cardiac silhouette** with clear lung fields when over about 250 mL; **pericardial calcification** on the lateral film in constriction (TB). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echo - the key test**: size, distribution, **RA collapse** (late diastole, lasting over one-third of the cycle - specific), **RV early diastolic collapse** (specific), **plethoric IVC with under 50% inspiratory collapse** (sensitive), swinging heart. [ESC 2025]`,`**Doppler in tamponade**: respiratory variation of **mitral inflow over 25-30%** and **tricuspid inflow over 40-60%**, hepatic vein expiratory diastolic reversal. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Tamponade is a clinical diagnosis supported by echo** - drain a haemodynamically compromised patient without waiting for further imaging. [ESC 2025]`,`**Constriction - echo (Mayo criteria)**: **respiratory septal shift** plus **medial e' 9 cm/s or more** or **hepatic vein expiratory diastolic reversal ratio 0.79 or more** - sensitivity 87%, specificity 91%; also septal bounce, annulus reversus, plethoric IVC. [Welch, Circ CV Imaging 2014]`,`**CT and CMR**: pericardial thickness **4 mm or more** (CT best for calcification), CMR real-time cine shows septal flattening on inspiration and **pericardial LGE** (inflammation - suggests transient, reversible constriction); a normal thickness does not exclude constriction in about 1 in 5. [ESC 2025]`,`**Cardiac catheterisation** when non-invasive tests disagree: **equalisation of diastolic pressures (within 5 mmHg)**, dip-and-plateau, RVEDP over one-third of RVSP, PA systolic under 50 mmHg, and **ventricular discordance (systolic area index over 1.1)**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pericardial fluid analysis**: cell count and differential, protein and LDH (exudate), glucose, Gram stain and culture, **ZN, Xpert MTB/RIF (CBNAAT or Truenat), mycobacterial culture**, **ADA (40 U/L or more supports TB)**, cytology, haematocrit (haemopericardium), triglycerides (chyle). [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Baseline bloods**: blood count, creatinine, TSH, CRP, coagulation and platelets (before drainage), HIV, ANA when indicated; **BNP is near normal in constriction** but high in restriction. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**Small asymptomatic effusion with no inflammation**: find the cause (TSH, renal function, drugs, TB screen) and repeat echo in 6 months or so. [ESC 2025]`,`**Moderate effusion**: echo every 3-6 months; **large idiopathic effusion**: every 3-6 months, drainage if symptomatic or growing. [ESC 2025]`,`Treat **anti-inflammatory only if pericarditis is present** - NSAIDs and colchicine do not shrink effusions without inflammation. [ESC 2025]`,`**Hypothyroid effusion** resolves slowly with thyroxine; **uraemic effusion** with intensified dialysis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Refer the same day** any effusion with breathlessness, hypotension, tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus or echo chamber collapse. [ESC 2025]`,`**Constriction - medical measures**: loop diuretics and salt restriction relieve congestion but do not cure; **avoid beta-blockers and rate-slowing drugs** because tachycardia maintains output. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB constriction**: start **NTEP ATT (2HRZE + 4HRE)**, which can halt progression of early (effusive or transient) disease. [INDEX-TB 2016]`,`**Transient constriction** (recent pericarditis, raised CRP, pericardial LGE on CMR): **anti-inflammatory therapy for 2-3 months** before considering surgery. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - tamponade and pericardiocentesis`,points:[`**Tamponade is an emergency**: urgent pericardiocentesis (or surgical drainage) is class I; **avoid diuretics and vasodilators**, and give a **250-500 mL fluid bolus** only as a temporary bridge in hypovolaemic patients. [ESC 2025]`,`**Avoid positive-pressure ventilation** before drainage - it reduces venous return and can precipitate arrest; drain under local anaesthesia first. [ESC 2025]`,`**Indications for pericardiocentesis**: tamponade; symptomatic moderate to large effusion not responding to medical therapy; suspected **bacterial, TB or neoplastic** cause needing diagnosis. [ESC 2025]`,`**Surgery instead of needle**: **type A aortic dissection** and **myocardial free-wall rupture** (needle drainage can worsen bleeding), purulent or loculated effusions, clotted haemopericardium and recurrent malignant effusion. [ESC 2025]`,`**Relative contraindications**: uncorrected coagulopathy, anticoagulation, **platelets under 50,000/µL**, small, posterior or loculated effusions. [ESC 2015]`,`**Technique**: echo-guided (or fluoroscopic); **subxiphoid approach** with the patient at 30-45 degrees - an 18G needle between xiphoid and left costal margin, angled 15-30 degrees to the skin, aimed at the **left shoulder**; or an apical or parasternal approach where fluid is closest on echo (avoid the internal thoracic artery). [ESC 2025]`,`Confirm position with **agitated saline on echo**, pass a guidewire (Seldinger), leave a **pigtail catheter**; drain no more than about **1 L at a time** to avoid **pericardial decompression syndrome** (acute RV dilatation and pulmonary oedema). [ESC 2025]`,`Keep the catheter for **extended drainage until output is under about 25-30 mL/day** - reduces recurrence. [ESC 2025]`,`**Complications** (about 4-10%, under 2% major with echo guidance): RV puncture, coronary laceration, arrhythmia, pneumothorax, liver or peritoneal puncture, infection and decompression syndrome. [ESC 2015]`,`**Recurrent malignant effusion**: extended drainage, **intrapericardial cisplatin** (lung cancer) or thiotepa (breast), **pericardial window** or percutaneous balloon pericardiotomy. [ESC 2025]`,`**Purulent pericarditis**: subxiphoid pericardiostomy with irrigation, **intrapericardial fibrinolysis** (urokinase or streptokinase) for loculations, IV antibiotics - high mortality even when treated. [ESC 2025]`,`**Type A dissection with tamponade**: emergency surgery; if surgery is not immediately available, **controlled drainage of small volumes** to keep systolic BP around 90 mmHg. [ESC 2014 Aortic]`]},{heading:`Constrictive pericarditis - definitive treatment`,points:[`**Pericardiectomy** is the treatment of chronic constriction: a **radical (phrenic-to-phrenic) pericardiectomy** is preferred over partial resection. [ESC 2025]`,`**Operative mortality is about 6-12%**, higher with advanced NYHA class, radiation aetiology, renal dysfunction and myocardial atrophy - **operate before end-stage**. [ESC 2015]`,`**TB constriction**: ATT for all; **pericardiectomy if the patient is not improving or is deteriorating after 4-8 weeks of ATT**. [ESC 2015]`,`**Long-term survival** after pericardiectomy depends on aetiology - best in idiopathic (about 88% at 7 years), intermediate post-surgical (66%), worst post-radiation (27%). [Bertog, JACC 2004]`,`Pre-operative optimisation: diuresis, nutrition (albumin), treat AF, correct anaemia and renal function. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Effusive-constrictive disease** may need **visceral epicardiectomy**, because pericardiocentesis alone leaves RA pressure high. [ESC 2025]`,`Persistent symptoms after surgery suggest incomplete resection, myocardial atrophy or coexisting restrictive myocardial disease (especially after radiation). [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Post-cardiac surgery**: effusions are common in the first week; **loculated posterior effusions** can cause late tamponade (days 7-14) especially on anticoagulants - echo before discharge. [ESC 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: small effusions are physiological in the third trimester; drainage is done with echo guidance (avoid fluoroscopy). [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Children**: purulent pericarditis (Staphylococcus, Haemophilus) and TB in India; post-operative effusions after congenital surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: uraemic effusion responds to intensified dialysis; drain if tamponade or no response in 10-14 days; watch for low-pressure tamponade with hypotension during dialysis. [ESC 2025]`,`**HIV**: large effusions are mostly TB; start ATT empirically when TB is likely and fluid tests are unavailable; avoid adjunctive steroids with low CD4. [WHO 2022]`,`**Cancer**: effusions are often malignant but may be radiation- or drug-related; cytology positive in about two-thirds of malignant effusions. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: bedside **POCUS (FAST)** identifies tamponade; a district doctor trained in subxiphoid pericardiocentesis can save a life before referral. [ESC 2025]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: large effusion with bradycardia - tamponade is rare; avoid needless drainage. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Tamponade** untreated progresses to PEA arrest; outcome after drainage depends on cause - poor in malignancy, good in idiopathic and uraemic. [ESC 2025]`,`**Malignant effusion** recurs in up to half after simple drainage and carries a poor median survival. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TB pericardial disease**: constriction develops in a substantial minority despite ATT; IMPI showed constriction in 4.4% with prednisolone versus 7.8% with placebo. [IMPI 2014]`,`**Untreated constriction**: progressive right heart failure, cardiac cirrhosis, protein-losing enteropathy, cachexia and AF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pericardiectomy complications**: low-output state, bleeding, arrhythmia, phrenic nerve injury and residual constriction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Post-pericardiotomy decompression syndrome** after large-volume drainage causes acute LV or RV failure - drain slowly. [ESC 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer immediately** any effusion with haemodynamic compromise, suspected purulent or malignant effusion, or effusion after MI or procedures. [ESC 2025]`,`After drainage: echo at **24-48 hours**, at 1-2 weeks and 1 month to detect re-accumulation or constriction. [ESC 2025]`,`**TB pericardial effusion**: echo at the end of the intensive phase and at the end of ATT to detect evolving constriction; register on **Nikshay**, nutritional support through **Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana**. [NTEP 2021]`,`**Post-pericardiectomy**: review congestion, diuretic need and renal function at 1, 3 and 6 months. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Prevention of TB constriction**: prompt diagnosis, adequate drainage of large effusions and full ATT; adjunctive steroids in HIV-negative patients reduce constriction. [WHO 2022]`,`**Counsel**: report breathlessness, dizziness, swelling or abdominal distension; complete ATT; pericardiectomy is covered under **AB PM-JAY** cardiothoracic packages for eligible families. [NTEP 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2025 myocarditis and pericarditis guideline** updates effusion triage, defines effusive-constrictive and transient constriction, and stresses **multimodality imaging (CT and CMR)** for constriction and inflammation. [ESC 2025]`,`ESC 2025 keeps **urgent pericardiocentesis or surgery for tamponade (class I)** and **echo-guided drainage** as the standard technique, with extended catheter drainage. [ESC 2025]`,`**CMR pericardial LGE** identifies inflammatory, potentially reversible constriction - ESC 2025 advises a trial of anti-inflammatory therapy before pericardiectomy in such cases. [ESC 2025]`,`Anti-inflammatory drugs are **not recommended for isolated effusion without systemic inflammation**. [ESC 2025]`,`**WHO 2022** consolidated TB guidance continues to allow adjunctive corticosteroids in tuberculous pericarditis. [WHO 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Beck triad**: hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds - acute surgical tamponade. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Kussmaul sign** = JVP rises on inspiration - constriction, restriction, RV infarction, severe TR; it is **unusual in tamponade**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**y descent**: absent in tamponade, steep in constriction (Friedreich sign) - a favourite JVP question. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulsus paradoxus is not paradoxical** - it is an exaggeration of the normal inspiratory fall over 10 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving diuretics to a hypotensive patient with a raised JVP and large effusion - this precipitates arrest. [ESC 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: needle drainage of haemopericardium from aortic dissection or free-wall rupture instead of emergency surgery. [ESC 2025]`,`**Indian viva**: young man with ascites, raised JVP, pericardial knock and calcified pericardium - TB constriction; treat with ATT and pericardiectomy. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Classification of pericardial effusion`,columns:[`Basis`,`Category`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Acute, subacute, chronic (over 3 months)`,`Chronic idiopathic large effusions can progress to tamponade`],[`Size (end-diastolic echo-free space)`,`Mild under 10 mm; moderate 10-20 mm; large over 20 mm`,`Large is a high-risk feature`],[`Distribution`,`Circumferential or loculated`,`Loculated after surgery; may compress one chamber`],[`Composition`,`Transudate, exudate, haemorrhagic, purulent, chylous`,`Haemorrhagic: TB, malignancy, trauma, dissection, rupture`],[`Haemodynamics`,`No compression, tamponade, effusive-constrictive`,`Effusive-constrictive: RA pressure stays high after drainage`]]},{heading:`Signs of tamponade and their meaning`,columns:[`Sign`,`Finding`,`Meaning or caveat`],rows:[[`Beck triad`,`Hypotension, raised JVP, muffled sounds`,`Acute surgical tamponade; often incomplete in slow tamponade`],[`Pulsus paradoxus`,`Inspiratory fall in systolic BP over 10 mmHg`,`Absent with ASD, AR, LV dysfunction, ventilation, loculation`],[`JVP`,`Raised with absent y descent`,`Not raised in low-pressure tamponade`],[`ECG`,`Low voltage, electrical alternans, sinus tachycardia`,`Total alternans is highly specific`],[`CXR`,`Water-bottle heart, clear lungs`,`Needs over about 250 mL`],[`Echo: RA collapse`,`Late diastolic, over one-third of cycle`,`Sensitive; longer duration more specific`],[`Echo: RV collapse`,`Early diastolic`,`Specific for tamponade`],[`Echo: IVC`,`Dilated, under 50% inspiratory collapse`,`Sensitive; absent makes tamponade unlikely`],[`Doppler`,`Mitral E variation over 25-30%; tricuspid over 40-60%`,`Ventricular interdependence`]]},{heading:`Pericardial fluid analysis`,columns:[`Test`,`Result`,`Interpretation`],rows:[[`Protein and LDH`,`Protein over 3 g/dL, fluid/serum protein over 0.5, LDH over 200 U/L, fluid/serum LDH over 0.6`,`Exudate (most pericardial effusions)`],[`Cells`,`Lymphocytes predominate`,`TB, malignancy, lymphoma`],[`Cells`,`Neutrophils, low glucose, organisms`,`Purulent pericarditis`],[`ADA`,`40 U/L or more`,`Supports TB in endemic setting`],[`Xpert MTB/RIF or Truenat`,`Positive`,`TB with rifampicin resistance status`],[`Cytology`,`Malignant cells`,`Malignant effusion`],[`Haematocrit`,`Near blood`,`Haemopericardium: trauma, rupture, dissection`],[`Triglycerides`,`Over 500 mg/dL`,`Chylopericardium`]]},{heading:`Constrictive pericarditis versus restrictive cardiomyopathy versus tamponade`,columns:[`Feature`,`Constrictive pericarditis`,`Restrictive cardiomyopathy`,`Tamponade`],rows:[[`JVP waveform`,`Steep y descent (Friedreich)`,`Steep y descent`,`Absent y descent`],[`Kussmaul sign`,`Present`,`Often present`,`Usually absent`],[`Pulsus paradoxus`,`About one-third`,`Rare`,`Usual`],[`Extra sound`,`Pericardial knock`,`S3`,`None; muffled sounds`],[`Pericardium`,`Thick (4 mm+), calcified`,`Normal`,`Effusion`],[`Medial e'`,`9 cm/s or more; annulus reversus`,`Under 6-7 cm/s`,`Not used`],[`Mitral E respiratory variation`,`Over 25%`,`Minimal`,`Over 25-30%`],[`BNP`,`Near normal`,`Markedly raised`,`Variable`],[`PA systolic pressure`,`Under 50 mmHg`,`Often over 50 mmHg`,`Normal`],[`Cath`,`Dip-and-plateau, equal diastolic pressures, discordance`,`Dip-and-plateau, LVEDP over RVEDP by 5+, concordance`,`Equal pressures, no dip-and-plateau`],[`Treatment`,`Pericardiectomy (ATT if TB)`,`Disease-specific, transplant`,`Pericardiocentesis`]]},{heading:`Drugs and procedures in effusion, tamponade and constriction`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Normal saline bolus (tamponade bridge)`,`250-500 mL IV over 10-15 min`,`Once, only if hypovolaemic, while arranging drainage`,`Can worsen tamponade if euvolaemic`],[`Pericardiocentesis with pigtail catheter`,`Echo-guided subxiphoid or apical; drain under 1 L at a time`,`Leave catheter until output under 25-30 mL/day`,`RV or coronary puncture, arrhythmia, decompression syndrome`],[`HRZE fixed-dose combination (TB)`,`Weight band daily (e.g. 4 tablets for 50-64 kg)`,`2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy (pyridoxine), optic neuritis`],[`Prednisolone (TB, IMPI)`,`120, 90, 60, 30, 15, 5 mg/day for one week each`,`6 weeks with ATT, HIV-negative`,`Avoid in HIV with low CD4; hyperglycaemia`],[`Furosemide (constriction)`,`20-80 mg orally or IV daily`,`Symptom relief before surgery`,`Hypotension, AKI - avoid in tamponade`],[`Intrapericardial urokinase`,`Instilled via catheter in loculated purulent or TB effusion`,`Repeated doses as needed`,`Bleeding`],[`Intrapericardial cisplatin`,`Instilled via catheter (lung cancer)`,`Single or repeated`,`Chemical pericarditis, marrow effects`],[`Colchicine`,`0.5 mg once or twice daily by weight`,`3 months if inflammatory effusion or transient constriction`,`Diarrhoea; not for effusion without inflammation`]]}],redFlags:[`Hypotension with raised JVP and an effusion - tamponade; drain now.`,`Pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg or echo RV diastolic collapse.`,`Effusion after MI, cardiac surgery, PCI, ablation or pacemaker, or in a patient on anticoagulants.`,`Effusion with tearing chest pain or pulse deficit - type A dissection; surgery, not needle.`,`Fever with sepsis and effusion - purulent pericarditis.`,`Rapidly enlarging effusion or effusion over 20 mm with symptoms.`,`Ascites with raised JVP and Kussmaul sign - constriction; refer for imaging and surgical opinion.`],pearls:[`**Beck triad**: hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds.`,`**Tamponade kills the y descent**; constriction exaggerates it.`,`**Speed, not size**: 150 mL fast can tamponade, 1.5 L slow may not.`,`**RV early diastolic collapse** is the specific echo sign; plethoric IVC is the sensitive one.`,`**Total electrical alternans** = swinging heart = tamponade.`,`**No diuretics, no vasodilators, no ventilator before drainage** in tamponade.`,`**Dissection or rupture = surgeon, not needle**.`,`**Kussmaul + knock + ascites + calcified pericardium** = constriction (TB in India).`,`TB constriction: **ATT first; pericardiectomy if no improvement in 4-8 weeks**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-q1`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with a 6-week febrile illness becomes breathless and dizzy. BP 84/60 mmHg, pulse 124/min, JVP raised to the ear lobes with absent y descent, and systolic BP falls 18 mmHg on inspiration. Echo shows a 28 mm circumferential effusion with early diastolic RV collapse. What is the immediate management?`,options:[`IV furosemide 40 mg to relieve venous congestion`,`Echo-guided pericardiocentesis`,`Intubation and positive-pressure ventilation`,`IV nitroglycerin infusion`],answer:1,explanation:`Hypotension, raised JVP with absent y descent, pulsus paradoxus and RV diastolic collapse define cardiac tamponade, and urgent pericardiocentesis (echo-guided) is class I treatment. Diuretics and nitrates reduce preload in a heart that depends on high filling pressure and can precipitate arrest, and positive-pressure ventilation reduces venous return further, so intubation should follow drainage where possible.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-q2`,stem:`Pulsus paradoxus may be absent despite cardiac tamponade in which situation?`,options:[`Severe acute asthma`,`Tension pneumothorax`,`Large atrial septal defect`,`Massive pulmonary embolism`],answer:2,explanation:`In an atrial septal defect, inspiratory increases in systemic venous return are balanced by shunt flow between the atria, so the reciprocal respiratory filling that produces pulsus paradoxus is lost; severe AR, high LVEDP, ventilation and loculated effusion act similarly. Severe asthma, tension pneumothorax and massive PE are causes of pulsus paradoxus without tamponade, not reasons for its absence.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-q3`,stem:`A 32-year-old man treated irregularly for pulmonary TB 2 years ago has progressive abdominal distension and leg swelling. JVP is raised and rises on inspiration, with a high-pitched early diastolic sound. Lateral chest X-ray shows pericardial calcification. Which catheterisation finding is most specific for his diagnosis?`,options:[`LVEDP exceeding RVEDP by 10 mmHg`,`PA systolic pressure of 65 mmHg`,`Concordant rise and fall of LV and RV systolic pressures with respiration`,`Discordant respiratory change in LV and RV systolic pressures (systolic area index over 1.1)`],answer:3,explanation:`Constrictive pericarditis produces exaggerated ventricular interdependence, so in inspiration the RV systolic pressure rises as the LV falls; this discordance (systolic area index over 1.1) is the most specific invasive sign. A diastolic pressure difference over 5 mmHg, PA systolic over 50 mmHg and concordant ventricular pressures all favour restrictive cardiomyopathy, while dip-and-plateau occurs in both conditions.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-q4`,stem:`A 68-year-old man collapses with tearing chest pain radiating to the back. BP 80/50 mmHg, JVP raised; bedside echo shows a dilated aortic root with an intimal flap and a moderate pericardial effusion with RA collapse. What is the best management?`,options:[`Emergency surgical repair of the aorta, with only controlled small-volume drainage if surgery is delayed`,`Immediate full pericardiocentesis to dryness`,`IV heparin and thrombolysis`,`Large-volume IV fluids and observation`],answer:0,explanation:`Haemopericardium from type A aortic dissection is treated by emergency surgery; draining to dryness raises BP and can extend the dissection and bleeding, so if surgery is not immediately available only small controlled volumes are removed to keep systolic BP around 90 mmHg. Anticoagulation and thrombolysis are catastrophic in dissection, and fluids alone only delay definitive repair.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-q5`,stem:`After pericardiocentesis of a tuberculous effusion, pericardial pressure falls to near zero, but right atrial pressure stays at 18 mmHg and the patient remains congested. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Recurrent tamponade from catheter blockage`,`Restrictive cardiomyopathy`,`Effusive-constrictive pericarditis`,`Pericardial decompression syndrome`],answer:2,explanation:`Failure of right atrial pressure to fall by 50% or below 10 mmHg after intrapericardial pressure is normalised defines effusive-constrictive pericarditis, in which the thickened visceral pericardium keeps constraining the heart; it is common in TB and may need epicardiectomy. A blocked catheter would leave pericardial pressure high, restrictive cardiomyopathy has no effusion component, and decompression syndrome is acute ventricular failure after large-volume drainage.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the pathophysiology, clinical features, diagnosis and management of cardiac tamponade. Write the indications and technique of pericardiocentesis.`,openingLines:[`Cardiac tamponade is life-threatening compression of the heart by accumulated pericardial fluid, blood, pus or gas, when intrapericardial pressure rises to equal and impede cardiac filling, reducing stroke volume and output.`,`It is a clinical diagnosis - hypotension, raised JVP, muffled sounds (Beck triad) and pulsus paradoxus - supported by echo, and is treated by urgent drainage; in India tuberculosis is the leading cause of large effusions.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`TB (India), malignancy, idiopathic/viral, uraemia, hypothyroidism; haemopericardium - trauma, MI rupture, type A dissection, procedures, anticoagulants.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Pericardial pressure-volume curve: rate of accumulation matters more than volume.`,`Equalisation of diastolic pressures; ventricular interdependence causes pulsus paradoxus; y descent lost.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Breathlessness, dizziness, oliguria; tachycardia, hypotension, raised JVP, muffled sounds, pulsus paradoxus over 10 mmHg.`,`Low-pressure tamponade in hypovolaemia; pulsus absent with ASD, AR, LV dysfunction, ventilation.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG: low voltage, electrical alternans; CXR: water-bottle heart.`,`Echo: effusion size, RA late diastolic and RV early diastolic collapse, plethoric IVC, mitral inflow variation over 25%.`,`Fluid: protein, LDH, cells, ADA, Xpert, culture, cytology.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Emergency drainage; avoid diuretics, vasodilators, positive-pressure ventilation; small fluid bolus as bridge.`,`Surgery for dissection, rupture, purulent, loculated or clotted effusion; treat the cause (ATT for TB).`]},{heading:`Pericardiocentesis - indications and technique`,points:[`Indications: tamponade; symptomatic large effusion; suspected bacterial, TB or malignant cause.`,`Echo-guided subxiphoid: 30-45 degrees head-up, 18G needle between xiphoid and left costal margin, 15-30 degrees to skin, towards left shoulder.`,`Confirm with agitated saline, Seldinger pigtail, drain under 1 L at a time, keep until under 25-30 mL/day.`,`Complications: RV or coronary puncture, arrhythmia, pneumothorax, decompression syndrome.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pericardial pressure-volume curve: slow versus rapid accumulation`,`Diagram of subxiphoid pericardiocentesis needle path towards the left shoulder`,`JVP waveform: absent y descent in tamponade`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and aetiology`,marks:1},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pericardiocentesis indications and technique`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`Beck triad`,`pulsus paradoxus`,`RV collapse`,`electrical alternans`,`pericardiocentesis`,`subxiphoid`]},{id:`cardiovascular-pericardial-effusion-tamponade-constriction-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate constrictive pericarditis from restrictive cardiomyopathy.`,openingLines:[`Both present with right heart failure, raised JVP with steep y descent and preserved systolic function, but constrictive pericarditis is a surgically curable pericardial disease while restrictive cardiomyopathy is a myocardial disease.`,`The distinction rests on pericardial imaging, ventricular interdependence on echo and catheterisation, tissue Doppler e' and BNP.`],answer:[{heading:`Clinical`,points:[`Constriction: past TB, surgery, radiation; pericardial knock; MR/TR uncommon.`,`Restriction: amyloid, sarcoid, iron, EMF; S3; MR/TR common; systemic clues.`]},{heading:`Imaging`,points:[`Constriction: thick or calcified pericardium, septal bounce and shift, mitral E variation over 25%, medial e' 9 cm/s or more, expiratory hepatic vein reversal.`,`Restriction: normal pericardium, minimal variation, e' under 6-7 cm/s, inspiratory hepatic vein reversal, myocardial LGE.`]},{heading:`Biomarkers and catheterisation`,points:[`BNP near normal in constriction, high in restriction.`,`Constriction: equal diastolic pressures, PA systolic under 50, discordant ventricular pressures; restriction: LVEDP over RVEDP by 5+, PA over 50, concordant.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Constriction: pericardiectomy (ATT first if TB); restriction: disease-specific therapy, diuretics, transplant.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table with 10 rows`,`Sketch of discordant versus concordant LV and RV pressure tracings in inspiration`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical differences`,marks:1},{part:`Echo, CT and CMR differences`,marks:2},{part:`BNP and catheterisation`,marks:1.5},{part:`Treatment`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`septal bounce`,`annulus reversus`,`medial e'`,`discordance`,`pericardiectomy`,`BNP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - pericardial disease chapter`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - pericardial diseases`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of myocarditis and pericarditis (Eur Heart J 2025)`,`2015 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of pericardial diseases`,`INDEX-TB Guidelines on extrapulmonary tuberculosis for India, ICMR and Central TB Division, 2016; NTEP Guidelines for Programmatic Management of TB, 2021`,`WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis, Module 4: Treatment, 2022; Mayosi BM et al. IMPI trial, NEJM 2014`,`Welch TD et al. Echocardiographic diagnosis of constrictive pericarditis: Mayo Clinic criteria, Circ Cardiovasc Imaging 2014`]});var o=[];o.push({id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease`,title:`Rheumatic heart disease in adults: prophylaxis, screening and the RHD programme`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Rheumatic heart disease (RHD)** is chronic valve damage - chiefly **mitral**, then aortic - left by one or more attacks of **acute rheumatic fever**, an autoimmune sequel of **group A streptococcal pharyngitis**; it is confirmed on echo (**WHF 2023 criteria**), its progression is prevented by **benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units deep IM every 4 weeks**, and AF with rheumatic mitral stenosis needs a **vitamin K antagonist, not a DOAC** (INVICTUS).`,keywords:[`RHD`,`acute rheumatic fever`,`ARF`,`Jones criteria 2015`,`WHF 2023 echo criteria`,`latent RHD`,`subclinical carditis`,`benzathine penicillin G`,`BPG`,`secondary prophylaxis`,`group A streptococcus`,`ASO titre`,`anti-DNase B`,`Aschoff body`,`INVICTUS`,`GOAL trial`,`REMEDY registry`,`RBSK`,`acenocoumarol`,`Sydenham chorea`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Rheumatic heart disease** is permanent valve damage that follows **acute rheumatic fever (ARF)**, a non-suppurative autoimmune complication appearing about **3 weeks (range 1-5 weeks)** after **group A streptococcal (GAS) pharyngitis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **40 million people live with RHD** worldwide and about **300,000 die of it every year**, almost all in low- and middle-income countries - it is the commonest acquired heart disease of children and young adults there. [GBD 2019]`,`**India carries the largest number of cases** - roughly a quarter of the global burden, about 13 million people in the GBD 2015 analysis. [Watkins, NEJM 2017]`,`By auscultation the prevalence in Indian schoolchildren is now **under 1 per 1000**, but **echo screening finds about 20 per 1000** - most RHD is clinically silent (**latent RHD**). [RHEUMATIC study, Heart 2011]`,`Adults with RHD are young: in the 14-country **REMEDY registry** (India included) the **median age was 28 years** and **two-thirds were women**; a third already had heart failure and about a fifth had AF. [REMEDY 2015]`,`Valve pattern: the **mitral valve alone in about two-thirds**, mitral plus aortic in about a quarter; isolated aortic disease is uncommon, organic tricuspid disease occurs only with mitral disease and the pulmonary valve is almost never involved. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **half of adults with RHD recall no attack of ARF** - the first presentation is often breathlessness, AF, stroke or decompensation in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`RHD is the **commonest heart disease complicating pregnancy in India** and an important indirect cause of maternal death. [Braunwald 12e]`,`The **World Health Assembly resolution WHA71.14 (2018)** commits member states, India included, to RHD registers, a secure benzathine penicillin supply and access to valve surgery. [WHO 2018]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Only **throat infection with GAS (Streptococcus pyogenes)** is proven to trigger ARF; GAS skin infection (impetigo, infected scabies) and group C/G streptococci are suspected contributors in tropical communities. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **0.3-3% of untreated GAS pharyngitis** is followed by ARF; in someone who has already had ARF the risk after a fresh GAS infection is far higher. [Harrison 22e]`,`Only **3-6% of any population is susceptible**; twin studies show high heritability, with associations to HLA class II alleles and innate immune genes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Household crowding** is the strongest environmental risk factor, with poverty, poor housing, undernutrition and poor access to primary care - RHD is a marker of social inequity. [WHO 2024]`,`First attacks peak at **5-15 years**; recurrences cluster in the next 5-10 years and a first attack after 35 years is rare - adults present with the consequences, not the fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`Progression to chronic RHD depends on the **severity of the first carditis** and the **number of recurrences** - each recurrence adds fresh valve damage. [Harrison 22e]`,`High-risk groups in India are **girls and young women**, rural and tribal communities and urban slums; women are also exposed through pregnancy, when the disease is unmasked. [ICMR Jai Vigyan report 2010]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the mitral valve scars`,points:[`**Molecular mimicry**: antibodies and T cells raised against the streptococcal **M protein** and **group A carbohydrate (N-acetyl-glucosamine)** cross-react with **cardiac myosin, tropomyosin, laminin and valve endothelium**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Antibody binding activates valve endothelium (**VCAM-1** upregulation), letting **CD4 T cells** into the valve - the valvulitis is T cell-driven, so scarring can progress without further infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`ARF is a **pancarditis**, but only the **valvulitis** leaves permanent damage; heart failure in acute carditis comes from valve regurgitation, not from myocardial contractile failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aschoff bodies** (fibrinoid necrosis with lymphocytes and **Anitschkow 'caterpillar' cells**) are pathognomonic of rheumatic carditis; the **MacCallum patch** is subendocardial thickening of the posterior left atrial wall. [Robbins 11e]`,`**Acute valvulitis causes regurgitation** - annular dilatation, chordal elongation and anterior leaflet prolapse - so young patients with ARF have MR with or without AR, never fresh stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Years of healing produce **commissural fusion, leaflet thickening, chordal fusion and shortening, then calcification** - the stenotic **'fish-mouth' mitral valve** of chronic RHD. [Robbins 11e]`,`Valves are hit in proportion to their **closing pressure**: mitral more than aortic, aortic more than tricuspid, and the pulmonary valve almost never. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Sydenham chorea** follows antibodies cross-reacting with **basal ganglia neurons** (lysoganglioside and dopamine receptors); arthritis is immune-complex driven, migratory and non-deforming. [Harrison 22e]`,`Preventing new GAS infections stops new immune hits - this is why **secondary prophylaxis** halts progression and allows a good proportion of mild lesions to regress. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Diagnosing ARF: 2015 revised Jones criteria (recap)`,points:[`Diagnosis needs **evidence of preceding GAS infection** (raised or rising **ASO or anti-DNase B**, positive throat culture or rapid antigen test) plus **2 major, or 1 major and 2 minor** criteria for an initial attack. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`A **recurrence** in someone with prior ARF or RHD can be diagnosed with 2 major, 1 major plus 2 minor, or **3 minor criteria**. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**India is a moderate/high-risk population** (ARF incidence over 2 per 100,000 school-age children a year, or all-age RHD prevalence over 1 per 1000), so the high-risk set of criteria applies. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`High-risk major criteria: **carditis (clinical or subclinical)**, **arthritis - monoarthritis, polyarthritis or polyarthralgia**, **Sydenham chorea**, **erythema marginatum** and **subcutaneous nodules**. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`High-risk minor criteria: **monoarthralgia**, **fever 38.0°C or more**, **ESR 30 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more**, and an age-corrected **prolonged PR interval** (not if carditis is already counted). [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`Low-risk populations accept only polyarthritis as the joint major and need fever of 38.5°C and ESR of 60 mm/h - these stricter cut-offs must not be used in India. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Echocardiography in every suspected ARF** is the biggest change since 1992: **subclinical carditis** (pathological MR or AR on Doppler without a murmur) counts as a major criterion. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Sydenham chorea** and **indolent (late) carditis** are the two exceptions that need no proof of streptococcal infection. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`Where ARF is likely but criteria are not met in a high-risk setting, it is reasonable to give **12 months of secondary prophylaxis** and then re-evaluate with echo. [AHA 2015 Jones]`]},{heading:`Echocardiographic diagnosis and staging: WHF 2023`,points:[`The **World Heart Federation 2023 criteria** (published 2024) update the 2012 criteria and set the **minimum echo findings for RHD**, for experts confirming disease found on screening or in clinic. [WHF 2023]`,`**Pathological MR**: seen in 2 views, jet length **2 cm or more** in at least one view, peak velocity **3 m/s or more** and **pansystolic** in at least one envelope; WHF 2023 adds weight-based jet-length cut-offs for small children. [WHF 2023]`,`**Pathological AR**: seen in 2 views, jet length **1 cm or more**, peak velocity **3 m/s or more** and **pandiastolic** in at least one envelope. [WHF 2023]`,`Rheumatic **mitral morphology**: anterior leaflet thickening, chordal thickening, restricted leaflet motion (often the posterior leaflet first) and excessive tip motion; aortic: irregular or focal thickening, coaptation defect, restricted motion or prolapse. [WHF 2012]`,`A **mean mitral gradient of 4 mmHg or more** with rheumatic morphology indicates mitral stenosis and definite RHD. [WHF 2012]`,`WHF 2023 staging by risk of progression: **Stage A** - early valve change meeting minimum criteria without rheumatic morphology (formerly borderline); **Stage B** - mild RHD, pathological regurgitation plus morphological features. [WHF 2023]`,`**Stage C** - moderate or severe established valve disease; **Stage D** - established valve disease plus complications such as heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke or the need for valve surgery. [WHF 2023]`,`**GOAL (Uganda, 2022)**: 4-weekly BPG for 2 years in 5-17-year-olds with latent RHD cut echo progression from **8.2% to 0.8%** (NNT 13) - the evidence base for treating screen-detected disease. [GOAL 2022]`,`**Handheld echo by trained non-experts** using simplified criteria (for example any MR jet of 2 cm or more, or any AR) is accurate enough for screening; positives need a full echo by an expert. [WHF 2023]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history and examination in the adult OPD`,points:[`**Exertional breathlessness and fatigue** are often accepted as normal by young patients - ask what they could do a year ago and whether they keep pace with peers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpitations** with sudden worsening of breathlessness suggest new **AF**; a stroke, TIA or cold limb in a young person should prompt a search for rheumatic mitral disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Orthopnoea, PND, **haemoptysis**, hoarseness (Ortner syndrome) and dysphagia point to **mitral stenosis** with a large left atrium; angina or syncope on exertion point to aortic stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about childhood **sore throats, migratory joint pain, chorea** and 'monthly injections' that were stopped, and about previous PTMC or valve surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a **prophylaxis history**: date of the last BPG injection, doses missed in the past year and why (pain, fear of reaction, stock-outs, distance). [Australian RHD guideline 2020]`,`**Fever in a known RHD patient** is infective endocarditis until proven otherwise; fever with joint pain and a changing murmur suggests **recurrent ARF**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulse**: irregularly irregular with a pulse deficit (AF), low volume (MS), collapsing (AR) or slow-rising (AS); look for **malar flush**, raised JVP, hepatomegaly and oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precordium**: tapping apex (MS), displaced hyperdynamic apex (MR, AR), left parasternal heave and **palpable P2** (pulmonary hypertension). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Auscultation**: loud S1, opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble (MS); apical pansystolic murmur to the axilla (MR); early diastolic murmur at the left sternal edge (AR) - details are in the individual valve topics. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **IE stigmata** (splinter haemorrhages, splenomegaly, Osler nodes, dental caries) and **ARF signs** (subcutaneous nodules over extensor tendons, erythema marginatum, choreiform movements). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Mitral valve prolapse**: mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur; echo shows redundant leaflets displaced over 2 mm without commissural fusion. [ASE 2017]`,`**Physiological regurgitation**: short (under 2 cm), non-pansystolic, low-velocity jets in normal valves - the reason WHF insists on all Doppler criteria before calling RHD. [WHF 2023]`,`**Flow murmurs** (anaemia, pregnancy, thyrotoxicosis, fever) are soft and ejection in timing, with structurally normal valves on echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bicuspid aortic valve**: ejection click and aortic murmur in a young person, but with a normal mitral valve; rheumatic aortic disease almost always comes with mitral disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis**: non-migratory, persistent joint pain with a short latency (under 10 days) and poor response to aspirin - still watch for carditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other arthritis mimics in India: **chikungunya**, SLE, rheumatoid arthritis, gonococcal arthritis and septic arthritis - serology and the absence of carditis separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Degenerative mitral stenosis** from annular calcification (elderly, CKD) and **left atrial myxoma** (positional symptoms, tumour plop) mimic rheumatic MS; echo distinguishes both. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Transthoracic echocardiography** is the diagnostic test: rheumatic morphology, severity of every lesion, LA size, LV size and function, PASP and thrombus (TTE misses most LA appendage thrombi). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ECG**: AF, P mitrale, right axis deviation and RVH in MS; LVH in MR or AR; a **prolonged PR** in active rheumatic carditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: LA enlargement (straight left heart border, double right heart border, splayed carina), upper lobe venous diversion, Kerley B lines and enlarged pulmonary arteries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptococcal serology** only when recurrent ARF is suspected: ASO peaks at **3-6 weeks**; anti-DNase B rises later and stays raised longer, helpful in chorea or late carditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ESR and CRP** are raised in active ARF (minor criteria ESR 30 mm/h or more, CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more in high-risk populations); a normal CRP argues against active rheumatic inflammation. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Three sets of blood cultures** before any antibiotic whenever a patient with RHD has unexplained fever. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**INR** for patients on a VKA - target **2.0-3.0** for AF with rheumatic MS; point-of-care INR meters help in remote areas. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**TOE** before PTMC or cardioversion (LA appendage thrombus) and to assess repairability; **coronary angiography** before valve surgery in older patients or those with risk factors. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Haemoglobin, renal function, TSH (new AF) and a **dental assessment** before any planned valve intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD: secondary prophylaxis`,points:[`**Secondary prophylaxis** - regular antibiotic to prevent new GAS infections and so new ARF - is the cheapest and most effective intervention in RHD; it cuts recurrences, slows progression and lets mild disease regress. [WHO 2024]`,`Drug of choice: **benzathine penicillin G (BPG) 1.2 million units deep IM every 4 weeks**; 600,000 units in small children (cut-off 20-30 kg depending on the guideline). [WHO 2024]`,`Shorten to **every 3 weeks** in those who have a breakthrough ARF despite full adherence; penicillin levels after BPG fall below protective concentrations before 4 weeks in many people. [AHA 2009]`,`**Intramuscular BPG is preferred over oral penicillin** - it prevents more recurrences and streptococcal throat infections, and adherence is visible on the injection card. [WHO 2024]`,`Injection technique: warm the vial to room temperature, give slowly into the ventrogluteal area or lateral thigh, and **reconstitute with 1% lidocaine** to reduce pain; observe for 15-30 minutes. [Australian RHD guideline 2020]`,`**Oral alternative** when injections are refused or impossible: **penicillin V 250 mg twice daily** - less effective, and adherence falls with time. [AHA 2009]`,`**Duration (AHA)**: ARF without carditis - 5 years after the last attack or until age 21; carditis without residual disease - 10 years or until 21; **residual valve disease - 10 years or until age 40**, whichever is longer, sometimes lifelong. [AHA 2009]`,`**Indian practice (WHO 2004, IAP)**: no carditis - 5 years or until 18; mild or healed carditis - 10 years or until 25; **moderate-severe valve disease or after valve surgery - lifelong**. [WHO TRS 923; IAP 2008]`,`**Continue prophylaxis after PTMC or valve replacement** - the procedure does not stop the rheumatic process and the other valves remain at risk. [WHO TRS 923]`,`Run it as a **register with recall**: injection card, due-date reminders by ASHA or ANM, injection at the nearest Ayushman Arogya Mandir or PHC; at least **80% of scheduled doses** received is the adherence target. [Australian RHD guideline 2020]`]},{heading:`Management in the OPD: AF, anticoagulation and valve care`,points:[`Anticoagulate **AF with clinically significant rheumatic MS regardless of the CHA2DS2-VA score** - the score was never validated here and embolic risk is very high. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**INVICTUS (2022)**: 4565 patients with RHD and AF (mean age 50, 72% women, mostly with mitral stenosis, many from India) - **rivaroxaban was inferior to VKA**, with more deaths and vascular events and no less major bleeding. [INVICTUS 2022]`,`So use a **VKA (warfarin or acenocoumarol), target INR 2.0-3.0**, for AF with rheumatic MS; ESC/EACTS 2025 makes DOACs contraindicated when the rheumatic **mitral valve area is 2 cm² or less**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`In RHD with AF but **no significant MS** (pure MR or AR), DOACs are acceptable under ESC rules, but rheumatic evidence is thin and many Indian units still prefer a VKA. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`A VKA is also indicated for **LA thrombus** and **prior systemic embolism**, and should be considered in sinus rhythm with severe MS and dense spontaneous echo contrast or an LA over 50 mm. [ESC/EACTS 2021]`,`Starting a VKA: **warfarin 2-5 mg or acenocoumarol 1-2 mg daily**, INR every 3-7 days until stable, then every 4 weeks; keep green leafy vegetable intake steady and check every new drug for interactions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Rate control**: metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg once daily or bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg first, add **digoxin 0.125-0.25 mg** if needed; diltiazem if beta-blockers are contraindicated. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Loop diuretic** (furosemide 20-40 mg once or twice daily) and salt restriction for congestion - but drugs never remove a mechanical valve lesion, so refer for PTMC or surgery on time. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Every visit: **dental hygiene** review, influenza and pneumococcal vaccination, and **contraception and pregnancy planning** for women. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: pulmonary oedema, new AF with fast rate and symptoms, stroke or limb embolism, suspected IE, recurrent ARF with carditis, massive haemoptysis, syncope, or major bleeding on a VKA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary oedema in MS**: sit up, oxygen if SpO2 under 90%, **IV furosemide 40-80 mg**, and **slow the heart** (IV metoprolol 2.5-5 mg slowly, repeated, if BP allows) - tachycardia shortens diastolic filling and raises LA pressure. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**AF with fast rate**: rate control plus heparin, bridging to a VKA; cardioversion only after TOE excludes LA thrombus or after 3 weeks of therapeutic INR. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Recurrent ARF**: BPG at diagnosis, echo, **aspirin 50-60 mg/kg/day in 4 divided doses** (adults about 4-8 g/day) or **naproxen 10-20 mg/kg/day** for arthritis; treat heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe carditis with heart failure**: prednisolone 1-2 mg/kg/day (maximum 80 mg) is often used but has no proven long-term benefit; urgent surgery if a chord or leaflet ruptures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever with a murmur**: three blood culture sets, echo, then empirical antibiotics and early surgical review - see the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Massive haemoptysis in MS**: sit up, lower LA pressure with diuretic and rate control, correct over-anticoagulation carefully, and arrange urgent PTMC. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Monitor**: daily weight, intake-output, sodium, potassium and creatinine on IV diuretics, and daily INR while bridging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Penicillin allergy label**: true IgE allergy is rare - **under 5% of labelled people** are allergic on testing - so refer for allergy assessment and delabelling rather than abandoning BPG. [WHO 2024]`,`With BPG, allergic reactions occur in about **3% of patients** and anaphylaxis in about **0.2%**; keep adrenaline 0.5 mg IM ready wherever BPG is given. [IRFSG, Lancet 1991]`,`**Confirmed penicillin allergy**: **erythromycin 250 mg twice daily** (watch QT and interactions with warfarin and amiodarone); sulfadiazine 1 g daily is an alternative where available. [AHA 2009]`,`**Severe valve disease**: rare sudden collapse soon after BPG in severe MS, AS or pulmonary hypertension is probably a vasovagal reaction on a fixed cardiac output - give after a meal, lying down, observe, or consider oral penicillin. [AHA 2022 advisory]`,`**Pregnancy**: continue BPG (safe); women with **MVA under 1.5 cm²** should have PTMC before conception; warfarin over 5 mg/day in the first trimester risks embryopathy - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Contraception**: combined oestrogen pills are contraindicated in complicated valve disease (AF, pulmonary hypertension, prior endocarditis); offer an IUCD, implant or progestogen-only method. [WHO MEC 2015]`,`**Endocarditis prophylaxis**: native RHD is **intermediate risk** - not routinely given; after valve replacement or repair with prosthetic material it is high risk - **amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes** before dental procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`A patient on **long-term penicillin** carries penicillin-resistant oral streptococci - for dental prophylaxis use another class, such as **azithromycin 500 mg** or doxycycline 100 mg. [AHA 2021 IE statement]`,`**Older adults**: prophylaxis can usually stop after 40 if no recurrence, but continue in those with heavy exposure to children (teachers, mothers of young children, health workers). [AHA 2009]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`Complications: **heart failure**, **AF**, **stroke and systemic embolism**, pulmonary hypertension, **infective endocarditis**, ARF recurrences, pregnancy decompensation and complications of anticoagulation and prosthetic valves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**REMEDY 2-year follow-up**: about **17% died** despite a median age near 30, mostly from heart failure; mortality was higher in lower-income countries and with severe disease, heart failure, pulmonary hypertension and AF. [REMEDY 2016]`,`Adherent prophylaxis lets a good proportion of **mild RHD regress**; non-adherence is the main reason mild disease becomes severe. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Follow-up by severity**: severe RHD - specialist review every 3-6 months; moderate - every 6 months with yearly echo; mild - yearly review with echo every 1-3 years. [Australian RHD guideline 2020]`,`At every visit check **BPG dates and adherence**, NYHA class, pulse rhythm, BP, weight, signs of heart failure or IE, the **INR log**, dental status and pregnancy plans. [Australian RHD guideline 2020]`,`On a VKA, check **INR every 4 weeks** once stable and aim for time in therapeutic range of **65-70% or more**; poor control is an indication to review adherence and diet. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Refer to a cardiologist**: any symptoms, moderate or severe lesions, new AF, embolism, PASP over 50 mmHg, LV dilatation or dysfunction, and every woman planning pregnancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Prevention, screening and the RHD programme`,points:[`**Primordial**: less crowding, better housing and nutrition, and poverty reduction - the decline of RHD in rich countries preceded penicillin. [WHO 2024]`,`**Primary prevention**: treat GAS pharyngitis within **9 days** of onset - **BPG 1.2 million units IM once**, or **amoxicillin 50 mg/kg once daily (max 1 g) for 10 days**, or penicillin V for 10 days. [AHA 2009]`,`Penicillin-allergic primary treatment: **azithromycin 12 mg/kg (max 500 mg) once daily for 5 days**, or cephalexin for 10 days if the allergy is not anaphylactic. [AHA 2009]`,`**Secondary prevention** through a **register-based BPG programme** - find, register, inject every 4 weeks, follow up - is the core of RHD control. [WHO 2024]`,`**Tertiary prevention**: timely PTMC and valve surgery (covered under **PM-JAY** packages), anticoagulation clinics and post-operative follow-up. [NHA PM-JAY]`,`**ICMR Jai Vigyan Mission (2001-2010)** ran community RF/RHD control with registries at several centres, including Vellore, and showed control can be sustained within primary care. [ICMR Jai Vigyan report 2010]`,`**RBSK** screens children aged 0-18 years for the '4 Ds' and lists **rheumatic heart disease** among its conditions; screen-positive children go to the District Early Intervention Centre for echo. [RBSK 2013]`,`**Echo screening** of schoolchildren and of pregnant women with handheld devices finds latent RHD, but only helps if it is linked to a prophylaxis register. [WHF 2023]`,`**BPG is on India's NLEM 2022** and must be stocked at PHC level; global BPG shortages have repeatedly broken programmes - report stock-outs. [NLEM 2022]`,`There is **no licensed GAS vaccine** yet; candidate M-protein and conserved-antigen vaccines are in trials. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**WHO 2024 guideline** on prevention and diagnosis of RF/RHD: endorses the 2015 Jones criteria, echo (including handheld) in diagnosis, IM BPG over oral antibiotics, and addresses latent RHD and penicillin allergy. [WHO 2024]`,`**WHF 2023 echo criteria** (published 2024): a stage A-D system based on risk of progression replaces 'borderline' and 'definite' RHD, with weight-based jet lengths for children. [WHF 2023]`,`**GOAL 2022** established secondary prophylaxis for latent RHD in children and adolescents (progression 0.8% vs 8.2%). [GOAL 2022]`,`**INVICTUS 2022** changed practice: VKA, not rivaroxaban, for RHD-associated AF; ESC 2024 AF and ACC/AHA 2023 AF advise against DOACs in moderate-severe MS. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** defines the DOAC contraindication by valve area - **rheumatic MS with MVA 2 cm² or less** - with individual decisions above that. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis**: native RHD is intermediate risk - routine dental prophylaxis is not recommended; clindamycin is dropped as a prophylactic alternative. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**AHA 2022 presidential advisory** on BPG reactions in severe RHD: counsel, give after food and lying down, and consider oral penicillin in the sickest patients. [AHA 2022 advisory]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva favourite: **'India is a high-risk population'** - so monoarthritis and polyarthralgia count as major criteria and fever of 38°C and ESR of 30 mm/h as minor. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`**Acute carditis gives regurgitation**; stenosis needs years of scarring - severe MS in a teenager is **juvenile MS**, characteristic of India. [Harrison 22e]`,`Durations to recite: **5 years or 21, 10 years or 21, 10 years or 40 (or lifelong)** - and note that Indian practice uses 18, 25 and lifelong. [AHA 2009]`,`Common mistake: **stopping BPG after PTMC or valve replacement**. [WHO TRS 923]`,`Common mistake: using the **CHA2DS2-VA score** to decide anticoagulation in AF with mitral stenosis - every such patient needs a VKA. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing ARF on a **raised ASO alone** - it only proves a recent streptococcal infection. [AHA 2015 Jones]`,`Common mistake: giving **amoxicillin dental prophylaxis** to a patient on long-term penicillin - choose another class. [AHA 2021 IE statement]`,`Named lines: **Carey Coombs murmur** (soft mid-diastolic murmur of acute mitral valvulitis) versus **Austin Flint murmur** (AR); **Aschoff body** with Anitschkow cells. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in RHD: prophylaxis, acute rheumatic fever and AF`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Benzathine penicillin G (secondary prophylaxis)`,`1.2 million units deep IM (600,000 units in small children, cut-off 20-30 kg)`,`Every 4 weeks; every 3 weeks after breakthrough ARF; duration per severity`,`Injection pain (use 1% lidocaine diluent); allergy 3%, anaphylaxis 0.2%; vasovagal collapse in severe valve disease`],[`Penicillin V`,`250 mg oral twice daily`,`Same duration as BPG when injections impossible`,`Less effective than BPG; adherence falls`],[`Erythromycin`,`250 mg oral twice daily`,`Same duration; for confirmed penicillin allergy`,`GI upset, QT prolongation; raises INR with warfarin`],[`Sulfadiazine`,`1 g oral once daily (0.5 g if 27 kg or less)`,`Same duration; limited availability in India`,`Rash, blood dyscrasias; avoid near term (kernicterus)`],[`Benzathine penicillin G (primary - GAS pharyngitis)`,`1.2 million units IM once (600,000 units under 27 kg)`,`Single dose within 9 days of sore throat`,`As above`],[`Amoxicillin (primary)`,`50 mg/kg oral once daily, maximum 1 g`,`10 days`,`Rash (especially with EBV infection), diarrhoea`],[`Azithromycin (primary, penicillin allergy)`,`12 mg/kg oral once daily, maximum 500 mg`,`5 days`,`QT prolongation, GI upset; macrolide resistance`],[`Aspirin (ARF arthritis)`,`50-60 mg/kg/day oral in 4 divided doses (adults about 4-8 g/day)`,`Until arthritis settles (1-2 weeks), then taper`,`Tinnitus (salicylism), gastritis; Reye syndrome with influenza or varicella`],[`Naproxen (ARF arthritis)`,`10-20 mg/kg/day oral in 2 doses (adults 250-500 mg twice daily)`,`Until arthritis settles`,`GI bleeding, renal impairment`],[`Prednisolone (severe carditis)`,`1-2 mg/kg/day oral, maximum 80 mg`,`1-3 weeks, then taper`,`No proven long-term benefit; hyperglycaemia, fluid retention`],[`Warfarin / acenocoumarol (AF with MS, LA thrombus, embolism)`,`Warfarin 2-5 mg or acenocoumarol 1-2 mg oral daily, titrated`,`Target INR 2.0-3.0, long term`,`Bleeding; teratogenic in first trimester; many drug and food interactions`],[`Metoprolol succinate (rate control)`,`25-200 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate every 1-2 weeks to symptom control`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm; caution in decompensated failure`]]},{heading:`2015 revised Jones criteria`,columns:[`Criterion`,`Low-risk population`,`Moderate/high-risk population (India)`],rows:[[`Major: carditis`,`Clinical and/or subclinical (echo)`,`Clinical and/or subclinical (echo)`],[`Major: arthritis`,`Polyarthritis only`,`Monoarthritis, polyarthritis or polyarthralgia`],[`Major: chorea, erythema marginatum, subcutaneous nodules`,`Yes`,`Yes`],[`Minor: joints`,`Polyarthralgia`,`Monoarthralgia`],[`Minor: fever`,`38.5°C or more`,`38.0°C or more`],[`Minor: inflammatory markers`,`ESR 60 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more`,`ESR 30 mm/h or more and/or CRP 3.0 mg/dL or more`],[`Minor: ECG`,`Prolonged PR for age (unless carditis counted)`,`Prolonged PR for age (unless carditis counted)`],[`Initial ARF`,`2 major, or 1 major + 2 minor, plus GAS evidence`,`2 major, or 1 major + 2 minor, plus GAS evidence`],[`Recurrent ARF`,`2 major, 1 major + 2 minor, or 3 minor, plus GAS evidence`,`2 major, 1 major + 2 minor, or 3 minor, plus GAS evidence`]]},{heading:`Duration of secondary prophylaxis`,columns:[`Category`,`AHA 2009`,`WHO 2004 / IAP 2008 (Indian practice)`],rows:[[`ARF without carditis`,`5 years after last attack or until age 21, whichever longer`,`5 years after last attack or until age 18, whichever longer`],[`Carditis without residual valve disease (or mild, healed)`,`10 years or until age 21, whichever longer`,`10 years or until age 25, whichever longer`],[`Carditis with persistent valve disease`,`10 years or until age 40, whichever longer; sometimes lifelong`,`Lifelong for moderate or severe disease`],[`After PTMC or valve surgery`,`Continue according to residual disease`,`Lifelong`],[`Adults beyond the end date with heavy child contact`,`Consider continuing (teachers, parents, health workers)`,`Individual decision`]]},{heading:`WHF 2023 echocardiographic stages of RHD`,columns:[`Stage`,`Echo definition`,`What it means for care`],rows:[[`A - early (formerly borderline)`,`Minimum echo criteria met: pathological regurgitation without rheumatic morphology`,`Repeat echo; prophylaxis individualised (favoured in children and adolescents after GOAL)`],[`B - mild RHD`,`Pathological regurgitation plus rheumatic morphological features`,`Secondary prophylaxis, yearly review, periodic echo`],[`C - moderate to severe`,`Established moderate or severe valve disease, including any mitral stenosis`,`Prophylaxis, cardiology follow-up, plan timing of intervention`],[`D - with complications`,`Established valve disease plus heart failure, arrhythmia, stroke or need for surgery`,`Prophylaxis, anticoagulation where indicated, PTMC or surgery`]]},{heading:`Is it rheumatic? Differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue that favours it`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Mitral valve prolapse`,`Mid-systolic click, late systolic murmur`,`Echo: leaflet displacement over 2 mm, no commissural fusion`],[`Physiological regurgitation / flow murmur`,`Anaemia, pregnancy, fever; soft ejection murmur`,`Echo: normal valves, short low-velocity jets`],[`Bicuspid aortic valve`,`Ejection click, young patient, normal mitral valve`,`Echo: two cusps, aortic dilatation`],[`Infective endocarditis`,`Fever, splenomegaly, embolic phenomena`,`Blood cultures, vegetation on echo`],[`Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis`,`Non-migratory, persistent arthritis, latency under 10 days, poor aspirin response`,`No carditis; echo follow-up`],[`Chikungunya or rheumatoid arthritis`,`Symmetrical small-joint arthritis, rash, epidemic setting`,`Chikungunya IgM/PCR; RF and anti-CCP`],[`Degenerative MS (annular calcification)`,`Elderly, CKD, no opening snap`,`Echo: annular calcification, no commissural fusion`],[`Left atrial myxoma`,`Positional symptoms, tumour plop, fever, weight loss`,`Echo: mobile LA mass on the septum`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness at rest, orthopnoea or pink frothy sputum - pulmonary oedema, usually MS with fast AF.`,`New AF with a fast rate, or AF with rheumatic MS not on a VKA.`,`Stroke, TIA, sudden visual loss or a cold pale limb - systemic embolism.`,`Fever for more than a week, or fever with a new or changing murmur, splinter haemorrhages or splenomegaly - endocarditis.`,`Fever with arthritis or chorea and a new murmur - recurrent ARF with carditis.`,`Haemoptysis, exertional syncope or exertional chest pain.`,`Pregnant woman with RHD and any symptoms or a severe stenotic lesion.`,`Bleeding with a high INR on warfarin or acenocoumarol.`],pearls:[`**5-21, 10-21, 10-40**: years of BPG for no carditis, healed carditis and residual valve disease (AHA); Indian practice 18, 25 and lifelong.`,`Jones majors as **J-O-N-E-S**: Joints, O (heart shape) for carditis, Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea.`,`**India is high-risk**: monoarthritis and polyarthralgia are majors, subclinical carditis counts - echo every suspected ARF.`,`**Chorea and indolent carditis** need no proof of streptococcal infection.`,`Acute rheumatic carditis means **regurgitation**; stenosis takes years.`,`**INVICTUS**: in RHD with AF, VKA beats rivaroxaban - fewer deaths, no more bleeding.`,`**GOAL**: BPG cut latent RHD progression from 8.2% to 0.8% (NNT 13).`,`**Never stop BPG** after PTMC or valve replacement.`,`On long-term penicillin, dental prophylaxis must be a **non-penicillin**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-q1`,stem:`A 19-year-old from rural Bihar has fever of 38.2°C and pain in several joints without swelling for 5 days. ESR is 42 mm/h and ASO is raised. There is no murmur, but echo shows a pansystolic MR jet of 2.4 cm at 3.4 m/s in two views, with anterior mitral leaflet thickening. Applying the 2015 Jones criteria, what is the diagnosis?`,options:[`Possible ARF only, because carditis must be audible to count as a major criterion`,`Definite initial ARF: subclinical carditis and polyarthralgia are both major criteria in a high-risk population`,`Not ARF, because polyarthralgia is only a minor criterion in every population`,`Recurrent ARF, because three minor criteria are present`],answer:1,explanation:`In moderate/high-risk populations such as India, polyarthralgia counts as a major criterion and echo-detected (subclinical) carditis also counts as a major criterion, so two majors plus streptococcal evidence give definite initial ARF. Audible carditis is not required since 2015, polyarthralgia is a minor criterion only in low-risk populations, and the 'three minor' rule applies to recurrences, not first attacks.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old woman had ARF with carditis at 14 and now has moderate rheumatic mitral regurgitation. She has received BPG every 4 weeks since then and asks when she can stop. Using the AHA scheme, what is the correct answer?`,options:[`Stop now, because 10 years have passed since the last attack`,`Continue until age 21 only`,`Continue for at least 10 years after the last attack or until age 40, whichever is longer`,`Stop now and switch to yearly echo surveillance`],answer:2,explanation:`Carditis with persistent valve disease needs prophylaxis for 10 years after the last attack or until age 40, whichever is longer, and sometimes lifelong; Indian (WHO/IAP) practice is lifelong for moderate-severe disease. Ten years alone, or age 21, applies only when there is no residual valve disease, and echo surveillance does not replace protection from new streptococcal infections.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-q3`,stem:`A 42-year-old man with rheumatic mitral stenosis (MVA 1.3 cm²) has permanent AF. His CHA2DS2-VA score is 0 and he asks for a tablet that does not need blood tests. What is the best anticoagulant plan?`,options:[`No anticoagulant, because his CHA2DS2-VA score is 0`,`Rivaroxaban 20 mg once daily, which was non-inferior to VKA in INVICTUS`,`Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily, because it needs no monitoring`,`Warfarin or acenocoumarol to a target INR of 2.0-3.0`],answer:3,explanation:`AF with clinically significant rheumatic MS needs anticoagulation whatever the score, and INVICTUS showed rivaroxaban was inferior to VKA in RHD-associated AF, with more deaths and no less bleeding. ESC 2024 AF and ESC/EACTS 2025 advise against DOACs in rheumatic MS (MVA 2 cm² or less), so rivaroxaban and dabigatran are wrong, and withholding anticoagulation because of a low score is a classic error.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-q4`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve, on BPG every 4 weeks and warfarin, needs a dental extraction. She is not allergic to penicillin. Which endocarditis prophylaxis is most appropriate?`,options:[`Azithromycin 500 mg orally 30-60 minutes before the procedure`,`Amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before the procedure`,`No prophylaxis, because her monthly BPG already covers it`,`Clindamycin 600 mg orally 30-60 minutes before the procedure`],answer:0,explanation:`A prosthetic valve makes her high risk, so prophylaxis is needed. Long-term penicillin selects penicillin-resistant viridans streptococci in the mouth, so a drug from another class such as azithromycin is chosen. Amoxicillin would face those resistant organisms, BPG levels are not bactericidal prophylaxis, and clindamycin has been dropped by AHA 2021 and ESC 2023 because of C. difficile risk.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-q5`,stem:`On school echo screening, a 12-year-old with no murmur has a pansystolic MR jet of 2.2 cm at 3.2 m/s in two views, with chordal thickening and restricted posterior leaflet motion. Under WHF 2023, what stage and action fit best?`,options:[`Stage A - discharge, as there is no murmur`,`Stage B (mild RHD) - start BPG every 4 weeks and arrange periodic echo`,`Stage C - refer for PTMC`,`Stage D - start warfarin`],answer:1,explanation:`Pathological MR plus rheumatic morphological features is mild RHD, WHF 2023 stage B, and GOAL showed 4-weekly BPG cut progression from 8.2% to 0.8% in such children. Stage A lacks morphological features and is still followed, not discharged. Stage C needs moderate-severe disease and PTMC treats stenosis, not MR. Stage D implies complications, and warfarin has no role without AF or thrombus.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the diagnosis and secondary prophylaxis of rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease in adults. Add a note on anticoagulation in RHD with AF and on the strategy to control RHD in India.`,openingLines:[`Rheumatic heart disease is chronic, mainly mitral, valve damage left by acute rheumatic fever, the autoimmune sequel of group A streptococcal pharyngitis.`,`India carries about a quarter of the global burden; echo screening finds about 20 per 1000 schoolchildren affected, most of them silent, and secondary prophylaxis with BPG is the most effective control measure.`],answer:[{heading:`Burden and pathogenesis`,points:[`Global 40 million cases, 300,000 deaths a year; India the largest share; REMEDY median age 28, two-thirds women.`,`Molecular mimicry between M protein and cardiac myosin and valve endothelium; T cell valvulitis; acute MR then chronic fusion and stenosis.`,`Mitral alone two-thirds, mitral plus aortic a quarter.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis of ARF: 2015 Jones (high-risk set for India)`,points:[`Evidence of GAS plus 2 major or 1 major + 2 minor; recurrence also 3 minor.`,`Majors: carditis including subclinical, monoarthritis/polyarthritis/polyarthralgia, chorea, erythema marginatum, nodules.`,`Minors: monoarthralgia, fever 38°C or more, ESR 30 or more / CRP 3 mg/dL or more, prolonged PR.`,`Exceptions: chorea and indolent carditis.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis of RHD: echo and WHF 2023`,points:[`Pathological MR: 2 views, 2 cm or more, 3 m/s or more, pansystolic; pathological AR: 2 views, 1 cm or more, 3 m/s or more, pandiastolic.`,`Morphology: anterior leaflet and chordal thickening, restricted motion.`,`Stages A (early), B (mild), C (moderate-severe), D (with complications).`]},{heading:`Secondary prophylaxis`,points:[`BPG 1.2 million units deep IM every 4 weeks (3 weeks after breakthrough); penicillin V 250 mg BD; erythromycin 250 mg BD if allergic.`,`Duration: 5 y/21, 10 y/21, 10 y/40 or lifelong (AHA); Indian practice 18, 25, lifelong; continue after PTMC or surgery.`,`Delabel penicillin allergy; in severe valve disease give after food, lying down, or consider oral penicillin.`]},{heading:`Anticoagulation in RHD with AF`,points:[`AF with rheumatic MS: VKA to INR 2.0-3.0 regardless of score.`,`INVICTUS 2022: rivaroxaban inferior to VKA; ESC/EACTS 2025 contraindicates DOACs when MVA is 2 cm² or less.`]},{heading:`Control strategy in India`,points:[`Primordial: housing and crowding; primary: treat sore throat (BPG once or amoxicillin 10 days).`,`Secondary: register-based BPG with ASHA recall at Ayushman Arogya Mandir; BPG in NLEM.`,`Tertiary: PTMC and valve surgery under PM-JAY; RBSK child screening; echo screening linked to registers (WHO 2024, WHA71.14).`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of 2015 Jones criteria: low-risk versus moderate/high-risk populations`,`Flow chart: sore throat to ARF to RHD, with primordial, primary, secondary and tertiary prevention marked`,`Table of prophylaxis durations (AHA versus Indian practice)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, burden and pathogenesis`,marks:1},{part:`Jones criteria and echo diagnosis`,marks:3},{part:`Secondary prophylaxis: drug, dose, interval, duration`,marks:3},{part:`Anticoagulation in RHD AF`,marks:1},{part:`Control strategy in India`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Jones 2015`,`WHF 2023`,`BPG`,`INVICTUS`,`GOAL`,`RBSK`,`PM-JAY`,`secondary prophylaxis`]},{id:`cardiovascular-rheumatic-heart-disease-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`core`,question:`Write a short note on secondary prophylaxis of rheumatic fever.`,openingLines:[`Secondary prophylaxis is the regular administration of an antibiotic to a person with previous ARF or RHD to prevent new streptococcal infections and so recurrent ARF.`,`It is the most cost-effective intervention in RHD: it prevents recurrences, slows progression and allows mild valve disease to regress.`],answer:[{heading:`Regimens`,points:[`Benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units deep IM every 4 weeks (600,000 units in small children); every 3 weeks after a breakthrough attack.`,`Oral penicillin V 250 mg twice daily if injections impossible; erythromycin 250 mg twice daily if truly allergic.`]},{heading:`Duration`,points:[`No carditis: 5 years or until 21; healed carditis: 10 years or until 21; residual disease: 10 years or until 40, sometimes lifelong (AHA 2009).`,`Indian practice: 18, 25 and lifelong; lifelong after valve surgery; continue after PTMC.`]},{heading:`Practical issues`,points:[`Lidocaine diluent and slow injection reduce pain; observe 15-30 minutes with adrenaline at hand.`,`Delabel false penicillin allergy; in severe valve disease give after food, lying down, or consider oral penicillin.`,`Register, injection card, ASHA reminders; target 80% or more of doses.`]},{heading:`Evidence and updates`,points:[`GOAL 2022: BPG in latent RHD cut progression from 8.2% to 0.8%.`,`WHO 2024 prefers IM BPG over oral antibiotics.`]}],mustDraw:[`Duration table (AHA versus Indian practice)`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and rationale`,marks:1},{part:`Regimens and doses`,marks:2},{part:`Duration`,marks:1},{part:`Practical issues and evidence`,marks:1}],keywords:[`benzathine penicillin`,`secondary prophylaxis`,`duration`,`penicillin allergy`,`GOAL`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - chapters on acute rheumatic fever and valvular heart disease`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease`,`WHO guideline on the prevention and diagnosis of rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease, World Health Organization, 2024`,`Rwebembera J et al. 2023 World Heart Federation guidelines for the echocardiographic diagnosis of rheumatic heart disease. Nat Rev Cardiol 2024`,`Gewitz MH et al. Revision of the Jones criteria for the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever in the era of Doppler echocardiography. AHA Scientific Statement, Circulation 2015`,`Connolly SJ et al. Rivaroxaban in rheumatic heart disease-associated atrial fibrillation (INVICTUS). N Engl J Med 2022; Beaton A et al. Secondary antibiotic prophylaxis for latent rheumatic heart disease (GOAL). N Engl J Med 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation; 2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease`,`Consensus guidelines on pediatric acute rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease, IAP Cardiology Chapter, Indian Pediatrics 2008; ICMR Jai Vigyan Mission report on RF/RHD 2001-2010`]}),o.push({id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis`,title:`Mitral stenosis`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Mitral stenosis (MS)** is obstruction to left ventricular inflow from narrowing of the mitral orifice - almost always **rheumatic commissural fusion** in India - becoming **clinically significant at a valve area of 1.5 cm² or less**; it causes a raised left atrial pressure, AF, embolism and pulmonary hypertension, and is treated by **percutaneous transmitral commissurotomy (PTMC)** when the valve is suitable (**Wilkins score 8 or less**, no LA thrombus, no more than mild MR), with a **VKA** for AF.`,keywords:[`MS`,`rheumatic mitral stenosis`,`mitral valve area`,`MVA`,`opening snap`,`A2-OS interval`,`mid-diastolic murmur`,`presystolic accentuation`,`Wilkins score`,`PTMC`,`BMV`,`PBMV`,`Inoue balloon`,`closed mitral valvotomy`,`juvenile mitral stenosis`,`Lutembacher syndrome`,`Ortner syndrome`,`pressure half-time`,`mitral annular calcification`,`acenocoumarol`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Mitral stenosis** is narrowing of the mitral orifice that obstructs flow from left atrium to left ventricle in diastole; the **normal mitral valve area is 4-6 cm²**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Symptoms usually begin when the area falls below about **2.5 cm² on exertion** and **1.5 cm² at rest**; MS is **clinically significant at 1.5 cm² or less** and very severe at 1.0 cm² or less. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Rheumatic heart disease causes over 90%** of MS worldwide; about two-thirds of rheumatic MS patients are **women**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In high-income countries MS presents in the **5th-6th decade** after a long latent period; in India it often presents in the **2nd-3rd decade**, with rapid progression. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Juvenile MS** - severe MS before age 20 - is characteristic of India and other endemic regions, and these pliable valves are usually ideal for PTMC. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In the 14-country REMEDY registry of RHD (median age 28), mitral stenosis alone or with MR was among the commonest lesions and AF was present in about a fifth. [REMEDY 2015]`,`**Degenerative MS** from mitral annular calcification is rising in the elderly and in CKD, but has a different anatomy and management. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`India performs one of the largest volumes of **PTMC (balloon mitral valvotomy)** in the world, much of it in young adults and pregnant women. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`**Rheumatic**: commissural fusion, leaflet thickening, chordal fusion and shortening - the only cause in which commissures fuse, which is why balloon splitting works. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Degenerative (calcific)**: mitral annular calcification extending into the leaflet bases, with open commissures - elderly, CKD, diabetes, hypertension. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Congenital**: parachute mitral valve (single papillary muscle), supravalvular ring, Shone complex (with coarctation and subaortic stenosis). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Rare**: radiation, carcinoid (left-sided only with a shunt or lung metastases), SLE, rheumatoid nodules, mucopolysaccharidoses, ergot or serotoninergic drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional mimics** of MS: left atrial myxoma, large vegetation or thrombus, cor triatriatum and a prosthetic valve with thrombus or pannus. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lutembacher syndrome** is an atrial septal defect with (usually rheumatic) mitral stenosis. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs and drugs make sense`,points:[`A narrowed orifice needs a **pressure gradient** to maintain flow; the gradient rises with the **square of flow** and with **shorter diastole**, so exercise, fever, pregnancy, anaemia and fast AF all raise LA pressure abruptly. [Harrison 22e]`,`Raised **LA pressure** passes back to pulmonary veins and capillaries - when it exceeds about 25 mmHg plasma leaks out: **exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema**. [Harrison 22e]`,`This is why **heart rate control** is the key drug effect: beta-blockers lengthen diastole, lowering the gradient and LA pressure at the same flow. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension** is first passive (backward LA pressure), then **reactive** (pulmonary arteriolar vasoconstriction and remodelling - the 'second stenosis'), causing RV hypertrophy, functional TR and right heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **left atrium dilates** and its wall is inflamed and fibrosed - leading to **AF**, stasis in the appendage and **thrombus**; loss of atrial kick and fast rates make AF poorly tolerated. [Braunwald 12e]`,`The **left ventricle is protected** (underfilled): LV size and EF are usually normal, and cardiac output is fixed and low - fatigue, cold extremities and malar flush. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loud S1**: the gradient keeps leaflets wide open until ventricular systole slams them shut from a wide excursion; **opening snap**: the domed anterior leaflet stops abruptly as it opens. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **higher the LA pressure, the earlier the valve opens** after aortic closure - so a **short A2-OS interval** means severe MS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Haemoptysis** comes from rupture of dilated bronchial veins (pulmonary apoplexy), pulmonary oedema, pulmonary infarction or bronchitis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Severity by valve area**: progressive MS - over 1.5 cm²; **clinically significant (severe) - 1.5 cm² or less**; **very severe - 1.0 cm² or less**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Pressure half-time**: under 150 ms in progressive MS, **150 ms or more** in severe and **220 ms or more** in very severe MS (MVA = 220 / PHT). [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Mean gradient** is supportive only, because it depends on heart rate and flow: typically under 5 mmHg mild, 5-10 moderate, **over 10 mmHg severe** at a normal heart rate. [EACVI 2009]`,`**PASP**: under 30 mmHg in mild, 30-50 moderate and **over 50 mmHg** severe pulmonary hypertension - over 50 at rest is an indication to intervene even without symptoms. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA stages**: A at risk (mild diastolic doming), B progressive, **C asymptomatic severe**, **D symptomatic severe**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Wilkins (MGH) echo score** grades leaflet mobility, thickening, calcification and subvalvular thickening 1-4 each (total 4-16); **8 or less predicts a good PTMC result**. [Wilkins 1988]`,`NYHA functional class (I-IV) is part of every severity statement, because symptoms and anatomy together decide intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Exertional breathlessness** is the commonest symptom, progressing to orthopnoea, PND and pulmonary oedema; patients often limit activity unconsciously. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Precipitants** of first decompensation: **AF onset, pregnancy (second trimester and peripartum), fever, anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, exercise** and sexual activity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpitations** from AF or atrial ectopy; **fatigue** from low fixed cardiac output. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Haemoptysis** - sudden, sometimes large (pulmonary apoplexy), pink frothy sputum in oedema, or blood-streaked sputum with winter bronchitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Embolic events** - stroke, TIA, limb or mesenteric ischaemia - may be the first presentation, even in sinus rhythm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoarseness (Ortner syndrome)** from compression of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve by a dilated pulmonary artery and LA; **dysphagia** from a giant LA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right heart symptoms** late: ankle swelling, abdominal fullness, right upper quadrant pain from hepatic congestion. [Harrison 22e]`,`Ask about rheumatic fever, **BPG injections**, previous PTMC or surgery, anticoagulant use and INR control, and pregnancy plans. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Mitral facies (malar flush)**: purple-red cheeks from low output and peripheral vasoconstriction in severe MS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulse**: low volume; irregularly irregular with a pulse deficit in AF. **JVP**: prominent a wave with pulmonary hypertension (lost in AF), giant v wave with TR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Apex**: not displaced, **tapping** (palpable S1); **left parasternal heave** (RV) and **palpable P2** mean pulmonary hypertension; a diastolic thrill at the apex in the left lateral position. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loud S1** (soft when leaflets calcify) and **opening snap** - high-pitched, best between apex and left sternal edge with the diaphragm, 40-120 ms after A2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mid-diastolic rumbling murmur** - low-pitched, best at the apex with the bell, patient in the **left lateral position** at end-expiration, after brief exercise; **presystolic accentuation** in sinus rhythm (lost in AF). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severity markers**: a **short A2-OS interval (under 80 ms)**, a **long murmur** (duration, not loudness), signs of pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Graham Steell murmur** (early diastolic murmur of pulmonary regurgitation) and the pansystolic murmur of **TR increasing with inspiration (Carvallo sign)** indicate severe pulmonary hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Silent MS**: murmur inaudible with very low output, fast AF, obesity, emphysema or severe pulmonary hypertension - echo every unexplained AF or pulmonary hypertension in a young adult. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Austin Flint murmur** (severe AR): mid-diastolic apical rumble without a loud S1 or opening snap, with collapsing pulse and an early diastolic murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carey Coombs murmur**: soft, short mid-diastolic murmur of acute rheumatic valvulitis - no opening snap, disappears as carditis settles. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Left atrial myxoma**: positional symptoms, syncope, a **tumour plop**, fever, weight loss and raised ESR; echo shows a mobile mass on the interatrial septum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flow murmurs** across a normal mitral valve: severe MR, VSD and PDA cause short mid-diastolic flow rumbles; ASD causes a tricuspid flow murmur. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Tricuspid stenosis**: murmur at the left lower sternal edge, louder on inspiration, with giant a waves - almost always alongside rheumatic MS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Opening snap versus mimics**: split S2 (at the base, widens on inspiration), **S3** (later, 120-160 ms, low-pitched, at the apex) and pericardial knock. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary pulmonary hypertension** or **mitral annular calcification MS** in the elderly: no commissural fusion on echo. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: **P mitrale** (broad notched P over 120 ms in II; deep terminal negative P in V1), AF, right axis deviation and RVH with pulmonary hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: LA enlargement - **straight left heart border**, **double density** on the right, **widened carina** and elevated left main bronchus; upper lobe diversion, **Kerley B lines**, enlarged pulmonary arteries; occasionally haemosiderosis or ossific nodules. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is diagnostic: **diastolic doming ('hockey-stick') anterior leaflet**, **'fish-mouth' orifice** on short axis, commissural fusion, LA size, PASP, TR and other valves. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**MVA by 2D/3D planimetry** is the reference; **pressure half-time** (220/PHT) is unreliable immediately after PTMC, with significant AR or reduced LV compliance. [EACVI 2009]`,`**TOE before PTMC or cardioversion** to exclude **LA and appendage thrombus**, and to assess commissures and MR severity. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Exercise (stress) echo** when symptoms and resting severity disagree: an exercise **mean gradient over 15 mmHg** or PASP over 60 mmHg supports intervention. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Cardiac catheterisation** only when non-invasive data are discordant; **coronary angiography** before surgery in men over 40, postmenopausal women and those with risk factors. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Baseline blood tests: haemoglobin, creatinine, electrolytes, **TSH** (AF), INR, and NT-proBNP when symptoms are unclear. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Choosing the intervention: PTMC or surgery`,points:[`**PTMC is the treatment of choice** in symptomatic clinically significant MS (**MVA 1.5 cm² or less**) with favourable anatomy - results equal open commissurotomy and the procedure can be repeated. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Contraindications to PTMC**: MVA over 1.5 cm², **LA thrombus**, **more than mild MR**, severe or bicommissural calcification, no commissural fusion, severe aortic or combined tricuspid disease needing surgery, and CAD needing bypass. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Unfavourable features** (not absolute): older age, previous commissurotomy, NYHA IV, permanent AF, severe pulmonary hypertension, **Wilkins score over 8**, very small MVA and severe TR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Asymptomatic** clinically significant MS: consider PTMC when there is high embolic risk (prior embolism, dense LA smoke, new AF) or high risk of decompensation (**PASP over 50 mmHg at rest**, planned pregnancy or major surgery). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Technique**: transseptal puncture and an **Inoue balloon** (or Indian Accura balloon, or the reusable metallic commissurotome) stepwise dilated under echo guidance. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Success**: MVA 1.5 cm² or more with no more than moderate MR; complications - severe MR (2-10%), tamponade, embolism, residual ASD, mortality under 1%. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Surgery** (open commissurotomy or **mitral valve replacement**) when PTMC is contraindicated or fails; **closed mitral valvotomy** is still done in resource-limited Indian centres for pliable valves without thrombus. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Valve choice in young Indians: **mechanical valves** (lifelong VKA) because bioprostheses degenerate fast in the young; bioprosthesis for women wanting pregnancy or where INR monitoring is impossible is a shared decision. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`Drugs relieve symptoms but **do not change the obstruction** - every symptomatic patient with MVA 1.5 cm² or less should be referred for intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Heart rate control**: **metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg once daily** or **bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg**; in sinus rhythm, **ivabradine 5-7.5 mg twice daily** if beta-blockers are not tolerated; diltiazem 30-60 mg three times daily as alternative. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Diuretic** for congestion: **furosemide 20-40 mg** once or twice daily, lowest effective dose; salt restriction. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**AF**: rate control (beta-blocker, add **digoxin 0.125-0.25 mg daily**) and **VKA (warfarin or acenocoumarol) to INR 2.0-3.0** - DOACs are contraindicated with rheumatic MVA 2 cm² or less. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**VKA also for** LA thrombus or prior embolism (any rhythm), and consider in sinus rhythm with dense spontaneous echo contrast or an LA over 50 mm. [ESC/EACTS 2021]`,`**Rhythm control**: cardioversion is worthwhile mainly after successful PTMC or surgery in recent-onset AF, after 3 weeks of therapeutic INR or TOE; amiodarone helps maintain sinus rhythm. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis with BPG 1.2 million units every 4 weeks** continues (lifelong in Indian practice for moderate-severe disease) - see the RHD topic. [WHO 2024]`,`Advise avoiding **sudden strenuous exertion** in severe MS, treat anaemia and fever early, vaccinate (influenza, pneumococcal) and maintain dental hygiene. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Women**: counsel before pregnancy; avoid combined oestrogen pills with AF, pulmonary hypertension or prior embolism - use IUCD or progestogen-only methods. [WHO MEC 2015]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: pulmonary oedema, fast AF with symptoms, embolism, massive haemoptysis, suspected endocarditis, syncope, or a pregnant woman in NYHA III-IV. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Acute pulmonary oedema**: sit up, oxygen to SpO2 94% or more, **IV furosemide 40-80 mg**, and **slow the heart** - IV metoprolol 2.5-5 mg over 2 minutes, repeat to 15 mg if BP allows; avoid pure vasodilators. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Fast AF**: IV beta-blocker or diltiazem, digoxin if hypotensive or LV function is reduced; **heparin** (UFH infusion or enoxaparin 1 mg/kg 12-hourly) bridging to a VKA. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Haemodynamically unstable AF**: urgent DC cardioversion with heparin cover. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock** from critical MS: **urgent PTMC** (or surgery) is lifesaving, including in pregnancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Embolic stroke**: standard stroke pathway (thrombolysis only if INR 1.7 or less), then restart anticoagulation timed by infarct size. [AHA/ASA 2019]`,`**Monitor**: urine output, daily weight, potassium and creatinine on IV diuretics, heart rate (target under 80-90 at rest), INR daily while bridging. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: a 30-50% rise in output and heart rate raises the gradient - decompensation peaks in the **second trimester and peripartum**; severe MS is **mWHO class IV**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`Women with **MVA under 1.5 cm²** should have **PTMC before conception**; in pregnancy use **metoprolol** (not atenolol) and diuretics for congestion, and anticoagulate AF, LA thrombus or prior embolism. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**PTMC during pregnancy** - preferably after 20 weeks with abdominal shielding - for NYHA III-IV or PASP over 50 mmHg despite drugs; vaginal delivery with epidural is preferred in most. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly with MAC-related MS**: no commissural fusion, so PTMC does not work; medical therapy first, surgery is high risk, and transcatheter valve-in-MAC is limited to expert centres. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Juvenile MS**: young age, rapid progression and pliable valves - PTMC gives excellent results; strict BPG adherence is essential. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Lutembacher syndrome**: the ASD decompresses the LA, so pulmonary oedema is less but RV volume overload is greater; PTMC with device ASD closure or surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Non-cardiac surgery**: in severe symptomatic MS consider PTMC before elevated-risk elective surgery; avoid tachycardia, fluid overload and hypotension peri-operatively. [ESC 2022 NCS]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: closed mitral valvotomy and acenocoumarol with point-of-care INR keep care affordable; PTMC and valve replacement are covered under PM-JAY. [NHA PM-JAY]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**AF** develops in about 30-40% of symptomatic patients and abruptly worsens symptoms. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic embolism** occurs in 10-20% of untreated MS - risk rises with age, AF, LA size, low output and dense spontaneous echo contrast; a third of emboli occur within a month of AF onset. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure**, functional TR, haemoptysis, recurrent chest infections and endocarditis (less common than in regurgitant lesions). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Natural history**: once significantly symptomatic, untreated 10-year survival is only **0-15%**; with severe pulmonary hypertension mean survival is under 3 years. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Causes of death in untreated MS: **heart failure** (about two-thirds), systemic embolism, pulmonary embolism and infection. [Braunwald 12e]`,`After successful PTMC, most patients remain free of reintervention for **many years**, but **restenosis** occurs, especially with high Wilkins scores; repeat PTMC is possible if anatomy allows. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Refer**: any symptomatic MS, MVA 1.5 cm² or less, PASP over 50 mmHg, new AF, embolism, and every woman with MS planning pregnancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Echo follow-up**: asymptomatic clinically significant MS **yearly**; progressive (MVA over 1.5 cm²) every **2-3 years** (ACC/AHA 3-5 years for MVA over 1.5 cm² that is stable). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`After PTMC: echo at baseline (24-72 hours after), then yearly; restenosis or new MR prompts specialist review. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Each visit: symptoms (NYHA), heart rate and rhythm, signs of pulmonary hypertension, **INR log**, BPG card, bleeding, dental status. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`On a VKA: INR every 4 weeks once stable; time in therapeutic range of **65-70% or more**; patient education on interactions. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Prevention**: secondary prophylaxis with BPG, prompt treatment of sore throat in the family, and RHD register enrolment at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: DOAC contraindication in rheumatic MS is now defined by valve area - **MVA 2 cm² or less** - with individual decisions above that (reflects INVICTUS). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**INVICTUS 2022**: rivaroxaban inferior to VKA in rheumatic AF (most had MS) - more deaths without less bleeding. [INVICTUS 2022]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: VKA for AF with moderate-severe MS; CHA2DS2-VA (sex removed) does not apply to rheumatic MS. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** keeps PTMC as first choice for suitable rheumatic MS and lists degenerative (MAC) MS separately, with transcatheter options only in expert heart valve centres. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020** staging (A-D) with MVA 1.5 cm² and PHT 150 ms as the severe cut-offs remains the standard US reference. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline**: pre-conception intervention for MVA under 1.5 cm² and multidisciplinary pregnancy heart teams. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Severity at the bedside** = short A2-OS gap + long murmur + signs of pulmonary hypertension - not the loudness of the murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic viva: 'Why is S1 loud?' - leaflets held open by the gradient close from a wide excursion; **soft S1 and absent OS mean calcified immobile leaflets** (poor PTMC candidate). [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic viva: 'Why does AF cause pulmonary oedema?' - **loss of atrial kick and short diastole** raise the gradient. [Harrison 22e]`,`Wilkins components: **mobility, thickening, calcification, subvalvular apparatus** - 1-4 each, 8 or less favourable. [Wilkins 1988]`,`Common mistake: offering a **DOAC** for AF with rheumatic MS, or withholding anticoagulation because CHA2DS2-VA is low. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Common mistake: forgetting **TOE before PTMC** - LA appendage thrombus is missed by TTE. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Common mistake: atenolol in pregnancy (growth restriction) - use metoprolol. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`Eponyms: **Ortner** (hoarseness), **Lutembacher** (ASD + MS), **Graham Steell** (PR murmur), **Carvallo** (TR louder on inspiration). [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in mitral stenosis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metoprolol succinate`,`25-200 mg oral once daily; IV 2.5-5 mg over 2 min, repeat to 15 mg`,`Titrate every 1-2 weeks to resting HR about 60-70 in sinus rhythm, under 80-90 in AF`,`Bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm; safe in pregnancy`],[`Bisoprolol`,`2.5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Titrate every 2 weeks`,`As for metoprolol`],[`Ivabradine (sinus rhythm only)`,`5 mg oral twice daily, up to 7.5 mg twice daily`,`When beta-blockers not tolerated`,`Visual phosphenes, bradycardia; useless in AF; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Diltiazem`,`30-60 mg oral three times daily (or SR 90-180 mg twice daily)`,`Alternative rate control`,`Hypotension, constipation; avoid with LV dysfunction`],[`Digoxin`,`0.125-0.25 mg oral once daily (0.0625-0.125 mg elderly/CKD)`,`Add-on rate control in AF`,`Toxicity with hypokalaemia, CKD, amiodarone; narrow window`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg oral once or twice daily; 40-80 mg IV in pulmonary oedema`,`Lowest effective dose`,`Hypokalaemia, dehydration lowering output; check K and creatinine`],[`Warfarin / acenocoumarol`,`Warfarin 2-5 mg or acenocoumarol 1-2 mg oral daily, titrated`,`INR 2.0-3.0, long term`,`Bleeding; embryopathy; interactions; DOACs contraindicated if rheumatic MVA 2 cm² or less`],[`Enoxaparin / unfractionated heparin`,`Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly; UFH infusion to aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control`,`Bridge until INR therapeutic`,`Bleeding, HIT; reduce enoxaparin in CKD`],[`Amiodarone`,`200 mg three times daily for 1 week, twice daily for 1 week, then 100-200 mg daily`,`Rhythm control after relief of MS`,`Thyroid, lung, liver toxicity; raises INR and digoxin levels`],[`Benzathine penicillin G`,`1.2 million units deep IM`,`Every 4 weeks; lifelong in moderate-severe disease (Indian practice)`,`Pain; rare anaphylaxis`]]},{heading:`Grading the severity of mitral stenosis`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Progressive (mild-moderate)`,`Severe (clinically significant)`,`Very severe`],rows:[[`Mitral valve area`,`Over 1.5 cm²`,`1.5 cm² or less`,`1.0 cm² or less`],[`Pressure half-time`,`Under 150 ms`,`150 ms or more`,`220 ms or more`],[`Mean gradient (normal heart rate)`,`Under 5-10 mmHg`,`Over 10 mmHg (supportive)`,`Usually over 15 mmHg`],[`PASP`,`Under 30-50 mmHg`,`Often over 50 mmHg`,`May be over 60-70 mmHg`],[`A2-OS interval`,`Over 100 ms`,`Under 80 ms`,`Very short (around 40-60 ms)`],[`ACC/AHA stage`,`B`,`C (asymptomatic) or D (symptomatic)`,`C or D`]]},{heading:`Wilkins (MGH) echocardiographic score`,columns:[`Grade`,`Leaflet mobility`,`Leaflet thickening`,`Subvalvular thickening`,`Calcification`],rows:[[`1`,`Highly mobile, only tips restricted`,`Near normal (4-5 mm)`,`Minimal, just below leaflets`,`Single area of brightness`],[`2`,`Mid and base portions normally mobile`,`Mid-leaflets normal, margins thickened (5-8 mm)`,`Up to one-third of chordal length`,`Scattered areas at leaflet margins`],[`3`,`Valve moves forward in diastole, mainly from the base`,`Whole leaflet thickened (5-8 mm)`,`Extends to the distal third of chords`,`Brightness into mid-leaflets`],[`4`,`No or minimal forward movement in diastole`,`Considerable thickening of all tissue (over 8-10 mm)`,`Extensive thickening and shortening to papillary muscles`,`Extensive brightness throughout leaflets`],[`Total`,`Score 4-16; 8 or less favourable for PTMC`,`Over 8: higher failure, restenosis, MR`,`Calcification and subvalvular disease weigh most`,`Always combine with MR grade and TOE for thrombus`]]},{heading:`Mid-diastolic murmur at the apex: differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Distinguishing features`,`Confirming test`],rows:[[`Rheumatic MS`,`Loud S1, opening snap, presystolic accentuation, tapping apex`,`Echo: doming, commissural fusion, low MVA`],[`Austin Flint (severe AR)`,`No OS, normal or soft S1, collapsing pulse, early diastolic murmur`,`Echo: severe AR, normal mitral valve`],[`Carey Coombs (acute rheumatic carditis)`,`Soft, short, no OS, fever and arthritis`,`Echo: valvulitis with MR; raised ESR/CRP`],[`Left atrial myxoma`,`Tumour plop, positional change, fever, weight loss`,`Echo: mobile LA mass`],[`Flow murmur (severe MR, VSD, PDA)`,`Pansystolic or continuous murmur, S3`,`Echo: shunt or MR`],[`Tricuspid stenosis`,`Left lower sternal edge, louder on inspiration, giant a wave`,`Echo: tricuspid doming`],[`Degenerative MS (MAC)`,`Elderly, CKD, no OS, often soft murmur`,`Echo: annular calcification, open commissures`]]},{heading:`PTMC or surgery: what decides`,columns:[`Factor`,`Favours PTMC`,`Favours surgery`],rows:[[`Valve anatomy`,`Wilkins 8 or less, pliable, commissural fusion`,`Heavy or bicommissural calcification, no commissural fusion`],[`Mitral regurgitation`,`None or mild`,`More than mild`],[`LA thrombus on TOE`,`Absent`,`Present and persisting despite anticoagulation`],[`Other valves / coronaries`,`Isolated MS`,`Severe aortic or tricuspid disease, CAD needing CABG`],[`Patient`,`Young, pregnant, high surgical risk`,`Failed or unsuitable PTMC with low surgical risk`]]}],redFlags:[`Breathlessness at rest, orthopnoea or frothy pink sputum - pulmonary oedema.`,`New AF with fast rate, or any AF not on a VKA.`,`Stroke, TIA, sudden limb pain or pallor - embolism.`,`Haemoptysis, especially large-volume.`,`Pregnant woman with MS and NYHA II or worse, or MVA under 1.5 cm².`,`Signs of right heart failure or PASP over 50 mmHg on echo.`,`Fever with a changing murmur - endocarditis.`,`Syncope or exertional chest pain.`],pearls:[`**MVA 1.5 cm² or less** = clinically significant MS; 1.0 or less very severe.`,`**MVA = 220 / pressure half-time**.`,`Severity = **short A2-OS gap and long murmur**, not loudness.`,`**Loud S1 + OS** = pliable valve; soft S1 and no OS = calcified (poor PTMC candidate).`,`**Wilkins 8 or less**, no LA thrombus, no more than mild MR = PTMC.`,`**Slow the heart** - diastole is when the LA empties.`,`AF with rheumatic MS: **VKA, never a DOAC** (MVA 2 cm² or less, ESC/EACTS 2025).`,`**TOE before PTMC** - TTE misses appendage thrombus.`,`Eponyms: **Ortner** hoarseness, **Lutembacher** ASD + MS, **Graham Steell** PR murmur.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-q1`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman has a loud S1, an opening snap and a mid-diastolic rumble at the apex. Which bedside finding best indicates that her mitral stenosis is severe?`,options:[`A very loud mid-diastolic murmur`,`Presystolic accentuation of the murmur`,`A short interval between A2 and the opening snap`,`A loud first heart sound`],answer:2,explanation:`The higher the LA pressure, the earlier the mitral valve opens after aortic closure, so a short A2-OS interval (under 80 ms) indicates severe MS, along with a long murmur. Loudness of the murmur depends on flow and falls with low output. Presystolic accentuation only shows sinus rhythm, and a loud S1 shows pliable leaflets rather than severity.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old man in NYHA class III has rheumatic MS with MVA 0.9 cm² by planimetry, Wilkins score 7, mild MR and no thrombus on TOE. He is in sinus rhythm. What is the best definitive treatment?`,options:[`Percutaneous transmitral commissurotomy`,`Mitral valve replacement with a mechanical valve`,`Increase metoprolol and furosemide and review in 6 months`,`Ivabradine plus warfarin and yearly echo`],answer:0,explanation:`Symptomatic clinically significant MS with favourable anatomy (Wilkins 8 or less, no more than mild MR, no LA thrombus) is a class I indication for PTMC, which matches surgical results without a prosthesis. Valve replacement is for unsuitable anatomy, and drugs only relieve symptoms without relieving obstruction. Warfarin is not indicated in sinus rhythm without thrombus or embolism.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-q3`,stem:`A primigravida at 24 weeks has MVA 0.9 cm² with a pliable valve and no LA thrombus. Despite metoprolol and furosemide she remains in NYHA class III with PASP 60 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Termination of pregnancy`,`Immediate caesarean section`,`Mitral valve replacement on cardiopulmonary bypass`,`PTMC during pregnancy with abdominal shielding`],answer:3,explanation:`Severe MS with persisting NYHA III-IV symptoms or PASP over 50 mmHg despite medical therapy in pregnancy should have PTMC, preferably after 20 weeks, with abdominal shielding. Bypass surgery carries high fetal loss, delivery at 24 weeks is extremely preterm and does not fix the valve, and termination is not required when PTMC can relieve the obstruction.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-q4`,stem:`On echo, the mitral pressure half-time in a patient with pure rheumatic MS is 220 ms. What is the estimated mitral valve area?`,options:[`0.5 cm²`,`1.0 cm²`,`1.5 cm²`,`2.2 cm²`],answer:1,explanation:`MVA is estimated as 220 divided by the pressure half-time, so 220/220 gives 1.0 cm², which is very severe MS (PHT 220 ms or more). The 1.5 cm² value corresponds to a half-time of about 150 ms, the severe threshold. The method is unreliable just after PTMC, with significant AR or a stiff LV.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-q5`,stem:`Which finding is an absolute contraindication to proceeding with PTMC in a symptomatic patient with MVA 1.1 cm²?`,options:[`Permanent atrial fibrillation`,`Pregnancy at 22 weeks`,`Thrombus in the left atrial appendage on TOE`,`Age 55 years`],answer:2,explanation:`LA or appendage thrombus is a contraindication because the balloon and wires can dislodge it and cause stroke; the patient is anticoagulated and re-imaged, with surgery if the thrombus persists. AF and older age are only unfavourable clinical features, and pregnancy after 20 weeks is a recognised setting for PTMC when symptoms are refractory.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the pathophysiology, clinical features and assessment of severity of mitral stenosis. Outline its management, with a note on percutaneous transmitral commissurotomy.`,openingLines:[`Mitral stenosis is obstruction to LV inflow from narrowing of the mitral orifice (normal 4-6 cm²), almost always due to rheumatic commissural fusion in India.`,`It is clinically significant when the valve area is 1.5 cm² or less, and PTMC is the treatment of choice when anatomy is favourable.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,points:[`Rheumatic (commissural fusion) in over 90%; degenerative MAC in elderly; congenital rare.`,`Gradient rises with flow squared and short diastole; LA pressure, pulmonary venous congestion, passive then reactive PH, RV failure.`,`LA dilatation, AF, thrombus; LV underfilled with low fixed output.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, haemoptysis, palpitations, embolism, Ortner hoarseness; precipitants AF, pregnancy, fever.`,`Malar flush, tapping apex, parasternal heave, loud S1, OS, MDM with presystolic accentuation; Graham Steell, TR.`]},{heading:`Assessment of severity`,points:[`Bedside: short A2-OS gap, long murmur, PH signs.`,`Echo: MVA by planimetry (1.5 cm² or less severe; 1.0 or less very severe), PHT 150/220 ms, mean gradient, PASP; Wilkins score; TOE for thrombus.`,`ECG P mitrale and AF; CXR LA enlargement and Kerley B lines.`]},{heading:`Medical management`,points:[`Rate control (metoprolol, bisoprolol, ivabradine in sinus rhythm), furosemide, salt restriction.`,`AF, thrombus or embolism: VKA to INR 2.0-3.0; no DOAC (ESC/EACTS 2025); BPG secondary prophylaxis.`]},{heading:`Intervention and PTMC`,points:[`Indication: symptomatic MVA 1.5 cm² or less; asymptomatic with PASP over 50, high embolic risk or planned pregnancy.`,`PTMC: transseptal Inoue balloon; needs Wilkins 8 or less, no more than mild MR, no LA thrombus; complications severe MR, tamponade, embolism.`,`Surgery (CMV, open commissurotomy, MVR) when PTMC unsuitable or failed.`]},{heading:`Special situations and follow-up`,points:[`Pregnancy: PTMC before conception if MVA under 1.5 cm²; PTMC in pregnancy if refractory.`,`Echo yearly in severe asymptomatic MS; INR and BPG adherence at each visit.`]}],mustDraw:[`Pathophysiology flow: narrowed orifice to LA pressure to PH to RV failure, with AF and thrombus side branch`,`Severity grading table (MVA, PHT, gradient, PASP)`,`Management flow chart: symptomatic MVA 1.5 cm² or less to Wilkins score to PTMC or surgery`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, aetiology, pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Assessment of severity`,marks:2},{part:`Medical management and anticoagulation`,marks:2},{part:`PTMC and surgery`,marks:2}],keywords:[`MVA`,`A2-OS`,`Wilkins`,`PTMC`,`VKA`,`pulmonary hypertension`,`pregnancy`]},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-stenosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate the causes of a mid-diastolic murmur at the apex.`,openingLines:[`A mid-diastolic murmur at the apex arises from turbulent flow across the mitral valve in the rapid filling phase - from organic narrowing or increased flow across a normal valve.`,`The opening snap, S1 intensity and associated findings separate rheumatic MS from its mimics.`],answer:[{heading:`Organic mitral obstruction`,points:[`Rheumatic MS: loud S1, OS, presystolic accentuation, tapping apex; echo shows doming and commissural fusion.`,`Degenerative MAC MS: elderly or CKD, no OS; LA myxoma: tumour plop, positional, constitutional symptoms.`]},{heading:`Functional obstruction`,points:[`Austin Flint: severe AR jet hits the anterior leaflet; no OS, collapsing pulse, early diastolic murmur.`,`Carey Coombs: acute rheumatic valvulitis; soft, short, no OS, with fever and arthritis.`]},{heading:`Increased flow`,points:[`Severe MR, VSD, PDA: short flow rumble after S3, with pansystolic or continuous murmur.`]},{heading:`Comparison table`,points:[`Columns: S1, OS, presystolic accentuation, associated signs, echo finding - one row for each cause.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: rheumatic MS, Austin Flint, Carey Coombs, LA myxoma, flow murmur`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and classification`,marks:1},{part:`Differentiating features`,marks:3},{part:`Investigation that confirms each`,marks:1}],keywords:[`Austin Flint`,`Carey Coombs`,`opening snap`,`tumour plop`,`flow murmur`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Mitral stenosis`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Mitral valve disease`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease. Eur Heart J 2025`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the management of patients with valvular heart disease. Circulation 2021`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation; Connolly SJ et al. INVICTUS. N Engl J Med 2022`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy`,`Baumgartner H et al. EAE/ASE recommendations on echocardiographic assessment of valve stenosis, 2009 (2017 update); Wilkins GT et al. Br Heart J 1988`]}),o.push({id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation`,title:`Mitral regurgitation and mitral valve prolapse`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Mitral regurgitation (MR)** is systolic backflow from LV to LA, either **primary** (disease of the leaflets or chords - prolapse, rheumatic, endocarditis) or **secondary** (a normal valve pulled open by a dilated **ventricle** or **atrium**); severe MR is **EROA 0.40 cm² or more, regurgitant volume 60 mL or more**, and severe primary MR is operated - **repair preferred** - when symptoms appear or when **LVEF falls to 60% or less or LVESD reaches 40 mm**, while secondary MR is treated with GDMT first and **TEER** (MitraClip) in COAPT-type patients.`,keywords:[`MR`,`mitral incompetence`,`primary MR`,`secondary MR`,`functional MR`,`atrial functional MR`,`mitral valve prolapse`,`MVP`,`Barlow disease`,`flail leaflet`,`papillary muscle rupture`,`EROA`,`regurgitant volume`,`vena contracta`,`Carpentier classification`,`mitral valve repair`,`TEER`,`MitraClip`,`COAPT`,`mitral annular disjunction`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Mitral regurgitation** is retrograde flow from LV to LA in systole due to failure of leaflet coaptation; it is the **commonest valve lesion** in community echo studies of high-income countries. [Harrison 22e]`,`Moderate or severe MR affects about **1.7% of adults** overall and about **9-10% of those 75 or older**. [Nkomo, Lancet 2006]`,`**Primary (degenerative or organic) MR** arises from the valve apparatus itself; **secondary (functional) MR** from LV or LA remodelling with structurally normal leaflets. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** splits secondary MR into **ventricular** (ischaemic or dilated cardiomyopathy) and **atrial** (AF or HFpEF with annular dilatation) types, which are managed differently. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`In India, **rheumatic MR** dominates in the young (it is the lesion of acute rheumatic carditis), while **degenerative prolapse and ischaemic MR** dominate after 50. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mitral valve prolapse** affects about **2-3% of the population** (Framingham 2.4%) and is the commonest cause of isolated severe primary MR needing surgery in high-income countries. [Harrison 22e]`,`Secondary MR is present in a large proportion of patients with **HFrEF** and independently predicts death and HF hospitalisation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`**Degenerative**: myxomatous **Barlow disease** (thick redundant bileaflet prolapse, younger) and **fibroelastic deficiency** (thin leaflets, single-segment chordal rupture, usually P2, older). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Rheumatic**: leaflet thickening and retraction with chordal shortening; acute carditis causes MR through annular dilatation and anterior leaflet prolapse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infective endocarditis**: leaflet perforation, chordal rupture or vegetation preventing coaptation - often acute. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Ischaemic**: papillary muscle rupture after MI (acute) or chronic posterior LV remodelling tethering the leaflets (secondary MR). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventricular secondary MR**: dilated or ischaemic cardiomyopathy, peripartum cardiomyopathy, LBBB dyssynchrony. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Atrial secondary MR**: long-standing AF and HFpEF dilate the LA and annulus with normal LV size. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Others**: congenital cleft mitral valve (with primum ASD), connective tissue disease (Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos), SLE (Libman-Sacks), drugs (ergot, fenfluramine, benfluorex), radiation, annular calcification, HCM with systolic anterior motion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - acute versus chronic`,points:[`MR is a **volume overload**: the LV ejects into two outlets, the aorta and a low-pressure LA, so total stroke volume rises while forward output may fall. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic compensated MR**: eccentric hypertrophy and LV dilatation raise total stroke volume; a **large compliant LA** keeps LA pressure near normal, so patients stay asymptomatic for years. [Harrison 22e]`,`Because the LV unloads into the LA, **EF is supernormal** in compensated MR - an **LVEF of 60% or less already means contractile dysfunction**, which is why surgery thresholds look 'normal'. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Decompensation**: progressive dilatation, rising end-systolic size and falling EF, raised LA pressure, AF, pulmonary hypertension - damage that may not reverse after surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute MR**: a normal-sized, non-compliant LA receives a sudden regurgitant volume - LA pressure rises steeply (**giant v wave**), causing **pulmonary oedema and cardiogenic shock** with a normal-sized heart. [Harrison 22e]`,`In acute MR the LA-LV gradient equalises in late systole, so the murmur is **short, early systolic and may be soft or absent**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Secondary MR** is dynamic: it varies with loading, rises with exercise and ischaemia, and falls with diuresis, vasodilators, **GDMT** and **CRT**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Afterload reduction** (vasodilators) reduces MR by favouring forward flow - useful in acute MR and secondary MR, but not shown to delay surgery in chronic primary MR. [ACC/AHA 2020]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading`,points:[`**Carpentier functional classification**: type I - normal leaflet motion (annular dilatation, perforation); type II - **excessive motion** (prolapse, flail); type IIIa - restricted in systole and diastole (rheumatic); type IIIb - restricted in systole (ischaemic tethering). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Severe MR**: **EROA 0.40 cm² or more**, **regurgitant volume 60 mL or more**, **regurgitant fraction 50% or more**, **vena contracta 0.7 cm or more**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Mild MR**: EROA under 0.20 cm², RVol under 30 mL, RF under 30%, vena contracta under 0.3 cm; moderate lies between. [ASE 2017]`,`**Specific signs of severe MR**: flail leaflet or ruptured papillary muscle, **systolic flow reversal in pulmonary veins**, large central jet over 40-50% of LA area, dense triangular CW Doppler signal. [ASE 2017]`,`In **secondary MR** with low flow or an elliptical orifice, **EROA 0.30 cm² and RVol 45 mL** may already be severe - the threshold used in COAPT. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA stages**: A at risk, B progressive, **C1** severe asymptomatic with LVEF over 60% and LVESD under 40 mm, **C2** severe asymptomatic with LVEF 60% or less or LVESD 40 mm or more, **D** symptomatic. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Proportionate versus disproportionate** secondary MR: MR severe relative to LV size (large EROA, smaller LV) responds to TEER; MR proportionate to a huge LV does not. [Grayburn, JACC 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Chronic MR is **asymptomatic for years**; the first symptoms are **exertional dyspnoea and fatigue** from reduced forward output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Palpitations** from AF are common as the LA enlarges; AF onset often brings the patient to the clinic. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Acute MR** presents as **sudden pulmonary oedema or shock** - after an MI (days 2-7), with endocarditis, or with sudden chordal rupture in known prolapse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MVP**: often found incidentally; may cause palpitations, atypical chest pain, anxiety, presyncope - symptoms correlate poorly with MR severity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Red-flag history in MVP**: syncope, sustained palpitations or family history of sudden death suggest **arrhythmic MVP**. [EHRA 2022]`,`Ask about rheumatic fever and BPG, previous MI, heart failure, fever (IE), connective tissue disease, drugs, and pregnancy plans. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pulse**: normal volume with a brisk upstroke (rapid LV emptying); irregular in AF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Apex**: **displaced, diffuse and hyperdynamic** in chronic severe MR; a systolic thrill at the apex; a left parasternal lift may be LA expansion rather than RV. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Murmur**: **apical pansystolic, blowing, radiating to the axilla**, best heard in the left lateral position; increased by handgrip (afterload). [Harrison 22e]`,`Radiation tells the leaflet: **posterior leaflet prolapse** throws the jet anteriorly to the **base and aortic area**; **anterior leaflet prolapse** throws it posteriorly to the **axilla and back**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Soft S1**, **S3** (rapid filling of large volume, not necessarily failure) and a **short flow mid-diastolic rumble** indicate severe MR; wide split S2 from early A2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MVP**: **mid-systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur**; standing and Valsalva (smaller LV) bring the click earlier and lengthen the murmur; squatting and handgrip move them later. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary MR**: signs of the underlying cardiomyopathy - displaced apex, S3, raised JVP; the murmur may be soft despite significant MR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Look for **Marfan features**, pectus excavatum, straight back syndrome (MVP associations) and stigmata of endocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**VSD**: pansystolic murmur at the left lower sternal edge with a thrill, radiating across the precordium, not to the axilla. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tricuspid regurgitation**: left lower sternal edge, **louder on inspiration (Carvallo)**, giant cv waves and pulsatile liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aortic stenosis**: ejection systolic, radiates to the carotids; in the elderly the **Gallavardin phenomenon** can mimic MR at the apex. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy**: ejection murmur louder with Valsalva and standing, quieter with squatting and handgrip; MR from systolic anterior motion coexists. [ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy]`,`**Acute MR versus post-MI VSD**: both give a new murmur and shock days after MI; echo (and step-up in saturation) separates them. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Physiological MR** on echo: trivial, short, non-pansystolic jets in normal valves need no follow-up. [ASE 2017]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: LA enlargement (P mitrale), **AF**, LVH by voltage; Q waves suggest ischaemic MR; inferior T-wave inversion with ectopy in arrhythmic MVP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: cardiomegaly with LA and LV enlargement in chronic MR; **pulmonary oedema with a normal heart size** in acute MR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** defines mechanism (Carpentier type, segment), severity by an **integrated approach** (EROA by PISA, RVol, vena contracta, pulmonary vein flow), LV and LA size, LVEF and PASP. [ASE 2017]`,`**MVP on echo**: systolic displacement of a leaflet **2 mm or more beyond the annular plane in the parasternal long-axis view** (the saddle-shaped annulus makes the 4-chamber view over-diagnose); classic MVP if leaflets 5 mm or thicker. [ASE 2017]`,`**TOE (especially 3D)**: when TTE is non-diagnostic, to plan repair or TEER, and in suspected endocarditis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Exercise echo** in asymptomatic severe MR to unmask symptoms, exercise PASP rise or loss of contractile reserve; **NT-proBNP** for risk stratification. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cardiac MRI**: regurgitant volume and LV volumes when echo is equivocal; **late gadolinium enhancement** of papillary muscles in arrhythmic MVP. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Coronary angiography** (or CT coronary angiography in low risk) before mitral surgery and to define ischaemic MR; **Holter** for MVP with palpitations or syncope. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - chronic primary MR`,points:[`**No drug delays surgery** in chronic primary MR with preserved LV function; vasodilators are not indicated in normotensive asymptomatic patients. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`Treat **hypertension** (ACE inhibitor or ARB), and give **GDMT for HFrEF** (beta-blocker, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor) if LV dysfunction appears and surgery is not possible. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**AF**: rate control and anticoagulation by CHA2DS2-VA; **DOACs are acceptable** in MR (unlike rheumatic MS); rheumatic MR in Indian practice often still uses a VKA. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Surgery indicated (class I)** in severe primary MR with **symptoms**, or without symptoms when **LVEF is 60% or less** or **LVESD 40 mm or more** (ESC 2025 also accepts indexed LVESD 20 mm/m² or more). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Earlier repair in asymptomatic patients** with preserved LV function is favoured by ESC/EACTS 2025 (new class I) when risk is low, durable repair is likely, and markers such as **AF, PASP over 50 mmHg** or marked LA dilatation are present. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Repair over replacement** whenever durable repair is likely - better survival, preserved LV function, no lifelong anticoagulation; posterior leaflet prolapse repair succeeds in over 90% at expert centres. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Rheumatic MR** repairs are less durable; young Indians often need replacement (mechanical valve and VKA). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TEER** (edge-to-edge repair with MitraClip or PASCAL) for severe symptomatic primary MR at **high or prohibitive surgical risk** with suitable anatomy - EVEREST II showed surgery reduces MR more, TEER is safer. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Secondary prophylaxis** (BPG) for rheumatic MR, dental hygiene, and endocarditis prophylaxis only after repair with a ring or replacement. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Management - secondary MR and TEER`,points:[`**Ventricular secondary MR**: first optimise **GDMT** (sacubitril/valsartan, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor) and **CRT** if indicated - MR often improves substantially. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**COAPT (2018)**: TEER plus GDMT in symptomatic HFrEF with moderate-severe or severe secondary MR halved **HF hospitalisation (35.8% vs 67.9% per patient-year)** and reduced **all-cause death (29.1% vs 46.1%)** at 2 years. [COAPT 2018]`,`**MITRA-FR (2018)** showed no benefit - patients had larger LVs and less MR (**proportionate MR**), explaining the contrast with COAPT. [MITRA-FR 2018]`,`**RESHAPE-HF2 (2024)**: TEER in moderate-severe secondary MR reduced HF hospitalisation and cardiovascular death and improved quality of life. [RESHAPE-HF2 2024]`,`**COAPT criteria for TEER**: LVEF **20-50%**, LVESD **70 mm or less**, PASP **70 mmHg or less**, persistent NYHA II-IV despite GDMT, no severe RV dysfunction, suitable anatomy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** upgrades TEER to a **class I recommendation** for symptomatic ventricular secondary MR with LVEF under 50% meeting these criteria. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Mitral surgery** for secondary MR is reasonable when **CABG** or another cardiac operation is planned. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Atrial secondary MR**: control AF (rhythm control helps) and congestion first; **surgery class IIa, TEER class IIb** in ESC/EACTS 2025. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Advanced HF with very large LV, RV failure or severe PH: TEER is unlikely to help - consider LVAD, transplant or palliation. [ESC 2023 HF]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency - acute MR`,points:[`**Admit**: acute MR (sudden pulmonary oedema, new murmur after MI), endocarditis with MR, flail leaflet with symptoms, or decompensated chronic MR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Echo urgently** (TOE if needed) to define mechanism: papillary muscle rupture, chordal rupture, perforation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Afterload reduction**: **IV nitroglycerin 10-20 mcg/min titrated** or **sodium nitroprusside 0.3-0.5 mcg/kg/min** if BP allows; **IV furosemide 40-80 mg**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Hypotension or shock**: inotropes (dobutamine 2.5-10 mcg/kg/min, noradrenaline) plus **intra-aortic balloon pump** or other mechanical support as a bridge. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Papillary muscle rupture** (posteromedial far more often - single blood supply from the PDA) needs **emergency surgery**; transcatheter edge-to-edge repair is an option at high surgical risk. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Infective endocarditis** with acute severe MR and heart failure is an indication for **urgent (within days) or emergency surgery**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Monitor**: arterial line, urine output, lactate, oxygenation; non-invasive ventilation for pulmonary oedema. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Mitral valve prolapse - special points`,points:[`**MVP** is systolic billowing of one or both leaflets 2 mm or more above the annulus; most people have a **benign course** with normal life expectancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Associations**: Marfan, Ehlers-Danlos, osteogenesis imperfecta, adult polycystic kidney disease, pectus excavatum, straight back and secundum ASD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Complications**: progressive MR, **chordal rupture with flail leaflet**, endocarditis (when MR is present), AF, and rarely **sudden cardiac death** (about 0.2-0.4% a year). [EHRA 2022]`,`**Arrhythmic MVP**: bileaflet myxomatous prolapse, **mitral annular disjunction (MAD)**, inferolateral T-wave inversion, complex ventricular ectopy, and papillary muscle **LGE on MRI** - refer for Holter, MRI and electrophysiology review. [EHRA 2022]`,`**Treatment**: reassurance and explanation; **beta-blocker** (propranolol 10-40 mg three times daily or metoprolol) for palpitations or chest pain; avoid stimulants. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endocarditis prophylaxis is not recommended** in MVP; aspirin only for TIA in sinus rhythm, anticoagulation for AF by risk score. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Follow-up**: MVP without significant MR - echo every 3-5 years; with moderate MR - every 1-2 years; severe - every 6-12 months. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**First-degree relatives** of patients with familial or arrhythmic MVP may be screened with echo. [EHRA 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: chronic MR is well tolerated (falling systemic resistance reduces MR); severe MR with LV dysfunction should be repaired before pregnancy - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: high surgical risk, frailty and comorbidity make TEER more attractive; ensure GDMT and rhythm control first in secondary MR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: annular calcification causes mixed MR and MS; surgery carries high risk. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Diabetes and ischaemic MR**: revascularisation with or without mitral surgery; residual MR after MI predicts death. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Young Indian with rheumatic MR**: secondary prophylaxis, earlier referral because LV deteriorates silently, and surgery before LVEF falls to 60%. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: clinical follow-up with ECG and CXR, echo at district hospital yearly; MV repair and replacement are covered under PM-JAY. [NHA PM-JAY]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Complications: **LV dysfunction and heart failure**, AF, pulmonary hypertension, endocarditis, thromboembolism (with AF) and, in MVP, sudden death. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flail leaflet** carries a high event rate - about 6-7% annual mortality on medical therapy - and justifies early surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Secondary MR in HFrEF** roughly doubles the risk of death and HF admission compared with no MR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Echo follow-up** in asymptomatic primary MR: mild every 3-5 years, moderate every 1-2 years, **severe every 6-12 months**, sooner if values approach thresholds. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Refer** any symptoms, severe MR, LVEF 60% or less, LVESD approaching 40 mm, new AF, PASP over 50 mmHg, or flail leaflet - to a **heart valve centre** where repair rates are high. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`After repair: echo at baseline, at 1 year and then periodically; endocarditis prophylaxis is needed after ring annuloplasty or replacement. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: new **class I** recommendation for repair in selected **asymptomatic primary MR** at low risk when durable repair is likely. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: secondary MR divided into **atrial** (surgery IIa, TEER IIb) and **ventricular** types. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: TEER upgraded to **class I** for symptomatic ventricular secondary MR with LVEF under 50% after GDMT, meeting COAPT-like criteria (RESHAPE-HF2). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** adds an indexed LVESD (20 mm/m² or more) to the classic 40 mm threshold in primary MR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020** harmonised severe secondary MR with primary MR (EROA 0.40 cm², RVol 60 mL), while noting smaller values may be severe in low flow. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**EHRA 2022 expert consensus** defines arrhythmic MVP and MAD and how to risk-stratify them. [EHRA 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Viva: 'Why is **LVEF 60%** an indication for surgery?' - MR unloads the LV into the LA, so a 'normal' EF already means dysfunction. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Acute vs chronic MR**: acute - normal heart size, short early murmur, giant v wave, pulmonary oedema; chronic - large LV and LA, pansystolic murmur, compensated for years. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary vs secondary**: fix the valve in primary; treat the ventricle (GDMT, CRT) first in secondary. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**COAPT vs MITRA-FR**: disproportionate vs proportionate MR. [Grayburn, JACC 2019]`,`Post-MI papillary rupture is **posteromedial** because it has a single blood supply (PDA). [Harrison 22e]`,`MVP manoeuvres: **anything that makes the LV smaller** (standing, Valsalva) brings the click earlier and the murmur longer. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing MVP from the **apical 4-chamber view**. [ASE 2017]`,`Common mistake: waiting for symptoms when **LVESD reaches 40 mm** - the LV may never recover. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in mitral regurgitation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`20-80 mg oral daily; 40-80 mg IV in pulmonary oedema`,`Lowest dose that keeps patient euvolaemic`,`Hypokalaemia, hypotension, renal dysfunction`],[`Sacubitril/valsartan (secondary MR with HFrEF)`,`24/26 mg oral twice daily, up to 97/103 mg twice daily`,`Double every 2-4 weeks`,`Hypotension, hyperkalaemia, angioedema; 36 h gap after ACE inhibitor; contraindicated in pregnancy`],[`Metoprolol succinate / bisoprolol`,`12.5-200 mg / 1.25-10 mg oral once daily`,`Double every 2-4 weeks`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; propranolol 10-40 mg TDS for MVP symptoms`],[`Spironolactone`,`12.5-50 mg oral once daily`,`Check K and creatinine at 1 week and 4 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia`],[`Dapagliflozin / empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`No titration`,`Genital mycotic infection, euglycaemic ketoacidosis`],[`Sodium nitroprusside (acute MR)`,`0.3-0.5 mcg/kg/min IV, titrate to 3 mcg/kg/min`,`Hours, with arterial line`,`Hypotension, cyanide toxicity in prolonged or renal failure use`],[`Nitroglycerin IV (acute MR)`,`10-20 mcg/min, increase by 10-20 every 5 min`,`Hours`,`Headache, hypotension; avoid after PDE-5 inhibitor`],[`Dobutamine (shock)`,`2.5-10 mcg/kg/min IV`,`Bridge to surgery`,`Tachyarrhythmia, hypotension`],[`DOAC (apixaban) for AF with non-rheumatic MR`,`5 mg oral twice daily (2.5 mg if 2 of: age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5 mg/dL or more)`,`Long term by CHA2DS2-VA`,`Bleeding; not for mechanical valves or rheumatic MS`],[`Warfarin (after mechanical MVR, rheumatic AF)`,`2-5 mg oral daily, titrated`,`INR 2.5-3.5 for mechanical mitral; 2.0-3.0 for AF`,`Bleeding, interactions, embryopathy`]]},{heading:`Grading MR severity on echo`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`EROA (PISA)`,`Under 0.20 cm²`,`0.20-0.39 cm²`,`0.40 cm² or more (0.30 may be severe in secondary MR with low flow)`],[`Regurgitant volume`,`Under 30 mL`,`30-59 mL`,`60 mL or more (45 mL in low-flow secondary MR)`],[`Regurgitant fraction`,`Under 30%`,`30-49%`,`50% or more`],[`Vena contracta`,`Under 0.3 cm`,`0.3-0.69 cm`,`0.7 cm or more`],[`Pulmonary vein flow`,`Systolic dominance`,`Systolic blunting`,`Systolic flow reversal`],[`Structural signs`,`Normal LA and LV`,`Variable`,`Flail leaflet, ruptured papillary muscle, dilated LA and LV`]]},{heading:`Carpentier classification of MR mechanism`,columns:[`Type`,`Leaflet motion`,`Typical causes`,`Usual treatment`],rows:[[`I`,`Normal`,`Annular dilatation (atrial secondary MR), leaflet perforation (IE), cleft`,`Annuloplasty, patch repair, AF control`],[`II`,`Excessive (prolapse, flail)`,`Barlow disease, fibroelastic deficiency, chordal or papillary rupture`,`Repair (resection, neochords); TEER if high risk`],[`IIIa`,`Restricted in systole and diastole`,`Rheumatic, radiation, drugs`,`Often replacement in rheumatic disease`],[`IIIb`,`Restricted in systole (tethering)`,`Ischaemic or dilated cardiomyopathy`,`GDMT, CRT, then TEER (COAPT criteria)`]]},{heading:`Acute versus chronic MR`,columns:[`Feature`,`Acute MR`,`Chronic MR`],rows:[[`Causes`,`Papillary muscle rupture, chordal rupture, endocarditis`,`MVP, rheumatic, secondary MR`],[`LA size and compliance`,`Normal size, non-compliant`,`Large, compliant`],[`LA pressure`,`Very high, giant v wave`,`Near normal until late`],[`Presentation`,`Sudden pulmonary oedema, shock`,`Years asymptomatic, then dyspnoea, AF`],[`Murmur`,`Early systolic, short, may be soft or absent`,`Pansystolic to axilla`],[`Heart size on X-ray`,`Normal`,`Enlarged LA and LV`],[`Treatment`,`Afterload reduction, IABP, emergency surgery`,`Surveillance, surgery by symptoms and LV thresholds`]]},{heading:`Indications for intervention in severe MR`,columns:[`Situation`,`Recommendation`,`Source`],rows:[[`Symptomatic severe primary MR, operable`,`Surgery, repair preferred (class I)`,`ESC/EACTS 2025; ACC/AHA 2020`],[`Asymptomatic primary MR with LVEF 60% or less or LVESD 40 mm or more (20 mm/m²)`,`Surgery (class I)`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`],[`Asymptomatic primary MR, preserved LV, low risk, durable repair likely, with AF, PASP over 50 or LA dilatation`,`Repair (new class I in 2025)`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`],[`Symptomatic primary MR, high surgical risk`,`TEER if anatomy suitable`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`],[`Ventricular secondary MR, LVEF under 50%, symptoms despite GDMT/CRT, COAPT criteria`,`TEER (class I)`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`],[`Secondary MR with planned CABG`,`Concomitant mitral surgery`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`],[`Atrial secondary MR`,`AF and volume control; surgery IIa, TEER IIb`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden pulmonary oedema or shock with a new systolic murmur, especially 2-7 days after MI.`,`Fever with MR - endocarditis.`,`Flail leaflet or chordal rupture on echo.`,`Asymptomatic severe MR with LVEF 60% or less or LVESD 40 mm or more.`,`New AF or PASP over 50 mmHg in severe MR.`,`Syncope or sustained palpitations in MVP - arrhythmic MVP.`,`Worsening heart failure in secondary MR despite GDMT.`],pearls:[`**EROA 0.40 cm², RVol 60 mL, RF 50%, vena contracta 0.7 cm** = severe MR.`,`In MR, **LVEF 60% is the new 50%** - and LVESD 40 mm is the other trigger.`,`**Repair beats replacement** whenever durable repair is likely.`,`Primary MR: **fix the valve**; secondary MR: **treat the ventricle first**.`,`**COAPT** worked, **MITRA-FR** did not - disproportionate vs proportionate MR.`,`Acute MR: **normal heart size + pulmonary oedema + soft early murmur**.`,`Posterior leaflet prolapse radiates to the **base**; anterior leaflet to the **axilla**.`,`MVP: **standing/Valsalva** - click earlier, murmur longer.`,`Papillary muscle rupture is **posteromedial** (single PDA supply).`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-q1`,stem:`A 52-year-old asymptomatic man has severe degenerative MR from P2 prolapse. Echo shows LVEF 58% and LVESD 41 mm. What is the best recommendation?`,options:[`Watchful waiting with echo every 6 months until symptoms appear`,`Start an ACE inhibitor to delay surgery`,`Mitral valve repair now at an experienced centre`,`TEER with MitraClip because he is asymptomatic`],answer:2,explanation:`In severe primary MR, LVEF of 60% or less or LVESD of 40 mm or more signals LV dysfunction and is a class I indication for surgery even without symptoms, and P2 prolapse is highly repairable. Waiting risks irreversible LV damage, vasodilators do not delay surgery in primary MR, and TEER is reserved for patients at high or prohibitive surgical risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-q2`,stem:`Four days after an inferior MI, a 64-year-old develops sudden pulmonary oedema and hypotension. A soft early systolic murmur is heard at the apex. Chest X-ray shows pulmonary oedema with a normal heart size. What is the most likely cause?`,options:[`Rupture of the posteromedial papillary muscle`,`Left ventricular free wall rupture`,`Chronic ischaemic MR from LV remodelling`,`Right ventricular infarction`],answer:0,explanation:`Acute severe MR from papillary muscle rupture appears 2-7 days after MI, typically inferior, because the posteromedial muscle depends on a single PDA supply; the non-compliant LA explains pulmonary oedema with a normal-sized heart and a soft, short murmur. Free wall rupture causes tamponade, chronic remodelling develops over months, and RV infarction gives hypotension with clear lungs.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 32%, LVESD 55 mm, PASP 45 mmHg) has severe secondary MR (EROA 0.35 cm²) and remains in NYHA III despite 3 months of maximally tolerated GDMT. QRS is 100 ms. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Cardiac resynchronisation therapy`,`Surgical mitral valve replacement alone`,`Add hydralazine-nitrate and review in 1 year`,`Transcatheter edge-to-edge repair (TEER)`],answer:3,explanation:`He meets COAPT criteria - LVEF 20-50%, LVESD 70 mm or less, PASP 70 mmHg or less, persistent symptoms on GDMT - where TEER cut HF admissions and death, now a class I recommendation in ESC/EACTS 2025. CRT needs a wide QRS, isolated mitral surgery without CABG has no proven survival benefit in ventricular secondary MR, and adding a vasodilator alone does not address severe MR.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-q4`,stem:`In a 30-year-old woman with a mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur, which manoeuvre makes the click occur earlier and the murmur longer?`,options:[`Squatting`,`Standing up from squatting`,`Sustained handgrip`,`Passive leg raising`],answer:1,explanation:`Standing (or the Valsalva strain) reduces venous return and LV size, so the redundant leaflets prolapse earlier in systole - the click moves towards S1 and the murmur lengthens. Squatting and passive leg raising increase LV volume and delay the click, and handgrip raises afterload and LV size, also delaying the click although it may make MR louder.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-q5`,stem:`Which echocardiographic finding is a specific sign of severe mitral regurgitation?`,options:[`Vena contracta width of 0.4 cm`,`Systolic flow reversal in the pulmonary veins`,`Regurgitant fraction of 35%`,`A central jet occupying 15% of LA area`],answer:1,explanation:`Systolic flow reversal in the pulmonary veins means LA pressure exceeds pulmonary venous pressure in systole and is a specific sign of severe MR, as is a flail leaflet or vena contracta 0.7 cm or more. A vena contracta of 0.4 cm and a regurgitant fraction of 35% fall in the moderate range, and a jet of 15% of LA area suggests mild MR.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify mitral regurgitation. Discuss the evaluation of chronic MR and the indications for intervention, including the role of transcatheter edge-to-edge repair.`,openingLines:[`Mitral regurgitation is systolic backflow from LV to LA from failure of leaflet coaptation, either primary (valve apparatus disease) or secondary (ventricular or atrial remodelling with normal leaflets).`,`Severe MR is EROA 0.40 cm² or more and regurgitant volume 60 mL or more; primary MR is treated by surgery, repair preferred, and secondary MR by GDMT then TEER in selected patients.`],answer:[{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Primary (degenerative MVP, rheumatic, IE, congenital) vs secondary (ventricular, atrial); acute vs chronic.`,`Carpentier I normal motion, II excessive, IIIa restricted systole and diastole, IIIb restricted systole.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Volume overload, eccentric LVH, compliant LA; supernormal EF so 60% or less means dysfunction.`,`Acute MR: non-compliant LA, giant v wave, pulmonary oedema with normal heart size.`]},{heading:`Clinical evaluation`,points:[`Dyspnoea, fatigue, palpitations; displaced hyperdynamic apex, pansystolic murmur to axilla, soft S1, S3.`,`MVP click and late systolic murmur with dynamic manoeuvres.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG (AF, LA enlargement), CXR, TTE: mechanism, EROA, RVol, RF, vena contracta, pulmonary vein flow, LVEF, LVESD, PASP.`,`TOE for repair planning, exercise echo, CMR, BNP, coronary angiography before surgery.`]},{heading:`Indications for intervention - primary MR`,points:[`Symptoms; LVEF 60% or less; LVESD 40 mm or more (20 mm/m²); AF, PASP over 50 or LA dilatation in low-risk asymptomatic with likely durable repair.`,`Repair over replacement; TEER if high surgical risk.`]},{heading:`Secondary MR and TEER`,points:[`GDMT plus CRT first; TEER if LVEF 20-50%, LVESD 70 mm or less, PASP 70 mmHg or less, persistent symptoms (COAPT; class I ESC/EACTS 2025).`,`COAPT benefit vs MITRA-FR neutral: disproportionate vs proportionate MR; atrial SMR - AF control, surgery IIa.`]}],mustDraw:[`Carpentier classification diagram`,`Echo severity table (EROA, RVol, RF, vena contracta)`,`Flow chart: severe primary MR - symptoms or LV thresholds - repair; secondary MR - GDMT/CRT - TEER`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology and clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations and severity`,marks:2},{part:`Indications in primary MR`,marks:2},{part:`Secondary MR and TEER`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Carpentier`,`EROA`,`LVESD 40 mm`,`LVEF 60%`,`repair`,`TEER`,`COAPT`,`MITRA-FR`]},{id:`cardiovascular-mitral-regurgitation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate acute from chronic mitral regurgitation.`,openingLines:[`The difference between acute and chronic MR lies in LA compliance: a normal, stiff LA cannot absorb a sudden regurgitant volume, whereas a slowly enlarging LA can.`,`Acute MR is a surgical emergency; chronic MR is followed until symptoms or LV thresholds trigger surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Acute: papillary muscle rupture, chordal rupture, IE perforation, trauma.`,`Chronic: MVP, rheumatic, secondary MR.`]},{heading:`Haemodynamics and clinical picture`,points:[`Acute: giant v wave, pulmonary oedema, shock, short early systolic murmur, normal apex.`,`Chronic: compliant large LA, years asymptomatic, displaced apex, pansystolic murmur, AF.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Acute: normal heart size with pulmonary oedema on CXR; echo flail or ruptured papillary muscle.`,`Chronic: cardiomegaly, LA enlargement; echo dilated LV and LA.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Acute: nitroprusside or nitroglycerin, diuretics, IABP, emergency surgery.`,`Chronic: surveillance and surgery by symptoms, LVEF 60% or less, LVESD 40 mm or more.`]}],mustDraw:[`Comparison table: acute vs chronic MR`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism (LA compliance)`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical and investigation differences`,marks:3},{part:`Management differences`,marks:1}],keywords:[`LA compliance`,`giant v wave`,`papillary muscle rupture`,`IABP`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Mitral regurgitation and mitral valve prolapse`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Mitral valve disease`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease. Eur Heart J 2025`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the management of patients with valvular heart disease. Circulation 2021`,`Zoghbi WA et al. ASE recommendations for noninvasive evaluation of native valvular regurgitation. J Am Soc Echocardiogr 2017`,`Stone GW et al. Transcatheter mitral-valve repair in patients with heart failure (COAPT). N Engl J Med 2018; Anker SD et al. RESHAPE-HF2. N Engl J Med 2024`,`EHRA expert consensus statement on arrhythmic mitral valve prolapse and mitral annular disjunction, Europace 2022`]}),o.push({id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis`,title:`Aortic stenosis`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Aortic stenosis (AS)** is fixed obstruction to LV outflow - in adults usually **calcific degeneration** of a tricuspid or **bicuspid** valve, or **rheumatic** disease with mitral involvement - that is **severe when Vmax is 4 m/s or more, mean gradient 40 mmHg or more or valve area 1.0 cm² or less**; once **angina, syncope or breathlessness** appear, survival is short without **valve replacement**, by **TAVI (now favoured from age 70, ESC/EACTS 2025)** or **SAVR**, chosen by the Heart Team.`,keywords:[`AS`,`calcific aortic stenosis`,`bicuspid aortic valve`,`rheumatic aortic stenosis`,`aortic sclerosis`,`pulsus parvus et tardus`,`ejection systolic murmur`,`Gallavardin phenomenon`,`Heyde syndrome`,`Vmax`,`aortic valve area`,`low-flow low-gradient`,`dobutamine stress echo`,`calcium score`,`TAVI`,`TAVR`,`SAVR`,`EARLY TAVR`,`balloon aortic valvuloplasty`,`Myval`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Aortic stenosis** is narrowing of the aortic valve orifice causing a systolic pressure gradient between LV and aorta; the **normal valve area is 3-4 cm²**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Calcific AS is the **commonest valve disease needing intervention** in high-income countries; **aortic sclerosis** (thickening without obstruction) is present in about **25% of people over 65**. [Harrison 22e]`,`In people aged **75 or older**, about **12% have some AS and 3.4% severe AS** (meta-analysis). [Osnabrugge, JACC 2013]`,`**Bicuspid aortic valve** occurs in **1-2% of the population** (male-to-female about 3:1) and presents with AS 1-2 decades earlier than tricuspid calcific AS, typically at 50-70. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In India, **rheumatic AS** (almost always with mitral disease) and **bicuspid AS** dominate in younger patients, while degenerative AS is rising with an ageing population. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TAVI** has been performed in India for over a decade and an **Indian-made balloon-expandable valve (Myval)** was non-inferior to contemporary valves in the LANDMARK trial. [LANDMARK 2024]`,`Untreated symptomatic severe AS has a mortality of about **50% within 2 years** - one of the most time-critical diagnoses in cardiology OPD. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Calcific (degenerative)** AS of a trileaflet valve: age over 65, shares risk factors with atherosclerosis - **hypertension, smoking, diabetes, LDL and especially lipoprotein(a)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bicuspid valve**: commonest congenital heart anomaly; associated with **aortopathy** (dilated ascending aorta, dissection risk), **coarctation** and **Turner syndrome**; familial in about 9% (first-degree relatives). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Rheumatic**: commissural fusion with a central triangular orifice; almost always with rheumatic mitral disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital**: unicuspid valve (presents in childhood or youth), **subvalvular** membrane, **supravalvular** stenosis (Williams syndrome, homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Accelerating factors**: CKD and dialysis (calcium-phosphate), Paget disease, chest radiation, alkaptonuria (ochronosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Statins do not slow calcific AS** (SALTIRE, SEAS, ASTRONOMER) - they are given only for coexistent atherosclerotic risk. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Calcific AS is an **active, atherosclerosis-like process**: endothelial injury, lipid and Lp(a) infiltration, inflammation, then **osteogenic transformation of valve interstitial cells** and calcification. [Harrison 22e]`,`Progression: on average **Vmax rises about 0.3 m/s, mean gradient about 7 mmHg and AVA falls about 0.1 cm² per year** - faster with heavy calcification and bicuspid valves. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Pressure overload causes **concentric LVH**, normalising wall stress and preserving EF for years at the cost of **diastolic dysfunction** and raised LV filling pressure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angina**: hypertrophied myocardium needs more oxygen while high LV diastolic pressure and short diastole cut subendocardial perfusion - angina occurs even with normal coronary arteries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exertional syncope**: exercise vasodilatation with a **fixed cardiac output** drops BP; abnormal LV baroreceptor reflexes and arrhythmias contribute. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dyspnoea and heart failure**: diastolic dysfunction first, later **afterload mismatch** and systolic failure - EF falls when the LV can no longer overcome the valve. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AF is poorly tolerated** because the stiff hypertrophied LV depends on atrial kick for filling. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Heyde syndrome**: high shear stress across the valve destroys large von Willebrand multimers (**acquired von Willebrand disease type 2A**), causing bleeding from GI angiodysplasia - corrected by valve replacement. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and grading of severity`,points:[`**Severe AS**: **Vmax 4.0 m/s or more**, **mean gradient 40 mmHg or more**, **AVA 1.0 cm² or less** (indexed 0.6 cm²/m² or less), velocity ratio 0.25 or less. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Very severe AS**: **Vmax 5.0 m/s or more** or mean gradient 60 mmHg or more - an indication to consider intervention even without symptoms. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Moderate AS**: Vmax 3.0-3.9 m/s, mean gradient 20-39 mmHg, AVA 1.0-1.5 cm²; **mild**: Vmax 2.6-2.9 m/s, gradient under 20 mmHg, AVA over 1.5 cm². [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Aortic sclerosis**: valve thickening or calcification with **Vmax 2.5 m/s or less** - no obstruction, but a marker of cardiovascular risk. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Classical low-flow low-gradient AS**: AVA 1.0 cm² or less, mean gradient under 40 mmHg, **LVEF under 50%**, stroke volume index 35 mL/m² or less. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Paradoxical low-flow low-gradient AS**: same valve findings with **LVEF 50% or more** but low stroke volume (small, thick LV) - typical of elderly hypertensive women. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA stages**: A at risk, B progressive, **C1** asymptomatic severe with normal EF, **C2** asymptomatic severe with EF under 50%, **D1** symptomatic high-gradient, **D2** low-flow low-gradient reduced EF, **D3** low-gradient preserved EF. [ACC/AHA 2020]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Long asymptomatic phase; the classic triad is **angina, exertional syncope and breathlessness (heart failure)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Classic survival after symptom onset without valve replacement: **angina about 5 years, syncope about 3 years, heart failure about 2 years** (Ross and Braunwald). [Braunwald 12e]`,`The commonest early symptom is **reduced exercise tolerance or exertional breathlessness** - elderly patients often blame age and limit activity, so ask what they have stopped doing. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Exertional presyncope or syncope** is a red flag; syncope at rest suggests arrhythmia (AV block from calcium extending into the conduction system, or VT). [Harrison 22e]`,`**GI bleeding with anaemia** in an elderly patient with an ejection murmur suggests **Heyde syndrome**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Sudden death** is rare in truly asymptomatic patients (**about 1% a year or less**) but common once symptoms appear. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Ask about comorbidities that shape the TAVI-versus-SAVR decision: frailty, lung disease, previous cardiac surgery or chest radiation, CKD, cognition and life expectancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pulse**: **slow-rising, low-volume (pulsus parvus et tardus)**, best felt at the carotid; anacrotic notch; narrow pulse pressure; brachioradial delay. [Harrison 22e]`,`The elderly with stiff arteries may have a **normal-feeling carotid upstroke and normal pulse pressure** despite severe AS - do not exclude AS by the pulse. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Apex**: **sustained, heaving, not displaced** (concentric LVH); displaced only with LV failure; a **palpable S4** may be felt. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systolic thrill** at the aortic area, suprasternal notch and carotids in significant AS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ejection systolic murmur**: harsh, crescendo-decrescendo, loudest at the **right second intercostal space**, radiating to the **carotids**; best heard sitting forward in expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Markers of severity**: **late-peaking murmur**, **soft or absent A2** (single S2), **reversed splitting** of S2, S4, slow-rising pulse and signs of LV failure - **not** the loudness of the murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ejection click** after S1 in bicuspid AS with a mobile valve - it disappears as the valve calcifies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Gallavardin phenomenon**: the high-frequency component of the AS murmur is heard at the apex and mistaken for MR in the elderly. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In **low-output states** the murmur softens and may disappear - a soft murmur with heart failure may still be critical AS. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy**: jerky (bisferiens) pulse, no ejection click, murmur at the left sternal edge **louder on standing and Valsalva, softer on squatting and handgrip**, not radiating to carotids. [ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy]`,`**Aortic sclerosis**: soft early-peaking murmur with normal pulse, normal A2 and Vmax 2.5 m/s or less. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Mitral regurgitation**: apical pansystolic murmur to the axilla, no change in length with beat-to-beat variation in AF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Flow murmur** (anaemia, pregnancy, thyrotoxicosis, fever): soft, early-peaking, normal S2 and pulse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Subvalvular or supravalvular AS**: no ejection click; supravalvular gives unequal arm BP (right higher, Coanda effect) and Williams facies. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary stenosis** and **VSD**: left upper sternal edge with an ejection click that varies with respiration (PS), or a pansystolic murmur at the left lower sternal edge (VSD). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coronary disease** causes angina in AS patients too - coexistent CAD is common in the elderly and is assessed before valve replacement. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: **LVH with strain** in most severe AS; LA enlargement, LBBB, AV block (calcium in the conduction system); a normal ECG does not exclude severe AS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: often normal heart size; **post-stenotic dilatation of the ascending aorta**, valve calcification on the lateral film; pulmonary congestion when failing. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is diagnostic: valve morphology (bicuspid or tricuspid), calcification, **Vmax, mean gradient, AVA by continuity equation**, LVEF, stroke volume index, LV hypertrophy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Discordant grading** (AVA 1.0 cm² or less but gradient under 40 mmHg) - check for measurement error and low flow (SVi 35 mL/m² or less) before calling AS moderate. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Low-dose dobutamine stress echo** (up to 20 mcg/kg/min) in classical low-flow AS: **true severe** if mean gradient reaches 40 mmHg with AVA staying 1.0 cm² or less; **pseudo-severe** if AVA rises above 1.0 cm²; no flow reserve if stroke volume rises under 20%. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CT aortic valve calcium score**: severe AS **likely at 2000 AU or more in men and 1200 AU or more in women**, highly likely at 3000 and 1600 AU - for low-gradient cases. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Exercise testing** in **asymptomatic** severe AS (never in symptomatic) to unmask symptoms or a fall in BP; **BNP/NT-proBNP** for risk stratification. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Pre-procedure**: coronary angiography or **CT coronary angiography** (low CAD probability); **TAVI-CT** for annulus sizing, coronary heights and iliofemoral access; aortic imaging in bicuspid valves. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Indications for aortic valve replacement`,points:[`**Symptomatic severe high-gradient AS**: intervention recommended (class I) - no medical therapy improves survival. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Symptomatic low-flow low-gradient AS with reduced EF**: intervene when severity is confirmed (flow reserve on dobutamine echo, or calcium score when there is no flow reserve). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Symptomatic paradoxical low-flow low-gradient AS with preserved EF**: intervene after careful confirmation (CT calcium score, exclusion of measurement error). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Asymptomatic severe AS with LVEF under 50%** without another cause, or symptoms or BP fall on **exercise testing**: intervention recommended. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Asymptomatic severe high-gradient AS with preserved EF**: early intervention **should be considered (class IIa, new in 2025)** when procedural risk is low - supported by **EARLY TAVR, AVATAR and RECOVERY**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Other asymptomatic triggers: **Vmax 5 m/s or more**, rapid progression (0.3 m/s or more a year), markedly raised BNP, heavy calcification, LVEF under 55%. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Moderate or severe AS during CABG** or other cardiac surgery: replace the valve at the same operation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Futility**: intervention is not recommended when comorbidity means life expectancy under 1 year or quality of life will not improve - palliative care instead. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`TAVI versus SAVR - choosing the procedure`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: **TAVI recommended for patients aged 70 or older** with tricuspid AS and suitable transfemoral anatomy, regardless of surgical risk (lowered from 75 in 2021); **SAVR for those under 70** at low surgical risk. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020** uses different bands: **SAVR under 65**, **shared decision 65-80**, **TAVI over 80** or high risk - note the difference from ESC. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Evidence**: TAVI beat medical therapy in inoperable patients (PARTNER 1B) and is non-inferior or better than SAVR at high, intermediate and **low risk** (PARTNER 3, Evolut Low Risk, NOTION, DEDICATE). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Favour SAVR**: younger age (long valve durability needed), **bicuspid valve with unfavourable anatomy or aortopathy**, need for CABG or other valve or aortic surgery, low coronary heights, small or very large annulus, active endocarditis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Favour TAVI**: older age, frailty, porcelain aorta, previous cardiac surgery, chest radiation, severe lung disease, good transfemoral access. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**TAVI complications**: **permanent pacemaker** (more with self-expanding valves), new LBBB, paravalvular leak, stroke, vascular injury, coronary obstruction, annular rupture, subclinical leaflet thrombosis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**After TAVI**: **single antiplatelet** (aspirin 75-100 mg or clopidogrel 75 mg) lifelong if no other indication; **oral anticoagulant alone** if AF (POPular TAVI). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Balloon aortic valvuloplasty** is only a **bridge** to TAVI or SAVR in unstable patients or before urgent non-cardiac surgery - restenosis within months. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Valve choice in SAVR**: mechanical (lifelong VKA) in younger patients; bioprosthesis in older patients, with valve-in-valve TAVI as a later option. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD / primary care`,points:[`**No drug changes the natural history** of AS; the OPD job is to **detect symptoms early** and refer at the right time. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Educate the patient to **report breathlessness, chest pain, dizziness or syncope immediately**; avoid strenuous isometric exertion and competitive sport in severe AS. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Hypertension**: treat it - start low and titrate (ACE inhibitor or ARB, amlodipine); target conventional BP while avoiding hypotension. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Avoid** nitrates, PDE-5 inhibitors and aggressive vasodilators or diuresis in severe AS - preload-dependent LV, risk of collapse. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AF**: rate control (beta-blocker cautiously), anticoagulation by CHA2DS2-VA - **DOACs are allowed** in AS (and after bioprosthetic or TAVI valves beyond 3 months). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Statins** for atherosclerotic risk, not for the valve; manage diabetes, smoking and CKD. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Dental hygiene** twice-yearly; endocarditis prophylaxis is needed only after valve replacement (including TAVI). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Rheumatic AS: **BPG secondary prophylaxis** as for RHD. [WHO 2024]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD / emergency`,points:[`**Admit**: syncope, angina at rest, heart failure, new AF with fast rate, GI bleeding with anaemia (Heyde), or suspected endocarditis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Decompensated severe AS**: cautious IV furosemide, oxygen, avoid hypotension; noradrenaline or phenylephrine to maintain coronary perfusion pressure if hypotensive. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Low-output severe AS with reduced EF and high SVR**: **nitroprusside under invasive monitoring** can improve output (expert ICU use only). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Urgent TAVI** or **balloon valvuloplasty as a bridge** in cardiogenic shock from critical AS. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Fast AF**: restore sinus rhythm promptly (DC cardioversion if unstable) - loss of atrial kick can precipitate collapse. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Complete heart block** (calcific extension, or after TAVI): temporary pacing then permanent pacemaker. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Monitor**: BP (arterial line in shock), urine output, renal function, rhythm; avoid spinal anaesthesia sympathectomy in severe AS. [ESC 2022 NCS]`]},{heading:`Special situations and pre-operative assessment`,points:[`**Non-cardiac surgery**: in **symptomatic severe AS** before elective intermediate- or high-risk surgery, **AVR (TAVI or SAVR) is recommended first**; asymptomatic severe AS can usually have low-risk surgery with careful haemodynamics. [ESC 2022 NCS]`,`**Anaesthesia in severe AS**: avoid hypotension and tachycardia, maintain sinus rhythm and preload; **neuraxial (spinal) block is relatively contraindicated** - prefer general or carefully titrated epidural. [ESC 2022 NCS]`,`**Pregnancy**: severe symptomatic AS is **mWHO class IV** - intervene before pregnancy; asymptomatic severe AS needs specialist care; bicuspid aortopathy adds dissection risk - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Bicuspid aortopathy**: image the ascending aorta; surgery when **55 mm or more** (50 mm with risk factors such as coarctation, hypertension, family history of dissection or growth 3 mm/year or more); screen first-degree relatives with echo. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Elderly and frail**: assess frailty (gait speed, grip, ADLs), cognition and goals; TAVI often restores independence, but futility must be recognised. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CKD**: AS progresses faster; contrast load in TAVI-CT and angiography needs planning; dialysis patients are high-risk for both procedures. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: clinical surveillance with ECG for LVH strain and an echo every 6-12 months at district level; refer to a TAVI or cardiac surgical centre (SAVR covered by PM-JAY). [NHA PM-JAY]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Complications: **heart failure, sudden death, syncope, AF, AV block, infective endocarditis, Heyde bleeding** and embolic calcium (rare). [Harrison 22e]`,`Asymptomatic severe AS: event-free survival falls to about **30-50% at 2 years**, worse with Vmax over 5 m/s, heavy calcification or rising BNP. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Echo follow-up**: mild AS every 3-5 years, moderate every 1-2 years, **severe asymptomatic every 6-12 months** (sooner with heavy calcification or Vmax over 4.5 m/s). [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`Every visit: **specific symptom questions**, BP, signs of failure, ECG for new AF or block; any new symptom means urgent referral, not waiting for the next echo. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Refer** every patient with moderate or severe AS, any AS with symptoms, LVEF under 50%, or bicuspid valve with a dilated aorta, to a **Heart Valve Centre / Heart Team**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`After TAVI or SAVR: echo at 1-3 months as baseline, then yearly after 5 years for bioprostheses; ECG for conduction disease; endocarditis prophylaxis for dental procedures. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: **TAVI age threshold lowered to 70 years** (from 75) for tricuspid AS with suitable anatomy, regardless of surgical risk. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025**: **early intervention in asymptomatic severe high-gradient AS** is a new class IIa recommendation when procedural risk is low. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**EARLY TAVR (2024)**: in 901 asymptomatic severe AS patients aged 65 or more, early TAVI cut death, stroke or unplanned cardiovascular admission from **45.3% to 26.8%** (HR 0.50); most surveillance patients needed AVR within a few years. [EARLY TAVR 2024]`,`**AVATAR (2022)** and **RECOVERY (2020)** supported early surgery in asymptomatic severe or very severe AS; **EVOLVED (2024)** - fibrosis-guided early AVR - did not significantly reduce its primary endpoint. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**DEDICATE (2024)**: TAVI non-inferior to SAVR in low-to-intermediate-risk patients around 74 years old. [DEDICATE 2024]`,`**LANDMARK (2024)**: the Indian Myval valve was non-inferior to Sapien and Evolut valves at 30 days. [LANDMARK 2024]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020** (65 and 80 years) remains the US reference - the main age conflict with ESC/EACTS 2025. [ACC/AHA 2020]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Triad **SAD**: **S**yncope, **A**ngina, **D**yspnoea - survival about 3, 5 and 2 years respectively without AVR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Severe AS numbers: **4 m/s, 40 mmHg, 1.0 cm²**; very severe: **5 m/s, 60 mmHg**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Bedside severity = **slow-rising pulse, soft A2, late-peaking murmur, S4, reversed split** - not loudness. [Harrison 22e]`,`Common mistake: **exercise testing a symptomatic patient** with severe AS - contraindicated. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Common mistake: calling low-gradient AS moderate without checking **stroke volume and calcium score**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Common mistake: giving **sublingual nitrate** for chest pain in severe AS - risk of collapse. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **Heyde syndrome** = AS + angiodysplasia bleeding + acquired von Willebrand type 2A. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: AS vs HOCM by **Valsalva** - HOCM murmur louder, AS murmur softer. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in aortic stenosis and after valve replacement`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg oral daily; 20-40 mg IV in congestion`,`Lowest dose; cautious`,`Over-diuresis drops preload and output - hypotension, AKI`],[`Telmisartan / ramipril (hypertension)`,`Telmisartan 20-80 mg or ramipril 1.25-10 mg oral daily`,`Start low, titrate every 2-4 weeks`,`Hypotension in severe AS; hyperkalaemia; teratogenic`],[`Amlodipine (hypertension)`,`2.5-10 mg oral daily`,`Titrate slowly`,`Hypotension, oedema`],[`Metoprolol succinate (AF rate, angina)`,`12.5-100 mg oral daily`,`Cautious titration`,`Negative inotropy in decompensated AS`],[`Apixaban (AF, also after TAVI/bioprosthesis beyond 3 months)`,`5 mg oral twice daily (2.5 mg if 2 of: age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5 mg/dL or more)`,`Long term`,`Bleeding; not with mechanical valves`],[`Aspirin or clopidogrel (after TAVI)`,`Aspirin 75-100 mg or clopidogrel 75 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong single antiplatelet if no OAC indication`,`Bleeding; avoid adding to OAC (POPular TAVI)`],[`Warfarin (mechanical aortic valve)`,`2-5 mg oral daily, titrated`,`INR 2.5 (2.0-3.0); 3.0 if extra risk factors; lifelong`,`Bleeding, interactions, embryopathy; DOACs contraindicated`],[`Atorvastatin (CAD risk)`,`10-80 mg oral daily`,`Long term`,`Myalgia; does not slow AS`],[`Dobutamine (diagnostic stress echo)`,`Start 2.5-5 mcg/kg/min IV, increase to max 20 mcg/kg/min`,`Minutes, in echo lab`,`Arrhythmia, hypotension`],[`Amoxicillin (dental IE prophylaxis after AVR or TAVI)`,`2 g oral 30-60 min before procedure`,`Single dose`,`Allergy - azithromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 100 mg instead`]]},{heading:`Grading AS severity on echo`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Aortic sclerosis`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`Peak velocity (Vmax)`,`2.5 m/s or less`,`2.6-2.9 m/s`,`3.0-3.9 m/s`,`4.0 m/s or more (very severe 5.0)`],[`Mean gradient`,`-`,`Under 20 mmHg`,`20-39 mmHg`,`40 mmHg or more (very severe 60)`],[`Aortic valve area`,`-`,`Over 1.5 cm²`,`1.0-1.5 cm²`,`1.0 cm² or less`],[`Indexed AVA`,`-`,`Over 0.85 cm²/m²`,`0.60-0.85 cm²/m²`,`0.6 cm²/m² or less`],[`Velocity ratio (LVOT/AV)`,`-`,`Over 0.50`,`0.25-0.50`,`0.25 or less`]]},{heading:`Low-gradient AS: types and how to confirm`,columns:[`Type`,`Findings`,`Confirm with`,`Action`],rows:[[`Classical low-flow low-gradient`,`AVA 1.0 cm² or less, MG under 40, LVEF under 50%, SVi 35 or less`,`Low-dose dobutamine echo; CT calcium if no flow reserve`,`True severe: AVR; pseudo-severe: treat LV dysfunction`],[`Paradoxical low-flow low-gradient`,`AVA 1.0 cm² or less, MG under 40, LVEF 50% or more, SVi 35 or less`,`CT calcium score (men 2000, women 1200 AU or more)`,`AVR if symptomatic and confirmed`],[`Normal-flow low-gradient`,`AVA 1.0 cm² or less, MG under 40, normal SVi`,`Recheck LVOT measurement`,`Usually moderate AS - surveillance`],[`High-gradient`,`MG 40 mmHg or more, Vmax 4 m/s or more`,`Not needed`,`AVR if symptomatic or other triggers`]]},{heading:`Ejection systolic murmur: differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Pulse and S2`,`Murmur behaviour`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Valvular AS`,`Slow-rising; soft A2`,`Right 2nd ICS to carotids; softer with Valsalva`,`Echo Vmax, gradient, AVA`],[`HOCM`,`Jerky or bisferiens; normal A2`,`Left sternal edge; louder with Valsalva and standing`,`Echo: septal hypertrophy, SAM, LVOT gradient`],[`Aortic sclerosis`,`Normal pulse and A2`,`Soft, early-peaking`,`Echo: Vmax 2.5 m/s or less`],[`Flow murmur`,`Normal; bounding in anaemia`,`Soft, early, varies with state`,`Hb, TSH, echo normal`],[`Pulmonary stenosis`,`Normal pulse; soft delayed P2`,`Left 2nd ICS; click softer on inspiration`,`Echo pulmonary valve`],[`Supravalvular AS`,`Right arm BP higher`,`No click; radiates to right carotid`,`Echo/CT; Williams syndrome`]]},{heading:`TAVI or SAVR: Heart Team factors`,columns:[`Factor`,`Favours TAVI`,`Favours SAVR`],rows:[[`Age (ESC/EACTS 2025)`,`70 or older`,`Under 70 at low surgical risk`],[`Age (ACC/AHA 2020)`,`Over 80 (65-80 shared decision)`,`Under 65`],[`Valve anatomy`,`Tricuspid, suitable annulus`,`Bicuspid with unfavourable anatomy, low coronary heights`],[`Other cardiac disease`,`None needing surgery`,`Needs CABG, mitral, or aortic root surgery`],[`Patient factors`,`Frailty, porcelain aorta, previous sternotomy, chest radiation, lung disease`,`Active endocarditis, poor femoral access`]]}],redFlags:[`Exertional syncope or presyncope.`,`Angina, especially at rest or on minimal exertion.`,`Breathlessness, orthopnoea or signs of heart failure.`,`New AF or AV block on ECG.`,`GI bleeding with anaemia in a patient with an ejection murmur (Heyde syndrome).`,`Severe AS needing urgent non-cardiac surgery.`,`Pregnant woman with moderate or severe AS or bicuspid aortopathy.`,`Fever with an aortic murmur or prosthetic valve.`],pearls:[`Severe AS: **4 m/s, 40 mmHg, 1.0 cm²**.`,`**SAD** triad - syncope 3 years, angina 5, dyspnoea 2 years without AVR.`,`Severity at the bedside: **slow-rising pulse, soft A2, late-peaking murmur**, never loudness.`,`**Gallavardin phenomenon**: AS murmur heard at the apex, mimicking MR.`,`**Heyde syndrome**: AS + angiodysplasia + acquired vWD type 2A.`,`Never exercise-test **symptomatic** severe AS; never give nitrates freely.`,`ESC/EACTS 2025: **TAVI from 70**; ACC/AHA 2020: 65-80 shared decision.`,`Low gradient + low flow: **dobutamine echo** (EF low) or **CT calcium** (EF normal).`,`Statins do **not** slow calcific AS.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-q1`,stem:`A 74-year-old man has exertional breathlessness. Echo shows a calcified tricuspid aortic valve with Vmax 4.3 m/s, mean gradient 46 mmHg, AVA 0.8 cm² and LVEF 60%. He has suitable transfemoral access and low surgical risk. Under ESC/EACTS 2025, what is the preferred treatment?`,options:[`Surgical aortic valve replacement, because his surgical risk is low`,`Transfemoral TAVI`,`Balloon aortic valvuloplasty`,`Medical therapy with diuretic and review in 6 months`],answer:1,explanation:`Symptomatic severe high-gradient AS needs valve replacement, and ESC/EACTS 2025 recommends TAVI for patients aged 70 or older with tricuspid AS and suitable transfemoral anatomy regardless of surgical risk (the threshold was 75 in 2021). Low surgical risk alone no longer favours SAVR at this age, balloon valvuloplasty is only a bridge, and medical therapy does not change survival.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-q2`,stem:`Which bedside finding best indicates that aortic stenosis is severe?`,options:[`A grade 4/6 murmur with a thrill`,`An early-peaking ejection systolic murmur`,`An ejection click after S1`,`A late-peaking murmur with a soft or absent A2`],answer:3,explanation:`As AS worsens, ejection takes longer so the murmur peaks later, and the calcified immobile cusps make A2 soft or absent. Loudness depends on flow and falls when the LV fails, so a loud murmur is not a reliable guide. An early-peaking murmur suggests mild disease or sclerosis, and an ejection click indicates a mobile (often bicuspid) valve, usually not yet heavily calcified.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-q3`,stem:`A 70-year-old with dyspnoea has AVA 0.8 cm², mean gradient 26 mmHg, LVEF 30% and stroke volume index 28 mL/m². On low-dose dobutamine echo, stroke volume rises by 30%, mean gradient rises to 44 mmHg and AVA stays at 0.85 cm². What is the interpretation?`,options:[`True severe low-flow low-gradient AS - aortic valve replacement is indicated`,`Pseudo-severe AS - treat the cardiomyopathy alone`,`Moderate AS - repeat echo in 1 year`,`No contractile reserve - CT calcium score is needed`],answer:0,explanation:`With flow (contractile) reserve shown by a stroke volume rise of 20% or more, the gradient reaching 40 mmHg or more while AVA stays 1.0 cm² or less confirms true severe classical low-flow low-gradient AS, an indication for AVR. Pseudo-severe AS would show the valve area rising above 1.0 cm². The calcium score is used when there is no flow reserve, which is not the case here.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-q4`,stem:`A 78-year-old with an ejection systolic murmur has recurrent melaena. Colonoscopy shows angiodysplasia. Which haematological abnormality explains the link with her valve disease?`,options:[`Immune thrombocytopenia`,`Factor VIII inhibitor (acquired haemophilia A)`,`Acquired von Willebrand disease with loss of large multimers`,`Disseminated intravascular coagulation`],answer:2,explanation:`Heyde syndrome links AS with GI angiodysplasia bleeding: shear stress across the stenotic valve unfolds and cleaves large von Willebrand multimers, causing acquired von Willebrand disease type 2A, which corrects after valve replacement. Thrombocytopenia, factor VIII inhibitors and DIC are not caused by aortic stenosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-q5`,stem:`A 66-year-old asymptomatic woman has severe AS (Vmax 4.4 m/s, LVEF 62%). She asks why her cardiologist is discussing a valve procedure before symptoms. Which trial most directly supports early TAVI in her situation?`,options:[`PARTNER 1B`,`EARLY TAVR`,`COAPT`,`SEAS`],answer:1,explanation:`EARLY TAVR randomised asymptomatic severe AS patients aged 65 or more to early TAVI or surveillance; early TAVI cut death, stroke or unplanned cardiovascular admission from 45.3% to 26.8%, underpinning the new ESC/EACTS 2025 class IIa recommendation. PARTNER 1B studied inoperable symptomatic patients, COAPT studied mitral TEER, and SEAS showed lipid lowering does not slow AS.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, clinical features and assessment of severity of aortic stenosis. Outline the indications for valve replacement and the choice between TAVI and SAVR.`,openingLines:[`Aortic stenosis is fixed obstruction to LV outflow at the aortic valve; in adults it is usually calcific degeneration of a tricuspid or bicuspid valve, or rheumatic disease with mitral involvement.`,`It is severe at Vmax 4 m/s, mean gradient 40 mmHg or AVA 1.0 cm², and once symptoms appear valve replacement - TAVI or SAVR - is the only effective treatment.`],answer:[{heading:`Aetiology`,points:[`Calcific tricuspid (over 65; Lp(a), HT, smoking), bicuspid (1-2%, aortopathy, coarctation), rheumatic (with MS), congenital sub- and supravalvular.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Pressure overload, concentric LVH, diastolic dysfunction; subendocardial ischaemia (angina), fixed output (syncope), afterload mismatch (failure); Heyde syndrome.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Triad angina, syncope, dyspnoea; survival 5, 3, 2 years.`,`Slow-rising low-volume pulse, narrow pulse pressure, heaving non-displaced apex, S4, ESM to carotids, soft A2, reversed split, ejection click in bicuspid; Gallavardin.`]},{heading:`Assessment of severity`,points:[`ECG LVH strain; CXR post-stenotic dilatation; echo Vmax, MG, AVA, velocity ratio, LVEF, SVi.`,`Low-gradient AS: dobutamine echo (reduced EF), CT calcium score (men 2000, women 1200 AU); exercise test only if asymptomatic; BNP.`]},{heading:`Indications for AVR`,points:[`Symptomatic severe AS; asymptomatic with LVEF under 50% or positive exercise test; Vmax 5 m/s, rapid progression, high BNP; with CABG.`,`Asymptomatic severe high-gradient AS at low risk: early AVR class IIa (ESC/EACTS 2025, EARLY TAVR).`]},{heading:`TAVI versus SAVR`,points:[`ESC/EACTS 2025: TAVI 70 or older with tricuspid valve and femoral access; SAVR under 70 low risk; ACC/AHA 2020: under 65 SAVR, 65-80 shared, over 80 TAVI.`,`SAVR for bicuspid with aortopathy, concomitant CABG; TAVI for frailty, porcelain aorta, previous surgery.`,`Post-TAVI single antiplatelet; OAC alone if AF; pacemaker risk.`]},{heading:`Medical care and pre-operative points`,points:[`No drug alters course; treat HT carefully, avoid nitrates and over-diuresis; DOAC allowed for AF.`,`Symptomatic severe AS: AVR before elective non-cardiac surgery; avoid spinal anaesthesia.`]}],mustDraw:[`Severity grading table (Vmax, mean gradient, AVA)`,`Flow chart of low-gradient AS work-up (dobutamine echo, CT calcium score)`,`TAVI versus SAVR decision table`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Severity assessment`,marks:2},{part:`Indications for AVR`,marks:2},{part:`TAVI vs SAVR and medical care`,marks:2}],keywords:[`Vmax 4 m/s`,`AVA 1.0 cm²`,`low-flow low-gradient`,`TAVI`,`SAVR`,`EARLY TAVR`,`Heyde`,`bicuspid`]},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-stenosis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on low-flow low-gradient aortic stenosis.`,openingLines:[`Low-flow low-gradient AS is a severe-range valve area (1.0 cm² or less) with a mean gradient under 40 mmHg because stroke volume is low (SVi 35 mL/m² or less).`,`It occurs with reduced EF (classical) or preserved EF (paradoxical) and must be separated from pseudo-severe and moderate AS before valve replacement.`],answer:[{heading:`Types`,points:[`Classical: LVEF under 50%; paradoxical: LVEF 50% or more with small thick LV (elderly hypertensive women); normal-flow low-gradient: usually moderate AS.`]},{heading:`Confirmation`,points:[`Dobutamine stress echo up to 20 mcg/kg/min: true severe if MG 40 mmHg or more with AVA 1.0 cm² or less; pseudo-severe if AVA rises over 1.0 cm²; no flow reserve if SV rises under 20%.`,`CT calcium score: men 2000 AU, women 1200 AU or more - likely severe.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Confirmed true severe and symptomatic: AVR (TAVI often preferred given age and LV dysfunction).`,`Pseudo-severe: treat the cardiomyopathy with GDMT and follow up.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: AVA 1.0 or less + MG under 40 - check SVi and EF - dobutamine echo or CT calcium - AVR or not`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and types`,marks:1},{part:`Dobutamine echo interpretation`,marks:2},{part:`CT calcium and management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`SVi 35`,`dobutamine stress echo`,`flow reserve`,`calcium score`,`pseudo-severe`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Aortic stenosis`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Aortic valve disease; Transcatheter aortic valve replacement`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease. Eur Heart J 2025`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the management of patients with valvular heart disease. Circulation 2021`,`Généreux P et al. Transcatheter aortic-valve replacement for asymptomatic severe aortic stenosis (EARLY TAVR). N Engl J Med 2024`,`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardiovascular assessment and management of patients undergoing non-cardiac surgery`,`Baumgartner H et al. Recommendations on the echocardiographic assessment of aortic valve stenosis (EACVI/ASE focused update), 2017`,`Kharbanda RK et al. Myval versus contemporary valves (LANDMARK trial). Lancet 2024`]});var s=[];s.push({id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation`,title:`Aortic regurgitation`,oneLiner:`**Aortic regurgitation (AR)** is diastolic backflow from the aorta into the left ventricle through an incompetent valve, due to **leaflet disease** (rheumatic, bicuspid, endocarditis) or **aortic root dilatation**; chronic AR is a long-silent **volume plus pressure overload** with a **wide pulse pressure, collapsing pulse and early diastolic murmur**, and surgery is timed by **symptoms, LVEF and LV end-systolic dimension** - acute AR is a surgical emergency.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`AR`,`aortic insufficiency`,`aortic incompetence`,`collapsing pulse`,`water-hammer pulse`,`Corrigan pulse`,`de Musset sign`,`Quincke sign`,`Duroziez sign`,`Traube sign`,`Hill sign`,`Austin Flint murmur`,`early diastolic murmur`,`bicuspid aortic valve`,`aortic root dilatation`,`Marfan syndrome`,`syphilitic aortitis`,`LVESD`,`Bentall procedure`,`valve-sparing root replacement`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Aortic regurgitation** is diastolic backflow of blood from the aorta into the left ventricle through an incompetent aortic valve, caused by disease of the **leaflets**, the **aortic root** or both. [Harrison 22e]`,`It imposes a combined **volume and pressure overload** on the LV and produces the largest end-diastolic volumes seen in any heart disease - the old **cor bovinum**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In the **Euro Heart Survey**, AR made up about **13%** of single native left-sided valve disease, behind aortic stenosis and mitral regurgitation. [Euro Heart Survey 2003]`,`**Trace AR** on echo is common in older and hypertensive adults and is of no consequence; the clinically important lesion is **moderate or severe AR**. [ASE 2017]`,`In high-income countries the leading causes are **aortic root dilatation** and **bicuspid aortic valve**; in India **rheumatic heart disease** remains a leading cause, usually alongside mitral disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`About **40 million** people live with RHD worldwide and India carries one of the largest absolute burdens; echo screening of Indian school children finds subclinical RHD many times more often than auscultation. [GBD 2019]`,`In the **REMEDY** registry of RHD patients from low- and middle-income countries including India, multivalve disease was common and AR usually accompanied mitral disease rather than occurring alone. [REMEDY 2015]`,`**Bicuspid aortic valve** affects about **1-2%** of people with a male predominance of about **3:1**, and is the commonest congenital cause of AR in young adults. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Other causes that matter in Indian practice are **Takayasu arteritis** in young women, **ankylosing spondylitis**, **infective endocarditis** and, now uncommonly, **tertiary syphilis**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Leaflet causes**: rheumatic (thickened, retracted cusps with commissural fusion), bicuspid valve, infective endocarditis, degenerative calcific disease, trauma and myxomatous degeneration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Root causes**: age- and hypertension-related dilatation, **Marfan syndrome**, Loeys-Dietz and Ehlers-Danlos syndromes, annuloaortic ectasia, bicuspid aortopathy and **aortic dissection** - the cusps are normal but cannot meet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bicuspid aortic valve** causes AR by cusp prolapse or by the associated **ascending aortopathy**; it also causes AS and predisposes to endocarditis and coarctation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Infective endocarditis** causes AR by cusp perforation, vegetations preventing coaptation or a root abscess - with **aortic dissection** it is the commonest cause of **acute AR**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Syphilitic aortitis** appears 10-30 years after infection: endarteritis of the vasa vasorum gives **tree-bark** intimal wrinkling, ascending aneurysm, root dilatation and **coronary ostial stenosis**. [Robbins 11e]`,`**Seronegative spondyloarthropathies** (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive and psoriatic arthritis; HLA-B27) cause aortitis of the root with subaortic fibrosis, often with **AV block**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Large-vessel vasculitis**: Takayasu arteritis and giant cell arteritis dilate the root and ascending aorta - Takayasu is the one to think of in a young Indian woman with absent arm pulses. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other leaflet causes**: SLE (Libman-Sacks), rheumatoid arthritis, **supracristal VSD** with right coronary cusp prolapse, subaortic membrane, and drugs (fenfluramine, pergolide, ergotamine, high-dose cabergoline). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Iatrogenic**: after balloon aortic valvotomy, paravalvular leak after TAVI, or degeneration of a bioprosthesis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Factors that speed progression**: uncontrolled hypertension, a growing root, bicuspid valve, connective-tissue disease and recurrent rheumatic fever. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology: chronic and acute AR`,points:[`In **chronic AR** the LV receives normal left atrial inflow plus the regurgitant volume, so **end-diastolic volume rises** and the Frank-Starling mechanism raises total stroke volume to keep forward output normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`The large stroke volume ejected into the aorta raises **systolic pressure**, while diastolic run-off back into the LV lowers **diastolic pressure** - the **wide pulse pressure** behind every peripheral sign. [Harrison 22e]`,`Unlike MR, which leaks into the low-pressure left atrium, AR ejects the whole stroke volume into the high-pressure aorta - **volume plus afterload overload**, giving eccentric hypertrophy with some wall thickening. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Laplace law**: wall stress equals pressure x radius / (2 x wall thickness); when wall thickening cannot keep pace with dilatation, **afterload mismatch** lowers ejection fraction. [Braunwald 12e]`,`LV compliance increases, so **LV end-diastolic pressure stays normal** for years despite huge volumes - the reason for the long asymptomatic phase. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Irreversible myocardial fibrosis** can set in before symptoms; the **LV end-systolic dimension** reflects contractility and afterload together, which is why it, not symptoms alone, times surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Angina without coronary disease**: low aortic diastolic pressure cuts coronary perfusion while a large, thick LV needs more oxygen; **nocturnal angina** appears as heart rate slows in sleep. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart rate matters**: bradycardia lengthens diastole and increases regurgitant volume per beat, tachycardia shortens it - why **beta-blockers are avoided** in pure AR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Afterload reduction** lowers aortic diastolic pressure and the regurgitant gradient, reducing regurgitant volume - the basis for vasodilators in hypertensive or inoperable patients. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In **acute AR** the normal-sized, stiff LV cannot dilate: diastolic pressure rises steeply, closes the mitral valve early (**premature mitral closure**) and causes pulmonary oedema and **cardiogenic shock** - with no wide pulse pressure. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification, staging and severity grading`,points:[`**By onset**: acute (endocarditis, dissection, trauma) versus chronic (rheumatic, bicuspid, root dilatation) - they differ in signs, tempo and treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Functional (El Khoury) classification** used to plan repair: **type I** normal cusp motion with root dilatation or cusp perforation, **type II** cusp prolapse, **type III** restricted cusps (rheumatic, calcific). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA stages**: **A** at risk (bicuspid valve, sclerosis, rheumatic fever, prior IE), **B** progressive (mild or moderate AR), **C** asymptomatic severe, **D** symptomatic severe. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Stage C1** is asymptomatic severe AR with **LVEF over 55%** and LVESD 50 mm or less; **stage C2** has **LVEF 55% or less** or **LVESD over 50 mm** (or indexed over 25 mm/m²). [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Severe AR on echo**: vena contracta over 6 mm, jet width 65% or more of the LVOT, pressure half-time under 200 ms, **holodiastolic flow reversal** in the descending aorta, regurgitant volume 60 mL or more, regurgitant fraction 50% or more, EROA 0.30 cm² or more. [ASE 2017]`,`**Mild AR on echo**: vena contracta under 3 mm, jet width under 25% of the LVOT, pressure half-time over 500 ms, regurgitant volume under 30 mL, regurgitant fraction under 30%, EROA under 0.10 cm². [ASE 2017]`,`Severity is graded by an **integrated approach** - no single parameter decides it, and pressure half-time also shortens with raised LV diastolic pressure. [ASE 2017]`,`**Bedside clues to severe chronic AR**: a long murmur, diastolic BP under 50 mmHg, displaced heaving apex, Austin Flint murmur, S3 and a Hill gradient over 60 mmHg. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Chronic AR is **asymptomatic for years**; the first complaint is often an uncomfortable awareness of the heartbeat or **pounding in the head and neck**, worse lying on the left side. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Exertional breathlessness**, then orthopnoea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, mark LV decompensation; fatigue reflects falling forward output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Angina** is less common than in aortic stenosis; **nocturnal angina with sweating** is characteristic and responds poorly to nitrates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Syncope is rare** in pure AR - if present, look for associated AS or an arrhythmia. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ask for the cause**: rheumatic fever and penicillin injections in childhood; fever, weight loss or recent dental work (endocarditis); morning back stiffness (ankylosing spondylitis); arm claudication (Takayasu); past genital ulcer (syphilis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Family history** of aortic dissection, sudden death or a tall, lanky habitus with lens dislocation suggests **heritable thoracic aortic disease** such as Marfan syndrome. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Acute AR** presents abruptly with breathlessness, pulmonary oedema or shock - tearing chest or back pain points to **dissection**, fever with a new murmur to **endocarditis**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Atypical routes to diagnosis**: an incidental murmur at a school or pre-employment check, a wide pulse pressure labelled as isolated systolic hypertension, or heart failure first appearing in pregnancy. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pulse**: large-volume **collapsing (water-hammer) pulse** with a sharp upstroke and rapid fall, felt best with the palm over the radial artery and the arm raised; a **bisferiens pulse** suggests severe AR or combined AS and AR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood pressure**: wide pulse pressure with a low diastolic (often under 60 mmHg); Korotkoff sounds may persist to zero, so record the **phase IV** muffling point. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Eponymous run-off signs**: Corrigan (visible carotid pulsation), de Musset (head nodding), Muller (uvular pulsation), Quincke (nail-bed capillary pulsation), Traube (pistol-shot femorals), Duroziez (to-and-fro femoral murmur) and **Hill sign** - see the table. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Apex**: displaced down and out, diffuse and **hyperdynamic** (volume overload); it becomes sustained when the LV fails. [Harrison 22e]`,`**The murmur**: high-pitched, blowing, **early diastolic decrescendo** murmur starting right after A2, loudest at the **left 3rd intercostal space (Erb point)** with the diaphragm, patient sitting forward in held expiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Length tracks severity** in chronic AR, but in acute or very severe AR the murmur is **short** because aortic and LV diastolic pressures equalise early. [Harrison 22e]`,`A murmur loudest at the **right sternal border** (Harvey sign) suggests **aortic root** disease - Marfan, syphilis, dissection - rather than a leaflet cause. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Associated sounds**: a basal **systolic ejection flow murmur** from the large stroke volume (not necessarily AS), soft A2 with leaflet disease or loud tambour A2 with syphilitic root, and an S3 in LV failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Austin Flint murmur**: the regurgitant jet hits the anterior mitral leaflet and limits its opening, giving a low-pitched apical mid-diastolic rumble with **no opening snap and no loud S1**, unlike organic mitral stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Manoeuvres**: sustained handgrip (raises afterload) louder AR and Austin Flint murmurs; **amyl nitrite** softens both but increases the true mitral stenosis murmur. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Pulmonary regurgitation (Graham Steell murmur)**: early diastolic at the left 2nd space, louder on inspiration, with loud P2 and RV heave of pulmonary hypertension - pulse pressure is normal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mitral stenosis** versus Austin Flint: a loud S1, opening snap, tapping apex and presystolic accentuation favour organic MS; echo settles it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hyperdynamic circulation** (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, fever, beriberi, AV fistula, Paget disease, cirrhosis) gives a collapsing pulse and wide pulse pressure but **no early diastolic murmur**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Patent ductus arteriosus**, **ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm** and **coronary AV fistula** give a collapsing pulse with a **continuous** murmur; a ruptured sinus presents suddenly with chest pain and heart failure. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Aortic stenosis with AR**: slow-rising or bisferiens pulse, harsh ejection murmur with thrill and a calcified valve - Doppler gradient separates true AS from an AR flow murmur. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Isolated systolic hypertension of the elderly** widens the pulse pressure without a diastolic murmur or LV dilatation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VSD with AR** (supracristal VSD with cusp prolapse) gives a pansystolic plus an early diastolic murmur - a to-and-fro, not continuous, murmur. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: LVH with a **volume-overload pattern** - tall R and deep narrow Q waves in I, aVL and V5-V6 with upright T waves early, ST-T strain later; a lengthening PR suggests root abscess or spondyloarthropathy. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: cardiomegaly with LV enlargement, dilated ascending aorta and prominent knuckle; linear calcification of the ascending aorta suggests syphilis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is the key test: cause (cusp number, prolapse, perforation, vegetation, root), integrated severity, **LVEDD, LVESD and LVESD indexed to BSA**, LVEF, and aortic diameters at annulus, sinuses, sinotubular junction and ascending aorta. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Use **LVESD indexed to body surface area** in small patients - women and Indians of small build - in whom absolute dimensions understate dilatation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**LV global longitudinal strain** and LV volumes add prognostic information when LVEF and dimensions are borderline. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Transoesophageal echo** for suspected endocarditis or abscess, dissection, unclear cusp anatomy and planning valve repair. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cardiac MRI** quantifies regurgitant volume, regurgitant fraction and LV volumes when echo is equivocal or does not match the symptoms. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CT aortography** when the root or ascending aorta is dilated; **coronary angiography** (CT coronary angiography in lower-risk patients) before surgery in men over 40, postmenopausal women and anyone with angina or risk factors. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Exercise testing** unmasks symptoms in patients who say they are well; **BNP/NT-proBNP** rise with decompensation and support the timing of surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cause-directed tests**: 3 sets of blood cultures before antibiotics if febrile, VDRL or RPR with TPHA, ESR and CRP, HLA-B27 and ASO titre as the history suggests. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`The family physician's job: **confirm by echo**, find the cause, control BP, keep surveillance on time and refer **before the LV fails** - no drug postpones the need for surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Surveillance**: mild AR - echo every **3-5 years**; moderate AR - every **1-2 years**; severe asymptomatic AR with normal LV - every **6-12 months**, and every 3-6 months when LV size approaches the surgical limits. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Hypertension** (systolic over 140 mmHg) is treated, preferably with a **dihydropyridine CCB or ACE inhibitor/ARB** - amlodipine 5-10 mg once daily, nifedipine extended-release 20-60 mg daily, enalapril 2.5-20 mg twice daily or losartan 25-100 mg daily. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Vasodilators are not given to normotensive asymptomatic patients** to delay surgery - the early nifedipine benefit was not confirmed in later randomised trials. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Avoid beta-blockers in pure AR** (bradycardia lengthens diastole) - the exception is **Marfan syndrome**, where a beta-blocker and/or **losartan** slows aortic root growth. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Symptomatic or LV-impaired patients who cannot have surgery**: loop diuretic (furosemide 20-80 mg daily) with ACE inhibitor, ARB or **sacubitril-valsartan** as guideline-directed therapy. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Exercise**: no restriction in mild or moderate AR with normal LV; in severe AR with normal LV, recreational aerobic exercise is fine but avoid heavy isometric lifting and competitive strenuous sport. [ESC 2020 Sports]`,`**Rheumatic AR**: continue secondary prophylaxis with **benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units deep IM every 3-4 weeks** (3-weekly in India) - duration as in the RHD topic. [WHO 2024 RHD]`,`**Dental hygiene** with 6-monthly dental review; antibiotic prophylaxis is **not** recommended for native AR without previous endocarditis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Cardiovascular syphilis**: benzathine penicillin G **2.4 million units IM weekly for 3 weeks** after excluding neurosyphilis; refer vasculitis and spondyloarthropathy to rheumatology. [CDC STI 2021]`]},{heading:`Surgery: indications and choice of operation`,points:[`**Symptomatic severe AR**: surgery is recommended **regardless of LV function** (Class I). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Asymptomatic severe AR**: surgery when **LVESD is over 50 mm**, **LVESD index over 25 mm/m²** or **resting LVEF 50% or less** (Class I). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Earlier surgery** may be considered in low-risk asymptomatic patients at a Heart Valve Centre when **LVESD index exceeds 20 mm/m²** or **LVEF is 55% or less**. [ESC 2021]`,`**ACC/AHA** makes **LVEF 55% or less** a Class 1 trigger (stage C2) - stricter than the ESC 50% - and LVESD over 50 mm or over 25 mm/m² a Class 2a indication. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Severe AR in a patient having CABG, ascending aortic or other valve surgery** is corrected at the same operation; moderate AR is reasonably corrected too. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Aortic root or ascending aorta**: replace at **55 mm or more**, at **50 mm or more in Marfan syndrome** (lower with risk factors), and replace a root of **45 mm or more** during aortic valve surgery - see the Aortic dissection and aneurysm topic. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Operations**: aortic valve replacement (mechanical in the young, bioprosthetic in the older patient), **Bentall** composite valve-graft for root plus valve disease, and **valve-sparing root replacement** (David reimplantation, Yacoub remodelling) when cusps are normal. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Ross procedure** (pulmonary autograft to the aortic position) suits selected young adults and women planning pregnancy in expert centres, because it avoids anticoagulation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Transcatheter implantation for pure AR** is hard (no calcium to anchor); dedicated devices tested in the **ALIGN-AR** trial may be considered in inoperable or very high-risk patients in experienced centres. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`After surgery the LV shrinks within weeks and EF improves over months; poor recovery is predicted by a **large preoperative LVESD, low EF and long symptom duration**. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Acute AR: IPD and emergency management`,points:[`**Acute severe AR is a surgical emergency**: admit to the CCU, obtain an urgent echo and involve cardiothoracic surgery the same day. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Find the cause fast**: blood cultures before antibiotics for endocarditis; CT angiography or TOE for **type A aortic dissection**; history of trauma or a prosthetic valve. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Presentation**: sudden dyspnoea, pulmonary oedema, tachycardia, hypotension; pulse pressure near normal, S1 soft, murmur short and soft, peripheral signs absent - easy to miss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echo red flag**: **premature mitral valve closure** or diastolic mitral regurgitation means LV diastolic pressure exceeds LA pressure - operate immediately. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Bridge to surgery**: IV **sodium nitroprusside 0.3-10 microgram/kg/min** to unload the LV, **dobutamine 2.5-10 microgram/kg/min** for low output, and IV furosemide 40-80 mg for pulmonary oedema. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Intra-aortic balloon pump is contraindicated** in significant AR - diastolic balloon inflation drives more blood back into the ventricle. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Beta-blockers are avoided** because they remove compensatory tachycardia - except in **aortic dissection**, where they are used with great caution if AR is severe. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Endocarditis with acute AR**: **emergency surgery within 24 hours** for refractory pulmonary oedema or shock, **urgent surgery within 3-5 days** for severe regurgitation with heart failure - see the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**District hospital**: oxygen, IV furosemide, IV nitroglycerin if BP allows, no beta-blocker or balloon pump, and early transfer to a cardiac surgical centre. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: chronic AR is usually well tolerated as SVR falls and heart rate rises; women with severe AR and symptoms or impaired LV function should be treated before conception. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Drugs in pregnancy**: stop ACE inhibitors, ARBs and sacubitril-valsartan (fetotoxic); use nifedipine, hydralazine, nitrates and diuretics for symptoms. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Marfan syndrome**: pregnancy is not advised with an aortic root **over 45 mm** (prophylactic surgery first); a bicuspid aortopathy **over 50 mm** carries the same advice. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: hypertension-related root dilatation is common; frailty, comorbidity and coronary disease shape the choice between surgery and medical therapy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Adolescents**: rheumatic AR in India presents in the second decade; a supracristal VSD with cusp prolapse is closed early to stop AR progressing. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Bicuspid valve**: offer echo screening to **first-degree relatives** and keep following the ascending aorta even after valve replacement. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: auscultation, ECG and chest X-ray identify likely severe AR; refer for echo and to a cardiac surgical centre, where **PM-JAY** covers valve replacement. [NHA PM-JAY 2024]`,`**CKD on dialysis**: an AV fistula and anaemia add a hyperdynamic load that can mimic or worsen AR - interpret pulse pressure with care. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Asymptomatic severe AR with normal LV**: progression to symptoms or LV dysfunction is **under 6% per year**, and sudden death **under 0.2% per year**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Asymptomatic LV dysfunction**: more than **25% per year** develop symptoms - the reason LVEF and LVESD trigger surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Symptomatic AR** without surgery carries a mortality of over **10% per year** with angina and over **20% per year** with heart failure. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Complications**: LV failure, ventricular arrhythmias, infective endocarditis, and dissection or rupture of a dilated root. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated acute severe AR** progresses to death from pulmonary oedema, arrhythmia or shock within hours to days. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Operating before the LV dilates beyond the limits restores near-normal survival; late surgery leaves **persistent LV dysfunction** and heart failure. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Prosthesis-related risks** after surgery - thromboembolism, bleeding on warfarin, prosthetic endocarditis and structural valve deterioration - are covered in the Prosthetic heart valves topic. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer to cardiology**: every new diastolic murmur, moderate or severe AR, any symptom, LV dimensions or EF nearing thresholds, or an aortic root of **45 mm or more**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Same-day referral**: suspected acute AR, endocarditis or dissection. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**At each review check**: exercise tolerance and NYHA class, BP and pulse pressure, signs of heart failure, and on echo the **LVESD (and index), LVEF and aortic diameters**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`A root growing **3 mm or more per year** needs closer imaging and earlier surgical review. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Primary prevention of rheumatic fever**: treat streptococcal sore throat with **benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units IM once** (600,000 units if under 27 kg) or oral amoxicillin for 10 days. [WHO 2024 RHD]`,`**Programme links**: **RBSK** screens children for congenital and rheumatic heart disease, **NP-NCD** and **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** handle hypertension and follow-up, and **PM-JAY** funds valve surgery. [MoHFW 2024]`,`**Counselling**: warning symptoms (breathlessness, angina, fever), dental care, adherence to prophylaxis, treatment of sexually transmitted infections, pregnancy planning and **genetic counselling** in Marfan syndrome. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular heart disease guideline** replaces the 2021 version; it keeps the Class I AR triggers (symptoms, LVESD over 50 mm or over 25 mm/m², LVEF 50% or less) and stresses surgery in a **Heart Valve Centre**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** gives more weight to **indexed LV volumes** (3D echo or CMR) and CMR quantification for timing surgery in asymptomatic AR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** accepts **transcatheter valve implantation for pure AR** with dedicated devices in selected inoperable patients, after the **ALIGN-AR** trial (2024). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020** remains the US standard and differs from ESC by making **LVEF 55% or less** (not 50%) a Class 1 indication - a guideline conflict worth quoting. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**ESC 2024 peripheral arterial and aortic disease guideline** updates root and ascending aortic thresholds and the work-up of heritable thoracic aortic disease. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** redefines urgent surgery as **within 3-5 days** and keeps dental prophylaxis for high-risk groups only. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**WHO 2024** issued its first guideline on prevention and diagnosis of **rheumatic fever and RHD**, reinforcing penicillin-based primary and secondary prophylaxis. [WHO 2024 RHD]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline** updates counselling for valve disease and aortopathy before conception. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: list the peripheral signs of AR - aim for at least eight eponyms with their mechanism, and end with the line that echo, not signs, grades severity. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Differentiate Austin Flint from mitral stenosis**: no opening snap, no loud S1, softened by amyl nitrite, and a normal mitral valve on echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why is the IABP contraindicated?** Diastolic inflation increases regurgitation. **Why avoid beta-blockers?** Slower rate, longer diastole, more regurgitation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling a short soft murmur mild AR - in acute or very severe AR the murmur is short because pressures equalise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling the basal systolic flow murmur as aortic stenosis without a Doppler gradient. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: waiting for symptoms - an asymptomatic patient with **LVESD over 50 mm or LVEF 50% or less** needs surgery now. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Hill sign is reversed in coarctation** (arm pressure higher than leg) - a favourite paired viva question. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Right-sided early diastolic murmur** means a root cause (Marfan, syphilis, dissection); left-sided means a leaflet cause. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in aortic regurgitation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amlodipine`,`5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Long term for hypertension with AR`,`Ankle oedema, flushing; does not delay surgery in normotensives`],[`Nifedipine extended-release`,`20-60 mg oral daily in 1-2 doses`,`Long term; safe in pregnancy`,`Headache, flushing, oedema; never sublingual`],[`Enalapril`,`2.5 mg oral twice daily, up to 20 mg twice daily`,`Titrate every 2 weeks; check creatinine and K`,`Cough, angioedema, hyperkalaemia; contraindicated in pregnancy`],[`Losartan`,`25-50 mg oral daily, up to 100 mg`,`Long term; Marfan root protection or ACE-intolerant`,`Hyperkalaemia, renal dysfunction; contraindicated in pregnancy`],[`Sacubitril-valsartan`,`24/26 mg to 97/103 mg oral twice daily`,`Inoperable AR with LV dysfunction; 36-hour gap after ACE inhibitor`,`Hypotension, hyperkalaemia, angioedema; not in pregnancy`],[`Furosemide`,`20-80 mg oral daily; 40-80 mg IV in pulmonary oedema`,`Lowest dose that keeps the patient dry`,`Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, pre-renal AKI`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`IV infusion 0.3-10 microgram/kg/min`,`Hours, as bridge to surgery in acute AR`,`Hypotension, cyanide and thiocyanate toxicity; protect from light`],[`Dobutamine`,`IV infusion 2.5-10 microgram/kg/min`,`Short term for low output in acute AR`,`Tachyarrhythmia, ischaemia`],[`Benzathine penicillin G (rheumatic prophylaxis)`,`1.2 million units deep IM every 3-4 weeks (3-weekly in India)`,`Per RHD topic: years to lifelong`,`Anaphylaxis (rare); test-dose policy per local protocol; painful injection`],[`Benzathine penicillin G (cardiovascular syphilis)`,`2.4 million units IM weekly`,`3 doses after excluding neurosyphilis`,`Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction, penicillin allergy`]]},{heading:`Echocardiographic grading of chronic AR`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`Vena contracta width`,`Under 3 mm`,`3-6 mm`,`Over 6 mm`],[`Central jet width / LVOT width`,`Under 25%`,`25-64%`,`65% or more`],[`Pressure half-time`,`Over 500 ms`,`200-500 ms`,`Under 200 ms`],[`Descending aorta flow reversal`,`Brief, early diastolic`,`Intermediate`,`Holodiastolic (end-diastolic velocity over 20 cm/s); abdominal aorta reversal also`],[`Regurgitant volume`,`Under 30 mL`,`30-59 mL`,`60 mL or more`],[`Regurgitant fraction`,`Under 30%`,`30-49%`,`50% or more`],[`Effective regurgitant orifice area`,`Under 0.10 cm²`,`0.10-0.29 cm²`,`0.30 cm² or more`],[`LV size (chronic AR)`,`Normal`,`Normal or mildly dilated`,`Usually dilated`]]},{heading:`Peripheral signs of aortic regurgitation`,columns:[`Sign`,`What is seen or heard`,`Mechanism / meaning`],rows:[[`Corrigan pulse (water-hammer)`,`Abrupt upstroke and rapid collapse of radial pulse; visible carotid pulsation`,`Large stroke volume with diastolic run-off`],[`de Musset sign`,`Head nods with each heartbeat`,`Forceful systolic ejection`],[`Muller sign`,`Systolic pulsation of the uvula`,`Wide pulse pressure`],[`Quincke sign`,`Capillary pulsation in the nail bed on light pressure`,`Pulsatile capillary flow`],[`Traube sign`,`Pistol-shot sound over the femoral artery`,`Sudden distension of the artery in systole`],[`Duroziez sign`,`Systolic and diastolic (to-and-fro) femoral murmur on partial compression`,`Diastolic flow reversal in the aorta; fairly specific`],[`Hill sign`,`Popliteal systolic exceeds brachial: 20-40 mmHg mild, 40-60 moderate, over 60 severe`,`Amplified pulse wave in lower limbs`],[`Becker sign`,`Visible pulsation of retinal arteries`,`Wide pulse pressure`],[`Mayne sign`,`Diastolic BP falls over 15 mmHg when the arm is raised`,`Low diastolic run-off pressure`],[`Rosenbach and Gerhardt signs`,`Pulsatile liver and spleen`,`Hyperdynamic circulation`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a collapsing pulse or early diastolic murmur`,columns:[`Condition`,`Shared feature`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Pulmonary regurgitation (Graham Steell)`,`Early diastolic murmur`,`Left 2nd space, louder on inspiration, loud P2, RV heave, normal pulse pressure`],[`Mitral stenosis`,`Apical mid-diastolic rumble (vs Austin Flint)`,`Loud S1, opening snap, tapping apex, presystolic accentuation`],[`Hyperdynamic states (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, pregnancy, beriberi)`,`Collapsing pulse, wide pulse pressure`,`No early diastolic murmur; treat the cause and the sign goes`],[`Patent ductus arteriosus`,`Collapsing pulse`,`Continuous machinery murmur at left infraclavicular area`],[`Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm`,`Collapsing pulse, acute heart failure`,`Sudden onset, continuous murmur, aorta-to-right heart shunt on echo`],[`Isolated systolic hypertension`,`Wide pulse pressure`,`No diastolic murmur, LV not dilated`],[`VSD with aortic cusp prolapse`,`Early diastolic murmur`,`Added pansystolic murmur; to-and-fro rather than continuous`]]},{heading:`Acute versus chronic aortic regurgitation`,columns:[`Feature`,`Acute AR`,`Chronic AR`],rows:[[`Common causes`,`Endocarditis, type A dissection, trauma, prosthetic failure`,`Rheumatic, bicuspid valve, root dilatation, syphilis, spondyloarthropathy`],[`LV size and compliance`,`Normal size, stiff`,`Dilated, compliant`],[`Symptoms`,`Sudden pulmonary oedema, shock`,`Asymptomatic for years, then exertional dyspnoea`],[`Pulse pressure`,`Near normal`,`Wide; diastolic often under 60 mmHg`],[`Peripheral signs`,`Absent`,`Present`],[`Murmur`,`Short, soft, early diastolic`,`Long, blowing, decrescendo`],[`S1`,`Soft (premature mitral closure)`,`Normal`],[`Treatment`,`Emergency surgery; nitroprusside bridge; no IABP`,`Surveillance; surgery on symptoms or LV thresholds`]]},{heading:`Indications for surgery in severe AR: ESC versus ACC/AHA`,columns:[`Situation`,`ESC/EACTS 2025`,`ACC/AHA 2020`],rows:[[`Symptomatic severe AR`,`Class I, regardless of LV function`,`Class 1 (stage D)`],[`LV ejection fraction`,`LVEF 50% or less: Class I; 55% or less considered if low risk`,`LVEF 55% or less: Class 1 (stage C2)`],[`LV end-systolic dimension`,`Over 50 mm or over 25 mm/m²: Class I`,`Over 50 mm or over 25 mm/m²: Class 2a`],[`Earlier surgery in low-risk patients`,`LVESD index over 20 mm/m² considered (2021 wording); indexed volumes added`,`Progressive LV dilatation (LVEDD over 65 mm) or falling EF: Class 2b`],[`Concomitant cardiac surgery`,`Severe AR: Class I`,`Severe: Class 1; moderate: Class 2a`],[`Dilated root or ascending aorta`,`Replace at 55 mm (50 mm Marfan); 45 mm during valve surgery`,`Replace at 55 mm (lower in heritable disease); 45 mm during valve surgery`]]}],redFlags:[`Sudden breathlessness or pulmonary oedema with a new early diastolic murmur - acute AR, emergency surgery`,`Fever with a new or changing regurgitant murmur - infective endocarditis until proved otherwise`,`Tearing chest or back pain, pulse deficit or new AR - type A aortic dissection`,`Hypotension, tachycardia and cold peripheries in known AR - cardiogenic shock`,`New angina, syncope or heart failure symptoms in severe AR - surgery is now indicated`,`Asymptomatic severe AR with LVEF 50% or less or LVESD over 50 mm on echo`,`Aortic root 50 mm or more, or growing 3 mm or more a year, especially in Marfan syndrome`,`Prolonging PR interval or new AV block in AR with fever - aortic root abscess`],pearls:[`**Wide pulse pressure plus early diastolic murmur at Erb point** is chronic AR until echo says otherwise.`,`**Acute AR has none of the classic signs** - normal pulse pressure, short murmur, soft S1; think of it in shock with pulmonary oedema.`,`**Premature mitral closure on echo** in acute AR means operate today.`,`**IABP is contraindicated in AR** - it inflates in diastole and worsens the leak.`,`**LVESD over 50 mm or 25 mm/m², or LVEF 50% or less** - operate even without symptoms (ACC/AHA uses 55%).`,`**Right sternal border murmur = root disease** (Harvey sign): Marfan, syphilis, dissection.`,`**Austin Flint = functional MS without an opening snap**; amyl nitrite softens it but makes a true MS murmur louder.`,`**Hill sign over 60 mmHg = severe AR**; reversed in coarctation.`,`Mnemonic for causes, **CRAB-SM**: Congenital bicuspid, Rheumatic, Aortic root dilatation, Bacterial endocarditis, Syphilis, Marfan.`,`**Vasodilators treat hypertension in AR, not the valve** - they do not postpone surgery.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-q1`,stem:`A 34-year-old man has a collapsing pulse and an early diastolic murmur. The popliteal systolic pressure is 72 mmHg higher than the brachial systolic pressure. What does this finding indicate?`,options:[`Coarctation of the aorta`,`Severe chronic aortic regurgitation`,`Severe aortic stenosis`,`Measurement error from a small cuff on the arm`],answer:1,explanation:`This is Hill sign: an exaggerated popliteal-over-brachial systolic gradient from amplification of a large stroke volume, and a gradient over 60 mmHg indicates severe chronic AR. Coarctation does the opposite, with arm pressure higher than leg pressure and radio-femoral delay. Aortic stenosis gives a slow-rising, small-volume pulse and narrow pulse pressure. A small arm cuff overestimates brachial pressure, which would shrink rather than widen the gradient.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-q2`,stem:`A 40-year-old woman with known chronic severe aortic regurgitation has a low-pitched apical mid-diastolic rumble. Which finding best indicates that this is an Austin Flint murmur rather than coexisting rheumatic mitral stenosis?`,options:[`The rumble is louder in the left lateral position`,`The rumble increases with sustained handgrip`,`The rumble is heard with the bell at the apex`,`There is no opening snap and the first heart sound is soft`],answer:3,explanation:`Austin Flint is a functional mitral obstruction from the AR jet hitting the anterior mitral leaflet, so the leaflets are pliable but not fused: there is no opening snap and S1 is not loud. Organic MS has a loud S1 and an opening snap. Left lateral position and the bell favour any low-pitched apical rumble, so they do not discriminate. Handgrip raises afterload and increases AR, so it makes the Austin Flint murmur louder too and cannot separate the two.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-q3`,stem:`A 28-year-old injecting drug user with Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia develops sudden pulmonary oedema and BP 85/50 mmHg. Echo shows a perforated aortic cusp with severe AR and premature mitral valve closure. Which intervention must be avoided while arranging surgery?`,options:[`Intra-aortic balloon pump counterpulsation`,`Intravenous sodium nitroprusside infusion`,`Intravenous dobutamine infusion`,`Intravenous furosemide`],answer:0,explanation:`The intra-aortic balloon pump inflates in diastole and pushes more blood back through the incompetent valve into the LV, so it is contraindicated in significant AR. Nitroprusside reduces afterload and regurgitant volume, dobutamine supports output and furosemide relieves pulmonary oedema - all are appropriate bridges. Premature mitral closure means LV diastolic pressure exceeds atrial pressure, and the definitive treatment is emergency surgery within 24 hours.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with rheumatic severe aortic regurgitation is asymptomatic and walks 5 km daily. Echo shows LVEF 60%, LV end-systolic dimension 53 mm and normal aortic root. BP is 138/52 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Repeat echo in 12 months`,`Start nifedipine to delay the need for surgery`,`Refer for aortic valve surgery`,`Start a beta-blocker to slow progression`],answer:2,explanation:`An LVESD over 50 mm in asymptomatic severe AR is a Class I indication for surgery in the ESC guideline, because it signals contractile reserve is being lost before symptoms appear. Waiting 12 months risks irreversible LV dysfunction. Vasodilators treat hypertension but do not postpone surgery in randomised trials, and this patient is not hypertensive. Beta-blockers slow the heart, lengthen diastole and worsen regurgitant volume in pure AR.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-q5`,stem:`In a 30-year-old tall man with arachnodactyly, the early diastolic murmur of AR is louder at the right 3rd intercostal space than at the left sternal edge. What does this localisation suggest?`,options:[`Rheumatic involvement of the aortic cusps`,`Dilatation of the aortic root as the cause of regurgitation`,`Coexisting pulmonary regurgitation`,`Mild rather than severe regurgitation`],answer:1,explanation:`A right-sided AR murmur (Harvey sign) points to root disease - here Marfan syndrome - because the dilated root pushes the regurgitant jet rightward. Leaflet disease such as rheumatic AR is loudest at the left sternal border at Erb point. Pulmonary regurgitation is heard at the left 2nd space and increases with inspiration. Murmur location does not grade severity; duration and echo do.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the aetiology, pathophysiology, clinical features, evaluation and management of chronic aortic regurgitation. Add a note on its peripheral signs.`,openingLines:[`Aortic regurgitation is diastolic backflow of blood from the aorta into the left ventricle through an incompetent aortic valve, from disease of the leaflets, the aortic root, or both.`,`Chronic AR is a combined volume and pressure overload that stays silent for years; surgery is timed by symptoms, LVEF and LV end-systolic dimension, not by the murmur.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Aetiology`,points:[`**Leaflet**: rheumatic (commonest in India, with mitral disease), bicuspid valve, infective endocarditis, degenerative, SLE, supracristal VSD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Root**: hypertensive and age-related dilatation, Marfan syndrome, bicuspid aortopathy, syphilitic aortitis, ankylosing spondylitis, Takayasu arteritis, dissection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute AR**: endocarditis, type A dissection, trauma, prosthetic valve failure. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`2. Pathophysiology`,points:[`Regurgitant volume raises **LV end-diastolic volume**; Frank-Starling raises total stroke volume and keeps forward output. [Harrison 22e]`,`Large stroke volume into the aorta raises systolic pressure; diastolic run-off lowers diastolic pressure - **wide pulse pressure**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Volume plus pressure overload** gives eccentric hypertrophy; compliance rises, so LVEDP stays normal for years. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Afterload mismatch and fibrosis eventually lower EF; **LVESD** marks this before symptoms. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Low diastolic pressure reduces coronary perfusion - **nocturnal angina**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`3. Clinical features and peripheral signs`,points:[`Long asymptomatic phase; awareness of heartbeat, exertional dyspnoea, orthopnoea, nocturnal angina. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Collapsing pulse**, wide pulse pressure, displaced hyperdynamic apex. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Peripheral signs**: Corrigan, de Musset, Muller, Quincke, Traube, Duroziez, Hill (over 60 mmHg severe), Becker, Mayne. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Early diastolic decrescendo murmur** at left 3rd space, sitting forward in expiration; right-sided if root disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`Systolic flow murmur and **Austin Flint** apical rumble without opening snap. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`4. Evaluation`,points:[`ECG: LVH with volume-overload pattern; CXR: LV enlargement, dilated ascending aorta. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TTE**: cause, integrated severity (vena contracta over 6 mm, PHT under 200 ms, holodiastolic reversal, RF 50% or more), LVESD and LVESD index, LVEF, aortic diameters. [ASE 2017]`,`TOE, CMR and CT aorta when needed; coronary angiography before surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Cause-directed tests: blood cultures, VDRL/TPHA, ESR/CRP, HLA-B27. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`5. Medical management and surveillance`,points:[`Echo every 3-5 years (mild), 1-2 years (moderate), 6-12 months (severe, normal LV). [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`Treat hypertension with **dihydropyridine CCB or ACE inhibitor/ARB**; avoid beta-blockers except in Marfan. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`Secondary rheumatic prophylaxis; dental hygiene; no routine endocarditis prophylaxis for native AR. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Inoperable symptomatic patients: diuretics and GDMT (ACE inhibitor, ARB or sacubitril-valsartan). [ACC/AHA 2020]`]},{heading:`6. Surgical indications and options`,points:[`Symptomatic severe AR; or asymptomatic with **LVESD over 50 mm, over 25 mm/m² or LVEF 50% or less** (ACC/AHA: 55%). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Severe AR at the time of other cardiac surgery; root of 55 mm (50 mm Marfan) or 45 mm at valve surgery. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`AVR (mechanical or tissue), Bentall, valve-sparing root replacement, Ross procedure; transcatheter in selected inoperable patients. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: regurgitant volume to raised LVEDV, raised stroke volume, wide pulse pressure, LV dilatation and failure`,`Table of echo severity grading (mild, moderate, severe)`,`Algorithm for timing surgery: symptoms, LVEF, LVESD`],markSplit:[{part:`Aetiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Pathophysiology with flow chart`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and peripheral signs`,marks:2.5},{part:`Investigations and severity grading`,marks:1.5},{part:`Medical management and surgical indications`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`aortic regurgitation`,`collapsing pulse`,`Austin Flint`,`LVESD`,`Hill sign`,`valve replacement`]},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-regurgitation-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate acute from chronic aortic regurgitation.`,openingLines:[`Acute and chronic AR share a lesion but not a physiology: the chronic LV has dilated to accommodate the leak, while the acute LV is normal-sized and stiff.`,`Hence chronic AR shows wide pulse pressure and peripheral signs, whereas acute AR presents as pulmonary oedema and shock with few signs and needs emergency surgery.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Cause and physiology`,points:[`**Acute**: endocarditis, type A dissection, trauma, prosthetic failure; **chronic**: rheumatic, bicuspid, root dilatation, syphilis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute**: stiff normal LV, steep rise in LVEDP, premature mitral closure; **chronic**: dilated compliant LV with normal LVEDP. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`2. Clinical picture`,points:[`**Acute**: sudden pulmonary oedema, tachycardia, hypotension; **chronic**: years without symptoms, then exertional dyspnoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute**: near-normal pulse pressure, no peripheral signs, soft S1, short soft murmur; **chronic**: wide pulse pressure, eponymous signs, long murmur, Austin Flint. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute**: ECG and CXR show pulmonary oedema with normal heart size; **chronic**: LVH and cardiomegaly. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`3. Management`,points:[`**Acute**: emergency surgery; nitroprusside and dobutamine as bridge; IABP and beta-blockers avoided (beta-blocker only in dissection, cautiously). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Chronic**: surveillance echo, BP control, surgery on symptoms or LV thresholds. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: acute versus chronic AR`],markSplit:[{part:`Cause and physiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical and investigation differences`,marks:2},{part:`Management differences`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`acute aortic regurgitation`,`premature mitral closure`,`IABP contraindicated`,`wide pulse pressure`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Aortic regurgitation chapter`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Aortic valve disease`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease (European Heart Journal, 2025)`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Valvular Heart Disease (Circulation, 2021)`,`Zoghbi WA et al. Recommendations for noninvasive evaluation of native valvular regurgitation, American Society of Echocardiography, 2017`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of peripheral arterial and aortic diseases`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`WHO Guideline for the prevention and diagnosis of rheumatic fever and rheumatic heart disease, 2024`]}),s.push({id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve`,title:`Tricuspid and pulmonary valve disease`,oneLiner:`Right-sided valve disease is dominated by **secondary (functional) tricuspid regurgitation** from RV or RA dilatation due to left heart disease, **pulmonary hypertension** or AF; it shows as a **large cv wave, systolic hepatic pulsation and a pansystolic murmur louder on inspiration (Carvallo sign)**, while **tricuspid stenosis** is rheumatic, **pulmonary stenosis** is congenital and treated by **balloon valvuloplasty**, and **carcinoid** scars both right-sided valves - intervention should come **before RV failure**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`TR`,`TS`,`PS`,`PR`,`tricuspid regurgitation`,`functional TR`,`Carvallo sign`,`hepatic pulsation`,`cv wave`,`Graham Steell murmur`,`pulmonary stenosis`,`balloon pulmonary valvuloplasty`,`carcinoid heart disease`,`Ebstein anomaly`,`tricuspid annuloplasty`,`TriClip`,`T-TEER`,`EVOQUE`,`TRI-SCORE`,`Tetralogy of Fallot pulmonary regurgitation`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Tricuspid regurgitation (TR)** is systolic backflow from the RV into the right atrium; **tricuspid stenosis (TS)** obstructs diastolic RA-to-RV flow; **pulmonary stenosis (PS)** obstructs RV outflow; **pulmonary regurgitation (PR)** is diastolic backflow into the RV. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **80-90% of significant TR is secondary (functional)** - a structurally normal valve distorted by RV or RA dilatation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Moderate or greater TR** affects about **0.55%** of the general population and about **4%** of people over 75, rising with AF, heart failure and pulmonary hypertension. [Topilsky 2019]`,`Significant TR is **not benign**: survival falls with each grade independent of LV function and pulmonary pressure - about **64% 1-year survival with severe TR** in a large veterans cohort. [Nath 2004]`,`**Tricuspid stenosis** is rare and almost always **rheumatic**, accompanying mitral stenosis; isolated TS should prompt a search for carcinoid or a right atrial mass. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary stenosis** is congenital in nearly all cases and makes up about **8-10% of congenital heart disease**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary regurgitation** is trivial and physiological in most normal hearts; clinically important PR follows **pulmonary hypertension** or **Tetralogy of Fallot repair** with a transannular patch. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**India**: functional TR from rheumatic mitral disease with pulmonary hypertension is the commonest significant right-sided lesion; organic rheumatic tricuspid disease and **endomyocardial fibrosis** (Kerala, coastal south India) also contribute. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Right-sided endocarditis** in people who inject drugs is a growing cause of primary TR in young adults. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Secondary ventricular TR**: RV dilatation or dysfunction from left-sided disease (rheumatic MS and MR, LV failure), **pulmonary hypertension** of any cause, RV infarction or RV cardiomyopathy - leaflets are normal but tethered. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Secondary atrial TR**: long-standing **AF** or HFpEF dilates the right atrium and annulus with a normal-sized RV - common in the elderly. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CIED-related TR**: a pacemaker or ICD lead crossing the valve impinges on or tethers a leaflet, or causes adhesion or perforation - now classified as its own type. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Primary TR**: rheumatic disease, endocarditis (Staphylococcus aureus in people who inject drugs), carcinoid, **Ebstein anomaly**, myxomatous prolapse, blunt chest trauma, RV biopsy injury, radiation and ergot-derived drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endomyocardial fibrosis** (tropical; south India and Africa) obliterates the RV apex and tethers the tricuspid apparatus, giving TR with restrictive physiology. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tricuspid stenosis**: rheumatic (with mitral disease), carcinoid, Fabry and Whipple disease, endomyocardial fibrosis; mimics are a right atrial myxoma, large vegetation, thrombus or lead-related fibrosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary stenosis**: dome-shaped congenital valve (commonest), **dysplastic valve in Noonan syndrome**, branch and supravalvular stenosis in **Williams syndrome** and congenital rubella, part of Tetralogy; acquired from carcinoid. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary regurgitation**: pulmonary hypertension with PA dilatation, after Tetralogy repair or balloon valvotomy, endocarditis, carcinoid, idiopathic PA dilatation and congenitally absent pulmonary valve. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Factors that drive TR progression**: pulmonary hypertension, AF, untreated left-sided valve disease, RV dysfunction and a dilated annulus left alone at mitral surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The tricuspid annulus is **saddle-shaped and largely non-fibrous**; when the RV or RA dilates, it enlarges mainly along the free wall, flattens and pulls the leaflets apart. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TR begets TR**: regurgitant volume dilates the RV further, widening the annulus and tethering the leaflets more - so secondary TR may progress even after the left-sided cause is corrected. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Chronic RV volume overload leads to **RV systolic dysfunction** and a diastolic septal shift into the LV (D-shaped septum), lowering LV filling and cardiac output. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Systemic venous hypertension** transmits the regurgitant wave to the neck (large cv wave) and liver (systolic pulsation), and causes ascites, oedema and **congestive hepatopathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiorenal and gut effects**: renal venous congestion lowers GFR and bowel-wall oedema blunts oral diuretic absorption - why IV or high-bioavailability loop diuretics are needed. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Carvallo sign** explained: inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and increases venous return and RV stroke volume, so right-sided murmurs get louder - the sign is lost when the RV fails. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **massive or torrential TR**, RA and RV pressures equalise and flow becomes laminar and low-velocity, so the murmur may be soft or absent and Doppler **underestimates pulmonary pressure**. [ASE 2017]`,`**Tricuspid stenosis**: a mean gradient of only **5 mmHg** raises RA pressure enough for systemic congestion, while reduced RV output **protects the lungs** in coexisting mitral stenosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary stenosis** causes RV pressure overload and concentric RVH; after valvuloplasty the hypertrophied infundibulum may obstruct dynamically - the **suicide right ventricle**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Chronic severe PR** after Tetralogy repair is tolerated for decades, but progressive RV dilatation leads to RV dysfunction, TR, ventricular tachycardia and sudden death. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Classification and severity grading`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025 TR types**: primary, secondary **ventricular**, secondary **atrial**, and **CIED-related** - the mechanism decides repair, replacement or transcatheter therapy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Severe TR on echo**: vena contracta **7 mm or more**, PISA EROA **0.40 cm² or more**, regurgitant volume **45 mL or more**, **systolic flow reversal in the hepatic veins**, dense triangular early-peaking CW signal, central jet area over 10 cm². [ASE 2017]`,`**Mild TR**: vena contracta under 3 mm, EROA under 0.20 cm², regurgitant volume under 30 mL; moderate lies in between. [ASE 2017]`,`**Beyond severe**: a proposed five-grade scale adds **massive** (vena contracta 14-20 mm) and **torrential** (21 mm or more) TR, used to select and judge transcatheter therapy. [Hahn and Zamorano 2017]`,`**ACC/AHA stages A-D** apply to TR as to left-sided lesions: stage C is asymptomatic severe TR and stage D symptomatic severe TR. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Annular dilatation**: a tricuspid annulus of **40 mm or more** (or over 21 mm/m²) on TTE marks a valve likely to progress and supports repair during left-sided surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Severe TS**: mean gradient **5 mmHg or more**, valve area **1.0 cm² or less**, pressure half-time **190 ms or more**, inflow VTI over 60 cm. [EAE/ASE 2009]`,`**PS grading** by peak Doppler velocity: mild under 3 m/s (gradient under 36 mmHg), moderate 3-4 m/s (36-64 mmHg), **severe over 4 m/s (over 64 mmHg)**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Severe PR**: dense CW signal with steep deceleration (pressure half-time under 100 ms), diastolic flow reversal in the branch pulmonary arteries, and a large regurgitant fraction on CMR. [ASE 2017]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: symptoms and bedside signs`,points:[`**Symptoms of right-sided failure**: fatigue and poor exercise tolerance (low forward output), leg swelling, abdominal distension, right upper quadrant ache, early satiety, anorexia and weight loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe TR is tolerated for years**; patients often present late with ascites, jaundice or cardiac cachexia after RV and liver damage is established. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Ask for the cause**: rheumatic fever, prior mitral surgery, AF, a pacemaker, injecting drug use, flushing and diarrhoea (carcinoid), or a childhood murmur or cyanosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**JVP in TR**: raised, with a **large systolic cv (v) wave**, lost x descent and steep y descent - the earlobes may move with each beat (**Lancisi sign**). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic pulsation**: a tender, enlarged, pulsatile liver - **systolic** in TR, **presystolic** in TS with sinus rhythm; it fades once cardiac cirrhosis sets in. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TR murmur**: blowing pansystolic at the lower left sternal edge, **louder on inspiration (Carvallo sign)**, not radiating to the axilla; an RV S3 also increases on inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other signs of TR**: left parasternal heave, loud P2 with pulmonary hypertension, ascites, right pleural effusion, pitting oedema, jaundice and peripheral cyanosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TS**: giant a waves with a **slow y descent** in sinus rhythm, a soft mid-diastolic rumble at the lower left sternal edge louder on inspiration, and congestion out of proportion to breathlessness in a patient with MS. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PS**: ejection systolic murmur and thrill at the left 2nd space, an **ejection click that softens with inspiration** (the only right-sided sound to do so), wide split S2 with soft P2, prominent a wave and RV heave. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PR**: high-pitched early diastolic **Graham Steell murmur** with loud P2 in pulmonary hypertension; a low-pitched, short, delayed diastolic murmur in low-pressure PR after Tetralogy repair. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**TR versus MR**: MR is apical, radiates to the axilla and does not increase on inspiration; TR is at the lower sternal edge with cv waves and a pulsatile liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TR versus VSD**: a VSD murmur is harsh with a thrill at the left 3rd-4th space, unchanged with respiration, with a normal JVP. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TS versus MS**: the MS rumble is apical, heard in the left lateral position with a loud S1 and opening snap, and does not increase on inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PS versus ASD flow murmur**: ASD gives fixed wide splitting of S2 without an ejection click; an innocent flow murmur has neither a click nor wide splitting. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PS versus AS**: the AS murmur is at the right 2nd space radiating to the carotids, and its click does not vary with respiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Graham Steell versus AR**: AR has a wide pulse pressure and peripheral signs and does not increase on inspiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right heart failure from TR versus constrictive pericarditis or cirrhosis**: constriction gives Kussmaul sign and a pericardial knock without a murmur; cirrhosis has a normal JVP. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ECG**: P pulmonale (over 2.5 mm in lead II), right axis deviation and RVH (PS, pulmonary hypertension), incomplete RBBB and AF; **Ebstein** shows giant P waves, RBBB and sometimes pre-excitation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: enlarged right atrium (right heart border) and RV, right pleural effusion; in valvular PS a **post-stenotic dilatation of the main and left pulmonary artery**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo**: mechanism (leaflet, annulus, lead), severity, annulus diameter, RV size and function (abnormal if **TAPSE under 17 mm**, S' under 9.5 cm/s or FAC under 35%), RA size and IVC. [ASE 2015 Chamber]`,`**Estimated PA systolic pressure** = 4 x (TR peak velocity)² + RA pressure; a dilated IVC (over 21 mm) collapsing under 50% means RA pressure about 15 mmHg. [ASE 2015 Chamber]`,`**3D echo and TOE** define leaflet anatomy, coaptation gap and lead position - essential before transcatheter repair or replacement. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cardiac MRI** is the reference for RV volumes, RV EF and regurgitant fraction (especially PR after Tetralogy); **CT** sizes the annulus for transcatheter replacement. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Right heart catheterisation** before intervention for severe TR: measures PA pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance - severe pre-capillary PH makes isolated tricuspid intervention futile. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Blood tests**: bilirubin, albumin and liver enzymes, creatinine, sodium and potassium, NT-proBNP; blood cultures if febrile; **24-hour urinary 5-HIAA** for suspected carcinoid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**6-minute walk or exercise testing** objectively measures functional capacity in patients who underreport symptoms. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Mild or moderate TR without symptoms**: treat the cause and review with echo; the TR itself needs no drug. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Salt under 5 g/day**, daily weights and fluid of about 1.5 litres/day when congestion or hyponatraemia is present. [ESC HF 2021]`,`**Loop diuretic**: furosemide 20-80 mg orally once or twice daily, or **torsemide 10-40 mg daily** (better absorbed with gut oedema), titrated to weight and JVP. [ESC HF 2021]`,`**Mineralocorticoid antagonist**: spironolactone 25-50 mg daily counters the secondary hyperaldosteronism of hepatic congestion; check potassium and creatinine at 1 week and then monthly. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Avoid over-diuresis**: the RV is preload-dependent, so excess diuresis causes hypotension and pre-renal AKI. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Treat the cause**: correct mitral disease (balloon mitral valvotomy before TR becomes irreversible), full heart failure GDMT including SGLT2 inhibitors, and PAH-specific drugs via a PH centre. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**AF**: control rate, and consider rhythm control in **atrial TR**, where restoring sinus rhythm can shrink the annulus and reduce TR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Lead-related TR**: refer to the device team - options include lead repositioning or extraction and leadless pacing. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Mild PS** or physiological PR needs no treatment and no activity restriction - reassure and review periodically. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Prevention**: rheumatic secondary prophylaxis where relevant and **harm reduction** (needle-syringe programmes, opioid substitution) for people who inject drugs. [NACO 2023]`]},{heading:`When to intervene: tricuspid regurgitation and stenosis`,points:[`**Severe TR at the time of left-sided valve surgery**: repair the tricuspid valve at the same operation (Class I). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Mild or moderate secondary TR with a dilated annulus** (40 mm or more, or over 21 mm/m²) during left-sided surgery: tricuspid repair should be considered. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Isolated severe primary TR**: surgery for symptoms or progressive RV dilatation, ideally **before severe RV dysfunction** sets in. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Isolated severe secondary TR**: surgery is considered in symptomatic patients or those with RV dilatation, provided there is **no severe RV or LV dysfunction and no severe pulmonary vascular disease**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Timing is everything**: isolated tricuspid surgery carries an in-hospital mortality of about **8-10%**, largely because patients arrive late with RV, liver and kidney damage. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TRI-SCORE** estimates surgical risk from age 70 or more, NYHA III-IV, right heart failure signs, furosemide 125 mg/day or more, eGFR under 30, raised bilirubin, LVEF under 60% and RV dysfunction. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Operation**: ring annuloplasty is preferred to suture (De Vega) annuloplasty; replacement uses a **bioprosthesis** in preference to a mechanical valve because of thrombosis risk in the low-pressure right heart. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Transcatheter options** for symptomatic severe TR at high surgical risk: **edge-to-edge repair (T-TEER: TriClip, PASCAL)**, orthotopic **replacement (EVOQUE)**, heterotopic caval valves, and valve-in-valve for a failed bioprosthesis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Evidence**: TRILUMINATE (2023) and TRISCEND II (2024) showed less TR and better quality of life than medical therapy alone, without a proven mortality benefit so far. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Tricuspid stenosis**: intervene for symptomatic severe TS, usually at left-sided valve surgery; balloon commissurotomy suits selected isolated rheumatic TS without significant TR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Pulmonary stenosis and regurgitation: when to intervene`,points:[`**Balloon valvuloplasty** is the treatment of choice for valvular PS with a mobile dome-shaped valve, with excellent long-term results. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Intervene for severe PS** - peak gradient **over 64 mmHg** (velocity over 4 m/s) - even without symptoms, provided RV function is normal. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Lower gradients** warrant intervention if there are symptoms, falling RV function, important arrhythmia or right-to-left shunting through an ASD or VSD. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Surgery** is preferred for a **dysplastic valve (Noonan)**, hypoplastic annulus, subvalvular or supravalvular stenosis, or when another lesion needs repair. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`After valvuloplasty, a dynamic infundibular gradient usually regresses over months; a **beta-blocker** helps if it causes symptoms, and mild PR is common and benign. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PR from pulmonary hypertension** is managed by treating the pulmonary hypertension; the PR itself rarely needs intervention. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PR after Tetralogy repair**: pulmonary valve replacement for symptomatic severe PR, or without symptoms when the RV dilates (**RVEDVi 160 mL/m² or more, or RVESVi 80 mL/m² or more**), RV function falls, TR progresses or sustained arrhythmia appears. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Transcatheter pulmonary valve implantation** (Melody, SAPIEN, Harmony) is preferred where RVOT anatomy allows; otherwise surgical replacement. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`In repaired Tetralogy, a **QRS duration of 180 ms or more** predicts ventricular tachycardia and sudden death - a classic viva fact. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Carcinoid heart disease`,points:[`**Carcinoid heart disease** develops in about **20-50%** of patients with carcinoid syndrome - usually a midgut neuroendocrine tumour with **liver metastases**. [ENETS 2022]`,`**Mechanism**: serotonin, tachykinins and growth factors released from liver metastases bypass hepatic inactivation and reach the right heart; the **lungs inactivate them**, so left-sided valves are usually spared. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Left-sided involvement** suggests a patent foramen ovale, a bronchial primary or very high serotonin levels. [ENETS 2022]`,`**Pathology**: fibrous plaques on the endocardium and leaflets make the tricuspid and pulmonary leaflets **thickened, retracted and immobile** - fixed in a half-open position, causing **TR (almost always), TS, PS and PR**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Clinical picture**: flushing, diarrhoea and bronchospasm plus right heart failure; the TR murmur may be soft because the leaflets do not coapt at all. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Screening**: NT-proBNP and echo in every patient with carcinoid syndrome or a high urinary 5-HIAA, repeated periodically. [ENETS 2022]`,`**Medical treatment**: **octreotide LAR 20-30 mg IM every 4 weeks** or **lanreotide 120 mg deep SC every 4 weeks** controls the syndrome; telotristat ethyl 250 mg three times daily for refractory diarrhoea where available; diuretics for congestion. [ENETS 2022]`,`**Valve surgery** (tricuspid and pulmonary replacement) for symptomatic severe disease or progressive RV dilatation in patients with controlled tumour; give **IV octreotide 50-100 microgram/hour** perioperatively to prevent carcinoid crisis. [ENETS 2022]`,`Carcinoid heart disease roughly **halves survival** compared with carcinoid syndrome without cardiac involvement; timely valve surgery improves symptoms and outlook in selected patients. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`**Admit** for decompensated right heart failure with anasarca, AKI, symptomatic hyponatraemia or hypotension, suspected endocarditis, or syncope or cyanosis in severe PS. [ESC HF 2021]`,`**IV loop diuretic**: start at 1-2 times the total daily oral dose as IV furosemide (bolus or infusion); check **spot urine sodium at 2 hours** - under 50-70 mmol/L means an inadequate response, so double the dose. [ESC HF 2021]`,`**Diuretic resistance**: add **acetazolamide 500 mg IV** daily (ADVOR) or a thiazide such as metolazone 2.5-5 mg, watching potassium and sodium closely. [ESC HF 2023]`,`**Monitor** daily weight, intake-output, creatinine, electrolytes and BP; perform therapeutic paracentesis for tense ascites. [ESC HF 2021]`,`**Low-output RV failure**: dobutamine 2.5-10 microgram/kg/min and treatment of the precipitant (arrhythmia, infection, pulmonary embolism, PH crisis). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Right-sided endocarditis**: 3 sets of blood cultures, then empirical **cloxacillin 2 g IV 4-hourly** (add vancomycin if MRSA is likely) - full regimens are in the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Surgery in right-sided IE** is considered for bacteraemia persisting over 7 days despite therapy, tricuspid vegetations **over 20 mm with recurrent septic pulmonary emboli**, or right heart failure from severe TR unresponsive to diuretics. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Neonate with critical PS**: duct-dependent pulmonary flow - start IV **prostaglandin E1** and transfer for urgent balloon valvuloplasty. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: TR and PR are usually well tolerated; use diuretics cautiously to protect placental flow; severe symptomatic PS can be treated by balloon valvuloplasty before or, if needed, during pregnancy. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Ebstein anomaly in pregnancy**: tolerated if acyanotic with good RV function; cyanosis, arrhythmia or heart failure raise maternal and fetal risk. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Lithium** in early pregnancy has a weak association with Ebstein anomaly - offer fetal echocardiography. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Elderly with AF**: atrial functional TR responds to rhythm control and diuretics; transcatheter therapy is an option for persistent symptoms at high surgical risk. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Children**: congenital PS is treated by balloon valvuloplasty; Noonan dysplastic valves often need surgery. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Haemodialysis**: volume overload worsens functional TR, and tunnelled catheters predispose to tricuspid endocarditis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Pacemaker patients**: new right heart failure after lead implantation warrants an echo for lead-related TR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: the JVP waveform, pulsatile liver and inspiratory change of the murmur identify TR at the bedside; start diuretics and refer for echo. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Complications**: right heart failure, congestive hepatopathy and **cardiac cirrhosis**, cardiorenal syndrome, AF, endocarditis, protein-losing enteropathy, cachexia and paradoxical embolism through a PFO. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Late TR after mitral surgery** carries a poor prognosis, and reoperation is high-risk - the reason annular dilatation is repaired at the first operation. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Refer to cardiology**: moderate or severe TR, unexplained right heart failure, TR after lead implantation, PS gradient over 64 mmHg or symptoms, and every adult with repaired Tetralogy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**A practical schedule**: severe asymptomatic TR - clinical review and echo every 6-12 months for RV size and function; moderate TR - every 1-2 years. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Repaired Tetralogy**: lifelong review in an adult congenital heart disease clinic with ECG (QRS width), echo, periodic CMR and Holter. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Monitor at each visit**: weight, JVP, liver size, ascites, creatinine, sodium, potassium, liver function and NT-proBNP. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Prevention**: prevent rheumatic fever (RBSK school screening, penicillin for sore throat), correct mitral stenosis before TR sets in, control AF and use careful lead implantation technique. [WHO 2024 RHD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025** classifies TR as primary, secondary ventricular, secondary atrial and CIED-related, and pushes earlier referral before RV failure and end-organ damage. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** expands the role of **transcatheter tricuspid therapy** (T-TEER, transcatheter replacement) for symptomatic severe TR unsuitable for surgery, decided by a Heart Valve Centre team. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Trials behind the change**: TRILUMINATE Pivotal (NEJM 2023), TRISCEND II (NEJM 2024) and Tri.fr (2024) - consistent gains in quality of life and TR grade. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**TRI-SCORE** is now widely used to predict in-hospital mortality of isolated tricuspid surgery and to choose surgery or transcatheter therapy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** sets right-sided IE surgery criteria (vegetation over 20 mm with emboli, persistent bacteraemia, refractory right heart failure). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ENETS 2022 guidance** recommends NT-proBNP and echo screening for carcinoid heart disease and multidisciplinary timing of valve surgery. [ENETS 2022]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022 PH guideline** lowered the PH threshold to **mean PA pressure over 20 mmHg** (PVR over 2 Wood units) - relevant when grading secondary TR. [ESC/ERS 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Carvallo sign**: inspiratory increase of the TR murmur - lost in severe RV failure, a frequently asked follow-up. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hepatic pulsation is systolic in TR and presystolic in TS** - the timing names the lesion. [Harrison 22e]`,`**The only right-sided event that softens on inspiration** is the pulmonary ejection click. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TS protects the lungs** in mitral stenosis: breathlessness improves as congestion shifts to the liver and legs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling ascites as cirrhosis without looking at the JVP - always look for cv waves and a pulsatile liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: assuming a soft murmur means mild TR - torrential TR can be nearly silent. [ASE 2017]`,`**Common mistake**: trusting the echo-estimated PA pressure in torrential TR - it underestimates; catheterise before intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Classic questions**: causes of functional TR, carcinoid heart disease, indications for tricuspid repair at mitral surgery, and the management of valvular PS. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in right-sided valve disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`20-80 mg oral once or twice daily; IV at 1-2 times oral daily dose in decompensation`,`Titrate to weight and JVP`,`Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, pre-renal AKI, ototoxicity at high IV doses`],[`Torsemide`,`10-40 mg oral once daily`,`Long term; preferred with gut oedema`,`Electrolyte loss, hypotension`],[`Spironolactone`,`25-50 mg oral once daily`,`Long term; K and creatinine at 1 week, then monthly`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia; avoid if eGFR under 30 or K over 5.0`],[`Acetazolamide`,`500 mg IV once daily`,`Days, with loop diuretic for resistance`,`Metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia; sulfonamide allergy`],[`Metolazone`,`2.5-5 mg oral daily or alternate days`,`Short courses only`,`Profound hyponatraemia and hypokalaemia`],[`Dobutamine`,`2.5-10 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Hours to days in low-output RV failure`,`Tachyarrhythmia, hypotension`],[`Octreotide LAR`,`20-30 mg IM every 4 weeks`,`Long term in carcinoid syndrome`,`Gallstones, steatorrhoea, hyperglycaemia, bradycardia`],[`Lanreotide autogel`,`120 mg deep SC every 4 weeks`,`Long term in carcinoid syndrome`,`Gallstones, abdominal pain, injection-site reactions`],[`Octreotide IV (perioperative)`,`50-100 microgram/hour IV infusion`,`Before, during and after valve surgery`,`Prevents carcinoid crisis; hypoglycaemia, bradycardia`],[`Cloxacillin`,`2 g IV every 4 hours`,`Per IE topic (methicillin-sensitive S. aureus)`,`Phlebitis, hepatitis; penicillin allergy`],[`Prostaglandin E1 (alprostadil)`,`0.05-0.1 microgram/kg/min IV`,`Until balloon valvuloplasty in critical neonatal PS`,`Apnoea, fever, hypotension`]]},{heading:`Types of tricuspid regurgitation (ESC/EACTS 2025)`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Typical causes`,`Treatment implication`],rows:[[`Primary`,`Diseased leaflets or chordae`,`Rheumatic, endocarditis, carcinoid, Ebstein, prolapse, trauma`,`Repair or replace; treat the cause`],[`Secondary ventricular`,`RV dilatation and leaflet tethering`,`Left heart disease, pulmonary hypertension, RV infarction`,`Treat the cause; surgery only without severe PH or RV failure`],[`Secondary atrial`,`RA and annular dilatation with normal RV`,`Long-standing AF, HFpEF, elderly`,`Rhythm control, annuloplasty, transcatheter repair`],[`CIED-related`,`Lead impingement, adhesion or perforation`,`Pacemaker or ICD lead across the valve`,`Device team review; lead extraction, leadless pacing or surgery`]]},{heading:`Echo severity cut-offs for right-sided lesions`,columns:[`Lesion and parameter`,`Mild`,`Moderate`,`Severe`],rows:[[`TR vena contracta`,`Under 3 mm`,`3-6.9 mm`,`7 mm or more (massive 14-20, torrential 21 or more)`],[`TR EROA (PISA)`,`Under 0.20 cm²`,`0.20-0.39 cm²`,`0.40 cm² or more`],[`TR regurgitant volume`,`Under 30 mL`,`30-44 mL`,`45 mL or more`],[`Hepatic vein flow`,`Systolic dominance`,`Systolic blunting`,`Systolic flow reversal`],[`TS mean gradient / area`,`Not graded`,`Not graded`,`Mean gradient 5 mmHg or more; area 1.0 cm² or less`],[`PS peak velocity (gradient)`,`Under 3 m/s (under 36 mmHg)`,`3-4 m/s (36-64 mmHg)`,`Over 4 m/s (over 64 mmHg)`],[`PR pressure half-time`,`Long, gradual deceleration`,`Intermediate`,`Under 100 ms; branch PA diastolic flow reversal`]]},{heading:`Right-sided valve lesions at the bedside`,columns:[`Lesion`,`JVP`,`Murmur and site`,`Respiratory change`,`Other clues`],rows:[[`Tricuspid regurgitation`,`Large cv wave, steep y descent`,`Pansystolic, lower left sternal edge`,`Louder on inspiration (Carvallo)`,`Systolic hepatic pulsation, RV heave, ascites`],[`Tricuspid stenosis`,`Giant a wave, slow y descent`,`Mid-diastolic rumble, lower left sternal edge`,`Louder on inspiration`,`Presystolic hepatic pulsation; lungs clear despite MS`],[`Pulmonary stenosis`,`Prominent a wave`,`Ejection systolic with thrill, left 2nd space`,`Click softer on inspiration`,`Wide split S2, soft P2, RVH, post-stenotic PA dilatation`],[`Pulmonary regurgitation (high pressure)`,`Raised if RV failure`,`Early diastolic (Graham Steell), left 2nd space`,`Louder on inspiration`,`Loud P2, signs of pulmonary hypertension`],[`Pulmonary regurgitation (low pressure)`,`Usually normal`,`Short, low-pitched, delayed diastolic`,`Louder on inspiration`,`Scar of Tetralogy repair; RV dilatation on CMR`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,columns:[`Condition`,`Shared feature`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Mitral regurgitation`,`Pansystolic murmur`,`Apical, radiates to axilla, no inspiratory increase, no cv waves`],[`Ventricular septal defect`,`Pansystolic murmur at left sternal edge`,`Harsh with thrill, no respiratory change, normal JVP`],[`Mitral stenosis`,`Mid-diastolic rumble`,`Apical, loud S1 and opening snap, no inspiratory increase`],[`Atrial septal defect`,`Pulmonary ejection murmur`,`Fixed wide split S2, no ejection click`],[`Aortic regurgitation`,`Early diastolic murmur`,`Wide pulse pressure, peripheral signs, no inspiratory increase`],[`Constrictive pericarditis`,`Raised JVP, ascites, oedema`,`Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock, no murmur`],[`Cirrhosis of liver`,`Ascites, oedema, jaundice`,`JVP not raised; stigmata of chronic liver disease`]]},{heading:`When to intervene on right-sided valves`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Intervention indicated`,`Preferred procedure`],rows:[[`Severe TR at left-sided valve surgery`,`Always (Class I)`,`Ring annuloplasty at the same operation`],[`Mild-moderate TR with annulus 40 mm or more at left-sided surgery`,`Should be considered`,`Ring annuloplasty`],[`Isolated severe primary TR`,`Symptoms or progressive RV dilatation, before severe RV dysfunction`,`Repair; bioprosthetic replacement if not repairable`],[`Isolated severe secondary TR`,`Symptoms or RV dilatation without severe PH or RV/LV failure`,`Surgery if low risk; T-TEER or transcatheter replacement if high risk`],[`Severe TS`,`Symptomatic`,`Surgery with left-sided repair; balloon commissurotomy in selected cases`],[`Valvular PS`,`Peak gradient over 64 mmHg, or lower with symptoms, RV dysfunction or shunt`,`Balloon valvuloplasty; surgery for dysplastic valve`],[`PR after Tetralogy repair`,`Symptoms, RVEDVi 160 or RVESVi 80 mL/m² or more, RV dysfunction, arrhythmia`,`Transcatheter or surgical pulmonary valve replacement`]]}],redFlags:[`Rapidly increasing ascites, anasarca or jaundice with a raised JVP - decompensated right heart failure`,`Fever with a new TR murmur in a person who injects drugs or has a dialysis catheter - right-sided endocarditis`,`Recurrent pneumonia-like shadows or haemoptysis with a tricuspid vegetation - septic pulmonary emboli`,`Rising creatinine with hypotension on diuretics in severe TR - preload-dependent RV, cardiorenal syndrome`,`Syncope, exertional chest pain or cyanosis in pulmonary stenosis - severe obstruction or right-to-left shunt`,`Cyanosed neonate with a pulmonary ejection murmur - critical PS, start prostaglandin and transfer`,`Sustained palpitations or syncope in an adult with repaired Tetralogy - ventricular tachycardia risk`,`Flushing, diarrhoea and a new right-sided murmur - carcinoid heart disease`],pearls:[`**80-90% of significant TR is functional** - look for the left heart, lung or AF cause first.`,`**Carvallo sign**: TR murmur louder on inspiration; lost when the RV fails.`,`**Systolic hepatic pulsation = TR; presystolic = TS.**`,`**Pulmonary ejection click is the only right-sided sound that softens on inspiration.**`,`**Torrential TR can be silent** and makes Doppler underestimate PA pressure.`,`**Repair the tricuspid at mitral surgery if the annulus is 40 mm or more** - reoperation later is high-risk.`,`**Carcinoid spares the left heart** because the lungs inactivate serotonin - unless there is a PFO or a bronchial carcinoid.`,`**PS over 64 mmHg (4 m/s) = balloon valvuloplasty**, even without symptoms.`,`**Repaired Tetralogy: QRS 180 ms or more predicts sudden death.**`,`Mnemonic for primary TR causes, **CREEPS**: Carcinoid, Rheumatic, Ebstein, Endocarditis, Prolapse, Stimulator (pacemaker) lead.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-q1`,stem:`A 50-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis has a pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge. Which finding best confirms that this murmur is due to tricuspid regurgitation rather than mitral regurgitation?`,options:[`Radiation of the murmur to the left axilla`,`A soft first heart sound`,`Increase in murmur intensity during inspiration`,`A third heart sound at the apex`],answer:2,explanation:`Inspiration increases venous return and RV stroke volume, so the TR murmur gets louder - the Carvallo sign. Axillary radiation is typical of MR, not TR. A soft S1 and an apical S3 can accompany either regurgitant lesion and do not localise the murmur to the right heart. The sign can be lost in severe RV failure, when the RV cannot increase its output.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man is scheduled for mitral valve replacement for rheumatic mitral stenosis. Pre-operative TTE shows moderate tricuspid regurgitation with a tricuspid annulus of 44 mm. What is the best plan for the tricuspid valve?`,options:[`Tricuspid ring annuloplasty at the same operation`,`Leave it alone because TR will regress once the mitral valve is fixed`,`Mechanical tricuspid valve replacement at the same operation`,`Transcatheter edge-to-edge tricuspid repair 3 months after surgery`],answer:0,explanation:`A dilated annulus of 40 mm or more (or over 21 mm/m²) predicts progression of secondary TR after left-sided surgery, so concomitant ring annuloplasty should be considered even when TR is only mild or moderate. Leaving it relies on regression that often does not occur, and late reoperation for TR is high-risk. Mechanical tricuspid replacement is avoided because of thrombosis risk, and a staged transcatheter repair adds a procedure that is not needed.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old man with flushing, diarrhoea and hepatic metastases from a small-bowel neuroendocrine tumour develops right heart failure. Echo shows thickened, retracted, immobile tricuspid and pulmonary leaflets with normal mitral and aortic valves. Why are the left-sided valves usually spared?`,options:[`Left-sided valves have thicker collagen that resists fibrosis`,`Higher left-sided pressures wash plaques off the leaflets`,`Somatostatin is secreted by the lungs and protects the left heart`,`The lungs inactivate serotonin and related vasoactive substances`],answer:3,explanation:`Vasoactive products from liver metastases reach the right heart directly, but monoamine oxidase in the lungs inactivates serotonin before blood reaches the left heart, so left-sided disease suggests a PFO, a bronchial primary or very high hormone levels. Leaflet collagen thickness and pressure do not explain the distribution. The lungs do not secrete protective somatostatin; somatostatin analogues are given as treatment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-q4`,stem:`A 22-year-old asymptomatic student has an ejection systolic murmur at the left 2nd intercostal space with an ejection click that softens on inspiration. Echo shows a mobile dome-shaped pulmonary valve with a peak gradient of 72 mmHg and normal RV function. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Observation with echo every 5 years because he has no symptoms`,`Percutaneous balloon pulmonary valvuloplasty`,`Surgical pulmonary valve replacement`,`Beta-blocker to reduce the outflow gradient`],answer:1,explanation:`A peak gradient over 64 mmHg (velocity over 4 m/s) is severe valvular PS, and intervention is indicated even without symptoms; balloon valvuloplasty is the treatment of choice for a mobile dome-shaped valve. Observation would leave severe RV pressure overload untreated. Surgery is reserved for dysplastic valves (Noonan) or complex anatomy. Beta-blockers help only dynamic infundibular obstruction after valvuloplasty, not fixed valvular stenosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-q5`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman with permanent AF has massive ascites and torrential tricuspid regurgitation on echo. The TR murmur is very soft and the estimated PA systolic pressure from the TR jet is 28 mmHg. Before any tricuspid intervention, what is the next step?`,options:[`Accept the PA pressure as normal and proceed to repair`,`Repeat transthoracic echo after 6 months of diuretics`,`Right heart catheterisation to measure PA pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance`,`CT pulmonary angiography to exclude pulmonary embolism`],answer:2,explanation:`In torrential TR, RA and RV pressures equalise and the jet is laminar, so the Bernoulli estimate underestimates PA pressure; severe pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension would make isolated tricuspid intervention futile, so invasive measurement is needed. Accepting the echo figure risks operating on a patient who will not benefit. Delaying 6 months allows RV and liver damage to progress. CT pulmonary angiography does not measure pulmonary vascular resistance.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Enumerate the causes of tricuspid regurgitation. Describe its clinical features, evaluation and management, including the role of transcatheter therapy.`,openingLines:[`Tricuspid regurgitation is systolic backflow from the right ventricle into the right atrium; in 80-90% it is secondary (functional), from RV or RA dilatation with structurally normal leaflets.`,`It is not benign - survival falls with each grade - and intervention must come before RV failure and liver and kidney damage set in.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Causes (ESC/EACTS 2025 types)`,points:[`**Secondary ventricular**: left heart disease (rheumatic MS, MR, LV failure), pulmonary hypertension, RV infarction. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Secondary atrial**: long-standing AF, HFpEF. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CIED-related**: pacemaker or ICD lead. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Primary**: rheumatic, endocarditis, carcinoid, Ebstein, prolapse, trauma, endomyocardial fibrosis. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`2. Pathophysiology in brief`,points:[`Annular dilatation along the free wall and leaflet tethering; **TR begets TR**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Systemic venous congestion: hepatopathy, ascites, cardiorenal syndrome, gut oedema. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`3. Clinical features`,points:[`Fatigue, oedema, abdominal distension, right upper quadrant pain, anorexia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Large cv wave**, systolic hepatic pulsation, RV heave, ascites, jaundice. [Harrison 22e]`,`Pansystolic murmur at lower left sternal edge, **louder on inspiration (Carvallo)**; may be silent if torrential. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`4. Evaluation`,points:[`ECG (P pulmonale, AF, RBBB), CXR (RA and RV enlargement). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**TTE**: mechanism, severity (vena contracta 7 mm or more, EROA 0.40 cm² or more, hepatic vein systolic reversal), annulus size, RV function (TAPSE), PASP. [ASE 2017]`,`TOE or 3D echo, CMR for RV volumes, CT for transcatheter planning, **right heart catheterisation** before intervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`LFT, renal function, NT-proBNP; 5-HIAA if carcinoid suspected. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`5. Medical management`,points:[`Salt restriction, **loop diuretic** (furosemide or torsemide) plus **spironolactone**; avoid over-diuresis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Treat the cause: mitral intervention, heart failure GDMT, PH therapy, AF rhythm control, lead management. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`6. Surgery and transcatheter therapy`,points:[`Severe TR at left-sided surgery: repair (Class I); moderate TR with annulus 40 mm or more: repair considered. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`Isolated severe TR: surgery for symptoms or RV dilatation before severe RV dysfunction; ring annuloplasty, bioprosthesis if replaced. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`High surgical risk (TRI-SCORE): **T-TEER** (TriClip, PASCAL) or transcatheter replacement (EVOQUE) - better quality of life in TRILUMINATE and TRISCEND II. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]}],mustDraw:[`JVP waveform showing the large cv wave and steep y descent of TR`,`Flow chart: from left heart disease and pulmonary hypertension to RV dilatation, annular dilatation and TR`,`Table of TR types with their treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Causes and classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Evaluation`,marks:2},{part:`Medical management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Surgical and transcatheter options`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`tricuspid regurgitation`,`Carvallo sign`,`annuloplasty`,`T-TEER`,`TRI-SCORE`,`functional TR`]},{id:`cardiovascular-right-sided-valve-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`uncommon`,question:`Write a short note on carcinoid heart disease.`,openingLines:[`Carcinoid heart disease is fibrotic plaque deposition on the right-sided valves and endocardium in patients with carcinoid syndrome, usually from a midgut neuroendocrine tumour with liver metastases.`,`It affects about a fifth to a half of patients with carcinoid syndrome, causes tricuspid regurgitation and pulmonary valve disease, and roughly halves survival.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Pathogenesis`,points:[`Serotonin and tachykinins from liver metastases reach the right heart; **lungs inactivate them**, sparing the left heart unless PFO or bronchial primary. [Harrison 22e]`,`Plaques make leaflets **thickened, retracted and immobile**: TR, TS, PS, PR. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`2. Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm plus right heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`Raised urinary 5-HIAA; **NT-proBNP and echo screening**; CMR for RV assessment. [ENETS 2022]`]},{heading:`3. Management`,points:[`**Octreotide LAR 20-30 mg IM** or **lanreotide 120 mg SC** every 4 weeks; diuretics. [ENETS 2022]`,`Valve replacement for symptomatic severe disease with controlled tumour, under **IV octreotide 50-100 microgram/hour** cover to prevent carcinoid crisis. [ENETS 2022]`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram: liver metastasis to right heart to lungs (inactivation), showing why left valves are spared`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and pathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2}],keywords:[`carcinoid heart disease`,`5-HIAA`,`serotonin`,`octreotide`,`tricuspid regurgitation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Tricuspid and pulmonic valve disease`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Tricuspid, pulmonic and multivalvular disease`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Valvular Heart Disease`,`2020 ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease`,`Zoghbi WA et al. Recommendations for noninvasive evaluation of native valvular regurgitation, American Society of Echocardiography, 2017`,`ENETS 2022 guidance paper for carcinoid syndrome and carcinoid heart disease (Journal of Neuroendocrinology, 2022)`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`]}),s.push({id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis`,title:`Infective endocarditis`,oneLiner:`**Infective endocarditis (IE)** is microbial infection of the endocardial surface - native or prosthetic valves, cardiac devices or shunts - forming **vegetations** that destroy valves, embolise and seed the blood; it is diagnosed by the **ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria** (blood cultures plus echo, CT or PET), treated with **prolonged bactericidal IV antibiotics (2-6 weeks)** chosen by organism, and needs **surgery** for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`IE`,`SBE`,`bacterial endocarditis`,`modified Duke criteria`,`Duke-ISCVID`,`vegetation`,`Osler nodes`,`Janeway lesions`,`Roth spots`,`splinter haemorrhages`,`viridans streptococci`,`Staphylococcus aureus`,`Enterococcus faecalis`,`HACEK`,`culture-negative endocarditis`,`prosthetic valve endocarditis`,`OPAT`,`POET trial`,`endocarditis prophylaxis`,`amoxicillin 2 g`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Infective endocarditis** is infection of the endocardial surface of the heart - native valves, prosthetic valves, cardiac implantable electronic devices (CIEDs), septal defects or shunts - with a **vegetation** of platelets, fibrin and micro-organisms as its hallmark. [Harrison 22e]`,`Global incidence is about **3-10 per 100,000 per year**, rising with age, prosthetic valves, devices, haemodialysis and injecting drug use; in-hospital mortality remains **15-30%**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In high-income countries **Staphylococcus aureus** is now the commonest cause and patients are older, with degenerative valves, prostheses and healthcare contact. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In **India**, patients are younger, the commonest substrate is still **rheumatic heart disease** followed by congenital heart disease, and **viridans streptococci and staphylococci** dominate. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Culture-negative IE** is far commoner in Indian series - often 30% or more - mainly because antibiotics are given before blood cultures are drawn. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Healthcare-associated IE** (central lines, haemodialysis, cardiac surgery, devices) is rising in Indian tertiary centres, bringing S. aureus, enterococci, gram-negative bacilli and Candida. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`About **one-quarter to one-half** of patients undergo cardiac surgery during the admission, so IE is managed by a multidisciplinary **Endocarditis Team**. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Aetiology, risk factors and classification`,points:[`**Cardiac risk factors (highest)**: previous IE, **prosthetic valve** or valve-repair material, transcatheter valve, unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease, and ventricular assist devices; **intermediate**: rheumatic heart disease, degenerative and bicuspid valves, mitral prolapse with regurgitation, HCM and CIEDs. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Non-cardiac risk factors**: injecting drug use, haemodialysis, central venous catheters, poor dental hygiene, diabetes, immunosuppression, HIV and older age. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Organisms**: **oral (viridans) streptococci** after dental bacteraemia (including daily brushing); **Staphylococcus aureus** - acute, destructive IE in drug users, dialysis and line patients; **coagulase-negative staphylococci** in early prosthetic IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis)** is linked with **colorectal neoplasia**, **Enterococcus faecalis** affects elderly men after GU or bowel procedures, and **HACEK** organisms (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) give large vegetations. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Culture-negative causes**: prior antibiotics (commonest), Coxiella burnetii (Q fever), Bartonella, Brucella, Tropheryma whipplei and fungi (Candida, Aspergillus after long-term lines, parenteral nutrition or immunosuppression). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Classification by site**: left-sided native valve IE, **prosthetic valve IE** - **early** (within 1 year, mostly perioperative staphylococci) or **late** (after 1 year, community spectrum) - right-sided IE and **CIED-related IE**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**By acquisition**: community-acquired, healthcare-associated (nosocomial or non-nosocomial) and injecting drug use-associated. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**By tempo**: **acute** IE (S. aureus; days, toxic, destructive) versus **subacute** IE (viridans streptococci; weeks to months, low-grade fever, weight loss, immune signs). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By activity**: active versus healed; recurrence is **relapse** (same organism within about 6 months) or **reinfection** (different organism or later). [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Endothelial injury** from a high-velocity regurgitant jet, a catheter or injected particulate exposes subendothelial matrix, on which platelets and fibrin deposit as **non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transient bacteraemia** (dental, gut, skin, urinary) seeds this thrombus; streptococci adhere via surface adhesins and S. aureus via fibrinogen- and fibronectin-binding proteins, even to intact endothelium. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Vegetations sit on the **low-pressure side of a jet** - atrial surface of the mitral, ventricular surface of the aortic valve - where turbulence deposits organisms. [Harrison 22e]`,`Inside the avascular vegetation organisms reach **very high density in a biofilm**, are metabolically slow and shielded from phagocytes - hence **prolonged, high-dose, bactericidal** therapy. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Local destruction**: leaflet perforation and chordal rupture (acute regurgitation, heart failure), and perivalvular extension to **abscess, pseudoaneurysm and fistula** - which can cut the conduction system and lengthen the PR interval. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolism**: fragments of vegetation lodge in brain, spleen, kidney, limbs or (right-sided IE) lungs, and infected emboli weaken arterial walls to form **mycotic aneurysms**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Immune-complex disease**: persistent antigenaemia drives glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots, low complement and a positive rheumatoid factor. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Embolic risk is highest in the first 2 weeks** and falls steeply once effective antibiotics start - the reason early surgery is considered for large vegetations. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Fever** is present in about 90% - in subacute IE often low-grade with night sweats, anorexia, weight loss, arthralgia and fatigue lasting weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fever plus a heart murmur, prosthetic valve or device = IE until proved otherwise** - draw blood cultures before any antibiotic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic presentations**: stroke or TIA, sudden limb ischaemia, left upper quadrant pain (splenic infarct), loin pain and haematuria (renal infarct), or headache from a mycotic aneurysm. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heart failure** from acute valve destruction presents as breathlessness, orthopnoea or pulmonary oedema - the commonest reason for surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE** in people who inject drugs presents with fever, cough, pleuritic pain and haemoptysis from **septic pulmonary emboli**, often without a murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations**: the elderly, immunosuppressed or those on antibiotics may be afebrile; IE is often missed as **PUO, rheumatic activity, stroke or glomerulonephritis**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**History to take**: known valve disease or rheumatic fever, prior IE, prosthetic valve or device, recent dental work, catheters, dialysis, injecting drug use, and antibiotics already taken. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**New or changing regurgitant murmur** in most left-sided IE; a murmur may be absent in right-sided IE and early acute IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Petechiae** (conjunctiva, palate, skin) are the commonest peripheral sign; **splinter haemorrhages** are linear dark-red streaks under the nail bed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Osler nodes**: tender, pea-sized nodules in the finger and toe pulps - immune-complex vasculitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Janeway lesions**: painless, flat haemorrhagic macules on the palms and soles - septic micro-emboli, commoner in acute S. aureus IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Roth spots**: retinal haemorrhages with a pale centre on fundoscopy - also seen in leukaemia and severe anaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Splenomegaly** and **clubbing** appear in long-standing subacute IE; pallor reflects anaemia of chronic inflammation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Look for complications**: signs of heart failure, focal neurological deficit, absent limb pulses, splenic tenderness and a **prolonged PR or new AV block** on the ECG (root abscess). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Look for the portal of entry**: dental caries and periodontitis, injection marks, line sites, skin infection and the dialysis access. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute rheumatic fever (recurrence)**: fever, arthritis and a murmur, but cultures are negative, ASO is raised and there are no embolic phenomena. [WHO 2024 RHD]`,`**Non-bacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis**: sterile vegetations in cancer or hypercoagulable states, with emboli but negative cultures. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Libman-Sacks endocarditis and antiphospholipid syndrome**: sterile vegetations with positive ANA or antiphospholipid antibodies. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other causes of fever with bacteraemia**: line sepsis, osteomyelitis, pyelonephritis - S. aureus bacteraemia always needs an echo to exclude IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Atrial myxoma**: fever, emboli, weight loss and a changing murmur, but a pedunculated left atrial mass and sterile blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PUO mimics in India**: enteric fever, tuberculosis, brucellosis, malaria and lymphoma - a murmur and positive cultures point to IE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vasculitis (polyarteritis nodosa) and systemic lupus**: purpura, nephritis and neuropathy, but no vegetation and negative cultures. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Diagnosis: blood cultures and the ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria`,points:[`**Blood culture technique**: **3 sets** (aerobic plus anaerobic bottle, **10 mL per bottle**) from **separate peripheral venepunctures** with strict asepsis, ideally 30 minutes apart, **before antibiotics**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Bacteraemia in IE is continuous, so **there is no need to wait for a fever spike**; never sample from an existing line alone. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`In a septic or unstable patient draw the 3 sets within an hour and **start empirical therapy immediately**; in a stable patient already on antibiotics, discuss stopping them for 2-3 days to re-culture. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Major microbiological criterion**: typical organisms from 2 separate cultures (oral streptococci, S. gallolyticus, HACEK, S. aureus, **E. faecalis**), persistently positive cultures, or **Coxiella burnetii** in one culture or phase I IgG titre over 1:800. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Major imaging criterion**: vegetation, perforation, abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula or new prosthetic dehiscence on **echo or cardiac CT**, or abnormal paravalvular activity on **18F-FDG PET/CT** (prosthesis over 3 months old). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Minor criteria**: predisposition, fever over 38 C, embolic vascular phenomena (including silent emboli found only on imaging), immunological phenomena, and microbiological evidence not meeting a major criterion. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Definite IE** = 2 major, or 1 major plus 3 minor, or 5 minor; **possible IE** = 1 major plus 1 minor, or 3 minor. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`The **2023 Duke-ISCVID criteria** similarly add CT, PET, PCR and intra-operative findings and expand the list of typical organisms; both perform better than the 2000 modified Duke criteria. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`]},{heading:`Investigations: echo, imaging and supporting tests`,points:[`**TTE first** in every suspected case; sensitivity is about 70% for native and 50% for prosthetic valve vegetations. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**TOE** when TTE is negative but suspicion is high, in all prosthetic valve or device cases, and in most positive TTEs to look for abscess - sensitivity over 90%. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Repeat TTE/TOE within 5-7 days** if the first is negative but suspicion persists, and whenever a new complication is suspected (new murmur, embolism, persistent fever, heart failure, AV block). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Echo in all S. aureus bacteraemia**, and in E. faecalis and typical streptococcal bacteraemia, because IE is common in these. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Cardiac CT** defines abscess and pseudoaneurysm and can replace coronary angiography before surgery; **18F-FDG PET/CT** helps in prosthetic valve and device IE and finds distant foci. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Brain imaging** (CT or MRI) for neurological symptoms and before surgery; abdominal CT for splenic or renal infarcts and abscesses. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Blood and urine**: normocytic anaemia, raised WBC, ESR and CRP, microscopic haematuria and proteinuria, raised creatinine, positive rheumatoid factor and low complement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ECG** at baseline and repeatedly: new PR prolongation or AV block suggests a perivalvular aortic root abscess. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Culture-negative work-up**: serology for Coxiella, Bartonella and Brucella; ANA and antiphospholipid antibodies; and **PCR or 16S rRNA sequencing of excised valve tissue**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Colonoscopy** for S. gallolyticus IE, and should be considered for E. faecalis IE without another source; dental assessment (orthopantomogram) for all. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Antibiotic therapy: empirical and definitive`,points:[`**Start after cultures** - **community-acquired native valve IE or late PVE**: **ampicillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses plus ceftriaxone 4 g/day IV in 2 doses**, or ampicillin plus **cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses** plus **gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day IV once daily**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Penicillin allergy (community)**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV in 2 doses** (with cefazolin or gentamicin per local guidance). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Early PVE (within 1 year) or healthcare-associated IE**: **vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day IV in 2 doses** (or daptomycin 10 mg/kg/day) plus **gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day** plus **rifampicin 900-1200 mg/day** in 2-3 doses, starting rifampicin 3-5 days later. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Penicillin-susceptible oral streptococci and S. gallolyticus (MIC 0.125 mg/L or less)**: **penicillin G 12-18 million units/day IV** in 4-6 doses, or amoxicillin 100-200 mg/kg/day IV, or **ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily** - **4 weeks** native, **6 weeks** prosthetic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Two-week regimen** for uncomplicated native valve streptococcal IE with normal renal function: penicillin, amoxicillin or ceftriaxone **plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day** for 2 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MSSA native valve**: **cloxacillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses** (or cefazolin 6 g/day in 3 doses) for **4-6 weeks**; gentamicin is no longer added (nephrotoxicity without benefit). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**MRSA or penicillin allergy**: **vancomycin 30-60 mg/kg/day IV in 2-3 doses** or **daptomycin 10 mg/kg/day IV once daily** for 4-6 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Staphylococcal PVE**: cloxacillin (MSSA) or vancomycin (MRSA) for **6 weeks or more**, plus **rifampicin 900 mg/day** for 6 weeks or more, plus **gentamicin for 2 weeks**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Enterococcus faecalis**: **amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day IV plus ceftriaxone 4 g/day IV (2 g 12-hourly) for 6 weeks** - preferred, effective against high-level aminoglycoside resistance and kinder to kidneys; amoxicillin plus gentamicin for 2 weeks is the alternative. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**HACEK**: **ceftriaxone 2 g/day IV** for 4 weeks (native) or 6 weeks (prosthetic). [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Culture-negative IE, partial oral therapy and OPAT`,points:[`**Culture-negative IE**: empirical cover as for the clinical setting (community: ampicillin plus ceftriaxone, or ampicillin plus cloxacillin plus gentamicin) while serology and valve PCR are pending. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Brucella**: doxycycline 200 mg/day plus co-trimoxazole plus rifampicin for **at least 3-6 months**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Coxiella burnetii (Q fever)**: **doxycycline 200 mg/day plus hydroxychloroquine 200-600 mg/day for 18 months or more**, guided by serology. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Bartonella**: doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 4 weeks plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day for 2 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Partial oral therapy (POET trial)**: stable patients with left-sided IE from streptococci, E. faecalis, S. aureus or coagulase-negative staphylococci can switch to oral combinations after **at least 10 days of IV therapy** (7 days after surgery). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**POET switch criteria**: afebrile, falling CRP and WBC, negative cultures, no abscess on TOE and no need for surgery; examples include **amoxicillin 1 g four times daily plus moxifloxacin 400 mg daily** for streptococci. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**OPAT** (outpatient parenteral therapy) suits stable patients after the **first 2 weeks**, with no heart failure, no surgical indication, reliable IV access, home support and close review - **ceftriaxone 2 g once daily** is the classic OPAT drug in India. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**OPAT monitoring**: weekly clinical review, CBC, creatinine and CRP, a plan for any new symptom, and a named team to call. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Counting duration**: from the first day of effective therapy with negative cultures, or restart from the day of surgery if the excised valve culture is positive. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Surgery: indications and timing`,points:[`**Emergency surgery (within 24 hours)**: aortic or mitral IE with severe acute regurgitation, obstruction or fistula causing **refractory pulmonary oedema or cardiogenic shock**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Urgent surgery (within 3-5 days)**: severe regurgitation or obstruction causing **heart failure** symptoms or echo signs of poor haemodynamic tolerance. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Uncontrolled infection (urgent)**: abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, prosthetic dehiscence or **new AV block**; and infection by **fungi or multi-resistant organisms**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Persistent bacteraemia** despite appropriate therapy and control of metastatic foci, and **PVE due to S. aureus or non-HACEK gram-negatives**, should prompt surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic prevention (urgent)**: aortic or mitral vegetation **10 mm or more with one or more embolic episodes** despite appropriate therapy (Class I); vegetation 10 mm or more with severe valve dysfunction and low surgical risk is also an indication. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**After ischaemic stroke**, indicated surgery should not be delayed provided intracranial haemorrhage is excluded and the patient is not comatose; after **intracranial haemorrhage**, surgery is usually postponed for about 4 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**CIED infection**: complete removal of the device and all leads plus antibiotics; re-implant only after cultures clear, at a new site. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Right-sided IE**: surgery for bacteraemia persisting over 7 days, tricuspid vegetations **over 20 mm with recurrent septic pulmonary emboli**, or right heart failure from severe TR - repair rather than valvectomy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Endocarditis Team**: cardiologist, cardiac surgeon, infectious disease specialist, microbiologist, imaging and neurology - recommended for all complicated IE and for decisions on surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Prosthetic valve IE**: higher mortality; early PVE is usually staphylococcal; TOE and PET/CT are central and surgery is often needed - see the Prosthetic heart valves topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**People who inject drugs**: right-sided MSSA IE has a good prognosis; uncomplicated cases may be treated with **2 weeks of cloxacillin**; offer opioid substitution and harm reduction. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Pregnancy**: rare but lethal for mother and fetus; penicillins, cephalosporins and vancomycin are safe; avoid aminoglycosides where possible; surgery is reserved for life-threatening indications. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: atypical presentations, more E. faecalis and S. gallolyticus (look for colon cancer), more renal toxicity - prefer amoxicillin plus ceftriaxone for enterococci. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Haemodialysis**: S. aureus from vascular access, mitral annular calcification as substrate, and high mortality; dose vancomycin by levels after dialysis. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Children**: underlying congenital heart disease, especially after surgery with prosthetic material; doses are weight-based (for example amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**CKD**: avoid gentamicin where possible, monitor vancomycin levels and adjust doses; daptomycin needs creatine kinase monitoring. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: insist on 3 blood culture sets before antibiotics even at a district hospital, start empirical therapy, and refer early to a centre with TOE and cardiac surgery (covered by **PM-JAY**). [ICMR AMR 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Heart failure** is the commonest complication and the main reason for surgery, especially with aortic involvement. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolic events** occur in about 20-50% of patients - brain and spleen most often in left-sided IE, lungs in right-sided IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Neurological complications** (ischaemic stroke, haemorrhage, abscess, meningitis, mycotic aneurysm) occur in about 15-30% and drive mortality. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Perivalvular extension** (abscess, fistula, heart block) is commoner in aortic and prosthetic IE and usually needs surgery. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Prognosis**: in-hospital mortality is about **15-30%**; worse with S. aureus, fungi, prosthetic valves, heart failure, stroke, diabetes and old age. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Refer same day**: any patient with fever and a prosthetic valve, device or known valve disease, or with S. aureus bacteraemia. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**During treatment**: daily temperature, blood cultures every 24-48 hours until negative, weekly CRP and CBC, twice-weekly creatinine and electrolytes, drug levels and ECG for PR interval. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**At the end of therapy**: TTE as a new baseline for valve and ventricular function, and dental review to clear residual foci. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**After discharge**: review at 1, 3, 6 and 12 months; teach the patient to report fever and to have blood cultures before any antibiotic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Lifelong prophylaxis status**: everyone who has had IE is in the **highest-risk group** for future dental procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Prevention: dental prophylaxis and counselling`,points:[`**Highest-risk patients (prophylaxis recommended)**: previous IE; surgically implanted prosthetic valves or valve-repair material; **transcatheter valves**; untreated cyanotic CHD and CHD repaired with prosthetic material (for 6 months, or lifelong if residual shunt); ventricular assist devices. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Procedures needing cover**: dental work manipulating the **gingival or periapical region** or perforating the oral mucosa (scaling, extraction, root canal). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Regimen**: **amoxicillin 2 g orally or IV 30-60 minutes before** the procedure (children 50 mg/kg). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Penicillin allergy**: **azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg orally** (children 15 mg/kg), or doxycycline 100 mg orally; **clindamycin is no longer recommended** because of C. difficile and adverse reactions. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Intermediate-risk patients** (rheumatic heart disease, bicuspid valve, mitral prolapse, degenerative disease, HCM, CIEDs): prophylaxis is **not routinely** recommended but may be individualised - **oral hygiene** and 6-monthly dental review are essential. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Non-dental procedures** (GI, GU, respiratory, skin): routine prophylaxis is not recommended; treat any established infection appropriately before the procedure. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Healthcare-associated prevention**: aseptic line care, minimise catheters, and perioperative antibiotics (for example **cefazolin**) for cardiac surgery, TAVI and device implantation. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Counselling**: brush twice daily, avoid tattoos and piercings in high-risk patients, never self-medicate fever with antibiotics, and carry a prophylaxis card. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** updates the modified Duke criteria: **cardiac CT and 18F-FDG PET/CT** become major imaging criteria and **silent embolic events on imaging** count as minor. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023** makes **E. faecalis a typical organism** regardless of where it was acquired. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023** redefines **urgent surgery as within 3-5 days** (previously a few days) and emergency surgery as within 24 hours. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023** endorses **partial oral therapy** (POET) and **OPAT** in stable patients - a shift from the old rule of 4-6 weeks inpatient IV therapy. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023 prophylaxis**: transcatheter valves and ventricular assist devices added to the high-risk group, an intermediate-risk group defined, and **clindamycin removed** from alternatives. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Duke-ISCVID 2023 criteria** (International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases) add PCR, intra-operative findings and an expanded organism list - a parallel update to the ESC version. [Duke-ISCVID 2023]`,`**AHA 2021 scientific statement** on prevention of viridans streptococcal IE keeps prophylaxis for the same high-risk groups and drops clindamycin; **NICE** still advises against routine prophylaxis - a conflict worth quoting. [AHA 2021]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: modified Duke criteria - write major and minor criteria in a table and the definite/possible rules. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Osler is painful (O for Ouch), Janeway is painless** - Osler immunological, Janeway embolic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Blood cultures**: 3 sets, separate sites, 10 mL per bottle, before antibiotics - no need to wait for fever spikes. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Common mistake**: giving oral antibiotics for fever in a patient with RHD or a prosthetic valve - this creates culture-negative IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Common mistake**: adding gentamicin to MSSA native valve IE - no longer recommended. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing dental prophylaxis for every RHD patient instead of improving oral hygiene - RHD is intermediate risk in ESC 2023. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**S. gallolyticus means colonoscopy**; **new PR prolongation means aortic root abscess**. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Viva pairs**: acute versus subacute IE, native versus prosthetic IE, and empirical versus definitive regimens. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Antibiotic regimens in infective endocarditis (adult doses)`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Ampicillin + ceftriaxone (empirical, community)`,`Ampicillin 12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses + ceftriaxone 4 g/day IV in 2 doses`,`Until organism known`,`Rash, C. difficile; penicillin allergy`],[`Penicillin G (susceptible streptococci)`,`12-18 million units/day IV in 4-6 doses`,`4 weeks native; 6 weeks prosthetic`,`Hypersensitivity, seizures at high doses in renal failure`],[`Ceftriaxone (streptococci, HACEK, OPAT)`,`2 g IV or IM once daily`,`4 weeks native; 6 weeks prosthetic`,`Biliary sludge, hypersensitivity`],[`Gentamicin (synergy)`,`3 mg/kg/day IV once daily`,`2 weeks only`,`Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity; trough under 1 mg/L`],[`Cloxacillin (MSSA)`,`12 g/day IV in 4-6 doses (2 g 4-hourly)`,`4-6 weeks native; 6 weeks or more prosthetic`,`Phlebitis, hepatitis, interstitial nephritis`],[`Cefazolin (MSSA, non-severe penicillin allergy)`,`6 g/day IV in 3 doses`,`4-6 weeks`,`Hypersensitivity`],[`Vancomycin (MRSA, allergy, empirical early PVE)`,`30-60 mg/kg/day IV in 2-3 doses`,`4-6 weeks; trough 15-20 mg/L or AUC-guided`,`Nephrotoxicity, red man syndrome, ototoxicity`],[`Daptomycin (MRSA alternative)`,`10 mg/kg IV once daily`,`4-6 weeks`,`Myopathy (weekly CK), eosinophilic pneumonia`],[`Rifampicin (prosthetic staphylococcal IE)`,`900-1200 mg/day IV or oral in 2-3 doses`,`6 weeks or more; start after bacteraemia clears`,`Hepatitis, drug interactions (warfarin), orange secretions`],[`Amoxicillin + ceftriaxone (E. faecalis)`,`Amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day IV in 4-6 doses + ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly`,`6 weeks`,`Rash, C. difficile; avoids aminoglycoside toxicity`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B (Candida)`,`3-5 mg/kg/day IV`,`6 weeks or more after surgery; then oral fluconazole`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypokalaemia, infusion reactions`],[`Amoxicillin (dental prophylaxis)`,`2 g oral or IV, 30-60 min before procedure (child 50 mg/kg)`,`Single dose`,`Penicillin allergy - use azithromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 100 mg`]]},{heading:`ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria`,columns:[`Category`,`Criteria`],rows:[[`Major - microbiology`,`Typical organisms in 2 separate cultures (oral streptococci, S. gallolyticus, HACEK, S. aureus, E. faecalis); persistently positive cultures; Coxiella single culture or phase I IgG over 1:800`],[`Major - imaging`,`Vegetation, perforation, abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula or new prosthetic dehiscence on echo or cardiac CT; abnormal paravalvular uptake on FDG PET/CT`],[`Minor - predisposition`,`Predisposing heart condition (high or intermediate risk) or injecting drug use`],[`Minor - fever`,`Temperature over 38 C`],[`Minor - vascular`,`Arterial emboli (including imaging-only silent emboli), septic pulmonary infarcts, mycotic aneurysm, intracranial haemorrhage, Janeway lesions, conjunctival haemorrhage`],[`Minor - immunological`,`Glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots, rheumatoid factor`],[`Minor - microbiology`,`Positive culture not meeting a major criterion, or serology of active infection with a plausible organism`],[`Definite IE`,`2 major; or 1 major + 3 minor; or 5 minor`],[`Possible IE`,`1 major + 1 minor; or 3 minor`]]},{heading:`Organisms by clinical setting`,columns:[`Setting`,`Usual organisms`,`Clinical note`],rows:[[`Community native valve (India: rheumatic or congenital)`,`Viridans streptococci, S. aureus, enterococci`,`Subacute; dental source; high culture-negative rate after antibiotics`],[`Injecting drug use`,`S. aureus (tricuspid), Pseudomonas, Candida`,`Septic pulmonary emboli; often no murmur`],[`Early prosthetic valve (within 1 year)`,`Coagulase-negative staphylococci, S. aureus, gram-negatives, fungi`,`Perioperative; abscess and dehiscence common`],[`Late prosthetic valve (after 1 year)`,`Streptococci, S. aureus, enterococci`,`Community pattern`],[`Healthcare-associated, dialysis`,`S. aureus (including MRSA), enterococci, Candida`,`Line or access source`],[`Elderly, GI or GU disease`,`S. gallolyticus, E. faecalis`,`Colonoscopy for S. gallolyticus`],[`Culture-negative`,`Prior antibiotics, Coxiella, Bartonella, Brucella, Tropheryma, fungi`,`Serology and valve PCR`]]},{heading:`Indications and timing of surgery (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Indication`,`Timing`,`Examples`],rows:[[`Heart failure with shock or refractory pulmonary oedema`,`Emergency (within 24 hours)`,`Severe acute AR or MR, fistula, valve obstruction`],[`Heart failure with severe valve dysfunction`,`Urgent (within 3-5 days)`,`Severe regurgitation with symptoms or poor haemodynamic tolerance on echo`],[`Locally uncontrolled infection`,`Urgent`,`Abscess, pseudoaneurysm, fistula, enlarging vegetation, new AV block, dehiscence`],[`Difficult organisms`,`Urgent or elective`,`Fungi, multi-resistant organisms; PVE from S. aureus or non-HACEK gram-negatives`],[`Persistent bacteraemia`,`Urgent`,`Positive cultures despite appropriate therapy and control of metastatic foci`],[`Prevention of embolism`,`Urgent`,`Vegetation 10 mm or more with embolism despite therapy; or with severe valve dysfunction`],[`Right-sided IE`,`Individualised`,`Vegetation over 20 mm with recurrent septic emboli, persistent bacteraemia, refractory right heart failure`]]},{heading:`Dental prophylaxis: who needs it (ESC 2023)`,columns:[`Risk group`,`Conditions`,`Prophylaxis for dental procedures`],rows:[[`High risk`,`Previous IE; surgical or transcatheter prosthetic valves; valve repair with material; untreated cyanotic CHD; CHD repaired with prosthetic material (6 months or residual shunt); ventricular assist devices`,`Recommended: amoxicillin 2 g oral 30-60 min before`],[`Intermediate risk`,`Rheumatic heart disease, degenerative valve disease, bicuspid valve, mitral prolapse, HCM, CIEDs`,`Not routine; individualise; strict oral hygiene`],[`Low risk`,`Normal heart, innocent murmur, isolated secundum ASD, repaired defects without residua after 6 months`,`Not recommended`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of fever with a murmur`,columns:[`Condition`,`Overlap with IE`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Acute rheumatic fever recurrence`,`Fever, arthritis, new murmur`,`Raised ASO, Jones criteria, negative cultures, no emboli`],[`Marantic (NBTE) endocarditis`,`Vegetations and emboli`,`Cancer or hypercoagulability; sterile cultures`],[`Libman-Sacks / antiphospholipid syndrome`,`Sterile vegetations, emboli`,`ANA or antiphospholipid antibodies positive`],[`Atrial myxoma`,`Fever, emboli, changing murmur, weight loss`,`Pedunculated LA mass on echo; sterile blood`],[`Enteric fever, TB, brucellosis`,`PUO with anaemia and splenomegaly`,`No vegetation; specific cultures or serology`],[`Line-related bacteraemia`,`Positive blood cultures`,`No vegetation on TOE; clears after line removal`]]}],redFlags:[`Fever in a patient with a prosthetic valve, cardiac device or known valve disease - blood cultures and same-day referral`,`Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia from any source - echo to exclude IE`,`New breathlessness, pulmonary oedema or hypotension in IE - acute valve destruction, emergency surgery`,`New PR prolongation or AV block during IE - aortic root abscess`,`Focal neurological deficit, severe headache or confusion - embolic stroke, haemorrhage or mycotic aneurysm`,`Persistent fever or positive cultures after 7 days of appropriate antibiotics - uncontrolled infection`,`Large mobile vegetation (10 mm or more) with an embolic event despite therapy`,`Cough, pleuritic pain and multiple cavitating lung shadows in a person who injects drugs - right-sided IE with septic emboli`],pearls:[`**Fever + murmur = blood cultures before antibiotics** - 3 sets, separate venepunctures, 10 mL per bottle.`,`**Osler = Ouch (painful, immunological); Janeway = painless (embolic).**`,`**ESC 2023 Duke**: CT and PET are now major imaging criteria; E. faecalis is a typical organism.`,`**Definite IE: 2 major, 1 major + 3 minor, or 5 minor.**`,`**E. faecalis: amoxicillin + ceftriaxone for 6 weeks** - no aminoglycoside toxicity.`,`**No gentamicin for MSSA native valve IE.**`,`**Urgent surgery = within 3-5 days; emergency = within 24 hours.**`,`**S. gallolyticus = colonoscopy.**`,`**Dental prophylaxis: amoxicillin 2 g; clindamycin is out** - only for highest-risk patients.`,`Mnemonic for surgery indications, **HUE**: Heart failure, Uncontrolled infection, Embolism prevention.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral regurgitation has had low-grade fever for 3 weeks. She is haemodynamically stable and has not received antibiotics. Which blood culture strategy is most appropriate before starting treatment?`,options:[`One set drawn at the peak of the next fever spike`,`Three sets from separate peripheral venepunctures, about 30 minutes apart`,`Two sets drawn through a newly inserted cannula after the first antibiotic dose`,`One set daily for 3 days while giving oral amoxicillin`],answer:1,explanation:`Bacteraemia in IE is continuous, so three sets from separate peripheral venepunctures with strict asepsis, each with aerobic and anaerobic bottles of about 10 mL, give the best yield and show persistence. Waiting for a fever spike is unnecessary and a single set cannot fulfil the major criterion of two separate positive cultures. Sampling after an antibiotic dose, or while giving oral amoxicillin, creates culture-negative IE - a common Indian problem.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-q2`,stem:`Under the ESC 2023 guideline, which patient should receive antibiotic prophylaxis before a dental extraction?`,options:[`A 25-year-old with rheumatic mitral stenosis`,`A 40-year-old with mitral valve prolapse and moderate regurgitation`,`A 35-year-old with a bicuspid aortic valve and mild stenosis`,`A 70-year-old who had transcatheter aortic valve implantation last year`],answer:3,explanation:`ESC 2023 places surgically and transcatheter-implanted prosthetic valves in the highest-risk group, so amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before the extraction is recommended. Rheumatic heart disease, mitral prolapse and bicuspid valve are intermediate-risk conditions in which prophylaxis is not routinely recommended; for them strict oral hygiene and regular dental review matter more than a single pre-procedure dose.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-q3`,stem:`A 74-year-old man with diabetes and an eGFR of 40 mL/min has native aortic valve endocarditis. Blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis with high-level gentamicin resistance. Which regimen is best?`,options:[`Amoxicillin 200 mg/kg/day IV plus ceftriaxone 2 g IV 12-hourly for 6 weeks`,`Amoxicillin plus gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day for 4 weeks`,`Ceftriaxone 2 g IV once daily alone for 4 weeks`,`Cloxacillin 12 g/day IV for 4 weeks`],answer:0,explanation:`Amoxicillin plus ceftriaxone achieves synergy through saturation of different penicillin-binding proteins, works against high-level aminoglycoside-resistant E. faecalis and avoids nephrotoxicity in an elderly patient with CKD; it is given for 6 weeks. Gentamicin is ineffective against high-level resistance and toxic here. Enterococci are intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins, so ceftriaxone alone fails, and cloxacillin has no useful enterococcal activity.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-q4`,stem:`A 38-year-old man with streptococcal mitral valve endocarditis has been on appropriate IV antibiotics for 8 days and is haemodynamically stable. He develops left upper quadrant pain and CT shows a splenic infarct. TOE shows a 14 mm mobile mitral vegetation with moderate regurgitation. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Continue antibiotics and repeat TOE in 2 weeks`,`Add aspirin 300 mg daily to prevent further emboli`,`Refer for urgent mitral valve surgery`,`Switch to partial oral therapy as he is stable`],answer:2,explanation:`A vegetation of 10 mm or more with one or more embolic episodes despite appropriate antibiotics is a Class I indication for urgent surgery to prevent further embolism, as recurrence risk is highest early. Waiting 2 weeks leaves him exposed. Antiplatelet therapy does not reduce embolism in IE and increases bleeding. Partial oral therapy is only for stable patients without a surgical indication.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-q5`,stem:`Which of the following counts as a MAJOR criterion in the ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria?`,options:[`Osler nodes on the finger pulps`,`Coxiella burnetii phase I IgG antibody titre of 1:1600`,`Fever of 38.6 C in a patient with rheumatic heart disease`,`A silent splenic infarct detected only on CT`],answer:1,explanation:`A Coxiella burnetii phase I IgG titre over 1:800, or a single positive culture, is a major microbiological criterion because Q fever is a classic culture-negative cause. Osler nodes are a minor immunological criterion. Fever over 38 C and a predisposing heart condition are each minor criteria. ESC 2023 added imaging-only silent emboli, but as a minor vascular criterion, not a major one.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 28-year-old man with rheumatic heart disease presents with fever for 4 weeks and a changing murmur. Discuss the diagnosis and management of infective endocarditis, including indications for surgery.`,openingLines:[`Infective endocarditis is microbial infection of the endocardial surface, native or prosthetic valves or devices, with vegetation formation; in India rheumatic heart disease remains its commonest substrate.`,`Diagnosis rests on the ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria - blood cultures and imaging - and treatment on prolonged bactericidal antibiotics, with surgery for heart failure, uncontrolled infection or embolic risk.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Clinical features`,points:[`Fever, weight loss, malaise; new or changing murmur; petechiae, splinters, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, Roth spots, splenomegaly, clubbing. [Harrison 22e]`,`Complications: heart failure, stroke, splenic or renal infarct, AV block, glomerulonephritis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`2. Diagnosis`,points:[`**3 blood culture sets** from separate venepunctures before antibiotics, 10 mL per bottle. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**TTE then TOE**; cardiac CT and FDG PET/CT where needed. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria**: major (typical organisms in 2 cultures, Coxiella, imaging lesions) and minor (predisposition, fever, vascular, immunological, microbiological); definite = 2 major, 1 major + 3 minor or 5 minor. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Supporting tests: CBC, ESR, CRP, urine for haematuria, renal function, ECG for PR interval, rheumatoid factor; serology and valve PCR if culture-negative. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`3. Empirical therapy`,points:[`Community native valve: **ampicillin 12 g/day + ceftriaxone 4 g/day**, or ampicillin + cloxacillin 12 g/day + gentamicin 3 mg/kg/day. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Penicillin allergy or healthcare-associated: vancomycin 30 mg/kg/day based regimens. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`4. Definitive therapy`,points:[`Streptococci: penicillin G 12-18 MU/day or ceftriaxone 2 g/day for 4 weeks (2 weeks with gentamicin if uncomplicated). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`MSSA: cloxacillin 12 g/day 4-6 weeks; MRSA: vancomycin or daptomycin. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`E. faecalis: amoxicillin + ceftriaxone 6 weeks; HACEK: ceftriaxone 4 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Partial oral therapy after 10 days IV in stable patients; OPAT after 2 weeks. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`5. Surgery`,points:[`**Heart failure**: emergency within 24 hours for shock or refractory oedema; urgent within 3-5 days otherwise. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Uncontrolled infection**: abscess, fistula, new AV block, fungi, resistant organisms, persistent bacteraemia. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Embolism**: vegetation 10 mm or more with embolic event despite therapy. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`6. Monitoring and prevention`,points:[`Repeat cultures until negative, drug levels, renal function, ECG, repeat echo; Endocarditis Team. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Dental hygiene; secondary rheumatic prophylaxis; future dental prophylaxis with amoxicillin 2 g as he is now high risk. [ESC 2023 IE]`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of ESC 2023 modified Duke criteria (major and minor)`,`Flow chart: suspected IE to blood cultures, TTE, TOE, Duke classification and Endocarditis Team`,`Table of surgical indications with timing`],markSplit:[{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnosis and Duke criteria`,marks:3},{part:`Antibiotic therapy`,marks:3},{part:`Surgery indications`,marks:1.5},{part:`Monitoring and prevention`,marks:1}],keywords:[`infective endocarditis`,`Duke criteria`,`blood cultures`,`TOE`,`vegetation`,`surgery timing`]},{id:`cardiovascular-infective-endocarditis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on antibiotic prophylaxis of infective endocarditis.`,openingLines:[`Antibiotic prophylaxis aims to prevent IE from transient bacteraemia during dental procedures, and is now restricted to patients at the highest risk of IE or of a poor outcome from it.`,`The ESC 2023 guideline recommends amoxicillin 2 g orally before dental procedures involving the gingiva or periapical region in high-risk patients, with oral hygiene as the main preventive measure for everyone else.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Who - high-risk patients`,points:[`Previous IE; surgical or transcatheter prosthetic valves and valve-repair material; untreated cyanotic CHD and CHD repaired with prosthetic material (6 months or residual defect); ventricular assist devices. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Intermediate risk (RHD, bicuspid, prolapse, HCM, CIED): not routine, individualise. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`2. When - procedures`,points:[`Dental work manipulating gingival or periapical tissue or perforating the mucosa. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Not routinely for GI, GU, respiratory or skin procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`3. What - regimens`,points:[`**Amoxicillin 2 g oral or IV** 30-60 minutes before (child 50 mg/kg). [ESC 2023 IE]`,`Allergy: azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg, or doxycycline 100 mg; **clindamycin no longer recommended**. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`4. Non-antibiotic prevention`,points:[`Twice-daily brushing, 6-monthly dental review, aseptic line care, avoid tattoos and piercing in high-risk patients. [ESC 2023 IE]`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: high, intermediate and low risk groups with prophylaxis advice`],markSplit:[{part:`High-risk groups`,marks:2},{part:`Procedures`,marks:1},{part:`Regimens`,marks:1.5},{part:`Oral hygiene and other measures`,marks:.5}],keywords:[`endocarditis prophylaxis`,`amoxicillin 2 g`,`high-risk cardiac conditions`,`ESC 2023`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Infective endocarditis`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Infective endocarditis`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis (European Heart Journal, 2023)`,`Fowler VG et al. The 2023 Duke-International Society for Cardiovascular Infectious Diseases criteria for infective endocarditis (Clinical Infectious Diseases, 2023)`,`Wilson WR et al. Prevention of viridans group streptococcal infective endocarditis: AHA scientific statement, 2021`,`Iversen K et al. Partial oral versus intravenous antibiotic treatment of endocarditis (POET), New England Journal of Medicine, 2019`,`ICMR Treatment Guidelines for Antimicrobial Use in Common Syndromes, 2nd edition, 2022`]}),s.push({id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves`,title:`Prosthetic heart valves: follow-up, anticoagulation and complications`,oneLiner:`**Prosthetic heart valves** are either **mechanical** (durable but needing **lifelong vitamin K antagonist** at an INR target set by valve type, position and patient risk factors) or **bioprosthetic** (no lifelong anticoagulation but **structural degeneration** over 10-20 years); **DOACs are contraindicated with mechanical valves**, and follow-up targets the big complications - **valve thrombosis, thromboembolism, bleeding, paravalvular leak, haemolysis, prosthetic endocarditis and degeneration**.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`prosthetic valve`,`mechanical valve`,`bioprosthetic valve`,`tissue valve`,`MVR`,`AVR`,`INR target`,`warfarin`,`acenocoumarol`,`valve thrombosis`,`pannus`,`paravalvular leak`,`haemolysis`,`prosthetic valve endocarditis`,`structural valve degeneration`,`valve-in-valve`,`TAVI`,`patient-prosthesis mismatch`,`On-X`,`TTK Chitra valve`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`A **prosthetic heart valve** replaces a diseased native valve; **mechanical** valves are made of pyrolytic carbon and metal, **bioprosthetic** valves of porcine valve or bovine pericardium on a stent, and **transcatheter** valves are bioprostheses on an expandable frame. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Worldwide over **300,000** valve replacements are done each year, and transcatheter implantation now exceeds surgical aortic replacement in older patients in high-income countries. [Braunwald 12e]`,`In **India**, most valve replacements are for **rheumatic heart disease** in young adults, often women of child-bearing age, and the majority receive **mechanical** valves because tissue valves degenerate fast in the young. [Braunwald 12e]`,`The indigenous **TTK Chitra tilting-disc valve** (Sree Chitra Tirunal Institute) is widely implanted in India alongside imported bileaflet valves - know which valve your patient has. [SCTIMST]`,`**Anticoagulation quality is the weak link** in Indian practice: rural residence, cost, irregular INR testing and diet make time in therapeutic range frequently poor. [Braunwald 12e]`,`With good care, **thromboembolism** with a mechanical valve on warfarin runs at about **1-2% per patient-year** and major bleeding at about **1% per year**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`The family physician is often the person who checks the INR, treats intercurrent illness and first sees the complications - making this a high-yield OPD topic. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Types of prosthesis and choice of valve`,points:[`**Mechanical valves**: **bileaflet** (St Jude, On-X, Carbomedics - commonest), **single tilting disc** (Medtronic Hall, TTK Chitra, Bjork-Shiley) and the obsolete **caged-ball** (Starr-Edwards). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Bioprosthetic valves**: stented porcine or bovine pericardial valves, stentless valves and homografts; **transcatheter** valves are balloon-expandable or self-expanding bioprostheses. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mechanical pros and cons**: lifelong durability, but lifelong VKA with bleeding risk, thrombosis risk and teratogenicity. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Bioprosthetic pros and cons**: no lifelong anticoagulation, but **structural valve degeneration** - faster in the young, in CKD, hyperparathyroidism and pregnancy - with eventual reintervention. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Age thresholds (ESC 2021)**: mechanical favoured under **60 (aortic)** or **65 (mitral)**; bioprosthetic favoured over **65 (aortic)** or **70 (mitral)**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Age thresholds (ACC/AHA 2020)**: mechanical under **50**, either valve between **50 and 65** after shared decision-making, bioprosthetic over **65**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**ESC/EACTS 2025** stresses **lifetime management** - planning the first valve so that a later valve-in-valve procedure remains possible - and shared decision-making over fixed age cut-offs. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Bioprosthesis is preferred** when good-quality anticoagulation is unlikely, for women wanting pregnancy (after counselling), and in those with high bleeding risk or a short life expectancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Mechanical valve is reasonable** when the patient already needs lifelong anticoagulation, has accelerated degeneration risk (young age, dialysis) or a small root where a future valve-in-valve is not feasible. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology of prosthetic complications`,points:[`**Thrombogenicity**: blood contact with carbon, sewing ring and areas of stasis activates platelets and coagulation - mitral and tricuspid positions (low velocity) clot more than aortic. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Valve thrombosis** follows subtherapeutic INR: thrombus jams the leaflets, causing **obstruction** (stenosis) or failure to close (regurgitation) - a sudden, life-threatening event. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Pannus** is fibrous tissue ingrowth from the sewing ring, growing slowly over years and impinging on the leaflets - it does not respond to thrombolysis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Paravalvular leak** arises when sutures pull out of a calcified or infected annulus; the high-velocity jet shears red cells causing **haemolysis**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Structural valve degeneration** of tissue valves is calcification and collagen breakdown of the leaflets, causing stenosis, regurgitation or both; **hypoattenuated leaflet thickening (HALT)** is subclinical leaflet thrombosis seen on CT. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Prosthetic endocarditis** begins at the sewing ring, so it spreads to the annulus, causing abscess, dehiscence and paravalvular leak more often than native IE. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Patient-prosthesis mismatch**: an orifice too small for body size leaves a high residual gradient - severe in the aortic position when indexed EOA is **0.65 cm²/m² or less**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Why DOACs fail on mechanical valves**: contact activation via the intrinsic pathway on artificial surfaces generates thrombin that direct inhibitors at fixed doses do not suppress as reliably as VKA, shown in RE-ALIGN and PROACT Xa. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`INR targets and the anticoagulation plan`,points:[`**Mechanical valve INR (ESC)** depends on **prosthesis thrombogenicity** and **patient risk factors** - mitral or tricuspid position, previous thromboembolism, AF, mitral stenosis of any degree and LVEF under 35%. [ESC 2021]`,`**Low-thrombogenicity valves** (bileaflet St Jude, On-X, Carbomedics; Medtronic Hall): target INR **2.5 with no risk factor**, **3.0 with one or more** risk factors. [ESC 2021]`,`**Medium-thrombogenicity** (other bileaflet valves with limited data): INR **3.0**, or **3.5** with risk factors; **high-thrombogenicity** (caged-ball, older tilting disc): INR **3.5**, or **4.0** with risk factors. [ESC 2021]`,`**ACC/AHA simplification**: mechanical aortic bileaflet or current tilting-disc without risk factors INR **2.5**; mechanical aortic with risk factors or older valve, and **any mechanical mitral**, INR **3.0**. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**On-X aortic valve**: after 3 months at standard intensity, an INR of **1.5-2.0 plus aspirin** may be considered (PROACT trial). [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Aim for the median target**, not a range, and use a **stable VKA dose**; INR variability itself predicts thrombosis and death. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Aspirin add-on** (75-100 mg) to VKA is no longer routine: consider it with coexisting atherosclerotic disease and low bleeding risk, or after a thromboembolic event despite a therapeutic INR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Surgical bioprosthesis**: low-dose aspirin or VKA for the first **3 months** after aortic replacement; VKA (INR 2.5) for 3 months after mitral or tricuspid replacement; then aspirin or nothing if no other indication. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**TAVI**: lifelong **single antiplatelet** (aspirin 75-100 mg or clopidogrel 75 mg) if no indication for anticoagulation; routine DAPT or routine anticoagulation is not recommended. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**DOACs**: **contraindicated with mechanical valves** of any type and position; allowed with a **bioprosthesis plus AF**, generally after the first 3 months (RIVER trial for mitral bioprostheses). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history at follow-up`,points:[`**Ask at every visit**: breathlessness, exercise tolerance, chest pain, syncope, palpitations, fever, and any change in the **click** the patient hears. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Embolic symptoms**: transient weakness, speech or vision loss, sudden limb pain - suspect thrombosis or endocarditis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Bleeding symptoms**: gum bleeding, menorrhagia, haematuria, melaena, easy bruising - check INR and look for a cause. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Haemolysis**: dark urine, jaundice, fatigue and worsening anaemia suggest a paravalvular leak. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Adherence and INR history**: missed doses, recent INR values, change of brand (warfarin versus acenocoumarol), new drugs (antibiotics, antiepileptics, NSAIDs, herbal products) and diet. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Women**: contraception (avoid oestrogen-containing pills with mechanical valves), pregnancy plans and menstrual losses. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Atypical presentations**: valve thrombosis may present as gradually worsening breathlessness or as a 'chest infection'; prosthetic endocarditis as low-grade fever after a course of oral antibiotics. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Mechanical valves give crisp metallic closing clicks**: in the aortic position a loud click replacing A2; in the mitral position a loud click replacing S1, often audible without a stethoscope. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Muffled or absent click** in a mechanical valve is **valve thrombosis until proved otherwise** - an emergency. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**A soft ejection systolic murmur** across an aortic prosthesis is normal (all prostheses are mildly stenotic); a **harsh, late-peaking** murmur suggests obstruction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Any diastolic murmur with an aortic prosthesis**, or **any pansystolic murmur with a mitral prosthesis**, means regurgitation (transvalvular or paravalvular) and needs echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bioprosthetic valves** sound near-normal; a new murmur suggests degeneration. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Look for**: pallor and icterus (haemolysis), signs of heart failure, peripheral stigmata of endocarditis, bruising, and the sternotomy scar. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Rhythm**: AF raises the INR target and the stroke risk - document it. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a deteriorating prosthesis`,points:[`**Thrombus versus pannus**: thrombus has a short history, a **subtherapeutic INR**, a larger soft mobile mass and responds to lysis; pannus has a long history, therapeutic INR, a small dense mass and needs surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Valve obstruction versus patient-prosthesis mismatch**: mismatch has a high gradient from the start with normal leaflet motion; obstruction shows a rising gradient and restricted leaflet motion. [ASE 2009]`,`**High gradient from high flow** (anaemia, fever, pregnancy, sepsis) is not obstruction - check the Doppler velocity index. [ASE 2009]`,`**Haemolysis from paravalvular leak versus other causes**: autoimmune haemolysis is Coombs-positive; drug-induced and G6PD haemolysis lack schistocytes on the smear. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Breathlessness from LV dysfunction, other valve disease or AF** rather than the prosthesis - echo separates them. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Fever from a simple infection versus prosthetic endocarditis**: in any prosthetic valve patient with unexplained fever, take blood cultures before antibiotics. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,points:[`**Baseline TTE** after surgery (usually 6-12 weeks, once the patient has recovered) records the normal gradients of that valve - every later study is compared with it. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Aortic prosthesis obstruction**: mean gradient **35 mmHg or more** (or a rise of 10 mmHg or more from baseline), **Doppler velocity index under 0.25**, acceleration time over 100 ms. [ASE 2009]`,`**Mitral prosthesis obstruction**: mean gradient **over 10 mmHg**, pressure half-time over 200 ms, EOA under 1.0 cm² - interpret with heart rate. [ASE 2009]`,`**TOE** for suspected thrombosis, endocarditis or mitral prosthetic regurgitation (the mitral prosthesis shadows the left atrium on TTE). [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cinefluoroscopy** quickly shows restricted opening or closing angles of mechanical leaflets - cheap and available in Indian cath labs. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Cardiac CT** distinguishes pannus from thrombus and detects HALT in bioprostheses; **FDG PET/CT** helps in suspected prosthetic endocarditis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Haemolysis screen**: Hb, reticulocytes, LDH (raised), haptoglobin (low), bilirubin, peripheral smear for schistocytes, urine haemosiderin. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**INR monitoring**: weekly until stable, then at least **every 4 weeks**; point-of-care devices and patient self-testing improve control where available. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Blood cultures**: 3 sets before antibiotics for any unexplained fever in a prosthetic valve patient. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`**Warfarin initiation/maintenance**: typical doses 2-10 mg/day; **acenocoumarol** (common in India) 1-8 mg/day - roughly 1 mg acenocoumarol to 2-2.5 mg warfarin; never switch brands without closer INR checks. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Out-of-range INR without bleeding**: adjust the weekly dose by 10-20%; for INR **over 10** hold the VKA and give low-dose **oral vitamin K 1-2.5 mg**, avoiding high doses that cause resistance and thrombosis. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Low INR**: check adherence and interactions, increase the dose and recheck in 1 week; bridge with LMWH or UFH if INR is well below target in a mitral or high-risk valve. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Drug interactions**: INR rises with metronidazole, fluconazole, co-trimoxazole, macrolides, amiodarone and paracetamol in regular high doses; falls with rifampicin, carbamazepine, phenytoin and some herbal products. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Diet**: keep vitamin K intake **consistent** (green leafy vegetables) rather than avoiding it; limit alcohol. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Minor procedures**: dental extraction, cataract and skin surgery can be done **without stopping VKA** if INR is in range, using local haemostasis such as tranexamic acid mouthwash. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Major surgery**: stop VKA about 5 days before; **bridge with UFH or LMWH** in mechanical mitral valves, older aortic valves or with risk factors - see the Anticoagulants in practice topic. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Endocarditis prophylaxis**: **amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before** dental procedures involving the gingiva; azithromycin 500 mg if allergic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Rheumatic patients** continue secondary penicillin prophylaxis after valve replacement as advised by the RHD programme. [WHO 2024 RHD]`,`**Contraception**: progestogen-only methods or a copper IUD; avoid combined oestrogen pills with mechanical valves. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency: thrombosis, bleeding and embolism`,points:[`**Suspected obstructive mechanical valve thrombosis** (new breathlessness, muffled click, embolism, low INR): admit, IV heparin, urgent TTE/TOE and cinefluoroscopy, and call the surgical team. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Urgent surgery** is recommended for obstructive thrombosis in critically ill patients without serious comorbidity. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Fibrinolysis** is considered when surgery is unavailable or very high-risk, or for right-sided valve thrombosis; **low-dose slow-infusion alteplase (25 mg over 6 hours, repeated if needed)** has fewer complications than older regimens. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Fibrinolysis risks**: stroke and systemic embolism (about 5-10%) and major bleeding; contraindicated with recent stroke, active bleeding or large mobile thrombus. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Non-obstructive thrombus**: optimise anticoagulation with IV UFH and add aspirin, then repeat imaging; surgery if a large thrombus (over 10 mm) persists or embolises. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Bioprosthetic valve thrombosis or HALT**: VKA anticoagulation, usually for **3 months**, generally resolves it. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Major bleeding on VKA**: stop VKA, give **4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate 25-50 IU/kg** plus **vitamin K 5-10 mg IV**; use **FFP 15 mL/kg** where PCC is unavailable; restart anticoagulation once bleeding is controlled, usually within days. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Intracranial haemorrhage**: reverse fully; restart VKA in mechanical valves typically after **1-2 weeks** based on neurosurgical advice and valve risk. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Embolic stroke with therapeutic INR**: echo and TOE to exclude thrombosis or endocarditis, then raise the INR target or add aspirin. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Other complications: paravalvular leak, haemolysis, endocarditis and degeneration`,points:[`**Paravalvular leak** affects a few per cent of surgical valves (more in the mitral position) and is common after TAVI; most are mild and harmless. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Treat significant paravalvular leak** with reoperation or **transcatheter plug closure** when it causes heart failure or haemolysis needing repeated transfusion. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Haemolysis medical care**: iron and folic acid, beta-blocker to lower shear, erythropoietin and transfusion as needed while intervention is planned. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Prosthetic valve endocarditis**: early (within 1 year) is mostly staphylococcal; mortality is high; TOE and PET/CT are central; regimens add **rifampicin and gentamicin** for staphylococci and surgery is often needed - see the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Structural valve degeneration** of tissue valves: rising gradient or new regurgitation; manage by **redo surgery or transcatheter valve-in-valve** depending on risk and valve size. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Patient-prosthesis mismatch** is prevented at surgery by root enlargement or a larger valve; once present, it cannot be fixed medically. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Heart failure after valve replacement**: look for prosthetic dysfunction, other valve disease, LV dysfunction and AF before simply increasing diuretics. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Special situations: pregnancy and others`,points:[`**Pregnancy with a mechanical valve** is **mWHO class III** (high risk) with real maternal mortality and fetal loss; manage in a pregnancy heart team at an expert centre and plan before conception. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Warfarin in weeks 6-12** causes **embryopathy** (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses) in a dose-dependent way and fetal bleeding later; **LMWH** avoids embryopathy but carries a higher valve thrombosis risk. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**First trimester choice**: if warfarin **5 mg/day or less** (acenocoumarol 2 mg or less), continuing VKA is reasonable after counselling; if higher, switch to **LMWH twice daily with anti-Xa monitoring** in weeks 6-12. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy]`,`**LMWH monitoring**: weekly **peak anti-Xa** 4-6 hours after dose - target about **0.8-1.2 IU/mL (aortic)** and **1.0-1.2 IU/mL (mitral or right-sided)** - plus a pre-dose trough. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy]`,`**Second and third trimesters**: VKA until about **36 weeks**, then switch to LMWH or UFH; stop UFH 4-6 hours before a **planned delivery**; vaginal delivery is usual, caesarean if labour starts on VKA. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Valve thrombosis in pregnancy** presents as breathlessness or embolism - urgent echo; it is a leading cause of maternal death in Indian series. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: higher bleeding risk; prefer bioprosthetic or TAVI valves; review falls risk and drug interactions. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: accelerated bioprosthetic calcification and more bleeding on VKA; the dialysis unit must know the INR target. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Rural and resource-limited settings**: INR at the district hospital or point-of-care device, warfarin from **Jan Aushadhi** stores, and a written INR diary kept by the patient. [MoHFW 2024]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Thromboembolism**: about **1-2% per patient-year** with mechanical valves on VKA - higher in the mitral position, with AF and with poor INR control. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Major bleeding** on VKA occurs in about **1% per year**, rising with age, high INR targets and added aspirin. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Bioprosthetic durability**: structural degeneration usually appears after **10 years** and reintervention by 15-20 years, much sooner in patients under 40. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Prosthetic endocarditis** affects about 1-6% of patients over their lifetime and carries in-hospital mortality of 20-40%. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Survival** after valve replacement depends more on preoperative LV function, age and comorbidity than on valve type. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Redo surgery** carries higher risk than the first operation - valve-in-valve TAVI has reduced this for failing bioprostheses. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Follow-up schedule**: first review at 6-12 weeks with baseline TTE, then **yearly clinical review** for all prosthetic valves. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Echo for mechanical valves**: when symptoms or signs change - routine yearly echo is not required. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**Echo for surgical bioprostheses**: at **5 and 10 years**, then yearly; after **TAVI**: yearly. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**INR**: at least every 4 weeks when stable; more often after dose changes, new drugs, illness or diarrhoea. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Refer urgently**: muffled click, new murmur, breathlessness, embolism, fever, haemolysis, major bleeding, or pregnancy. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Each visit**: symptoms, click and murmurs, BP, rhythm, signs of bleeding, Hb, INR log and adherence, dental status. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Patient education**: valve card stating valve type, position and INR target; what raises and lowers INR; when to seek help; prophylaxis before dental work. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EACTS 2025 valvular guideline** replaces 2021: lifetime management, shared decision-making on valve choice, and transcatheter valve-in-valve for failing bioprostheses. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**PROACT Xa (2022)** - apixaban versus warfarin in On-X aortic valves - was stopped early for excess thromboembolism, confirming that **DOACs must not be used with mechanical valves**. [PROACT Xa 2022]`,`**RIVER (2020)**: rivaroxaban was non-inferior to warfarin in AF with a **bioprosthetic mitral valve**, supporting DOACs in bioprostheses. [RIVER 2020]`,`**INVICTUS (2022)**: in rheumatic AF, rivaroxaban was worse than VKA - a reminder that rheumatic MS with AF also needs VKA. [INVICTUS 2022]`,`**ACC/AHA 2020**: aspirin with VKA is no longer routine for mechanical valves; On-X aortic low-INR strategy allowed. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline**: pregnancy heart team, pre-conception planning and close anticoagulation monitoring for mechanical valves. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline**: transcatheter valves now in the highest-risk group for dental prophylaxis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic question**: anticoagulation of prosthetic valves - INR by valve type, position and risk factors, plus pregnancy management. [ESC 2021]`,`**Muffled click = thrombosis**; any diastolic murmur with an aortic prosthesis is abnormal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**DOACs are contraindicated** with mechanical valves (RE-ALIGN, PROACT Xa) but allowed with bioprosthesis plus AF. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Thrombus versus pannus**: low INR and short history versus long history and therapeutic INR. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: giving high-dose vitamin K for a high INR without bleeding in a mechanical valve - it causes warfarin resistance and thrombosis risk. [ACCP 2012]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping warfarin for a tooth extraction - continue it with local haemostasis. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: starting antibiotics for fever without blood cultures in a prosthetic valve patient. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Warfarin embryopathy** (weeks 6-12): nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses - dose-dependent, low at 5 mg/day or less. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in prosthetic valve care`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Warfarin`,`2-10 mg oral once daily (evening)`,`Lifelong for mechanical; 3 months after mitral bioprosthesis; INR at least every 4 weeks`,`Bleeding, embryopathy (weeks 6-12), skin necrosis; many drug interactions`],[`Acenocoumarol`,`1-8 mg oral once daily`,`As for warfarin; do not switch brands casually`,`Bleeding; shorter half-life gives more INR swings`],[`Aspirin`,`75-100 mg oral once daily`,`3 months after surgical aortic bioprosthesis; lifelong after TAVI; add to VKA only if indicated`,`GI bleeding; adds to VKA bleeding`],[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg oral once daily`,`Alternative single antiplatelet after TAVI`,`Bleeding; CYP2C19 variability`],[`Enoxaparin (bridging or pregnancy)`,`1 mg/kg SC twice daily; adjust to anti-Xa in pregnancy`,`Bridging days; pregnancy weeks 6-12 and after 36 weeks`,`Bleeding, valve thrombosis if underdosed; renal adjustment`],[`Unfractionated heparin`,`IV 80 U/kg bolus then 18 U/kg/h; aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control`,`Valve thrombosis, peripartum, perioperative`,`Bleeding, heparin-induced thrombocytopenia`],[`Vitamin K (phytomenadione)`,`1-2.5 mg oral for INR over 10 without bleeding; 5-10 mg slow IV for major bleeding`,`Single dose; recheck INR in 24 hours`,`Anaphylactoid reaction with fast IV; overcorrection causes resistance`],[`4-factor prothrombin complex concentrate`,`25-50 IU/kg IV by INR`,`Once for major bleeding`,`Thrombosis; FFP 15 mL/kg if unavailable`],[`Alteplase (valve thrombosis)`,`25 mg IV over 6 hours, repeated if needed (low-dose slow infusion)`,`Until gradients and leaflet motion normalise`,`Stroke, embolism, bleeding; contraindicated after recent stroke`],[`DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran)`,`Standard AF doses`,`Bioprosthesis plus AF only, usually after 3 months`,`Contraindicated with mechanical valves and rheumatic moderate-severe MS`]]},{heading:`Target INR for mechanical valves (ESC 2021)`,columns:[`Prosthesis thrombogenicity`,`Examples`,`No patient risk factor`,`One or more risk factors`],rows:[[`Low`,`Bileaflet St Jude, On-X, Carbomedics; Medtronic Hall`,`2.5`,`3.0`],[`Medium`,`Other bileaflet valves with limited data`,`3.0`,`3.5`],[`High`,`Caged-ball (Starr-Edwards), Lillehei-Kaster, Omniscience, older tilting disc`,`3.5`,`4.0`],[`Patient risk factors`,`Mitral or tricuspid position, previous thromboembolism, AF, any mitral stenosis, LVEF under 35%`,`-`,`-`]]},{heading:`Mechanical versus bioprosthetic valves`,columns:[`Feature`,`Mechanical`,`Bioprosthetic (surgical or transcatheter)`],rows:[[`Durability`,`Lifelong`,`Degenerates; reintervention often by 10-20 years, sooner if young`],[`Anticoagulation`,`Lifelong VKA; DOAC contraindicated`,`3 months aspirin or VKA after surgery; lifelong single antiplatelet after TAVI`],[`Preferred age (ESC 2021)`,`Under 60 aortic, under 65 mitral`,`Over 65 aortic, over 70 mitral`],[`Pregnancy`,`High risk (mWHO III): thrombosis versus embryopathy`,`Safer pregnancy, but faster degeneration`],[`Auscultation`,`Crisp metallic clicks`,`Near-normal sounds`],[`Main complications`,`Thrombosis, embolism, bleeding`,`Structural degeneration, HALT`]]},{heading:`Echo and laboratory cut-offs for prosthetic dysfunction`,columns:[`Finding`,`Normal`,`Suggests dysfunction`],rows:[[`Aortic prosthesis mean gradient`,`Similar to baseline (usually under 20 mmHg)`,`35 mmHg or more, or rise of 10 mmHg or more from baseline`],[`Doppler velocity index (aortic)`,`0.30 or more`,`Under 0.25`],[`Mitral prosthesis mean gradient`,`Under 5 mmHg`,`Over 10 mmHg`],[`Mitral pressure half-time`,`Under 130 ms`,`Over 200 ms`],[`Indexed EOA (aortic) - mismatch`,`Over 0.85 cm²/m²`,`0.65 cm²/m² or less = severe mismatch`],[`Haemolysis labs`,`Normal LDH and haptoglobin`,`Raised LDH, low haptoglobin, schistocytes, reticulocytosis`]]},{heading:`Thrombus versus pannus`,columns:[`Feature`,`Thrombus`,`Pannus`],rows:[[`History`,`Short (days to weeks)`,`Long (months to years)`],[`INR`,`Often subtherapeutic`,`Usually therapeutic`],[`Mass on TOE/CT`,`Larger, soft, mobile, low CT attenuation`,`Small, dense, fixed, high CT attenuation`],[`Embolism`,`Common`,`Rare`],[`Treatment`,`Heparin, fibrinolysis or surgery`,`Surgery only`]]}],redFlags:[`Muffled or absent mechanical valve click - valve thrombosis`,`New or worsening breathlessness in a patient with a mechanical valve and low INR`,`TIA, stroke or limb embolism in a prosthetic valve patient`,`Fever in a prosthetic valve patient - blood cultures and same-day referral for prosthetic endocarditis`,`Major bleeding or INR over 10 with bleeding on VKA`,`New regurgitant murmur, dark urine or falling Hb - paravalvular leak with haemolysis`,`Pregnancy (planned or confirmed) in a woman with a mechanical valve - urgent pregnancy heart team review`,`Patient switched to a DOAC while having a mechanical valve - stop it and restart VKA with bridging`],pearls:[`**DOACs are contraindicated with mechanical valves** - RE-ALIGN and PROACT Xa both failed.`,`**INR by valve and risk**: low-thrombogenic bileaflet aortic without risk factors 2.5; with mitral position or AF 3.0.`,`**Muffled click = thrombosis until proved otherwise.**`,`**Thrombus: low INR, short history, lysable; pannus: therapeutic INR, slow, surgery only.**`,`**Warfarin 5 mg/day or less** in the first trimester carries low embryopathy risk.`,`**Do not stop warfarin for a dental extraction** - use tranexamic mouthwash.`,`**High INR without bleeding**: hold dose and give low-dose oral vitamin K, never 10 mg IV.`,`**TAVI: lifelong single antiplatelet** if no other indication for anticoagulation.`,`**Every prosthetic valve patient is high-risk for IE**: amoxicillin 2 g before dental work.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-q1`,stem:`A 35-year-old man with a bileaflet mechanical mitral valve and atrial fibrillation finds weekly INR tests difficult and asks to switch to apixaban. What is the correct advice?`,options:[`Switch to apixaban 5 mg twice daily, since AF is the main indication`,`Switch to dabigatran 150 mg twice daily, which was tested in mechanical valves`,`Continue warfarin targeting INR 3.0 and arrange point-of-care INR testing`,`Stop anticoagulation and give aspirin plus clopidogrel`],answer:2,explanation:`DOACs are contraindicated with mechanical valves: dabigatran in RE-ALIGN caused more thrombosis and bleeding, and apixaban in PROACT Xa caused excess thromboembolism. A mechanical mitral valve with AF needs a VKA at a median INR of about 3.0, and point-of-care or self-testing addresses the practical difficulty. Dual antiplatelet therapy gives no adequate protection against valve thrombosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve has missed her warfarin for 10 days. She presents with breathlessness for 3 days; the valve click is muffled and INR is 1.3. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Obstructive mechanical valve thrombosis`,`Pannus ingrowth`,`Patient-prosthesis mismatch`,`Paravalvular leak with haemolysis`],answer:0,explanation:`A short history, subtherapeutic INR and muffled click in a mitral mechanical valve point to obstructive thrombosis, an emergency needing heparin, echo, cinefluoroscopy and surgery or fibrinolysis. Pannus grows slowly over years with a therapeutic INR. Mismatch is present from the day of surgery. Paravalvular leak causes a regurgitant murmur and haemolysis, not loss of the click.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old man with a bileaflet mechanical aortic valve on warfarin has an INR of 11 with no bleeding. What is the best management?`,options:[`Vitamin K 10 mg IV and prothrombin complex concentrate`,`Fresh frozen plasma 15 mL/kg`,`Continue warfarin at the same dose and recheck in a week`,`Withhold warfarin and give oral vitamin K 1-2.5 mg, recheck INR next day`],answer:3,explanation:`With INR over 10 and no bleeding, the VKA is withheld and low-dose oral vitamin K given, with an INR check the next day and a dose review. High-dose IV vitamin K and PCC are for major bleeding; in a mechanical valve they overshoot and cause warfarin resistance and thrombosis risk. FFP is unnecessary without bleeding, and continuing the same dose leaves a dangerous INR uncorrected.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-q4`,stem:`A woman with a mechanical mitral valve, stable on warfarin 7 mg daily, has a positive pregnancy test at 5 weeks. Which plan best follows ESC guidance for the first trimester?`,options:[`Continue warfarin 7 mg throughout the pregnancy`,`Switch to twice-daily LMWH from week 6 to 12 with weekly anti-Xa monitoring, then return to warfarin`,`Switch to apixaban for the whole pregnancy`,`Stop anticoagulation and give aspirin 75 mg until 12 weeks`],answer:1,explanation:`With a warfarin dose above 5 mg/day, embryopathy risk in weeks 6-12 is significant, so dose-adjusted LMWH twice daily with weekly anti-Xa monitoring is advised for that window, returning to VKA until about 36 weeks. Continuing 7 mg risks embryopathy. DOACs are contraindicated with mechanical valves and in pregnancy. Stopping anticoagulation risks catastrophic valve thrombosis.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-q5`,stem:`A 72-year-old woman had a surgical bovine pericardial aortic valve replacement 2 weeks ago. She is in sinus rhythm and has no other indication for anticoagulation. What antithrombotic therapy is most appropriate for the first 3 months?`,options:[`Lifelong warfarin with INR 3.0`,`Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily for 12 months`,`Low-dose aspirin 75-100 mg daily (or a VKA) for 3 months`,`No antithrombotic therapy at all`],answer:2,explanation:`After a surgical aortic bioprosthesis in sinus rhythm, low-dose aspirin, or a VKA, is recommended for the first 3 months while the sewing ring endothelialises, then continued antiplatelet or none depending on other indications. Lifelong warfarin at 3.0 is a mechanical mitral target. Rivaroxaban is reserved for bioprosthesis with AF. Giving nothing ignores the early thromboembolic risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the follow-up of a patient with a mechanical prosthetic heart valve, including anticoagulation targets, management during pregnancy and the complications of prosthetic valves.`,openingLines:[`Prosthetic valves are mechanical (durable, needing lifelong vitamin K antagonist) or bioprosthetic (no lifelong anticoagulation, but structural degeneration); in India most recipients are young rheumatic patients with mechanical valves.`,`Follow-up aims at a stable INR at the target set by valve type, position and risk factors, and at early detection of thrombosis, bleeding, endocarditis, paravalvular leak and haemolysis.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Types and choice`,points:[`Mechanical (bileaflet, tilting disc, caged-ball) versus bioprosthetic (porcine, pericardial, transcatheter). [Braunwald 12e]`,`ESC 2021: mechanical under 60 aortic/65 mitral; bioprosthetic over 65 aortic/70 mitral; ACC/AHA: under 50 and over 65. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`2. Anticoagulation`,points:[`**INR by thrombogenicity and risk factors**: low 2.5/3.0, medium 3.0/3.5, high 3.5/4.0. [ESC 2021]`,`Risk factors: mitral or tricuspid position, previous embolism, AF, mitral stenosis, LVEF under 35%. [ESC 2021]`,`Aspirin only for atherosclerosis or embolism despite good INR; **DOACs contraindicated**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`INR at least 4-weekly; interactions; consistent vitamin K diet; dental work without stopping VKA; bridging for major surgery. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`3. Pregnancy`,points:[`mWHO III; pre-conception counselling; pregnancy heart team. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`Weeks 6-12: VKA if warfarin 5 mg/day or less, else LMWH with anti-Xa monitoring; VKA to 36 weeks; then LMWH/UFH and planned delivery. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy]`]},{heading:`4. Complications`,points:[`**Valve thrombosis**: muffled click, low INR; heparin, surgery or low-dose slow alteplase. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`,`**Thromboembolism and bleeding**: about 1-2% and 1% per year; PCC and vitamin K for major bleeding. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Paravalvular leak and haemolysis**, **prosthetic endocarditis**, **pannus**, **structural degeneration**, **patient-prosthesis mismatch**. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`5. Follow-up schedule`,points:[`Baseline TTE at 6-12 weeks; yearly clinical review; echo for change in symptoms (mechanical); bioprosthesis echo at 5 and 10 years then yearly. [ACC/AHA 2020]`,`Valve card, IE prophylaxis, contraception advice, RHD penicillin prophylaxis. [ESC 2023 IE]`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of target INR by prosthesis thrombogenicity and patient risk factors`,`Flow chart of anticoagulation through pregnancy (weeks 6-12, to 36 weeks, delivery)`,`Comparison table: thrombus versus pannus`],markSplit:[{part:`Types and choice of valve`,marks:1},{part:`Anticoagulation targets and monitoring`,marks:3},{part:`Pregnancy`,marks:2},{part:`Complications and their management`,marks:3},{part:`Follow-up schedule`,marks:1}],keywords:[`mechanical valve`,`INR target`,`valve thrombosis`,`pregnancy anticoagulation`,`pannus`,`paravalvular leak`]},{id:`cardiovascular-prosthetic-valves-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate mechanical from bioprosthetic heart valves.`,openingLines:[`Mechanical valves trade lifelong durability for lifelong vitamin K antagonist therapy; bioprosthetic valves trade freedom from anticoagulation for structural degeneration and reintervention.`,`The choice depends on age, ability to take anticoagulation safely, pregnancy plans, bleeding risk and life expectancy, decided with the patient.`],answer:[{heading:`1. Structure and durability`,points:[`**Mechanical**: pyrolytic carbon bileaflet or tilting disc; lifelong. **Bioprosthetic**: porcine or pericardial tissue; degenerates after about 10 years. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`2. Anticoagulation`,points:[`**Mechanical**: lifelong VKA, INR 2.5-4.0 by valve and risk; DOAC contraindicated. **Bioprosthetic**: aspirin or VKA for 3 months; DOAC allowed if AF. [ESC/EACTS 2025]`]},{heading:`3. Indications and special groups`,points:[`**Mechanical**: young (under 60 aortic, 65 mitral), already anticoagulated. **Bioprosthetic**: older, bleeding risk, pregnancy wish, poor INR access. [ESC 2021]`,`Pregnancy: mechanical is mWHO III; bioprosthetic safer but degenerates faster. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`]},{heading:`4. Complications and sounds`,points:[`**Mechanical**: crisp clicks; thrombosis, embolism, bleeding. **Bioprosthetic**: near-normal sounds; degeneration, HALT; valve-in-valve possible. [Harrison 22e]`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table of mechanical versus bioprosthetic valves`],markSplit:[{part:`Structure and durability`,marks:1},{part:`Anticoagulation`,marks:1.5},{part:`Indications and pregnancy`,marks:1.5},{part:`Complications`,marks:1}],keywords:[`mechanical valve`,`bioprosthetic valve`,`valve choice`,`anticoagulation`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Prosthetic heart valves`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease: A Textbook of Cardiovascular Medicine, 12th edition, 2022 - Prosthetic heart valves`,`2025 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease`,`2021 ESC/EACTS Guidelines for the management of valvular heart disease (INR target table)`,`2020 ACC/AHA Guideline for the Management of Patients With Valvular Heart Disease`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy (and 2018 ESC pregnancy guideline for anti-Xa targets)`,`Zoghbi WA et al. Recommendations for evaluation of prosthetic valves with echocardiography and Doppler ultrasound, ASE 2009`,`Wang TY et al. Apixaban or warfarin in patients with an On-X mechanical aortic valve (PROACT Xa), NEJM Evidence, 2023`]});var c=[];c.push({id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd`,title:`Congenital heart disease in adults: ASD, VSD, PDA, coarctation, Tetralogy and Eisenmenger`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Adult congenital heart disease (ACHD)** is a structural heart defect present from birth and seen after age 18 - either **unrepaired shunts or obstructions** (ASD, VSD, PDA, coarctation) or **repaired lesions with residua** (repaired Tetralogy) - whose end-stage is **Eisenmenger syndrome**, where pulmonary vascular disease reverses a left-to-right shunt and causes cyanosis; shunts are closed only while **PVR is under 3 Wood units** (ESC 2020, ESC/ERS 2022).`,keywords:[`ACHD`,`GUCH`,`grown-up congenital heart`,`atrial septal defect`,`ostium secundum`,`ostium primum`,`sinus venosus`,`ventricular septal defect`,`maladie de Roger`,`patent ductus arteriosus`,`machinery murmur`,`coarctation of aorta`,`rib notching`,`radio-femoral delay`,`Tetralogy of Fallot`,`Eisenmenger syndrome`,`differential cyanosis`,`Lutembacher syndrome`,`Holt-Oram`,`bosentan`,`Qp:Qs`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Congenital heart disease (CHD)** occurs in about **8-10 per 1000 live births** worldwide, and with modern surgery **over 90% of children now reach adult life**, so adults with CHD now outnumber children with it. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`In **India** an estimated **more than 2 lakh children are born with CHD every year**, about a fifth with critical lesions needing early intervention; many never reach surgery and present as adults with **unrepaired shunts or Eisenmenger syndrome**. [Saxena, Ann Pediatr Cardiol 2016]`,`Excluding bicuspid aortic valve, **secundum ASD** is the commonest CHD first diagnosed in adults, because it is often silent until the 3rd-4th decade. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VSD** is the commonest CHD at birth (about a third of all CHD), but many small muscular defects close spontaneously in childhood, so the adult VSD is usually a **small perimembranous restrictive defect**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coarctation of aorta** forms about **5-8% of CHD**, is commoner in males and is a classic curable cause of **hypertension in the young**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Tetralogy of Fallot** is the commonest **cyanotic** CHD beyond infancy (about 7-10% of CHD); almost all adults with TOF in practice have been **repaired in childhood** and carry late residua. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eisenmenger syndrome** is the most advanced form of PAH due to CHD - a large unrepaired shunt with **pulmonary vascular resistance raised enough to reverse the shunt**; it is now rare in high-income countries but still seen in Indian OPDs. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`Heart failure is the **leading cause of death** and arrhythmia the **commonest cause of hospital admission** in adults with CHD. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Most CHD is **multifactorial**; a definable genetic syndrome is found in about 1 in 5, so look for dysmorphism in every adult with CHD. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Down syndrome**: about half have CHD, most commonly **atrioventricular septal defect (AVSD, ostium primum ASD)**, followed by VSD and PDA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Turner syndrome (45,X)**: **coarctation** and **bicuspid aortic valve**; check BP in both arms and a femoral pulse in every short girl with primary amenorrhoea. [Harrison 22e]`,`**22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge)** is found in about **15% of Tetralogy of Fallot** and in truncus arteriosus and interrupted arch - test before the patient plans a pregnancy (50% transmission). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Holt-Oram syndrome (TBX5)** - secundum ASD with **upper limb (thumb, radial) defects**; **Noonan syndrome** - pulmonary stenosis and HCM; **Williams syndrome** - supravalvular aortic stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Maternal rubella** in the first trimester causes **PDA and peripheral pulmonary stenosis**; prematurity and birth at **high altitude** also raise the PDA rate. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Teratogens**: **lithium (Ebstein anomaly)**, valproate, phenytoin, isotretinoin, **warfarin embryopathy**, alcohol (ASD, VSD) and **pre-gestational diabetes** (TGA, VSD, truncus). [Braunwald 12e]`,`A parent or sibling with CHD raises the offspring risk from about 1% to **about 3-5%** (higher if the mother is affected), and to **50%** with autosomal dominant syndromes. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Lutembacher syndrome** is an ASD with **rheumatic mitral stenosis** - still seen in India; the ASD decompresses the left atrium so MS is better tolerated, but RV overload is severe. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why the signs appear`,points:[`A **left-to-right shunt** sends oxygenated blood round the lungs again; its size depends on defect size and on the **relative resistance or compliance** of the two circulations, expressed as **Qp:Qs** (over 1.5 is significant). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ASD (pre-tricuspid shunt)**: flow depends on the stiffer LV versus the compliant RV, so the **RA, RV and pulmonary artery are volume-loaded**; as LV compliance falls with age (hypertension, CAD) the shunt grows, explaining onset of symptoms in the 40s. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wide fixed split S2 in ASD**: P2 is late because of the large RV stroke volume and the high-capacitance pulmonary bed (long hang-out interval); it is **fixed** because inspiration raises venous return but equally reduces the shunt, so RV volume does not change with respiration. [Harrison 22e]`,`The ASD murmur is **not from the defect** (low-pressure gradient) but from **increased flow across the pulmonary valve** (ejection systolic) and, when Qp:Qs exceeds about 2, across the tricuspid valve (mid-diastolic rumble). [Harrison 22e]`,`**VSD and PDA (post-tricuspid shunts)**: the shunted blood returns to the **LA and LV**, causing left-sided volume overload (LA and LV dilatation, apical mitral flow rumble) while transmitting **systemic pressure** to the lungs - so pulmonary vascular disease starts earlier than with ASD. [Harrison 22e]`,`A PDA shunts from aorta to pulmonary artery in **both systole and diastole**, giving the **continuous machinery murmur**, the diastolic run-off giving a **wide pulse pressure and bounding pulses**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pulmonary vascular disease**: high flow and pressure injure the endothelium, causing medial hypertrophy, intimal proliferation and fibrosis and finally **plexiform lesions** (Heath-Edwards grades I-VI; grade IV onward irreversible). [Robbins 11e]`,`**Eisenmenger reversal**: once PVR approaches or exceeds systemic resistance the shunt becomes right-to-left, causing cyanosis, **secondary erythrocytosis**, platelet dysfunction, hyperuricaemia and paradoxical emboli; closing the defect then removes the RV's only 'pop-off' and causes RV failure. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Coarctation**: a juxtaductal shelf distal to the left subclavian artery raises upper body pressure (renin activation and fixed resistance) while the lower body is perfused late through **intercostal and internal mammary collaterals**, which erode the ribs (notching) and cause radio-femoral delay. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Tetralogy**: anterior-cephalad deviation of the outlet septum produces all four features - **subpulmonary (infundibular) stenosis, malaligned VSD, overriding aorta and RV hypertrophy**; the degree of RVOT obstruction decides cyanosis, and dynamic infundibular spasm causes hypercyanotic spells. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Physiological classification**: **acyanotic with left-to-right shunt** (ASD, VSD, PDA, AVSD), **acyanotic obstructive** (coarctation, pulmonary stenosis, congenital AS) and **cyanotic** (TOF, TGA, Ebstein, tricuspid atresia, truncus, TAPVC, Eisenmenger). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ASD types**: **ostium secundum** about 75% (fossa ovalis), **ostium primum** about 15-20% (part of AVSD, cleft mitral valve), **sinus venosus** 5-10% (usually superior, with **partial anomalous pulmonary venous drainage** of right upper pulmonary vein) and **coronary sinus (unroofed)** under 1%. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **patent foramen ovale** (present in about 25% of adults) is not an ASD - no tissue is missing, there is no left-to-right volume load, and its relevance is paradoxical embolism. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**VSD types**: **perimembranous** about 80%, **muscular** (trabecular) about 15-20%, **outlet or doubly committed subarterial** (commoner in Asians, causes **aortic cusp prolapse and AR**) and **inlet** (AVSD type). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**VSD by size**: **restrictive** (small, Qp:Qs under 1.5, normal PA pressure - maladie de Roger), **moderately restrictive** (LV volume overload, mild PH) and **non-restrictive** (large, equal RV and LV pressure, early Eisenmenger). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**ESC 2020 complexity grading**: **mild** (isolated small ASD/VSD/PDA, mild PS, repaired simple shunts without residua), **moderate** (AVSD, coarctation, repaired TOF, Ebstein, PAPVC, moderate-severe PS) and **severe** (Eisenmenger and any CHD with PVD, unrepaired cyanotic CHD, Fontan, TGA, truncus). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**AHA/ACC ACHD anatomic-physiological (AP) classification**: anatomy **I-III** (simple, moderate, great complexity) plus physiological stage **A-D** (NYHA class, arrhythmia, hypoxaemia, PH, end-organ dysfunction) - it decides follow-up intensity. [AHA/ACC 2018 ACHD]`,`**PAH associated with CHD** is **group 1.4.4** of the PH classification, divided into **Eisenmenger syndrome**, PAH with prevalent systemic-to-pulmonary shunts, PAH with small or coincidental defects, and PAH after defect correction. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**ASD**: often found incidentally (murmur, ECG or chest X-ray in a pre-employment check); symptoms appear in the 30s-40s - **effort dyspnoea, fatigue, palpitations from atrial flutter or AF**, recurrent chest infections, or a **paradoxical embolic stroke**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Small VSD**: asymptomatic, found because of a loud murmur; a moderate VSD gives effort intolerance and **endocarditis** may be the first event; ask about **dental infections and fevers**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**PDA**: small ones are silent; moderate ones cause effort dyspnoea, palpitations and **endarteritis**; in older adults the duct may **calcify** (relevant at surgery). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Coarctation**: usually **asymptomatic hypertension in a young adult**; ask about **headache, epistaxis, cold feet, leg claudication** and a previous repair or balloon in childhood. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Repaired Tetralogy**: ask about **operation details and date** (Blalock-Taussig shunt, transannular patch), **declining exercise capacity**, palpitations, **syncope** (VT) and right heart failure symptoms. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Eisenmenger syndrome**: lifelong cyanosis or a murmur that **disappeared** in childhood, effort dyspnoea, **haemoptysis**, syncope, chest pain, **hyperviscosity symptoms** (headache, dizziness, visual blurring, paraesthesia, myalgia) and gout. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Squatting** after exertion in childhood points to unrepaired TOF - it raises systemic vascular resistance and diverts blood into the lungs. [Harrison 22e]`,`Always ask **pregnancy plans, contraception, family history of CHD** and drug exposure (lithium, valproate) - ACHD counselling is as much reproductive as cardiac. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Secundum ASD**: **wide fixed split S2**, soft **ejection systolic murmur in the pulmonary area** (left 2nd space), **tricuspid mid-diastolic rumble** at the lower left sternal edge with a large shunt, **RV heave** and palpable PA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primum ASD** adds an **apical pansystolic murmur** of mitral regurgitation through the cleft anterior mitral leaflet. [Harrison 22e]`,`**VSD**: **harsh pansystolic murmur with thrill at the left 3rd-4th space**, radiating across the precordium; the smaller the defect the louder the murmur; an apical mid-diastolic rumble means a large shunt, and an early diastolic murmur means **aortic cusp prolapse**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PDA**: **continuous machinery (Gibson) murmur** in the left infraclavicular area, peaking around S2, with **bounding pulses and wide pulse pressure**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Coarctation**: **radio-femoral delay**, weak femorals, upper limb hypertension with **arm-leg systolic difference over 20 mmHg** (normally leg BP is higher), systolic murmur at the left infraclavicular area and **between the scapulae**, and continuous murmurs over collaterals. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Suzman sign**: visible pulsating collateral arteries around the scapulae when the patient bends forward with arms hanging - coarctation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Repaired TOF**: midline sternotomy scar, often a **lateral thoracotomy scar with weak radial pulse on that side** (old Blalock-Taussig shunt - measure BP in the other arm), RV heave, **to-and-fro murmur of pulmonary regurgitation**, single or soft P2. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Eisenmenger syndrome**: **central cyanosis and clubbing**, RV heave, **loud palpable P2**, pulmonary ejection click, **Graham Steell murmur** (PR) and TR, with the original shunt murmur **gone**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**S2 in Eisenmenger tells the level of shunt**: **wide fixed split in ASD, single loud S2 in VSD, normally split S2 in PDA**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Differential cyanosis** - cyanosis and clubbing of the **toes but not the fingers** (left hand sometimes partly affected) - is pathognomonic of **PDA with Eisenmenger reversal**, as desaturated blood enters the aorta beyond the left subclavian. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**ASD versus pulmonary stenosis**: both give a pulmonary-area ejection murmur, but PS has an **ejection click, soft P2 and a split that still moves** with respiration, and ECG shows RVH without RBBB. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ASD versus innocent flow murmur** (thin chest, pregnancy, anaemia): the innocent murmur has a **normally split S2**, no RV heave and normal ECG. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**VSD versus mitral regurgitation**: MR is maximal at the **apex radiating to the axilla**, VSD at the **lower left sternal edge** with a thrill; **TR** is distinguished by an inspiratory increase (Carvallo sign) and giant v waves. [Harrison 22e]`,`**PDA versus other continuous murmurs**: **venous hum** (disappears lying flat or on jugular compression), **ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm** (sudden onset, lower sternal edge), coronary AV fistula, aortopulmonary window; combined AS plus AR gives a **to-and-fro**, not continuous, murmur. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Coarctation versus Takayasu arteritis**: both cause young hypertension with unequal pulses, but Takayasu (common in Indian women) shows **raised ESR or CRP, multiple stenoses, absent arm pulses and constitutional symptoms**, with no rib notching from birth. See the Takayasu topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coarctation versus essential or renovascular hypertension**: always feel the femorals simultaneously with the radial - a missed coarctation is a classic error in young hypertensives. See the Secondary hypertension topic. [ESC 2024 HT]`,`**Eisenmenger versus unrepaired TOF**: TOF has **oligaemic lungs, soft P2 and a loud pulmonary ejection murmur**; Eisenmenger has **dilated central PAs with peripheral pruning, loud P2** and no shunt murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eisenmenger versus idiopathic PAH**: an echo bubble study or TEE shows the shunt; iPAH has no cyanosis at rest unless a PFO opens. See the Pulmonary hypertension topic. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Pulse oximetry in all four limbs**: a toe saturation lower than the right hand by more than a few percent supports **PDA with reversal**; resting SpO2 under 90% defines significant cyanosis. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**ECG - secundum ASD**: **incomplete RBBB (rSR' in V1) with right axis deviation**, sometimes a **crochetage notch** on R in the inferior leads; **primum ASD: RBBB with left axis deviation and first-degree AV block**; **sinus venosus: ectopic atrial rhythm with inverted inferior P waves**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ECG - other lesions**: VSD and PDA give **LA enlargement and LVH**, large VSD with PH gives biventricular hypertrophy (Katz-Wachtel); coarctation gives LVH; repaired TOF gives **RBBB - QRS 180 ms or more predicts VT and sudden death**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Chest X-ray**: ASD - **RA and RV enlargement, prominent PA, plethora, small aortic knuckle**; coarctation - **rib notching of the 3rd-8th posterior ribs** (Roesler sign) and a **figure-of-3 sign**; unrepaired TOF - **boot-shaped heart** with oligaemia; Eisenmenger - **large central PAs with peripheral pruning**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transthoracic echo** is the first-line test: defect site and size, shunt direction, RV/LV volume overload, PA systolic pressure from TR jet, **agitated saline bubble study** for occult shunts; **TEE** measures ASD rims and finds sinus venosus defects. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Cardiac MRI** quantifies **RV volumes and function** (reference standard in repaired TOF), Qp:Qs, pulmonary regurgitant fraction and anomalous veins; **CT angiography** images coarctation, collaterals and stents. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Cardiac catheterisation** is mandatory before closing a shunt when echo suggests PH: measure **PVR** - **under 3 Wood units** closure, **3-5 WU** individual decision, **5 WU or more** closure only if PVR falls under 5 with PAH therapy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Cyanotic patients**: Hb and haematocrit, MCV, **ferritin and transferrin saturation** (iron deficiency is common and masked by high Hb), platelets, uric acid, creatinine, NT-proBNP, 6-minute walk, coagulation (sample corrected for haematocrit). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Cardiopulmonary exercise testing** objectively times interventions (falling peak VO2 in repaired TOF), and **Holter** screens for atrial and ventricular arrhythmia. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - lesion by lesion`,points:[`**ASD - close** when there is **RV volume overload and PVR under 3 WU, regardless of symptoms**; **device closure is the method of choice for secundum ASD** with adequate rims, while primum, sinus venosus and coronary sinus defects need surgery. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**ASD in the elderly**: closure still improves symptoms, but in LV diastolic dysfunction a **balloon test occlusion** is needed first, because closing the defect can flood a stiff LV and cause pulmonary oedema. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**VSD - close (usually surgically)** when there is **LV volume overload and PVR under 3 WU**, after **endocarditis**, or with **aortic cusp prolapse causing progressive AR**; small restrictive VSDs are left alone and followed for IE and AR. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**PDA - close (transcatheter device preferred)** when there is **LV volume overload and PVR under 3 WU**; a silent PDA found only on echo needs no closure. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Coarctation - intervene** when the upper-lower limb pressure difference is **over 20 mmHg with upper limb hypertension (140/90 or more), an exaggerated exercise BP response or significant LVH**; consider it in hypertensives with **50% or more narrowing** whatever the gradient; **stenting** is preferred in adults when anatomy suits. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Repaired TOF - pulmonary valve replacement** (surgical or **transcatheter**) for symptomatic severe PR, or asymptomatic severe PR with falling exercise capacity, **RV end-systolic volume index 80 mL/m2 or more or end-diastolic 160 mL/m2 or more**, progressive RV dysfunction or TR. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Eisenmenger - targeted therapy**: **bosentan 62.5 mg twice daily for 4 weeks, then 125 mg twice daily** in WHO functional class III (BREATHE-5); add **sildenafil 20 mg three times daily** or tadalafil if not improving. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Eisenmenger - general care**: avoid **dehydration, heat, strenuous isometric exercise, high altitude, smoking and pregnancy**; correct **iron deficiency** carefully; vaccinate against influenza, pneumococcus and COVID-19. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Contraception**: avoid **oestrogen-containing pills** in cyanotic or Eisenmenger patients (thrombosis); prefer **levonorgestrel IUD, implant or desogestrel pill**; bosentan reduces the efficacy of hormonal contraception, so use two methods. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Arrhythmia**: atrial flutter and AF are common after ASD, TOF and Fontan; give **oral anticoagulation to all patients with moderate or complex repairs, cyanosis or Fontan** who have atrial flutter or AF, and use CHA2DS2-VA in simple lesions. See the Atrial fibrillation topic. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Haemoptysis in Eisenmenger**: admit, stop antiplatelets and anticoagulants, CT chest (pulmonary infarction, in-situ PA thrombus, bronchial collaterals), avoid bronchoscopy unless essential, and consider bronchial artery embolisation. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Phlebotomy is not routine**: perform it only for **moderate-severe hyperviscosity symptoms with haematocrit over 65%** after excluding dehydration and iron deficiency, removing 250-500 mL with **isovolumic saline replacement**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**IV lines in any right-to-left shunt need air filters** to prevent paradoxical air embolism to the brain. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Non-cardiac surgery in Eisenmenger** carries high risk: do it in an ACHD centre with an experienced anaesthetist, avoid sudden falls in systemic vascular resistance (which increase right-to-left shunting), and avoid dehydration and prolonged fasting. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Fever and headache with focal signs in a cyanotic adult** means **brain abscess** until CT proves otherwise - the lung filter is bypassed by the shunt. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypercyanotic (tet) spell** in an unrepaired child or adolescent: knee-chest position, oxygen, **morphine 0.1-0.2 mg/kg**, IV fluids, IV propranolol and phenylephrine to raise systemic resistance. [Nelson 22e]`,`**Sustained VT in repaired TOF**: DC cardioversion if unstable, then electrophysiology study, ablation of the RVOT isthmus and **ICD** for secondary prevention. See the Ventricular tachycardia topic. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Atrial flutter in ACHD** (often slow 2:1 intra-atrial re-entry that is easily missed) is poorly tolerated - cardiovert promptly after excluding thrombus, and refer for ablation. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Special situations - pregnancy, elderly, children, resource-limited`,points:[`**mWHO class I**: successfully repaired simple lesions (ASD, VSD, PDA, anomalous pulmonary venous drainage) and small PDA; **class II**: unoperated ASD or VSD and repaired TOF; **class II-III**: repaired coarctation; **class IV (pregnancy contraindicated)**: **Eisenmenger, PAH and severe native coarctation**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Eisenmenger pregnancy** has a high maternal mortality (historically 30-50%) from RV failure, thrombosis and haemorrhage around delivery, and very high fetal loss from cyanosis; advise against pregnancy and discuss termination if it occurs. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Coarctation in pregnancy**: risk of **aortic dissection and pre-eclampsia-like hypertension**; image the aorta before conception and control BP with labetalol. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Fetal echocardiography at 18-22 weeks** is offered to every mother with CHD or a previously affected child. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly with ASD**: AF, flutter and tricuspid regurgitation dominate; closure helps symptoms but does not abolish AF, which needs anticoagulation on its own merits. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Children and adolescents** in India are screened under **RBSK (Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram)** for defects at birth, with referral through **District Early Intervention Centres (DEIC)** and free surgery or device closure via RBSK and **PM-JAY** packages. [RBSK MoHFW]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: a stethoscope, four-limb oximetry, ECG and chest X-ray identify most adult shunts; the key decision - whether a shunt can still be closed - needs **echo plus catheter PVR at a tertiary centre**, so refer before PH becomes fixed. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Air travel and altitude**: commercial flights are usually tolerated by Eisenmenger patients with hydration and leg exercise, but **high-altitude residence** worsens hypoxaemia. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Arrhythmias**: atrial flutter and AF (ASD, repaired TOF), **VT and sudden death** (repaired TOF), heart block (primum ASD repair, perimembranous VSD device closure). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Pulmonary hypertension**: develops in a minority with ASD (late, often in women) and early with large VSD or PDA; once Eisenmenger physiology sets in, the defect can no longer be closed. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Infective endocarditis** is a lifelong risk in **small VSD and PDA** (high-velocity jets) and repaired TOF, but very rare in isolated secundum ASD. See the Infective endocarditis topic. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Paradoxical embolism and stroke** occur with any intracardiac shunt; **brain abscess** is a risk in cyanotic CHD. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Coarctation late outcomes**: persistent or new **hypertension** in a third or more despite repair, **recoarctation**, aneurysm at the repair site, bicuspid valve disease, aortic dissection, **premature CAD** and rupture of **berry aneurysms (about 10%)**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`Untreated coarctation has a **mean survival of about 35 years**, with death from heart failure, aortic rupture, endocarditis or intracranial haemorrhage. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ASD closed before age 25** gives near-normal survival; later closure still improves symptoms and RV size but leaves a higher risk of AF, stroke and heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eisenmenger syndrome** survival is better than idiopathic PAH at the same pressure (the RV is trained from birth and the shunt decompresses it); many live into their 40s-50s, dying of RV failure, haemoptysis, sudden death or pregnancy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`Every adult with **moderate or severe complexity CHD** should be seen at least once in an **ACHD specialist centre**, and severe lesions should be followed there. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Follow-up interval**: severe complexity **6-12 monthly**, moderate **12-24 monthly**, mild (for example a small restrictive VSD) **every 3-5 years**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**After ASD device closure**: review in the first months and at 1-2 years (residual shunt, erosion, arrhythmia, PA pressure), then every 2-5 years depending on residua; **antiplatelet therapy for 6 months** is usual after device closure. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Repaired coarctation**: BP at every visit (including **ambulatory BP or exercise BP**), and **CMR or CT of the aorta every 3-5 years** for recoarctation and aneurysm. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Repaired TOF**: yearly review with ECG (QRS width), echo, **CMR every 2-3 years**, Holter and CPET as indicated, at an ACHD centre. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Eisenmenger**: 6-12 monthly at a PH or ACHD centre with SpO2, Hb, ferritin, renal function, uric acid, NT-proBNP and 6-minute walk. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Refer urgently** for a new cyanosis, syncope, sustained palpitations, haemoptysis, a pregnancy in a moderate or high-risk lesion, or fever with a known shunt or prosthetic material. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`In primary care, keep a **lesion card** listing the anatomy, operations, IE risk and anticoagulation, so emergency teams do not treat the patient from first principles. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Rubella immunisation** of girls and women (MR vaccine under the Universal Immunisation Programme) prevents congenital rubella PDA and pulmonary artery stenosis. [MoHFW UIP]`,`**Preconception care**: periconceptional **folic acid**, tight glycaemic control in diabetic mothers, and switching teratogens (lithium, valproate, warfarin, ACE inhibitors) before conception. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Newborn pulse oximetry and RBSK screening** pick up critical CHD; mobile health teams screen children in Anganwadis and schools and refer to DEICs. [RBSK MoHFW]`,`**Dental hygiene and skin care** are the main IE prevention for all CHD; antibiotic prophylaxis is reserved for **high-risk** patients before dental procedures. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**Exercise**: most patients with repaired simple lesions can play competitive sport; Eisenmenger, significant PAH and severe obstruction need **low-intensity dynamic** exercise only. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Genetic counselling** before pregnancy: offspring recurrence about 3-5% overall, 50% for autosomal dominant conditions such as **22q11 deletion and Holt-Oram**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Transition clinics** from paediatric to adult care prevent the commonest failure in ACHD - **loss to follow-up in late adolescence**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2025)`,points:[`**ESC/ERS 2022 PH guideline** lowered the PH definition to **mPAP over 20 mmHg and PVR over 2 WU**, kept PAH-CHD in group 1, and set shunt-closure thresholds at **PVR under 3 WU (close), 3-5 WU (individual), 5 WU or more (only if it falls with PAH therapy, fenestrated)**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline**: high-risk CHD for prophylaxis is **untreated cyanotic CHD** and CHD repaired with prosthetic material - surgical or transcatheter - **for 6 months**, or lifelong if a residual shunt or valve regurgitation persists. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2023** dropped **clindamycin** from dental prophylaxis because of C. difficile; penicillin-allergic patients get **azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg** or **doxycycline 100 mg** orally. [ESC 2023 IE]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline** keeps the **mWHO classification**, requires a **Pregnancy Heart Team** for mWHO II-III and above, and recommends pre-pregnancy counselling and imaging for all women with CHD. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**ESC 2024 hypertension guideline** lists coarctation among the secondary causes to exclude in young hypertensives - **feel the femorals at the first visit**. [ESC 2024 HT]`,`The **ESC 2020 ACHD guideline** remains the current ESC document for ACHD - device closure as method of choice for secundum ASD, stenting for coarctation, CMR volume thresholds for pulmonary valve replacement, and **no shunt closure in Eisenmenger (class III)**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic viva: **why is S2 fixed in ASD?** - inspiration increases venous return but reduces the left-to-right shunt equally, so RV volume and P2 timing stay constant. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why differential cyanosis in PDA?** - the duct enters the aorta **beyond the left subclavian**, so only the lower body receives desaturated blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why rib notching, and why not before age 5?** - dilated tortuous intercostal collaterals erode the inferior rib margins over years; the **first two ribs are spared** because their intercostals arise from the costocervical trunk above the coarctation. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Why not phlebotomise every cyanotic patient?** - erythrocytosis is compensatory; repeated venesection causes **iron deficiency, microcytosis and worse hyperviscosity and stroke risk**. [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**Commonest mistake**: advising closure of a shunt without measuring PVR - closing an Eisenmenger defect is lethal (class III). [ESC 2020 ACHD]`,`**ECG clue pairs**: secundum ASD = incomplete RBBB + **right** axis; primum ASD = RBBB + **left** axis + long PR; repaired TOF = RBBB with **QRS 180 ms or more as a sudden-death risk marker**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lutembacher syndrome** (ASD + mitral stenosis) and **Holt-Oram** (ASD + thumb anomaly) are favourite Indian viva eponyms. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Common adult congenital lesions - signs, ECG and imaging`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Key bedside signs`,`ECG`,`Chest X-ray / echo clue`],rows:[[`Secundum ASD`,`Wide fixed split S2, pulmonary ESM, tricuspid MDM if large, RV heave`,`Incomplete RBBB, right axis, crochetage notch`,`RA/RV enlarged, prominent PA, plethora; RV volume overload on echo`],[`Primum ASD (partial AVSD)`,`As secundum plus apical PSM of cleft mitral valve`,`RBBB, left axis deviation, first-degree AV block`,`Cleft anterior mitral leaflet; Down syndrome association`],[`Sinus venosus ASD`,`As secundum; often missed on TTE`,`Ectopic atrial rhythm, inverted inferior P waves`,`TEE or CMR shows defect and anomalous right upper pulmonary vein`],[`VSD (restrictive)`,`Harsh PSM with thrill, left 3rd-4th space; louder when smaller`,`Normal or LVH`,`Normal heart size; colour jet on echo`],[`VSD (large) / PDA`,`Apical MDM, displaced apex; PDA - machinery murmur, bounding pulse`,`LA enlargement, LVH; biventricular if PH`,`LA/LV dilated, plethora; ductal flow on echo`],[`Coarctation`,`Radio-femoral delay, arm-leg gradient over 20 mmHg, interscapular murmur`,`LVH`,`Rib notching 3rd-8th ribs, figure-of-3; Doppler diastolic tail`],[`Repaired TOF`,`Sternotomy, PR to-and-fro murmur, RV heave, weak arm after BT shunt`,`RBBB; QRS 180 ms or more is high risk`,`Dilated RV on CMR; free pulmonary regurgitation`],[`Eisenmenger`,`Cyanosis, clubbing, loud P2, Graham Steell murmur, shunt murmur gone`,`RVH, right axis, RA enlargement`,`Large central PAs, peripheral pruning; bidirectional or right-to-left shunt`]]},{heading:`When to close or intervene (ESC 2020 ACHD, ESC/ERS 2022 PH)`,columns:[`Lesion`,`Intervene when`,`Do not intervene when`,`Preferred method`],rows:[[`Secundum ASD`,`RV volume overload and PVR under 3 WU, symptoms or not; paradoxical embolism`,`Eisenmenger; PVR 5 WU or more that does not fall with therapy`,`Transcatheter device if rims adequate`],[`Primum / sinus venosus ASD`,`RV volume overload, PVR under 3 WU`,`Eisenmenger physiology`,`Surgical patch (and PAPVC baffle or cleft repair)`],[`VSD`,`LV volume overload with PVR under 3 WU; previous IE; aortic cusp prolapse with progressive AR`,`Small restrictive VSD without overload; Eisenmenger`,`Surgical closure; device in selected muscular defects`],[`PDA`,`LV volume overload with PVR under 3 WU`,`Silent PDA; Eisenmenger`,`Transcatheter device or coil`],[`Coarctation`,`Gradient over 20 mmHg with upper limb HT, exercise HT or LVH; or 50% or more narrowing in a hypertensive`,`Mild narrowing without hypertension`,`Stenting in adults; surgery for complex arch anatomy`],[`Repaired TOF`,`Severe PR with symptoms, or RVESVi 80 or RVEDVi 160 mL/m2 or more, RV dysfunction, falling exercise capacity`,`Mild PR with stable RV size`,`Surgical or transcatheter pulmonary valve replacement`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of the adult congenital murmur`,columns:[`Presentation`,`Lesion`,`Look-alike`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Pulmonary-area ejection murmur`,`ASD`,`Pulmonary stenosis`,`PS has ejection click, soft P2 and a split that moves with respiration`],[`Pansystolic murmur`,`VSD`,`Mitral regurgitation / TR`,`MR at apex to axilla; TR increases with inspiration and has giant v waves`],[`Continuous murmur`,`PDA`,`Venous hum, ruptured sinus of Valsalva, coronary AV fistula`,`Venous hum disappears supine or with jugular pressure; sinus of Valsalva rupture is sudden and lower`],[`Young hypertensive with unequal pulses`,`Coarctation`,`Takayasu arteritis`,`Takayasu has high ESR, absent arm pulses, multiple stenoses, no rib notching`],[`Cyanosis with clubbing`,`Eisenmenger`,`Unrepaired TOF`,`TOF has oligaemic lungs and soft P2; Eisenmenger has loud P2 and pruned peripheral vessels`],[`Cyanosis of toes only`,`PDA with reversal`,`Peripheral vascular disease, cold exposure`,`Differential SpO2 between hand and toe with clubbing of toes`]]},{heading:`Drugs used in adult congenital heart disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Bosentan (ERA)`,`62.5 mg orally twice daily, then 125 mg twice daily`,`Up-titrate after 4 weeks; long-term in Eisenmenger WHO-FC III`,`Hepatotoxic - monthly LFT; teratogenic; anaemia; lowers hormonal contraceptive levels`],[`Sildenafil (PDE5 inhibitor)`,`20 mg orally three times daily`,`Long-term; add-on if not improving on ERA`,`Headache, flushing, epistaxis, visual change; never with nitrates or riociguat`],[`Tadalafil (PDE5 inhibitor)`,`40 mg orally once daily`,`Long-term; alternative to sildenafil`,`As sildenafil; reduce dose in renal impairment`],[`Amoxicillin (IE prophylaxis)`,`2 g orally 30-60 minutes before dental procedure`,`Single dose; high-risk patients only`,`Penicillin allergy - use azithromycin or clarithromycin 500 mg or doxycycline 100 mg (not clindamycin)`],[`Oral iron (ferrous sulphate)`,`1 tablet (about 60 mg elemental iron) orally once daily or alternate days`,`Until ferritin and transferrin saturation normal; recheck Hb in 2-4 weeks`,`GI upset; overshoot of haematocrit - monitor; IV ferric carboxymaltose if intolerant`],[`Aspirin / clopidogrel after device closure`,`Aspirin 75-100 mg orally daily (clopidogrel 75 mg if intolerant)`,`Usually 6 months after ASD or PFO device`,`Bleeding; avoid routine aspirin in Eisenmenger (haemoptysis)`],[`Beta-blocker (metoprolol succinate)`,`25-100 mg orally once daily`,`Long-term for residual hypertension after coarctation repair`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; ACEi or ARB are alternatives`]]},{heading:`Pregnancy risk and endocarditis prophylaxis by lesion`,columns:[`Lesion`,`mWHO class (ESC 2025)`,`IE prophylaxis for dental work`,`Main pregnancy issue`],rows:[[`Repaired ASD, VSD, PDA without residua`,`I`,`No (only 6 months after prosthetic patch or device)`,`Low risk; routine care`],[`Unoperated ASD or VSD`,`II`,`No`,`Paradoxical embolism, arrhythmia; low risk overall`],[`Repaired Tetralogy of Fallot`,`II`,`Only if prosthetic valve or conduit, or residual shunt next to prosthetic material`,`RV failure with severe PR; arrhythmia; 22q11 recurrence`],[`Repaired coarctation`,`II-III`,`No unless prosthetic material within 6 months or residual defect`,`Hypertension, pre-eclampsia, aortic dissection`],[`Severe native coarctation`,`IV`,`No`,`Aortic dissection and rupture; repair before pregnancy`],[`Eisenmenger syndrome`,`IV`,`Yes - untreated cyanotic CHD`,`Maternal mortality historically 30-50%; advise against pregnancy`]]}],redFlags:[`**New or worsening cyanosis**, SpO2 falling below the patient's baseline, or syncope in any adult with CHD.`,`**Haemoptysis** in Eisenmenger syndrome.`,`**Fever with a known shunt, conduit or prosthetic material** - blood cultures before antibiotics, rule out endocarditis.`,`**Fever, headache and focal deficit in a cyanotic patient** - brain abscess until proved otherwise.`,`**Sustained palpitations or broad-complex tachycardia** in repaired TOF (VT, sudden death risk).`,`**Pregnancy** in a woman with Eisenmenger, PAH, severe coarctation or any moderate-severe CHD.`,`**Severe chest or interscapular pain** in coarctation or bicuspid valve - aortic dissection.`],pearls:[`**Fixed split S2 = ASD** until proved otherwise; the murmur is pulmonary flow, not the hole.`,`**Smaller the VSD, louder the murmur** - a loud VSD murmur is reassuring; one that fades with a loud P2 is ominous.`,`**S2 in Eisenmenger**: ASD wide fixed, VSD single, PDA normally split.`,`**Differential cyanosis (toes, not fingers) = PDA with reversal.**`,`**Secundum = incomplete RBBB + right axis; primum = RBBB + left axis + long PR.**`,`**Feel the femorals in every young hypertensive** - radio-femoral delay is coarctation until proved otherwise.`,`**Close shunts only when PVR is under 3 WU**; never close an Eisenmenger defect.`,`**Repaired TOF: QRS 180 ms or more and RVEDVi 160 mL/m2 or more** are the numbers that trigger action.`,`**Phlebotomy in cyanotic CHD only for symptomatic hyperviscosity with Hct over 65%** - otherwise it causes iron deficiency.`,`**No oestrogen pill and no pregnancy in Eisenmenger.**`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old woman has effort dyspnoea. There is an RV heave, a soft ejection systolic murmur in the left 2nd intercostal space and a widely split S2 that does not vary with respiration. ECG shows rSR' in V1 with right axis deviation. The most likely diagnosis is:`,options:[`Ostium primum atrial septal defect`,`Valvular pulmonary stenosis`,`Ostium secundum atrial septal defect`,`Small perimembranous ventricular septal defect`],answer:2,explanation:`Wide fixed splitting of S2 with a pulmonary flow murmur, RV heave and incomplete RBBB with right axis deviation is the classic ostium secundum ASD. An ostium primum defect also gives RBBB but with left axis deviation, a long PR and a mitral regurgitant murmur. Pulmonary stenosis has an ejection click and a split that still moves with respiration, and a small VSD gives a harsh pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge with a normal ECG.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-q2`,stem:`A 22-year-old man with BP 168/96 mmHg in the right arm has weak, delayed femoral pulses. Chest X-ray shows notching of the inferior margins of several posterior ribs. The notching is caused by:`,options:[`Erosion by dilated, tortuous intercostal collateral arteries`,`Pressure from a dilated post-stenotic aortic segment`,`Bone resorption from secondary hyperparathyroidism`,`Erosion by enlarged internal mammary veins`],answer:0,explanation:`In coarctation the lower body is perfused through intercostal and internal mammary arterial collaterals; the dilated tortuous intercostal arteries erode the inferior rib margins (Roesler sign), usually 3rd-8th ribs and rarely before age 5. The first two ribs are spared because their intercostals arise above the coarctation. Post-stenotic dilatation gives the figure-of-3 sign, not notching; hyperparathyroidism causes subperiosteal resorption elsewhere; the collaterals are arteries, not veins.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old woman with Eisenmenger syndrome due to a large VSD has mild headache and fatigue. Haematocrit is 66%, MCV 74 fL and ferritin 9 ng/mL. She is well hydrated. The most appropriate next step is:`,options:[`Regular phlebotomy of 500 mL every 2 weeks to keep haematocrit under 60%`,`Start warfarin to prevent thrombosis`,`Urgent surgical closure of the VSD`,`Cautious iron replacement with monitoring of haematocrit`],answer:3,explanation:`Secondary erythrocytosis in cyanotic CHD is compensatory, and microcytic iron-deficient red cells are stiff and worsen hyperviscosity symptoms; mild symptoms with iron deficiency are treated by careful iron replacement. Routine phlebotomy is avoided and reserved for moderate-severe hyperviscosity with haematocrit over 65% after dehydration and iron deficiency are corrected. Routine anticoagulation risks haemoptysis, and closing an Eisenmenger VSD is contraindicated because the RV loses its pop-off.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-q4`,stem:`A 28-year-old man had Tetralogy of Fallot repaired with a transannular patch at age 2. He is asymptomatic. CMR shows free pulmonary regurgitation with RV end-diastolic volume index 170 mL/m2. The best management is:`,options:[`Reassurance and review in 5 years`,`Pulmonary valve replacement (surgical or transcatheter)`,`Start bosentan for right heart pressure`,`Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator alone`],answer:1,explanation:`Late severe pulmonary regurgitation after transannular repair dilates and then weakens the RV; ESC 2020 recommends pulmonary valve replacement in asymptomatic severe PR once RVEDVi reaches 160 mL/m2 or RVESVi 80 mL/m2, falling exercise capacity, RV dysfunction or progressive TR appear. Waiting 5 years risks irreversible RV failure and VT. This is volume overload, not pulmonary arterial hypertension, so bosentan has no role, and an ICD without a VT indication does not treat the cause.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-q5`,stem:`Under the ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline, which patient should receive antibiotic prophylaxis before a dental extraction?`,options:[`A 40-year-old with an unrepaired small secundum ASD`,`A 30-year-old with a small restrictive VSD and no previous endocarditis`,`A 25-year-old woman 3 months after transcatheter device closure of an ASD`,`A 50-year-old 10 years after surgical PDA ligation with no residual shunt`],answer:2,explanation:`ESC 2023 classes CHD repaired with prosthetic material, whether surgical or transcatheter, as high risk for 6 months after the procedure (lifelong only if a residual shunt or valve regurgitation persists), so the patient 3 months after device closure gets amoxicillin 2 g orally 30-60 minutes before the extraction. An unrepaired secundum ASD, a small restrictive VSD and a remotely ligated PDA without residua are not high-risk categories; they need good dental hygiene, not antibiotics.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the types, clinical features, investigations and management of atrial septal defect in adults. Add a note on Eisenmenger syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Atrial septal defect is a congenital deficiency of the interatrial septum causing a left-to-right shunt at atrial level, with volume overload of the right atrium, right ventricle and pulmonary circulation.`,`Excluding bicuspid aortic valve it is the commonest congenital heart disease first diagnosed in adults; it should be closed while pulmonary vascular resistance is under 3 Wood units (ESC 2020 ACHD).`],answer:[{heading:`Types and associations`,points:[`Ostium secundum about 75%, ostium primum 15-20% (partial AVSD, cleft mitral valve, Down syndrome), sinus venosus 5-10% (anomalous right upper pulmonary vein), coronary sinus under 1%.`,`Associations: Holt-Oram (thumb anomalies), Lutembacher (with rheumatic MS); PFO is not an ASD.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Shunt size depends on defect size and the compliance of RV versus LV; falling LV compliance with age increases the shunt.`,`RA, RV and PA volume overload; late PAH in a minority; atrial stretch causes flutter and AF.`,`Fixed split S2: inspiration raises venous return but reduces the shunt equally.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Asymptomatic for years; then effort dyspnoea, palpitations (AF, flutter), chest infections, paradoxical stroke, right heart failure.`,`Signs: RV heave, wide fixed split S2, pulmonary ejection systolic murmur, tricuspid mid-diastolic rumble when Qp:Qs over 2; apical PSM in primum.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG: secundum - incomplete RBBB, right axis, crochetage; primum - RBBB, left axis, long PR; sinus venosus - ectopic atrial rhythm.`,`Chest X-ray: RA and RV enlargement, prominent PA, plethora, small aortic knuckle.`,`TTE with bubble study; TEE for rims and sinus venosus; CMR for Qp:Qs and RV volume; catheter PVR when PA pressure is raised.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Close when RV volume overload and PVR under 3 WU regardless of symptoms; 3-5 WU individual decision; 5 WU or more only if PVR falls with PAH therapy.`,`Device closure for secundum ASD with adequate rims; surgery for primum, sinus venosus and coronary sinus defects.`,`Elderly with LV disease: balloon test occlusion first; aspirin for 6 months after device; anticoagulate AF on its own merits.`,`Follow-up for residual shunt, erosion, arrhythmia and PA pressure.`]},{heading:`Note on Eisenmenger syndrome`,points:[`Large unrepaired shunt with pulmonary vascular disease raising PVR to systemic level, reversing the shunt; commonest cause large VSD.`,`Cyanosis, clubbing, erythrocytosis, loud P2, Graham Steell murmur, haemoptysis; S2 fixed split (ASD), single (VSD), normal (PDA); differential cyanosis in PDA.`,`Management: no defect closure; bosentan 62.5 then 125 mg twice daily (BREATHE-5), add PDE5 inhibitor; avoid dehydration, altitude, pregnancy and oestrogen; phlebotomy only for symptomatic Hct over 65%; air filters on IV lines.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the interatrial septum showing the sites of secundum, primum, sinus venosus and coronary sinus defects`,`Flow chart: ASD with raised PA pressure - catheter PVR under 3, 3-5 and 5 or more Wood units and the decision for each`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, types and pathophysiology`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and fixed split S2`,marks:2},{part:`Investigations (ECG, X-ray, echo, catheter)`,marks:2},{part:`Management and closure indications`,marks:2},{part:`Eisenmenger syndrome note`,marks:2}],keywords:[`ASD`,`fixed split S2`,`device closure`,`PVR`,`Wood units`,`Eisenmenger`,`bosentan`,`Lutembacher`]},{id:`cardiovascular-adult-chd-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on coarctation of the aorta in the adult.`,openingLines:[`Coarctation is a congenital narrowing of the aorta, usually juxtaductal just distal to the left subclavian artery, forming 5-8% of CHD.`,`In adults it presents as hypertension with radio-femoral delay and is a curable secondary cause that is missed when the femorals are not felt.`],answer:[{heading:`Associations`,points:[`Bicuspid aortic valve in about half, Turner syndrome, berry aneurysms (about 10%), Shone complex.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Headache, epistaxis, cold legs, claudication, or asymptomatic hypertension.`,`Radio-femoral delay, arm-leg systolic gradient over 20 mmHg, interscapular systolic murmur, collateral murmurs, Suzman sign.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`ECG LVH; chest X-ray rib notching 3rd-8th ribs, figure-of-3 sign.`,`Echo suprasternal Doppler with diastolic tail; CT or CMR angiography defines anatomy and collaterals.`]},{heading:`Management and follow-up`,points:[`Intervene if gradient over 20 mmHg with upper limb HT, exercise HT or LVH, or 50% or more narrowing in a hypertensive; stenting preferred in adults.`,`Lifelong follow-up: BP (ambulatory or exercise), CMR or CT every 3-5 years for recoarctation and aneurysm; treat residual hypertension.`,`Pregnancy: severe native coarctation mWHO IV; repaired II-III.`]}],mustDraw:[`Sketch of the aortic arch showing the coarctation distal to the left subclavian and the intercostal collateral route`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and associations`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1},{part:`Management and follow-up`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`coarctation`,`radio-femoral delay`,`rib notching`,`bicuspid aortic valve`,`Turner`,`stenting`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Congenital heart disease in the adult`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Congenital heart disease in the adult and pediatric patient`,`2020 ESC Guidelines for the management of adult congenital heart disease (Eur Heart J 2021)`,`2022 ESC/ERS Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of pulmonary hypertension`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy`,`2018 AHA/ACC Guideline for the management of adults with congenital heart disease`,`Rashtriya Bal Swasthya Karyakram (RBSK) operational guidelines, MoHFW, Government of India`]}),c.push({id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension`,title:`Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Pulmonary hypertension** is a **mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest on right heart catheterisation**, called **pre-capillary** when **PAWP is 15 mmHg or less and PVR over 2 Wood units** (ESC/ERS 2022), grouped into **five WHO clinical groups** - left heart disease and lung disease being commonest - while **cor pulmonale** is right ventricular hypertrophy, dilatation or failure caused by disease of the lungs, chest wall or pulmonary vessels.`,keywords:[`PH`,`PAH`,`pulmonary arterial hypertension`,`cor pulmonale`,`mPAP`,`PVR`,`Wood units`,`right heart catheterisation`,`CTEPH`,`pulmonary endarterectomy`,`balloon pulmonary angioplasty`,`sildenafil`,`tadalafil`,`bosentan`,`ambrisentan`,`macitentan`,`riociguat`,`selexipag`,`epoprostenol`,`sotatercept`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**PH** is **mPAP over 20 mmHg at rest** measured by right heart catheterisation; the old threshold of 25 mmHg was lowered because 20 mmHg is the upper limit of normal (mean 14 plus 2 SD). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Pre-capillary PH**: mPAP over 20, **PAWP 15 mmHg or less, PVR over 2 WU**; **isolated post-capillary PH**: PAWP over 15 and PVR 2 WU or less; **combined pre- and post-capillary PH**: PAWP over 15 and PVR over 2 WU. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Exercise PH** is a **mPAP/cardiac output slope over 3 mmHg/L/min** between rest and exercise - an early-disease concept, used only in expert centres. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`PH affects about **1% of the world population** and up to **10% of people over 65**; **left heart disease (group 2)** is the commonest cause, followed by **lung disease (group 3)**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**PAH (group 1)** is rare - about **6 new cases per million adults per year** and a prevalence of about **50 per million**; idiopathic PAH is commoner in women, and recent registries show an older mean age at diagnosis (50-65 years). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`In **India** there is no national PH registry; hospital series show **rheumatic mitral valve disease, COPD (tobacco and biomass smoke), post-tuberculosis lung disease and unrepaired congenital shunts** as dominant causes, with **high-altitude PH** in Himalayan residents. [Indian hospital series]`,`**Cor pulmonale** is RV hypertrophy and/or dilatation from a primary disorder of the respiratory system (parenchyma, airways, chest wall, ventilatory control or pulmonary vessels); RV changes due to left heart disease or CHD are excluded by definition. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronic cor pulmonale** is most often due to **COPD**; **acute cor pulmonale** is sudden RV dilatation from massive pulmonary embolism or ARDS. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Heritable PAH**: **BMPR2 mutations** in about 75% of familial PAH and 10-20% of apparently sporadic cases (autosomal dominant, reduced penetrance); also ACVRL1, ENG, SMAD9, TBX4, EIF2AK4 (PVOD). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Drugs and toxins with definite association**: **aminorex, fenfluramine, dexfenfluramine, benfluorex, methamphetamine, dasatinib**, toxic rapeseed oil; likely associations include amphetamines, cocaine, interferons, some chemotherapy (mitomycin C). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Associated PAH**: **connective tissue disease** (systemic sclerosis is commonest - 5-12% of SSc develop PAH), **HIV**, **portal hypertension** (portopulmonary hypertension), **CHD** and **schistosomiasis** (common globally, rare in India). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 2 risk profile**: older age, **hypertension, obesity, diabetes, AF** and HFpEF; in India also **rheumatic mitral stenosis and regurgitation**. See the Mitral stenosis topic. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 3**: COPD, **interstitial lung disease**, combined pulmonary fibrosis and emphysema, **obstructive sleep apnoea and obesity hypoventilation**, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, **post-TB fibrosis and bronchiectasis**, living at high altitude. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**CTEPH** follows acute PE in about **2-4% of survivors**, but up to a quarter of CTEPH patients have no history of a clinical PE; risk factors include unprovoked or recurrent VTE, **antiphospholipid syndrome**, splenectomy, ventriculo-atrial shunts and infected pacing leads. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 5 (unclear or multifactorial)**: **chronic haemolytic anaemia (sickle cell, thalassaemia)**, myeloproliferative disease, **sarcoidosis**, CKD on dialysis, fibrosing mediastinitis (TB, histoplasmosis) and tumour thrombotic microangiopathy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - from vessel to RV failure`,points:[`**Three-pathway imbalance in PAH**: too little **nitric oxide** and **prostacyclin** and too much **endothelin-1**, causing vasoconstriction, smooth-muscle proliferation and in-situ thrombosis - each pathway is a drug target (PDE5i/sGC stimulator, prostanoids, ERA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**BMPR2-activin imbalance**: loss of anti-proliferative BMPR2 signalling with excess **activin/growth differentiation factor** signalling drives vascular remodelling; **sotatercept** is a ligand trap that rebalances this fourth pathway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Histology of PAH**: medial hypertrophy, intimal proliferation and fibrosis, adventitial thickening and the hallmark **plexiform lesion**, all in small pre-capillary arterioles. [Robbins 11e]`,`**Group 2**: raised left atrial pressure is transmitted backwards (isolated post-capillary PH); chronic exposure causes reactive pre-capillary remodelling (combined PH), which carries a worse prognosis - common in long-standing **mitral stenosis**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 3 / cor pulmonale**: **hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction**, acidosis, loss of capillary bed (emphysema, fibrosis) and hypoxaemic **erythrocytosis** raise PVR; hypoxaemia is the most reversible element, hence the survival benefit of oxygen. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Oedema in COPD** often reflects **hypercapnia-driven renal sodium and water retention** (RAAS activation, low renal blood flow) rather than RV failure alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RV response**: the thin-walled RV tolerates volume but not pressure; it first hypertrophies (adaptive), then dilates with **TR, septal flattening (D-shaped LV)**, reduced LV filling, falling cardiac output and **RV ischaemia** - RV failure is the cause of death in PAH. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Fixed cardiac output** explains **exertional syncope**: the RV cannot raise output across a fixed high resistance while peripheral vessels dilate on exercise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CTEPH**: unresolved organised thrombi obstruct proximal pulmonary arteries, and a secondary small-vessel arteriopathy develops in unobstructed areas - why some patients remain hypertensive after successful endarterectomy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Group 1 - PAH**: idiopathic, heritable, drug/toxin-induced, associated (CTD, HIV, portal hypertension, CHD, schistosomiasis), **long-term responders to calcium channel blockers**, PAH with venous/capillary involvement (PVOD/PCH), and persistent PH of the newborn. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 2 - PH due to left heart disease**: heart failure (HFpEF, HFrEF/HFmrEF), valvular heart disease, and congenital or acquired conditions causing post-capillary PH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 3 - PH due to lung disease and/or hypoxia**: obstructive, restrictive and mixed lung disease, hypoventilation syndromes, hypoxia without lung disease (high altitude), developmental lung disorders. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 4 - PH due to pulmonary artery obstruction**: **CTEPH** and other obstructions (PA sarcoma, arteritis such as **Takayasu**, congenital stenosis, **hydatid cyst**). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 5 - unclear and/or multifactorial**: haematological, systemic (sarcoidosis, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, neurofibromatosis), metabolic (Gaucher, glycogen storage), CKD on dialysis, complex CHD, fibrosing mediastinitis. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**WHO functional class I-IV** (adapted from NYHA) - class IV includes **syncope or signs of RV failure**, and functional class is the strongest single bedside prognostic marker. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Risk stratification**: at diagnosis a **3-strata model** (low under 5%, intermediate 5-20%, high over 20% one-year mortality); at follow-up a **4-strata model** (low, intermediate-low, intermediate-high, high) using **WHO-FC, 6-minute walk distance and BNP/NT-proBNP**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Severe PH in groups 2 and 3** is defined as **PVR over 5 WU** - these patients are referred to a PH centre. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Progressive exertional dyspnoea** and fatigue are the commonest presenting symptoms; the average delay from symptom onset to diagnosis of PAH is **over 2 years**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Exertional chest pain** (RV ischaemia or compression of the left main coronary artery by a dilated PA) and **exertional syncope or presyncope** signal advanced disease and a low, fixed cardiac output. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Late symptoms of RV failure**: ankle oedema, abdominal distension, right upper quadrant pain (hepatic congestion), anorexia and early satiety. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hoarseness (Ortner or cardiovocal syndrome)** from compression of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve by a dilated PA, and **haemoptysis** from bronchial collaterals or PA rupture, are rare but classic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**History for the cause**: orthopnoea or rheumatic fever (group 2), smoking, biomass exposure, old TB or snoring (group 3), previous DVT or PE (group 4), Raynaud, skin tightening, joint pain (SSc), liver disease, HIV risk, appetite suppressants or methamphetamine, family history. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Chronic cor pulmonale** presents as a COPD or ILD patient with worsening breathlessness, **cyanosis, morning headache (hypercapnia), drowsiness and oedema**, often triggered by an exacerbation. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Loud, often palpable P2** with a narrowly split or single S2, **left parasternal (RV) heave** and a pulmonary ejection click. [Harrison 22e]`,`**JVP**: prominent **a wave** (stiff hypertrophied RV) and, once TR develops, large **v (cv) waves** with a pulsatile liver. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pansystolic murmur of TR** at the lower left sternal edge that **increases with inspiration (Carvallo sign)**, and the early diastolic **Graham Steell murmur** of pulmonary regurgitation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Right-sided S3 and S4**, hepatomegaly, ascites and pitting oedema indicate RV failure; **hypotension and cool peripheries** indicate low output (high risk). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Central cyanosis** suggests a right-to-left shunt (PFO, Eisenmenger), lung disease or severe low output; **clubbing** suggests CHD, ILD or PVOD and is not a feature of idiopathic PAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Look for the cause**: sclerodactyly, telangiectasia and digital ulcers (SSc), spider naevi and splenomegaly (portal hypertension), fine crackles (ILD), hyperinflation and wheeze (COPD), obesity and a crowded oropharynx (OSA), mitral facies and an opening snap (MS). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Cor pulmonale signs** may be masked by hyperinflation - the RV heave is often felt in the **subxiphoid (epigastric)** area and heart sounds are distant. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**HFpEF with PH (group 2)**: older, hypertensive, obese, diabetic or in **AF**, with a **dilated LA** and diastolic dysfunction on echo; PAWP over 15 mmHg on catheter separates it from PAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Mitral stenosis**: loud S1, **opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble**; 'silent' MS with low output and severe PH is easily mislabelled PAH in Indian practice - echo settles it. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**CTEPH versus PAH**: both have pre-capillary haemodynamics, but CTEPH shows **mismatched segmental perfusion defects on V/Q** and is potentially curable by surgery. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Constrictive pericarditis and restrictive cardiomyopathy** also give a raised JVP, oedema and ascites, but with **Kussmaul sign, pericardial knock** and normal PA pressure or only mild PH. See the Pericardial disease topic. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Eisenmenger syndrome or unrepaired ASD**: cyanosis, clubbing or wide fixed split S2; a **bubble echo** finds the shunt. See the Adult congenital heart disease topic. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Pulmonary veno-occlusive disease**: PAH physiology with **very low DLCO, severe hypoxaemia, septal lines, centrilobular ground-glass and mediastinal nodes on CT**, and **pulmonary oedema on PAH drugs**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Anaemia, thyrotoxicosis and deconditioning** cause exertional breathlessness with a mildly raised estimated PASP from high flow - correct them before labelling PH. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**First line in the OPD**: **ECG, chest X-ray, NT-proBNP, spirometry, SpO2** (and ABG if low), Hb, TSH, creatinine and liver tests - they suggest PH and often its cause. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**ECG**: right axis deviation, **RVH (R/S over 1 in V1)**, RV strain (T inversion V1-V4), RBBB and **P pulmonale (over 2.5 mm in II)**; a normal ECG does not exclude PH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray**: **enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning**, RA and RV enlargement (filled retrosternal space on lateral film), plus clues - hyperinflation, fibrosis, Kerley lines, LA enlargement. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echo probability** rests on **peak TR velocity**: **2.8 m/s or less (or unmeasurable)** low, **2.9-3.4 m/s** intermediate, **over 3.4 m/s** high - upgraded by other PH signs from at least two categories (ventricles, PA, IVC/RA). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Supporting echo signs**: RV/LV basal diameter ratio over 1.0, **flattened septum (LV eccentricity index over 1.1)**, **TAPSE/sPAP under 0.55 mm/mmHg**, RVOT acceleration time under 105 ms or mid-systolic notching, PA diameter over 25 mm, IVC over 21 mm with poor collapse, RA area over 18 cm2. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**V/Q scan** is the screening test for CTEPH - a **normal perfusion scan excludes it** and it is more sensitive than CT pulmonary angiography; CTPA, pulmonary angiography or dual-energy CT then define operability. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Right heart catheterisation** confirms PH (mPAP, PAWP, PVR, cardiac output, SvO2) and is mandatory before PAH-specific therapy; do it at a PH centre. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Vasoreactivity testing** (inhaled nitric oxide or iloprost) is done only in **idiopathic, heritable or drug-induced PAH**: a positive response is a **fall in mPAP of 10 mmHg or more to 40 mmHg or less with unchanged or increased output** (about 10% of IPAH). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Cause work-up**: HRCT chest, full PFTs with **DLCO**, ABG, **polysomnography**, ANA, anti-centromere and anti-Scl-70, HIV, hepatitis B and C serology, abdominal ultrasound for portal hypertension, and genetic testing in familial or young IPAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Risk assessment tools**: **6-minute walk distance** (over 440 m low risk, under 165 m high risk), **NT-proBNP** (under 300 ng/L low, over 1100 ng/L high), CPET and cardiac MRI. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - general measures and groups 2, 3 and 4`,points:[`**General measures in PAH**: **supervised exercise training**, **influenza, pneumococcal and COVID-19 vaccination**, psychosocial support, **diuretics** for fluid retention, and **long-term oxygen if arterial PO2 is under 60 mmHg (8 kPa)**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Correct iron deficiency** (IV iron if oral fails); **avoid pregnancy** with effective contraception; **ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNI, SGLT2 inhibitors, beta-blockers and ivabradine are not recommended in PAH** unless another condition needs them. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Anticoagulation is not routine in PAH**; it is mandatory and **lifelong in CTEPH** (warfarin INR 2-3 or a DOAC; **warfarin if antiphospholipid syndrome**). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 2**: treat the left heart disease - **GDMT for heart failure, BP and AF control, and valve intervention** (successful **PTMC for MS** often lowers PA pressure markedly); **PAH drugs are not recommended** and can cause pulmonary oedema. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 3**: optimise the lung disease, **long-term oxygen when indicated, CPAP for OSA, NIV for hypoventilation**, pulmonary rehabilitation; **inhaled treprostinil may be considered in PH with ILD** (INCREASE trial); **riociguat is harmful in PH with idiopathic interstitial pneumonia** and **ambrisentan in IPF**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Severe PH in group 2 or 3 (PVR over 5 WU)** or a pre-capillary component out of proportion should be referred to a PH centre for individual decisions. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Group 4 (CTEPH)**: refer every case to a CTEPH team; **pulmonary endarterectomy** is the treatment of choice for operable disease and is potentially curative; **balloon pulmonary angioplasty** for inoperable or residual disease; **riociguat 1-2.5 mg three times daily** for inoperable or persistent PH (CHEST-1). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Chronic cor pulmonale**: **long-term oxygen at least 15 hours a day** when PaO2 is **55 mmHg or less** (SaO2 88% or less), or 55-60 mmHg with cor pulmonale, oedema or haematocrit over 55% - the only measure proven to improve survival. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Diuretics in cor pulmonale**: low-dose furosemide (20-40 mg orally) for oedema, cautiously - over-diuresis drops RV preload and causes **metabolic alkalosis that depresses ventilation**; add spironolactone if hypokalaemic. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Group 1 PAH - drug therapy at the PH centre`,points:[`**Vasoreactive IPAH/HPAH/drug-induced PAH**: **high-dose CCB** - **nifedipine 120-240 mg/day, diltiazem 240-720 mg/day or amlodipine up to 20 mg/day**; reassess at 3-6 months, and continue only if WHO-FC I-II with near-normal haemodynamics. **Never verapamil** (negative inotrope). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Low or intermediate risk without cardiopulmonary comorbidity**: **initial dual oral combination** of an **ERA plus PDE5 inhibitor** (ambrisentan plus tadalafil - AMBITION; or macitentan plus tadalafil). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**High risk at diagnosis**: **initial triple therapy including an IV or SC prostacyclin analogue** (epoprostenol or treprostinil) with an ERA and PDE5i, and early **lung transplant referral**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**PAH with cardiopulmonary comorbidities** (older, obese, diabetic, hypertensive, CAD, low DLCO): start **monotherapy with a PDE5i or ERA** and escalate individually. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Follow-up escalation (4-strata)**: intermediate-low risk on ERA plus PDE5i - **add selexipag** or **switch PDE5i to riociguat**; intermediate-high or high risk - **add IV or SC prostacyclin** and refer for transplant. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Sotatercept** (activin signalling inhibitor) **0.3 mg/kg SC, then 0.7 mg/kg every 3 weeks**, added to background therapy, improved exercise capacity and reduced clinical worsening (STELLAR 2023) and reduced death, transplant or hospitalisation in high-risk patients (ZENITH 2025). [WSPH 2024]`,`**Drug interactions to remember**: **PDE5i with nitrates or riociguat is contraindicated** (hypotension); **bosentan** induces CYP3A4 and lowers hormonal contraceptive and sildenafil levels; ERAs and riociguat are **teratogenic**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**In India**, generic **sildenafil, tadalafil, bosentan and ambrisentan** are affordable and widely used; inhaled iloprost is available, but continuous IV epoprostenol, selexipag and sotatercept are limited to a few centres. [Indian practice]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Admit** for decompensated RV failure (rising creatinine, hypotension, syncope, worsening hypoxaemia), new arrhythmia, haemoptysis or acute cor pulmonale with hypercapnic acidosis. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Find and treat the trigger**: infection, arrhythmia (AF or flutter - **restore sinus rhythm** as the atrial kick matters), drug interruption, anaemia, PE, non-adherence to diuretics or salt. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Volume**: most decompensated RVs are **overloaded** - IV loop diuretics to relieve congestion; avoid reflex fluid boluses, which dilate the RV further and squeeze the LV. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Haemodynamic support**: **noradrenaline** to keep systemic pressure (and RV coronary perfusion) above PA pressure, **dobutamine** for inotropy, and inhaled nitric oxide or IV prostacyclin as pulmonary vasodilators in PAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Avoid intubation if possible** - induction drops systemic pressure and positive pressure raises RV afterload, often causing cardiac arrest; ECMO is a bridge to recovery or transplant in selected patients. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Acute exacerbation with cor pulmonale**: controlled oxygen to **SpO2 88-92%**, bronchodilators, prednisolone 40 mg for 5 days, antibiotics if purulent sputum, and **NIV if pH under 7.35 with PaCO2 over 45 mmHg**. [GOLD 2025]`,`**Massive PE causing acute cor pulmonale**: systemic thrombolysis if hypotensive, avoiding large fluid loads. See the Pulmonary embolism topic. [ESC 2019 PE]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy in PAH is mWHO class IV** - advise against it; if it occurs, discuss termination; if continued, manage at a PH centre with planned delivery, as death risk peaks in the **first weeks after delivery**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Contraception**: progestogen implant, **levonorgestrel IUD** or sterilisation; avoid oestrogen; remember that **bosentan reduces hormonal contraceptive efficacy**, so use two methods; monthly pregnancy tests on ERA or riociguat. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Elderly**: most new PH over 65 is **group 2 (HFpEF)**; true PAH in the elderly usually has comorbidities and is started on monotherapy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Systemic sclerosis**: **annual screening** with echo, DLCO and NT-proBNP (DETECT algorithm) because SSc-PAH has a poorer prognosis than IPAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Surgery and anaesthesia** in PH carry high risk; plan at a centre with PH expertise, avoid hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis and hypotension, and prefer regional anaesthesia with careful titration. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**High altitude** (Ladakh, Sikkim, Himalayan postings): hypoxic PH and chronic mountain sickness improve with **descent to low altitude**; patients with known PH should use oxygen on flights if resting PO2 is under 60 mmHg. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Children**: persistent PH of the newborn and CHD-PAH dominate; refer early, as shunt closure is possible only before pulmonary vascular disease is fixed. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**RV failure** is the usual cause of death; others are **sudden death**, haemoptysis, PA dissection and **left main compression** by a dilated PA. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Atrial flutter and AF** are poorly tolerated and precipitate decompensation - aim for sinus rhythm. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Untreated IPAH** had a median survival of about **2.8 years** in the NIH registry; modern combination therapy has substantially improved survival, with low-risk patients having a one-year mortality under 5%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Refer to a PH centre** any patient with intermediate or high echo probability **without** obvious left heart or lung disease, any suspected PAH or CTEPH, and severe PH in group 2 or 3. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Fast-track (immediate) referral**: rapidly progressive symptoms, **syncope, signs of RV failure, low cardiac output** or arrhythmia. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**After acute PE**, review at **3-6 months**; persistent dyspnoea or functional limitation needs **echo and V/Q scan** to detect CTEPH or chronic thromboembolic pulmonary disease. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**PAH follow-up every 3-6 months**: WHO-FC, 6-minute walk, NT-proBNP and echo, recalculating the 4-strata risk; goal is to reach and keep **low-risk status**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Drug monitoring**: **monthly LFT on bosentan**, Hb on ERAs, BP on riociguat, **Hb and platelets before each early sotatercept dose**, pregnancy tests monthly on teratogenic drugs. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Lung transplant referral** when patients remain at intermediate-high or high risk despite maximal therapy. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Primary care role**: early suspicion from symptoms plus ECG, X-ray and NT-proBNP, vaccination, oxygen, diuretic titration and recognising decompensation early. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Tobacco cessation and clean cooking fuel** (Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana LPG connections) reduce COPD, the commonest cause of cor pulmonale in Indian women and men. [NP-NCD]`,`**NP-NCD** screening and the COPD services at **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** identify chronic lung disease early; spirometry and oxygen access are expanding at district level. [NP-NCD]`,`**TB control and post-TB care** under **NTEP** reduce fibro-cavitary sequelae and bronchiectasis that later cause cor pulmonale. [NTEP]`,`**Rheumatic fever prophylaxis and timely PTMC** prevent group 2 PH from mitral stenosis. [ICMR STW]`,`**Adequate anticoagulation and follow-up after PE** prevent and detect CTEPH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Avoid anorexigens and methamphetamine**; offer genetic counselling and screening to relatives of patients with heritable PAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Weight reduction and CPAP** for obesity hypoventilation and OSA prevent group 3 PH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2025)`,points:[`**ESC/ERS 2022**: PH redefined as **mPAP over 20 mmHg** and pre-capillary PH as **PVR over 2 WU** (was 25 mmHg and 3 WU); **exercise PH** reintroduced as mPAP/CO slope over 3 mmHg/L/min. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022** added **TAPSE/sPAP ratio** to echo probability, introduced the **4-strata follow-up risk model**, and recommended **initial dual oral therapy** for most PAH and **initial monotherapy for PAH with cardiopulmonary comorbidities**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022** upgraded **supervised exercise training to class I**, set **PVR over 5 WU** as severe PH in groups 2 and 3, and introduced the term **chronic thromboembolic pulmonary disease (CTEPD) with or without PH**. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**7th World Symposium on PH (2024)** kept the 2022 haemodynamic definitions and placed **sotatercept** as add-on therapy for patients not at low risk on dual or triple therapy. [WSPH 2024]`,`**Sotatercept trials**: **STELLAR (2023)** in background-treated PAH, **ZENITH (2025)** in high-risk PAH and **HYPERION (2025)** in recently diagnosed intermediate/high-risk PAH all showed fewer clinical worsening events. [NEJM 2023-2025]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy guideline** keeps **PAH in mWHO class IV** and requires care by a Pregnancy Heart Team at a PH centre. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Current definition** is the commonest one-mark question: **mPAP over 20, PAWP 15 or less, PVR over 2 WU** - writing 25 mmHg loses marks. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Why oxygen in cor pulmonale but not in PAH generally?** - it reverses **hypoxic vasoconstriction** and erythrocytosis in lung disease; in PAH it is given only for PO2 under 60 mmHg. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why CCBs only for responders?** - in non-responders they cause hypotension and RV failure without lowering PVR; vasoreactivity testing is limited to IPAH, HPAH and drug-induced PAH. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Why V/Q and not CTPA to screen for CTEPH?** - V/Q is more sensitive for chronic segmental defects; a normal scan rules CTEPH out. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Common mistake**: giving sildenafil or bosentan to group 2 PH (HFpEF, MS) - it can precipitate pulmonary oedema; treat the left heart instead. [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`,`**Eponyms**: **Graham Steell** (PR murmur), **Carvallo** (TR louder on inspiration), **Ortner** (hoarseness), **Heath-Edwards** (vascular grading). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mnemonic for PAH targets** - 'NO, PG, ET and activin': PDE5i and riociguat (NO), prostanoids and selexipag (PG), ERAs (ET) and sotatercept (activin). [ESC/ERS 2022 PH]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Haemodynamic definitions of pulmonary hypertension (ESC/ERS 2022)`,columns:[`Type`,`mPAP (mmHg)`,`PAWP (mmHg)`,`PVR (Wood units)`,`Typical groups`],rows:[[`Pulmonary hypertension`,`Over 20`,`Any`,`Any`,`All`],[`Pre-capillary PH`,`Over 20`,`15 or less`,`Over 2`,`1, 3, 4, 5`],[`Isolated post-capillary PH`,`Over 20`,`Over 15`,`2 or less`,`2, 5`],[`Combined pre- and post-capillary PH`,`Over 20`,`Over 15`,`Over 2`,`2, 5`],[`Exercise PH`,`mPAP/CO slope over 3 mmHg/L/min`,`-`,`-`,`Early disease (expert centres)`],[`Severe PH in groups 2 and 3`,`Over 20`,`Any`,`Over 5`,`2, 3 - refer to PH centre`]]},{heading:`Clinical classification (WHO groups) and the first treatment`,columns:[`Group`,`Main causes`,`Clue`,`Core treatment`],rows:[[`1 - PAH`,`Idiopathic, heritable (BMPR2), drugs, SSc, HIV, portal HT, CHD`,`Young woman, clear lungs, low DLCO`,`PAH-specific drugs at PH centre; CCB only if vasoreactive`],[`2 - Left heart disease`,`HFpEF, HFrEF, mitral and aortic valve disease`,`Older, AF, LA dilated, PAWP over 15`,`Treat the heart: GDMT, valve repair, PTMC; no PAH drugs`],[`3 - Lung disease / hypoxia`,`COPD, ILD, OSA, obesity hypoventilation, post-TB, altitude`,`Abnormal PFT/HRCT, hypoxaemia`,`Oxygen, CPAP/NIV, treat lung; inhaled treprostinil in PH-ILD`],[`4 - PA obstruction`,`CTEPH, sarcoma, arteritis, hydatid`,`Previous VTE, mismatched V/Q`,`Lifelong anticoagulation; PEA, BPA, riociguat`],[`5 - Multifactorial`,`Sickle cell, thalassaemia, MPN, sarcoidosis, dialysis`,`Systemic disease`,`Treat the cause; individual PH centre decision`]]},{heading:`Echocardiographic probability of PH (ESC/ERS 2022)`,columns:[`Peak TR velocity`,`Other echo PH signs`,`Probability`,`Next step`],rows:[[`2.8 m/s or less or unmeasurable`,`Absent`,`Low`,`Look for another cause; follow up if risk factors`],[`2.8 m/s or less or unmeasurable`,`Present`,`Intermediate`,`Assess left heart and lung disease; refer if none`],[`2.9-3.4 m/s`,`Absent`,`Intermediate`,`Assess left heart and lung disease; refer if none`],[`2.9-3.4 m/s`,`Present`,`High`,`Work-up for cause; RHC at PH centre`],[`Over 3.4 m/s`,`Not required`,`High`,`Work-up for cause; RHC at PH centre`]]},{heading:`Four-strata follow-up risk model in PAH (ESC/ERS 2022)`,columns:[`Variable`,`Low`,`Intermediate-low`,`Intermediate-high`,`High`],rows:[[`WHO functional class`,`I or II`,`-`,`III`,`IV`],[`6-minute walk distance (m)`,`Over 440`,`320-440`,`165-319`,`Under 165`],[`NT-proBNP (ng/L)`,`Under 300`,`300-649`,`650-1100`,`Over 1100`],[`BNP (ng/L)`,`Under 50`,`50-199`,`200-800`,`Over 800`],[`Action`,`Continue therapy`,`Add selexipag or switch to riociguat`,`Add parenteral prostacyclin; transplant referral`,`Parenteral prostacyclin; transplant referral`]]},{heading:`Drugs for pulmonary arterial hypertension`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Sildenafil (PDE5i)`,`20 mg orally three times daily`,`Long-term`,`Headache, flushing, epistaxis, visual change; never with nitrates or riociguat`],[`Tadalafil (PDE5i)`,`40 mg orally once daily`,`Long-term; 20 mg in renal or hepatic impairment`,`As sildenafil; back pain, myalgia`],[`Bosentan (ERA)`,`62.5 mg orally twice daily for 4 weeks, then 125 mg twice daily`,`Long-term; monthly LFT`,`Hepatotoxicity, anaemia, oedema, teratogenic; lowers contraceptive levels`],[`Ambrisentan (ERA)`,`5 mg orally once daily, up to 10 mg`,`Long-term`,`Peripheral oedema, anaemia, teratogenic; contraindicated in IPF`],[`Macitentan (ERA)`,`10 mg orally once daily`,`Long-term`,`Anaemia, headache, nasopharyngitis, teratogenic`],[`Riociguat (sGC stimulator)`,`1 mg orally three times daily, up by 0.5 mg every 2 weeks to 2.5 mg three times daily`,`Long-term; also inoperable CTEPH`,`Hypotension, dyspepsia; never with PDE5i or nitrates; harmful in PH-IIP; teratogenic`],[`Selexipag (IP receptor agonist)`,`200 mcg orally twice daily, up by 200 mcg weekly to max 1600 mcg twice daily`,`Long-term`,`Headache, diarrhoea, jaw pain, flushing`],[`Epoprostenol (IV prostacyclin)`,`Continuous IV infusion from 2 ng/kg/min, titrated`,`Long-term via central line`,`Line sepsis; rebound PH crisis if infusion stops (half-life minutes)`],[`Iloprost (inhaled prostanoid)`,`2.5-5 mcg by nebuliser 6-9 times daily`,`Long-term`,`Cough, flushing, jaw pain`],[`Sotatercept (activin signalling inhibitor)`,`0.3 mg/kg SC, then 0.7 mg/kg SC every 3 weeks`,`Add-on to background therapy`,`Raised Hb, thrombocytopenia, telangiectasia, bleeding, epistaxis; teratogenic`],[`Nifedipine (vasoreactive only)`,`120-240 mg/day orally in divided doses (SR)`,`Reassess at 3-6 months`,`Hypotension, oedema; only after positive vasoreactivity test; never verapamil`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs in suspected PH`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding suggesting PH or its cause`,`Cut-off / meaning`],rows:[[`ECG`,`Right axis, RVH, RV strain, P pulmonale`,`R/S over 1 in V1; P over 2.5 mm in II; normal ECG does not exclude`],[`NT-proBNP`,`RV wall stress`,`Under 300 ng/L low risk; over 1100 high risk`],[`Echo`,`Peak TR velocity, RV size, TAPSE/sPAP`,`TRV over 3.4 m/s high probability; TAPSE/sPAP under 0.55`],[`V/Q scan`,`Mismatched segmental perfusion defects`,`Normal scan excludes CTEPH`],[`PFT with DLCO / HRCT`,`Lung disease or PVOD`,`DLCO under 45% suggests PVOD, SSc or lung disease`],[`Right heart catheter`,`mPAP, PAWP, PVR, CO`,`mPAP over 20, PAWP 15 or less, PVR over 2 WU = pre-capillary`],[`Vasoreactivity test`,`Fall in mPAP with inhaled NO`,`Fall 10 mmHg or more to 40 or less with stable CO = responder`],[`6-minute walk`,`Functional capacity`,`Over 440 m low risk; under 165 m high risk`]]}],redFlags:[`**Exertional syncope or presyncope** in a patient with suspected PH.`,`**Signs of RV failure** - raised JVP, hepatomegaly, ascites, oedema - with hypotension or cool peripheries.`,`**Rapidly progressive breathlessness** (WHO class III-IV) or new resting hypoxaemia.`,`**New atrial flutter or AF** in known PH.`,`**Haemoptysis** in PAH or Eisenmenger syndrome.`,`**Pregnancy** in a woman with PAH or CTEPH.`,`**COPD exacerbation with drowsiness, pH under 7.35 or rising PaCO2** in cor pulmonale - needs NIV.`],pearls:[`**PH = mPAP over 20; pre-capillary = PAWP 15 or less and PVR over 2 WU** (ESC/ERS 2022).`,`**Most PH is group 2 or 3** - treat the heart or lung; PAH drugs are for group 1 and CTEPH.`,`**Echo screens, catheter confirms** - never start PAH drugs without right heart catheterisation.`,`**V/Q scan screens for CTEPH; a normal scan excludes it.**`,`**CTEPH is the curable PH** - pulmonary endarterectomy.`,`**CCB only for the 10% who are vasoreactive; never verapamil.**`,`**PDE5i + nitrate or PDE5i + riociguat = contraindicated.**`,`**Bosentan: monthly LFT and two methods of contraception.**`,`**Oxygen 15 h/day is the survival treatment of cor pulmonale.**`,`**Clubbing in 'IPAH' means look again** - CHD, ILD or PVOD.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-q1`,stem:`According to the ESC/ERS 2022 guideline, which set of right heart catheter values defines pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension?`,options:[`mPAP 25 mmHg or more, PAWP 15 mmHg or less, PVR over 3 Wood units`,`mPAP over 20 mmHg, PAWP 15 mmHg or less, PVR over 2 Wood units`,`mPAP over 20 mmHg, PAWP over 15 mmHg, PVR 2 Wood units or less`,`Systolic PA pressure over 35 mmHg on echo with TR velocity over 2.8 m/s`],answer:1,explanation:`ESC/ERS 2022 lowered the definition to mPAP over 20 mmHg and defined pre-capillary PH as PAWP 15 or less with PVR over 2 Wood units. The 25 mmHg and 3 Wood unit thresholds belong to the old 2015 definition. PAWP over 15 with PVR 2 or less is isolated post-capillary PH (left heart disease). Echo estimates only give a probability; PH is defined by catheter measurements.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-q2`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman with idiopathic PAH, WHO class II, has a fall in mPAP from 52 to 34 mmHg with unchanged cardiac output during inhaled nitric oxide testing. The most appropriate initial therapy is:`,options:[`Verapamil SR 240 mg daily`,`Initial triple therapy with IV epoprostenol`,`Warfarin with oxygen only`,`High-dose nifedipine or diltiazem`],answer:3,explanation:`A fall in mPAP of 10 mmHg or more to 40 mmHg or less with unchanged or increased output is a positive vasoreactivity response, seen in about 10% of IPAH; these patients are treated with high-dose nifedipine, diltiazem or amlodipine and reassessed at 3-6 months. Verapamil is avoided because of its negative inotropy. Parenteral triple therapy is for high-risk patients, and warfarin with oxygen is not PAH treatment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-q3`,stem:`A 50-year-old man has progressive breathlessness 9 months after an acute pulmonary embolism treated with 3 months of anticoagulation. Echo shows a high probability of PH. The best screening test for chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension is:`,options:[`Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scan`,`Repeat D-dimer`,`Lower limb venous Doppler`,`High-resolution CT chest without contrast`],answer:0,explanation:`The V/Q scan is the recommended screening test for CTEPH: it is more sensitive than CT pulmonary angiography for chronic segmental defects, and a normal perfusion scan effectively excludes the diagnosis. D-dimer is neither sensitive nor specific for chronic organised thrombus, leg Doppler only finds residual DVT, and non-contrast HRCT assesses parenchyma, not the pulmonary arteries.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-q4`,stem:`A 64-year-old woman with COPD from biomass smoke exposure has ankle oedema, a raised JVP and a resting PaO2 of 52 mmHg on room air while stable. Which intervention has been shown to improve her survival?`,options:[`Sildenafil 20 mg three times daily`,`Digoxin 0.25 mg daily`,`Long-term oxygen therapy for at least 15 hours a day`,`High-dose furosemide to achieve a dry weight`],answer:2,explanation:`Long-term oxygen for at least 15 hours a day in COPD with PaO2 55 mmHg or less (or 55-60 with cor pulmonale) improves survival (NOTT and MRC trials) by relieving hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction. Sildenafil is not recommended in routine group 3 PH. Digoxin has no survival benefit in cor pulmonale and is used only for AF rate control. Aggressive diuresis reduces RV preload and causes metabolic alkalosis that depresses ventilation.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-q5`,stem:`Sotatercept, added to background therapy in PAH, acts by:`,options:[`Stimulating soluble guanylate cyclase independently of nitric oxide`,`Trapping activin-class ligands to rebalance BMPR2 signalling`,`Blocking endothelin-A receptors on smooth muscle`,`Activating the prostacyclin IP receptor`],answer:1,explanation:`Sotatercept is an activin receptor type IIA-Fc fusion protein that traps activins and growth differentiation factors, restoring the balance between pro-proliferative activin signalling and anti-proliferative BMPR2 signalling - a fourth pathway beyond NO, prostacyclin and endothelin. sGC stimulation describes riociguat, endothelin receptor blockade describes ambrisentan and other ERAs, and IP receptor activation describes selexipag. Watch for raised Hb, thrombocytopenia and bleeding.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define pulmonary hypertension as per the ESC/ERS 2022 guideline. Give its clinical classification and discuss the diagnostic approach and management of pulmonary arterial hypertension.`,openingLines:[`Pulmonary hypertension is a mean pulmonary artery pressure over 20 mmHg at rest on right heart catheterisation; pre-capillary PH adds PAWP 15 mmHg or less and PVR over 2 Wood units (ESC/ERS 2022).`,`It is classified into five clinical groups; left heart and lung disease are commonest, while group 1 PAH is rare but has specific vasodilator-antiproliferative therapy.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and haemodynamic types`,points:[`PH mPAP over 20; pre-capillary PAWP 15 or less, PVR over 2; isolated post-capillary PAWP over 15, PVR 2 or less; combined both raised; exercise PH slope over 3 mmHg/L/min.`]},{heading:`Clinical classification`,points:[`Group 1 PAH (idiopathic, heritable, drugs, CTD, HIV, portal, CHD, schistosomiasis, CCB responders, PVOD, PPHN).`,`Group 2 left heart disease; group 3 lung disease/hypoxia; group 4 CTEPH and other PA obstruction; group 5 multifactorial.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Low NO and prostacyclin, high endothelin-1, BMPR2-activin imbalance; plexiform lesions; RV hypertrophy then dilatation and failure.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Exertional dyspnoea, fatigue, chest pain, exertional syncope, oedema; loud P2, RV heave, TR (Carvallo), Graham Steell, raised JVP with a and v waves.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic approach`,points:[`Step 1 (primary care): ECG, chest X-ray, NT-proBNP, spirometry, SpO2.`,`Step 2: echo probability by TR velocity (2.8 or less low, 2.9-3.4 intermediate, over 3.4 high) and supporting signs; look for left heart and lung disease.`,`Step 3 (PH centre): V/Q scan for CTEPH, HRCT, DLCO, serology, RHC, vasoreactivity test in IPAH.`,`Risk stratify: WHO-FC, 6MWD, NT-proBNP (3-strata at diagnosis, 4-strata at follow-up).`]},{heading:`Management of PAH`,points:[`General: exercise training, vaccination, diuretics, oxygen if PO2 under 60 mmHg, iron, contraception, avoid pregnancy.`,`Vasoreactive: high-dose nifedipine, diltiazem or amlodipine.`,`Non-vasoreactive low/intermediate risk: initial ERA plus PDE5i; high risk: triple therapy with IV/SC prostacyclin; comorbid: monotherapy.`,`Escalate by 4-strata risk: add selexipag or switch to riociguat; parenteral prostacyclin; sotatercept add-on (WSPH 2024); lung transplant.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagnostic flow chart: suspicion - first-line tests - echo probability - left heart/lung disease - PH centre with V/Q and RHC`,`Table of the five clinical groups with examples`,`Treatment algorithm for PAH by risk at diagnosis`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and haemodynamic types`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical classification`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`Diagnostic approach and risk stratification`,marks:2.5},{part:`Management of PAH`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`mPAP over 20`,`PVR over 2 WU`,`WHO groups`,`echo probability`,`right heart catheter`,`ERA`,`PDE5 inhibitor`,`sotatercept`]},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-hypertension-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on chronic cor pulmonale.`,openingLines:[`Cor pulmonale is hypertrophy and/or dilatation of the right ventricle due to a primary disorder of the lungs, chest wall, ventilatory control or pulmonary vasculature, excluding left heart and congenital heart disease.`,`COPD is the commonest cause; hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is the key reversible mechanism, so long-term oxygen improves survival.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`COPD, ILD, post-TB fibrosis and bronchiectasis, OSA and obesity hypoventilation, kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, high altitude, CTEPH.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Hypoxic vasoconstriction, acidosis, capillary loss and erythrocytosis raise PVR; RV hypertrophy then failure; hypercapnia causes salt and water retention.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and investigations`,points:[`Cyanosis, oedema, raised JVP, epigastric RV heave, loud P2, TR murmur, hepatomegaly; drowsiness and headache with hypercapnia.`,`ECG RAD, P pulmonale, RVH; X-ray big PAs; ABG; echo TR velocity and RV size; spirometry; Hb.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Treat lung disease, stop tobacco and biomass exposure, vaccinate, pulmonary rehabilitation.`,`LTOT at least 15 h/day if PaO2 55 or less, or 55-60 with cor pulmonale or Hct over 55%.`,`Cautious diuretics; CPAP/NIV for OSA or hypoventilation; exacerbation: controlled O2 88-92%, NIV if pH under 7.35.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: lung disease - hypoxia - pulmonary vasoconstriction - raised PVR - RVH - RV failure, with oxygen breaking the cycle`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:1},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`cor pulmonale`,`COPD`,`hypoxic vasoconstriction`,`LTOT`,`P pulmonale`,`NIV`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pulmonary hypertension`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Pulmonary hypertension`,`2022 ESC/ERS Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of pulmonary hypertension (Eur Heart J 2022; Eur Respir J 2023)`,`Proceedings of the 7th World Symposium on Pulmonary Hypertension, Barcelona 2024 (Eur Respir J 2024)`,`Hoeper MM et al. Phase 3 trial of sotatercept for PAH (STELLAR), N Engl J Med 2023`,`Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) Report 2025`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy`]}),c.push({id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy`,title:`Heart disease in pregnancy`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Heart disease in pregnancy** complicates **1-4% of pregnancies** and is a leading indirect cause of maternal death; risk is graded by the **modified WHO (mWHO) classes I-IV** (ESC 2025) and **CARPREG II**, the dangerous periods are the **late second trimester, labour and the first hours after delivery**, and in India **rheumatic mitral stenosis** is the commonest lesion - managed with beta-blockers, diuretics and, if refractory, **PTMC**.`,keywords:[`cardiac disease in pregnancy`,`mWHO classification`,`CARPREG II`,`ZAHARA`,`pregnancy heart team`,`mitral stenosis in pregnancy`,`PTMC`,`PBMV`,`mechanical valve pregnancy`,`warfarin embryopathy`,`LMWH anti-Xa`,`peripartum cardiomyopathy`,`PPCM`,`bromocriptine`,`PMSMA`,`high-risk pregnancy`,`labetalol`,`metoprolol`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Cardiovascular disease complicates 1-4% of pregnancies** and is the leading cause of **indirect maternal death**; in high-income countries it is now the commonest single cause of maternal death overall. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`In high-income countries the burden has shifted to **congenital heart disease, cardiomyopathy and aortic disease** in older mothers; in India **rheumatic heart disease** remains the commonest cardiac lesion in pregnancy, mitral stenosis above all. [Braunwald 12e]`,`India's **maternal mortality ratio is about 93-97 per 100,000 live births** (SRS 2018-21); heart disease is a major indirect cause, especially undiagnosed MS presenting with pulmonary oedema in the third trimester or postpartum. [SRS RGI 2021]`,`Many Indian women learn of their **RHD for the first time in pregnancy**, when the volume load unmasks a previously silent mitral stenosis. [Indian hospital series]`,`**Hypertensive disorders** affect about 5-10% of pregnancies and add long-term cardiovascular risk; see the Hypertension topic for drug choice. [ESC 2024 HT]`,`**Peripartum cardiomyopathy** affects roughly 1 in 1000-4000 deliveries worldwide, with higher rates in Africa and parts of Asia. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`The **maternal cardiac event rate** ranges from about **2.5-5% in mWHO class I to 40-100% in class IV**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Physiology - why pregnancy unmasks heart disease`,points:[`**Plasma volume rises 40-50%** and red cell mass about 20-30%, peaking around **32 weeks** - the cause of dilutional anaemia and of decompensation in stenotic lesions. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac output rises 30-50%** (stroke volume first, then **heart rate up 10-20 bpm**), reaching its peak in the **late second trimester**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Systemic vascular resistance falls** (progesterone, placental low-resistance bed), so BP dips in mid-pregnancy - which is why **regurgitant lesions are well tolerated** and **stenotic lesions are not**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Labour**: each contraction autotransfuses **300-500 mL** into the circulation, and cardiac output rises by up to 50% in the second stage, with pain and Valsalva adding sympathetic load. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Immediately after delivery** relief of IVC compression and uterine autotransfusion raise **cardiac output by 60-80%** - the single most dangerous moment for MS and PAH. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Hypercoagulability**: fibrinogen and factors VII, VIII and X rise and protein S falls - a fivefold rise in VTE risk and the reason mechanical valves thrombose in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Supine hypotension**: the gravid uterus compresses the IVC after 20 weeks, so examine and resuscitate in **left lateral tilt**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`Haemodynamics return largely to baseline within **2 weeks** of delivery, but full remodelling takes up to **6 months**. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Risk classification - mWHO and CARPREG II`,points:[`**mWHO class I** (event risk 2.5-5%): small or mild PS, PDA or MVP, successfully repaired simple shunts, isolated ectopic beats - local care, 1-2 cardiology visits. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**mWHO class II** (5.7-10.5%): unoperated ASD or VSD, repaired Tetralogy, most supraventricular arrhythmias, Turner syndrome without aortic dilatation - review each trimester. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**mWHO class II-III** (10-19%): mild LV impairment (EF over 45%), **HCM**, native or tissue valve disease not in class I or IV (mild MS, moderate AS), Marfan without dilatation, repaired coarctation - referral hospital, review every 2 months. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**mWHO class III** (19-27%): **moderate LV impairment (EF 30-45%)**, previous PPCM with recovered LV, **mechanical valve**, **moderate MS**, severe asymptomatic AS, Fontan, unrepaired cyanotic CHD, moderate aortic dilatation, VT - expert centre, monthly or 2-monthly review. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**mWHO class IV - pregnancy contraindicated** (40-100%): **PAH**, **EF under 30% or NYHA III-IV**, previous PPCM with residual LV impairment, **severe MS**, severe symptomatic AS, severe aortic dilatation (Marfan over 45 mm, bicuspid over 50 mm), vascular Ehlers-Danlos, severe coarctation, complicated Fontan. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**CARPREG II** gives points for **prior cardiac event or arrhythmia (3), NYHA III-IV or cyanosis (3), mechanical valve (3)**, ventricular dysfunction (2), high-risk left-sided valve disease or LVOT obstruction (2), PH (2), CAD (2), high-risk aortopathy (2), no prior cardiac intervention (1) and **late first antenatal visit after 20 weeks (1)**. [CARPREG II, JACC 2018]`,`**CARPREG II risk**: 0-1 points about 5%, 2 points 10%, 3 points 15%, 4 points 22%, **over 4 points 41%** risk of a maternal cardiac event. [CARPREG II, JACC 2018]`,`ESC recommends using **mWHO as the primary framework**, refined by CARPREG II or ZAHARA, plus **NT-proBNP, functional class and exercise capacity** before conception. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Normal pregnancy mimics heart disease**: breathlessness on exertion, fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance, ankle oedema and palpitations are common and benign. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Symptoms that are not physiological**: **orthopnoea or PND, breathlessness at rest, haemoptysis, exertional syncope, chest pain, progressive NYHA class**, and sustained palpitations. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**MS** classically decompensates at **20-30 weeks or in the peripartum period** with sudden pulmonary oedema, often triggered by AF, anaemia, infection or tocolytics. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**PPCM** presents in the last month of pregnancy or the months after delivery with breathlessness, oedema, cough and fatigue - easily dismissed as normal postpartum tiredness. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`Ask about **past rheumatic fever, penicillin prophylaxis, prior cardiac surgery or PTMC, valve type and anticoagulant dose**, previous pregnancy outcomes and pre-eclampsia. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Late booking** (first visit after 20 weeks) is itself a CARPREG II risk factor and is common in rural India. [CARPREG II, JACC 2018]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Normal findings**: bounding pulse, wide pulse pressure, **soft ejection systolic flow murmur** (pulmonary area), **physiological S3**, loud S1, slightly displaced apex, **mammary souffle** and venous hum. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Always abnormal**: a **diastolic murmur**, a loud pansystolic murmur, a **loud P2** with RV heave, cyanosis, clubbing, a raised JVP with basal crackles, or an S4. [Harrison 22e]`,`**MS in pregnancy**: loud S1, **opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble** at the apex (best heard in left lateral position with the bell); tachycardia shortens the rumble and can hide it. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Mechanical valve**: crisp metallic closing clicks - a **muffled click** with breathlessness or embolism means **valve thrombosis** until proved otherwise. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Pre-eclampsia and PPCM** both cause oedema and crackles; check BP, urine protein and fundi at every visit. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`Measure **SpO2** at rest - saturation under 85% in cyanotic CHD predicts a live-birth rate of only about 12%. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis - breathlessness and oedema in pregnancy`,points:[`**Physiological dyspnoea of pregnancy**: gradual, no orthopnoea, normal SpO2, normal chest examination and no diastolic murmur. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia** (very common in India, NFHS-5 about 52% of pregnant women): pallor, flow murmur, high-output symptoms; correct it because it worsens MS and HF. [NFHS-5]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, hypoxaemia, leg swelling - compression ultrasound first, then CTPA or V/Q. See the Pulmonary embolism topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Pre-eclampsia pulmonary oedema**: hypertension, proteinuria, headache, raised liver enzymes, low platelets; LV often normal. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**PPCM versus pre-existing DCM**: PPCM appears in late pregnancy or after delivery with no prior cardiac history; DCM decompensating in the second trimester suggests pre-existing disease. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Tocolytic pulmonary oedema** (beta-agonists such as terbutaline, often with steroids and fluids) and **amniotic fluid embolism** (collapse at delivery with DIC) are obstetric look-alikes. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Asthma and pneumonia** cause dyspnoea with wheeze or fever; **SCAD** is the commonest cause of myocardial infarction in pregnancy and the postpartum period. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Echocardiography** is the key test - safe, repeatable, and indicated in any pregnant woman with unexplained cardiovascular symptoms or new murmur; record valve areas, gradients, LVEF and PA pressure. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**ECG**: normal pregnancy shows a **leftward axis shift**, sinus tachycardia, small Q and inverted T in lead III; AF, LVH, RVH or ST deviation are abnormal. [Harrison 22e]`,`**NT-proBNP** is little changed by normal pregnancy; a normal value has a high negative predictive value for cardiac events, while a rise suggests decompensation. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Chest X-ray** with abdominal shielding gives a fetal dose far below **50 mGy**, the level below which no measurable fetal harm is expected - never withhold it in suspected pulmonary oedema. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**MRI without gadolinium** is safe for aortic and complex CHD imaging; **CT** is used when essential (e.g., PE, dissection), as fetal dose from chest CT is low. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Pre-pregnancy exercise testing** assesses functional capacity in asymptomatic valve or CHD patients; in pregnancy only submaximal testing (about 80% of predicted heart rate) is used. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Mechanical valve on LMWH**: weekly **anti-Xa** - peak 4-6 hours after dose **0.8-1.2 IU/mL (aortic) or 1.0-1.2 IU/mL (mitral or right-sided)**; trough levels guide twice-daily dosing. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Fetal echocardiography at 19-22 weeks** for mothers with CHD; Hb, ferritin, TSH and urine protein at booking. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD - pre-conception and antenatal`,points:[`**Pre-pregnancy counselling** for every woman with heart disease: mWHO class, CARPREG II, offspring risk, medication review, and intervention first where indicated (PTMC for MS, AS repair, aortic surgery). [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Pregnancy Heart Team** (cardiologist, obstetrician, anaesthetist, neonatologist) should plan care for **mWHO II-III and above**, with a written plan for delivery and postpartum. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**MS with valve area under 1.5 cm2**: advise **PTMC before conception**; avoid pregnancy until it is done. See the Mitral stenosis topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**MS in pregnancy**: **beta-blocker** (metoprolol 25-100 mg twice daily) to slow heart rate and lengthen diastole; **furosemide 20-40 mg** for congestion; restrict activity; correct anaemia. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Anticoagulate MS** with LMWH or UFH (VKA in 2nd-3rd trimester) if **AF, LA thrombus or previous embolism**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Continue secondary prophylaxis of rheumatic fever** - benzathine penicillin G 1.2 million units IM every 3-4 weeks is safe in pregnancy. [ICMR STW]`,`**AF or flutter**: rate control with beta-blocker or digoxin; **DC cardioversion is safe in all trimesters**; anticoagulate with LMWH or VKA (not DOACs). [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Contraindicated drugs**: **ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitors, statins, ivabradine, atenolol (growth restriction), amiodarone (fetal thyroid) unless no alternative, DOACs, endothelin antagonists and riociguat**; warfarin above 5 mg/day in weeks 6-12. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Safe drugs**: labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa, metoprolol, hydralazine, nitrates, furosemide, digoxin, adenosine, heparins and low-dose aspirin. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Mechanical valves - anticoagulation regimens`,points:[`**Mechanical valve is mWHO III**: the risk is valve thrombosis (hypercoagulability) versus fetal harm from warfarin - discuss both before conception. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**VKA throughout pregnancy** gives the **lowest risk of valve thrombosis**; **warfarin embryopathy** (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses) arises from exposure in **weeks 6-12** and is dose dependent, low at **5 mg/day or less**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Low-dose requirement (warfarin 5 mg/day or less, acenocoumarol 2 mg/day or less)**: continuing VKA in the first trimester should be considered after informed discussion. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**High-dose requirement**: switch to **adjusted-dose LMWH twice daily** (anti-Xa monitored) or UFH (aPTT 2 times control) **between weeks 6 and 12**, then back to VKA. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Weeks 13-36**: **VKA** with INR to the valve target (usually 2.5-3.5 for mitral); LMWH throughout is an alternative only with **weekly anti-Xa monitoring** - LMWH without monitoring has a high thrombosis rate. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**At 36 weeks**: stop VKA and switch to LMWH or UFH; **switch LMWH to IV UFH at least 36 hours before planned delivery**, stop UFH 4-6 hours before, and restart 4-6 hours after delivery if no bleeding. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Labour on VKA** means the fetus is anticoagulated - **caesarean section** is indicated to avoid fetal intracranial haemorrhage. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**In India**, low-dose warfarin or acenocoumarol throughout pregnancy is often chosen because **anti-Xa monitoring is not widely available**; switching any regimen should be done in hospital. [Indian practice]`,`**DOACs are contraindicated** both in mechanical valves and in pregnancy. See the Prosthetic heart valves topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD - delivery planning and emergencies`,points:[`**Vaginal delivery is preferred** for most women with heart disease - less blood loss, infection and VTE than caesarean; plan **induction around 39-40 weeks** in a centre matched to mWHO class. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Caesarean for cardiac reasons**: labour on oral anticoagulants, **aggressive aortic disease** (Marfan aorta over 45 mm, acute dissection), acute intractable heart failure and severe PAH. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Labour care**: **left lateral position**, **early epidural analgesia** (reduces pain-driven tachycardia), **assisted second stage** (forceps or vacuum) to avoid prolonged pushing, and careful fluid balance. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Third stage**: **slow oxytocin infusion** (avoid rapid bolus - hypotension); **avoid ergometrine** in heart disease (vasoconstriction, hypertension) and use carboprost with caution (raises PA pressure). [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Postpartum monitoring** for at least 24-48 hours (longer in mWHO III-IV): fluid shifts peak in the first hours, so watch SpO2, urine output and lung bases. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Pulmonary oedema in MS**: sit up, oxygen, **IV furosemide**, IV beta-blocker (metoprolol) to slow the rate, treat AF; if refractory with NYHA III-IV or sPAP over 50 mmHg, **PTMC after 20 weeks** in an experienced centre with abdominal shielding. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Cardiac arrest in pregnancy**: standard ALS with **manual left uterine displacement**, and **perimortem caesarean within 5 minutes** if no return of circulation after 20 weeks. [ERC 2021]`,`**Antibiotic prophylaxis for endocarditis** is not recommended for vaginal or caesarean delivery solely for the heart lesion. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Peripartum cardiomyopathy and other special situations`,points:[`**PPCM** is HF with **LVEF under 45%** in the last months of pregnancy or the months after delivery (mostly first month postpartum), with no other identifiable cause. See the Dilated and peripartum cardiomyopathy topic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Risk factors**: multiparity, age over 30, **pre-eclampsia or hypertension**, twin pregnancy, prolonged tocolysis and African ancestry; the mechanism involves a **16-kDa prolactin fragment** that is anti-angiogenic. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**PPCM in pregnancy**: beta-blocker (metoprolol), **hydralazine plus nitrate** instead of ACE inhibitor, diuretics; **after delivery** full GDMT - enalapril or captopril are compatible with breastfeeding. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Bromocriptine 2.5 mg** may be considered (with **prophylactic or therapeutic anticoagulation** because it is prothrombotic) - one week in uncomplicated cases, or 2.5 mg twice daily for 2 weeks then once daily for 6 weeks in severe PPCM. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**PPCM outcome**: LV recovery in about half within 6 months; **persistent LVEF under 50% makes a future pregnancy mWHO IV**, and even after recovery recurrence risk remains. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Aortopathy**: Marfan women need a **beta-blocker throughout pregnancy** and aortic imaging every 4-12 weeks; aortic root over 45 mm means advise against pregnancy. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Acute coronary syndrome in pregnancy** is most often **SCAD**; troponin is unaffected by normal pregnancy, and primary PCI is the reperfusion of choice. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Hypertension pointer**: ESC now treats confirmed **BP 140/90 or more** in pregnancy (CHAP trial) with **labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa**, and BP **160/110 or more is an emergency**; give low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks to high-risk women. See the Hypertension topic. [ESC 2024 HT]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Maternal**: heart failure and pulmonary oedema (commonest), arrhythmia (AF, SVT, VT), thromboembolism and **mechanical valve thrombosis**, aortic dissection, endocarditis and death. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Fetal and neonatal**: miscarriage, **growth restriction**, preterm birth, low birth weight, fetal loss in cyanosis, warfarin embryopathy and **recurrence of CHD (about 3-5%)**. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Obstetric overlap**: pre-eclampsia and postpartum haemorrhage are commoner in women with heart disease and on anticoagulants. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**ROPAC registry**: maternal mortality is low overall but **highest in PAH**, and heart failure occurs in about a quarter of women with moderate-severe MS. [ROPAC, ESC registry]`,`**Long-term**: pre-eclampsia, gestational hypertension and gestational diabetes roughly **double later cardiovascular risk** - flag them as risk enhancers. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Subsequent pregnancies** carry recurrence risk for PPCM and progression for valve disease; counsel before each pregnancy. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Follow-up by class**: mWHO I - 1-2 visits; II - each trimester; II-III - every 2 months; III - every 1-2 months; IV - monthly, at an expert centre. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Care location**: mWHO I and II local hospital; II-III referral hospital; **III and IV expert centre with cardiac surgery** and a Pregnancy Heart Team. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Refer immediately**: NYHA III-IV, pulmonary oedema, new AF, syncope, haemoptysis, muffled valve click, chest pain, SpO2 under 90%. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Repeat echo** each trimester in valve disease and at 20-24 weeks (peak haemodynamic load) in most cardiac pregnancies; earlier with new symptoms. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Postpartum review** at 6 weeks and 6 months with echo after PPCM or decompensation; restart drugs that were stopped for pregnancy. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Contraception** after delivery: LNG-IUD, copper IUD, implant or progestogen-only methods; **avoid oestrogen** in mechanical valves, PAH, cyanosis, Fontan and prior thrombosis. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Breastfeeding-compatible drugs**: metoprolol, propranolol, labetalol, nifedipine, enalapril, captopril, warfarin and heparins; avoid amiodarone, ARNI and SGLT2 inhibitors. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**PMSMA (Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan)** offers a specialist antenatal check on the 9th of every month - the opportunity to auscultate, identify heart disease and tag the woman as **high-risk pregnancy** on the MCP card. [PMSMA MoHFW]`,`**JSSK and SUMAN** entitle high-risk mothers to free transport, drugs and delivery at a facility with appropriate care; mWHO III-IV women should be booked at a **medical college or tertiary centre**. [MoHFW]`,`**RHD prevention**: treat streptococcal sore throat, keep up benzathine penicillin prophylaxis, and screen adolescent girls - see the Rheumatic heart disease topic. [ICMR STW]`,`**Correct anaemia early** under **Anemia Mukt Bharat** (IFA 60 mg iron and 500 mcg folic acid daily from the second trimester) - anaemia precipitates failure in MS. [Anemia Mukt Bharat]`,`**Family planning counselling** is part of cardiac care: women in mWHO IV need highly effective contraception; sterilisation is an option after counselling. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Folic acid 400 mcg** (higher if on antiepileptics or with a previous CHD child) from before conception reduces neural tube and some cardiac defects. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2023-2025)`,points:[`**ESC 2025 cardiovascular disease and pregnancy guideline** replaces ESC 2018; it keeps the **mWHO four-class system** as the core risk tool, refined by CARPREG II, ZAHARA and NT-proBNP. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**ESC 2025** makes a multidisciplinary **Pregnancy Heart Team** the standard for women at moderate or high risk, with pre-conception counselling and a documented delivery plan. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**ESC 2025** keeps **VKA as the most protective regimen for mechanical valves** (low-dose VKA in the first trimester an option) and requires **anti-Xa-monitored LMWH** whenever heparin is used. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Newer HF drugs**: SGLT2 inhibitors, ARNI and MRAs remain **contraindicated** in pregnancy, so GDMT for PPCM is staged around delivery and breastfeeding. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Hypertension**: ESC 2024 and ESC 2025 lower the drug-treatment threshold in pregnancy to **140/90 mmHg** for all hypertensive disorders, following the CHAP trial. [ESC 2024 HT]`,`**ESC 2023 endocarditis guideline** confirms no routine IE prophylaxis for vaginal or caesarean delivery. [ESC 2023 IE]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Most dangerous time** for MS and PAH is **immediately after delivery** (autotransfusion, cardiac output up 60-80%), not the moment of birth. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Stenotic lesions (MS, AS) tolerate pregnancy badly; regurgitant lesions (MR, AR) tolerate it well** - because of rising volume and heart rate with falling SVR. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Commonest heart disease in Indian pregnancy is rheumatic MS**; the treatment of refractory MS in pregnancy is **PTMC**, not surgery. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Four contraindications to pregnancy** to list in viva: **PAH, EF under 30% or NYHA III-IV, severe MS or symptomatic severe AS, and Marfan aorta over 45 mm** (plus previous PPCM with residual dysfunction). [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Common mistake**: stopping warfarin in a woman with a mechanical mitral valve and giving fixed-dose LMWH without anti-Xa monitoring - the classic route to valve thrombosis. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Mnemonic for drugs to stop when planning pregnancy - 'A SMART Way'**: ACE inhibitors/ARBs/ARNI, SGLT2i, MRA, Amiodarone, Ribavirin-like teratogens and riociguat, sTatins, Warfarin above 5 mg. [ESC 2025 pregnancy]`,`**Normal pregnancy signs** (S3, flow ESM, mammary souffle, small Q and T inversion in III) are a favourite trap - a **diastolic murmur is never normal**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Haemodynamic changes in pregnancy and their consequences`,columns:[`Parameter`,`Change`,`Peak / timing`,`Clinical consequence`],rows:[[`Plasma volume`,`Up 40-50%`,`Around 32 weeks`,`Dilutional anaemia; MS and LV dysfunction decompensate`],[`Cardiac output`,`Up 30-50%`,`Late second trimester; up to 80% just after delivery`,`Pulmonary oedema in stenotic lesions`],[`Heart rate`,`Up 10-20 bpm`,`Third trimester`,`Shorter diastole raises LA pressure in MS`],[`Systemic vascular resistance`,`Falls`,`Mid-pregnancy`,`Regurgitant lesions improve; shunts may reverse in Eisenmenger`],[`Coagulation factors`,`Fibrinogen, VII, VIII, X up; protein S down`,`Highest late and postpartum`,`VTE and mechanical valve thrombosis`],[`Labour`,`300-500 mL per contraction; CO up 50%`,`Second stage`,`Epidural and assisted second stage protect`]]},{heading:`Normal pregnancy versus heart disease`,columns:[`Feature`,`Normal pregnancy`,`Suggests heart disease`],rows:[[`Breathlessness`,`Gradual, exertional, no orthopnoea`,`Orthopnoea, PND, at rest, progressive NYHA`],[`Murmur`,`Soft ejection systolic, mammary souffle, venous hum`,`Any diastolic murmur, loud pansystolic, thrill`],[`Heart sounds`,`Loud S1, physiological S3`,`Opening snap, loud P2, S4, muffled valve click`],[`Oedema`,`Mild ankle oedema`,`Raised JVP, crackles, hepatomegaly`],[`ECG`,`Left axis shift, small Q and T inversion in III, sinus tachycardia`,`AF, LVH, RVH, ST deviation, VT`],[`SpO2 and syncope`,`Normal SpO2; postural dizziness`,`Hypoxaemia, exertional syncope`]]},{heading:`mWHO classification of maternal cardiovascular risk (ESC 2025)`,columns:[`Class`,`Examples`,`Maternal event rate`,`Care and follow-up`],rows:[[`I`,`Mild PS, small PDA, MVP, repaired simple shunts, isolated ectopics`,`2.5-5%`,`Local hospital; 1-2 visits`],[`II`,`Unoperated ASD/VSD, repaired TOF, most SVT, Turner without aortic dilatation`,`5.7-10.5%`,`Local hospital; each trimester`],[`II-III`,`LVEF over 45%, HCM, mild MS, moderate AS, Marfan without dilatation, repaired coarctation`,`10-19%`,`Referral hospital; every 2 months`],[`III`,`LVEF 30-45%, recovered PPCM, mechanical valve, moderate MS, severe asymptomatic AS, Fontan, cyanotic CHD`,`19-27%`,`Expert centre; monthly or 2-monthly`],[`IV`,`PAH, LVEF under 30% or NYHA III-IV, PPCM with residual LV dysfunction, severe MS, severe symptomatic AS, Marfan aorta over 45 mm, severe coarctation`,`40-100%`,`Pregnancy contraindicated; if pregnant, expert centre monthly`]]},{heading:`CARPREG II score`,columns:[`Predictor`,`Points`],rows:[[`Prior cardiac event or arrhythmia`,`3`],[`Baseline NYHA III-IV or cyanosis`,`3`],[`Mechanical valve`,`3`],[`Ventricular dysfunction`,`2`],[`High-risk left-sided valve disease or LVOT obstruction`,`2`],[`Pulmonary hypertension`,`2`],[`Coronary artery disease`,`2`],[`High-risk aortopathy`,`2`],[`No prior cardiac intervention`,`1`],[`Late pregnancy assessment (after 20 weeks)`,`1`],[`Risk of cardiac event: 0-1 = 5%, 2 = 10%, 3 = 15%, 4 = 22%, over 4 = 41%`,`Total`]]},{heading:`Anticoagulation for mechanical valves in pregnancy (ESC 2025)`,columns:[`Period`,`Low VKA dose (warfarin 5 mg or less)`,`High VKA dose (warfarin over 5 mg)`,`Monitoring`],rows:[[`Pre-conception`,`Counsel; continue VKA`,`Counsel; plan switch at 6 weeks`,`INR; pregnancy test early`],[`Weeks 6-12`,`Continue VKA (consider)`,`LMWH twice daily or IV UFH, in hospital switch`,`Weekly anti-Xa (peak 0.8-1.2 aortic, 1.0-1.2 mitral) or aPTT 2 times control`],[`Weeks 13-36`,`VKA`,`VKA`,`INR to valve target`],[`From 36 weeks`,`Switch to LMWH or UFH`,`Switch to LMWH or UFH`,`Anti-Xa weekly`],[`Delivery`,`IV UFH at least 36 h before; stop 4-6 h before`,`Same`,`Caesarean if labour starts on VKA`],[`After delivery`,`Restart UFH/LMWH 4-6 h after if no bleeding; VKA from day 1-2`,`Same`,`Bridge until INR in range`]]},{heading:`Drugs in heart disease in pregnancy`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Metoprolol (MS, AF, PPCM, Marfan)`,`25-100 mg orally twice daily`,`Through pregnancy; titrate to HR about 60-70`,`Fetal bradycardia, growth restriction - monitor growth; preferred over atenolol`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg orally or IV, repeat as needed`,`Short courses for congestion`,`Reduced placental perfusion if overused; hypokalaemia`],[`Labetalol (hypertension)`,`100-400 mg orally 2-3 times daily; IV 20 mg bolus in emergency`,`Through pregnancy`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm - avoid in asthma`],[`Hydralazine plus isosorbide dinitrate (PPCM in pregnancy)`,`Hydralazine 25-50 mg orally 3-4 times daily with ISDN 20-40 mg 3 times daily`,`Until delivery, then ACE inhibitor`,`Headache, hypotension, lupus-like syndrome`],[`Enoxaparin (LMWH)`,`1 mg/kg SC twice daily (therapeutic), adjusted to anti-Xa`,`Weeks 6-12 and from 36 weeks for valves; VTE treatment`,`Bleeding, HIT (rare); switch to UFH 36 h before delivery`],[`Warfarin`,`Dose to INR target; aim 5 mg/day or less`,`Weeks 13-36 (and first trimester if low dose)`,`Embryopathy weeks 6-12; fetal bleeding; never in labour`],[`Bromocriptine (PPCM)`,`2.5 mg orally once daily for 1 week; severe: 2.5 mg twice daily 2 weeks then daily 6 weeks`,`Postpartum only`,`Thrombosis - give with anticoagulation; stops lactation`],[`Benzathine penicillin G (RHD)`,`1.2 million units IM`,`Every 3-4 weeks, continue in pregnancy`,`Anaphylaxis (rare) - test dose policy as per local protocol`]]}],redFlags:[`**Orthopnoea, breathlessness at rest or pulmonary oedema** in any pregnant or postpartum woman.`,`**New AF or sustained palpitations** in a woman with mitral stenosis.`,`**Muffled mechanical valve click**, new breathlessness or embolism on any anticoagulant switch.`,`**Exertional syncope, chest pain or haemoptysis** in pregnancy.`,`**SpO2 under 90%** or cyanosis.`,`**Postpartum breathlessness and oedema** within months of delivery - PPCM until echo proves otherwise.`,`**Severe chest or back pain in Marfan or bicuspid aortopathy** - aortic dissection.`,`**BP 160/110 or more** with headache, visual change or proteinuria.`],pearls:[`**Stenosis bad, regurgitation good** in pregnancy.`,`**Most dangerous moment = just after delivery** (cardiac output up 60-80%).`,`**Indian pregnancy + new heart disease = rheumatic MS until proved otherwise.**`,`**Refractory MS in pregnancy: PTMC after 20 weeks** in an expert centre.`,`**Warfarin 5 mg or less = low embryopathy risk**; switch at 36 weeks; caesarean if labour on VKA.`,`**LMWH for a mechanical valve only with anti-Xa monitoring.**`,`**Metoprolol yes, atenolol no; labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa for BP.**`,`**No ACEi, ARB, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2i, statin or DOAC in pregnancy.**`,`**Diastolic murmur in pregnancy is never physiological.**`,`**PPCM with persistent EF under 50% = mWHO IV for the next pregnancy.**`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-q1`,stem:`A woman with moderate mitral stenosis has an uneventful labour. At which time is she at the greatest risk of acute pulmonary oedema?`,options:[`At 12 weeks of gestation`,`During the first stage of labour`,`During the second stage while pushing`,`In the first hours immediately after delivery`],answer:3,explanation:`Immediately after delivery, relief of caval compression and autotransfusion of uterine blood raise cardiac output by 60-80% and pour volume into a left atrium that cannot empty through a stenotic valve, so pulmonary oedema peaks then. The first trimester has only small haemodynamic changes, and although labour contractions and pushing add load, an epidural and an assisted second stage blunt it - the postpartum surge is the bigger hazard.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old primigravida at 26 weeks has rheumatic mitral stenosis with valve area 0.9 cm2, favourable valve morphology and no LA thrombus. She remains NYHA class III with PA systolic pressure 58 mmHg despite metoprolol and furosemide. The best next step is:`,options:[`Percutaneous transvenous mitral commissurotomy (PTMC)`,`Open mitral valve replacement on cardiopulmonary bypass`,`Immediate termination of pregnancy`,`Add digoxin and continue until term`],answer:0,explanation:`Severe MS that stays NYHA III-IV or has PA systolic pressure over 50 mmHg despite beta-blocker and diuretic should undergo PTMC in pregnancy, preferably after 20 weeks, in an experienced centre with abdominal shielding - it is effective and far safer for the fetus than surgery. Cardiopulmonary bypass carries a high fetal loss rate. Termination at 26 weeks does not relieve the obstruction, and digoxin does not slow sinus rhythm or relieve MS.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-q3`,stem:`A 27-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve takes warfarin 3 mg daily with a stable INR. She wishes to conceive. Per ESC guidance, which anticoagulant plan is most appropriate?`,options:[`Switch to a DOAC before conception`,`Fixed-dose prophylactic enoxaparin 40 mg daily throughout pregnancy`,`Continue low-dose warfarin through the first trimester after counselling, switch to LMWH or UFH at 36 weeks`,`Stop anticoagulation in the first trimester and use aspirin alone`],answer:2,explanation:`VKA throughout pregnancy gives the lowest risk of valve thrombosis, and at 5 mg/day or less the risk of embryopathy is low, so continuing warfarin in the first trimester can be considered after informed discussion, with a switch to heparin at 36 weeks before planned delivery. DOACs are contraindicated with mechanical valves and in pregnancy. Prophylactic fixed-dose LMWH without anti-Xa monitoring has a high thrombosis rate, and aspirin alone is inadequate.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-q4`,stem:`A pregnant woman at 22 weeks with mild LV dysfunction and hypertension is on several drugs. Which one must be stopped?`,options:[`Metoprolol succinate`,`Enalapril`,`Hydralazine`,`Nifedipine extended-release`],answer:1,explanation:`ACE inhibitors are fetotoxic in the second and third trimesters, causing oligohydramnios, renal tubular dysgenesis, skull hypoplasia and neonatal anuria, so enalapril is stopped and replaced (hydralazine plus nitrate for LV dysfunction, labetalol or nifedipine for BP). Metoprolol, hydralazine and nifedipine are compatible with pregnancy and are first-choice agents; metoprolol is preferred to atenolol because atenolol causes growth restriction.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-q5`,stem:`A 31-year-old woman develops breathlessness 3 weeks after delivery of twins; her pregnancy was complicated by pre-eclampsia. Echo shows a dilated LV with ejection fraction 30% and no other cause. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`Bromocriptine should be given without anticoagulation because it lowers thrombotic risk`,`ACE inhibitors are contraindicated because she is breastfeeding`,`If LVEF recovers fully, a future pregnancy carries no extra risk`,`If LVEF stays below 50%, a future pregnancy is mWHO class IV`],answer:3,explanation:`This is peripartum cardiomyopathy (LVEF under 45% after delivery, risk factors twins and pre-eclampsia). Persistent LV dysfunction makes another pregnancy mWHO class IV, and even full recovery leaves a recurrence risk, so a future pregnancy is not risk-free. Bromocriptine is prothrombotic and must be combined with anticoagulation, and enalapril or captopril are compatible with breastfeeding and form part of postpartum GDMT.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the haemodynamic changes of pregnancy. How will you assess the risk and manage a pregnant woman with heart disease, with special reference to mitral stenosis and mechanical heart valves?`,openingLines:[`Heart disease complicates 1-4% of pregnancies and is a leading indirect cause of maternal death; in India rheumatic mitral stenosis is the commonest lesion.`,`Risk is graded by the modified WHO classification (ESC 2025) refined by CARPREG II, and care is planned by a Pregnancy Heart Team.`],answer:[{heading:`Haemodynamic changes`,points:[`Plasma volume up 40-50% (peak 32 weeks), cardiac output up 30-50%, HR up 10-20, SVR down, hypercoagulability.`,`Labour: 300-500 mL per contraction; immediately postpartum CO up 60-80% - the most dangerous moment.`]},{heading:`Risk assessment`,points:[`mWHO I-IV with event rates 2.5-5% to 40-100%; class IV list (PAH, EF under 30%, severe MS, symptomatic severe AS, Marfan aorta over 45 mm, PPCM with residual dysfunction).`,`CARPREG II predictors and points; NT-proBNP, NYHA class, echo.`]},{heading:`General antenatal management`,points:[`Pre-conception counselling, Pregnancy Heart Team, follow-up frequency by class, echo each trimester, anaemia correction, stop teratogens (ACEi, ARB, ARNI, MRA, SGLT2i, statins, atenolol, amiodarone).`]},{heading:`Mitral stenosis`,points:[`MVA under 1.5 cm2 - PTMC before pregnancy.`,`In pregnancy: metoprolol, furosemide, anticoagulation for AF/LA thrombus/embolism; PTMC after 20 weeks if NYHA III-IV or sPAP over 50 despite drugs.`,`Vaginal delivery with epidural and assisted second stage; caesarean if NYHA III-IV or PH despite therapy and PTMC not possible.`]},{heading:`Mechanical valves`,points:[`mWHO III; VKA safest for valve, embryopathy risk weeks 6-12 dose dependent.`,`Warfarin 5 mg or less: continue; higher dose: LMWH (anti-Xa) or UFH weeks 6-12; VKA to 36 weeks; switch to heparin; IV UFH 36 h before delivery; caesarean if labour on VKA.`]},{heading:`Delivery and postpartum`,points:[`Vaginal delivery preferred, left lateral, epidural, slow oxytocin, avoid ergometrine, monitor 24-48 h; contraception without oestrogen in high-risk lesions; watch for PPCM.`]}],mustDraw:[`Graph of cardiac output across pregnancy, labour and the postpartum period`,`Table of mWHO classes I-IV with examples and event rates`,`Timeline of mechanical valve anticoagulation by gestational week`],markSplit:[{part:`Haemodynamic changes`,marks:2},{part:`Risk assessment (mWHO, CARPREG II)`,marks:2},{part:`General antenatal and drug management`,marks:1.5},{part:`Mitral stenosis`,marks:2},{part:`Mechanical valves`,marks:1.5},{part:`Delivery and postpartum`,marks:1}],keywords:[`mWHO`,`CARPREG II`,`mitral stenosis`,`PTMC`,`mechanical valve`,`warfarin embryopathy`,`anti-Xa`,`Pregnancy Heart Team`]},{id:`cardiovascular-heart-pregnancy-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on peripartum cardiomyopathy.`,openingLines:[`Peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure secondary to LV systolic dysfunction (LVEF under 45%) towards the end of pregnancy or in the months after delivery, with no other identifiable cause.`,`It is a diagnosis of exclusion; about half recover LV function within 6 months.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors and mechanism`,points:[`Multiparity, age over 30, pre-eclampsia, twins, tocolysis, African ancestry; oxidative stress cleaves prolactin into an anti-angiogenic 16-kDa fragment.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Breathlessness, oedema, orthopnoea; raised NT-proBNP; echo LVEF under 45%; exclude pre-existing DCM, valve disease, MI, PE.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`Antepartum: beta-blocker, hydralazine plus nitrate, diuretics; no ACEi/ARB/ARNI/MRA/SGLT2i.`,`Postpartum: full GDMT (enalapril compatible with breastfeeding); bromocriptine with anticoagulation may be considered; anticoagulate if LV thrombus or very low EF; ICD decisions deferred (wearable defibrillator).`]},{heading:`Prognosis and counselling`,points:[`Recovery in about half; persistent EF under 50% - future pregnancy mWHO IV; recurrence possible even after recovery; non-oestrogen contraception.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: suspected PPCM - NT-proBNP and echo - antepartum versus postpartum drug choices - follow-up echo at 6 weeks and 6 months`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Pathogenesis`,marks:.5},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Treatment`,marks:1.5},{part:`Prognosis and counselling`,marks:1}],keywords:[`PPCM`,`LVEF under 45%`,`prolactin`,`bromocriptine`,`hydralazine`,`mWHO IV`]}],references:[`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy (Eur Heart J 2025)`,`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Pregnancy and heart disease`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Pregnancy and heart disease`,`Silversides CK et al. CARPREG II risk score, J Am Coll Cardiol 2018`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of elevated blood pressure and hypertension`,`2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of endocarditis`,`Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) and Anemia Mukt Bharat guidelines, MoHFW`,`Sample Registration System Special Bulletin on Maternal Mortality in India 2019-21, Registrar General of India`]}),c.push({id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart`,title:`The heart in systemic disease: thyroid, anaemia, beriberi, HIV, CKD and cancer therapy`,frequency:`less-common`,oneLiner:`Many systemic diseases act on the heart: **thyrotoxicosis** causes AF and high-output failure, **hypothyroidism** bradycardia and pericardial effusion, **severe anaemia, AV fistula and wet beriberi** cause **high-output heart failure** (beriberi responding dramatically to **IV thiamine**), **HIV and CKD** accelerate atherosclerosis, and **anthracyclines and trastuzumab** cause **cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (CTRCD)** graded by the **ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline**.`,keywords:[`thyrotoxic heart disease`,`thyrotoxic AF`,`myxoedema heart`,`hypothyroid pericardial effusion`,`high-output heart failure`,`anaemic heart failure`,`AV fistula`,`Nicoladoni-Branham sign`,`wet beriberi`,`Shoshin beriberi`,`thiamine`,`HIV cardiomyopathy`,`REPRIEVE`,`cardiorenal`,`CKD cardiovascular disease`,`cardio-oncology`,`CTRCD`,`anthracycline`,`trastuzumab`,`Libman-Sacks`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**High-output heart failure** is HF with a **cardiac output above normal (cardiac index over about 4 L/min/m2)** and low systemic vascular resistance; it accounts for only a few percent of HF but is often reversible once the cause is treated. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AF occurs in about 10-15% of thyrotoxic patients**, more in the elderly; and about 1-2% of new AF is due to overt hyperthyroidism, so **TSH is checked in every new AF**. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Anaemia in India**: NFHS-5 found anaemia in **57% of women aged 15-49** and about 25% of men - severe anaemia remains a common cause of breathlessness and high-output failure in Indian OPDs. [NFHS-5]`,`**Beriberi** still occurs in India - outbreaks in rice-dependent communities in the North-East, in **alcohol use disorder**, hyperemesis, malnutrition, prolonged diuretic use and dialysis; Shoshin beriberi is reported in Indian ICUs. [Indian case series]`,`**People living with HIV** have about **1.5-2 times** the risk of MI of HIV-negative people even when virologically suppressed; India has about **2.5 million PLHIV**. [NACO 2023]`,`**Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of death in CKD** - most patients with CKD stage 3-4 die of CVD before reaching dialysis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Cancer**: India records about **14-15 lakh new cancers a year** (ICMR NCRP), and improving survival makes **cardiotoxicity** a growing late problem in breast cancer and lymphoma survivors. [ICMR NCRP 2022]`,`**SLE and RA** carry a **1.5-2 fold** (RA) and much higher in young women (SLE) risk of premature coronary disease, independent of traditional risk factors. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Aetiology - the systemic causes to list`,points:[`**High-output states**: **severe anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, AV fistula** (dialysis access, traumatic), **beriberi, Paget disease**, pregnancy, cirrhosis, sepsis, obesity, myeloproliferative disease and hypercapnic lung disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Endocrine**: thyrotoxicosis, hypothyroidism, acromegaly (cardiomyopathy), phaeochromocytoma (catecholamine cardiomyopathy), Cushing, diabetes, and primary aldosteronism (LVH, AF). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nutritional**: **thiamine deficiency** (wet beriberi), selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), severe protein-energy malnutrition, and carnitine deficiency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infection**: **HIV** (cardiomyopathy, CAD, PAH, effusion), **tuberculosis** (pericarditis - the commonest cause of large effusion in Indian PLHIV), Chagas disease, rheumatic fever. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal**: CKD-related LVH, accelerated atherosclerosis, vascular calcification, uraemic pericarditis, volume overload and high-flow fistulae. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Cancer therapy**: **anthracyclines** (doxorubicin, epirubicin), **HER2 agents** (trastuzumab), **immune checkpoint inhibitors**, fluoropyrimidines (5-FU, capecitabine), VEGF inhibitors, some TKIs, and **chest radiotherapy**. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Autoimmune**: SLE, RA, systemic sclerosis, antiphospholipid syndrome, spondyloarthritis and sarcoidosis. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology - why each disease hits the heart`,points:[`**Thyroid hormone (T3)** increases expression of **SERCA2 and alpha-myosin heavy chain** and reduces phospholamban, increasing contractility and relaxation; it lowers SVR directly and through tissue thermogenesis, so cardiac output rises and **renin-driven volume expansion** follows. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxic AF** results from shortened atrial refractoriness and increased automaticity; persistent tachycardia can cause **tachycardia-mediated cardiomyopathy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypothyroidism** does the reverse - low contractility, bradycardia, raised SVR (diastolic hypertension), raised LDL and accelerated atherosclerosis; capillary leak of protein-rich fluid causes **pericardial effusion**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**High-output failure**: low SVR (vasodilatation, arteriovenous shunting) lowers effective arterial volume, activating **sympathetic and RAAS systems**, which retain salt and water - congestion develops despite a high output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia** lowers blood viscosity and causes hypoxic and NO-mediated vasodilatation; a normal heart tolerates **Hb down to about 7 g/dL**, but with valve or coronary disease failure occurs at higher Hb. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thiamine** is the cofactor of **pyruvate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase**; deficiency stops aerobic metabolism, causing **lactic acidosis, peripheral vasodilatation** (high output) and later myocardial energy failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: chronic immune activation and inflammation drive atherosclerosis; direct viral and opportunistic myocarditis, ART metabolic effects and smoking add risk. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD**: traditional risk factors plus **anaemia, CKD-mineral bone disorder** (phosphate, FGF23, vascular calcification), uraemic toxins, inflammation and volume overload - LVH is present in most patients by dialysis start. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Anthracycline cardiotoxicity** is driven by **topoisomerase II-beta** inhibition and iron-dependent free radicals, causing irreversible cardiomyocyte loss that is **cumulative-dose dependent**; **trastuzumab** blocks HER2 survival signalling, causing largely **reversible** dysfunction. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Classification that examiners ask`,points:[`**Heart failure by output**: low-output (ischaemic, dilated, valvular) versus **high-output** (anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, AV fistula, beriberi, Paget, pregnancy). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beriberi types**: **dry** (peripheral neuropathy), **wet** (high-output cardiac failure with oedema), **Shoshin** (fulminant wet beriberi with shock and lactic acidosis), and **Wernicke-Korsakoff** (cerebral). [Harrison 22e]`,`**CTRCD (ESC 2022)**: **symptomatic** (HF, graded mild to very severe) or **asymptomatic** - **severe: new LVEF under 40%**; **moderate: LVEF fall of 10 points or more to 40-49%**, or a smaller fall to 40-49% with **GLS relative fall over 15%** or new biomarker rise; **mild: LVEF 50% or more with GLS fall over 15% and/or new troponin or NP rise**. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Type I versus type II cardiotoxicity**: type I (anthracyclines) - dose-dependent, structural cell death, largely irreversible; type II (trastuzumab) - not dose-dependent, functional, usually reversible. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**HFA-ICOS baseline risk** stratifies patients before cancer therapy into **low, moderate, high and very high** risk from prior CVD, LVEF, biomarkers, age, risk factors and previous cardiotoxic treatment. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Cardiorenal syndromes (Ronco)**: type 1 acute cardiac causing AKI, type 2 chronic HF causing CKD, type 3 AKI causing cardiac dysfunction, type 4 CKD causing cardiac disease, type 5 systemic disease (sepsis, SLE, amyloid) affecting both. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis**: **type 1** (iodine-induced excess synthesis in a nodular or Graves thyroid, high uptake, Doppler flow) and **type 2** (destructive thyroiditis, low flow) - treated with thionamide and steroid respectively. [ETA 2018]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`**Thyrotoxic heart**: palpitations, exertional dyspnoea, heat intolerance, weight loss with good appetite; the elderly may show **apathetic hyperthyroidism** - AF or heart failure alone. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypothyroid heart**: fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, constipation, exertional breathlessness, angina when thyroxine is started too fast. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemic heart failure**: breathlessness, fatigue, palpitations, angina in older patients; ask about **menorrhagia, worms, diet, GI bleeding, NSAIDs and pregnancy**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beriberi**: a rice-dependent diet, **alcohol**, hyperemesis, postpartum state or bariatric surgery, with **breathlessness, oedema and palpitations**, often with paraesthesia or calf tenderness of dry beriberi. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: chest pain at a young age, breathlessness from cardiomyopathy or PAH, fever with effusion (TB); ask about ART regimen and adherence. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cancer therapy**: breathlessness, oedema, palpitations or chest pain during or years after chemotherapy; ask the **regimen, cumulative anthracycline dose, chest radiation** and baseline echo. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Immune checkpoint inhibitor myocarditis** typically starts within the **first 3 months**, often with **myositis and myasthenia-like ptosis** - the overlap syndrome. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**High-output signs**: **warm extremities**, bounding collapsing pulse, wide pulse pressure, tachycardia, hyperdynamic apex, **ejection systolic flow murmur, S3**, raised JVP and oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxicosis**: tremor, warm moist skin, goitre or eye signs, irregularly irregular pulse, systolic hypertension, and the **Means-Lerman scratch** (systolic scratch at the left 2nd space from a hyperdynamic PA). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Hypothyroidism**: bradycardia, **diastolic hypertension**, soft heart sounds, delayed relaxation of ankle jerks, puffy face, non-pitting oedema; large effusion **rarely causes tamponade** because it accumulates slowly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AV fistula**: thrill and continuous bruit over the access; **Nicoladoni-Branham sign** - compressing the fistula slows the heart rate and raises BP. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Wet beriberi**: warm peripheries, tachycardia, wide pulse pressure, oedema, raised JVP, cardiomegaly, with **loss of ankle jerks and calf tenderness**; Shoshin adds shock, cyanosis and severe acidosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SLE**: pericardial rub, malar rash, alopecia, oral ulcers; **RA**: rheumatoid nodules, deformities; **systemic sclerosis**: sclerodactyly, telangiectasia, loud P2 (PAH). [EULAR 2022 CVD]`,`**Severe anaemia**: pallor, koilonychia, glossitis, flow murmur, **retinal haemorrhages** and oedema; look for icterus (haemolysis) and splenomegaly. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**High-output versus low-output HF**: warm peripheries, wide pulse pressure and a low SVR point to high output; cold peripheries and narrow pulse pressure to low output. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyrotoxic AF versus other AF**: check TSH and free T4 in every new AF; subclinical hyperthyroidism (TSH under 0.1) also triples AF risk. See the Atrial fibrillation topic. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Hypothyroid effusion versus tuberculous or malignant effusion**: hypothyroid effusion is painless, slow, without fever, with **bradycardia rather than tachycardia** despite its size. See the Pericardial effusion topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Wet beriberi versus alcoholic cardiomyopathy**: both affect drinkers, but beriberi is **high-output with warm peripheries and lactic acidosis** and responds within hours to thiamine; alcoholic cardiomyopathy is low-output dilated CM. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anthracycline cardiomyopathy versus other DCM**: timing after chemotherapy and cumulative dose support it; exclude ischaemia, myocarditis and tachycardia-mediated CM. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**HIV pericardial effusion**: in India think **tuberculosis first**, then lymphoma, Kaposi sarcoma (rare in India) and bacterial infection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Breathlessness in CKD**: volume overload, anaemia, uraemic pericarditis, HFpEF from LVH, and **flash pulmonary oedema** of renal artery stenosis. [KDIGO 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Thyroid**: TSH, free T4 and T3; ECG for AF, sinus tachycardia or **low voltage, bradycardia and long QT** (hypothyroid); echo for effusion or LV dysfunction; lipid profile and CK are raised in hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anaemia**: CBC, peripheral smear, reticulocytes, **ferritin and transferrin saturation**, B12 and folate, stool occult blood, haemolysis screen; in HF, **iron deficiency = ferritin under 100, or 100-299 with TSAT under 20%**. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**Beriberi**: diagnosis is **clinical and therapeutic** - lactic acidosis, high-output echo, and a **dramatic response to IV thiamine**; whole-blood thiamine or **erythrocyte transketolase activity** confirm but should never delay treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AV fistula**: access flow over about **1.5-2 L/min** or a ratio of access flow to cardiac output over 0.3 suggests a high-output fistula; echo shows a high cardiac index. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**HIV**: lipid profile, HbA1c, ECG, echo for LV function and PA pressure; pericardial fluid **ADA and GeneXpert** for TB effusion. [NACO 2021]`,`**CKD**: troponin is often chronically raised - use the **rise and fall (delta)**; echo for LVH; potassium, calcium, phosphate, PTH; UACR. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Cardio-oncology baseline**: ECG, **echo with LVEF and global longitudinal strain (GLS)**, troponin and NT-proBNP before cardiotoxic therapy in all, then serial imaging by risk. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Thalassaemia**: cardiac MRI **T2* - under 20 ms indicates myocardial iron, under 10 ms severe** with high HF risk. [TIF 2021]`,`**SLE**: echo for effusion, valve vegetations and PA pressure; **antiphospholipid antibodies**; maternal **anti-Ro/SSA** for congenital heart block risk. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD`,points:[`**Thyrotoxic AF**: **propranolol 40-80 mg three to four times daily** (or metoprolol, bisoprolol) for rate, **carbimazole** to make the patient euthyroid; most revert to sinus within **8-12 weeks** of euthyroidism; cardiovert only after the patient is euthyroid. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Anticoagulation in thyrotoxic AF** follows the usual **CHA2DS2-VA** score, not the thyroid state. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Hypothyroidism with heart disease**: start **levothyroxine 12.5-25 mcg daily** in the elderly or those with CAD, increase by 12.5-25 mcg every 4-6 weeks; young healthy adults can start at about **1.6 mcg/kg/day**. [ATA 2014]`,`**Anaemia**: treat the cause; oral iron (about 60 mg elemental iron daily or alternate days) or IV iron; in **HFrEF or HFmrEF with iron deficiency**, **IV ferric carboxymaltose or ferric derisomaltose** improves symptoms and reduces HF hospitalisation. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**Beriberi in OPD (mild)**: thiamine **100 mg orally daily** plus a balanced diet, B-complex, stopping alcohol; give thiamine to every alcohol-dependent and malnourished patient. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HIV**: continue **dolutegravir-based ART (TLD)**, which has few lipid and statin interactions; start a statin - **pitavastatin 4 mg** reduced MACE by about a third in PLHIV aged 40-75 at low-moderate risk (REPRIEVE); avoid simvastatin and lovastatin with boosted protease inhibitors. [REPRIEVE NEJM 2023]`,`**CKD**: BP control, **ACEi or ARB at maximum tolerated dose** for albuminuria, **SGLT2 inhibitor** for eGFR 20 or more, **finerenone** for type 2 diabetes with albuminuria, statin or statin-ezetimibe for age 50 or more not on dialysis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Cardio-oncology prevention**: control risk factors; in **high or very-high HFA-ICOS risk** before anthracycline or trastuzumab, consider **ACEi/ARB plus beta-blocker (carvedilol)**, and statin for anthracycline; dexrazoxane or liposomal doxorubicin in high cumulative dose. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**RA and SLE**: control disease activity (reduces CV risk), minimise steroids and NSAIDs, use **hydroxychloroquine** in SLE, screen and treat risk factors; EULAR multiplies RA risk scores by **1.5**. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergencies`,points:[`**Wet or Shoshin beriberi**: give **thiamine 100 mg IV slowly as a test dose**, then **100-200 mg IV three times daily for 3-7 days**, then 100 mg orally daily; **before any glucose**; expect diuresis, slowing of heart rate and rising SVR within hours to 48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**After thiamine**, SVR rises and can unmask LV dysfunction - watch for pulmonary oedema and give diuretics; correct magnesium, which thiamine-dependent enzymes need. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Severe anaemia with HF**: transfuse **packed red cells slowly** (one unit over 3-4 hours) with **IV furosemide 20-40 mg** before or between units, and treat the cause; avoid whole blood. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thyroid storm**: propranolol (60-80 mg orally 4-hourly), **PTU 500-1000 mg load then 250 mg 4-hourly** (blocks T4 to T3 conversion), **iodine 1 hour after** thionamide, **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 8-hourly**, cooling and treatment of the trigger; use esmolol if HF limits beta-blockade. [ATA 2016]`,`**Myxoedema coma** with hypotension: ICU care, **IV levothyroxine 200-400 mcg load**, hydrocortisone 100 mg IV 8-hourly, passive rewarming, cautious fluids. [ATA 2014]`,`**ICI myocarditis**: stop the checkpoint inhibitor, admit with monitoring, **methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg IV daily for 3-5 days**, then oral steroid taper; add second-line immunosuppression if refractory - mortality is high. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Uraemic pericarditis**: intensify dialysis (heparin-free if effusion); tamponade needs pericardiocentesis. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Fluoropyrimidine chest pain**: stop the infusion, give nitrate and CCB for vasospasm, and do not rechallenge without cardio-oncology review. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: hyperemesis can cause beriberi and Wernicke's - give thiamine with IV fluids; thyrotoxicosis in the first trimester is treated with **PTU** (carbimazole embryopathy), switching to carbimazole later. [ATA 2017 pregnancy]`,`**Elderly**: apathetic hyperthyroidism presenting as AF; subclinical hyperthyroidism (TSH under 0.1) in over-65s should be treated to protect against AF and bone loss. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone**: contains about **37% iodine by weight**; check TSH before and **every 6 months**; type 2 thyrotoxicosis responds to **prednisolone 40 mg/day**. [ETA 2018]`,`**Dialysis patients**: high-flow fistulae may need **banding or ligation**; volume control through dry weight; avoid starting statins de novo in dialysis (no benefit in 4D and AURORA) but continue if already taking. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Thalassaemia major**: iron overload cardiomyopathy is the leading cause of death; **deferiprone** (alone or with deferoxamine) is most effective at clearing cardiac iron. [TIF 2021]`,`**Children and adolescent cancer survivors** treated with anthracyclines or chest radiation need lifelong cardiac surveillance. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Neonates of anti-Ro positive mothers**: about **1-2% develop congenital complete heart block**; screen with fetal echo from 16-26 weeks; hydroxychloroquine reduces recurrence. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Thyrotoxicosis**: AF with stroke, tachycardia cardiomyopathy and high-output HF are **largely reversible** once euthyroid. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Untreated Shoshin beriberi** is rapidly fatal, while treated wet beriberi recovers fully - the classic example of a curable cardiomyopathy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anthracyclines**: HF risk is about **5% at a cumulative doxorubicin dose of 400 mg/m2** and over a quarter at 550 mg/m2; **250 mg/m2 or more is a high-risk dose**; dysfunction can appear decades later. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Trastuzumab**: LV dysfunction in roughly 5-15%, usually recovering after interruption and HF treatment. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Radiation heart disease** appears 10-20 years later: CAD (ostial lesions), valve thickening (aortic, mitral), constrictive pericarditis and conduction disease. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**CKD on dialysis**: **sudden cardiac death** causes about a quarter of deaths; LVH, hyperkalaemia and fluid shifts are triggers. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**SLE**: Libman-Sacks vegetations cause embolism and valve regurgitation; women aged 35-44 with SLE have about a 50-fold higher MI risk than peers (Framingham comparison). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and monitoring`,points:[`**Thyroid**: TSH 6-8 weeks after a dose change; echo after euthyroid if LV dysfunction persists; consider **radioiodine or surgery** for recurrent thyrotoxicosis with AF. [ATA 2016]`,`**Anthracycline monitoring**: echo (LVEF, GLS) and troponin at baseline, during treatment at intervals set by risk, and **at 12 months after completion** in all; high-risk patients need long-term surveillance. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**Trastuzumab monitoring**: **echo every 3 months** during therapy and at 12 months after; a fall to moderate CTRCD prompts cardioprotection, severe CTRCD prompts interruption. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**ICI therapy**: baseline ECG and troponin, troponin before doses 2-4 in high-risk patients, and urgent review for chest pain, dyspnoea or muscle weakness. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**HIV**: lipids and HbA1c at ART start and yearly; 10-year risk scored with HIV as a **risk enhancer**. [AHA/ACC 2019 primary prevention]`,`**CKD**: eGFR and UACR at least yearly (more often in higher stages); refer at eGFR under 30 or UACR over 300 mg/g. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**Refer urgently**: new LVEF under 40% during chemotherapy, suspected ICI myocarditis, thyroid storm, suspected Shoshin beriberi, and HF from an AV fistula. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Prevention, counselling and programme link`,points:[`**Anemia Mukt Bharat** (6x6x6 strategy): prophylactic IFA, deworming, testing with digital haemoglobinometers and treatment - prevents anaemic heart failure. [Anemia Mukt Bharat]`,`**Thiamine-rich diet and supplementation**: parboiled rather than polished rice, pulses; thiamine for alcohol users, hyperemesis, bariatric and dialysis patients. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Iodised salt** (National Iodine Deficiency Disorders Control Programme) and thyroid screening of high-risk groups. [NIDDCP]`,`**NACO** free ART (TLD) and integration of NCD screening (BP, diabetes, tobacco) into ART centres. [NACO 2021]`,`**NP-NCD** screening at 30+ for hypertension and diabetes - the two biggest drivers of CKD and its CVD. [NP-NCD]`,`**National Sickle Cell Anaemia Elimination Mission (2023)** screens tribal populations; sickle cell disease causes PH and cardiomyopathy. [MoHFW 2023]`,`**Cardio-oncology services**: baseline cardiac assessment before cardiotoxic therapy; risk factor control and exercise in survivors. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2025)`,points:[`**ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline** (first ESC guideline in the field) introduced **HFA-ICOS baseline risk**, the **CTRCD definitions using LVEF, GLS and biomarkers**, and protocols for anthracyclines, HER2 agents, ICIs and radiation. [ESC 2022 cardio-oncology]`,`**ESC 2023 HF focused update**: **IV iron (ferric carboxymaltose or derisomaltose)** is class I for symptoms and quality of life in HFrEF/HFmrEF with iron deficiency, and IIa to reduce HF hospitalisation. [ESC 2023 HF update]`,`**ESC 2024 AF guideline** replaced CHA2DS2-VASc with **CHA2DS2-VA** (sex removed) - applies equally to thyrotoxic AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**REPRIEVE (2023)**: pitavastatin 4 mg in PLHIV at low-moderate risk reduced MACE by about 35%; US HIV guidance (2024) now recommends statins for PLHIV aged 40-75. [REPRIEVE NEJM 2023]`,`**KDIGO 2024 CKD guideline**: **SGLT2 inhibitors for eGFR 20 or more** in CKD with albuminuria or HF regardless of diabetes, finerenone in T2D, and GLP-1 RA (semaglutide, FLOW 2024) for cardiorenal protection in T2D. [KDIGO 2024]`,`**EULAR 2022** cardiovascular recommendations for rheumatic and musculoskeletal diseases emphasise disease control, CV risk screening and the RA risk multiplier. [EULAR 2022 CVD]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**List the causes of high-output failure** - mnemonic **'A THIN PAB'**: Anaemia, Thyrotoxicosis, Hepatic cirrhosis, Infection (sepsis), No thiamine (beriberi), Pregnancy and Paget, AV fistula, Bone marrow (myeloproliferative). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thiamine test dose**: the dramatic diuresis and fall in heart rate after IV thiamine are both diagnostic and therapeutic - and thiamine must precede glucose. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why low voltage and bradycardia in hypothyroid effusion** - pericardial fluid damps the signal while low thyroid hormone slows the sinus node; tamponade is rare because accumulation is slow. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why cardiovert only after euthyroid** - AF recurs while thyrotoxic, and two-thirds revert spontaneously once euthyroid. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Anthracycline versus trastuzumab** is a favourite 'differentiate' - dose-dependent and irreversible versus dose-independent and reversible. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Common mistake**: treating breathlessness in a malnourished alcoholic with diuretics alone - furosemide increases thiamine loss and can precipitate Shoshin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nicoladoni-Branham sign** (bradycardia on fistula compression) and **Means-Lerman scratch** (thyrotoxicosis) are classic viva eponyms. [Braunwald 12e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Causes of high-output heart failure`,columns:[`Cause`,`Mechanism`,`Clinical clue`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`Severe anaemia`,`Low viscosity, NO vasodilatation`,`Pallor, Hb under 7 g/dL, flow murmur`,`Treat cause; slow packed cell transfusion with furosemide`],[`Thyrotoxicosis`,`Direct inotropy, low SVR, volume expansion`,`Tremor, goitre, AF, weight loss`,`Beta-blocker, carbimazole, definitive therapy`],[`Wet beriberi`,`Thiamine deficiency, vasodilatation, lactic acidosis`,`Alcohol or polished rice diet, neuropathy, warm oedema`,`IV thiamine 100-200 mg TDS, before glucose`],[`AV fistula`,`Low-resistance arteriovenous shunt`,`Thrill, bruit, Nicoladoni-Branham sign`,`Banding or ligation of access`],[`Paget disease`,`Vascular bone acting as AV shunts`,`Bone pain, skull enlargement, high ALP`,`Bisphosphonate`],[`Cirrhosis / sepsis`,`Splanchnic and systemic vasodilatation`,`Liver stigmata; fever`,`Treat underlying disease`],[`Pregnancy`,`Volume and output rise`,`Third trimester or postpartum`,`See the Heart disease in pregnancy topic`]]},{heading:`Thyroid and the heart - hyper versus hypo`,columns:[`Feature`,`Thyrotoxicosis`,`Hypothyroidism`],rows:[[`Heart rate and rhythm`,`Sinus tachycardia, AF (10-15%)`,`Sinus bradycardia, long QT`],[`Blood pressure`,`Systolic hypertension, wide pulse pressure`,`Diastolic hypertension, narrow pulse pressure`],[`Contractility and output`,`Increased - high-output state`,`Reduced - low output`],[`Pericardium`,`Means-Lerman scratch (not pericardial)`,`Effusion common, tamponade rare`],[`Lipids and vessels`,`Low cholesterol`,`High LDL, accelerated atherosclerosis, high CK`],[`ECG`,`AF, tachycardia, LVH`,`Low voltage, bradycardia, flat T waves`],[`Treatment caution`,`Beta-blocker first; cardiovert when euthyroid`,`Start levothyroxine low (12.5-25 mcg) in elderly or CAD`]]},{heading:`Cancer therapies and their cardiac effects (ESC 2022)`,columns:[`Therapy`,`Main cardiotoxicity`,`Monitoring`,`Prevention / response`],rows:[[`Anthracyclines (doxorubicin)`,`Dose-dependent LV dysfunction (type I); high risk at 250 mg/m2 or more`,`Echo with GLS and troponin at baseline, during therapy and at 12 months`,`Dexrazoxane, liposomal form, ACEi/ARB + beta-blocker, statin in high risk`],[`Trastuzumab (HER2)`,`Reversible LV dysfunction (type II)`,`Echo every 3 months during therapy`,`Continue with cardioprotection if moderate; interrupt if LVEF under 40%`],[`Immune checkpoint inhibitors`,`Myocarditis (often with myositis), pericarditis`,`Baseline ECG, troponin; troponin early doses in high risk`,`Stop ICI, high-dose methylprednisolone`],[`Fluoropyrimidines (5-FU, capecitabine)`,`Coronary vasospasm, chest pain, MI`,`ECG during symptoms`,`Stop infusion, nitrate, CCB`],[`VEGF inhibitors / TKIs`,`Hypertension, HF, QT prolongation, arterial thrombosis`,`BP weekly early, ECG for QT`,`Antihypertensives; correct electrolytes`],[`Chest radiotherapy`,`Late CAD, valve disease, constriction, conduction block`,`Screening from 5 years after, lifelong`,`Minimise dose; risk factor control`]]},{heading:`Cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (CTRCD) - ESC 2022 definitions`,columns:[`Grade`,`Definition`,`Usual action (HER2 therapy example)`],rows:[[`Symptomatic CTRCD`,`Heart failure, graded mild to very severe`,`Interrupt therapy; HF GDMT; multidisciplinary decision`],[`Asymptomatic severe`,`New LVEF under 40%`,`Interrupt therapy; start HF therapy`],[`Asymptomatic moderate`,`LVEF fall of 10 points or more to 40-49%, or smaller fall to 40-49% with GLS fall over 15% or new biomarker rise`,`Continue with ACEi/ARB + beta-blocker; closer monitoring`],[`Asymptomatic mild`,`LVEF 50% or more with relative GLS fall over 15% and/or new troponin or NP rise`,`Continue; consider cardioprotection; repeat imaging`]]},{heading:`Drugs for the heart in systemic disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Thiamine`,`100 mg IV slowly (test dose), then 100-200 mg IV TDS`,`3-7 days IV, then 100 mg orally daily`,`Rare anaphylaxis; give before glucose`],[`Propranolol (thyrotoxicosis)`,`40-80 mg orally 3-4 times daily`,`Until euthyroid`,`Bronchospasm, bradycardia; caution in decompensated HF`],[`Carbimazole`,`10-15 mg orally three times daily initially`,`Titrate to TFT; 12-18 months`,`Agranulocytosis (sore throat - stop and check count), rash; avoid first trimester`],[`Levothyroxine`,`12.5-25 mcg daily in elderly/CAD; 1.6 mcg/kg/day in young`,`Increase every 4-6 weeks by TSH`,`Angina, AF if overtreated`],[`Ferric carboxymaltose`,`500-1000 mg IV per dose (by weight and Hb)`,`Repeat by ferritin and TSAT`,`Hypophosphataemia, hypersensitivity`],[`Furosemide (with transfusion)`,`20-40 mg IV`,`With each unit of packed cells as needed`,`Hypokalaemia, thiamine loss`],[`Pitavastatin (PLHIV)`,`4 mg orally once daily`,`Long-term`,`Myalgia; few ART interactions`],[`Methylprednisolone (ICI myocarditis)`,`500-1000 mg IV daily`,`3-5 days, then oral taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection`],[`Dexrazoxane (anthracycline cardioprotection)`,`10:1 ratio to doxorubicin dose IV before each dose`,`With anthracycline in high cumulative dose`,`Myelosuppression`]]},{heading:`The heart in connective tissue disease`,columns:[`Disease`,`Commonest cardiac lesion`,`Other lesions`,`Key point`],rows:[[`SLE`,`Pericarditis`,`Libman-Sacks endocarditis, myocarditis, premature CAD, PAH`,`Anti-Ro mothers - congenital heart block`],[`Antiphospholipid syndrome`,`Valve thickening and vegetations`,`Arterial and venous thrombosis, MI`,`Warfarin, not DOAC, in high-risk APS`],[`Rheumatoid arthritis`,`Premature CAD`,`Pericarditis, nodules, myocarditis, AA amyloid`,`Multiply CV risk score by 1.5`],[`Systemic sclerosis`,`Myocardial fibrosis`,`PAH, pericardial effusion, arrhythmia`,`Yearly PAH screening`],[`Ankylosing spondylitis`,`Aortitis with aortic regurgitation`,`Conduction block`,`Listen for AR`]]}],redFlags:[`**Alcohol-dependent or malnourished patient with warm peripheries, oedema, hypotension and lactic acidosis** - Shoshin beriberi; IV thiamine now.`,`**Thyroid storm** - fever, tachyarrhythmia, heart failure, confusion.`,`**Severe anaemia (Hb under 5-7 g/dL) with breathlessness at rest or HF.**`,`**New LVEF under 40% or HF symptoms during anthracycline or trastuzumab therapy.**`,`**Chest pain, dyspnoea, ptosis or myalgia within weeks of starting a checkpoint inhibitor** - ICI myocarditis.`,`**Hypothyroid patient with hypotension, hypothermia or drowsiness** - myxoedema coma.`,`**Pericardial rub or effusion in a dialysis patient** - uraemic pericarditis, risk of tamponade.`],pearls:[`**Check TSH in every new AF.**`,`**Thyrotoxic AF: beta-blocker plus carbimazole; cardiovert only when euthyroid; anticoagulate by CHA2DS2-VA.**`,`**Hypothyroid heart: bradycardia, diastolic hypertension, effusion, low voltage - start thyroxine low and go slow.**`,`**Warm hands + heart failure = high-output state.**`,`**Thiamine before glucose; response to thiamine is the diagnosis of wet beriberi.**`,`**Transfuse anaemic HF slowly with furosemide, packed cells only.**`,`**Anthracycline type I (dose-dependent, irreversible); trastuzumab type II (reversible).**`,`**CTRCD severe = LVEF under 40%; GLS fall over 15% is the early warning.**`,`**PLHIV 40-75 years: offer a statin (REPRIEVE).**`,`**Do not start a statin de novo in dialysis; continue if already on it.**`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-q1`,stem:`A 68-year-old woman presents with palpitations and weight loss. ECG shows AF at 140/min; TSH is under 0.01 mIU/L with high free T4. BP is normal and she is not in heart failure. The best initial plan is:`,options:[`Immediate DC cardioversion followed by carbimazole`,`Beta-blocker for rate control, carbimazole, and anticoagulation decided by CHA2DS2-VA`,`Amiodarone loading to restore sinus rhythm`,`Digoxin alone and recheck TSH in 6 months`],answer:1,explanation:`Thyrotoxic AF is rate-controlled with a beta-blocker (propranolol also reduces peripheral T4 to T3 conversion) while carbimazole restores euthyroidism; most patients revert to sinus within 8-12 weeks, and anticoagulation follows the usual CHA2DS2-VA score. Cardioversion before euthyroidism usually fails, amiodarone adds an iodine load that worsens thyrotoxicosis, and digoxin is less effective in the high-output thyrotoxic state.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-q2`,stem:`A 45-year-old man with heavy alcohol use for 15 years presents with breathlessness, pitting oedema, warm peripheries, BP 90/40 mmHg, pulse 124/min and serum lactate 8 mmol/L. Echo shows a hyperdynamic LV. Before starting IV dextrose, the most important step is:`,options:[`IV furosemide 80 mg`,`Start dobutamine infusion`,`IV thiamine 100 mg slowly, then 100-200 mg three times daily`,`Coronary angiography`],answer:2,explanation:`High-output failure with low SVR, warm peripheries and lactic acidosis in a drinker is Shoshin (fulminant wet) beriberi; IV thiamine given before glucose is both the diagnostic test and the cure, with diuresis and a fall in heart rate within hours. Furosemide alone increases urinary thiamine loss, dobutamine does not correct the metabolic defect, and angiography is irrelevant to a hyperdynamic LV with lactic acidosis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-q3`,stem:`A 48-year-old woman on adjuvant trastuzumab for HER2-positive breast cancer is asymptomatic. Her LVEF has fallen from 62% to 47%, with a 17% relative fall in global longitudinal strain. Per the ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline, the best management is:`,options:[`Stop trastuzumab permanently`,`Continue trastuzumab with no change in monitoring`,`Start dexrazoxane and continue`,`Continue trastuzumab, start an ACE inhibitor or ARB plus beta-blocker, and monitor more closely`],answer:3,explanation:`An LVEF fall of 10 points or more to 40-49% is asymptomatic moderate CTRCD; ESC 2022 recommends continuing HER2 therapy with ACEi/ARB plus beta-blocker and closer surveillance, because trastuzumab dysfunction is usually reversible and stopping it compromises cancer outcome. Interruption is advised for severe CTRCD (LVEF under 40%) or symptoms. Doing nothing ignores a significant fall, and dexrazoxane is an anthracycline cardioprotectant, not a treatment for HER2-related dysfunction.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-q4`,stem:`A 55-year-old woman with fatigue and weight gain has pulse 52/min, BP 150/100 mmHg and soft heart sounds. ECG shows low-voltage complexes and echo a large pericardial effusion without chamber collapse. TSH is 68 mIU/L. The most appropriate management is:`,options:[`Levothyroxine started at a low dose and titrated, with clinical and echo follow-up`,`Urgent pericardiocentesis`,`Pericardial window surgery`,`Anti-tuberculous therapy`],answer:0,explanation:`Hypothyroid pericardial effusion accumulates slowly, so even large effusions rarely cause tamponade, and the bradycardia and diastolic hypertension fit myxoedema rather than tuberculosis or tamponade (which causes tachycardia and hypotension). It resolves over weeks to months with levothyroxine, started low (12.5-25 mcg) in an older patient to avoid precipitating ischaemia. Pericardiocentesis or a window is needed only for tamponade, and there is no feature of TB.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-q5`,stem:`A 50-year-old man living with HIV, virologically suppressed on tenofovir-lamivudine-dolutegravir, has LDL 120 mg/dL and a low-to-moderate 10-year cardiovascular risk. Based on REPRIEVE, which is most appropriate?`,options:[`No statin until LDL exceeds 190 mg/dL`,`Start pitavastatin 4 mg daily`,`Switch ART to a boosted protease inhibitor regimen`,`Start simvastatin 40 mg with ritonavir boosting`],answer:1,explanation:`REPRIEVE showed that pitavastatin 4 mg reduced major adverse cardiovascular events by about a third in PLHIV aged 40-75 at low-to-moderate risk, so a statin is recommended; pitavastatin has minimal interaction with ART. Waiting for LDL over 190 ignores HIV as a risk enhancer, boosted protease inhibitors worsen lipids, and simvastatin with ritonavir is contraindicated because CYP3A4 inhibition causes myopathy and rhabdomyolysis.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Enumerate the causes of high-output cardiac failure. Discuss the pathophysiology, clinical features and management of high-output failure, with special reference to wet beriberi and severe anaemia.`,openingLines:[`High-output heart failure is heart failure with a cardiac output above normal (cardiac index over about 4 L/min/m2) and low systemic vascular resistance, in which neurohormonal activation causes congestion despite high flow.`,`In India the commonest causes are severe anaemia, thyrotoxicosis and AV fistulae, and wet beriberi is a curable cause that responds within hours to IV thiamine.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Anaemia, thyrotoxicosis, AV fistula, beriberi, Paget disease, pregnancy, cirrhosis, sepsis, obesity, myeloproliferative disease, hypercapnic lung disease (A THIN PAB).`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Low SVR from vasodilatation or shunting, low effective arterial volume, sympathetic and RAAS activation, salt and water retention, congestion with high output; prolonged load leads to ventricular dilatation.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Breathlessness, oedema, palpitations; warm extremities, bounding pulse, wide pulse pressure, hyperdynamic apex, flow murmur, S3, raised JVP.`,`Cause-specific: pallor (anaemia), goitre and AF (thyroid), bruit and Nicoladoni-Branham sign (fistula), neuropathy and alcohol history (beriberi).`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, iron studies, TFT, lactate, thiamine or transketolase, ECG, chest X-ray, echo (high cardiac index), fistula flow.`]},{heading:`Wet beriberi`,points:[`Thiamine is cofactor of pyruvate dehydrogenase; deficiency causes lactic acidosis and vasodilatation; types dry, wet, Shoshin.`,`Thiamine 100 mg IV slowly (test dose), then 100-200 mg IV TDS 3-7 days, then oral; before glucose; watch for LV failure as SVR rises; diet and alcohol counselling.`]},{heading:`Severe anaemia`,points:[`Packed red cells slowly (1 unit over 3-4 h) with IV furosemide 20-40 mg; treat cause (iron, B12, folate, worms, bleeding); IV iron in HFrEF with iron deficiency (ESC 2023).`]},{heading:`Other causes`,points:[`Thyrotoxicosis: beta-blocker, carbimazole; AV fistula: banding or ligation; Paget: bisphosphonate.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart of the neurohormonal cycle in high-output failure (low SVR - RAAS and sympathetic activation - salt retention - congestion)`,`Table of high-output causes with clues and specific treatment`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Pathophysiology`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and investigations`,marks:2},{part:`Wet beriberi management`,marks:2.5},{part:`Severe anaemia and other causes`,marks:2}],keywords:[`high-output failure`,`anaemia`,`thyrotoxicosis`,`AV fistula`,`beriberi`,`thiamine`,`Shoshin`]},{id:`cardiovascular-systemic-heart-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate anthracycline-induced from trastuzumab-induced cardiotoxicity, and outline monitoring as per the ESC 2022 cardio-oncology guideline.`,openingLines:[`Cancer therapy-related cardiac dysfunction (CTRCD) is defined by ESC 2022 using LVEF, global longitudinal strain and cardiac biomarkers, graded mild, moderate and severe, symptomatic or asymptomatic.`,`Anthracyclines cause type I (dose-dependent, irreversible) and trastuzumab type II (dose-independent, reversible) injury.`],answer:[{heading:`Differences`,points:[`Mechanism: topoisomerase II-beta and free radicals versus HER2 signalling blockade.`,`Dose: cumulative dose-dependent (high risk 250 mg/m2 or more doxorubicin) versus not dose-dependent.`,`Histology: myocyte necrosis and vacuolation versus no structural change.`,`Reversibility: largely irreversible, may appear years later versus usually reversible on interruption.`,`Rechallenge: not advised after severe injury versus often possible after recovery.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Baseline HFA-ICOS risk, ECG, echo with LVEF and GLS, troponin and NT-proBNP.`,`Anthracycline: echo and troponin during therapy by risk and at 12 months after completion.`,`Trastuzumab: echo every 3 months during therapy and at 12 months.`]},{heading:`Action`,points:[`Mild or moderate CTRCD: continue with ACEi/ARB plus beta-blocker; severe (LVEF under 40%) or symptomatic: interrupt and treat HF.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column comparison table: anthracycline versus trastuzumab cardiotoxicity`],markSplit:[{part:`Differences (table)`,marks:2.5},{part:`CTRCD definition and monitoring`,marks:1.5},{part:`Action by grade`,marks:1}],keywords:[`CTRCD`,`anthracycline`,`trastuzumab`,`GLS`,`HFA-ICOS`,`dexrazoxane`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Cardiac manifestations of systemic disease; Thiamine deficiency`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Endocrine disorders and the heart; High-output heart failure; Cardio-oncology`,`2022 ESC Guidelines on cardio-oncology (developed with EHA, ESTRO and IC-OS)`,`2023 Focused Update of the 2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation`,`KDIGO 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Evaluation and Management of Chronic Kidney Disease`,`Grinspoon SK et al. Pitavastatin to prevent cardiovascular disease in HIV infection (REPRIEVE), N Engl J Med 2023`,`EULAR 2022 recommendations for cardiovascular risk management in rheumatic and musculoskeletal diseases; NFHS-5 (2019-21) and Anemia Mukt Bharat, MoHFW`]});var l=[];l.push({id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing`,title:`Bradyarrhythmias, heart block and pacemakers`,oneLiner:`**Bradyarrhythmia** is a heart rate **under 50 bpm** (ESC 2021) from **sinus node dysfunction** or **atrioventricular (AV) block**; unstable bradycardia is treated with **atropine 1 mg IV** (to 3 mg) and then **transcutaneous pacing or an adrenaline/isoprenaline infusion** while reversible causes (drugs, hyperkalaemia, ischaemia) are corrected, and a **permanent pacemaker** is indicated for symptomatic sinus node dysfunction and for **Mobitz II, high-degree and complete heart block even without symptoms**.`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`bradycardia`,`sinus node dysfunction`,`sick sinus syndrome`,`tachy-brady syndrome`,`first-degree AV block`,`Wenckebach`,`Mobitz I`,`Mobitz II`,`complete heart block`,`third-degree AV block`,`Stokes-Adams attack`,`atropine`,`isoprenaline`,`transcutaneous pacing`,`temporary pacing wire`,`permanent pacemaker`,`DDD VVI pacing mode`,`leadless pacemaker`,`left bundle branch area pacing`,`pacemaker syndrome`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Bradycardia** is a heart rate **under 50 bpm** in the ESC 2021 pacing guideline (the older ACC/AHA/HRS 2018 convention uses under 60 bpm); it matters only when it causes symptoms or reflects conduction disease that can progress. [ESC 2021]`,`**Sinus node dysfunction (SND, sick sinus syndrome)** includes persistent sinus bradycardia, **sinus pauses or arrest**, sinoatrial exit block, **chronotropic incompetence** and the **tachycardia-bradycardia syndrome**. [ESC 2021]`,`**AV block** is delayed or failed conduction from atria to ventricles, graded as **first-degree** (PR over 200 ms), **second-degree** (Mobitz I, Mobitz II, 2:1, high-grade) and **third-degree (complete) heart block**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SND incidence** is about **0.8 per 1000 person-years**, rising steeply after 60 years; most patients are in their 70s. [ARIC cohort, Harrison 22e]`,`**First-degree AV block** is present in about **1-2% of adults** and becomes commoner with age; isolated, it is usually benign. [Framingham, Harrison 22e]`,`About **one million pacemakers** are implanted worldwide each year; **SND is the leading indication in Europe**, whereas **AV block dominates in India and much of Asia**. [World Survey of Cardiac Pacing]`,`In India per-capita implant rates remain a small fraction of European rates, and many elderly patients with complete heart block present late with **falls, syncope or heart failure** - a detection gap rather than a lower incidence. [CSI, World Survey of Cardiac Pacing]`,`Bradycardia is a common **drug side effect** in the NCD clinic, because beta-blockers, diltiazem, digoxin and ivabradine are prescribed to the same elderly patients with hypertension, angina, AF and heart failure. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`Causes are **intrinsic** (disease of the node or conduction system) or **extrinsic** (drugs, autonomic, metabolic); extrinsic causes are reversible and must be excluded before a pacemaker. [ESC 2021]`,`**Idiopathic degenerative fibrosis** is the commonest chronic cause - **Lenegre disease** (proximal fibrosis in younger patients) and **Lev disease** (calcific sclerosis of the cardiac skeleton in the elderly). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ischaemia**: the **right coronary artery supplies the AV node in about 90%**, so **inferior MI causes nodal, transient block**, whereas **anterior MI causes infranodal block** from septal necrosis with a far worse prognosis. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Drugs** are the commonest reversible cause: **beta-blockers (including timolol eye drops), verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, amiodarone, ivabradine, clonidine, lithium and cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil)**; beta-blocker plus verapamil is especially dangerous. [ESC 2021]`,`**Hyperkalaemia** (K over 6.5 mmol/L) causes sinoatrial block, AV block and wide QRS; with AV nodal blockers and renal failure it produces the self-amplifying **BRASH syndrome** (bradycardia, renal failure, AV nodal blockade, shock, hyperkalaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Metabolic and autonomic**: **hypothyroidism, hypothermia, raised intracranial pressure (Cushing reflex), hypoxia, obstructive jaundice, obstructive sleep apnoea**, vasovagal and carotid sinus reflexes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infection and inflammation in India**: **diphtheritic myocarditis** in unimmunised children, **acute rheumatic fever** (PR prolongation), **infective endocarditis with aortic root abscess**, viral and scrub typhus myocarditis; **Lyme carditis and Chagas disease are not endemic in India**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Infiltrative and genetic**: **cardiac sarcoidosis** (suspect in AV block under 60 years), amyloidosis, haemochromatosis, SLE, systemic sclerosis, myotonic dystrophy, and **SCN5A or LMNA** mutations (LMNA also causes DCM and sudden death). [ESC 2021]`,`**Iatrogenic and congenital**: block after **TAVI**, valve surgery, septal reduction or AV nodal ablation; **congenital complete heart block** from maternal **anti-Ro/SSA antibodies** (neonatal lupus). [ESC 2021]`,`**Poisonings seen in Indian emergency rooms**: **organophosphates** (muscarinic bradycardia), **yellow oleander (Thevetia) and Cerbera odollam seeds** (cardiac glycosides), and beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and mechanism`,points:[`The **sinoatrial node** lies at the SVC-right atrial junction (sulcus terminalis), supplied by the **RCA in about 60% and the circumflex in about 40%**; its rhythm comes from the **funny current (If) through HCN4 channels**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acetylcholine** acting on **M2 receptors** opens IKACh and lowers cAMP to slow the node, while **noradrenaline on beta-1 receptors** increases If and L-type calcium current - the basis for **atropine** and **isoprenaline**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`The **AV node** conducts slowly through calcium-dependent tissue, is **decremental** and is richly vagally innervated, whereas the **His-Purkinje system** conducts fast through sodium channels and is **barely influenced by the vagus** - why atropine helps nodal but not infranodal block. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mobitz I** is decremental fatigue of the AV node (each beat conducts more slowly until one fails), whereas **Mobitz II** is sudden all-or-none failure in diseased His-Purkinje tissue, so it heralds complete block. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Escape hierarchy**: a **junctional escape (40-60 bpm, narrow QRS)** is stable, whereas a **ventricular escape (20-40 bpm, wide QRS)** is slow and unreliable and may stop, causing **Stokes-Adams attacks or asystole**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiac output = heart rate x stroke volume**; the slow heart compensates with a large stroke volume, producing a **wide pulse pressure and a systolic flow murmur**, but loss of AV synchrony removes the **atrial kick (15-30% of filling)**, more so in LVH. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Slow rates **prolong the QT** and pauses trigger early afterdepolarisations, so complete heart block can present as **pause-dependent torsades de pointes**. [ESC 2021]`,`In **inferior MI**, block arises from the vagal **Bezold-Jarisch reflex** and nodal ischaemia with local **adenosine release** (which explains atropine-resistant block that may respond to aminophylline). [Braunwald 12e]`,`In **tachy-brady syndrome**, termination of AF is followed by a long pause because **overdrive suppression** of a diseased sinus node delays its recovery, causing syncope on conversion. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Classification and ECG - the one examiners ask`,points:[`**First-degree AV block**: PR over 200 ms with every P conducted; usually nodal and benign, but **PR over 300 ms** can cause pseudo-pacemaker syndrome. [ESC 2021]`,`**Mobitz I (Wenckebach)**: **progressive PR lengthening until one P fails to conduct**; the PR after the pause is the shortest, RR intervals shorten before the drop, and the pause is less than twice the PP interval (group beating). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mobitz II**: **constant PR** with a sudden non-conducted P, usually with **bundle branch block**; infranodal, unpredictable and high risk of progression - it needs pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`**2:1 AV block** cannot be labelled Mobitz I or II from one strip: **narrow QRS with long PR suggests nodal**, **wide QRS with normal PR suggests infranodal**; improvement with exercise or atropine suggests nodal. [ESC 2021]`,`**High-grade (advanced) AV block**: **two or more consecutive P waves fail to conduct** (3:1, 4:1) but some conduction persists; treated like complete block. [ESC 2021]`,`**Third-degree (complete) block**: **no P wave conducts**; P waves march through at their own rate, **atrial rate exceeds ventricular rate**, PR varies randomly and an escape rhythm drives the ventricles. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AV dissociation is not the same as complete block**: in accelerated junctional rhythm or isorhythmic dissociation the ventricular rate equals or exceeds the atrial rate and conduction is intact. [Harrison 22e]`,`**SND patterns**: sinus bradycardia, **sinus arrest (pause over 3 s while awake is significant)**, sinoatrial exit block (pause an exact multiple of PP), chronotropic incompetence and tachy-brady syndrome. [ESC 2021]`,`**Bundle branch disease**: bifascicular block is RBBB with left anterior or left posterior fascicular block; **alternating bundle branch block** means imminent complete block and is a Class I pacing indication. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history in the OPD`,points:[`Symptoms come from cerebral hypoperfusion and low output: **dizziness, presyncope, syncope, fatigue, breathlessness, exercise intolerance, angina, confusion and worsening heart failure**. [ESC 2021]`,`A **Stokes-Adams attack** is sudden loss of consciousness without warning, pallor during the attack and **flushing on recovery**, sometimes with brief jerks - often mislabelled as epilepsy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chronotropic incompetence** presents as effort fatigue and breathlessness with a normal resting ECG; ask about reduced walking distance in the elderly. [ESC 2021]`,`Take a **complete drug history**: beta-blockers (including **eye drops**), diltiazem, verapamil, digoxin, amiodarone, ivabradine, donepezil, lithium, recent dose increases and any **seed or pesticide ingestion**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Palpitations followed by dizziness or syncope as they stop** point to **tachy-brady syndrome** with post-conversion pauses. [ESC 2021]`,`**Context of syncope**: on exertion suggests infranodal block; on **neck turning, shaving or a tight collar** suggests carotid sinus syndrome; nocturnal slow rates with snoring suggest sleep apnoea. [ESC 2018 Syncope]`,`Ask for clues to a cause: **chest pain** (MI), recent **TAVI or valve surgery**, **fever** (endocarditis, rheumatic fever, diphtheria in a child), rash or arthritis (SLE, sarcoid), muscle weakness (myotonic dystrophy), cold intolerance (hypothyroidism). [ESC 2021]`,`Ask about **family history** of pacemakers, cardiomyopathy or sudden death under 50 years, which suggests **genetic conduction disease** (SCN5A, LMNA). [ESC 2021]`,`In the elderly the presentation is often **atypical** - recurrent falls, fractures, cognitive decline or simply weakness - so check the pulse and ECG in every faller. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Pulse**: slow and regular in complete or 2:1 block; **regularly irregular with group beating in Wenckebach**; a pulse rate half the ECG rate suggests non-perfused ventricular bigeminy (pseudo-bradycardia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Irregular (intermittent) cannon a waves** in the JVP mean the atrium is contracting against a closed tricuspid valve at random - the bedside sign of **AV dissociation in complete heart block**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Variable intensity of the first heart sound** (bruit de canon) occurs because the PR interval varies beat to beat in complete block. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Large-volume pulse, wide pulse pressure and an ejection systolic flow murmur** reflect the large compensatory stroke volume. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Check **BP, postural BP, perfusion, crepitations and raised JVP** to grade haemodynamic compromise. [ESC 2021]`,`Look for causes: **myxoedema**, hypothermia, **miosis, salivation and fasciculations** (organophosphate), **hypertension with papilloedema** (raised intracranial pressure, Cushing reflex), endocarditis stigmata, myotonia with frontal balding and cataract. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Carotid sinus massage** (10 s each side, supine then upright, with ECG and BP monitoring) is positive with a **pause of 3 s or more and/or systolic fall over 50 mmHg** reproducing symptoms; avoid with a carotid bruit or TIA/stroke in the last 3 months. [ESC 2018 Syncope]`,`In a **paced patient** inspect the pocket for **redness, swelling or erosion** (device infection), arm swelling (venous thrombosis), hiccups or muscle twitching (diaphragmatic or pectoral stimulation) and cannon waves with hypotension (pacemaker syndrome). [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Physiological sinus bradycardia** (athletes, sleep) versus SND: the athlete's rate rises normally on exercise and nocturnal Mobitz I disappears with activity. [ESC 2021]`,`**Blocked premature atrial beats** mimic sinus pauses or Mobitz II - look for an early P wave deforming the preceding T wave. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Mobitz I versus Mobitz II**: compare the PR before and after the dropped beat - it shortens after the pause in Mobitz I and is unchanged in Mobitz II. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Isorhythmic AV dissociation and accelerated junctional rhythm** versus complete block: in dissociation the ventricular rate is equal to or faster than the atrial rate. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AF with a regular slow ventricular rate** is not controlled AF - it is **complete heart block with a junctional escape**, classically from **digoxin toxicity**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Seizure versus Stokes-Adams**: prolonged tonic-clonic activity, tongue biting and post-ictal confusion favour epilepsy; abrupt onset, pallor, quick recovery with flushing favour bradyarrhythmia. [ESC 2018 Syncope]`,`**Reflex (vasovagal or carotid sinus) syncope** has prodrome and situational triggers and a normal baseline ECG, whereas intrinsic conduction disease shows bundle branch block or AV block on the resting ECG. [ESC 2018 Syncope]`,`**Hypothermia** produces sinus bradycardia with **Osborn (J) waves** and resolves with rewarming - do not pace a cold heart. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG with a long lead II rhythm strip** is the key test: map every P to its QRS, measure PR and QRS width, look for bundle branch block and measure the **QT**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Bloods**: potassium, magnesium, calcium, creatinine (accumulation of digoxin, sotalol, atenolol), **TSH**, glucose, troponin if ischaemia, **serum digoxin** if on it, and **cholinesterase** if organophosphate poisoning is suspected. [ESC 2021]`,`**Ambulatory ECG** is matched to symptom frequency: **24-48 h Holter** for daily symptoms, external loop or patch recorder for weekly symptoms and an **implantable loop recorder** for infrequent unexplained syncope. [ESC 2021]`,`**Exercise ECG** tests chronotropic incompetence (failure to reach 80% of heart-rate reserve) and unmasks **exercise-induced AV block**, which is infranodal and an indication to pace. [ESC 2021]`,`**Echocardiography** is recommended before pacing to assess LVEF (choice of RV, conduction system or biventricular pacing), valves, infiltration and congenital disease. [ESC 2021]`,`**Cardiac MRI or FDG-PET** should be considered in **unexplained AV block before 60 years** to detect sarcoidosis, myocarditis or other infiltrative disease. [ESC 2021]`,`**Electrophysiology study** is used in syncope with bundle branch block: an **HV interval of 70 ms or more**, or infranodal block on pacing or drug challenge, is a Class I pacing indication. [ESC 2021]`,`**Genetic testing** should be considered in **early-onset (under 50 years) progressive conduction disease** or a family history of it, looking for SCN5A and LMNA variants. [ESC 2021]`,`A **sleep study** is indicated when bradycardia or pauses occur only at night with snoring, because treating sleep apnoea abolishes them. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD and primary care`,points:[`Decide two things first: **is the patient symptomatic** and **is the block nodal or infranodal**; asymptomatic sinus bradycardia, first-degree block and Mobitz I need **no treatment**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Stop or reduce offending drugs** - beta-blockers, diltiazem, verapamil, digoxin, ivabradine, donepezil - and recheck the ECG; drug-induced block often resolves within 24-72 h. [ESC 2021]`,`If the drug is essential (beta-blocker in heart failure or angina, rate control in AF), **pacing to permit the drug** is the correct answer rather than withdrawing life-saving therapy. [ESC 2021]`,`Correct **hyperkalaemia**, treat **hypothyroidism** with levothyroxine (start **12.5-25 micrograms daily** in the elderly or those with ischaemic heart disease) and treat sleep apnoea with **CPAP**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Mobitz II, 2:1 with wide QRS, high-grade or complete block** found in the OPD - even if asymptomatic - needs **same-day referral** by ambulance with monitoring; never add an AV nodal blocking drug. [ESC 2021]`,`There is **no long-term oral drug** for symptomatic bradycardia; theophylline or salbutamol tablets are not substitutes for pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`Before transfer: **IV access, cardiac monitor, defibrillator with pacing pads if available, atropine drawn up**, and a referral note with the ECG. [ERC 2021]`,`Advise patients with syncope **not to drive** until the cause is treated, and explain that pacemaker implantation is a short procedure under local anaesthesia, covered under **PM-JAY**. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Management in IPD and emergency`,points:[`Assess **ABCDE**, attach monitor, obtain IV access and a 12-lead ECG, and look for **adverse features**: shock (systolic under 90 mmHg), syncope, myocardial ischaemia, heart failure or altered sensorium. [AHA 2025 / ERC 2021]`,`**Atropine 1 mg IV bolus**, repeated every **3-5 minutes to a maximum of 3 mg** (AHA 2025); ERC uses 500 micrograms repeated to 3 mg - it acts in **nodal block** and is ineffective in **infranodal block or a transplanted (denervated) heart**. [AHA 2025]`,`If atropine fails: **transcutaneous pacing** and/or an infusion of **adrenaline 2-10 micrograms/min**, **dopamine 5-20 micrograms/kg/min** or **isoprenaline 5 micrograms/min** (range 2-10), as a bridge to transvenous or permanent pacing. [AHA 2025 / ERC 2021]`,`**Transcutaneous pacing**: anterior-posterior pads, demand mode at **60-70 bpm**, increase current until **electrical capture** (wide QRS and T wave after every spike), confirm **mechanical capture by the femoral pulse**, and give analgesia and sedation. [ERC 2021]`,`**Temporary transvenous pacing** is for persistent compromise refractory to drugs; insert it via the **right internal jugular or femoral vein** to keep the left subclavian free for the permanent system, and avoid it where possible because of infection risk. [ESC 2021]`,`**Hyperkalaemia with bradycardia**: **10% calcium gluconate 10-30 mL IV over 5-10 minutes** (repeat if ECG unchanged), then insulin-dextrose and removal of potassium. [KDIGO / Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-blocker overdose**: **glucagon 5-10 mg IV** then an infusion, plus **high-dose insulin euglycaemic therapy**; **calcium channel blocker overdose**: IV calcium plus high-dose insulin; **digoxin or oleander poisoning**: atropine, correct potassium and **digoxin-specific Fab** where available. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inferior MI with AV block** is usually nodal and transient - give atropine and proceed to **primary PCI**; **anterior MI with high-degree block** usually needs temporary pacing. [ESC 2023 ACS]`,`**Timing of the permanent pacemaker**: post-MI block that does not resolve after **at least 5 days**; after cardiac surgery an observation period of about **5 days**; after TAVI, **persistent high-degree or complete block for 24-48 h**. [ESC 2021]`,`**Admit with monitoring**: Mobitz II, high-grade or complete block, symptomatic SND with syncope, pauses over 3 s while awake, and bradycardia from hyperkalaemia or poisoning. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Permanent pacing: indications, modes and devices`,points:[`**SND**: pace when symptoms are **clearly attributable** to bradycardia (Class I), and in tachy-brady syndrome to allow rate-control drugs or when ablation is not chosen. [ESC 2021]`,`**AV block**: **Mobitz II, infranodal 2:1, high-grade and complete heart block** in sinus rhythm are paced **irrespective of symptoms** (Class I); symptomatic Mobitz I or Mobitz I that is infranodal at EP study should be considered for pacing (IIa). [ESC 2021]`,`**Bundle branch block with unexplained syncope**: pace if **HV 70 ms or more** or infranodal block is shown at EP study (Class I); **alternating bundle branch block** is Class I even without symptoms. [ESC 2021]`,`**Reflex syncope**: dual-chamber pacing should be considered in patients **over 40 years** with recurrent, unpredictable syncope and documented symptomatic asystolic pauses (cardioinhibitory). [ESC 2021]`,`**NBG pacing code**: first letter **chamber paced**, second **chamber sensed**, third **response to sensing** (I = inhibit, T = trigger, D = dual), fourth **R = rate response**; e.g. **DDD** for AV block in sinus rhythm, **VVIR** for permanent AF with slow rate. [NASPE/BPEG]`,`**Mode choice**: **dual-chamber pacing with minimal ventricular pacing** for SND (Class I); dual-chamber pacing is preferred over single-chamber ventricular pacing in AV block with sinus rhythm to avoid pacemaker syndrome. [ESC 2021]`,`**HFrEF needing ventricular pacing**: **CRT rather than RV pacing** in patients with reduced EF (under 40%) and high-degree AV block, regardless of NYHA class. [ESC 2021]`,`**Conduction system pacing** (His bundle or **left bundle branch area pacing**) gives a narrow paced QRS and avoids RV pacing-induced dyssynchrony; it is reasonable when **LVEF is 36-50% and ventricular pacing over 40%** is expected. [HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2023]`,`**Leadless pacemakers** (implanted in the RV via the femoral vein) should be considered when there is **no upper-limb venous access** or a **high infection risk** (haemodialysis, prior device infection); dual-chamber leadless systems became available in 2023. [ESC 2021]`,`**Peri-procedural**: **cefazolin 1-2 g IV within 1 hour** before implant (Class I); continue warfarin uninterrupted rather than bridging with heparin (less pocket haematoma). [ESC 2021, BRUISE CONTROL]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: congenital complete block with a narrow escape is usually tolerated; if pacing is needed, implant with **echo guidance and minimal fluoroscopy**, preferably **after 8 weeks**. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy]`,`**Neonatal lupus**: maternal **anti-Ro/SSA** antibodies cause fetal complete block, usually at **16-24 weeks**; **hydroxychloroquine** in a subsequent pregnancy roughly halves the recurrence rate. [PATCH 2020]`,`**Elderly**: drugs are the commonest cause; review every AV nodal blocker, donepezil and eye drops, and discuss frailty and goals of care before a device. [ESC 2021]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: watch for **BRASH** (hyperkalaemia plus AV nodal blockers); prefer **leadless or epicardial** systems to protect veins needed for AV fistulae. [ESC 2021]`,`**Children**: congenital complete block is paced when **symptomatic**, with **ventricular rate under 50-55 bpm in infants**, wide QRS escape, long QTc or ventricular dysfunction; **diphtheritic myocarditis** with complete block carries high mortality and needs temporary pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`**Athletes**: resting rates in the 40s and nocturnal Mobitz I are physiological and disappear on exercise - no restriction or pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`**Heart transplant recipients**: the heart is denervated, so **atropine is ineffective** and may provoke block; use isoprenaline or theophylline and halve the adenosine dose. [ESC 2021]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: without pacing pads, run **adrenaline 1 mg in 500 mL saline (2 micrograms/mL) at 1-5 mL/min** (2-10 micrograms/min) during transfer to a centre with pacing. [AHA 2025]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and prevention`,points:[`Untreated symptomatic complete heart block has a poor outlook - in historical series about **half of patients died within a year** - and pacing restores near-normal survival when the heart is otherwise healthy. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pacing improves survival in AV block**, but in **SND it relieves symptoms without prolonging life**. [ESC 2021]`,`Complications of bradycardia itself: **syncope with injury, falls and fractures, heart failure, angina, pause-dependent torsades** and sudden asystolic death. [ESC 2021]`,`**Tachy-brady syndrome** carries a high risk of **AF-related stroke**; anticoagulate by the CHA2DS2-VA score (see the Atrial fibrillation topic). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Early pacing complications**: pneumothorax, pocket haematoma, **lead dislodgement (mostly in the first weeks)**, cardiac perforation and tamponade. [ESC 2021]`,`**Late complications**: **device infection** (higher at generator replacement), erosion, venous occlusion, lead fracture, tricuspid regurgitation and **twiddler syndrome** (patient rotates the generator, retracting the leads). [ESC 2021]`,`**Pacing-induced cardiomyopathy** develops in about **10-20%** of patients with a high RV pacing burden (over 20-40%), and responds to upgrade to CRT or conduction system pacing. [HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2023]`,`**Prevent drug-induced bradycardia**: avoid beta-blocker plus verapamil or diltiazem, dose digoxin by renal function, and check potassium after starting RAS blockers or spironolactone in NP-NCD clinics. [ESC 2021]`,`**Diphtheria immunisation** under the **Universal Immunization Programme** (pentavalent at 6, 10, 14 weeks, DPT boosters at 16-24 months and 5 years, Td at 10 and 16 years) prevents diphtheritic heart block. [UIP, MoHFW]`,`Check the **pulse and ECG in every elderly faller** and in patients with unexplained syncope at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir before labelling them as giddiness or epilepsy. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and pacemaker problems`,points:[`**Wound review at 7-10 days**; keep the wound dry and avoid lifting the arm above the shoulder or heavy lifting on the implant side for about **4-6 weeks** (lead dislodgement). [ESC 2021]`,`**Device checks**: before discharge, at **2-12 weeks** after implant, then every **6-12 months** (pacemaker) or **3-6 months** (ICD or CRT) in person, or remotely with a yearly in-person visit. [HRS 2015 consensus, ESC 2021]`,`**Battery** lasts about **8-12 years**; the **elective replacement indicator** (fall in magnet rate) gives about 3 months to change the generator. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Failure to capture** (spike with no QRS) suggests lead dislodgement, raised threshold (hyperkalaemia, ischaemia, flecainide) or fracture; **failure to output** (no spike when expected) suggests battery depletion, lead fracture or **oversensing** (myopotentials, electromagnetic interference). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Undersensing** produces spikes falling on native QRS or T waves (risk of R-on-T); it needs reprogramming of sensitivity. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Pacemaker syndrome**: single-chamber ventricular pacing with retrograde VA conduction causes **cannon waves, hypotension, dizziness and fatigue** - upgrade to dual-chamber pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`**Pacemaker-mediated (endless-loop) tachycardia** in DDD mode: retrograde P waves are tracked at the upper rate; **a magnet terminates it**, then the atrial refractory period (PVARP) is reprogrammed. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Magnet response**: on a pacemaker it forces **asynchronous pacing** (VOO/DOO); on an **ICD it suspends shocks only** and does not change pacing. [ESC 2021]`,`**Pocket infection or Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia** in a device patient needs blood cultures, TOE and **complete system extraction** - antibiotics alone or incising the pocket fail. [EHRA 2020, ESC 2021]`,`**Daily life**: keep mobile phones **15 cm** from the device (opposite ear), walk normally through security gates, avoid arc welding, carry the device card, and have **MRI only with an MR-conditional system** under protocol. [ESC 2021]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2021-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2021 pacing and CRT guideline**: bradycardia defined as under 50 bpm; pace Mobitz II, high-grade and complete block irrespective of symptoms; **CRT over RV pacing in HFrEF with AV block**; leadless pacing when venous access is poor or infection risk high. [ESC 2021]`,`ESC 2021 also introduced **imaging for unexplained AV block under 60 years**, **genetic testing in early-onset conduction disease**, defined waiting periods after MI (5 days), cardiac surgery (5 days) and TAVI (24-48 h). [ESC 2021]`,`**2023 HRS/APHRS/LAHRS physiologic pacing guideline**: conduction system or biventricular pacing is reasonable when LVEF is 36-50% and ventricular pacing over 40% is expected; **left bundle branch area pacing** is an alternative when CRT fails or is not possible. [HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2023]`,`**AHA 2025 adult advanced life support** retains **atropine 1 mg every 3-5 minutes (maximum 3 mg)**, followed by transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline or dopamine infusion. [AHA 2025]`,`**Dual-chamber leadless pacing** (two communicating leadless devices in RA and RV) received regulatory approval in 2023, extending leadless therapy to AV block in sinus rhythm. [FDA 2023]`,`**ESC 2024 AF guideline** uses **CHA2DS2-VA** (sex removed) for anticoagulation decisions in tachy-brady syndrome with AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **draw Mobitz I, Mobitz II and complete block**, list **ESC 2021 permanent pacing indications**, explain the **NBG code**, and give the **bradycardia algorithm with atropine dose**. [ESC 2021]`,`Common mistake: calling **2:1 block Mobitz II** - it cannot be classified from the strip; QRS width and response to atropine or exercise decide the level. [ESC 2021]`,`Common mistake: **pacing a drug- or potassium-induced block** without first stopping the drug and correcting potassium. [ESC 2021]`,`Remember **atropine works above the His bundle**: it may worsen Mobitz II or wide-QRS complete block by increasing atrial rate into a diseased conduction system. [Harrison 22e]`,`A **paced QRS from the RV apex has a LBBB pattern with left axis**; a **RBBB-pattern paced QRS** suggests the lead is in the LV or has perforated the septum (unless deliberate left bundle branch area pacing). [Braunwald 12e]`,`Bedside triad of complete block for the clinical exam: **slow regular pulse, irregular cannon a waves and variable first heart sound**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Viva: **regular slow ventricular rhythm in a patient with AF on digoxin** means complete heart block from digoxin toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs used in bradycardia and its toxic causes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Atropine`,`1 mg IV bolus (AHA 2025); ERC uses 0.5 mg`,`Repeat every 3-5 min to a maximum of 3 mg`,`Ineffective in infranodal block and transplanted heart; tachycardia, ischaemia, urinary retention, glaucoma`],[`Adrenaline infusion`,`2-10 micrograms/min IV`,`Titrate to heart rate and BP; bridge to pacing`,`Tachyarrhythmia, ischaemia, hypertension; give via large vein`],[`Isoprenaline infusion`,`5 micrograms/min IV (range 2-10)`,`Titrate to rate; bridge to pacing`,`Vasodilatation and hypotension, ischaemia, ventricular arrhythmia; avoid in acute ischaemia`],[`Dopamine infusion`,`5-20 micrograms/kg/min IV`,`Titrate; bridge to pacing`,`Tachyarrhythmia, extravasation necrosis`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10-30 mL IV over 5-10 min`,`Repeat if ECG unchanged; then insulin-dextrose`,`Tissue necrosis if extravasated; caution in digoxin toxicity`],[`Glucagon (beta-blocker overdose)`,`5-10 mg IV over 1-2 min, then infusion`,`Until haemodynamic recovery`,`Vomiting, hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia`],[`Atropine (organophosphate poisoning)`,`1.8-3 mg IV, doubling every 5 min`,`Until atropinised (clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80), then infusion`,`Delirium, hyperthermia, ileus from over-atropinisation`],[`Digoxin-specific Fab`,`Dose from ingested amount or level (one 40 mg vial binds about 0.5 mg digoxin)`,`Single dose, repeat if toxicity recurs`,`Hypokalaemia, recurrence of AF or heart failure; scarce and costly in India`]]},{heading:`Classification of AV block`,columns:[`Type`,`ECG`,`Usual level`,`Atropine or exercise`,`Pacing`],rows:[[`First-degree`,`PR over 200 ms, all P waves conducted`,`AV node`,`PR shortens`,`Only if symptomatic with very long PR (over 300 ms)`],[`Mobitz I (Wenckebach)`,`Progressive PR prolongation then a dropped beat; group beating`,`AV node`,`Improves`,`Only if symptomatic or infranodal at EP study`],[`Mobitz II`,`Constant PR with sudden dropped beat; often BBB`,`His-Purkinje`,`No help; may worsen`,`Yes, irrespective of symptoms`],[`2:1 block`,`Alternate P waves conducted`,`Either - narrow QRS nodal, wide QRS infranodal`,`Nodal improves, infranodal worsens`,`If infranodal or symptomatic`],[`High-grade (advanced)`,`Two or more consecutive non-conducted P waves`,`Usually infranodal`,`Little help`,`Yes`],[`Complete (third-degree)`,`AV dissociation, atrial rate faster than ventricular`,`Node (narrow escape 40-60) or infranodal (wide escape 20-40)`,`Narrow escape may rise slightly`,`Yes, irrespective of symptoms (unless reversible)`]]},{heading:`Permanent pacing indications (ESC 2021)`,columns:[`Situation`,`Recommendation`,`Class`],rows:[[`SND with symptoms clearly due to bradycardia`,`Pace; dual-chamber with minimal ventricular pacing`,`I`],[`Mobitz II, infranodal 2:1, high-grade or complete AV block in sinus rhythm`,`Pace irrespective of symptoms`,`I`],[`Permanent AF with symptomatic slow ventricular rate`,`Single-chamber ventricular pacing (VVIR)`,`I`],[`Syncope with BBB and HV 70 ms or more or infranodal block at EP study`,`Pace`,`I`],[`Alternating bundle branch block`,`Pace even without symptoms`,`I`],[`HFrEF (EF under 40%) needing ventricular pacing for AV block`,`CRT rather than RV pacing`,`I`],[`Post-MI AV block not resolving`,`Pace after waiting at least 5 days`,`I`],[`Recurrent cardioinhibitory reflex syncope, age over 40, documented pauses`,`Dual-chamber pacing`,`IIa`],[`Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia, first-degree block or Mobitz I`,`Do not pace`,`III`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a slow pulse`,columns:[`Condition`,`Clue`,`Discriminating test`],rows:[[`Athlete's heart`,`Young, fit, asymptomatic, HR 40s`,`Normal chronotropic response on exercise`],[`Drug-induced bradycardia`,`Beta-blocker, diltiazem, digoxin, ivabradine, donepezil`,`Resolves within 24-72 h of stopping drug`],[`Hyperkalaemia`,`CKD, ACE inhibitor or spironolactone; peaked T, wide QRS`,`Serum potassium`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Weight gain, cold intolerance, low-voltage ECG`,`TSH`],[`Blocked premature atrial beats`,`Pause preceded by early P in T wave`,`Careful look at T-wave morphology`],[`Ventricular bigeminy (pseudo-bradycardia)`,`Pulse half the apex or ECG rate`,`ECG shows coupled ectopics`],[`Organophosphate poisoning`,`Miosis, salivation, fasciculations, pesticide history`,`Plasma or RBC cholinesterase; response to atropine`],[`Raised intracranial pressure`,`Headache, vomiting, hypertension, papilloedema`,`CT brain`]]},{heading:`Pacemaker problems at follow-up`,columns:[`Problem`,`ECG or clinical picture`,`Common causes`,`Action`],rows:[[`Failure to capture`,`Pacing spike not followed by QRS`,`Lead dislodgement, raised threshold (hyperkalaemia, ischaemia), lead fracture`,`Chest X-ray, electrolytes, device check; increase output or reposition`],[`Failure to output`,`No spike when the rate falls below the lower rate`,`Battery depletion, lead fracture, oversensing`,`Magnet to force pacing; urgent device check`],[`Undersensing`,`Spikes on native QRS or T waves`,`Low intrinsic signal, lead problem`,`Reprogram sensitivity`],[`Oversensing`,`Pauses with no spikes`,`Myopotentials, electromagnetic interference, T-wave sensing`,`Reprogram; avoid source`],[`Pacemaker syndrome`,`Dizziness, hypotension, cannon waves during VVI pacing`,`Loss of AV synchrony, retrograde VA conduction`,`Upgrade to dual-chamber`],[`Pacemaker-mediated tachycardia`,`Paced tachycardia at upper tracking rate`,`Retrograde P waves tracked in DDD`,`Magnet to stop; lengthen PVARP`],[`Pocket infection or erosion`,`Redness, swelling, discharge, exposed device, fever`,`Staphylococci at implant or replacement`,`Blood cultures, TOE, complete extraction`]]}],redFlags:[`Syncope, presyncope or falls with any bradycardia or AV block on ECG`,`Mobitz II, 2:1 with wide QRS, high-grade or complete heart block - even if asymptomatic`,`Bradycardia with systolic BP under 90 mmHg, shock, chest pain, heart failure or confusion`,`Pauses over 3 seconds while awake or ventricular rate under 40 bpm`,`Bradycardia with potassium over 6.5 mmol/L or widening QRS`,`Suspected beta-blocker, calcium channel blocker, digoxin, oleander or organophosphate poisoning`,`New AV block with fever (endocarditis abscess, myocarditis, diphtheria) or after MI, TAVI or cardiac surgery`,`Paced patient with pocket redness, erosion, bacteraemia or recurrent syncope`],pearls:[`Atropine works above the His bundle - nodal block responds, infranodal block does not and may worsen.`,`Longer, longer, longer, drop - that is Wenckebach; constant PR then a sudden drop is Mobitz II, and Mobitz II is paced.`,`Slow regular pulse plus irregular cannon a waves plus variable first heart sound equals complete heart block at the bedside.`,`Regular slow ventricular rhythm in AF on digoxin is complete heart block from digoxin toxicity, not good rate control.`,`2:1 block is neither Mobitz I nor II until proven - the QRS width is the best clue to the level.`,`RCA supplies the AV node: inferior MI block is nodal and transient, anterior MI block is infranodal and lethal.`,`Pacing saves lives in AV block but only relieves symptoms in sinus node dysfunction.`,`Magnet on a pacemaker gives asynchronous pacing; magnet on an ICD switches off shocks but not pacing.`,`Before you pace, check the five: drugs, potassium, ischaemia, thyroid and temperature.`],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Classify atrioventricular (AV) block. Describe the ECG features, causes and emergency management of complete heart block, and list the indications for permanent pacing.`,openingLines:[`AV block is delay or failure of conduction of the atrial impulse to the ventricles, graded as first-degree (PR over 200 ms), second-degree (Mobitz I, Mobitz II, 2:1, high-grade) and third-degree (complete) block.`,`Its prognosis depends on the level of block - nodal block is usually benign and atropine-responsive, whereas infranodal (His-Purkinje) block is unstable and needs a permanent pacemaker even without symptoms (ESC 2021).`],answer:[{heading:`Classification with ECG`,points:[`First-degree: PR over 200 ms, every P conducted.`,`Mobitz I: progressive PR lengthening until a P is dropped; group beating; nodal.`,`Mobitz II: constant PR with sudden dropped P; often BBB; infranodal.`,`2:1 and high-grade (two or more consecutive non-conducted P waves).`,`Complete block: AV dissociation, atrial rate faster than ventricular; junctional (narrow, 40-60) or ventricular (wide, 20-40) escape.`]},{heading:`Causes of complete heart block`,points:[`Degenerative fibrosis (Lenegre, Lev) - commonest chronic cause.`,`Ischaemia: inferior MI (nodal, transient) versus anterior MI (infranodal).`,`Drugs: beta-blockers, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin, amiodarone; hyperkalaemia.`,`Infection and inflammation: endocarditis abscess, diphtheria, rheumatic fever, myocarditis, sarcoidosis.`,`Iatrogenic (TAVI, valve surgery) and congenital (maternal anti-Ro).`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Stokes-Adams attacks, dizziness, fatigue, heart failure, angina, confusion in the elderly.`,`Slow regular pulse, wide pulse pressure, irregular cannon a waves, variable S1, systolic flow murmur.`]},{heading:`Emergency management`,points:[`ABCDE, monitor, IV access, 12-lead ECG; look for shock, syncope, ischaemia, heart failure.`,`Atropine 1 mg IV every 3-5 min to 3 mg (often ineffective in wide-QRS block).`,`Transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline 2-10 micrograms/min, dopamine 5-20 micrograms/kg/min or isoprenaline 5 micrograms/min.`,`Temporary transvenous pacing via right internal jugular vein if persistent.`,`Correct the cause: stop drugs, treat hyperkalaemia, reperfuse MI, antidotes for poisoning.`]},{heading:`Permanent pacing indications (ESC 2021)`,points:[`Mobitz II, infranodal 2:1, high-grade and complete block in sinus rhythm - irrespective of symptoms.`,`Symptomatic SND; alternating BBB; syncope with BBB and HV 70 ms or more.`,`Post-MI block persisting at least 5 days; post-TAVI block persisting 24-48 h.`,`HFrEF with AV block - CRT rather than RV pacing.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Wound check 7-10 days; device checks at 2-12 weeks then 6-12 monthly or remote monitoring.`,`Watch for pocket infection, lead dislodgement, pacemaker syndrome and pacing-induced cardiomyopathy.`]}],mustDraw:[`ECG strips of first-degree, Mobitz I, Mobitz II and complete heart block`,`Bradycardia emergency algorithm (atropine, pacing, infusions)`],markSplit:[{part:`Classification with ECG features`,marks:3},{part:`Causes`,marks:2},{part:`Emergency management`,marks:3},{part:`Pacing indications and follow-up`,marks:2}],keywords:[`AV block`,`Mobitz II`,`complete heart block`,`atropine`,`transcutaneous pacing`,`permanent pacemaker`,`ESC 2021`]},{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: pacemaker code, modes and common problems at follow-up.`,openingLines:[`Pacemaker function is described by the NBG (NASPE/BPEG) code: chamber paced, chamber sensed, response to sensing and rate modulation.`,`Choosing the mode and recognising failure to capture, sensing errors, pacemaker syndrome and device infection are core skills for the family physician who sees paced patients between device clinic visits.`],answer:[{heading:`NBG code and modes`,points:[`Position I chamber paced, II chamber sensed (O, A, V, D), III response (I inhibit, T trigger, D dual), IV R rate response.`,`DDD for AV block in sinus rhythm; DDD with minimal ventricular pacing for SND; VVIR for permanent AF.`,`CRT for HFrEF needing ventricular pacing; conduction system pacing for EF 36-50% with high pacing need; leadless when venous access poor.`]},{heading:`Problems`,points:[`Failure to capture (spike without QRS) and failure to output (no spike).`,`Undersensing and oversensing (myopotentials, interference).`,`Pacemaker syndrome (VVI, cannon waves, hypotension) and pacemaker-mediated tachycardia (DDD; stop with magnet).`,`Lead dislodgement, pocket haematoma, infection and erosion (needs complete extraction), pacing-induced cardiomyopathy.`]},{heading:`Follow-up advice`,points:[`Wound check 7-10 days; avoid arm above shoulder for 4-6 weeks.`,`Device check 2-12 weeks then 6-12 monthly or remote; battery 8-12 years.`,`Phone 15 cm away, MRI only if MR-conditional, carry device card.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table of the NBG pacing code`,`ECG showing failure to capture versus normal capture`],markSplit:[{part:`Code and modes`,marks:2},{part:`Problems`,marks:2},{part:`Follow-up advice`,marks:1}],keywords:[`NBG code`,`DDD`,`VVI`,`pacemaker syndrome`,`failure to capture`,`magnet`]}],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-q1`,stem:`A 72-year-old man has had two episodes of sudden loss of consciousness without warning. ECG shows sinus rhythm with a constant PR interval of 160 ms, occasional non-conducted P waves, and RBBB with left anterior fascicular block. Potassium and TSH are normal and he takes no drugs. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Reassure and repeat the ECG in 6 months`,`Start oral theophylline and review in 2 weeks`,`Admit for monitoring and permanent pacemaker implantation`,`Prescribe a 24-hour Holter and treat only if pauses exceed 6 seconds`],answer:2,explanation:`Constant PR with sudden dropped beats is Mobitz II, and with bifascicular block it is infranodal disease that progresses unpredictably to complete block; ESC 2021 gives it a Class I indication for permanent pacing irrespective of symptoms, let alone with syncope. Reassurance ignores a lethal rhythm, theophylline has no role as definitive therapy, and waiting for a Holter delays a decision that the resting ECG has already made.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-q2`,stem:`A 58-year-old man with an inferior STEMI has a heart rate of 48 bpm with Mobitz I (Wenckebach) block, narrow QRS complexes and BP 108/70 mmHg. He is alert with no heart failure. What is the best immediate approach?`,options:[`Proceed with primary PCI, keep atropine ready and observe the rhythm`,`Insert a temporary transvenous pacing wire before angiography`,`Implant a permanent pacemaker before discharge`,`Start an isoprenaline infusion to keep the heart rate over 70 bpm`],answer:0,explanation:`The RCA supplies the AV node in about 90%, so block in inferior MI is nodal, vagally mediated and usually transient; a stable patient needs reperfusion with atropine on standby. Temporary pacing is reserved for haemodynamic compromise or infranodal block, a permanent pacemaker is considered only if block persists beyond at least 5 days, and isoprenaline increases myocardial oxygen demand in acute ischaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-q3`,stem:`In which of the following situations is IV atropine LEAST likely to raise the ventricular rate?`,options:[`Sinus bradycardia during a vasovagal reaction`,`Mobitz I block in acute inferior myocardial infarction`,`Sinus bradycardia due to organophosphate poisoning`,`Complete heart block with a wide-QRS ventricular escape at 30 bpm`],answer:3,explanation:`Atropine blocks vagal M2 receptors, which act on the sinus node and AV node; a wide-QRS escape at 30 bpm means block below the His bundle, where vagal tone has little influence, so atropine fails and pacing is needed. Vasovagal bradycardia, nodal Wenckebach in inferior MI and muscarinic bradycardia from organophosphates are all vagal or nodal and respond well.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-q4`,stem:`A 76-year-old woman with a single-chamber ventricular (VVI) pacemaker implanted for intermittent AV block complains of dizziness, fatigue and pounding in the neck. Her BP falls during paced beats and the JVP shows cannon waves. The pacing spikes capture normally. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`Pacemaker-mediated (endless-loop) tachycardia`,`Pacemaker syndrome`,`Twiddler syndrome`,`Failure to capture from lead dislodgement`],answer:1,explanation:`Pacemaker syndrome follows loss of AV synchrony with ventricular pacing: retrograde VA conduction makes the atria contract against closed AV valves, producing cannon waves, hypotension and fatigue, and the cure is upgrade to dual-chamber pacing. Endless-loop tachycardia needs an atrial lead (DDD), twiddler syndrome is lead retraction from rotating the generator, and normal capture rules out lead dislodgement.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-bradyarrhythmias-pacing-q5`,stem:`A 66-year-old man with ischaemic cardiomyopathy (LVEF 30%, NYHA class II on optimal therapy) develops persistent complete heart block with no reversible cause. Which device does the ESC 2021 guideline recommend?`,options:[`Single-chamber ventricular pacemaker (VVI)`,`Dual-chamber pacemaker with an RV apical lead`,`Cardiac resynchronisation therapy (biventricular pacing) device`,`Atrial-only pacemaker (AAI)`],answer:2,explanation:`In HFrEF with an indication for ventricular pacing because of high-degree AV block, ESC 2021 recommends CRT rather than RV pacing regardless of NYHA class, because near-100% RV pacing worsens dyssynchrony and heart failure. Single- or dual-chamber RV pacing will cause pacing-induced deterioration, and an atrial-only device cannot pace the ventricles in complete block.`,difficulty:`hard`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Bradyarrhythmias: disorders of the sinoatrial node and AV conduction`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Bradyarrhythmias and pacemakers`,`Glikson M et al. 2021 ESC Guidelines on cardiac pacing and cardiac resynchronization therapy. Eur Heart J 2021`,`Kusumoto FM et al. 2018 ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline on the Evaluation and Management of Patients With Bradycardia and Cardiac Conduction Delay`,`Chung MK et al. 2023 HRS/APHRS/LAHRS Guideline on Cardiac Physiologic Pacing for the Avoidance and Mitigation of Heart Failure`,`American Heart Association 2025 Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care - Adult Advanced Life Support`,`European Resuscitation Council Guidelines 2021 - Adult Advanced Life Support (peri-arrest arrhythmias)`,`Universal Immunization Programme schedule, Ministry of Health and Family Welfare, Government of India`]}),l.push({id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw`,title:`Narrow-complex tachycardia, atrial flutter and WPW`,oneLiner:`**Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)** is a tachycardia over 100 bpm whose circuit needs the atria or AV node - mainly **AVNRT, AVRT (accessory pathway) and focal atrial tachycardia** - with **atrial flutter** as a macro-reentry around the tricuspid annulus; stable regular narrow-complex tachycardia is terminated by the **modified Valsalva manoeuvre**, then **adenosine 6 mg, 12 mg (and 18 mg)** rapid IV, then **verapamil/diltiazem or a beta-blocker**, with **synchronised cardioversion** for instability and **catheter ablation** as definitive therapy, while **pre-excited AF in WPW** must never receive AV nodal blockers (ESC 2019).`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`SVT`,`PSVT`,`AVNRT`,`AVRT`,`accessory pathway`,`bundle of Kent`,`WPW`,`delta wave`,`pre-excited AF`,`focal atrial tachycardia`,`multifocal atrial tachycardia`,`atrial flutter`,`cavotricuspid isthmus`,`modified Valsalva`,`REVERT`,`adenosine`,`verapamil`,`diltiazem`,`catheter ablation`,`PJRT`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**SVT** is any tachycardia (atrial rate over 100 bpm) whose mechanism involves tissue **above the bundle of His** - atrial myocardium, AV node or an accessory pathway; in practice the term usually means **paroxysmal regular narrow-complex tachycardia** (AVNRT, AVRT, AT). [ESC 2019]`,`**Narrow-complex tachycardia** has QRS **120 ms or less**; nearly all are supraventricular, while a broad-complex tachycardia may be VT or SVT with aberrancy or pre-excitation (see the VT topic). [ESC 2019]`,`**Prevalence of paroxysmal SVT** is about **2.3 per 1000** with an incidence of about **35 per 100,000 per year**; women are affected about twice as often as men. [Marshfield study, ESC 2019]`,`**AVNRT** is the commonest regular SVT in adults (about 60%), typically in women aged 30-50; **AVRT** is commoner in children and young men; **focal AT** makes up about 10% and rises with age. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Ventricular pre-excitation (WPW pattern)** is seen in about **1-3 per 1000** ECGs; **WPW syndrome** is pre-excitation plus symptomatic arrhythmia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Atrial flutter** is less common than AF, has an incidence that rises sharply with age, and usually coexists with AF, heart failure, COPD, or follows cardiac surgery. [ESC 2019]`,`No Indian national registry exists; tertiary **Indian electrophysiology series** mirror global data, with **AVNRT the commonest ablation substrate followed by accessory pathways**, and ablation is now covered under PM-JAY packages in many states. [CSI, Indian EP series]`,`SVT is rarely life-threatening; the exceptions are **pre-excited AF** (risk of VF) and **incessant tachycardia causing cardiomyopathy**. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**AVNRT** arises from **dual AV nodal physiology** - an anatomically normal but functionally dual approach to the compact node - and needs no structural heart disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AVRT** needs an **accessory pathway** (bundle of Kent) - a congenital muscular bridge across the AV ring; **Ebstein anomaly** is associated with multiple right-sided pathways. [ESC 2019]`,`**Focal AT** is linked to structural heart disease, atrial scars, **digoxin toxicity** (AT with block), alcohol, stimulants and hypokalaemia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Multifocal atrial tachycardia** occurs in **COPD exacerbation**, hypoxia, theophylline toxicity, hypomagnesaemia and sepsis - the treatment is the underlying illness. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Typical atrial flutter** is associated with **COPD, heart failure, dilated atria, rheumatic mitral disease, thyrotoxicosis, alcohol and post-cardiac surgery**; atypical flutter follows atrial scars from surgery or AF ablation. [ESC 2019]`,`**Triggers** of episodes: caffeine excess, alcohol, sleep loss, **thyrotoxicosis**, stimulants (salbutamol, decongestants, cannabis), anaemia, pregnancy and physical or emotional stress. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Class IC drugs (flecainide, propafenone)** slow flutter enough to allow **1:1 AV conduction** with a dangerously fast broad-complex rate (IC flutter) unless an AV nodal blocker is given too. [ESC 2019]`,`**Familial pre-excitation** with **PRKAG2** mutation causes WPW with LVH and conduction disease; **Danon disease** (LAMP2) also causes pre-excitation with hypertrophy. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and mechanism`,points:[`Most SVTs are **re-entry**: a circuit with two limbs of differing conduction and refractoriness plus a **premature beat that blocks in one limb** and returns through the other. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Typical (slow-fast) AVNRT** (about 90%): a premature atrial beat finds the **fast pathway refractory**, conducts down the **slow pathway** (PR jumps) and returns up the fast pathway, so the retrograde P falls inside or just after the QRS. [Harrison 22e]`,`Because atria and ventricles contract almost together against closed AV valves in AVNRT, patients feel **neck pounding (frog sign)** from regular cannon waves, and atrial stretch releases **natriuretic peptide causing polyuria**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Orthodromic AVRT** (about 90-95% of AVRT) goes **down the AV node and up the accessory pathway**, giving a narrow QRS with a retrograde P **more than 70 ms after the QRS**; **antidromic AVRT** goes the other way and gives a **broad, fully pre-excited QRS** that mimics VT. [ESC 2019]`,`A **concealed pathway** conducts only retrogradely, so the resting ECG is normal but orthodromic AVRT can still occur. [ESC 2019]`,`**WPW pattern**: the pathway bypasses the AV node's delay, so the ventricle is activated early - **short PR (under 120 ms), slurred QRS upstroke (delta wave), wide QRS** and secondary ST-T changes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pre-excited AF is the lethal mechanism**: an accessory pathway with a short refractory period lets AF impulses reach the ventricle at **250-300 per minute or faster**, degenerating to VF; blocking the AV node with adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem, beta-blockers or digoxin pushes even more impulses down the pathway. [ESC 2019]`,`**Typical atrial flutter** is a macro-reentrant circuit around the **tricuspid annulus**, constrained by the crista terminalis, which must pass through the **cavotricuspid isthmus** - the target for ablation; atrial rate is about **300 per minute** (240-350), usually with **2:1 conduction giving a ventricular rate near 150**. [ESC 2019]`,`**Adenosine** acts on **A1 receptors** to open IKAdo potassium channels and transiently block the AV node (half-life under 10 seconds); it **terminates AV node-dependent re-entry** (AVNRT, AVRT) but only **unmasks** flutter or AT. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Incessant tachycardias** (PJRT, focal AT, flutter with fast rates) cause **tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy**, which reverses after rate control or ablation. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Classification and ECG`,points:[`**Classify by RP interval** in a regular narrow-complex tachycardia: **short RP** (RP shorter than PR) - typical AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT; **long RP** (RP longer than PR) - focal AT, atypical AVNRT, PJRT and sinus tachycardia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Typical AVNRT**: P wave hidden in the QRS or seen as a **pseudo-r prime in V1** and **pseudo-S in II, III, aVF**; RP under 70 ms; rate 140-250. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Orthodromic AVRT**: narrow QRS with an inverted retrograde P in the ST segment, **RP over 70 ms**; **QRS alternans** is common but not specific. [ESC 2019]`,`**Focal AT**: P waves of **abnormal morphology** before each QRS, isoelectric baseline between P waves, warm-up and cool-down; AV block can occur without stopping the tachycardia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Typical counter-clockwise flutter**: **negative sawtooth F waves in II, III, aVF** and positive in V1, no isoelectric baseline; clockwise typical flutter has positive inferior F waves. [ESC 2019]`,`**WPW location**: a **positive delta wave in V1** (tall R, type A) suggests a left-sided pathway; a **negative delta in V1** (type B) suggests a right-sided pathway. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pre-excited AF**: **irregularly irregular, broad and bizarre QRS complexes that vary beat to beat**, at rates often over 200 - unlike AF with fixed bundle branch block, where QRS shape is constant. [ESC 2019]`,`**Multifocal AT**: rate over 100 with **three or more P-wave morphologies** and varying PR, irregular - often mistaken for AF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**A regular narrow tachycardia at exactly 150 bpm is atrial flutter with 2:1 block until proven otherwise** - look for the hidden second F wave. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Classic story: **sudden onset and sudden offset of regular fast palpitations**, often first in youth, triggered by bending, exertion or caffeine, and stopped by breath-holding or straining. [ESC 2019]`,`**Neck pounding** (frog sign) and **polyuria after an attack** point to AVNRT. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Associated symptoms: **dizziness, breathlessness, chest discomfort, anxiety**; syncope is uncommon and should raise concern about pre-excited AF, structural disease or a post-termination pause. [ESC 2019]`,`Ask how attacks end - **self-terminating, vagal manoeuvres, or hospital injections** - and how often they occur; this guides ablation versus drugs. [ESC 2019]`,`**Gradual onset and offset** suggest sinus tachycardia or focal AT; **irregular** palpitations suggest AF, MAT or ectopics (see the Approach to palpitations topic). [ESC 2019]`,`Ask about **thyrotoxic symptoms, anaemia, stimulants, alcohol, pregnancy** and drugs such as salbutamol, theophylline and decongestants. [Harrison 22e]`,`In **flutter**, patients often present with breathlessness, fatigue or heart failure rather than palpitations, and sometimes with stroke. [ESC 2019]`,`**Family history** of WPW, sudden death or cardiomyopathy and **high-risk occupation** (pilot, driver) matter for risk stratification of pre-excitation. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`During an attack: **regular rapid pulse** of 150-250; check **BP, perfusion, consciousness, chest pain and crepitations** to decide stable versus unstable. [ESC 2019]`,`**Regular cannon a waves** (frog sign) in AVNRT because atria and ventricles contract simultaneously. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Flutter waves in the JVP** may be visible at about 300 per minute; carotid massage transiently slows the ventricular rate in flutter in steps. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Between attacks** the examination is usually normal; look for **thyroid signs**, anaemia, murmurs (rheumatic mitral disease, Ebstein anomaly) and features of heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy** signs - raised JVP, S3, oedema - in a patient with incessant tachycardia should prompt urgent rhythm control. [ESC 2019]`,`A **pulse deficit** and irregular pulse suggest AF or MAT rather than a re-entrant SVT. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Sinus tachycardia**: gradual onset, rate varies with activity, normal P axis, and an obvious cause (fever, anaemia, hypovolaemia, PE, thyrotoxicosis) - never cardiovert it. [ESC 2019]`,`**Inappropriate sinus tachycardia** and **POTS** are diagnoses of exclusion in young women; ivabradine is used for the former. [ESC 2019]`,`**Atrial flutter with 2:1 block** mimics AVNRT at 150 bpm; adenosine or vagal manoeuvres reveal sawtooth waves. [ESC 2019]`,`**AF** is irregularly irregular with no P waves; at fast rates it looks regular - measure RR intervals with calipers. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**VT** and **SVT with aberrancy** cause broad-complex tachycardia; in a patient with prior MI, a broad tachycardia is **VT until proven otherwise** (see the VT topic). [ESC 2022]`,`**Antidromic AVRT** and **pre-excited AF** produce broad complexes and are treated as VT or with cardioversion - never with verapamil. [ESC 2019]`,`**Panic attack** produces sinus tachycardia with gradual offset; abrupt termination and documented rates over 180 favour SVT. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG during tachycardia** is the single most valuable test - record it before and during adenosine with a running rhythm strip. [ESC 2019]`,`**Resting 12-lead ECG in sinus rhythm**: look for **pre-excitation (delta wave, short PR)**, LVH, and previous MI. [ESC 2019]`,`**Bloods**: TSH, haemoglobin, potassium, magnesium, and troponin only if ischaemia is suspected - a small troponin rise after prolonged SVT is common and not an MI by itself. [ESC 2019]`,`**Ambulatory monitoring**: Holter for frequent attacks; event recorders or **smartphone/smartwatch ECGs** for infrequent ones - a patient-recorded single-lead strip is acceptable evidence. [ESC 2019]`,`**Echocardiography** for structural disease, Ebstein anomaly and LV function (tachycardiomyopathy). [ESC 2019]`,`**Exercise testing** in asymptomatic pre-excitation: **abrupt and complete loss of the delta wave at higher heart rates** marks a low-risk pathway. [ESC 2019]`,`**Electrophysiology study** confirms the mechanism and is usually followed by ablation in the same sitting; it risk-stratifies pre-excitation by the **shortest pre-excited RR interval in AF (SPERRI) - 250 ms or less is high risk**. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Acute management (OPD, emergency and IPD)`,points:[`**Haemodynamically unstable** (shock, syncope, ischaemia, acute heart failure): **synchronised DC cardioversion** under sedation (AHA 2025: regular narrow 50-100 J biphasic, escalate). [ESC 2019, AHA 2025]`,`**Stable regular narrow-complex**: record a 12-lead ECG, then **vagal manoeuvres** - preferably the **modified Valsalva** with leg elevation (Class I). [ESC 2019]`,`**Modified Valsalva (REVERT)**: semi-recumbent patient **strains for 15 s** (blowing into a 10 mL syringe enough to move the plunger, about 40 mmHg), then is **laid flat with legs raised to 45 degrees for 15 s**; conversion about **43% versus 17%** with standard Valsalva. [REVERT, Lancet 2015]`,`**Carotid sinus massage** (5-10 s, one side, after auscultating for bruits) is an alternative in younger patients; avoid in the elderly or with prior TIA or stroke; **eyeball pressure is abandoned**. [ESC 2019]`,`**Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV bolus** through a large antecubital vein with a **20 mL saline flush**, then **12 mg** and, per ESC, **18 mg** if needed; warn of transient chest tightness, flushing and a sense of doom; use **3 mg** via a central line or after heart transplant. [ESC 2019]`,`If adenosine fails or is contraindicated: **verapamil 5 mg IV over 2 min** (0.075-0.15 mg/kg, repeat after 15-30 min, maximum 20 mg) or **diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg IV over 2 min** (IIa), or **metoprolol 2.5-5 mg IV** every 5 min to 15 mg or esmolol (IIa); if drugs fail, **synchronised cardioversion** (Class I). [ESC 2019]`,`**Never give verapamil** for broad-complex tachycardia, pre-excited AF, hypotension, HFrEF, after IV beta-blocker, or in infants. [ESC 2019]`,`**Pre-excited AF**: unstable - **immediate synchronised cardioversion**; stable - **IV ibutilide or procainamide** (IIa) or flecainide/propafenone (IIb), or elective cardioversion; **adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem, beta-blockers and digoxin are contraindicated**, and IV amiodarone is best avoided as it has precipitated VF. [ESC 2019]`,`**Atrial flutter acute**: rate control with diltiazem, verapamil or beta-blocker; rhythm control by **low-energy synchronised cardioversion**, ibutilide, or rapid atrial pacing; anticoagulate as for AF before elective cardioversion if over 48 h. [ESC 2019]`,`**Admit** if unstable, refractory, pre-excited AF, recurrent episodes despite treatment, tachycardiomyopathy, pregnancy with compromise, or significant structural heart disease. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Long-term management and ablation`,points:[`**Catheter ablation** is **first-line** for recurrent symptomatic **AVNRT** and **AVRT** (Class I) - success about **95-97%**, risk of **complete heart block under 1%** for AVNRT slow-pathway ablation. [ESC 2019]`,`Patients who decline ablation: **verapamil or diltiazem** (e.g. verapamil SR 120-240 mg daily) or a **beta-blocker** (metoprolol 25-100 mg twice daily) for AVNRT without pre-excitation (IIa). [ESC 2019]`,`**Flecainide or propafenone** may be considered for AVRT in patients **without ischaemic or structural heart disease** who decline ablation (IIb). [ESC 2019]`,`**Teach self-termination**: modified Valsalva at home, cold water facial immersion, and when to come to hospital (attack over 20-30 minutes, dizziness, chest pain). [ESC 2019]`,`**Typical flutter**: **cavotricuspid isthmus ablation** after a first symptomatic episode should be considered (IIa) and is recommended for recurrence (Class I); acute success over 95%, but many patients develop AF later. [ESC 2019]`,`**Anticoagulation in flutter** follows the AF rules (CHA2DS2-VA, DOAC preferred) - stroke risk is similar; see the Atrial fibrillation topic. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Focal AT**: ablation (Class I for recurrent or incessant); beta-blockers or non-dihydropyridine CCBs if ablation declined. [ESC 2019]`,`**MAT**: treat COPD, hypoxia and sepsis, correct magnesium and potassium; verapamil or a cardioselective beta-blocker for rate; **cardioversion does not work**. [ESC 2019]`,`**Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy**: ablation is recommended (Class I) and LV function usually recovers within months. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Asymptomatic pre-excitation (WPW pattern)`,points:[`Risk of sudden death is **low but real** and is often the first manifestation; risk stratification is advised rather than reassurance alone. [ESC 2019]`,`**EP study with isoprenaline** is **recommended (Class I)** in asymptomatic pre-excitation with **high-risk occupations** (pilots, drivers) and **competitive athletes**. [ESC 2019]`,`EP study **should be considered** (IIa) in other asymptomatic individuals; **non-invasive tests** (abrupt loss of pre-excitation on exercise or Holter) identify low-risk pathways (IIb). [ESC 2019]`,`**High-risk features** that indicate ablation (Class I): **SPERRI 250 ms or less**, pathway refractory period 250 ms or less, **multiple pathways** or **inducible pathway-mediated tachycardia**. [ESC 2019]`,`Ablation **may be considered** even in low-risk asymptomatic patients after informed discussion, in experienced centres. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: vagal manoeuvres then **adenosine** (safe); **cardioversion is safe in all trimesters**; for prophylaxis use **metoprolol or propranolol** (avoid atenolol - growth restriction), verapamil second line; **avoid amiodarone**. [ESC 2018 Pregnancy, ESC 2019]`,`Refractory SVT in pregnancy: **zero-fluoroscopy catheter ablation** should be considered in experienced centres. [ESC 2019]`,`**Infants and children**: AVRT predominates; use **ice-water facial immersion** (diving reflex); **adenosine 0.1 mg/kg (max 6 mg), then 0.2 mg/kg (max 12 mg)**; **verapamil is contraindicated under 1 year** (cardiovascular collapse). [AHA PALS 2025]`,`**Elderly**: more often focal AT or flutter; screen for structural heart disease, avoid carotid massage with cerebrovascular disease, and anticoagulate flutter by risk score. [ESC 2019]`,`**COPD and asthma**: adenosine can cause bronchospasm; prefer verapamil or diltiazem for MAT or SVT; avoid non-selective beta-blockers. [ESC 2019]`,`**Heart failure**: avoid verapamil and diltiazem in HFrEF; use cardioversion, amiodarone or ablation. [ESC 2019]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: modified Valsalva needs only a 10 mL syringe; if adenosine is unavailable, IV verapamil or diltiazem is the next step for a stable narrow-complex tachycardia with no pre-excitation. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT have an **excellent prognosis**; the burden is quality of life, emergency visits and occupational limitation. [ESC 2019]`,`**Pre-excited AF degenerating to VF** is the main cause of sudden death in WPW; it may be the first presentation. [ESC 2019]`,`**Tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy** complicates incessant AT, PJRT and flutter. [ESC 2019]`,`**Atrial flutter** carries stroke risk similar to AF and **1:1 conduction** (exercise, IC drugs, thyrotoxicosis) can cause collapse. [ESC 2019]`,`**Ablation complications**: AV block (septal pathways, AVNRT), tamponade, vascular injury; overall major complications are uncommon (around 1%). [ESC 2019]`,`**Adenosine risks**: transient asystole, bronchospasm, and **induction of AF (1-15%)**, dangerous when a pathway is present - keep a defibrillator ready. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Refer every patient with documented SVT** for an electrophysiology opinion on ablation, and **urgently any patient with pre-excitation and palpitations or syncope**. [ESC 2019]`,`Refer **asymptomatic pre-excitation** in athletes, drivers, pilots and children before competitive sport for risk stratification. [ESC 2019]`,`On drug therapy, review **every 3-6 months**: attack frequency, side effects (bradycardia, hypotension, constipation with verapamil) and ECG for PR and QRS (IC drugs widen QRS). [ESC 2019]`,`After ablation: aspirin is not routinely needed for right-sided procedures; review at 4-6 weeks; recurrence usually appears within 3 months. [ESC 2019]`,`**Counselling**: limit caffeine and alcohol excess, avoid stimulant decongestants, treat thyroid disease, and carry the ECG of the attack. [ESC 2019]`,`**Driving**: patients with SVT causing syncope or presyncope should not drive until the arrhythmia is controlled. [ESC 2019]`,`**Programme links**: NP-NCD clinics and Ayushman Arogya Mandirs record ECGs for palpitations; teach the modified Valsalva to patients and staff. [NP-NCD]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2019-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2019 SVT guideline** (current European standard): modified Valsalva endorsed, **sotalol and amiodarone removed or downgraded** for SVT, **verapamil and diltiazem downgraded to IIa**, digoxin dropped, and **ablation first-line** for AVNRT, AVRT and recurrent flutter. [ESC 2019]`,`ESC 2019 expanded **EP-study-based risk stratification and ablation of asymptomatic pre-excitation**, especially in athletes and high-risk occupations. [ESC 2019]`,`**ESC 2024 AF guideline** applies **CHA2DS2-VA** (sex removed) to anticoagulation decisions in atrial flutter as in AF. [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**AHA 2025 ACLS and PALS** keep adenosine **6 mg then 12 mg** in adults (0.1 then 0.2 mg/kg in children) and synchronised cardioversion for unstable tachycardia. [AHA 2025]`,`**Etripamil**, a self-administered **intranasal calcium channel blocker**, terminated PSVT within 30 min in **64% versus 31%** with placebo in the RAPID trial and was approved in the US in late 2025; it is not available in India. [RAPID, Lancet 2023]`,`**Zero- or minimal-fluoroscopy ablation** with 3D electroanatomic mapping is now routine in many Indian centres and is the preferred approach in pregnancy and children. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **management of a stable SVT in the emergency room**, **ECG of WPW**, **why verapamil is contraindicated in WPW with AF**, and **adenosine dose and side effects**. [ESC 2019]`,`Common mistake: giving **adenosine or verapamil to an irregular broad-complex tachycardia** - that is pre-excited AF until proven otherwise; cardiovert or give procainamide or ibutilide. [ESC 2019]`,`Common mistake: **slow adenosine injection through a distal vein** - it is destroyed in seconds; give rapidly with a flush via the antecubital fossa. [ESC 2019]`,`**Adenosine is both treatment and test**: termination means AV node-dependent re-entry; persisting atrial activity with AV block means flutter or AT. [ESC 2019]`,`Viva: **150 bpm regular narrow tachycardia** - think flutter with 2:1 block. [ESC 2019]`,`Viva: **delta wave + short PR + wide QRS** = WPW pattern; add arrhythmia = WPW syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for narrow-complex tachycardia and flutter`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Adenosine`,`6 mg rapid IV bolus with 20 mL flush; then 12 mg; ESC allows 18 mg`,`Doses 1-2 min apart; half-life under 10 s`,`Flushing, chest tightness, transient asystole, bronchospasm, induces AF; 3 mg via central line, transplant or dipyridamole`],[`Verapamil`,`5 mg IV over 2 min (0.075-0.15 mg/kg); oral 40-120 mg three times daily or SR 120-240 mg`,`Repeat IV after 15-30 min, max 20 mg`,`Hypotension, bradycardia; contraindicated in broad-complex tachycardia, WPW AF, HFrEF, infants`],[`Diltiazem`,`0.25 mg/kg IV over 2 min; oral 60-120 mg three times daily or SR`,`Repeat 0.35 mg/kg after 15 min`,`Hypotension, bradycardia; avoid in HFrEF and pre-excited AF`],[`Metoprolol`,`2.5-5 mg IV every 5 min to 15 mg; oral 25-100 mg twice daily`,`Long term if ablation declined`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm, fatigue; avoid in decompensated HF`],[`Esmolol`,`500 micrograms/kg IV over 1 min, then 50-300 micrograms/kg/min`,`Short acting; titrate`,`Hypotension; avoid with IV verapamil`],[`Ibutilide`,`1 mg IV over 10 min (0.01 mg/kg if under 60 kg)`,`Repeat once after 10 min`,`Torsades in up to 4% - monitor QT 4 h, correct K and Mg`],[`Flecainide`,`50-150 mg oral twice daily`,`Long term in AVRT without structural disease`,`Contraindicated after MI, in HFrEF and LVH; give AV nodal blocker in flutter (1:1 risk)`],[`Synchronised cardioversion`,`Narrow regular or flutter 50-100 J biphasic (AHA 2025); escalate`,`Under sedation`,`Contraindicated in sinus tachycardia and digoxin toxicity; anticoagulate flutter over 48 h`]]},{heading:`Mechanisms and ECG of regular SVT`,columns:[`Rhythm`,`Circuit`,`ECG during tachycardia`,`Response to adenosine`],rows:[[`Typical AVNRT`,`Down slow pathway, up fast pathway in AV node`,`P hidden in QRS; pseudo-r prime V1, pseudo-S inferior; RP under 70 ms`,`Terminates`],[`Orthodromic AVRT`,`Down AV node, up accessory pathway`,`Narrow QRS, retrograde P in ST, RP over 70 ms, QRS alternans`,`Terminates`],[`Antidromic AVRT`,`Down accessory pathway, up AV node`,`Broad pre-excited QRS (VT mimic)`,`Terminates but risk of AF - avoid`],[`Focal atrial tachycardia`,`Automatic or triggered focus in atrium`,`Abnormal P before each QRS, long RP, warm-up`,`AV block with continuing P waves; occasionally terminates`],[`Typical flutter`,`Macro-re-entry around tricuspid annulus through CTI`,`Sawtooth F waves negative in II, III, aVF; 2:1 at 150`,`Transient AV block reveals F waves`],[`PJRT`,`Slow decremental posteroseptal pathway`,`Incessant long-RP tachycardia, deep inverted inferior P`,`Terminates transiently, recurs`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a regular tachycardia`,columns:[`Condition`,`Onset and rate`,`Key ECG clue`,`Discriminating step`],rows:[[`Sinus tachycardia`,`Gradual, 100-160, varies`,`Normal P axis before each QRS`,`Find and treat cause; adenosine only slows transiently`],[`AVNRT`,`Abrupt, 140-250`,`No visible P or pseudo-r prime`,`Terminates with vagal or adenosine`],[`Orthodromic AVRT`,`Abrupt, 150-250`,`Retrograde P in ST; delta wave in sinus rhythm (unless concealed)`,`Terminates with adenosine`],[`Atrial flutter 2:1`,`Abrupt, about 150 fixed`,`Sawtooth waves, second F wave hidden`,`Adenosine unmasks F waves`],[`Focal AT`,`Variable, 100-250`,`Abnormal P morphology, isoelectric baseline`,`Adenosine causes AV block, P persists`],[`VT`,`Abrupt, 120-250`,`Broad QRS, AV dissociation, capture or fusion beats`,`Treat as VT; cardiovert or amiodarone`]]},{heading:`Risk stratification of pre-excitation`,columns:[`Finding`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`Intermittent pre-excitation on resting ECG or Holter`,`Long pathway refractory period - low risk`,`Reassure; ablation optional`],[`Abrupt loss of delta wave on exercise`,`Low-risk pathway`,`No ablation needed if asymptomatic`],[`SPERRI or pathway ERP 250 ms or less`,`High risk of rapid conduction in AF`,`Ablation (Class I)`],[`Multiple pathways`,`High risk`,`Ablation (Class I)`],[`Inducible AVRT at EP study`,`High risk`,`Ablation (Class I)`],[`Athlete or high-risk occupation`,`Consequences of event serious`,`EP study with isoprenaline (Class I)`],[`Symptomatic WPW syndrome`,`Risk of pre-excited AF and SCD`,`Ablation first-line (Class I)`]]}],redFlags:[`Tachycardia with hypotension, syncope, chest pain, acute heart failure or altered sensorium - synchronised cardioversion`,`Irregular broad-complex tachycardia with varying QRS shape - pre-excited AF, risk of VF`,`Syncope in a patient with a delta wave on the resting ECG`,`Broad-complex tachycardia in a patient with previous MI or cardiomyopathy - treat as VT`,`Incessant tachycardia with breathlessness, raised JVP or reduced LVEF (tachycardiomyopathy)`,`Flutter with 1:1 conduction (rate around 250-300) especially on flecainide or propafenone`,`SVT failing two adenosine doses or recurring repeatedly in the same visit`,`Family history of sudden death under 40 with pre-excitation`],pearls:[`Modified Valsalva: strain 15 s semi-recumbent, then lie flat with legs up 15 s - more than doubles conversion (REVERT).`,`Adenosine 6-12-18 fast and flushed through the antecubital vein; 3 mg through a central line or after heart transplant.`,`Adenosine terminates AVNRT and AVRT; it only unmasks flutter and atrial tachycardia.`,`Irregular, broad, bizarre and fast - pre-excited AF; no ABCD (adenosine, beta-blocker, calcium channel blocker, digoxin).`,`Regular narrow tachycardia at 150 bpm is atrial flutter with 2:1 block until proven otherwise.`,`Short RP: AVNRT (under 70 ms) and orthodromic AVRT (over 70 ms); long RP: atrial tachycardia, atypical AVNRT, PJRT.`,`WPW triad: short PR, delta wave, wide QRS; positive delta in V1 means a left-sided pathway.`,`Never give verapamil to a broad-complex tachycardia or an infant.`,`IC drugs in flutter without a nodal blocker can produce 1:1 conduction - IC flutter.`],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`A 28-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset regular palpitations at 186 bpm, BP 110/70 mmHg. Discuss the differential diagnosis, ECG mechanisms and stepwise management of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, and add a note on Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia is an abrupt-onset regular narrow-complex tachycardia whose re-entry circuit involves the AV node or atria - AVNRT (about 60%), AVRT via an accessory pathway, and focal atrial tachycardia.`,`A stable patient is treated with the modified Valsalva manoeuvre, then adenosine 6 mg, 12 mg and 18 mg rapid IV, then verapamil, diltiazem or a beta-blocker, with synchronised cardioversion for instability and catheter ablation as cure (ESC 2019).`],answer:[{heading:`Differential diagnosis of regular narrow-complex tachycardia`,points:[`Sinus tachycardia, AVNRT, orthodromic AVRT, focal atrial tachycardia, atrial flutter with 2:1 block, PJRT.`,`Short RP (AVNRT, AVRT) versus long RP (AT, atypical AVNRT, PJRT).`]},{heading:`Mechanisms and ECG`,points:[`AVNRT: slow-fast dual pathway re-entry; P hidden or pseudo-r prime V1; neck pounding and polyuria.`,`AVRT: down AV node, up accessory pathway; retrograde P in ST, RP over 70 ms.`,`Flutter: CTI-dependent macro-re-entry, sawtooth inferior F waves, 2:1 at 150.`]},{heading:`Immediate assessment`,points:[`ABCDE, 12-lead ECG, BP and adverse features (shock, syncope, ischaemia, heart failure).`,`Unstable: synchronised cardioversion under sedation.`]},{heading:`Stepwise management of the stable patient`,points:[`Modified Valsalva (REVERT): strain 15 s semi-recumbent, then supine with legs raised 15 s.`,`Adenosine 6 mg rapid IV with flush, then 12 mg (18 mg per ESC); record strip.`,`Verapamil 5 mg IV over 2 min (max 20 mg) or diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg, or metoprolol 2.5-5 mg IV.`,`Cardioversion if drugs fail; check TSH, potassium, triggers.`]},{heading:`Long-term care`,points:[`Catheter ablation first-line (95-97% success, AV block risk under 1% in AVNRT).`,`Verapamil, diltiazem or beta-blocker if ablation declined; teach self-termination.`]},{heading:`WPW syndrome`,points:[`Short PR, delta wave, wide QRS plus arrhythmia; prevalence 1-3 per 1000 ECGs.`,`Pre-excited AF: irregular broad bizarre QRS; no adenosine, verapamil, beta-blocker or digoxin; cardiovert or ibutilide/procainamide.`,`Ablation for symptomatic WPW and high-risk asymptomatic pathways (SPERRI 250 ms or less).`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of slow-fast AVNRT and orthodromic AVRT circuits`,`Flow chart: stable narrow-complex tachycardia management`,`ECG of WPW showing short PR and delta wave`],markSplit:[{part:`Differential and mechanisms`,marks:3},{part:`Acute management`,marks:4},{part:`Long-term care`,marks:1},{part:`WPW note`,marks:2}],keywords:[`AVNRT`,`AVRT`,`modified Valsalva`,`adenosine`,`verapamil`,`WPW`,`pre-excited AF`,`ablation`]},{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate AVNRT, orthodromic AVRT and typical atrial flutter.`,openingLines:[`All three are re-entrant supraventricular tachycardias, but AVNRT uses dual pathways within the AV node, AVRT uses an accessory pathway, and typical flutter is a right atrial macro-re-entry through the cavotricuspid isthmus.`,`The distinction decides whether adenosine will terminate the tachycardia and what the ablation target is.`],answer:[{heading:`Point-wise comparison`,points:[`Circuit: AV node slow-fast pathways / AV node plus accessory pathway / tricuspid annulus through CTI.`,`Patient: women 30-50 / children and young men, may have delta wave / elderly with COPD, HF, surgery.`,`ECG: P hidden, pseudo-r prime V1 / retrograde P in ST, RP over 70 ms / sawtooth F waves at 300, 2:1 at 150.`,`Adenosine: terminates / terminates / reveals F waves only.`,`Ablation: slow pathway / accessory pathway / cavotricuspid isthmus.`,`Anticoagulation: not needed / not needed / as for AF by CHA2DS2-VA.`]},{heading:`Clinical pearls`,points:[`Neck pounding and polyuria favour AVNRT.`,`Pre-excitation on the sinus ECG favours AVRT and warns against AV nodal blockers if AF occurs.`]}],mustDraw:[`Three-column comparison table`,`Sketch of sawtooth flutter waves in lead II`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and ECG`,marks:3},{part:`Treatment differences`,marks:2}],keywords:[`AVNRT`,`AVRT`,`atrial flutter`,`cavotricuspid isthmus`,`RP interval`]}],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-q1`,stem:`A 30-year-old woman has a regular narrow-complex tachycardia at 190 bpm, BP 118/76 mmHg, and no chest pain. Which first step has the best evidence for terminating the arrhythmia?`,options:[`Carotid sinus massage for 30 seconds on both sides together`,`Valsalva strain for 15 seconds semi-recumbent followed by lying flat with legs raised`,`Eyeball pressure for 10 seconds`,`Immediate synchronised DC cardioversion`],answer:1,explanation:`The modified Valsalva manoeuvre from the REVERT trial (strain 15 s semi-recumbent, then supine with legs raised to 45 degrees) converted about 43% versus 17% for standard Valsalva and is the ESC 2019 Class I first step. Bilateral carotid massage is dangerous, eyeball pressure was abandoned because of retinal injury, and cardioversion is reserved for the unstable patient or failed drugs.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-q2`,stem:`A 24-year-old man with palpitations has an irregular broad-complex tachycardia at 240 bpm with QRS complexes that vary in width and shape from beat to beat. BP is 112/70 mmHg. Which drug must be avoided?`,options:[`IV procainamide`,`IV ibutilide`,`Synchronised cardioversion under sedation`,`IV verapamil`],answer:3,explanation:`Irregular, broad and beat-to-beat varying QRS complexes at very fast rates indicate AF conducting down an accessory pathway (pre-excited AF). Verapamil (like adenosine, beta-blockers and digoxin) blocks the AV node and pushes more impulses down the pathway, precipitating VF. Procainamide and ibutilide slow pathway conduction and are the ESC 2019 IIa drugs, and cardioversion is always appropriate.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-q3`,stem:`A regular narrow-complex tachycardia at exactly 150 bpm is given adenosine 12 mg IV. The ventricular rate slows transiently, revealing negative sawtooth waves in II, III and aVF at 300 per minute, and the tachycardia then resumes. What is the diagnosis?`,options:[`Typical counter-clockwise atrial flutter with 2:1 block`,`Atrioventricular nodal re-entrant tachycardia`,`Orthodromic atrioventricular re-entrant tachycardia`,`Sinus tachycardia`],answer:0,explanation:`Adenosine produced AV block but did not terminate the rhythm, unmasking negative inferior sawtooth F waves at 300 - typical counter-clockwise flutter conducting 2:1. AVNRT and AVRT both need the AV node in the circuit and would have terminated with AV nodal block, and sinus tachycardia shows normal P waves, not sawtooth waves at 300.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-q4`,stem:`A 35-year-old patient has adenosine prescribed for SVT. In which situation should the initial adenosine dose be reduced to 3 mg?`,options:[`Regular coffee intake`,`Treatment with theophylline for asthma`,`Previous orthotopic heart transplant`,`Pregnancy in the second trimester`],answer:2,explanation:`A denervated transplanted heart is supersensitive to adenosine, so ESC 2019 advises 3 mg (as with a central line or dipyridamole or carbamazepine). Caffeine and theophylline antagonise adenosine receptors and blunt the effect rather than exaggerate it, and pregnancy needs no dose change - adenosine is the drug of choice in pregnancy.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-svt-wpw-q5`,stem:`An asymptomatic 22-year-old professional cricketer has a WPW pattern on a pre-participation ECG. Electrophysiology study shows a shortest pre-excited RR interval of 220 ms during induced AF. What is the best management?`,options:[`Reassure; no treatment as he is asymptomatic`,`Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway`,`Long-term oral verapamil`,`Long-term oral digoxin`],answer:1,explanation:`A SPERRI of 250 ms or less marks a high-risk pathway capable of very fast conduction in AF and VF, and ESC 2019 gives catheter ablation a Class I recommendation, especially in athletes. Reassurance ignores sudden-death risk, and verapamil and digoxin can accelerate conduction over the pathway during AF and are contraindicated in pre-excitation.`,difficulty:`hard`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Supraventricular tachyarrhythmias`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Supraventricular arrhythmias`,`Brugada J et al. 2019 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with supraventricular tachycardia. Eur Heart J 2020`,`Page RL et al. 2015 ACC/AHA/HRS Guideline for the Management of Adult Patients With Supraventricular Tachycardia`,`Van Gelder IC et al. 2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation. Eur Heart J 2024`,`Appelboam A et al. Postural modification to the standard Valsalva manoeuvre for emergency treatment of SVT (REVERT). Lancet 2015`,`American Heart Association 2025 Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care - Adult and Pediatric Advanced Life Support`,`Regitz-Zagrosek V et al. 2018 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular diseases during pregnancy`]}),l.push({id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd`,title:`Ventricular tachycardia, sudden cardiac death and the ICD`,oneLiner:`**Ventricular tachycardia (VT)** is three or more consecutive ventricular beats at over 100 bpm, **sustained** if it lasts **30 s or more** or needs termination; a **broad-complex tachycardia is VT until proven otherwise**, unstable VT is **cardioverted** at once, stable monomorphic VT is cardioverted or treated with **IV amiodarone (or procainamide) or lidocaine**, and **sudden cardiac death** - mostly from **VT/VF on a substrate of coronary disease or cardiomyopathy** - is prevented by **beta-blockers, heart-failure therapy and an ICD** for survivors and for **LVEF 35% or less** despite 3 months of optimal therapy (ESC 2022).`,frequency:`core`,keywords:[`VT`,`ventricular tachycardia`,`ventricular fibrillation`,`broad complex tachycardia`,`wide QRS tachycardia`,`Brugada criteria`,`Vereckei aVR algorithm`,`fusion beat`,`capture beat`,`electrical storm`,`amiodarone`,`lidocaine`,`procainamide`,`PVC`,`ICD`,`subcutaneous ICD`,`wearable defibrillator`,`sudden cardiac death`,`SADS`,`RVOT VT`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**VT** is three or more consecutive beats of ventricular origin at over 100 bpm; **non-sustained VT (NSVT)** lasts under 30 s, **sustained VT** lasts **30 s or more** or needs termination for haemodynamic compromise. [ESC 2022]`,`**Sudden cardiac death (SCD)** is unexpected natural death of presumed cardiac cause **within 1 hour of symptom onset** when witnessed, or **within 24 hours of last being seen well** when unwitnessed. [ESC 2022]`,`**Electrical (VT) storm** is **three or more sustained episodes of VT or VF, or appropriate ICD shocks, within 24 hours**. [ESC 2022]`,`SCD causes about **15-20% of all deaths** worldwide, with an incidence in Western populations of roughly **50-100 per 100,000 per year**, and most occur at home. [ESC 2022]`,`In India, coronary disease strikes about a decade earlier than in the West, and SCD in young adults is a growing public concern; survival after out-of-hospital cardiac arrest is **lower than the global 8-10%** because of low bystander CPR and scarce public defibrillators. [ICMR, Harrison 22e]`,`The **ICMR-NIE multicentric case-control study (2023)** of unexplained sudden deaths in adults aged 18-45 found **no increased risk from COVID-19 vaccination**; risk rose with **family history of sudden death, binge drinking, recreational drug use and vigorous exertion in the preceding 48 hours**. [ICMR 2023]`,`**PVCs** are found on Holter in up to 50-75% of healthy people; a **burden of 10% or more** can cause PVC-induced cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Coronary artery disease** underlies about **75-80% of SCD** over 35 years - acute ischaemia triggers polymorphic VT/VF, and **healed MI scar** causes monomorphic VT years later. [ESC 2022]`,`**Cardiomyopathies**: dilated (including Chagas abroad and post-myocarditis), **hypertrophic**, **arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC)**, cardiac sarcoidosis and **LMNA** disease. [ESC 2022]`,`**In the young (under 35)**: cardiomyopathies, **channelopathies** (long QT, Brugada, CPVT), myocarditis, anomalous coronary arteries, WPW, drugs (cocaine, anabolic steroids) - and about a third are **autopsy-negative (SADS)**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Reversible triggers**: **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia**, acute ischaemia, QT-prolonging drugs, digoxin toxicity, thyrotoxicosis, hypoxia and sympathomimetics. [ESC 2022]`,`**Indian toxic causes**: **aconite** (Aconitum, in some Ayurvedic preparations) causes bidirectional or polymorphic VT; **organophosphates** prolong QT and cause torsades; scorpion envenomation causes myocarditis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Idiopathic VT** (about 10% of VT) occurs in structurally normal hearts - **RVOT VT** (most common) and **fascicular VT**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Strongest risk marker for SCD** is **reduced LVEF**, but most SCDs occur in people with **preserved EF** or no known heart disease, which limits EF-based prevention. [ESC 2022]`,`**Repaired congenital heart disease** (Tetralogy of Fallot) and **valve disease** (severe aortic stenosis, mitral valve prolapse with annular disjunction) are further substrates. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and mechanism`,points:[`**Scar-related re-entry** is the mechanism of most monomorphic VT: surviving myocyte bundles running through fibrous **MI scar** form slowly conducting channels (the **isthmus**) - the target for ablation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Triggered activity** from **early afterdepolarisations** (long QT, bradycardia, hypokalaemia) causes **torsades de pointes**; **delayed afterdepolarisations** from calcium overload cause **CPVT, digoxin toxicity and RVOT VT**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Abnormal automaticity** drives VT in **acute ischaemia** and **accelerated idioventricular rhythm** (60-120 bpm), a benign reperfusion rhythm needing no treatment. [Harrison 22e]`,`**RVOT VT** is **cAMP-mediated triggered activity**, so it is provoked by exercise or stress and terminated by **adenosine or beta-blockers**; **fascicular VT** is re-entry in the left posterior fascicle, terminated by **verapamil**. [ESC 2022]`,`**VF** is chaotic activation by multiple wavelets and rotors with no output; untreated, it degrades to asystole within minutes, and survival falls about **7-10% per minute without CPR**. [AHA 2025]`,`**Haemodynamic tolerance depends on rate and LV function**, not on the mechanism - a young patient with VT at 160 may be alert, so **stability never excludes VT**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Amiodarone** blocks potassium, sodium and calcium channels and beta receptors; **lidocaine** blocks sodium channels in depolarised (ischaemic) tissue - why it is better in ischaemic VT; **beta-blockers** are the only antiarrhythmics proven to reduce SCD. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Class IC drugs** (flecainide) slow conduction in scar and **increase mortality after MI** (CAST), so they are banned in structural heart disease. [CAST, Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**By duration**: NSVT (3 beats to under 30 s) versus sustained (30 s or more, or needing termination). [ESC 2022]`,`**By morphology**: **monomorphic** (one QRS shape - usually scar re-entry), **polymorphic** (changing shape - ischaemia, channelopathy), **torsades de pointes** (polymorphic with long QT, twisting axis) and **bidirectional** (digoxin, CPVT, aconite). [ESC 2022]`,`**By bundle-branch pattern**: **LBBB-like VT** arises from the RV or septum (RVOT, ARVC); **RBBB-like VT** arises from the LV (post-MI, fascicular). [Harrison 22e]`,`**By substrate**: ischaemic, non-ischaemic cardiomyopathy, channelopathy, idiopathic (structurally normal heart). [ESC 2022]`,`**Haemodynamic status**: pulseless (cardiac arrest), unstable with pulse, or haemodynamically tolerated - this decides immediate treatment. [AHA 2025]`,`**Electrical storm** and **incessant VT** (continuous for hours despite interventions) are separate emergencies. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Diagnosis of broad-complex tachycardia`,points:[`Broad-complex tachycardia (QRS **over 120 ms**) is **VT in about 80%** of all cases and in **over 90%** of patients with prior MI or structural heart disease - treat as VT if in doubt. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada algorithm (1991)**: (1) **absence of an RS complex in all precordial leads** - VT; (2) **R-to-S interval over 100 ms** in any precordial lead - VT; (3) **AV dissociation** - VT; (4) **VT morphology criteria in V1-V2 and V6** - VT; otherwise SVT with aberrancy. [Brugada 1991]`,`**Diagnostic features of VT**: **AV dissociation, fusion beats, capture (Dressler) beats**, **precordial concordance** (all positive or all negative), **northwest axis** (-90 to 180 degrees) and QRS **over 140 ms (RBBB-like)** or **over 160 ms (LBBB-like)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Vereckei aVR algorithm**: an **initial R wave in aVR**, an initial r or q over 40 ms, a notch on the downstroke of a negative onset, or Vi/Vt ratio 1 or less indicates VT. [Vereckei 2008]`,`**History beats algorithms**: age over 35, previous MI, heart failure or cardiomyopathy make VT almost certain. [ESC 2022]`,`**Never give verapamil** to a broad-complex tachycardia - if it is VT, the negative inotropy and vasodilatation cause collapse; adenosine is safe only for a regular monomorphic tachycardia when the diagnosis is genuinely uncertain. [AHA 2025]`,`**Mimics**: SVT with bundle branch block, SVT with pre-excitation (antidromic AVRT, pre-excited AF), **hyperkalaemia** and **sodium-channel blocker toxicity** (TCA overdose) widening the QRS, and paced rhythms. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**Presentations**: palpitations, dizziness, breathlessness, chest pain, syncope, heart failure, ICD shock or cardiac arrest. [ESC 2022]`,`Ask about **prior MI, heart failure, cardiomyopathy, cardiac surgery**, and current drugs (QT-prolonging drugs, diuretics causing hypokalaemia, digoxin). [ESC 2022]`,`**Exertional syncope or palpitations** in a young person suggests HCM, ARVC, anomalous coronary, CPVT or LQT1. [ESC 2022]`,`**Family history** of sudden death under 50, drowning, unexplained road accidents, epilepsy or cardiomyopathy points to inherited disease. [ESC 2022]`,`**Examination during VT**: **irregular cannon a waves** and **variable S1 and pulse volume** reflect AV dissociation; look for shock, pulmonary oedema and altered sensorium. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Between episodes**: signs of heart failure, LV scar (displaced apex), HCM murmur, and stigmata of sarcoidosis or muscular dystrophy. [ESC 2022]`,`**PVCs** cause skipped beats, a thump, or a pause, often worse at rest; frequent PVCs may cause fatigue and breathlessness. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG of the tachycardia** whenever the patient is stable enough - it decides mechanism, origin and later ablation strategy. [ESC 2022]`,`**Bloods**: potassium (keep **4.5-5.0 mmol/L**), magnesium, calcium, troponin, TSH, renal function, digoxin level and drug screen where relevant. [ESC 2022]`,`**Sinus-rhythm ECG**: Q waves (old MI), QT, Brugada pattern, **epsilon waves and T inversion V1-V3** (ARVC), LVH, pre-excitation. [ESC 2022]`,`**Echocardiography** for LVEF, wall motion, HCM, valves; **cardiac MRI with late gadolinium enhancement** is recommended in new VA to define scar and non-ischaemic disease. [ESC 2022]`,`**Coronary angiography** for sustained VA with suspected ischaemia, and **urgent angiography** after resuscitated arrest with ST elevation. [ESC 2022]`,`**Holter or patch monitoring** quantifies **PVC burden** and NSVT; **exercise testing** for exercise-induced VA (CPVT, RVOT VT, ischaemia). [ESC 2022]`,`**Electrophysiology study (programmed stimulation)** risk-stratifies post-MI patients with LVEF 40% or less and NSVT, and guides ablation. [ESC 2022]`,`**Genetic testing** when an inherited cardiomyopathy or channelopathy is suspected, and **molecular autopsy** for young SCD victims. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Acute management of VT`,points:[`**Pulseless VT or VF**: CPR and **defibrillation** (biphasic 120-200 J or maximum), adrenaline 1 mg every 3-5 min after the third shock, **amiodarone 300 mg IV after the third shock and 150 mg after the fifth**, or **lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg**. [AHA 2025, ERC 2021]`,`**Unstable VT with a pulse** (shock, syncope, ischaemia, heart failure): **immediate synchronised cardioversion** under sedation (AHA: about 100 J biphasic, escalate); if it fails after three shocks, amiodarone 300 mg over 10-20 min and repeat. [AHA 2025, ERC 2021]`,`**Stable monomorphic VT**: **synchronised cardioversion is first-line** if sedation risk is low (Class I); **IV procainamide** should be considered (IIa, PROCAMIO) and **IV amiodarone** may be used when the diagnosis is uncertain or procainamide is unavailable, as in India. [ESC 2022]`,`**Amiodarone dosing**: **150 mg IV over 10 min** (repeat if needed) then **1 mg/min for 6 h and 0.5 mg/min for 18 h** (AHA), or **300 mg over 20-60 min then 900 mg over 24 h** (ESC/ERC); give via a large or central vein. [AHA 2025, ESC 2022]`,`**Lidocaine**: **1-1.5 mg/kg IV**, then 0.5-0.75 mg/kg every 5-10 min to 3 mg/kg, infusion 1-4 mg/min - preferred in **ischaemic VT**; watch for confusion and seizures. [AHA 2025]`,`**Idiopathic VT**: **fascicular VT** responds to **IV verapamil** and **RVOT VT** to **IV beta-blocker** or adenosine - only when the structurally normal heart and diagnosis are certain. [ESC 2022]`,`**Polymorphic VT**: with **normal QT** think ischaemia - beta-blocker, amiodarone and **urgent angiography**; with **long QT** treat as **torsades** - magnesium, potassium, raise heart rate (see the Long QT topic). [ESC 2022]`,`**Correct triggers**: K to 4.5-5.0, magnesium sulfate 2 g IV, stop QT-prolonging drugs, treat ischaemia and heart failure. [ESC 2022]`,`**After resuscitated arrest**: targeted temperature control (avoid fever), coronary angiography when indicated, and neuroprognostication not before 72 h. [AHA 2025]`]},{heading:`Electrical storm and PVCs`,points:[`**Storm**: admit to ICU, **mild to moderate sedation** (Class I) to blunt sympathetic drive, and deep sedation and intubation if needed. [ESC 2022]`,`**Antiarrhythmics in storm**: **beta-blocker (non-selective propranolol preferred) plus IV amiodarone** (IIa); lidocaine is added for ischaemic storm. [ESC 2022]`,`**Catheter ablation** is recommended for **storm or incessant monomorphic VT refractory to drugs** (Class I); stellate ganglion block or mechanical circulatory support in refractory cases. [ESC 2022]`,`**Check the ICD**: reprogram to avoid repeated shocks; repeated shocks for sinus tachycardia or AF are inappropriate and need rate control, not more amiodarone. [ESC 2022]`,`**PVCs in a normal heart**: reassure, cut caffeine and alcohol; **beta-blocker or non-dihydropyridine CCB** for symptoms; **catheter ablation first-line for symptomatic RVOT PVCs** (Class I). [ESC 2022]`,`**PVC-induced cardiomyopathy**: suspect when LVEF is reduced and **PVC burden is 10% or more**; ablation is recommended and LV function often recovers. [ESC 2022]`,`**High-risk PVCs**: short-coupled (R-on-T) PVCs triggering polymorphic VT, exercise-induced complex ectopy, or PVCs with structural disease warrant CMR and specialist review. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`ICD and long-term prevention`,points:[`**Secondary prevention (Class I)**: ICD for **survivors of VF or haemodynamically not-tolerated VT** without a reversible cause, if expected survival with good function is over 1 year. [ESC 2022]`,`**Primary prevention in ischaemic cardiomyopathy (Class I)**: ICD when **LVEF is 35% or less** with **NYHA II-III despite at least 3 months of optimal medical therapy**, at least **40 days after MI** and **90 days after revascularisation**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Primary prevention in non-ischaemic DCM**: ICD **should be considered (IIa)** with LVEF 35% or less after 3 months of therapy - downgraded after DANISH; also consider when LVEF under 50% with **two or more risk factors** (syncope, LGE on CMR, inducible VT, LMNA/PLN/FLNC/RBM20 variants). [ESC 2022]`,`**Do not implant an ICD within 40 days of MI** (DINAMIT, IRIS); reassess LVEF at **6-12 weeks**; a **wearable cardioverter-defibrillator** may bridge high-risk patients (IIb). [ESC 2022]`,`**Subcutaneous ICD** is an alternative when no pacing, CRT or anti-tachycardia pacing is needed - suitable for the young and those with poor venous access or high infection risk (IIa); the **extravascular (substernal) ICD** was approved in 2023. [ESC 2022]`,`**ICD programming**: long detection times and high rate cut-offs with **anti-tachycardia pacing** reduce inappropriate and unnecessary shocks (Class I). [ESC 2022]`,`**Drugs**: **beta-blockers and full heart-failure therapy** (ARNI, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor) reduce SCD; **amiodarone** or sotalol reduce shocks but not mortality; avoid class IC drugs in structural disease. [ESC 2022]`,`**Catheter ablation**: recommended over escalating antiarrhythmics for recurrent VT in ischaemic heart disease on amiodarone (Class I); **VANISH2 (2024-25)** found first-line ablation better than drugs in ischaemic cardiomyopathy. [ESC 2022, VANISH2]`,`**ICD not indicated**: life expectancy under 1 year, incessant VT until controlled, **NYHA IV refractory heart failure** not eligible for CRT, LVAD or transplant. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: cardioversion is safe at any stage; continue **beta-blockers in LQTS and CPVT** through pregnancy and postpartum; an ICD can be implanted with echo guidance if indicated; avoid amiodarone except when life-threatening. [ESC 2022]`,`**Elderly**: weigh ICD benefit against competing mortality and frailty; discuss **deactivation of shocks** in advance care planning. [ESC 2022]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: hyperkalaemia and dialysis shifts trigger VA; lidocaine accumulates less than procainamide; ICD benefit is less certain in dialysis. [ESC 2022]`,`**Athletes**: idiopathic RVOT ectopy is common; exclude ARVC, HCM and myocarditis before clearing for sport. [ESC 2020 Sports]`,`**Children**: CPVT and LQTS present as exercise-induced syncope often misdiagnosed as epilepsy. [ESC 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: if a defibrillator is unavailable for unstable VT, give **amiodarone 300 mg IV** and arrange transfer; **public access AEDs and community CPR** save more lives than any drug. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Sustained VT degenerates to **VF and SCD**; recurrence after a first VT is high without ablation or ICD. [ESC 2022]`,`**ICD reduces all-cause mortality** in secondary prevention by roughly a quarter to a third (AVID, CIDS, CASH) and in ischaemic primary prevention (MADIT-II, SCD-HeFT). [Harrison 22e]`,`**ICD complications**: inappropriate shocks (AF, sinus tachycardia, lead noise, T-wave oversensing), lead failure, infection, and **anxiety, depression and PTSD** after shocks. [ESC 2022]`,`**Amiodarone toxicity**: hypo- or hyperthyroidism, pulmonary fibrosis, liver injury, corneal deposits, photosensitivity, blue-grey skin, neuropathy - check **TFT and LFT every 6 months** and chest X-ray yearly. [ESC 2022]`,`**Electrical storm** carries a high early mortality and is a marker of advanced heart failure. [ESC 2022]`,`Idiopathic VT has an **excellent prognosis** and ablation cures most. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Referral, follow-up and the ICD patient in the OPD`,points:[`**Refer urgently**: any sustained VT, syncope with structural heart disease, NSVT with reduced LVEF, family history of young SCD, or high PVC burden with falling EF. [ESC 2022]`,`**ICD follow-up** every 3-6 months in person or remotely; **one shock with full recovery** - inform device clinic within days; **two or more shocks in 24 hours** or shock with symptoms - emergency. [ESC 2022]`,`**Magnet on an ICD** suspends tachycardia therapy (useful for repeated inappropriate shocks or surgery) but does not change pacing. [ESC 2022]`,`**Driving**: after secondary-prevention ICD or appropriate shock, private driving is restricted for **3 months**; professional driving is permanently barred. [ESC 2022]`,`**End-of-life**: discuss **deactivating shock therapy** with patients and families; it is ethically and legally a withdrawal of treatment, not euthanasia. [ESC 2022]`,`Monitor **potassium, magnesium, renal function, thyroid and liver tests** on amiodarone and diuretics; titrate heart-failure therapy (see Chronic heart failure topic). [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`SCD prevention, screening in the young and programme links`,points:[`**Pre-participation screening** of competitive athletes with history, examination and 12-lead ECG read by **international athlete ECG criteria** should be considered. [ESC 2022, ESC 2020 Sports]`,`**Unexplained sudden death under 50**: full **autopsy with histology and toxicology** and **retention of blood or tissue for molecular autopsy** are recommended (Class I). [ESC 2022]`,`**First-degree relatives of young SCD victims** need clinical evaluation - ECG, echo, exercise ECG, Holter, and **cascade genetic testing** when a pathogenic variant is found. [ESC 2022]`,`**Public access defibrillation** at sites where arrest is likely (stations, airports, stadiums, malls) and **community CPR training** are Class I recommendations. [ESC 2022]`,`The **Indian Resuscitation Council** promotes **Compression-Only Life Support (COLS)** for lay rescuers - call for help, push hard and fast at 100-120 per minute. [IRC 2017]`,`**Primary prevention of CAD** through NP-NCD screening, tobacco cessation (NTCP) and control of BP, diabetes and lipids is the largest lever against SCD in India. [NP-NCD]`,`**Counsel families**: avoid binge drinking and recreational drugs, and do not ignore exertional chest pain, syncope or palpitations before strenuous exercise. [ICMR 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2022 VA/SCD guideline**: cardioversion first-line for tolerated monomorphic VT, procainamide over amiodarone (IIa), CMR in new VA, defined electrical storm, and **DCM ICD downgraded to IIa** with risk-factor-based selection. [ESC 2022]`,`ESC 2022 introduced recommendations on **PVC-induced cardiomyopathy (burden 10% or more)**, first-line ablation of RVOT VT/PVC, and **public access defibrillation**. [ESC 2022]`,`**ESC 2023 cardiomyopathy guideline** uses genotype and CMR (LGE) with **risk calculators** (HCM Risk-SCD, LMNA, ARVC) for ICD decisions. [ESC 2023 CMP]`,`**VANISH2 (NEJM, 2024-25)**: in ischaemic cardiomyopathy with VT, **catheter ablation as initial therapy** reduced death, storm, shocks and treated VT compared with antiarrhythmic drugs. [VANISH2]`,`**Extravascular ICD** with a substernal lead was approved in 2023, adding anti-tachycardia pacing to the non-transvenous option. [FDA 2023]`,`**AHA 2025 resuscitation guidelines** retain amiodarone or lidocaine for shock-refractory VF/pVT and emphasise high-quality CPR and early defibrillation. [AHA 2025]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **approach to broad-complex tachycardia**, **ICD indications**, **management of stable versus unstable VT**, **electrical storm** and **SCD in the young**. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: diagnosing SVT with aberrancy because the patient is **haemodynamically stable** - stability does not exclude VT. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: **verapamil for broad-complex tachycardia** - can cause cardiovascular collapse if it is VT. [AHA 2025]`,`**Capture and fusion beats** and **AV dissociation** are the most specific VT signs; concordance is specific but insensitive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-blockers** are the only antiarrhythmic class that lowers sudden death; amiodarone is mortality-neutral (SCD-HeFT). [ESC 2022]`,`Viva: **no ICD within 40 days of MI** - reassess EF after revascularisation and therapy. [ESC 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for ventricular arrhythmias`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amiodarone (arrest)`,`300 mg IV bolus after 3rd shock; 150 mg after 5th`,`During resuscitation`,`Hypotension, bradycardia`],[`Amiodarone (VT with pulse)`,`150 mg IV over 10 min then 1 mg/min 6 h, 0.5 mg/min 18 h (AHA); or 300 mg then 900 mg/24 h (ESC/ERC)`,`Max about 2.2 g in 24 h; oral 200 mg three times daily then taper to 200 mg daily`,`Phlebitis (central line), hypotension; long term thyroid, lung, liver, eye, skin toxicity, QT prolongation`],[`Lidocaine`,`1-1.5 mg/kg IV, then 0.5-0.75 mg/kg every 5-10 min; infusion 1-4 mg/min`,`Max 3 mg/kg bolus total; 24-48 h`,`Confusion, perioral numbness, seizures; reduce in liver failure and elderly`],[`Procainamide`,`10-17 mg/kg IV at 20-50 mg/min`,`Stop at hypotension, QRS widening over 50% or max dose`,`Hypotension, QT prolongation; limited availability in India`],[`Magnesium sulfate`,`2 g IV over 10-15 min (faster if pulseless)`,`Repeat once; infusion if recurrent`,`Flushing, hypotension; for torsades and hypomagnesaemia`],[`Propranolol (storm)`,`40 mg oral every 6 h or 1 mg IV every 5 min to 3-5 mg`,`Titrate to heart rate and BP`,`Hypotension, bronchospasm; avoid in cardiogenic shock`],[`Metoprolol / bisoprolol`,`Metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg daily; bisoprolol 1.25-10 mg daily`,`Lifelong in HFrEF and post-MI`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; do not stop abruptly`],[`Verapamil (fascicular VT only)`,`5-10 mg IV over 2 min`,`Only with confirmed structurally normal heart`,`Collapse if given for scar VT; never for undiagnosed broad tachycardia`]]},{heading:`VT versus SVT with aberrancy`,columns:[`Feature`,`Favours VT`,`Favours SVT with aberrancy`],rows:[[`History`,`Age over 35, prior MI, heart failure, cardiomyopathy`,`Young, recurrent episodes since youth, known BBB or pre-excitation`],[`AV relationship`,`AV dissociation, fusion and capture beats`,`1:1 with P before each QRS`],[`QRS width`,`Over 140 ms (RBBB-like) or over 160 ms (LBBB-like)`,`Typical BBB width, often under 140 ms`],[`Axis`,`Northwest (-90 to 180 degrees)`,`Normal or typical BBB axis`],[`Precordial leads`,`Concordance; no RS complex; R-to-S over 100 ms`,`Typical RBBB or LBBB morphology`],[`aVR`,`Initial R wave in aVR`,`Initial rS or Q in aVR`],[`Comparison with sinus ECG`,`Different QRS from baseline`,`Identical to baseline BBB`]]},{heading:`ICD indications (ESC 2022)`,columns:[`Situation`,`Recommendation`,`Class`],rows:[[`Survived VF or haemodynamically not-tolerated VT, no reversible cause`,`ICD`,`I`],[`Ischaemic cardiomyopathy, NYHA II-III, LVEF 35% or less after 3 months OMT`,`ICD (at least 40 days after MI)`,`I`],[`Non-ischaemic DCM, LVEF 35% or less after 3 months OMT`,`ICD should be considered`,`IIa`],[`DCM LVEF under 50% with 2 or more risk factors (syncope, LGE, inducible VT, high-risk genes)`,`ICD should be considered`,`IIa`],[`CAD, LVEF 40% or less, NSVT, inducible sustained VT at EP study`,`ICD`,`I`],[`Early post-MI (under 40 days) high-risk`,`Wearable defibrillator may be considered`,`IIb`],[`No pacing or ATP need, young or poor venous access`,`Subcutaneous ICD alternative`,`IIa`],[`Life expectancy under 1 year or NYHA IV not eligible for CRT/LVAD/transplant`,`No ICD`,`III`]]},{heading:`Causes of sudden cardiac death by age`,columns:[`Age group`,`Common causes`,`Key screening test`],rows:[[`Under 35 years`,`HCM, ARVC, channelopathies (LQTS, Brugada, CPVT), myocarditis, anomalous coronaries, WPW, drugs; SADS in about a third`,`ECG, echo, exercise ECG, family screening, molecular autopsy`],[`35 years and over`,`Coronary artery disease (acute MI or old scar) in 75-80%; DCM, HCM, valve disease`,`Risk factor screening, ECG, echo for LVEF`],[`Athletes`,`HCM, ARVC, anomalous coronary, myocarditis, premature CAD in masters athletes`,`Pre-participation ECG with athlete criteria`],[`Any age, toxic`,`QT-prolonging drugs, aconite, organophosphates, cocaine, hypokalaemia`,`QTc, electrolytes, drug history`]]},{heading:`Investigations in ventricular arrhythmia`,columns:[`Test`,`What it shows`,`Cut-off or action`],rows:[[`12-lead ECG of tachycardia`,`VT versus SVT, origin, morphology`,`AV dissociation, fusion, capture = VT`],[`Potassium and magnesium`,`Reversible trigger`,`Keep K 4.5-5.0 mmol/L; give magnesium`],[`Echocardiography`,`LVEF and structural disease`,`LVEF 35% or less: ICD evaluation`],[`Cardiac MRI`,`Scar (LGE), ARVC, sarcoid, myocarditis`,`LGE adds SCD risk in DCM`],[`Holter`,`PVC burden, NSVT`,`PVC burden 10% or more: risk of cardiomyopathy`],[`Coronary angiography`,`Ischaemic substrate`,`Urgent if arrest with ST elevation`],[`EP study`,`Inducible sustained VT`,`Post-MI LVEF 40% or less with NSVT: ICD if inducible`]]}],redFlags:[`Any broad-complex tachycardia - treat as VT and manage in a monitored setting with a defibrillator`,`VT with hypotension, chest pain, heart failure, syncope or altered sensorium - immediate synchronised cardioversion`,`Exertional syncope or syncope with structural heart disease or a family history of young sudden death`,`Two or more ICD shocks in 24 hours or electrical storm`,`Polymorphic VT - check QT, potassium, magnesium, and look for acute ischaemia`,`NSVT or frequent PVCs with reduced LVEF`,`Resuscitated cardiac arrest - post-arrest care, angiography and ICD evaluation`],pearls:[`Broad-complex tachycardia is VT until proven otherwise - especially after MI, where it is VT in over 90%.`,`Stability does not exclude VT.`,`AV dissociation, capture and fusion beats are the hallmarks of VT.`,`Brugada steps: no RS, RS over 100 ms, AV dissociation, morphology.`,`Never verapamil for a broad-complex tachycardia - except proven fascicular VT.`,`Beta-blockers are the only antiarrhythmics that reduce sudden death; amiodarone reduces shocks, not mortality.`,`ICD: survivors always; primary prevention when LVEF is 35% or less after 3 months of optimal therapy - not within 40 days of MI.`,`Storm: sedate, beta-block, amiodarone, ablate.`,`Magnet on an ICD stops shocks, not pacing.`],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Define sudden cardiac death. Discuss its causes, and outline the approach to a broad-complex tachycardia and the indications for an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD).`,openingLines:[`Sudden cardiac death is unexpected natural death of presumed cardiac cause within 1 hour of symptom onset when witnessed, or within 24 hours of last being seen well when unwitnessed (ESC 2022).`,`It is usually due to VT or VF on a substrate of coronary disease (75-80% over 35 years) or cardiomyopathy and channelopathy in the young, and is prevented by treating the substrate, beta-blockers and an ICD in selected patients.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and burden`,points:[`Definition as above; 15-20% of all deaths; most at home.`,`Indian context: earlier CAD, poor bystander CPR, ICMR 2023 young sudden death study.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`CAD - acute ischaemia and old scar (75-80%).`,`Cardiomyopathies: DCM, HCM, ARVC, sarcoid; myocarditis.`,`Channelopathies: LQTS, Brugada, CPVT, short QT; WPW.`,`Drugs and toxins: QT-prolonging drugs, aconite, cocaine; electrolytes.`]},{heading:`Approach to broad-complex tachycardia`,points:[`Assess pulse and stability; pulseless - ALS with defibrillation.`,`Unstable - synchronised cardioversion.`,`Stable - 12-lead ECG; Brugada criteria (no RS, RS over 100 ms, AV dissociation, morphology), capture and fusion beats.`,`Treat as VT: cardioversion or amiodarone 150-300 mg IV, lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg; correct K and Mg.`,`Never verapamil.`]},{heading:`ICD indications (ESC 2022)`,points:[`Secondary: survived VF or unstable VT without reversible cause (Class I).`,`Primary: ischaemic, NYHA II-III, LVEF 35% or less after 3 months OMT, over 40 days post-MI (I); non-ischaemic (IIa).`,`Inherited disease with high-risk features (HCM risk score, LQTS with arrest).`,`Subcutaneous ICD when no pacing needed; wearable bridge early post-MI.`]},{heading:`Prevention in the community`,points:[`Public access AEDs, compression-only CPR training, athlete ECG screening, family screening and molecular autopsy.`,`NP-NCD risk factor control, tobacco cessation.`]}],mustDraw:[`Brugada algorithm flow chart for broad-complex tachycardia`,`Tachycardia algorithm: pulseless, unstable, stable branches`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:3},{part:`Broad-complex tachycardia approach`,marks:4},{part:`ICD indications and prevention`,marks:3}],keywords:[`sudden cardiac death`,`VT`,`Brugada criteria`,`amiodarone`,`ICD`,`ESC 2022`,`LVEF 35%`]},{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Short note: electrical storm.`,openingLines:[`Electrical storm is three or more sustained episodes of VT or VF, or appropriate ICD shocks, within 24 hours (ESC 2022).`,`It is a medical emergency with high early mortality, managed by sedation, beta-blockade, amiodarone, correction of triggers and catheter ablation.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes and triggers`,points:[`Ischaemia, decompensated heart failure, hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia, QT-prolonging drugs, thyrotoxicosis, infection.`,`Inappropriate shocks (AF, lead fracture) must be distinguished.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`ICU, monitor, interrogate and reprogram the ICD; magnet suspends shocks if inappropriate.`,`Mild to moderate sedation; intubation if refractory.`,`Propranolol plus IV amiodarone; lidocaine in ischaemia.`,`Correct K to 4.5-5.0 and magnesium; treat ischaemia and heart failure.`,`Catheter ablation for refractory monomorphic VT; stellate ganglion block or mechanical support as bridge.`]},{heading:`Torsades storm`,points:[`Magnesium, potassium, stop QT drugs, overdrive pacing or isoprenaline (acquired LQT).`]}],mustDraw:[`Ladder: sedation, beta-blocker, amiodarone, ablation, mechanical support`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and triggers`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:4}],keywords:[`electrical storm`,`propranolol`,`amiodarone`,`sedation`,`ablation`]}],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-q1`,stem:`A 64-year-old man who had an anterior MI 5 years ago presents with palpitations. ECG shows a regular broad-complex tachycardia at 170 bpm. BP is 118/78 mmHg and he is alert. What is the most likely diagnosis?`,options:[`SVT with right bundle branch block aberrancy`,`Sinus tachycardia with left bundle branch block`,`Ventricular tachycardia`,`Antidromic AVRT`],answer:2,explanation:`In a patient with prior MI, a broad-complex tachycardia is VT in over 90% of cases, arising from re-entry around the healed scar; normal BP and alertness do not argue against VT. SVT with aberrancy and antidromic AVRT are far less likely in this substrate, and sinus tachycardia at 170 with LBBB in a resting man is implausible.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-q2`,stem:`Which ECG finding during a broad-complex tachycardia most strongly supports ventricular tachycardia?`,options:[`Capture and fusion beats`,`QRS duration of 125 ms`,`Typical RBBB pattern identical to the sinus-rhythm ECG`,`Heart rate of 150 bpm`],answer:0,explanation:`Capture beats (a sinus beat conducting normally) and fusion beats (sinus and ventricular wavefronts merging) prove AV dissociation and are highly specific for VT. A QRS of 125 ms is only modestly widened, a pattern identical to baseline BBB favours SVT with aberrancy, and the rate does not distinguish VT from SVT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-q3`,stem:`A 58-year-old man had an anterior STEMI treated with primary PCI 3 weeks ago. LVEF is 30% and he is on full heart-failure therapy. When should an ICD for primary prevention be considered?`,options:[`Immediately, before discharge`,`At 3 weeks, as LVEF is under 35%`,`Only if he survives a cardiac arrest`,`After reassessing LVEF at least 40 days after MI and 90 days after revascularisation on optimal therapy`],answer:3,explanation:`LVEF often recovers after revascularisation and therapy, and early ICD implantation after MI did not reduce mortality (DINAMIT, IRIS), so ESC 2022 advises reassessment after at least 40 days post-MI (90 days after revascularisation) and 3 months of optimal therapy. Implanting now ignores this recovery, and waiting for an arrest abandons proven primary prevention; a wearable defibrillator may bridge the gap.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-q4`,stem:`A patient with an ICD for ischaemic cardiomyopathy receives four appropriate shocks for monomorphic VT in 12 hours. Potassium and magnesium are normal. Besides sedation and amiodarone, which drug is recommended?`,options:[`Flecainide`,`Non-selective beta-blocker such as propranolol`,`Digoxin`,`IV verapamil`],answer:1,explanation:`This is electrical storm, driven by sympathetic activation; ESC 2022 recommends sedation and a beta-blocker (non-selective propranolol preferred) with IV amiodarone, followed by catheter ablation if refractory. Flecainide is contraindicated in scar-related VT (CAST), digoxin has no antiarrhythmic role here, and verapamil can cause collapse in scar VT.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-ventricular-arrhythmias-scd-q5`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman has frequent palpitations. Holter shows PVCs with LBBB morphology and inferior axis, burden 18%, and echo shows LVEF 42% with no scar on cardiac MRI. What is the most appropriate management?`,options:[`Reassurance only, as PVCs are benign`,`Flecainide long term`,`Catheter ablation of the PVC focus`,`Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator`],answer:2,explanation:`LBBB morphology with inferior axis indicates RVOT origin, and a burden of 10% or more with reduced LVEF and no scar suggests PVC-induced cardiomyopathy; ESC 2022 recommends catheter ablation, after which LV function usually recovers. Reassurance ignores the cardiomyopathy, flecainide is less effective and undesirable with LV dysfunction, and an ICD does not treat a reversible PVC cardiomyopathy.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Ventricular arrhythmias; Cardiovascular collapse, cardiac arrest and sudden cardiac death`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Ventricular arrhythmias; Cardiac arrest and sudden cardiac death`,`Zeppenfeld K et al. 2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death. Eur Heart J 2022`,`Arbelo E et al. 2023 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiomyopathies. Eur Heart J 2023`,`American Heart Association 2025 Guidelines for CPR and Emergency Cardiovascular Care - Adult Advanced Life Support`,`Brugada P et al. A new approach to the differential diagnosis of a regular tachycardia with a wide QRS complex. Circulation 1991`,`ICMR-National Institute of Epidemiology. Factors associated with unexplained sudden deaths among adults aged 18-45 years in India - a multicentric matched case-control study. Indian J Med Res 2023`,`Indian Resuscitation Council. Compression Only Life Support (COLS) guidelines, 2017`]}),l.push({id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt`,title:`Long QT, Brugada and drug-induced QT prolongation`,oneLiner:`**Cardiac channelopathies** are inherited ion-channel disorders in structurally normal hearts that cause **syncope and sudden death from polymorphic VT or VF** - **long QT syndrome** (QTc **480 ms or more**, or Schwartz score over 3; treated with **non-selective beta-blockers**), **Brugada syndrome** (**coved type 1 ST elevation in V1-V2**; avoid trigger drugs and treat fever), **CPVT**, short QT and early repolarisation syndrome - while **acquired QT prolongation** from drugs and low potassium or magnesium causes **torsades de pointes**, treated with **IV magnesium 2 g**, potassium to 4.5-5.0 mmol/L and stopping the drug.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`long QT syndrome`,`LQTS`,`QTc`,`Bazett`,`Fridericia`,`torsades de pointes`,`Schwartz score`,`Romano-Ward`,`Jervell and Lange-Nielsen`,`CredibleMeds`,`Brugada syndrome`,`ajmaline challenge`,`CPVT`,`short QT syndrome`,`early repolarisation syndrome`,`J wave`,`magnesium sulfate`,`nadolol`,`SCN5A`,`KCNQ1 KCNH2`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Channelopathies** (primary electrical diseases) are genetic disorders of cardiac ion channels or their regulators that cause ventricular arrhythmias **without structural heart disease**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Congenital LQTS** has a prevalence of about **1 in 2000**; **Brugada syndrome** about **1 in 2000** (higher in Southeast Asia); **CPVT** about **1 in 10,000**; short QT and early repolarisation syndrome are rare. [ESC 2022]`,`Channelopathies explain a large share of **autopsy-negative sudden death in the young (SADS)**, where cardiac genetic testing finds a cause in a minority. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada syndrome** is about **8-10 times commoner in men**, presents around **40 years** and underlies **sudden unexplained nocturnal death syndrome** in Southeast Asia (Lai Tai, Pokkuri, Bangungut). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug-induced QT prolongation** is far commoner than congenital LQTS and is a practical OPD and ward problem - antimicrobials, antiemetics, antipsychotics and **NTEP drug-resistant TB regimens** all prolong QT. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`Indian population data are limited to case series from tertiary centres; **fever-induced Brugada pattern** is increasingly reported in febrile illnesses such as dengue and malaria. [Indian case series]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and genetics`,points:[`**LQT1 (KCNQ1, loss of IKs)** about 35%; **LQT2 (KCNH2/hERG, loss of IKr)** about 30%; **LQT3 (SCN5A, gain of late sodium current)** about 10%; together over 90% of genotyped LQTS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Romano-Ward syndrome** is autosomal dominant LQTS without deafness; **Jervell and Lange-Nielsen** is autosomal recessive (KCNQ1/KCNE1), with **congenital sensorineural deafness** and very long QT. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Andersen-Tawil (LQT7, KCNJ2)**: periodic paralysis, dysmorphism, prominent U waves and bidirectional VT; **Timothy syndrome (CACNA1C)**: syndactyly, autism and extreme QT prolongation. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada syndrome**: **SCN5A loss-of-function** variants in about **20-25%**; most patients have no identifiable single-gene cause. [ESC 2022]`,`**CPVT**: **RYR2** (autosomal dominant, about 60%) and **CASQ2** (recessive) cause diastolic calcium leak from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. [ESC 2022]`,`**Short QT syndrome**: gain-of-function in **KCNH2, KCNQ1 or KCNJ2** potassium channels. [ESC 2022]`,`**Acquired QT prolongation**: drugs, **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia**, bradycardia and AV block, hypothyroidism, hypothermia, starvation and anorexia, **subarachnoid haemorrhage**, myocardial ischaemia, Takotsubo, cirrhosis and **organophosphate poisoning**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Risk factors for drug-induced torsades**: **female sex**, age over 65, low K or Mg, bradycardia, heart failure or LVH, renal or hepatic impairment, high or rapid IV doses, **drug interactions (CYP3A4 inhibitors)**, baseline long QT and **latent congenital LQTS**. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`The **QT interval** reflects ventricular depolarisation plus repolarisation; repolarisation depends on outward potassium currents **IKr and IKs** balanced against inward late sodium and calcium currents. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Loss of potassium current or gain of late sodium current** prolongs the action potential, permitting **early afterdepolarisations (EADs)** that trigger **torsades de pointes**, especially after a **pause (short-long-short sequence)**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Transmural dispersion of repolarisation** (M cells repolarising later than epicardium and endocardium) provides the substrate for re-entry, and the twisting QRS axis of torsades. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Almost all QT-prolonging drugs block hERG (IKr)**; normal individuals tolerate this because of **repolarisation reserve** (IKs), which is lost in LQTS, hypokalaemia and with multiple drugs. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Gene-specific triggers**: LQT1 - **exercise, especially swimming** (IKs needed to shorten QT with sympathetic drive); LQT2 - **sudden noise (alarm clock, phone), emotion, postpartum**; LQT3 - **rest and sleep** at slow heart rates. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada syndrome**: reduced sodium current in the **right ventricular outflow tract epicardium** with a prominent Ito creates loss of the action potential dome, ST elevation and phase 2 re-entry; **fever, vagal tone and sodium-channel blockers** worsen it. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**CPVT**: catecholamines increase diastolic calcium leak via RYR2, producing **delayed afterdepolarisations** and **bidirectional or polymorphic VT** during exercise or emotion. [ESC 2022]`,`**Hypokalaemia** reduces IKr conductance paradoxically and **magnesium** suppresses EADs by blocking L-type calcium current - why magnesium works even when serum magnesium is normal. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]},{heading:`Measuring the QT and the cut-offs`,points:[`Measure QT in **lead II or V5** from QRS onset to **T-wave end by the tangent method** (steepest downslope tangent meeting the baseline), excluding a separate U wave; average 3-5 beats. [AHA 2009 ECG standards]`,`**Bazett**: **QTc = QT / square root of RR (s)** - over-corrects at fast rates and under-corrects at slow rates. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fridericia**: **QTc = QT / cube root of RR** - more accurate at heart rates outside 60-100 and used in drug safety and **NTEP DR-TB monitoring (QTcF)**. [NTEP PMDT, AHA 2020]`,`**Framingham** (QT + 0.154 x (1 - RR)) and **Hodges** (QT + 1.75 x (HR - 60)) are linear alternatives; use the same formula consistently for serial ECGs. [AHA 2009 ECG standards]`,`**Prolonged QTc**: **450 ms or more in men and 460 ms or more in women**; **over 500 ms** or an **increase over 60 ms** from baseline markedly raises torsades risk and means stop or change the drug. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`In **wide QRS (BBB or pacing)** use the **JT interval** or subtract the excess QRS width; in AF average QT over 10 beats. [AHA 2009 ECG standards]`,`**Short QT**: QTc **360 ms or less** with supporting features, or **320 ms or less** alone, suggests short QT syndrome. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Diagnosis and classification`,points:[`**LQTS is diagnosed** with **QTc 480 ms or more** on repeated 12-lead ECGs, **Schwartz (LQTS risk) score over 3**, or a **pathogenic LQTS variant** irrespective of QT (Class I). [ESC 2022]`,`**LQTS should be considered** with **QTc 460 ms or more** plus **arrhythmic syncope** in the absence of secondary causes (IIa). [ESC 2022]`,`**Schwartz score**: QTc 480 or more (3), 460-479 (2), 450-459 in men (1); torsades (2); T-wave alternans (1); notched T in 3 leads (1); low heart rate for age (0.5); syncope with stress (2) or without (1); congenital deafness (0.5); family history (1 or 0.5). Score **3.5 or more = high probability**. [Schwartz 2011]`,`**Brugada type 1 (coved)**: **ST elevation of 2 mm or more** with a **coved shape and negative T wave** in at least one right precordial lead (V1-V2, recorded in 2nd, 3rd or 4th intercostal space) - the only diagnostic pattern. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada type 2 (saddleback)**: ST elevation with a saddleback shape and positive or flat T - **not diagnostic**; a sodium-channel blocker test (ajmaline, flecainide or procainamide) may convert it to type 1. [ESC 2022]`,`**Spontaneous type 1 = Brugada syndrome**; a **drug- or fever-induced type 1** needs additional features (arrhythmic syncope or nocturnal agonal breathing, family history of BrS or sudden death under 45 with negative autopsy). [ESC 2022]`,`**CPVT** is diagnosed by **exercise-induced bidirectional or polymorphic VT** in a structurally normal heart, or a pathogenic RYR2/CASQ2 variant; the resting ECG is normal. [ESC 2022]`,`**Early repolarisation pattern**: **J-point elevation of 1 mm or more** with notching or slurring in **two or more inferior and/or lateral leads**; **ER syndrome** adds resuscitated unexplained VF or polymorphic VT. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Presentations: **syncope, seizures, nocturnal agonal breathing, palpitations, aborted cardiac arrest or sudden death** - often in children and young adults. [ESC 2022]`,`**Arrhythmic syncope** is abrupt without prodrome, often with exercise, emotion, noise or in sleep - and is frequently **misdiagnosed as epilepsy**; drowning while swimming should suggest LQT1 or CPVT. [ESC 2022]`,`Ask about **triggers**: swimming or exertion (LQT1, CPVT), alarm clock or postpartum (LQT2), sleep (LQT3, Brugada), **fever** and heavy meals or alcohol (Brugada). [ESC 2022]`,`Take a **meticulous drug history** including over-the-counter and traditional remedies: macrolides, quinolones, antimalarials, domperidone, ondansetron, antipsychotics, antidepressants, methadone and TB drugs. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Family history**: sudden death, drowning, unexplained accidents, epilepsy or deafness in relatives, and early pacemakers. [ESC 2022]`,`Examination is usually **normal**; look for **deafness** (Jervell and Lange-Nielsen), syndactyly (Timothy), periodic paralysis and dysmorphism (Andersen-Tawil), and bradycardia. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Congenital versus acquired long QT**: acquired has a clear drug, electrolyte or bradycardic cause and normalises on removal, but a persistent borderline QT after correction suggests latent congenital LQTS. [ESC 2022]`,`**Torsades versus polymorphic VT with normal QT**: polymorphic VT with a normal QT suggests **acute ischaemia** or CPVT and needs angiography, not just magnesium. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada phenocopies**: hyperkalaemia, **RV ischaemia**, pulmonary embolism, pectus excavatum, mediastinal tumour, high V1-V2 electrode placement, and **ARVC** - all mimic the right precordial pattern. [ESC 2022]`,`**Early repolarisation pattern** in fit young men is usually benign; distinguish from **STEMI** (reciprocal change, dynamic evolution) and **pericarditis** (diffuse ST elevation with PR depression). [ESC 2022]`,`**Epilepsy versus arrhythmic syncope**: any 'seizure' with exertion, noise, swimming or sleep in a young patient needs an ECG with QTc before an antiepileptic is started. [ESC 2018 Syncope]`,`**RBBB** produces rSR prime in V1 without the coved ST elevation and negative T wave of Brugada type 1. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Serial 12-lead ECGs** with careful manual QT measurement - automated QTc is unreliable when T waves are flat or notched. [ESC 2022]`,`**Bloods**: potassium, magnesium, calcium, TSH, renal and liver function, glucose; drug levels where relevant. [ESC 2022]`,`**Exercise testing**: failure of QT to shorten and **QTc 480 ms or more at the 4th minute of recovery** supports LQT1; exercise reproduces ventricular ectopy and bidirectional VT in **CPVT**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Holter** for QT dynamics, T-wave alternans and nocturnal Brugada patterns; **high precordial leads (2nd-3rd intercostal space)** increase sensitivity for Brugada type 1. [ESC 2022]`,`**Sodium-channel blocker challenge** (ajmaline 1 mg/kg, flecainide or procainamide) under monitoring with resuscitation equipment unmasks type 1 Brugada; **ajmaline is not marketed in India**, limiting testing to specialist centres. [ESC 2022]`,`**Echocardiography and cardiac MRI** to exclude structural disease (ARVC, cardiomyopathy) before labelling a channelopathy. [ESC 2022]`,`**Genetic testing** is recommended in LQTS, CPVT and Brugada (SCN5A) probands with genetic counselling, followed by **cascade testing of relatives** (Class I). [ESC 2022, EHRA/HRS 2022 genetic testing]`]},{heading:`Management of torsades de pointes (emergency)`,points:[`**Pulseless or sustained torsades**: **unsynchronised defibrillation** (polymorphic VT cannot be synchronised) and ALS. [AHA 2025]`,`**Magnesium sulfate 2 g IV** (8 mmol, 4 mL of 50% diluted) over 10-15 min (faster if pulseless), repeated once if needed, then infusion - **effective even with normal serum magnesium**. [ESC 2022, AHA 2025]`,`**Potassium** replacement to **4.5-5.0 mmol/L**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Stop all QT-prolonging drugs** and correct calcium, bradycardia and hypothyroidism. [ESC 2022]`,`**Increase heart rate** to shorten QT and prevent pauses: **temporary overdrive pacing** (atrial or ventricular, around 90-110 bpm) or **isoprenaline infusion** in **acquired, pause-dependent** torsades. [ESC 2022]`,`**Avoid isoprenaline in congenital adrenergic-dependent LQTS** (LQT1, LQT2) - use beta-blockade and pacing instead. [ESC 2022]`,`**Lidocaine** may help (especially LQT3); **do not give amiodarone, sotalol or procainamide**, which further prolong QT. [ESC 2022]`,`**Monitor** in a high-dependency bed until the QTc falls below 500 ms and the offending drug has washed out. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`]},{heading:`Long-term management of channelopathies`,points:[`**LQTS - all patients**: avoid QT-prolonging drugs (**CredibleMeds list**), correct electrolytes during vomiting and diarrhoea, and avoid gene-specific triggers (competitive swimming in LQT1, loud alarms in LQT2). [ESC 2022]`,`**Beta-blockers** are recommended in LQTS with QT prolongation (Class I) and considered in genotype-positive normal-QT patients (IIa); **nadolol (1-2 mg/kg/day) or propranolol (2-4 mg/kg/day)** are preferred - **propranolol is the practical choice in India**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Mexiletine** is recommended in **LQT3** patients with prolonged QT, as it blocks the late sodium current. [ESC 2022]`,`**ICD** in LQTS for **survivors of cardiac arrest** (Class I) and for **arrhythmic syncope or VT despite adequate beta-blockade**; **left cardiac sympathetic denervation (LCSD)** when beta-blockers fail, are not tolerated, or ICD shocks recur. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada syndrome**: avoid trigger drugs (**brugadadrugs.org**), excess alcohol and large meals, and **treat any fever promptly with paracetamol** (Class I); **ICD for aborted arrest or spontaneous sustained VT** (Class I) and should be considered for **type 1 pattern with arrhythmic syncope** (IIa). [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada adjuncts**: **quinidine** for patients who need but decline an ICD or have recurrent shocks; **isoprenaline infusion for electrical storm**; **epicardial RVOT ablation** for recurrent appropriate shocks. [ESC 2022]`,`**CPVT**: avoid competitive and strenuous exercise and emotional stress; **beta-blocker (nadolol preferred)** for all (Class I); add **flecainide** for recurrent events; **LCSD**; ICD only with optimised drugs, as shocks can trigger catecholamine storms. [ESC 2022]`,`**Short QT**: ICD for survivors; quinidine (prolongs QT) as alternative; **ER syndrome**: ICD for survivors, isoprenaline for storm, quinidine for recurrence; **asymptomatic ER pattern needs no treatment**. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Drug-induced QT prolongation - the practical list`,points:[`**CredibleMeds categories**: **known risk of torsades**, **possible risk**, **conditional risk** (only with other risk factors), and **drugs to avoid in congenital LQTS**. [CredibleMeds]`,`**Antiarrhythmics**: quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide, **sotalol**, dofetilide, ibutilide, dronedarone; **amiodarone prolongs QT but rarely causes torsades**. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Antimicrobials**: **azithromycin, clarithromycin, erythromycin, moxifloxacin, levofloxacin, ciprofloxacin, fluconazole, ketoconazole**, pentamidine, and antimalarials (**chloroquine, hydroxychloroquine, quinine, mefloquine**, halofantrine). [CredibleMeds]`,`**DR-TB drugs**: **bedaquiline, delamanid, moxifloxacin and clofazimine** - NTEP requires baseline and serial ECGs and stops QT-prolonging drugs if **QTcF exceeds 500 ms** on repeat ECG. [NTEP PMDT guidelines]`,`**Psychotropics and others**: **haloperidol (IV)**, chlorpromazine, thioridazine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, **citalopram and escitalopram (dose-dependent)**, tricyclics, **methadone**, lithium. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Antiemetics and GI**: **domperidone** (keep to 10 mg up to three times daily, short course), **IV ondansetron** in high doses, and cisapride (withdrawn). [CredibleMeds]`,`**Oncology**: arsenic trioxide, vandetanib, nilotinib, ribociclib; **avoid combining two QT drugs** - for example **azithromycin plus domperidone or hydroxychloroquine** - and inhibitors of their metabolism. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Safe practice**: baseline ECG before QT drugs in at-risk patients, correct K and Mg, use the lowest dose, avoid rapid IV infusion, and **recheck ECG** after starting or increasing the drug. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: continue **beta-blockers in LQTS and CPVT** throughout pregnancy and especially **postpartum** (LQT2 risk peaks in the 9 months after delivery). [ESC 2022]`,`**Children**: CPVT and LQT1 present in childhood with exercise or swimming syncope; neonatal LQTS may present as 2:1 AV block or **sudden infant death**. [ESC 2022]`,`**Elderly and inpatients**: multiple QT drugs, diuretic hypokalaemia, renal impairment and bradycardia combine to cause torsades on the wards. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**Fever in Brugada**: in dengue, malaria or COVID, treat fever early, avoid trigger drugs and monitor ECG in known Brugada patients. [ESC 2022]`,`**Anaesthesia**: avoid **propofol infusions and bupivacaine** in Brugada where possible; avoid QT drugs and keep electrolytes normal in LQTS. [ESC 2022]`,`**Sport**: patients on treatment may participate in many sports after **shared decision-making** with an expert; competitive swimming is discouraged in LQT1. [ESC 2020 Sports]`,`**Organophosphate poisoning**: QTc prolongation predicts ventricular arrhythmia - monitor ECG, correct electrolytes, give magnesium for torsades. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**Untreated symptomatic LQTS** carries high mortality; **beta-blockers reduce cardiac events by about two-thirds** in LQT1 and LQT2. [ESC 2022]`,`**Highest-risk LQTS**: QTc **over 500 ms**, prior arrest, syncope on beta-blockers, LQT2 women, LQT3 men, JLN syndrome; the **1-2-3 LQTS risk calculator** estimates 5-year risk. [ESC 2022]`,`**Brugada risk**: annual arrhythmic event rate is highest after aborted arrest, intermediate with spontaneous type 1 and syncope, and low in asymptomatic drug-induced type 1. [ESC 2022]`,`**Beta-blocker adherence** is the commonest reason for breakthrough events in LQTS and CPVT - check pulse response and supply at each visit. [ESC 2022]`,`**Follow-up** in a specialist inherited-arrhythmia clinic every 6-12 months: ECG, QTc, dose adjustment for growth in children, ICD checks. [ESC 2022]`,`**Psychological burden** of genetic diagnosis and ICDs in the young needs counselling. [ESC 2022]`]},{heading:`Family screening, counselling and prevention`,points:[`**Screen first-degree relatives** of probands and of young SADS victims: history, **12-lead ECG (with high precordial leads)**, exercise test, Holter, and **cascade genetic testing** when a pathogenic variant is known. [ESC 2022]`,`**Genetic counselling** before and after testing is recommended for all families (Class I). [ESC 2022]`,`Give patients a **written list of drugs to avoid** (CredibleMeds, brugadadrugs.org) and ask them to show it to every prescriber and pharmacist. [ESC 2022]`,`Teach families **CPR and AED use**, and provide an **AED at home or school** where risk is high. [ESC 2022]`,`**Primary care prevention**: check QTc before combining QT drugs, avoid azithromycin-domperidone combinations, and correct potassium in diarrhoea and on diuretics. [AHA 2020 Drug-induced arrhythmia]`,`**NTEP link**: ECG monitoring of patients on bedaquiline-containing DR-TB regimens at district DR-TB centres. [NTEP PMDT guidelines]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2022 VA/SCD guideline**: LQTS diagnosis at **QTc 480 ms or more** or risk score over 3; **mexiletine for LQT3**; LCSD for refractory cases; **1-2-3 risk calculator** for ICD discussion. [ESC 2022]`,`ESC 2022 tightened **Brugada diagnosis** - only spontaneous type 1 is diagnostic on its own; induced type 1 needs additional clinical features; asymptomatic induced type 1 does not need an ICD. [ESC 2022]`,`ESC 2022 defined **short QT (QTc 360 ms or less with features, or 320 ms or less)** and **ER syndrome** criteria, and made **molecular autopsy** a Class I recommendation in young sudden death. [ESC 2022]`,`**EHRA/HRS/APHRS/LAHRS 2022 expert consensus on genetic testing** lists which genes have definitive evidence (fewer than previously tested), reducing false-positive variant reports. [EHRA/HRS 2022]`,`**India adopted the BPaLM DR-TB regimen (bedaquiline, pretomanid, linezolid, moxifloxacin) in 2024**, increasing the need for **QTcF monitoring** in primary and district care. [NTEP 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`Classic questions: **causes of prolonged QT**, **management of torsades**, **ECG of Brugada type 1**, **QTc formulas**, and **drugs that prolong QT**. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: giving **amiodarone for torsades** - it prolongs QT further; magnesium is the drug. [ESC 2022]`,`Common mistake: **isoprenaline in congenital LQT1/2** - it aggravates adrenergic-dependent torsades; it is for acquired pause-dependent torsades only. [ESC 2022]`,`**Mnemonic for LQT triggers**: LQT1 - Swimming (exercise); LQT2 - Sound (alarm) and post-partum; LQT3 - Sleep. [ESC 2022]`,`**Bazett over-corrects at fast heart rates** - use Fridericia in tachycardia and drug monitoring. [AHA 2020]`,`Viva: a young man with **syncope during fever and coved ST elevation in V1-V2** has Brugada syndrome - treat the fever, avoid sodium-channel blockers, refer for ICD. [ESC 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in channelopathies and torsades`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Magnesium sulfate`,`2 g IV (8 mmol) over 10-15 min; faster if pulseless`,`Repeat once, then 3-20 mg/min infusion`,`Flushing, hypotension, loss of reflexes; caution in renal failure`],[`Potassium chloride`,`IV 10-20 mmol/h via monitored line`,`To K 4.5-5.0 mmol/L`,`Phlebitis, hyperkalaemia; never as bolus`],[`Isoprenaline`,`2-10 micrograms/min IV infusion`,`Target heart rate about 90-110 until pacing or washout`,`Contraindicated in congenital LQT1/LQT2 and ischaemia; used for Brugada storm`],[`Propranolol`,`2-4 mg/kg/day oral in 2-3 doses (adults 40-80 mg two to three times daily)`,`Lifelong in LQTS and CPVT`,`Bradycardia, fatigue, bronchospasm; adherence crucial`],[`Nadolol`,`1-2 mg/kg/day oral once daily`,`Lifelong; preferred in LQT2 and CPVT`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; limited availability in India`],[`Mexiletine`,`150-200 mg oral three times daily`,`Lifelong in LQT3 if QTc shortens`,`Nausea, tremor, dizziness`],[`Flecainide (CPVT)`,`100-150 mg oral twice daily (or 2-3 mg/kg/day)`,`Added to beta-blocker`,`Contraindicated in Brugada and structural heart disease`],[`Quinidine`,`600-1200 mg/day oral in divided doses`,`Brugada, short QT, ER syndrome`,`Diarrhoea, thrombocytopenia, QT prolongation; scarce`]]},{heading:`Congenital LQTS subtypes`,columns:[`Type`,`Gene and current`,`Trigger`,`ECG T wave`,`Specific treatment`],rows:[[`LQT1 (about 35%)`,`KCNQ1 - loss of IKs`,`Exercise, swimming`,`Broad-based T wave`,`Beta-blocker very effective; avoid competitive swimming`],[`LQT2 (about 30%)`,`KCNH2 (hERG) - loss of IKr`,`Sudden noise, emotion, postpartum`,`Low-amplitude notched or bifid T`,`Beta-blocker (nadolol); keep K high-normal; remove alarm triggers`],[`LQT3 (about 10%)`,`SCN5A - gain of late INa`,`Rest, sleep`,`Long isoelectric ST, late peaked T`,`Mexiletine plus beta-blocker; ICD lower threshold`],[`Jervell and Lange-Nielsen`,`KCNQ1/KCNE1 recessive`,`Exercise, emotion`,`Very long QT`,`Beta-blocker, early ICD, LCSD`],[`Andersen-Tawil (LQT7)`,`KCNJ2`,`Variable`,`Prominent U waves, bidirectional VT`,`Beta-blocker, flecainide`]]},{heading:`Comparison of inherited arrhythmia syndromes`,columns:[`Syndrome`,`ECG`,`Typical trigger`,`First-line treatment`],rows:[[`Long QT`,`QTc 480 ms or more, abnormal T`,`Exercise, noise, sleep by type`,`Beta-blocker, avoid QT drugs`],[`Brugada`,`Coved ST elevation 2 mm or more V1-V2, negative T`,`Fever, sleep, alcohol, sodium-channel blockers`,`Avoid triggers, treat fever, ICD if arrest or syncope`],[`CPVT`,`Normal at rest; bidirectional VT on exercise`,`Exercise, emotion`,`Beta-blocker plus flecainide, LCSD`],[`Short QT`,`QTc 360 ms or less, peaked T, AF in young`,`Variable`,`ICD for survivors, quinidine`],[`Early repolarisation syndrome`,`J-point elevation 1 mm or more inferior/lateral`,`Rest, vagal`,`ICD for survivors; isoprenaline for storm`]]},{heading:`Causes of acquired QT prolongation`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Action`],rows:[[`Electrolytes`,`Low potassium, magnesium, calcium`,`Replace to K 4.5-5.0 and normal Mg`],[`Antimicrobials`,`Macrolides, quinolones, azoles, chloroquine, HCQ, quinine`,`Choose alternative; ECG if combined`],[`DR-TB drugs`,`Bedaquiline, delamanid, moxifloxacin, clofazimine`,`QTcF monitoring; stop if over 500 ms confirmed`],[`Psychotropics`,`Haloperidol, chlorpromazine, citalopram, escitalopram, TCAs, methadone`,`Lowest dose, baseline ECG`],[`Antiemetics`,`Domperidone, high-dose IV ondansetron`,`Short course, low dose`],[`Antiarrhythmics`,`Sotalol, quinidine, disopyramide, ibutilide, dofetilide`,`Start in hospital with QT monitoring`],[`Clinical states`,`Bradycardia, AV block, hypothyroidism, hypothermia, SAH, ischaemia, organophosphates, starvation`,`Treat cause; pace bradycardia`]]},{heading:`QT measurement and cut-offs`,columns:[`Item`,`Value`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Bazett`,`QT divided by square root of RR`,`Over-corrects at fast rates`],[`Fridericia`,`QT divided by cube root of RR`,`Preferred for drug monitoring and NTEP`],[`Normal upper limit`,`Men under 450 ms; women under 460 ms`,`AHA/ACCF/HRS standards`],[`LQTS diagnosis`,`480 ms or more repeatedly`,`Or Schwartz score over 3 or pathogenic variant`],[`High torsades risk`,`Over 500 ms or rise over 60 ms`,`Stop or change drug`],[`Short QT`,`360 ms or less with features; 320 ms or less alone`,`ESC 2022`]]}],redFlags:[`Syncope during exercise, swimming, sudden noise or sleep in a young person`,`QTc over 500 ms or rise over 60 ms after starting a drug`,`Torsades de pointes or polymorphic VT on monitor`,`Spontaneous coved type 1 Brugada pattern with syncope or nocturnal agonal breathing`,`Coved Brugada pattern appearing during fever`,`Family history of sudden death, drowning or unexplained accident under 40`,`Seizure-like episodes with exertion or noise before starting antiepileptics`,`QT-prolonging drug in a patient with low potassium, bradycardia or heart failure`],pearls:[`Swimming, Sound, Sleep - triggers of LQT1, LQT2 and LQT3.`,`Torsades: magnesium 2 g IV even if serum magnesium is normal; potassium to 4.5-5.0.`,`No amiodarone for torsades - it lengthens the QT further.`,`Isoprenaline for acquired pause-dependent torsades, never for congenital LQT1/LQT2.`,`QTc over 500 ms or a rise over 60 ms: stop the drug.`,`Brugada type 1 is coved; saddleback (type 2) is never diagnostic on its own.`,`In Brugada, fever is a trigger - treat every fever with paracetamol promptly.`,`CPVT: normal resting ECG, bidirectional VT on the treadmill.`,`Deafness plus long QT is Jervell and Lange-Nielsen.`,`Use Fridericia, not Bazett, at fast heart rates and for NTEP monitoring.`],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Discuss the causes, diagnosis and management of long QT syndrome, and outline the emergency management of torsades de pointes.`,openingLines:[`Long QT syndrome is prolongation of ventricular repolarisation (QTc 480 ms or more, or Schwartz score over 3) that predisposes to torsades de pointes, syncope and sudden death; it is congenital (ion-channel mutations) or, far more commonly, acquired from drugs and electrolyte disturbance.`,`Torsades is treated with IV magnesium, potassium to 4.5-5.0 mmol/L, withdrawal of offending drugs and increasing the heart rate, and congenital LQTS with non-selective beta-blockers and trigger avoidance (ESC 2022).`],answer:[{heading:`Measurement and definition`,points:[`QT from QRS onset to T end (tangent), lead II or V5; Bazett QT/square root RR; Fridericia QT/cube root RR.`,`Prolonged: 450 ms or more men, 460 ms or more women; over 500 ms high risk.`]},{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Congenital: LQT1 (KCNQ1), LQT2 (KCNH2), LQT3 (SCN5A); Romano-Ward, Jervell and Lange-Nielsen.`,`Acquired: drugs (macrolides, quinolones, antimalarials, antipsychotics, domperidone, bedaquiline, sotalol), low K/Mg/Ca, bradycardia, hypothyroidism, SAH, organophosphates.`]},{heading:`Clinical features and diagnosis`,points:[`Syncope, seizures, sudden death with gene-specific triggers (swimming, noise, sleep).`,`ESC 2022: QTc 480 ms or more, Schwartz score over 3, or pathogenic variant.`,`Exercise test, Holter, genetic testing, family screening.`]},{heading:`Emergency management of torsades`,points:[`Pulseless: unsynchronised defibrillation.`,`Magnesium sulfate 2 g IV over 10-15 min, repeat once; K to 4.5-5.0.`,`Stop QT drugs; overdrive pacing or isoprenaline in acquired pause-dependent cases.`,`Avoid amiodarone and sotalol.`]},{heading:`Long-term management`,points:[`Avoid QT drugs (CredibleMeds), correct electrolytes, avoid triggers.`,`Propranolol or nadolol; mexiletine in LQT3.`,`ICD after arrest or breakthrough events; LCSD if refractory.`,`Cascade screening and genetic counselling of relatives.`]}],mustDraw:[`ECG of torsades de pointes with short-long-short initiation`,`Table of LQT1, LQT2 and LQT3 with gene, trigger and T-wave shape`],markSplit:[{part:`Measurement and causes`,marks:3},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Torsades management`,marks:3},{part:`Long-term care`,marks:2}],keywords:[`long QT`,`QTc`,`torsades`,`magnesium`,`beta-blocker`,`Schwartz score`,`CredibleMeds`]},{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Short note: Brugada syndrome.`,openingLines:[`Brugada syndrome is an inherited channelopathy (SCN5A loss of function in 20-25%) with coved ST elevation of 2 mm or more and negative T waves in V1-V2 that predisposes to polymorphic VT, VF and sudden death, typically in men around 40 years during sleep or fever.`,`Management is trigger avoidance - especially prompt treatment of fever - and an ICD for survivors of arrest or patients with arrhythmic syncope (ESC 2022).`],answer:[{heading:`ECG and diagnosis`,points:[`Type 1 coved pattern diagnostic when spontaneous; type 2 saddleback not diagnostic.`,`High precordial leads; sodium-channel blocker challenge; fever-induced pattern.`,`Phenocopies: hyperkalaemia, RV ischaemia, PE, pectus, ARVC.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Avoid sodium-channel blockers, TCAs, lithium, propofol, bupivacaine, cocaine, excess alcohol and big meals (brugadadrugs.org).`,`Treat fever with paracetamol promptly.`,`ICD for aborted arrest or sustained VT (I); type 1 with arrhythmic syncope (IIa).`,`Quinidine, isoprenaline for storm, epicardial RVOT ablation.`,`Family screening and genetic counselling.`]}],mustDraw:[`Brugada type 1 (coved) versus type 2 (saddleback) ECG in V1`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and ECG`,marks:2},{part:`Management and screening`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Brugada`,`coved ST elevation`,`SCN5A`,`fever`,`ICD`,`quinidine`]}],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old woman on furosemide is admitted with pneumonia and started on IV azithromycin and ondansetron. On day 2 she has recurrent self-terminating polymorphic VT with a twisting QRS axis. QTc is 560 ms and potassium 3.1 mmol/L. Besides stopping the offending drugs, what is the best immediate treatment?`,options:[`IV amiodarone 300 mg`,`IV procainamide`,`Oral sotalol`,`IV magnesium sulfate 2 g and potassium replacement`],answer:3,explanation:`This is acquired torsades de pointes from QT-prolonging drugs plus diuretic hypokalaemia; IV magnesium suppresses early afterdepolarisations even with normal serum magnesium, and potassium should be raised to 4.5-5.0 mmol/L. Amiodarone, procainamide and sotalol all prolong the QT further and can perpetuate torsades.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-q2`,stem:`A 14-year-old girl has had two episodes of collapse, one while swimming in a pool. Her QTc is 500 ms with broad-based T waves. Which gene is most likely affected?`,options:[`KCNH2 (hERG)`,`KCNQ1`,`SCN5A`,`RYR2`],answer:1,explanation:`Exercise-triggered events, especially swimming, with broad-based T waves are typical of LQT1 from KCNQ1 loss of the slow potassium current (IKs), which normally shortens the QT during sympathetic drive. KCNH2 (LQT2) events follow sudden noise or the postpartum period, SCN5A (LQT3) events occur at rest or in sleep, and RYR2 causes CPVT with a normal resting QT.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-q3`,stem:`A 38-year-old man is admitted with dengue fever (temperature 39.5 C). His ECG shows 3 mm coved ST elevation with T-wave inversion in V1 and V2. He reports a past episode of unexplained nocturnal syncope. Which statement is correct?`,options:[`This is a fever-induced Brugada type 1 pattern; treat fever promptly, avoid sodium-channel blockers and refer for ICD evaluation`,`This is anterior STEMI and he needs immediate thrombolysis`,`This is benign early repolarisation and needs no action`,`This is right bundle branch block and needs no action`],answer:0,explanation:`Coved ST elevation of 2 mm or more with a negative T wave in V1-V2 is the type 1 Brugada pattern; fever unmasks it, and with arrhythmic syncope an ICD should be considered (ESC 2022 IIa) while fever is treated aggressively. STEMI shows territorial evolution and reciprocal change, early repolarisation affects inferior or lateral leads, and RBBB gives rSR prime without coved ST elevation.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-q4`,stem:`A patient on a bedaquiline-containing drug-resistant TB regimen has a QTcF of 520 ms confirmed on a repeat ECG. Potassium and magnesium are normal. What is the correct action under NTEP guidance?`,options:[`Continue all drugs and repeat the ECG after 3 months`,`Add oral sotalol to control the heart rate`,`Stop the QT-prolonging drugs and seek specialist review of the regimen`,`Switch the formula to Bazett, which will give a shorter value`],answer:2,explanation:`A confirmed QTcF over 500 ms on bedaquiline, moxifloxacin, delamanid or clofazimine carries a high torsades risk, and NTEP advises stopping the QT-prolonging drugs and revising the regimen at the DR-TB centre. Continuing ignores the risk, sotalol itself prolongs the QT, and changing formulas to obtain a lower number does not change the underlying risk.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-channelopathies-qt-q5`,stem:`A 10-year-old boy collapses while playing football. His resting ECG and echocardiogram are normal. On treadmill testing, ventricular ectopics increase with workload and progress to bidirectional VT. What is the first-line long-term treatment?`,options:[`Verapamil`,`Non-selective beta-blocker such as nadolol, with exercise restriction`,`Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator alone without drugs`,`Mexiletine`],answer:1,explanation:`Exercise-induced bidirectional VT with a normal resting ECG and heart is CPVT from RYR2 calcium leak; ESC 2022 recommends a beta-blocker (nadolol preferred) and avoidance of strenuous exercise, adding flecainide or LCSD if needed. Verapamil is not first line, an ICD alone is hazardous because shocks can trigger catecholamine storms, and mexiletine is used in LQT3.`,difficulty:`hard`}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - Ventricular arrhythmias and inherited arrhythmia syndromes`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - Genetics of cardiac arrhythmias`,`Zeppenfeld K et al. 2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death. Eur Heart J 2022`,`Tisdale JE et al. Drug-Induced Arrhythmias: A Scientific Statement From the American Heart Association. Circulation 2020`,`Wilde AAM et al. European Heart Rhythm Association/HRS/APHRS/LAHRS expert consensus statement on the state of genetic testing for cardiac diseases. Europace 2022`,`Schwartz PJ, Crotti L. QTc behavior during exercise and genetic testing for the long-QT syndrome (updated diagnostic criteria). Circulation 2011`,`CredibleMeds QTdrugs list (crediblemeds.org), accessed 2026`,`Guidelines for Programmatic Management of Drug-Resistant Tuberculosis in India, National TB Elimination Programme, MoHFW (2021, with 2024 BPaLM update)`]});var u=[];u.push({id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin`,title:`Antiarrhythmic drugs and digoxin toxicity`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Antiarrhythmic drugs** change ion-channel, receptor or calcium handling in cardiac cells and are grouped by the **modernised Vaughan Williams classification (classes 0-VII, 2018)**; every one can be **proarrhythmic**, so each has its own safety rule - flecainide only in a **structurally normal heart**, sotalol and dofetilide only with **QT and creatinine-clearance monitoring**, amiodarone with **6-monthly thyroid and liver tests** - while **digoxin toxicity** (narrow therapeutic index, worsened by **hypokalaemia, renal failure and P-glycoprotein inhibitors**) presents with nausea, visual change and **atrial tachycardia with block or bidirectional VT**, and is reversed by **digoxin-specific Fab**.`,keywords:[`antiarrhythmic drugs`,`Vaughan Williams classification`,`amiodarone`,`dronedarone`,`flecainide`,`propafenone`,`sotalol`,`dofetilide`,`lidocaine`,`mexiletine`,`adenosine`,`verapamil`,`digoxin toxicity`,`digitalis`,`DigiFab`,`Digibind`,`bidirectional VT`,`yellow oleander`,`proarrhythmia`,`CAST trial`],sections:[{heading:`Definition, scope and burden`,points:[`**Antiarrhythmic drugs (AADs)** suppress or prevent abnormal rhythms by modifying ion channels, receptors, pumps or intracellular calcium handling; they act on the rhythm, not the substrate, so **ablation, devices and treating the cause** often outperform them. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **Vaughan Williams scheme (1970, four classes)** was expanded by **Lei and colleagues in 2018 to classes 0-VII**, adding HCN-channel blockers, calcium-handling modulators, mechanosensitive and gap-junction blockers and upstream therapy. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`Most AAD use is for **atrial fibrillation** - about **60 million people** live with AF or flutter worldwide - followed by ventricular and supraventricular tachycardia. [GBD 2019]`,`Indian AF patients in the **IHRS-AF registry** had a mean age in the **mid-50s**, far younger than Western cohorts, with **rheumatic mitral disease** a major substrate - so **digoxin and amiodarone** remain the most prescribed rhythm drugs. [IHRS-AF registry 2017]`,`**Proarrhythmia** - a new or worse arrhythmia caused by the drug - is the defining hazard: in **CAST (1989)** suppressing post-MI ectopy with **flecainide or encainide raised mortality about 2.5-fold** (7.7% vs 3.0%). [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Digoxin** is a cardiac glycoside with a **narrow therapeutic index**; most toxicity is **chronic** in elderly patients with falling renal function, and a minority is **acute** after deliberate overdose. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`In **South India and Sri Lanka** ingestion of **yellow oleander (Thevetia peruviana)** seeds or **Cerbera odollam** (suicide tree) causes an identical cardiac glycoside poisoning - a common rural self-poisoning. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**NLEM 2022** lists **amiodarone, digoxin, adenosine, verapamil, metoprolol and lignocaine**, the drugs a district hospital will stock; **digoxin Fab is rarely available** in India. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Mechanism: the electrophysiology behind the drugs`,points:[`Ventricular action potential: **phase 0 fast sodium upstroke (INa)**, phase 2 **L-type calcium plateau**, phase 3 **potassium repolarisation (IKr, IKs)**; the SA and AV nodes depend on **calcium-driven phase 0 and the HCN funny current (If)** in phase 4. [Harrison 22e]`,`Arrhythmias arise from **abnormal automaticity**, **triggered activity** - early afterdepolarisations with long QT, **delayed afterdepolarisations with calcium overload** (digoxin toxicity) - and **re-entry** around a fixed or functional obstacle. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Class I sodium blockers slow conduction** and widen QRS; they terminate re-entry by converting slow conduction to block, but in **scarred myocardium** they can create new re-entry - the CAST mechanism. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Use dependence**: class Ic drugs unbind slowly, so block grows at **fast rates** - QRS widens on exercise, and atrial flutter can be slowed enough for the AV node to conduct **1:1** with a very fast broad-complex rhythm. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Reverse use dependence**: IKr blockers (sotalol, dofetilide) prolong repolarisation most at **slow rates**, so **torsades de pointes** follows pauses, bradycardia and hypokalaemia. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Amiodarone** blocks sodium, potassium (IKr, IKs) and calcium channels plus alpha and beta receptors; it prolongs QT yet causes **torsades in under 1%** because repolarisation is lengthened uniformly across the wall. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Digoxin inhibits Na+/K+-ATPase**: intracellular sodium rises, the sodium-calcium exchanger removes less calcium, SR calcium rises and contraction strengthens; at toxic levels **calcium overload drives delayed afterdepolarisations** and ectopy. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Digoxin also **raises vagal tone**, slowing the SA node and AV conduction - the basis of rate control in AF - so toxicity combines **increased automaticity with conduction block**, the electrocardiographic signature. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Potassium competes with digoxin** at the pump: **hypokalaemia increases binding** and toxicity, while **hyperkalaemia in acute poisoning** reflects pump blockade in skeletal muscle and marks severity. [Goldfrank 11e]`]},{heading:`Classification: modernised Vaughan Williams (2018)`,points:[`**Class 0**: HCN (If) channel blocker **ivabradine** - slows the sinus node without negative inotropy. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`**Class I** (sodium channel): **Ia** quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide (intermediate kinetics, widen QRS and QT); **Ib** lidocaine, mexiletine (fast kinetics, act on ischaemic tissue); **Ic** flecainide, propafenone (slow kinetics, marked QRS widening); **Id** ranolazine (late sodium current). [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`**Class II** (autonomic): **IIa beta-blockers**, IIb beta-agonists (isoprenaline), IIc muscarinic blockers (atropine), **IId muscarinic M2 activators including digoxin**, **IIe adenosine A1 activators (adenosine)**. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`**Class III** (potassium channel): non-selective **amiodarone and dronedarone**; IKr blockers **sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide**; IKur blocker vernakalant; **IIIb** KATP openers such as nicorandil. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`**Class IV** (calcium handling): **IVa L-type blockers verapamil and diltiazem**; **IVb ryanodine receptor (RyR2) blockade by flecainide and propafenone** - the reason flecainide works in CPVT. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`**Class V** (mechanosensitive channels) and **class VI** (gap junctions) have no routine clinical drug; **class VII upstream therapy** - ACE inhibitors, ARBs, statins, omega-3 fatty acids - remodels the substrate. [Lei, Circulation 2018]`,`Many drugs span classes: **sotalol (II + III)**, **amiodarone (I, II, III, IV)**, **propafenone (Ic + beta-blockade)** - predict effects from every action, not from one label. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Older texts put **digoxin and adenosine** in a 'miscellaneous' group; examiners accept either, but naming the 2018 classes shows the answer is current. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Choosing the drug: rhythm, substrate and acute doses`,points:[`**AF rate control**: beta-blocker, diltiazem or verapamil (only if **LVEF over 40%**), **digoxin**, or a combination; aim for a resting rate **under 110/min** initially. [ESC 2024]`,`**AF rhythm control by substrate**: **flecainide or propafenone** if there is no significant LVH, HFrEF or coronary disease; **amiodarone** in HFrEF; **dronedarone** in HFmrEF/HFpEF or coronary disease; **sotalol** only with close QT, potassium and renal monitoring. [ESC 2024]`,`**Recent-onset AF cardioversion**: oral or IV flecainide/propafenone in a normal heart (converts within hours); **IV amiodarone** with structural disease (slower, often 8-12 hours). [ESC 2024]`,`**Regular narrow-complex tachycardia**: vagal manoeuvre, then **adenosine 6 mg rapid IV push with a flush, then 12 mg**; alternative **verapamil 5-10 mg IV over 2 minutes** if adenosine fails or is contraindicated. [AHA 2025]`,`**IV beta-blockers**: **metoprolol 2.5-5 mg IV over 2 minutes, up to 3 doses**, or **esmolol 500 µg/kg bolus then 50-300 µg/kg/min**; never give IV verapamil and IV beta-blocker together. [ESC 2024]`,`**Stable monomorphic VT**: **amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 minutes** (or procainamide); unstable VT needs synchronised cardioversion; **shock-refractory VF/pulseless VT: amiodarone 300 mg then 150 mg, or lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg then 0.5-0.75 mg/kg**. [AHA 2025]`,`**Never give verapamil or diltiazem for a broad-complex tachycardia** (assume VT) or for **pre-excited AF (WPW)** - they cause collapse or accelerate accessory-pathway conduction. [AHA 2025]`,`**Recurrent VT or ICD shocks**: beta-blocker plus amiodarone (or sotalol), mexiletine as add-on, and early **catheter ablation**; inherited syndromes: **nadolol/propranolol** in LQTS and CPVT, **flecainide** add-on in CPVT, **quinidine** in Brugada electrical storm. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Do not treat asymptomatic ventricular ectopy after MI** with class I drugs; treat the substrate - revascularisation, beta-blocker and heart-failure therapy. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Amiodarone: dosing, toxicity and monitoring`,points:[`**IV amiodarone**: **300 mg (5 mg/kg) in 250 mL 5% dextrose over 30-60 minutes**, then **900-1200 mg over 24 hours**, preferably via a central line - peripheral infusion causes **phlebitis**, and the vehicle causes hypotension and bradycardia. [ESC 2024]`,`**Oral amiodarone**: load about **600-800 mg daily in divided doses until about 10 g in total**, then maintain on **100-200 mg once daily** - the lowest dose that works. [ESC 2024]`,`**Kinetics**: lipophilic, huge volume of distribution, **elimination half-life about 50-60 days** - effects and toxicity persist for months; it is **37% iodine**, releasing several milligrams of free iodine a day from a 200 mg tablet. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Thyroid (about 15-20%)**: **hypothyroidism** (Wolff-Chaikoff effect, commoner with TPO antibodies) is treated with **levothyroxine while amiodarone continues**; early **high T4, high reverse T3 and a transient TSH rise** in the first 3 months are expected, not disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis**: **type 1** (iodine-fuelled synthesis in a nodular or Graves gland, increased Doppler flow) - **carbimazole, plus potassium perchlorate**; **type 2** (destructive thyroiditis in a normal gland, absent flow) - **prednisolone 30-40 mg daily** tapered over about 3 months. [ETA 2018]`,`**Lung (1-5%)**: dose-related (over 400 mg/day), commoner in the elderly and with lung disease - subacute cough, breathlessness, fever, **interstitial pneumonitis or organising pneumonia** with a **DLCO fall over 15-20%**; stop the drug, give steroids; mortality about 10%. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Liver**: transaminase rise in **15-30%**; **reduce or stop if over 2-3 times the upper limit** or if hepatitis appears; cirrhosis is rare. [HRS 2007]`,`**Eye**: **corneal microdeposits (vortex keratopathy) in over 90%** are harmless; **optic neuropathy (under 2%)** - painless visual loss or disc swelling - mandates stopping. [HRS 2007]`,`**Skin and nerves**: **photosensitivity** (advise sunscreen), **blue-grey skin** with long use, tremor, ataxia and peripheral neuropathy; **heart**: bradycardia, AV block and QT prolongation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Monitoring**: baseline **TSH, LFT, chest X-ray, PFT with DLCO and ECG**; then **TSH and LFT every 6 months**, **chest X-ray yearly**, ECG at each visit; PFT, HRCT and eye examination when symptoms appear. [HRS 2007]`]},{heading:`Sodium-channel blockers: flecainide, propafenone, lidocaine and others`,points:[`**Flecainide**: **50-100 mg twice daily**, maximum **150 mg twice daily**; reduce the dose when creatinine clearance is under 35 mL/min. [BNF 2025]`,`**The three flecainide rules**: (1) **no structural heart disease** - no prior MI, coronary disease, LV dysfunction or significant LVH; (2) **always add an AV-nodal blocker** (beta-blocker, verapamil or diltiazem) to prevent **1:1 flutter**; (3) **repeat the ECG** and reduce or stop if **QRS widens by more than 25%**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Propafenone**: **150-300 mg three times daily**; it also **blocks beta receptors** (bronchospasm, bradycardia), causes a metallic taste and is metabolised by CYP2D6; the structural-heart rules are the same as flecainide. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Pill-in-the-pocket**: **flecainide 200 mg (under 70 kg) or 300 mg (70 kg or more)**, or **propafenone 450 mg or 600 mg**, taken once at the start of paroxysmal AF, after the first dose has been tested under monitoring. [ESC 2024]`,`Class Ic drugs can **unmask a Brugada pattern** (flecainide and ajmaline are the provocation drugs) - stop the drug if coved ST elevation appears in V1-V2. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Lidocaine (Ib)**: **1-1.5 mg/kg IV bolus**, repeat 0.5-0.75 mg/kg every 5-10 minutes to **3 mg/kg**, then **1-4 mg/min**; toxicity is neurological - perioral numbness, tremor, confusion, seizures - and is commoner in heart or liver failure. [AHA 2025]`,`**Mexiletine (oral Ib)** 150-200 mg three times daily with food is an add-on for recurrent VT and for **LQT3**; nausea and tremor limit it. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Procainamide (Ia)** IV is used for stable monomorphic VT and pre-excited AF where available; stop when **QRS widens by 50%**, hypotension occurs or 17 mg/kg is given; long-term use causes **drug-induced lupus** in slow acetylators. [AHA 2025]`,`**Quinidine** survives for **Brugada electrical storm and short-QT syndrome**; it causes **cinchonism** (tinnitus, headache), diarrhoea and torsades ('quinidine syncope'). [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`QT-prolonging drugs: sotalol, dofetilide and torsades`,points:[`**Sotalol** is a racemate: the l-isomer is a **beta-blocker** and both isomers block **IKr**; start **80 mg twice daily**, titrate to **120-160 mg twice daily**. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Sotalol is **excreted unchanged by the kidney**: creatinine clearance **over 60 mL/min - twice daily; 40-60 - once daily; under 40 - avoid for AF**. [FDA label 2020]`,`**Sotalol initiation**: baseline **QTc under 450 ms**, in hospital with continuous ECG for **at least 3 days (5-6 doses)**, QTc checked 2-4 hours after each dose, **reduce or stop if QTc exceeds 500 ms**; an IV loading protocol (FDA 2020) shortens the stay. [FDA label 2020]`,`**Dofetilide** (pure IKr blocker, not marketed in India) is dosed by creatinine clearance: **over 60 - 500 µg twice daily; 40-60 - 250 µg; 20-40 - 125 µg; under 20 - contraindicated**. [FDA label]`,`**Dofetilide loading** is a mandatory 3-day inpatient protocol: if QTc rises **over 15% or above 500 ms** after the first dose, halve the dose; if it exceeds 500 ms after the second dose, **stop the drug**. [FDA label]`,`Dofetilide is contraindicated with **verapamil, cimetidine, trimethoprim, ketoconazole, hydrochlorothiazide and dolutegravir**; never combine two QT-prolonging drugs (macrolides, fluoroquinolones, azoles, antipsychotics, ondansetron, domperidone, hydroxychloroquine). [FDA label]`,`**Torsades risk factors**: **female sex**, bradycardia or pauses, recent conversion from AF, **hypokalaemia and hypomagnesaemia**, renal impairment, high dose, baseline long QT, heart failure and diuretic use. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Keep **potassium above 4.0 mmol/L and magnesium above 2.0 mg/dL** during initiation; treat torsades with **magnesium sulphate 2 g IV**, withdrawal of the drug and **overdrive pacing or isoprenaline** to raise the heart rate. [AHA 2025]`,`**Ibutilide** (IV, for AF and flutter conversion) carries a **torsades risk of about 4%** - monitor ECG for at least 4 hours after the infusion. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Digoxin: pharmacology, dosing and levels`,points:[`**Uses now**: rate control in AF (especially with HFrEF, hypotension or a sedentary lifestyle) and symptom relief in HFrEF on full GDMT; in the **DIG trial it cut HF admissions by about 28% without changing mortality**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Kinetics**: oral bioavailability **60-80%**, **half-life 36-48 hours** (up to 4-6 days in anuria), **60-80% excreted unchanged by the kidney** via P-glycoprotein, large volume of distribution in skeletal muscle - so **dialysis does not remove it** and lean elderly patients reach higher levels. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Maintenance dose**: **0.0625-0.25 mg orally once daily**; start **0.0625-0.125 mg** in the elderly, women, low body weight and CKD; steady state takes about **7 days** without a loading dose. [ESC 2024]`,`**Rapid loading**: **0.5 mg IV bolus**, then further doses to a total of **0.75-1.5 mg over 24 hours** in divided doses; do not load a patient who took digoxin in the past week or who has renal failure. [ESC 2024]`,`**Serum level**: sample **at least 6 hours after the last dose** (8-12 hours is ideal); target **0.5-0.9 ng/mL in heart failure** (levels of 1.2 or more raised mortality in DIG) and **under 1.2 ng/mL in AF**; **1 ng/mL = 1.28 nmol/L**. [ESC 2024]`,`A level over **2 ng/mL** usually means toxicity, but **toxicity occurs at 'therapeutic' levels** when potassium or magnesium is low, calcium is high or the patient is hypothyroid or hypoxic - treat the patient, not the number. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Digoxin effect (therapeutic, not toxic)**: **sagging 'reverse-tick' ST depression** in lateral leads, **short QT**, flat or inverted T waves, prominent U waves and mild PR prolongation; it makes **exercise ECG uninterpretable**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Contraindications and cautions**: **WPW with AF** (speeds accessory-pathway conduction), **obstructive HCM**, second- or third-degree AV block without a pacemaker, and **cardiac amyloidosis** (binds fibrils, toxicity at normal levels). [Harrison 22e]`,`**RATE-AF (2020)**: in older patients with permanent AF and heart-failure symptoms, **low-dose digoxin matched bisoprolol** for quality of life at 6 months, with fewer adverse events and lower NT-proBNP at 12 months. [JAMA 2020]`]},{heading:`Digoxin toxicity: precipitants, symptoms and ECG`,points:[`**Precipitants**: age over 75, **renal impairment or dehydration** (vomiting, diarrhoea, heat), **hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia**, hypothyroidism, hypoxia, acidosis, low muscle mass and myocardial ischaemia. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Drugs that raise the level** (mostly P-glycoprotein inhibition): **amiodarone (roughly doubles it - halve digoxin)**, **dronedarone (about 2.5-fold)**, **verapamil (50-75%)**, quinidine, diltiazem, propafenone, **clarithromycin, erythromycin**, itraconazole, ciclosporin and spironolactone. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Indirect precipitants**: **loop and thiazide diuretics** (low potassium and magnesium), steroids, amphotericin, NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors (lower renal clearance); **rifampicin, antacids and cholestyramine** lower levels. [BNF 2025]`,`**Gastrointestinal symptoms come first**: anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain - in the elderly often dismissed as gastroenteritis, which then worsens the dehydration. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Neurological and visual**: fatigue, weakness, confusion, delirium; **xanthopsia (yellow-green vision)**, **haloes around lights**, blurred vision and scotomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Arrhythmias** - almost any except a fast supraventricular rhythm: **ventricular ectopics and bigeminy (commonest)**, **atrial tachycardia with block**, **accelerated junctional rhythm**, **regularised AF** (complete heart block with junctional escape), sinus bradycardia, AV block, **bidirectional VT** and VF. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute overdose** produces vomiting, **hyperkalaemia** and bradycardia or AV block within hours; **chronic toxicity** produces vague malaise, confusion, ventricular ectopy and often **hypokalaemia** from diuretics. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Potassium predicts outcome in acute poisoning**: before Fab, mortality was about **50% with potassium 5.0-5.5 mmol/L** and near **100% above 5.5** - so **potassium over 5.0 is an indication for Fab**. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Yellow oleander** poisoning gives vomiting, **sinus bradycardia, AV block and hyperkalaemia**; the digoxin immunoassay cross-reacts and gives a qualitative positive. [Goldfrank 11e]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Hyperkalaemia from CKD or ACE inhibitor/ARB/spironolactone**: peaked T waves, broad QRS and bradycardia, but no yellow vision and no ectopy-plus-block pattern; the drug history and level separate them. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Beta-blocker or calcium-channel-blocker overdose**: bradycardia, AV block and hypotension with **hypoglycaemia (beta-blocker)** or **hyperglycaemia (verapamil, diltiazem)**, without ventricular ectopy or hyperkalaemia. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Degenerative conduction disease or sick sinus syndrome** in the elderly: chronic bradycardia without GI or visual symptoms and with a normal level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bidirectional VT** is also seen in **CPVT** (exercise or emotion in a young patient) and **aconite poisoning** (Ayurvedic preparations; oral tingling and paraesthesia). [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Drug-induced torsades** from sotalol, dofetilide or other QT drugs gives **long QT** with polymorphic VT; digoxin shortens QT. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Ischaemic ST depression versus digoxin effect**: ischaemia is horizontal or downsloping, dynamic and symptomatic; digoxin gives a scooped ST with a **short QT** that does not change with pain. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Uraemia, hypothyroidism, gastroenteritis and sepsis** cause nausea, confusion and bradycardia in the elderly - check the digoxin level whenever a patient on digoxin vomits or becomes confused. [Goldfrank 11e]`]},{heading:`Investigations and monitoring tests`,points:[`**12-lead ECG and rhythm strip** at baseline and after each start or dose change: rate, PR, **QRS width (Ic drugs)**, **QTc (III drugs)** and AV block; measure QTc in lead II or V5 and use the Fridericia correction at fast rates. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**Electrolytes**: potassium (high in acute, low in chronic digoxin toxicity), **magnesium and calcium**; **creatinine with Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance** to dose digoxin, sotalol, dofetilide and flecainide. [ESC 2024]`,`**Serum digoxin** at least 6 hours post-dose (sample at once in an emergency, but read it against time); after Fab, standard assays measure **bound plus free digoxin and are falsely high** for days. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Echocardiography before any class Ic or III drug** - LV function, LVH and valve disease decide which drug is safe; exclude coronary disease when symptoms suggest it. [ESC 2024]`,`**Amiodarone thyrotoxicosis work-up**: TSH, free T4 and T3, **thyroid colour-flow Doppler** (increased in type 1, absent in type 2) and TPO/TRAb antibodies; radioiodine uptake is low in both because of the iodine load. [ETA 2018]`,`**Suspected amiodarone lung**: chest X-ray, **HRCT** (ground glass, consolidation, reticulation), **DLCO** compared with baseline, and exclusion of infection and heart failure. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Holter or event monitoring** checks the response to therapy and the burden of ectopy; the palpitations topic covers the choice of device. [ESC 2024]`,`In acute glycoside poisoning add **glucose, blood gas and serial potassium every 1-2 hours**, because rising potassium tracks worsening pump blockade. [Goldfrank 11e]`]},{heading:`Emergency management of digoxin toxicity`,points:[`**Admit** any patient on digoxin with an arrhythmia, abnormal potassium, a high level with symptoms, or a deliberate overdose; **stop digoxin and interacting drugs**, cardiac monitor, IV access. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Activated charcoal 50 g (1 g/kg in children)** within 1-2 hours of acute ingestion if the airway is safe; repeat doses may help with enterohepatic recirculation but did not reduce mortality in the large yellow-oleander trial. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Correct potassium and magnesium**: keep potassium **4.0-5.0 mmol/L** in chronic toxicity; give **magnesium sulphate 2 g IV** for ventricular ectopy unless renal failure or hyperkalaemia. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Hyperkalaemia**: insulin-dextrose (10 units with 25 g glucose), salbutamol nebulisation and bicarbonate if acidotic; **Fab is the definitive treatment**; IV calcium was traditionally avoided ('stone heart'), and newer data suggest it is less dangerous than taught. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Bradycardia or AV block**: **atropine 0.5-1 mg IV**, repeated to 3 mg; temporary pacing only if Fab is unavailable and atropine fails - the irritable ventricle may fibrillate on pacing. [AHA 2025]`,`**Ventricular arrhythmias**: **lidocaine 1-1.5 mg/kg IV** and magnesium; **avoid amiodarone (raises digoxin) and class Ia drugs**; DC shock only for pulseless VT/VF, as cardioversion of a perfusing rhythm risks refractory VF. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Fab indications**: life-threatening arrhythmia (VT, VF, asystole, symptomatic bradycardia unresponsive to atropine), **potassium over 5.0 mmol/L in acute poisoning**, ingestion over **10 mg in adults or 4 mg (0.1 mg/kg) in children**, level **over 10 ng/mL at steady state or over 15 ng/mL at any time**, and chronic toxicity with organ dysfunction. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Fab dose**: **one 40 mg vial binds about 0.5 mg digoxin**; vials = (mg ingested x 0.8) / 0.5, or **level (ng/mL) x weight (kg) / 100**; empirically **10 vials in acute and 3-6 vials in chronic toxicity**. [DigiFab label]`,`After Fab, response begins in **30-60 minutes**; watch for **hypokalaemia**, return of a fast AF rate or heart failure, and **rebound toxicity in renal failure**; where Fab is unavailable, call the **AIIMS National Poisons Information Centre (1800-116-117)**. [Goldfrank 11e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: **avoid amiodarone** (fetal hypothyroidism, goitre, growth restriction) unless the arrhythmia is life-threatening; **beta-blockers (not atenolol), digoxin, adenosine, sotalol and flecainide** are usable, and **digoxin or flecainide** treats **fetal SVT** transplacentally. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Older adults**: **Beers 2023** advises against digoxin as first-line for AF or HF and against doses **over 0.125 mg/day**, and against amiodarone as first-line for AF unless there is heart failure or substantial LVH. [AGS Beers 2023]`,`**CKD**: reduce **digoxin** and check levels; avoid **sotalol (clearance under 40)** and **dofetilide (under 20)**; reduce **flecainide**; procainamide's metabolite NAPA accumulates; amiodarone, lidocaine and mexiletine need no renal adjustment. [ESC 2024]`,`**Liver or heart failure** slows **lidocaine** clearance - halve the infusion; propafenone and mexiletine also accumulate in liver disease. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Thyroid disease**: hyperthyroid patients need more digoxin and respond poorly; hypothyroid patients become toxic at low doses - check TSH in unexplained toxicity or resistance. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Children**: accidental ingestion of a grandparent's digoxin is the classic scenario - **Fab if over 4 mg or 0.1 mg/kg**, or with hyperkalaemia or arrhythmia. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**HCV treatment**: **sofosbuvir with amiodarone** causes severe, sometimes fatal bradycardia - avoid the combination, relevant under India's **National Viral Hepatitis Control Programme**. [FDA 2015]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: where Fab is unavailable, outcomes rest on **early charcoal, potassium and magnesium correction, atropine, temporary pacing and referral** - digoxin and amiodarone are cheap generics through **Jan Aushadhi**. [NLEM 2022]`]},{heading:`Follow-up, counselling and referral`,points:[`**Before starting any AAD** document: indication, echo findings, baseline **ECG (PR, QRS, QTc)**, potassium, magnesium, creatinine clearance, TSH and LFT, and the **stopping rule**. [ESC 2024]`,`**Review 1-2 weeks after starting or changing a dose** with ECG and potassium/creatinine; then every **6-12 months** (digoxin and flecainide) or **6-monthly** with blood tests (amiodarone). [ESC 2024]`,`**Check interactions with every new prescription**: amiodarone plus **warfarin** (reduce warfarin by 30-50%, INR weekly), plus **simvastatin** (maximum 20 mg), plus **digoxin** (halve); any QT drug plus a macrolide, fluoroquinolone, azole, ondansetron or antipsychotic. [BNF 2025]`,`**Sick-day advice for digoxin**: during vomiting, diarrhoea or poor intake, seek review for potassium and creatinine - dehydration and diuretics are the commonest route to toxicity. [Goldfrank 11e]`,`**Counsel**: report palpitations, fainting, visual change, cough or breathlessness; use sunscreen on amiodarone; avoid over-the-counter QT drugs; take pill-in-the-pocket sitting down. [HRS 2007]`,`**Refer** to a cardiologist or electrophysiologist for any class Ic or III initiation, recurrent arrhythmia on drugs, **QTc over 500 ms**, QRS widening over 25%, amiodarone organ toxicity, and for **ablation or ICD** decisions. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`Link chronic patients to **NP-NCD clinics at Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for BP, diabetes and drug refills, with ECG and electrolyte checks at the CHC or district hospital. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates (2022-2026)`,points:[`**ESC 2024 AF (AF-CARE)**: rhythm-control drug chosen by substrate - **flecainide/propafenone** without significant LVH, HFrEF or coronary disease, **amiodarone** in HFrEF, **dronedarone** in HFmrEF/HFpEF and coronary disease. [ESC 2024]`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: **digoxin is a first-line rate-control option** with beta-blockers and diltiazem/verapamil, using low doses aiming for a **serum level under 1.2 ng/mL**. [ESC 2024]`,`**AHA 2025 adult ALS**: **amiodarone or lidocaine** for shock-refractory VF/pulseless VT unchanged; adenosine 6 mg then 12 mg; atropine 1 mg for bradycardia. [AHA 2025]`,`**ESC 2022 VA/SCD**: IV **procainamide** preferred for haemodynamically tolerated monomorphic VT where available, **catheter ablation** over escalating AADs for recurrent ischaemic VT. [ESC 2022 VA]`,`**VANISH2 (2024)**: in ischaemic cardiomyopathy with VT, **first-line catheter ablation** reduced death, VT storm and ICD shocks compared with antiarrhythmic drugs. [NEJM 2024]`,`**DIGIT-HF (2025)**: **digitoxin** added to GDMT in HFrEF reduced death or HF hospitalisation (hazard ratio about 0.82); digitoxin is not marketed in India. [NEJM 2025]`,`**AGS Beers 2023**: avoid digoxin over **0.125 mg/day** and as first-line for AF or HF in older adults; avoid dronedarone in permanent AF or decompensated HF. [AGS Beers 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: modernised Vaughan Williams classification; adverse effects and monitoring of amiodarone; ECG of digoxin toxicity; management of digoxin toxicity with Fab dosing; why flecainide is contraindicated after MI (CAST). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: calling the **reverse-tick ST depression** digoxin toxicity - it shows the drug is present; toxicity needs symptoms, arrhythmia or a high level. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting to **halve digoxin and cut warfarin** when amiodarone is added, or measuring digoxin **within 6 hours** of a dose. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Common mistake**: giving **IV verapamil to a broad-complex tachycardia** or to AF with WPW; and starting sotalol when creatinine clearance is **under 40 mL/min**. [AHA 2025]`,`**Viva one-liners**: lupus - **procainamide**; cinchonism - **quinidine**; blue-grey skin and corneal deposits - **amiodarone**; phosphenes - **ivabradine**; CPVT add-on - **flecainide**; antidote - **digoxin-specific Fab**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Viva**: 'What is regularised AF?' - AF with a slow regular ventricular rhythm because of complete heart block and a junctional escape, a sign of digoxin toxicity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Why does hypokalaemia worsen digoxin toxicity?' - potassium and digoxin compete for the same site on Na+/K+-ATPase, so low potassium increases binding. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Modernised Vaughan Williams classification (Lei 2018)`,columns:[`Class`,`Target / mechanism`,`Drugs`,`ECG effect and key use`],rows:[[`0`,`HCN (If) channel block`,`Ivabradine`,`Slows sinus rate only; inappropriate sinus tachycardia, HFrEF in sinus rhythm`],[`Ia`,`Na block, intermediate kinetics, plus IKr block`,`Quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide`,`QRS and QT longer; Brugada storm (quinidine), pre-excited AF (procainamide)`],[`Ib`,`Na block, fast kinetics (ischaemic tissue)`,`Lidocaine, mexiletine`,`Little ECG change; ischaemic VT, LQT3`],[`Ic`,`Na block, slow kinetics; RyR2 block`,`Flecainide, propafenone`,`QRS widens; AF in a structurally normal heart, CPVT add-on`],[`Id`,`Late sodium current`,`Ranolazine`,`Angina; minor antiarrhythmic effect`],[`II`,`Autonomic: beta-block (IIa), muscarinic block (IIc), M2 activation (IId), A1 activation (IIe)`,`Beta-blockers; atropine; digoxin; adenosine`,`Slow HR, longer PR; rate control, post-MI, LQTS, CPVT, SVT termination`],[`III`,`Potassium channel block (IKr, IKs, IKur); IIIb KATP opening`,`Amiodarone, dronedarone, sotalol, dofetilide, ibutilide; nicorandil`,`QT longer; AF rhythm control, VT`],[`IV`,`Calcium handling: L-type block (IVa), RyR2 block (IVb)`,`Verapamil, diltiazem; flecainide, propafenone`,`Longer PR; AVNRT, AF rate control`],[`V and VI`,`Mechanosensitive channels; gap junctions`,`None in clinical use`,`Experimental`],[`VII`,`Upstream substrate remodelling`,`ACE inhibitors, ARBs, statins, omega-3`,`Prevents remodelling; no acute effect`]]},{heading:`Antiarrhythmic and digoxin drug table`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`IV 300 mg over 30-60 min then 900-1200 mg/24 h; oral 600-800 mg/day to about 10 g`,`Maintain 100-200 mg once daily; lowest effective dose`,`Thyroid, lung, liver, eye, skin toxicity; halve digoxin, cut warfarin 30-50%`],[`Dronedarone`,`400 mg oral twice daily with meals`,`Long term; LFT and creatinine early`,`Contraindicated in decompensated or NYHA IV HF and permanent AF; hepatotoxicity`],[`Flecainide`,`50-100 mg oral twice daily; pill-in-pocket 200-300 mg`,`Max 150 mg twice daily; ECG after each change`,`No MI, CAD, LV dysfunction or significant LVH; add AV-nodal blocker; stop if QRS up over 25%`],[`Propafenone`,`150-300 mg oral three times daily; pill-in-pocket 450-600 mg`,`Titrate weekly with ECG`,`Same rules as flecainide; bronchospasm, metallic taste`],[`Sotalol`,`80 mg oral twice daily, up to 160 mg twice daily`,`Start in hospital, 3 days ECG; once daily if CrCl 40-60`,`Avoid if CrCl under 40 or QTc over 450 ms; torsades; asthma`],[`Dofetilide (not in India)`,`500 µg twice daily if CrCl over 60; 250 µg if 40-60; 125 µg if 20-40`,`3-day inpatient loading with QTc checks`,`Torsades; contraindicated with verapamil, trimethoprim, HCTZ, ketoconazole`],[`Lidocaine`,`1-1.5 mg/kg IV, then 0.5-0.75 mg/kg to 3 mg/kg; infusion 1-4 mg/min`,`Hours to days in VT`,`Perioral numbness, confusion, seizures; reduce in liver or heart failure`],[`Adenosine`,`6 mg rapid IV push with flush, then 12 mg`,`Single doses`,`Flushing, chest tightness, brief asystole; 3 mg with central line, dipyridamole or transplant`],[`Verapamil`,`5-10 mg IV over 2 min; oral 40-120 mg three times daily`,`Repeat IV after 15-30 min`,`Never in broad-complex tachycardia, WPW-AF or HFrEF; constipation`],[`Metoprolol / esmolol`,`Metoprolol 2.5-5 mg IV up to 3 doses; esmolol 500 µg/kg then 50-300 µg/kg/min`,`Oral metoprolol 25-100 mg twice daily`,`Bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm; avoid in acute decompensated HF`],[`Digoxin`,`0.0625-0.25 mg oral daily; load 0.5 mg IV then to 0.75-1.5 mg in 24 h`,`Level at 6+ h; target 0.5-0.9 ng/mL (HF), under 1.2 (AF)`,`Toxicity with low K, CKD, amiodarone, verapamil; avoid in WPW-AF, obstructive HCM`],[`Digoxin-specific Fab`,`40 mg vial binds 0.5 mg digoxin; IV over 30 min`,`Acute 10 vials, chronic 3-6 vials; repeat if needed`,`Hypokalaemia, return of fast AF or HF; rebound in renal failure`]]},{heading:`Amiodarone toxicity and monitoring`,columns:[`Organ`,`Toxicity and frequency`,`Test and timing`,`Action`],rows:[[`Thyroid`,`Hypothyroidism or thyrotoxicosis in 15-20%`,`TSH baseline and 6-monthly`,`Hypo: levothyroxine, continue drug; AIT type 1 carbimazole plus perchlorate, type 2 prednisolone`],[`Lung`,`Pneumonitis or fibrosis in 1-5%; dose-related`,`CXR baseline and yearly; PFT with DLCO baseline and if symptoms`,`Stop drug, steroids; exclude infection and heart failure`],[`Liver`,`Transaminase rise 15-30%; hepatitis rare`,`LFT baseline and 6-monthly`,`Reduce or stop if over 2-3 times upper limit`],[`Eye`,`Corneal deposits over 90%; optic neuropathy under 2%`,`Eye examination if visual symptoms`,`Deposits: continue; optic neuropathy: stop`],[`Skin`,`Photosensitivity; blue-grey pigmentation`,`Clinical review`,`Sunscreen, protective clothing`],[`Heart`,`Bradycardia, AV block, QT prolongation`,`ECG baseline and each visit`,`Reduce dose; pacing if needed`],[`Nerves`,`Tremor, ataxia, peripheral neuropathy`,`Clinical review`,`Reduce dose`]]},{heading:`Digoxin interactions and precipitants`,columns:[`Factor`,`Effect`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Amiodarone`,`Level roughly doubles (P-gp inhibition)`,`Halve digoxin; level after 1-2 weeks`],[`Dronedarone`,`Level about 2.5-fold`,`Halve digoxin or avoid`],[`Verapamil, diltiazem`,`Level up 20-75%; additive AV block`,`Reduce digoxin; watch heart rate`],[`Clarithromycin, erythromycin, itraconazole`,`Level rises (P-gp, gut flora)`,`Use another antibiotic or monitor`],[`Loop or thiazide diuretics`,`Low potassium and magnesium`,`Check K and Mg; keep K 4-5 mmol/L`],[`NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, dehydration`,`Lower renal clearance`,`Avoid NSAIDs; sick-day review`],[`Hypothyroidism, hypoxia, hypercalcaemia`,`Toxicity at normal level`,`Correct the cause; lower dose`],[`Rifampicin, antacids, cholestyramine`,`Level falls`,`Separate doses; recheck level`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of the unwell patient on digoxin`,columns:[`Condition`,`Shared features`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Digoxin toxicity`,`Nausea, confusion, bradycardia, ectopy`,`Xanthopsia, atrial tachycardia with block, bidirectional VT, high level`],[`Hyperkalaemia (CKD, RAS blockers)`,`Bradycardia, broad QRS`,`Peaked T waves; no ectopy-plus-block; level normal`],[`Beta-blocker or CCB overdose`,`Bradycardia, AV block, hypotension`,`Glucose low (beta-blocker) or high (CCB); no ectopy`],[`Sick sinus syndrome`,`Bradycardia, pauses, dizziness`,`Chronic, no GI or visual symptoms, normal level`],[`CPVT or aconite poisoning`,`Bidirectional VT`,`Young patient on exertion (CPVT); oral paraesthesia after herbal remedy (aconite)`],[`Drug-induced torsades`,`Polymorphic VT, syncope`,`Long QT on sotalol, dofetilide or QT drugs; digoxin shortens QT`]]},{heading:`Digoxin numbers to remember`,columns:[`Item`,`Value`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Half-life`,`36-48 hours`,`Up to 4-6 days in anuria; steady state about 7 days`],[`Sampling time`,`At least 6 hours after dose`,`Earlier samples read falsely high`],[`Target level`,`0.5-0.9 ng/mL in HF; under 1.2 in AF`,`Over 2 ng/mL usually toxic`],[`Fab binding`,`1 vial (40 mg) binds 0.5 mg`,`Vials = level x weight / 100`],[`Acute Fab triggers`,`K over 5.0; over 10 mg adult; over 4 mg child`,`Or life-threatening arrhythmia`],[`Empiric Fab dose`,`10 vials acute; 3-6 vials chronic`,`Repeat if no response in 1 hour`],[`Conversion`,`1 ng/mL = 1.28 nmol/L`,`Labs may report either unit`]]}],redFlags:[`Any **ventricular arrhythmia, bidirectional VT or VF** in a patient taking digoxin.`,`Heart rate **under 40/min, high-grade AV block, hypotension or syncope** on digoxin or an antiarrhythmic.`,`Acute digoxin or **yellow oleander** ingestion with **potassium over 5.0 mmol/L**, or digoxin over 10 mg (adult) or 4 mg (child).`,`**QTc over 500 ms**, a QTc rise over 60 ms, or torsades/syncope on sotalol, dofetilide or another QT-prolonging drug.`,`**QRS widening over 25%** or a very fast broad-complex tachycardia (1:1 flutter) on flecainide or propafenone.`,`New **cough, breathlessness and infiltrates** on amiodarone (pulmonary toxicity).`,`**Thyrotoxicosis on amiodarone** with worsening arrhythmia or heart failure.`,`**Painless visual loss or disc swelling** on amiodarone (optic neuropathy).`],pearls:[`Digoxin toxicity = **increased automaticity plus impaired conduction**: atrial tachycardia with block and bidirectional VT are the signature rhythms.`,`The **reverse-tick ST depression** is digoxin effect, not toxicity.`,`Adding amiodarone? **Halve the digoxin, cut warfarin by a third to a half, cap simvastatin at 20 mg.**`,`Flecainide rules: **normal heart, AV-nodal blocker on board, ECG for QRS widening.**`,`Sotalol and dofetilide: **dose by creatinine clearance, start in hospital, stop at QTc 500 ms.**`,`Amiodarone: **TSH and LFT every 6 months, chest X-ray every year**; corneal deposits are harmless, optic neuropathy is not.`,`AIT type 1 = **Iodine-driven, treat with thIonamides**; type 2 = **inflammatory, treat with steroids**.`,`Low potassium makes digoxin toxic at a normal level; **high potassium in acute overdose predicts death**.`,`CAST taught that **suppressing ectopy is not the same as preventing death**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-q1`,stem:`An 80-year-old woman on digoxin and furosemide presents with vomiting and yellow vision. Which ECG finding is most specific for digoxin toxicity?`,options:[`Sagging ST depression with a short QT interval`,`Bidirectional ventricular tachycardia`,`Peaked T waves with a broad QRS`,`Polymorphic VT with a long QT interval`],answer:1,explanation:`Bidirectional VT (alternating QRS axis beat to beat) is near pathognomonic of digoxin toxicity, reflecting calcium-overload delayed afterdepolarisations. Sagging ST depression with a short QT is the digoxin effect seen at therapeutic levels, peaked T waves point to hyperkalaemia, and polymorphic VT with long QT is torsades from QT-prolonging drugs - digoxin shortens QT.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-q2`,stem:`A 70-year-old man with permanent AF and HFrEF is stable on digoxin 0.25 mg daily. The cardiologist starts oral amiodarone for recurrent VT. What is the correct change to his digoxin?`,options:[`Continue the same dose and recheck the level in 6 months`,`Double the digoxin dose because amiodarone induces its metabolism`,`Stop amiodarone because the combination is absolutely contraindicated`,`Reduce the digoxin dose by about half and recheck the level after 1-2 weeks`],answer:3,explanation:`Amiodarone inhibits P-glycoprotein and roughly doubles the digoxin level, so the digoxin dose is halved and a level checked once the new steady state is approached. Continuing the same dose invites toxicity, amiodarone does not induce digoxin clearance, and the combination is not contraindicated - it is commonly used in HFrEF with dose adjustment.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-q3`,stem:`A 60-year-old man with a previous anterior MI and LVEF 35% develops symptomatic paroxysmal AF. Which drug is contraindicated for his rhythm control?`,options:[`Flecainide`,`Amiodarone`,`Bisoprolol`,`Digoxin`],answer:0,explanation:`Flecainide, a class Ic sodium-channel blocker, increased mortality after MI in the CAST trial and is contraindicated with prior MI, coronary disease or LV dysfunction because slowed conduction in scar promotes re-entrant VT. Amiodarone is the rhythm-control drug of choice in HFrEF, while bisoprolol and digoxin are appropriate rate-control drugs.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-q4`,stem:`A 25-year-old woman ingested 40 tablets of her mother's digoxin 0.25 mg 3 hours ago. HR is 38/min with second-degree AV block and serum potassium is 6.1 mmol/L. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`IV calcium gluconate to stabilise the membrane`,`Urgent haemodialysis to remove digoxin`,`Digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab)`,`IV amiodarone loading`],answer:2,explanation:`Acute ingestion of 10 mg with bradyarrhythmia and potassium over 5.0 mmol/L meets several Fab indications, and Fab corrects both the hyperkalaemia and the block. Haemodialysis does not remove digoxin because of its large volume of distribution, IV calcium was traditionally avoided and does not treat the cause, and amiodarone raises digoxin levels and worsens AV block.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-q5`,stem:`A 55-year-old man on amiodarone for 2 years develops thyrotoxicosis. Thyroid examination is normal, colour-flow Doppler shows absent vascularity and antibodies are negative. What is the best treatment?`,options:[`Carbimazole with potassium perchlorate`,`Oral prednisolone 30-40 mg daily, tapered over about 3 months`,`Levothyroxine and continue amiodarone`,`Radioiodine ablation`],answer:1,explanation:`A normal gland with absent Doppler flow and negative antibodies is type 2 amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis, a destructive thyroiditis that responds to glucocorticoids. Carbimazole with perchlorate is for type 1 (iodine-driven synthesis in an abnormal gland with increased flow), levothyroxine treats amiodarone hypothyroidism, and radioiodine fails because iodine-loaded glands have very low uptake.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Describe the mechanism of action and pharmacokinetics of digoxin. Discuss the precipitating factors, clinical features, ECG changes and management of digoxin toxicity.`,openingLines:[`Digoxin is a cardiac glycoside that inhibits myocardial Na+/K+-ATPase, giving positive inotropy and vagally mediated slowing of the SA and AV nodes; its narrow therapeutic index makes toxicity common in the elderly.`,`Digoxin toxicity is a clinical diagnosis - GI, visual and neurological symptoms with arrhythmias of increased automaticity plus conduction block - supported by the serum level and potassium, and reversed by digoxin-specific Fab.`],answer:[{heading:`Mechanism and pharmacokinetics`,points:[`Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition raises intracellular sodium, reduces calcium extrusion by the Na-Ca exchanger and increases SR calcium - **positive inotropy**.`,`Vagomimetic action slows SA node and AV conduction - **rate control in AF**.`,`Bioavailability 60-80%, **half-life 36-48 hours**, 60-80% renal excretion, large volume of distribution - not dialysable.`,`Dose **0.0625-0.25 mg daily**; level at least 6 hours post-dose; target **0.5-0.9 ng/mL in HF**.`]},{heading:`Precipitating factors`,points:[`Old age, **renal impairment**, dehydration, low muscle mass.`,`**Hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypercalcaemia**, hypothyroidism, hypoxia, ischaemia.`,`Drugs: **amiodarone, dronedarone, verapamil**, quinidine, macrolides, azoles; diuretics via low potassium.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`GI: anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain - earliest.`,`Neurological: fatigue, confusion, delirium; visual: **xanthopsia**, haloes, blurring.`,`Acute overdose: vomiting, **hyperkalaemia**, bradycardia; chronic: vague malaise, ectopy, often hypokalaemia.`]},{heading:`ECG changes`,points:[`Digoxin effect: reverse-tick ST depression, short QT - not toxicity.`,`Toxicity: **ventricular bigeminy**, atrial tachycardia with block, accelerated junctional rhythm, regularised AF.`,`Sinus bradycardia, AV block, **bidirectional VT**, VF.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Admit, monitor, **stop digoxin** and interacting drugs; activated charcoal 50 g within 1-2 hours of acute ingestion.`,`Correct potassium (keep 4-5) and magnesium; treat hyperkalaemia with insulin-dextrose.`,`Atropine 0.5-1 mg for bradycardia; lidocaine for ventricular arrhythmia; avoid amiodarone and class Ia.`,`**Fab** for life-threatening arrhythmia, K over 5.0 (acute), over 10 mg ingested, level over 10-15 ng/mL; vials = level x weight / 100; empiric 10 vials acute, 3-6 chronic.`,`Pacing only if Fab is unavailable; contact the AIIMS Poisons Information Centre.`]},{heading:`Prevention`,points:[`Low dose in the elderly and CKD (Beers: avoid over 0.125 mg/day); halve with amiodarone.`,`Check potassium and creatinine with diuretics and during intercurrent illness.`]}],mustDraw:[`ECG strips: reverse-tick ST depression versus bidirectional VT`,`Flow chart: suspected digoxin toxicity to Fab decision`],markSplit:[{part:`Mechanism and pharmacokinetics`,marks:2},{part:`Precipitating factors`,marks:1},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`ECG changes`,marks:2},{part:`Management including Fab`,marks:3}],keywords:[`digoxin`,`Na+/K+-ATPase`,`xanthopsia`,`bidirectional VT`,`hyperkalaemia`,`digoxin Fab`,`regularised AF`]},{id:`cardiovascular-antiarrhythmic-digoxin-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on amiodarone: adverse effects and monitoring.`,openingLines:[`Amiodarone is a multichannel class III antiarrhythmic (also class I, II and IV actions) with a half-life of about 50-60 days and 37% iodine content.`,`It is the rhythm-control drug of choice in structural heart disease and HFrEF, but organ toxicity demands baseline and 6-monthly monitoring.`],answer:[{heading:`Dose`,points:[`IV 300 mg over 30-60 min then 900-1200 mg/24 h; oral load to about 10 g, then 100-200 mg daily.`]},{heading:`Adverse effects`,points:[`**Thyroid**: hypothyroidism (levothyroxine, continue) or thyrotoxicosis - type 1 thionamides plus perchlorate, type 2 steroids.`,`**Lung**: pneumonitis or fibrosis in 1-5%, DLCO fall; stop and give steroids.`,`**Liver**: enzyme rise 15-30%; stop if over 2-3 times normal.`,`**Eye**: corneal deposits (harmless), optic neuropathy (stop); **skin**: photosensitivity, blue-grey colour.`,`**Heart**: bradycardia, AV block, QT prolongation; torsades under 1%.`]},{heading:`Monitoring`,points:[`Baseline TSH, LFT, chest X-ray, PFT with DLCO, ECG.`,`TSH and LFT every 6 months; chest X-ray yearly; ECG each visit; eye and PFT if symptoms.`]},{heading:`Interactions`,points:[`Halve digoxin, reduce warfarin 30-50%, simvastatin maximum 20 mg; avoid sofosbuvir and other QT drugs.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: organ, toxicity, test and timing, action`],markSplit:[{part:`Dose and pharmacology`,marks:1},{part:`Adverse effects`,marks:2},{part:`Monitoring schedule`,marks:1},{part:`Interactions`,marks:1}],keywords:[`amiodarone`,`AIT type 1 and 2`,`pulmonary toxicity`,`corneal microdeposits`,`monitoring`,`interactions`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of atrial fibrillation (AF-CARE)`,`2022 ESC Guidelines for the management of patients with ventricular arrhythmias and the prevention of sudden cardiac death`,`2025 American Heart Association Guidelines for Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation and Emergency Cardiovascular Care: Adult Advanced Life Support`,`Lei M et al. Modernized classification of cardiac antiarrhythmic drugs. Circulation 2018`,`Goldfrank's Toxicologic Emergencies, 11th edition, 2019; National List of Essential Medicines, MoHFW 2022`]}),u.push({id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism`,title:`Pulmonary embolism: risk stratification and inpatient management`,frequency:`core`,oneLiner:`**Pulmonary embolism (PE)** is obstruction of the pulmonary arteries by thrombus, usually from the leg veins; diagnosis follows **clinical probability (Wells, PERC, YEARS) then D-dimer (age-adjusted) or CTPA**, and treatment follows the **ESC risk class** - **systemic thrombolysis for high-risk (shock) PE**, anticoagulation with monitoring and rescue reperfusion for **intermediate-high risk (RV dysfunction plus troponin)**, and **early discharge on a DOAC for low-risk PE (sPESI 0 or Hestia negative)** - with at least **3 months of anticoagulation** and follow-up for **CTEPH**.`,keywords:[`pulmonary embolism`,`PE`,`venous thromboembolism`,`VTE`,`Wells score`,`PERC`,`YEARS algorithm`,`age-adjusted D-dimer`,`CTPA`,`V/Q scan`,`sPESI`,`PESI`,`Hestia criteria`,`massive PE`,`submassive PE`,`alteplase`,`catheter-directed thrombolysis`,`McConnell sign`,`S1Q3T3`,`CTEPH`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Pulmonary embolism** is obstruction of the pulmonary arterial tree by material from elsewhere - in over 90% **thrombus from the deep veins of the legs or pelvis**; with DVT it forms one disease, **venous thromboembolism (VTE)**. [ESC 2019]`,`PE is the **third commonest acute cardiovascular syndrome** after myocardial infarction and stroke, with an annual incidence of about **39-115 per 100,000**, rising steeply after 60 years. [ESC 2019]`,`**Compression ultrasound finds DVT in about 30-50%** of patients with PE, and silent PE is common in proximal DVT - the two are managed as one disease. [ESC 2019]`,`**Early mortality depends on the right ventricle**: 30-day mortality is about **1% with sPESI 0** and about **11% with sPESI 1 or more**, and highest in shock or cardiac arrest. [ESC 2019]`,`**India**: the multinational **ENDORSE study**, which included Indian hospitals, found about half of hospitalised patients at risk of VTE but recommended prophylaxis given to only about half of them - PE is **under-diagnosed and under-prevented**. [ENDORSE, Lancet 2008]`,`Indian hospital series describe VTE at a **younger age**, with the **postpartum state, antiphospholipid syndrome and hyperhomocysteinaemia** as frequent associations, and many deaths recognised only at autopsy. [API Textbook 2022]`,`Asian populations were once thought to have little VTE; wider access to **CT pulmonary angiography** has shown incidence far higher than assumed. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Virchow triad**: venous **stasis**, **endothelial injury** and **hypercoagulability** - most patients have more than one. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Strong risk factors (odds ratio over 10)**: lower-limb fracture, hip or knee replacement, major trauma, spinal cord injury, hospital admission for heart failure or AF within 3 months, MI within 3 months, and **previous VTE**. [ESC 2019]`,`**Moderate (odds ratio 2-9)**: knee arthroscopy, central venous lines, **chemotherapy**, heart or respiratory failure, **oral contraceptives and HRT**, IVF, **postpartum**, infection (pneumonia, UTI, HIV), inflammatory bowel disease, **cancer (highest with metastases)**, paralytic stroke, superficial vein thrombosis and thrombophilia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Weak (odds ratio under 2)**: bed rest over 3 days, long-distance travel, age, laparoscopic surgery, obesity, **pregnancy**, varicose veins, diabetes and hypertension. [ESC 2019]`,`**Provoked versus unprovoked**: a **major transient factor** (surgery under general anaesthesia over 30 minutes, bed-bound 3 days or more with acute illness, trauma with fracture) within 3 months predicts low recurrence; none identified predicts higher recurrence. [ESC 2019]`,`**Thrombophilia**: inherited - antithrombin, **protein C and protein S deficiency**, prothrombin G20210A and factor V Leiden (less common in Indians than Europeans); acquired - **antiphospholipid syndrome**, nephrotic syndrome, myeloproliferative neoplasm, PNH, hyperhomocysteinaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-thrombotic emboli**: **fat** (long-bone fracture - petechiae, confusion), **amniotic fluid** (labour - collapse and DIC), air, tumour, **septic** (tricuspid endocarditis, IV drug use) and bone cement. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Clot obstruction plus **vasoconstriction from thromboxane A2 and serotonin** raises pulmonary vascular resistance; a previously normal RV cannot acutely generate a mean pulmonary pressure above about **40 mmHg**. [ESC 2019]`,`The RV **dilates**, tricuspid regurgitation appears and RV contraction is prolonged into early LV diastole, so the septum bows left (**D-shaped LV**) - **ventricular interdependence** cuts LV filling and cardiac output. [ESC 2019]`,`Falling aortic pressure with rising RV wall stress starves the RV of coronary flow, causing **RV ischaemia and troponin release** - a spiral into **obstructive shock** that explains why RV dysfunction plus troponin predicts death. [ESC 2019]`,`**Hypoxaemia** comes from **ventilation-perfusion mismatch** (flow diverted to non-embolised lung), low mixed venous oxygen from low output, and **right-to-left shunting through a PFO**; hyperventilation causes hypocapnia. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pulmonary infarction** is uncommon (about 10%) because of dual bronchial supply; small distal emboli cause **pleuritic pain, haemoptysis and a wedge-shaped pleural opacity**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Why fluids can harm**: an overloaded RV dilates further with volume, worsening septal shift - only a cautious bolus is given, and **norepinephrine** restores RV perfusion pressure. [ESC 2019]`,`**Why thrombolysis works early**: plasmin lyses fresh fibrin and rapidly lowers pulmonary resistance; benefit is greatest within **48 hours** but persists to about **14 days**, after which clot organises. [ESC 2019]`,`**CTEPH** follows incomplete resolution with **fibrotic organisation of thrombus** plus a small-vessel arteriopathy, keeping pulmonary resistance high months after the event. [ESC/ERS 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification and risk classes`,points:[`**ESC 2019 early mortality risk** has four classes: **high, intermediate-high, intermediate-low and low** - based on haemodynamics, PESI/sPESI, RV dysfunction and troponin. [ESC 2019]`,`**Haemodynamic instability (high risk)**: cardiac arrest; **obstructive shock** (systolic under 90 mmHg or vasopressors needed despite adequate filling, with end-organ hypoperfusion); or **persistent hypotension** (systolic under 90 or a fall of 40 mmHg or more for over 15 minutes, not due to arrhythmia, hypovolaemia or sepsis). [ESC 2019]`,`**Intermediate-high**: normotensive, PESI III-V or sPESI 1 or more, with **both RV dysfunction (echo or CTPA) and raised troponin**; **intermediate-low**: one or neither. [ESC 2019]`,`**Low risk**: PESI I-II or **sPESI 0**, no RV dysfunction and normal troponin if measured; a patient with sPESI 0 but RV dysfunction or raised troponin is classed intermediate-low. [ESC 2019]`,`**Older terms**: **massive PE** = high-risk, **submassive PE** = normotensive with RV dysfunction or myocardial necrosis (intermediate risk). [AHA 2011]`,`**Anatomical** labels - saddle, main, lobar, segmental, **subsegmental** - describe clot location but **do not define risk**; a saddle embolus in a normotensive patient is not high-risk PE. [ESC 2019]`,`**Recurrence-risk classes** after stopping anticoagulation: **low (under 3% per year)** after a major transient factor, **intermediate (3-8%)** with minor or persistent factors or none, **high (over 8%)** with active cancer, previous VTE or antiphospholipid syndrome. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features: history and examination`,points:[`**Dyspnoea** (about three-quarters, sudden or progressive) and **pleuritic chest pain** (about 45%) are the commonest symptoms; cough, **haemoptysis** (about 10-15%) and calf pain or swelling follow. [PIOPED II]`,`**Syncope or presyncope** signals a large embolus with RV failure and transient low output - always look for PE in unexplained syncope with breathlessness or tachycardia. [ESC 2019]`,`**Atypical presentations**: worsening breathlessness in COPD or heart failure, new **AF**, unexplained tachycardia or hypoxia after surgery or delivery, low-grade fever, or **PEA cardiac arrest**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Take a **risk-factor history**: recent surgery, plaster cast, travel, immobility, **oral contraceptives**, pregnancy or delivery, cancer symptoms (weight loss, bleeding), previous VTE and family history. [ESC 2019]`,`**Tachypnoea (respiratory rate over 20)** is the commonest sign, with tachycardia, hypoxia and sometimes fever; the chest is often clear despite marked breathlessness - a clue in itself. [PIOPED II]`,`**Signs of DVT**: unilateral leg swelling (calf difference **3 cm or more**, measured 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity), pitting oedema and tenderness along the deep veins. [NICE NG158]`,`**RV strain signs**: **raised JVP, left parasternal heave, loud P2, right-sided S3 and TR murmur**; with hypotension, cold peripheries or confusion, the patient is in high-risk PE. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pleural rub or small effusion** suggests pulmonary infarction; paradoxical embolism through a PFO can present as **stroke**. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Clinical probability and D-dimer`,points:[`**Wells score**: DVT signs 3, PE the most likely diagnosis 3, heart rate over 100 1.5, immobilisation or surgery within 4 weeks 1.5, previous VTE 1.5, haemoptysis 1, cancer 1; **over 4 = PE likely**, 4 or less = PE unlikely. [ESC 2019]`,`**Revised Geneva score** uses only objective items (age over 65, previous VTE, surgery or fracture, cancer, unilateral leg pain, haemoptysis, heart rate, leg vein tenderness with oedema) - useful where 'most likely diagnosis' is contested. [ESC 2019]`,`**PERC rule** - only when clinical gestalt is low (under 15%): if **all eight are absent** (age 50 or more, HR 100 or more, SpO2 under 95%, unilateral leg swelling, haemoptysis, recent surgery or trauma, prior VTE, hormone use) **no D-dimer is needed**. [ESC 2019]`,`**D-dimer** (high-sensitivity ELISA) has over **95% sensitivity** but low specificity; use it only when PE is unlikely or probability is low - a **negative result excludes PE**; never use it to rule out PE when probability is high. [ESC 2019]`,`**Age-adjusted cut-off**: over 50 years use **age x 10 µg/L (FEU)**; in ADJUST-PE this raised the proportion of over-75s in whom PE was excluded from about 6% to about **30%** with a miss rate of 0.3%. [ESC 2019]`,`**YEARS algorithm**: three items (DVT signs, haemoptysis, PE most likely); **no items and D-dimer under 1000 ng/mL** or **one or more items and D-dimer under 500** excludes PE, otherwise CTPA - it reduces CT use. [ESC 2019]`,`D-dimer is often raised in **cancer, pregnancy, infection, surgery, inflammation, old age and inpatients**, so its yield is low in these groups. [ESC 2019]`,`With **high or intermediate probability, start parenteral anticoagulation before imaging** unless bleeding risk is high. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Imaging and bedside tests`,points:[`**CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA)** is first-line; it also shows **RV/LV diameter ratio 1.0 or more** (RV dysfunction) and alternative diagnoses; a normal CTPA excludes PE in low or intermediate probability. [ESC 2019]`,`**V/Q scan** is preferred in **pregnancy, severe CKD, contrast allergy and young women**; a **normal perfusion scan excludes PE**, a high-probability scan confirms it, and non-diagnostic results need further testing. [ESC 2019]`,`**Compression ultrasound**: a proximal DVT in a patient with suspected PE **confirms VTE** and justifies treatment without CTPA - useful when CT is unavailable or contraindicated. [ESC 2019]`,`**Echocardiography** is essential in the unstable patient: **RV dilatation, McConnell sign** (free-wall akinesia with apical sparing), **60/60 sign**, **TAPSE under 16 mm**, D-shaped LV, right-heart thrombus; **no RV dysfunction virtually excludes PE as the cause of shock**. [ESC 2019]`,`**ECG**: sinus tachycardia is commonest; **S1Q3T3**, new RBBB, right axis deviation, **T-wave inversion V1-V4** and AF indicate RV strain - insensitive, but useful against ACS. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Chest X-ray** is often normal or nonspecific - **Westermark sign** (oligaemia), **Hampton hump** (pleural-based wedge), **Fleischner sign** (enlarged pulmonary artery), small effusion - its main use is excluding pneumothorax, pneumonia and oedema. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Arterial blood gas**: hypoxaemia, hypocapnia, respiratory alkalosis and a raised A-a gradient; a **normal SpO2 does not exclude PE**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Biomarkers**: **troponin** (RV injury), **NT-proBNP 600 pg/mL or more** (RV strain) and **lactate 2 mmol/L or more** predict adverse outcome. [ESC 2019]`,`**Before anticoagulation**: blood count and platelets, PT/INR, aPTT, creatinine with **creatinine clearance**, LFT and a pregnancy test in women of child-bearing age. [NICE NG158]`,`**District hospital without CT**: Wells or YEARS, D-dimer, **leg compression ultrasound, bedside echo** and ECG allow treatment decisions and safe referral. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Risk stratification after diagnosis`,points:[`**Step 1 - haemodynamics**: shock, persistent hypotension or arrest means **high-risk PE** - reperfusion now; no further scoring needed. [ESC 2019]`,`**Step 2 - sPESI** (1 point each): **age over 80, cancer, chronic heart or lung disease, heart rate 110 or more, systolic under 100 mmHg, SaO2 under 90%**; sPESI 0 = 30-day mortality about 1%. [ESC 2019]`,`**PESI classes**: I (65 or less) and II (66-85) are low risk; III (86-105), IV (106-125) and V (over 125) carry rising mortality. [ESC 2019]`,`**Step 3 - RV and troponin** in sPESI 1 or more: **both abnormal = intermediate-high**, one or none = intermediate-low. [ESC 2019]`,`**Hestia criteria** (11 questions) are an alternative for selecting outpatients: any 'yes' - instability, need for lysis, bleeding risk, oxygen over 24 hours, PE on anticoagulation, IV analgesia, social reason, **creatinine clearance under 30**, severe liver disease, **pregnancy** or HIT - means admit. [ESC 2019]`,`Additional adverse markers: **syncope, lactate 2 mmol/L or more, concomitant proximal DVT, NT-proBNP over 600** - prompt closer monitoring in the intermediate-high group. [ESC 2019]`,`The class decides **site of care** (home, ward, HDU/ICU), **reperfusion readiness** and monitoring intensity - write the class in the case notes. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute coronary syndrome**: central pressing pain, regional ST change and coronary troponin rise; PE gives tachycardia, hypoxia, right-sided ECG changes and RV dilatation. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumonia**: fever, productive cough, focal consolidation and raised CRP; infarction from PE can mimic it, so check D-dimer or CTPA when risk factors coexist. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Pneumothorax**: sudden pleuritic pain with absent breath sounds and hyperresonance - seen on chest X-ray. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aortic dissection**: tearing pain to the back, pulse or BP asymmetry, widened mediastinum - **thrombolysis would be fatal**, so exclude it before lysis when doubt exists. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Cardiac tamponade**: hypotension and raised JVP with **pulsus paradoxus**, small QRS complexes and a pericardial effusion on echo. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Acute heart failure** and **COPD or asthma exacerbation**: crackles, wheeze, raised BNP with LV dysfunction - PE can coexist and precipitate either. [ESC 2019]`,`**RV infarction**: inferior STEMI with ST elevation in V4R, hypotension and clear lungs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Anxiety hyperventilation and musculoskeletal pain** are diagnoses of exclusion - PE is the classic missed diagnosis in the young woman on the pill labelled 'panic'. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Initial anticoagulation and outpatient treatment`,points:[`**LMWH or fondaparinux is preferred to UFH** for initial treatment: **enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly** (or 1.5 mg/kg daily); **fondaparinux 5 mg (under 50 kg), 7.5 mg (50-100 kg), 10 mg (over 100 kg) SC daily**. [ESC 2019]`,`**Unfractionated heparin** - **80 units/kg IV bolus, then 18 units/kg/h**, aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control - is used in **haemodynamic instability, likely thrombolysis, creatinine clearance under 30 and severe obesity**. [ESC 2019]`,`**DOACs are preferred to warfarin**: **apixaban 10 mg twice daily for 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily**; **rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily for 21 days, then 20 mg daily with food**; dabigatran 150 mg twice daily or edoxaban 60 mg daily after at least 5 days of LMWH. [ESC 2019]`,`**Warfarin**: overlap with LMWH for **at least 5 days and until INR is 2-3 on two days**; target INR 2.5 (range 2-3). [ESC 2019]`,`**Avoid DOACs** in severe renal impairment, **pregnancy and breastfeeding**, **antiphospholipid syndrome (especially triple-positive)**, mechanical valves and with strong interacting drugs such as rifampicin, carbamazepine or HIV protease inhibitors. [ESC 2019]`,`**Early discharge (within 24-48 hours)** on a DOAC is safe in **low-risk PE** (sPESI 0 or Hestia negative) with no RV dysfunction, good home support and access to follow-up. [ESC 2019]`,`**Supportive care**: oxygen if SpO2 is under 90%, paracetamol for pleuritic pain (avoid NSAIDs with anticoagulants), and **early mobilisation** - bed rest is not needed. [NICE NG158]`,`**Isolated subsegmental PE** without DVT in a low-risk patient may be managed by clinical surveillance with serial leg ultrasound rather than anticoagulation. [CHEST 2021]`,`**Counsel** on bleeding signs, adherence, drug interactions and the planned stop date; DOAC generics are now widely available in India at low cost. [NICE NG158]`]},{heading:`High-risk and intermediate-high PE: inpatient and emergency care`,points:[`**High-risk PE**: give **UFH 80 units/kg IV bolus at once**, then **systemic thrombolysis** - it reduces death and recurrence, with clinical response in over 90%. [ESC 2019]`,`**Thrombolytic doses**: **alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours** (or 0.6 mg/kg, maximum 50 mg, over 15 minutes in extremis); **streptokinase 1.5 million IU over 2 hours**; **urokinase 3 million IU over 2 hours**; UFH may continue during alteplase but is stopped during streptokinase or urokinase. [ESC 2019]`,`**Tenecteplase** (weight-based single bolus, 30-50 mg) was used in PEITHO and is widely stocked in India, though not licensed for PE; streptokinase is cheapest but should not be repeated beyond 5 days of a previous dose because of antibodies. [ESC 2019]`,`**Absolute contraindications to lysis**: previous haemorrhagic or unknown stroke, ischaemic stroke within 6 months, CNS neoplasm, major trauma, surgery or head injury within 3 weeks, bleeding diathesis, active bleeding; in **high-risk PE most relative contraindications are outweighed**. [ESC 2019]`,`If lysis is **contraindicated or fails**: **surgical embolectomy** or **percutaneous catheter-directed treatment** (low-dose local lysis, aspiration or mechanical thrombectomy); **VA-ECMO** for refractory collapse at expert centres. [ESC 2019]`,`**Haemodynamic support**: oxygen; **fluid bolus no more than 500 mL** only if CVP is low; **norepinephrine 0.2-1.0 µg/kg/min** and **dobutamine 2-20 µg/kg/min**; avoid intubation if possible, because induction and positive pressure can precipitate arrest. [ESC 2019]`,`**Intermediate-high risk**: anticoagulate (LMWH or UFH) and **monitor in HDU for 48-72 hours**; **routine full-dose lysis is not recommended** - in PEITHO it halved decompensation (2.6% vs 5.6%) but raised **haemorrhagic stroke to 2%**; give **rescue thrombolysis** if the patient deteriorates. [ESC 2019]`,`**Catheter-directed therapy** suits deteriorating intermediate-high patients or high bleeding risk; **PEERLESS (2024)** favoured large-bore mechanical thrombectomy over catheter thrombolysis on a composite of deterioration and ICU use, without a mortality difference. [PEERLESS, Circulation 2024]`,`A multidisciplinary **PE response team (PERT)** should decide reperfusion in high-risk and deteriorating intermediate-risk PE. [ESC 2019]`,`**PE cardiac arrest**: give thrombolysis and **continue CPR for 60-90 minutes**; an **IVC filter** is only for absolute contraindication to anticoagulation or recurrence despite adequate treatment, and should be retrieved. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Duration of anticoagulation`,points:[`**Every patient gets at least 3 months** of therapeutic anticoagulation. [ESC 2019]`,`**First PE provoked by a major transient factor** (surgery, trauma, immobilisation): **stop at 3 months** - recurrence is under 3% per year. [ESC 2019]`,`**Unprovoked PE** (no identifiable factor): **consider extended, open-ended anticoagulation** if bleeding risk is acceptable. [ESC 2019]`,`**Persistent non-cancer risk factor or minor transient factor**: extended anticoagulation should be considered. [ESC 2019]`,`**Recurrent VTE** not linked to a major transient factor: **indefinite** anticoagulation; **antiphospholipid syndrome**: **indefinite warfarin** (INR 2-3), not a DOAC. [ESC 2019]`,`**Extended phase**: after 6 months, **apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily or rivaroxaban 10 mg daily** reduce recurrence with bleeding similar to aspirin. [ESC 2019]`,`**Cancer-associated PE**: LMWH or a DOAC (apixaban, edoxaban, rivaroxaban) for **at least 6 months**, continued while cancer is active; prefer LMWH or apixaban in luminal GI or GU cancers with bleeding risk. [ESC 2019]`,`**Aspirin** is far less effective and is only for patients who refuse or cannot take extended anticoagulation. [ESC 2019]`,`Reassess **bleeding risk, renal function, adherence and patient preference at least yearly** in anyone on extended therapy. [ESC 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations: pregnancy, cancer, elderly, CKD`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: PE is a leading cause of maternal death in high-income countries; VTE risk is highest in the **first 6 weeks postpartum**, and D-dimer rises normally through pregnancy. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Diagnosis in pregnancy**: the **pregnancy-adapted YEARS algorithm** (Artemis) - leg ultrasound first if DVT signs, then YEARS with D-dimer - avoided CTPA in about 39%; if imaging is needed, chest X-ray then **perfusion scan or CTPA**, both with fetal doses far below harmful levels. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Treatment in pregnancy**: **weight-based therapeutic LMWH** (based on early-pregnancy weight); **warfarin and DOACs are avoided**; continue for at least 3 months in total and until at least **6 weeks postpartum**. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Around delivery**: stop therapeutic LMWH **24 hours before** planned delivery or neuraxial anaesthesia; switch to UFH near term if delivery timing is uncertain; postpartum **LMWH or warfarin** are safe in breastfeeding, DOACs are not. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**High-risk PE in pregnancy**: **thrombolysis or surgical embolectomy** is still given - pregnancy is a relative, not absolute, contraindication when the mother is in shock. [ESC 2019]`,`**Elderly**: use the **age-adjusted D-dimer**, check creatinine clearance and fall risk, and do not withhold anticoagulation for age alone. [ESC 2019]`,`**CKD (clearance under 30)**: use **UFH then warfarin**, choose **V/Q over CTPA**; avoid fondaparinux and most DOACs. [ESC 2019]`,`**Unprovoked PE**: do a **limited cancer screen** (history, examination, blood count, calcium, LFT, urinalysis, chest X-ray, age-appropriate screening) - routine CT abdomen adds nothing. [NICE NG158]`,`**Resource-limited settings**: **streptokinase** for high-risk PE, LMWH then warfarin with INR monitoring or a generic DOAC, and early transfer of intermediate-high patients to a centre with ICU. [API Textbook 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications, follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**Early death** is mostly from RV failure in the first hours to days; **recurrent VTE**, **anticoagulant bleeding** (highest in the first 3 months) and **post-PE syndrome** follow. [ESC 2019]`,`**Post-PE impairment**: up to **half of survivors** report persistent breathlessness or poor exercise capacity months to years later. [ESC 2019]`,`**CTEPH** develops in about **2-4%** of survivors, usually within 2 years; suspect it in persistent or new breathlessness after **3 months of effective anticoagulation**. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**CTEPH work-up**: echo for PH probability, then **V/Q scan (a normal scan excludes CTEPH)**, then right-heart catheterisation - **mean PAP over 20 mmHg, PAWP 15 or less, PVR over 2 Wood units** with mismatched perfusion defects. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**CTEPH treatment** at an expert centre: lifelong anticoagulation, **pulmonary endarterectomy**, balloon pulmonary angioplasty or **riociguat** - see the pulmonary hypertension topic. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**Follow-up at 3-6 months**: symptoms, bleeding, adherence, haemoglobin, creatinine clearance; decide stop or extend; screen breathless patients for CTEPH. [ESC 2019]`,`**Hospital prophylaxis**: risk-score every admission (**Padua 4 or more** in medical patients, **Caprini** in surgical); **enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily**, or intermittent pneumatic compression if bleeding risk is high; mobilise early. [CHEST 2021]`,`**Counselling**: avoid oestrogen contraception after VTE (use progestogen-only or IUD), hydrate and walk on long journeys, and plan **pregnancy prophylaxis with LMWH** in women with previous VTE. [NICE NG158]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2019 (with ERS)** remains the current ESC PE guideline: four risk classes, **age-adjusted D-dimer**, YEARS accepted, **DOACs first-line**, early discharge for low risk and structured follow-up for CTEPH. [ESC 2019]`,`**CHEST 2021 (second update)**: DOACs over warfarin, reduced-dose extended DOAC, surveillance for low-risk isolated subsegmental PE, and catheter-assisted thrombus removal for deterioration after anticoagulation. [CHEST 2021]`,`**NICE NG158 (updated 2023)**: apixaban or rivaroxaban first-line, outpatient treatment of low-risk PE using a validated tool (PESI or Hestia), and stopping at 3 months after provoked VTE. [NICE NG158]`,`**ESC/ERS 2022 PH**: CTEPH defined at **mean PAP over 20 mmHg and PVR over 2 Wood units**; recognises **CTEPD without PH**; V/Q scan is the screening test. [ESC/ERS 2022]`,`**ESC 2025 pregnancy**: pregnancy-adapted YEARS diagnostic pathway and therapeutic LMWH as standard; DOACs and warfarin avoided in pregnancy. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**PEERLESS (2024)**: first large randomised comparison of catheter techniques - mechanical thrombectomy reduced deterioration and ICU use versus catheter thrombolysis in intermediate-risk PE. [PEERLESS, Circulation 2024]`,`**Cancer-associated VTE (ASCO 2023, ITAC 2022)**: DOACs (apixaban, edoxaban, rivaroxaban) are first-line alongside LMWH, with caution in luminal GI and GU cancers. [ASCO 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic long question**: approach to suspected PE (probability, D-dimer, CTPA), ESC risk classes and management of massive (high-risk) PE with thrombolysis doses. [ESC 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: ordering D-dimer when clinical probability is high - go straight to CTPA (and anticoagulate meanwhile). [ESC 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: calling a normotensive patient with a saddle embolus 'massive PE' - risk is defined by **haemodynamics, RV function and troponin**, not clot size. [ESC 2019]`,`**Common mistake**: giving large fluid boluses in RV failure, or intubating without preparing for collapse. [ESC 2019]`,`**Viva**: 'Commonest ECG finding?' - **sinus tachycardia**; S1Q3T3 is classic but uncommon. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'Which test in pregnancy?' - leg ultrasound if DVT signs, then perfusion scan or CTPA; treat with LMWH. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Viva**: 'What is the McConnell sign?' - RV free-wall akinesia with preserved apical motion on echo, suggesting acute PE. [ESC 2019]`,`**Viva**: 'Which patients get indefinite anticoagulation?' - recurrent unprovoked VTE, antiphospholipid syndrome (warfarin) and active cancer. [ESC 2019]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in pulmonary embolism`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Unfractionated heparin`,`80 units/kg IV bolus, then 18 units/kg/h`,`aPTT 1.5-2.5 x control, check 6-hourly until stable`,`HIT, bleeding; preferred in shock, lysis, CrCl under 30`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC 12-hourly or 1.5 mg/kg daily`,`At least 5 days before dabigatran/edoxaban or with warfarin until INR 2-3`,`Bleeding; accumulates if CrCl under 30; pregnancy drug of choice`],[`Fondaparinux`,`5 / 7.5 / 10 mg SC daily by weight (under 50, 50-100, over 100 kg)`,`Initial phase`,`Avoid if CrCl under 30; no HIT`],[`Apixaban`,`10 mg oral twice daily for 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily`,`Extended: 2.5 mg twice daily after 6 months`,`Bleeding; avoid in pregnancy, APS, severe CKD`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`15 mg oral twice daily for 21 days, then 20 mg daily with food`,`Extended: 10 mg daily after 6 months`,`Bleeding; avoid in pregnancy, APS, CrCl under 30`],[`Dabigatran / edoxaban`,`Dabigatran 150 mg twice daily; edoxaban 60 mg daily (30 mg if CrCl 15-50 or 60 kg or less)`,`After at least 5 days of parenteral heparin`,`Dyspepsia (dabigatran); renal dosing`],[`Warfarin`,`5 mg oral daily start, adjust to INR 2-3`,`Overlap with LMWH 5+ days; indefinite in APS`,`Bleeding, interactions; teratogenic`],[`Alteplase`,`100 mg IV over 2 h; or 0.6 mg/kg (max 50 mg) over 15 min in arrest`,`Single course; continue UFH`,`Intracranial bleeding; check lysis contraindications`],[`Streptokinase`,`250,000 IU over 30 min then 100,000 IU/h for 12-24 h; or 1.5 million IU over 2 h`,`Stop UFH during infusion`,`Hypotension, allergy; do not repeat after 5 days`],[`Tenecteplase`,`Single weight-based IV bolus 30-50 mg`,`Off-label for PE (PEITHO)`,`Haemorrhagic stroke about 2% in PEITHO`],[`Norepinephrine / dobutamine`,`Norepinephrine 0.2-1.0 µg/kg/min; dobutamine 2-20 µg/kg/min`,`Titrate to MAP and perfusion`,`Arrhythmia; dobutamine may worsen hypotension`]]},{heading:`Clinical decision rules`,columns:[`Rule`,`Items`,`Cut-off and action`],rows:[[`Wells (two-level)`,`DVT signs 3; PE most likely 3; HR over 100 1.5; immobilisation/surgery 1.5; previous VTE 1.5; haemoptysis 1; cancer 1`,`Over 4 likely: CTPA; 4 or less: D-dimer`],[`PERC`,`Age 50+, HR 100+, SpO2 under 95%, leg swelling, haemoptysis, recent surgery/trauma, prior VTE, hormones`,`Low gestalt and all absent: no testing`],[`YEARS`,`DVT signs, haemoptysis, PE most likely`,`0 items: D-dimer under 1000 excludes; 1+ items: under 500 excludes`],[`Age-adjusted D-dimer`,`Age over 50`,`Cut-off = age x 10 µg/L FEU`],[`sPESI`,`Age over 80, cancer, chronic heart/lung disease, HR 110+, SBP under 100, SaO2 under 90%`,`0 = low risk (about 1% 30-day mortality)`],[`Hestia`,`11 questions: instability, lysis, bleeding, oxygen, PE on anticoagulation, pain, social, CrCl under 30, liver, pregnancy, HIT`,`All 'no': outpatient treatment`]]},{heading:`ESC 2019 early mortality risk classes`,columns:[`Risk class`,`Haemodynamic instability`,`PESI III-V or sPESI 1+`,`RV dysfunction (echo/CTPA)`,`Raised troponin`,`Management`],rows:[[`High`,`Yes`,`(Yes)`,`Yes`,`(Yes)`,`UFH plus systemic thrombolysis; surgery or catheter therapy if lysis contraindicated`],[`Intermediate-high`,`No`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`Yes`,`LMWH/UFH, HDU monitoring 48-72 h, rescue reperfusion if deterioration`],[`Intermediate-low`,`No`,`Yes`,`One or neither positive`,`One or neither positive`,`Admit, anticoagulate (LMWH or DOAC)`],[`Low`,`No`,`No`,`No (if assessed)`,`No (if assessed)`,`Early discharge on DOAC`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of suspected PE`,columns:[`Condition`,`Shared features`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Acute coronary syndrome`,`Chest pain, troponin rise, tachycardia`,`Regional ST change, wall-motion defect in coronary territory`],[`Pneumonia`,`Pleuritic pain, fever, haemoptysis`,`Productive cough, consolidation, high CRP`],[`Pneumothorax`,`Sudden pleuritic pain, breathlessness`,`Absent breath sounds, visible pleural line`],[`Aortic dissection`,`Chest pain, shock`,`Tearing back pain, pulse deficit, wide mediastinum`],[`Cardiac tamponade`,`Hypotension, raised JVP`,`Pulsus paradoxus, effusion on echo`],[`Acute heart failure`,`Breathlessness, hypoxia, raised BNP`,`Crackles, LV dysfunction, pulmonary oedema on X-ray`],[`RV infarction`,`Hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs`,`Inferior STEMI with ST elevation in V4R`]]},{heading:`Investigations and their cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Finding`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`D-dimer`,`Under 500 µg/L (or age x 10 over 50)`,`Excludes PE if probability low`],[`CTPA`,`Filling defect; RV/LV ratio 1.0 or more`,`Confirms PE; RV dysfunction`],[`V/Q scan`,`Normal perfusion / high probability`,`Excludes / confirms PE`],[`Compression ultrasound`,`Non-compressible proximal vein`,`Confirms VTE; treat`],[`Echo`,`RV dilatation, McConnell sign, TAPSE under 16 mm, 60/60 sign`,`RV dysfunction; excludes PE as cause of shock if normal`],[`Troponin`,`Above assay upper limit`,`Myocardial injury; with RV dysfunction = intermediate-high`],[`NT-proBNP`,`600 pg/mL or more`,`RV strain, adverse prognosis`],[`Lactate`,`2 mmol/L or more`,`Hypoperfusion, adverse prognosis`]]},{heading:`Duration of anticoagulation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Duration`,`Note`],rows:[[`Major transient factor (surgery, trauma)`,`3 months, then stop`,`Recurrence under 3% per year`],[`Minor transient or persistent non-cancer factor`,`Consider extended`,`Reduced-dose DOAC after 6 months`],[`Unprovoked first PE`,`Extended (open-ended)`,`If bleeding risk acceptable; review yearly`],[`Recurrent VTE`,`Indefinite`,`Unless a major transient factor explains it`],[`Antiphospholipid syndrome`,`Indefinite warfarin, INR 2-3`,`Avoid DOACs`],[`Active cancer`,`At least 6 months, while cancer active`,`LMWH or DOAC`],[`Pregnancy`,`At least 3 months and 6 weeks postpartum`,`LMWH; warfarin postpartum allowed`]]}],redFlags:[`**Systolic BP under 90 mmHg**, need for vasopressors, or a fall of 40 mmHg or more - high-risk PE needing reperfusion now.`,`**Syncope**, cardiac arrest or PEA in a patient with suspected PE.`,`**SpO2 under 90%** on air, respiratory rate over 30 or rising oxygen need.`,`**RV dilatation on echo with raised troponin** in a normotensive patient (intermediate-high risk).`,`**Lactate 2 mmol/L or more**, cold peripheries, confusion or falling urine output.`,`PE in **pregnancy or postpartum**, active cancer, or with **creatinine clearance under 30**.`,`**Major bleeding** on anticoagulation, or PE recurring despite adequate anticoagulation.`],pearls:[`PERC = **HAD CLOTS**: Hormones, Age 50+, DVT/PE history, Coughing blood, Leg swelling, O2 under 95%, Tachycardia 100+, Surgery/trauma.`,`**Risk is haemodynamic, not anatomical**: a saddle embolus with normal BP is not high-risk PE.`,`**Age-adjusted D-dimer = age x 10** after 50.`,`**Normal echo in shock = look for another cause**; RV dilatation in shock = treat as PE.`,`**Intermediate-high = RV dysfunction plus troponin**: anticoagulate, monitor and keep lysis for rescue.`,`**Alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours**; in arrest give lysis and continue CPR 60-90 minutes.`,`**Fluids under 500 mL, norepinephrine first** - the failing RV drowns with volume.`,`**Provoked = 3 months; unprovoked = consider forever; APS = warfarin.**`,`**Breathless 3 months after PE = think CTEPH**; a normal V/Q scan excludes it.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-q1`,stem:`A 32-year-old woman on a combined oral contraceptive has pleuritic chest pain. Clinical gestalt is low; HR 88/min, SpO2 98%, no leg swelling, no haemoptysis or prior VTE. What is the next step?`,options:[`Discharge without testing because she is PERC negative`,`CT pulmonary angiography immediately`,`Measure D-dimer`,`Start therapeutic LMWH and arrange a V/Q scan`],answer:2,explanation:`Hormone use is one of the eight PERC items, so she is PERC positive and PE cannot be excluded on clinical grounds; with low probability the next step is D-dimer, which excludes PE if negative. Discharging without testing ignores the hormone item, CTPA first exposes a low-probability patient to unnecessary radiation, and empirical LMWH is for high or intermediate probability.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-q2`,stem:`A 78-year-old man has a Wells score of 2 (PE unlikely). His D-dimer is 650 µg/L FEU (laboratory cut-off 500). What should be done?`,options:[`PE is excluded using the age-adjusted cut-off; look for another cause`,`Proceed to CTPA because the D-dimer exceeds 500`,`Start anticoagulation and repeat the D-dimer in a week`,`Perform a leg ultrasound and treat if negative`],answer:0,explanation:`Over 50 years the D-dimer cut-off is age x 10, here 780 µg/L, so 650 is negative and PE is excluded in a PE-unlikely patient (ADJUST-PE). Proceeding to CTPA uses the unadjusted cut-off and adds needless imaging; empirical anticoagulation has no basis; and treating after a negative leg ultrasound is illogical.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-q3`,stem:`A 66-year-old woman with confirmed PE has BP 118/74 mmHg, HR 112/min, sPESI 1, RV/LV ratio 1.3 on CTPA and an elevated troponin. What is the best management?`,options:[`Immediate full-dose systemic alteplase`,`Discharge on apixaban with review in 1 week`,`IVC filter insertion`,`Therapeutic anticoagulation with HDU monitoring and rescue reperfusion if she deteriorates`],answer:3,explanation:`She is normotensive with both RV dysfunction and raised troponin, which is intermediate-high risk: anticoagulate, monitor closely and reserve thrombolysis or catheter therapy for deterioration. Routine full-dose lysis in this group (PEITHO) prevented decompensation but raised haemorrhagic stroke to about 2%; early discharge is for low risk only; and an IVC filter is for patients who cannot be anticoagulated.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-q4`,stem:`A woman 28 weeks pregnant collapses with BP 76/50 mmHg; bedside echo shows a dilated RV with McConnell sign. What is the most appropriate treatment?`,options:[`Prophylactic-dose LMWH and urgent delivery`,`UFH bolus and systemic thrombolysis`,`Oral rivaroxaban 15 mg twice daily`,`Warfarin with LMWH bridging`],answer:1,explanation:`This is high-risk PE in pregnancy; maternal survival comes first, so UFH and systemic thrombolysis (or surgical embolectomy) are given - pregnancy is only a relative contraindication. Prophylactic LMWH does nothing for obstructive shock, DOACs are avoided in pregnancy, and warfarin is teratogenic and far too slow.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-q5`,stem:`A 42-year-old man with a first unprovoked PE has completed 6 months of apixaban without bleeding. His bleeding risk is low. What is the recommended plan?`,options:[`Stop anticoagulation because 3-6 months is sufficient for all PE`,`Switch to aspirin 75 mg daily lifelong`,`Continue extended apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily with yearly review`,`Resume apixaban 10 mg twice daily indefinitely`],answer:2,explanation:`Unprovoked PE carries a recurrence risk of several percent a year after stopping, so extended anticoagulation is advised when bleeding risk is acceptable, and reduced-dose apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily after 6 months is effective with bleeding similar to aspirin. Stopping suits provoked PE, aspirin is far less effective, and 10 mg twice daily is only the first-week loading dose.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`core`,question:`Describe the diagnostic approach to a patient with suspected acute pulmonary embolism. How will you stratify risk, and how will you manage high-risk (massive) pulmonary embolism?`,openingLines:[`Pulmonary embolism is obstruction of the pulmonary arteries, usually by thrombus from the deep leg veins, and with DVT forms venous thromboembolism.`,`Diagnosis rests on pre-test probability, D-dimer and CTPA, and treatment is chosen by the ESC risk class defined by haemodynamics, PESI, RV function and troponin.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors and clinical features`,points:[`Strong factors: fracture, joint replacement, major trauma, previous VTE; moderate: cancer, OCP, postpartum, chemotherapy.`,`Dyspnoea, pleuritic pain, haemoptysis, syncope; tachypnoea, tachycardia, hypoxia, DVT signs, RV strain signs.`]},{heading:`Diagnostic approach in the stable patient`,points:[`Clinical probability: **Wells** or revised Geneva; PERC if gestalt low.`,`PE unlikely: **D-dimer** (age x 10 over 50; YEARS thresholds); negative excludes.`,`PE likely or D-dimer positive: **CTPA**; V/Q in pregnancy, CKD, contrast allergy; leg ultrasound confirms VTE.`,`Supporting tests: ECG (sinus tachycardia, S1Q3T3), chest X-ray, ABG.`]},{heading:`Suspected PE with shock`,points:[`Bedside **echo**: RV dilatation means treat as PE if CTPA is not immediately possible; normal RV means look for another cause.`]},{heading:`Risk stratification (ESC 2019)`,points:[`High: shock, persistent hypotension or arrest.`,`Intermediate-high: sPESI 1+ with **RV dysfunction and raised troponin**; intermediate-low: one or neither.`,`Low: sPESI 0, normal RV and troponin; Hestia negative - outpatient.`]},{heading:`Management of high-risk PE`,points:[`Oxygen, cautious fluid (no more than 500 mL), **norepinephrine**, dobutamine; avoid intubation if possible.`,`**UFH 80 units/kg bolus**, then **alteplase 100 mg over 2 hours** (or streptokinase 1.5 million IU over 2 hours).`,`Lysis contraindicated or failed: surgical embolectomy or catheter-directed therapy; ECMO at expert centres.`,`Then anticoagulation for at least 3 months; duration by provoked or unprovoked status.`]},{heading:`Follow-up`,points:[`Review at 3-6 months; breathless patients screened for CTEPH with echo and V/Q scan.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagnostic flow chart: Wells/PERC, D-dimer, CTPA`,`ESC risk-class table with management in the last column`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, risk factors, clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnostic algorithm`,marks:3},{part:`Risk stratification`,marks:2},{part:`Management of high-risk PE`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Wells`,`PERC`,`D-dimer`,`CTPA`,`sPESI`,`RV dysfunction`,`troponin`,`alteplase`,`embolectomy`]},{id:`cardiovascular-pulmonary-embolism-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on pulmonary embolism in pregnancy.`,openingLines:[`Pregnancy raises VTE risk several-fold, highest in the first 6 weeks postpartum, and PE is a leading direct cause of maternal death in high-income countries.`,`Diagnosis uses the pregnancy-adapted YEARS pathway, and treatment is weight-based therapeutic LMWH.`],answer:[{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`D-dimer rises normally; use **pregnancy-adapted YEARS** (Artemis).`,`Leg ultrasound first if DVT signs; then chest X-ray and perfusion scan or CTPA - fetal radiation is very low.`]},{heading:`Treatment`,points:[`**Therapeutic LMWH** by early-pregnancy weight (enoxaparin 1 mg/kg twice daily); no warfarin or DOAC.`,`High-risk PE: thrombolysis or surgical embolectomy.`]},{heading:`Delivery and postpartum`,points:[`Stop LMWH 24 hours before planned delivery or neuraxial block; UFH near term if timing uncertain.`,`Continue for at least 6 weeks postpartum and 3 months in total; LMWH or warfarin in breastfeeding.`]},{heading:`Future pregnancy`,points:[`Prophylactic LMWH in future pregnancies; avoid oestrogen contraception.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: pregnancy-adapted YEARS pathway`],markSplit:[{part:`Risk and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Treatment`,marks:2},{part:`Delivery and postpartum`,marks:1}],keywords:[`pregnancy`,`YEARS`,`Artemis`,`LMWH`,`postpartum`,`V/Q`,`CTPA`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2019 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of acute pulmonary embolism, developed with the ERS`,`Antithrombotic Therapy for VTE Disease: Second Update of the CHEST Guideline, 2021`,`NICE NG158: Venous thromboembolic diseases - diagnosis, management and thrombophilia testing (2020, updated 2023)`,`2022 ESC/ERS Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of pulmonary hypertension`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]}),u.push({id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm`,title:`Aortic dissection and aortic aneurysm`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Acute aortic dissection** is an intimal tear that lets blood split the media into a **true and false lumen**; **Stanford type A** (ascending aorta involved) needs **emergency surgery**, **type B** is treated with **impulse control (IV beta-blocker first, heart rate under 60, systolic 100-120 mmHg)** and **TEVAR when complicated**; an **abdominal aortic aneurysm** is a diameter of **30 mm or more**, screened by ultrasound in men 65 and over and repaired at **55 mm in men, 50 mm in women** or growth of 10 mm a year (ESC 2024).`,keywords:[`aortic dissection`,`acute aortic syndrome`,`Stanford classification`,`DeBakey classification`,`ADD-RS`,`intramural haematoma`,`penetrating aortic ulcer`,`abdominal aortic aneurysm`,`AAA`,`thoracic aortic aneurysm`,`TEVAR`,`EVAR`,`esmolol`,`Marfan syndrome`,`bicuspid aortopathy`,`aortic rupture`,`CT aortography`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Acute aortic syndrome (AAS)** covers **classic dissection, intramural haematoma (IMH), penetrating atherosclerotic ulcer (PAU)** and contained rupture - emergencies that share pain, imaging and impulse control. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Aortic dissection** is a tear in the intima that lets blood enter the media and separate it, creating a **true and a false lumen** that can propagate, rupture or occlude branches. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Aortic aneurysm** is a permanent localised dilatation of at least **50% above normal**; an **abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA)** is an infrarenal diameter of **30 mm or more**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`Dissection incidence is about **3-6 per 100,000 per year**, two-thirds men, mean age about 60; untreated **type A mortality is about 1-2% per hour** in the first 48 hours. [IRAD / ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`AAA affects about **1-2% of men over 65** in screened Western populations, falling with less smoking; rupture carries **over 80% overall mortality**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**India**: hospital series show dissection at a **younger age** than in IRAD, driven by **uncontrolled hypertension**, Marfan syndrome and bicuspid valves, and AAA linked to smoking and **Takayasu arteritis** in young women; no national screening programme exists. [API Textbook 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Hypertension** is present in about three-quarters of dissections - the commonest and most modifiable risk factor. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Heritable thoracic aortic disease (HTAD)**: **Marfan (FBN1)**, **Loeys-Dietz (TGFBR1/2, SMAD3)**, **vascular Ehlers-Danlos (COL3A1)**, familial TAAD, **Turner syndrome**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Bicuspid aortic valve aortopathy**, coarctation and previous aortic valve surgery dilate the ascending aorta. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Acquired**: atherosclerosis (PAU, AAA), **cocaine and amphetamine use**, heavy weightlifting, pregnancy (third trimester and postpartum), blunt chest trauma, and **iatrogenic** injury at catheterisation or cardiac surgery. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Inflammatory**: **Takayasu** and giant-cell arteritis, syphilitic aortitis (ascending aneurysm with tree-bark intima), and infective (**mycotic**) aneurysm from Salmonella or staphylococci. [Harrison 22e]`,`**AAA risk factors**: **smoking (strongest)**, male sex, age over 65, family history (first-degree relative), hypertension and atherosclerosis; **diabetes is protective**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Fluoroquinolones** are linked to aneurysm and dissection; avoid them in patients with known aneurysm or HTAD when alternatives exist. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Medial degeneration** (loss of elastic fibres and smooth-muscle cells, mucoid change - formerly cystic medial necrosis) weakens the wall; raised wall stress (hypertension) tears the intima. [Harrison 22e]`,`Blood dissects along the media driven by **dP/dt (rate of pressure rise)** - the reason **beta-blockade before vasodilators** is the first step; a vasodilator alone causes reflex tachycardia and raises dP/dt. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`Entry tears lie mostly in the **right lateral ascending aorta** (type A) or just distal to the **left subclavian** (type B), where shear stress is highest. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Complications by mechanism**: rupture into the **pericardium (tamponade)**, pleura or mediastinum; **aortic regurgitation** (annular dilatation or cusp prolapse); and **branch malperfusion** - coronary (usually RCA - inferior MI), carotid (stroke), spinal, mesenteric, renal and limb. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Intramural haematoma** is bleeding from vasa vasorum without an intimal tear; **PAU** is an atheromatous ulcer penetrating the elastic lamina - both can progress to dissection or rupture. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**AAA**: proteolysis (matrix metalloproteinases) and inflammation degrade elastin and collagen; by **Laplace's law** wall tension rises with radius, so growth accelerates and rupture risk climbs steeply above 55 mm. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`**Stanford A**: involves the **ascending aorta** regardless of entry site - surgical; **Stanford B**: does not involve the ascending aorta - usually medical or endovascular. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**DeBakey I**: ascending plus arch and beyond; **II**: ascending only; **III**: descending only (**IIIa** thoracic, **IIIb** extending below the diaphragm). [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Non-A non-B** dissection involves the **arch without the ascending aorta**; **TEM classification** (Type, Entry, Malperfusion) adds entry site and branch involvement and is endorsed by ESC 2024. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Timing**: hyperacute (under 24 hours), **acute (1-14 days)**, subacute (15-90 days), chronic (over 90 days) - risk is highest in the first days. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Complicated type B**: rupture or impending rupture, **malperfusion**, refractory pain or hypertension, rapid expansion; **uncomplicated high-risk** features include a large false lumen, total aortic diameter over 40 mm and a large proximal entry tear. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**AAA by size**: small 30-54 mm in men (30-49 mm in women) - surveillance; large 55 mm or more in men (50 mm or more in women) - repair; and by morphology - **fusiform** or **saccular** (saccular repaired earlier). [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**Dissection pain** is abrupt, maximal at onset and **'tearing' or 'ripping'** in about half; anterior chest pain suggests type A, **interscapular back pain** type B, and pain migrating with propagation. [IRAD / Harrison 22e]`,`**Atypical presentations**: painless dissection (elderly, Marfan, diabetics), **syncope** (tamponade or stroke), stroke, paraplegia, abdominal pain (mesenteric ischaemia), acute limb ischaemia or heart failure from acute AR. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Signs**: **pulse deficit or inter-arm BP difference over 20 mmHg**, early diastolic murmur of **acute AR**, hypotension with raised JVP and muffled sounds (**tamponade**), focal neurology. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`Most type B patients are **hypertensive** at presentation; **hypotension** in type A usually means tamponade, rupture or acute AR - a sign of imminent death. [IRAD]`,`**Asymptomatic AAA** is usually found on ultrasound or CT done for something else, or as a **pulsatile, expansile** abdominal mass above the umbilicus; examination misses many in obese patients. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Ruptured AAA triad**: **abdominal or back pain, hypotension and a pulsatile mass** - often misdiagnosed as **renal colic** in an older man. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Marfan clues**: tall stature, arm span over height, arachnodactyly (**wrist and thumb signs**), pectus, high arched palate, **ectopia lentis** (upward), and mitral prolapse. [Ghent 2010]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Acute coronary syndrome**: commonest mimic; check for dissection features before giving **antiplatelets, heparin or lysis** - an inferior STEMI can itself be caused by type A dissection. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Pulmonary embolism**: pleuritic pain, hypoxia, RV dilatation - no pulse deficit or widened mediastinum. [ESC 2019]`,`**Acute pericarditis**: positional pleuritic pain with diffuse ST elevation and PR depression; a new pericardial effusion with chest pain should prompt aortic imaging. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Renal colic, perforated ulcer, pancreatitis** mimic ruptured AAA or type B dissection - always feel the abdomen and do bedside ultrasound in an older man with 'renal colic'. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Stroke or spinal cord syndrome** with chest or back pain is dissection until proven otherwise - thrombolysis would be lethal. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Musculoskeletal and oesophageal pain** (spasm, Boerhaave) are diagnoses of exclusion. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**ADD-RS (Aortic Dissection Detection Risk Score)**: one point per category - **high-risk condition** (Marfan, family history, known aortic disease, recent aortic manipulation), **high-risk pain** (abrupt, severe, tearing), **high-risk signs** (pulse deficit, BP difference, focal neurology with pain, new AR murmur, hypotension or shock). [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**ADD-RS 0-1 with D-dimer under 500 ng/mL** makes dissection very unlikely; ADD-RS 2-3 or a positive D-dimer needs **CT angiography**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**CT aortography** (ECG-gated, chest to pelvis) is the first-line test: flap, true and false lumen, entry tear, extent, branch involvement and rupture. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Bedside TTE**: dissection flap in the root, **pericardial effusion/tamponade**, acute AR and LV function; **TOE** in theatre or when the patient is too unstable for CT. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**ECG**: normal or LVH in most; inferior ST elevation in RCA involvement; **chest X-ray**: widened mediastinum, double aortic contour, calcium sign (displaced intimal calcium over 5 mm) - a normal film does not exclude dissection. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Bloods**: haemoglobin, cross-match, creatinine, **lactate** (malperfusion), troponin, LFT, amylase, coagulation. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**AAA**: **abdominal ultrasound** for screening and surveillance (inner-to-inner or outer-to-outer measurement, stated consistently); **CT angiography** for repair planning or suspected rupture in a stable patient. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Thoracic aneurysm**: echo for root and ascending aorta; CT or MRI for the whole aorta; screen the whole aorta once an aneurysm is found anywhere. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Emergency and inpatient management of dissection`,points:[`**Impulse control first**: IV beta-blocker to **heart rate under 60/min** and **systolic 100-120 mmHg** within minutes; give adequate **IV morphine** - pain drives tachycardia and hypertension. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Esmolol 500 µg/kg IV bolus, then 50-300 µg/kg/min**, or **labetalol 20 mg IV then 20-80 mg every 10 minutes** (or 1-2 mg/min infusion); metoprolol 5 mg IV every 5 minutes is an alternative. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`If beta-blockers are contraindicated (asthma) use **IV diltiazem or verapamil**; add a vasodilator (**nicardipine, sodium nitroprusside, IV nitroglycerin**) only **after beta-blockade** if systolic stays above 120. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Type A**: **emergency surgery** - replacement of the ascending aorta (with root or arch as needed, plus valve repair or replacement); mortality without surgery approaches 50% at 48 hours. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Uncomplicated type B**: ICU, impulse control, then oral BP drugs and **CT at days 7-14**; **pre-emptive TEVAR** in the subacute phase may be considered with high-risk features. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Complicated type B** (rupture, malperfusion, refractory pain or hypertension, expansion): **TEVAR** to cover the entry tear is first-line; open surgery if anatomy is unsuitable. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Tamponade in type A**: go to theatre - **pericardiocentesis is avoided** (it can provoke fatal re-bleeding), apart from controlled small-volume drainage as a bridge in extremis. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Never give** antiplatelets, heparin or thrombolysis on suspicion of ACS until dissection is excluded in a patient with high-risk features; **IMH and PAU** of the ascending aorta are treated like type A, of the descending like type B. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Management of aortic aneurysm`,points:[`**Screening**: one-time **ultrasound for all men aged 65 or more**; consider women 65 or more who smoke(d), and first-degree relatives of AAA patients. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Surveillance intervals**: **30-39 mm every 3 years**; **40-49 mm yearly**; men **50-54 mm** (women 45-49 mm) every **6 months**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**AAA repair threshold**: **55 mm in men, 50 mm in women**, growth of **10 mm or more per year**, symptoms (tenderness, pain) or rupture. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**EVAR versus open repair**: EVAR has lower early mortality and shorter stay but more reinterventions and needs **lifelong imaging**; open repair suits younger fit patients with long life expectancy. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Medical therapy for every aneurysm**: **stop smoking**, statin, BP control (target under 130/80 if tolerated), antiplatelet if atherosclerotic disease; **no drug (doxycycline, ACEi) slows AAA growth**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Ascending aorta repair thresholds**: **55 mm** generally; **Marfan 50 mm** (45 mm with risk factors - family dissection, growth 3 mm/year, planned pregnancy); **bicuspid 55 mm** (50 mm with risk factors); 45 mm if undergoing valve surgery. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Descending thoracic aneurysm**: TEVAR at about **55 mm**; **ruptured AAA**: permissive hypotension (systolic about 70-90, conscious patient), straight to theatre; **EVAR preferred** where anatomy allows. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Primary care**: advise **avoiding heavy straining/isometric lifting** with large aneurysms, report sudden pain, and avoid fluoroquinolones. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Marfan syndrome**: **beta-blocker and/or ARB (losartan)** to slow root growth; yearly echo (more often if growing); **Ghent 2010 criteria** - aortic root Z-score 2 or more plus ectopia lentis, or FBN1 mutation. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Pregnancy**: dissection risk peaks in the **third trimester and postpartum**; Marfan women with **aortic root over 45 mm** should avoid pregnancy; use labetalol or metoprolol, and caesarean section is advised for larger roots. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Turner syndrome**: index aortic size to body surface area (ASI); Loeys-Dietz and vascular EDS dissect at smaller diameters - specialist thresholds apply. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Elderly and frail**: weigh repair against life expectancy - many with AAA 55-60 mm and limited survival are managed medically; painless dissection is commoner. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**CKD**: contrast is still given for suspected dissection or rupture - the diagnosis outweighs nephropathy risk; MRI or TOE for elective surveillance. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Takayasu arteritis** in young Indian women causes aneurysms and stenoses; aortic surgery is deferred until inflammation is controlled - see the Takayasu topic. [API Textbook 2022]`,`**Resource-limited hospital**: impulse control with **IV labetalol or metoprolol**, morphine, bedside echo and immediate transfer to a cardiothoracic centre (Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY covers aortic surgery and endovascular repair). [PM-JAY HBP 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`**Type A** operative mortality is about **15-25%** in registries; untreated, about half die within 48 hours. [IRAD]`,`**Type B** in-hospital mortality is about **10%** overall, higher when complicated; late **aneurysmal dilatation of the false lumen** occurs in 20-40% over years. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Malperfusion**: stroke, MI, paraplegia (spinal ischaemia - also after TEVAR), mesenteric infarction, renal failure and limb ischaemia. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**AAA rupture risk** rises steeply with diameter - small below 55 mm and much higher above 60-70 mm; women rupture at smaller sizes. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**EVAR complications**: **endoleak** (type I and III need treatment, type II often observed), limb occlusion, migration and post-implantation syndrome. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Open repair** complications: MI, renal failure, colonic ischaemia, impotence and late para-anastomotic aneurysm or aorto-enteric fistula (GI bleed after graft). [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Follow-up and prevention`,points:[`**After dissection** (treated or not): oral BP control to **under 130/80**, heart rate about 60 with a beta-blocker, statin and smoking cessation. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Imaging after dissection**: CT or MRI at **1, 6 and 12 months**, then yearly if stable; after TEVAR or EVAR, lifelong imaging (CT, then duplex or MRI). [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Family screening**: first-degree relatives of patients with **HTAD or dissection under 60** get imaging and genetic testing. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Counselling**: avoid **competitive and isometric sports** with significant dilatation, avoid cocaine and stimulants, and carry an alert card with diameters and medications. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Opportunistic screening**: feel the abdomen and order ultrasound in men 65+ who smoke attending **NP-NCD clinics at Ayushman Arogya Mandir** for hypertension. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Hypertension control** is the main population prevention of dissection - see the Hypertension topic. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2024 Peripheral Arterial and Aortic Diseases** (first joint guideline) replaces ESC 2014 aorta. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**ADD-RS plus D-dimer** formally recommended to triage suspected acute aortic syndrome. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**TEM classification** and **non-A non-B** category adopted alongside Stanford. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Sex-specific AAA repair** thresholds: 55 mm men, **50 mm women**; surveillance at 3-yearly, yearly and 6-monthly intervals by size. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Pre-emptive TEVAR** may be considered in uncomplicated type B with high-risk features. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**ACC/AHA 2022 Aortic Disease guideline**: surgery for sporadic ascending aneurysm at 55 mm (50 mm at experienced centres), and shared decision-making for AAA. [ACC/AHA 2022]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: Stanford vs DeBakey, management of acute dissection, AAA screening and repair thresholds, Marfan criteria. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Common mistake**: starting a **vasodilator before a beta-blocker** - reflex tachycardia increases dP/dt and propagates the dissection. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Common mistake**: giving **thrombolysis or heparin** to 'inferior STEMI' or 'stroke' with tearing chest pain and a pulse deficit. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Common mistake**: pericardiocentesis for tamponade in type A dissection - the patient needs theatre. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Viva**: 'Targets in acute dissection?' - **heart rate under 60, systolic 100-120 mmHg**. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Viva**: 'Old man with renal colic and hypotension?' - **ruptured AAA until proven otherwise**. [Harrison 22e]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in acute aortic syndrome and aneurysm`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Esmolol`,`500 µg/kg IV bolus, then 50-300 µg/kg/min`,`Titrate to HR under 60, SBP 100-120`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm; avoid in AR with shock`],[`Labetalol`,`20 mg IV, then 20-80 mg every 10 min (max 300 mg) or 1-2 mg/min`,`Switch to oral when stable`,`Bronchospasm, heart block`],[`Metoprolol`,`5 mg IV every 5 min up to 15 mg; oral 50-100 mg twice daily`,`Lifelong after dissection`,`Bradycardia, asthma`],[`Diltiazem`,`0.25 mg/kg IV over 2 min, then infusion`,`If beta-blocker contraindicated`,`Hypotension, avoid in LV dysfunction`],[`Nicardipine`,`5 mg/h IV, increase by 2.5 mg/h every 5-15 min to 15 mg/h`,`Only after beta-blockade`,`Reflex tachycardia, headache`],[`Sodium nitroprusside`,`0.3-10 µg/kg/min IV`,`Only after beta-blockade; short use`,`Cyanide toxicity, hypotension`],[`Morphine`,`2-5 mg IV, repeat as needed`,`Throughout acute phase`,`Respiratory depression, hypotension`],[`Losartan (Marfan)`,`25-100 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`Hyperkalaemia; teratogenic`],[`Atorvastatin`,`40-80 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong in AAA`,`Myalgia, transaminase rise`]]},{heading:`Classification of aortic dissection`,columns:[`System`,`Type`,`Extent`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Stanford`,`A`,`Involves ascending aorta`,`Emergency surgery`],[`Stanford`,`B`,`Ascending aorta spared`,`Impulse control; TEVAR if complicated`],[`DeBakey`,`I`,`Ascending, arch and beyond`,`Surgery`],[`DeBakey`,`II`,`Ascending only`,`Surgery`],[`DeBakey`,`III (a/b)`,`Descending only (a above, b below diaphragm)`,`Medical or TEVAR`],[`ESC 2024`,`Non-A non-B`,`Arch without ascending`,`Individualised, often TEVAR or hybrid`]]},{heading:`ADD-RS and D-dimer triage`,columns:[`Category`,`Features (any = 1 point)`,`Use`],rows:[[`High-risk condition`,`Marfan or HTAD, family history, known aortic disease or valve, recent aortic manipulation`,`Score 0-1 plus D-dimer under 500 = dissection unlikely`],[`High-risk pain`,`Abrupt onset, severe, ripping or tearing chest, back or abdominal pain`,`Score 2-3 = CT angiography directly`],[`High-risk signs`,`Pulse deficit, BP difference, focal neurology with pain, new AR murmur, hypotension or shock`,`Unstable = bedside echo, then theatre or CT`]]},{heading:`AAA surveillance and repair (ESC 2024)`,columns:[`Diameter`,`Men`,`Women`,`Action`],rows:[[`Under 30 mm`,`Normal`,`Normal`,`No follow-up`],[`30-39 mm`,`Every 3 years`,`Every 3 years`,`Risk-factor control`],[`40-49 mm`,`Yearly`,`Yearly (6-monthly from 45 mm)`,`Statin, stop smoking`],[`50-54 mm`,`Every 6 months`,`Repair`,`Refer vascular surgeon`],[`55 mm or more`,`Repair`,`Repair`,`EVAR or open repair`],[`Growth 10 mm/year or symptoms`,`Repair`,`Repair`,`Urgent referral`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of acute aortic syndrome`,columns:[`Condition`,`Shared features`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Acute coronary syndrome`,`Chest pain, ST change, troponin`,`No pulse deficit; pressing pain; regional wall motion defect`],[`Pulmonary embolism`,`Chest pain, tachycardia, collapse`,`Hypoxia, RV dilatation, normal mediastinum`],[`Pericarditis`,`Chest pain, effusion`,`Positional pain, diffuse ST elevation, no flap`],[`Renal colic`,`Loin pain in older man`,`Haematuria, normal BP; ruptured AAA has hypotension and pulsatile mass`],[`Acute stroke`,`Focal neurology`,`Chest or back pain, pulse deficit suggest dissection`]]}],redFlags:[`**Abrupt tearing chest or back pain** with a pulse deficit or arm BP difference over 20 mmHg.`,`Chest pain with **new aortic regurgitation murmur, syncope or focal neurology**.`,`**Hypotension with raised JVP** in suspected type A dissection (tamponade).`,`Older man with **abdominal or back pain, hypotension and a pulsatile mass** (ruptured AAA).`,`Known aneurysm with **new pain or tenderness** (symptomatic aneurysm).`,`AAA **55 mm or more in men, 50 mm in women**, or growth of 10 mm in a year.`,`Marfan or bicuspid patient with ascending aorta **50 mm or more** or planning pregnancy.`],pearls:[`**Beta-blocker before vasodilator**: HR under 60 first, then SBP 100-120.`,`**Stanford A = Ascending = Always surgery.**`,`**ADD-RS 0-1 plus D-dimer under 500 = dissection unlikely.**`,`**Inferior STEMI with tearing pain** may be type A dissection involving the RCA.`,`**No pericardiocentesis** for tamponade in type A - go to theatre.`,`AAA: **screen men at 65**; repair **55 men, 50 women**, or 10 mm growth a year.`,`**'Renal colic' in an older hypotensive man = ruptured AAA** until proven otherwise.`,`**No drug slows AAA growth** - stop smoking and give a statin.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-q1`,stem:`A 58-year-old hypertensive man has sudden tearing interscapular pain. BP is 190/110 mmHg right arm and 160/95 left arm, HR 108/min. CT shows dissection starting distal to the left subclavian artery, with normal limb and visceral perfusion. What is the first treatment?`,options:[`IV sodium nitroprusside to lower systolic below 120 mmHg`,`Emergency open replacement of the descending aorta`,`IV esmolol to heart rate under 60/min, then add a vasodilator if needed`,`Aspirin 300 mg and IV heparin`],answer:2,explanation:`This is uncomplicated Stanford type B dissection, treated with impulse control: IV beta-blocker first to slow the heart below 60, then a vasodilator if systolic stays above 120. Nitroprusside alone causes reflex tachycardia and raises dP/dt, open surgery is for complicated cases unsuited to TEVAR, and antithrombotics can cause fatal bleeding.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-q2`,stem:`Which finding defines a Stanford type A aortic dissection?`,options:[`Involvement of the ascending aorta, wherever the entry tear lies`,`Entry tear distal to the left subclavian artery`,`Extension below the diaphragm`,`Involvement of the renal arteries`],answer:0,explanation:`Stanford type A means the ascending aorta is involved, whatever the entry site, and it needs emergency surgery. An entry distal to the left subclavian without ascending involvement is type B, extension below the diaphragm is DeBakey IIIb, and renal involvement describes malperfusion, not type.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-q3`,stem:`A 68-year-old male smoker has an asymptomatic infrarenal AAA of 47 mm on ultrasound. According to ESC 2024, what is the correct plan?`,options:[`Elective EVAR now`,`Repeat ultrasound in 3 years`,`Doxycycline to slow growth`,`Yearly ultrasound with smoking cessation and statin`],answer:3,explanation:`A 40-49 mm AAA in a man is surveyed yearly while risk factors are treated; repair is at 55 mm in men or growth of 10 mm a year. EVAR now is premature, 3-yearly scans are for 30-39 mm, and doxycycline has not been shown to slow aneurysm growth.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-q4`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with chest pain has 2 mm ST elevation in II, III and aVF, an early diastolic murmur and a right radial pulse much weaker than the left. What should be done before giving antithrombotic therapy?`,options:[`Give thrombolysis immediately as door-to-needle time matters`,`Urgent CT aortography or bedside echo to exclude type A dissection`,`Give aspirin and clopidogrel and proceed to angiography`,`Treat as pericarditis with NSAIDs`],answer:1,explanation:`A new AR murmur and a pulse deficit with inferior ST elevation suggest type A dissection extending into the right coronary ostium; imaging must exclude it because thrombolysis or antiplatelets would be lethal. Pericarditis does not cause a pulse deficit or localised inferior ST elevation with an AR murmur.`,difficulty:`hard`},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-q5`,stem:`A 26-year-old woman with Marfan syndrome plans pregnancy. Her aortic root measures 47 mm. What is the best advice?`,options:[`Pregnancy is safe if she takes losartan throughout`,`Pregnancy is safe; review only after delivery`,`Advise against pregnancy until the root has been repaired`,`Proceed and plan vaginal delivery with an epidural`],answer:2,explanation:`In Marfan syndrome an aortic root over 45 mm carries a high dissection risk in pregnancy, so pregnancy is discouraged and prophylactic root surgery is considered first. Losartan is teratogenic and must be stopped in pregnancy, and proceeding without repair ignores the risk peak in the third trimester and postpartum.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Classify acute aortic dissection. Discuss its clinical features, diagnosis and management.`,openingLines:[`Aortic dissection is a tear in the intima that allows blood into the media, creating a true and false lumen; it is the commonest acute aortic syndrome.`,`Stanford type A (ascending aorta involved) needs emergency surgery; type B is treated by impulse control, with TEVAR when complicated.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors and pathophysiology`,points:[`Hypertension, Marfan and other HTAD, bicuspid valve, cocaine, pregnancy, iatrogenic.`,`Medial degeneration plus wall stress; propagation driven by dP/dt.`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Stanford A/B; DeBakey I, II, III; non-A non-B; acute, subacute, chronic; complicated vs uncomplicated type B.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Abrupt tearing chest or interscapular pain; syncope, stroke, paraplegia, limb or mesenteric ischaemia.`,`Pulse deficit, BP difference over 20 mmHg, AR murmur, tamponade.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`**ADD-RS** with D-dimer; **CT aortography** first-line; bedside TTE, TOE if unstable.`,`ECG (exclude or detect RCA involvement), chest X-ray (wide mediastinum), lactate, creatinine, cross-match.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Morphine; **IV esmolol or labetalol** to HR under 60 and SBP 100-120; vasodilator only after beta-blockade.`,`Type A: emergency surgery; no pericardiocentesis for tamponade.`,`Type B: medical; TEVAR if complicated; pre-emptive TEVAR with high-risk features.`,`Long term: BP under 130/80, beta-blocker, imaging at 1, 6, 12 months then yearly; family screening.`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of Stanford and DeBakey types`,`Flow chart: ADD-RS, D-dimer, CT, management by type`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition, risk factors, classification`,marks:3},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:2},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Management`,marks:3}],keywords:[`Stanford`,`DeBakey`,`ADD-RS`,`CT aortography`,`esmolol`,`TEVAR`,`tamponade`]},{id:`cardiovascular-aortic-dissection-aneurysm-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Write a short note on screening, surveillance and repair of abdominal aortic aneurysm.`,openingLines:[`An abdominal aortic aneurysm is an infrarenal aortic diameter of 30 mm or more, usually asymptomatic until rupture, which carries over 80% mortality.`,`ESC 2024 recommends one-time ultrasound screening of men aged 65 and repair at 55 mm in men and 50 mm in women.`],answer:[{heading:`Risk factors`,points:[`Smoking, male sex, age over 65, family history, hypertension; diabetes is protective.`]},{heading:`Screening`,points:[`Ultrasound for all men 65+; consider women 65+ who smoke(d) and first-degree relatives.`]},{heading:`Surveillance`,points:[`30-39 mm every 3 years; 40-49 mm yearly; 50-54 mm (men) every 6 months; stop smoking, statin, BP control.`]},{heading:`Repair`,points:[`55 mm men, 50 mm women, growth 10 mm/year or symptoms; EVAR (lifelong imaging) or open repair.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: diameter, surveillance interval, action`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and risk factors`,marks:1},{part:`Screening`,marks:1},{part:`Surveillance`,marks:1.5},{part:`Repair thresholds and options`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`AAA`,`ultrasound screening`,`surveillance`,`55 mm`,`EVAR`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of peripheral arterial and aortic diseases`,`2022 ACC/AHA Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Aortic Disease`,`International Registry of Acute Aortic Dissection (IRAD) reports`,`2025 ESC Guidelines for the management of cardiovascular disease and pregnancy`,`Revised Ghent nosology for Marfan syndrome, 2010`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]}),u.push({id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia`,title:`Acute limb ischaemia and chronic limb-threatening ischaemia`,frequency:`common`,oneLiner:`**Acute limb ischaemia (ALI)** is a sudden fall in limb perfusion (under 2 weeks) threatening viability - recognised by the **6 Ps**, graded by **Rutherford class (I, IIa, IIb, III)** and treated with **immediate IV heparin and same-day vascular referral** for embolectomy, thrombolysis or bypass; **chronic limb-threatening ischaemia (CLTI)** is PAD with **ischaemic rest pain over 2 weeks or tissue loss**, staged by **WIfI**, and needs revascularisation, wound care and best medical therapy to avoid major amputation (ESC 2024, ESVS).`,keywords:[`acute limb ischaemia`,`ALI`,`6 Ps`,`Rutherford classification`,`embolectomy`,`Fogarty catheter`,`catheter-directed thrombolysis`,`compartment syndrome`,`fasciotomy`,`critical limb ischaemia`,`CLTI`,`WIfI`,`Fontaine classification`,`ankle-brachial index`,`toe pressure`,`diabetic foot`,`Buerger disease`,`amputation`,`BEST-CLI`,`BASIL-2`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Acute limb ischaemia** is a sudden decrease in limb perfusion, of **less than 2 weeks' duration**, that threatens limb viability. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Chronic limb-threatening ischaemia (CLTI)** is objectively proven PAD with **ischaemic rest pain for over 2 weeks**, **non-healing ulcer or gangrene** - the end stage of PAD; it replaces the term critical limb ischaemia. [GVG 2019]`,`ALI occurs in about **1-2 per 10,000 people a year**; despite treatment, **30-day amputation is about 10-15%** and mortality about **15-20%**, reflecting the cardiac disease behind it. [ESVS 2020]`,`About **1-3% of PAD patients progress to CLTI**; CLTI carries about **20-25% mortality at 1 year** and a high major-amputation rate if not revascularised. [GVG 2019]`,`**India**: with about **101 million adults with diabetes (ICMR-INDIAB 2023)**, **diabetic foot disease** with PAD is the leading cause of non-traumatic amputation; rheumatic AF supplies emboli in younger patients. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)** in young male smokers, including **bidi smokers**, remains a distinct Indian cause of CLTI and digital gangrene. [API Textbook 2022]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`**Embolism** (about a third to a half of ALI): from the heart in most - **AF**, LV thrombus after MI, rheumatic mitral stenosis, prosthetic valves, endocarditis, myxoma - or from proximal aneurysm and atheroma. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Thrombosis in situ**: on an atherosclerotic stenosis, in a **bypass graft or stent**, or in a **popliteal artery aneurysm** (which also embolises distally). [ESVS 2020]`,`**Other causes**: **aortic dissection** (malperfusion), trauma and iatrogenic injury (cardiac catheterisation, IABP), **hypercoagulable states** (cancer, HIT, antiphospholipid syndrome, COVID-19), paradoxical embolism, and vasospasm (ergot). [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**CLTI risk factors**: **diabetes** (strongest for below-knee disease and amputation), **smoking**, CKD and dialysis, age, hypertension and dyslipidaemia. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Buerger disease**: inflammatory occlusive disease of small and medium arteries and veins in young smokers, with migratory superficial thrombophlebitis and Raynaud; **only total tobacco cessation** halts it. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Other non-atherosclerotic causes**: Takayasu and other vasculitis, popliteal entrapment, cystic adventitial disease, radiation arteritis. [ESC 2024 PAD]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`**Embolism** occludes a previously normal artery with **no collaterals**, so ischaemia is severe and abrupt; emboli lodge at **bifurcations** - commonest the **common femoral**, then popliteal, aortic and brachial. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thrombosis on chronic PAD** occurs in a limb with established collaterals, so presentation is often **less severe and slower**. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Tissue tolerance**: nerves are most sensitive (sensory loss early), then muscle - **irreversible muscle necrosis begins after about 4-6 hours** of complete ischaemia, skin tolerates longest. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Sensory loss and weakness** therefore signal threatened viability, which is why the Rutherford grade is built on them. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Reperfusion injury**: restored flow releases **potassium, lactate, myoglobin** and free radicals - hyperkalaemia, acidosis, **myoglobinuric AKI**, arrhythmia - and capillary leak causes **compartment syndrome**. [ESVS 2020]`,`**CLTI**: multilevel, often infrapopliteal disease drops resting perfusion below metabolic need; **microvascular dysfunction, neuropathy and infection** in diabetes accelerate tissue loss. [GVG 2019]`,`**Medial arterial calcification** in diabetes and CKD makes arteries incompressible, so **ABI is falsely normal or over 1.4** - toe pressures are needed. [ESC 2024 PAD]`]},{heading:`Classification and staging`,points:[`**Rutherford I (viable)**: no sensory loss, no weakness, arterial and venous Doppler audible - not immediately threatened. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Rutherford IIa (marginally threatened)**: minimal sensory loss (toes), no weakness, arterial Doppler often inaudible - salvageable with **prompt** treatment. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Rutherford IIb (immediately threatened)**: sensory loss beyond toes with rest pain, **mild-moderate weakness**, arterial Doppler inaudible - needs **immediate revascularisation**. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Rutherford III (irreversible)**: profound anaesthesia, **paralysis and rigor**, arterial and venous Doppler inaudible, fixed mottling - **primary amputation** or palliation. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Chronic PAD - Fontaine**: I asymptomatic, IIa claudication over 200 m, IIb under 200 m, **III rest pain**, **IV ulcer or gangrene**; Rutherford chronic categories 4 (rest pain), 5 (minor tissue loss), 6 (major tissue loss). [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**WIfI** grades **Wound (0-3), Ischaemia (0-3) and foot Infection (0-3)** into clinical stages 1-4 that predict 1-year amputation risk and benefit of revascularisation. [GVG 2019]`,`**GLASS** grades the infra-inguinal anatomy, and the **PLAN** approach (Patient risk, Limb severity, ANatomy) guides the choice between endovascular and open repair. [GVG 2019]`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`**6 Ps**: **Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paraesthesia, Paralysis, Perishing cold (poikilothermia)** - paraesthesia and paralysis mark the threatened limb. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Embolic ALI**: sudden onset (patient often recalls the minute), **AF or recent MI**, no prior claudication, **normal pulses in the other leg**, a sharp level of colour change. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Thrombotic ALI**: history of **claudication**, absent contralateral pulses, bruits, gradual worsening, less severe pain because of collaterals. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Popliteal aneurysm**: a **prominent, easily felt popliteal pulse** in the other leg (bilateral in about half) and a pulsatile popliteal mass suggest it. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Early ALI** - white marbled skin; **later** - fixed mottling that does not blanch, muscle tenderness and rigidity mean irreversibility. [ESVS 2020]`,`**CLTI rest pain** is burning forefoot pain at night, **relieved by hanging the leg down** (patients sleep sitting in a chair), with dependent oedema from sitting. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**CLTI signs**: absent foot pulses, **Buerger test** - pallor on elevation to 45 degrees and **dependent rubor**, slow capillary refill, hair loss, shiny skin, toe gangrene, punched-out heel or toe ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetic foot**: neuropathy masks pain, so **tissue loss may be painless**; infection with ischaemia (neuro-ischaemic foot) progresses within days. [IWGDF 2023]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**Phlegmasia cerulea dolens** (massive DVT): swollen, **blue**, painful leg with venous gangrene - pulses may be lost late; leg is swollen, not shrunken and white. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Acute aortic dissection**: limb ischaemia with chest or back pain and an arm BP difference - CT aortography, not embolectomy, first. [ESC 2024 Aorta]`,`**Low-output state or vasopressors**: bilateral cool, pulseless limbs in shock; improve with cardiac output. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Acute compressive neuropathy or spinal cord lesion**: weakness and numbness with **normal pulses and Doppler signals**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CLTI versus neuropathic pain**: neuropathic pain is **not relieved by dependency** and pulses or toe pressures are normal; **venous ulcers** are at the gaiter area with pigmentation. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Buerger disease versus atherosclerosis**: young smoker under 45, upper-limb and distal involvement, superficial thrombophlebitis, normal proximal arteries with **corkscrew collaterals** on angiography. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Handheld Doppler at the bedside** (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial): loss of arterial signal with an audible venous signal defines threatened (II) ALI. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Imaging in ALI**: **CT angiography** is first-line when it does not delay treatment; **Rutherford IIb goes to theatre**, where on-table angiography is done. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Bloods in ALI**: blood count, platelets (HIT), coagulation, creatinine, **potassium, CK, lactate**, troponin, group and save. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Find the source**: **ECG and Holter for AF, echocardiography** (LV thrombus, mitral stenosis, vegetations), and aortic imaging; thrombophilia work-up only in young patients without a cause. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**ABI**: **0.90 or less diagnoses PAD**, under 0.40 suggests CLTI; **over 1.40 means incompressible arteries** - use toe-brachial index (abnormal under 0.70). [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**CLTI haemodynamic thresholds**: **ankle pressure under 50 mmHg, toe pressure under 30 mmHg or TcPO2 under 30 mmHg** support CLTI and predict poor healing. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Imaging in CLTI**: **duplex ultrasound** first, then **CT or MR angiography** or catheter angiography with run-off to the foot for planning. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Diabetic foot ulcer**: probe-to-bone test and X-ray (or MRI) for osteomyelitis, deep-tissue culture, HbA1c and **toe pressure in every ulcer**. [IWGDF 2023]`]},{heading:`Emergency and inpatient management of ALI`,points:[`**Give IV unfractionated heparin at once** - **5000 units (or 80 units/kg) bolus, then infusion** (aPTT 1.5-2.5 times control) - unless contraindicated (dissection, bleeding). [ESVS 2020]`,`**Analgesia** (IV opioid), oxygen if hypoxic, IV fluids, keep the limb **dependent and warm but not heated**, and correct hypovolaemia. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Refer the same day**: Rutherford **IIb - immediate revascularisation**, **IIa - urgent (within hours)**, I - urgent imaging and planned treatment. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Embolism**: **surgical embolectomy with a Fogarty balloon catheter** (often under local anaesthesia via the femoral or popliteal artery), with on-table angiography. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Thrombosis on PAD or graft**: **catheter-directed thrombolysis** (low-dose alteplase via an intra-thrombus catheter) or mechanical thrombectomy for IIa, followed by angioplasty or stent of the culprit lesion, or **bypass**. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Catheter thrombolysis** is slower and unsuitable for IIb with motor loss; contraindications include recent stroke, surgery or bleeding. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Fasciotomy**: four-compartment calf fasciotomy for compartment syndrome or after revascularisation of **prolonged (over 4-6 hours) ischaemia**; pain on passive stretch is the earliest sign. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Watch for reperfusion syndrome**: hourly urine, **potassium**, CK and creatinine; treat hyperkalaemia and give fluids to protect against myoglobinuric AKI. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Rutherford III**: **primary amputation** or palliative care - revascularising a dead limb causes lethal reperfusion injury. [ESVS 2020]`]},{heading:`Management of CLTI and secondary prevention`,points:[`**Refer all CLTI to a vascular team** urgently; aim to assess for revascularisation within days in tissue loss, faster with infection. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Revascularise when feasible** to relieve rest pain and heal wounds; choose **endovascular or bypass** by patient risk, limb (WIfI), anatomy (GLASS) and the availability of **great saphenous vein**. [GVG 2019]`,`**BEST-CLI (2022)**: in patients with an adequate single-segment saphenous vein, **surgical bypass** reduced major adverse limb events or death compared with endovascular first. [NEJM 2022]`,`**BASIL-2 (2023)**: for **infrapopliteal** disease, a **best endovascular first** strategy gave better amputation-free survival than vein bypass. [Lancet 2023]`,`**Antithrombotic therapy**: single antiplatelet (**clopidogrel 75 mg or aspirin 75-100 mg**); after revascularisation consider **rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin** (VOYAGER-PAD) in those without high bleeding risk. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Statin to LDL under 55 mg/dL** with 50% reduction (atorvastatin 40-80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg), **complete tobacco cessation** (cigarettes, bidi, chewed), BP under 140/90 (130/80 if tolerated), and glycaemic control. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Wound care**: debridement, moist dressings, **offloading** (total contact cast or removable walker), treat infection (culture-directed antibiotics; urgent drainage of abscess or wet gangrene). [IWGDF 2023]`,`**No revascularisation option**: analgesia, wound care; prostanoids (iloprost) and spinal cord stimulation have limited evidence; **primary amputation** when pain or sepsis cannot be controlled. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Cilostazol 100 mg twice daily** is for claudication only (not CLTI) and is contraindicated in heart failure. [ESC 2024 PAD]`]},{heading:`Amputation: thresholds and choices`,points:[`**Indications for major amputation**: Rutherford III ALI, **extensive necrosis or infection** threatening life, unreconstructable disease with intractable rest pain, and a **non-ambulatory patient with fixed flexion contracture**. [GVG 2019]`,`**Level**: **below-knee preserves the knee** and gives the best chance of walking with a prosthesis; above-knee when the knee is not salvageable or the patient will not walk. [GVG 2019]`,`**Minor amputation** (toe, ray, transmetatarsal) after revascularisation preserves walking and foot function. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Dry gangrene** may be allowed to demarcate after revascularisation; **wet gangrene** with sepsis needs urgent debridement or guillotine amputation. [IWGDF 2023]`,`Major amputation carries high peri-operative mortality and **poor 1-year survival**, so revascularisation, rehabilitation and the patient's goals are discussed before deciding. [GVG 2019]`,`**Rehabilitation**: early physiotherapy, stump care, prosthetic fitting through **district disability rehabilitation centres**; protect the other foot, which is at high risk. [GVG 2019]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Diabetes**: check foot pulses and ABI/toe pressures at least yearly; **toe pressure under 30 mmHg or TcPO2 under 25 mmHg** in an ulcer needs urgent imaging and revascularisation. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**CKD and dialysis**: severe calcification, high amputation rates; minimise contrast with duplex planning and CO2 angiography where available. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Elderly and frail**: weigh revascularisation against life expectancy and walking status; palliative care is valid. [GVG 2019]`,`**Pregnancy**: ALI is rare - consider paradoxical embolism, peripartum cardiomyopathy thrombus and antiphospholipid syndrome; heparin is safe. [ESC 2025 Pregnancy]`,`**Children and young adults**: think trauma, catheter injury, popliteal entrapment, **Takayasu**, Buerger disease and thrombophilia. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: give heparin, analgesia and refer immediately; Fogarty embolectomy under local anaesthesia is feasible at many district surgical units, and PM-JAY covers embolectomy, bypass and amputation. [PM-JAY HBP 2022]`,`**Upper-limb ALI**: usually embolic to the brachial artery, better tolerated; brachial embolectomy under local anaesthesia. [ESVS 2020]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis and follow-up`,points:[`**ALI complications**: limb loss, **reperfusion syndrome**, compartment syndrome, AKI, recurrent embolism, bleeding from heparin or lysis and death from underlying heart disease. [ESVS 2020]`,`**After embolic ALI**: **lifelong anticoagulation** if the source is AF or cardiac thrombus (DOAC, or warfarin in rheumatic mitral stenosis or mechanical valve). [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**After thrombotic ALI or CLTI revascularisation**: antiplatelet plus statin, graft surveillance by **duplex at 1, 3, 6 and 12 months** for vein grafts, and clinical review with ABI. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Diabetic foot follow-up**: podiatry and foot inspection every **1-3 months** in high-risk feet (previous ulcer or amputation); protective footwear. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**CLTI prognosis**: about **1 in 4 dies within a year**, mostly from MI and stroke - treat it as a coronary-equivalent disease. [GVG 2019]`,`**Family physician role**: recognise the 6 Ps, give heparin and refer within the hour; examine feet at every **NP-NCD diabetes visit** and enforce tobacco cessation. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Prevention and counselling`,points:[`**Anticoagulate AF** by CHA2DS2-VA score - the most effective prevention of embolic ALI (see the AF topic). [ESC 2024 AF]`,`**Tobacco in any form** (cigarettes, bidi, gutkha) must stop completely; offer counselling, nicotine replacement or varenicline and the **National Tobacco Quitline (1800-112-356)**. [NTCP]`,`**Daily foot care** for diabetics: inspect, wash and dry, moisturise (not between toes), cut nails straight, never walk barefoot, check shoes. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**Supervised exercise** for claudication improves walking distance and is first-line before revascularisation in non-CLTI disease. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Seek help the same day** for a suddenly cold, painful, numb or weak limb - delay beyond 6 hours costs muscle. [ESVS 2020]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC 2024 Peripheral Arterial and Aortic Diseases**: combined guideline; ABI and toe pressures for diagnosis, statin to LDL under 55 mg/dL, low-dose rivaroxaban plus aspirin after revascularisation. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**ESVS 2020 ALI guideline**: immediate heparin, Rutherford-based urgency, CTA first-line imaging, liberal fasciotomy. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Global Vascular Guidelines 2019**: CLTI terminology, **WIfI and GLASS staging**, PLAN framework. [GVG 2019]`,`**BEST-CLI (2022)** favours vein bypass when good saphenous vein exists; **BASIL-2 (2023)** favours endovascular first for infrapopliteal disease. [NEJM 2022; Lancet 2023]`,`**IWGDF 2023**: test for PAD in every diabetic foot ulcer, toe pressure and TcPO2 thresholds, urgent revascularisation for ischaemic ulcers. [IWGDF 2023]`,`**ESVS 2024 claudication guideline**: supervised exercise and risk-factor control before any intervention for intermittent claudication. [ESVS 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic questions**: 6 Ps, embolic vs thrombotic ALI, Rutherford classes, management of ALI, Fontaine classification, diabetic foot, Buerger disease. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: waiting for imaging or the morning list with **sensory or motor loss** - IIb needs theatre now. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Common mistake**: trusting a **normal ABI in a diabetic** - calcified vessels give falsely high values; use toe pressures. [ESC 2024 PAD]`,`**Common mistake**: forgetting fasciotomy and potassium checks after revascularising a long-ischaemic limb. [ESVS 2020]`,`**Viva**: 'Commonest site of an embolus?' - **common femoral bifurcation**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Viva**: 'What relieves rest pain?' - hanging the foot down (gravity raises perfusion pressure). [ESC 2024 PAD]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in acute and chronic limb ischaemia`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration / titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Unfractionated heparin`,`5000 units (80 units/kg) IV bolus, then 18 units/kg/h`,`Until revascularisation; aPTT 1.5-2.5 x`,`Bleeding, HIT; avoid if dissection suspected`],[`Alteplase (catheter-directed)`,`Low-dose infusion via intra-thrombus catheter (about 0.5-1 mg/h)`,`Up to 24-48 h with check angiograms`,`Bleeding, stroke; not for IIb with motor loss`],[`Aspirin`,`75-100 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI bleeding`],[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong (preferred single antiplatelet in PAD)`,`Bleeding; CYP2C19 interactions`],[`Rivaroxaban (vascular dose)`,`2.5 mg oral twice daily plus aspirin`,`Long term after revascularisation`,`Bleeding; avoid with high bleeding risk`],[`Atorvastatin / rosuvastatin`,`40-80 mg / 20-40 mg oral daily`,`Lifelong; LDL under 55 mg/dL`,`Myalgia, transaminase rise`],[`Apixaban (embolic, AF source)`,`5 mg oral twice daily (2.5 mg if 2 of: age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+)`,`Lifelong`,`Bleeding; warfarin instead in rheumatic MS or mechanical valve`],[`Cilostazol`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`Claudication only; review at 3 months`,`Headache, palpitations; contraindicated in heart failure`],[`Morphine`,`2.5-5 mg IV, titrate`,`Acute phase`,`Respiratory depression, hypotension`]]},{heading:`Rutherford classification of acute limb ischaemia`,columns:[`Class`,`Sensory loss`,`Muscle weakness`,`Arterial Doppler`,`Venous Doppler`,`Action`],rows:[[`I Viable`,`None`,`None`,`Audible`,`Audible`,`Urgent imaging, planned treatment`],[`IIa Marginally threatened`,`Minimal (toes)`,`None`,`Often inaudible`,`Audible`,`Revascularise urgently (hours)`],[`IIb Immediately threatened`,`Beyond toes, rest pain`,`Mild to moderate`,`Inaudible`,`Audible`,`Immediate revascularisation`],[`III Irreversible`,`Profound anaesthesia`,`Paralysis, rigor`,`Inaudible`,`Inaudible`,`Primary amputation or palliation`]]},{heading:`Embolic versus thrombotic acute limb ischaemia`,columns:[`Feature`,`Embolic`,`Thrombotic`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Sudden, exact time recalled`,`Gradual over hours to days`],[`Source`,`AF, recent MI, valve disease`,`Pre-existing PAD, graft or stent`],[`Previous claudication`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Contralateral pulses`,`Normal`,`Often absent`],[`Severity`,`Severe (no collaterals)`,`Less severe (collaterals)`],[`Treatment`,`Fogarty embolectomy; anticoagulate long term`,`Thrombolysis, angioplasty or bypass; antiplatelet`]]},{heading:`Chronic PAD staging: Fontaine, Rutherford and WIfI`,columns:[`Fontaine`,`Rutherford chronic`,`Clinical picture`,`WIfI ischaemia grade (ABI / toe pressure)`],rows:[[`I`,`0`,`Asymptomatic`,`0: ABI 0.80 or more / toe 60 mmHg or more`],[`IIa / IIb`,`1-3`,`Claudication over / under 200 m`,`1: ABI 0.60-0.79 / toe 40-59`],[`III`,`4`,`Ischaemic rest pain (CLTI)`,`2: ABI 0.40-0.59 / toe 30-39`],[`IV`,`5-6`,`Ulcer or gangrene (CLTI)`,`3: ABI under 0.40 / toe under 30`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Ankle-brachial index`,`0.90 or less`,`PAD present`],[`ABI`,`Over 1.40`,`Incompressible arteries; use toe pressure`],[`Toe-brachial index`,`Under 0.70`,`PAD in diabetes or CKD`],[`Ankle pressure`,`Under 50 mmHg`,`Supports CLTI`],[`Toe pressure`,`Under 30 mmHg`,`CLTI; poor wound healing`],[`TcPO2`,`Under 30 mmHg (under 25 in IWGDF)`,`Impaired healing; revascularise`],[`Bedside Doppler in ALI`,`Arterial absent, venous present`,`Threatened limb (IIa/IIb)`]]}],redFlags:[`**Sudden cold, white, pulseless limb** - give heparin and refer the same hour.`,`**Sensory loss beyond the toes or any muscle weakness** (Rutherford IIb).`,`**Fixed mottling, paralysis or rigid calf** (Rutherford III).`,`**Pain on passive stretch or tense calf** after revascularisation (compartment syndrome).`,`**Wet gangrene, spreading cellulitis or sepsis** in an ischaemic or diabetic foot.`,`**Rest pain relieved by dependency** or a non-healing foot ulcer with absent pulses (CLTI).`,`Limb ischaemia with **chest or back pain** (possible aortic dissection).`],pearls:[`**6 Ps**: pain, pallor, pulselessness, paraesthesia, paralysis, perishing cold.`,`**Sensory loss or weakness = threatened limb = theatre now.**`,`**Embolus = AF, sudden, normal other leg; thrombosis = claudicant, absent other pulses.**`,`**Heparin first, imaging second** - unless dissection is suspected.`,`**Doppler rule**: arterial gone, venous present = threatened; both gone = irreversible.`,`**Muscle dies after about 6 hours** of complete ischaemia - fasciotomy after long ischaemia.`,`**Diabetic with normal ABI and gangrene** - check toe pressure; calcified arteries lie.`,`**Rest pain eased by hanging the foot down** is ischaemic; neuropathic pain is not.`,`**Buerger = young smoker, distal disease, thrombophlebitis** - tobacco cessation is the treatment.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-q1`,stem:`A 66-year-old woman with untreated AF has a sudden painful cold right leg for 3 hours. Femoral pulse is absent, the left leg pulses are normal, sensation is reduced over the toes only and power is normal. What is the Rutherford class?`,options:[`I - viable`,`IIa - marginally threatened`,`IIb - immediately threatened`,`III - irreversible`],answer:1,explanation:`Minimal sensory loss limited to the toes without weakness is Rutherford IIa, salvageable with prompt revascularisation after IV heparin. Class I has no sensory loss, IIb has sensory loss beyond the toes with weakness and needs immediate theatre, and III has paralysis and profound anaesthesia with inaudible venous signals.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-q2`,stem:`In a patient presenting with suspected embolic acute limb ischaemia, what is the first drug to give, before imaging?`,options:[`Aspirin 300 mg orally`,`IV alteplase 100 mg systemically`,`Oral apixaban 10 mg`,`IV unfractionated heparin bolus followed by infusion`],answer:3,explanation:`Immediate IV unfractionated heparin limits propagation of thrombus and is reversible if the patient goes to theatre. Aspirin alone does not stop clot propagation, systemic thrombolysis is not used for ALI (thrombolysis is catheter-directed), and an oral DOAC loading dose delays surgery and cannot be titrated.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-q3`,stem:`Six hours after femoral embolectomy for an 8-hour-old ischaemic leg, a patient has severe calf pain on passive toe extension and a tense calf, with palpable foot pulses. What is the next step?`,options:[`Four-compartment fasciotomy`,`Increase the heparin infusion`,`Elevate the leg and give more analgesia`,`Repeat the embolectomy`],answer:0,explanation:`Pain on passive stretch and a tense calf after reperfusion of a long-ischaemic limb are compartment syndrome; palpable pulses do not exclude it, and urgent fasciotomy saves the muscle. Elevation and analgesia delay treatment, more heparin is irrelevant, and repeat embolectomy addresses a problem that is not present.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-q4`,stem:`A 62-year-old man with 15 years of diabetes has a painless non-healing ulcer on the hallux and absent foot pulses. ABI is 1.45. What is the best next test?`,options:[`Reassure - ABI is normal`,`Repeat ABI after exercise`,`Toe pressure or toe-brachial index`,`Nerve conduction studies`],answer:2,explanation:`An ABI over 1.40 indicates incompressible calcified arteries, common in diabetes, and is unreliable; toe pressure (under 30 mmHg) or toe-brachial index (under 0.70) assesses perfusion and decides on revascularisation. Reassurance would miss CLTI, exercise ABI shares the same calcification error, and nerve studies do not assess ischaemia.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-q5`,stem:`A 50-year-old man has CLTI with tissue loss from long femoropopliteal occlusion and a good-quality single-segment great saphenous vein. He is fit for surgery. According to BEST-CLI, which strategy gives fewer major adverse limb events or death?`,options:[`Primary below-knee amputation`,`Surgical bypass using the saphenous vein`,`Cilostazol and supervised exercise`,`Iloprost infusion`],answer:1,explanation:`BEST-CLI (2022) showed that, in patients with adequate single-segment great saphenous vein, surgical bypass reduced major adverse limb events or death compared with endovascular first. Primary amputation is for unsalvageable limbs, cilostazol and exercise are claudication treatments not suited to tissue loss, and prostanoids are reserved for non-revascularisable disease.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Discuss the causes, clinical features, classification and management of acute limb ischaemia.`,openingLines:[`Acute limb ischaemia is a sudden decrease in limb perfusion of less than two weeks' duration that threatens limb viability.`,`It is a vascular emergency: IV heparin and same-day revascularisation guided by the Rutherford class decide whether the limb and the patient survive.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Embolism: AF, LV thrombus, rheumatic MS, endocarditis, aneurysm.`,`Thrombosis: on PAD, graft or stent, popliteal aneurysm; dissection, trauma, hypercoagulable states.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`6 Ps: pain, pallor, pulselessness, paraesthesia, paralysis, perishing cold.`,`Embolic versus thrombotic features (onset, AF, claudication history, contralateral pulses).`]},{heading:`Classification`,points:[`Rutherford I, IIa, IIb, III by sensory loss, weakness and Doppler signals.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`Bedside Doppler; CT angiography if it does not delay; on-table angiography for IIb.`,`Potassium, CK, creatinine, lactate, coagulation; ECG and echo for source.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`**IV UFH 5000 units bolus then infusion**, analgesia, fluids; same-day vascular referral.`,`Embolus: Fogarty embolectomy; thrombosis: catheter-directed lysis, angioplasty or bypass.`,`Fasciotomy for compartment syndrome; monitor for reperfusion injury (K, myoglobin, AKI).`,`Rutherford III: primary amputation or palliation.`]},{heading:`Secondary prevention`,points:[`Anticoagulation for cardiac embolism; antiplatelet, statin, tobacco cessation for atherosclerosis.`]}],mustDraw:[`Rutherford ALI table`,`Flow chart: suspected ALI to heparin, class, revascularisation`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and causes`,marks:2},{part:`Clinical features and embolic vs thrombotic`,marks:2},{part:`Rutherford classification`,marks:2},{part:`Management including complications`,marks:4}],keywords:[`6 Ps`,`Rutherford`,`heparin`,`embolectomy`,`thrombolysis`,`fasciotomy`,`reperfusion injury`]},{id:`cardiovascular-acute-limb-ischaemia-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`common`,question:`Differentiate embolic from thrombotic acute limb ischaemia, and outline the staging of chronic limb-threatening ischaemia.`,openingLines:[`Embolic ALI occludes a previously normal artery without collaterals and is sudden and severe; thrombotic ALI occurs on pre-existing PAD with collaterals and is less abrupt.`,`CLTI is PAD with rest pain over 2 weeks or tissue loss, staged by Fontaine/Rutherford and WIfI.`],answer:[{heading:`Embolic versus thrombotic`,points:[`Onset sudden vs gradual; source AF/MI vs PAD; claudication absent vs present.`,`Contralateral pulses normal vs absent; treatment embolectomy vs lysis/angioplasty/bypass.`]},{heading:`CLTI staging`,points:[`Fontaine III rest pain, IV tissue loss; Rutherford 4-6.`,`WIfI: wound, ischaemia (ABI, toe pressure under 30), foot infection - predicts amputation risk.`]}],mustDraw:[`Two-column table: embolic versus thrombotic`,`WIfI grid`],markSplit:[{part:`Embolic versus thrombotic table`,marks:3},{part:`CLTI staging`,marks:2}],keywords:[`embolic`,`thrombotic`,`CLTI`,`Fontaine`,`WIfI`,`toe pressure`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025`,`2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of peripheral arterial and aortic diseases`,`European Society for Vascular Surgery (ESVS) 2020 Clinical Practice Guidelines on the Management of Acute Limb Ischaemia`,`Global Vascular Guidelines on the Management of Chronic Limb-Threatening Ischemia, 2019`,`IWGDF 2023 Guidelines on the prevention and management of diabetes-related foot disease`,`Farber A et al. Surgery or endovascular therapy for chronic limb-threatening ischemia (BEST-CLI). NEJM 2022`,`ICMR-INDIAB study, Lancet Diabetes Endocrinology 2023`,`API Textbook of Medicine, 12th edition, 2022`]});var d=[];d.push({id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis`,title:`Takayasu arteritis, Raynaud phenomenon and vasospastic disorders`,oneLiner:`**Takayasu arteritis** is a granulomatous **large-vessel vasculitis** of the aorta, its branches and the pulmonary arteries in **women under 40** (pulseless disease; renovascular hypertension in India), treated with **prednisolone plus methotrexate** and revascularisation only in remission; the vasospastic group - **Raynaud phenomenon**, **Buerger disease** of young tobacco users and **Prinzmetal angina** - responds to warmth, complete tobacco cessation and **calcium channel blockers**.`,frequency:`less-common`,keywords:[`Takayasu arteritis`,`pulseless disease`,`aortic arch syndrome`,`Numano classification`,`ACR/EULAR 2022 criteria`,`giant cell arteritis`,`temporal arteritis`,`Raynaud phenomenon`,`nailfold capillaroscopy`,`Buerger disease`,`thromboangiitis obliterans`,`Prinzmetal angina`,`vasospastic angina`,`COVADIS`,`tocilizumab`,`methotrexate`,`nifedipine`,`iloprost`,`renovascular hypertension`,`ITAS2010`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Takayasu arteritis (TAK)** is a chronic **granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis** of the aorta, its major branches and the pulmonary arteries, causing stenosis, occlusion, dilatation and aneurysm; it is also called **pulseless disease** or aortic arch syndrome. [Harrison 22e]`,`About **80-90% of patients are women**, with onset usually between **10 and 40 years**; the female predominance is less marked in Indian than in Japanese series. [Harrison 22e]`,`TAK is commonest in **Asia** (Japan, India, China, Korea) and Mexico; the reported annual incidence is only about **1-3 per million**, and lower still in Europe and North America. [Harrison 22e]`,`Indian data come from large tertiary cohorts (Lucknow, Vellore, Delhi, Chandigarh): Indian patients more often have **abdominal aortic and renal artery disease** (Numano types IV and V) with **hypertension as the leading presentation**, whereas Japanese patients more often have arch disease. [Indian TAK cohorts]`,`**Giant cell arteritis (GCA)** is the large-vessel vasculitis of people **aged 50 or more** (peak 70-80 years), affecting cranial branches of the carotid and the aorta; it is common in Northern Europeans and **rare in India**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raynaud phenomenon (RP)** is episodic, reversible digital vasospasm triggered by cold or emotion, affecting about **3-5% of the population**; **primary RP** (no underlying disease) forms the large majority and mostly affects young women. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger disease)** is a non-atherosclerotic, segmental, inflammatory occlusive disease of small and medium arteries and veins of the limbs, almost confined to **young tobacco users**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Buerger disease made up only **0.5-5.6% of PAD in Western Europe** but **45-63% in India** in classic series - bidi smoking makes it a disease examiners expect Indian candidates to know. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**Vasospastic (Prinzmetal, variant) angina (VSA)** is angina at rest caused by transient focal or diffuse **epicardial coronary spasm**, classically with transient ST elevation, and is commoner in **East Asians** and smokers. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and risk factors`,points:[`TAK has **no known cause**; the strongest genetic association is with **HLA-B52**, and non-HLA loci such as **IL12B and IL6** are also linked. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Tuberculosis** is over-represented in Indian and Mexican TAK (latent infection, mycobacterial DNA in aortic tissue, HSP65 cross-reactivity), but causality is unproven and **antitubercular therapy does not treat TAK**. [Indian TAK cohorts]`,`The TAK risk profile is **female sex, age under 40 and Asian ancestry** - conventional atherosclerotic risk factors are usually absent, which helps separate it from atherosclerosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`GCA risk rises with **every decade after 50**, and with female sex, Northern European ancestry and **HLA-DR4**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary RP** risk factors: female sex, onset at **15-30 years**, family history, low body weight and migraine. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary RP causes**: **systemic sclerosis** (over 90% have RP), mixed connective tissue disease, SLE, Sjogren syndrome, dermatomyositis; **hand-arm vibration syndrome**; thoracic outlet syndrome; Buerger disease and atherosclerosis; cryoglobulins, cold agglutinins, polycythaemia; hypothyroidism. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drugs that provoke RP**: non-selective **beta-blockers**, ergotamine, triptans, amphetamines and ADHD stimulants, cocaine, **bleomycin, cisplatin and vinblastine**, interferon-alpha and ciclosporin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Buerger disease** depends on tobacco - cigarettes, **bidis**, chewing tobacco and cannabis all sustain it; onset is usually **before 45-50 years**, mostly in men, with periodontitis also linked. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**VSA triggers**: **smoking** (the strongest), cold, hyperventilation, emotional stress, binge alcohol, magnesium deficiency, **cocaine and amphetamines**, triptans, ergots and **5-fluorouracil or capecitabine**. [ESC 2024]`,`VSA patients are younger with fewer classic risk factors; an **ALDH2 polymorphism** contributes in East Asians, and VSA clusters with **migraine and Raynaud** - a shared vasospastic tendency. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`TAK begins in the **adventitia**: activated dendritic cells around the **vasa vasorum** recruit CD4 T cells, cytotoxic CD8 and NK cells and macrophages, producing a **granulomatous panarteritis** with giant cells that spreads inwards. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Th1 (interferon-gamma)** responses sustain granulomas, and **Th17 (IL-17)** responses are relatively steroid-resistant - one explanation for relapse on glucocorticoid taper and the need for steroid-sparing drugs. [Harrison 22e]`,`**IL-6** drives the acute-phase response (fever, raised ESR and CRP, anaemia, thrombocytosis) - the target of **tocilizumab**, which therefore normalises CRP whatever the disease activity. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TNF-alpha** maintains granulomas - the target of infliximab and adalimumab, and the reason these drugs reactivate latent TB. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`Media destruction by **matrix metalloproteinases** weakens the wall (dilatation, **aneurysm**, aortic root dilatation with **aortic regurgitation**), while intimal hyperplasia and adventitial fibrosis cause **stenosis and occlusion** - stenosis predominates. [Harrison 22e]`,`Lesions are segmental with **skip areas**, and the **subclavian arteries** are among the commonest involved - explaining arm claudication and pulse loss; slow progression lets **collaterals** keep most limbs viable. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Hypertension in TAK** has three mechanisms: **renal artery stenosis** (renin-angiotensin activation), **suprarenal aortic narrowing** (middle aortic syndrome) and a stiff aorta with impaired baroreceptor function. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Raynaud**: cold constricts digital arteries via sympathetic **alpha-2C adrenoceptors**; in primary RP this response is exaggerated, while in secondary RP **intimal narrowing**, endothelial dysfunction (less nitric oxide and prostacyclin, more **endothelin-1**) and platelet activation turn normal spasm into ischaemia and ulcers. [Harrison 22e]`,`The **colour phases** follow the physiology - **white** (digital artery spasm), **blue** (deoxygenated blood in dilated capillaries and venules), **red** (reactive hyperaemia) - and the drugs follow it too: CCBs, PDE5 inhibitors, prostacyclin analogues and endothelin antagonists. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Buerger disease** is a highly cellular **inflammatory thrombus** occluding artery and companion vein with the **internal elastic lamina preserved** (unlike necrotising vasculitis); late fibrosis encases the neurovascular bundle. **Coronary vasospasm** comes from smooth-muscle hyperreactivity via **Rho-kinase** plus endothelial dysfunction, so acetylcholine constricts instead of dilating. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Classification and classification criteria`,points:[`**Chapel Hill 2012** places TAK and GCA among the **large-vessel vasculitides**; TAK usually begins before 50 and GCA after 50 - age at onset is the main separating feature. [CHCC 2012]`,`TAK has three overlapping phases: a **pre-pulseless systemic phase** (fever, weight loss, arthralgia), a **vascular inflammatory phase** (vessel pain, bruits, pulse loss) and a **burnt-out fibrotic phase** (fixed stenoses with ischaemia). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Numano angiographic classification (1996)**: type I arch branches; IIa ascending aorta, arch and branches; IIb IIa plus descending thoracic aorta; III descending thoracic and abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries; IV abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries; V combined IIb and IV. [Numano 1996]`,`Add **C(+)** for coronary and **P(+)** for pulmonary artery involvement to the Numano type. [Numano 1996]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 TAK criteria** - absolute requirements: **age 60 or less at diagnosis** and **imaging evidence of large-vessel vasculitis**; a score of **5 or more** from clinical and imaging items then classifies TAK (sensitivity about 94%, specificity about 99%). [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`Clinical items: **female sex +1**; angina or ischaemic cardiac pain +2; arm or leg claudication +2; vascular bruit +2; reduced upper-limb pulse +2; reduced carotid pulse or carotid tenderness +2; **inter-arm systolic difference of 20 mmHg or more +1**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`Imaging items: one arterial territory +1, two +2, **three or more +3**; symmetric involvement of paired arteries +1; **abdominal aorta with renal or mesenteric involvement +3**. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`The older **ACR 1990 criteria** (3 of 6, onset at **40 or younger**) and **Sharma's modified Ishikawa criteria** (from Chandigarh) are still quoted; classification criteria are for studies and do not replace clinical diagnosis. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Ishikawa complications** that set prognosis: Takayasu retinopathy, secondary hypertension, aortic regurgitation and aneurysm - patients are grouped by the number and severity of these. [Ishikawa 1994]`,`**Activity** is judged by the **NIH (Kerr) criteria** - new or worse in at least 2 of: systemic features, raised ESR, ischaemic or inflammatory vascular features, typical angiographic change - or the Indian **ITAS2010** score. [ITAS2010]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history and examination (Takayasu)`,points:[`**Systemic symptoms** - fever, night sweats, malaise, weight loss, arthralgia, myalgia - may precede pulse loss by months and present as **PUO** in a young woman. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Upper-limb claudication** (the arm aches on combing hair or washing clothes), cold hands and **lower-limb claudication** reflect subclavian and aorto-iliac stenosis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Neurological**: headache, dizziness, visual blurring on standing or looking up, amaurosis, TIA or stroke; **syncope or vertigo on arm exercise** suggests **subclavian steal**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Cardiopulmonary**: exertional angina (coronary ostial stenosis), breathlessness from hypertension, **aortic regurgitation** or heart failure, and haemoptysis or dyspnoea from **pulmonary arteritis**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Carotidynia** (a tender carotid), **post-prandial abdominal pain** (mesenteric ischaemia) and **erythema nodosum** are less common clues. [Harrison 22e]`,`Feel **every pulse on both sides**: absent or weak radial, brachial or carotid pulses are the hallmark; record the pulse deficit and **radio-radial and radio-femoral asymmetry**. [Harrison 22e]`,`Measure **BP in all four limbs**: an inter-arm systolic difference of **10 mmHg or more** (ACR 1990) or **20 or more** (ACR/EULAR 2022) is significant, and arm BP is falsely low distal to a stenosis. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Reversed coarctation**: with bilateral subclavian stenosis the leg BP greatly exceeds arm BP; if all four limbs are involved, true pressure is measured **centrally at angiography**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Listen for **bruits** over carotids, supraclavicular fossae, axillae, abdominal aorta, renal areas and femorals, and over the heart for an **early diastolic murmur** at the right sternal edge (aortic root AR), LVH and a loud P2. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fundus**: **Takayasu retinopathy** (Uyama stages - dilated small vessels, microaneurysms, wreath-like arteriovenous anastomoses, then complications) reflects hypoperfusion; in India **hypertensive retinopathy** from renal artery stenosis is commoner. [Indian TAK cohorts]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis (with the GCA pointer)`,points:[`**GCA**: age **50 or more**, new temporal headache, scalp tenderness, **jaw claudication**, visual loss (up to 20%), polymyalgia rheumatica; ESR usually over 50 mm/h; temporal artery **halo sign** on ultrasound - large-vessel GCA can mimic TAK in the axillary and subclavian arteries. [Harrison 22e]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 GCA criteria**: age 50 or more is absolute; a score of **6 or more** from items such as positive biopsy or halo sign (+5), sudden visual loss (+3), ESR 50 mm/h or more or CRP 10 mg/L or more (+3), jaw claudication, new temporal headache, scalp tenderness and shoulder stiffness. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**Atherosclerosis**: older, smoker, diabetic, dyslipidaemic patient; ostial, calcified, eccentric plaques without long smooth tapering or wall enhancement; normal ESR. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibromuscular dysplasia**: young to middle-aged women with renal or carotid stenosis, but a **string-of-beads** mid-distal renal artery, no systemic inflammation and no aortic wall thickening. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Congenital coarctation**: discrete juxtaductal narrowing, rib notching, bicuspid aortic valve, childhood onset; TAK causes a long-segment, often abdominal **atypical (middle aortic) coarctation**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Infective aortitis**: **syphilis** (ascending aortic aneurysm, AR, tree-bark calcification; VDRL/TPHA), **tuberculous aortitis** by contiguous spread, and mycotic aneurysm with bacteraemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Other inflammatory aortitis: **Behcet disease** (oral and genital ulcers, aneurysms, venous thrombosis), IgG4-related periaortitis, Cogan syndrome, spondyloarthritis, sarcoidosis and relapsing polychondritis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Raynaud look-alikes: **acrocyanosis** (persistent painless blue hands, no pallor), chilblains (cold-induced tender red-purple papules), **erythromelalgia** (burning red warm feet - think myeloproliferative neoplasm) and embolic blue toe from AF, endocarditis or atheroemboli. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Buerger disease versus atherosclerotic PAD**: Buerger is young, distal (below knee and elbow), involves the arms and veins, and lacks diabetes, hyperlipidaemia and proximal plaque. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**VSA versus ACS**: VSA pain comes at rest, often at night, with ST elevation that resolves within minutes on nitrate; persisting ST elevation is managed as STEMI until angiography shows no occlusion - see the ACS topic. [ESC 2024]`]},{heading:`Investigations and activity assessment`,points:[`**First line (district hospital)**: CBC (normocytic anaemia, thrombocytosis), **ESR and CRP**, creatinine and urine routine, glucose, lipids, ECG and chest X-ray - inflammatory markers are raised in most, but **normal in a sizeable minority** with active disease on imaging. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Echocardiography** for aortic root dilatation and **AR**, LV hypertrophy and function, regional wall motion (coronary disease) and **pulmonary artery pressure**. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Colour Doppler ultrasound** of carotid, subclavian and axillary arteries shows **homogeneous concentric wall thickening (the macaroni sign)**, stenosis and reversed vertebral flow in subclavian steal - cheap, radiation-free and ideal for follow-up. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**Renal artery Doppler**: a peak systolic velocity over **180-200 cm/s** or a renal-aortic velocity ratio over **3.5** suggests stenosis of 60% or more. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**MR angiography** is the first-line imaging test in suspected TAK: wall thickening, oedema and enhancement plus luminal stenosis, without radiation - preferred in young women. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**CT angiography** shows wall thickening with early **double-ring enhancement**, stenoses, aneurysms and later calcification; it is good for the thoracic aorta and for planning intervention, at the cost of radiation and contrast. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**18F-FDG PET-CT**: vessel-wall uptake **greater than liver uptake** suggests active vasculitis; useful in PUO and to judge activity, but costly and limited in availability in India. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**Catheter angiography** is no longer a diagnostic first step; it is used for **intervention** and to measure **central aortic pressure** when all four limbs are involved. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**Before immunosuppression**: IGRA or Mantoux plus chest X-ray, HBsAg, anti-HBc, anti-HCV, HIV, VDRL/TPHA, pregnancy test, liver function and baseline bone health. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Activity** is judged from symptoms, four-limb BP, pulses, bruits, ESR/CRP and imaging together (NIH criteria, ITAS2010) - no single marker is reliable. [ITAS2010]`]},{heading:`Management of Takayasu arteritis in the OPD`,points:[`**Goals**: induce and maintain remission, stop new vessel damage, control BP and other complications, and keep glucocorticoid exposure low. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Prednisolone 40-60 mg/day** orally (about 0.5-1 mg/kg) as a single morning dose induces remission; once controlled, taper to **15-20 mg/day by 2-3 months** and to **5 mg/day or less after 1 year**. [EULAR 2022]`,`Start a **non-glucocorticoid agent with the steroid in every patient** with TAK - glucocorticoids alone usually relapse on taper. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Methotrexate** 15-25 mg **once weekly** orally or SC (start 10-15 mg, increase by 2.5-5 mg every 2-4 weeks) with **folic acid 5 mg weekly** on another day; alternatives are **azathioprine 2 mg/kg/day**, **mycophenolate 2-3 g/day** in two doses or **leflunomide 20 mg/day**. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Relapsing or refractory disease**: add **tocilizumab** 8 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks or 162 mg SC weekly, or a **TNF inhibitor** (infliximab 5 mg/kg IV at 0, 2 and 6 weeks then every 4-8 weeks; adalimumab 40 mg SC every 2 weeks); ACR/VF 2021 favours TNF inhibitors over tocilizumab. [EULAR 2022]`,`**TB screening** is mandatory: latent TB gets **TB preventive therapy** (6 months of isoniazid or 12 weekly doses of isoniazid-rifapentine) before biologics, and active TB is treated first. [NTEP TPT 2021]`,`**Hypertension**: measure in a reliable limb; **amlodipine** and beta-blockers are safe, and **ACE inhibitors or ARBs** help unilateral renal artery stenosis but can precipitate renal failure in **bilateral stenosis or a single kidney** - recheck creatinine at 1-2 weeks. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Low-dose aspirin 75-150 mg** is reserved for critical cranial, vertebrobasilar or coronary involvement; routine antiplatelet or anticoagulant therapy is **not** recommended. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Glucocorticoid protection**: calcium and vitamin D, a bisphosphonate when fracture risk warrants, glucose and BP checks, a PPI if on aspirin, inactivated vaccines (influenza, pneumococcal) and **no live vaccines** on high-dose steroid or biologics. [EULAR 2022]`,`Do **not** start empirical antitubercular therapy for TAK without proof of TB, and never stop steroids abruptly - give the patient a steroid card. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Inpatient and emergency care`,points:[`**Admit TAK** with stroke or TIA, critical limb or mesenteric ischaemia, hypertensive emergency, flash pulmonary oedema, ACS, decompensated AR or heart failure, or aneurysm expansion or dissection. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Organ-threatening flare**: IV methylprednisolone **500-1000 mg daily for 3 days** then oral prednisolone; otherwise ACR/VF 2021 prefers high-dose oral over IV pulse glucocorticoid in TAK. [ACR/VF 2021]`,`**Lower BP gradually** in TAK with severe hypertension (IV labetalol or nicardipine), because cerebral and renal flow beyond fixed stenoses depends on perfusion pressure - see the Hypertensive urgency and emergency topic. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Revascularisation** (angioplasty, stent or surgical bypass) for renovascular hypertension, critical cerebral, coronary or limb ischaemia, severe AR or an enlarging aneurysm is done **electively in stable remission**, decided by a multidisciplinary team. [EULAR 2022]`,`Renal **balloon angioplasty** controls BP well in short TAK lesions; **stents restenose more often**, and surgical bypass gives better long-term patency in long-segment disease. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**GCA with visual symptoms**: start **IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg daily for 3 days** the same day, before biopsy (biopsy stays positive for 1-2 weeks), then oral prednisolone 40-60 mg/day. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Critical digital ischaemia** (RP, systemic sclerosis, Buerger): admit, keep warm, give opioid analgesia, stop vasoconstrictors, treat infection and give **IV iloprost 0.5-2 ng/kg/min over 6 hours daily for 3-5 days**; urgent imaging excludes a proximal embolic or occlusive source. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**VSA attack**: sublingual **glyceryl trinitrate 0.4-0.5 mg** every 5 minutes up to 3 doses; ST elevation that does not resolve goes to the cath lab as STEMI, where intracoronary nitrate unmasks spasm. [ESC 2024]`,`Coronary spasm can cause **AV block, VT or VF and sudden death**; an **ICD should be considered** in survivors of cardiac arrest due to vasospasm, alongside maximal CCB therapy. [ESC 2022 VA]`]},{heading:`Raynaud phenomenon: primary versus secondary`,points:[`**Raynaud phenomenon** is episodic, sharply demarcated **pallor, then cyanosis, then rubor** of the fingers (sometimes toes, ears or nose) on cold or stress, lasting minutes and recovering on rewarming. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Primary RP (LeRoy-Medsger criteria)**: symmetric attacks, no peripheral vascular disease, **no ulcers, pitting scars or gangrene**, **normal nailfold capillaries**, **negative ANA** and a normal ESR. [LeRoy-Medsger 1992]`,`**Suspect secondary RP** with onset after 30-40 years, male sex, asymmetry, severe pain, digital ulcers or pits, puffy fingers, sclerodactyly, systemic symptoms, abnormal capillaroscopy or a positive ANA. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Nailfold capillaroscopy** at the bedside (dermatoscope, or ophthalmoscope at +40 dioptres with a drop of oil): **giant capillaries, haemorrhages and capillary dropout** form the scleroderma pattern, and with an SSc-specific antibody strongly predict systemic sclerosis. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Work-up**: CBC, ESR/CRP, ANA by immunofluorescence, then anti-centromere, anti-Scl-70 and anti-RNA polymerase III; TSH; cryoglobulins and complement when suggested; Allen test, cervical rib X-ray and arterial Doppler for unilateral RP. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Non-drug measures first**: keep the **whole body warm** (layers, gloves, socks), avoid sudden cold and vibrating tools, **stop smoking** and stop provoking drugs; during an attack rewarm the hands in warm, not hot, water. [Harrison 22e]`,`**First-line drug**: a dihydropyridine CCB - **nifedipine retard 10-20 mg twice daily** (or extended-release 30-60 mg once daily) or **amlodipine 5-10 mg once daily**, through winter or long term; headache, flushing and ankle oedema limit the dose. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Next**: a **PDE5 inhibitor** (sildenafil 20 mg three times daily or tadalafil 10-20 mg daily; never with nitrates); topical glyceryl trinitrate or the alpha-1 blocker prazosin are weaker alternatives. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Severe or ulcerating RP**: IV iloprost for attacks and ulcer healing, **bosentan** to reduce new digital ulcers in systemic sclerosis, and **botulinum toxin** injection or digital sympathectomy when refractory. [EULAR SSc 2023]`,`**Primary RP is benign** and does not cause tissue loss; ANA-positive patients need yearly review with capillaroscopy because a minority evolve into connective tissue disease. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Buerger disease (thromboangiitis obliterans)`,points:[`**Typical patient**: a man **under 45** who smokes (often **bidis**) with distal limb ischaemia - **instep (foot) claudication**, rest pain, and painful ulcers or gangrene of toes and fingers. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`The **upper limbs** are often involved (abnormal **Allen test**), and **superficial migratory thrombophlebitis** and Raynaud phenomenon are clues absent in atherosclerotic PAD. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**Examination**: absent dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial, radial or ulnar pulses with **normal femoral and popliteal pulses**; Buerger's test shows pallor on elevation and dusky rubor on dependency. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Shionoya criteria**: smoking history, onset before 50, **infrapopliteal occlusions**, upper-limb involvement or phlebitis migrans, and no atherosclerotic risk factor other than smoking. [Shionoya 1998]`,`**Olin criteria**: age under 45, current or recent tobacco use, distal ischaemia confirmed by non-invasive testing, and exclusion of autoimmune disease, thrombophilia, diabetes and a proximal embolic source. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**Investigations** exclude look-alikes - glucose/HbA1c, lipids, ANA, RF, complement, antiphospholipid antibodies, cryoglobulins, echo for an embolic source - while Doppler or angiography shows segmental distal occlusions with **corkscrew collaterals (Martorell sign)** and normal proximal arteries. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**Complete abstinence from all tobacco** is the only treatment that halts the disease - even a few bidis or chewing tobacco keep it active - so use counselling with **varenicline or bupropion** rather than relying on nicotine replacement. [Olin NEJM 2000]`,`**IV iloprost** (0.5-2 ng/kg/min over 6 hours daily for up to 4 weeks) relieved rest pain and healed ulcers better than aspirin; cilostazol, aspirin, analgesia, foot care and wound care are supportive. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Procedures**: lumbar or thoracic **sympathectomy** for rest pain and superficial ulcers (widely used in India, modest evidence), spinal cord stimulation and **conservative minor amputation**; bypass is seldom possible because distal run-off is poor. [Harrison 22e]`,`India has approved **cell therapy** (stempeucel, allogeneic bone-marrow mesenchymal stromal cells) for critical limb ischaemia due to Buerger disease; life expectancy is normal, and **major amputation is largely confined to those who keep using tobacco**. [CDSCO]`]},{heading:`Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina`,points:[`**Prinzmetal (variant) angina** is rest angina, often between **midnight and early morning**, lasting 5-15 minutes, often in clusters, with good exercise tolerance by day. [ESC 2024]`,`ESC 2024 places VSA within **ANOCA/INOCA** (angina or ischaemia with non-obstructive coronary arteries) alongside microvascular angina - both are under-recognised in women with 'normal' angiograms. [ESC 2024]`,`**COVADIS criteria**: (1) **nitrate-responsive angina**; (2) **transient ischaemic ECG change** during an episode (ST elevation or depression of 0.1 mV or more, or new negative U waves, in 2 contiguous leads); (3) documented **coronary spasm** - 1 plus 2 or 3 is definite VSA. [COVADIS 2017]`,`Spasm on provocation is **transient total or subtotal occlusion (90% or more constriction)** with reproduction of angina and ECG change after intracoronary **acetylcholine** (or ergonovine); hyperventilation is a non-invasive alternative. [COVADIS 2017]`,`**Investigations**: ECG during pain, **Holter monitoring** for nocturnal ST elevation and arrhythmia, troponin, echo, and coronary angiography (or CT coronary angiography) to exclude fixed obstruction. [ESC 2024]`,`**Stop smoking** and avoid triggers (cocaine, amphetamines, triptans, ergots, cold, binge alcohol); spasm frequently settles after quitting. [ESC 2024]`,`**CCBs are first line**: diltiazem SR 90-180 mg twice daily (up to 360 mg/day), verapamil SR 120-240 mg twice daily, nifedipine extended-release 30-90 mg/day or amlodipine 5-10 mg/day, with an evening dose to cover night attacks. [Braunwald 12e]`,`Add a **long-acting nitrate** (isosorbide mononitrate ER 30-60 mg once daily) or nicorandil when a CCB alone fails; refractory cases use **two CCBs together** (a dihydropyridine plus diltiazem). [ESC 2024]`,`**Avoid non-selective beta-blockers** such as propranolol - unopposed alpha-mediated vasoconstriction worsens spasm; coexisting atheroma still needs a statin and aspirin. [ESC 2024]`,`Prognosis is good on CCB therapy, but **multivessel spasm, previous cardiac arrest and continued smoking** predict events, and PCI does not treat pure spasm. [Braunwald 12e]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Pregnancy in TAK**: plan conception during remission; the main risks are **hypertension, pre-eclampsia, fetal growth restriction** and heart failure with AR, so co-manage with obstetrics and measure BP in a reliable limb - see the Heart disease in pregnancy topic. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**Drugs and pregnancy**: stop **methotrexate 1-3 months** and **mycophenolate at least 6 weeks** before conception and wash out **leflunomide** with cholestyramine; prednisolone, azathioprine and TNF inhibitors can continue; use labetalol or nifedipine for BP and no ACE inhibitor or ARB. [ACR Reproductive Health 2020]`,`**Children**: TAK is a leading cause of **renovascular hypertension in Indian children** - feel the femoral pulses and check four-limb BP in every hypertensive child. [Indian TAK cohorts]`,`**Elderly**: new cranial or large-vessel symptoms after 50 mean **GCA** until proven otherwise, and Raynaud starting after 40 is **secondary** until proven otherwise. [Harrison 22e]`,`**CKD and renal artery disease**: prefer Doppler or non-contrast MRA when eGFR is low, avoid ACE inhibitors/ARBs in bilateral stenosis, and avoid methotrexate when eGFR is under 30. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: high-dose steroids cause hyperglycaemia - monitor glucose and adjust therapy; diabetes is an exclusion in the Olin criteria for Buerger disease. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: diagnosis rests on four-limb BP, pulses, bruits and Doppler, and **prednisolone, methotrexate and azathioprine** are inexpensive essential medicines (NLEM 2022, Jan Aushadhi); biologics remain a tertiary-centre option. [NLEM 2022]`,`**Cancer therapy**: 5-fluorouracil and capecitabine cause coronary vasospasm - stop the drug, give nitrate and a CCB, and discuss with oncology before rechallenge; see the Heart in systemic disease topic. [ESC Cardio-oncology 2022]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**TAK complications**: renovascular hypertension and CKD, stroke, AR with heart failure, coronary ischaemia, aneurysm rupture or dissection, pulmonary hypertension, visual loss and **steroid toxicity**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Prognosis**: in Ishikawa's Japanese cohort 15-year survival was about **96% without** and **66% with a major complication** - hypertension, AR, retinopathy and aneurysm drive mortality. [Ishikawa 1994]`,`**Relapse** is common during glucocorticoid taper and progression can be **silent**, so imaging is repeated even in clinical remission. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**Follow-up**: every 1-3 months while active and 3-6 months in remission - symptoms, **four-limb BP, pulses and bruits**, ESR/CRP, creatinine and urine - with non-invasive imaging (MRA or Doppler) at an individualised interval, often yearly. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Drug monitoring**: CBC, liver enzymes and creatinine every 2-4 weeks for the first 3 months of methotrexate, azathioprine, mycophenolate or leflunomide, then every 8-12 weeks. [ACR RA 2015]`,`**Refer** every suspected TAK or GCA to a rheumatologist (with cardiology or vascular surgery for intervention), RP with secondary features to rheumatology, Buerger disease to a vascular surgeon, and suspected VSA to cardiology for angiography and provocation testing. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Prevention**: tobacco cessation through the **National Tobacco Control Programme** - quitline **1800-112-356** and mCessation - is the core prevention for Buerger disease, VSA and secondary RP. [NTCP]`,`**NP-NCD link**: a young hypertensive picked up at an Ayushman Arogya Mandir or screening camp needs **pulse and four-limb BP examination** and referral for a secondary cause, not lifelong unexplained antihypertensives. [NP-NCD 2023]`,`**Counselling**: carry a steroid card, never stop steroids abruptly, use contraception on methotrexate, mycophenolate or leflunomide, avoid live vaccines on biologics, and report new pain, visual loss, weakness or limb colour change at once. [EULAR 2022]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ACR/EULAR 2022 TAK criteria** replace ACR 1990: the age limit rises from 40 to **60**, imaging evidence is mandatory, and a weighted score of **5 or more** classifies. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**ACR/EULAR 2022 GCA criteria**: age 50 or more and a weighted score of **6 or more**, with an ultrasound halo sign counted equal to a positive biopsy (+5). [ACR/EULAR 2022]`,`**EULAR 2022 LVV update** (published 2024): a non-glucocorticoid agent for **all TAK**; tocilizumab or TNF inhibitors for relapsing or refractory disease; prednisolone **5 mg/day or less after 1 year** (the 2018 version allowed 10 mg in TAK); no routine antiplatelets; interventions in remission. [EULAR 2022]`,`**EULAR 2023 imaging update**: MRI first in suspected TAK; ultrasound of **temporal and axillary arteries** first in suspected GCA; positive imaging with high clinical suspicion can confirm GCA without biopsy. [EULAR imaging 2023]`,`**ACR/Vasculitis Foundation 2021**: high-dose oral glucocorticoid over IV pulse in TAK, a non-glucocorticoid agent added, **TNF inhibitors preferred over tocilizumab** for refractory TAK, and regular non-invasive imaging. [ACR/VF 2021]`,`**Upadacitinib** (JAK1 inhibitor, 15 mg daily with a 26-week steroid taper) improved sustained remission in GCA in **SELECT-GCA (NEJM 2025)** and is US FDA-approved for GCA; JAK inhibitors are still under study in TAK. [SELECT-GCA 2025]`,`**ESC 2024 chronic coronary syndromes**: ANOCA/INOCA endotypes, **invasive coronary function testing** (acetylcholine) when symptoms persist with non-obstructive arteries, and CCBs as first-line therapy for VSA. [ESC 2024]`,`**EULAR 2023 systemic sclerosis update** (published 2025): dihydropyridine CCBs and **PDE5 inhibitors** first line for SSc-related Raynaud, IV iloprost for severe disease, and PDE5 inhibitors and bosentan for digital ulcers. [EULAR SSc 2023]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic viva**: why measure BP in all four limbs in a young hypertensive woman? Subclavian stenosis hides true pressure, and TAK is a treatable cause of hypertension. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Name the Numano types** and say which predominate in India (IV and V) - the commonest short-note angle. [Numano 1996]`,`**Common mistake**: labelling a young woman's hypertension essential without palpating the femoral pulses or listening for abdominal bruits. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: starting empirical antitubercular therapy for TAK, or doing angioplasty while ESR and imaging show active inflammation. [EULAR 2022]`,`**Eponyms**: Martorell sign (corkscrew collaterals, Buerger), macaroni sign (TAK carotid), halo sign (GCA temporal artery), Prinzmetal angina, Uyama retinopathy stages, Allen test. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Differentiate questions**: primary vs secondary Raynaud, Buerger vs atherosclerotic PAD, TAK vs GCA, VSA vs atherosclerotic angina - answer with a 6-8 row table. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug viva**: propranolol worsens Prinzmetal angina (unopposed alpha), CRP is useless on tocilizumab (IL-6 blockade), and nicotine itself can sustain Buerger disease. [ESC 2024]`,`**MCQ traps**: the TAK age limit is now 60 in the 2022 criteria; RP colours run white, blue, red; primary RP never causes gangrene; Buerger spares proximal arteries and involves veins. [ACR/EULAR 2022]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in Takayasu arteritis, Raynaud, Buerger disease and vasospastic angina`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Prednisolone`,`40-60 mg (0.5-1 mg/kg) oral once daily in the morning`,`Induction about 4 weeks; taper to 15-20 mg by 2-3 months and 5 mg or less by 1 year`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis, infection, cataract, adrenal suppression; never stop abruptly`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`500-1000 mg IV once daily`,`3 days for organ-threatening TAK or GCA with visual loss`,`Hyperglycaemia, hypertension, psychosis, arrhythmia with rapid infusion`],[`Methotrexate`,`10-15 mg once weekly oral/SC, up to 20-25 mg; folic acid 5 mg weekly`,`Increase 2.5-5 mg every 2-4 weeks; continue 1-2 years or more in remission`,`Cytopenia, hepatotoxicity, pneumonitis, mouth ulcers; teratogenic; avoid if eGFR under 30`],[`Azathioprine`,`2 mg/kg/day oral (start 50 mg)`,`Long-term maintenance`,`Marrow suppression (TPMT deficiency), hepatitis, pancreatitis; allopurinol interaction`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`1-1.5 g oral twice daily`,`Long-term maintenance`,`Diarrhoea, cytopenia, infection; teratogenic - stop 6 weeks before conception`],[`Leflunomide`,`20 mg oral once daily`,`Long-term maintenance`,`Hepatotoxicity, hypertension, diarrhoea; teratogenic - cholestyramine washout`],[`Tocilizumab`,`8 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks or 162 mg SC weekly`,`Relapsing or refractory TAK; steroid-sparing in GCA`,`Infection, neutropenia, raised lipids and liver enzymes, bowel perforation; masks CRP`],[`Infliximab / adalimumab`,`Infliximab 5 mg/kg IV at 0, 2, 6 weeks then every 4-8 weeks; adalimumab 40 mg SC every 2 weeks`,`Refractory TAK`,`TB reactivation (screen first), hepatitis B flare, infusion reactions; avoid in NYHA III-IV heart failure`],[`Nifedipine retard / amlodipine`,`Nifedipine 10-20 mg twice daily (ER 30-60 mg once daily); amlodipine 5-10 mg once daily`,`Winter or long term in RP; long term in VSA`,`Headache, flushing, ankle oedema, palpitations; never sublingual nifedipine`],[`Diltiazem SR`,`90-180 mg oral twice daily (max 360 mg/day)`,`Long term in VSA`,`Bradycardia, AV block, constipation; caution with beta-blockers and in HFrEF`],[`Sildenafil`,`20 mg oral three times daily`,`Second line in RP; digital ulcers`,`Headache, flushing, hypotension; contraindicated with nitrates or nicorandil`],[`Iloprost`,`0.5-2 ng/kg/min IV infusion over 6 hours daily`,`3-5 days in RP or critical digital ischaemia; up to 4 weeks in Buerger`,`Headache, flushing, hypotension, jaw pain, nausea`],[`Glyceryl trinitrate / isosorbide mononitrate`,`GTN 0.4-0.5 mg sublingual every 5 min (max 3); ISMN ER 30-60 mg once daily`,`Attacks; prophylaxis in VSA`,`Headache, hypotension, tolerance; no PDE5 inhibitor within 24-48 hours`],[`Varenicline`,`0.5 mg daily days 1-3, 0.5 mg twice daily days 4-7, then 1 mg twice daily`,`12 weeks (Buerger and every smoker)`,`Nausea, vivid dreams; halve dose if eGFR under 30`]]},{heading:`Numano angiographic classification of Takayasu arteritis (1996)`,columns:[`Type`,`Vessels involved`,`Typical clinical picture`],rows:[[`I`,`Branches of the aortic arch only`,`Pulseless arms, arm claudication, carotidynia, syncope, visual symptoms`],[`IIa`,`Ascending aorta, aortic arch and its branches`,`As type I plus aortic root dilatation and AR`],[`IIb`,`Type IIa plus descending thoracic aorta`,`Upper-limb ischaemia, AR, thoracic aortic narrowing`],[`III`,`Descending thoracic aorta, abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries`,`Atypical coarctation, renovascular hypertension, leg claudication`],[`IV`,`Abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries`,`Renovascular hypertension, abdominal bruit, mesenteric angina - common in India`],[`V`,`Combined features of IIb and IV (whole aorta)`,`Mixed picture; the commonest type in many Indian series`],[`C(+) / P(+)`,`Coronary or pulmonary artery involvement added to any type`,`Angina or MI; pulmonary hypertension, haemoptysis`]]},{heading:`ACR/EULAR 2022 classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis`,columns:[`Domain`,`Item`,`Points`],rows:[[`Absolute requirement`,`Age 60 years or less at diagnosis`,`Required`],[`Absolute requirement`,`Imaging evidence of vasculitis in the aorta or branch arteries`,`Required`],[`Clinical`,`Female sex`,`+1`],[`Clinical`,`Angina or ischaemic cardiac pain`,`+2`],[`Clinical`,`Arm or leg claudication`,`+2`],[`Clinical`,`Vascular bruit`,`+2`],[`Clinical`,`Reduced pulse in an upper extremity`,`+2`],[`Clinical`,`Reduced carotid pulse or carotid tenderness`,`+2`],[`Clinical`,`Inter-arm systolic BP difference of 20 mmHg or more`,`+1`],[`Imaging`,`Affected arterial territories: 1 / 2 / 3 or more`,`+1 / +2 / +3`],[`Imaging`,`Symmetric involvement of paired arteries`,`+1`],[`Imaging`,`Abdominal aorta plus renal or mesenteric involvement`,`+3`],[`Threshold`,`Total needed to classify as TAK`,`5 or more`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of large-vessel and vasospastic disorders`,columns:[`Disorder`,`Who`,`Vessels and pattern`,`Discriminating feature`],rows:[[`Takayasu arteritis`,`Women 10-40 years, Asia`,`Aorta, arch branches, renal and pulmonary arteries; long smooth stenoses`,`Pulse deficit, four-limb BP gap, raised ESR, wall thickening on MRA`],[`Giant cell arteritis`,`Over 50, Northern European`,`Temporal, ophthalmic, axillary arteries, aorta`,`Jaw claudication, visual loss, PMR, halo sign; rare in India`],[`Atherosclerosis`,`Older smoker, diabetic`,`Ostial, calcified, eccentric plaques`,`Risk factors, normal ESR, no wall enhancement`],[`Fibromuscular dysplasia`,`Women 20-50`,`Mid-distal renal, carotid, vertebral arteries`,`String of beads; no inflammation`],[`Congenital coarctation`,`Childhood onset, often male`,`Discrete juxtaductal aortic narrowing`,`Rib notching, bicuspid valve, radio-femoral delay`],[`Syphilitic aortitis`,`Middle age, untreated syphilis`,`Ascending aorta and aortic root`,`Aneurysm, AR, tree-bark calcification, positive TPHA`],[`Buerger disease`,`Men under 45 using tobacco`,`Distal small and medium arteries and veins`,`Instep claudication, migratory thrombophlebitis, corkscrew collaterals`],[`Vasospastic angina`,`Younger smokers, East Asians`,`Epicardial coronary spasm`,`Night rest angina, transient ST elevation, prompt nitrate response`]]},{heading:`Primary versus secondary Raynaud phenomenon`,columns:[`Feature`,`Primary Raynaud`,`Secondary Raynaud`],rows:[[`Age at onset`,`15-30 years`,`Usually over 30-40 years`],[`Sex`,`Mostly women`,`Either; a man raises suspicion`],[`Distribution`,`Symmetric, all fingers`,`Often asymmetric, one digit or one hand`],[`Severity`,`Mild, brief attacks`,`Severe, painful, prolonged`],[`Tissue damage`,`None`,`Digital ulcers, pitting scars, gangrene`],[`Nailfold capillaries`,`Normal`,`Giant loops, haemorrhages, dropout`],[`ANA and ESR`,`Negative ANA, normal ESR`,`ANA often positive (SSc antibodies); ESR may be raised`],[`Associated features`,`None; family history, migraine`,`Sclerodactyly, puffy fingers, rash, arthritis, dysphagia`],[`Treatment`,`Warmth, avoid cold; CCB if needed`,`Treat the cause; CCB, PDE5 inhibitor, iloprost, bosentan for ulcers`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Finding / cut-off`],rows:[[`ESR / CRP`,`All suspected vasculitis`,`Raised supports activity; normal does not exclude active TAK; GCA ESR usually over 50 mm/h`],[`Four-limb BP`,`Every young hypertensive and every TAK visit`,`Inter-arm gap of 20 mmHg or more; leg BP far above arm means bilateral subclavian disease`],[`Carotid/subclavian Doppler`,`First screen in suspected TAK`,`Concentric wall thickening (macaroni sign), stenosis, reversed vertebral flow`],[`Renal artery Doppler`,`Hypertension in TAK`,`PSV over 180-200 cm/s or renal-aortic ratio over 3.5 means stenosis of 60% or more`],[`MR angiography`,`First imaging in suspected TAK (EULAR 2023)`,`Wall thickening, oedema, enhancement, stenosis, aneurysm`],[`FDG PET-CT`,`Uncertain activity, PUO`,`Vessel-wall uptake greater than liver suggests active vasculitis`],[`Temporal and axillary ultrasound`,`Suspected GCA`,`Non-compressible halo sign`],[`Nailfold capillaroscopy`,`Every patient with RP`,`Giant capillaries, haemorrhages, dropout = scleroderma pattern`],[`Intracoronary acetylcholine test`,`Suspected VSA with non-obstructive arteries`,`90% or more constriction with angina and ischaemic ECG change`]]}],redFlags:[`New visual loss, amaurosis fugax or diplopia in suspected GCA or TAK - same-day high-dose steroid and referral.`,`TIA, stroke, or syncope on arm exercise with carotid or vertebral involvement.`,`Rest chest pain with ST elevation, or angina in a young woman with absent pulses (coronary ostial TAK) - treat as ACS until proven otherwise.`,`Hypertensive emergency, flash pulmonary oedema, or a creatinine rise over 30% after an ACE inhibitor or ARB (bilateral renal artery stenosis).`,`Critical digital ischaemia: pallor or cyanosis that persists after rewarming, a new digital ulcer or gangrene.`,`Sudden severe chest, back or abdominal pain in TAK - aneurysm rupture or dissection.`,`Post-prandial abdominal pain with weight loss (mesenteric ischaemia) or acute limb ischaemia.`,`Pregnancy in a woman with TAK and uncontrolled BP, heart failure or severe AR.`],pearls:[`**Pulseless disease** of young women: in every young hypertensive, feel both radials and a femoral together and measure BP in all four limbs.`,`Indian TAK is **abdominal aorta and renal arteries (Numano IV and V)** - renovascular hypertension is the commonest presentation.`,`**Reversed coarctation**: leg BP far above arm BP means bilateral subclavian stenosis, not leg hypertension.`,`**Macaroni sign** (concentric carotid wall thickening on ultrasound) means TAK; the temporal artery **halo sign** means GCA.`,`Treat the inflammation first and **revascularise only in remission** - angioplasty of an inflamed artery restenoses.`,`On **tocilizumab, CRP is always normal** - judge activity clinically and by imaging.`,`Raynaud colours **White-Blue-Red** = spasm, deoxygenation, reperfusion; tissue loss is never primary Raynaud.`,`**Buerger**: young male bidi smoker + instep claudication + migratory thrombophlebitis + corkscrew collaterals; the cure is stopping all tobacco.`,`**Prinzmetal angina**: midnight-to-dawn rest pain with transient ST elevation - CCB yes, propranolol no.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-q1`,stem:`A 22-year-old woman with Takayasu arteritis has CT angiography showing involvement of the abdominal aorta and both renal arteries, with a normal aortic arch, arch branches and thoracic aorta. Her Numano angiographic type is:`,options:[`Type I`,`Type IIb`,`Type IV`,`Type V`],answer:2,explanation:`Numano type IV is confined to the abdominal aorta and/or renal arteries - the pattern behind renovascular hypertension in many Indian patients. Type I involves only the arch branches, type IIb adds the descending thoracic aorta to the ascending aorta and arch, and type V combines IIb and IV (the whole aorta), which needs thoracic disease that is absent here.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-q2`,stem:`A 28-year-old woman with Takayasu arteritis has BP 90/60 mmHg in the left arm and 105/70 mmHg in the right arm. Doppler shows stenosis of both subclavian arteries; femoral pulses are bounding and the fundus shows arteriovenous nipping. Which measurement best reflects her true aortic pressure?`,options:[`The right-arm reading, being the higher of the two arms`,`Thigh-cuff BP in a leg with normal femoral pulses`,`The mean of the two arm readings`,`Wrist BP measured with an automated device`],answer:1,explanation:`Bilateral subclavian stenosis lowers pressure in both arms, so arm readings underestimate aortic pressure and hide hypertension, which the fundus already shows. With an unaffected aorto-iliac segment, leg BP reflects central pressure; if all four limbs are involved, central aortic pressure is measured at angiography. Taking the higher arm, averaging two falsely low arm readings or measuring further downstream at the wrist does not correct the error.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-q3`,stem:`A 25-year-old woman has newly diagnosed active Takayasu arteritis (fever, ESR 80 mm/h, carotidynia, wall enhancement on MRA) with an 80% left renal artery stenosis and BP 160/100 mmHg on amlodipine. What is the most appropriate next step?`,options:[`Prednisolone 40-60 mg/day with methotrexate, deferring revascularisation until stable remission`,`Immediate renal artery stenting before starting immunosuppression`,`Empirical antitubercular therapy for 6 months`,`Aspirin and a statin alone, as for atherosclerotic renal artery stenosis`],answer:0,explanation:`EULAR 2022 recommends high-dose glucocorticoid (40-60 mg/day prednisolone equivalent) combined with a non-glucocorticoid agent such as methotrexate for every patient with active TAK, and elective vascular procedures only in stable remission because angioplasty or stenting of an inflamed artery restenoses early. Antitubercular therapy does not treat TAK without proven TB, and aspirin with a statin does nothing for the vasculitis.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-q4`,stem:`A 34-year-old woman reports cold-induced white, then blue fingers for 1 year. Which finding most strongly suggests secondary rather than primary Raynaud phenomenon?`,options:[`Symmetric involvement of both hands`,`Attacks provoked by emotional stress`,`Complete return of normal colour on rewarming`,`Giant nailfold capillary loops with areas of capillary dropout`],answer:3,explanation:`Giant capillaries, haemorrhages and capillary loss on nailfold capillaroscopy mark the microangiopathy of systemic sclerosis and related connective tissue disease, and with a positive ANA are the strongest predictors of secondary disease. Symmetric attacks, emotional triggers and full recovery on rewarming are all typical of primary Raynaud phenomenon, which by definition has normal capillaries, a negative ANA and no tissue loss.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-q5`,stem:`A 42-year-old smoker has recurrent chest pain between 2 and 5 am lasting about 10 minutes, with transient inferior ST elevation on Holter monitoring that settles with sublingual nitrate. Coronary angiography shows no obstructive disease. Which drug should be avoided?`,options:[`Diltiazem sustained-release`,`Isosorbide mononitrate`,`Propranolol`,`Amlodipine`],answer:2,explanation:`Non-selective beta-blockers such as propranolol leave alpha-adrenergic coronary vasoconstriction unopposed and can worsen vasospastic angina. Calcium channel blockers (diltiazem, amlodipine, nifedipine) are first line in ESC 2024 because they relax coronary smooth muscle and prevent attacks, and long-acting nitrates such as isosorbide mononitrate are added when a CCB alone is not enough.`,difficulty:`moderate`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Describe the aetiopathogenesis, clinical features, classification and management of Takayasu arteritis. Add a note on how you would measure blood pressure in such a patient.`,openingLines:[`Takayasu arteritis is a chronic granulomatous large-vessel vasculitis of the aorta, its major branches and the pulmonary arteries, typically in women under 40, producing stenosis, occlusion and aneurysm.`,`It is commonest in Asia; Indian patients often have abdominal aortic and renal artery disease (Numano IV and V) presenting as renovascular hypertension.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`Large-vessel vasculitis (Chapel Hill 2012); 80-90% women, onset 10-40 years; incidence 1-3 per million.`,`Commonest in Japan, India, Korea, China; India - Numano IV/V, hypertension leading presentation.`]},{heading:`Aetiopathogenesis`,points:[`Unknown cause; HLA-B52, IL12B; TB over-represented but not causal.`,`Adventitial vasa vasorum inflammation, dendritic cells, Th1 and Th17 cells, granulomatous panarteritis with giant cells.`,`IL-6 drives acute phase (target of tocilizumab), TNF maintains granulomas (target of TNF inhibitors).`,`Intimal hyperplasia and fibrosis cause stenosis; medial destruction causes aneurysm and AR.`,`Hypertension from renal artery stenosis, middle aortic syndrome and a stiff aorta.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Pre-pulseless phase: fever, weight loss, arthralgia, PUO.`,`Vascular phase: arm claudication, carotidynia, subclavian steal syncope, visual blur, TIA, stroke, angina, abdominal angina, haemoptysis.`,`Signs: absent pulses, inter-arm BP difference, bruits (carotid, subclavian, renal), AR murmur, Takayasu or hypertensive retinopathy.`]},{heading:`Measuring BP in TAK`,points:[`Measure BP in all four limbs; use the highest reliable reading.`,`Bilateral subclavian stenosis makes arm BP falsely low (reversed coarctation) - use leg BP.`,`If all four limbs are involved, measure central aortic pressure at angiography.`]},{heading:`Classification and criteria`,points:[`Numano types I, IIa, IIb, III, IV, V with C(+) and P(+).`,`ACR/EULAR 2022: age 60 or less plus imaging evidence; score 5 or more from clinical and imaging items.`,`Activity: NIH (Kerr) criteria, ITAS2010.`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`CBC, ESR/CRP (may be normal), creatinine, urine, ECG, echo.`,`Doppler (macaroni sign, renal artery velocity), MRA first line (EULAR 2023), CTA, PET-CT for activity.`,`Screen for TB, hepatitis B and C, HIV before immunosuppression.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Prednisolone 40-60 mg/day, taper to 15-20 mg by 2-3 months and 5 mg or less by 1 year.`,`Methotrexate 15-25 mg weekly with folic acid from the start; azathioprine, MMF or leflunomide as alternatives.`,`Refractory: tocilizumab 8 mg/kg IV 4-weekly or TNF inhibitor (infliximab, adalimumab).`,`BP control (care with ACE inhibitors in bilateral RAS); aspirin only for critical cranial or coronary disease.`,`Angioplasty or bypass electively in remission; bone protection, vaccines, TB preventive therapy.`]},{heading:`Complications and prognosis`,points:[`Renovascular hypertension, CKD, stroke, AR, heart failure, aneurysm, pulmonary hypertension.`,`15-year survival about 96% without and 66% with a major complication (Ishikawa).`]}],mustDraw:[`Diagram of the aorta and its branches marking Numano types I-V`,`Treatment flow chart: glucocorticoid plus methotrexate, then biologic, then intervention in remission`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and epidemiology`,marks:1},{part:`Aetiopathogenesis`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features and BP measurement`,marks:2},{part:`Classification and criteria`,marks:1.5},{part:`Investigations`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management`,marks:2.5}],keywords:[`Takayasu arteritis`,`Numano`,`ACR/EULAR 2022`,`four-limb BP`,`methotrexate`,`tocilizumab`,`renovascular hypertension`]},{id:`cardiovascular-takayasu-vasculitis-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`differentiate`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Differentiate primary and secondary Raynaud phenomenon. Outline the management.`,openingLines:[`Raynaud phenomenon is episodic, reversible vasospasm of digital arteries on exposure to cold or emotion, producing sharply demarcated white, blue and red colour changes.`,`Primary Raynaud has no underlying disease; secondary Raynaud is a marker of connective tissue disease (over 90% of systemic sclerosis), vascular disease, occupation or drugs.`],answer:[{heading:`Colour phases and mechanism`,points:[`White: digital artery spasm; blue: deoxygenation; red: reactive hyperaemia.`,`Exaggerated alpha-2C adrenergic response in primary; structural and endothelial disease in secondary.`]},{heading:`Differences`,points:[`Onset 15-30 vs over 30-40; symmetric vs asymmetric; mild vs severe and painful.`,`No tissue loss vs ulcers, pitting scars, gangrene.`,`Normal vs abnormal nailfold capillaries (giant loops, dropout).`,`Negative ANA and normal ESR vs positive ANA, SSc antibodies, raised ESR.`]},{heading:`Work-up`,points:[`CBC, ESR, ANA, anti-centromere, anti-Scl-70, TSH, nailfold capillaroscopy.`,`Unilateral: Allen test, cervical rib X-ray, arterial Doppler.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Whole-body warmth, gloves, stop smoking and provoking drugs.`,`Nifedipine retard 10-20 mg twice daily or amlodipine 5-10 mg daily.`,`Next: sildenafil 20 mg three times daily, topical nitrate; severe: IV iloprost; bosentan for recurrent SSc ulcers.`,`Critical digital ischaemia: admit, iloprost, analgesia, antibiotics for infected ulcers.`]}],mustDraw:[`Table: primary vs secondary Raynaud (age, symmetry, tissue loss, capillaroscopy, ANA)`,`Triphasic colour change: white, blue, red with the mechanism of each`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and phases`,marks:.5},{part:`Differences table`,marks:2},{part:`Work-up`,marks:1},{part:`Management`,marks:1.5}],keywords:[`Raynaud phenomenon`,`LeRoy-Medsger`,`nailfold capillaroscopy`,`systemic sclerosis`,`nifedipine`,`iloprost`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - vasculitis syndromes, Raynaud phenomenon and peripheral arterial disease`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - peripheral artery disease, aortic disease and vasospastic angina`,`Grayson PC et al. 2022 ACR/EULAR classification criteria for Takayasu arteritis (and Ponte C et al. for giant cell arteritis), Ann Rheum Dis 2022`,`Hellmich B et al. 2022 update of the EULAR recommendations for the management of large vessel vasculitis, Ann Rheum Dis 2024; Dejaco C et al. EULAR recommendations for imaging in large vessel vasculitis: 2023 update, Ann Rheum Dis 2024`,`Maz M et al. 2021 American College of Rheumatology/Vasculitis Foundation guideline for the management of giant cell arteritis and Takayasu arteritis, Arthritis Rheumatol 2021`,`Vrints C et al. 2024 ESC Guidelines for the management of chronic coronary syndromes; Beltrame JF et al. COVADIS diagnostic criteria for vasospastic angina, Eur Heart J 2017`,`Del Galdo F et al. EULAR recommendations for the treatment of systemic sclerosis: 2023 update, Ann Rheum Dis 2025`,`Olin JW. Thromboangiitis obliterans (Buerger's disease), N Engl J Med 2000; Misra R et al. Indian Takayasu Clinical Activity Score (ITAS2010), Rheumatology 2013`]}),d.push({id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special`,title:`Familial hypercholesterolaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia and therapy beyond statins`,oneLiner:`**Familial hypercholesterolaemia** is an autosomal codominant defect of LDL clearance (LDLR, APOB, PCSK9) affecting about **1 in 250**, suspected at an **LDL of 190 mg/dL or more**, scored by the **Dutch Lipid Clinic Network** criteria and found in relatives by **cascade screening**; when a statin is not enough or not tolerated the ladder adds **ezetimibe, bempedoic acid, PCSK9 antibodies or inclisiran**, while **triglycerides of 500 mg/dL or more** (pancreatitis risk above 880-1000) need fibrates, fat restriction and treatment of secondary causes, and **Lp(a)** is measured once in every adult.`,frequency:`common`,keywords:[`familial hypercholesterolaemia`,`FH`,`Dutch Lipid Clinic Network`,`cascade screening`,`ezetimibe`,`PCSK9 inhibitor`,`evolocumab`,`alirocumab`,`inclisiran`,`bempedoic acid`,`CLEAR Outcomes`,`icosapent ethyl`,`fenofibrate`,`saroglitazar`,`hypertriglyceridaemia`,`chylomicronaemia`,`lipoprotein(a)`,`statin intolerance`,`LAI`,`CSI 2024`],sections:[{heading:`Definition and epidemiology`,points:[`**Familial hypercholesterolaemia (FH)** is an inherited disorder of LDL receptor-mediated clearance causing lifelong high LDL cholesterol, tendon xanthomas and **premature coronary disease**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heterozygous FH (HeFH)** affects about **1 in 250-300** people worldwide; **homozygous FH (HoFH)** about **1 in 300,000**, and it is commoner in founder and consanguineous populations. [EAS 2023]`,`Fewer than **1 in 10** people with FH are diagnosed in most countries, and far fewer in India - most are first identified after a myocardial infarction in their 30s or 40s. [EAS 2023]`,`Untreated HeFH carries roughly a **10-20-fold** higher coronary risk; men typically have events before 55 and women before 60, and HoFH causes coronary and aortic valve disease in childhood. [Braunwald 12e]`,`**ICMR-INDIAB** found at least one lipid abnormality in about **81% of Indian adults** - low HDL commonest, **hypertriglyceridaemia in about one-third** and high LDL in about one-fifth - the atherogenic pattern of South Asians. [ICMR-INDIAB 2023]`,`**Hypertriglyceridaemia** is fasting triglyceride (TG) **150 mg/dL or more**; **severe** is **500 mg/dL or more**, and TG over **880-1000 mg/dL** carries a real risk of **acute pancreatitis**. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`TG-induced pancreatitis is the **third commonest cause** of acute pancreatitis after gallstones and alcohol, and is over-represented in pregnancy and uncontrolled diabetes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lipoprotein(a)** is an LDL-like particle carrying apolipoprotein(a); about **20%** of people have a level of **50 mg/dL (about 105-125 nmol/L) or more**, an inherited, causal risk factor for MI, stroke and aortic stenosis. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`,`This topic covers the special lipid disorders and non-statin drugs; risk categories and statin choice are in the Dyslipidaemia topic. [LAI 2023]`]},{heading:`Aetiology and genetics`,points:[`FH is **autosomal codominant**: pathogenic variants in **LDLR** (most cases), **APOB** (defective ligand) or gain-of-function **PCSK9**; one copy gives HeFH, two give HoFH. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Autosomal recessive hypercholesterolaemia** (LDLRAP1) mimics HoFH; **sitosterolaemia** (ABCG5/8) causes xanthomas with modest LDL and responds to ezetimibe. [Harrison 22e]`,`Many patients with LDL over 190 mg/dL have **polygenic hypercholesterolaemia** (many small-effect LDL-raising alleles) rather than monogenic FH - the family pattern and the absence of xanthomas help. [EAS 2023]`,`**Familial combined hyperlipidaemia** (raised apoB with variable LDL and TG in family members) is the commonest genetic dyslipidaemia in premature CAD and common in South Asians. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dysbetalipoproteinaemia (type III)** needs **APOE2/E2** homozygosity plus a second hit (diabetes, obesity, hypothyroidism) - remnant accumulation with roughly equal raised cholesterol and TG. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial chylomicronaemia syndrome (FCS)** is rare monogenic loss of lipoprotein lipase activity (**LPL, APOC2, APOA5, GPIHBP1, LMF1**); far commoner is **multifactorial chylomicronaemia** - polygenic susceptibility plus a secondary cause. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Secondary causes of high TG**: uncontrolled diabetes, obesity, alcohol, hypothyroidism, CKD and nephrotic syndrome, pregnancy, and drugs - oral oestrogens, tamoxifen, corticosteroids, retinoids, thiazides, non-selective beta-blockers, protease inhibitors, atypical antipsychotics and ciclosporin. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Secondary causes of high LDL**: hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, cholestasis, anorexia nervosa, and drugs (ciclosporin, steroids, some progestins) - exclude these before labelling FH. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Lp(a)** level is about **90% genetically determined** by the LPA gene (number of kringle IV type 2 repeats sets apo(a) size), so it changes little with diet or exercise. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology and drug mechanisms`,points:[`Hepatic **LDL receptors** bind apoB-100 on LDL, internalise it and recycle to the surface about 150 times; **PCSK9** binds the receptor and sends it to the lysosome instead - fewer receptors, higher LDL. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`In FH defective receptors (or apoB ligand) double LDL from birth, so the **cumulative LDL burden** (LDL-years) reaches the threshold for coronary disease decades early - the reason to treat children. [EAS 2023]`,`**Statins** inhibit HMG-CoA reductase; the fall in intracellular cholesterol activates SREBP-2, which raises both LDL receptors and PCSK9 - one reason doubling the statin dose adds only about 6% more LDL lowering. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Ezetimibe** blocks the intestinal **NPC1L1** sterol transporter, cutting cholesterol absorption and lowering LDL a further **20-25%** on top of a statin. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies** (evolocumab, alirocumab) neutralise circulating PCSK9 and lower LDL about **60%**; **inclisiran**, a GalNAc-conjugated siRNA, silences PCSK9 synthesis in hepatocytes and lowers LDL about **50%** with twice-yearly injection. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Bempedoic acid** is a prodrug activated by very-long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase-1, present in liver but **not skeletal muscle**; it inhibits **ATP citrate lyase** upstream of HMG-CoA reductase and lowers LDL about **20%** - the reason it suits statin-intolerant patients. [CLEAR Outcomes 2023]`,`**Lipoprotein lipase** on capillary endothelium (anchored by GPIHBP1, activated by apoC-II, inhibited by **apoC-III and ANGPTL3**) hydrolyses TG in chylomicrons and VLDL; failure lets chylomicrons accumulate. [Harrison 22e]`,`Chylomicrons over 1000 mg/dL plug pancreatic capillaries; pancreatic lipase releases **free fatty acids** that injure acinar cells and the microcirculation - the mechanism of TG pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Fibrates** activate PPAR-alpha, raising LPL and lowering apoC-III (TG down **30-50%**); **insulin** activates LPL, which is why an insulin infusion clears TG in pancreatitis. [Goodman and Gilman 14e]`,`**Lp(a)** is atherogenic (LDL-like), pro-inflammatory (oxidised phospholipids) and antifibrinolytic (apo(a) resembles plasminogen); statins do not lower it and may raise it slightly. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`]},{heading:`Classification and diagnostic criteria`,points:[`**Dutch Lipid Clinic Network (DLCN) score**: family history, personal premature CAD or vascular disease, **tendon xanthoma (6)**, corneal arcus before 45 (4), LDL level (**330 mg/dL or more = 8**; 250-329 = 5; 190-249 = 3; 155-189 = 1) and a pathogenic variant (8). [DLCN]`,`DLCN totals: **more than 8 definite FH**, 6-8 probable, 3-5 possible, under 3 unlikely; it applies to adults not on treatment (estimate untreated LDL if on a statin). [DLCN]`,`**Simon Broome criteria**: total cholesterol over 290 mg/dL or LDL over 190 mg/dL in an adult, plus tendon xanthoma in the patient or relative (definite) or family history of premature MI or high cholesterol (possible). [Simon Broome]`,`**HoFH** is suspected with untreated LDL over **400 mg/dL** (or 300 or more on treatment), cutaneous or tendon xanthomas before age 10, and both parents with FH. [EAS 2023]`,`**Fredrickson phenotypes**: I chylomicrons, IIa LDL, IIb LDL plus VLDL, III remnants (IDL), IV VLDL, V chylomicrons plus VLDL - types I and V cause pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**TG severity**: normal under 150 mg/dL; mild-moderate 150-499; severe 500 or more; very severe (chylomicronaemia, high pancreatitis risk) over 880-1000 mg/dL. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Statin intolerance** (NLA 2022, ILEP): adverse effects on at least **two statins**, one at the lowest starting dose, resolving on stopping; **partial** if some dose is tolerated, **complete** if none. [NLA 2022]`,`**Statin muscle syndromes**: myalgia with normal CK (commonest, often nocebo), myopathy with CK over 4 times normal, **rhabdomyolysis** with CK over 10 times normal and renal injury, and rare anti-HMGCR **immune-mediated necrotising myopathy**. [ILEP 2022]`,`**Lp(a) thresholds**: under 30 mg/dL low risk, **50 mg/dL or more risk-enhancing**, and over **180 mg/dL (about 430 nmol/L)** carries a lifetime risk similar to HeFH. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`]},{heading:`Clinical features - history`,points:[`Ask about **personal premature ASCVD** - MI, stent or stroke in a man under 55 or a woman under 60 - the commonest way FH presents in India. [EAS 2023]`,`Take a **three-generation family history**: premature MI or sudden death, known high cholesterol, children with high LDL, and consanguinity (HoFH). [EAS 2023]`,`Ask about **lumps on tendons or eyelids**, painful Achilles tendons (Achilles tendinitis can be the first sign) and a white ring around the cornea at a young age. [Harrison 22e]`,`**HoFH** presents in childhood with yellow skin xanthomas over knuckles, elbows, knees and buttocks, exertional angina, syncope (supravalvular aortic stenosis) or sudden death. [EAS 2023]`,`**Severe HTG** is often silent until **pancreatitis** (epigastric pain radiating to the back, vomiting), but ask about recurrent abdominal pain, crops of itchy yellow papules, memory lapses and dyspnoea (chylomicronaemia syndrome). [Harrison 22e]`,`Screen for **secondary causes**: alcohol, diabetes control, weight gain, cold intolerance (hypothyroidism), frothy urine (nephrotic), pruritus with jaundice (cholestasis), pregnancy and the full drug list. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Statin intolerance history**: symmetric proximal aching or weakness starting within weeks of a statin or dose rise, resolving within 2-4 weeks of stopping and recurring on rechallenge; one-sided, joint or long-standing pain is unlikely to be the statin. [ILEP 2022]`,`Ask about **drugs interacting with statins** (clarithromycin, azoles, ciclosporin, gemfibrozil, diltiazem, verapamil, amiodarone, colchicine, protease inhibitors), heavy exercise, hypothyroidism and low vitamin D. [ILEP 2022]`]},{heading:`Examination findings`,points:[`**Tendon xanthomas** - firm nodules on the **Achilles tendons** and the extensor tendons of the knuckles - are virtually pathognomonic of FH (or rare sitosterolaemia, cerebrotendinous xanthomatosis). [Harrison 22e]`,`**Corneal arcus before 45** suggests FH; after 45 it is non-specific (arcus senilis). [DLCN]`,`**Xanthelasma** (eyelid plaques) is non-specific - half of patients have normal lipids - but warrants a lipid profile. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Planar xanthomas** in finger webs and over joints from childhood suggest **HoFH**; **palmar crease xanthomas** (xanthoma striata palmaris) and **tuberoeruptive xanthomas** suggest **dysbetalipoproteinaemia**. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Eruptive xanthomas** - crops of small yellow papules with a red halo on the buttocks, elbows and knees - mean **TG usually over 1000 mg/dL**; they vanish within weeks of lowering TG. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Lipaemia retinalis** - creamy-white retinal vessels on fundoscopy - and **hepatosplenomegaly** accompany chylomicronaemia. [Harrison 22e]`,`Look for **ASCVD**: carotid and femoral bruits, absent pedal pulses, an ejection systolic murmur (supravalvular or valvular aortic stenosis in HoFH and high Lp(a)). [EAS 2023]`,`Look for **secondary causes**: BMI and waist, goitre or slow-relaxing reflexes, oedema (nephrotic), jaundice, acanthosis nigricans and features of Cushing syndrome. [ESC/EAS 2025]`]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis`,points:[`**FH versus polygenic hypercholesterolaemia**: both may reach 190-250 mg/dL, but FH has tendon xanthomas, LDL usually over 250, a vertical (dominant) family pattern and premature CAD in relatives. [EAS 2023]`,`**Hypothyroidism** raises LDL that falls once thyroxine is replaced - always check **TSH** before diagnosing FH or blaming statin myalgia. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Nephrotic syndrome** raises LDL and TG with oedema and proteinuria; **cholestasis** raises lipoprotein X (falsely high LDL, no ASCVD risk) - check urine and liver tests. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Familial combined hyperlipidaemia**: fluctuating phenotype (IIa, IIb or IV) in different relatives, high apoB, no tendon xanthomas. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Dysbetalipoproteinaemia**: total cholesterol and TG both about 300-400 mg/dL, palmar crease xanthomas, apoE2/E2 genotype, and good response to fibrates and statins. [Harrison 22e]`,`**FCS versus multifactorial chylomicronaemia**: FCS starts in childhood, TG stays over 880 mg/dL despite treatment, BMI is normal and secondary causes absent; multifactorial cases are adults with diabetes, obesity or alcohol. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Pancreatitis with milky serum**: amylase may be **falsely normal** in lipaemic serum and sodium falsely low (**pseudohyponatraemia**) - ask the lab to clear the sample or use lipase. [Harrison 22e]`,`**True statin myopathy versus other causes**: hypothyroidism, vitamin D deficiency, polymyalgia rheumatica, exertional injury, inflammatory myositis and anti-HMGCR myopathy, which persists after the statin is stopped. [ILEP 2022]`]},{heading:`Investigations`,points:[`**Lipid profile**: non-fasting is acceptable for screening; repeat fasting if TG over 400 mg/dL; calculate **non-HDL cholesterol** (total minus HDL) every time. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Friedewald LDL** is invalid when TG is over 400 mg/dL and inaccurate at low LDL - use the **Martin-Hopkins or Sampson (NIH) equation** or direct LDL, common issues in Indians with high TG. [LAI 2023]`,`**Apolipoprotein B** reflects the number of atherogenic particles and is the better marker when TG is high, in diabetes and obesity; 130 mg/dL or more is risk-enhancing. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Lp(a) once in every adult** (and in FH, premature ASCVD and family history of high Lp(a)), ideally in nmol/L with an isoform-insensitive assay. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`,`**Secondary-cause screen**: TSH, fasting glucose and HbA1c, creatinine and eGFR, urine protein, liver function, and pregnancy test where relevant. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Genetic testing** for LDLR, APOB and PCSK9 confirms FH, strengthens cascade screening and motivates families; it is available in a few Indian centres but a negative test does not exclude clinical FH. [EAS 2023]`,`**Before non-statin drugs**: baseline ALT, CK if muscle symptoms, uric acid before bempedoic acid, and creatinine before fenofibrate (it raises creatinine reversibly). [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Subclinical atherosclerosis**: coronary artery calcium score or carotid/femoral plaque on ultrasound refines risk in FH and borderline cases; echocardiography for aortic valve disease in HoFH and very high Lp(a). [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**In TG pancreatitis**: lipase, TG, calcium, glucose, blood gas and lactate, and CT after 72 hours if the diagnosis or severity is uncertain. [Harrison 22e]`]},{heading:`Management in OPD: the non-statin ladder`,points:[`**Targets (LAI 2023)**: LDL under 70 mg/dL in FH without ASCVD (high risk), **under 50** with ASCVD or very high risk, and **30 or less** in extreme-risk B; non-HDL target is the LDL target plus 30 mg/dL - see the Dyslipidaemia topic. [LAI 2023]`,`**CSI 2024** sets **under 55 mg/dL with at least 50% reduction** for very high risk and under 70 for high risk, close to ESC; LAI is stricter - either is acceptable if applied consistently. [CSI 2024]`,`**Step 1**: high-intensity statin - **rosuvastatin 20-40 mg** or **atorvastatin 40-80 mg** daily at any time - plus diet, exercise and no tobacco; expect a 50% fall. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Step 2**: add **ezetimibe 10 mg once daily** (fixed-dose combinations are cheap in India); start statin plus ezetimibe together when baseline LDL is far above target, as in most FH. [CSI 2024]`,`**Step 3**: add a **PCSK9 antibody** - **evolocumab 140 mg SC every 2 weeks or 420 mg monthly**, or **alirocumab 75-150 mg SC every 2 weeks** - or **inclisiran 284 mg SC** at day 0, 3 months and then every 6 months, given by a health worker. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Bempedoic acid 180 mg once daily** (alone or with ezetimibe 10 mg as a single pill) lowers LDL a further 20-25% (about 38% with ezetimibe) and is the oral option when PCSK9 agents are unaffordable or statins are not tolerated. [CLEAR Outcomes 2023]`,`**Outcome evidence**: ezetimibe (IMPROVE-IT), evolocumab (FOURIER), alirocumab (ODYSSEY OUTCOMES) and bempedoic acid (CLEAR Outcomes) all reduce events in proportion to LDL lowering; inclisiran outcome trials are ongoing. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**HoFH**: maximal statin plus ezetimibe plus PCSK9 antibody (works only with residual receptor function), then **evinacumab** (ANGPTL3 antibody, 15 mg/kg IV monthly), **lomitapide** or **lipoprotein apheresis** weekly or fortnightly at a specialist centre. [EAS 2023]`,`**Mild-moderate HTG (150-499 mg/dL)**: treat secondary causes, alcohol abstinence, weight loss, fewer refined carbohydrates and sugary drinks, statin for risk; in ASCVD or diabetes with TG 135-499 on a statin, **icosapent ethyl 2 g twice daily** reduces events (REDUCE-IT). [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Mixed fish-oil (EPA plus DHA) capsules do not reduce events** (STRENGTH) and **fibrates added to a statin do not** (ACCORD, PROMINENT); **niacin** is obsolete (AIM-HIGH, HPS2-THRIVE). [ESC/EAS 2025]`]},{heading:`Severe hypertriglyceridaemia and pancreatitis: inpatient care`,points:[`**Admit** TG-induced pancreatitis, TG over 2000 mg/dL with abdominal pain, and chylomicronaemia in pregnancy. [Harrison 22e]`,`Standard pancreatitis care - **IV Ringer's lactate** (goal-directed, avoid over-hydration), analgesia, early oral or enteral feeding once pain settles - with **zero fat** for the first 24-48 hours. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Insulin infusion 0.1-0.3 units/kg/hour** with 5-10% dextrose (even in non-diabetics) activates LPL and lowers TG by about half in 24-48 hours; check glucose hourly and potassium 4-6 hourly. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Plasmapheresis** lowers TG rapidly in very high TG with worsening organ failure, hypocalcaemia, lactic acidosis or pregnancy, but outcome benefit is unproven - use where available, never instead of insulin and fat restriction. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Heparin** is no longer advised: it releases LPL briefly then depletes it, and risks haemorrhagic pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`Target **TG under 500 mg/dL** before discharge, then start **fenofibrate 145-160 mg daily** (micronised 200 mg), control diabetes, stop alcohol for life and withdraw offending drugs. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Diet for severe HTG**: fat under 15-20% of calories (under 20 g/day in FCS), **no alcohol**, no sugar-sweetened drinks, **medium-chain triglyceride oil** for cooking (absorbed into the portal vein, not as chylomicrons). [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**FCS** responds poorly to fibrates; the **APOC3** antisense drug **olezarsen** (FDA-approved 2024) and volanesorsen (EU) cut TG and pancreatitis episodes; refer to a lipid clinic. [ESC/EAS 2025]`]},{heading:`Lipoprotein(a)`,points:[`Measure **Lp(a) once in a lifetime** in every adult (ESC/EAS 2025, CSI 2024), and in children with FH or a family history of high Lp(a) or premature ASCVD. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**50 mg/dL or more** upgrades risk and favours earlier, more intensive LDL lowering; over **180 mg/dL** behaves like HeFH and justifies cascade testing of relatives. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`,`South Asians have relatively **high Lp(a)**, and it partly explains premature CAD in Indians with otherwise modest LDL. [LAI 2023]`,`There is **no approved Lp(a)-specific drug**: lower LDL aggressively, control all other risk factors, and consider aspirin only per the usual indications. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`,`**PCSK9 antibodies** lower Lp(a) by about 25-30% and inclisiran by about 20%; statins do not lower it and **niacin** is not recommended. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Lipoprotein apheresis** lowers Lp(a) acutely and is used for progressive ASCVD with very high Lp(a) in some countries. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`,`Antisense **pelacarsen** and siRNAs **olpasiran, lepodisiran and zerlasiran** lower Lp(a) by 80-95%; their outcome trials will show whether lowering Lp(a) itself prevents events. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`Lp(a) is **not affected by fasting** and varies little over time, so one reliable value is enough unless a new drug is being considered. [EAS Lp(a) 2022]`]},{heading:`Statin intolerance`,points:[`Most muscle complaints on statins are **not caused by the statin**: in blinded trials muscle symptoms are almost equally common on placebo (SAMSON, StatinWISE; CTT 2022). [CTT 2022]`,`**Nocebo effect**: expectation causes the symptom - explain this, and show the patient that the benefit of LDL lowering far outweighs a small excess of mild aches. [ILEP 2022]`,`**Check CK** only with symptoms: under 4 times normal - continue or reduce dose; **over 4 times** - stop and recheck in 2-6 weeks; **over 10 times** - stop, check creatinine and urine myoglobin for rhabdomyolysis. [ILEP 2022]`,`**Correct contributors**: hypothyroidism, drug interactions, heavy alcohol, vitamin D deficiency, and dehydration; then **rechallenge** after a 2-4 week washout. [ILEP 2022]`,`**Rechallenge strategies**: a different statin at low dose (rosuvastatin 5 mg, pitavastatin 1-2 mg, pravastatin), or **alternate-day or twice-weekly rosuvastatin or atorvastatin** (long half-life). [NLA 2022]`,`Add non-statin therapy to whatever statin dose is tolerated: **ezetimibe**, then **bempedoic acid** (CLEAR Outcomes, 13% fewer major events in statin-intolerant patients) or a **PCSK9 agent**. [CLEAR Outcomes 2023]`,`**Liver**: an ALT rise under 3 times normal is not a reason to stop; statins are safe in fatty liver disease. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Anti-HMGCR immune-mediated necrotising myopathy** - proximal weakness and high CK that persist after stopping - needs a rheumatologist and immunosuppression, and statins must never be restarted. [ILEP 2022]`]},{heading:`Special situations`,points:[`**Children with HeFH**: screen from age 5 (earlier if HoFH suspected); start a statin at **8-10 years** with diet, aiming for LDL **under 135 mg/dL** (or 50% reduction) after age 10. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Pregnancy**: stop statins, ezetimibe, PCSK9 agents and bempedoic acid **1-3 months before conception** or on confirmation; bile acid sequestrants are the safe option, and apheresis is used for HoFH. [EAS 2023]`,`**Pregnancy and TG**: TG rises 2-3-fold in the third trimester and can trigger pancreatitis in predisposed women - very low-fat diet, MCT oil, omega-3, insulin for diabetes, and plasmapheresis if needed. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Diabetes**: atherogenic dyslipidaemia (high TG, low HDL, small dense LDL); statin first, then ezetimibe; **saroglitazar 4 mg daily** (Indian dual PPAR alpha/gamma agonist) lowers TG but lacks outcome data. [LAI 2023]`,`**CKD**: ezetimibe and PCSK9 agents need no dose change; avoid fenofibrate when eGFR is under 30; bempedoic acid is not studied in severe CKD; never combine **gemfibrozil with a statin**. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Elderly**: continue non-statin therapy in those with ASCVD; start cautiously in the frail and those with limited life expectancy, prioritising interactions and pill burden. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**HIV, transplant and antipsychotics**: drug-induced HTG and statin interactions (protease inhibitors, ciclosporin) favour pitavastatin, rosuvastatin at low dose or ezetimibe. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Resource-limited setting**: statins and ezetimibe are cheap generics (Jan Aushadhi); bempedoic acid is affordable in India, while PCSK9 antibodies and inclisiran are reserved for FH and extreme risk in tertiary care. [LAI 2023]`]},{heading:`Complications, prognosis, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Untreated HeFH**: about half of men have coronary disease by 50 and a third of women by 60; early statin treatment brings risk close to that of the general population. [EAS 2023]`,`**Untreated HoFH**: coronary events and aortic stenosis in childhood and death often before 30; survival improves with combined therapy and apheresis. [EAS 2023]`,`**Severe HTG**: recurrent pancreatitis, pancreatic diabetes and exocrine failure; mortality of TG pancreatitis is at least that of other causes. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Monitoring**: lipid profile **4-12 weeks** after each change, then every 6-12 months; ALT before and 8-12 weeks after starting a statin; CK only with symptoms; uric acid on bempedoic acid; creatinine on fenofibrate. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Cascade screening**: test all **first-degree relatives** of an index FH case (lipids, and genetic testing if a variant is known), extending to second-degree relatives; children from age 5. [EAS 2023]`,`**Refer** to a lipid clinic or cardiologist: suspected HoFH, LDL not at target on statin plus ezetimibe, TG persistently over 880 mg/dL or recurrent pancreatitis, suspected FCS, and statin intolerance needing PCSK9 therapy. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Counselling**: lifelong treatment, the inherited nature of FH and the need to test family, no tobacco, regular exercise, Indian-style diet low in saturated fat and refined carbohydrate, and alcohol avoidance in HTG. [LAI 2023]`,`**NP-NCD link**: Ayushman Arogya Mandirs screen adults 30+ for diabetes and hypertension; add a lipid profile at diagnosis, and flag any adult with LDL 190 mg/dL or more or premature MI for FH cascade screening. [NP-NCD 2023]`]},{heading:`Latest guideline updates`,points:[`**ESC/EAS 2025 focused update**: **bempedoic acid** recommended for statin-intolerant patients to reduce events (CLEAR Outcomes); Lp(a) over 50 mg/dL a risk enhancer; only **icosapent ethyl** among omega-3s; supplements (red yeast rice, plant sterols alone) not recommended for event reduction. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**ESC/EAS 2025**: early high-intensity statin plus ezetimibe in ACS and very high risk when LDL is far from target, and evinacumab for HoFH. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**CLEAR Outcomes (NEJM 2023)**: bempedoic acid in 13,970 statin-intolerant patients lowered LDL 21% and major cardiovascular events by **13%**, with more gout and gallstones. [CLEAR Outcomes 2023]`,`**CSI 2024 dyslipidaemia guideline**: LDL under 55 mg/dL for very high risk, upfront combination therapy, non-HDL as co-target and Lp(a) measurement in all - aligned with ESC rather than the stricter LAI targets. [CSI 2024]`,`**LAI 2023**: Indian risk categories with extreme-risk targets of LDL under 50 (A) and 30 or less (B), and routine Lp(a) testing. [LAI 2023]`,`**EAS 2023 HoFH consensus**: diagnosis now leans on phenotype (untreated LDL over 400 mg/dL) as much as genotype; early combination therapy including evinacumab and apheresis. [EAS 2023]`,`**APOC3 silencing**: olezarsen approved by the US FDA in 2024 for familial chylomicronaemia syndrome; plozasiran showed large TG and pancreatitis reductions in phase 3 (2025). [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**LENS trial (2024)**: fenofibrate slowed progression of diabetic retinopathy - a non-cardiac reason to use it in diabetes with retinopathy. [LENS 2024]`]},{heading:`Exam and viva angle`,points:[`**Classic short note**: Dutch Lipid Clinic Network criteria - remember tendon xanthoma 6, arcus before 45 years 4, LDL 330 or more 8, and more than 8 is definite. [DLCN]`,`**Viva**: why does bempedoic acid not cause myopathy? Its activating enzyme (ACSVL1) is present in liver but absent from skeletal muscle, so the drug is inert in muscle. [CLEAR Outcomes 2023]`,`**Viva**: why insulin in non-diabetic TG pancreatitis? Insulin activates lipoprotein lipase and clears chylomicrons. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Common mistake**: diagnosing FH without checking TSH and urine protein, or forgetting to screen the patient's children. [EAS 2023]`,`**Common mistake**: prescribing over-the-counter fish-oil capsules for cardiovascular protection - only pure EPA (icosapent ethyl, 4 g/day) has outcome evidence. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**Common mistake**: combining **gemfibrozil with a statin** (myopathy) - use fenofibrate if a fibrate is needed. [ESC/EAS 2025]`,`**MCQ traps**: Lp(a) is not lowered by statins; Friedewald fails above TG 400; eruptive xanthomas mean TG usually over 1000; types I and V cause pancreatitis. [Harrison 22e]`,`**Drug identification**: inclisiran = siRNA twice yearly; evolocumab and alirocumab = antibodies every 2 weeks; evinacumab = anti-ANGPTL3; olezarsen = anti-APOC3 antisense. [ESC/EAS 2025]`]}],tables:[{heading:`Lipid-lowering drugs beyond statins`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration/titration`,`Key adverse effects / contraindications`],rows:[[`Rosuvastatin / atorvastatin (reference)`,`Rosuvastatin 20-40 mg or atorvastatin 40-80 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong; lipids at 4-12 weeks`,`Myalgia, ALT rise, new diabetes (small); pregnancy contraindication; interactions`],[`Ezetimibe`,`10 mg oral once daily (alone or fixed-dose with statin)`,`Lifelong; LDL down further 20-25%`,`Well tolerated; rare ALT rise with statin; avoid in moderate-severe liver failure`],[`Bempedoic acid`,`180 mg oral once daily (or 180/10 mg with ezetimibe)`,`Lifelong; LDL down 20-25% (about 38% with ezetimibe)`,`Hyperuricaemia and gout, gallstones, small creatinine rise, tendon rupture; avoid with simvastatin over 20 mg`],[`Evolocumab`,`140 mg SC every 2 weeks or 420 mg SC monthly`,`Lifelong; LDL down about 60%`,`Injection-site reaction, nasopharyngitis; cost`],[`Alirocumab`,`75-150 mg SC every 2 weeks (or 300 mg monthly)`,`Lifelong; LDL down 45-60%`,`Injection-site reaction; cost`],[`Inclisiran`,`284 mg SC at day 0, 3 months, then every 6 months (by a health worker)`,`Lifelong; LDL down about 50%`,`Injection-site reaction; outcome trials pending`],[`Icosapent ethyl`,`2 g oral twice daily with food (4 g/day)`,`Lifelong in ASCVD or diabetes with TG 135-499 on statin`,`Atrial fibrillation, bleeding, peripheral oedema; mixed fish oils are not substitutes`],[`Fenofibrate`,`145-160 mg oral once daily (micronised 200 mg)`,`Long term for TG 500 or more; TG down 30-50%`,`Reversible creatinine rise, gallstones, myopathy with statin; avoid if eGFR under 30; never gemfibrozil with statin`],[`Saroglitazar`,`4 mg oral once daily`,`Diabetic dyslipidaemia with high TG (India)`,`Weight gain, oedema; no outcome data`],[`Insulin infusion (TG pancreatitis)`,`Regular insulin 0.1-0.3 units/kg/h IV with 5-10% dextrose`,`Until TG under 500 mg/dL`,`Hypoglycaemia, hypokalaemia; hourly glucose`],[`Evinacumab`,`15 mg/kg IV every 4 weeks`,`HoFH, specialist centre`,`Infusion reactions; teratogenic`],[`Lomitapide`,`5 mg oral daily, titrated to 60 mg`,`HoFH, specialist centre`,`Diarrhoea, hepatic steatosis, ALT rise; strict low-fat diet`]]},{heading:`Dutch Lipid Clinic Network criteria for familial hypercholesterolaemia`,columns:[`Domain`,`Criterion`,`Points`],rows:[[`Family history`,`First-degree relative with premature CAD (men under 55, women under 60) or LDL above 95th centile`,`1`],[`Family history`,`First-degree relative with tendon xanthoma or arcus, or child under 18 with LDL above 95th centile`,`2`],[`Clinical history`,`Premature CAD in the patient`,`2`],[`Clinical history`,`Premature cerebral or peripheral vascular disease`,`1`],[`Examination`,`Tendon xanthoma`,`6`],[`Examination`,`Corneal arcus before 45 years`,`4`],[`LDL (untreated)`,`330 mg/dL or more / 250-329 / 190-249 / 155-189`,`8 / 5 / 3 / 1`],[`DNA`,`Pathogenic variant in LDLR, APOB or PCSK9`,`8`],[`Interpretation`,`More than 8 definite; 6-8 probable; 3-5 possible; under 3 unlikely`,`Total`]]},{heading:`Fredrickson classification and triglyceride severity`,columns:[`Phenotype`,`Lipoprotein raised`,`Typical cause`,`Clinical clue`],rows:[[`I`,`Chylomicrons`,`Familial chylomicronaemia syndrome (LPL, APOC2)`,`Childhood pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas, lipaemia retinalis`],[`IIa`,`LDL`,`FH, polygenic`,`Tendon xanthomas, premature CAD`],[`IIb`,`LDL and VLDL`,`Familial combined hyperlipidaemia`,`Premature CAD, high apoB`],[`III`,`Remnants (IDL)`,`Dysbetalipoproteinaemia (apoE2/E2)`,`Palmar crease and tuberoeruptive xanthomas`],[`IV`,`VLDL`,`Polygenic, diabetes, obesity, alcohol`,`Commonest in Indians; metabolic syndrome`],[`V`,`Chylomicrons and VLDL`,`Multifactorial chylomicronaemia`,`Adult pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas`],[`TG severity`,`Under 150 normal; 150-499 mild-moderate; 500 or more severe; over 880-1000 very severe`,`-`,`Pancreatitis risk rises steeply above 880-1000 mg/dL`]]},{heading:`Differential diagnosis of a very high LDL or TG`,columns:[`Condition`,`Lipid pattern`,`Discriminating feature`,`Key test`],rows:[[`Heterozygous FH`,`LDL usually 190-400 mg/dL`,`Tendon xanthomas, dominant family history, premature CAD`,`DLCN score, genetic test`],[`Homozygous FH`,`LDL over 400 mg/dL untreated`,`Planar xanthomas in childhood, aortic stenosis`,`Both parents FH; genetic test`],[`Polygenic hypercholesterolaemia`,`LDL 190-250 mg/dL`,`No xanthomas, weaker family pattern`,`Polygenic score (research)`],[`Hypothyroidism`,`Raised LDL, sometimes TG`,`Fatigue, weight gain, goitre`,`TSH`],[`Nephrotic syndrome`,`Raised LDL and TG`,`Oedema, frothy urine`,`Urine protein, albumin`],[`Cholestasis`,`Very high total cholesterol (lipoprotein X)`,`Jaundice, pruritus, xanthomas`,`ALP, bilirubin`],[`Familial chylomicronaemia`,`TG over 880 mg/dL from childhood`,`Normal BMI, poor fibrate response`,`Genetic panel, low post-heparin LPL`],[`Multifactorial chylomicronaemia`,`TG over 880 mg/dL in adults`,`Diabetes, alcohol, obesity, drugs`,`HbA1c, alcohol history, drug review`]]},{heading:`Investigations and cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`When`,`Cut-off / interpretation`],rows:[[`LDL cholesterol`,`Every lipid profile`,`190 mg/dL or more: think FH; Friedewald invalid if TG over 400`],[`Non-HDL cholesterol`,`Every profile, especially high TG`,`Target = LDL target plus 30 mg/dL`],[`Apolipoprotein B`,`High TG, diabetes, obesity`,`130 mg/dL or more risk-enhancing`],[`Lp(a)`,`Once in every adult`,`50 mg/dL (about 105-125 nmol/L) or more risk-enhancing; over 180 mg/dL HeFH-equivalent`],[`Triglycerides`,`Every profile`,`500 or more severe; over 880-1000 pancreatitis risk`],[`TSH, urine protein, LFT, HbA1c`,`Before labelling primary dyslipidaemia`,`Exclude secondary causes`],[`CK`,`Muscle symptoms on statin`,`Over 4 times normal: stop; over 10 times: rhabdomyolysis work-up`],[`Uric acid`,`Before and on bempedoic acid`,`Gout risk rises with hyperuricaemia`]]}],redFlags:[`Abdominal pain with TG over 880-1000 mg/dL or milky serum - acute pancreatitis until proven otherwise.`,`Child with planar xanthomas or LDL over 400 mg/dL - suspected homozygous FH, refer urgently.`,`Chest pain, syncope or an ejection systolic murmur in a young person with FH (coronary disease or aortic stenosis).`,`Muscle weakness with dark urine or CK over 10 times normal on a statin - rhabdomyolysis.`,`Proximal weakness with high CK persisting after stopping a statin - anti-HMGCR myopathy.`,`Pregnant woman with TG over 1000 mg/dL or a known chylomicronaemia disorder.`,`Recurrent pancreatitis or TG persistently over 880 mg/dL despite treatment - suspected FCS.`],pearls:[`LDL **190 mg/dL or more** in an adult is FH until proven otherwise - score it and screen the family.`,`**Tendon xanthoma = 6 points**; Achilles tendon thickening is the most specific bedside sign of FH.`,`One FH patient means half the first-degree relatives have it - **cascade screening** finds them.`,`Ladder: **statin, then ezetimibe, then bempedoic acid or PCSK9 agent** - start statin plus ezetimibe together when LDL is far from target.`,`**Bempedoic acid** spares muscle because the activating enzyme is absent in skeletal muscle.`,`**Insulin, not heparin**, for TG pancreatitis; fenofibrate once TG is under 500.`,`Only **pure EPA (icosapent ethyl, 4 g/day)** reduces events; ordinary fish oil does not.`,`Measure **Lp(a) once in a lifetime** - statins do not lower it; PCSK9 antibodies lower it by about a quarter.`,`Most statin myalgia is **nocebo** - rechallenge, alternate-day dosing and add-ons before giving up.`,`Never **gemfibrozil with a statin**.`],mcqs:[{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-q1`,stem:`A 38-year-old untreated man has LDL 260 mg/dL, thickened Achilles tendons with nodules, and a father who had a myocardial infarction at 50. No genetic test has been done. His Dutch Lipid Clinic Network category is:`,options:[`Unlikely FH`,`Possible FH`,`Probable FH`,`Definite FH`],answer:3,explanation:`LDL 250-329 mg/dL scores 5, tendon xanthoma 6 and a first-degree relative with premature CAD 1, a total of 12 - more than 8 is definite FH. Possible (3-5) and probable (6-8) would apply only if the tendon xanthoma were absent, and unlikely is under 3. A genetic test is not needed for a definite clinical diagnosis.`,difficulty:`easy`},{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-q2`,stem:`Why is bempedoic acid unlikely to cause the muscle symptoms seen with statins?`,options:[`It acts only in the intestine by blocking NPC1L1`,`Its activating enzyme, very-long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase-1, is absent in skeletal muscle`,`It is a monoclonal antibody that cannot enter muscle cells`,`It lowers LDL by increasing bile acid excretion`],answer:1,explanation:`Bempedoic acid is a prodrug activated by ACSVL1, which is present in the liver but not in skeletal muscle, so ATP citrate lyase is inhibited only in hepatocytes - the basis of its use in statin intolerance (CLEAR Outcomes). Blocking NPC1L1 describes ezetimibe, a monoclonal antibody describes evolocumab or alirocumab, and raising bile acid excretion describes the sequestrants.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-q3`,stem:`A 35-year-old non-diabetic man who drinks heavily presents with epigastric pain radiating to the back, lipase 8 times normal and milky serum with triglycerides of 2400 mg/dL. Besides fluids and analgesia, which measure most rapidly and safely lowers his triglycerides?`,options:[`IV regular insulin infusion with dextrose`,`IV unfractionated heparin infusion`,`Oral fenofibrate 160 mg started on day 1`,`Oral icosapent ethyl 4 g/day`],answer:0,explanation:`Insulin activates lipoprotein lipase and halves triglycerides within 24-48 hours even in non-diabetics; dextrose prevents hypoglycaemia. Heparin releases LPL briefly, then depletes it and risks haemorrhagic pancreatitis, so it is no longer advised. Fenofibrate and omega-3 act over weeks and are started once the patient eats and TG is falling.`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-q4`,stem:`A 45-year-old woman with premature coronary disease has Lp(a) 190 mg/dL and LDL 95 mg/dL on rosuvastatin 20 mg. Which of these drugs also lowers her Lp(a)?`,options:[`Increasing rosuvastatin to 40 mg`,`Ezetimibe 10 mg daily`,`Evolocumab 140 mg every 2 weeks`,`Fenofibrate 160 mg daily`],answer:2,explanation:`PCSK9 monoclonal antibodies such as evolocumab lower Lp(a) by about 25-30% while cutting LDL by about 60%, and part of their benefit in FOURIER was linked to the Lp(a) fall. Statins do not lower Lp(a) and may raise it slightly, ezetimibe has little effect on it, and fenofibrate acts on triglycerides, not Lp(a).`,difficulty:`moderate`},{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-q5`,stem:`Two months after starting atorvastatin 40 mg after an MI, a 58-year-old man reports diffuse aches in both thighs. CK and TSH are normal and LDL has fallen from 160 to 85 mg/dL. What is the best next step?`,options:[`Stop all statins permanently and start fenofibrate`,`Continue atorvastatin 40 mg and add colchicine for the pain`,`Stop for 2-4 weeks, then rechallenge with a low-dose or alternate-day statin and add ezetimibe`,`Check anti-HMGCR antibodies and start prednisolone`],answer:2,explanation:`With normal CK and TSH most symptoms are nocebo or mild statin myalgia, so the statin is paused, symptoms reassessed and a different or lower-dose (or alternate-day) statin restarted, with ezetimibe added to reach the post-MI target. Stopping statins permanently and substituting fenofibrate throws away proven benefit, colchicine raises myopathy risk with statins, and anti-HMGCR myopathy is suspected only when weakness and high CK persist after stopping.`,difficulty:`hard`}],theory:[{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-t1`,paper:`I`,kind:`long`,marks:10,minutes:18,frequency:`common`,question:`Define familial hypercholesterolaemia. Discuss its genetics, diagnosis (including the Dutch Lipid Clinic Network criteria), cascade screening and management, with a note on non-statin lipid-lowering drugs.`,openingLines:[`Familial hypercholesterolaemia is an autosomal codominant disorder of LDL receptor-mediated clearance (LDLR, APOB or PCSK9 variants) causing lifelong high LDL, tendon xanthomas and premature coronary disease.`,`Heterozygous FH affects about 1 in 250 people, yet fewer than 1 in 10 are diagnosed; an LDL of 190 mg/dL or more in an adult should prompt the diagnosis.`],answer:[{heading:`Definition, genetics and burden`,points:[`LDLR (most), APOB, gain-of-function PCSK9; HeFH 1 in 250, HoFH 1 in 300,000.`,`Untreated HeFH: coronary disease before 55 in men and 60 in women; HoFH in childhood.`]},{heading:`Pathophysiology`,points:[`Fewer functional LDL receptors double LDL from birth; cumulative LDL burden brings atherosclerosis decades early.`,`PCSK9 degrades LDL receptors - target of antibodies and inclisiran.`]},{heading:`Clinical features`,points:[`Premature MI, family history, tendon xanthomas (Achilles, knuckles), arcus before 45, xanthelasma.`,`HoFH: planar xanthomas in childhood, aortic stenosis, LDL over 400 mg/dL.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Exclude hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, cholestasis, drugs.`,`DLCN: tendon xanthoma 6, arcus under 45 4, LDL 330+ 8 / 250-329 5 / 190-249 3 / 155-189 1, mutation 8; more than 8 definite.`,`Simon Broome criteria; genetic testing where available; Lp(a) once.`]},{heading:`Cascade screening`,points:[`Test all first-degree relatives, then second-degree; children from age 5.`,`Statin in children from 8-10 years; LDL under 135 mg/dL after age 10.`]},{heading:`Management`,points:[`Lifestyle, no tobacco; high-intensity statin plus ezetimibe upfront.`,`Targets: LDL under 70 mg/dL (FH without ASCVD), under 50-55 with ASCVD (LAI/CSI).`,`Add PCSK9 antibody (evolocumab 140 mg SC 2-weekly), inclisiran 284 mg 6-monthly or bempedoic acid 180 mg daily.`,`HoFH: evinacumab, lomitapide, lipoprotein apheresis.`]},{heading:`Non-statin drugs - evidence`,points:[`Ezetimibe (IMPROVE-IT), evolocumab (FOURIER), alirocumab (ODYSSEY), bempedoic acid (CLEAR Outcomes, 13% fewer events).`,`Pregnancy: stop lipid drugs 1-3 months before conception; bile acid sequestrants safe.`]}],mustDraw:[`LDL receptor cycle showing statin, ezetimibe, PCSK9 antibody, inclisiran and bempedoic acid sites of action`,`DLCN scoring table`,`Cascade screening flow chart from the index case`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and genetics`,marks:1.5},{part:`Clinical features`,marks:1.5},{part:`DLCN and diagnosis`,marks:2},{part:`Cascade screening`,marks:1.5},{part:`Management and non-statin drugs`,marks:3.5}],keywords:[`familial hypercholesterolaemia`,`DLCN`,`cascade screening`,`ezetimibe`,`PCSK9`,`inclisiran`,`bempedoic acid`]},{id:`cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia-special-t2`,paper:`I`,kind:`short-note`,marks:5,minutes:9,frequency:`less-common`,question:`Write a short note on hypertriglyceridaemia-induced acute pancreatitis.`,openingLines:[`Hypertriglyceridaemia causes acute pancreatitis when triglycerides exceed about 880-1000 mg/dL, when chylomicrons plug pancreatic capillaries and lipase releases toxic free fatty acids.`,`It is the third commonest cause of acute pancreatitis after gallstones and alcohol, and is over-represented in pregnancy and uncontrolled diabetes.`],answer:[{heading:`Causes`,points:[`Multifactorial chylomicronaemia (diabetes, alcohol, obesity, drugs, pregnancy) far commoner than familial chylomicronaemia syndrome.`,`Drugs: oestrogens, tamoxifen, steroids, retinoids, thiazides, protease inhibitors, antipsychotics.`]},{heading:`Diagnosis`,points:[`Pain, lipase over 3 times normal; milky serum; eruptive xanthomas, lipaemia retinalis.`,`Amylase may be falsely normal; pseudohyponatraemia.`]},{heading:`Acute management`,points:[`IV fluids, analgesia, nil fat; insulin 0.1-0.3 units/kg/h with dextrose.`,`Plasmapheresis for severe cases with organ failure or in pregnancy; avoid heparin.`]},{heading:`Prevention of recurrence`,points:[`Fenofibrate 145-160 mg once TG under 500; fat under 15-20% of calories, MCT oil, no alcohol.`,`Control diabetes, stop offending drugs; olezarsen for FCS.`]}],mustDraw:[`Flow chart: TG pancreatitis - fluids, insulin-dextrose, plasmapheresis if severe, then fenofibrate and diet`],markSplit:[{part:`Definition and mechanism`,marks:1},{part:`Causes`,marks:1},{part:`Diagnosis`,marks:1},{part:`Management and prevention`,marks:2}],keywords:[`hypertriglyceridaemia`,`chylomicronaemia`,`pancreatitis`,`insulin infusion`,`plasmapheresis`,`fenofibrate`]}],references:[`Harrison's Principles of Internal Medicine, 22nd edition, 2025 - disorders of lipoprotein metabolism`,`Braunwald's Heart Disease, 12th edition, 2022 - lipoprotein disorders and cardiovascular disease`,`Mach F et al. 2025 Focused Update of the 2019 ESC/EAS Guidelines for the management of dyslipidaemias, Eur Heart J 2025`,`Cardiological Society of India clinical practice guidelines for dyslipidaemia management, 2024; Lipid Association of India expert consensus statement on management of dyslipidaemia, 2023`,`Cuchel M et al. Homozygous familial hypercholesterolaemia: European Atherosclerosis Society consensus statement, Eur Heart J 2023; Kronenberg F et al. EAS consensus statement on lipoprotein(a), Eur Heart J 2022`,`Nissen SE et al. Bempedoic acid and cardiovascular outcomes in statin-intolerant patients (CLEAR Outcomes), N Engl J Med 2023`,`Banach M et al. ILEP statin intolerance recommendations 2022; Cheeley MK et al. NLA scientific statement on statin intolerance, J Clin Lipidol 2022`,`Goodman and Gilman's The Pharmacological Basis of Therapeutics, 14th edition, 2023`]});var f=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a,...o,...s,...c,...l,...u,...d];export{f as default};